cs -- is hosting the seventh -- look at her, the 79th Annual Tony Awards this Sunday on CBS, and so we are so happy that she`s joining us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Hi!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did I mention we are so happy? Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When I heard you were doing this, I thought, lucky us that you agreed to host the Tonys.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So when they came out to you, I`m assuming it was immediate yes, was it?\par \par \uc2 PINK: It was an almost immediate yes. It was a yes, but or yes, and let me get permission from Willow, because this is her lane. I try to stay out of her lane. She`s all about Broadway. She goes to Performing Arts High School, and she was like, "Do I get a seat?" I was like, "I think so." So, here we go.\par \par \uc2 Also, it`s -- yes, I`m in a yes place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does this mean, I`m in a yes place? What does that mean exactly?\par \par \uc2 PINK: I`ve always just been a touring artist. I tour, I put my head down, and I beat the pavement, and I do it all over the world and back again a thousand times, and I don`t really do a lot of other things.\par \par \uc2 And this year was, I was like, you know what, we`re following Willow to New York City.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: I`m going to just say yes to things that I wouldn`t normally say yes to.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talk about the decision, I love that you guys have done this, to follow Willow to New York City. Why? What`s her dream?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Her dream is to be on Broadway. She is musical theater obsessed. She has followed me around the world her whole life, on and off tour. And I told her, it`s your turn, we`ll follow you, and that`s what we do for our kids. We show up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: If we can, if we can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: And I`m lucky that we can. And she`s phenomenal. And I would tell -- she`s very talented, and she`s also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you are just saying that because you`re her mother.\par \par \uc2 PINK: No, she`s no -- I would be -- I would be like, you can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you say that if --\par \par \uc2 PINK: I would say it in a nicer way? But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How would you say it?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Maybe! Also science.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You know Dentistry is good.\par \par \uc2 PINK: She`s more talented than I ever was at her age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where were you guys watching the Tonys last year?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Last year, we were at home, Willow was watching the Tonys on television from the coffee table. She stood up on the coffee table, she was dressed in her Gatsby costume that she designed herself on tour, which is four quadrants, the four main characters of Great Gatsby. It`s adorable, and she was just bright-eyed and bushy-tailed, and now she`ll be in the seat watching.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes watching mom.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Making fun of her mama.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Watching mom, also watching the show. What are you most excited about? Because number one everybody judges from the opening number, Pink.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you have plans for that?\par \par \uc2 PINK: It is goofy and over the top, and it`s sort of a nod back, it`ll all make sense, and there`s going to be 170 people on stage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PINK: It`s going to be so fun, and I`m an idiot from the very first moment, which was very important to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To make fun of yourself?\par \par \uc2 PINK: To make fun of myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why -- why did you want to do that?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Because I haven`t been on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 PINK: And I wanted to just speak to the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You want people to know, I know I haven`t been on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 PINK: I know, I know I haven`t been here. I know that I`m an absolute idiot. And my whole point this year is to celebrate all of you, and we just - we`re just going to have fun. We`re going to have so much fun. I`m excited!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am, too. I am, too. What are you most looking forward to of the evening? Because you know they always think there`s got to be surprises. They always think there has got to be --\par \par \uc2 PINK: There are so many surprises\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- that`s unexpected.\par \par \uc2 PINK: There`s a lot of really cool anniversaries. "The Book of Mormon," and there`s "Chorus Line." The "In Memoriam" is going to be amazing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because whoever is doing that, that will be good\par \par \uc2 PINK: And also all the shows that are on this year, it is going to be a really fun year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love Broadway, I love a good musical, I love the it can transform you and take you to a totally different place, and all the kids, just think of all the kids, like your daughter, who was sitting there dreaming of one day, I want to do this.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, you know, she gets this opportunity that you know, a lot of people think I would kill to do this.\par \par \uc2 PINK: I want theater to be available for every kid, because I think more than most things are our art classes, and our theater classes are the first things being stripped from our schools, and I find those to be the only places in towns and cities where kids can be goofy together, and it`s -- and they can be in a safe space, and they can be fully themselves, or they can be fully something else other than themselves, and they come together as this group of supportive, nobody`s a weirdo, and it`s just the most wholesome, beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 I want it for every single kid, and that`s again why Broadway is always -- theater has always been really important to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you just say, will there be any acrobatic moments?\par \par \uc2 PINK: There will be acrobatic moments, but not in the way that you would normally see me doing acrobatic moments.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, I don`t know what that means.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Neither do I yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know what that means.\par \par \uc2 PINK: But I know there is a harness or two.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh boy! I don`t know what that means. Just very excited, cheering you on. I can`t wait for Sunday.\par \par \uc2 PINK: Me, too!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I cannot wait.\par \par \uc2 PINK: It`s going to be awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I cannot.\par \par \uc2 You can watch Pink host the 79th Annual Tony Awards live this Sunday. Tune in at 8:00 Eastern, 5:00 Pacific, right here in streaming on where, Pink?\par \par \uc2 PINK: CBS and Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260602em6200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 U.S. Launches New Strikes on Iran; DOJ Criminal Investigation Targets E. Jean Carroll; More Deaths Confirmed in Chemical Tank Implosion; Jill Biden Scared by Joe`s Debate Performance; Rubio: No Ebola Patients Allowed into U.S.; Mission to Save Miners in Laos Cave; CIA Official Accused of Stealing $40 Million in Gold; \uc2 Google\uc2  Engineer Arrested for \uc2 Polymarket\uc2  Trades; GPS Data Shakes Up Bahama Missing Woman Case; Community Backlash to Growing A.I. Data Centers; James Talarico on Crucial Senate Showdown; Meta Rolling Out Paid Social Subscriptions; Dealing with High- Functioning Burnout; America 250; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Joshua Henry on Broadway Revival of "Ragtime;" \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Vladimir Duthiers, Adriana Diaz, Imtiaz Tyab, Jericka  Duncan, Carter Evans, Weijia Jiang, Mark Strassmann, Matt Gutman, Rob  Marciano, Kelly O`Grady, Cristian Benavides, Jo Ling Kent, Ed O`Keefe \par \uc2 15,037 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to get you started on this Thursday, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And I`m Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: New American attacks against Iran, and the Iranians say they hit back.\par \par \uc2 Plus, another critic of President Trump is targeted by the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): Iran is very much intent, they want very much to make a deal. we`re not satisfied with it, but we will be. We will be either that or we`ll have to just finish the job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The U.S. launches new air strikes against Iran. Could they jeopardize the peace talks?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Justice Department investigates Trump accuser, E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: If the DOJ were to bring a case, I think it would face significant legal hurdles.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Jill Biden speaks exclusively to CBS News about the debate that helped end her husband`s reelection bid.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN (Former First Lady of the United States of America): As I watched it, I thought, "Oh my God, he`s having a stroke."\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: A drone light show over the city of Windsor in the U.K. tells the story of Peter Pan in the night sky.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: There are nine spellers left in the 2026 Scripps National Spelling Bee.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN BAILEY (Scripps National Spelling Bee contestant): I-Z-A Jatrorrhiza.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And correct!\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: That`s a tricky word, Jatrorrhiza --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: It`s not a real word.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: It`s a real word. Everything in the dictionary is four --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: No, they made that one up.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Yes, it is --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: It`s made up.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You may remember this image from earlier in the \uc2 Knicks\uc2  playoff run.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Timothee Chalamet is in the hot seat for how he took a seat.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Tina Fey settles an important debate. Is Chalamet a man spreader?\par \par \uc2 TINA FEY (Comedian): I got a big old can. Timothee`s legs took the front. My big old can was taken aback. We had no beef.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve got a big can, too. We love all things, Tina Fey, don`t we?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We love Tina Fey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love everything about her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But man spreading, you know, many, many years ago on this show, I did a piece on man spreading.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Did you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. I remember I did that story --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do remember that, yes. I do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- where I went on the subway, and I asked, why are guys -- like, why do men sit like that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And what did you find in your original report?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In my original reporting, I found that men were just like -- you know, what men say, oh, you know, need a lot of room, you know and it was just like, come on, Chalamet, bring it in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bring it in!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bring it in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with some breaking news overnight at the request of Jon Tower. More strikes by the U.S. against Iran.\par \par \uc2 It`s the second round of attacks this week, and it could put those peace talks in big jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re also following fallout from President Trump`s comments during a Cabinet meeting on Wednesday, where he threatened to bomb one of our allies.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab has the latest from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, good morning to you. What is Iran saying about all of this?\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Gayle, a very good morning to you, too.\par \par \uc2 Well, Iran`s Revolutionary Guard has said it has targeted an American air base after a U.S. attack overnight near one of its coastal airports. Now, the IRGC did not say which U.S. base it had targeted, but warned of a more decisive response to come.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: As I`ve said a thousand times, their Navy is gone, their Air Force is gone, everything is gone, and they`re negotiating on fumes.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: That was President Trump`s claims at yesterday`s White House Cabinet meeting, but this morning, Iran says it fired at a U.S. air base in retaliation, Tehran said, for a U.S. strike overnight near Bandar Abbas Airport on its western coast.\par \par \uc2 Kuwait, which hosts a large U.S. Army base, today said it intercepted missile and drone threats without saying where it came from.\par \par \uc2 The flare-up follows the Pentagon`s announcement on Monday, U.S. forces carried out "defensive strikes" on Iranian missile launch sites and mine lane boats in Southern Iran.\par \par \uc2 Iranian state T.V. later broadcast this video of what it says is the Revolutionary Guard tracking and firing at a U.S. F-35 jet and downing an MQ-9 Reaper drone.\par \par \uc2 We have no way of independently verifying the claims, but as Washington and Tehran continue to negotiate the future of the Strait of Hormuz after imposing dueling blockades on it and sending global energy prices soaring, President Trump appeared to threaten a key ally.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Nobody is going to control it, it is international waters, and Oman will behave just like everybody else, or we will have to blow them up.\par \par \uc2 They understand that, they`ll be fine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now, it is unclear whether the President meant that the U.S. would take military action against former mediator, Oman, but the growing frequency of what appears to be tit-for-tat U.S. and Iranian strikes continues to raise serious questions about just how durable this ceasefire really is -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Imtiaz Tyab in Tel Aviv, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We have breaking news overnight on another critic of President Trump being targeted by his administration. The Justice Department has started a criminal investigation of E. Jean Carroll, the author who sued Trump. She won a pair of multi-million dollar settlements in connection with an alleged sexual assault in the 90s.\par \par \uc2 Our national correspondent, Jericka Duncan is here.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 This investigation, led by the U.S. Attorney`s Office for the Northern District of Illinois, really focuses on whether E. Jean Carroll lied in a 2022 deposition when she said she received no outside funding to pay for her lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: E. Jean Carroll sued President Donald Trump twice, winning both civil judgments accusing him of sexual abuse and defamation, but after her two legal victories with juries awarding Carroll nearly $90 million in damages, the Justice Department is investigating whether the former magazine columnist committed perjury.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell CBS News the criminal investigation hinges on a 2022 deposition where Carroll said she received no outside funding, though it was later revealed that billionaire, Reid Hoffman had paid some legal fees and expenses.\par \par \uc2 The Second Circuit U.S. Court of Appeals accepted Carroll`s explanation that she had simply forgotten about the outside funding and found it did not reflect poorly on her credibility, as she was not involved in the matter of who was or was not funding the costs of her litigation.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: I do not think that this is a strong case for perjury. What you have is a situation where there was a misstatement in a deposition.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: CBS News legal contributor, Jessica Levinson.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: One argument that E. Jean Carroll would make is that this is just part of a pattern of President Trump going after political adversaries, not about any facts and law that would give rise to a perjury charge.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: After a jury found Trump liable for sexual abuse and defamation in 2023, he continued to deny Carroll`s claims against him.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I have no idea who she is, where she came from. This is another scam. It`s a political witch hunt.\par \par \uc2 It`s a rigged deal. It`s a made-up, fabricated story.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Carroll sued again, saying Trump`s denials were defamatory. We spoke to her in 2024 after a jury awarded her $83 million.\par \par \uc2 E. JEAN CARROLL (Donald Trump accuser): If Donald Trump needs to use me again to raise campaign funds, he will, I think, he will do it. He`s just using us, and if he needs us, he will again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, a source tells CBS News, Acting Attorney General Todd Blanche, who represented Trump in some of the litigation, has recused himself from the case. CBS News has asked the U.S. Attorney`s Office for the Northern District of Illinois for comment on the investigation, as well as Roberta Kaplan, the attorney who represented Carroll in the two lawsuits. We have not heard back -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Jericka, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the latest on the deadly chemical disaster at a paper mill in Washington State.\par \par \uc2 Newly released video shows the immediate aftermath of the implosion, which killed at least two people. Nine others are missing. Tragically, they are also presumed to have died.\par \par \uc2 A federal investigation is now underway.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans is near the scene. Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 The catastrophic chemical tank failure occurred early Tuesday morning, and it was right after a shift change. There were lots of people in the area, and this morning, several families are still waiting to learn the fate of their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOV. BOB FERGUSON (D-WA): We`re bracing ourselves for this being the deadliest industrial tragedy in modern Washington state history.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: With nine people still missing and presumed dead, this is now a recovery mission in Longview, Washington, where a tank filled with a deadly chemical mix ruptured at a paper mill Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WOOD (Nippon Dynawave Packaging Co. Director of Support Services): These are our people. We are focused on our people. We are focused on helping our responders find and recover those people.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: This is the aftermath. A devastating scene crushed vehicles and overturned trucks. On Wednesday, authorities confirmed a second fatality. Eight others were injured, including a firefighter.\par \par \uc2 Among those killed, Dillon Miller, the sole breadwinner for his fiance and their three children; and Gilbert Bernal, an electrician at the facility. His family described him as an incredible man and an inspiration.\par \par \uc2 The family of Jared Ammons believe he was also killed, according to their GoFundMe page. He is survived by his wife, two kids, and another on the way.\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL MOLDENHAUER (Longview, Washington resident): I want to know why these families have lost these loved ones. What could have been done? What do we need to investigate?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Crystal Moldenhauer says she knows some of the missing and wants answers.\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL MOLDENHAUER: I`m seeing wives being devastated over losing their -- the love of their life. We have small children that have lost their dads, parents that have lost children.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And on my way here to Washington, I met a couple that was headed to see their son in the Burn Unit. They tell me he has severe chemical burns over 25 percent of his body -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh man! That`s tough to hear. Carter, thank you so much. Hope he`s going to be all right.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, former First Lady Jill Biden is talking about how she felt watching the 2024 debate that we all watched that forced then President Joe Biden to abandon his reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 In an exclusive interview with "CBS Sunday Morning," she says she thought her husband was having a stroke. Weijia Jiang has more from The White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN (Former First Lady of the United States of America): I was frightened because I had never ever seen Joe like that before or since.\par \par \uc2 RITA BRAVER: Never since?\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Yes, never.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In a new interview with Rita Braver on "CBS Sunday Morning," former First Lady Jill Biden revealed what was going through her mind as she watched this --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN (Former President of the United States of America): with the COVID -- excuse me with --\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Former President Biden stumbled through an answer while debating then candidate Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN: Dealing with everything we have to do with -- look, if -- we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 RITA BRAVER: What happened?\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: I don`t know what happened. I mean, when I -- as I watched it, I thought, "Oh my God, he`s having a stroke," and it scared me to death.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But shortly after the debate, she had a very different reaction.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN: Joe, you did such a great job, you answered every question. You knew all the facts.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Former President Biden blamed a cold and jet lag, insisting the episode was a one-off, but pressed if he would prove it with a cognitive test, Biden said.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN (Former President of the United States of America): I have a cognitive test every single day, every day, I get a test.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The shaky debate performance only amplified concerns about the 81-year-old`s mental fitness, and just 24 days later, Biden bowed out of the race.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: That left only a little more than three months for his Vice President, Kamala Harris, to take on President Trump. A long-awaited new report from the Democratic National Committee does not address Biden`s decision to run at his age, while there were questions about his health. A spokesman for the former President says he has nothing to add to his wife`s new comments -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Weijia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 You can see more of that interview with former First Lady Jill Biden this weekend on "Sunday Morning."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Trump administration is making a big policy change in the fight against Ebola amid growing concerns about a deadly outbreak in Central Africa. Secretary of State Marco Rubio says no Ebola patients will be allowed into the U.S. including Americans exposed to the virus.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Mark, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Hey, Vlad! Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Today, New York`s JFK Airport joins airports here in Atlanta, Houston, and Washington, D.C. with enhanced Ebola screening. They`re looking for arriving international passengers who have passed through three East African countries. No one wants the Ebola crisis there to spread here.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This week, Houston`s Bush International became one of several U.S. airports playing defense against Ebola. All Americans returning from three African countries will undergo mandatory enhanced screening.\par \par \uc2 This Houston airport expects to average up to 50 screened passengers a day.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (U.S. Secretary of State): We cannot and will not allow any cases of Ebola to enter the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On Wednesday, multiple administration officials told CBS News the Trump administration will set up a quarantine facility for Americans in Kenya. A former CDC official talked to CBS News, calling that move unethical and irresponsible because Kenya doesn`t have the proper containment facility or much experience dealing with Ebola.\par \par \uc2 One infected American missionary, Dr. Peter Stafford, has responded well in a Berlin hospital to antiviral and supportive treatments. Six other Americans have been transported to Germany and the Czech Republic for monitoring.\par \par \uc2 In another sign of the growing African crisis, Uganda closed its border with the Democratic Republic of Congo. That`s where the outbreak has been the worst. South Sudan is also worrisome.\par \par \uc2 The WHO has declared the deadly outbreak a public health emergency of international concern. For now, there is no vaccine.\par \par \uc2 JEAN KASEYA (Director general of Africa Centres for Disease Control and Prevention): Now, what we are doing, we are accelerating with the vaccine. Africa CDC is part of the team that is working on the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Another potential Ebola worry, the upcoming World Cup, hosted by the U.S. Thousands of arriving fans and players, and it`s two weeks away -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s coming up, Mark. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We have new information on the desperate mission to rescue seven gold miners from a flooded cave in Southeast Asia. I can`t get enough of this story. Five of the men trapped for more than a week were found alive on Wednesday. Rescuers are still looking for the other two. The question now is, how to get the five men out safely.\par \par \uc2 Chief correspondent, Matt Gutman has an update from the lead rescue diver.\par \par \uc2 Matt, good morning. What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Hey, good morning, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 I just got off the phone with him, and they are still not sure how they`re going to get them out. It`s a five-hour round trip inside to the miners and back, and they`ve brought them food by this point, but he told us that originally he and his partner had expected to find bodies, not living men, and so when they saw these five faces looking back at them in the dark after eight days, there were smiles and tears.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Norrased Palasing/Metra Tham Kalasin Rescue)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: In that cave flooded with water and drained of hope in recent days, the spark of discovery bringing tears and joy.\par \par \uc2 Divers finally locating five of the seven missing artisanal miners from a nearby village. They were perched there on that narrow stone shelf in an oxygen-starved chamber.\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mikko Paasi)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: The men had become trapped in this cave in Central Laos for the past eight days after monsoon rains flooded the entrance and many of the passages. The man you see here is Mikko Paasi, the lead rescue diver, describing the moment they found them in an interview this morning.\par \par \uc2 MIKKO PAASI (Lead rescue diver): Yes, it was emotional, when they realized -- when they realized that we are there to get them out. There was a crying going on, and there was lots of smiles, happy smiles.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: We also spoke with him right after the discovery yesterday, while he was still inside a chamber in the cave, the rescuers had rigged up Wi-Fi.\par \par \uc2 MIKKO PAASI: I`m getting ready for another dive to bring some food for the guys and supplies so they can survive while we think about how to bring them out.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Okay, so that`s the plan, is to bring them food and just keep them alive for now until you can figure it out.\par \par \uc2 MIKKO PAASI: Yes, that and, it`s all a mystery still how, but we got -- we got some ideas.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And outside the cave, rapture. Members of the search team cheering and hugging.\par \par \uc2 And late Wednesday, in Laos, Passe and his team plunged back into the murk in search of the other two miners.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any idea where the other two are?\par \par \uc2 MIKKO PAASI: Not yet, not yet. We need to, we need to concentrate on the five first.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: In those spaces, they have been feeling their way by fingers, squeezing into passages entombed in jagged rock. In some places, they had to shimmy in and drag their scuba tanks after them, struggling for breath.\par \par \uc2 MIKKO PAASI: I think that`s a tricky thing that they asked, that like, are you going to take us out now? You can`t really answer this. You can`t. You don`t want to break their trust or give them too much hope or too little hope.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MIKKO PAASI: But this is a question that is very hard to answer for that, because you don`t even know the answer yourself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: So, Mikko, that lead diver also told us hope for finding the other two is fading at this point. They`d hope to be able to pump the water out of the cave and have the miners walk and crawl out, but that doesn`t seem to be working. So, in the coming days, they`re going to see if they can teach them how to scuba dive, which could actually be very dangerous for the miners and the rescuers.\par \par \uc2 But what`s clear now is that they`re going to be there for at least a couple of additional days -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Matt, you`re getting amazing information on this story. Let`s all hope it turns out as well as it can at this particular point. It`s just great to see them alive. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: That is what we are hoping for.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes, we are. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Severe flash flooding has moved north. CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is tracking more storms in the South today.\par \par \uc2 Okay, Rob, what do you see?\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Well, Ohio, Indiana, yesterday had that flooding you talked about, and Virginia had some storm reports. We`re luckily getting the rain out of Texas today, but you see it in Oklahoma and Kansas, Louisiana getting up to Alabama and Georgia throughout the day today, and then tomorrow as well.\par \par \uc2 So this is where we`re going to see the heavier rains, two to five inches of additional rainfall. You`ve already seen a wet week that`s going to continue, it looks like at least in Georgia through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, speaking of weekends, we`ve had a string of bad ones. Now, the low dropping down into the Northeast, Boston looks pretty bad on Saturday. See the way it`s going to be chilly from New York down to Philly.\par \par \uc2 That`s checking on what`s happening nationally. Time now for your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, we`ve got new reporting on the case of an American woman missing in the Bahamas. It is raising fresh questions about her husband`s version of events.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the backlash against the data centers that power the A.I. Revolution. Why some Americans are protesting against them.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of A.I., we will show you how the NBA is changing the way its games are called.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. it is 7:30. That means it`s time to look at some of the other headlines that "Caught Our Eye" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady is here, ready to go.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I am ready to go, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 All right, good morning to all of you.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start with the former CIA official who`s been arrested after the FBI allegedly discovered $40 million worth of gold bars at his home, okay.\par \par \uc2 So investigators say that`s not all. David Rush is accused of lying about his credentials for years. They say he made false claims about degrees and military service. He allegedly asked for the gold for work reasons, but is accused of taking it for himself. His attorney has declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Next up, now to another serious allegation. A software engineer at Google is accused of illegally making more than a million dollars on Polymarket with insider information.\par \par \uc2 Now, officials say he bet on which celebrities would make Google`s most searched list in 2025. Because of his job, he already knew that info weeks before the public. The engineer, who lives in Switzerland, is now out on bond. No comment from his attorneys.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to a terrible turkey at a cafe in Australia. You`ve got to check this out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Maria Ghobrial/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Oh my goodness! The owner says the bird is a regular visitor who likes to look for food scraps, but on this day, she says her feathered foe was "ready for war."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What? No gravy!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Nope. Very upset there was no gravy, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I love how Australians say "no." No!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But for her to be a regular visitor, she didn`t seem happy to see her.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Well, yes. Look at what happened in that kitchen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I will be a little scared.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, finally I want to stick with the animal theme. A canoe team in California got quite the surprise when an unexpected passenger hopped on board in the middle of a race. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Alan Lucknow/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Unfortunately, for the sea lion, all the seats were taken.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yup!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: But see that girl did not miss a beat. I mean, look at that in the front row. It was like, okay, no, I`ve got a job to do.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She shakes it off, and we`re rowing!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pretty cool!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes. That is terrifying. Could you imagine?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is pretty scary. I mean, just out of nowhere, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That would be scary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But everybody is okay. Thank you, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Did they win?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Including the sea lion.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I hope they won. Yes. We`ll have to follow up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Thanks, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got an important development in the search for a Michigan woman in the Bahamas. You remember this story, it could shake up her husband`s story that she fell overboard during a boat trip back in April.\par \par \uc2 So, a source tells CBS News the GPS data does not match the boat`s reported movements that day. Cristian Benavides is covering the story for us.\par \par \uc2 Cristian, this is an interesting turn. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This is the most significant development in the search for Lynette Hooker so far. It comes as authorities are asking for family members to provide DNA, which could help identify the 55-year-old mother in the event that a body is found.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: U.S. authorities are narrowing the search for Lynette Hooker`s body, focusing on a previously unexamined area of Bahamian waters identified in GPS data from an electronic device seized by investigators.\par \par \uc2 LYNETTE HOOKER (Missing Michigan woman): We thought we`d try to be in a sailboat today.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The new forensic evidence appears to undercut Brian Hooker`s account of his wife`s disappearance, according to an official familiar with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Now, Coast Guard investigators are seeking Bahamian permission to send a dive team to the location.\par \par \uc2 LYNETTE HOOKER: A little dinghy sunset.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Hooker previously told authorities he and his wife were on their dinghy going back to their sailboat named Soulmate on the night of April 4th, but rough waters knocked Lynette into the sea and left him drifting for more than eight hours.\par \par \uc2 In the days that followed, Hooker shared these maps with friends and searchers, marking where he says his wife went overboard and his path.\par \par \uc2 When you think back to those maps, what`s your response?\par \par \uc2 BLAINE STEVENSON (Friend): Well, the maps were his story. That doesn`t necessarily mean it was the truth.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Blaine and Marnie Stevenson were friends with the Hookers. in early 2024, Lynette told Marnie about marital troubles out at sea.\par \par \uc2 MARNIE STEVENSON (Friend): I`m hoping this is a step in the right direction for justice for Lynette.\par \par \uc2 BLAINE STEVENSON: I have faith that following their GPS coordinates, they`re going to find something. Is it everything we`re going to want? I don`t know, but hopefully we can find enough to know what happened.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Hooker denies any wrongdoing. He was held for questioning in the Bahamas, but told CBS News this after he released without charges.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that Lynette is still alive?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN HOOKER (Lynette Hooker`s husband): I do. I believe I`ve been told that people have lasted in the Bahamas after falling overboard for days and even weeks. Of course, you think about alternatives to that, but I`m not really capable of just turning away from this.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: It`s not yet clear when the Coast Guard will actually return to the Bahamas. Now, CBS News has learned that evidence that was seized from the sailboat is also being analyzed at an FBI lab in Quantico, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 We did reach out to Brian Hooker`s Michigan attorney who has refused to answer our calls -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Cristian, you`ve been on the story from the beginning, excellent reporting, my friend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the rising backlash against data centers powering the A.I. boom. Why many people think they are bad neighbors. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This morning, we`re looking at the controversy over data centers in communities across America. They`re filled with servers and other equipment that power the internet and artificial intelligence. The Pew Research Center says that the U.S. already has 3,000 of them, and there are plans to build 1,500 more, but there have been protests in some areas because these facilities use a lot of water and power.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Nationwide, there`s a growing backlash to data centers, those sprawling warehouse-like buildings housing computers and servers that power A.I. and the internet.\par \par \uc2 And now, some government leaders say they`re taking action.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MIKIE SHERRILL (D-NJ): Today, I`m stepping up to establish clear guardrails for data centers in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In New Jersey, on Wednesday, Governor Mikie Sherrill introduced a new plan that would require data centers to "pay their own way" to contract directly with power generators and shell out for their own grid upgrades.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, Governor Josh Shapiro announced similar new standards. Meantime, last week, New York Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez called out water quality problems in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): This is the current drinking water in Morgan County, Georgia, right after a data center was constructed -- the Meta data center was constructed.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Meta says, in part, that an independent study found that "... our data center operations and construction had no impact on the residents."\par \par \uc2 And some tech companies say data centers often bring jobs and revenue into communities, but the mounting pressure comes as 78 percent of Americans say they`re somewhat or very concerned that new data centers being built will send their energy bills up, and one estimate predicts that data centers could use 12 percent of all electricity consumed in the U.S. by 2028.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER BORICK (Muhlenberg College): The real question always comes down, why is it their communities that might have to take on the lion`s share of the negatives or the costs with that --\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Christopher Borick of Muhlenberg College calls it a rare national issue that has brought together Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER BORICK: If you go to these local meetings, you will see individuals that might describe themselves as MAGA in the same partnership, if you will, with people that are very progressive in their politics, and that`s forcing the hand of elected officials.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jo Ling Kent.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very interesting debate having there between artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, love to argue with the refs on our T.V.s while watching the NBA? Well, could we soon be arguing with artificial intelligence instead?\par \par \uc2 Plus, the woman accused of doing something illegal when she was pulled over. But wait until you hear what it was. You`re not going to believe this.\par \par \uc2 Stick around, we`ll tell you. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m hung up on you!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You go! Okay, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It took me a second. I was like, Madonna. Yes! Activate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is here for Thursday`s "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Activate! Activate!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Activate!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hollywood has been warning us about the use of artificial intelligence. Now, the first fully A.I.-generated movie will premiere at a major film festival.\par \par \uc2 The docudrama "Dreams of Violets" will make its world debut at New York`s Tribeca Film Festival on June 10.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dreams of Violets/Fountain O)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everything you see in this 75-minute movie, including the actors --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- the sets, all of this that you`re watching right now was generated by artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it`s not real. None of this real.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nope! Nope!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Producers say the movie took three months to make.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That person is not real?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is not a real actor, that is an artificially -- that is artificial intelligence that has created all these images, including all of these people.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How much did it cost?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It only cost $2,000.00 to make.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow! Wow! Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And only three months, so it`s really, really interesting discussion point. Last week, the fully A.I.-generated movie "Hell Grind" premiered at a side event of the Cannes Film Festival.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hell Grind"/JoBlo Movie Network)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But it was not part of the official program. So, what do we think?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m very torn about this, because all of Hollywood, most of Hollywood is against