ere`s been a lot of complaints about missed calls during the Western Conference Finals --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NBA/NBC Sports)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- including a play on Tuesday, where the ball went out of bounds off the Thunder, but the ref said the Spurs knocked it out. Yes! Okay.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s what Commissioner Adam Silver revealed: The league plans to adopt an A.I. automated system to make these types of calls. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM SILVER (NBA commissioner): I think, in terms of replay, I think we`re going to get to the point fairly quickly, where, for example, out of bounds, the very play that you were talking about --\par \par \uc2 PAT MCAFEE ("The Pat McAfee Show"/ESPN): Chet Holmgren`s foot, it is big deal.\par \par \uc2 ADAM SILVER: Yes, yes, yes, yes. Those calls will be done by an A.I. automated system with cameras lined around the court, and it will take all of those so-called objective calls out of the hands of the referees.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, Silver also addressed claims that the Thunder players are flopping. He said he doesn`t mind players embellishing a little, as long as they`re not fooling the refs.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s where A.I. --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the point he is making though. I think that this is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This might be a good use of artificial intelligence to take away --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Adam Silver knows what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- take away the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Whatever he says.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- human error that might exist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like that.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But the refs will continue to make calls.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The refs will still be there.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They could just be overturned by A.I.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, the refs will make calls for everything else, but in the cases that he just mentioned, in those instances, artificial intelligence --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, where you can see the concrete evidence.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 All right, a Florida driver got the last laugh on Tuesday when her traffic ticket was dismissed. Let me break down what happened.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Everybody giggling.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is -- I know -- this story is crazy.\par \par \uc2 In February, a Palm Beach County deputy pulled her over, saying that she had been driving with a phone in her right hand. That`s an important detail, but watch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Today, we`re doing an operation for a distracted driver and you drove past me holding the phone with your right hand, manipulating that phone -- I mean, I saw you with a phone in your hand.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So obviously not. So you want to just call this a day or?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: I don`t want to call it a day. You had a hand up manipulating a phone.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You just said my right hand.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Oh, I thought I saw your right hand, you had that hand --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So you didn`t --\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: The right hand, I saw you manipulating --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So you didn`t.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: With the right hand? Perhaps not, right?\par \par \uc2 You did not have your phone in your hand?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I did not.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Hand to God, you didn`t have one in your hand.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hand to God.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: The other hand to God, you don`t have a phone on your hand?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hand to God!\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Cool! You have your driver`s license?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Some would go out on a limb here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you. And say that the driver was obviously innocent, right? I mean, what do you like?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I love how she`s like, so should we call it a day?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And he said, I am not calling it a day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, do you want to call it -- is it okay to laugh at that? Because there`s something funny about it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is laughing about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there is something that is not -- yes, she`s laughing at it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She was laughing, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. She`s an influencer. She`s laughing about it. The case was dismissed, obviously, but come on, when you see that as a police officer, I don`t know, you should probably just say my bad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am sorry. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I thought I saw a hand when clearly --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: He just kept digging.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And she was like, you didn`t. You didn`t.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Then she said --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The way she raised it, too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Then she says, "Hand to God with the hand."\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up in our next hour, we will talk to Dr. Sue Varma about solutions to what`s called "high-functioning burnout." Is this you? That is when you get things done, but you are always exhausted. She says it`s especially common among moms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the roots of reality T.V. and how it has changed our country. It`s part of our series, USA to Z. I can`t wait for that. Vlad did that one, but right now, it is time check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Coming up, my "CBS Saturday Morning" sister, Kelly is back to talk about some big changes coming to your social media feeds.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And we are talking about this all the time, right? Subscriptions, they are everywhere. They are coming in at Meta, WhatsApp, Facebook. We are going to show you what you are going to get in return for paying for them when we come back.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: See you there.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour, big changes to social media. Meta says it plans to offer paid subscriptions in exchange for some special features.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady will explain what this means for you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 We`ll explore the fine line between busy and burnt out. Dr. Sue Varma will tell us how to avoid losing contact with joy in our busy lives.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 We`re also talking with powerhouse performer, Tony nominee, Joshua Henry, how his role in the Broadway musical "Ragtime" helps tell the story of America.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to meet him.\par \par \uc2 All of that and more. The 8:00 hour of CBS MORNINGS starts --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right now!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right now, we are ready, but are you guys as excited as I am about Joshua Henry?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gosh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love him so much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We saw "Ragtime."\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and we were blown away by the entire production, but Joshua Henry --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What a star!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to meet him. I cannot wait. Moments away.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin, though, with this: The closely watched Senate race. It`s in Texas that could have national implications.\par \par \uc2 Republican state attorney general, that would be Ken Paxton, is facing off against Democratic state representative, that would be James Talarico.\par \par \uc2 So, Paxton has spent years fighting corruption allegations and Talarico who calls himself a progressive Christian is now facing questions about some of his past statements.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe spoke to Mr. Talarico in his first network news interview since Paxton officially became his opponent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: This Lone Star State showdown promises to be expensive and ugly.\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON (R) (Texas Senatorial candidate): My opponent is the most extreme radical the Democrats have ever nominated.\par \par \uc2 JAMES TALARICO (D) (Texas Senatorial Candidate): Ken Paxton is the most corrupt politician in America.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Ken Paxton and James Talarico are facing off in a race that may determine control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): I`ve got a message to Republicans in Texas and nationwide, do not take this general election for granted.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Talarico promises to take on the scandal-plagued Paxton with a focus on corruption.\par \par \uc2 JAMES TALARICO: The real fight in this country is not left versus right, it`s top versus bottom. We`re all getting screwed. None of us can afford anything. None of us can get ahead.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Paxton calls Talarico radical, in part for comments during debate over transgender rights.\par \par \uc2 Do you still believe there are six biological sexes?\par \par \uc2 JAMES TALARICO: I know there are two sexes, men and women. I also know there`s a very small percentage of people who have these chromosomal abnormalities, and I believe they deserve to be treated with dignity and respect.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And this --\par \par \uc2 JAMES TALARICO: God is nonbinary.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: What did you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 JAMES TALARICO: I was being intentionally provocative with that statement, but what it means is that God can`t be defined by human categories. The Apostle Paul, in his letter to the Galatians, says that in Christ there is neither male nor female.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You call it intentionally provocative. You regret describing it that way, or talking about God that way?\par \par \uc2 JAMES TALARICO: I -- there are some statements that I`ve made that I -- that I certainly regret, but Ken Paxton is intentionally clipping my cringey comments to distract from his career of corruption.\par \par \uc2 Ken Paxton has a criminal record. I have a legislative record.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The other one they keep harping on, and it`s perhaps the most curious of all. They say you`re a vegan,\par \par \uc2 JAMES TALARICO: I am not a vegan. I`m an eighth generation Texan. I`ve been eating barbecue since before Ken Paxton`s first indictment, and this campaign basically runs on Texas barbecue.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: For CBS mornings, I`m Ed O`Keefe in Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Paxton has so far declined multiple CBS News requests for an interview, but I know Ed O`Keefe will keep trying.\par \par \uc2 Don`t mess with beef and meat in Texas.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t do that at all.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They will get offended.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, not good!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, very, and more litigious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Turning now to social media.\par \par \uc2 Paid subscriptions are coming to some of your favorite apps. The social media giant, Meta has announced it will offer consumer subscription plans across Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp.\par \par \uc2 The monthly fees will pay for extra features like profile customization, super reactions, and story insights. I don`t know why I thought that was funny, but I guess that`s my reaction.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Can you imagine all the emojis?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, all the emojis that mom would be sending.\par \par \uc2 Meta says it plans to roll out more subscription plans for its A.I. chat box. To break it all down, we`re joined by "CBS Saturday Morning" co-host, Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, all right, what will these subscriptions cost, and what are you actually getting? I mean, to add extra emojis or reactions, do I really need to pay a couple of bucks for that?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Wait for the crazy emoji I send you in your DMs.\par \par \uc2 Okay, all right, let`s start with the price. It is $3.99 a month for Instagram Plus and for Facebook Plus, and for WhatsApp Plus. It`s going to be $2.99 a month.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You have to pay them separately?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Exactly, this is all going to be separate. There is no bundle yet.\par \par \uc2 What you`re going to get again is going to depend on the app, but for the social media focused ones, it`s things like extending the life of your story, which only lasts for about 24 hours right now, being able to search --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But that`s the point of stories.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Hey, sometimes you`ve got to do a story --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know, I am like yelling at her, I am like, what are you doing to us?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: You can also -- you`ll be able to search for who has watched your story, how many times they`ve rewatched it.\par \par \uc2 For WhatsApp, it`s going to be more messaging features like custom ringtones. I think the takeaway here is Meta is saying we`ve got heavy users, people that want to grow their followings. Let`s find a way to monetize them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What if you just want to do what you`re always doing?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Then it is free.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You don`t have to pay.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And you keep it on. And by the way, this is sort of how it`s existed. It`s advertising, it`s advertising supported.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I think what`s really interesting here is Meta is following the trend of many other companies, like X, like YouTube, and saying, hey, you know, we`re spending tons of money on A.I., we need other revenue streams, and social media is becoming sort of tiered, it`s pay-to-play if you`re a heavy user.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do you think most users will respond to this?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: You know, right, like this is the interesting part. I think that you have people who are going to be frustrated and say, I am so -- I have subscriptions fatigue. I have to pay for everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes. Just hearing you say bundle triggered me earlier.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Triggered you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It did. No more bundles! Not another bundled.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 There is a severe storm threat in the Northwest. Let`s check in with CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Rob.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hello, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we don`t talk about the Northwest for severe storms, typically. We`ve been talking about the Southeast, you`re going to get more in the way of rain, it should be nice across the Northeast for today, but the Northwest, we`ve got a little bit of an issue here.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a couple areas of low pressure. This one will kind of drift back towards the North and West. So, folks east of the Cascades, meaning Bend over to Pendleton, maybe Yakima will see some damaging winds later on today, scattered hail, a slight chance of seeing a tornado risk as well, and obviously we`re watching all of the storms move into Georgia.\par \par \uc2 That`s a check on what is happening nationally. It`s time now for a look at your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In case you missed it on CBS MORNINGS deals, we showed you the instant eye tightener from Peter Thomas Roth.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Tell us all about this product.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So if you`re someone who has puffiness under your eyes or lines around your eyes --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Instant Firmx Eye Easy Wear is for you. It`s like Spanx for your under-eye area, it sucks everything in, it smooths everything out, and it keeps it that way all day long.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Get this, folks, it`s still available for 28 percent off the retail price. Yep.\par \par \uc2 Plus coming in today`s Deals, we`ll show you an adjustable table tray that`s designed to make meal times easier, right? Very cool and it is available with an exclusive discount.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: For many people, the line between being busy and burnt out may be a bit blurry.\par \par \uc2 Mental health experts call this high-functioning burnout. It describes someone who appears successful and capable on the outside, but on the inside feels chronically exhausted, depleted and even disconnected from joy.\par \par \uc2 Board certified psychiatrist, Dr. Sue Varma says it is something she sees in many of her patients and friends. She is also the author of "Practical Optimism," a book we love.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Varma, thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA (Board certified psychiatrist "Practical Optimism" author): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So this idea of high-functioning burnout, how is that different from just being stressed or overwhelmed?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just like you said, Adriana, high-functioning burnout looks like achievement, competence on the outside, but it comes at a huge price of a person feeling chronically depleted and exhausted.\par \par \uc2 And I always say, just because someone is functioning doesn`t mean they`re doing well, because you have to ask, how do you sleep at night when your head hits the pillow? Are you able to fall asleep? Do you experience joy? Do you love doing the things that you once used to?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: More common in women, men, married or not?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes, much --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does that matter?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: It does. And we know that the statistics show that for women, married women with children specifically, they experience 68 percent burnout, moms, compared to 42 percent of dads. So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how do you know you have it? I still don`t understand. How do I know I have this if on the outside I appear to be doing okay?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes, so a couple of things. A person will say that I feel wired but tired, so my brain is always on, and rest is not restorative. So, even after a full night of sleep, they come and they feel angry or irritable out of proportion.\par \par \uc2 So, something that I hear a lot, especially in women going through perimenopause, or menopause, they will say, "My husband chewing" or "my husband snoring makes me want to gouge his eyes out."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, what are the long-term health consequences if you live in a chronic state of burnout?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes, so this -- you know, fight or flight was meant to get us through a very short period of time, and you know, when you have stress hormones, cortisol, it`s not a bad thing. All of us have cortisol, it rises early in the morning, and supposed to, in theory, subside later on in the day.\par \par \uc2 But if you are on all the time, where you`re performing success, that`s the thing with high-functioning burnout, is you`re performing a lot, and you`re always on. You never are able to close the loop. You have a lower immune system, so you`re going to be getting chronic colds and infections, autoimmune diseases, cardiovascular disease, which more women are prone to later in life anyway.\par \par \uc2 And also depression and anxiety, and eventually this high-functioning burnout isn`t going to be so high-functioning anymore, right? The problem is, most people will not offer you help because they`ll say you look so well put together.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Can`t even imagine the inside and the outsides don`t match.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: If you`re a high-functioning person, right, and you`re trying to just like push through, you`re not going to be the one to say, I think I need help.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Absolutely!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How can you try to identify these things in yourself? And then, how can you help yourself?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Asking for a friend.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: The first thing I would say is, stop glorifying exhaustion. We have a sense of this pride, of course, of being able to keep a tidy home and work and kids, but it is not possible for one person. This was meant literally for an entire village to support you.\par \par \uc2 Delegate tasks that are daily tasks, because a lot of times the dads in the home are doing the things that are quarterly, or maybe weekly, or maybe monthly, the lawn. You are negotiating the invisible load, right? There`s an invisible labor and mental load that women are always carrying. What are we eating tonight? Like, ask any dad, like, who is your kid`s best friend? Who`s the pediatrician? What size shoes do they wear? They can`t answer that. You have these ongoing lists of tasks, so offload them as soon as possible, especially the daily ones.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dr. Sue Varma, always great advice. Thank you, my friend. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right ahead in our series, "USA to Z," we delve into the power of reality television with a "Real Housewives" star.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s you and Dorinda!\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real Housewives of New York"/Bravo)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Dorinda!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dorinda Medley in the house.\par \par \uc2 Stick around for that. We`re back in 90 seconds with Dorinda.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Dorinda!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Our "USA to Z" series celebrates 250 years of American history and culture. Today is R for Reality Television, one of the most beloved and controversial genres to come out of the 20th Century.\par \par \uc2 No matter how you feel about reality T.V., its impact on popular culture cannot be denied. We met with two reality T.V. veterans to discuss what makes the genre so watchable.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is the true story.\par \par \uc2 True story.\par \par \uc2 What happens when things start being real? Or is it?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "America`s Top Model"/United Paramount Network)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "RuPaul`s Drag Race"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Apprentice"/MGM Television)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Reality television has become a new language --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Survivor"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real Housewives of New Jersey"/Bravo)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- through explosive moments, scandals --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Keeping Up with the Kardashians"/E!)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- and internet memes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Keeping Up with the Kardashians"/E!)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Bachelor"/ABC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But what is it about watching real people that has captivated audiences for more than 30 years?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Top Chef"/Bravo)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real World"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: To understand that, we`ll need to take a step back to the 90s --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real World"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When a new genre pushed the limits of what television could be.\par \par \uc2 JUDD WINICK ("The Real World" cast member): I thought it`d be fun, I thought it`d be cool, and I was broke.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real World"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Judd Winick starred in season three of MTV`s "The Real World," the hit social experiment widely credited for creating modern reality television.\par \par \uc2 The show put seven strangers under one roof.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real World"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But "The Real World" would go on to change more than just television.\par \par \uc2 JUDD WINICK: I don`t think even the producers knew what kind of impact it was going to have, and who we`re talking about is Pedro Zamora.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pedro Zamora was one of WINICK`s housemates on "The Real World."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real World"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was a 22-year-old openly gay man living with AIDS.\par \par \uc2 JUDD WINICK: He was funny and soap opera handsome, and at the time it was remarkable, because no one knew anyone who was living with AIDS.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real World"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Zamora`s death after the season finale touched the nation.\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON (Former President of the United States of America): Pedro became a member of all of our families.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Showing that reality T.V. could shape how Americans see each other.\par \par \uc2 As reality television began to evolve, it would go from "The Real World" to "Real Housewives."\par \par \uc2 What would your life be like if you were not a reality T.V. star?\par \par \uc2 DORINDA MEDLEY ("The Real Housewives of New York City"): You know, I really think I probably would have cooked another husband.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Reality star Dorinda Medley represents that shift.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real Housewives of New York"/Bravo)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her full-time job now is simply being Dorinda Medley.\par \par \uc2 Medley joined "The Real Housewives of New York" in 2015 where she became a fan favorite.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real Housewives of New York"/Bravo)\par \par \uc2 DORINDA MEDLEY: I`ll be honest with you, from day one, I loved it. I saw the camera, and I thought, "Oh."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Yes, Dorinda.\par \par \uc2 DORINDA MEDLEY: I love the camera, and the camera seems to love me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s leveraged that fame into spinoffs, including two stints on "The Traitors" and multiple business ventures.\par \par \uc2 DORINDA MEDLEY: Who would have thought that this person here at 61 could start a whole new blossoming career?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is now a hallmark of the reality T.V. machine, turning charismatic personalities like Medley into successful brands.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LORA (Pop culture expert): How much empathy are you owed on reality T.V.?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Josh Lora is a content creator who analyzes pop culture.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LORA: We`re seeing things like "Traitors", "The Mob," "House of Villains," where these shows now are taking people whose job it is to be a professional reality star. There is now brand deals on social media and careers after this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Real Housewives of New York"/Bravo)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But that attention can come at a cost.\par \par \uc2 Is there also an aspect of it -- let`s face it, if there`s a little bit of messy?\par \par \uc2 DORINDA MEDLEY: A lot of messy.\par \par \uc2 You have to be able to not care, just say, you know what, what I put out and what I`m going to give to the world will be more important than some of the bad they`re going to see.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jersey Shore"/MTV)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For years, critics have questioned the ethics of exploiting questionable behavior (on camera).\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Vanderpump Rules"/Bravo)\par \par \uc2 JOSH LORA: Not every show is "Housewives" or "Bad Girls Club" or "Love Island" or "Love is Blind," right? There are shows like "The Amazing Race" or "Love on the Spectrum," where we are seeing these very, very pure positive representations of humanity.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And few people understand that better than WINICK.\par \par \uc2 JUDD WINICK: there are people who weren`t born when we did the show, who come up and talk to us about is, and this is why he did the show. He did to speak to young people, young people just like him, so they would feel brave and empowered, and they could see that they could live their lives openly and it worked.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pedro Zamora really did change how we thought about reality television.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: As for -- and Judd is a gem, he`s an incredible person. He is still married his wife, Pam, and Dorinda is Dorinda.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s about her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s watching with her mom, Diane. Shoutout to her mom!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hi, Dorinda!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Hi Dorinda and Diane.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she always owns it and she was never obnoxious, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Never! She is a gem!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Big fan of Dorinda.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: "Talk of the Table" is next.\par \