r \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How much are we talking here?\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: It is 50 percent off at $39.99. One other thing you can do is scan an item and do the same thing, it`ll break down the nutritional value for you on the scale. Isn`t that incredible?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: I know, so now --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am definitely getting one of this.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: Chef Vlad has got one of these. It is the best thing ever.\par \par \uc2 All right, finally we`re wrapping it up. I`m sure you were looking at my watch all day, and you`re like, "wow."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: That is so beautiful. DKNY, iconic fashion brand, accessible price point, that`s why I love this brand.\par \par \uc2 We have two different kinds of watches, so many colorways on CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Father`s Day is coming up.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: Graduation gifts, Father`s Day, for sure. There`s men`s ones online. We have the Soho mini watch here, the iconic city sport one here. They`re both water resistant. You can wear it on the beach to dinner all day long. It is so lightweight.\par \par \uc2 My mom has this stainless steel one here. She wears it to art class, tennis, walks the dog, goes to dinner.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is very cool. I love this one.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: She forgets it is on. Isn`t that so beautiful? And there are different men`s ones online, like I said, really big for graduation gifts. These are starting at $59.99.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: That`s crazy, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Up to 40 percent off, folks.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: Yes, exactly!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Can`t go wrong.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Skyler Bouchard, really great to have you.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER BOUCHARD: It is so good to be here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw today, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBS Deals.com.\par \par \uc2 Up next, four-time Tony nominee, "Ragtime" star, Joshua Henry is here. Ahead, he is going to tell us about his hit show and balancing his Broadway dreams with fatherhood.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our country`s upcoming 250th birthday has put a new focus on what the American Dream really means today. That theme is explored in the Broadway musical, "Ragtime."\par \par \uc2 The current revival has received -- count them -- 11 Tony Award nominations.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yup!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about it, including its lead actor, Joshua Henry.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the thing about Joshua Henry: He is a four-time Tony nominee, and we all think soon to be Tony winner.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ragtime"/Vivian Beaumont Theater)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just saying. We`re going put it out in the universe.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ragtime"/Vivian Beaumont Theater)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Henry stars as Coalhouse Walker, Jr. a Harlem musician flourishing at the start of the 20 Century. So, his story overlaps with the lives of a Jewish immigrant and an upper-class mother, all searching for their own version of the American Dream. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ragtime"/Vivian Beaumont Theater)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, we`ve just to clap right here again!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Bravo!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wooh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All you can do is go, um-um-um-um-um-um!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. That`s what the audience was doing to you last night, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re happy to say Joshua Henry joins us now. Congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY ("Ragtime" actor): Joshua, I was blown away. I had goosebumps, the hair stood on the back of my neck. Please tell me, how you feel up there, and what this role means to you at this time in your life and career?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Thank you, Gayle. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We`ve all seen it by the way.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Thanks for coming. You all came yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: I -- this moment as a father, as an artist, this moment I`m having in "Ragtime" right now is the pinnacle of my time in New York.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow!\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ragtime"/Vivian Beaumont Theater)\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: For me, the role of Coalhouse Walker, Jr. that I get to play, he is a really complex guy who goes on a massive journey from hoping and dreaming to finding the love of his life, to having something really tragic happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: And it`s like how he recovers from that, much like what we all go through. It`s like how we fall down and how we recover. How do we maintain a dream and a hope throughout all of that?\par \par \uc2 So I feel a really big connection with Coalhouse, and I think his story and the other stories in "Ragtime" are really resonating with audiences.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, this musical takes place at the start of the 20th Century, and it emphasizes race, class, immigration, inequity, but it feels very reflective of the current anxieties that people are feeling right now. How so?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Absolutely, I mean, you -- I think the magic of this piece is that it`s showing us a mirror of who we are, you know, and with a Black family, a Jewish family, an upper class White family at the turn of the century, and how they collide, and they`re asking themselves what does it really mean to have this American Dream?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ragtime"/Vivian Beaumont Theater)\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: It is a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: It`s also a really complicated thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: And it doesn`t quite pan out for everyone in the same way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: That`s the moment we`re having now. Are we -- we are asking ourselves questions that we`ve asked before, but they feel more pressing than ever. And I think Terrence McNally, the incredible book writer, and Ahrens and Flaherty, the composer and lyricist, really captured that question right now, just as they did in 1998 when it originally debuted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It can be very painful.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You know, when I saw it with Kelly O`Grady, our colleague, both of us were sitting there just silently crying. Can you hear people crying in the audience? What does that do to you?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: You know, I can, because the stage of the Vivian Beaumont on three sides is surrounded by the audience, and so when you hear, a gasp --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A gasp! Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: -- for instance, like at the end of the piece, there`s a really unexpected moment, let`s say, musically.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: And it almost like takes the rug under -- from under you, and you hear the audience go "huh?" You know, there are many moments like that that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is audible.\par \par \uc2 But I want to know about you, Joshua, and what you`re thinking when you`re up there singing the way you`re singing. What is your intention when you`re up there? Because I was so deeply affected and deeply moved by you, your expressions in your voice.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: I`m so -- thank you. I --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: For me -- what am I doing? Well, I`m having a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are you thinking?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: But I, I grew up singing in church, and I -- my first intention and experience with singing for real was singing to glorify God, or for singing for a higher purpose, serving something.\par \par \uc2 So, when I sing each day before I go on that stage, it`s not just about, hey, let me sound good, let me sound pretty.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ragtime"/Vivian Beaumont Theater)\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: I`ve always been obsessed with shifting people, with shifting people`s emotional state for a higher purpose, and so as I sing Coalhouse, as I sing a song like "Wheels of a Dream" or "New Music," my hope is that people feel something coming through me that is not just pretty, but that could change their life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you do a little bit of "Wheels of a Dream." I know it`s early, but can you do that?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: I sure can. The beginning of the song is when I`m looking at my child and having a dream, and I say, "I see his face, I hear his heartbeat."\par \par \uc2 And for me, I`m a father, I have an eight-year-old, I have five year old twins, all boys, and I remember that moment of seeing them for the first time, and that`s all I need.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you do the belting part?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Can I get --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have less than -- in 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Gayle you`re always -- oh, oh, oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you do the belting part?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: You know, we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you go wheels of a dream, I mean, it was -- it was amazing.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: If I say on "wheels of a" will you say the dream part?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: "On the Wheels of a --"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dream.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dream.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Dream.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Hey, there it is!\par \par \uc2 You`re hired! You`re hired!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the Tony goes to -- Joshua Henry.\par \par \uc2 We thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Joshua Henry! Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s so amazing.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How emotional.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So emotional.\par \par \uc2 Joshua Henry, this was so wonderful to have you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re amazing.\par \par \uc2 "Ragtime" is playing at the Vivian Beaumont Theater at Lincoln Center. You can watch the 79th Tony Awards, hosted by Pink, on June 7th right here on CBS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And see Joshua win!\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow, right here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA HENRY: Hey!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260528em5s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 SUNDAY WEEKEND NEWS for \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Olivia Rinaldi, Tim McNicholas, Jonathan  Vigliotti, Cristian Benavides, Andrew Kozak, Imtiaz Tyab, Itay Hod \par \uc2 3,748 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, air show collision.\par \par \uc2 Two Navy fighter jets fly right into each other in the sky over Idaho. The crews ejecting, the jets crash into the ground. What we know at this hour.\par \par \uc2 President Trump`s approval sinks as costs soar. New CBS polling as the Iran war drags on, and rising prices test voters` patience.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I barely can get by, you know, and it`s too expensive.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RINALDI: I`m Olivia Rinaldi at The White House, with new reporting about why the affordability crisis that fueled President Trump`s return to power could be a threat to Republicans with the midterms fast approaching.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Massive fire. An 8,000-gallon fuel tanker ignites in New Jersey, shutting down a major interstate. More than 150 firefighters on the scene.\par \par \uc2 An historic strike threatens commuter chaos.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): And I believe, I know a deal can be reached.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Just as ridership on trains and buses picks up.\par \par \uc2 I`m Tim McNicholas in New York City.\par \par \uc2 With gas prices up, so is ridership on mass transit. We`ll hop on board.\par \par \uc2 All of that, plus, \uc2 Swatch\uc2  watch frenzy.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Shoppers worldwide swarm stores this weekend.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: I`m Jonathan Vigliotti in Los Angeles. The rush to buy a piece of nostalgia shows there`s no time like the present for retail fever. How a pocket watch is causing chaos in malls worldwide.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening, everyone, and thank you for joining us on this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, an Idaho Air Force is locked down after an air show crash. It happened near the Mountain Home Air Force Base, about 50 miles southeast of Boise.\par \par \uc2 The mid-air collision involved two EA-18 jet fighters, part of a \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  demonstration team. The crews ejected. Officials say they are in stable condition tonight. Their jets crashed to the ground. An investigation is now underway.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, President Trump`s war on Iran and rising costs fueled by it are impacting how Americans feel about the economy and his presidency. Trump`s approval now at the lowest level of his second term. Only 27 percent of Americans approve of how he is handling inflation, and 76 percent now say they are concerned about their personal finances.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Olivia Rinaldi is at The White House with more on our poll and the latest in politics.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, good evening.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RINALDI: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 That new poll also found that 69 percent of Americans say they don`t have a clear understanding of the situation in the Strait of Hormuz as the war is adding to economic concerns here at home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RINALDI (voiceover): President Trump visited his Virginia Golf Club shortly after issuing a new warning to Tehran. The administration`s focus on Iran has Americans feeling the pinch.\par \par \uc2 In new CBS News polling, almost 60 percent of Americans described gas prices in May as a financial hardship. Last week, the president dismissed affordability concerns.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tuesday)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States of America): I don`t think about Americans` financial situation. I think about one thing: We cannot let Iran have a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RINALDI: The President doubled down in an interview with Fox in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News/Friday)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: That`s right. It is a perfect sense. I`ll make it again. When you tell somebody you are going to have to pay a little more, and not that much more, a little more for gasoline for a very short period of time, because we want to stop the threat of being blown to pieces by a lunatic, everybody says that`s fine.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RINALDI: President Trump ran on an economic revival, yet nearly two- thirds of the country say his policies are making the economy worse in the short term.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): So, as soon as we get that straightened out, we will get back to the kitchen table issues.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RINALDI: But the President scored a political win last night, boxing out sitting Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  from a runoff in the Louisiana Senate primary.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy voted to convict Trump in his second impeachment trial after the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): When you participate in democracy, sometimes it doesn`t turn out the way you want it to, but you don`t pout, you don`t whine, you don`t claim the election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RINALDI: The President is hoping for another political win this week, the possible defeat of Republican Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  of Kentucky in his primary on Tuesday. Massie was one of the leaders of the bipartisan push in the House to release the Epstein files -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Olivia Rinaldi at The White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A massive fire has shut down a major interstate in New Jersey. One person was killed after an SUV struck the rear of a tanker truck carrying over 8,000 gallons of fuel. Both vehicles were engulfed in flames. It happened about 30 miles southwest of New York City, more than 150 firefighters responded.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, North America`s largest commuter rail system remains shut down. It`s day two of a strike by Long Island Railroad workers. The railroad serves roughly 300,000 people in New York City and eastern suburbs.\par \par \uc2 With the work week looming, officials are urging commuters to work-from- home.\par \par \uc2 And with gas prices up, more people are relying on mass transit. CBS` Tim McNicholas is in New York City, and following that part of the story tonight -- Tim.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 The Long Island Railroad may be idled by a strike, but passenger trains and bus lines across the country say ridership is up, and they say higher prices at the pump is one big reason why.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS (voiceover): Transportation hubs like Union Station in Washington, D.C. are even busier than usual lately. The railroad giant, Amtrak reports a five percent increase in passengers in March compared to a year ago.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Instead of getting a rental car and driving to D.C., I decided to take the train.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It is a lot easier to take the train and save a few bucks.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: Gas prices are up more than 50 percent since the war with Iran began, that`s driving Americans out of their cars and on to public transit.\par \par \uc2 INGRID MCQUEEN (Traveler): I filled my tank up last week, it was $55.00 to fill it up. That`s the first time I`ve ever spent that much on my vehicle.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: A lot of the big cities in this country, the ridership of public transportation went way down after COVID. It is going to start ticking up. People are going to be frustrated, they`re not going to want to drive as much.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: And it`s not just trains, the e-scooter and bike company, VEO says it surveyed its riders in late March, 68 percent of respondents said they`re replacing some car rides with e-scooters and bikes due to recent gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Trailways, the largest network of bus companies in North America, also saw a five percent increase in March compared to last year.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BERARDI (Trailways.com president): And then we see April also up nine percent That`s when we know, it`s not just natural growth, this is from the gas prices.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: Trailways.com, President Alex Berardi says long distance travelers are also steering toward buses with jet fuel prices pushing skyward and air fares up 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BERARDI: People were booking last second a little bit more. We think that could be around the uncertainty in the travel market. People are looking and they`re shocked by the prices and they`re trying to figure out how am I going to get there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: Transportation experts say gas prices could hit $5.00 per gallon by Memorial Day. Berardi tells us Trailways has added drivers and is looking to buy more buses to meet demand, but has not raised fares yet -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tim McNicholas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The United States is ramping up pressure on Cuba tonight with a potential indictment on former President Raul Castro.\par \par \uc2 CBS Cristian Benavides is in Miami with new details on what`s next -- Cristian.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Jericka, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Here in Little Havana, they have been longing for change in Cuba for decades, and now this indictment gives the U.S. an opportunity to enact that change.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voiceover): Once rare protests have become an almost nightly occurrence in Cuba, an island struggling with rolling blackouts, lack of food and fuel, in part due to decades-long government mismanagement, the takedown of Venezuela`s Nicolas Maduro, and amplified U.S. pressure.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is offering $100 million in humanitarian assistance in exchange for meaningful reforms.\par \par \uc2 Cuba has no other option.\par \par \uc2 EDUARDO GAMARRA (Florida International University Professor of Politics and International Relations): It`s really at the end of the road.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Sources tell CBS News the U.S. is planning to indict the 94-year-old former leader, Raul Castro, brother of Fidel Castro, over the shoot down of humanitarian planes by a Miami organization in 1996.\par \par \uc2 Three U.S. citizens and one U.S. resident working for Brothers to the Rescue were killed by the regime.\par \par \uc2 Is the U.S. going to go in and take Raul Castro?\par \par \uc2 EDUARDO GAMARRA: We don`t know, right? Or is this indictment a way to pressure Cuba to, in fact, allow a peaceful transition?\par \par \uc2 ERNESTO MARTIN (Human rights activist): Get out!\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Cuban-born human rights activist Ernesto Martin.\par \par \uc2 in Venezuela, they still have basically the same government in charge. Is that acceptable for Cuba?\par \par \uc2 ERNESTO MARTIN: We do not support any sort of deal that leaves the Castro regime in power.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The United Nations warns that Cuba is on the brink of collapse. Now, this Wednesday, the four families of those who were killed during that 1996 incident will be honored at an event here in Miami, and "The Miami Herald" reports that the indictment could be unsealed on that day -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Cristian Benavides, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight Minnesota`s National Guard has been mobilized to help fight wildfires across the state. This fire destroyed several buildings along the state`s north shore near Duluth, and across the Plains, well, severe storms are firing up tornadoes, including this one, tearing across rural Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Andrew Kozak is here with everything you need to know.\par \par \uc2 Andrew, good evening.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yes, Jericka. A busy Sunday night, and it`s not just the wind reports, the hail reports that we`ve already seen, but now the tornado threat for places like Omaha, Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we already have multiple tornado watches, and even a few warnings that will take us through the next couple of hours, keeping an eye on this, and by the time we get into the late night, the storms become more of a straight line wind damage type of situation. We call that the bow echo when the storm continues to kind of bow out.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you the setup, because we once again are going to be watching for severe weather on Monday. Warm air to the south, cold air, even a winter storm to the North and the West, places like Casper, Wyoming getting accumulating snow, but right in the middle is where we have that really sultry air, and that`s where once again going to be focusing on severe weather for Monday, this time Kansas, Missouri, and up toward Iowa, with tornado threats along with hail and gusty winds.\par \par \uc2 On the East Coast, warm surges, we`re talking 90s and even close to triple digits heading into Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Andrew Kozak, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to an extraordinary moment in retail that turned unruly.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Jonathan Vigliotti is in Los Angeles with details on what is happening at these Swatch stores -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, and good evening to you, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 The scenes are what we`d expect on Black Friday, but instead of a holiday deal, it is a new watch that many people hope turns into a great investment.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Move over Stanley Cup and Labubu Doll, society or a good social media blitz , has now decided the hottest accessory is a pocket watch, the kind your great grandfather might have checked before boarding a steam train.\par \par \uc2 COLE RIKERT (Philadelphia): There is probably like 200 to 300 people packed in there, just trying to be like a first in line at the door.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The frenzy is over a new collab between watchmaker Swatch and luxury brand, Audemars Piguet, whose timepieces normally sell for the price of a luxury car. This version costs around $400.00, which in luxury watch math qualifies as affordable, or to those trying to get their hands on one, a great investment.\par \par \uc2 Some are reselling online for thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Enter chaos!\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: You will be arrested. This is your final notice.\par \par \uc2 COLE RIKERT: It could have become like a stampede. If they did open the doors, it would have been a free for all.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: In suburban Philadelphia, nearly a hundred officers responded after crowds at a mall became unruly. One shopper apparently tried forcing their way inside before opening.\par \par \uc2 And in Milan, video captured shoppers brawling. It`s unclear if this latest trend will stand the test of time or wind up forgotten when the clock strikes Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And Swatch says, this watch is not a limited edition, which means there will be plenty more, though, Jericka, it`s still unclear if that will be enough to ease the frenzy that we saw this weekend.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, it is crazy when these things happen. Jonathan Vigliotti, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, in 1973 the New Jersey country rock band, Dr. Hook & the Medicine Show got their wish.\par \par \uc2 That`s when "Rolling Stone" Magazine put them on the cover. The song, one of many hits for the band. Today, we learned that the band`s front man, Dennis Locorriere died after battling kidney disease. He was 76 years old.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, a new warning about a global health emergency. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, outbreaks of the deadly Ebola virus in two African countries have prompted the World Health Organization to declare a Global Health Emergency.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Imtiaz Tyab of our London Bureau reports on the efforts to contain it -- Imtiaz.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Jericka, good evening.\par \par \uc2 There is a major push to contain this fast-moving and deadly outbreak of the Ebola virus. The Democratic Republic of Congo`s government has said it has activated a Public Health Emergency Operations Center, strengthened epidemiological and laboratory surveillance, and ordered the rapid deployment of response teams in an attempt to contain the outbreak.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voiceover): This remote corner of Eastern Congo is on edge. In a nearby gold mining area, Ebola is spreading fast, and people are dying.\par \par \uc2 John Casembo (sp?) is a motorcycle taxi driver.\par \par \uc2 (JOHN CASEMBO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "We are very afraid," he says. "Since the beginning of May, many people have died, and it keeps happening. We`re so scared."\par \par \uc2 Casembo is right to be afraid, spread through direct contact with bodily fluids and through broken skin, Ebola causes severe bleeding and organ failure. There is no proven cure for the virus, and according to the World Health Organization, the average fatality rate is around 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical correspondent, Dr. Celine Gounder says containing the virus won`t be easy.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It`s already a big outbreak at the point that we`re hearing about it. There have already been a number of deaths, and this is a strain of Ebola, for which we have no treatment, no vaccines.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: This is the Democratic Republic of Congo`s 17th outbreak of the deadly viral disease, which was first discovered in 1976 in what is now the DRC and is thought to have spread from bats, it`s also unfolding against a backdrop of a deepening security crisis in the region, where clashes between rival militia groups have killed dozens of civilians in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And tonight, global health experts are warning the Trump administration`s dismantling of USAID and withdrawal from the WHO may have a serious impact on the global effort to contain the virus, Jericka, to an Ebola outbreak, which has already claimed dozens of lives.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Imtiaz Tyab, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, he`s known for mastering the court, but now basketball great, Shaquille O`Neal is adding yet another title to his name.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, two new records when it comes to scaling Mount Everest. Kami Rita Sherpa made it to the top of the world`s highest peak for the 32nd time. The 56-year-old started guiding other climbers back in 1994.\par \par \uc2 Also today, Lhakpa Sherpa, known as the Mountain Queen, made her 11th summit, that is the most for any woman on Mount Everest.\par \par \uc2 Well, the last place to ski in this country is now closed for the season. Skiers at Colorado`s Arapaho Basin took the last runs today, some of them across water. The resort is usually open until mid-June, but below average snowfall took its toll, forcing most Rocky Mountain resorts to close early.\par \par \uc2 Four-time NBA champion Shaquille O`Neal was called to the graduation stage this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Shaquille "I hate Charles Barkley" O`Neill.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You heard that correctly. Shaq trolled his friend and former NBA rival, Charles Barkley and enjoyed the moment, as you see right there, accepting his Masters of Liberal Arts Degree at Louisiana State University. Shaq also earned his Bachelor`s from there back in 2000.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations!\par \par \uc2 Next on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, embracing A.I. as an education tool, is it helping though? CBS` Itay Hod will show us, but first, a look back at this day in history.\par \par \uc2 Graphics onscreen: This Day In History --\par \par \uc2 THIS DAY IN HISTORY: 1792\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK STOCK EXCHANGE FOUNDED\par \par \uc2 THIS DAY IN HISTORY: 1966\par \par \uc2 BOB DYLAN HECKLED FOR PLAYING ELECTRIC GUITAR\par \par \uc2 MAN: Judas!\par \par \uc2 BOB DYLAN (Singer): I don`t believe you.\par \par \uc2 THIS DAY IN HISTORY: 2006\par \par \uc2 USS ORISKANY SUNK TO CREATE ARTIFICIAL REEF\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: She was called The Mighty O. The USS Oriskany, an aircraft carrier that fought in the Korean and Vietnam Wars and today was her last voyage.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Finally, the rise of artificial intelligence is proving to be one of the biggest challenges for schools, with educators forced to adapt to rapidly changing technology.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s "Weekend Journal," CBS` Itay Hod in San Francisco shows us how A.I. is revolutionizing the classroom.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD (voiceover): For a lot of kids, school is something you suffer through, but not for 13-year-old June Rockefeller, at least not anymore.\par \par \uc2 JUNE ROCKEFELLER (13 years old): It`s really fun.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: June is a student at Alpha, a new school in San Francisco that`s using A.I. to reimagine learning. Instead of teachers, especially trained guides help kids when they get stuck. Students spend just two hours a day on course subjects, all taught by A.I., the rest is dedicated to real-world projects.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN WONG (14 years old): My app, called Moleculab.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Fourteen-year-old, Ethan Wong is designing an app on Molecular Gastronomy.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN WONG: It`s an app that helps students --\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Today`s class: How to pitch a startup.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to guess in your other schools you weren`t creating apps.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN WONG: No, not at all.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: His dad, James, says he noticed a change almost immediately.\par \par \uc2 JAMES WONG (Ethan`s Dad): I had not seen that smile when he exited school in a very long time.\par \par \uc2 CARSON LEHMANN (Alpha School): Okay.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Carson Lehmann, Alpha`s lead guide, says schools have been slow to change.\par \par \uc2 CARSON LEHMANN: I want them to see that they are able to create something. They don`t need a crazy degree in Computer Science, they are able to create a functioning app.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Of course, not everyone agrees.\par \par \uc2 Cassondra Curiel, president of United Educators of San Francisco says two hours of digital learning is just not enough.\par \par \uc2 CASSONDRA CURIEL (United Educators of San Francisco president): Some of the consequences of unregulated and currently unstudied A.I. in education settings could have potentially really impactful and negative influences on our students.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: The cost, $75,000.00 a year. Alpha says they`re getting results, claiming their students score in the top two percent nationally.\par \par \uc2 For June, there`s no going back.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything that you miss at all from your older school?\par \par \uc2 JUNE ROCKEFELLER: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Still, it remains to be seen if A.I. can get an A+.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN WONG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Itay Hod, CBS News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Time will tell, but that is quite the investment.\par \par \uc2 That is the CBS WEEKEND NEWS for this Sunday. We thank you so much for watching.\par \par \uc2 I`m Jericka Duncan in New York. Have a great week.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020260518em5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Iran Attacks Ships in Strait of Hormuz; Trump Extends Iran Ceasefire Indefinitely; Wild Weather: Fire and Ice; Wildfires Spread Across Florida and Georgia; Floating Blocks of Ice Damage Michigan Homes; Wildfires and Red Flag Warnings; Indicted Florida Congresswoman Resigns; Virginia Approves New Districts in Win for Democrats; ChatGPT Investigated Over Florida School Shooting; Transportation Secretary on Upgrades for ATC Systems; New Video Show L.A. Plane Crash; Electric Wheelchair Bursts into Flames; U.K. Passes Nationwide Tobacco Ban; Slain Student`s Parents Say Murder was Preventable; Vehicle Crashes Through Philly Police Station; Elizabeth Smart`s Powerful New Image; Impact of GLP-1 Drugs on Diet Culture; Tips for College Grads on Landing a Job; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Matt Gutman, Charlie D`Agata, Nancy  Cordes, Samantha Vinograd, Rob Marciano, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Jo Ling Kent,  Kris Van Cleave, Shanelle Kaul, Adriana Diaz, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 15,058 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on your Wednesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And I`m Matt Gutman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is breaking news from the Persian Gulf. Iran fires shots and seizes cargo ships after a big reversal from President Trump, and we`ve got extreme weather in the north and the south. Oh boy!\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Iran fires on ships in the Strait of Hormuz hours after President Trump extends the ceasefire deadline.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): It`s like the cantina bar scene in "Star Wars." Some of them are scared, all of them are angry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Historic drought is fueling out-of-control wildfires in the south forcing residents from their homes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We have a lot of friends and family that are evacuated right now.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: A Florida Congresswoman resigned amid a fraud scandal, the third member of Congress to step down in a week.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2  (R-CO): Three resignations, the swamp is kind of draining itself right now.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Police in Philadelphia say a man deliberately drove into one of their police stations. Five people were taken to hospitals with minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #1: Josh Allen, the Bills` quarterback, a man of the people firing up the crowd before the Buffalo Sabers playoff game, did not get the result they wanted with the \uc2 Bruins\uc2  evening this series.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #2: At Wrigley, a rat was found running on the field.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: They`re calling him the Rally Rat, after the Cubs extend their win streak to seven games. The furry field invader even spooked the \uc2 Phillies\uc2  dugout.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Oh, my God! It is the worst thing -- come on!\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to pass on the Rally Rat.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pass on the Rally Rat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going to pass on that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right on that, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with this breaking news from the Middle East, where as many as three cargo ships have come under attack in the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 Iran`s Revolutionary Guards say two ships are now under their control. Now this comes just hours after President Trump extended the ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is tracking the latest from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning. What is the latest?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Another developing story, so we first heard from a British maritime security group about Iranian gunboats firing on three passing ships. Then we got confirmation from Iran`s Revolutionary Guards. They not only fired on two of those ships, but also seized the vessels, they are saying they were operating without authorization, and that one of those ships, the Francesca, was linked to Israel.\par \par \uc2 They were seized in the Strait of Hormuz. We all know about that. One of them suffered damage to the hull. As you said, this, of course, comes on the verge of a U.S. ceasefire and the capture of an Iranian flagship earlier this week, and it raised the stakes over that ceasefire that was supposed to run out today, but has been extended indefinitely overnight by President Trump.\par \par \uc2 It is worth reminding that this ceasefire is not just between the United States and Iran, but Israel and Iran, too. So we are now awaiting response from the Israelis if this is indeed a ship that`s linked to this country, and what their response will be -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Charlie D`Agata tracking it force in Tel Aviv. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Trump`s decision to extend the ceasefire came after he made new threats against Iran.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at The White House with the latest on this. Nancy, good morning, it seems like a lot of mixed signals here.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Yes, good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 President Trump made this decision to extend the ceasefire at the request of the Pakistanis who have been brokering these talks.\par \par \uc2 Vice President J.D. Vance was all set to travel to Pakistan for the talks yesterday, but then the talks were called off with each side blaming the other and President Trump, who had vowed to resume the bombing of Iran if there was no deal, well, he ended up extending the ceasefire indefinitely, instead.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Trump extended the ceasefire with Iran minutes before meeting with college athletes. In a post on Truth Social he said, "The government of Iran is seriously fractured. We have been asked to hold our attack on the country of Iran until such time as their leaders and representatives can come up with a unified proposal."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R) (President of the United States of America): Well, I expect to be bombing.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Just eight hours earlier, Trump had insisted he was not inclined to extend the two-week ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I don`t want to do that. We don`t have that much time, because by the time both parties get there, as you know, they just got the okay to go forward, which I knew they were going to do anyway.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That claim turned out to be premature. Instead, the Iranians refuse to attend planned talks in Pakistan until Trump lifts his blockade of Iranian ports. They called the blockade, quote "an act of war and thus a violation of the ceasefire." So Vice President Vance never left for Islamabad.\par \par \uc2 The setback nudged worldwide oil prices up three percent and left Congress unclear about the road ahead.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SMITH (D-WA): Everyone seems to think that Trump has some sort of mad genius to what he`s doing. It seems to me like he`s just an arrogant egotist popping off in ways that aren`t well-thought out and don`t advance any sort of strategic plan.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump says he has directed the U.S. military to continue the blockade, and U.S. forces are also expanding their operations into the Indian Ocean, where they seized a sanctioned tanker suspected of smuggling Iranian oil yesterday, and escorted another vessel off of India`s Western Coast -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in Samantha Vinograd. She is a CBS News National Security contributor, and by the way, former Assistant Secretary for Counterterrorism at DHS.\par \par \uc2 So Sam, we know you know some stuff, so help us sort this out. First, the ceasefire is on, it is extended. No, it`s not We`re going to bomb. No, we are. J.D. Vance is going; yes, he is. He`s still here. How do you make sense of all of this?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Well, Gayle, it`s okay to be confused.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: There are a lot of conflicting not just signals publicly, but also developments on the ground and I do think part of that is attributable to the fact that these negotiations are playing out in a very unusual way.\par \par \uc2 It was smart of the President to signal to the Iranian regime that they couldn`t indefinitely engage in negotiations and just buy time to rebuild their capabilities, but typically communicating deadlines is done privately so that both parties have the time and space to negotiate and really assess that the other party is being credible in negotiating in good faith.\par \par \uc2 Setting these arbitrary and frankly unrealistic deadlines publicly boxed the U.S. delegation into a corner, and they felt unnecessary pressure to meet a deadline that was just unrealistic based on the complexity of what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: So the President seems to have bought some time. He also has given the impression that the deals could be wrapped up in a single day, but it does not seem to be the case that Iran is in a hurry right now. So what`s the way forward?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: We need to let the experts do their jobs. In my experience, preparation really matters, and what happens before negotiators actually meet on the ground is almost as important as what happens in the room itself. We need to let the Intelligence Community assess whether the Iranian regime is serious about making a deal.\par \par \uc2 We have to let the Intelligence Community assess what the Iranian regime really cares about, what their red lines are, what their leverage points are. We need to let Iran experts weigh in on the personalities at the negotiating table, that preparation has to be used to inform the strategy that J.D. Vance, Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner are going to use on the ground itself. Without that preparation, I don`t think these negotiations will be successful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are they the right Intelligence experts to be representing us at this time? Iran does not seem to be intimidated at all.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: We have no indication that the Intelligence Community is traveling with the delegation to Pakistan. What we don`t know is if Intelligence analysts, nuclear experts and others are talking to the delegation prior to their departure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to extreme weather, many Americans are waking up to dangerous ice or fire this morning. There are major wildfires burning in Florida. They`re driven by the worst drought in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 In Northern Michigan, ice blocks are smashing into homes after historic flooding. CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano has more on these weather threats.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: A wildfire northwest of Miami has exploded to 5000 acres, and it`s only 25 percent contained.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN GUTHRIE (Executive Director for the Florida Division of Emergency Management): My genuine concern about this drought and this fire season is it`s just getting started.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Florida now facing its worst drought since 2012. Extreme or exceptional drought covers more than 70 percent of the state, leaving the ground dry and vulnerable to fires.\par \par \uc2 In Southeast Georgia, a 3,800-acre blaze has burned down more than 40 buildings and forced dozens of people to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is the first time where it`s kind of been right in the middle of the middle of the county and burning in multiple different directions all at one time today.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: And it`s the opposite problem in the Upper Midwest.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER NARSESIAN (Cheboygan County, Michigan resident): It`s just unreal. I mean, the pictures don`t do it justice. These chunks are massive.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Still facing historic flooding, the wind and the water`s current has sent huge chunks of ice smashing into homes in Northern Michigan, taking out walls and windows. Many who have evacuated haven`t been able to get back home to see the damage yet.\par \par \uc2 Christopher Narsesian told us his neighborhood is still under water.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER NARSESIAN: It`s more than just stuff that we lost. These are people`s homes, you know, and that they won`t be able to be back in for a long time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: It is going to take a while for that water to recede, not just there, but there are over two dozen river gate sites in Michigan, Wisconsin and Illinois that are either in moderate or major flood stage before all these drains mostly up into the Great Lakes.\par \par \uc2 All right, you`re Florida are improving a little bit today, but no big rain, the critical fire danger and the red flag warnings are more towards the west. So, we`ve got a pretty strong front that`s heading this way, low levels of humidity, but look at these wind gusts, 35 to 40 miles per hour from Denver up through 