 the aircraft tumbling from the sky.\par \par \uc2 Good Samaritans pulled the pilot out of the cockpit to safety. Authorities say the man is in his 70s, and he was taken to the hospital in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 And a Florida homeowner narrowly avoided disaster after her electric wheelchair burst into flames. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 The homeowner says she heard a clicking noise as the chair was charging, and it was also hot to the touch, so she decided to bring it outside, and that`s when it blew up right there, you can see it.\par \par \uc2 The officials say she was using a non-manufacturer charger, which can cause batteries to overheat.\par \par \uc2 And students in Los Angeles schools are about to see their screen time slashed. On Tuesday, the Los Angeles School Board voted unanimously to prohibit students in the first grade and younger from using electronic devices for school work. The district will also create a screen time policy outlining limits for elementary and middle school students.\par \par \uc2 The resolution also encourages teachers to use more "pen and paper assignments." Remember those? The new rules are set to go into effect next school year.\par \par \uc2 And finally, the U.K. is taking drastic steps to create a smoke free generation, an entire generation. A law passed by Parliament Tuesday will ban the sale of tobacco products to anyone born in 2009 or later.\par \par \uc2 That means anyone who is not already 18 years old right now will never be able to buy a tobacco product in the U.K. for the rest of their lives.\par \par \uc2 The bill could go into law as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Interesting to see how they enforce that, would you say?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Matt, could you buy me cigarettes for me?\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Yes. I mean, there is going to be like a black market for cigarettes in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I like that step, though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To have a smokeless generation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And the school --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Smoke free.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: -- smartphone band for Los Angeles as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I like this one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Adriana, we like you, too. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I love you, guys.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: You`re the best. Thank you so much. Really appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Love you, too.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Now to a CBS News exclusive about a story that made national headlines.\par \par \uc2 The parents of a Loyola University Chicago freshman killed last month are now speaking out for the first time. Eighteen-year-old Sheridan Gorman was gunned down while on an early morning walk with her friends just a few blocks from her dorm. Authorities say the suspect, who was lurking behind a lighthouse on a Lake Michigan pier was living in the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 Gorman`s parents, who are understandably so raw from the tragedy, described a level of heartbreak they did not know existed.\par \par \uc2 They say when your child goes off to college, you worry about drugs, alcohol, maybe even tuition, but not this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN (Mother of 18-year-old Sheridan Gorman): She was really an amazing person. I actually -- I grieve not only for all of the people that she touched in this life, but the people she won`t touch.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Sheridan Gorman was an 18-year-old freshman at Loyola University Chicago. She was filled with promise and pep. She was her parents` joy.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: No one should have to deal with the police coming to your door at 6:00 A.M., falling to your knees when you hear the news that no parent should ever hear.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS GORMAN (Father of Sheridan Gorman): There is nothing that prepares you for going to the Medical Examiner`s Office and filling out the paperwork.\par \par \uc2 Your hand is trembling, and then you have to go identify your daughter`s body, and they ask you if you`re ready, and nothing makes you ready for that.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: On March 19th, Gorman and her friends went to catch a glimpse of the Northern Lights on Lake Michigan at around 1:30 A.M. They were just about a block-and-a-half from campus, but as Gorman reached the end of this pier, she encountered a masked gunman.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: He like, moved towards her threateningly. She turned around, and she warned her friends that there was a man with a gun and you need to run and they ran. She ran. She made it like --\par \par \uc2 THOMAS GORMAN: Forty feet.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: But she made it like 40 feet and he shot her in the back. It came out her neck.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And she saved her friends.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: Presumably, we heard that. They say it was a senseless tragedy. It wasn`t, it was a murder. It was a preventable murder.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Police arrested 26-year-old Jose Medina, a Venezuelan who authorities say entered the country illegally. His lawyer says her client lost a portion of his brain and skull after he was robbed and shot in the head in Colombia in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers said he had the mental capacity of a child.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: We have to live -- he has the mental capacity in the wherewithal to buy a gun, to have a mask on and to be waiting. The mask was on and he had a gun pointed at my daughter when she passed him.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS GORMAN: A child does not do that.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: A man does not --\par \par \uc2 THOMAS GORMAN: -- shoot a retreating child in the back.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: The Department of Homeland Security says Medina was first apprehended by U.S. Border Patrol in May of 2023 and released into the country under the Biden administration, and just over a month later, they say he was caught shoplifting in Chicago, arrested and released again.\par \par \uc2 An arrest warrant was issued after he didn`t show up for court.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about the policies that you think made this happen.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: Honestly, we`re really still trying to explore that.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS GORMAN: Clearly there needs to be some cooperation between state and local government and federal officials. When someone commits a crime and they`ve been here illegally, there needs to be cooperation. He was -- you know, he was arrested for, yes, a nonviolent crime, but he also was here illegally. In our mind, he should have been handed over to the feds at that point.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: The Trump administration has used Gorman`s death to push for a tougher stance on Immigration. And while Gorman`s parents say they don`t want their daughter`s death to be about politics, they do want accountability.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GORMAN: I don`t know what the longevity of this effort takes, but I will never rest. And yes, they`ve awakened a bear. They`ve awakened a big grizzly Mama Bear. I`ve got to fight for my child. I have to fight for yours. I have to turn this devastating darkness into light somehow.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And having met them and spent some time with them, I am sure they will turn it into light somehow.\par \par \uc2 Now, Medina has not yet entered a plea and is set to be arraigned next week. He faces several charges, including first degree murder and illegal possession of a firearm. He could face 20 years to life in prison if convicted.\par \par \uc2 But what Sheridan`s parents want more than anything is that no other family should ever have to experience this, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that they`re speaking up. Number one, my heart breaks for them, but I love that they are calling it out. Don`t call this a senseless tragedy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a murder that could have been prevented.\par \par \uc2 And the way that they talk about the detail, you know, we hear it about in the movies, and we read about it, identifying the body, filling out the paperwork, but to know that you have to go through that, Matt was just tough.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you have to bury your kid, that is one thing no parent wants to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But the word that keeps jumping out to me, it is like you said, Gayle, that world "preventable."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is the word that just breaks my heart.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And nothing accounts for it, and nothing will ever make that family better. They just hope to be able to prevent other people from ever experiencing that again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am in awe of them in the way that they`re speaking out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And their strength.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It will make a difference.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: All right, we`re going to shift gears here.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a crazy video showing what happened when a car rammed into a police station.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What you are watching is a wild video of a car barreling in to a police station in Philadelphia on Tuesday. Five people were taken to hospitals and police think the driver did it on purpose.\par \par \uc2 Shanelle Kaul reports on the crash and the people who made it out alive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Surveillance video from Philadelphia`s Second Police District shows the moment a car came crashing through the lobby Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN BETHEL (Philadelphia Police Department Commissioner): It is clearly an absolute deliberate act based on the video that we watched.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: The Philadelphia Police Commissioner said officers encountered the suspect earlier in the day as they responded to a reported disturbance between the individual and his father, then resumed patrol.\par \par \uc2 Later the Commissioner said, the suspect was driving near the Second District Station house before appearing to intentionally make a hard right turn into the building.\par \par \uc2 Clarence Armstrong says he was inside the station`s bathroom when the car rammed through.\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE ARMSTRONG (Witness): I knelt down like this, I thought the police station was under attack, and I see a car and glass and metal every single place it could possibly be.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Police say five others were in the lobby.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER KEVIN BETHEL: Fortunately, very fortunate today, that those individuals were not seriously harmed and even more fortunate, we have a homeless male who oftentimes frequents our lobby, he had just apparently got up and went to the restroom.\par \par \uc2 So we are very, very fortunate today that those individuals were not seriously injured.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: Police say the suspect is in custody and the motive remains under investigation.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Shanelle Kaul.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, advocate and abduction survivor, Elizabeth Smart just revealed a major transformation on social media. Why she says she was too afraid to tell people.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Vlad will have the stories you will be talking about in "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 Vlad, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You got that right, Nate.\par \par \uc2 New England Patriots Coach Mike Vrabel is speaking out after candid photos raised questions about his relationship with a "New York Times" reporter. We are going to tell you what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 Plus, 