ur of CBS MORNINGS starts when Matt?\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Right now!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have a great hour coming up for you, but we begin with the damaging weather in Florida and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 A major wildfire has burned more than 5,000 acres northwest of Miami. Other fires are burning around the state and take a look at this, rivers are overflowing in Northern Michigan after heavy rain. Ice chunks on the rivers are ramming into houses, causing heavy damage.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is here with more.\par \par \uc2 Rob, what is going on?\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: I mean, if not a flood is not bad enough, you get an ice jam shoved into your house. I mean, that is just horrible. And it`s going to be slow time for that weather to recede and those people to get back and assess the damage.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the southeast, we`ve had the opposite going on. These are seven larger fires, over a thousand acres. Over 130 fires in total across the state of Florida. Of course, this is spawned by the drought.\par \par \uc2 The southeast, total of 97 percent is under drought. Most of that is severe drought. We need a good foot of rain here in the next month to alleviate any of that and we don`t see that happening. Really, probably need to see some sort of tropical storm or hurricane come as we head towards June and July.\par \par \uc2 All right, meanwhile, the fire danger is going to be across parts of the West as a strong front is coming. It`s going to kick up the winds and the humidity levels are pretty low here as well, so 30 to 50 mile per hour winds here over the next two days.\par \par \uc2 And then here comes that front, tomorrow afternoon, fire up the storm from Kansas City up to Des Moines, Minneapolis. Late afternoon, tornadoes are possible with this, certainly some damaging wind, large hail as well.\par \par \uc2 And then we sink it to the south during the day on Friday. Then we bring in Oklahoma City, Dallas up to Memphis, the Mid-South getting into play here, multiple rounds morning and the evening with this.\par \par \uc2 Then we`re going to reset the table for Saturday and Sunday and this looks to be pretty potent as well as we head towards the weekend. So lots of talk about severe weather-wise as we go forward.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, over to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Potent weather never sounds good.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Rob.\par \par \uc2 Now to a new chapter for Elizabeth. Smart. You remember her? She survived a high profile abduction as a teenager, a story that riveted the nation.\par \par \uc2 We all followed this, and now she has revealed a very surprising transformation.\par \par \uc2 She posted a photo on social media from a body building competition. Look at her. Go Elizabeth! She`s reflecting on her journey to reclaim her strength.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz has more on Smart`s story of resilience.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: On stage, flexing with confidence, Elizabeth Smart shared this photo of herself in a body building competition.\par \par \uc2 In the caption, Smart revealed she had four competitions, but was hesitant to share, worried she might be perceived as less than or unworthy to continue to work as an advocate for all survivors.\par \par \uc2 Then she said it struck her that these feelings are familiar for all survivors. Writing, "I don`t want to reach the end of my life and look back and feel regret for only living a half-life, not going after all the things I want to do and try."\par \par \uc2 It marks a powerful moment for Elizabeth Smart.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: This morning, a desperate search for a kidnapped 14-year-old Utah girl.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: In 2002, at just 14 years old, she was kidnapped at knife point from her bedroom in Salt Lake City and held captive for nine months.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SMART (Kidnapping survivor): That was the beginning of the worst nine months of my life.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Smart was repeatedly assaulted, threatened with death and forced to comply with her captors. She sat down with Gayle King in 2022.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was your method on how you were going to survive? What did you do to survive?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SMART: I mean, the most important decision I made happened that first day that I was kidnapped. I decided I was going to do whatever it took. It didn`t matter what it was. I`d do whatever it took to survive.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Smart went on to become a leading advocate for other survivors, even launching a self-defense training program in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Now she writes, she is proud of and wants to celebrate her body, which has carried her through every worst day, created and nurtured three beautiful children and risen to every single challenge that life has presented.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Wow!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I mean, we can all imagine trying something new, feeling like you`re a little worried about being judged, but she says she refuses to be embarrassed about trying something that she has always wanted to do, and she wants to live her life at the fullest that she possibly can and inspire others to do the same and guess what, guys? She won first place in the novice category.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Third in what`s called the Masters 35+ category, according to reports.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am so excited for her. We all remember this story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I remember the joy I felt when they -- she was found alive. I reached out to her yesterday because I`ve been in touch with her, and she said she`s so surprised that it`s gotten this much attention.\par \par \uc2 I go, "Have you looked at yourself in the mirror?"\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She looks fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she`s going to come to us. She is going to come to us May 4th, live.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Oh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is going to be in the studio. She`s taking some time off to go away with her husband. They -- you know, she wants to take a minute, but she is just blown away by people`s interest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And people are surprised that that she`s done this. I can`t wait to talk to her.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And I think we`re still fascinated by her because of her incredible resilience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Right? She went through the unimaginable. She`s able to have a family and to do this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know that I`d be inspired this morning, she said, I don`t want to feel regret for only living a half-life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s deep right there. Go out and do it all, try it all.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She is amazing!\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And as Adriana showed us, she is living a full life.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Sure is.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s lovely and so smart, and we`re all happy and pulling for her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can`t wait to see her in her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: All right, so up ahead, if your kids are about to graduate from college, we want to help them get a job.\par \par \uc2 A LinkedIn careers expert will be here to talk about the skills they need to compete.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we have an exclusive preview of tonight`s episode of "Survivor 50," but first, time for a check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from New Kids on the Block-Kids/BMG)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Forever young, baby. That`s Donnie Wahlberg in the band, New Kids on the Block, and we will talk with Wahlberg about the band`s Las Vegas residency and his hit CBS show "Boston Blue."\par \par \uc2 Stick around. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The use of GLP-1 weight loss drugs is more common than ever. For many, people, they believe, it can be life-changing and it has been. But there are also concerns that they`re impacting the effort to challenge diet culture, which idealizes fitness.\par \par \uc2 Katie Sturino is the founder of Megababe Beauty, there she is, Mega Babe -- and an outspoken body acceptance advocate. So she opened up about her decision to start taking GLP-1s last year.\par \par \uc2 We`re also joined by psychiatrist, Dr. Sue Varma, she is the author of "Practical Optimism." We need that, too. "The Art, the Science and Practice of Exceptional Well-Being." I like that.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Varma and Katie, we are glad to say are here in the studio.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA (Board certified psychiatrist "Practical Optimism" author): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, Katie, congratulations on wearing Sneex, they are some of my favorite shoes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, those are fire!\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are so comfy and they look great on you.\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO (Megababe Beauty founder and body acceptance advocate): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You already get ten points in my book.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: They are heel sneakers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, from Sara Blakely and Spanx. I love it. Sneex.\par \par \uc2 Now, but let`s talk about you because you said it was a complicated decision for you, complex, to take GLP-1s. Why was it so hard for you?\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: Because I have seen --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: After the doctor told you that you should, by the way.\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: The doctor told me for years that I should and I resisted because I was like, you know what, I can make changes through lifestyle. I can do what I`ve always kind of done, but I found that with my mindset of body acceptance that I was unable to go back to those crash diet mind-sets and I couldn`t lose the weight I needed to lose to change my blood work on my own and I did need the medication, but I was not willing to give up the work I had done on my brain to not hate my body every day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you feel you were betraying yourself and betraying others?\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: I feared that, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that the message that you were sending?\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: Yes, I feared that I would, but what I found in being open is that so many people were struggling with gut and shame around having to be on this medication, because everyone`s bodies and brains work differently and there`s so much nuance in the conversation, but we don`t really get that nuance with the way that GLP-1s are served to us through pop culture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: An Dr. Varma, you know, Katie has obviously worked on herself internally before deciding to take the GLP-1, but you said there are certain practices that you should try to establish in your life before doing so.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes, and I think it`s so important that doctors ask the question not just what is your weight, but what is your relationship to food? What is your relationship to your body? And what is your relationship to your emotions?\par \par \uc2 Because I always say that our body will express what our mind cannot, and so many people miss the fact that for example, untreated ADHD has a 40 percent higher risk of obesity, has four times of risk of binge eating disorders, are twice as likely to be depressed and 75 percent more likely to have trauma. So, we cannot neglect the emotional component of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha!\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: I think getting a proper mental health evaluation to say what else is going on? What medications could you be taking for your mental health could be increasing your weight? What is going on with food? A preoccupation with food, rigidity around food, secrecy around food, eating things that you wouldn`t eat in front of other people?\par \par \uc2 So, a proper mental health evaluation and also getting your micro nutrients checked, iron deficiency, vitamin B12 deficiency and vitamin D. Some of this could be low to begin with and some can be low as a result of the GLP- 1 use.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: so, maybe a double-barreled question here. On the one hand, if someone has diabetes, they are not afraid of taking modern medicine to help them get better.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: So why is there such a leap to take a GLP-1 if we know it can make you fitter, healthier, change your blood work and help change your mindset? Why is that such a big leap for people?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, good question.\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: I think it`s a slippery slope because of the way that GLP-1s are marketed towards people and the way that we`re seeing celebrities show up on the red carpet. The danger in pushing GLP-1s for everyone is that we are going to risk putting thin as the only option back in popularity. We already did that. We should not go back there. We already know it was bad.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: And people don`t realize that you could be what doctors might consider a healthy weight and still have disordered eating.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people have dysregulated eating and they could be a normal weight. So, the preoccupation in how much time it takes from you and I think that there is a lot of shame and guilt and secrecy around it.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Just a synopsize, you could be healthy and bigger and you can be thin and unhealthy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you guys say to people that feel societal pressure though to lose weight and take the drugs?\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: I would say, listen, it`s a hundred percent normal. I get where you`re coming from, I think with the GLP-1s, we have sort of democratized a lot of things that were not available to people.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it is expensive and not everyone has access to it, so there is more intense pressure. I see a lot of people exaggerating their symptoms to their doctors in order to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just so they can get it.\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: Just so they can get it or lying because what this says to me is that there is a desperation that I need to fit into the norm and I would say things like even a short course of something like a cognitive behavioral therapy which is gold standard for binge eating disorder, it is not a medication but it really supports your thoughts and the behavior and getting healthy habits in place -- bone density, muscle mass, getting a DEXA scan, muscle mass 25 to 40 percent of muscle mass weight loss can be from muscle and making sure that you get your labs checked periodically for those micronutrients.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Such an enlightening conversation. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you feel good, you look good.\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Definitely look good!\par \par \uc2 DR. SUE VARMA: She looks fabulous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: It`s been fun to have both of you here.\par \par \uc2 KATIE STURINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Thank you so much, Katie Sturino and Dr. Sue Varma.\par \par \uc2 Now, ahead, important career advice for college grads facing a tough job market. We`ll talk with LinkedIn expert, Catherine Fisher about what grads can do now to stand out and just land a job.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in 90 seconds. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: All right, we`re back.\par \par \uc2 If you have a college grad stepping into the workforce this year, and we don`t envy you, listen up!\par \par \uc2 New data from LinkedIn shows that they are facing obviously a more challenging job market. Since last year, the hiring rate for entry level jobs has declined six percent, and that`s according to a study of LinkedIn users.\par \par \uc2 Now, career expert, Catherine Fisher is here. She joins us with advice for recent grads.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Catherine.\par \par \uc2 It seems so gloomy and so dark out there for the job market. I think a lot of people, we had someone here just yesterday who was saying, go for a career. Go for your life`s work. You`re saying, just get your foot in the door with something right now after grad.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (LinkedIn career expert): Yes. You have to start somewhere and I love the life`s work, and think of your career as a stepping stone, right?\par \par \uc2 So that first job is going to help you build the connections, get the skills, understand how to work in these different environments. So you want to approach that job search in a different way than maybe we all did when we were graduating.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s less about I`m going to get that perfect job, and more about, okay, I`m going to get something that`s going to open a door, maybe freelance work. It may be contract or internship.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but -- go ahead, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Would you recommend, like, Technical College for people at this point to learn HVAC, electrical?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Absolutely, yes. And this is what we are seeing also with the Gen Z`s, that they are looking at other things beyond just like that corporate job. A lot of them are even becoming solopreneurs.\par \par \uc2 So they`re looking like, hey, I could go be a social media expert and do my own business right now. So yes, it`s having that different look at, you know what that perfect job is?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The word you just said? Solopreneurs?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Solopreneurs, yes. So they`re building their own companies, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: By themselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it`s scary to me when I see entry level jobs are down. Even the market for entry level jobs are down. So what should they be doing?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: So they should be leaning into their network.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do they have networks?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Well, so they do. They don`t realize it. And what we hear is that, you know, 44 percent of the Gen Zer`s that we talked to said, oh my gosh. They think that their barrier is access.\par \par \uc2 And really, they do have a network. Think about the summer jobs that you had, or the professor or you know the person that you babysat for that is your network.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to want to get really comfortable with reaching out, having specific questions and leaning into that, but being really like, deliberate in terms of how you build that network, starting with that, you know, the type of jobs that you`re interested in, go for those people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, some say looking for a job is like looking for a home. What I mean by that is, it`s all about location, location, location.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what are some areas around the country that young people should be looking for when they`re looking for a job?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, so we did some research on this as well, and we found, you know, some of the usual -- San Francisco, Seattle -- those are great places to get a job.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: They`re expensive, but other emerging cities, like, you know, Atlanta, Charleston, Salt Lake City, Austin, like really fun places to live, more affordable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Those are some really good places to go look for a job, because there`s some jobs in those areas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How important is your degree when it comes to these early -- your early days of starting your career?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, so it`s less about the degree and your major, it`s more about the skills. So when you`re going into that interview, don`t say like, hey, I studied Psychology, but what you could say is, what I really enjoyed about school was the research, the writing that you know, collaboration and leaning into those experiences. That`s what`s going to be interesting to that hiring manager.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, I hope people at home are taking notes. You`re always so helpful.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Catherine, appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, Vlad, what have you got for us, baby?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m here with Donnie Wahlberg. He is here! He`s going to tell us about starring in the hit CBS series "Boston Blue" and the Las Vegas residency for his band, of course, New Kids on the Block that`s coming up next. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We have an exclusive sneak peek at tonight`s episode of the hit CBS show "Survivor 50."\par \par \uc2 Now for the first time 