VED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260422em4m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Artemis\uc2  II Speeds Toward Moon; Trump Fires Attorney General Bondi; Secretary Hegseth Fires Army`s Top General; Bodycam Shows Tiger Woods` Arrest; Maui Doctor Questioned in Attempted Murder Trial; Pope Loe Returns to Tradition Around Good Friday; Severe Storm Threat and Weekend Warmth; New Focus on Risks of Microplastics; Rapper Gucci Mane Kidnapped and Robbed; Man Shoves Stranger Toward Incoming Train; \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  Raising Checked Bag Fees; \uc2 British Airways\uc2  Rolls Out On-Flight Video Calls; Judge Tosses Blake Lively Sexual Harassment Claims; Missing Arizona Girl Found After 32 Years; "48 Hours" Investigates "The Root Beer Float Murder;" Concerns Over New Epstein-Related Video Game; Number of Women in Workforce Projected to Decline; Mumford & Sons on New Album and Upcoming Tour; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Adriana Diaz, Kelly O`Grady, Kris Van Cleave, Weijia  Jiang, Nicole Valdes, Carter Evans, Chris Livesay, Rob Marciano, Celine  Gounder, Jericka Duncan, Tom Hanson, Peter Van Sant, Meg Oliver, Anthony  Mason \par \uc2 13,769 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Good Friday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And I`m Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a major milestone for the \uc2 Artemis\uc2  astronauts on their way to the moon.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the fallout from the firing of U.S. Attorney General Pam Bondi.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Artemis\uc2  two crew leaves Earth`s orbit on their way to the moon. We`ll hear from the astronauts for the first time since liftoff.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: There is nothing normal about this. Sending four humans 250,000 miles away is a Herculean effort.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: President Trump fires U.S. Attorney General, Pam Bondi. Find out what`s behind the President`s decision.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE (Former Deputy Assistant Attorney General): I think President Trump felt she didn`t have her foot on the accelerator.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Police release bodycam video of the moment Tiger Woods was arrested on the DUI charge in Florida.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Let`s get you down here with us, please.\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS (Professional golfer): Yes, I was talking to the President.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A music studio ambush. Prosecutors say rapper, Gucci Mane was targeted, kidnapped and robbed.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Baggage handlers at Kansas City International Airport had passengers laughing as they struggled to fold a baby stroller.\par \par \uc2 Passengers gathered to watch this like it is like a show.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): I`ve got moon madness. One space fan, I believe, captured the true spirit of the moment.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Why do you love being a part of history?\par \par \uc2 BOY: We`re going back to the (bleep) moon. That`s why.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes! Yes! Come on. You tell them, Camera Head Kid!\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kids say the darndest things, and he is very comfortable.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, he was.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He did not stutter.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I bet his parents were so mortified, but so proud.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So proud.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. Right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They would want to see.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: He`s not wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is not wrong. I was going to say, Adriana, that kid spoke for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 We are going to begin this morning with the Artemis II mission, now out of Earth orbit and on its way to the moon, just like he said.\par \par \uc2 The four astronauts will spend the day doing a series of checks to make sure the spacecraft can support future missions to the moon.\par \par \uc2 So, they are more than -- listen these numbers -- 74,000 miles from the Earth, traveling at 4,700 miles per hour. Wow!\par \par \uc2 In a few days, they will be farther out in space than any human has ever gone.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleve is at the Johnson Space Center.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning to you. These numbers never get old. This story is so exciting to so many.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Yes, think about those 74,000 to 75,000 miles. That`s nearly 10 Earths away from home. They are really moving, and we are tracking all of it here in our newsroom at the Johnson Space Center in Houston. That is a live look at mission control, and we are hearing from the astronauts for the first time in space, the first people to leave orbit since 1972.\par \par \uc2 That decision to move forward with this Trans Lunar Injection was not made lightly. It was made after a thorough review to make sure all systems were go.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Integrity, good engine, good control.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And it`s go for the moon.\par \par \uc2 For nearly six minutes, Orion`s main engine fired with 6,000 pounds of thrust to give the capsule a 900-mile an hour boost, sending it and its four astronauts on a path around the moon.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Humanity has once again shown what we are capable of.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The Trans Lunar Injection burn also sets up Orion`s return to Earth. Mission Control can make small adjustments to the course to set it up for re-entry.\par \par \uc2 REID WISEMAN (Artemis II Mission Commander): We all wanted to give a shoutout to our families first, because we are pretty far from Earth, and we have not gotten to say hi to them yet.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Overnight, the Artemis crew spoke to the media from space about the journey so far.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA KOCH (Artemis II Mission Specialist): Having just experienced incredible views of planet Earth, and seeing the entire planet out the window in one pane, knowing that we`re about to have some similar views of the moon in that same way is definitely getting me more excited for it.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR GLOVER (Artemis II Pilot): We`re just getting started. You know, we`ve worked really hard, and we have a lot of mission left ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Earlier Tuesday, the crew awoke to a fitting song, "Greenlight" by John Legend and Andre 3000.\par \par \uc2 In order to be ready to go for their mission, getting regular sleep is key. It`s about a four-day trip to the moon, living in a space about the size of two minivans.\par \par \uc2 I don`t see full down beds. So how does it work?\par \par \uc2 MAN: There are no bunk beds.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: We were given special access inside the mock up Orion capsule astronauts used for training at the Johnson Space Center.\par \par \uc2 Inside, each crew member has a sleeping bag.\par \par \uc2 They tie it off wherever.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Wherever they need to, and then you get into it. It`s got a space for your head to stick out. It`s got, actually, a little band that you can put over your forehead to kind of hold you down.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Is there any trick to sleeping in zero gravity?\par \par \uc2 MAN: There is, from the standpoint of being able to control the crew member and not have them floating around, which is why we have the sleeping bags.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: You don`t want to play sleeping bumper cars.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And over the next several days, the astronauts will be doing a host of things. Remember, this is still a test flight, so a lot of things to validate, including pressurizing their space suits and, of course, documenting what they`re seeing along the way.\par \par \uc2 They may see parts of the moon no human eyes have ever seen before. Adriana, just think of the images they may be sending back home to Earth.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They are going to be incredible. Chris, I cannot wait. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Moving on now, one of the top names in President Trump`s Cabinet is out this morning. The President removed Attorney General Pam Bondi after months of criticism, including of her handling of the Jeffrey Epstein investigation.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at The White House with more.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Adriana, good morning. Good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 This is the second time in less than one month that President Trump ousted a member of his Cabinet. In a statement, Bondi said she will be, "moving to an important private sector role," and she is thrilled about "... where I will continue fighting for President Trump and this administration."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI (Outgoing U.S. Attorney General): There is no greater President than Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Pam Bondi was a fierce and unflinching defender of President Trump. She ran a Justice Department that investigated frequent Trump targets like Letitia James, James Comey and Jerome Powell.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R) (President of the United States of America): Hey, Pam. I think -- thank you. What a great job!\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But that loyalty was not enough to save her job as the nation`s top law enforcement official. Just one day after the President and Bondi attended a Supreme Court hearing together, Trump announced her departure Thursday and praised Bondi for overseeing a massive crackdown in crime across our country, adding, "We love Pam."\par \par \uc2 Trump did not always love the way Bondi handled the Jeffrey Epstein case, after she promoted conspiracy theories about a possible Epstein client list.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI: It is sitting on my desk right now to review. That`s been a directive.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: She later said it never existed.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI: There was no client list.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And last September, the President also complained publicly that Bondi didn`t succeed in prosecuting his political rivals. "Pam, all talk, no action. Nothing is being done."\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE: I think serving as President Trump`s Attorney General is a hazardous business, and --\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Tom Dupree served as a Deputy Assistant Attorney General under President George W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE: Her failing was not a lack of loyalty, it was a failure to execute. The President made clear from day one that a top priority of his was using the Justice Department as a way to go after the people he felt had treated him wrongly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: President Trump has named Bondi`s former top Deputy Todd Blanche as acting Attorney General, but sources tell CBS News, the EPA Administrator and former New York Congressman Lee Zeldin is a frontrunner to take the post permanently.\par \par \uc2 This week, Zeldin met privately with Trump -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And Weijia, there are a lot of changes going on right now, with Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth also removing the Army`s top general in the middle of a war. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Army Chief-of-Staff General Randy George is now among more than a dozen senior military officials removed from Defense Secretary Hegseth`s team. George has been asked to step down and retire immediately.\par \par \uc2 A senior defense official tells CBS News, "We`re grateful for his service, but it was time for a leadership change." -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Weijia, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, well, I hate to say it, but gasoline prices show no sign of falling from the recent highs. This is five weeks after the U.S. and Israel started the war against Iran.\par \par \uc2 Now, the price of crude oil on World Market surged around 10 percent again yesterday, and remains well above $100.00 a barrel, and it`s up more than 60 percent from the start of the war, and that`s partly because the vital Strait of Hormuz remains closed to nearly all oil tankers.\par \par \uc2 President Trump posted this video online, claiming it shows the destruction of a bridge outside Iran`s capital. He said, the U.S. military, in his words, "hasn`t even started destroying what`s left in Iran."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are learning more this morning about Tiger Woods` DUI arrest last week in South Florida.\par \par \uc2 Officials released this body camera video on Thursday. It shows Tiger Woods doing a field sobriety test after crashing his SUV.\par \par \uc2 Now, Woods has pleaded not guilty. He says he is seeking treatment.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: At this time, I do believe your normal faculties are impaired, okay? And you`re under an unknown substance, so at this time, you`re under arrest for DUI.\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS: I`m being arrested?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Newly released body camera video shows Tiger Woods in handcuffs after last week`s car crash near his Jupiter Island home.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Who`s involved.\par \par \uc2 WITNESS: Tiger Woods.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Oh (bleep). All right.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Woods hit the trailer of this pickup truck, according to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He didn`t see me, I think and he just kept coming.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Then he flipped his own SUV..\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS: Looked down at my phone, and all of a sudden, boom!\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: The 50-year-old wasn`t hurt and was walking away from law enforcement on his phone.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Just keep you down here with us, please.\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS: Yes, I was talking to the President.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: A deputy then leads Woods to a patrol car for a field sobriety test.\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS One, two. One, two.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Which he performed while leaning against a cruiser.\par \par \uc2 After putting him in handcuffs, Deputies spot two white pills loose in his front pocket.\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS: Yes, that`s a Norco.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: That`s a Norco?\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Law enforcement said they were hydrocodone pills.\par \par \uc2 While Woods admitted he takes a few prescription medications, it is unclear if hydrocodone is one of them.\par \par \uc2 TIGER WOODS: It`s just one.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Cuffed in the back of a patrol car, Woods appears to doze off.\par \par \uc2 The star golfer was first charged with DUI in 2017 after police found him asleep at the wheel in Palm Beach County.\par \par \uc2 Dave Aronberg prosecuted that case.\par \par \uc2 DAVE ARONBERG (Former Palm Beach County State Attorney): They`ve got a video that shows that Tiger Woods probably should not have been driving, but at the same time, the defense can still argue that the reason why he looks so disoriented was because he had just been in a serious rollover accident.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Now this week, a judge ruled Tiger Woods can leave the country for treatment. Woods says he hopes to "focus on his health and work towards lasting recovery."\par \par \uc2 Now, we have reached out to The White House to confirm whether Woods did, in fact, call the President at the scene of the crash. Trump did tell "The New York Post" that he had spoken with the golfer after the incident -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, we all noticed that he said he was just talking to the President on that body camera footage.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you so much for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 Turning to new testimony by a Maui doctor accused of trying to push his wife off a cliff last year.\par \par \uc2 Gerhardt Konig took the stand for a second day, Thursday. He insisted he hit his wife with a rock in self-defense during a hike.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans has our report, and we should warn you, some of the footage is very disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GERHARDT KONIG (Defendant): I almost went off the cliff.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Gerhardt Konig testified Thursday that his wife, Arielle had tried to push him off this narrow path in March 2025. He said he left her behind after an argument on the trail before running into her on the walk back.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: It was a surprise, because you still had a chance to go through with your plan to push Ariel off the cliff, isn`t that right?\par \par \uc2 GERHARDT KONIG: That is not right at all.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Prosecutors pressed Konig about months of disputes with his wife prior to the incident, driven largely by an emotional affair she admitted to having with a co-worker. Konig said that topic was what prompted their fight on the trail.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: And then you say that Arielle pulled you onto the ground, right?\par \par \uc2 GERHARDT KONIG: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Last week, Arielle gave a different version of events.\par \par \uc2 ARIELLE KONIG (Gerhardt Konig`s wife and alleged victim): And he said, I`m so (bleep) sick of it. Get back over there, and he starts pushing me back towards the cliff.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Earlier this week, Konig admitted he beat Arielle with a rock, but said it was in self-defense. Prosecutors pressed him on that claim Thursday and showed images of the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: That Arielle took this rock and hit you in the face, isn`t that right?\par \par \uc2 GERHARDT KONIG: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: And you said the first hit, she got me kind of good, right?\par \par \uc2 GERHARDT KONIG: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: And you say only two hits to Arielle, right?\par \par \uc2 GERHARDT KONIG: I only hit her two times.\par \par \uc2 PROSECUTOR: This is the front of Arielle`s face after those two hits, right?\par \par \uc2 GERHARDT KONIG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Konig has been charged with attempted murder in the second degree. He has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: As Christians around the world mark Holy Week, an American born Pope is putting his own stamp on the papacy.\par \par \uc2 Leo is celebrating his first Easter season as Pope, lending tradition with a more outspoken voice on global issues.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay has more from Rome.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: It`s a tradition that dates back centuries. The leader of the Catholic Church washing feet just like Jesus washed the feet of the 12 Apostles, only Pope Leo is doing it his way.\par \par \uc2 These are the feet of 12 priests, a return to tradition after his predecessor, Pope Francis, chose to wash the feet of prisoners instead.\par \par \uc2 It is one of the signs of Pope Leo charting his own path, says CBS News Vatican analyst, Father Anthony Figueiredo.\par \par \uc2 MONSIGNOR ANTHONY FIGUEIREDO: Pope Francis`s pontificate was choppy. It was turbulent. Pope Leo, I would say, wants a church that is secure.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: When it comes symbols, you can see it in the liturgical dress. Leo is more old fashioned, but when it comes to actions, the American pontiff is beginning to be more bold.\par \par \uc2 Take this week, imploring world leaders and President Trump by name, to find an off ramp to the war in Iran.\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV (Bishop of Rome, head of the Catholic Church and sovereign of the Vatican City State): Hopefully he`s looking for a way to decrease the amount of violence, of bombing, which would be a significant contribution to removing the hatred that`s being created, and that`s increasing constantly in the Middle East and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Last weekend, on Palm Sunday, Pope Leo told the tens of thousands gathered here that God does not hear the prayers of those with blood on their hands.\par \par \uc2 Later today, he will perform the ritual of the Stations of the Cross at the Coliseum with another return to tradition, he will carry the cross himself -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Chris Livesay, reporting from London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to weather, including more severe storms in the Mississippi Valley, tornadoes, hail and flooding hit that area very hard.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go now to CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano for more on that and what`s in store for the weekend. This is a big weekend for the faithful, Rob.\par \par \uc2 I hope there is good weather.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: I know. We`ve got a big storm that`s going to be rolling across the country that will impact services and celebrations this weekend, for sure.\par \par \uc2 First off, you saw that severe weather on the northern part of that not just yesterday, but today. We`ve got more snow coming across parts of Wyoming, the Dakotas, up through northern parts of Michigan, Minnesota, and also Northern Wisconsin, where yesterday they had several reports of frozen precipitation, some ice there in Green Bay, 63 of them, so we`ll get another batch of that today, on top of the severe weather threat.\par \par \uc2 So this is a fairly slow moving front. It will begin to march from Kansas City, Oklahoma City today into the Ohio Valley tomorrow, and then on Sunday it does get to the East Coast. So it does look to be a little bit of a rain event for Easter Sunday, all at I-95.\par \par \uc2 That`s a look at what`s happening nationally. Time now for your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: She is the editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News. Good morning, Dr. Gounder.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Okay, so what`s the timeline for enforcing limits on microplastics here?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is the very beginning of what is a rather long timeline. So there are a number of different things that have to happen before we get to enforceable limits.\par \par \uc2 So first of all, we need to have standardized measures for how do you measure how much microplastics are in water? If you test this at different labs, you get different results.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, we need to figure out how much microplastics is, in fact, in the drinking water, and that`s going to vary from place to place. And then thirdly, is there actually a documentable risk of health effects with the levels of exposure people are typically getting in drinking water?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It`s helpful context. We`re at the beginning of this all. What would this look like in practice, though? Because I`m thinking about, I`ve got my plastic water bottle, I`ve got my plastic, you know, microwaveable things like, how would we actually do this?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, the most important thing, and this we can actually start doing now ourselves, is using less plastic, producing less plastic, figuring out substitutes for plastic, because we`re going to need to be doing all of those things individually, as well as companies and so on, doing these things in advance of any enforcement of regulations.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a lot to be done in the interim.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Okay, all right, so it starts with us right at home. Start reducing those things.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, really appreciate your time today.\par \par \uc2 Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Still ahead, a major setback for actor, Blake Lively in her legal battle with actor and director, Justin Baldoni. We will show you what happened.\par \par \uc2 Then, a missing Arizona girl is found alive after 32 years. How police got their big break in the case.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" the big surprise. One fisherman reeled in during a day at the beach.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Ancestry. Discover. Preserve. Share.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, time for a look at some of the other headlines that caught our eye with Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 All right, so this morning, we`re learning about allegations of a stunning crime.\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors say multiple people, including Atlanta rap star, Gucci Maine, were kidnapped and robbed at gunpoint inside a Dallas recording studio earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Gucci Mane, whose real name is Roderick Davis, is a Grammy nominated rapper and the founder of the 1017 record label. The Justice Department says nine people, including Memphis rapper, Pooh Shiesty, who is signed to Gucci Mane`s label, are you still with me, all right, also a rapper named Big 30 that they have been charged.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the victims were lured to the studio for what they believed was a business meeting about a recording contract.\par \par \uc2 But prosecutors say the group then carried out a coordinated armed takeover, robbing them of cash, jewelry and watches.\par \par \uc2 One of the victims was allegedly held at gunpoint with an AK-style weapon and forced to sign a release from a recording contract.\par \par \uc2 Eight suspects have been arrested, if convicted, they could face life in prison.\par \par \uc2 All right now to an incredibly close call in Seattle. This video is so disturbing. It shows the moment a man tried to shove a stranger in front of a moving train. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from King County Prosecutor`s Office/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: In the security footage, you can see the attacker sneak up behind the victim, who was looking down at his phone. Moments later, he pushes the man just as the train is entering the station.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, the victim is able to steady himself as the attacker fled. He was later tracked to a local mental health facility where he was arrested, and he is now facing attempted murder charges.\par \par \uc2 And you may have seen some sad news online yesterday, a social media post announcing the death of the world`s oldest living land animal, Jonathan the Tortoise, at the age of 193 years young.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a twist. It turns out the post was a hoax, and he is very much alive. "The Post" claimed it was from Joe Hollins, that`s the vet who takes care of Jonathan, except it was not Joe Hollins, the real Hollins posted on Facebook: "This is not true," alleging "The hoaxer is asking for crypto donations. It`s a con."\par \par \uc2 You can`t get Jonathan the Tortoise down, ladies and gentlemen. He lives to see another day.\par \par \uc2 And your next flight might cost you a whole lot more. United Airlines announced it is raising its checked bag fees on most domestic routes starting today. Your first and second bags will cost you another $10.00 each. So that`s 45 and 55 bucks. You want to check a third bag? I hope you`re sitting down. That`s going to cost you $50.00 more and will run you $200.00. The airline did not give a reason, but it is the second carrier to hike bag fees this week.\par \par \uc2 JetBlue did the same thing and cited rising operating costs, and if that wasn`t enough of a headache, this one really grinds my gears. You might want to start traveling with ear plugs, because British Airways will now allow passengers to conduct phone and video calls while in flight.\par \par \uc2 The change comes -- groan from the newsroom -- the change comes as airlines roll out powerful Wi-Fi using Elon Musk`s Starlink, so British Airways, basically, the planes will have the Wi-Fi capacity to have these calls, but they did tell passengers to, "Be mindful of volume. Use headphones and keep your voice low to avoid disturbing others," because flyers are so good at that, right, ladies?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Listen, it`s bad enough when you`re on an elevator going from floor 10 to the bottom, when somebody is on the friggin` phone. Imagine if you`re sitting on a flight and have to listen to a conversation.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, this is awful. I`m flying British Airways in May. I don`t love this update.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes! It is not great. You know and like, already we`ve had all these issues of people having fights on planes. That`s just going to make it worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 Kelly and I do not approve.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But no one asked us. Thank you, Adriana. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge has thrown out actor, Blake Lively`s sexual harassment claims in the legal battle against former director and co-star, Justin Baldoni.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A majority of Lively`s claims against Baldoni were dismissed yesterday, including claims that he sexually harassed her while filming the movie, "It Ends With Us," but it is not over yet.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A federal judge has taken action on the Hollywood legal battle between actors, Blake Lively and Justin Baldoni, dismissing Lively`s sexual harassment claims against her "It Ends With Us" co-star.\par \par \uc2 In the ruling on Thursday, the judge tossed out 10 of Lively`s 13 claims against Baldoni and other parties, including sexual harassment, defamation and conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: The judge tossed those, but not based on a finding that there was no evidence of sexual harassment, but instead finding the vehicles that Blake Lively tried to bring those claims, the federal law, the state law weren`t ones that she could use.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The lawsuit filed in December of 2024 followed the co-stars filming of "It Ends With Us," which earned more than $350 million at the box office.\par \par \uc2 Baldoni, who also directed the film, has denied all claims against him. His countersuit against Lively over defamation claims was dismissed by a judge last June.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In a statement, Lively`s legal team said, "This case has always been and will remain focused on the devastating retaliation and the extraordinary steps the defendants took to destroy Blake Lively`s reputation and how lively `looks forward to testifying at trial.`"\par \par \uc2 Baldoni`s legal team, says they were pleased the court dismissed all sexual harassment claims and look forward to presenting their defense to the remaining claims in court.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: When this case goes to trial, it will be significantly narrowed. This could motivate both sides to go back to the settlement table and try and find some sort of agreement.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "It Ends With Us"/Sony Pictures Releasing)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So a lot of back and forth in this case. The trial date is set for May 18th. The jury will hear Blake Lively`s remaining claims, including breach of contract and retaliation.\par \par \uc2 You know, I remember when both of them were here promoting that movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Separately.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Separately, and I remember talking to Baldoni about the movie then, and him talking about how much of an impact it had on him in terms of learning more about domestic violence and how important it was for men to speak up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So to see a movie that dealt with so much sensitivity and all of this stuff happening behind-the-scenes, allegedly, is interesting, and I will be covering that trial in May, so I`ll be all over it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I think that`s one of the tragedies, is that it takes away from the message of the movie, which is so important, because we`re all distracted with all of this legal back and forth between the two of them.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think everybody -- and I really did like, to your point, I really like the movie. I remember when Blake was here, she privately was saying there was some issues. So, I think we`ll all be glad when this is over.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, Jericka, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, she vanished in 1994 when she was just 13. Now police have found her safe and sound.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: They will tell us how they did it.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This next story is something you almost never see. Police in Arizona have located a woman who disappeared as a teenager, 32 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson shows how police unraveled this mystery from the 90s.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: On Wednesday, the Sheriff`s Office in Gila County, Arizona announced they had found a missing girl alive more than three decades after she disappeared.\par \par \uc2 The Sheriff`s Office told CBS News affiliate KPHO, Christina Marie Plante ran away and was with an undisclosed family member.\par \par \uc2 In May 1994, the then 13-year-old went missing in the small town of Star Valley, about an hour-and-a-half from Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 The Gila County Sheriff`s Office says Plante vanished in broad daylight under suspicious circumstances as she walked to a nearby horse stable. Despite exhaustive searches, Plante`s whereabouts eluded investigators for years.\par \par \uc2 At what point does a case go cold?\par \par \uc2 LANCE LEISING (Retired FBI Supervisory Special Agent): When you lack any viable tips anymore, you know you`ve -- you`re right in that cold case area and risk not solving it.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Lance Leising is a retired FBI supervisory special agent. He says investigative technology has grown exponentially since the early 90s, before law enforcement was even analyzing cell phone data.\par \par \uc2 LANCE LEISING: What these investigators are looking at are interviews, interview reports and maybe some surveillance logs, and that might be about it from that time period.\par \par \uc2 So there`s not much to go on, but you shake that tree enough, and something falls ou