cted to leave the workforce in the coming years. See what`s behind this trend and how to build a career that works for you.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Ahead, "48 Hours" investigates the case of a burglary that led to a far more sinister crime.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: I`m Peter Van Sant, "48 Hours."\par \par \uc2 A burglar caught red-handed leads police to a killer and the murder weapon was unique, a poisoned root beer float.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Adriana Diaz. That`s Kelly O`Grady. We`re all here.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour, investigators uncover a bizarre plot to kill a man with listen to this, a root bear float, boys and girls, you heard that correctly. This weekend`s "48 Hours" shows the family drama behind it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Why are women leaving the workforce exactly at the time when they would earn the most money? An expert will be here to show you how you can boost your career and your pay.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And Anthony Mason talks with folk rock superstars Mumford & Sons about the changes that led to their new album called "Prize Fighter."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know we`re all sitting here. Take a look at this picture, the three of us together. I have to say, I`ve been here 14 years, I`ve never seen three women at the same time.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It looks good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is. It is like, who runs the world?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Girls!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Girls!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all very happy to be here on this Good Friday with you.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this 8:00 hour, though, with the Artemis II mission to the moon. The four crew members spoke to reporters and family members overnight, revealing how the mission has gone so far, and it seems like so far so good.\par \par \uc2 This was the first time we heard from them since they lifted off on Wednesday. That was spectacular.\par \par \uc2 It follows one of the most crucial moments of the 10-day trip.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is at the Johnson Space Center with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning to you. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The Artemis crew successfully navigated that Trans Lunar Injection burn, and that allows them to become the first humans to leave Earth orbit since Apollo 17 back in 1972.\par \par \uc2 The TLI, as it is called puts them on what is known as a return -- a free return trajectory that will carry them on a loop back past the moon and then home to Earth, where they will crash down in off the coast of San Diego.\par \par \uc2 Now, Orion is set to loop its nearest neighbor, the moon, on day six of its 10-day mission, and during a special call, we got to hear from the four astronauts for the first time since they arrived in space.\par \par \uc2 They said hi to their families, addressed the now famous malfunctioning toilet, which they say is good for use, and spoke about just how beautiful the Earth looks from their vantage point.\par \par \uc2 Now, over the next few days, they are going to be putting Orion through its paces. They are also going to be testing things like their pressure suits and taking a lot of pictures as they go around the moon. They will likely see the far side of the moon, parts of the moon that the human eye has never seen before. They are hoping to capture that and send those images back home to us.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Kris, I can`t wait to see it. I can`t get enough of this story. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And our newsroom looks amazing.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, this week`s "48 Hours" looks at a case that started with a burglary and ended with a murder investigation.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of text messages, exotic poisons and one root beer float revealed the harrowing story behind the 2022 death of one Indiana man.\par \par \uc2 "48 Hours: correspondent Peter Van Sant reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: In September 2023, Jackson County Sheriff`s Department Lieutenant Adam Nicholson was called to investigate a burglary at the Freetown, Indiana home of Marsha Allen.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT ADAM NICHOLSON (Jackson County Sheriff`s Department): She had recently put a home security system in her house.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Marsha lived alone. Her husband Harold, had died just the year before.\par \par \uc2 Surprisingly, Detective Clint Burcham says Marsha told police she recognized one of the burglars.\par \par \uc2 CLINT BURCHAM (Detective): Marsha Allen described Stephen White as her daughter, Ashley Jones` best friend.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Police found Steven White living nearby. They asked him about the burglary.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NICHOLSON: Did she know anything about it?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN WHITE (Suspect): No.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: But White couldn`t lie for long.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NICHOLSON: I got a picture here.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN WHITE: Can I see it?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: After Nicholson showed him a security camera screenshot, White admitted to the burglary, but then went on to tip investigators to an entirely new case, and this one was a murder claiming that Marsha was responsible for her husband`s death.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NICHOLSON: Steven White told me that Marsha Allen had killed her husband, Harold by poisoning a root beer float.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN WHITE: Marsha went into the kitchen and made a root beer float with whipped cream and sprinkles on top of it and she poured something out of a bottle into there.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Investigators started looking into White`s allegations. They quickly uncovered nearly 7,000 texts between Marsha and her daughter, Ashley, sent in the three months before Harold died.\par \par \uc2 CLINT BURCHAM: There`s something going on that they were indeed trying to poison Harold Allen.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Police say they created a timeline showing the mother daughter duo had tried to kill Harold over and over with different poisons, including ethylene glycol, an ingredient in antifreeze ordered online.\par \par \uc2 The night before he died, Marsha texted Ashley, "He`s all in for root beer floats."\par \par \uc2 ADAM NICHOLSON: You put it in a root beer float with ice cream and root beer, you`re not going to know until it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And Peter Van Sant joins us now. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is such a crazy story.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: It is.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: What was the poison that they ended up putting in the root beer float?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: It was this ethylene glycol, which goes into antifreeze. It has a very sweet taste, so if you put it in with something sweet, like a root beer float, you just don`t -- you don`t have a chance. You won`t know you`ve been drinking poison.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s the perfect mixer for a murder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was divorce not an option?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: What was what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was divorce not an option?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Apparently not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t kill me, just leave.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: They`d only been together for about a year before this happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That makes it even worse.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: And these text messages that are discovered, and you`ll see in the course of this, these two women, which stepdaughter and wife, this was like a sport. They were enjoying this. It`s like nothing I have seen before on a "48 Hour" story.\par \par \uc2 And it was those text messages, though, that led to their downfall.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Oh, my goodness. Peter Van Sant, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And you can watch Peter`s report "The Root Beer Float Murder" on "48 Hours" tomorrow night at 10/9 Central here on CBS and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ahead, a warning to parents about a disturbing new video game for kids related to the Jeffrey Epstein case.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will reveal a surprising future trend for women in the workplace. What you need to know to stay competitive in the job market, but first, it`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Graham Norton Show"/BBCI)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s "Rubber Band Man" by Mumford & Sons featuring Hozier. Ahead they`re going to tell Anthony, as in, Mason, about the new album and regaining their confidence as a band. When did they lose confidence? They were so good.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We always thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, parents, listen up.\par \par \uc2 A new online game connected to the Jeffrey Epstein case is raising some serious concerns. The game`s premise: Survive five nights without being caught by the convicted sex offender at his home.\par \par \uc2 Social media videos show kids playing it in classrooms, and there are tens of thousands of downloads.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver is here with a look at the pushback.\par \par \uc2 Meg, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The game is modeled after the horror game "Five Nights at Freddy`s" and simulates security camera views to move between different rooms to escape Epstein.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with a therapist and mom about the disturbing themes in the game and what parents should know.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Can you imagine what would draw kids to play something like this?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SAN GIACOMO (Clinical Social Work and therapist): No, I mean, it`s, it`s very Wow. It`s very life-like you`re there.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s just teaching them the shock value.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Do you think kids really know what they`re doing?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SAN GIACOMO: No. I mean, I can`t imagine that kids know the severity of this game in comparison to the people who actually experienced these horrors.\par \par \uc2 So, I can`t imagine they know the full picture of what this means, so much so as it`s just the thing online for them to do.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Are you surprised that students are playing this game in schools, on school issued laptops?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SAN GIACOMO: I mean, yes, how do they have the time? But that`s one question for them, but being able to gain access to these things is just so concerning that they have the time or the access to be able to distract themselves from what they need to be doing.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: We don`t want to promote this in any way. But why is it important to discuss it?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SAN GIACOMO: I think it`s important to discuss it just like we discussed drugs and alcohol and all the other risk factors that are accessible to our kids, we have to protect them.\par \par \uc2 So, it really comes down to parents and clinicians and educators to work together to do those things.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: What`s your first reaction when you look at this? What`s your takeaway?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SAN GIACOMO: It`s just so concerning that kids are really vulnerable right now, especially that adolescent time period where they`re trying to figure out their way.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it`s important for families to have conversations about the values, the values in their home, the values in schools, the values in their communities, and without those values as an anchor, do we want values such as this to be what our kids pick up?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what`s the name of the game so parents know what to look out for? It`s just even disturbing to hear Jeffrey Epstein and a video game for kids on the same sentence.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: All in the same sentence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, in the same sentence.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: "Five Nights at Epstein`s" and the therapist that we talked to is Ashley San Giacomo. She had never seen this, or ever heard about it before our camera started rolling, but she was floored when she went home that night. Her kids did know about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: It led to a tough conversation, but an important one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And you have an update since your interview, plus some tips for parents.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Yes. So here are some tips.\par \par \uc2 A few things you can do right away, keep out the computers, the laptops in living areas where you can observe. Check the browser history and always keep cell phones and computers out of children`s bedrooms. Those are things you can do right away.\par \par \uc2 In terms of talking to your kids about this, check in with yourself first. Are you comfortable about this topic? Approach it calmly. Ask them what they know, to get information, then give them the correct information, so you can educate them and in the end, set a goal. It can be as simple as bringing those computers out of bedrooms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That advice about checking the browser, really good.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, and what about -- I mean, school districts, are they doing anything to prevent kids from accessing the game? I realize it would just be at school, but it is something.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Right. So, we reached out to all the school districts where we found that kids were playing this game, and they have blocked the website hosting the game. But we all know there are ways around that, so it would be almost impossible unless a school district stops using laptops in the classroom to take this on by themselves.\par \par \uc2 So they`re asking parents to educate and teach responsible tech.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just the fact that somebody, guys, would even set up a game like this --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- and I think that this is good for the sake of money or whatever they`re doing it for is so disturbing in the world we live in today.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I`m glad you are doing this, Meg.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really glad.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, new research shows more women are choosing to leave the workforce. We`ll talk with an expert about what`s behind this trend and how women can protect their career growth.\par \par \uc2 We are back in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does anybody work nine to five these days?\par \par \uc2 I don`t know!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Hard no!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hard no!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Five to nine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Five to nine.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we have a wake-up call for women in the workforce, talking to you. The percentage working through their peak earning years, that`s from age 40 to 54 is expected to decline in the next decade. Yikes!\par \par \uc2 That`s according to jobs website, Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Priya Rathod is Indeed`s career expert and workplace trends editor is here with what women need to know. We are all listening to you, Miss Priya with both ears.\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD (Indeed`s career expert and workplace trends editor): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So why is this happening? And you mentioned the ages of 40 to 55, but isn`t it wider than that? Forty to 54? Isn`t wider than that?\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Yes, I mean, 34 to 54 is what we`re really talking about here.\par \par \uc2 On the surface, it looks good, right? You know, the labor force participation rate is higher for women than men, but underneath that surface, there`s this intergenerational pressure cooker, and it`s ready to pop.\par \par \uc2 You know, women are disproportionately taking on household tasks as it relates to raising children, taking care of elderly parents. There is rising health care and housing costs, and the workforce isn`t keeping up with flexibility and the ability to work remote, which women need to stay in the workforce.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Women have always taken on more responsibilities, but it`s a variable that`s changing now, the fact that the cost for child care and elder care is going up, so women are taking on more of that load. Is that what`s changing?\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Absolutely! And so the costs are going up, and also they need to be able to have that flexibility in order to manage everything.\par \par \uc2 And when we see workforces reducing that flexibility, you`re pushing these women out, and frankly, you`re losing all of the great experience that they are here to give you.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: What questions as a woman is interviewing, should she be asking to these employers to identify, you know, the benefits of whether that culture is going to be a good fit?\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Absolutely. As a mom of three and a workforce expert, I tell my friends this all the time, test that flexibility before you say yes.\par \par \uc2 Don`t be afraid to ask those hard questions. We think they`re going to disqualify us, but it has to fit into your life. Ask --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are the hard questions?\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: What does it look like if have to leave for an unplanned appointment during the day? What does it look like if I have to pick up my child and then log back on later at night? Right?\par \par \uc2 The job has to fit into your lifestyle. And remember, you have a lot to offer that these workforces need.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I know and if I`m sitting in an interview, I`m going to think that that`s going to think that that`s going to get me -- that`s a red flag.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It will get you, next.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is going to be a problem, don`t you think?\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Listen, no one is more productive than a mom who has to pick up her child ta 3:00 P.M.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Preach!\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Right? I mean, we all know this. So you`re also bringing your productivity to the table, right? And you`re saying that in an output generated job, I`m still going to do the job, but I want to make sure that this fits into my lifestyle, too.\par \par \uc2 And I do think it`s okay to say that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve heard you say that it`s so much more than your compensation, that you should look at your overall package, such as?\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Exactly. So you know that old adage, time is money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: It`s very true here, right? So think about in an environment right now where raises aren`t as common. What can you ask for? Can you ask to work remote two days a week? Can you ask for a hybrid schedule, and then think about the things they offer that are going to save you money.\par \par \uc2 Child care assistance, backup child care assistance, FSA funds, programs that help you do remote doctor`s appointments. All of those things contribute to where your time is put and how productive you are.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: We`re also told, I feel like all the time to our authentic selves, and especially as we grow older, that becomes more important. We don`t want to deal with all the B.S.\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: But I also feel like that advice is not actually what employers want often. You`re told to, whether it`s dress a certain way, talk a certain way.\par \par \uc2 How should women who are getting older in their careers navigate that challenge when they want to bring their authentic selves to work?\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: You know, I think that`s such a good question. I think it`s important to understand the environment and the culture that you`re in right now.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`ll speak to myself I work at Indeed, I`ve been there remote for 10 years. There`s certain meetings in which I do bring my authentic self, and there are certain meetings in which let`s talk about the data, and I`m going to show you how it`s backed up.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s very much situationally based. You know, it depends on where you are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You also say you should consider a lateral move. Because I was raised -- who considers a lateral move in less than 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: I mean, exactly. I think I talk to my friends about this all the time. Think of your career like a lattice, not a ladder, right upward momentum. It`s not always achieved by the next obvious step up. Sometimes it`s a lateral move about transferable skills, you learn more A.I.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So true!\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much Priya for all of these different things --\par \par \uc2 PRIYA RATHOD: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: -- to think about. We so appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 In case you missed it, on CBS MORNINGS deals this week, we showed you a selection of bamboo bed sheets.\par \par \uc2 All right, so they are available for up to 62 percent off the retail price.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping right now, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 "Talk of the Table" coming up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It sure is a beautiful day.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It`s time to bring you the stories that are talk of the table.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, you`re up first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beautiful day anytime you can hear U2, and my "Talk of the Table" -- it is U2 dropping new music today to mark Good Friday. It`s called the "Easter Lily EP." It features six new tracks exploring themes of friendship, loss, hope and ultimately, renewal.\par \par \uc2 So the band calls the closing track, "Coexist." I love the word. It is a prayer for religions to figure out ways to better accommodate each other. Here`s a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Coexist (I Will Bless the Lord at All Times)"/U2/Island Records Limited)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Only loveliness." One of the lines says, "Drones hover without any consciousness over war crimes, I will bless the Lord at all times."\par \par \uc2 It`s a very different sound from U2 and Bono describes it this way, "Love is bigger than anything in its way, even if there is a lot in its way." I like that.\par \par \uc2 "Easter Lily" ecstatic music to these antagonistic times. It`s really beautiful. This track is released today, intentionally, intentionally.\par \par \uc2 "Easter Lily" he says is a result of band members digging deeper into their lives, asking very personal questions to deal with what they call these wilderness years, because these are very tumultuous times we are living in.\par \par \uc2 The band is speaking to this. It`s a bit of a departure -- well, you know, they are often outspoken about different things, but they wanted to get this moment right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they do.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: On Good Friday and Passover, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I am definitely going to be listening to that after this. So thank you for putting that on my list.\par \par \uc2 Okay, switching gears a little bit. My talk of the table is Zendaya making headlines just before her new movie, "The Drama" opens in theaters today.\par \par \uc2 All right, so ever since she started her press tour, rumors about a secret marriage to long time beau, Tom Holland have been swirling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is just --\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I mean, fire!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Never misses!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes. She`s a fashion queen, an icon.\par \par \uc2 Zendaya has done little to dispel those rumors. Now, she showed up at the L.A. premiere in a white gown she wore to the Oscars in 2015 making fans wonder if it was her something old.\par \par \uc2 She also had appeared with what to be a gold wedding band on her ring finger.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yup!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: She is -- hey, why not drum up "The Drama" for that drama. Next was something new at the Paris Premier, a Louis Vuitton number in bridal white --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gorgeous!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And then --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the back is out on that. It is gorgeous!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Oh, well. I mean, she never misses.\par \par \uc2 Something borrowed was next, Zendaya wore a black gown made for Cate Blanchett to the premiere in Rome. Blanchett wore that gown to last year`s Venice Film Festival and the 2022 SAG Awards.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Zendaya showed up to the movie`s New York premiere on Wednesday in a gown that some called bridal blue. This is phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Absolutely stunning. Meanwhile, you might remember this back in February, Zendaya surprised a bride at a bridal shop in New York City and brought her dream wedding dress for her.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So what do we think? Like is she trying to tell us something? Is she drumming up some drama for "The Drama"?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just think she and La Roche know exactly what they`re doing. That was him to the side, her stylist.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Her stylist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That dress. I mean, the jaws dropped when she came on the red carpet last night. Schiaparelli, it is so, so, so spectacular.\par \par \uc2 She hasn`t had a miss -- she hasn`t had a misstep, ever!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And she is always good at sort of doing things that help with the movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at the -- look at her facial expression.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Some people miss on that, but she definitely doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I think she`s teasing us and enjoying it every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we are here for it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We are here for it. Love to be.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is a discussion between young parents that`s heating up on Reddit. So basically, the mother stays home all day with the six-month-old child the mother has to nurse the baby to sleep every night.\par \par \uc2 The mother likes to take a shower at night, but the father asked the mother, actually told her, please don`t shower at night, because when you`re gone, she cries and you know, she only wants you, and the mother is upset because she`s like, I can`t shower during the day, I`m alone all day with the baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: My initial thought is a bit controversial, because usually I am Team Mom.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Give it to us, come on.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But I`m thinking, well, during the day the baby naps. You can bring the baby into the bathroom in a bouncer and take a shower, like a lot of mothers have had to do that. So I`m thinking, they can get creative.\par \par \uc2 But I read a little more deeply, and she`s cleaning at night also like, why is this father not stepping up? And he is not even trying to soothe her, apparently, at night while the mother is in the shower.\par \par \uc2 So, I think they have to have a little bit of speaking to each other and figuring out how they can both work better together to take care of this baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going to stay with Team Mom.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, Team Mom all the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And with the ladies, of course, we are going to root for the mom, right?\par \par \uc2 An opportunity to step up, Father!\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, Mumford & Sons talks with Anthony Mason about collaborating with artists like Hozier, Chris Stapleton and Gracie Abrams on their new record.\par \par \uc2 But first, a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Jonathan Ross Show"/ITV)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Man, they`re so good. Mumford & Sons is on a roll. Less than one year after releasing their last album, the band`s new record, "Prize Fighter" recently debuted in the Top 10 of the Billboard Hot 200.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Anthony Mason sat down with the Grammy winners in the place where "Prize Fighter" began.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When Mumford & Sons started working on their new album, "Prize Fighter" at Long Pond Studio in Hudson, New York.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD (Mumford & Sons): Kind of the most Mumford & Sons record we`ve made, I think.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I think something happened for Marcus Mumford, Ted Dwane and Ben Lovett.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: This is like body confidence record or something. You know, like, there are other records where you like try on different clothes, and that`s really fun to do.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Playing dress up a little bit here and there, and you make sure you`re within it. This one, we like to call our clothes off. We stood in the mirror and we`re like, all right, that`s it, then.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rubber Band Man"/Mumford & Sons/Glassnote Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So it kind of all started in here.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Yes. This is the room.\par \par \uc2 BEN LOVETT (Mumford & Sons): Hallowed ground.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The band had been working on their last album, "Rushmere" here at Electric Lady Studios in New York when producer Aaron Dessner happened by the control room and shared some music.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: But I always sat here, and when Aaron started playing it to us off his phone from the corner, and on the first run through, I had a legal pad here with a pen and just started writing lyrics.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It became the song, "Rubber Band Man" and Hozier joined them on the track, just as Chris Stapleton would join them on "Here" and Gracie Abrams did on "Badlands."\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: And then basically, she became like a fairy godmother for the record. She was cheerleading.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The band, says Dessner, who produced the album, unlocked something.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: We kind of got our confidence back on this record.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: You know, I think our bands kind of been on the back foot for a while.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Creatively.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Why is that do you think? Why was it?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Insecurity.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: We got big really fast.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: We outgrew ourselves a little bit.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: We climbed all the rungs on the ladder, certainly live. You know, we went from pubs to clubs to academies to theaters to arenas and then stadiums. And it happened really fast, and suddenly we`re standing on stage at the Grammys winning Album of the Year and like, it kind of went over our heads that whole period of time, honestly, because it was really fast.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Little Lion Man"/Mumford & Sons/Glassnote Records)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mumford & Sons led a folk rock revival in the early 2010s that made them one of the biggest bands in the world.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said that Pharrell basically changed the way you thought about yourself.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Yes. He really did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It was Pharrell Williams who told Mumford, he needed to own up to being an artist.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: And he took me next door and gave me a pep talk like a coach. And he said, he said some very nice things, which I`m too English to repeat, really. And was like, look, man, basically you`ve got this.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: It`s time for you to grow up a little bit.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: And inhabit the person that we see you as.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Was there a reluctance to see yourself as an artist?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Yes, yes. I was in denial about that for a long time. I think partly, it is cultural like art, the way I was brought up, not because of family, more because of school. Art was extracurricular.\par \par \uc2 Yes, in England, if you go around calling yourself an artist, you`re a bit of an (bleep).\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In 2021, when founding member, Winston Marshall left the band, Mumford, Lovett and Dwane reached a crossroads. Suddenly the quartet was a trio.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was your choice to go on. You didn`t have to.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And maybe you considered you didn`t want to. I don`t know. What were you thinking at that point?\par \par \uc2 TED DWANE (Mumford & Sons): There was never a conversation about not continuing. There was never any real certainty around continuing. It was like --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TED DWANE: It was just an assumption, I think, just an implicit belief between us that, like we love what we do, and nothing`s really changed.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Were you concerned at all when Winston left, that somehow the chemistry would change? Because it can.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: Totally can, yes. I`d say, not really concerned. We`re more curious.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN LOVETT: The best way to answer that question for us was just to get in a room and make music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN LOVETT: I think within like three hours, we answered the question.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In October of 2022, when Mumford was touring his first solo album, Lovett and Dwane joined him at the Ryman in Nashville, the first time they`d sung together on stage in nearly three years.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: At that moment, I was -- I was like, yes! Yes! Yes! What we shouldn`t have done was broken up and done a reunion tour.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That does work!\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MUMFORD: You know what I mean. Great business plan.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It works every time.\par \par \uc2 Mumford & Sons will tour arenas and stadiums across America this summer.\par \par \uc2 BEN LOVETT: And now, these are going to be the biggest shows of our career, which is just pretty fun this, just you know, dip in to feel like we`re still climbing up, you know, some sort of there`s still places to explore, stages to stand on that we haven`t.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, they are going to be playing Red Rocks, Wrigley Field, Fenway Park, three nights at Madison Square Garden.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is the biggest tour they`ve ever had.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is the music out now? Because I want to get it right now.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is out now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a collaboration.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s a really great album. It`s a -- it`s a very Mumford & Sons album.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I bet.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But it has an even brighter sound to it, I think.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it`s interesting Pharell gave him a pep talk, like he doubted himself.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is so good.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It came at a moment when he was in the studio. Somebody walked in and he got very shy, and he kind of backed away and didn`t want to see want to sing and Pharell is like, what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And that`s when that happened.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, I can`t wait to listen to this.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at back of the week. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This is what I sang karaoke with Chris Stover, who is our senior producer watching in the Control Room right now. Okay, I have to keep going. We are out of time.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this weekend, the culinary traditions of West Africa by way of Baltimore. "CBS Saturday Morning" takes us to Jollof Bowl, where spiced rice is the main attraction.\par \par \uc2 Then on "CBS Sunday Morning," the acclaimed series "Hacks" is back for its final season -- say it ain`t so -- Tracy Smith catches up with stars, Jean Smart and Hannah Einbinder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then what happens tomorrow and Saturday?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: These two!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We will see you there. Bye!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I made clear from the beginning of Operation Epic Fury, that we will continue until our objectives are fully achieved.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Speaking at a prime time address to the nation, the president defended his decision to attack Iran and said the U.S. military needs more time.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Trump did not offer any new details about a time frame, a plan for winding down, or what victory would look like.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Three, two, one! Booster ignition and liftoff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The story that has captivated all of our imaginations, the Artemis II mission to the moon.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It`s a 10-day mission and four astronauts will loop around the moon. The ultimate goal: To establish a permanent lunar base, and then one day launch a trip to Mars.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can`t make history without a little inspiration. So every day, they`ll be waking up with music, usually a song sent to them by their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re talking to you about NASCAR. NASCAR. So take us back why the sport means so much to you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JORDAN (23XI co-owner): My father, actually, he was a die-hard mechanic, and he likes driving fast. My mom likes driving fast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you like driving fast, Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JORDAN: No, I never drive fast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t believe you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JORDAN: Yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (Bleep)! My God! Oh my God! Oh my God! Oh my God! Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 MAN: There is one and two!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, don`t talk. Don`t talk. Don`t talk.\par \par \uc2 My wife is driving I always --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JORDAN: Pay attention.\par \par \uc2 TYLER REDDICK (Race car driver): Yes!\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JORDAN: I`ve got to pay attention. I put my seatbelts on and I pay attention.\par \par \uc2 TYLER REDDICK: When we approach a corner, my hands go out.\par \par \uc2 DENNY HAMLIN (23XI co-owner): I don`t get it. Why does it have to be all gas or all brake?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Michigan Police are searching for a woman who made a daring escape after being arrested. The woman was handcuffed and taken into custody over the weekend. She somehow managed to squeeze through and not even fall on her head. She fell right on her feet! Unbelievable!\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: I can`t get over that escape artist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, the lady.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: She has got to go in "America`s Got Talent."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Adriana, how is she still at large? Has anyone seen a woman her hands cuffed behind her back running down the street.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: She is talented.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Legendary actor and comedian, Kenan Thompson.\par \par \uc2 KENAN THOMPSON (Comedian and author): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is going to tell us about his very funny new children`s book.\par \par \uc2 KENAN THOMPSON: It`s pretty funny. I think it`s pretty funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s called "Unfunny Bunny." But Matt and I were saying, look, I was cracking up, so was he on the third page. What does a shoe like to eat for dinner, Kenan?\par \par \uc2 KENAN THOMPSON: Spaghetti and feetballs.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a classic!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why are socks so good at golf, Kenan?\par \par \uc2 KENAN THOMPSON: Because there`s always a hole in one. Come on!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Okay, that one actually took me a second. Now I get it.\par \par \uc2 Time for "What to Watch." with her, her, her, her!\par \par \uc2 Take it away, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I mean, yes, yes! Vlad!\par \par \uc2 Don`t get me started, Gayle, okay. I was one of those TikTok dancers. I am really savage!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wondering why Vlad and Nate are not here? Listen to this. They`re actually both on vacation in the same place. They are in Portugal. They didn`t even know they were going until last minute, and then they decided to meet up for dinner. You see them all there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when you`re on vacation, do you want to see your co- workers?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, I do. Gayle, you don`t want to see us?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am like, oh, you`re here! Great!\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m like, hi, Gayle! Should we have dinner?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Should we have dinner?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260403em4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS EVENING NEWS for March 17, 2026, CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Kris Van Cleave, Dave Malkoff, Ed O`Keefe, Charlie  D`Agata, Jonathan Vigliotti, Juan Palop, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 3,745 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to be with you. I`m Tony Dokoupil. The breaking news right now.\par \par \uc2 Travel chaos: The major American airports forced to close security checkpoints tonight as thousands of TSA officers quit or call out sick. Entire airports could shut down next. What the Trump administration is saying.\par \par \uc2 The other developing story tonight. Digging out and cleaning up after that massive cross-country storm. And now sub-freezing temperatures moving in.\par \par \uc2 And the war in Iran: New questions tonight about who`s running that country as two more major regime leaders are killed in airstrikes, according to Israel.\par \par \uc2 And the political fight back here at home. A top Trump administration official resigns in protest, the president`s response tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also, day 45 of the search for Nancy Guthrie. The new images from cameras on her property. What investigators are now saying.\par \par \uc2 Also, that fireball over the U.S. and the sonic boom that followed it. What the heck was it? And the grandparents` happy hour bill. What could possibly go wrong?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News Headquarters in New York, this is the CBS EVENING NEWS with Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good evening. And the situation at American airports has gone from bad to worse over the past 24 hours. Democrats are refusing to fund the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  unless Republicans agree to changes in immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 But guess who`s caught in the middle? 50,000 TSA officers working without pay amid a partial government shutdown or not working at all. About 10 percent of them have been calling out in recent days. And that leaves more than two million airline passengers a day to deal with multi-hour security lines and heightened concern about the risk of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Also late today, we learned that half the checkpoints at Philadelphia International Airport will be closed effective tomorrow. And that`s not all. Senior Transportation Correspondent Kris Van Cleave is all over this story for us. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good evening. How bad could this get?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, Tony, it could become a situation where those two plus hour lines become more common than not at airports across the country and could get worse yet. If an airport can`t staff its checkpoints, that airport may have to close for the day.\par \par \uc2 Flyers prepare for the airport to get a lot less fun this spring break. In Denver, it was a rocky morning.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: This is nuts. I`ve never seen \uc2 DIA\uc2  like this.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): At the Atlanta airport, another day of wait times topping two hours. Nearly 40 percent of the airport`s TSA officers called out sick Monday, forcing a checkpoint to close.\par \par \uc2 JIM MARCOTTE (Atlanta Flyer): If I didn`t come two hours earlier, I`d miss my flight. Look at this line.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): And tomorrow, three of the TSA checkpoints at the Philadelphia airport will be closed. TSA sick calls are surging since officers missed a paycheck Friday due to the month-long partial government shutdown. Monday saw the most yet. More than 10 percent of officers nationwide called out. That`s more than five times a normal day.\par \par \uc2 ADAM STAHL (TSA Acting Deputy Administrator): Our people are hurting every single day. We have individuals sleeping in their cars, drawing blood to afford to pay for gas to get to work. So, the situation financially is dire for officers.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Acting Deputy TSA Administrator Adam Stahl.\par \par \uc2 ADAM STAHL: If the cholera rate does climb, there could be scenarios where we may have to shut down airports. This is a serious situation.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And it sounds like every day this goes on, you think it`s going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 ADAM STAHL: Yes. The longer we do not receive funding from both the department and for TSA, this will get worse. This will not get better.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Wait times are already increasing.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WALDRON (Houston Flyer): We`re going to make our flight. We`re thinking this is probably at least an hour`s wait.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): At Houston`s Bush Airport today, the lines hit at least 103 minutes, 86 minutes in San Juan and an hour at Chicago O`Hare.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now, it does not sound like airport closures are imminent. First, the TSA would look to reduce the number of open lanes and the number of open checkpoints. But particularly for smaller airports, that is a possibility. What can flyers do? Check your airport`s website before you head to the airport. Most have up-to-date wait times. And then leave yourself some extra time when you get to the airport, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the weather tonight, parts of the West, there are already extreme heat alerts days before spring starts. But further east, cold air has moved in, lingering after that major storm dropped feet of snow across the region.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent Dave Malkoff is following the cleanup.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): The relentless winter set its sights today on Watertown, New York, where heavy snow and slick roads caused hazardous driving conditions. More than a dozen vehicles crashed on an interstate closing the highway in both directions for miles.\par \par \uc2 In southern Minnesota, neighbors are digging out after getting slammed with more than two feet of snow.\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: It`s a lot. It`s the most we`ve had ever that I remember at one time.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): And further south, a strong line of storms brought heavy rain and damaging winds to places like Tennessee and Georgia. We met one family in Atlanta who told us winds as strong as a Cat 1 hurricane brought a tree down onto their home.\par \par \uc2 JERRY COLLIER (Homeowner): I heard metal breaking, glass breaking, so I jumped up.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): Tony, she says her 95-year-old mother was in bed at the time. This is what`s left of the bathroom and closet.\par \par \uc2 JERRY COLLIER: My mother, she fell.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JERRY COLLIER: I bumped her head, and -- but she went to New York. She was five.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): The warning`s out west. Dangerous heat is on the way. Our Carter Evans is in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: As temperatures approach 100 degrees in parts of Los Angeles, the fire department here is preparing for an influx of heat-related calls. And one of the first things they do when they find someone in distress is get these ice packs under their arms and start an I.V. to rapidly cool down the body.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 1: Someone may not feel as hot. They may not be sweating. And that`s, you know, that`s a point of like, hey, maybe we`re reaching a heat stroke kind of situation where they`re showing later signs and don`t realize it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Heat in the west and a freeze warning here in the southeast until tomorrow morning. Rosalynn Brown, who is 95 years old, who lived here, told me she is still in disbelief. She`s going back to the hospital, Tony, tomorrow morning to have another brain scan. It`s incredible that she survived this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dave, thank you very much. To other news now, in rare public remarks today, Supreme Court Justice John Roberts defended the independence of judges, denying that any of his colleagues are somehow working on behalf of the president who appointed them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS (Chief Justice): The notion that we carry forward the views of the people that appointed us is absurd. President George W. Bush appointed me 20 years ago. The idea that I`m carrying out his agenda somehow is absurd.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Roberts, who did not mention President Trump by name, also said personal criticism of federal judges is dangerous and, quote, "it`s got to stop."\par \par \uc2 To other news out of Washington today, for weeks, the war in Iran has exposed a political fault line here at home. Some prominent supporters of President Trump have argued hard against U.S. involvement. And now, for the first time, a top official in the Trump administration itself has resigned in protest over the war.\par \par \uc2 Here`s CBS Senior White House Correspondent Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): National Counterterrorism Center Director Joe Kent, a staunch ally of the president, today became the first senior government official to openly criticize and resign because of the decision to attack Iran. In his surprise resignation letter to President Trump, Kent, a retired Army Green Beret, said Iran posed no imminent threat to our nation and it`s clear that we started this war due to pressure from Israel and its powerful American lobby.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (U.S. President): I always thought he was a nice guy, but I always thought he was weak on security.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Trump praised Kent last year when he nominated him. Today, he brushed him off.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: When I read his statement, I realized that it`s a good thing that he`s out because he said that Iran was not a threat. Iran was a threat. Every country realized what a threat Iran was.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): The president`s claims that Iran was poised to strike the U.S. have been disputed. Kent`s defection reiterates the ongoing split in the MAGA coalition. After the president campaigned on ending wars --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 2024)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: Instead of a planet on the edge of global war, we will have world peace.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): His decision to start one has infuriated high- profile personalities on the right, like Tucker Carlson, Joe Rogan, and former congresswoman Marjorie Taylor Greene.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Megyn Kelly Show)\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE (Former Congresswoman, R-Georgia): We have seen enough of our American troops dead and murdered for foreign countries.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Kent faced bipartisan criticism today for suggesting Israel had influenced Trump`s decision to attack Iran, but several Democrats said they agreed with his concerns.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-Connecticut): Nobody should know better than the chief of antiterrorism that Iran posed no imminent threat.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ed, while we have you, the Republican-controlled House Oversight Committee subpoenaed Pam Bondi to testify about the Epstein files. What more can you tell us there?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yeah, that`s right, Tony. This issue just simply won`t go away. The committee is ordering the attorney general to appear next month to discuss whether the department is complying with a law requiring it release all of the files related to Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ called it unnecessary and wouldn`t say if Bondi plans to comply. She and her deputy, Todd Blanche, are scheduled to meet with the committee tomorrow to discuss the matter.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Trump says he doesn`t know who`s running Iran, not after U.S. and Israeli airstrikes decimated senior leadership. And the question of who`s in charge got even harder overnight after Israel took out two more regime leaders, including the man thought to be running the country. Here`s Senior National Security Correspondent Charlie D`Agata.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): Ali Larijani was last seen in public just a few days ago denouncing President Trump in an open-air rally in the Iranian capital. Iran`s Security Chief, Larijani, was in charge of the bloody crackdown that left thousands of demonstrators dead earlier this year. During this war, responsible for directing attacks across the region, like this one in Iraq, a swarm of drones targeting the U.S. embassy compound in Baghdad overnight, most shot out of the sky with no casualties reported, launching much larger ballistic missiles against Israel in an effort to penetrate one of the most advanced civilian defense systems in the world.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: We got rare access to one of Israel`s most advanced launchers, the Arrow 3. When it comes to Israeli air defenses, this system is at the top, both in terms of altitude and capability, designed to take out long-range missiles at a great distance before they become a threat on the ground.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): But the intense barrages across the Middle East have raised concerns that interceptor missile stockpiles might run low. Major A, who cannot reveal his identity for security reasons, is an Israeli air defense officer.\par \par \uc2 MAN 3: We know how much they have, we know how much we need to defend against it, and we`re ready for as long as we need to be here.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: You`re not running short.\par \par \uc2 MAN 3: I`m very satisfied with what we`re doing and how long we`re going.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Major told us those missiles also take part in the protection of U.S. forces in the region. Tonight, defense officials tell CBS News more than 200 service members have been wounded across seven countries. The vast majority have returned to duty.\par \par \uc2 Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We are nearly 50 days now since Savannah Guthrie`s mother disappeared. The suspected abduction remains unsolved tonight, but we`ve learned investigators have recovered additional images from Nancy Guthrie`s surveillance cameras. National Correspondent Jonathan Vigliotti has our update.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Law enforcement sources tell CBS News the additional images were obtained over the last couple of weeks from surveillance cameras installed at Nancy Guthrie`s Tucson home. One camera was fastened to a fence and focused on the back of the house, including the swimming pool area. A second camera showed the driveway and front of the garage door.\par \par \uc2 The new images captured family members, landscapers, and pool workers stretching back weeks prior to the kidnapping, but nothing deemed suspicious. Week seven of the search for Guthrie and the person or people that abducted her, this doorbell camera video remains the only known image of the suspect.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 LANCE LEISING (Retired FBI Supervisory Special Agent): It`s lack of meaningful leads. That`s the initial thing it says to me.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): DNA recovered from the crime scene is still being analyzed.\par \par \uc2 LANCE LEISING: If they`re using forensic genetic genealogy, it takes a while. Maybe you`ve got multiple people within the same DNA family that could potentially be suspects, and then you have to investigate each one of those independently.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Federal investigators used genetic profiling to track down Bryan Kohberger following the Idaho University murders. Authorities continue to receive tips. Over 1,500 leads came into the FBI after the Guthrie family announced a million-dollar reward.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (Nancy Guthrie`s Daughter): We still believe.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): It`s been nearly three weeks since the family called on the public for help and more than a month since law enforcement has held a press conference.\par \par \uc2 LANCE LEISING: I`d like to be hopeful that -- and I`m sure the family is incredibly hopeful, that the silence is because they`re on to something. They just need to investigate harder.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It took 47 days to track down and arrest Bryan Kohberger. We are now on day 45 with this investigation. The FBI continuing to call on the public for help. Anyone with information is asked to call those numbers. They`re on the screen, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Cuba, a country that President Trump said he expects to have the honor of taking, as he put it yesterday, adding today that the U.S. will be doing something about Cuba very soon.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the U.S. cut off Cuba`s access to Venezuelan oil, and since then, the country has seen rolling blackouts and protests, too. Reporter Juan Palop is in Havana with more. Juan, good evening.\par \par \uc2 JUAN PALOP (Reporter): Good evening, Tony. Here in Havana, there is little fuel left, transportation is limited, and very few public services are available.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUAN PALOP (voiceover): And now, once more, no power across the island. The nationwide blackout yesterday is still keeping much of the country in the dark. In a nation used to hardship, Cubans say the situation has never been worse.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of protesters have taken to the streets in Havana and in cities like Moron, where last Friday, demonstrations turned violent.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: We`ll be doing something with Cuba very soon.\par \par \uc2 JUAN PALOP (voiceover): Meanwhile, President Trump is ramping up the pressure, stating that it would be an honor to take over Cuba.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JUAN PALOP: On the island, Tony, many are just pleading for an end, whatever comes next.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Juan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, a fireball in the sky and the question on everyone`s mind, what the heck was that? We`ve got the answer next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was seven tons of something moving at more than 40,000 miles an hour across the skies of the central U.S. today, and it came with a sonic boom, otherwise normal Tuesday morning. So, what was it? CBS` Mark Strassmann got answers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Watch screen right, something streaking over Mercer, Pennsylvania. Truck driver James Eaker had thoughts.\par \par \uc2 JAMES EAKER: What the fact was that?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: How would you describe your reaction in that moment?\par \par \uc2 JAMES EAKER: Unfiltered. It took me a second to register, you know, that, I mean, honestly, for a second there, I just thought I was seeing things.\par \par \uc2 PITTSBURGH POLICE, FIRE AND EMS: Some type of rocket or something burning.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): From Maryland to Michigan, people and cameras saw the same thing, a seven-ton meteoroid hurtling 45,000 miles per hour. They also heard something.\par \par \uc2 CLEVELAND POLICE AND METRO HOUSING AUTHORITY: Something went boom earlier. Shook us over here.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): It was a shockwave as atmospheric pressure exploded the meteoroid over Medina County, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 BILL COOKE (NASA`s Meteoroid Environments Office Head): Some fragments, some tiny pieces of it, actually made it to the ground.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Bill Cooke charts plummeting space objects for NASA when they`re big enough. This one, six feet in diameter, was too tiny to track.\par \par \uc2 BILL COOKE: This could just be a small asteroid that hung out in the belt and eventually migrated here, or it could be a fragment from a larger one. We really don`t know.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Online theories were more definitive. A streak of revelry on St. Patrick`s Day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A fireball this size flashes over North America about once a month. Ninety-five percent of small asteroids and meteoroids vaporize before they ever have a chance to land on Earth or on social media.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s the other five percent I`m worried about, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You, me, both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a high-rise fire in Manhattan just minutes before the St. Patrick`s Day parade. How it unfolded, how it ended, we`ll tell you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A skyscraper fire brought parts of New York City to a standstill today. Take a look at these pictures. Flames and thick black smoke pouring from the roof of a building undergoing renovations. This is just blocks from Times Square. We`re told it started in the HVAC system. Three people hurt before the FDNY put it out. Nice job.\par \par \uc2 It`s five o`clock somewhere, but not for senior citizens in Minnesota. The fight to change that, next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Only in America tonight, a fight described as an effort to legalize happy hour for grandma and grandpa. Now you may think the freedom to knock one back with friends is near universal in America, at least after the age of 21, or certainly the Vista of 65 plus. But a senior living facility in Minnesota was recently told they`d need a liquor license for any gathering involving alcoholic beverages.\par \par \uc2 And the regulators in this case, I have to say, were right. The law was on their side, but they were messing with the wrong generation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANITA LEBRUN: Just because we are older and live in an assisted living doesn`t mean that we should have fewer freedoms than anyone else.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here, here, Anita LeBron, a grandmother who lives at that senior living facility, she lobbied state lawmakers saying she shouldn`t have to worry about slipping on the ice while getting her own box of wine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANITA LEBRUN: My friends and I love happy hour, just like many of you do, I am sure. Over a shared drink, we get to reminisce about parts of our life, military service, raising a family, the loss of a friend, and celebrating the golden phase of our lives too.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anita`s senior living center backs her effort calling the license requirement red tape without value, and her local state senator introduced a bill to change things.\par \par \uc2 In fact, after Anita`s testimony, the only pushback was good-natured. A 38- year-old state senator was told he was too young to join the happy hour himself. A final vote could be as soon as this month.\par \par \uc2 That`s another day in America, St. Patrick`s Day, so we`ll leave you with pictures from today`s beautiful parade in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (PARADE)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020260318em3h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 13, 2026, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Jonah Kaplan, Ash-Har Quraishi, Scott MacFarlane,  Matt Gutman, Elaine Quijano, Lonnie Quinn, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,805 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to be with you. I`m Tony Dokoupil with the breaking news right now.\par \par \uc2 Why he did it, what we`ve learned about the suspect now dead in that attack on a Jewish synagogue and school in Michigan, his family ties to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants in Lebanon and the wider threat of antisemitic violence, what authorities are now warning.\par \par \uc2 Also developing tonight, day 14 of the war with Iran and the U.S. death toll rises. The Pentagon confirming the loss of six crew aboard that refueling plane killed while supporting Operation Epic Fury as Marines and more American warships head toward the region, what President Trump is saying.\par \par \uc2 And the war hits home for Americans at the pump. Nearly two weeks of gas price hikes, one family`s hard choices tonight.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: How does that affect your budget?\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY (sp?): It affects it a lot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The blizzard threat in the Midwest, plus severe thunderstorms elsewhere, what it means for your weekend.\par \par \uc2 And mass time, speed equals flight. What happened when she landed?\par \par \uc2 Also, Steve Hartman "On the Road" with the story of Dave the Rave, a local legend, tonight going national.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News Headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News with Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good evening. American and Israeli weapons have hammered more than 15,000 targets inside Iran. That is the latest count from the Pentagon, tallying what is a massive and ongoing U.S. and Israeli air campaign. But two attacks back here at home have authorities on alert and everyday Americans worrying about what might be targeted next.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we`ll have more on the man who opened fire on a classroom of ROTC cadets in Virginia, how he allegedly got a gun despite a past felony conviction for supporting the \uc2 ISIS\uc2  terror group. But we`re going to begin in Michigan, where we`ve learned that the man who rammed a truck packed with explosives into a school has family ties to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , that Iran- backed militant group in Lebanon. We have two reports from Michigan this evening. We`re going to begin with CBS News Correspondent Jonah Kaplan outside Temple Israel in West Bloomfield. Jonah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Tony, good evening. Temple Israel, it`s one of the biggest synagogues in America, and it`s not just a house of worship. There`s also a preschool, a kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, all the congregants, the members, the staff, the clergy, the students, the teachers, everyone`s safe, physically, but this attack has left the community feeling both rattled and resilient.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN (voiceover): Investigators are still sifting through the damage and collecting evidence from Thursday`s attack on Temple Israel. Staff member Cassie Cohen was working inside when a truck packed with explosive materials rammed into the building.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE COHEN (Staff Member): I can`t get out of my mind the sound of the car crashing and then the gunfire.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN (voiceover): On Friday, federal agents raided the Dearborn Heights home of 41-year-old Ayman Mohamad Ghazali, a Lebanese-born American citizen identified by investigators as the suspect. Ghazali died from a self-inflicted gunshot wound to the head.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: This is a physical building, but I imagine this hits your emotional, your spiritual core.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE COHEN: Yeah, this is my spiritual home. You know, my kids belong here.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN (voiceover): Officers stormed the building as it burned to help evacuate more than 100 students and staff. Some of those officers were treated for smoke inhalation. Clergy members are grateful no one was seriously hurt, but Rabbi Jen Lader says this community is now grappling with an endless amount of emotional wounds.\par \par \uc2 JEN LADER (Rabbi): None of us signed up for this. This was not -- this was not the feeling in America when we became rabbis.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN (voiceover): Just two months ago, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s Detroit office led an active shooter response training for Temple Israel staff and clergy, many here crediting that training for saving lives.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: What is the message to Jewish institutions and other houses of worship around the country?\par \par \uc2 JEN LADER: Thanks to these protocols and these safety procedures and the strength and bravery of our people, this is not a different story.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: And while this is still an active crime scene, Tony, it still is the Sabbath here at sundown, and Temple Israel, it`s holding services tonight for Shabbat and tomorrow. There`s even a bar mitzvah that`s going to be celebrated, but at a different location. Meanwhile, security at this synagogue and all others, it`s not free. The \uc2 Jewish Federations of North America\uc2  estimate the cost to secure Jewish life in America is about three quarters of a billion dollars every year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You mentioned that resilience, let`s linger on that. Jonah Kaplan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to what we know about the man behind that attack just described, who he was, how he came to America. Let`s bring in Ash-Har Quraishi. Good evening, Ash-har.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Good evening, Tony. Well, CBS News is learning more about Ayman Mohamad Ghazali tonight. Overnight, federal agents raided his home behind me. Just two days before the synagogue attack, Ghazali, seen here on this security video, bought more than $2,000 worth of fireworks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the FBI confirmed that fireworks were found in the truck. Ghazali was born in Lebanon and entered the U.S. legally in 2011 on a spousal visa, becoming a U.S. citizen five years later. He lived alone in Dearborn Heights, just down the block from his job at this restaurant.\par \par \uc2 Now, on March 5th, family members, including Ghazali`s two brothers, niece, and nephew, were killed when an Israeli drone attack struck their village in Lebanon, leaving him distraught, according to community members. A local journalist in Lebanon spoke to villagers who claimed Ghazali`s brothers were members of Hezbollah`s rocket unit, but would not provide their names.\par \par \uc2 Now, Dearborn Heights Mayor Mo Baydoun said this afternoon that while Ghazali recently suffered a tragedy, there is no excuse for violence. He also pledged support for the local Jewish community.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ash-Har, thank you very much. We`re going to turn now to what happened in Virginia yesterday, the new information coming in on the man with prior ties to ISIS who opened fire on an ROTC class at Old Dominion University. The instructor he killed as well, a former combat helicopter pilot and the cadets who prevented an even wider tragedy. Here`s justice correspondent, Scott MacFarlane.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yeah, Tony, police are investigating it as an act of terror and not the first attempt by Mohamed Jalloh of Virginia. He`d been released from prison in 2024 after serving eight years for trying to donate money to ISIS and for plotting a terror attack. Investigators say Jalloh`s gun had an obliterated serial number, complicating their tracing of the gun.\par \par \uc2 Late today, the Department of Justice has charged a man suspected of selling that gun to Jalloh, despite Jalloh`s felony conviction. He`s a former Army National Guardsman. He killed the instructor of the ROTC class at Old Dominion University, Lieutenant Colonel Brandon Shah, who was an Army pilot who flew helicopters over Iraq and over Afghanistan and who left behind a spouse and a child.\par \par \uc2 Two others were injured in the attack and CBS News has learned at least one of the students in the class stopped Jalloh and killed him with a knife. He was let out early of his federal prison term after completing a drug treatment program in 2024, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Another big story in Washington, a federal judge has blocked a part of the Justice Department`s probe into Fed Chair Jerome Powell, ruling that it could not force officials at the Fed to cooperate. President Trump has had a long running feud with Powell, but in January, the Justice Department launched a criminal investigation into him with the president alleging that a $2.5 billion renovation was so over budget as to be suspicious. The judge today said he`s seen no evidence of a crime. The administration, meanwhile, is appealing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO (Interim U.S. Attorney: He has neutered the grand jury`s ability to investigate crime. This is wrong and it is without legal authority.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Powell`s term ends in May.\par \par \uc2 Turned out of the war with Iran, tonight we`ve learned that all six crew members died on that refueling plane that crashed in Iraq, bringing the U.S. death toll to 13 in Operation Epic Fury. As the war`s major focus turns now to Iran`s efforts to disrupt the world`s oil supply. Chief Correspondent Matt Gutman has it all from the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (voiceover): With tankers on fire and ships dead in the water, today, Secretary Pete Hegseth tried to project that the U.S. was in control of the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (U.S. Secretary of Defense): They are exercising sheer desperation in the Straits of Hormuz. Something we`re dealing with, we have been dealing with it, and don`t need to worry about it.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (voiceover): CBS News has learned the Pentagon is sending more warships and up to 5,000 sailors and Marines to the Middle East as hundreds of tankers remain stuck, terrorized by Iranian attacks. The Trump administration says opening the Strait is a top priority to keep the global oil supply moving. General Clinton high notes studied what it would take to do just that.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Why is it so difficult to quote-unquote, "open the Strait of Hormuz?"\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. S. CLINTON HINOTE (Ret.) (United States Air Force): It`s such a narrow waterway. And because they know where you`re going to be, they can plan out their attacks, say eight times we get tankers through and two times we don`t. Well, no company out there is going to take that risk.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (voiceover): The Pentagon says the pace of Iran`s missile and drone attacks has decreased by over 90 percent. But Iran has attacked at least 16 ships in the Persian Gulf since the war began. Earlier this week, that Thai flag tanker was hit by a suspected missile. The Thai Navy says three crew members are still trapped on board, but it`s too dangerous to rescue them.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: What are the options that the U.S. currently has in terms of getting ships through there?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. S. CLINTON HINOTE: You`re going to need to control the bank, the Iranian coastline. And if you do that, you`re almost certainly going to need ground troops.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And Tony, that additional amphibious force being sent to the region also includes a contingent of Marines who are trained to land on shore and control territory. But General Hinote tells me that the least painful outcome for all sides would be a negotiated settlement.\par \par \uc2 Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Matt, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With a key source of the world`s oil supply choked off by Iran, oil prices are up more than 40 percent since the start of the war. Here in the U.S., that has pushed gas prices up more than 60 cents a gallon over the very same time period. And families feeling the most shock in this are the more than 30 million American households living paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Correspondent Elaine Quijano has one family`s story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): When Kelly Sudevansky (sp?) hits the road, she knows just how much it costs to fill up her Toyota Corolla.\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY (sp?) (Consumer): It`s around $25 to $30.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: How much does it cost now to fill your tank?\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY: I would say around 50.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: How does that affect your budget?\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY: It affects it a lot.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): The mother of three earns about $18 an hour as a senior caregiver near Los Angeles and drives three hours round trip to work. She worries if prices keep rising.\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY: We can`t afford to do the commute for work. We would have to quit the job.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): She and her husband had been saving up for a family spring break road trip to Las Vegas and the Grand Canyon. But now --\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY: We canceled it. With gas being that high, I can`t do the extra two, $300 that it`s going to cost.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: What is this like for you?\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY: I`m depressed. I promised my kids stuff and I hardly ever, ever break my promises. This time around, I have to. It breaks my heart. That extra $300 and I can`t do it.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): California already has one of the highest gas taxes in the country. And in the past month, the average price for regular gas jumped about 19 percent. More than 80 cents a gallon. Nationwide prices are up too. Regular gas increased 23 percent or about 70 cents on average over the same period. The disappointment hits Sudevansi`s kids hard.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: I was like, oh man.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 1: I was very excited.\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: Gas prices are high. I mean, we can just like go to Drummer`s house. It`s not really that far.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): Sudevansi who voted for President Trump blames the war in Iran for higher gas costs.\par \par \uc2 KELLY SUDEVANSKY: I believe in our president, okay? It`s great that he wants to help another country, but it`s not great when your homeland, your people is having to suffer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Adding to the cost this time of year, gas stations tend to switch from winter blend gasoline to the more expensive summer blend.\par \par \uc2 Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Elaine, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A major update tonight in the case of the teenagers who accidentally ran over their high school math teacher. This happened after the teens had toilet paper in the teacher`s home as part of a senior prank, a prank that the teacher, Jason Hughes, was in on.\par \par \uc2 When he ran out after them, he fell and was hit. But Hughes` widow had asked for all charges to be dropped, hoping to prevent what she called a separate tragedy of ruined young lives. And today, prosecutors in Gainesville, Georgia agreed.\par \par \uc2 Now to the weather. We are heading into the last weekend of winter and a coast-to-coast storm is on the march. Lonnie Quinn tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: You know, Tony, believe it or not, that coast-to-coast storm has its origins in Hawaii right now. They`re dealing with their own rough weather. If you look at the maps, the jet stream will pick that energy up, pull it into the Pacific Northwest and make it go from West Coast to East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Look at almost every sort of warning or watch you can think of throughout the weekend, blizzard warnings, high wind warnings. There`s two storms. One exits the East Coast on Saturday, but that second storm moves into the Midwest on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 So, places like Green Bay into the UP of Michigan, you could have blizzard conditions, one, two, three feet of snow south of it. Anywhere from Shreveport into portions of Indiana, like around Louisville. I mean, you could be dealing with tornadoes out there.\par \par \uc2 By the time you get to Monday, the East Coast is covered with rain. Top to bottom, it`s a bad Monday out there, particularly Charleston to Washington, D.C. That`s where you could have severe weather. It exits on Tuesday, but it`s significantly colder behind that front. If we come back here live here in New York City, all right, on Tuesday, St. Patrick`s Day, we typically have a reading of around 50 degrees and that wind is kicking in.\par \par \uc2 Tony, it`s going to be cold St. Patrick`s Day. We`ll be getting ready for the parade. It`s around freezing in the city itself for the biggest St. Patrick`s Day parade anywhere in the country. It`s going to be a cold one this year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No one`s going to feel it and you know exactly why, Lonnie. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the broadcast tonight, the age of the flying car has not yet arrived, but this happened, we`ll explain.\par \par \uc2 And later, Steve Hartman "On the Road" with the ultimate sports fan and what he means to his neighbors.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: After spending most of last week in the Middle East, a region at war since before America was even born, I was really excited to get home because we are living a miracle here, a country of 330 million people from everywhere somehow formed into one of the most tolerant and flourishing societies the world has ever known. But this time it felt like the war had followed me home. The day I got back, two young men, said to be inspired by ISIS, tossed homemade bombs into a crowd of cops and protesters, hoping for carnage.\par \par \uc2 A few days later, another man opened fire on a classroom of ROTC cadets, murdering their instructor. And just after that, another man, this one driving a truck full of explosives, rammed his way through the entrance of a synagogue full of children at school.\par \par \uc2 What unites these cases, authorities say, is that terrorism was the goal. And if not for extraordinary acts of heroism, the cops here in New York, the quick-thinking ROTC cadets in Virginia, the private security guard in Michigan, we right now would be a very different country, not on alert, as the headlines say, but utterly changed, mourning the death of police, of college kids, of an entire building of children and their teachers.\par \par \uc2 This week, with a little luck as well, the protections we have in place worked. The question for us now, as we consider what could have been, is whether we and our leaders are truly prepared for when the protections don`t work.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Drivers pull gently to the side of the road when they see police -- when they see police in the rearview mirror. This woman in Wisconsin didn`t do that. Hitting 96 miles an hour before losing control Wednesday, careening into a ditch and lift off. Police smashed a window to save the driver and the 20-year-old passenger. Both were already on probation.\par \par \uc2 And you would think it`s difficult to keep a crime a secret with this many co-conspirators. You`re looking at a jewelry heist last year in Fremont, California. The video just released by the Justice Department. Two dozen bandits took part, making off with $1.7 million, but in nearly nine months, only four arrests. None of those guys are talking.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Steve Hartman "On the Road" with a small-town celebrity named "Dave the Rave."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Finally tonight, a small-town sports fan who`s become much more than just a face in the crowd. Here`s Steve Hartman "On the Road."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Clinton, New Jersey is known primarily for its old gristmill, its quaint downtown, and its historic Dave.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 2: Dave is, I would say, like a pillar of the community.\par \par \uc2 MAN 3: Everybody knows Dave.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 3: He is the essence of our town, I think.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Dave is Dave Robbins.\par \par \uc2 MAN 4: What`s up, brother?\par \par \uc2 DAVE ROBBINS: Hello.\par \par \uc2 MAN 4: How you doing, man?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Dave was born with an intellectual disability, struggled with academics, but excelled at school spirit. He quickly became a fixture at local sporting events, often donning his trademark Dave the Rave cape.\par \par \uc2 MAN 5: He`s at every single football game. He`s at every basketball game. Win, lose, he`s there.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): But what`s really interesting here is that back when all this started, back in the 60s, special needs kids were often isolated, sometimes even institutionalized. And yet Dave has been embraced, really celebrated for more than half a century now.\par \par \uc2 For example, Dave turned 80 last week and the town made a huge deal of it at multiple events, going way over the top, as they so often do with all things Dave.\par \par \uc2 GENE ROBBINS (Dave Robbins` Brother): It`s somewhat annoying to go to the supermarket with Dave and everybody says hello to Dave and nobody says hello to you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): This is Gene Robbins.\par \par \uc2 GENE ROBBINS: Oh, Dave the Rave, I saw you at the game. He`s Dave`s jokingly jealous brother and caretaker.\par \par \uc2 GENE ROBBINS: Here you go, bud.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Fortunately, Gene says his bruised ego is way outweighed by the gratitude he feels for this community.\par \par \uc2 DAVE ROBBINS: Good to see you, Linda.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): During our visit, Gene pointed out that no matter where Dave goes, there`s always someone watching out, guiding him gently or just engaging him in conversation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 5: They always just kept tabs on where Dave was and what he was doing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 6: I`m like, oh, Dave, hop in, you know, we`ll give you a ride home.\par \par \uc2 MAN 6: Dave, like we wouldn`t want it any other way.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): In America, inclusion is now everywhere. But it`s important to remember that this embrace began long ago in small pockets in communities like Clinton, where acceptance was and still is all the rave.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, on the road in Clinton, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Steve. Thank you, Clinton, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 That`s another day in America. I`m Tony Dokoupil. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020260314em3d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 6, 2026, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Matt Gutman, Kelly O`Grady, Omar Villafranca, Rob  Marciano, Jonathan Vigliotti, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,691 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening. I`m Major Garrett in Washington. Tony Dokoupil is heading back from the war zone.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, one week into the war, President Trump says there will be no deal with Iran and calls for the regime`s, quote, "unconditional surrender," unquote. Chief Correspondent Matt Gutman leads our coverage from the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Stocks tumble while oil surges to its highest price in years. MoneyWatch correspondent Kelly O`Grady on the economic impacts of the war.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our new "60 Minutes" interview with Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Does this put U.S. personnel in any more danger than it otherwise would?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (U.S. Secretary of Defense): Well --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The Russians involved?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: No one`s putting us in danger. We`re putting the other guys in danger.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Also breaking tonight, deadly storms rip across the central U.S. The monster tornado tearing through southern Michigan this afternoon. On the ground for possibly over an hour, ripping roofs off businesses, causing widespread damage. And a mother and daughter killed by a tornado in Oklahoma. That storm threat, not letting up tonight. Meteorologist Rob Marciano here with the timing and track.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a cat stuck in a tree for three days. So, they called in a specialist.\par \par \uc2 And Steve Hartman "On the Road." A little boy goes in for heart surgery and comes out with so much more.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News Headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS with Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening, everyone. I`m Major Garrett coming to you from Washington, D.C. Tony Dokoupil, the only network anchor to report from the Middle East this week, will be back at the CBS Broadcast Center in New York Monday.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with new developments in the war with Iran. One weekend, President Trump says the only way it ends will be, not with a deal, but with the Iranian regime`s complete surrender.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the White House confirmed the president will be at Dover Air Force Base in Delaware tomorrow, when the remains of six \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  reservists killed in an Iranian drone strike return home.\par \par \uc2 On assignment today for "60 Minutes," we spoke exclusively with Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  about Russia providing the Iranians crucial intelligence. That is coming up.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Matt Gutman is leading our coverage of the war tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Matt, good evening.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: Hey, good evening, Major. Today, Iran`s President Pezeshkian claimed that some countries have begun mediations to end a war that he says Iran did not start. But in a response, in a social media post, President Trump offering his latest evolution on an endgame on this war, saying he would only agree to a deal after Iran`s unconditional surrender.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (voiceover): Explosions and flames rocked Iran today as Israeli fighter jets targeted the Iranian regime`s primary wartime bunker. 100 munitions dropped in a fiery instant. Smoke blanketed Iran`s capital. A fierce blast could be seen near the airport. And another attack turned the iconic Azadi sports complex into a smoldering husk.\par \par \uc2 Seven days in, U.S. military says it has struck over 3,000 targets and damaged or destroyed 43 Iranian ships, sinking this drone carrier, bombing planes on the ground and jet hangars. This past week, a strike destroyed an Iranian elementary school. Dozens of girls were killed. And a person familiar with the ongoing inquiry told \uc2 CBS\uc2  that investigators believe the U.S. may have been responsible. And we`re told by sources that the Israeli military was not operating there at the time.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, as the war expands geographically, \uc2 CBS\uc2  has learned that Russia, a major U.S. adversary, is helping Iran identify U.S. targets in the region, including U.S. troop locations.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): It clearly is not making a difference with respect to the military operations in Iran because we are completely decimating them.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (voiceover): That is overnight. One of two missile strikes aimed at a major U.S. air base in Kuwait was successfully intercepted. Though Israel says it has destroyed up to 80 percent of Iran`s missile capacity, missiles continued to blaze toward Tel Aviv overnight. But noticeably fewer.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: The sirens just sounded here. You can see this woman and her daughter running to a shelter. Streets are empty here, but this is only the second sirens in the past 20 hours.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (voiceover): Decreasing drone and missile attacks from Iran have enabled charter flights carrying Americans to trickle back to the U.S. Some Democrats have blasted the administration for its chaotic handling of Americans stuck in the conflict zone.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY KIM (D-NJ): It`s clear that this administration has just been making this up as they go along.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (voiceover): The president is also facing criticism in the Persian Gulf. A Dubai billionaire asking the president on X, "Did you calculate the collateral damage before pulling the trigger?"\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And today Qatar`s energy minister has warned that continued war could bring down the economies of the world, and, quote, predicting that all Gulf energy exporters would shut down production within weeks and drive oil to $150,000 or $150 a barrel. It is already at its highest level in more than 18 months.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the impact of the war on the U.S. economy. Oil and gas prices are going up and stock value is coming down. The fighting thus far has not been good for our wallets and our 401(k)s.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Money Watch Correspondent and CBS Saturday Morning Co- Anchor, Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Well, today marked another tough day for Wall Street with the Dow off by about 450 points. Two things drove the volatility, oil and jobs.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with energy. U.S. crude surged again, jumping over 12 percent to top $91 per barrel. That`s the biggest one-day gain since 2020. How the spike followed President Trump`s comments, the conflict in Iran would continue until there`s unconditional surrender.\par \par \uc2 That has traders worried the energy supply crunch could drag on. Drivers are already feeling it. The national average for gas is now $3.32 a gallon up 34 cents in just one week. And as oil prices march towards $100 a barrel, fears are growing we could eventually return to record $5 gas.\par \par \uc2 Our rising energy prices could also cause a ripple through the economy. For example, jet fuel prices could impact airfare and transport costs could affect groceries. All of this, though, will depend on the scale and length of the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Now to the jobs front. The February report showed the U.S. economy lost 92,000 jobs, while the unemployment rate rose to 4.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 A big part was due to bad weather and a health care strike, but three of the last five months showed net job losses. And these figures don`t reflect any potential impact linked to the Middle East uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Normally a slowing job market could open the door to interest rate cuts, but with inflation risk building, the Fed`s job just got a lot harder.\par \par \uc2 Matt?\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN: And indeed it did. Kelly O`Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now for more on the war and the rest of the day`s news, let`s go back to Major Garrett in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Our thanks to Matt Gutman in the Middle East and Kelly O`Grady at the Broadcast Center in New York.\par \par \uc2 We turn our attention now to our exclusive "60 Minutes" interview with Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth. Matt mentioned reports that Russia`s got new involvement in this war, providing intelligence to Iran about U.S. positions and movements in the region. We asked the Secretary about that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: Well, we`re tracking everything. Our commanders are aware of everything. We have the best intelligence in the world. We`re aware of who`s talking to who, why they`re talking to them, how accurate that information might be, how we factor that into our battle plans, our CENTCOM commander, so we know what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 And the president has an incredible knack at knowing how to mitigate those risks, and so the American people can rest assured. Their commander-in- chief is well aware of who`s talking to who, and anything that shouldn`t be happening, whether it`s in public or back-channeled, is being confronted and confronted strongly.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The American people can therefore expect conversations with the Russians to stop this?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: Well, I -- President Trump, as people have seen, has a unique relationship with a lot of world leaders where he can get things done that other presidents, certainly Joe Biden, never could have. And through direct conversations or indirect, through him one-to-one or through his cabinet, messages definitely can be delivered.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Does this put U.S. personnel in any more danger than they otherwise would be?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: Well --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The Russians involved?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: No one`s putting us in danger. We`re putting the other guys in danger. That`s our job. So, we`re not concerned about that. We mitigate it as we need to. Our commanders factor all of this, but the only ones that need to be worried right now are -- are Iranians that think they`re going to live.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: You can see much more of our exclusive interview with Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth Sunday night on "60 Minutes."\par \par \uc2 Turning to the weather, a monster tornado ripped through Oklahoma, causing widespread damage. It`s part of a dangerous storm system threatening the central U.S. and southern plains. More now from CBS News Correspondent Omar Villafranca in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): A tornado tore through southern Michigan today --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 1: Holy crap. Our roof just peeled off.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): -- leaving a path of destruction through the town of Three Rivers.\par \par \uc2 This man recorded the twister from inside a store as it approached. Debris could be seen flying through the air, everything in its path destroyed. The tornado so strong it ripped the roof off a hardware store and reportedly damaged the local high school.\par \par \uc2 RONDA THRAMS (Three Rivers Resident): Oh my, is what went through my mind. I didn`t think it was this bad.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): When it was safe, people could be seen checking on their vehicles in a strip mall parking lot.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 2: Oh my God. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): The severe weather is part of a storm system sweeping across the country.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: We have water over the bridge right here.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Causing flash flooding across parts of the Midwest. And spawning at least one deadly tornado last night in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 A mother and her teen daughter were killed while traveling in a van.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY ROBINSON (Major County, OK Sheriff): We believe that the vehicle was hit directly with a tornado.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): It was one of 11 tornadoes reported from Kansas to the Texas panhandle.\par \par \uc2 Today, crews scrambled to clean up and restore power.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: No rest for folks in Oklahoma tonight. In fact, storms have started firing off in that direction and in the Tulsa and Oklahoma City areas, there have already been several tornado warnings and much of the state is under a tornado watch until 10 p.m. tonight.\par \par \uc2 Major?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 That severe weather, of course, is still on the move, so let`s check in with CBS Meteorologist Rob Marciano on what`s the latest.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, Major, multiple rounds of it. So, the same areas that got hit with that tornado have another severe line of storms that`s on their doorstep right now. Let`s take a look at the radar and all the watch boxes that we have out now.\par \par \uc2 Severe thunderstorm watch out now for parts of Michigan, northern Indiana, from Gary to South Bend to Kalamazoo, you`re going to get hit, and then tornado watches out from central Kansas up through Des Moines and then much of Oklahoma, including Tulsa, OKC, and Dallas, all of these until 10 o`clock tonight, so a dangerous night ahead for sure.\par \par \uc2 There you see the storms firing overnight and then refiring during the day tomorrow. So, Dallas to Waco to Houston, you get it. So, does Jackson, Mississippi, and another batch heading into the Ohio River Valley and parts of western Pennsylvania. All fueled by record-setting heat. We had dozens of record highs today. We`ll see more in the way of them tomorrow. Near 70 in Detroit, 77 expected in Pittsburgh. Into the 80s in Atlanta, that continues into next week. Chicago will be in the 70s on Monday, and Raleigh will be in the 80s on Tuesday. New York City getting up and over potentially 70 degrees come Tuesday. Heading into spring, all this heat and humidity fuels these dangerous storms.\par \par \uc2 Major?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Difficult weather weekend ahead. Rob Marciano, thank you for the warnings on that.\par \par \uc2 Some of the biggest names in politics, particularly on the Democratic side of the aisle, were on hand today in Chicago for the funeral of Reverend Jesse Jackson. The mourners included former Presidents Biden, Obama, Clinton, former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton, former First Lady Jill Biden, and former Vice President Kamala Harris.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (Former Vice President): He gave us the language, the rainbow, to understand, as has been said, the beauty that comes after the storm when we see what is possible and what can be unburdened by what has been.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Mr. Obama said Jackson`s presidential campaigns in the 1980s paved the road for so many others to follow.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the CBS EVENING NEWS, a 22-year-old man is accused of killing three women. Why investigators say he did it, and importantly, how they caught him.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Now to a shocking story in Utah. A 22-year-old man from Iowa is accused of killing three women there. We get details now from CBS News National Correspondent, Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Late today in a Colorado courtroom, 22- year-old Ivan Miller was charged with murdering three women this week and leaving this quiet and rugged corner of Utah reeling.\par \par \uc2 BEAU MASON (Utah Department of Public Safety Commissioner): To have multiple homicides in one day is just unprecedented and it just -- is devastating to the communities.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Police say Miller, an Iowa native, was on a cross-country road trip. His alleged crime spree began when his truck hit an elk and while it was being fixed, he came to this home in the small community of Lyman, Utah, where prosecutors say he killed 86-year-old Margaret Oldroyd, stole her car and then abandoned it several miles away at this trailhead near Capitol Reef National Park. That`s where he allegedly killed Linda Dewey and her niece, Natalie Graves, and stole one of their cars.\par \par \uc2 Police were able to track Miller through a key fob and by using an automated license plate reader system as he allegedly drove the victim`s stolen Subaru on an hours-long journey from southern Utah to the mountain town of Pagosa Springs, Colorado, where he was arrested early Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: How do you make sense of what unfolded in the state?\par \par \uc2 BEAU MASON: I don`t know that we do make sense of this. To try to make sense of it is probably not even fair to the victims.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And according to charging documents, Miller told investigators he randomly targeted these three women because he needed money and transportation to get back home, Major.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, the suspect is in Colorado, so tell us what happens next.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Mm-hmm. Yeah, Major, he`s expected to be extradited back to Utah to face those charges. If he is found guilty, he could face the death penalty. Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Jonathan Vigliotti, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a little cat stuck in a big tree and a guy who turned tricky situations like this, he`s done this, made a whole career out of it. More on that when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: He can pitch, he can hit. Question, is there anything baseball`s Shohei Ohtani can`t do? Check this out. The 2-1 now.\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: Ohtani lights it up. It is, good!\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The L.A. Dodgers All-Star launched a grand slam at the World Baseball Classic in Tokyo, helping Japan crush Taipei in the opening game final, score 13-0. Major League Baseball`s opening day is, I`m very happy to say, less than three weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Chicago`s Ford Assembly Plant recently received orders to build an SUV for an unnamed VIP. Now, they know who. Pope Leo XIV, the Chicago native, received the custom Explorer hybrid, complete with a Windy City skyline emblazoned on the inside. The pope got the keys from Ford`s CEO and his wife, who paid for the vehicle themselves.\par \par \uc2 From Toms River, New Jersey tonight, we have video of something that feels to us like a strategic miscalculation. For three long days, high in a white pine tree sat Biscuit, a year-old tabby with lots of ambition but no clear exit strategy. An arborist named Steve Murrow got the call Sunday. Steve actually started his tree business as an outgrowth, see there, of rescuing stranded cats. He slowly gained Biscuit`s confidence, grabbed him at just the right moment, then carefully lowered him to his grateful owner. Steve has done more than 150 rescues like this since 2022.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Steve Hartman with the story of a little boy named True who found true love.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Finally tonight, a story about a hospital visit that for one little boy changed everything. Here is Steve Hartman "On the Road."\par \par \uc2 ALL TOGETHER: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Surrounded by friends and family on his birthday, this 10-year-old was on cloud nine. But as you`ll soon see, his bliss hasn`t come easy.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2022, that boy named True needed a heart procedure. He was under the care of social services at the time. And the day of the surgery, for whatever reason, he was just dropped off here at Children`s Nebraska. Anesthesiologist Amy Beethe found him in pre-op.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY BEETHE (Anesthesiologist): He was just sitting there all alone and --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): So, he`s there for a major procedure and there`s no adult with him at all?\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY BEETHE: No adult with him at all.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Why were you there by yourself?\par \par \uc2 TRUE BEETHE: I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Did you know why you were there?\par \par \uc2 (SHOOK HEAD)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): The procedure lasted about seven hours. And through it all, Dr. Beethe says she just kept staring at the sweet face of the poor boy who at that moment had no mom or dad or stable home life. And that`s when Amy decided that even though she already had six kids, she just had to take in this seventh.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY BEETHE: After I dropped True off in recovery, I called my husband and I just said, we need to have a talk when we get home and I need you to have an open mind.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Ryan says he was a little hesitant at first.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BEETHE: But it didn`t take long to hear what was needed and it just felt right.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): A year and a half later, their adoption was complete.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY BEETHE: So, yeah, that`s how the story goes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): But it`s not how the story ends. Up until that point, True had been living with five other siblings in that same unstable home environment. Amy knew she and Ryan couldn`t adopt all of them. So, the good doctor decided to do the next best thing.\par \par \uc2 First, she got her sister and her husband to agree to adopt Tylynn. Then her sister-in-law and her husband took Tyra. And finally, she got a co- worker and her husband to make Malia and Tacari part of their family.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY BEETHE: There was one left and then I went back to my husband.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): And that`s how Laney got adopted too. And all of this because of a doctor who believed that saving lives wasn`t just her day job. Who advocated for the voiceless and recruited a village to make six lives better for many birthdays to come.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, "On the Road" in Omaha, Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A family force multiplier if there ever was one. That is the CBS EVENING NEWS for this Friday.\par \par \uc2 For Tony Dokoupil, I`m Major Garrett in Washington. We thank you so much for watching. A reminder, that daylight saving time for most Americans begins at 2 a.m. Sunday morning. Don`t forget to turn your clocks ahead one hour. Have a good night and a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020260307em3600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for February 24, 2026, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Weijia Jiang, Jonathan Vigliotti, Kris Van Cleave,  Rob Marciano, Nikole Killion, Holly Williams \par \uc2 3,911 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to be with you. I`m Tony Dokoupil with the breaking news right now.\par \par \uc2 The biggest speech of the year, president Trump now just hours away from his State of the Union address. What to listen for tonight on the economy and Iran and what the president is promising.\par \par \uc2 The special guests as well. The U.S. men`s hockey team, the military heroes and the Democratic response tonight. At least 50 say they`ll boycott the speech. What they`re planning instead.\par \par \uc2 Also developing tonight, digging out recovery from that historic blizzard when air travel may be back to normal as more snow is on the way. Meteorologist Rob Marciano with the timing and track.\par \par \uc2 And the latest in the Nancy Guthrie case. Day 24 now. Her daughter, "Today" co-anchor Savannah Guthrie, announcing a new million-dollar reward.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the heartbreaking reveal in her own words tonight.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (Nancy Guthrie`s Daughter): We need to know where she is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The coyote chasing a toddler outside his Southern California home. What happened next?\par \par \uc2 And the good stuff tonight. A split-second decision that saved a little girl`s life.\par \par \uc2 LIO CUNDIFF (Rescued Baby): Yeah, I would have just been right place, right time.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS with Tony Dokoupil reporting tonight from Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 And it`s so great to have you with us on a very big night here in our nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are just back from Capitol Hill and from the White House where the president previewed some of his speech tonight. And it`s a big one. The State of the Union address is typically a president`s biggest single audience of the year. An audience measured in the tens of millions. And there is so much at a crossroads tonight. An economy that is not working for most Americans according to the latest CBS News poll.\par \par \uc2 The looming threat of a U.S. military strike on Iran unless a new nuclear deal is reached. And the Democratic response. The potential for empty chairs tonight on the House floor as some 50 Democrats say they won`t be attending at all.\par \par \uc2 In just a moment, our exclusive conversation with House Speaker Mike Johnson on decorum. But first, CBS News Senior White House Correspondent Weijia Jiang on everything else about tonight`s speech. Weijia, good evening.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s speech is also a pivotal moment for President Trump to shape his message ahead of the midterm elections with that emphasis on the economy. Trump says it is going to be a long speech because there is so much to talk about, including his immigration crackdown and Iran.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): In his big speech, President Trump is expected to unveil new policies to address affordability and propose another round of tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): He`ll obviously tout the administration`s accomplishments over the past year. And he will, of course, lay out a very ambitious agenda.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): A new CBS News poll shows more than half of Americans say the condition of the U.S. economy is bad. Democrats argue Trump has failed to cut prices.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): He promised that he would lower costs on day one, and yet we will be on day 400 and the cost of groceries is up.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): Trump was banking on tariffs to bring his economic vision to life. But last week, the Supreme Court struck most of them down in a 6-3 ruling. Two justices he appointed sided with the majority.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (U.S. President): The Supreme Court`s ruling on tariffs is deeply disappointing, and I`m ashamed of certain members of the court.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): Those justices are expected to be in the audience tonight. So, are several of Trump`s guests, including Erika Kirk, the widow of conservative activist Charlie Kirk, who was shot and killed in September.\par \par \uc2 And after personally inviting the U.S. men`s hockey team to attend, fresh off their historic gold medal win at the Olympic Games, Trump sent a military aircraft to bring the athletes to Washington. Their first stop was the White House.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): And Tony, as you mentioned, it`s also notable who won`t be in that crowd. At least 50 Democrats plan to skip the speech, which would make it the biggest boycott of a State of the Union address in history.\par \par \uc2 Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be watching it, Weijia. Thank you very much. And tonight I`ll be here leading our coverage of the president`s State of the Union address, also the response from Democrats. It begins at 8 p.m. Eastern on CBS News 24/7 and then picks up right back here at 9:00.\par \par \uc2 And earlier today, I sat down with House Speaker Mike Johnson, Republican of Louisiana, to talk about what the president can say tonight and what Congress can potentially do to address the economy and prices, two issues that Americans are worried about. He told me the president`s big, beautiful bill speaks for itself.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA) (Speaker of the House): They`re beginning to feel that relief. It`s going to result and is already resulting in bigger tax refunds for people as they file their taxes this year, bigger paychecks that will be coming. The estimate is $10,000 per citizen on average that they`ll have more money in their pockets because of Republican policies that have been implemented and signed into law by President Trump. So, we`re on the right trajectory.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, anything new tonight or just touting the things that have already been done?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: I think it`s both. I think he`s going to -- he`s going to say that in the first year, the first term, we hit the largest priorities on the minds of the American people, and it began with the wide- open border. Clearly, that was the biggest issue in the election that every exit poll recorded. The president did that in record time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What about on the interior?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The president campaigned on closing the border and mass deportation. They were waving signs with that at the RNC. Something seems to have gone wrong, and terribly so. He`s now underwater on that issue, and I think we all know why.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: Well, there`s a perception out there that -- that it was overzealous in some respect, but the president`s intention has always been to rid the country of dangerous criminal illegals. That`s what they`ve accomplished.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is it merely a perception?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: I think Tom Holman has done a great job when he went into Minneapolis, for example, and he brought calm to the situation and restored common sense. Of course, we`ve removed the extra agents from there now because it was a mission accomplished.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Going back to tonight, what are you telling your members about decorum?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: Well, I told them this morning, tonight is going to be a great celebration. We get to celebrate America`s new golden era, literally and figuratively, because the hockey team will be there with their gold medals. We`ll -- we`ll talk about --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve worked it out.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: Yes, we have. Logistics were a bit of a challenge last minute, but I think we`ll get that done. I think it`s going to be a great celebration from our side. I think the admonition for decorum is to the other team. You saw the antics that they put on display, the Democrats, during the last day of the union. I had to actually pitch it somewhere --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Republicans over the years have been known to perform their own bits of antics and --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: And then at every turn, I have encouraged my colleagues to maintain decorum and the dignity of the office. I think it`s important for the institution and for the American people to see.\par \par \uc2 I think what we should put on display tonight, I told my colleagues this morning, is what the USA hockey teams did in the Olympics. We play for one team. We`re all for America. I think it`s detestable that members would -- would boycott the speech. We`ve never done that. It doesn`t matter if there`s a Democrat president. You go and respect the office. You respect the decorum, the institution, the tradition of having had this speech made. And it`s sad to me that Democrats are choosing to opt out of that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaker Johnson had much more to say about tariffs and the potential for strikes on Iran. You can see our full interview at CBSNews.com.\par \par \uc2 Now to new developments in the search for Savannah Guthrie`s mother, including a new million-dollar reward for her mother Nancy`s return. Here`s CBS News National Correspondent Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: We still believe in a miracle. We still believe that she can come home.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): But today, Savannah Guthrie acknowledging for the first time her mother Nancy may never return alive.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: She may already be gone. And if this is what it is to be, then we will accept it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: How did you process that video as you watched it?\par \par \uc2 LT. BOB KRYGIER (Ret.) (Pima County, Az Sheriff`s Department): Heart- wrenching. It doesn`t matter who you are in this world, your celebrity status or just your everyday person. That`s your mom.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): DNA recovered from Guthrie`s home may not yield a usable profile of the suspect, according to several sources, one calling it, quote, "low-level."\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: What does low-level in that context say to you?\par \par \uc2 LT. BOB KRYGIER: It`s just very difficult to work with.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): The sheriff`s department sent the DNA to a private lab in Florida instead of handing it over to the FBI.\par \par \uc2 LT. BOB KRYGIER: Top leadership should have said, how do we do this most efficiently that we get the results fastest? And if that didn`t happen, that was a failure in leadership. That`s frustrating.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): It`s also unclear if this image, released along with the doorbell video, was captured days before or hours leading up to the crime. Savannah Guthrie now offering a family reward up to $1 million for information leading to her mother`s recovery.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: So, that we can either celebrate a glorious, miraculous homecoming or celebrate the beautiful, brave and courageous and noble life that she has lived.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And investigators say cracking this case may come down to a single tip. The Guthries are asking anyone with information to contact the FBI directly. That number is there on the screen.\par \par \uc2 Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jonathan, thank you very much. We turn now to that massive cleanup underway after a blizzard rolled over the East Coast, canceling more than 2,000 flights today. CBS News Senior Transportation Correspondent Kris Van Cleave has an update.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: At airports across the Northeast, crews worked around the clock clearing feet of snow to get planes moving again. Flight radar 24`s tracking data show how the skies went from virtually empty to back in business by midday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 1: My flight was supposed to board at 2:35 in the afternoon, but it`s been delayed several times. It`s now leaving at about 4 o`clock.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: We were supposed to be in Nashville at -- I think it was like 5 o`clock, and of course that got canceled.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Snow plows were out in force to get drivers safely back on the road, and crews were out clearing tracks to get trains and commuter lines back running.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Since Sunday, more than 11,000 flights have been canceled, but when we look towards Wednesday, so far only about 130 canceled for tomorrow, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Kris, while we have you, I want to ask you about a bill that was voted down today by the House. It was designed to address airline safety gaps. Certainly sounds important. What happened?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Yeah, so this was the ROTOR Act, and it grew out of the deadly midair collision in Washington last year. It would have required most aircraft to have the ability to send and receive really detailed location data about other aircraft.\par \par \uc2 The NTSB believes if that had been in place a year ago, the midair collision probably wouldn`t have happened. It passed the Senate unanimously. It had the support of the crash families, airlines, pilots. It was based on NTSB recommendations, but was brought to the House floor by a mechanism that required a two-thirds majority vote. It fell one vote short after the Pentagon pulled its support, citing cost and security concerns.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Back to the blizzard tonight. Millions in the Northeast digging out from that historic blizzard, and residents in Boston shoveling out their cars from nearly a foot and a half of snow. Other parts of New England got more than three feet. In New York City, meanwhile, the police are investigating an incident you may have seen online.\par \par \uc2 In Washington Square Park, officers were pelted by snowballs. The police commissioner and union say this was a criminal act. Mayor Zohran Mamdani tried to defuse the situation, saying officers deserve respect, and he joked if anyone should be pelted, it`s him.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Meteorologist Rob Marciano has more on the storm now, and a new one moving in, saying it ain`t so, Rob. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: I`m afraid so, Tony. I tell you what, road crews have done an incredible job with this last storm, clearing the roads but adding a little bit of insult to injury. We do have a storm coming in tonight. It won`t be nearly as bad. Let`s get to it. We`re seeing some snow fly to Chicago, Milwaukee, Detroit. You`re going to see this clipper kind of roll across Pittsburgh in the morning, and then the I-95 corridor, Philly, north, where it should be snow right around the morning commute. That`s the tough part. I-95 will see a coating to maybe a couple of inches of snow up towards parts of New England.\par \par \uc2 It does get a little more warm in the afternoon, but it will stick in the morning. So, kids who are going back to school tomorrow, going back to work for sure, it`s going to be a little bit slick in the morning, could be several inches well inland. And then looking at temperatures, they do warm up some in the afternoon the next few days, but overnight low temperatures will once again be below freezing.\par \par \uc2 So, that slush on the roadways, the sidewalks, will stick around through the weekend, and more cold air coming in next week.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rob, thank you very much. We`re going to turn now to another big story here in Washington tonight, the growing calls for Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales to resign following the release of explicit text messages with a congressional aide who later died by suicide last year. This is one of those stories where we do caution some of the details to follow are graphic.\par \par \uc2 Here`s CBS News Congressional Correspondent Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Congressman Tony Gonzales was defiant today on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): I`m not going to resign. I work every day for the people of Texas.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): The Texas Republican has been under intense scrutiny after allegations resurfaced that he had an affair with a former staffer. The staffer, Regina Santos-Aviles, died last year by suicide after setting herself on fire. A police report revealing she called 911 at the time yelling, "please send help, it burned so bad." Text messages obtained by CBS News reveal in May 2024, Gonzales asked Santos-Aviles for a sexy pic. When she said she didn`t like taking photos of herself, he responded, "I`m just such a visual person, sorry." He also asked her what her favorite position is. Santos-Aviles later replied, "This is going too far, boss."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 2: Mr. Speaker, is the situation with Congressman Gonzales becoming a distraction now?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Speaker Mike Johnson said the matter is under investigation by the Office of Congressional Conduct during an exclusive interview with Tony.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON: The allegations are, I mean, it`s detestable what is being alleged.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): But the speaker stopped short of calling for Gonzales to step down despite a growing number of House Republicans who have called for his resignation.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: I don`t know why he`s sticking around, particularly with the texts that came out.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Congressman Gonzales has denied the affair with Santos-Aviles, but her husband tells CBS News this is not something that has been fabricated. Gonzales is up for reelection and faces a very contentious primary challenge next week, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the broadcast tonight from Washington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: I`m Holly Williams in Lviv. Four years after Russia invaded Ukraine, it`s using winter as a weapon. That`s coming up on the CBS EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back now from Washington, D.C., and another reason today matters. It marks four years since Russia invaded Ukraine, starting a war that has cost billions of dollars and left hundreds of thousands dead. Here`s CBS News Senior Foreign Correspondent Holly Williams on how we got here and where things are going.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): For four years, Ukrainian civilians have been in the firing line, terrorized by Russia`s ballistic missiles and drones. Part of Russia`s strategy is attacking Ukraine`s power grid, turning winter into a weapon.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The Ukrainian government has set up thousands of these heated tents called points of invincibility, and people who don`t have heating at home or who have limited electricity can come here, warm up, charge their phones and get a hot drink.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): This winter`s been especially harsh, with the temperature regularly dipping below zero. Ten-year-old Vanya (sp?) and his mother Anastasia (sp?) took us to see their apartment, where the heating system`s been shut down and the walls of their kitchen recently iced over.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Did you build this yourself?\par \par \uc2 VANYA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): They`re heating two rooms with electricity, but struggling to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Is it possible that Vladimir Putin will break your morale, will break Ukrainians` morale?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): "No," she told us. "We want to live in Ukraine." Like millions of Ukrainian children, Vanya`s grown-up sheltering from Russian bombs.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Do you try to be brave to help your mother?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): "Yes," he told us. I try to be cheerful.\par \par \uc2 VANYA: I love you, Mom.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: After four years of grinding war, Russia now controls around 20 percent of Ukraine`s territory. But last week, Ukraine`s president, Volodymyr Zelenskyy, said that his country`s forces recently clawed back over 100 square miles.\par \par \uc2 Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what sent this adorable toddler running for mom?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A car came out of nowhere, captured on police body cam. Take a look at this. The driver of the SUV on your screen blew past a stop sign through an intersection and right into that New York State trooper, knocking her down. All because the driver said the winter sunlight had blocked his vision. Good news is that trooper already back on the job.\par \par \uc2 Now, parents can tell you toddlers are way quicker than those little legs may suggest, and a family in Pasadena is grateful for that tonight.\par \par \uc2 That three-year-old running up his driveway, screaming for mom, was being chased by a coyote. But when Mama Bear, just off camera, screamed back, it was the coyote, as you see there on the run.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the good stuff. A man rescues a baby from an icy lake.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are back with the good stuff tonight. And as we broadcast from Washington, where our elected leaders gather to take on the problems of America, and yet so rarely even agree on what the biggest problems are, we wanted to end tonight with the opposite of all of that messy deliberation.\par \par \uc2 Last week in Chicago, Lio Cundiff was sitting on a bench along Lake Michigan when he heard a scream from the pier and saw a baby stroller caught in a gust of wind blown into the icy water.\par \par \uc2 Now as problems go, it doesn`t get much clearer than this one. Inside the stroller was an 8-month-old little girl and her mother, frozen in shock there at the water`s edge. Somebody had to do something. And somebody did.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LIO CUNDIFF: There was one moment where I was like, I don`t know how much longer I`m going to hold on. So, I just kind of like grabbed her hand and just like rubbed her hand a little and was like, we`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you notice in that interview with our friends at CBS News Chicago, Cundiff was in the hospital because it turns out it`s extra hard to rescue a baby from an icy lake when you never actually learn to swim yourself. But hey, it worked.\par \par \uc2 Lio saved that little girl, which is a reminder that while we are always waiting and waiting some more on action from Washington, every single day Americans are constantly doing for themselves. They are jumping in feet first when it matters and only later worrying about maybe learning to swim.\par \par \uc2 That`s another day in America. I`m Tony Dokoupil. Thank you for watching tonight. We`ll be back later for the State of the Union right here. And we`ll see you tomorrow night live from Baltimore, a special Live from America.\par \par \uc2 Good night for now.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020260225em2o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Power Outages Persist Amid Brutal Cold; Video of Prior Encounter Between ICE and Alex Pretti, Some Minnesotans Carrying Passports at All Times; Car Hits Orthodox Jewish Group`s Headquarters; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Raids Georgia Election Site for 2020 Voting Records; Bessent on Powell Investigation and Trump Accounts; Breaking Down Trump Accounts; Tom Brady Speaks to \uc2 Bill Belichick\uc2 ; Brendan Banfield Takes the Stand; Crew Prepares for Artemis II Moon Mission; Protests Over ICE Detention of Five-Year-Old-Boy; A Deep Dive on History of Diners; Jennifer Jimenez Wins 2026 Music Educator Awards; Alex Warren`s Extraordinary Journey to Stardom; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  Expert on Job Market and How to Stand Out; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Vladimir Duthiers, Kelly O`Grady, Tom Hanson, Rob  Marciano, Lana Zak, Skyler Henry, Jericka Duncan, Mark Strassmann, Lilia  Luciano, Natalie Morales \par \uc2 14,091 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2026\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear those trumpets on this Thursday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And I`m Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is on assignment.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got the latest on the dangerous cold and another possible storm on the way. Oh no!\par \par \uc2 Plus, there`s new video of the man shot and killed by federal agents in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 That deep freeze gets even deeper across the country, hundreds of thousands without power face dangerous temperature drops.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s like an icy tornado ripped through this city. Thousands are now on day four with no power and no running water.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: CBS News has obtained new video of Alex Pretti confronting federal agents days before he was shot to death.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  raids a Georgia office tied to the President`s false claims about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 JOSH MCLAURIN (D) (Georgia state senator): Fulton County and Georgia in general are tired of being the target of Donald Trump`s narcissism.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Bruce Springsteen is out with a new song called "The Streets of Minneapolis."\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: It condemns the action of ICE there.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  was attacked with a mystery liquid. It turns out the liquid there was apple cider vinegar.\par \par \uc2 DESI LYDIC ("The Daily Show"): Are you trying to pickle her? I`ll show you. Your skin is going to glow and you`ll never get a cold.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Here in New York, at the \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , for the first time, scientists have built a robot that can move its mouth exactly like a human.\par \par \uc2 ROBOT: Hello, I am Emo, a robot designed to simulate emotions and interact with humans in a more natural way.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I`m not sure, the lip syncing is what everyone is going to remember about that.\par \par \uc2 Hello, I`m Emo. I`m a robot designed to simulate a taxi driver from the Bronx circa 1971.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive. Making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stephen, he did seem warm and cuddly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: May we never lose emotion. May we never lose that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Seriously!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seriously is right. We`re going to start this morning with those dangerous cold temperatures for tens of millions of Americans after that massive winter storm. We feel it!\par \par \uc2 In northern Mississippi, the helicopters and the drones, they`re being used to help crews restore the power. Hundreds of thousands are still in the dark across the South, which is also affecting the water supply. So, you`re cold and you`ve got no water.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this video. It`s of a roof collapsed at a rodeo arena in Arkansas due to all the snow and the ice. So, all of this is happening as another possible storm could be on the way. The last thing we need. We`re going to have a full forecast in just a moment from Rob Marciano. He is here in studio, but we`re going to begin with Tom Hanson in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Tom, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hey there, Gayle. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know there are not enough coats in the world to make it feel warm this morning, but I want to give you the clearest visual of just how cold it is.\par \par \uc2 Take a look behind me, and you can see parts of the Hudson, those massive ice chunks, and you may be wondering how vessels pass through one of America`s busiest waterways when the conditions are like that, well, not without the help of the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  and we went along with them yesterday on their ice breaking fleet.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: In New York City, five days in a row of sub-freezing temperatures have turned the Hudson River into large chunks of ice.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TOTTEN (\uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2 ): Yes, this is nothing like I`ve seen before.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: It`s a critical waterway for barges moving through the Northeast. Jacob Totten from the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  says 75 percent of heating oil throughout the country transits through this region.\par \par \uc2 What kind of danger does that present for the boats that have to go through this area?\par \par \uc2 JACOB TOTTEN: It definitely creates a great danger for the boats going to transit through this area with getting stuck in the ice.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: On the roads in the Midwest, ice is making travel hazardous. In Indianapolis, this tractor trailer rolled on its side blocking four lanes of traffic Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, D.C., tractors are dumping what`s left of the last winter storm into dump trucks to cart it out of the city and temperatures there could shatter records.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is going to be one of the top five longest running cold streaks on record, and that is a dangerous thing for our community.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: in Oxford, Mississippi. Jerrica Pryor has been without power for five days, and now she`s without running water.\par \par \uc2 JERRICA PRYOR (Oxford, Mississippi resident): Unable to just wash your face, wash your hands, not being able to shower is one thing.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ROBYN TANNEHILL (Oxford, Mississippi): There`s so much damage to our critical infrastructure, so many poles broken, so many trees pulled down.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Our Kati Weis spoke to Robyn Tannehill, the mayor of Oxford, the small city home to Ole Mis, had to implement an emergency plan for its limited water supply to prioritize shortages at its hospital.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ROBYN TANNEHILL: This is going to be a long road to recovery. You know, it`s hard to even get our minds that far ahead right now, when we saw people without power and without water, we`re still really in emergency mode.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And here in New York City, the temperature is feeling about negative two. That`s actually just five degrees Fahrenheit. And some sad news here, there is not going to be any relief from these sub-freezing temperatures until after the weekend -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Tom, I`m staying inside this weekend then. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on the bitter cold and that potential new winter storm, let`s go to CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano.\par \par \uc2 Rob, I refuse another one is coming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Yes, well it just depends on where you live, right? It is not going to come to everybody, but it`s going to be all snow. And by the way, New York has been below freezing since Friday, and that`s when you start to get ice in the Hudson, and at least through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Looking at tomorrow`s temperatures, it is pretty widespread. Going to feel like minus six in Chicago, minus two in Cleveland, below freezing in Atlanta, back through Little Rock, Dallas as well. And then on Saturday, even colder. We`re talking about records here in a lot of spots, yes, in the north, but also down across the south. Dozens of them will fall Saturday and Sunday.\par \par \uc2 And temperatures in Mobile, Alabama will be 13, feeling like that come Saturday morning, and guess what? All right, here we go. Winter Storm Watches have just been posted by the National Weather Service, Eastern Georgia, the Carolinas, parts of Eastern Tennessee and Virginia, and this is all going to come together tonight through tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s go through the timing of this. A little coastal low coming through the Gulf, upper-level energy starting some snow across parts of the mountains as we head through tomorrow night into Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 It is really blowing in snow here. Could see blizzard conditions during the day on Saturday in the Carolinas. And then it looks like it`s going to skim the Northeast Coast. I don`t think New York is going to get much, but it looks like parts of the Carolinas, over eight inches of snow. That is a lot for that part of the country -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh boy! All right, Rob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Minneapolis, where new video shows an encounter between ICE agents and protester, Alex Pretti 11 days before he was killed.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has obtained footage from the media site "The News Movement," showing that earlier confrontation between Pretti and federal officers.\par \par \uc2 Lana Zak is in Minneapolis. Lana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 A law enforcement official confirms to CBS News that the agents that were involved in that fatal shooting of Alex Pretti are now on administrative leave, and new video shows that this wasn`t the first time that Pretti had a violent confrontation with federal agents.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Bleep)\par \par \uc2 (Car horns blaring)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE blowing whistles)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Footage shot by The News Movement and confirmed by the Pretti family, shows the 37-year-old yelling and cursing at federal immigration agents on January 13th. At one point, he is seen kicking their car and breaking his right tail light.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PRETTI (37-year-old ICU nurse): (Bleep) you!\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Officers then exit the vehicle and tackle him while deploying teargas into the surrounding crowd.\par \par \uc2 After a brief scuffle, Pretti is released. The officers return to their cars and drive away.\par \par \uc2 An attorney for Pretti`s family says he was assaulted despite posing no threat. Eleven days later, Pretti was killed during another confrontation with federal agents.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You must resign!\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Tensions flared again on Tuesday night when Representative Ilhan Omar, a frequent critic and target of President Trump, was sprayed with what sources say was a syringe full of apple cider vinegar during a news conference where she called for the abolition of ICE.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): Some landed on my binder, some landed on the podium. And then I think I felt something here, which is when I lunged towards him.\par \par \uc2 And you know, luckily, I didn`t catch an assault charge.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: The weeks of division and violence have fueled anxiety and fear in Minneapolis. Some U.S. citizens like Alejandra (sp?) have even started carrying their passports, hearing encounters with immigration officials.\par \par \uc2 Alejandra was originally born in Mexico, and says she recently took a course to better know her rights if she is stopped.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRA (U.S. citizen): I want to be prepared, because these ICE agent, as you`ve seen, they are aggressive and you just need to know in that situation how to respond, so that you know, nothing bad happens to you.\par \par \uc2 JANE GRAUPMAN (International Institute of Minnesota executive director): So this form here is a family preparedness emergency plan.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Jane Graupman is the executive director of the International Institute of Minnesota, which provides resources for immigrants, particularly refugees. She says she has worked with several people with legal status who have been wrongfully detained.\par \par \uc2 JANE GRAUPMAN: It`s terrible. I mean, these are people that are good citizens here. They`ve been persecuted in their own country, and then to come here and have that happen here is something I never could imagine would happen in my country. Never.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Graupman advises her clients not to leave the house without proof of legal status. On Wednesday, a federal judge blocked the Trump administration from arresting or detaining refugees as they await their permanent resident status here in Minnesota -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Lana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in New York City, a hate crime investigation is underway after a car rammed into the headquarters of a Jewish Orthodox group. It was caught on tape. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Daniel David Yeroshalmi/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You can see the person behind the wheel, a man in his 20s, according to police, ramming the building multiple times.\par \par \uc2 Now, this happened at the Chabad-Lubavitch headquarters in New York, Brooklyn rather. The building was damaged, but no one was hurt. A suspect is in custody, and police are still investigating the motive.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, New York Mayor Zohran Mamdani said, "Any threat to a Jewish institution or place of worship must be taken seriously. Antisemitism has no place in our city, and violence or intimidation against Jewish New Yorkers is unacceptable."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in Atlanta, FBI agents with search warrants raided a county election facility yesterday, looking for ballots and other records from the 2020 election. Now, this is all tied to President Trump`s ongoing claim that he actually won the state of Georgia. There is no evidence that that claim is correct, and Republican officials in the state have also disputed that.\par \par \uc2 Skyler Henry has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: FBI agents moved in and out of the Fulton County Elections Office, Wednesday executing a court authorized search warrant. An FBI spokesperson did not provide any details about the search.\par \par \uc2 The warrant cites possible violations of federal laws protecting election records and against procuring fraudulent votes. Democratic Georgia State Senator Josh McLaurin posted this video from inside the facility, raising alarms about what he says is a politically motivated operation.\par \par \uc2 JOSH MCLAURIN: I hope people realize how dangerous this is that the President of the United States is ordering federal agencies to pursue his conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: A source familiar with the investigation tells CBS News it is in connection with the 2020 election, when Democrats nearly won Georgia`s Electoral College votes. At the time, Trump had a call with Georgia`s top election official, Secretary of State Brad Raffensperger pressuring him to "Find the votes to win the state."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R) (President of the United States of America): I just want to find 11,780 votes, which is one more than we have.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Trump has continued to claim the 2020 election was rigged since returning to The White House, vowing to investigate those involved.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It was a rigged election. Everybody now knows that, they found out. People will soon be prosecuted for what they did.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: A narrative that has been repeatedly rejected by courts and election officials, including Raffensperger.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): President Trump didn`t win by hundreds of thousands of votes in Georgia, did he?\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER (Georgia Secretary of State): No, he did not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: County officials confirmed the FBI`s warrant was originally for the Fulton County Election Office, but state law says that ballots and other 2020 election records were sealed under the Superior Court Clerk, so agents had to seek a new warrant before being able to access those records.\par \par \uc2 We should also point out that the Director of National Intelligence, Tulsi Gabbard was here on the ground last night. In an unprecedented move, the administration says that she was "taking action under President Trump`s directive to ensure election integrity." -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Skyler, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the economy, Jerome Powell`s Federal Reserve voted to keep interest rates steady despite pressure from the President to cut them. That`s one of the topics we covered in our conversation with Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent.\par \par \uc2 We had a lot to discuss, including those new Trump accounts to help parents save, but let`s start with the Justice Department`s investigation of Powell.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent says no one is beyond reproach, even the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice is investigating Fed Chair Powell. What message should that send to future Fed Chairs, as the President is about to announce who will succeed Powell.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT (U.S. Treasury Secretary): I think that the message is that independence does not mean no accountability. I`ve been calling for the Fed to do an internal investigation on numerous things since last spring.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: We also discussed the new Trump accounts.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re going to give every newborn American child a financial stake in the future.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Tax deferred investment accounts, U.S. citizens with a child under 18 can opt into when filing taxes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT: For the families who have children between January 1, 2025 and December 31, 2028, you get a thousand dollars coming from the government.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Parents, employers and nonprofits can make contributions in any Trump account up to five grand annually. If the government`s initial thousand-dollar deposit is left alone, it could grow to $5,800.00 in 18 years. But by contributing the maximum, over $300,000.00,\par \par \uc2 How are you going to prevent the wealth gap from widening with this program?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT: Well, I think that that is a terrible criticism that shows how out of touch anyone who says that is because if they say only $5,000.00, these are families, huge number of families in America wouldn`t have $500 to meet a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: If they aren`t able to go out and pay for college, to go buy a home eventually, if it hasn`t really meaningfully changed their ability to do that, would you call this a failure? Or is there a different way that the Treasury measures success in this particular program?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT: As I said, so many Americans couldn`t even meet a $500.00 emergency, so maybe people just put it away, and it`s a rainy-day fund.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: CBS News business analyst, Jill Schlesinger is with us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Dolla` Dolla` Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Good morning. Good morning!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Okay, so you just heard the Treasury Secretary talk about the Trump accounts. What`s your read on what parents need to know and how this is all going to work?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: If you had a baby last year, and you`re going to have one in the next three years, take the $1,000.00. This is fantastic, right? I mean, it is free money.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Something is better than nothing.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Absolutely. And what we also know is that the money grows without any taxation. So you know, if you have money in the bank and you have an interest payment, that`s taxable. These accounts are not taxable until you take the money out, or until the beneficiary does.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it`s very interesting to me that there`s a way to have this money grow. Think of it more like your kid`s account, like as if it were an IRA, hasn`t been taxed yet. When you take it out, the taxes do whatever the tax bracket is of the kid.\par \par \uc2 So, all of this is good news. It may not be a game changer, but something is better than nothing.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And, you know, we hear a lot about the 529. Compare this?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Well, this is really a little bit more flexible in some ways, but if you are saving for education or maybe potentially for a little retirement, I much prefer the 529.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: First of all, the money that goes into the 529, you don`t get a deduction federally, but basically some states are giving you state income tax deduction, and that`s great. The money grows without taxation.\par \par \uc2 When you take it out for education, whether it`s secondary school or college, no tax due at all, so it makes it a much better plan. And also, any unused 529 plans can be rolled into a Roth IRA for the beneficiary up to $35,000.00. So, I think the 529 is a little bit stronger of a vehicle if you`re putting money in yourself.\par \par \uc2 Again, the $1,000.00 from the government is great, but before you put your contribution into that new account, I would really look at the 529.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, I think that`s a helpful comparison, and I like your thoughts on this. I mean, something is better than nothing, but if you aren`t able to continue contributing to it, it is not going to grow to that big number we did show on screen.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, we talked about the interest rate decision. Powell, you know, I`ve talked to the Secretary about that. What is this going to mean for consumers, right? Because we had a hold. It was unexpected.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Right, I mean, if you`re a borrower, it`s going to continue to cost money to pay that credit card balance. The auto loans are remaining high. Personal Loans remain high. If you`re a saver, hey, go and lock in those C.D. rates. They`re still pretty juicy, three or four percent, so try to do that.\par \par \uc2 No checking account money. Put it in high yield savings account. You`re still getting good interest.\par \par \uc2 So, something for borrowers that is not so good, but the savers do actually get more money in their pockets.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, appreciate you, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Thank you so much for the context.\par \par \uc2 And you`ll be able to watch our extended interview with Secretary Bessent when it is posted today on CBSNews.com and our CBS MORNINGS YouTube page.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, coming up, a man accused of conspiring with his au pair to kill his wife and a stranger takes a stand in his own defense at his own trial. But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, on our "Road to the Grammys," breakout star, Alex Warren talks about his mega hit song "Ordinary."\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" we`ve got an update on 