ar \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Do they ever come up with a doozy?\par \par \uc2 JUDD FRIELING: Oh, yes. Yes, they certainly come up -- they are very creative.\par \par \uc2 One minute to burn.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Copy.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Judd Frieling is one of the flight directors here.\par \par \uc2 JUDD FRIELING: Lift off confirmed. Copy liftoff.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The second Artemis II lifts off, Frieling will sit in the hot seat, NASA`s man in charge.\par \par \uc2 Can you ever truly adequately prepare for everything that could go wrong?\par \par \uc2 JUDD FRIELING: Not everything, but I think what the simulations we do teaches us is how to adapt. Right? If you don`t have a completely nailed down plan, at least you have a straw man of what the plan might be.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It`s why you practice for the big game.\par \par \uc2 JUDD FRIELING: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Remember the stakes in deep space, during an emergency, no one can call AAA for a tow back to Earth. That`s why everyone involved understands why these simulations matter, even if Artemis II Mission Specialist, Jeremy Hansen jokingly calls them --\par \par \uc2 JEREMY HANSEN (Artemis II Mission Specialist): These sort of evil -- these evil tests for us and we`ll be sitting in the sim, our mission controllers are in Mission Control, and then stuff will just start breaking. You`re like, "Oh, come on! Really that?"\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Yes, that! Sometimes several flashing light crises at once.\par \par \uc2 Do they really think you`re evil?\par \par \uc2 LISA VOILES: I think so. Yes. I think a little bit. You have to be a little evil, but all for good intentions.\par \par \uc2 Anybody have any questions about that malfunction?\par \par \uc2 We can`t predict everything that will happen. I think any scenario that you work through, even if it`s not the exact one that you see in space, all of that experience and all of that development together is going to benefit everyone.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Especially if the crew runs into trouble during its roughly half-million-mile round trip to the moon.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Mark Strassmann in Houston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up in "What to Watch" another Super Bowl ad exclusive. Right now though, it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead in our next hour, one of our favorite stories every year on our "Road to the Grammys," only on CBS MORNINGS -- love when that happens. We get to announce this year`s Music Educator of the Year.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`re going to introduce you to Grammy nominee, Alex Warren, whose song "Ordinary," I love that song -- is one of the biggest hits of the year. He tells Natalie Morales how his love story with his wife changed everything. We love a great love story.\par \par \uc2 Claus, don`t we love a great story?\par \par \uc2 CLAUS STUHLWEISSENBURG: Yes, dear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great love story! He said, "Yes, dear." Hi. Mary Beth. That`s his wife. But right now, Claus and I are heading over to the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Vlad is in place, getting ready for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Two Bruce`s -- Bruce Willis, Bruce Springsteen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, cute! Oh, that`s a very good tease.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a tease.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad is up next right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Perfect way to begin this "What to Watch" story, because we have an update, a health update on Bruce Willis from his wife.\par \par \uc2 The 70-year-old Emmy winner was diagnosed back in 2023 with frontotemporal dementia, which limits his ability to communicate. There is no known cure or treatment.\par \par \uc2 So, on a recent podcast, Emma Heming Willis says she -- that Bruce is unaware that he has dementia, but that he is still very present in his body. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS (Bruce Willis` wife): When someone says to me, like, you know, does Bruce still know who you are? Yes, he does, because he doesn`t have Alzheimer`s. He has FTD.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we have a way. He has a way of connecting with me, our children, that might not be the same as you would connect with you know your loved one, but it is still very beautiful. It`s still very meaningful. It`s just -- it`s just different. You just learn how to adapt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Emma is so lovely.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. It is a beautiful --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The whole family, they all rallied around him, and she says that he`s actually living a good life in terms of his family connection. It`s so important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, very much. Love Bruce.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love the family. They have a blended family. It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It works very well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, very well.\par \par \uc2 All right, only on CBS MORNINGS, we reveal a Super Bowl ad you will see during the big game next weekend. This one is from Oikos Yogurt, and it`s for anyone who`s ever faced an uphill battle. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Oikos Yogurt advertisement)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That of course, is the great Kathryn Hahn teaming up with Baltimore Ravens running back Derrick Henry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That looks good!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love Kathy Hahn and everything that she`s ever done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Need to get that yogurt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I guess it works, folks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I am going to get them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s talk about Bruce 