uirting thing. I don`t mind it at all.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour, though, with President Trump`s immigration crackdown and its impact on the communities. This image we all remember this with this little blue bunny hat, five-year-old, Liam Conejo Ramos sparked an outcry -- and in his little backpack when he and his father were arrested in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 They have since been moved to a detention facility in Texas where federal agents pepper sprayed protesters demanding their release and this impact of the crackdown is being felt in schools in Minneapolis, where officials say there is a drop in attendance at the schools.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano spoke to the principal at little Liam`s school.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: People are not going to work.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN (Valley View Elementary School principal): No.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: They`re not leaving the house to buy groceries.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: So that`s where this is all headed.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Yep, yep.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: At Columbia Heights Public School in Minnesota, staff are working second shifts to deliver.\par \par \uc2 How much of this need that you are supplying with this food has to do with the ICE operations?\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: A majority of it. Just last Friday, I delivered food, and they followed me around out of the trailer park that I was delivering to. And so we`ve had discussions with our Chief of Police to say, are we putting a target on our families? He goes, well, it`s probably --\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: By going in and delivering.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Yes, he goes, it`s probably safer for you to go in and deliver food than for them to go out.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In an elementary school of about 574 students, administrators say some 24 families have had parents detained under Operation Metro Surge.\par \par \uc2 But it was this image of one of their pre-K students, five-year-old, Liam Conejo Ramos that went viral. He and his father, Adrian, were detained in their driveway last week and later transferred to a detention center in Dilley, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, Texas Congressman Joaquin Castro visited them and called us afterwards.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Could you give us an update? That is so amazing?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOAQUIN CASTRO (D-TX): His dad did say that -- that he`s been depressed and hasn`t been eating especially well, you know, I was very firm about demanding his release, and so we`ll keep pushing.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Knowing that he has an advocate down there, too, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOAQUIN CASTRO (D-TX): Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Though Liam was asleep, his dad promised to pass along a message his teacher and classmates sent him.\par \par \uc2 (CHILDREN saying "We love you, Liam!")\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: A show of strength as they save Liam`s place.\par \par \uc2 And to see the cubby here --\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And another hat with the ears.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Just looking at it.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Yes, and like I said, his stuff is not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: What would you tell people who are watching this from somewhere else and thinking, ICE is doing its job.\par \par \uc2 JASON KUHLMAN: Open your eyes. Believe your eyes. Believe what you see. He`s not a criminal. It`s not politics.\par \par \uc2 It`s about treating people like humans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: So, the principal told us that folks at the school were relieved this week when a judge banned the government from deporting Liam and his dad. DHS says that they went to arrest Liam`s father as an "illegal alien who was released into the country by the Biden administration," that they kept Liam with his father and took them together, following the father`s wishes because the mother wasn`t coming out.\par \par \uc2 The principal tells us that there were other adults who had volunteered to keep Liam safe, but it seems that the agents refused that offer -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes. Appreciate you bringing us an update on that story about Liam. I think we all were impacted by that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: -- seeing those images. Lilia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That story is so upsetting. I can`t get enough of it. I don`t understand why he`s still in the detention center. When they keep saying the mother refused to get him, it`s because the dad was saying, don`t come out, because you could be arrested, too. And they had another child that was on their way back home. The context is so important in this story.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, now to the bitter cold and a new winter storm threat on the way. Hundreds of thousands are still without power in the South for a fifth day in a row.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` meteorologist, Rob Marciano has the forecast.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning again.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got friends outside of Nashville. They say that their power company telling them, it might be well over a week before they see their power restored, and they`ll get above freezing briefly today, but back below freezing.\par \par \uc2 You think today is cold across parts of the Northeast, tomorrow is even colder. Records, potentially Buffalo, Cleveland, Philadelphia with wind chills well below zero. And let`s talk about that winter storm. The watches are up, and let`s cut to the chase. How much snow do we expect? We`re getting a little bit more confidence here. This is going a high impact event for the Carolinas in through parts of Southern Virginia. Charlotte and Raleigh could see eight inches.\par \par \uc2 We could see blizzard conditions across parts of the Outer Banks with the storm. As it gets off the coastline, tomorrow night into Saturday morning, look at the radar really filling in here. A lot of snow here.\par \par \uc2 Some snow and wind across parts of Eastern Massachusetts, but Florida, 30s this morning. By Sunday morning, some of the coldest air we`ve seen since 2010. Meanwhile, record highs in Southern California, Kelly, that`s where you need to go if you want to warm up.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: All right, Rob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, our series "USA to Z" serves up a story about that quintessential American institution, the diner.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to learn all about the eateries homegrown`s history. But first, it is 8:07, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, Alex Warren talks to Natalie Morales about his journey from living in his car to becoming a Grammy nominee.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eternity"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Now, to our series "USA to Z" where we bring you stories from across the country to celebrate 250 years of America.\par \par \uc2 This week, we celebrate a staple of our cuisine and culture, D for diner.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: When it comes to American food, there`s perhaps nothing more quintessential than the American diner. Its roots date back to Providence, Rhode Island, over 150 years ago, a tasteful history diner expert, Richard Gutman has studied closely.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GUTMAN (Diner expert): The first diners were called night lunch wagons, and they served people who were out at night after the restaurants had closed. The diner was a place that was open to all and served quick food at a good price.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Lunch wagons later transformed into lunch cars, stationary fixtures that resembled train cars.\par \par \uc2 Wilson`s diner in Waltham, Massachusetts opened in 1946 and remains one of the few original lunch cars still operating.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Any French fries?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Lunch car design has helped inspire the classic diner look we see today.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GUTMAN: The diner is unique in the landscape and that no other building looks like a diner, but you know what it is when you see it, and what other building really announces itself in that way?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: At Boulevard Diner in Worcester, Massachusetts -- WOMAN: Who has the --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Well, I will --\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Once known as Lunch Car Number 730.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Good morning, Boulevard.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It`s as busy as it was when they first fired up the grill in 1936.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The George family is now on to its fourth generation of family members running the show in the kitchen, including short order cook, Gabriella George.\par \par \uc2 This is a multi-generational family business.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLA GEORGE (Boulevard Diner): Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: For many, that`s the American dream. How important is that to your family?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLA GEORGE: It`s very important. It means a lot.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Why do you think that people still enjoy the diner experience?\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLA GEORGE: I think they just -- they come for the vibes. They come for slightly cheaper food. They come for the home cooked meals. I think they could just come for the love.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Bacon.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Beyond fascination for the cuisine where ordering breakfast for dinner is a welcomed request, diners have also become a symbol of American culture.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GUTMAN: Diners have become iconic in films. They`re identifiable with American Culture.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Grease"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Gutman estimates there are around 2,000 diners operating across the country today, down from three times that number in the 1950s. Like many small businesses, rising rent and labor costs have driven some diners to close.\par \par \uc2 But in New Jersey, deemed the diner capital of the world, with at least 400 of them throughout the state, the appetite for diners remain steady.\par \par \uc2 Tops Diner in Newark was named the best diner in America in 2017 serving upwards of 15,000 customers a week under the helm of co-owner, James Golemis.\par \par \uc2 JAMES GOLEMIS (Tops Diner): Put that burger up right now.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Why do you think diners are so iconic to America?\par \par \uc2 JAMES GOLEMIS: I don`t know. It`s like, why is the Statue of Liberty in New York City so amazing? I`ve never been there. When you go outside you see, like, license plates from like other states, all over and they stop here, it`s such a good feeling.\par \par \uc2 Time to dig in.\par \par \uc2 Oh, my gosh! Oh, that`s really good.\par \par \uc2 While the number of diners has dwindled over the years, Gutman believes they might just hold the secret recipe for standing the test of time.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GUTMAN: The concept of the diner will be here forever, and I am one to say that they will never go away.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I would never like the diner to go away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kelly, no, I like -- I like looking at Kelly eat because you were really --\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I like eating. I was like, give me, give me!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Just keep talking so I can keep eating.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there`s nothing better, I think, than breakfast at a really good diner in New York.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yup!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you ever been to Golden Diner for their pancakes?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve been at Golden Diner. I`ve gotten the pancakes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The pancakes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are insane.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is worth waiting in line.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I did a "Dish" on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: You did?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was part of like the influencers, and we went to Golden and had the pancake.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, man, it`s --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Diners forever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I want to go to Tops now.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: We`ll go together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want -- oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, in our "Road to the Grammys," we will announce the 2026 Music Educator of the Year only on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now to one of our favorite stories as part of our "Road to the Grammys." Only on CBS MORNINGS, we are sharing the winner of the 2026 Music Educator Award presented by the Recording Academy and the Grammy Museum. So, can I have a little drum roll please.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Da-da-da-da-da-da!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This year, thank you crew, thank you, team.\par \par \uc2 This year, the honor goes to Jennifer Jimenez of South Miami Senior High School in Florida. Congratulations!\par \par \uc2 Skyler Henry visited the school to see what makes Miss Jimenez, a Grammy Award winning teacher.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALISSON MARTINEZ (Student): She lets us tell everyone our story with music.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Band director, Jennifer Jimenez holds her students` stories close. Inside the music wing at South Miami Senior High School, band is family.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JIMENEZ (South Miami Senior High School teacher): The joke about there being no I in team, there`s no I in band either.\par \par \uc2 Band is the family you choose, because you might not get to choose your family members, but you choose to be here.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Of course, rich sounds coming from the instruments fill the band room. But after 19 years of teaching here, Jimenez also puts an emphasis on hard work, teamwork and, most importantly, respect.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JIMENEZ: I let them choose their own music, which in the band world is like revolutionary. I want the students` feedback.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: And the results are on the wall.\par \par \uc2 I`m looking over your shoulder. There`s a lot of finalist, finalist, runners up, championship, finalist. Y`all don`t play here. You get it done.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JIMENEZ: That`s student driven, believe it or not.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Her methods are tried and true, shaped by her upbringing.\par \par \uc2 What teacher or person inspired you from a band perspective?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JIMENEZ: It`s all a little bit from everybody, and I think that`s the great thing we talk about, is no matter who your teacher is, no matter what the circumstances you`re in, you can always pull good from it, and you can always pull bad from it.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve just been really, really fortunate to have a bunch of people pour into me.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: How did music find you?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JIMENEZ: My parents put me in piano lessons. I think I was six. Somebody had given us a piano, and so anytime I was bored, we didn`t have cable, like we just couldn`t afford it, so I played piano, and that just opened up a whole world for me.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Jimenez`s lifelong love of music has spread to her students, and that feeling is mutual.\par \par \uc2 During lunch break at the high school, we asked the band students, what makes Miss Jimenez so special?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I think Miss Jimenez, it`s a great teacher, because she understands not only our instruments, but ourselves.\par \par \uc2 BOY: She really, really helped me restore my passion for music.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: She`s created an environment where everyone can just be themselves and they feel comfortable here.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JIMENEZ: It`s okay, it`s rehearsal. You have permission to fail in this room.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Why is Miss Jimenez such a great teacher?\par \par \uc2 ALISSON MARTINEZ: She knows when to be a mom with us and want to be our teacher, and that just shows the type of person that she is.\par \par \uc2 IZABELLA TAPANES: She sees potential in everyone.\par \par \uc2 MARIO PORTELLO PARDO (Former student): It`s blending the ability to care about the individual, not only how they`re doing in school, but how are they doing as a person.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Former student Mario Portello Pardo (sp?) followed in her footsteps and became a band director, but he says it took some tough love to get there.\par \par \uc2 MARIO PORTELLO PARDO: If it wasn`t because of music, I would have probably dropped out.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Really?\par \par \uc2 MARIO PORTELLO PARDO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Did Miss Jimenez pull you to the side and say, hey, no, you need to focus. You need to get this right.\par \par \uc2 MARIO PORTELLO PARDO: Yes. She helped me, you know, like, realize that life is about figuring out what you want and then finding a way there.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Lessons here go beyond the sheet music.\par \par \uc2 IZABELLA TAPANES: Band is a lot of mindset, right? So you`re going to get into the mindset, you`re going to play, you`re going to practice, you`re going to make it better, and that`s it. You cannot strive for perfection. You strive for improvement.\par \par \uc2 So, everybody in this band has the mindset of, okay, today we`re going to make one thing better.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JIMENEZ: You might not have natural talent, but you can choose to do the work, and they choose it every day. And that`s, you know, the results.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Mindsets in harmony, influenced by Miss Jimenez.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Skyler Henry in Miami.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Miss Jimenez! I like what she says, don`t strive for perfection, strive for improvement, and band is the family that you choose. We like her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love that music can bring people together and to inspire, and that`s exactly what she is doing every day, because she was inspired when she was young.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: We all need those kinds of teachers.\par \par \uc2 Well, our "Road to the Grammys" continues next with rising music star, Alex Warren. We`re going to be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ordinary"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this song! That`s Alex Warren`s hit love song, guess what it is called? "Ordinary."\par \par \uc2 It became Billboard`s song of the summer in 2025. Everybody was singing this number.\par \par \uc2 Part of our "Road to the Grammys" series, we`re highlighting the singer who was nominated for Best New Artist.\par \par \uc2 Alex Warren showed our Natalie Morales around his hometown of Carlsbad, California, which he says inspired much of his debut album.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Alex Warren`s fans shared in his first album`s release with a massive sing along.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ordinary"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: The 25-year-old has been soaring high on the wings of his hit single, "Ordinary," a love letter to his wife, Kouvr.\par \par \uc2 Life has been anything, but ordinary for you these days.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: Yes, it`s been -- it`s been pretty nuts, actually.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Did you know "Ordinary" was going to be like top of the charts?\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: I thought it would do really well. I don`t know what it was. I had a gut feeling, and I fought for that song really hard.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eternity"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Warren`s journey started in his hometown of Carlsbad, California.\par \par \uc2 DAD: We are here for the musical talent of Alex Warren.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Where his dad began shaping his love of performance.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: I have three other siblings. Every Friday, we do a talent show. We play one of our songs. We were so obsessed with failing because it was fun, and my dad was so obsessed with watching and experiencing those moments.\par \par \uc2 BOY: I love you, today.\par \par \uc2 DAD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: For Warren, those memories are bittersweet. At nine years old, he lost his father to cancer. He says his mom then struggled with alcoholism and became physically abusive, so he left home. She would later die from the disease.\par \par \uc2 He showed us the parking lot where he lived out of his car for months.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: I had no money to my name, but looking back at it and everything was better than the household I was in.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: while homeless, his then girlfriend, covert decided to live with him in his car.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ordinary"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: For a good month or two, it was terrifying. No, I do feel as if my wife was this weird thing sent by God to help me and do all this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Carry Me Home"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: He began posting videos about their relationship, which led to a following and some much-needed funds.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: All of a sudden we were like, oh, my God, we can afford peanut butter and jelly and yes, we just kept going and never looked back.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hype House"/Netflix)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: The couple then moved to L.A. to launch "Hype House," an influencer collective.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: One day, I posted a video of me singing a song, and I woke up the next day. I had 10 million views, and people were like, "You should be a singer. You`re so good."\par \par \uc2 I don`t know, it was the first time in my life I had validation since my Dad died. It was the first time where I felt like I could chase this dream that seems so unachievable.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Carry You Home"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Warren`s career took off, winning MTV`s Best New Artist Award and releasing his first album, "You`ll Be Alright, Kid."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "You`ll Be Alright, Kid"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: That album.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I mean, that`s an anthem to you, right?\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: Yes, oh my gosh. Oftentimes, now I look back at the things that I`ve gone through, and I think as if they were meant to happen.\par \par \uc2 These songs, it`s helping kids, it`s helping people, it`s helping adults go through grief and trauma and loss, and that`s kind of the important part to me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "You`ll Be Alright, Kid"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Is there a favorite track on the album for you?\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: "First Time on Earth," because I when I lost my Dad.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "First Time on Earth"/Alex Warren/Atlantic Records)\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: Because I -- when I lost my Dad, I wasn`t cognitive to be able to say goodbye. Even my mom, I would go after her because I felt as if she was doing wrong as a parent.\par \par \uc2 Truthfully, both of us, we`ve never done this before, so both our first times on Earth.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: The lyrics may be born from a painful past, but Warren says he finally feels at home.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WARREN: My Dad worked so hard to get me to be able to wake up and this be my backyard. That`s something beautiful. And I spent so long trying to run away from that. And I think for the first time in my life, I`m just so happy to be able to say that, like, that`s what I want to come back to, you know?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Natalie Morales in Carlsbad, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t even know what to say, Natalie.\par \par \uc2 I first got his album when it first came out, because I liked it, and still do. But now that you know the back story, I`ll bet I`ll hear it differently this time and shout out to Kouvr.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, look he took --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To the wife.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- what were trials and tribulations, a very tough life, sleeping in his car, losing both parents.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- to finding purpose and future through his music and turning all that grief and chaos into something beautiful. I love that.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes. I mean, you hear the song "Ordinary" and then now with the back story, the lyrics just means so much --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You think differently.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I know I can`t wait to hear it. I`ll listen to it on the plane heading to the Grammys.\par \par \uc2 I love Alex`s music. Bravo Alex! Bravo Kouvr who still stayed with him, even with those circumstances. That`s very special.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can watch the 68th Annual Grammy Awards this Sunday, 8:0 Eastern, 5:00 Pacific Time, right here on CBS.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, it`s streaming