mp says he won`t use force to take Greenland and claims there`s now a plan for the future of the island.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): It is what`s called an infinite deal, because it`s forever.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The verdict is in for a former police officer put on trial and accused of cowardice after the Uvalde school shooting.\par \par \uc2 FOREMAN: We, the jury, find defendant, Adrian Gonzales not guilty.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The House Oversight Committee voted to hold Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in contempt of Congress for defying a subpoena in the \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  investigation.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Oscar contenders cut loose. The Director of "Hamnet" posted this video of the cast dance party after their final day of filming.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Give yourselves a hand. If you were able to do this, that means the frostbite did not take your fingers.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Congratulations. Not just -- it`s not just New York. A huge swath of the country is now under an arctic air mass. So, for the record, Greenland attacked first.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Okay. They had it coming! You want a piece of us?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s also winter. It is supposed to be cold, but this is really cold.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it`s really cold, and the weather is no joke.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s where we`re going to start, actually. We begin this morning with about 180 million people bracing for a dangerous winter storm that could bring heavy snow and subzero temperatures, with cold air diving down from Canada, officials from Texas to New York are preparing for possible massive power outages and flight cancelations.\par \par \uc2 This storm will start affecting us in the next 24 hours, and it will only get worse through the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Ash-Har Quraishi is in Chicago, where it feels close to zero degrees right now. Ash-Har, good morning. I hope you`re staying warm.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Well, I don`t know about other people, but the answer to how I`m staying warm is layers of clothing, snow pants, gloves and a hat I like to call "The Beast." And while it`s cold now, it is expected to get much worse, with an extreme cold warning going into effect here in the next 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Now, some forecasters are saying this storm could impact a roughly 2,000- mile-long stretch of the Continental U.S.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Potential double-digit snowfalls and single-digit temperatures wreak havoc across the U.S., including in the Upper Midwest.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, many Minnesota schools announced early closures ahead of the dangerously cold Arctic air.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`ve been spending the last couple days inside.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: And in Chicago, Illinois, we spoke to residents about how they`re preparing for windchills that could feel as low as negative 40 degrees.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Bundling layers, lots of socks, got to have a lot of socks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You`ve got to just roll with it, especially if you live in Chicago, you`ve got to just roll with it.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: But other impacted areas are much less accustomed to severe winter weather. Atlanta`s airport is one of several in the Southern U.S. that could see delays with many airlines already waiving change fees for customers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We don`t get a lot of opportunities to engage in the snow event, and so we`re doing a lot of preparation. We have new equipment that will make us efficient in that effort.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Even as far south as Texas, officials are preparing for hazardous roads and power outages while people stock up at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We may not be able to get out and get anything for a couple or three days until it thaws out. So, we wanted to be sure we had everything we needed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Now, Texas Governor Greg Abbott has activated a state emergency response to deal with the unusually bad winter weather in his state. Emergency managers in states across the affected areas are urging residents to prepare early and not wait till the storm hits -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Ash-Har, I`m a size large, too, so if you want to send "The Beast" my way, I`ll appreciate that. Have a good one, man.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` New York chief weather caster, Lonnie Quinn is here with us.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie, good morning. How bad is it going to get? Be honest, man.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Okay, I think of everything involved here, and we`re talking snow and wind and cold and ice. I think the ice is going to be the worst of it. I mean, listen, you can drive, you at least get by driving on snow, you can`t do it on ice.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the deal, to get a storm you need cold air. It`s certainly going to be in place. Minneapolis to Chicago through the weekend, it feels like at times, with the wind 30 below to 60 below. You get down to Oklahoma City to San Antonio, could feel like 20 below at times.\par \par \uc2 Our storm hasn`t even come together yet, and yet, 125 million people from New Mexico into New York are under winter weather alerts and warnings and advisories. That`s going to be expanded as the storm does come together.\par \par \uc2 Watch this, guys. Watch this flow coming in from Mexico, a low off of Los Angeles and a low dropping in from the Northern Rockies. Now, it`s going to form. That`s your storm as it pushes across the country, again, ice right in the middle of it, snow to the north. We`ve got rain to the south and so, let`s talk some numbers out there.\par \par \uc2 What`s going to be the worst of it? Well, let`s begin in the Great Plains. This is Friday afternoon into Sunday afternoon. Oklahoma City, up to Springfield, nine to 12 inches of snow. Now I want to go south, down to say, the Texas into Southern Arkansas, Northern Louisiana, right over I-20, I-40, it could be a half an inch, even an inch of ice. Can`t drive on that.\par \par \uc2 The deep south of the Ohio Valley, biggest numbers of all, Louisville, Cincinnati, maybe 12 to 18 inches of snow. Now, I`m going to take us into that icing impact for the Tennessee Valley, half an inch to an inch as well, and that would be starting early Saturday into the northeast areas, and unfortunately, it`s right along the I-95 corridor that includes us, could potentially be 12 to 18 inches of snow.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, I don`t know if I want it, Lonnie, but thank you.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Been a while around here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I see.\par \par \uc2 Now to another big event for President Trump at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 This morning, he launched his so-called Board of Peace. It is designed to oversee Gaza. The President also tried to calm the concerns of NATO allies over his demand to take control of Greenland. He says they sorted out a framework of a future deal, his words.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is in Davos watching all of this for us.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning. What is the latest?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, Gayle, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Amid all this focus on Greenland in recent weeks, the President is spending his final hours overseas today on the two big foreign policy challenges that he has faced. He is set to meet with Ukraine`s President Volodymyr Zelenskyy in a little bit, telling reporters he wants to get that done, that conflict with Russia done.\par \par \uc2 And as you mentioned, he`s launched what he called the Board of Peace to help settle the dispute between Israel and Hamas and to rebuild the Gaza Strip, saying that this group will somehow make that happen in the coming years. But notably, there were several big American allies absent from the launch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You`re the biggest people, the most important people in the world.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Davos was the place President Trump officially launched his Board of Peace, a new body to help resolve the conflict in Gaza. On the list 21 nations, including countries like Saudi Arabia and NATO ally, Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Notably absent, Israel and other Western allies like France and the United Kingdom. The President has previously said the Board could replace some duties of the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: But there`s tremendous potential with the United Nations, and I think a combination of the Board of Peace with the kind of people we have here, coupled with the United Nations can be something very, very unique for the world.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But it was the President`s threats to take over Greenland that dominated discussions at Davos this week. And now --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We have a concept of a deal. I think it`s going to be very good deal for the United States, also for them, and we`re going to work together on something having to do with the Arctic as a whole, but also Greenland, and it has to do with the security, great security, strong security and other things.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The President said the breakthrough came after a meeting with the head of NATO, Trump posting he will not be imposing the tariffs on Denmark, which controls the island and seven other European nations.\par \par \uc2 He also vowed not to use the military to capture Greenland.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I don`t have to use force. I don`t want to use force. I won`t use force.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But Trump attacked NATO allies as unreliable partners.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We want strong allies, not seriously weakened ones.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: North Carolina Republican Senator Thom Tillis called the President`s rhetoric on Greenland a false crisis.\par \par \uc2 The fact that the President says U.S. military involvement in taking Greenland is off the table, does that ease your concern?\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): No, because it was never on the table. I mean, that`s a false conception. If anybody thinks that the U.S. Congress would have stood for any sort of troops move to Greenland, I believe we have veto proof majorities within two weeks.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Democrats like Kentucky Governor Andy Beshear say even though the President has backed off his threats, they damaged relationships.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDY BESHEAR (D-KY): The President`s speech was dangerous, disrespectful and unhinged. We are less safe when people believe that the United States isn`t committed to the NATO Alliance.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: European allies remain frustrated. The German Chancellor this morning, urged unity during what he called a new era of politics.\par \par \uc2 FRIEDRICH MERZ (German Chancellor): We have entered a time of great power politics. Today, its very foundations have been shaken.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now on Greenland, two diplomatic sources tell CBS News there is no agreement for U.S. control of ownership in that framework, ownership of Greenland, despite the President saying that he set up an infinite deal, or is working towards an infinite deal.\par \par \uc2 The White House`s State Department have not yet provided details on what exactly is in this framework. And this morning, Denmark`s Prime Minister welcomed the President, saying he`s not going to use military force to take Greenland, but he reiterated, we cannot -- we can negotiate on everything, political, security, investments, economy, but we cannot negotiate on our sovereignty. In other words, as far as they`re concerned, Gayle, the U.S. will never control Greenland.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Ed, before you go, let`s do a little deeper dive on this, because the President arrived, he brought lots of threats, lots of tension, got everybody all riled up, but he`s leaving, it seems, some say without much to show in regards especially to Greenland. Was this trip a success for him? What happened?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: If the ultimate long-term goal is to ensure that NATO and the United States are guarding the Arctic Circle, that region more closely, more carefully, putting more resources there? Then The White House is going to argue they are on the verge of achieving that goal, because you have already seen NATO members send more troops just in recent days, and now there`s going to be this talk about coming up with some new framework that expands that partnership.\par \par \uc2 But he didn`t leave with an agreement to take over the island entirely. There will be no U.S. military force, and Europe is quite upset with him. So in essence, he didn`t get necessarily what he wants, and he upset a lot of American allies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Ed, thank you very much. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, breaking overnight, a Texas jury has handed down its verdict in the trial of a former school police officer in the Uvalde school shooting.\par \par \uc2 Adrian Gonzales was the first to arrive at the scene of the 2022 shooting, was found not guilty of 29 counts of child abandonment or endangerment. Omar Villafranca is at the courthouse in Corpus Christi.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the rare cases where a police officer was charged for failing to act during the school shooting. Keep in mind, there were 376 officers at the school, and it took them 77 minutes to stop the shooter at Robb Elementary, but the jury decided that Gonzales` actions were not criminal.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FOREMAN: We the jury find defendant, Adrian Gonzales, not guilty.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: There was visible emotion in the courtroom Wednesday night as a Texas jury acquitted former school police officer, Adrian Gonzales for his role in responding to the deadly shooting at Robb Elementary School in Uvalde, Texas in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Both Gonzales and the courtroom filled with victims` families were crying.\par \par \uc2 NICO LAHOOD (Gonzales` defense attorney): There`s no real winners, I mean, because obviously there`s so much tragedy and hurt behind this.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Go! Run over there!\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: When a gunman attacked the school, Gonzales was the first officer on the scene and among the first five to enter the building.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Careful, guys. Shots fired!\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Prosecutors say Gonzales froze and failed in his duty to protect the children, but the jury agreed with the defense, who argued the former officer risked his life when he went into the school hallway and towards the gunshots.\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN GONZALES (Former Uvalde School Police officer): Thank you for the jury for considering all the evidence and making that verdict.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Outside the courthouse, families of the 19 fourth grade students and two teachers who died that day responded with sadness and anger.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: There was two monsters on May 24, and one was a shooter and the other was a monster that never went in.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What Texas just told us is that our kids didn`t mean anything to them.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But still, some had hope.\par \par \uc2 JESSE RIZO (Jackie Cazares` uncle): Faith is fractured, but you never lose faith. You don`t lose faith because these children that are no longer with us, that are at the cemetery, they can`t speak for themselves. We speak for them. We fight to the end.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The only other law enforcement official who has been charged in this case is former Uvalde School Police chief, Pete Arredondo. His trial date has not been set, but when asked about the outcome of the Gonzales trial, his attorney told CBS News, this was the only verdict that was possible -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have important new information this morning on the immigration crackdown in Minnesota. A memo obtained by CBS News shows that ICE agents have been told that they can force their way into suspects` homes without a judge`s search warrant.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there`s a new dispute between ICE and local officials in Minnesota that has officials calling ICE flat wrong.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sganga is in St. Paul covering this for us.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, good morning. What`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 ICE says it has taken hundreds of dangerous migrants off the streets here in this region. It also accuses state and local law enforcement officials of interfering with immigration enforcement, even letting some suspects go free.\par \par \uc2 Now, CBS News has reviewed state records that suggest some of those alleged criminals were already in prison or under state law enforcement supervision, and Minnesota`s top corrections official, his name is Paul Schnell, we spoke with him yesterday, seemed genuinely astonished by the Trump administration`s claim that the state is releasing criminals.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Does it feel like the Department of Homeland Security is lying when it says that state authorities are not cooperating?\par \par \uc2 PAUL SCHNELL (Minnesota`s Corrections Commissioner): It is fundamentally false. We cooperate with ICE and ICE detainers. We have, as a matter of policy, done that for a long, long time.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: As ICE raids have unfolded across the Twin Cities, the Trump administration has accused state leaders of protecting the worst of the worst.\par \par \uc2 MARCOS CHARLES (ICE Enforcement Removal Operations executive director): The best solution is to turn them over to us in a safe, controlled setting, like a jail or prison.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: But Minnesota Corrections Commissioner Paul Schnell says that`s already common practice.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SCHNELL: As they approach their release date, several weeks before our staff coordinates directly with the local ICE office. Staff do this on a routine basis. They make arrangements for the transfer of custody of that individual.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: For months, the Department of Homeland Security has released lists of mugshots, alleged criminals it says were seemingly tracked down and arrested by ICE, but Schnell told us that`s not completely true.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SCHNELL: These are people that are -- that in many -- in most instances, they`re in our system or have been in our system, but they were released.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: These worst of the worst criminals weren`t swept up by ice on city streets.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SCHNELL: No, they were released to ICE.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: In one example, Schnell said his department turned these two state prisoners over to immigration officials on January 12th. The next day, both appeared in a DHS press release highlighting people who had been arrested in ICE operations. That same release also claimed state leaders, "... will not let us into their jails."\par \par \uc2 PAUL SCHNELL: Coordinated that transfer of custody, and the next day, they showed up on a DHS list of among these people --\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The worst of the worst list.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SCHNELL: Among the worst of the worst. And yet, exactly what should have happened, happened.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: You say you need it to stop.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SCHNELL: We need them to stop telling stories that are simply untrue. That we will and do coordinate on the transfer of custody of people who are subject to ICE detainers, and we`ll continue to do that. Period.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: We should add that Minnesota Corrections said they didn`t even have criminal records for some of the alleged felons that ICE says they`ve arrested. Now, we did reach out to the Department of Homeland Security for clarity here, they did not address the discrepancy in the numbers, but in a statement, urged all Minnesota elected leaders to cooperate with ICE -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nicole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Prince Harry gets emotional telling a court how British tabloids allegedly invaded his privacy. That`s coming up, but first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, Hollywood`s biggest night is right around the corner.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sinners"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Oscar nominations are out this morning, and we`ll have the list of the top nominees.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bugonia"/Focus Features)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And in "What to Watch" a new A.I. project features the one and only Liza Minnelli, why she agreed to the collaboration.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Endurance Warranty. Protecting drivers for nearly 20 years. Learn more at EnduranceWarranty.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some very sad news this morning about a reporter and a photographer who helped CBS News bring you the facts on this war in Gaza. Freelance cameraman, his name was Abed Shaat was killed by an Israeli airstrike in Southern Gaza City yesterday, along with two other journalists.\par \par \uc2 They were recording video of aid distribution at the time with a group called the Egyptian Humanitarian Committee. The attack hit their vehicle, which was clearly marked with its logo. There is still an ongoing ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Israel Defense Forces say they were targeting a Hamas affiliated drone and that they were looking into the incident. Throughout this Israel-Hamas War, Abed was our eyes, he was our ears in Southern Gaza, sending video just like this.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, he was also just married two weeks ago. This is tough. We send our condolences to his family.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 The story that caught our eye at 7:30, it sounds like the plot of the movie, "Catch Me If You Can," if you remember it, right? Leo DiCaprio pretends to be a pilot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, in this story, a former flight attendant allegedly scammed airlines for more than four years by posing as a pilot to fly on hundreds of flights for free. His name is Dallas Pokornik. There he is. He`s from Canada. He faces two federal counts of wire fraud in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Court documents allege he used a false badge from a former employer and fraudulently represented himself to U(S-based airlines as a "pilot" or "flight attendant" to get free tickets.\par \par \uc2 Pokornik pleaded not guilty in federal court. He is set to face trial in March.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you want to tell the class like you were telling me and Nate. I think that guy, you want to be his friend. You think he is a hero.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No! I think that it`s interesting that people who are -- who have enough chutzpah to be able to exploit loopholes in the system, you can`t be mad that this guy found a loophole.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he can!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is not right. It`s not right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it`s not right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is not right, but he found that loophole. He got away with it. Now, he`s going to pay the price.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And America, Vlad said, "Good on him!"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You have to also be willing to pay the price. He paid the price. He got caught, now he`s got to go to jail.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad said, these flights are expensive. If you can fly free, that`s fine!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes! Exactly! That`s what he said --\par \par \uc2 Moving on, this morning, Prince Harry is in a courtroom for his second day in a lawsuit against the owner of two British tabloids. He claims that "The Daily Mail" and the "Mail on Sunday" turned his life upside down, and also broke the law while doing it.\par \par \uc2 "The Daily Mail" website is hugely popular here in this country. The newspaper`s owner, by the way, denies all of the allegations.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer reports on Harry`s testimony yesterday that got a little emotional.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: For the third time, Prince Harry is suing a British tabloid for invasion of privacy, and he looked cheerful arriving at court.\par \par \uc2 As a member of Britain`s Royal Family, Harry spent decades in the public eye at orchestrated photo opportunities. It was just part of the job. But this lawsuit alleges in Britain`s High Court that "The Daily Mail" went much further and broke the law to steal his personal information with phone hacks and private investigators, especially for articles about an old girlfriend, Chelsy Davy.\par \par \uc2 On the witness stand yesterday, he accused the publisher of "The Daily Mail" of wanting to drive him to drugs and drinking, and apparently on the verge of tears, he said, "They have made my wife`s life an absolute misery."\par \par \uc2 Roya Nikkhah is Royal correspondent for "The Sunday Times."\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: What was interesting to hear yesterday is how he feels press intrusion has affected his relationships with his current wife, with previous girlfriends and why he feels it`s important now to take a stand on that.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Six public figures, including Elton John and the actor, Liz Hurley are plaintiffs in this lawsuit with Prince Harry, who has already successfully sued the owners of two other tabloids.\par \par \uc2 He has now won two lawsuits. Have those two victories done anything to curb the excesses of the tabloid press?\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: The activities that he is complaining about and has gone to trial for, there are no claims in there from recent years. This is going back quite a long way, so I don`t think the media is behaving like that anymore.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Maybe not, but a big portion of the British public still thinks that the media is too intrusive and should be regulated. Now, the media pushes back, in part, because, as we all know, those spicy stories sell papers, or rather, these days, get lots of clicks -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but you`ve got to get it right though. Elizabeth Palmer, as you always do, thank you so much, reporting from London.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of clicks, are you getting tired of seeing all of those fake A.I. videos flooding your social media feeds? You know, the ones with the dancing babies and animals, the deepfake celebrity videos? These are often referred to as "A.I. slop."\par \par \uc2 So now, YouTube says it wants to get rid of it on its platform. Here to explain, CBS News "Money Watch" correspondent, Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, good morning. So, what is driving this push to clean up this A.I. content, and then what protections are out there that YouTube will put in place?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So, I mean, there`s just been an explosion of that low- quality content, as you said.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: To the extent that over a million YouTube channels each day in December used A.I. technology in creating those videos. So, there is a lot, and that creates a number of issues. One, you have viewer trust issues. You`ve got these deepfake videos that look really real.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And people don`t know if they are, and so that also leads to the platform becoming low quality as a whole, potentially and all of those A.I. slop videos sort of crowding out legitimate creators trying to make a living, trying to bring us good content.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ah, yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And that by the way, is not good for advertisers or brands either.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: No company wants an ad running next to a bad A.I. video.\par \par \uc2 You asked about protections, YouTube specifically does require any creator that has created an A.I.-generated video to say so. Their tool, specifically, if you use a video, or you create a video using their tools, there is a label that comes up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A label there, so you know.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Exactly! Exactly!\par \par \uc2 And then, in general, the algorithm tries to down rank low quality content, it doesn`t always work, and it often comes down to people having to do this when things are evolving so quickly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So OpenAI, the company behind ChatGPT, they announced that they are launching a new age protection tool. How could this better protect users?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So, the tool is trying to identify if an account is under 18, and it`s not just going to rely on, you know, your self-reporting, right? Because what kid is going to do that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It`s also going to look at your activity as well as your usage and your account history to try and determine, okay, is this person under 18? If they do, then they`re going to be protections around sensitive content. Think graphic violence, body shaming, sexual content.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: If it gets it wrong and you`re saying, hey, no, I`m actually above 18, you can use your government I.D. to prove that. And of course, this comes when OpenAI is facing a lot of scrutiny from parents, from regulators of what this platform is doing to their kids.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Such an ever-evolving industry.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, coming up, you might be surprised to learn that more than 300,000 Black women lost or left their jobs in just the first half of 2025. Jericka Duncan will show us why this could be a warning sign for the entire economy.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Instead of empowering bureaucrats, we`re firing them, and they`re going out and getting jobs in the private sector for two and three times what they were making in government.\par \par \uc2 So, they started off hating me when we fired them, and now they love me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was President Trump in Davos, Switzerland yesterday, talking about firing federal government workers during his first year in office. Now, one group of people hit hard by those layoffs is Black women, apparently.\par \par \uc2 While the country`s overall unemployment rate fell to 4.4 percent in the most recent Jobs Report, it rose to 7.3 percent when it came to Black women.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan spoke with two women who have faced prolonged unemployment to find out what`s behind this trend.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE BURCH (Former federal government employee): I loved my job. I loved it, and it made a difference to people.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But Adrinne Burch`s job with the Department of Agriculture was eliminated last February.\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE BURCH: I started crying. I said, oh, my God, I`m losing my job. I`m not prepared. I don`t have enough savings.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (Tesla CEO): The chainsaw for bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: As Elon Musk took a chainsaw to the federal government, Burch says she felt forced into early retirement at age 54.\par \par \uc2 Today, she says she gets one-third of her previous pay.\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE BURCH: To get to a point where you made a great salary, made six figures, and to have that ripped from you, and not because you didn`t do the job well is painful.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Pain that was just beginning as Burch started looking for work.\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE BURCH: I`ve applied for, I think about 35 jobs or so. I`ve gotten five rejections. Most of the other ones, nothing.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Any interviews?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE BURCH: No.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And she`s not alone, 41-year-old Audrey Malone lost her corporate job in Facilities Management about a year ago.\par \par \uc2 AUDREY MALONE (Former Facilities Management employee): I stopped counting my job applications after I hit like 150 or something like that.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You`ve applied to over 150 -- more than 150 jobs?\par \par \uc2 AUDREY MALONE: Oh, I`ve applied to more than 150 jobs. I just stopped counting at 150 and you look at it, and you go, I`m not enough. What`s wrong with me? What am I doing wrong?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Some economists say the proliferation of artificial intelligence and the elimination of DEI programs have negatively impacted Black women. And then there`s the cuts to government jobs, where Black women are represented at twice the rate as in the private sector. All of it has contributed to the more than 300,000 Black women who lost or left their jobs in the first half of 2025.\par \par \uc2 ANNA GIFTY OPOKU-AGYEMAN (Harvard economist): We almost are like the economic shock absorbers of what`s coming to us.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The canary in the coalmine.\par \par \uc2 ANNA GIFTY OPOKU-AGYEMAN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Harvard economist, Anna Gifty Opoku-Agyeman.\par \par \uc2 Why should people, not just Black women, care about this?\par \par \uc2 ANNA GIFTY OPOKU-AGYEMAN: If seven percent of your body wasn`t working, you wouldn`t be able to function. If Black women are not able to be full economic participants in our economy, our economy shrinks.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Last month, Representative Ayanna Pressley and more than 20 other members of Congress demanded that the Department of Labor take immediate action to address the rising unemployment crisis among Black women, a crisis that Adrienne Burch says needs attention.\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE BURCH: We`re not looking for handouts, but we`re like, hey, somebody, do something. Speak up for us, and until then, we have to help each other.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was Jericka Duncan reporting.\par \par \uc2 And to put this in perspective, Gayle and Nate, about 69 percent of Black mothers are bread winners. And Jericka told me something that stood out to her, which was that the economist she talked to who wrote that book says that our economic fates are linked. In other words, the broader population of the United States.\par \par \uc2 What happens to Black women or any marginalized group is a sign of things to come, because this is something that we knew when I worked on Wall Street. It`s an early warning system for the broader economy that when the workers that are most exposed to inequality or who are destabilized, when they are hit hard by what is happening in our economy, the ripple effects will affect everybody at some point.\par \par \uc2 So, Wall Street looks at that number, and they pinpoint what`s happening to people who are marginalized to make a prediction of what could happen to the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I like the analogy that was made in the piece that if seven -- over seven percent of your body wasn`t working, you would not be able to function.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thinking of the Black women as economic shock absorbers. That`s very enlightening, that piece.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It really was. Jericka, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, the stories that you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:45, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Saturday --you`re going to be singing that song all day. "Saturday, Saturday." Vlad was just singing it. Vlad, keep singing when we come over there.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in our next hour, we are counting down to this morning`s big reveal - -\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wicked: For Good"/Universal Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The nominations 00 I love this morning -- for the 98th Oscars nominations.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Marty Supreme"/A24)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fandango and Rotten Tomatoes` correspondent, that`s Erik Davis is going to be here very soon --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "If I Had Legs I`d Kick You")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- to break it all down, from the surprises to the snubs, there`s always those. And you can be sure he won`t pull back, because that`s what Eric does.\par \par \uc2 Plus, hit-series "Shrinking" is back for its one, two third season. We`re going to talk to one of the stars, Jessica Williams. Why she says her co- star, Harrison Ford, made her cry. We love him, too.\par \par \uc2 So all of that`s coming up on our next hour, but right now, heading over -- Claus and I, heading over to the table. I`ve got the hiccups.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m so excited. This is going to be the first "What to Watch" when we talk about Liza Minnelli.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Legend, ever!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ever?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ever.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, I`m really excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought you were going to sing "Saturday"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Saturday."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We don`t have time!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Saturday."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, what`s being called the first major album generated by artificial intelligence with some very big names agreeing to take part.\par \par \uc2 This is really cool. Legendary entertainer, Liza Minnelli is one of the 13 artists on the "Eleven" album -- that`s the name of it, where the songs are created by blending original music with ElevenLabs` A.I. program.\par \par \uc2 So Liza`s Track is called "Kids." Wait till you hear this and listen to it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eleven.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not sure what I`m listening to there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, that`s Liza`s voice, and artificial intelligence has been used in a way where she -- she didn`t go into the studio recording tracks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They`re just using her voice, and that`s what ultimately --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I think, as long as they tell you that that`s what it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. And they`re using -- and they`re paying her. They`re paying her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s not that they haven`t done this on their own. She`s doing this in collaboration.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, gotcha!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Collaboration.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, she is okay with it, because she`s getting paid.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the future. I am for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, one of the men arrested in the largest jewelry theft in the U.S. is looking like he`s getting off scot-free, thanks to ICE.\par \par \uc2 Yes, let me show you what happened.\par \par \uc2 Police bodycam video shows the aftermath of the heist on an armored truck in California back in 2022. Jeson Flores was one of seven men charged with stealing an estimated $100 million in gold and jewelry from the big rig. He now faces 15 years in prison if convicted, but when he was released on bail, he was picked up by ICE, and then he got deported to Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 So, prosecutors say they were not told until after it happened. So, much of the stolen jewelry has not been recovered. He`s in Ecuador. He can`t be charged. He is in Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up Academy Award nominations are out this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s saying thank you, ICE.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will talk about it!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Nate Burleson and that`s Vlad Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this eight o`clock hour, the top nominees for the Academy Awards, I cannot wait. We`ll show you the names out this morning and look at the surprises and of course, there`s always snubs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Our "USA to Z" series moves to C, the Constitution. Jan Crawford finds out why it`s getting much more attention from young Americans as we celebrate our nation`s 250th birthday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The hit series, "Shrinking" explores the inner lives of three psychiatrists. Jessica Williams plays one of them, and she will be here to tell us what her character is looking for in the new season.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait. The show is so good.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start this hour, though with a massive winter storm taking aim at tens of millions of Americans. We`ve been talking about it for the last 24 hours. It`s going to start hitting us today, is the word, but by the end of the weekend, it`s expected to dump heavy snow and even ice from Texas to New York.\par \par \uc2 CBS News New York chief weather caster, Lonnie Quinn, running in to position.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie, you made it. You made it. So, give it to us straight.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Thank you for stretching, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Look, here`s the deal. There`s some really interesting stuff to talk about. So 125 million people, you`re talking the numbers under some sort of winter weather advisory. We`ve got a gulf flow pushing a lot of moisture toward Texas. Pacific moisture pushing -- Pacific flow pushing moisture toward Texas, cold air coming from the north, all of those three have to come together to create our storm.\par \par \uc2 Then it is pushing ice down in the middle of the storm, snow to the north, rain to the south. It pushes across the country. Without question, ice is the most dangerous. And outside of Dallas, I-20, I-40, you could be dealing with a half inch to an inch of ice. Elevated roadways there, they freeze super-fast, so dangerous. And then in and around the triad, also half an inch or more of ice.\par \par \uc2 Snowfall, here is a real broad stroke anywhere outside of, say, Oklahoma- ish, all the way through the Ohio Valley into the northeast, including New York City, you could see double digit snowfall totals. And New Yorkers have got to be scratching their heads saying, what`s with the sixes? This is what I`m talking about. This what I was working on, Gayle, when I ran in here.\par \par \uc2 Think about this: 1996, all right, we had a storm that delivered 20.2 inches, the fifth biggest in city history. Ten years later, 2006, we pick up our second biggest snowstorm ever, 26.9. Ten years after that, 2016 we pick up the biggest snowstorm in New York City history, history, 27.5.\par \par \uc2 What -- ten years later, this Sunday on 2026 holds? We shall find out, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Nate, it is all yours.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. Lonnie, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, there`s something to the sixes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s turn to today`s "Money Watch." The president, he has signed a new executive order this week aimed at boosting the sluggish housing market. The goal is to increase the supply of homes to buy by preventing Wall Street investors from purchasing single family homes for the rental market.\par \par \uc2 All right, so CBS News business analyst is here, Jill Schlesinger. Thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Great to be with.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, what will this Executive Order do?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: I think it`s going to have limited impact. First of all, the share of the institutional investor market and housing is small. So, we`re talking again, as you said, a big company, piles up money, they buy up houses, they become landlords, and there`s a fear that if you do too much of that, then you really take a lot of housing stock offline.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: But I don`t think this is going to have a real move the needle issue. I think that it is a limited impact, as I said. So, I wouldn`t really focus on this when it comes to the housing market.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It sounds good. You hear about mortgage rates that are dropping. How do you think that`s going to impact the housing market? You think that it`s great for buyers? Not so great, maybe for sellers.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Well, I think it`s really good for the housing market, actually. And you know, remember, we peaked at 30-year mortgage rates at around, let`s call it eight percent in 2023.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Then we came down to around seven percent at the beginning of 2025, we ended the year at 6.06 --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, it is under six?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: -- but, but, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, but --\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: -- there is an evidence that this week we get a higher print, because, as we talked about yesterday, when bonds sell off, mortgage rates start to creep up. Again, with mortgage rates down, this is helpful for the housing market in general.\par \par \uc2 Just remember, it`s very difficult to think about listing your home if you`ve got a mortgage that is, say under five percent, right? I need another mortgage, but I don`t really want to give up my juicy three or four percent mortgage.\par \par \uc2 Well, almost 70 percent of homeowners have mortgage rates under five percent, so as mortgage interest rates come down, more people will be inclined to list their homes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What about prices for homes? Any relief there?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Weh! Weh! I`m so sorry.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m bringing the bummer!\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: I am so sorry.\par \par \uc2 So, at the end of last year, the median home price was around $428,000.00. Median, okay. So, that`s a big number. It is up more than 28 percent from five years ago.\par \par \uc2 I think the good news is that because the housing market has remained somewhat sluggish and affordability has been a problem, the rate of price increases has slowed down dramatically, but that overall price, a six percent mortgage, high property taxes, high insurance costs makes affordability a very difficult hurdle to get over if you`re a first-time home buyer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And if you`re a first-time home buyer, you have any advice?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Well, I mean, first of all, I`ve talked about this so often, like, don`t be afraid of renting if it`s a better alternative for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But people always say that`s like throwing money away. It is my rent --\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: It is not because if you rent and you had the amount of money you were going to pay towards carrying that house --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: You put that in your 401 (k), that`s not throwing money away. That`s being efficient and being a wealth accumulator. So go for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the phrase "wealth accumulator."\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: All right, I`m all in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, thank you, Jill. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We are just minutes away from learning this year`s Oscar nominees, Erik Davis has arrived. He`s here to talk about the stars, a surprise, and of course, the snubs. Talk about wonk-wonk!\par \par \uc2 Plus, our series "USA to Z" gets a close look at the U.S. Constitution, because we`re focusing on the letter C today, how it is inspiring future leaders. All of that`s coming up, but first, it is 8:06. It`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, "Shrinking" star, Jessica Williams will join us to talk about Season 3 of the hit series and why she loves acting opposite Harrison Ford.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to our series, "USA to Z" where we are celebrating 250 years of America. This week is C for Constitution. It is among our oldest and most important founding documents.\par \par \uc2 These days, faith in our democratic institutions is fading. Just over half of Americans say U.S. democracy is performing poorly, or even very poorly.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford takes us inside the National Archives, home of the Constitution and to a classroom to see how future generations are embracing what it stands for.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In this stately hall in the nation`s capital, the foundation of American government sits proudly on display.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: New Hampshire, Massachusetts, Connecticut, and they signed from north to south.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But below the crowds --\par \par \uc2 These are handwritten notes.\par \par \uc2 In the basement --\par \par \uc2 This is literally George Washington.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN BROWNING (U.S. National Archives and Records Senior Conservator): This is George Washington.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: His actual handwriting.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN BROWNING: His handwriting.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In a preservation lab built nearly a hundred years ago.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN BROWNING: He had the best handwriting than any of the Founding Fathers.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: This is where the magic happens.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "National Treasures"/Buena Vista Pictures)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s an art glamorized by a certain Hollywood treasure hunter --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "National Treasures"/Buena Vista Pictures)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But very much reality for senior conservator at the National Archives, Morgan Browning.\par \par \uc2 He and his team has spent years preparing the celebrations for America 250.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So this goes to Congress. Congress actually approves and says, yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Their latest effort was putting rarely seen amendments on display with the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN BROWNING: When I started out, I could only dream of working on things like this. And then, you know, fast forward 20 years, I mean, it`s an immense privilege, and it`s a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Do you ever kind of think about how it`s also such a huge responsibility?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN BROWNING: It`s a responsibility, especially when you think of the Constitution and the amendments. It`s so relevant today.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: About a million people every year come to see the original U.S. Constitution and its amendments with the framers` unique signatures.\par \par \uc2 JIM BYRON (Senior advisor to archivist): It`s an opportunity to renew those bonds as American --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Jim Byron is senior advisor to the archivist.\par \par \uc2 JIM BYRON: We have 13-and-a-half billion pieces of paper --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Billion?\par \par \uc2 JIM BYRON: Billion with a B, so America 250 is an incredible opportunity to expose and show more of what`s in the Archives.\par \par \uc2 AMY GLEASON (5th grade teacher, Center for Enriched Studies): How many of you found what you read empowering?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: In nearby Chevy Chase, Maryland --\par \par \uc2 AMY GLEASON: So, they were giving away their freedom papers to other --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Teacher, Amy Gleason, is working to keep it relevant.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: They just had to figure that out themselves.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Bringing the document to life for her students in a time when faith in government has been waning.\par \par \uc2 AMY GLEASON: I really want them to see the humanity in history, that it`s not just this old document.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: For Gleason`s fifth graders, the Constitution Unit is the highlight of the year, filled with reenactments, immersive lessons and even a Constitutional Convention.\par \par \uc2 AMY GLEASON: There are so many great things to know about who we are as a country and as a nation, and I think to celebrate that, we also have to look at those missteps. We also have to understand where we didn`t do it right so that we can do it right later. And I feel like as an educator, it`s my job to help these kids see that they are a part of this and that they can have a voice.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: A voice this group of 10-and-11-year-olds has been happy to embrace.\par \par \uc2 KIYAAN NIMAN (11 years old): It makes us feel like a part of history. So, we get to argue for our point and see everyone`s side and how they thought of it.\par \par \uc2 MARIAM ZAGHLOUL (11 years old): And it also made me feel like I was there at the time, and like, as a woman, they wouldn`t let them in. So, it was really cool just being there and being a part of history.\par \par \uc2 ASHER BLITSTEIN (10 years old): I could really understand it, because I was that person there.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You were a delegate.\par \par \uc2 ASHER BLITSTEIN: So, I could -- yes, I was Benjamin Franklin.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ASHER BLITSTEIN: So, I could easily, like, understand what it would be like to be there and having discussions on what America should be like.\par \par \uc2 AMY GLEASON: How many of you see that humanity again?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Discussions about America`s future and ideals written and signed in this enduring document.\par \par \uc2 And what`s the message there, do you think? What does that say to us that?\par \par \uc2 ASHER BLITSTEIN: We can accomplish things.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: We can accomplish things.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: We can make this world better. There`s things we can do to change it, if only one person would stand up and do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, if you want to see some of these historic documents and artifacts, but you can`t make it here to Washington, the National Archives is actually putting on a multi city road show to celebrate 250. This spring and summer, they are going to be taking some of those items out to cities across the country, maybe to a neighborhood near you -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, Jan, I love that story so much. I love what Amy Gleason said, because it really does make you understand that the U.S. Constitution is not a promise of perfection, it is just a framework for future generations to add to it, to understand it and to correct some of those mishaps as she stated.\par \par \uc2 Really great piece, Jan. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Welcome. Thanks, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, ahead, do you sometimes feel you need to be more confident and assertive? We`ll talk with a behavioral researcher who has some simple tips to help.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Trek: The Next Generation"/Paramount Television)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, man, if it was only that simple, Captain Picard.\par \par \uc2 Many of us struggle with feelings of self-doubt, hesitation, fear, but our next guest is here to help.\par \par \uc2 Shade Zahrai is a behavioral researcher and peak performance educator who`s coached Fortune 500 leaders. Her new book is called "Big Trust: Rewire Self-Doubt, Find Confidence and Fuel Success." Scan the Q.R. code on your screen right now to buy the book.\par \par \uc2 Welcome. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI (Behavioral researcher, peak performance educator, "Big Trust" author): Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, so this book was written for me because, seriously, as a child of immigrants, I was born here in this country, but, you know, we all first-generation Americans, I think, I`m generalizing here, but a lot of us suffer from impostor syndrome. We don`t feel as if we belong.\par \par \uc2 I sit here, I do this job with these wonderful colleagues, this wonderful team, and I wonder to myself, am I really meant to be here? Am I good enough to be sitting alongside of the people that I work with?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re wondering that, too. But Vlad, you know that -- you know nobody feels that way about you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you agree? Nobody feels that way about you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When you are in the quiet spaces of your mind, those are the things that you wrestle with. So, how do we overcome that?\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: So, the first thing is to acknowledge that it`s very, very common. We often think we`re the only ones with these kinds of insecurities, but that`s actually called pluralistic ignorance.\par \par \uc2 Many people, in fact, most people, have some kind of insecurity or self- doubt about where they`re from, where they belong, whether they`re worthy, whether they`re capable.\par \par \uc2 So, acknowledge it is never really going to go away, it just scales with responsibility. So, the key is not trying to eliminate the self-doubt. It is trying to strengthen something which is even more important, which is trust in yourself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You always say -- you`d say in the book, "... when channeled correctly, self-doubt can actually be useful, because self-doubt is not a one size fits all situation."\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nobody is 100 percent, I love this -- "... nobody is 100 percent secure in their self-worth."\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: And it`s comforting to know that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Even if you had a perfectly secure upbringing, as an adult, you might have internalized certain beliefs, certain labels about who you are and your place in the world. And so the acknowledgement that it`s okay, I`m not broken.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: And then the next step is recognizing, so I want to ask you this question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: What would you say is the opposite of self-doubt?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Confidence.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Confidence. So, 95 percent of people will say confidence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that -- 95 percent?\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Ninety-five percent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, give me another word. I don`t want to be predictable.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: I`ll give you another one. Well -- no, but this is great, because a lot of us think that we need to feel confident before we take action.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: So, we`re waiting to feel confident, but when we look at the literature, what we find is that that feeling of confidence actually comes after you take action.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to do the thing which builds capability and skill and self- efficacy, and then you feel confident.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: So, if this feeling we`re waiting for comes after, what comes first?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, that`s why you say, I love this, that you say "... prepare, don`t compare."\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody has compare-itis as you said.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Compare --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Prepare, don`t compare.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Prepare, don`t compare.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: And preparing is a sign of self-trust. So, what comes first before we take action is not the confidence. You don`t have to wait to feel confident. You have to trust yourself, which is what we call "Big Trust," this unshakable belief that I can handle this, I can figure it out. I am worthy, and even if it doesn`t work out, I will be okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I see why Fortune 500 companies --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are incredible.\par \par \uc2 You know, I see the words practice, and then practice schedule, and my eyes are drawn to it as a former athlete. You know, you talk about worrying. You say, create worry time zones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which is amazing to think about, and then you say, "... park those worries, dive into the worries, and then decide what`s next," and that you actually broke it down, saying, "... try to figure out if these worries are real or if you`re catastrophizing these things." And then go back and then look at the worries and say, did these worries actually turn into something real or catastrophic, like I thought it would.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because most of them don`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like what you said about the worry time zone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, why is that important?\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why is that important to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. What is that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- like lean into those worries?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: So, a lot of us think that we need to stop the worries, stop the overthinking, but we get stuck in them, and they constantly consume us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: You don`t actually have to eliminate them, because what you try to suppress just becomes louder --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bigger.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI:-- and takes over.\par \par \uc2 So, what you do is give it a time and a place, a stimulus-controlled environment. What does that mean? During the day, if a worry pops up, write it down and say, you know what, I will worry about you later.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHADE ZAHRAI: Then at the end of every day, schedule what we call worry time -- 10 minutes, 15 minutes, same time, same place, and allow yourself to worry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Shade Zahrai, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 This book is incredible. It`s called "Big Trust: Rewire Self-Doubt, Find Your Confidence and Fuel Success." It`s on sale right now. Scan the Q.R. code on your screen to buy it and get tips to learn how to achieve your potential. "Talk of the Table" coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or as Jared just said on jib, we`re going to talk about the table. What do you say about the table, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It looks beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. There you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is actually something that Vlad was going to do a little bit earlier. So, he passed me the ball on this one.\par \par \uc2 This is the drama over the college football transfer portal. So Duke University is suing -- that`s right -- suing its star quarterback, Darian Mensah after he tried to enter the portal.\par \par \uc2 So, Mensah signed a multi-year contract with the University last year, and Duke argues that he should not be allowed to transfer. Okay, it is a similar situation to the University of Washington and the quarterback that they have.\par \par \uc2 So, their star quarterback, Demond Williams Jr., from earlier this month, Williams, he agreed to a nearly $5 million contract with the Huskies. Then a few days later, he decided, you know what, I`m going to enter the transfer portal and get a $6 million offer somewhere else. He is now staying at Washington.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he did sign a deal though, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He did sign a deal.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How iron clad are those -- it`s a contract.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a contract, right, so let`s not get it confused. It`s not necessarily amateur sports as it used to be.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When you`re talking about signing guys to contracts, this is professional sports. But keep in mind, though, that this system was built off of the backs of these athletes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The reason these schools, they change conferences is because they want more money, bigger T.V. deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The way that they hire and fire and get rid of coaches, these coaches, they leave with their money. So, these athletes, for the first time, are now using their leverage, which we could not use before, and they`re looked at as selfish.\par \par \uc2 I`m not saying it`s right to break a contract if you sign one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you can`t label the athlete as selfish when that`s all these colleges have been for decades.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now Duke is playing hardball.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, they`re playing hardball, and I don`t mind it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Duke is playing --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you signed a contract --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Duke is playing hardball.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- listen, to the young guys, if you`re signing that contract --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- you will be held to that contract. But don`t feel bad if you`re exploring other opportunities.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, you`re not -- you`re saying it`s -- so it is looking for --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But maybe not sign.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe not sign it before --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table." -- I love this story, guys. It`s an amazing and unexpected story of love and can we say forgiveness between a woman and her former husband`s new wife in Fort Worth, Texas. What?\par \par \uc2 So, last year, Angela Maples found out she needed a kidney transplant.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was lucky enough to find a living donor who was a perfect match. Guess who it was? It turns out to be her husband`s ex-wife, Amanda McCowen, who didn`t think twice, volunteering to give her the life-saving organ.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what they told CBS News, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA MCCOWEN (Organ donor): Look at how far we`ve come in 10 years. Never, if you had asked me in 2015, it would have been -- it would have been an automatic no.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MAPLES (Organ recipient): Science didn`t put it in her heart to donate a kidney to me, especially after our tumultuous past. That`s the spirit of God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really is. Amanda said, look, when we first met, she said, I`m not going to like her. These are my kids. I`m not going to share them with anybody. But they`ve been co-parenting for over 10 years now, and they`ve actually become friends.\par \par \uc2 I just think this is an amazing, remarkable story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beautiful story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because when I first heard the ex, I thought she had given it to her ex-husband. I went, "Wow! That`s really nice." Then, I heard she gave it to the current wife. So you go, Amanda! You go, Angela! I love this.\par \par \uc2 She said -- Amanda says, "I joke that she is the tequila to my margarita." And so far so good. Knock on wood -- the transplant is going well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it`s an amazing story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beautiful story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Funny how time can heal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And also just brings out the humanity in people. You put all of those bygones aside.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you try to save somebody`s life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My "Talk of the Table" is an airline passenger named Violet --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- who flew 13 hours from Taiwan to Munich, Germany. She says it was frustrating to say the least, because take a look at the passenger sitting behind.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, no!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yep. What you`re seeing there is the passenger behind Violet resting her foot on Violet`s armrest. She says she asked the woman repeatedly, nicely, please do not do that, but she was ignored, and kept putting her foot there the entire flight.\par \par \uc2 Violet says she`s filed a complaint with the airline. I mean, I don`t know why we have to do this story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s just common courtesy, etiquette. Don`t put your foot on somebody else`s.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fact!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t clip your toenails. Don`t -- like there`s like all sorts of bad behavior you see in public, on planes, on trains.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does it matter she had her sock on?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No matter, like don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, I wouldn`t have liked it, but at least she had her socks.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I mean, if there was some toes, I probably would like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would have liked to do a little -- right on the toes. That`s flagrant, man!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is kind of flagrant. Especially after somebody says, please, can you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Once you`re asked, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it is my seat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Once you`re asked.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like it is my seat. It`s attached to my seat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, once you`re asked.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Treat each other kindly! Come on!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, actor and comedian, Jessica Williams is here. Ahead, we will find out about the highly anticipated third season of her hit show "Shrinking." Is she hitting the robot, right now?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Doing the robot!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. I see you.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Handsome machine.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But first -- with the homerun, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Homerun!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is amazing. I am excited for you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Our favorite T.V. therapists, we`re happy to say they are back for the third season of the hit series "Shrinking." You are watching!\par \par \uc2 The Emmy-nominated show follows a therapist, his name is Jimmy, that`s played by Jason Segel, as he navigates life after the loss of his wife with the help of fellow therapists, played by Jessica Williams and Harrison Ford.\par \par \uc2 So, Jimmy learns to heal and forgive. Here`s a preview of the upcoming Season 3 premiere.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that part, the hot best friend.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS ("Shrinking" actor): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hello! Hello, hot best friend.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Me!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jessica, congratulations. I love this show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome!\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was just going to watch the first couple of episodes to get an idea. I watched the whole things so I have to be very careful not to give anything away, especially with your character.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No spoilers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No spoilers. No spoilers, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she`ll come over and bitch slap me if I say something.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: I can`t. I would never raise my hand on you in my lifetime, my good dream of a B-slap to you. That`s an actual insane thing - -\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, yes, Jessica is like B-slap. But Gayle, full on --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: I would never -- I like -- it`s morning, Gayle. We can`t -- I`m never up this early.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We say that all the time.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: She said B-slap --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 But you know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Jessica, this is the thing. Your character has so much range. Number one, we got to see that there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Oh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I mean, she`s so blunt --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- she`s so vulnerable, she`s so kind. This is what I like about this ensemble. I think, God, I wish -- I wish I was friends with these guys.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Oh good!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you see therapists that are also having issues.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: That`s right. I also think -- I get that feedback a lot on the show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And I know a lot of the actors get that, too, where they`re like, oh , I wish I kind of could hang out with these people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And I think, you know, this is an ensemble comedy, and I think that`s why it works. It works when every character pops up and is your favorite character. And I feel like, especially in this season, we mix and match any of our, you know, two or three characters and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: -- we`ve just got a really, really funny and tender scene. We have incredible writers that really sort of are honed in on our dynamics.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The writing is so good --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes, it is sharp.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- sometimes, I think you must be adlibbing -- you must be improvising.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes, sometimes, but then a lot of it is just great writing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Like, you know, it`s really hard to make a T.V. show, especially one that`s a comedy and one that`s coming on for a third season. It`s really hard.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And for the first season, when they approached me to do the show, the gentleman who created it, Bill Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bill Lawrence, yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Brett Goldstein and Jason Segel, they were like, we don`t know that much about Gaby yet. We want to tailor her to the actress that plays her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh!\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And we want you to be able to shepherd your character and kind of take her over, but then we -- but we do know that we want her to be sort of a light in this office for these two men that are both separately grieving something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: So Jason Segel`s character, Jimmy is grieving the loss of his wife, and Harrison Ford`s character, Paul is grieving his Parkinson`s diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And he is grieving his body and all sorts of things that come with that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But Gaby got some issues, too.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: So -- she does, because she is an imperfect person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And that was one of the reasons why I was really excited about her, is because she`s got mad issues.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: My girl -- my girl has got some stuff going on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So then --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, then, how does your, in shaping Gaby, how does your own mental health play into how you shape this character over the course of the three seasons?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: A lot. I think for me, the hardest thing for me is, you know, people of color actors, we just need opportunity, and it`s all there. And I knew I wanted to be an actor for a long time, since I was 12, but for me, it`s like I`m channeling this character. She`s coming through me.\par \par \uc2 And so she`s my lens. It`s not my job to judge her. It`s not my job to sort of -- I don`t need to track her story too specifically, because Hollywood works when we`re all depending on each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: We`re a group of cooperating unions, labor unions, working to believe in this one idea, to make it happen. And so, I depend on the editors. I depend on our amazing directors, our writers, our crew is incredible, and I trust them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: There are so many talented actors and actresses who, especially people of color, whose stuff doesn`t see the light of day because it`ll get killed in the edit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Or the director doesn`t understand their sense of humor, or it`s too quirky or it`s weird. And for me, I`m really blessed and lucky because I`m -- I just happen to be on a particular show that`s hitting, you know, the culture in some way, where the directors and editors and creators --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: -- of the show are letting my performance come to the screen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you deliver.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: The studio, too. Even Apple T.V.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you deliver.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You deliver!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Oh, I appreciate you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh no!\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Real quick before we go, Michael J. Fox and Harrison Ford.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes, and Jeff Daniels.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Candice Bergen. Candice Bergen.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Candice Bergen. We`ve got some amazing guest stars.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is it like working with those individuals that are just so talented?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: I mean, especially with Michael J. Fox, I think Bill Lawrence knows a couple of people that have Parkinson`s, and so it`s sort of a full circle moment for him, having worked with him on "Spin City" for such a long time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Those were days where I kind of stayed after I was done working for the day and just watch --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: -- just to witness, you know, these two incredible actors work, and he`s incredible. Michael J. Fox is amazing on the show.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He really is.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: He is so funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is an amazing man!\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And sharp and just -- he just knows what he`s doing. He`s a legend. And so, I`m just so blessed that in my career, I`m able to witness that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: And every day is a learning experience.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And they are blessed to work with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And look at the people -- I was going to --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Oh! Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, I was going to say that, too.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: That`s really nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Seriously.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Okay, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And your friendship with Harrison Ford like off screen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On and off, we love.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: He is nice and crazy. I love him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jessica, you`re the best. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes. Thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love her clothes, too.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s like a Christopher John Rogers right there.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes. It is and you know it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want -- I want that dress.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jessica, congratulations. Congratulations. Season 3 of "Shrinking" kicks off with an hour-long premier this Wednesday on Apple T.V.\par \par \uc2 Oscar nominations, you look like an Oscar yourself this morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: I did that on purpose. I did it on purpose.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, well in fact --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: Yes, and thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- we are teasing the Oscar nominees. We`re going to be revealing them right here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Fandango and Rotten Tomatoes` correspondent --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh! Look at that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- Erik Davis is following it all for us, as always, he has some thoughts. We do, too, don`t we, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, we do!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t we, Jessica? Don`t we, Nate?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WILLIAMS: I can --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all got thoughts!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We got thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The nominations for the 98th Academy Awards are being announced right now as we speak.\par \par \uc2 With us now is Fandango and Rotten Tomatoes` correspondent Erik Davis to break it all down for us.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What we have so far, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are still coming in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning. All right, so they are still coming in right now, but we can share -- the first category we`re going to share Actress in a Supporting Role: Teyana Taylor, "One Battle After Another."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Amy Madigan, "Weapons."\par \par \uc2 Inga Ibsdotter Lilleaas, "Sentimental Value."\par \par \uc2 Wunmi Mosaku, "Sinners."\par \par \