 \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Come on, Delroy Lindo --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Delroy Lindo.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Delroy Lindo --\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Seventy-three years old!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: First ever nomination.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: First ever!\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Elroy Lindo.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I can`t believe he wasn`t nominated for "Malcolm X" back then.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, he was great in "Malcolm X."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Great! I mean, and that`s a surprise, he has not been in this category.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: So Delroy getting in there --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He was great in "Sinners."\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: "Sinners" I think, was on the verge when we sat down, of being the most nominated film in the history of the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sinners"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: History?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes, I believe it has reached that. Delroy getting in there, but it`s -- yes, fantastic for it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Miles Caton. Yes, Michael B. Jordan, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so let`s go now to Actor in a Leading Role.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, here we go. This is a big one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is the big one.\par \par \uc2 Timothee Chalamet, "Marty Supreme.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Leonardo DiCaprio, "One Battle After Another."\par \par \uc2 Michael B. Jordan, "Sinners."\par \par \uc2 Wagner Moura, "The Secret Agent."\par \par \uc2 And Ethan Hawke --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ethan Hawk?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Blue Moon."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was excellent in that. He was like non-stop talking in that movie.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: He was great!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he was.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who do you like, Erik?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes. I mean, I think it`s between Timothee Chalamet and Leonardo DiCaprio, right?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Marty Supreme"/A24)\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: You have this old classic --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not Michael B. Jordan?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Michael B. Jordan could be in there. I mean, "Sinners" is cleaning the house today --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: -- which is fantastic. Som, Michael B. could be in there, too.\par \par \uc2 Timothy Chalamet has been winning this award. He`s on a hot streak.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He has been.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He has been.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: You know, this is his third nomination for an Oscar.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sinners"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: So, this could be his year. Leo, I think, is also in there. Michael, this is his first ever nomination as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "One Battle After Another"/Warner Bros)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he just won in the Golden Globe category.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He did. All right, let`s go to Actress in a Leading Role.\par \par \uc2 Jessie Buckley, "Hamnet."\par \par \uc2 Renate Reinsve in "Sentimental Value."\par \par \uc2 Emma Stone, "Bugonia."\par \par \uc2 Rose Byrne, "If I Had Legs I`d Kick You." Love that movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She won, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Kate Hudson, "Song Sung Blue."\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes, I`d love to see Kate Hudson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would, too. I love that movie, "Song Sung Blue."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you love that movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that movie. I`m so glad she is nominated.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is a tough category.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People keep talking about Jessie Buckley, though, her performance in "Hamnet."\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes, her performance is just groundbreaking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It would make me happy --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I feel like Jessie, she has the edge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- that Kate Hudson --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hamnet"/Focus Features)\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: She has been winning this award in other awards, the Golden Globes, Critics` Choice. So yes, I think this is Jessie Buckley`s award to lose, but she`s fantastic in the film.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: I mean, one of the most powerful finales from a film that I`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Agree!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here`s the big one, Best Picture. Here they are,\par \par \uc2 "One Battle After Another."\par \par \uc2 "Sinners."\par \par \uc2 "Hamnet."\par \par \uc2 "Sentimental Value."\par \par \uc2 "Marty Supreme."\par \par \uc2 "Frankenstein."\par \par \uc2 "Train Dreams."\par \par \uc2 "The Secret Agent."\par \par \uc2 "Bugonia."\par \par \uc2 And "F1."\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "F1" for Best Picture?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. Yes. Wow!\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: You know what? I just put "F1" in there last night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: I did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did you really?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: You know what, it is such a fan favorite.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: And it`s such an entertaining movie. I`m glad to see that get in the Best Picture race.\par \par \uc2 "One Battle After Another," "Sinners." I think "Sinners" is the most nominated film in an Oscar history.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In Oscar history.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: This morning, Oscar history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Erik, would you make a pick in that category? Too tough?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What would you pick?\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: I think "One Battle After Another" will win. I think "Sinners" could take that casting category.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "One Battle After Another"/Warner Bros)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Erik Davis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yep! It`s just in, "Sinners" most nominations of all time.\par \par \uc2 ERIK DAVIS: Let`s go!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How many? Does it say how many? How many nominations so far?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, that does it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sixteen? Fifteen nominations?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fifteen!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXVI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020260122em1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for December 26, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Tom Hanson, Bill Kelly, Andres Gutierrez, Willie  James Inman, Kati Weis, Ash-Har Quraishi, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,781 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 December 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Christmas is over and now comes the white part. A snowstorm targets the northeast just as millions of holiday travelers are trying to get home.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight with the winter cold comes the winter flu. Cases are growing coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 1: Return fee.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGH)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Many unhappy returns. Major retailers are now charging fees if you ship back those unwanted holiday gifts.\par \par \uc2 And Steve Hartman with Samuel. Other kids flock around him to check out his special talents.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Do you ever think you might be a bird?\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (BIRD SOUND)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From the CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening everyone. I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Americans in record numbers are traveling for the holidays. Now with Christmas come and gone, many are heading home. On both coasts, weather is making that difficult. With hundreds of flights canceled, thousands more delayed.\par \par \uc2 A storm is bringing snow and ice from the Great Lakes to the northeast tonight here in New York City. As much as 10 inches of snow is possible. More than 40 million Americans are right now under winter storm warnings or weather advisories.\par \par \uc2 We have correspondence on both coasts and the latest forecast. We begin here in New York City with Tom Hanson. Tom?\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Major, good evening to you. Well, as you can see behind me, the snow is just dusting the ground, but the New Jersey governor has already declared a state of emergency. That`s because of the dangerous conditions this storm has created on the roads and in the skies just after the Christmas holiday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): Snow, ice, and freezing rain. That`s the triple threat bearing down right now in parts of the north. In western New York, wind-driven sleet and snow piled up on the roads and at the airport, but the rough weather didn`t stop this Delta jet from taking off safely.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, slippery roads caused several semi-trucks to jackknife. Traffic backed up for miles after parts of Interstate 80 closed down. This is what it looked like in Michigan, where freezing rain snapped tree branches and downed power lines.\par \par \uc2 More than 47,000 customers lost power according to poweroutage.com. As the storm barrels east, snowplow drivers are ready all across the region.\par \par \uc2 JOSH MORGAN (\uc2 Connecticut Department of Transportation\uc2 ): It`s going to be a statewide event, but this is what we`re built for. Hey, we`re the \uc2 Connecticut Department of Transportation\uc2 . We`re New Englanders. We know how to clear some snow.\par \par \uc2 Here in New York City, a blast of subzero weather is just the beginning. Up to nine inches of snow is expected to fall overnight. It could be the biggest snowfall in about four years.\par \par \uc2 HILA KREMER (New York City Resident): I have gloves, I have a coat, I have three layers under here, and then three layers for pants and things like that.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): Post-holiday travelers were urged to stay off the roads.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: Today is too much busy.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): At the airports, long lines and more than 7,000 flight delays and cancellations nationwide greeted people trying to return home after the Christmas holiday. More than 122 million people traveled this holiday season, according to AAA.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD KIRKPATRICK (Miami Traveler): We got here hours early, actually, so we`ve got some time to play around with.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And the snowfall is only expected to intensify from here on out. We are expecting one to two inches every hour throughout the evening, Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: A three-layer storm indeed. Tom Hanson, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 For the latest forecasts, we turn to Bill Kelly, Chief Meteorologist at CBS News Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Bill?\par \par \uc2 BILL KELLY: Major -- yeah, Major, good evening to you. Good evening, everybody.\par \par \uc2 We have three distinct weather features going on around the country. Let`s start with what Tom was just talking about. Over 50 million people under this winter weather alert, whether it`s snow and ice down near the Philadelphia area or just snow for New York and areas to the north, we`re talking anywhere between five to nine inches of snow in the heaviest part.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about number two. Look at these temperatures in the nation`s midsection. I want to show you, for example, Oklahoma City, 80 degrees forecast for your day tomorrow. That is 31 degrees above normal.\par \par \uc2 Only one other time in recorded history has Oklahoma City had a temperature of 80 degrees past Christmas, and all of these temperatures are going to continue. We go then to the third part of the story. We`ve been talking about this all week long, the record-setting rain out west into California.\par \par \uc2 Still, flood watches posted up and down, but fortunately, it is just one more wet night. Drier weather is ahead as we go into the week here, and that is so needed because the problems that have happened here have been pretty incredible. We`re going to watch that. Just be safe in the northeast if you are traveling.\par \par \uc2 Major?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Three, the thematic numbers so far early in this broadcast. Bill Kelly, thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 In the west, 30 million people in Southern California are under a flood watch. Andres Gutierrez is in Altadena.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: Tonight, the soggy California landscape is preparing for even more rain. Los Angeles, which started the year with the costliest fires in U.S. history, is ending it with the most rain the city has ever seen over Christmas. The atmospheric river that washed through Southern California this week downed trees and left thousands without power during family dinner.\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: Well, it was kind of romantic, having Christmas by candlelight. You know, had to eat the cold stuff, but what the heck?\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ (voiceover): The rain has slowed, but the rescues have not, including this one on a Ventura County freeway of a man stuck near a rising river.\par \par \uc2 San Bernardino County was hardest hit by the deluge, leaving homes buried in mud. Residents are now digging out, including Timothy Needham.\par \par \uc2 TIM NEEDHAM (San Bernardino, California, Resident): This trench is the big damage that`s going to keep myself and my neighbors from being able to live normal lives.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: We`ve followed him since he captured video of his neighbor`s rooftop rescue on Christmas Eve. But two days later, he still has limited access to basic utilities.\par \par \uc2 TIM NEEDHAM: It seems like most people either have water or have power. The water company in the area actually came by and walked over a case of water to us and to my neighbors.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ (voiceover): All this rain has kept California out of the drought conditions that plagued the state last year. Some much-needed cheer after a gloomy week.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: And it doesn`t seem like it right now, but Southern California is about to catch a break drying out this weekend with more rain possibly returning by New Year`s Eve.\par \par \uc2 Major?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Telling us the wet and muddy story all week in Southern California. Andres Gutierrez, thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 Details are emerging about the Christmas Day military strikes in northern Nigeria. The United States launched what President Trump has called a, quote, "powerful and deadly," unquote, strike on terrorists in the West African nation. The president says Christians there are being targeted.\par \par \uc2 Willie James Inman has more from the president`s Winter White House in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): The U.S. military fired more than a dozen Tomahawk cruise missiles at \uc2 ISIS\uc2  targets in Nigeria. And the timing was deliberate, President Trump told a radio interviewer.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (U.S. President): I said hit them on Christmas Day. It`ll be a Christmas present. We hit ISIS who are terrible. They`re butchers.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): People could be seen carrying debris away from one target site. The Pentagon`s initial assessment indicated multiple \uc2 ISIS\uc2  militants were killed. Trump warned last month the U.S. would go into Nigeria with guns a blazing if the government failed to protect Christians from \uc2 ISIS\uc2  attacks.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : They`re killing the Christians and killing them in very large numbers. We`re not going to allow that to happen.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): But Nigeria denied those accusations.\par \par \uc2 So, far this year, nearly 12,000 people have been killed. CBS News National Security Contributor Sam Vinograd.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Violence by insurgent groups, criminals and others in Nigeria doesn`t just target Christians, it also targets Muslims. And where and when these strikes occur will certainly be scrutinized by analysts to see who exactly is hit and whether the violence stops.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): Nigeria`s foreign minister said his government provided the U.S. military with intelligence before the strikes.\par \par \uc2 YUSUF TUGGAR (Nigeria Foreign Affairs): We have been working closely with the Americans, with other countries to combat terrorism, to stop the death of innocent Nigerians.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): Both the U.S. and Nigeria have signaled more strikes could follow. Republican lawmakers today backed the president`s action.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): The strikes that were delivered last night show the -- the commitment that he has to protecting people from \uc2 ISIS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): While Democrats question his authority.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): What are his plans? I believe under the War Powers Act he needs to be coming to Congress.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Meanwhile Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  today said he expects to travel to Florida to meet with President Trump this Sunday to discuss ending the war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 Major?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Willie James Inman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This has been a tough flu season. The latest CDC report tells us there have been close to five million cases and nearly 2,000 deaths including at least three children. Now if you skipped your flu shot this season, Kati Weis reports, doctors have a message for you.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voiceover): It has become a refrain we`ve heard all fall. Get your flu shot. This winter doctors are still saying it as the number of flu cases is up coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 DR. NED CALONGE (Colorado Chief Medical Officer): My hope is that we`re actually getting closer to the peak now.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voiceover): The CDC reports that flu levels are the highest in New York, Colorado and Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ALMANTE (Pharmacist): It`s just chaos all around.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voiceover): At this Walgreens in New York City pharmacist Ashley Almante got her flu shot.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ALMANTE: I`ve seen so many people get it that`s actually scaring me so I just want to make sure I was safe.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voiceover): Urgent care centers and hospitals in the city have faced a spike of flu patients even earlier than usual in the season. Some schools in Georgia and Kansas had so many students sick with the flu they had to close, prompting warnings from health officials concerned about spread from holiday travel.\par \par \uc2 DR. NED CALONGE: We recognize you want to be together but if you`re ill the best way to kind of keep your family safe is to say maybe I shouldn`t travel.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voiceover): Anne Falkenhagen flew out of the New Orleans airport for a holiday vacation last week.\par \par \uc2 ANNE FALKENHAGEN (Sick with the Flu): Suddenly I start having this little scratch in my throat so from being well to being very unwell within an hour.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voiceover): She came back with the flu.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: Tell me what that was like for you.\par \par \uc2 ANNE FALKENHAGEN: You just start aching, your bone, your skin hurt, the headache is 24/7. I don`t wish this on anybody.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: And here in New Orleans the number of cases of people being hospitalized with the flu has more than doubled in less than a month and doctors here tell me they`re preparing for that number to keep rising.\par \par \uc2 Major?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We learn this every year, Kati, flu is serious business. Kati Weis, thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 It`s not only the flu season, it`s the cold season, winter and the cost of heating a home, well it`s going through the metaphorical roof but there are steps you can take to try to keep your energy bills under some measure of control.\par \par \uc2 Here is Ash-Har Quraishi.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI (voiceover): Bundle up, this winter has already been a cold one and more frigid air is on the way.\par \par \uc2 HILA KREMER: I`m freezing, my nose hurts.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI (voiceover): Heating bills are already climbing due to higher demand and delivery costs and surging power usage. Experts predict a more than nine percent jump from last year costing homeowners with electric heat more than $1200 for the season and natural gas homeowners around $700.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN CASTRONOVA (Building Consultant): In this home in particular we can actually see there`s holes here and you can feel the cold air blowing through these gaps.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI (voiceover): Building Consultant Justin Castronova says homeowners can make fixes to lower energy costs.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN CASTRONOVA: Maintaining your weather stripping, caulking around the inside and outside with the appropriate caulk because there is different kinds and then around the outlets you could spray foam around it after turning off the power of course and then they do make gasketed covers with a weather seal to minimize the drafts.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI (voiceover): Another cost-saving upgrade, newer appliances.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN CASTRONOVA: And this unit is energy rated.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI (voiceover): It`s an upfront cost that saves you long term.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN CASTRONOVA: Anywhere from ten to fifty dollars a month depending on the size of the appliance and how many you have.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI (voiceover): And in older homes to help bring bills down just look up.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN CASTRONOVA: In this attic in particular there`s not anywhere near enough modern insulation. You have about one to two inches and normally I would want to see one to two feet.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI (voiceover): Some utility companies will give you a free home energy audit or even incentives to help you make energy saving upgrades.\par \par \uc2 Ash-Har Quraishi, CBS News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, two North Carolina police officers shot in the line of duty.\par \par \uc2 Also unhappy returns: Consumers who send back items through the mail are getting hit with fees.\par \par \uc2 And up in the sky, yeah it`s a bird, but down on the ground Steve Hartman tells us it is probably Samuel.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It`s a holiday tradition we sort of love. Shoppers returning to the stores on this day after Christmas to return gifts that well just weren`t quite what they wanted. For those shipping their gifts back however, Dave Malkoff reports they could be in for a surprise. It`s called a return fee.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 2: And now you`re telling me that you`re going to charge me $11.99 or whatever it is to return items. Are you out of your mind?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): Hassle-free online returns may be a thing of the past. Most major retailers are now charging customers to return items even if they`re unopened and in perfect condition.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 3: Who wants to lose money especially not in -- in this time and in this economy?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: $850 billion, that`s how much Americans will return to the stores this year. Nearly 20 percent of the stuff we buy online just ends up in the return pile.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 1: Return fee.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): Macy`s now charges $9.99 for mail-in returns. TJ Maxx and Marshalls charge $11.99. Other stores including \uc2 J.Crew\uc2 , \uc2 Abercrombie & Fitch\uc2 , \uc2 H&M\uc2  and Zara all now charge fees between four and eight bucks. It can now cost as much as $45 to return certain electronics at \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 . \uc2 Amazon\uc2  too has tightened its policy charging some customers unless they use its box-free in-person drop-off option.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SOBIE (Happy Returns Co-founder and CEO): Merchants now are under a tremendous amount of cost pressure. They`re really trying to offset some of the costs that they face in returns by asking shoppers to share some of the burden.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): David Sobie co-founded his company Happy Returns that uses these A.I. robots to help make returns easier.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Is there any way to avoid these fees?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SOBIE: Well the best way to avoid them is just to read the retailer`s return policy before you check out in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Okay here is the good news. If you physically hop in your car and come down here to the mall major most in-store returns are still free.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Still free that`s the key phrase. Dave Malkoff coming to us from Atlanta. Thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next the countdown to 2026 is on. We will show you how New York City is getting ready to ring in the New Year.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Two North Carolina police officers are in the hospital tonight. They were shot in the town of Mint Hill near Charlotte. Officials say the officers were responding to a call regarding a child custody exchange at a gift shop. It was there that a man opened fire. The officers returned fire and killed the gunman. Across the United States, more than 300 officers have been shot in the line of duty this year. More than 40 have died. That number is down slightly from last year.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two months after the fiery crash of a \uc2 UPS\uc2  cargo plane in Louisville, Kentucky, another person has died. A Cuban immigrant who worked at a scrap yard near the airport. The plane lost an engine and crashed shortly after takeoff, killing now 15 people.\par \par \uc2 In Times Square here in the heart of New York City, it is beginning to look a lot like New Year`s Eve. The seven-foot-tall numbers 2026 were illuminated today in a test run for the ball drop next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman is next with "On the Road."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Finally tonight, just as the swallows return to Capistrano, we return to a boy who can imitate a swallow and just about any other bird you can imagine. Steve Hartman met him, you guessed it, "On the Road."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (BIRD SOUND)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): That is not a blue jay you`re hearing. Not a robin, nor a tufted titmouse. Those sounds are all coming from a Samuel. An 11-year-old Samuel Henderson.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: I actually quite enjoy this.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): As we first reported last spring, Samuel, who is autistic, can perfectly imitate 50 birds.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: What`s the hardest bird to do?\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: Probably the barn swallow.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: How`s that go?\par \par \uc2 (BIRD SOUND)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Do you ever think you might be a bird?\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: Yeah. Some people say that I swallowed birds.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But you haven`t swallowed any birds?\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: No. Well, chickens.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): The kid is a born entertainer.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: Because we eat chicken.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yeah, I know. I got it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): But since he mostly practiced his calls on the playground, alone along the back fence, a lot of kids at his Choctaw, Oklahoma, school hadn`t ever heard. Until the annual Nicoma Park Intermediate School Talent Show.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Samuel really wanted to compete, but no kid from special ed had ever participated. And this would be mostly singing and dancing and band instruments. So, bird sounds? His mother was nervous.\par \par \uc2 LORI HENDERSON (Samuel Henderson`s Mother): Some kids can be mean. And --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And the thought of that happening to him on stage in front of the whole school?\par \par \uc2 LORI HENDERSON: Yes. It`d just break my heart if that were to have happened.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But Lori says his teacher convinced her that Samuel needed to spread his wings, so to speak. So, he took to the stage. At first, the other kids didn`t know what to think.\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: I was like, what is this?\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: This is a bald eagle.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): But as he continued and laid his passion bare, jaws dropped. The crowd came alive and Samuel was not only accepted, he was admired.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 4: Brave.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 5: He`s really brave. It takes like a lot of courage to get yourself up there.\par \par \uc2 MAN 3: He aced it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN 6: I don`t think I saw one single kid like not clapping their hands.\par \par \uc2 LORI HENDERSON: I was so happy to see him living his dreams. My heart went just exploded.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): It`s now been eight months since we first told this story, which went viral, leading to a slew of public appearances. His town held a Samuel Henderson Day, and \uc2 Bass Pro Shops\uc2  just named him its Junior Conservation Partner of the Year. But most importantly, guess who now rocks the playground?\par \par \uc2 Samuel, that`s who.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL HENDERSON: Everyone here can do a peacock. It goes like this.\par \par \uc2 (PEACOCK SOUND)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Now, that`s something to crow about.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman "On the Road" in Choctaw, Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Talent that shows us all what it means to soar.\par \par \uc2 That is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for tonight. Jericka Duncan will be here tomorrow with the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Major Garrett in New York. Thank you for watching. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020251227elcq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For December 21, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Anthony Salvanto \par \uc2 8,608 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington. And this week on "FACE THE NATION," the long-awaited release of the Epstein files. And will that good news about inflation improve America`s views about President Trump`s handling of the economy?\par \par \uc2 President Trump is wrapping up the year with a tighter focus on the economy and affordability, both in the Oval Office and on the midterm`s campaign trail, boasting of his 2025 accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): This is a far better term than it would have been had I done it the most traditional way. It really is. It`s more powerful term. It`s a more powerful term. And the biggest news of all is yesterday it was announced that inflation is far lower than anybody expected. I told you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s an exaggerated claim, but any sign of prices going lower is good news for Americans. Our new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll shows that half the country says Trump`s policies are making them financially worse off. We`ll ask White House Chief Economic Advisor Kevin Hassett about that and more.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a bipartisan conversation with the two members of Congress who pushed to pass the law calling for the Epstein files to be made public. Has the Trump administration obeyed that law? We`ll talk about Kentucky Republican Tom Massie and California Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finally, we`ll talk with the head of \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 , \uc2 Catherine Russell\uc2 , about the children of Sudan and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on "FACE THE NATION."\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to "FACE THE NATION."\par \par \uc2 So far there have been few surprises in the tens of thousands of documents related to the case against convicted sex offender and accused sex trafficker \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  and his partner, convicted child sex offender Ghislaine Maxwell. The Justice Department says that 1,200 names identified as victims or their relatives were redacted, along with materials that could result in their identification. Will the release of the documents satisfy Congress and the political forces who lobbied for their disclosure and, much more importantly, will it satisfy the survivors of \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  and their families?\par \par \uc2 The two House members who pushed their colleagues into passing the bill to release these documents, Republican \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2 , who`s in Garrison, Kentucky, and Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , who joins us this morning from \uc2 Palo Alto\uc2 , California.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to have you both of you here.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with you, Congressman Massie, because in these past 48 hours we have seen the Justice Department acknowledge it`s an incomplete release, but they have put out more than 13,000 files. They say more will come.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t everything you asked for just yet, but would you acknowledge that they are supplying with the spirit, if not the intent of your law?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE THOMAS MASSIE (R-KY): No, they`re flouting the spirit and the letter of the law. It`s very troubling, the posture that they`ve taken. And I won`t be satisfied until the survivors are satisfied.\par \par \uc2 You know, I said in the hours leading up to this release that we will know if they are complying if they implicate any of the other criminals that are involved in the -- suspects that are involved. The witnesses, the victims themselves have given to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and they`ve never been mentioned.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, there are some disclosures in here that seem new, Congressman Khanna. The documents confirm that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  received a complaint about \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  back in 1996, but the woman whose name is Maria Farmer, who made the complaint, said federal officials failed to follow up. She said the disclosure makes her feel redeemed and that this was one of the best days of her life, to see these documents made public. What action do you want taken around a case like this?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE RO KHANNA (D-CA): That was the single most important disclosure of what we got. I know Maria`s sister, she sounded the alarm in 1996. She complained to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . The problem is that Epstein was so connected with law enforcement and powerful politicians, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did nothing for a decade. Frankly, she deserves compensation. She deserves an explanation.\par \par \uc2 But the broader point is, Blanche was on this morning saying that Massie and I have a problem that it`s taking too long. That`s not the problem. The problem is, this was a slap in the face of survivors. What do they want? They want to know, who are the rich and powerful men who visited Epstein`s rape island and covered up the abuse. And the key documents that our law basically said needed to be released, the 60-count indictment that actually implicates a lot of these people, and the prosecution memo were not released. It`s not about the timeline, it`s about the selective concealment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, yet, right, the deputy attorney general said there are more disclosures that will be made. You believe that they will not release that particular part? I mean you did give them some leeway for redactions.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, first of all, the redactions were excessive. And even Harvard Law professors who have looked at that have said you can`t redact internal communications, you can`t redact workforce product. And the courts are going to find that these were excessive.\par \par \uc2 But my point, and I`m sure Congressman Massie can elaborate, is that we want the files out. So, if our sounding the alarm on what is missing is going to help them get those files out, fine. But this shouldn`t be a big document dump. There`s a pretty simple point, who raped these young girls, who covered it up and why are they getting away with it?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you are saying specific things regarding the indictment Congressman Massie, I saw in a video that you posted, you had a very specific benchmark of success here. You said you know there are at least 20 men accused of sex crimes known to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . How do you know that number? And do you know those names?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 THOMAS MASSIE\uc2 : Yes, the survivors lawyers have told me those numbers and they`ve described their professions in general. But they`ve only given me one of those name. And I mentioned that in a congressional hearing, Jeff Staley (ph). So, I searched these documents. I didn`t see Jeff Staley`s name. Nor did I see 19 other names.\par \par \uc2 And here`s why we shouldn`t be optimistic that Blanche or Bondi are going to release these thing. They sent a six-page memo to Congress and said that -- they cited pre-existing laws as reasons that they weren`t going to follow our law. For instance, our law requires them to release information regardless of embarrassment. But they are trying to say that a previous law prevails when it doesn`t. Common sense says it doesn`t. The privacy act doesn`t protect them from that. And then also, as Ro said, internal communication. Those -- they`re using a FOIA standard that doesn`t apply because our law already says they have to give us internal communications.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. In terms of the gentleman there, you referenced as accused, I mean, being in the document doesn`t necessarily prove criminal behavior, right? And in terms of the redactions, what the deputy attorney general said is that they were abiding by your law. And also a judge in New York had ordered them to listen to victims. So, they`re claiming that information that they had initially even published Friday, they had to pull down because they were trying to protect victims. You aren`t -- you don`t sound convinced that they are abiding by, you know, this intent.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE THOMAS MASSIE: No, actually -- no, they are not abiding. And all three judges who released the grand jury materials said that our law trumps the prior law. That`s just common sense. Law school 101. Yet, Todd Blanche is using a different theory that wouldn`t survive first contact with any court, which is, he`s trying to say that prior laws override our law. That`s not the way this works.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. What are you going to do about it to force them to comply? I mean can you do anything?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 THOMAS MASSIE\uc2 : Oh, absolutely. Look, people have talked about -- and by the way, Todd Blanche is the face of this. But it`s really the attorney general`s office, Pam Bondi, who is responsible. And there are several ways to get at this. Some take longer. Some are shorter.\par \par \uc2 The quickest way, and I think most expeditious way to get justice for these victims is to bring inherent contempt against Pam Bondi. And that doesn`t require going through the courts. And give her -- you know, and basically \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  and I are talking about and drafting that right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Well, I mean, on another network this morning Democratic Senator Tim Kaine said impeachment or contempt, it is premature, Congressman Khanna. And the depth attorney general said bring it on. They don`t seem to be taking it very seriously. And if you just don`t have the math in the Senate, including at least now this Democratic senator not being convinced, doesn`t that sort of show that you`re at the limit of pressure?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : No, we only need the House for inherent contempt. And we`re building a bipartisan coalition. And it would fine Pam Bondi for every day that she`s not releasing these documents.\par \par \uc2 I`ll tell you why -- I`ve talked to the survivors, why this is such a slap in the face. One of the survivors said they released her name accidentally but they still have not released the \uc2 FBI\uc2  file about the people who abused her at her request. And the problem here is that there are rich and powerful people -- we all know this, there are 1,200 victims, they`re rich and powerful people who either engaged in this abuse, covered it up or were on this island. And what the American people want to know is, who are these people. And instead of holding them accountable, Pam Bondi is breaking the law. And this is the corrupt system, the Epstein class that people are sick of.\par \par \uc2 So, I believe we`re going to get bipartisan support in holding her accountable and a committee of Congress should determine whether these redactions are justified or not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s sometime in January when you all come back, I assume, that that will happen.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you, though, Congressman Khanna, there have been images released in the past 48 hours, some of them include former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . There`s at least one image of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I should say neither man has been accused of any wrongdoing related to Epstein, though they clearly knew him.\par \par \uc2 But since Friday`s publication -- we`re showing the image here now -- one of the pictures was pulled down. There were 15 images total that were then redacted from the DOJ website. And it`s kind of hard to see, but it`s an open drawer with a photo of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think that was pulled down? Because the deputy attorney general says this was victims saying that their privacy or somehow their integrity was being compromised by having that image published.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, I`m less concerned about that photo. I don`t know the facts. My concern is the major documents that we know are out there that haven`t been released. The 60 count indictment that shows massive financial fraud and foreign interference. The prosecution memo. And look, they put out these \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  pictures --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Foreign interference?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, there are allegations that there are foreign governments that potentially were involved. And that all needs to come out. And it needs to come out in the indictments. It needs to come out in the prosecution memos.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, they put out these pictures of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Fine, put it all out, but don`t distract from the fundamental issue, which is, who are these powerful people who visited rape -- rape island? I mean there are 1,200 victims. It defies common sense that it was just Epstein and Maxwell. And they`re hiding something. And you know why the American people are so upset. My X account had MAGA supporters supporting me for the first time, saying, you know, Pam Bondi whiffed, according to Susan Wiles.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : She is breaking the law. She needs to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re referring to the "Vanity Fair" quote that the chief of staff gave, saying, the "whiffed." And she said that Pam Bondi gave them "binders full of nothingness." And then when it came to the client list she said it was on her desk, "there is no client list," quote, "and it sure as hell wasn`t on her desk."\par \par \uc2 Do you see that as an acknowledgment here though that the politics have overtaken the substance?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : No. For us it`s about the survivors. And if you look at \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  and my comments the morning of the release, we were giving Todd Blanche the benefit of the doubt. We said if it`s not all there, fine, just get the information out. It`s when the survivors said that this is an insult, this is a slap in their face, that we aren`t getting -- the truth about who these men were on the -- on rape island, that we started sounding the alarm. And this is more documents of nothingness.\par \par \uc2 And I guess the question the American people have, even to me, is, is the system so corrupt, is the system so corrupt that \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  and you defy the odds, pass a bill through a discharge petition, get the Senate, get the president to sign it, and still these rich and powerful people are being protected. Who has this kind of hold on our government? What are they hiding? Why are they not releasing this?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you want, what, people involved in those initial investigations down in Florida reexamined and possibly prosecuted?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : I want the information out which was in that prosecution memo that Acosta disregarded, that were in those indictments. There were 60 counts against Epstein. Only two counts were prosecuted. I want to know, who were these other people who were on the Epstein`s rape island, who were the people who saw young girls being paraded around at parties, 13, 14, 15-year-olds and didn`t say anything, and why is it so hard when there`s a law requiring these names to come out. What we`re seeing is survivors` names come out but not the men who abuse them. And instead of -- instead of holding elites accountable, Pam Bondi is break the law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we will see when you gentlemen come back to work sometime in January what your next measures are. It sounds like you`ve made it clear you`re moving action against the attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for your reaction today.\par \par \uc2 "FACE THE NATION" will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the economy and our executive director of elections and surveys, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the president was speaking throughout the past week about the economy, even in this Oval Office address. He`s trying to reframe public perception of what`s going on. We know sentiment has been low for years now. Do people really expect that a president can fix everything in the first year?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY SALVANTO: So, let me start with that big context because it`s so important. Yes, through 2025 people have said they thought the economy was bad. When you talk about going back years, this goes back to before the pandemic was the last time that a majority of Americans said the economy was good. So, we are in this long and a lot of people think structural change that the economy has gone through. Things got more expensive coming out of the pandemic and people still are trying to adjust to those higher costs. That`s what underpins all those sentiments.\par \par \uc2 Now, the political part of this is coming into 2025 more people did think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would fix it. The expectations were high. We measured that back in January. Right now you have this less than one in five that feel like his policies have made them financially better off. And even when you ask people then to look ahead to 2026, it`s a little bit better, but it`s not overwhelming that people think his policies will make them better off.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s this real disconnect there. And if you come back to the description of the economy, people right now, I ask them to just grade it, and they give it a C, a D or even worse, which is different than a lot of the times the way the administration has described the economy. That sets up the fundamental challenge I think you saw that this week for -- in the address.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, it`s not a new tactic for a current president to blame the last one for all the problems of today. But we checked and in that primetime address the president said it was Biden`s name, Biden`s fault about seven times. Does the public buy that?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY SALVANTO: Well, when you pick up on what we`ve just talked about, this long-term trend, the president did inherit economy where people thought -- a majority of people thought it was bad. At the same time, right now more people say the current economy is Donald Trump`s responsibility. He`s the one, it`s on his watch right now.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, he had been declining on his handling of the economy throughout the year. That`s leveled off now in recent weeks. And one thing you do find is, yes, there`s this disconnect. People think he is describing it as better than it really is. Maybe there`s political credit to be gained by starting to address what people are feeling.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, one thing we know is that health care costs for both private insurance users and government insurance users is going to be up. Congress is still debating what to do about the Obamacare act. But how much public focus is there on this issue?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY SALVANTO: So, if you look at the most immediate part of that argument, a big majority thinks that Congress should extend those added subsidies. But in the wider context of this, when we asked people, how do you feel about trying to afford all these various aspects, these basics, cost of living, health care is one of the ones that stands out that a lot of people say they have trouble affording.\par \par \uc2 And I think you`re picking that up in the general sentiment of how people process health care, health insurance costs as well. It`s a very important part, obviously, of people`s budgets, but also one that they tell us that concerns them a whole lot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, one of the structural changes you sort of alluded to earlier is the change coming from artificial intelligence. You were looking at this. We`ve seen a lot of discussion about how it`s reshaping things. Are Americans sort of digesting that? How do they understand it?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY SALVANTO: Yes, we`ve been following this all year because, right, it`s driving stock market gains. There`s a lot of investment in it as well.\par \par \uc2 Right now you see a lot of uncertainty from the public about it. On one hand, when I ask people, what parts of the economic sectors are you most at least optimistic about? Technology stands out as people being optimistic about it in general. But with regard to A.I. in particular, more people tell us they think it`s going to decrease jobs than increase them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the number of corporations forecasting a lot of spending on technology in the new year.\par \par \uc2 What do all these dynamics mean politically for the midterm races in 2026?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY SALVANTO: So, let me start with the president because one thing I asked heading into 2026, is there anything that the president could do to change your mind about them? And there are a substantial number of people that say, yes. Perhaps not surprisingly, when I follow and ask, well, what would that be about, they say it`s the economy.\par \par \uc2 The reason that`s important is, in the dynamic here, a president is always key. He`s not on the ballot, but he`s always on people`s minds in a midterm year. So, that`s the first thing to watch. But the other one, you know, if you`re at your holiday gathering and people are arguing about what`s going to happen, who`s going to win, I think it`s too far to know for certain. But at the same time, we can already see the conditions and the playing field that people will be fighting on. And that`s clearly cost of living. It`s clearly health care, among other things. And it`s the race to define how people evaluate it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anthony Salvanto, thank you for this.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to the director of the \uc2 White House National Economic Council\uc2  Kevin Hassett.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT (National Economic Council Director): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We took you literally. You said last Sunday you`d come back and talk to us about that household survey, the jobs picture. You said you really trust it. That report came out. It showed America added 64,000 jobs with unemployment ticking up to 4.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve seen analysis, including from the top economist at U.S. Navy Federal saying the U.S. is in a, quote, "hiring recession. Very few jobs being added since the spring and wage gains are slowing." Are we in a hiring recession?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: No, I don`t think so. I think that basically the number was about what the market expected. It was a number that was less than 100, which is a little bit lower than you`d like. But then, after that, we got the Consumer Price Index numbers, which were really amazing. And so, if you look at the three-month moving average of core consumer prices, then they`re running at an annual rate of about 1.6 percent, way below the Fed`s target.\par \par \uc2 And my old friend all the way back to grad school, Austan Goolsbee, who`s now one of the Fed governors voting on interest rates, conceded that they should have cut rates faster, and that he`s going to do so in the future because of this inflation number.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. He was one of the dissenters when the Fed`s decision came out.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But when it comes to inflation, as you just referred to, that number showed there was still a rise in inflation. It was just at a slower pace than expected. If you pull out energy and pull out food, it rose at the slowest pace since 2021, up 2.6 percent in November from a year ago. But then there are quirks in here because it looks like the prices weren`t gathered until Thanksgiving discounts kicked in. There was no increase reflected in terms of houses costs. Why do you trust the data now when you haven`t in the past?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Oh, you know, I think that you`re right to always be suspicious about data. And it will look a lot cleaner when we get a full survey, which we didn`t have these -- that time. But, you know, two of the three numbers, if we go back to the three month moving average, were fine. And so, I think that the error band around the 1.6 percent inflation, about that`s the -- the pace is running, is probably pretty tight. Surely there will be some revisions, but I think the numbers are about right.\par \par \uc2 And I usually, Margaret, don`t like to go to year over year when we`re talking about what`s going on with inflation because that includes a lot of high inflation Biden months in the back, and, you know, fewer and fewer as we get, you know, further into the year. But I think that the trajectory right now is best seen by the three month moving average. And that`s below two, below the Fed target. So it means the Fed, as Austan Goolsbee said, has plenty of room to cut rates.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. But the president said inflation has stopped. You`d acknowledge that`s an exaggeration.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, I guess the inflation being above the target has stopped for sure. And so inflation is not zero also. That`s fair.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. He did not give those caveats. He just said, inflation has stopped.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett, stay with us. I`ve got to take a quick break and I want to talk about the rest of what we`ve learned.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: OK.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back with a lot more "FACE THE NATION." White House Adviser -- economic Adviser Kevin Hassett is standing by, and we`ll hear from the new head of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops and also \uc2 Catherine Russell\uc2 , executive director of \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 . She`s just back from Sudan.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to "FACE THE NATION."\par \par \uc2 And we continue our conversation now with the director of the \uc2 White House National Economic Council\uc2  Kevin Hassett. This past week we saw the president announce further changes to immigration policy. He halted diversity visas. There are now 39 countries on this travel ban or restricted list. This is in addition to all the deportations and revocations of asylum.\par \par \uc2 The business community has made clear that the constant changes make it hard for them to plan, particularly in small business categories. They`re concerned about a labor shortage. And we`ve seen the U.S. workforce lose more than one million foreign-born workers in the past year. Are you hearing those concerns from the business community?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT (DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL): Actually, it`s the opposite. So, in the sense that native born workers are up more than two million. And so what we`ve seen is that as foreign-born workers, and a lot of them, don`t forget, were illegal immigrants that have been deported, when foreign-born workers depart, then it creates jobs for people who are native-born.\par \par \uc2 The interesting thing too, which I`d like to remind everybody, is that native-born Americans aren`t necessarily racially distinct at all. And so, the majority of the native-born jobs that have been created over the last year are Hispanic Americans. It`s, you know, just basically taking folks that are legally in the country and getting the jobs to them is what we see in the data.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the \uc2 Associated General Contractors of America\uc2  didn`t say they`re replacing those workers easily. They`re saying they`re having a hard time doing that, and issued a statement saying it`s a significant challenge, urging the administration to lock at ways to expand the construction workforce and allow more people to lawfully enter the country.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Right. Well, one of the things we`re seeing, this is exactly how markets work, right? So, construction workers, their salaries this year have gone up by $3,300 on average according to the latest report. And --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because of scarcity?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, but when the price goes up, then people say, oh, I should -- I should go and I used to be a construction worker but now I`m doing something else, I should go back to construction work because the salaries are so high. And so we`re seeing a lot of that go on. And so we`re very bullish on construction and really happy to see that wages are going way back up.\par \par \uc2 And so, instead of having, you know, basically illegal people come in and take jobs away from native-born Americans at low languages, we`re seeing people re-enter the labor force at high wages.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But to be clear, from the business community, part of the question is what is legal because you`re changing the definition and pulling back asylum from some groups. So, there is confusion of the constant change in policy. Will there be clarity in the new year?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Oh, there will definitely be clarity in the new year. And I think there`s clarity now that what we`ve done is that we`ve made it so that people are legally residents of the country and legally allowed to work, that they`re, you know, basically top of the list when you`re looking for somebody to hire.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let me ask you a bit about tariffs. Since as far back as July we have heard the president refer to this idea of $2,000 checks being given out to households. The treasury secretary said this would go to households making less than $100,000. Should Americans plan to receive those checks in 2026?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: That`s going to depend on what happens with Congress. Congress is going to have to send those monies to those people. But the thing we can say is that since July we`ve had a lot of positive news about the economy. We`ve had a couple of quarters of almost four percent growth. We`ve got a big government surplus actually running for a few months in a row. The deficit relative to last year is down by $600 billion.\par \par \uc2 And so, in the summer I wasn`t so sure that there was space for a check like that, but now I`m pretty sure that there is. And so, I would expect that in the new year the president will bring forth a proposal to Congress to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, a new proposal for these. It`s not necessarily coming from existing tariff revenue?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Oh, well, it could come from tariff revenue. But in the end, you know, we get taxes, we get tariffs, we get all -- revenue from lots of places, and then Congress decides how to spend those monies, that`s an appropriation. It`s -- this would have to be money that would an appropriation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. So, don`t bank on it, in other words.\par \par \uc2 So, are you projecting that in the new year certain tariffs will remain in place, or do you see some of them going away?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: I think that most of the tariffs that we passed this year have proven their metal. We`ve got still high growth, which people said we couldn`t if we had tariffs. We have reduction in the deficit. Reduction in the trade deficit. Imports from China are the lowest they`ve been since China entered the \uc2 WTO\uc2 . So, there`s a lot of success to crow about in the tariff space.\par \par \uc2 But there are also thing that we`re hearing when we talk to people, like trading partners and companies that trade a lot in the U.S., There are also things that maybe could be adjusted. You saw that we decided to exempt coffee, for example, because we don`t make a lot of coffee in the U.S. And so I think that --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And coffee prices are up.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And I think that if there`s something that`s not made in the U.S. at all, that there`s an appetite, and Jamieson Greer is leading the effort to study these matters, there`s an appetite too exempt things if they`re really not meant to be made in the U.S. because of, you know, like climate or things like that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve also go -- got the Supreme Court decision pending in terms of the specific IEEPA, as they`re called. These are tariffs where the justification was citing fentanyl, Canadian crude, some Chinese imports. If the Supreme Court doesn`t find in your favor, who gets the refund? Is it the companies or is it the consumers? How does that work?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes, you know, we really expect the Supreme Court is going to find with us. And I also think that if they didn`t find with us, then it`s going to be pretty unlikely that they`re going to call for widespread refunds because it would be an administrative problem to get those refunds out there. But basically, whoever paid the tariff, like actually cut the check to buy the thing, would be the person who would be getting the refund if there were one.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Doesn`t the administration claim it was the country that paid that? You`re saying the company?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: It would be -- like I`m talking -- I`m not talking about the incidents, right? The -- like, so the incidents of the tariff, so in the end who pays the tariff depends on the elasticity of supply and demand. We know that China cut the price a lot so that the post-tariff price from Chinese goods is about what it was before the tariff. But the people who pay the tariff -- if there is a refund, the people who actually paid for the good, the importer in most cases, they`re the ones who would be the first line of defense for refunding the tariff.\par \par \uc2 But I really, really don`t think that`s going to happen. It would be very complicated. And then that person would be responsible for allocating the tariff refund to the appropriate folks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That sounds like a mess.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes, it is a mess. And that`s why I think the Supreme Court wouldn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Well, we`ll wait and see.\par \par \uc2 I want to quickly ask you about the oil market. I asked you last Sunday about that decision to seize a tanker off the coast of Venezuela. We saw yet another one over the weekend. You said last Sunday it probably won`t affect oil prices to be taking this oil off the black market. Do you stand by that? And was all that oil going to China?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes. You know, I haven`t been briefed on the latest. I did see that this is another ship that was basically operating on the black market. And so there have been a little bit -- there`s been a little bit of black market activity in the oil sector to go to sanctioned countries and to get them some oil. And they`re getting that oil in order to avoid, you know, the reforms that we think would make the countries a better place. And so, it`s not a lot of oil compared to world supply. And so, I don`t think that people need to be worried here in the U.S. that the prices are going to go up because of these seizures of these ships. There`s just a couple of them. And they were black market ships.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Were these sanctioned ships going to China?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: I`m not sure where they were going.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Kevin Hassett, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have a great holiday.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: You, too.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  warns that more than 200 million children will require humanitarian assistance in the coming year. The largest crisis right now is Sudan.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Russell\uc2 , \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 `s executive director, is just back from Sudan and South Sudan.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2  (Executive Director, \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 ): Good morning, Margaret. How are you?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m well. But you are -- we want to draw attention here to those in need. And I know Secretary of State Marco Rubio just, on Friday, called for a cessation of hostilities. He said the U.S. is pushing for a humanitarian truce to allow some of these aid organizations to operate, but aid convoys have been struck on their way in, he also acknowledged. What kind of challenges are you facing?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Well, Sudan is, at this moment, the largest humanitarian crisis we`re facing. And there are many things happening there. One is that children in particular, but people are on the move. Millions of people are moving around the country. We have hunger, extreme hunger, including famine, designated in certain parts of the country. We`re also seeing just horrific levels of violence, excuse me, including rape, including against children. \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  did a report about that about a year ago. Even children under the age of one. Absolutely terrible stories.\par \par \uc2 And a woman I met in Chad, who had fled Darfur, told me about the effort to sort of rape women in front of their families and their communities. Really trying to terrorize them. And videotaping it to humiliate them. And so, the levels of violence are horrific. You have children out of school. So, the bottom line here is that the needs are enormous. The challenges are just absolutely staggering. And I think the world needs to come together, you know, in the short term for sure we need better humanitarian access. We need to reach all of these communities. That`s hard to do right now.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, Margaret, that what I heard from children across Sudan was the same thing, which is, I would say to them, you know, what do you think about what do you want? And they all said the same thing, which is, we want peace.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: I think they understand that the only answer here really is to solve the political problems so that people can get back to some sort of a decent life.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I just -- I don`t want to let go what you just mentioned in terms of why something like that would be done to children and to women in front of their families. The term genocide is used in Sudan. What you are describing there in terms of treatment of gender-based violence, it sounds like you are saying, that is an attempt to destroy a society by carrying out rape.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: I mean they are definitely atrocities that are intended to terrorize communities, to force them to move, to scare them. And, you know, they`re effective strategies for sure. And I think, you know, the world needs to take a look -- I mean Sudan, I know it seems so far away. And it is in many ways. But it is also a place where children are suffering so enormously. And I think especially, you know, now we`re in the holiday season, you know, you want to think about, you know, people being with their families, all the rest of it. I mean children in so many places are not -- are not having those sort of luxuries. They are really suffering. And I think the world needs to pay attention to that. And I commend, you know, Senator Rubio, Secretary Rubio now, for pointing this out because I think the key thing here is that we need attention to the problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the U.S. has historically been the largest donor to \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 . But we`ve seen tremendous cuts by this administration, including $142 million claw back of congressionally approved funds. Secretary Rubio said no one died from these aid cuts. And on Friday he said he`s very proud of the changes that were made in foreign aid. Tell us what the realty is of the funding cuts.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Yes, the funding cuts are challenging. And I think that the issue to understand is it`s not just the United States. It`s almost every donor to -- certainly to \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  but to the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  system broadly, and to humanitarian sector. And so when you add all of those together, the impacts are really dramatic because it means what, you know, what -- what financial support we do get, we have to spread now over more places and with less support from donor countries.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, you know, there are certainly, you know, there have been and will probably always be catastrophes that we`re trying to deal with. And children are suffering. And the United States has always proudly led, I think, on humanitarian response and on helping children. And, you know, we`re hopeful that some of the money that has been taken back will be restored to us. We continue to work with the State Department. There are efforts to fund a lot of the humanitarian and sort of life-saving work that we do. But really, you know, the needs around the world are just absolutely enormous. And it`s the United States and other donors would have to come in behind that, along with the private sector, I have to add that. That is also an important piece of this puzzle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. And I should point out that the U.S. is one of the largest foreign aid donors to South Sudan, but that foreign aid is also under threat at this moment because of the government not complying with what the U.S. is demanding.\par \par \uc2 Let me quickly --\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Yes, South Sudan is a challenge.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : I was just there too. And that is -- you know, the government there needs to do a better job.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, can I -- quickly, before I let you go, Gaza. How are things improving?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Well, again, I would say thanks to the ceasefire and the administration here was critical in doing that, things have gotten a bit better. We are able to move around better.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, there`s tremendous misery. The challenges for me, we have a lot of work that needs to get done. I mean you`ve seen all of the images, the destruction. You know, we still have serious levels of malnutrition. We are trying to get shelter in place. You know, there`s rain and just misery for children all around. So, I think, you know, would -- I would say there definitely is progress with the ceasefire. God willing that holds.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL: And then we can continue to get more and more aid in there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: God willing that holds, as you say.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Russell\uc2 , thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE RUSSELL\uc2 : Thanks. Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: One of the highlights for Catholics in the U.S. this year was the selection of an American pope, Pope Leo. We spoke Friday to the new head of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops, Archbishop \uc2 Paul Coakley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Father, I want to ask you about this role you have at the head of the Conference of Bishops. Last month there was this urgent message. It was a very rare statement addressing concern for the evolving situation impacting migrants. And the language was pretty sharp in it. Quote, "we oppose the indiscriminate mass deportation of people. We pray for an end to dehumanizing rhetoric and violence, whether directed at immigrants or at law enforcement."\par \par \uc2 What are bishops that you talk to seeing in perishes all around the country? What triggered this?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY (President, U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops): I think it varies from place to place for sure. So, in communities with a more dense migrant population, there`s a great deal of fear and uncertainty. Anxiety because of the level of rhetoric that is often employed when addressing usually around migration and the threats of deportation. So, there`s a lot of anxiety. So, our pastors are trying to accompany people, walk with people, reassure people, and that`s the message of the church. And that was the reason for our special message in November that we`re with you, we hear you, we`re aware of your suffering and your fears, and we fully intend to accompany you during this difficult time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, I know there have been bishops in places like Charlotte, in parts of California, who have formally granted permission or dispensation is the word used for people who skip Sunday mass if they`re afraid they could be targeted by ICE. Are you actually seeing a decline in the number of people attending mass because they`re afraid they`ll be arrested?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY: We are not seeing that here in Oklahoma. And I haven`t heard it reportedly widely from my brother bishops. So, I know that that is the case in some places. But I don`t think it`s as common, at least here locally, or in places that I have personal contact with. There`s a -- there`s an anxiety, there`s a fear. But I don`t think it`s kept people away in great numbers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, this video that you released really got a lot of attention. It certainly got ours. And it mentioned being troubled by threats against the sanctity of houses of worship, as well as hospitals and schools. You know, I spoke to the vice president, J.D. Vance, last year and asked him about this change in policy that would allow for ICE to enter churches and schools. And he really pushed back hard. He says that the catholic church receives funds to help re-settle immigrants, thus they are motivated by financial and not humanitarian reasons.\par \par \uc2 Have you had any kind of conversation with the Trump administration, with the vice president, to explain what it is you`re trying to say?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY: I have not had any personal conversations at this point with either the president or the vice president. So, I look forward to engaging with them over matters of mutual concern and certainly undoubtedly the question of immigration is going to come up, I think. We have opportunities to work together. We have opportunities to speak frankly with one another. So, I will look forward to that in the -- in the near future.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the vice president calls himself a devout catholic, which is also why I ask. I mean do you -- what do you say to Catholics like him who argue that these hardline immigration policies have had a result that they sought, that the end justifies the means?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY: I don`t think we can ever say that the end justifies the means. We have to treat everyone with respect. Respect of human dignity. \uc2 Dignity\uc2  of every person. There`s no conflict necessarily between advocating for safe and secure borders and treating people with respect and dignity. We always have to treat people with dignity. God-given dignity. The state doesn`t award it. And the state can`t take it away. So, it`s from the creator.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, as Christians, as believers, we -- that`s kind of a foundational bedrock thing for us, that people are to be respected and treated with dignity, whether they are documented or undocumented, whether they are here legally or illegally, they don`t forfeit their human dignity.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, what is it that you think is causing that to be an issue right now? Why do you think that there is a loss of dignity? Are there specific policies that you are thinking of when you say that?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY: Well, not necessarily. I mean I just -- to treat people with respect. To afford them due process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY: The deportations that are -- that are happening around the country can -- it`s instilling, as I said, fear in a rather widespread manner. So, I think that`s -- that`s something that concerns us all, that people have a -- have a right to live in security and without fear of random and -- of deportations. I think we`re believers, of course, in due process. We have provisions in law that award due process to everyone. So, that`s what we are wanting to emphasize.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What would you advise others, other people of faith, to do for these immigrants?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY: Well, we are a nation of immigrants ourselves. So, I think that`s something that we, as Americans, need to always come back to. We are founded upon the immigrant experience. So, I think we need to be generous in welcoming immigrants. We certainly have a right and a duty to respect borders of our nation. And I would say remind all of us that we have a right and a duty to respect sovereign borders of a state. But we also have a responsibility to welcome immigrants. This is kind of a fundamental principle in catholic social teaching regarding immigration and migration. People have a right to remain in their homeland, but they also ought to be allowed to migrate when conditions in their homeland are unsafe and necessitate moving to a place where they can find peace and security.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Archbishop \uc2 Paul Coakley\uc2  of Oklahoma City, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP PAUL COAKLEY: Thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. We want to wish all our viewers a very happy holiday season and also want to give a big thank you to Washington bureau managing editor Arden Farhi and justice reporter Jake Rosen for all their work and their team`s help sorting through the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for "FACE THE NATION," I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020251221elcl00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Rob and Michele Reiner`s Son Charged with Murder; Trump Orders Blockade on Venezuela Oil Tankers; New Storm Hits Northwest Amid Flooding Emergency; Funerals for Victims of Bondi Beach Mass Shooting; Trump Chief- of-Staff`s Scathing Comments; Brawl Breaks Out in Mexico City Congress; MIT Professor Shot and Killed at His Home; Second Doctor Sentenced in Matthew Perry`s Death; The Pros and Cons of Shopping with A.I.; Sylvester Stallone Like You`ve Never Seen Him; "Anaconda" Stars Jack Black and Paul Rudd; Sarah Gelman Shares Amazon`s Best Holiday Books; A Look Back at Tony`s Best Moments; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers,  Jonathan Vigliotti, Charlie D`Agata, Rob Marciano, Tom Hanson, Anna Coren,  Major Garrett, Anna Meiler, Vladimir Duthiers, Kelly O`Grady, Jill  Schlesinger \par \uc2 14,444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS, I`m telling you for the last time. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not too late to change your mind. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We do have a lot of news this morning, including new U.S. action against Venezuela, plus video of Rob Reiner`s son before his arrest. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rob and Michele Reiner`s son, Nick is officially charged with two counts of first degree murder as new video emerges.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HOCHMAN (Los Angeles County District Attorney): A maximum sentence of life in prison, without the possibility parole or the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The manhunt continues for the Brown University shooter, police released enhanced images of the suspect.\par \par \uc2 PETER NERONHA\uc2  (Rhode Island Attorney General): I`m confident that a member of the public is going to recognize this person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The President announces a new Venezuela blockade, while his Defense Secretary refuses to release video of a controversial boat strike.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (\uc2 U.S. Department of Defense\uc2  Secretary): Appropriate committees will see it, but not the general public.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Devastating floods turned deadly in Washington State, and another levee breaching as more rain threatens the region.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: \uc2 Derek Jeter\uc2  held his charity golf outing and rapper Ja Rule, this was his opening tee shot.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD say "Oh!")\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: I know it looks bad.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: I know it sounded bad. No one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Puts a pass to the corner. That will be -- yes!\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: It was time for the \uc2 Knicks\uc2  to win their first trophy in a long, long time. The \uc2 NBA\uc2  Cup on the line in Vegas. And you saw Spike taking it seriously.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Go ahead and celebrate it. \uc2 Knicks\uc2  won it 124 to 113.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BROWN (\uc2 New York Knicks\uc2  coach): I feel like we`re in New York right now! Let`s go! Let`s go!\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A sign of things to come, we all hope. We are cheering and loving on the \uc2 Knicks\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That level of excitement, by the way, let`s go, as Katy`s reaction to this being my last morning where she`s doing school drop off alone, because this is my last day at CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, and we`re going to talk about that throughout the show.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s bittersweet. I`ve already thrown the alarm clock in the Hudson River.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s bittersweet, but it is a whole lot of sweet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Smart move!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a whole lot of sweet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we are going to get to it. And I will also cover the news, thank you, Gayle. Thank you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to cover the news.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with tough information, new information on the deaths of Rob Reiner and his wife, Michele, and the arrest of their son, Nick. This is a family tragedy at this point.\par \par \uc2 The newly released footage shows him in a gas station convenience store just hours after the killings and minutes before he was taken into custody. He is now charged with two counts of first degree murder in the deaths of his parents -- just to say those words.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is in Los Angeles for us, once again. He`s been on the story for several days now.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Nick Reiner is being held here at this jail in downtown Los Angeles without bail. He is undergoing what we are told are routine medical clearances. He could make his first appearance in court for arraignment as early as today.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: New security video shows Nick Reiner in a striped shirt pacing inside a convenience store shortly before his arrest Sunday night. Outside the store, you see the \uc2 LAPD\uc2  arriving and arresting him. Police say he did not resist.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors allege the 32-year-old used a knife to fatally stab his parents, Rob and Michele Reiner in their Brentwood home. Nick Reiner is now facing two first degree murder charges.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HOCHMAN: These charges carry a maximum sentence of life in prison without the possibility parole or the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It`s still unclear when the alleged attack occurred and how long the couple had been dead before their bodies were discovered.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports a massage therapist arrived at the Reiner home on Sunday. When nobody came to the gate, she called the couple`s daughter, Romy who arrived and found her father`s body.\par \par \uc2 The day before the murders, Rob and Nick Reiner got into an argument at Conan O`Brien`s Christmas party. NBC News reports Nick interrupted former "SNL" comedian, Bill Hader before storming off.\par \par \uc2 Nick Reiner has been open about his struggles with addiction, recalling on a 2018 podcast, one incident where he became violent while on drugs.\par \par \uc2 NICK REINER (Rob and Michele Reiner`s son): I was up for days on end, and I started punching out different things in my guest house. I think I started with the T.V. and then I went over to the lamp. I just -- everything in the guest house got wrecked.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: While promoting the film he made with his father, Nick Reiner said he was grateful for his parents.\par \par \uc2 NICK REINER: I think I`m lucky in the sense that I have parents that care about me, and because of that, when I would go out and do drugs and stuff like that, I`d feel a tremendous amount of guilt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And close friends of Rob Reiner, including Billy Crystal and Larry David put out a joint statement yesterday to \uc2 The Associated Press\uc2 , writing in part, and I quote here: "Rob Reiner, not only was a great comic actor, he became a master storyteller. His greatest gift was freedom. If you had an idea, he listened, he brought you into the process. To be in his hands as a filmmaker was a privilege, but that is only part of his legacy."\par \par \uc2 As far as his son goes, he is represented by Alan Jackson. He is a high- profile defense attorney. Prior clients include \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2  and Kevin Spacey.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, we reached out for comment. We have not heard back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The more you hear, Jonathan, the worse it gets. It is heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 I think Tony said it best when he said it is a family tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, we thank you, and there`s more to come, I`m sure. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is ramping up his effort to force Venezuela`s President out of power. The U.S. has now put a blockade on many oil tankers using Venezuelan ports. This means that we could see more scenes like this one, U.S. forces seizing a ship just last week.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is tracking all of this for us.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot we don`t know about this plan, when it is going to be implemented, how many oil takers might be involved, but he says it won`t be lifted until, "our oil, land and any other assets are returned to United States," making it clear this is more than just about drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: On Tuesday night, President Trump posted on Truth Social announcing, "a total and complete blockade of all sanctioned oil tankers going in and out of Venezuela."\par \par \uc2 Venezuela produces nearly 900,000 barrels of oil daily and its economy relies on its oil exports, but the country led by dictator, Nicolas Maduro has faced heavy sanctions from the U.S. for years.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER STUENKEL (International Relations expert): Trump is ratcheting up the pressure on Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: International relations expert, Oliver Stuenkel thinks the ultimate goal is regime change.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER STUENKEL: I think there is a risk that the United States will inadvertently be pulled into a conflict without a clear exit strategy.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: For months, the Trump administration has built up its military might in the Caribbean. Last week, the U.S. seized a sanctioned 20-year-old oil tanker after it left port in Venezuela and has carried out at least 25 deadly strikes on alleged drug boats in the region.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Their policies in the Caribbean are unclear. We don`t know what the limits are. We don`t know when they will stop. We don`t know what the ultimate goal is.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: There is bipartisan concern over The White House`s actions in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I want to know what`s going to happen next. Is it the policy to take Maduro down? It should be, if it`s not. And if he goes, what`s going to happen next?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In a statement, the Maduro regime accused President Trump of violating international law and said it`s going to take it up at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Today on Capitol Hill, Admiral Mitch Bradley will brief lawmakers about the legality of those strikes -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Charlie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Another massive storm is pummeling the Pacific Northwest with rain, causing intense flooding for the second straight week, and now a new levee breach is impacting recovery efforts just outside of Seattle. At least one person has died in the storms.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` meteorologist, Rob Marciano has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Just south of Seattle, a levee failure along the White River flooding entire neighborhoods, forcing the immediate evacuation of hundreds of people yesterday in Pacific Washington.\par \par \uc2 You can see water completely surrounding homes and nearly reaching the roofs of cars. It`s a second levee along two different rivers in the state that broke in the last 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BEN FERGUSON (D-WA): I just want to emphasize to Washingtonians to be alert. If you get an evacuation order, for God`s sakes, follow it.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: State officials say a 33-year-old man died in hard hit Snohomish County after he drove past road closed signs into six-foot deep water. Others had to be rescued from fast, rising flood waters, including this man who climbed on the top of his car when he got stuck Monday night.\par \par \uc2 The historic flooding has washed away parts of Highway 2, connecting the Seattle area to the Cascade Mountains. Officials say it could be closed for months.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BEN FERGUSON (D-WA): And it`s not a one or two day event, right? We`re in for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: There is more rain to come, but now the wind has been an issue the past couple of hours. Over a half a million people now are without power in Oregon and Washington from those high winds and we`ve got high wind warnings, not just for the Northwest, but getting across into the Upper Plains as well. So that`s going to be the next thing we talk about here with this atmospheric river, a top of which we`ve got another system that`s coming in here.\par \par \uc2 So we`re tapping into some of the tropics. This is a high moisture content event, so Flood Watches remain up, not only that, but this is a slightly cooler event. So flood watches and warnings, and also Blizzard Warnings up in some of the mountains, including the Olympics, and that`s pretty rare. So very, very hard travel here as this rain and snow and wind continues to come in, and this is going to be a coast-to-coast system that`s hitting them right now. It gets into the Intermountain West later on.\par \par \uc2 Today, with the wind, with the rain, with the snow, gets all the way to Chicago by tomorrow afternoon, with heavy rain, maybe some severe weather as well, and notice, there is not a whole lot of white with this. So it`s going to be a warmer event when it gets to the Northeast, with wind and rain and difficult travel on Friday. But again, a warmer event, so not much in the way of snow and melting a lot of the snow that fell earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Tony, over to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The gunman who killed two people at Brown University is still on the loose. Days later now, police have released new, high quality videos of the suspect. They want the public to help identify the man on your screen here seen around the Ivy League campus before and after the shootings.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is following the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Videos released Tuesday show the suspected gunman in enhanced detail, walking and sometimes running in a residential area near Brown University`s campus hours before Saturday`s deadly mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 COL. OSCAR PEREZ (Providence, Rhode Island Police Chief): We believe that he was actually casing out this area to commit the crime, it is no -- that`s apparent that criminals do.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Police say they don`t know why the unidentified man appears to have a bag with him in some clips, while in others, it cannot be seen. They also have not determined the man`s approximate age or if he has a connection to the university.\par \par \uc2 PETER NERONHA\uc2 : I don`t think we know anything about this person`s motivations at this point.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Rhode Island Attorney General Peter Neronha says there is no surveillance video of the person of interest inside the building where two students were killed and nine others injured.\par \par \uc2 Brown has 1,200 cameras across campus, but Neronha says almost none were positioned to capture the shooter, citing the building`s placement on the edge of campus.\par \par \uc2 PETER NERONHA\uc2 : Whether that was by design by the shooter or not, I can`t tell you, but it is an unfortunate circumstance for us, because we`re -- you know, we just don`t have the kind of surveillance video that you might think.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Police insist there is no threat to the community, and they are confident there will be a breakthrough soon, despite the manhunt now entering its fifth day without answers.\par \par \uc2 The more time that passes, the more time this person of interest potentially has to get away.\par \par \uc2 PETER NERONHA\uc2 : Correct. But the key here is identifying the person. Once a person is identified, at that point, it is very difficult to hide from law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Meanwhile, the Providence community is grieving.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of people packed church pews at a vigil Tuesday night honoring victims and families.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We always assume like, if this happens, it`s going to be somewhere else. But today, we`re all someone else.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And seven of the nine injured students remained in the hospital. We`re also seeing an obituary published for Ella Cook, one of the two students who died in Saturday`s shooting. There will be a memorial service in her honor in her home state of Alabama, that will happen on Monday -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, the first funerals were held today for victims of Sunday`s mass shooting at Sydney`s Bondi Beach. Fifteen people were killed as they celebrated the first night of Hanukkah. The surviving suspect has now been charged with terrorism and murder.\par \par \uc2 Anna Coren reports on one of today`s farewells.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: The body of Rabbi Eli Schlanger arrived at the synagogue just a few blocks from where he and 14 other people were gunned down.\par \par \uc2 Inside an outpouring of grief for the father of five, whose youngest was born just seven weeks ago. His father-in-law unable to contain his sorrow while mourners sobbed and wailed.\par \par \uc2 As family and friends followed the hearse, their loss was palpable.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He was the heart and soul of them in the synagogue.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: And you have to do this again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Not once, not twice, with many more times.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Rabbi Eli Schlanger`s body is now being driven to the cemetery. This is the first of many funerals this week, as this tight-knit Jewish community farewells its fallen heroes.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, police have charged the 24-year-old surviving gunman with 15 counts of murder, grievous bodily harm and terrorism. Authorities believe the father and son gunmen had been inspired by Islamic State.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Anna Coren in Bondi Beach, Australia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now to a very candid new look inside the Trump White House from the President`s own Chief-of-Staff. Susie Wiles is her name. She granted a series of interviews to "Vanity Fair" and she did not seem to hold back here. She said President Trump, who doesn`t drink alcohol, had "an alcoholic`s personality," and that he operates with a view that there`s nothing he can`t do.\par \par \uc2 She also said Vice President J.D. Vance has been "a conspiracy theorist" for a decade. On the Epstein file, she reveals President Trump is in them. She says he was on Epstein`s plane. He`s on the manifest, but that he wasn`t doing anything awful, in her words. She goes on to say that Trump and Epstein were "sort of young single playboys together," and she faults Attorney General Pam Bondi for her handling of the Epstein files, saying this, "I think she completely whiffed understanding the importance of those files to some of Trump`s supporters."\par \par \uc2 Wiles now calls this article a disingenuously framed hit piece, but it sure is raising a whole lot of questions today.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by our chief Washington correspondent, Major Garrett, who, by the way, wrote the book on the first Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Major, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: One of many books written on the first Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right. You`re absolutely right about that.\par \par \uc2 There is so much to unpack here. Number one, we hear from The White House already that Susie Wiles is calling this a hit piece. But how unusual is it for a chief-of-staff to speak this way, this candidly, and in so many different interviews?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes, 11 interviews with Chris Whipple, who many in the White House knew he wrote a book about the Biden-Trump campaign in 2024 well known to this White House, and it`s worth it pointing out, Gayle, this "Vanity Fair" piece has intention behind it. Susie Wiles would not grant 11 interviews if she wasn`t intentionally wanting to have this portrayal of The White House available.\par \par \uc2 All those who work in the Trump inner circle would not have posed for the artistic and stylistically taken photographs if they did not want to participate as well, cementing their role in the first year of Trump 2.0 and all the work that they`re doing on behalf of this administration. So there`s tremendous intentionality about this.\par \par \uc2 And Susie Wiles, highly unusual for her to be this visible. She is a power broker inside The White House, rarely spoke during the campaign, has rarely spoken during this first year of the Trump second administration, but she is now, and her candor struck many as absolutely true, the things she said about Trump, things she said about J.D. Vance, J.D. Vance the Vice President, embraced them yesterday on the road; things about Pam Bondi, the attorney general, though, strike me as very important, because they were not praiseworthy at all.\par \par \uc2 Also, the relationship that Trump has with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  continues obviously, to be strained, because he was not portrayed favorably by the chief-of- staff either. I think those two portrayals are the most negative and the biggest takeaways for me about what was otherwise something we all knew and suspected about the Trump White House, that the chief-of-staff confirmed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we know that Susie Wiles is no novice. She`s very savvy. She knows what she`s doing. So clearly, there`s a strategy here.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about how The White House is handling the backlash that the President received to his comments about Rob Reiner`s death?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Well, the President is staying where he is, but no one in the Republican Party, at least on Capitol Hill, and many within the Trump White House, find any real room to defend the remarks. Because this is, as Jonathan Vigliotti just said, a massive family tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Rob Reiner was an artist who influenced American cultural life for three or four decades. That`s a stand-alone achievement in America, and to have that soiled by the President suggesting that there was some sort of Trump derangement syndrome behind this, when this was clearly a complex family tragedy and breakdown of massive murderous proportions, is something and it`s rare to say this about President Trump, beyond the pale, even for those closest to him, and they really don`t know what to do or say about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Major, the President, as we all know, is addressing the nation tonight, so there`s more to come, I`m sure tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, an MIT professor shot and killed at his home in Massachusetts. The latest on the search for suspect.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a driver takes a Ferrari on a wild joy ride in the streets of Miami, and we will show you what happened.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, Gayle`s emotional conversation with Kennedy Center Honoree, Sylvester Stallone.\par \par \uc2 Plus, movie stars, Jack Black and Paul Rudd stopped by to talk about their new movie, "Anaconda."\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch," we have a little something special planned for Tony as he gets ready to move to "CBS Evening News." I know, we are all sad about it. Say it ain`t so, Dokoupil!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It ain`t so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hear you`re going to embarrass me. Please don`t. Please don`t.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We