much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Also, we had a little session where we were going to talk about how Tony sometimes, like when Kristen Bell was on and he was like, "You`re Anna?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "From Frozen?" And she`s like, "Yes, I am."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fortunately, we only do the A Block on the "Evening News." Because I don`t know anything about culture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey listen, we`re going to continue to celebrate Tony, and we`ll talk about the more sincere moments, his good moments.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in his defense, those names were hard though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They were hard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am going to say --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love you all.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, our revealing and very touching conversation with Sylvester Stallone who is now, yes, a Kennedy Center Honoree.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil, and that is Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour, our deeply personal conversation with new Kennedy Center Honoree, Sylvester Stallone. How he says the seeds were planted in childhood for the man he is today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve also got two of the funniest people in show business, Jack Black and Paul Rudd, they are stars of the hilarious new comedy "Anaconda," and yes, it does involve a snake.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, some excellent holiday book recommendations from Amazon`s Sarah Gelman. We`ve got some possible gift ideas that you might also want to read yourself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to begin this hour with the first clear look at the job market since the government shutdown, and it includes a troubling sign about the economy. Numbers out Tuesday show the November unemployment rate rose 4.6 percent, highest level in four years.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to break it all down, what you need to know about the job market. Jill, great to have you here. What do you need to know about the job market?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know, this was the first report since the government shutdown, and we kind of got two months shoved into one. So in October, we saw job losses, 105,000 jobs lost. But this is primarily expected due to those folks in the federal government who took that early retirement offer earlier in the year.\par \par \uc2 Job creation bounced back in November, 64,000 jobs. But here`s what we know. The first four months of this year, we were kind of going great guns, more than 120,000 jobs a month or so. Then we get that tariff announcement, immigration policies, businesses freak out. They say, uh-oh, we don`t want to hire so much. Then we saw job creation really slow down.\par \par \uc2 This year, we are on pace right now for about 55,000 jobs a month. Last year it was like 170,000, the year before, it was 200,000.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a big difference.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : This is a real sign that the economy down shifted this year, again, probably because of uncertainty, but it`s sort of leveled out right now. That unemployment rate, it is a little scary to be up in the high fours or getting into the middle fours, but a lot of economists believe that a little of this is a statistical rounding error, so I don`t want to go crazy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say great guns, we start in 2025. How do you think 2026 is going to start and go?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think starting slow, because what happens is the job market lags the overall economy. And what we`re seeing and hearing from a lot of companies is, yes, we`re going to hire if we need to, but we`re not going crazy. This is called a low-hire, low-fire environment. We haven`t seen widespread layoffs.\par \par \uc2 But I do think towards the middle to the end of next year, we could see job creation pick up. That`s good news.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what about those looking to get hired? What advice do you give them?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, listen, there are those A.I. filters that help you with the holidays. They are also working against you sometimes in the employment landscape.\par \par \uc2 Use the key words that employers are using in their own job description.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha!\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Don`t mess around. Here is a great idea to combat A.I., use a human being. Talk to people you know, fire up your network. Every single favor you`re going to call in and you`re going to -- like, I want to get on evening news. I know a guy who might be able to help me, so I`m going to call him in. And just one quick moment when people ask me what it`s like to work at CBS MORNINGS, what I say is, this crew at this morning show is incredible. The nicest, most professional, delightful people to work with, except for Tony, so what happens next?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s pretty spot on. That`s what I say too.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So, we wish you all the very best luck. You deserve it all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Jill. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He does deserve it all.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very excited for him. Here we go!\par \par \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Movie legend -- legend is the word here -- Kennedy Center Honoree, Sylvester Stallone, he is the best, the best like you`ve never seen him before. Our revealing interview about his childhood, career -- his childhood, his career -- and creating his most famous films.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s still on fire, but first it is 8:03. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, actors Jack Black and Paul Rudd star in the highly anticipated new movie, "Anaconda."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Anaconda"/Sony Pictures Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is funny. They are funny, and they are here. That`s coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As if you didn`t know, that`s Sylvester Stallone in "Rocky" which premiered nearly half a century ago. So he starred in and wrote this classic underdog film that made him a household name.\par \par \uc2 There are many ways to describe the American icon, and icon is the word here, an action star, yep; a director, yep; prolific writer, yep, and proud father! Check, check, check, check, check.\par \par \uc2 And now he`s adding Kennedy Center Honoree to his list of accolades.\par \par \uc2 So we traveled to his home in Florida for a wide-ranging and emotional interview. I only had one request when we went into edit. Please don`t start with the "Rocky" music. And guess what we`re starting with?\par \par \uc2 Stallone told us how the story of one of his most famous characters resembles his own.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you think when you hear that dunt-dunt-dunt-dunt!\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE (Kennedy Center Honoree): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today, what do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: It`s almost Pavlovian, I start to like, drool!\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Watching "Rocky" with Rocky. That`s very cool!\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: He doesn`t even shave. Come on, Rock!\par \par \uc2 I remember we were just running and running. I go, "Where are we going?" They said, it doesn`t matter. Just keep running.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lifetime of training has led Sylvester Stallone to this seat.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts/The Honors Inc.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about your Kennedy Center Honorees. People start thinking about their legacy --\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- for many people, what would you like yours to be?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: I really want to be a symbol for how an average person, really an average person, can overcome overwhelming odds.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before he was Rocky Balboa --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky II"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- or John Rambo.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "First Blood"/Orion Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was just Sly with an unusually deep voice.\par \par \uc2 Did it bother you having a deep voice?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: It did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it`s working for you now?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: No, it did, because it intimidated people like you`re in your class. "Hi, my name is Billy." "I`m Sly. How are you doing?" It just was like, whoa! This guy eat a bullfrog?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re eight.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: I am eight years old. "Can Mary come out? I am 11, sir."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Eleven.\par \par \uc2 His deep voice and slur were a result of an injury during childbirth that also caused his face to droop. He says he was bullied relentlessly.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: I had no friends. I literally would -- I would find myself stealing money to buy friends. I`d get five bucks out of my mother`s purse, and I go "Would you be my friend?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really? Really? Sly, wow!\par \par \uc2 Life at home, it wasn`t much easier.\par \par \uc2 When he was 11, his parents divorced and he moved in with his father, who he says was both emotionally and physically abusive.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: It`s hard to navigate, because you`re going to catch it, especially when you`re a bit rebellious, like me, you`re going to get beaten. After a while, you learn to just expect it. For example, my father, when he used to whistle, I knew it was coming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stallone says he turned to movies to cope with reality.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: I worshiped escapism.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: And I didn`t think about actors, Gayle, at all. It was more or less like mythic heroes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like who?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Like Steve Reeves.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hercules"/Warner Bros.)\par \par \uc2 Steve Reid When I saw Steve Reeves at 12-and-a-half years old, it literally changed my life. I said, I`m going to be that guy. I don`t want to be what`s at the house, but I do want to be this noble creature.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In his early 20s, Stallone moved to New York City to pursue that dream, teaching himself to write after struggling as a young actor.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: I was able to plumb into everything I`ve been through and put it on the page. Every one of these is a screenplay, and it goes all the way around there, and what`s unusual is the one that`s behind you, Gayle. This is the one that started it all. This is "Rocky."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is the original?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: That`s the original in the case. And you know, just typed up on our little typewriter back in the day.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: This is, of course, look at the hat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let me see. Put it on, please.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Put it on.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: There you go. How are you doing? Oh, Adrianne.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Adrianne.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: You need somebody to walk you home tonight?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Because there`s a lot of creeps in your neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Well, you throw in -- you tell a creep by their hat.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that is a real robe.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: There`s only one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: There is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that mean to you, Sly, when you look at this?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The robe, the fedora, all of that?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: It is such -- did this really happen?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it almost didn`t. The studio was against Stallone starring in his own film.\par \par \uc2 You write "Rocky" yourself.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you go to pitch it to the studios. They wanted the script, but they didn`t want you. At the time when you really could have used the money, they offered you how much back then?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Well, it started at 20, and then went to 80, then went to 160 then went 360, which was like almost two-and-a-half to three million dollars today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: I couldn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a gamble that paid off big time. Rocky would go on to win three Academy Awards.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 49th Academy Awards/ABC)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Including Best Picture.\par \par \uc2 What did that feel like to you at the time?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Well, it`s a volcanic moment, and then he was very sad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Because --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh why?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: You want the people that you love that denied you, now you`re here, you`re at the Oscar, and they don`t want to go. (Bleep). You realize that at that moment that you`re never, ever going to come to terms with this. And it`s like, what more do you need? Really? What (bleep) more do you need to do to say, I`m here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did this. Are you talking about your parents?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you talking about -- yes.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Winning the Oscar. Wow!\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Yes. And to me, it`s like, it is a real learning lesson, Gayle, that parents should really wise up. Kids are the same as soft clay. They really are. You mold them and you dent them and you hurt them, or you drop them off the table, and they`re not the same shape anymore.\par \par \uc2 I still walk around with it, and I wish I couldn`t, and I pray and I do everything, but it`s always there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is always there.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: There is always some residual thing there. I`m a tough guy, trust me, and if it gets to me, I can imagine it gets to a lot of really strong men.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "First Blood"/Orion Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Following the success of rocky, Stallone co-wrote and starred in another blockbuster.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "First Blood"/Orion Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell me what John Rambo means to you and how he came about?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: John Rambo is definitely my alter ego. It`s -- he is my father. That`s 100 percent. That`s all I had to do is clone him. But the difference is, Rambo is sad.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "First Blood"/Orion Pictures)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Rambo is a disenfranchised child. He was a child of America, and America asked him to do a chore. He did the chore --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then America rejected him.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: They rejected him. And I go, I can relate to this, so I gave it that kind of empathy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: After two hit franchises early in his career, he endured a series of box office flops.\par \par \uc2 So there was a big drought in your career.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Huge!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your agents dropped you.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Well, we had locusts and frogs and drought and earthquake. It was more than a drought. It was about eight years of spider webs on the phone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what are you thinking about yourself during that time? How do you --\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: I was crumbling. I was my -- you know, like self- righteousness was crumbling, and I realized my daughters had no idea what I did for a living. They`ve never seen me act.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That changed when Rocky Balboa made a comeback in 2006.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: The studio said "Over our dead body. You`re done. Rocky is done."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he persisted.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky Balboa"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: That was going to be my last film, because I just didn`t want to go out a total loser, especially on "Rocky" and it came out pretty well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Then I go, maybe you`ve got one more in you, and then I do "Rambo."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rambo"/2008/\uc2 Lionsgate\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Expendables"/Lionsgate)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: And then maybe one more. Then I did "Expendables."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the work, well, it keeps coming.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Tulsa King"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s currently filming Season Four of his hit show, "Tulsa King," but there`s still one thing Stallone wants fans to get right about his cinematic origin story.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Gayle, the toughest thing about "Rocky" and even to this day, I bristle when I hear it`s a sports movie. It`s not.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: It`s a love story. It starts with love, this guy has been chosen, and then this girl --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: He realizes he can`t win, and this is my biography, I can`t win. And then I come home, and I`m lying there next to Adrianne, I go, I can`t win. He`s just too good. It`s impossible.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says?\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: What are we going to do?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The "we" part.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: And I go, there it is now.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: They go. But no, it`s boxing and I say, I am begging you, this movie will rise and fall on love, not fight.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rocky"/United Artists)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE: Everyone can sort of go, oh my God. It`s the little things in life. It`s the love, it`s the nurturing, that`s the victory.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can watch the 48th Annual Kennedy Center Honors on Tuesday, December 23rd at 8:00 Eastern and Pacific here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 I just have to say this is one of my favorite interviews I`ve ever done. I have a huge girl crush on him, and his wife, Jennifer, knows it. Number one, he is so thoughtful and so smart.\par \par \uc2 Did you all know he`s written over 40 scripts that have made it to the screen over the course of his career? I did not know that. He`s had at least one hit movie T.V. show in every decade from 1970 through the 20s.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think people really understand the breadth and the depth of the man and his work and I was so blown away by all the back stories that he had, the emotion he showed. I think that, you know, I`m sure he might feel a little sensitive about that, but I think people got to see there`s really strength in the vulnerability of that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s incredible strength.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I was so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Go ahead, we are going to have the same reaction.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was so touched!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and I`m thankful that you left that in there, because showing that vulnerability is strength and I think a lot of men need to see other men cry and tap into that emotion.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, that was one of the longer pieces we`ve done and I wanted more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it was long.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Please tell me, there`s more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wanted -- believe me, I wanted more, too, and I was told no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do we have an extended cut that we are putting on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we have more, but no -- but no, but I think that -- shoutout to you, Katie Smith (sp?), on my team. She took the lead on this one, because I fought a bit against opening with "Rocky" because I said, how cliche, everybody -- but it worked, so you were right, I was wrong. Craig Shea (sp?), who edited, knockout piece.\par \par \uc2 And special thank you to Jennifer, his lovely wife, because, because of Jennifer, we got the "Rocky" movie number six. She read the screenplay. She was choked up, and she advocated for him to do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s got three lovely daughters and a son, and he`s just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s an amazing, amazing individual.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanks to Sly for being so present. It means a lot. What he said about parenting, it means a lot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He hated his name Sly when he was little, but now he does --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Sly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was still talking --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, Jack Black and Paul Rudd tell us about their very funny new movie, "Anaconda" and what`s behind their comedic chemistry on screen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Anaconda"/Sony Pictures Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We know that song!\par \par \uc2 We welcome you back to the CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Our next guests are the hysterical actors, even I did, Jack Black and Paul Rudd, they are slithering stars, see -- of the new comedy, it is called "Anaconda."\par \par \uc2 So this movie follows best friends, Doug, that`s played by Jack Black and Griff that`s played by Paul Rudd. So after a midlife crisis, they try to fulfill a lifelong dream of remaking their favorite movie 1997`s "Anaconda." Let`s just say things do not go according to plan. An actual giant Anaconda appears on the movie set.\par \par \uc2 Jack and Paul dropped by the studio just yesterday, but before we get to our conversation, here`s a preview of the film.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Anaconda"/Sony Pictures Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 Jack Black and Paul Rudd are with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Hello, hello. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK ("Anaconda" actor): Hello!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to comment on that later, but I`ve just got to go to that scene, because, guys, I went to this movie, and I don`t want to be insulting. I didn`t see the first "Anaconda." I said, it`s not going to be my thing. It`s about snakes dah-dah-dah-dah!\par \par \uc2 So I went only because you two were coming, and before I knew it, I almost peed on myself. It is hilarious.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD ("Anaconda" actor): That`s better than a five-star review.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That scene right there in particular. You have to put it in context later, but let`s talk about the two of you, because you`re longtime friends. You know each other. You`ve been in movies, but this is the first time you`ve been in a role together as leads.\par \par \uc2 Is that a good dynamic? Is it hard? Is it easy? Paul, you go first.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Yes, well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was there jealousy involved?\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Not -- no jealousy, from my perspective --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: It is just sheer unfiltered, unadulterated enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Because I`ve wanted to work with Jack, kind of in this context.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have?\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: This is such a proper -- like the entire film, not just a scene, which we have done in the past --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: My whole career, I`m such a huge fan of his. So to get to actually not only work with Jack --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Anaconda"/Sony Pictures Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: -- but hang out with him, spend time. We spent months battling snakes in the Amazon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: So this was like, it was really, it was just one of the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you were looking for --\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: -- a great life experience --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: -- and just fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you worried, Jack? Because really, when you have two superstars together, it doesn`t always go according to plan.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Come on!\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: I was not worried. Look, this industry is like a high wire act, and you`re always like wanting to make the right decisions, and you want every decision to be a slam dunk. This can`t lose.\par \par \uc2 But this was a situation where I loved the filmmakers` earlier film they did called --\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: "The Unbearable Weight of Massive Talent."\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: And then I found out, Paul Rudd is already attached to this movie, and I`m like, this is a slam dunk. I want to work with him. I`ve been dying to work with him and the script was so funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is funny.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Anaconda"/Sony Pictures Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s hilarious!\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: That scene that we just saw is worth the price of admish --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: It`s a huge laugh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: And there`s like several worth the price of admish scenes in this movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The other thing that struck me is that it`s so laugh out loud funny. I thought they had to do a lot of improvisation. Did you? or was it just on the written page? Because I said they are making this up on the fly.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Oh, well, that`s good and it felt like we were making it on the fly. We weren`t really making it up on the fly.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: They had a great script.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: It was really funny. But at the end, like we would do a scene, and at the end of every shot, we would say, should we get one more (bleep) and giggles, is what we call it in the industry.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: But this guy -- this guy`s the miracle improv. You`ve got a beautiful mind. I couldn`t believe the work you had about your improv.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jack, I think you`re pretty good at improv, too.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: The only problem is that I think this -- I thought it was funny to wear the snake tied around my neck, and it was for a while --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What snake tied around your neck?\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: I feel like, it is cutting off the blood flow to my brain.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: It almost looks like you have whiplash, and you`ve got kind of like the, you know, the Brady Bunch guy that was in Robert Reed, threw the suitcase there.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: See that you take that thing off.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: There we go. It looks like a big bowtie, too. It is a constrictor. Well, this is why. It`s a boa constrictor.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Thank you. Much better. Oh, God!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I also say something about the music? I love the music in the movie. What role did music play for you all in that movie? I love the music choices.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: There is that, that that great theme --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "I don`t want to wait!"\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Yes, Paula Cole --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "For our lives to be over."\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: And that kind of comes full circle in that movie.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Yes, Tom Gormican, he used music really well in the movie and there`s some great needle drops, we like to say.\par \par \uc2 The score is really, really fantastic, but also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The cameos are good.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Yes. The cameos. And then you hear some like back and black. You get some really good jams in there that get the blood pumping. It was fun to watch it. The premiere is probably my favorite premiere of ever been.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why? Why?\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Because the crowd was losing their mind. My wife, who never likes my movies, was laughing her ass off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: My agent pissed a little bit, an actual urine. These are the reviews you want.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: That`s it. We want everyone to pee themselves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jack, I can so relate to that. I`m not kidding. I can`t stress enough how much I enjoyed this movie and the two of you together.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Before you leave, though, because we`ve had such a tough week with the death of Rob Reiner, I know both of you knew him well.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you want to say about him? Because I`m still trying to process the tragedy of that -- of what happened to him and Michele.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: I never got to work with Rob Reiner. I did not know him, but I loved him. I mean, I owe so much to him just in terms of inspiration. There would be no "Tenacious D" there would be no "School of Rock" without --\par \par \uc2 "Spinal Tap" was everything to me.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: I think it`s so tragic. It`s so awful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Hard to believe, and it`s like when people are talking about it, then all of a sudden, they`re talking about his films, and I think people should really take a step back and say, oh my God, of course, he`s one of the greatest directors ever, a great actor and a real activist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: And really, I mean an awful, awful, awful thing that happened, but a beloved person, so is his wife.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: I mean, every -- this entire, entire town is in shock.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And most people are remembering them that way.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Most people are remembering him that.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Yes, as they should.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, as they should, and we will leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, guys.\par \par \uc2 JACK BLACK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RUDD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anaconda, by the way, is in theaters on Christmas Day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Christmas Day is only eight days away. If you`re still on the hunt for Christmas presents, Sarah Gelman here of Amazon Books is going to tell you what could make a great gift. Isn`t that, right?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN (Amazon Books editorial director): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, if you are in search of some final gift ideas, Amazon Books editorial director, Sarah Gelman has you covered. She`s the gift that keeps on giving.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is here with her recommendations for books that make great presents this holiday season. So scan the Q.R. code on your screen right now to buy some of these recommendations that you are going to hear.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so you say "Good Things" is the perfect gift for foodies.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes. Everyone has a foodie on their list, and "Good Things" is one of my favorite cookbooks this year. First of all, it`s gorgeous. I mean, look at it. It has recipes that I actually want to cook and can cook.\par \par \uc2 And her writing is really beautiful. She tells these very personal stories in the book. So you can read it like a book. You sit down and read it, and her name might sound familiar, so she wrote "Salt, Fat, Acid, Heat," and the subsequent documentary. And this book is all about how cooking for people, including yourself, is the ultimate labor of love and I feel like that`s a great sentiment this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is totally true. This novel, "Theo of Golden" people are talking about.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Everyone is talking about this book. So, I think this is great for moms and grandmas, all their friends are talking about it. So one day, an older man named Theo comes to a town called Golden, Georgia, and through acts of thoughtful kindness, he makes connections with people in the town.\par \par \uc2 So who Theo is and why he`s in Golden is the central mystery in this book, and it really has echoes of a parable in it. This is a book. There are no dry eyes. No one`s leaving this with dry eyes, but it`s a great book for the season of giving.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And lucky you, you`ve got a book for Swifties in the audience.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I already want one. I bet you want it, too, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I was thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You wanted, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, tell me about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Taylor Swift all the songs. Is she behind this?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Well, it`s in conjunction. So I am a Swiftie. I sat down and lost myself in this book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re a Swiftie, too?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Obviously, I mean, come on, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh!\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I sat down, lost myself for an hour. So this takes every song, album by album, song by song, starting with her self-titled debut through The Eras Tour, and you`re learning background about the songs.\par \par \uc2 So I did not know "Love Story" was written in 20 minutes while she was sitting on her bedroom floor after her parents forbade her from going out with a guy she likes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like this.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some cool little nuggets about each song.\par \par \uc2 "Dune: The Graphic Novel" for young readers --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- or young adult readers.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: So, through the classic 1965 book, this is retold as a graphic novel. So for sci-fi fans, young adults and reluctant readers, so graphic novels are well known as a gateway into reading for reluctant readers.\par \par \uc2 I also love to give books that are based on movies to people that aren`t necessarily voracious readers because they recognize the story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like reluctant readers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I also like arts and crafts projects that are not messy, and you`ve got "My First Paint by Sticker." Take us --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Spoken like -- spoken like a parent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Me, too. So my kids and I love to do these paint by sticker books. This is for kids ages three to five, and this is basically a fine motor exercise in disguise of an adorable book so with farm animals and it has fun facts, like, chickens are descended from dinosaurs. I had no idea.\par \par \uc2 And parents will love, it`s mess free and screen free.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Teddy wrote his note to Santa in a Sharpie, and it went right through. So it`s now on the table forever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay!\par \par \uc2 Are chickens really descendants, or that`s just part of that book?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Apparently, they are. No, I mean, the facts in the book has got to be true, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So they have dino nuggets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have "Dog Only Knows."\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, these are portraits by artist, Alison Friend. They are dogs in anthropomorphic poses. That`s a hard word to say --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anthropomor -- what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Like, yes, like doing human things, like reading a book --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: -- eating a donut.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And I had this book on my table when I hosted Thanksgiving, and everyone loved it. They`re passing it around. It was a big hit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about a book of puzzles? Real quick.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Okay, so I am giving this book to everyone this holiday season. If you are a puzzle fan, if you like Wordle, connections, crossword puzzles, this is the book for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Sarah Gelman, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, to buy Amazon`s best books to gift someone, scan the Q.R. code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an emotional day, Gayle. Don`t you feel emotional?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is emotional -- yes, we had Sly Stallone, and now we have Tony Dokoupil around here, both, you know what`s funny about Sly is he was always told he wasn`t good looking as a kid. He`s very handsome as are you. There is a connection between Sly -- very good looking guys, smart too.\par \par \uc2 So ahead, we`re going to take a look back and how shall we say, Nate, some of Tony`s biggest moments?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are these the kinds ones?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Memorable moments.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or we still get made fun over here?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sincere moments.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As we get ready to say goodbye to Tony as he moves across the hall to evening news.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And transfer to a new department.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, y`all coming in with Boyz II Men.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Man!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen, this morning --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s the end of the road.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- is truly a bittersweet day for all of us here --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- at \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings, as you probably heard, Tony is trading in that seat for one behind the desk, beginning a new chapter just down the hall as anchor of the "CBS Evening News" and it is so well-deserved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony`s last day is today with us, and as we get ready to say goodbye, let`s take a look back at some of his time here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 So we pulled together some of Tony`s finest moments to show him how much he means to us. And of course, all of you at home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your manual labor has arrived.\par \par \uc2 And it looks like this! Yo! What?\par \par \uc2 Whoa! I`ve got one.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nice! This could be dinner.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 MAN: Nice one!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa! Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Eloise Judy Baird Dokoupil was born late Thursday, May 13th.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoever said "sleeps like a baby" did not have a baby anywhere near them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s go!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s do this!\par \par \uc2 MAN: That was both ends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: These are my reconciliation. He`s not a fan.\par \par \uc2 Who wants to play some blackjack?\par \par \uc2 See you, ladies!\par \par \uc2 MAN: Here, take the saw!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes!\par \par \uc2 MAN: Take the saw!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can I roam with the saw?\par \par \uc2 MAN: No! No!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate! Nate! Come on! I`ve got it.\par \par \uc2 I`m a man of the people. Don`t be fooled by these by the suit and tie this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Claus and I are heading over to the table to see what the Three Stooges are doing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at our looks. They`re complaining that we -- we didn`t call each other Tony, did we?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, we didn`t.\par \par \uc2 Okay, shorty! Go to a party. Yes, I think so.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, you okay, Tony, yes! Yes, that`s what I am talking about. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 Whoa!\par \par \uc2 Whoa! Whoa! Whoa! Whoa!\par \par \uc2 I was an outfielder, and they used to make fun of my arms.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When was the last time you took a bath?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re going to be good with these for a little while. It`s 104 diapers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Perfect!\par \par \uc2 MAN: There you go! There you go! Last one! Last one! Get there. Get there. Get there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I heard!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Cheers! Make you another drink --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember all of those.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It wouldn`t be a celebration without some cake, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Guys, this is really --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little cake, a little champagne to toast you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right! That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are so happy for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s toast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Toast. I am so thrilled.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is so wonderful for you. We`re sad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a bittersweet day. It`s really hard to find the words for what this day means, I have full such enormous gratitude for what the show has been for me and for -- you`ve made me a better person, a better husband, a better father, to work with such talented and curious and above all, forgiving people, because it doesn`t always go perfect on a morning show.\par \par \uc2 It has been the joy of my life, and it`s been a privilege to our viewers to just be in your homes for all of these years, almost seven years together, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but look at the memories that we`ve created.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It has been incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now -- but now, Tony, you`re going to make a whole lot of new memories, which I`m really -- we are all -- we`ll see the room in a second. Everybody is really happy and cheering for you. Always.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, we want an Emmy together here, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We did win an Emmy together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Best live news show --\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We did! We did!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- on any hour of the dial.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You are not just a consummate professional and journalist. You`re also a good friend. I think what those images show is how we, as a crew, have been over the course of the last seven years together or so. We`re friends, we`re family. We`ll always be no matter where you are, no matter where you go in the building, we`ll be cheering you on, always.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`ll say this, listen, there`s such a rich history here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 . And of course, when it comes to evening news, I don`t believe there`s anybody better and you are not only going to bring your intelligence, your candor, your sensitivity, but you`re also going to add value to that show into this network.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes! Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So change can be sad, it can be painful, but change can also be beautiful. And this is a beautiful thing for you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is the most gratifying professional experience of my life, and I am going to carry it with me every day in this new job and every day of my life. So thank you to everybody.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right, everybody is here to cheer you on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, one last time! One last time! Can we get a -- can we get an "Oh Tony" on three?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, Tony!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP shout "Oh Tony!")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love you all. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good! Cheers!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love you, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That does it for us. They`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020251217elch00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For December 14, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Hanson, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 December 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: breaking overnight, mass shootings at Brown University and now at a Hanukkah celebration in Australia.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our exclusive interview with Venezuelan opposition leader and Nobel Peace Prize winner Maria Corina Machado.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yesterday, the commander in chief paid tribute to our nation`s finest in that time-honored American tradition, the Army-Navy football game.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): We all set?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: It is tails.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Army won the coin toss, but Navy won the game on what was a sobering Saturday. Earlier, President Trump said the U.S. would respond to an \uc2 ISIS\uc2  attack in Syria that killed two American soldiers and a civilian interpreter.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Will the U.S. retaliate against \uc2 ISIS\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: As the foreign policy challenges continue for Mr. Trump, so do Americans` concerns about high prices, particularly painful this year as the holiday shopping season peaks.\par \par \uc2 At least 20 million Americans also face skyrocketing health care premiums under Obamacare in the new year. We will talk with White House chief economic adviser Kevin Hassett, Louisiana Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , and Virginia Democratic Senator Mark Warner.\par \par \uc2 Plus, in a conversation you will see only on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , Venezuelan opposition leader Maria Corina Machado tells us why she`s so committed to democracy in her country, despite the risks she faces.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 On this first day of Hanukkah, a horrific scene is unfolding nearly 10,000 miles away in the suburbs of Sydney, Australia, where gunmen attacked a Jewish holiday gathering at the city`s Bondi Beach, a popular tourist spot.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: At least 11 people have been killed and another 29 injured in an attack the Australian prime minister has called an act of evil, antisemitism and terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Video footage appears to show two gunmen on a bridge, one of them firing his weapon. Another clip shows what appears to be a bystander tackling and disarming one of the gunmen. Police confirm there were at least two suspects. One is dead, the other hospitalized following the attack, and they are looking into the possibility of additional suspects.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S., Saturday marked what`s at least the 39th school shooting on a college campus this year.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent Tom Hanson reports from Providence, Rhode Island, from the Brown University campus, where the attack occurred.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voice-over): After a night on lockdown at Brown University...\par \par \uc2 BRETT SMILEY (D-Mayor of Providence, Rhode Island): We have detained a person of interest involved in yesterday`s shooting.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: ... this morning, a brief exhale for a campus still on edge.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BRETT SMILEY: Seven individuals in stable condition, one in critical, but stable condition, and one has been discharged.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: It is a confirmed shooting.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: The first calls came in just after 4:00 p.m. Saturday.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: I`m being advised 167 Brook in the building, in the stairway, there`s a person shot.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: The university president says the shooting took place in the middle of a final exam review session at the university`s engineering and physics building.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And there was a huge response immediately. You can just look out the window, there`s tons of cop cars, tons of fire trucks. It was a lot.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: As some students evacuated, Brown`s campus, normally open and walkable, became a place to hide, the sense of security at this Ivy League institution now broken.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA PAXSON\uc2  (President, Brown University): We have a lot of recovery ahead of us. So my main goal in the coming days and weeks and months, it will take a long time, is to bring people back together and help them feel safe and secure on campus.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And there is still no word yet on a motive, but, according to one law enforcement source, the person of interest is 24 and did not attend Brown University.\par \par \uc2 Now, meanwhile, all remaining final exams have been postponed as investigators work with prosecutors to collect more evidence and conduct more interviews -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s Tom Hanson reporting from Providence, Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 We turn to our exclusive conversation with Venezuelan opposition leader Maria Corina Machado, who spoke to us Friday from Oslo, where she just received the Nobel Peace Prize. To get there, she traveled secretly by land, sea and air, as she faces arrest by the Maduro regime.\par \par \uc2 We wanted to hear about that journey, but she refused to talk about it, citing security concerns for those who helped her, although she has previously said she received support from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO (Venezuela Opposition Leader): I am not going to give more information regarding my trip to Norway.\par \par \uc2 But what am I going to say, how important it is for the Venezuelan people. This is a recognition to a nation that has fought tirelessly, courageously against a criminal narco-terrorist structure. And -- and I came to receive that prize, that award, and I`m going to bring it back home to the Venezuelan people as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You say you`re going back home. I know you sent your own children out of Venezuelan territory in order to protect them, your sons, your daughter.\par \par \uc2 Are they concerned that you plan to return to Venezuela? When are you going?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Well, of course they are concerned, as any other Venezuelan child that has their parents or relatives back in our country, because the regime, as I said, has persecuted, tortured, killed, disappeared thousands of Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 And, recently, it has turned even more violent, because, you know, when -- when a criminal regime is falling apart and they know their days are numbered, they turn even more aggressive, even more violent.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you help us understand what is going on? Because we are seeing here in the U.S. an increase in the pressure campaign, more sanctions on Venezuelan individuals and vessels. We saw an armed seizure of a vessel carrying oil out of Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 Selling oil on the black market is really important money for the Maduro regime. Do you endorse this idea of more seizures and possibly even a blockade?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Look, I absolutely support President Trump`s strategy.\par \par \uc2 And we, the Venezuelan people, are very grateful to him and to his administration, because I believe he is a champion of freedom in this hemisphere. And that`s why -- and I say this from Oslo right now -- I have dedicated this award to him, because I think that he finally has put Venezuela in where it should be, in terms of a priority for the United States` national security.\par \par \uc2 And we do support these actions because, Margaret, we are facing not a conventional dictatorship. This is a very complex criminal structure that has turned Venezuela into a safe haven of international crime and terrorist activities, starting with Russia, Iran, Cuba, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , the Colombian guerrilla, the drug cartels operating freely and directed in partnership with Maduro and his regime.\par \par \uc2 And, as every criminal structure is -- suffers is when the inflows from their criminal activities are cut. And these, in the case of Maduro regime, comes from the oil black market, to drug trafficking, gold smuggling, arms smuggling, even human smuggling and trafficking.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It sounds like you support more sanctions and possibly more seizures of oil. But isn`t there a risk that cutting off money will further hurt the already impoverished people of Venezuela? Isn`t that a risk?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Of course, what we`re doing is for the well-being of the Venezuelan people. What we want to do is to save lives.\par \par \uc2 But Maduro was the one who declared a war on the Venezuelan people, a war we didn`t want, a war we are suffering with hundreds of thousands of killings and forced executions in the last years. And, right now, I want to be very clear with the international community.\par \par \uc2 The resources Maduro gets are not going to schools or hospitals. In Venezuela, a teacher earns $1 a day. Pensions are less than $1 a month. Our children go twice a week to school. The sources, the cash the regime gets from these illegal activities goes to buy arms, to pay gang members, to spy and infiltrate, and to even further increase their illegal narcotics activities and so on.\par \par \uc2 So these resources are not going for to -- towards the people. They`re going for corruption and crime.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So it sounds like you want even more seizures and a blockade. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: We want every legal action through law enforcement approach, not only from the United States, also from other Caribbean, Latin American and European countries, that further block the illegal activities of the regime.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because we need to increase the cost of staying in power by force. Once you arrive to that point in which the cost of staying in power is higher than the cost of leaving power, the regime will fall apart. And it`s the moment where we, you know, advance into a negotiated transition, which is what in the beginning we offered Maduro when we won the presidential election by a landslide last year.\par \par \uc2 But he not only refused that option, but he, as you know, unleashed the worst, most brutal repression wave we`ve seen in our history.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And eight million Venezuelans have fled...\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... since he came to power, during that time.\par \par \uc2 But, in terms of the diplomacy, President Trump spoke with Maduro himself last month. We haven`t seen any evidence, though, that diplomacy, a negotiated exit like you mentioned, is happening. Are you seeing diplomatic success?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Well, in the last years, Venezuela has seen 17 dialogue initiatives, not once, not twice, 17.\par \par \uc2 Every single time, the opposition or our allies or the United States have complied with every single thing we offered. The regime of the country has violated every single thing they offered on their own part. What did they get? They got time. They got legitimacy. They got money.\par \par \uc2 And that`s precisely what they think they can get again -- once again. But, certainly, things have changed very much in the last months. Venezuela is a total different country. I mean, Maduro is weaker than ever. The armed forces, police are certainly divided and fractured, and our country is united, cohesive.\par \par \uc2 And we finally have the administration, in this case, President Trump, with a clear strategy that truly represents a credible threat for the regime. So if we had -- ever had a chance to finally move ahead into -- towards democracy in our country, it`s today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What evidence do you have that the military is weaker than ever? Was your escape proof of that?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: We`ve seen more and more members of the military finding ways to connect with us, to send messages to -- to -- to show that they are feeling the same of what the rest of the country is feeling.\par \par \uc2 When -- when they -- this military go back home, they find their children, their mother, their partners saying that they are hungry, that they are persecuted, that their families have been divided. They are scared. Yes, they are scared, because repression and persecution in the military is even tougher than on civilians.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, President Trump said that land strikes to stop drug trafficking specifically will start soon. He has been saying that for weeks. Do you know what he is talking about? And has he promised you anything?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: No, I don`t know. And well, if I knew, I wouldn`t say it, of course. But it`s not the case.\par \par \uc2 We are not involved and we will not get involved into another nation`s policy for their own national security.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But would you welcome U.S. military action?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: I will welcome more and more pressure so that Maduro understands that he has to go, that his time is over.\par \par \uc2 And I will insist something that I have said several times before. This is not conventional regime change. This cannot be compared to other cases, like countries in the Middle East. We had an election. Regime change was already mandated by over 70 percent of the population. And what we need is support to enforce that decision.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, how do you square military action with receiving a Peace Prize? Are we at the point it`s necessary?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Well, what we`re fighting for is precisely freedom in order to have democracy, and democracy in order to have peace.\par \par \uc2 And in order to maintain freedom and to achieve freedom, you do need strength. The contrary would be the peace of the dead, in which you have a country under absolute oppression and simply giving in. And that`s not the case.\par \par \uc2 It`s absolutely absurd that you see a regime, a criminal regime like Maduro, that gets support from Russia or from Iran, and then ask the Venezuelan people not to ask support from democratic countries and democratic leaders.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: That`s absolute absurd. We do not have arms. We have our will. We have the power of organization and the power of love, which, at the end, I have to say that peace is ultimately an act of love.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Maduro regime maintains it won the last election and considers Machado a fugitive. The regime also refers to U.S. actions as illegal and brutal interventionism.\par \par \uc2 In our next half-hour, we will speak with Machado about what`s ahead in a potential post-Maduro era in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 And we will be back in one minute, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re now joined by the top Democrat on the Senate Intelligence Committee, Mark Warner of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Senator, were you persuaded there by Maria Corina Machado`s argument that military action, pressure is virtuous and necessary?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (D-Virginia): Well, first of all, let me -- at the top of the show, we have got this tragic shooting at Brown University and then this obviously political strike against the Jewish community in Australia, both horrible, and particularly on the first day of Hanukkah, which is the celebration of light over darkness.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: And, boy, we could use a little more light these days.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Machado is a hero. She deserves to win the Nobel Prize. And one of the mistakes the Biden administration made last year, when the opposition candidates won overwhelmingly, was not put more pressure on Maduro then to leave. But the action now that Trump has chosen Maduro, who`s a bad guy, and saying -- and I`m not sure what his policy is, but at the very same time is pardoning, for example, a Honduran former president who was convicted in America of drug running.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: The president, who has not been willing to stand up strong against Putin, who`s probably the most brutal authoritarian leader in the world, as we go on and off with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s -- yes, it`d be great to get rid of Maduro. But in terms of the question...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: ... \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has not come to the American people and said, here are our goals in Venezuela. We have got troops potentially in harm`s way. We have got boat actions, for example, where we`re blowing up boats where we can obviously interdict, as we saw with the oil tanker.\par \par \uc2 I think the president needs to make the case if he`s going to try to put boots on the ground in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, at this point, all they have, as I understand it, asked for and justified to Congress were these strikes on the alleged drug boats, nothing with sovereign territory yet.\par \par \uc2 But that gets to your argument about, what is the end goal? Unlike other Democrats, when it comes to the drug boats, you have said you`re not ready to call them war crimes, particularly what happened on the September 2 double tap strike. You have questioned Admiral Bradley.\par \par \uc2 I know you want the video released to the public. What do you think that`s going to show to the public? What will that change?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, I think the video is chilling, disturbing, whatever term you want to use.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Because the idea of these two survivors hanging on to remnants of a boat, and then America taking a series of additional strikes. The Congress needs to see it, at the very minimum. I think the American public needs to see it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have seen it?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: I have seen it. It is -- it is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Only a limited number have.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: It is extraordinarily chilling.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: What I`m saying is, once you make the accusation of war crime, you can`t take that back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: And so what I have said...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But many of your Democrat colleagues have.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: And I know that.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I have said to the chairman of the Joint Chiefs, I want to see the written execution order. I want to see the legal opinion that was about this specific strike. I want to know whether the overarching policy memorandum, which was not finished until September 5, three days after the strike, was it changed?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You -- I saw you said that that`s not typical, is it? Don`t you normally come up with the legal justification before you take the action, rather than three days after it?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, this is where I have a question.\par \par \uc2 The justification was made in late July. They didn`t write it down until September 5.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Can -- I don`t have the answer.\par \par \uc2 I also want to see the after-action report. I want to give the military every opportunity to make their case, because once you -- again, once you make that claim, what it would do to how we view our military, morale, how we`re viewed in the world, it`s so important.\par \par \uc2 But this president has not -- and, frankly, Pete Hegseth even worse. Here`s the guy who goes out and brags about the strikes, until there`s controversy, and then he kind of runs for the hills.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the president said again this week -- and I will just read it to you -- that land strikes on Venezuela are being considered.\par \par \uc2 "Land strikes on horrible people that are bringing in drugs and killing our people. It doesn`t necessarily have to be in Venezuela. It`s people that are bringing in drugs to our country as targets."\par \par \uc2 Have you seen any legal justification to hit these people now designated as terrorists on sovereign territory, whether it`s Mexico or Colombia or Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: The vast majority of the drug -- particularly -- this is all cocaine. This is not fentanyl. The vast...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In terms of the drug boats that they hit.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: The vast majority is actually in the Eastern Pacific, yet most of the strikes have been in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: And does this mean that suddenly you`re going to be open warfare with all of American military against any and all drug dealers?\par \par \uc2 Again, the drug dealers are awful. We need to do all we can to stop them. But the president, when he puts American forces potentially in harm`s way, needs to come and explain to the American people what the goal is here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Particularly when he`s then, at the same breath, pardoning the former president of Honduras, who is a convicted drug dealer.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In terms of designated terrorists, \uc2 ISIS\uc2  is a designated terrorist group. We just saw the first combat deaths, essentially, of the Trump administration yesterday, three Americans killed, three injured in Syria in this apparent ambush.\par \par \uc2 The head of the \uc2 National Counterterrorism Center\uc2  said it was an insider attack. Are you supportive of keeping the nearly 1,000 U.S. troops in Syria? Do you have concerns that the Syrian government isn`t able to keep these infiltrators out?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, first of all, condolences to the three Americans` families, armed forces members, one an interpreter.\par \par \uc2 I think our troops have played an important role. We are working closely with, for example, the Kurds. We have literally tens of thousands of \uc2 ISIS\uc2  family members in certain prisons in that area. But the president...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you want that to continue?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: But the president -- I think pulling out without a plan would be a mistake, but the president, again, has sent mixed signals.\par \par \uc2 I mean, months ago, he said he`s thinking about taking all those American soldiers out without articulating a strategy. I mean, the president, if he wants to make the case to the American people of when our soldiers are in harm`s way, and particularly when we`re talking about the Caribbean, and now potential Venezuela, this has not been an overnight -- this buildup of forces been months.\par \par \uc2 He owes it to all of us to come in and explain what his goal is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Domestically, we have Senator Cassidy on the program. We taped with him earlier.\par \par \uc2 He says he`s looking for fellow Democrats to join him in a health care deal by March to make these health savings accounts. And he said he will offer to extend the existing subsidies for health care premiums.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, first of all...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you be on board with that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: ... we have got a crisis that hits as of Monday, tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Because that`s when people have to sign up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He says, retroactively, you can do it.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, listen, it`s a mess.\par \par \uc2 Once you have signed up or you have dropped out of the system, the idea that you`re retroactively going to fix it, fix it now, so people don`t go into Christmas thinking they`re going to not know if they will be able to afford health care. And the notion...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But nothing is happening.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: But the notion that what -- and \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  is a good guy. I have worked with him in a lot of different efforts.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that a one-time $1,500 payment into your pocket is going to suddenly make up -- I had a person on -- the other day, her current insurance is $544 a month.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: It`ll go up to over $1,500 a month.\par \par \uc2 A one-time $1,500 payment is not going to meet her needs...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: ... and particularly if that would mean that things like preexisting condition protections would disappear.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That sounds like you`re not on board in these remaining days of 2025.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I have to...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Fix the crisis right now. Extend the subsidies, and then let`s debate how we fix it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, we have to -- we have to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to "FACE THE NATION." We`re joined now by the director of the National Economic Council, Kevin Hassett.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT (National Economic Council Director): It`s great to be back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, people are holiday shopping. They`re aware of prices. The president said this week prices are coming down tremendously. The latest data, though, from the Consumer Price Index is up three percent year over year, Personal Consumption Index is up 2.8 percent year over year. What data is he looking at? What`s your benchmark?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Right. Well, one the things that, if you saw his presentation in Pennsylvania, as he put up a bunch of charts, which he loves to do, where he went through the individual items that have -- we`ve already sort of made a bunch of progress on.\par \par \uc2 And so, for example, under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , prescription drugs were up nine percent. So far this year they`re down 0.6 percent. Gasoline is way down. It was like the highest ever under Biden.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Eggs. He was talking about eggs. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes, he was talking about eggs.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think the -- the way to think about inflation, of course, is that there are like micro effects, like the time that we had the avian flu affecting drug prices.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And then macro effects, which is runaway spending, big deficits and an accommodated Fed policy. And I think that --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And now tariffs adding to some price increases according to the Fed chair.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, I -- you know, I think that the tariff evidence is mixed. But the point is that we`ve got deficit going way, way down. So, right now, it`s looking like the deficit for this year will be $600 billion lower than it was last year. That really helps lower inflation. We`ve got the trade deficit cut in half from last year. And so all these things are things that should continue to move us towards the Fed target of two percent.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Companies aren`t necessarily going to bring items down, though, in terms of pricing, right? I mean the president told "The Wall Street Journal," he didn`t know when all of this money, quote, "is going to kick in." I think he was talking to business investment and tariffs, "and help voters. All I can do is my job."\par \par \uc2 When do you anticipate voters will feel this positive impact?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Right. Well, it`s definitely the case that -- and you guys have been covering it a lot, that consumer sentiment right now is lower than anyone would like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: But, you know, we find that that very often happens during a government shutdown, where everybody`s really upset.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the last administration called that the vibe session, right?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: No, but --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The data`s good, but people feel bad.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: But what happened -- yes, but what`s going to happen is --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Same thing, you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: No, what`s going to happen -- I`m not saying that. What I`m saying is that people are going to see the high growth. Growth is four percent. Income growth is up about $1,200 this year. People look in their wallets and see they`re doing better, which is why we had the biggest Black Friday ever. And so on.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that the sentiment --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that`s more higher incomes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: The sentiment will catch up with the reality. Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the fact is that real purchasing power dropped by about 3,000. So far this year it`s up by about $1,200 per family. And the money that we`re talking about is like real money. So, for groceries, it went from $400 a month to $525 a month under Biden. So, that`s like an extra more than thousand dollars a year you have to spend on groceries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: It`s down this year for us, the grocery package. But it`s still got a lot of room to grow -- or to go.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A lot more to go.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the president reduced some tariffs on some of those grocery items with an eye towards that acknowledgement.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: On the food stuff. Yes, if we don`t make it here, then pretty much we don`t tariff it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let me ask you -- you mentioned gas.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Oil prices globally are relatively low.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We saw this Coast Guard interdiction with military forces boarding and seizing this vessel off the coast of Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you feel you have the leeway there, pricing-wise, to conduct more seizures, or even a blockade of oil from Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Without it hurting?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes, well, I`m not a foreign policy guy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: So, like what we do with Venezuela, whether we blockade is up to the president and Marco Rubio.\par \par \uc2 But I can say that --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you`ve got the leeway, right? Pricing (ph).\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: That what`s been going on is there`s been kind of a black market for oil amongst sort of this sanctioned countries. And what we`re doing is we`re shutting that black market down. Whether that affects prices outside of the sanctioned countries is kind of an open question. Probably not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You don`t think it will?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: No, because -- because there`s -- they`re already kind of on the ropes, these countries. They`re only getting oil when they get, like, sort of black fleets to sneak in and give them the oil.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And that`s what we`re trying to slow down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s -- OK.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about the Fed decision. The Federal Open Market Committee said job gains have slowed this year.\par \par \uc2 We also this survey from The Business Roundtable. I`m sure you saw it. They asked CEOs what they expect to happen. And they anticipate employment will decrease in 2026 rather than increase.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned about a hiring slowdown?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: The same Fed said that they were more bullish on growth for next year, right? And so, I think that we`re at a certain case --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But on the jobs picture?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: But on the jobs picture, you know, we`re going to get the data next week. And so, maybe next Sunday I`ll come back and we`ll talk about it because we`ve got two months of data coming out on Tuesday and we`ll get a clearer picture because right now we just have some very difficult to pin down surveys, like the gold standard for this will be really the household survey, which we don`t even get for October.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But these were CEOs. They`re the people who would be doing the hiring, right? And they`re saying, we`re not doing it.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: But they don`t have the broad perspective that we`ll get when he get the big data released next week.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you trust that big data?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: I trust the household survey especially. And, sadly, we won`t have one for October. But we`re going to get one for November and that`s going to be really important for thinking about where the job market is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Employment is one of the things that the Fed watches closely. And we heard again from the president that you were on this short list to be potentially the next Fed chair.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Although he was conducting interviews this week. He said you and Hoover`s \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2  are at the top of the list.\par \par \uc2 On Friday the president was asked how big of a role he wants to personally play in future Fed interest rate decisions.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think I certainly should have a role in talking to whoever the head of the Fed is, or the Fed. I don`t really -- you know, in the old days, that used to happen. Nowadays, it`s sort of -- they think it shouldn`t happen. But I`ve done great. I`ve made a lot of money. I`m very successful. I think my voice should be heard.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: As Fed chair, how frequently would you consult with the president when he says he wants his voice herd?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, I`m right now the top economic adviser for the president and I talk about almost everything with him almost every day. I`ve certainly talked about monetary policy. I think he has very strong and well-founded views about what we ought to do. But in the end, the job of the Fed is to be independent and the -- to work with the group of people that are on the Board of Governors at the FOMC, to drive a group consensus on where interest rates should be. And to do so, you know, basically with the guidance of the Fed chair. But in the end, it`s a committee that votes. And I`d be happy to talk to the president every day, you know, until both of us are dead because it`s so much fun to talk. And he`s --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Even if you were Fed chair?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Even if I were Fed chair. If I wasn`t Fed chair, I -- you know what, I would hope that \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2  would be talking to the president as well if he -- if he were Fed chair.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And voice should be heard is going to be --\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: I -- you know, every Fed chair talks to lots and lots of market experts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Equal to the other members of the -- voting members or --\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Excuse me?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would the president`s voice have equal weighting to the voting members?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: No. No. The -- he would have no weight. It`s just his opinion matters if it`s good. You know, if it`s based on data. And then if you go to the committee and you say, well, the president made this argument and that`s a really sound argument, I think, what do you think? If they reject it, then they`ll vote in a different way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood.\par \par \uc2 Well, we`ll be watching to see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thanks, as always.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Louisiana Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 . He joins us this morning from Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, your health care bill gives Americans up to $1,500 in tax-free accounts to spend on health care rather than extend these expiring \uc2 ACA\uc2  subsidies. On Thursday, your proposal failed 51-48, as did the Democratic proposal. So that means it looks like those receiving \uc2 ACA\uc2  premium subsidies will see their costs go up 114 percent. Bottom line here, did Republicans simply wait too long to come up with a health care alternative?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: Well, first, Margaret, let`s just kind of recap the question. We`ve got to do something for affordability for the people in the exchanges. And I will say that the only thing that Democrats would accept was something that would decrease the premiums. But if a family has a $6,000 out of pocket before they get into the strength of the insurance plan, then frankly the policy is catastrophic. It brings profit to the insurance company, but not benefit to the patient.\par \par \uc2 So, I would argue that it actually is -- there has to be a meeting of the minds between Democrats and Republicans. Let`s acknowledge, you`ve got to put cash in the patient`s pocket to pay the out of pocket. I would be willing to do a short-term extension of the premium tax credits for those people with higher premiums if they will concede that we`ve got to do something for the $6,000 out of pocket. I think there`s a deal to be had here. We need to push for that deal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you want to make sure that the average deductible is less than $6,000 is what I just heard you say there, and you`re open to an --\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: Absolutely. The net deductible, if you will --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: That $1,500 is per person. Give me a couple -- you know, for people over 50, give me a couple in their 50s with two children, 18 and 19. They would get $5,000 in an account, in their purse, pocketbook, you name it, in their pocket to pay the out of pocket for the insurance. Now, the insurance plan for that couple would be a $6,000 deductible. So, you end up decreasing the net deductible by doing this. That makes the policy actually functional for them. If the child has a sprained ankle and you bring to the urgent care, you can pay the bill, not kind of come with - - come at a -- come forward out of your own pocket.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I`m told that about 40 percent of Americans have less than $1,000 in their savings account. That kind of puts into relief why you need to have something in their pocket to pay the out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, I know the president has said that he likes the concept of your plan, but why didn`t the White House put its shoulder behind this before Thursday? Why didn`t they whip votes for you? Four of your Republican colleagues crossed the aisle and voted with Democrats on their proposal.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: All four of those voted for our proposal, too. And I think that shows you that there is -- there is interest in finding something which addresses both the critical problem of the out-of-pocket expense, but also the higher premiums.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But is the White House behind you on this? Like, are they helping you if you`re trying another --\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: Well, it certainly seemed like it. Their --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You want this up for another vote?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: Their initial plan had elements of what I am speaking about, helping people with that deductible. So, simply put, yes, the White House is there. But if you notice, our problem was not on the Republican side, our problem was on the Democratic side. So, what I`m saying is, is let`s just meet together.\par \par \uc2 As a physician, I can tell you, that deductible, the patient comes, she`s stressed. She can`t get the procedure, the x-ray, the whatever, because of her $6,000 deductible. Let`s just meet. Let`s address her concerns. And then this Republican would be willing to do something on a short-term extension of the premium tax credits if that`s what it takes to get the deal to help those folks with the more expensive premium, but also helping everybody with their deductible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you need more White House help on this? And, sir, you`re running out of time here. Do you really think you can get this done before January 1st?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: Our problem is not with Republicans. Our problem has been with Democrats. But, of course, they may say that about us. So, what I am kind of searching for is a deal in which both have their concerns addressed. If you do that and you put this in place, you really have until the end of, like, March to get it done because with these kind of accounts that people have money in, they just save the receipts, they submit them and they get reimburse. It happens -- it`s what we do in my family. We have a flexible spending account.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: And so, we can get this done. And I think we can meet the concerns, both about the out of pocket, but also about the premium.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I also want to ask you about what`s happening with the FDA right now. \uc2 CBS\uc2  has confirmed that there are no ongoing safety studies into the abortion drug Mifepristone. This was despite the Trump administration saying they were going to conduct one, and anti-abortion groups want this review to take place.\par \par \uc2 I saw a letter the FDA commissioner sent to you last week claiming they are reviewing the evidence. Do you believe him? And if not, what are you doing about it?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: I had a conversation with the commissioner this past week. It seems as if it`s taken a long time to get that study up and going, which suggests to me that it`s not a priority. For the pro-life community, this should be a priority. And if we`re going to make America healthy again, this seems like, my gosh, should be top priority.\par \par \uc2 So, there is -- I am disappointed, as well as others are disappointed, by the way this is being slow-walked. I think -- I think it just needs to happen. We need to have the evidence out there.\par \par \uc2 If you want to reassure the American people about something which is really top of mind, this is where they should be going.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  said that Mifepristone is safe and effective and that there are side effects in less than 1 percent of patients. I -- with that data point in place, why isn`t the White House pressing the FDA commissioner to make this happen? There`s some suggestion in reporting that it is for political concerns due to impact on the upcoming midterm races. Is this politically motivated, the slow-walking you say is happening?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: You`re asking me to look into the kind of motivations of folks with whom I`ve not had a personal conversation. I can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, did the FDA commissioners say that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: The FDA commissioners said that they`re trying to get the study together. But they`ve been in office long enough that it could be. And so, I think that it`s right to be impatient. It`s right to demand more.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if the decision starts with the FDA commissioner. You just suggested it starts in the White House. That might be the case. Point being, get it done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you have confidence in the FDA commissioner?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BILL CASSIDY: I think the FDA commissioner is working very hard. And I think that, in many cases, he`s receiving directives from on high. I don`t know that for sure, but I suspect that`s the case.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Dr. Cassidy, Senator Cassidy, we thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Here`s more of our exclusive interview with Venezuelan opposition leader, Maria Corina Machado.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The regime itself has done a number of things. They have revoked TV licenses for journalists to broadcast truthfully. He has jailed journalists. The United Nations says the national guard targeted political opponents, committed sexual violence, tortured people and committed other crimes against humanity.\par \par \uc2 So, from where you sit, is Maduro stepping down enough, or do you need the entire regime dismantled, in which case that sounds like a country in collapse.\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO (Venezuelan Opposition Leader): That`s a very good question because certainly Maduro is the head of the structure, but it`s like any other mafia system. You have families or groups that operate and sometimes even compete among each other.\par \par \uc2 There are different degrees in the crimes they have committed. Certainly those that have committed crimes against humanity, that have been reported by the fact finding missions (ph) of the United Nations should face justice. Local justice and international justice.\par \par \uc2 There are other members of the regime or the armed forces that have committed lesser crimes. And certainly we will search for justice, not revenge.\par \par \uc2 But this, I`m going to insist, what we`re living right now is chaos. Maduro represents chaos. We`re going to put order.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood. But respectfully, you also have acknowledged that the drug cartels are exploiting your country. That Russia is exploiting your country, Iran, Cuba, China.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  spoke with Maduro and said their strategic partnership is growing. Are you concerned here that Russia is going to shore up support for the military and intelligence?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Well, I`m absolutely convinced that Putin wants the world to think that, and especially wants President Trump to think that. But I would ask you a question. Do you think Putin has resources to spare at this time or does he have something that`s more urgent for him to invest those resources? I mean helping a regime that is going to fall apart anyway, regardless of what he does, I don`t think it`s a priority for him.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, I do not underestimate what the (INAUDIBLE) actors of Tarismo (ph) might be planning to do once we are in that transition to democracy. And that will require very efficient actions to isolate and neutralize those threats.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, we work very hard to understand what`s the true nature of our armed forces right now, identifying those military which we believe could be supportive of a transition to democracy and new government --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have they pledged that to you?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Some of them, yes. And we believe that over 80 percent of our armed forces will join in support the democratic movement once the transition is in place.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, tell me about that transition. What kind of support do you need to keep the country together, to prevent another refugee crisis? And do you have that pledge from the American government now?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: We will need a lot of support, advice, information, technology, not only from the United States, also from multilateral organizations, some of which we`re already working together. Not only in security issues, but power supply, food supply, how do we bring -- you know, put order in our financial accounts. How do we restructure our debt. How we open markets for international investment and give security to international investment in oil and gas and infrastructure, critical minerals. I mean, Venezuela is unique in terms of the amount of the natural endowments we have.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, we`re located three hours by plane from Florida. So, it`s hard to think, you know, in this shortening of the supply chains that is in the (INAUDIBLE) shoring process taking place, a better ally in the region than Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, you know, turning Venezuela from the criminal hub of the Americas into a security shield for the United States and for the rest of the region is a top priority for all.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we have the plans ready. We have the teams ready. We know what we have to do, the first 100 hours, and the next 100 days. And most importantly, and I want to insist on this, we have the people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would that include U.S. peacekeeping troops or other troops on the ground?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: I`m -- I cannot answer that question right now. I don`t think that`s the case. There are other countries that have offered support as well in order to strengthen democratic institutions once the government elect is in place. But the -- we will have to address that once we have the, you know, the government elect in place and put in order, bringing order back to our country.\par \par \uc2 And one thing that you mentioned for -- about the, you know, the refugee crisis, it`s going to be exactly the country. The day Maduro goes, you will see tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands of Venezuelan migrants coming back home from the United States and all over the world. I mean our diaspora is desperate to go back to Venezuela. So even from that perspective, it is a win-win situation to have democracy in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think it is safe for those Venezuelans in America to go back there now? Some temporary protected status was just revoked bit the president for more than half a million Venezuelans here. Is it safe for them?\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: Absolutely not. It will be safe once Maduro is gone and every single citizen will be, you know, living freedom and respect towards the law. We will bring back rule of law. We will liberate our political prisoners. And everybody will be free to speak out what they feel, what they think, what they want for the future of their -- for their own future and their families.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Maria Corina Machado, we thank you for your time today. And we will be watching.\par \par \uc2 MARIA CORINA MACHADO: I thank you and again (ph) for this great opportunity. And God bless Venezuela. Venezuela will be free.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our full conversation is available on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and podcast channels and cbsnews.com.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you missed the \uc2 CBS\uc2  town hall with Erika Kirk, widow of conservative activist Charlie Kirk, last night, you can see the rebroadcast tonight at 8:00 p.m. on our CBS News 24/7 streaming network.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching. Until next week. For "FACE THE NATION," I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020251214elce00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Report: Hegseth Signal Use Endangered Troops; Below Freezing Temperatures Gripping the Country; New Photos Show Epstein`s Notorious Island Home; Immigration Crackdown in New Orleans and Minneapolis; Agents Make Immigration Arrests Across New Orleans; Trump Continues Criticism of Somali Community; Ukraine Using Drone Technology to Gain Advantage; 1,600 Pounds of Meth Found in Blackberry Shipments; Olympic Flame Begins Journey to Italy; Escaped Georgia Inmate Caught on Video; Fighter Jet Explodes During Training; Doctor Sentenced for Matthew Perry`s Death; Study Tests Weight Loss Drugs on Fat Pets; Marching to the Beat of Her Own Drum; Trump Pardons Democrat Rep. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 ; A Look at Recent Controversial Trump Pardons; Remembering Guitar Legend, Steve Cropper; Tech Gifts for the Holidays; Italian Feast Gets Diners on Their Feet; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Lucy Liu on Producing and Starring in "Rosemead;" \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Charlie D`Agata, John  Elliott, Scott MacFarlane, Kati Weis, Jonah Kaplan, Holly Williams,  Vladimir Duthiers, Carter Evans, Jaime Yuccas, Ash-Har Quraishi, Nicole  Valdes \par \uc2 13,574 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are following new scrutiny of Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 . Plus, bitter cold temperatures for millions of Americans. It`s frosty!\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell CBS News, a new Pentagon investigation finds that Secretary Hegseth could have endangered troops by sharing details in a Signal chat.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): It`s mission critical piece of information that could have been used by our adversaries to blow those planes out of the sky. You just don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bitter cold settles over tens of millions of Americans as an arctic air mass threatens to break records.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: We have to go back to the 1800s to see temperatures that cold this early.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The doctor who pled guilty to supplying Matthew Perry with ketamine, is sentenced to two-and-a-half years in prison.\par \par \uc2 KEITH MORRISON (Broadcast journalist): Sometimes, apparently, nice guys do bad things.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: House Democrats released what they say are never before seen images from the infamous Epstein island in the U.S. Virgin Islands.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: \uc2 Spotify\uc2  released its Annual \uc2 Spotify\uc2  Wrapped feature.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Each year, the streaming service shows people their listening habits.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: This year, they added a listening age that tells people how old they seem.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Mine is old, they didn`t even give me a listening age. They just sent me a bottle of fiber gummies in the mail.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: It`s the era of Bad Bunny. The Puerto Rican rapper has dethroned Taylor Swift as this year`s most streamed artist.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s eye opener is presented by Progressive. Making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes! Halftime show is going to be lit!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On fire! I wish I knew what he was saying, but I love that song! I love that song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel like people are listening to it enough, they`re going to know Spanish by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`ve got some news to get to off the top here. We will begin with new trouble for Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 . A report expected from The Pentagon saying he could have endangered lives when he shared details of an upcoming airstrike in a group chat on a commercial app back in March. You remember this story?\par \par \uc2 He is already under pressure from both parties over U.S. military strikes on alleged drug boats in the Caribbean and Eastern Pacific.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is following news of this report. He`s in Washington for us. Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon says this Inspector General report is "a total exoneration" of Secretary Hegseth, but sources tell CBS News, it finds Hegseth violated \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  policies when he sent those texts in the commercial messaging app from his personal phone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The controversy surrounding Secretary of Defense \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  continues to grow this morning. Sources tell CBS News, a new Pentagon Inspector General`s report found he put U.S. servicemembers at risk by sharing sensitive details of an active bombing mission in Yemen in March in a Signal group chat with other top officials, which mistakenly included a journalist, he detailed the times F-18s would launch and "when the first bombs will definitely drop.\par \par \uc2 Hegseth denied wrongdoing when the story broke this past spring.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (\uc2 U.S. Department of Defense\uc2  Secretary): Nobody was texting war plans, and that`s all I have to say about that.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The White House said in a statement: "The review affirms no classified information was leaked," adding, "President Trump stands by Secretary Hegseth."\par \par \uc2 But the report found the key information posted by Hegseth came from a classified e-mail marked "secret," though it`s unclear whether Hegseth declassified that info beforehand.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats are voicing concern, including Virginia Senator Mark Warner, who is now calling for Hegseth to resign or be fired.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): There`s a pattern here of chaotic behavior. There`s a pattern here of kind of, I think, disrespect for our uniformed military.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Hegseth is also facing bipartisan questions about why the U.S. hit an alleged drug boat in the Caribbean a second time back in September to reportedly kill two survivors. A source familiar with the matter tells CBS News that the survivors were climbing back onto the boat and attempting to salvage some of the drugs they appeared to be communicating with other boats in the vicinity that could have come to help them.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Trump says he`s willing to release the video of the second strike, and is vowing to combat drug trafficking with strikes on land "very soon."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): I think you`re going to find that this is war, that these people were killing our people by the millions.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: So the top Navy Admiral who ordered that second strike on the alleged drug boat, that`s Admiral Frank Bradley is on Capitol Hill today to brief lawmakers about the administration`s campaign against drug traffickers in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 He is sure to face tough questions about the legality of his orders, and some lawmakers are calling that a war crime. U.S. official tells CBS News that the Chairman of Joint Chiefs-of-Staff General Dan Caine is expected to accompany Admiral Bradley to his meetings with Congress today -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Charlie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Much of the country is getting a bitter dose of winter this morning. snow in Colorado caused dozens of spin outs outside of Denver. This Arctic Blast is moving into the Midwest and Northeast with the possibility of record setting lows. So, let`s go to meteorologist John Elliott from CBS News New York.\par \par \uc2 John, good morning. I love that tie, by the way.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ELLIOTT: Because it might snow in North Carolina. I mean, this is crazy. I just want to show you this.\par \par \uc2 So far this morning, the wind chill in Duluth was 25 degrees below zero. Look at all of these subzero wind chills. Parts of the Western Lakes, Minneapolis, over to Green Bay, you may not ever even be at zero today, so just some brutal stuff there, and you were mentioning potential record lows. First off, wind chills are going to be really challenging. This afternoon. You`re going to see wind chills right around zero, Detroit down through Cleveland, you`re lucky to be at five, and we could even see some record low temperatures. These would not break record temperatures. These would smash record low temperatures by three to five degrees.\par \par \uc2 Your high in Chicago may only be three. I`ve got to tell you, this is just some brutal stuff. I want to show you a little bit of lake effect snow. I would be a little concerned about some potential squalls where you have that brief, blinding snow, so you may see some Squall Warnings, just be mindful of that.\par \par \uc2 And then the flip side of this, look at this. We`ve got heavy rain for parts of the Gulf. This is going to push into all of this cold air. So this is what I mean, ho, ho, ho! You bet that snow in Richmond and some icing elsewhere, even Memphis could see a little bit of snow and some ice.\par \par \uc2 So I know it`s cookie day, but there`s nothing sweet about this. Just make sure you`re staying careful and stay informed, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But at least we`ve got your tie to look at John. That looks good. Thank you, sir. Thank you very much. Show it off!\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Congress have released never before seen images from Jeffrey Epstein`s home in the U.S. Virgin Islands. Now, many of his accusers say they were abused there.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is in Washington, where he`s been covering the Epstein case. Scott, I saw some of the pictures yesterday. I`m really curious how a dental chair in your house makes sense.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration faces a deadline in two weeks to release the full \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  case files, but these videos released by congressional Democrats, they show a chief location of the convicted sex offender`s crimes against young women and girls.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This is the Virgin Islands home of \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 . You can see inside the bedrooms, the ocean views, the pool and the den, where a blackboard shows the words "power" and "deception" written; also, a dentist chair, which a source tells CBS News was put there for one of Epstein`s girlfriends, a European studying to be a dentist, and even a framed picture of Epstein and his co-conspirator, Ghislaine Maxwell, meeting with Pope \uc2 John Paul II\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The images were obtained by Democrats on the House Oversight Committee from authorities in the Virgin Islands.\par \par \uc2 Is one of the goals to apply more pressure on the administration to release their files?\par \par \uc2 REP. ROBERT GARCIA (D-CA): Absolutely. We`re going to -- we`re letting the administration know that we`re going to continue to get files, whether it`s through the Epstein estate, whether it`s through the Virgin Islands, whether it`s through the banks, and we`re going to put information together to ensure this investigation moves forward to get justice for the survivors.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: When CBS News visited the island by boat in 2020, people in nearby St. James told us they were aware of Epstein`s past, with some referring to the island as Pedophile Island.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Virgin Islands` government claims Epstein trafficked underage girls by helicopter. Virginia Giuffre, an Epstein survivor who died by suicide earlier this year, wrote that she was abused on the island and at a news conference at the capitol in September, survivor, Lisa Phillips described her time at Epstein`s estate there.\par \par \uc2 LISA PHILLIPS (Epstein accuser): And what I experienced there was a -- was a glimpse into a very dark and disturbing world.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Last month, amid bipartisan pressure, the President signed the bill to release the Department of Justice`s full Epstein case files, and a bipartisan group of lawmakers are now demanding an update from Attorney General Pam Bondi as the deadline for doing so is now about two weeks away.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But that release could be stalled. The President recently ordered the Department of Justice to investigate any ties between Democrats and \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 , and they could use that investigation as grounds to hold back some of those records -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Scott, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 President Trump`s immigration crackdown has spread to more major American cities, one of them is New Orleans, where federal sweeps included places like \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and Lowe`s known to attract migrants trying to find day work, day labor. It comes as the President continues to criticize Somali Americans as well living in Minneapolis and St. Paul and agents are cracking down in that community as well.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got correspondents in both cities, and we`ll start with Kati Weis in New Orleans -- Kati.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: Federal agents fanned out across the New Orleans Metropolitan Area, Wednesday. Social media showed them arresting people in a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  parking lot and outside a gas station next to a Lowe`s Home Improvement Store.\par \par \uc2 Multiple people captured the moment they targeted workers on the roof of what appeared to be a residential home. After several law enforcement vehicles stopped in front of that house, the men on the roof can be seen quickly pulling up their ladder as armed agents surrounded the property.\par \par \uc2 During our coverage of the operation, Wednesday, we ran in to Border Patrol Commander Gregory Bovino.\par \par \uc2 How many people did you detain today?\par \par \uc2 GREG BOVINO (U.S. Border Patrol Sector Chief of the El Centro Sector in Southern California): Still counting. A lot and still counting.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: He has become one of the faces of the Trump administration`s nationwide immigration crackdown after similar controversial, large scale arrests in other cities, including Los Angeles, Chicago and Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY BOVINO: We`ve got several hundred agents spread across the New Orleans area, so not just us. We`ve got a lot.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: Protesters have shouted their opposition in the city over the past several days --\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN shouting "Hate you!")\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: As community members say some immigrants have been too afraid to leave their house this week for work or even grocery shopping.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: People are just really scared, regardless of what their immigration status is, they`re worried about people being arrested. I`ve gotten calls from teachers who are worried because their students don`t want to show up to school.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: And \uc2 the city of New Orleans\uc2  has responded to this operation by setting up an online portal where residents can submit their complaints of potential abuse by federal immigration officers. Now, a similar operation is underway in Minneapolis, and that`s where we find my colleague, Jonah Kaplan.\par \par \uc2 Jonah, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Kati, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This is also a community on edge. There are roughly 80,000 Somalis living here in the area, and their leaders are warning that they are being unfairly blamed and targeted because of a fraud scandal worth more than a billion dollars that have implicated more than several dozen Somalis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Minnesota is reeling from a fraud scandal involving $1 billion siphoned from multiple federal programs during the COVID pandemic. In the past three years, 87 people have been charged, 61 convicted, most of Somali dissent.\par \par \uc2 Erica MacDonald was U.S. Attorney for the District of Minnesota during the first Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 What happened in Minnesota, where fraud was able to run rampant that it didn`t in other states?\par \par \uc2 ERICA MACDONALD (U.S. Attorney for District of Minnesota): Lack of accountability, frankly. We knew that fraud occurs, and there`s warning signs that come with it, and they were ignored in this case.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: According to investigators, the schemes involved bogus receipts and invoices for meal programs, housing assistance and behavioral health services charging the state millions of dollars for services that were never provided.\par \par \uc2 This week, the President has repeatedly lashed out at Minnesota`s Somali community.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re going to go the wrong way if we keep taking in garbage into our country.\par \par \uc2 JAMAL OSMAN (Immigrant and politician): I think it`s a beautiful country.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Jamal Osman, who moved from Somalia to Minnesota when he was 14 is a naturalized U.S. citizen and a member of the Minneapolis City Council.\par \par \uc2 JAMAL OSMAN: Anyone who looks like me are scared right now.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: The fear comes as the Somali community here is bracing for a surge of ICE agents cracking down on migrants who have been issued final deportation orders.\par \par \uc2 The President is pointing to the fraud cases in Minnesota, and we know that a majority of the suspects are of Somali descent. How is your community taking that?\par \par \uc2 JAMAL OSMAN: Well, my community is upset. Yes, people commit crimes, but the entire community should not be blamed based on what some individuals did.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: Republicans on the House Oversight Committee in Washington are also announcing in a news release their own investigation into the fraud and whether some of that money, stolen money could have made its way 