al container. Then it will be carried through hundreds of Italian towns and cities before arriving in Milan for the opening ceremony on February 6th.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, a doctor is convicted of supplying drugs to the late Matthew Perry. We will show you what Perry`s family said to him in court.\par \par \uc2 Plus, weight loss drugs like Ozempic are exploding in popularity, as you know, and I can`t believe I`m about to say this, but could your next family member to take them be your pet? Oh, look at that little chunky cat and dog. We`ll explain in just a bit. But right now, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, the one and only Lucy Liu will join us to talk about her latest role.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rosemead"/Vertical)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s being hailed as a career redefining performance.\par \par \uc2 We will also honor legendary guitar player, Steve Cropper. The name might not be familiar, but this soul man with some of the best known songs of the last years, he wrote them.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" we`ve got an update on that drunk raccoon in Virginia. Lordy! We`ve got the security footage. Yes, that`s a spoiler alert!\par \par \uc2 This CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Audrey Flournoyy/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So that is home surveillance video police say, shows an escaped inmate considered armed and dangerous knocking on a door near Atlanta on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Timothy Shane was captured soon after in an abandoned home nearby. The 52- year-old escaped from an Atlanta hospital on Monday. He faces charges for an alleged kidnapping last month that led to a high-speed chase.\par \par \uc2 Some scary stuff there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very scary.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Very scary.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, a judge sentences the doctor who supplied Matthew Perry with drugs before he died. We will have the latest when we come back. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Harry Huggins)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Here is a story that caught our eye this morning. A fighter jet exploded into a ball of flames during a routine training flight. You can actually see the Air Force pilot parachuting to safety. Now, this happened just outside of San Bernardino, California on Wednesday. The pilot`s injuries are not life threatening, that`s the good news. The cause of the explosion is still under investigation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A former doctor convicted in the tragic death of Matthew Perry was sentenced to prison yesterday. Salvador Plasencia is his name. He pleaded guilty to supplying ketamine to the actor and his assistant in the weeks before Perry died back in October of 2023.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans reports on the punishment that was handed down yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Friends"/Warner Bros Television)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: It`s been two years since beloved "Friends" star, Matthew Perry was found dead in a Jacuzzi at his Pacific Palisades home after overdosing on ketamine. And this morning, former Dr. Salvador Plasencia is in federal prison serving a 30-month sentence for his role in Perry`s death.\par \par \uc2 Plasencia pleaded guilty in July to four counts of distribution of ketamine. In court Wednesday, Perry`s sister said, "Instead of protecting you exploited," pointing to text messages where he bragged, "I wonder how much this moron will pay."\par \par \uc2 Perry`s mother, Suzanne Morrison, told Plasencia, "This is my boy. I want you to see his mother." Visibly sweating, Plasencia told Morrison he worries about one day explaining what he did to his two-year-old son, adding, "I`m just so sorry."\par \par \uc2 Perry`s stepfather, Keith Morrison.\par \par \uc2 KEITH MORRISON: Well, it`s a tragic story. You know, sometimes, apparently, nice guys do bad things.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: This sentence indicates the severity of the crime and the fact that we have a doctor breaking his oath, there is no allegation that the ketamine that he gave Matthew Perry was the ketamine that actually led to the death, and that`s what`s on the other side of the balance.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Plasencia is the first of five people sentenced in the case, all have pled guilty, including Jasveen Sangha, the so-called "Ketamine Queen" accused of selling the fatal dose.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: She obviously pled guilty to more serious charges. I think that will weigh in the judge`s mind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So Jasveen Sangha pleaded guilty to distribution of ketamine resulting in death, and that is a key difference here. She`ll be sentenced late February. She faces a maximum of up to 65 years in prison because of that tiny difference, it`s a far more severe penalty -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Carter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 To another story now. The huge runaway popularity of those GLP-1 weight loss drugs is taking a turn that maybe we all should have expected, and yet we can still be a little bit embarrassed about. Researchers are right now testing those drugs as a real, major scientific clinical trial to see if those same drugs can help pets lose weight.\par \par \uc2 National Consumer correspondent, Ash-Har Quraishi has graciously agreed to take a look at how these implants may soon help get America`s fat cats and dogs to slim down.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH TILKING (Pet owner): We`re just going to let him break it.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Savannah Tilking`s (sp?) 10-year-old cat, Runkle is sweet, sassy and loves to eat.\par \par \uc2 How much does Runkle weigh?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH TILKING: He, last time I weighed him, he weighed 21 pounds.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: And he doesn`t love anything involving physical activity.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH TILKING: He spends most of his days sleeping.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: He`s a lounger.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH TILKING: So, he is a lounger.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: And Runkle is far from alone. Experts say roughly six in 10 cats in the U.S. are carrying too much weight.\par \par \uc2 DR. ERNIE WARD (Pet obesity expert): In Veterinary Medicine for the last hundred years, the only prescription we had was feed less, exercise more, and that just doesn`t work for all types of obesity.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Dr. Ernie Ward, a top pet obesity expert says vets may be entering a new frontier using GLP-1 drugs like Ozempic to treat obese cats.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco based Okava Pharmaceuticals just launched MEOW-1, a clinical study that will utilize a tiny under the skin implant in 50 cats to release a GLP-1 medication drug over six months. Dr. Ward says the benefits could go well beyond weight loss, pointing to human data that shows improvement in diabetes risk and long-term health.\par \par \uc2 DR. ERNIE WARD: And that`s why we`re so excited, because when we look at especially in cats, one of the biggest health threats is obesity and then diabetes.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: And what`s the biggest unknown that still keeps you cautious?\par \par \uc2 DR. ERNIE WARD: The first, of course, is efficacy. Does it work safely? The second are the side effects, and then the third is the cost.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Okava says it is aiming for about $100.00 a month results from the MEOW-1 trial are expected next summer with the company seeking \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for cat use in the next two years, with dogs not far behind.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH TILKING: So I think that if my vet came to me and was like, he`s diabetic, we`ve tried everything else, we should consider that, then most definitely.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: If it works, chunky cats like Runkle could get their own Ozempet moment.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ash-Har Quraishi.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like that cat, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, just chilling, one hand in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It kind of makes sense to me, though, because when they say, eat less, exercise more, it doesn`t work for humans a lot of times.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You see why it clearly doesn`t work for the animals, if they can do it -- it is going to live a longer, healthier life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, but the problem with humans as I know well, I can buy my own food, so I just keep feeding myself. We feed the cat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, can`t we just feed them less?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Listen. Let me talk to the viewers. Give me my camera. Go grab your cats and dogs. I want to talk directly to them. Meow! Meow! Arf! Arf! Awoo!\par \par \uc2 I basically told them that you`re just find the way you are. Okay? Don`t let your owners change you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s up to you if you want to be chunky. We love you the way you are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, no body shaming here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Doolittle is what they call me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, there you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talking directly to them.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up in our series, "Never Too Late," somebody is over here like, "What is he doing?" A California woman who proves that the beat goes on no matter what age you might be and we all love this.\par \par \uc2 We have this year`s list of the finalists for the Grammy Music Educator Award. Stick around. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh! I love this song.\par \par \uc2 Our series, "Never Too Late" highlights people who tackle their next big adventure no matter their age. This morning, we meet a 78-year-old who found a whole new purpose after losing her job during the pandemic. She turned to drumming, and she is sharing the joy and health benefits with her fellow seniors.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: When you think of drum circles, you probably think of moments like this in Venice Beach or New York Central Park, but these seniors are just as ready to feel the rhythm.\par \par \uc2 The woman in charge --\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE (78 years old): Tilt it and beat on the drum that way --\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: -- is Robin Blate.\par \par \uc2 Yup! Tell me about your t-shirt.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: Well, I`m not a drummer girl, I`m a drummer lady, and it`s part of my wardrobe now.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: That wardrobe and her new identity started with a connection to Remo Belli, the founder of the percussion company, Remo.\par \par \uc2 So how did you become friends with Remo`s sister?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: Staying at a hotel, and she was there, and we just bonded immediately over blueberries and oatmeal.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It wasn`t until COVID lockdown, encouraged by her friend, that she took the idea seriously.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: I was in a drum circle all over the world. People were zooming in, and the facilitator said, go in the kitchen. You have a pot and a pan. You`ve got a drum.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Is that the OG original drum that you started out with in your kitchen during COVID?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: Uh-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And when lockdowns lifted, she began leading her own drum circles.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: Drumming with the kids is amazing. Some of them come in very shy, and they leave they are drumming. It`s just magic.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: She is now helping people like herself discover the joys of banging the drums.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: As far as seniors, the physical and mental benefits are amazing. Anxiety and stress, but the number one thing for seniors is socialization.\par \par \uc2 They said they feel like kids again.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What I experience with Robin was what I love to do. Move!\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Blate introduced me to different kinds of percussion.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like the ocean.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: At the height of her career, she met one of the most famous drummers in the world.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: I worked for CBS Records, and I was asked to present Ringo with his signing gift.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Wow! You got a kiss from Ringo. Wow, look at that. You`re on camera, so if you have a plea to Ringo.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: Please, I would love to see you and get that kiss again.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Until she can reconnect with Ringo Starr, she continues to educate herself and bring drum circles to more people.\par \par \uc2 She hopes sharing her passion will inspire others to follow theirs.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BLATE: I think when you`re young, you dream. When you get older, you forget to dream. I`m living the dream.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: For CBS Mornings, I`m Jamie Yuccas in Santa Clarita, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that. She was shooting her shot like Steph Curry to Ringo.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, the stories that you`ll be talking about in "What to Watch." But right now, it`s 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming at 8:00, Lucy Liu is coming 