t it. I get it.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with the latest controversial pardon from President Trump. Democratic Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  and his wife will no longer face charges of bribery and fraud. Wednesday`s announcement follows other recent pardons of a disgraced foreign leader, a billionaire with possible financial ties to the Trump family.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is with us once again.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 People are saying there are a whole lot of questions here. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey again, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is not a supporter of the President. In fact, he is a Texas Democrat who holds a competitive congressional seat on the Mexican border. Trump says Cuellar was targeted by a weaponized Biden administration, a claim he has made regarding other recent pardon decisions, which has critics saying Trump is politicizing his powers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): I am going to thank the President personally.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Democratic Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  praised President Trump Wednesday after being pardoned from fraud and bribery charges before his trial even began.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): Nothing has changed. I am a conservative Democrat, and I am very bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Cuellar and his wife, Imelda, were accused of accepting thousands of dollars to advance the interests of foreign figures in Azerbaijan. The couple denies all charges. Trump claims without evidence that Cuellar was prosecuted for defying then President Biden on border issues.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: He was treated very badly because he said that people should not be allowed to pour into our country and he was right.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BALLOU (Former federal prosecutor): It`s fundamentally anti- democratic.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Brendan Ballou is a former federal prosecutor who left the Department of Justice in January.\par \par \uc2 Is it common to see a pardon before there`s even a trial?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BALLOU: I think it`s virtually unprecedented. Fundamentally, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is personalizing that power and taking away or undermining the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump also pardoned hundreds of Capitol riot defendants before they went to trial, making the same argument, claiming Biden weaponized the Department of Justice against them. It`s a claim he also made when pardoning the former Honduran President who was convicted of leading a violent drug trafficking conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BALLOU: This President says that he opposes, you know, drug traffickers, and then he pardons a drug trafficker. He says that he wants to drain the swamp, and then he pardons governors and congresspeople that are convicted of crimes.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t about the rule of law, it is about the rule of one man.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Cuellar`s case matches a pattern of pardons the President has handed out for white collar crimes. He pardoned the founder of crypto company, Binance, who pleaded guilty to charges of enabling money laundering. Binance was involved in a $2 billion investment using a crypto currency backed by Liberty Financial, the company run by Donald Trump`s sons. Also recently, he pardoned a former investment manager, David Gentile, who was convicted of defrauding 10,000 people of more than $1.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 The president claims the Biden Department of Justice was overreaching in its prosecution of Gentile, but the indictment was unsealed in the second month of Biden`s first term, which means the case was built during Trump`s first term.\par \par \uc2 All of these cases take years to get a conviction and thousands of man hours of work and to rub salt in the wounds of those who lost money in the scheme, Gentile as other pardon defendants have found will no longer have to pay their restitution -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That will change someone`s holiday plans. That`s a big, big, big change.\par \par \uc2 Scott, wow, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Vlad is here with our next story , taking a turn. We are remembering a guitarist. Right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are remembering a legendary guitarist.\par \par \uc2 Steve Cropper was one of the most important musicians to come out of the soul music capital of Memphis, Tennessee. The guitar legend died Wednesday. He was 84 years old.\par \par \uc2 Cropper was best known as a member of Booker T. & the M.G.`s, but later on as part of the Blues Brothers Band. Fans, though, know he was much, much bigger than all of that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Steve Cropper`s guitar licks defined Memphis soul. Never the front man of the genre`s biggest hits, "\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 " called him the secret ingredient when it named him one of the Greatest Guitarists of All Time.\par \par \uc2 Britain`s "Mojo" Magazine had him at number two, second only to Jimi Hendrix.\par \par \uc2 Cropper was a fixture and founding guitarist of the legendary Stax Records house band in Memphis, playing and co-writing songs on the label`s line of prime classics, including Booker T and the M.G.s` "Green Onions," Wilson Pickets in "The Midnight Hour" and Otis Redding`s "Sitting on the Dock of the Bay."\par \par \uc2 Steve Cropper was born in 1941 on a farm in Missouri, and picked up the guitar at age 14 when his family moved to Memphis. It was there where he was exposed and influenced by gospel music and formed his first band, The Marquees, eventually recording "Last Night" in what would be one of Stax`s first releases in 1961.\par \par \uc2 That decade long run at Stax eventually led the Rock and Roll Hall of Famer to Los Angeles, where he became the go-to session musician for music`s biggest acts, including John Lennon, Ringo Starr, John Prine and even the Blues Brothers when he appeared in the 1980 film, and they even hit the SNL stage with the rendition of "Soul Man" together.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You might not know Steve Cropper`s name, but you know the hits, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The music. Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know "Sitting on the Dock of the Bay," you know "try a little tenderness," yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Tenderness."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, "In the Midnight Hour." If I start to sing -- if I go "Thunder, lightning,"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lightning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "The way you love me is --"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Frightening.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you. Knock on wood, that is Steve Cropper.\par \par \uc2 Jim Stewart, who is the co-founder of Stax said the Steve Cropper was involved in virtually every recording that was released by Stax from 1961 to 1970.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can see why your \uc2 Spotify\uc2  listening age is 72.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like good music, Tony. How can I say I?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And like the assist from Brian, "Frightening."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Brian knows.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was trying to swallow before I said frightening. I actually knew the answer to that. Vlad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Lucy Liu will be here to talk about her new movie. It`s called "Rosemead," and why she fought for years to tell the story about a mother`s very intense love for her son and what she did about it. But first, it is 8:08. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, our series, "The Dish" goes to Nashville and finds a different song and dance than you might expect.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \