ER: Yes, and you have told me this before, which really got me excited when I saw the perfect companion now to this phone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right!\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So these are amazing, the Galaxy Buds 3FE, and these buds, you go ahead and pop those in. I`m going to hit play here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, turn me up! Turn me up!\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: All right, all right. So now we`ve got music playing and with these, you`re going to hear the clearest sound. This has premium features. You can actually use the intuitive touch controls to change the volume, to answer your phone calls, just by touching.\par \par \uc2 When you`re listening, if you want to tune me out, you can actually do active noise cancelation so you only hear your music and not everything around you, or choose the ambient mode. So maybe you`re at the office, you`re out having a walk, you can hear what`s going on around you, and if you and I speak different languages, and you`ve got those in, your Galaxy A.I. will translate in real time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say what? What?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So if I`m speaking a different language from you, whether we`re standing in person or we`re on a call --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am going to Italy this summer. I need these!\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: These are the hottest gifts for the holidays. Do not miss out on these. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, for more information on these gifts, you can visit samsung.com.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up in our series, "The Dish," we take you to a Nashville restaurant that starts with an Italian feast then gets you on your feet. All right, we`ll be right back\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, our series "The Dish" goes to Nashville, a restaurant there where a family style feast is the first course, a lot more to come, a meal at Mangia is a celebration with singing and dancing. It`s like a big old Italian wedding, and Nicole Valdes says it`s almost closed down until the community stepped in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: The aroma of authentic Italian cuisine isn`t the only reason why this small Nashville restaurant is booming. It`s the music and the man in the kitchen that make more than just your taste buds dance.\par \par \uc2 That`s Nick Pellegrino, owner, chef and choreographer of one of the most unique dinner experiences you`ll find at Mangia, that`s Italian for eat.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO (Mangia owner and chef): You`re going to eat, baby. You come in here, you are eating. Wear your baggy pants. Ten dishes that come out.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Ten dishes.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: So mangia!\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: Ain`t getting no fresher than this, kids.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Pellegrino spends days preparing it all by hand and mostly by himself. But there`s an appetite for more than just good food here.\par \par \uc2 When the music changes, it`s time for guests to hop up on their feet.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: I`ll go to the tables and I say, "You ready to dance?" And people are like, "Well, I can`t dance." And I say, look around, nobody can dance in here. That`s why it`s fun.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: And it feels like this accomplished chef has a flair for fun and performance, it is probably because he started here cutting not onions, but albums with stars like Jon Bon Jovi.\par \par \uc2 It almost seems like this is the place where both of those loves meet.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: Hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: You get to be truly yourself.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: Hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: But when the pandemic hit, Mangia, like many restaurants, struggled, forcing Pellegrino to make a tough choice and close for good.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: And within minutes, I was just inundated with just beautiful texts from people, posts on our social media, how you can`t go away.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: What are some of the messages that have stuck with you?\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: We had a couple that said, we`re getting married. And I said, well, when`s the wedding? They said, right now, if this judge at our table will marry us, and they got married on the spot in the restaurant.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: At your restaurant?\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: At the restaurant. Insane, right?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: So to keep making memories, instead of opening seven days a week, he chopped it down to two --\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: There we go! Just brush it off and lift it up!\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Offering a one of a kind dining experience weekends only -- and that changed everything.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: One! Two! Three!\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Would you have ever thought that you`d be more successful being open only two days a week?\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: No? I was like, let me buy myself a little time.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: He also got a little help from a friend, Nashville celebrity chef, Maneet Chauhan won Food Network`s tournament of champions in 2021 and donated $10,000.00.\par \par \uc2 What inspired you to want to help him try to keep his restaurant alive.\par \par \uc2 MANEET CHAUHAN (Chef): It`s like he`s delivering the joy, you know, every week, which is, I think, incredible.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Joy that may just be the secret ingredient to his comeback.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: Everything that you want an Italian restaurant to be, it`s become, and it makes me want to never stop doing this.\par \par \uc2 NICK PELLEGRINO: Watch me now!\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Nicole Valdes in Nashville, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh! I am definitely pulling up because I love Italian food and a good party.\par \par \uc2 All right, tomorrow, you may not have heard her sing, but you definitely know her songs. Songwriter Amy Allen spoke with Anthony Mason about working with artists like Sabrina Carpenter on hits like "Espresso."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Espresso"/Sabrina Carpenter/Island Records)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You`ve written some of the most irrepressible, undeniable songs in ages. It`s like --\par \par \uc2 AMY ALLEN (Songwriter): Thank you. We have a lot of fun making them, and I think you can hear that. Her and I just want to write like the most honest truth in a blunt right hit at your heart way that we can, and she`s so witty.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, that`s coming up tomorrow. "Talk of the Table" coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. And Tony, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got a doozy.\par \par \uc2 All right, you`re never going to believe. You are never going to believe what happened to a Faberge egg, a rare Faberge egg in New Zealand. Let`s get a picture of the egg up.\par \par \uc2 It is encrusted in diamonds, in sapphires. When you open it up. There`s an 18-karat gold octopus inside.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s the Faberge 007 James Bond Special Edition Octopussy egg. They only made 50 of them. A man walked into a jewelry store in Auckland, New Zealand, and is accused of eating the egg. He ate the Octopussy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He swallowed it in his stomach.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That just took 