who I`m here with. We find the most amazing people here in our progressive greenroom. This is actor and director, Lucy Liu, here to tell us about a passion project, the new movie "Rosemead." It took seven years to get it on screen. We are going to ask her all about it. Awards buzz already in the air. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So we all know the lovely Lucy Liu. Lovely Lucy Liu. I like that alliteration -- from her roles as a genuine badass in "Charlie`s Angels," "Kill Bill." I still remember from "Ally McBeal." She had that death stare that used to scare me in my house. So the list goes on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 But her new project, it`s being called a career redefining performance -- I agree. Liu is the star and producer of the movie. It`s called "Rosemead" inspired by a true story.\par \par \uc2 So she plays Irene Shou, a Chinese immigrant who is battling cancer and also navigating her son`s schizophrenia. Irene is ashamed of the stigma around his diagnosis and tries to hide it from her community.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rosemead"/Vertical)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Man! Lucy Liu joins us in the studio. I wish we could show so much more of that movie, Lucy, because I watched it and it is so powerful. It`s so raw, it`s beautiful. It`s a love story. I felt so many emotions after I watched it, and to know that it`s a true story also adds another layer.\par \par \uc2 But we do see you in a way that we`ve never seen you before, and I know you`ve been working on this for seven years, it means so much to you. Why?\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU ("Rosemead" actor and producer): Because this story doesn`t exist in our library of filmography, and it is a story that is not just about the AANHPI community, it is a story that resonates universally, because it is about love, it`s about family, and it`s about the stigma of culture and what we carry and how we continue to pass that on.\par \par \uc2 And so I really felt strongly that this movie, this little indie movie, should be there and exist for people to access.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rosemead"/Vertical)\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU And that`s the impact of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was interesting and fascinating is to see you speaking in broken English and in Chinese. He is speaking in English, your son, and the back and forth between the two of you, that was important for you to do the movie that way. Why?\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU Well, my parents are immigrants, so I`m first generation. And the way that you receive something at home which is fluid, which is poetic, which is common language, the mother tongue. But then, when you go outside and as a child watching my parents receive, I guess, racism -- you know, I guess being condemned based on what they look like before they even opened their mouths and then when they did, it was fragmented conversation, and I think that that broke my heart watching it as a child, but it also taught me the wrong things, which is to keep silent, to hold in my expression or my emotions.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I had to really unlearn those things, and I`m still unlearning them. I mean, I think for my work, I can do that, but for my life, I`m also, like, incredibly private, and it`s sort of this feeling of extremes.\par \par \uc2 And I wanted Irene to also have that feeling of, I guess, to connect with the audience, that her struggle inside the house --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rosemead"/Vertical)\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU -- was very different from outside the house, and that fragmentation of dialogue and the way that she uses the words is powerful, but it`s also -- there`s a lot that is not being fed and said.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, and you mentioned the stigma, and it`s highlighted in this film, especially within the Chinese immigrant community. But like you said, this is something that we do.\par \par \uc2 And as a Black man, I can say that when I was growing up, we didn`t have daily conversations about our mental health, and my mom and dad did an amazing job, but they were only a reflection of how they were raised. So what is it that you want people to take away? I know you just touched on it a little bit, but as far as the stigma about mental health and also diagnosis that might happen within our own families.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the silence, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the silence --\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU I think it`s this, you know, in Chinese, this thing called, you know, "diu lian" which literally means throw face, and it`s like "piao diu lian" which means don`t throw face, which is, don`t shame us.\par \par \uc2 So all of the things that happen within your own home, stay in your own home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: Like, keep it inside. Don`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t air the dirty laundry.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: Yes, don`t show the dirty things. Don`t show the holes, don`t show the rips.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Keep the family business in the house.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: And so the presentation of, I think, what we all do, you know, just continues that cycle, and then so when something comes up, it`s so shocking, you know, when there`s something that`s happening next door, and there`s also a lot of judgment, societal judgment --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: -- which does not help other people to feel like they can open up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: And now with technology and social media, obviously, we want to show our best sides, and so it`s very difficult for younger people --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It makes it harder!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: And that the suicide rate and the -- it doesn`t even have to be a diagnosis, it just can be a feeling of not belonging and not sensing that you can talk about your feelings without having, you know, being sequestered or isolated, and that is exactly what happened to Irene in the story and that is heart --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I felt for Irene, because she`s diagnosed with cancer, her son is 17, about to turn 18 --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rosemead"/Vertical)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- dealing with this mental illness, and she knows what could happen when he turns 18, he becomes an adult, so she has to take -- she takes very drastic action. I wonder how you, in less than 30 seconds, what your message is for us to take away from this movie.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: My message --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am still thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: My hope is that people come and see this movie and understand that these stories are important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: And it is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And universal.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: And universal, and it`s impactful, and that it`s going to be something that will stay with you. I mean, this is what legacy is, right? Legacy is about changing things and making it better for the next generation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Healing generational trauma.\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: And for yourself. And this movie, I hope --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo Lucy Liu!\par \par \uc2 LUCY LIU: -- is about healing and bringing things from a compassionate point of view. It`s not just a headline.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wishing you an Oscar nomination --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020251204elc400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump Signs Funding Bill Reopening Government; Airport Cancellations Persist Despite Shutdown End; Trump Mentioned Multiple Times in Epstein E- mails; \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and Letitia James Push to Dismiss Cases; Starbucks Baristas Go on Strike; Baltimore Officer Charged After Police Chase; Dr. Gounder on Spike in Whooping Cough Cases; Tony Looks for Boston`s Hidden Treasures; Rev. \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  Hospitalized in Chicago; Pennies Scrapped After More than 230 Years; Delta CEO \uc2 Ed Bastian\uc2  on Shutdown and Ongoing Issues; "The Running Man" Star Glen Powell; Kenny Chesney`s Memoir is #1 NYT Bestseller; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Nikole Killion, Kris  Van Cleave, Scott MacFarlane, Tom Hanson, Celine Gounder, Tony Dokoupil,  Chris Tanaka, Rob Marciano, Carter Evans, Kelly O`Grady, Nancy Cordes \par \uc2 14,542 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Thursday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Tony will be joining us live from Boston. We will explain why in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have got breaking news. The government as you know, the shutdown is over. We`ll explain what happens next.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, the fallout from the newly released \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  e-mails about his relationship with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The bill is passed!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The President signs a bill ending the shutdown despite Democratic opposition over a big hike in health care costs.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): The extremists in the other party insisted on creating the longest government shutdown in American history.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Trump faces questions over newly released e-mails from convicted sex offender, \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  as The White House pushes back against more releases.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): They`re afraid, clearly, of what`s in those files.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Another twist in the bizarre case of a missing nine- year-old girl in California, why her mother is now charged with false imprisonment.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Maybe this most recent arrest can get some leverage over her.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: It`s the end of the road for the penny.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: After more than two centuries, the U.S. minted its final penny ever.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Devil Wears Prada II"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Miranda Priestley is back. We`re getting a first look at "The Devil wears Prada II.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Devil Wears Prada II"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: At first, we thought it was a dog. And then I said, no, that`s a pig! I couldn`t believe it!\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: An unlikely suspect on the run for sure. A porky perpetrator led police in New Mexico on this chase right through Albuquerque. Video released by the police shows the wild pig dodging cars and officers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It all came to an end with one officer picking the pig up, putting it in the back of the cruiser.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: We did it!\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was excited about getting that little porky.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They got it!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot to get to in this show, including newly released e-mails from accused sex trafficker, \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  raising questions about what President Trump may have known.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll have more on that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, have you heard? I know you have. The government shutdown is officially O-V-E-R over. After 43 days, the President signed a bill last night to reopen the government. Now, most Democrats, though, oppose this legislation because it does not address the rising health care costs.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill. Nikole, I know there`s a lot of relief, but there`s also a lot of frustration. Here`s the big question. How soon could things get back to normal?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hey, good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of federal workers are expected to be back on the job as soon as today and should get paid soon. Most SNAP recipients should see their benefits restored quickly and Smithsonian Museums here in the nation`s capital will reopen in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: With a stroke of his pen, President Trump ended the longest government shutdown in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I hope we can all agree that the government should never be shut down again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The bill is passed!\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The law took effect immediately after the House narrowly approved the funding package with six Democrats crossing party lines to join Republicans.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the Democrats who supported the package tonight?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): They did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The measure extends government funding through January 30th, pays back federal workers while reinstating those who were fired and guarantees SNAP or food assistance benefits for a full year.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADRIANO ESPAILLAT\uc2  (D-NY): I will be casting my vote as a "no." In fact, it will be a hell no!\par \par \uc2 REP. LISA MCCLAIN (R-MI): Vote "yes." If not, you`re the ones taking money from needy families.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Democrats largely oppose the legislation, arguing it failed to extend Affordable Care Act tax credits for 20 million Americans facing rising health insurance premiums.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): This fight is not over. We`re just getting started.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  filed a petition to force a vote to guarantee the subsidies for the next three years.\par \par \uc2 Are you still willing to negotiate on this issue with Republicans?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): We`ve always been willing to negotiate in a bipartisan way, but Republicans have been unwilling to sit down and even have a conversation with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Michael Galletly heads a union for federal workers in Utah and questioned whether the shutdown was worth it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GALLETLY (\uc2 AFGE\uc2  Local 4016 President): It`s kind of demoralizing, frankly, frustrating. I and my people are just trying to work for the American people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: President Trump said he`s open to working with Democrats on health care, although he`s made clear that he believes that the Affordable Care Act or Obamacare should be overhauled.\par \par \uc2 He also called for an end to the filibuster a longstanding tradition in the Senate to prevent future shutdowns -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nikole, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 You might want the end to the shutdown means an end to major airport delays, but unfortunately, that is not the case, at least not yet. Kris Van Cleave is at Sky Harbor Airport.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  order that requires airlines to cut flights is still in effect, but frozen at that six percent number that we saw yesterday. The agency says they are seeing staffing and sick calls in control towers across the country normalize to more like pre-shutdown levels.\par \par \uc2 Now, yesterday, it was better for the airlines, fewer delays and cancellations that dropped below a thousand for the first time in nearly a week, but we are starting today with around a thousand flight cancellations, largely due to that \uc2 FAA\uc2  order, as controllers wait for that lump sum back payment to hit their accounts.\par \par \uc2 Once the \uc2 FAA\uc2  gives the okay, airline sources tell me it will take 24 to 48 hours to ramp back up to full service. That`s in large part because they`re canceling flights a day or two in advance to give flyers certainty.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to employees, Southwest chief operating officer, Andrew Waterson wrote: "We`ll continue adjusting our schedule as needed to comply with the FAA`s order. We are optimistic that the \uc2 FAA\uc2  will allow airlines to resume normal operations within a few days, enabling us to return to our full schedule in the very near future."\par \par \uc2 Now, aviation analytics company, Sirium crunched some numbers and found the pace of bookings for Thanksgiving slowed noticeably starting November 1st. While Thanksgiving still looks to be busier than last year for the airlines, it does appear that some people actually re-thought booking a flight or took a wait-and-see approach -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In our next half hour, we`ll have an exclusive network interview with Delta CEO \uc2 Ed Bastian\uc2 . We will ask him whether all of this will affect Thanksgiving travel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, turning now to those new questions about President Trump`s relationship with convicted sex offender, \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a new batch of private e-mails released by Democratic lawmakers on Wednesday, Epstein alleges that Trump, "knew about the girls," although it`s not clear exactly what that means.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more on this.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning. So how is President Trump responding to all of this?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The President was meeting with and calling Republicans who are supporting this bill to release Epstein case files. After those meetings, the Republicans did not remove their names from the bills. The White House says it wasn`t a pressure campaign, but transparency.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: More than six years after disgraced and convicted sex predator, \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  died by suicide, U.S. House Democrats released on Wednesday a series of his private messages mentioning President Trump.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROBERT GARCIA (D-CA): I think, pretty serious stuff, disturbing stuff in his e-mails.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The e-mails include this one from Epstein to his co- conspirator, Ghislaine Maxwell in 2011, in which he wrote that "... Trump had spent hours at my house with one of Epstein`s victims." The White House identified that victim as Virginia Giuffre, who said she was recruited by Maxwell while working at Mar-a-Lago and died by suicide in April.\par \par \uc2 VIRGINIA GIUFFRE (\uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  accuser): Jeffrey was a sick pedophile.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But in a 2016 deposition, Giuffre says she didn`t think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  participated in anything. And in a separately newly released message from 2019, Epstein wrote to author, Michael Wolff about Trump, "... of course he knew about the girls as he asked Ghislaine to stop."\par \par \uc2 Although it`s unclear what he asked her to stop doing. Wolff whose writings sparked controversy in the past reacted to the e-mail Wednesday night in a podcast with "\uc2 The Daily Beast\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WOLFF (Author): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  had this deep relationship. They knew everything about each other. Their obsession was women, girls, models.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The e-mails were part of approximately 23,000 pages of records handed over by Epstein`s estate to the Republican-led House Oversight Committee under subpoena.\par \par \uc2 Trump has frequently denied any wrongdoing and has dismissed calls to release more information.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: So this is a Democrat hoax that never ends.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The White House downplayed Trump and Epstein`s relationship and blasted Democrats for cherry picking excerpts.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  was a member at Mar-a-Lago until President Trump kicked him out, because \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  was a pedophile and he was a creep.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meanwhile, a bipartisan group of lawmakers are one step closer to a vote to release all of the Epstein case files held by the Department of Justice, after Democratic Congresswoman Adelita Grijalva of Arizona was finally sworn in Wednesday after two months of waiting and became divisive signature on a petition to force that vote.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADELITA GRIJALVA (D-AZ): With my signing, we move one step closer to the truth, the truth that they will try to deny, but that survivors deserve.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: There were survivors of the Epstein case in the House chamber yesterday. They`ve been pushing for full transparency. The House Speaker says there could be a vote next week on this bill to force the release of Epstein case files. That would raise pressure, Nate, on the Senate to do the same.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Scott, you are in Virginia where who cases involving President Trump`s political rivals are back in court. What`s happening today?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: An unprecedented argument behind me at the federal courthouse. \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, and Attorney General Leticia James of New York will both argue to get their federal cases dismissed saying the prosecutor on each of them was unlawfully appointed. It will be a joint hearing, even though these are two separate cases. Both, Nate, deny wrongdoing in their cases.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: More than 1,000 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas at 65 stores around the U.S. plan to go on strike today. Now, they belong to a union and they want a contract. Today is one of the coffee giant`s busiest sales days of the year. Why? Because customers get those free reusable red cups just in time for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 The union calls the walkout a Red Cup Rebellion, their words. The workers want more pay and increased staffing. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  says the demands are unreasonable and does not expect a disruption at the vast majority of its 17,000 locations in this country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now a big update on a story we`ve been following. You may remember this video. A police cruiser chasing a suspect on foot nearly running him over. Now, the Baltimore Police officer who was driving that vehicle is charged with attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson reports on the fallout on a department already under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Slick Brown/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: That`s Baltimore Police officer Robert Parks, asking a pedestrian to come with him around 5:00 P.M. on October 28th.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Slick Brown/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: The man appears to refuse and starts walking away. Officer Parks then allegedly tells the individual he`s going to call the dogs and come get him, before returning to his vehicle and pursuing the man through a vacant lot in his cruiser at a high rate of speed, allegedly striking him and nearly running him over.\par \par \uc2 According to the indictment, the incident started moments earlier, when Officer Parks approached a group of people, including the man later seen on the video, who were standing on a sidewalk near a liquor store. Parks then allegedly said to the group, "It`s getting a little hot, guys. I just need you guys to take a lap."\par \par \uc2 The video begins shortly after the group dispersed.\par \par \uc2 IVAN BATES (Baltimore City State`s Attorney): The actions we saw in that video of Officer Parks are entirely unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Officer parks are also accused of driving down this alleyway behind businesses and homes and plowing through a chain link fence, nearly striking the man a second time.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the Fraternal Order of Police questioned why "... certain important and relevant facts that were known to officer during this encounter were not disclosed." The group did not elaborate on what those facts might be, but the state`s attorney said that the man Parks was chasing had a misdemeanor arrest warrant.\par \par \uc2 Baltimore PD has been on federal oversight since 2017 after the Justice Department found it engaged in a pattern of unconstitutional policing, including excessive force and racial profiling.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD WORLEY (Baltimore City Police Commissioner): And unfortunately, one bad incident like this sets you back months and years to all the progress that we make. That`s not what we teach our officers to do. That`s not what we want our officers doing and it`s going to damage our relationship with the community.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Tom Hanson.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This morning in "Health Watch," we want to focus on a dramatic spike in whooping cough cases across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Last year, there were six times as many cases reported as there were in 2023. CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is with us. She is editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Dr. Gounder.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, so what`s behind the surge and what symptoms should people be looking out for?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So, Texas is not unique in this. We`re seeing cases increasing across the country. There are three main factors that are driving this. One, we`ve seen an increase in non-medical exemptions to vaccination.\par \par \uc2 Texas, in fact, starting this school year has made it much easier to claim those non-medical exemptions.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, I was speaking to the Health Director in Dallas County yesterday. He was saying, normally, their back-to-school vaccination clinics are packed.\par \par \uc2 This year, they were very sparsely attended, and part of that is fears about ICE, so about 40 percent of Dallas County is Hispanic and they think that`s what`s driving some of that drop.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, there is waning immunity with the pertussis, whooping cough vaccines that we use. This is related to a change made a few years ago, over a decade ago, to have vaccines that cause fewer allergic reactions or side effects, but at the same time, the immunity doesn`t last as long.\par \par \uc2 And thirdly, we`re just testing more aggressively with PCR tests, people might remember that from COVID, and so we`re picking up more.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So what kind of symptoms should people be looking out for and what`s the treatment if you do get it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, so some of the symptoms can be very mild. And you may not even realize, so stuffy nose, mild cough, but if you have a lingering cough, that can really be a sign. And then the whooping cough, we call it that because of the kind of violent coughs people can get, sometimes they even vomit as a result.\par \par \uc2 One concern with treatment is we`re seeing increasing resistance to the first-line treatments like a azithromycin. And two-month-old or less, they can`t get the second line treatment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Dr. Celine Gounder, always good to have you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, as you may have noticed is not in the studio this morning, because he`s busy with our special series, we call it "Tony on the Town." Clever, right?\par \par \uc2 I like it.\par \par \uc2 It`s taken him about 200 miles away to the great city of Boston. Tony, what exactly are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the great city of Boston, historic Boston, revolutionary Boston.\par \par \uc2 We`re doing this in a bunch of our affiliate cities, but Boston is a favorite of mine. I feel connected to Boston. I had that \uc2 Bill Belichick\uc2  interview earlier this year, some viewers here locally will be like, is that the guy who asked those blistering questions like, how`d you meet your girlfriend? Yes, that was me.\par \par \uc2 Former Patriots Coach Belichick, sorry about the tough questioning. If you want to come down here to the Well Coffeehouse in Easty, we can talk all about it.\par \par \uc2 I also feel connected to Boston because my dad -- people think I`m joking about this, but this is true, I talk about the drug dealing -- he was convicted 17 tons of marijuana in the federal courthouse here in Boston, still lives in Cambridge, just over the Charles River.\par \par \uc2 Dad, I still love you. If you`re watching, come on down, we`ll talk. Well Coffeehouse here in Easty.\par \par \uc2 But I didn`t know Boston, know Boston, so I had to meet up with Chris Tanaka, who is right over here, he is a local anchor here at WBZ. He`s a strange guy.\par \par \uc2 He moved from Honolulu to Cleveland and then Boston, but he knows his Boston, so he gave me an insider`s tour of everything that makes this city great.\par \par \uc2 What`s up with that move?\par \par \uc2 I`ve also got Rob Marciano here and we`re talking weather.\par \par \uc2 Honolulu to Cleveland to Boston.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: I feel like he was following your father, maybe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, he is in the business.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: I wish I knew him back in the day.\par \par \uc2 I love coming to Boston. Such a great town. I am usually here during a snowstorm. Talked to my buddy Eric Fisher, WBZ, chief -- I met him last night, he said they haven`t had six inches or more of snow since 2022. Amazing stretch of dry weather.\par \par \uc2 But we have got some snow in New England, I want to show that to you. It`s at Jay Peak where they`re tearing up the slopes there. It is not officially open. Check out this video. Yes, they are ripping through it. Probably some tough guys, there are people climbing up there. Official opening is November 22nd.\par \par \uc2 We do have a little bit of light snow there today. We`re warming up on the East. The lake-effect snows are beginning to shut off, but the atmospheric rivers are beginning to crank and we`ve got one that`s coming into California today. San Francisco, eventually Santa Barbara, slight little risk of seeing some flooding there, then tomorrow down across parts of Southern California, and then over the weekend, especially on Saturday, Los Angeles to San Diego could see some heavy rain there, and maybe some debris flows and mud slides. We`ll worry about that.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, warming up here in Boston. A little quick trivia for you. 617, you know the area code here?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Legend has it, 617, the Battle of Bunker Hill was June 17th, 1775. So 617.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Okay, I like a little history. Some factoids.\par \par \uc2 Pat the Patriot, by the way, Pat Patriot, I think Pat is short for Patriot. So it is Patriot Patriot? I don`t know. we are going to ask him. He will be here later today. Another reason to come to the Well Coffeehouse.\par \par \uc2 Sending it back to you in the studio for now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Tony, you are unpacking a lot of stuff in that hit. We`re looking forward to seeing you a little bit later, man.\par \par \uc2 All right, check in with you in a bit.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, change is hard. America is no longer minting pennies. What that means for you and your pockets.\par \par \uc2 All right, but first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, Glen Powell is here from the new thriller "The Running Man."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Running Man"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why he really wanted to do his own stunts.\par \par \uc2 Plus, in "What to Watch," Adele is doing something she`s never done before.\par \par \uc2 And a big announcement from the Golden Globes. They are honoring an icon. We are going to tell you who it is.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Ancestry. Discover. Preserve. Share.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Breaking news overnight.\par \par \uc2 The Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  is hospitalized for treatment of a neurodegenerative illness. The 84-year-old Civil Rights leader revealed the diagnosis of the disease known as PSP earlier this year. Jackson was a protege of Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. He went on to create the Rainbow Push Coalition, which he has led for more than 50 years.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, forget the old saying, a penny saved is a penny earned. The last pennies have just been minted and we will show you what that could mean for you.\par \par \uc2 Make it make sense.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. A dramatic rescue in California after a driver plunged off a mountain road near Big Bear Lake.\par \par \uc2 Crews used a rope to get down to the female victim, whose vehicle broke through a guardrail and then fell over on its side on Tuesday. They say the woman was able to call 911 for help before she got out of the car and was found sitting next to it.\par \par \uc2 She was taken to the hospital for treatment. A wild, wild scene.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very wild.\par \par \uc2 This is also a wild story, Nate. It is very bizarre actually, from Santa Barbara County, California.\par \par \uc2 It is the story of a mother who will not tell police about her nine-year- old daughter who hasn`t been seen for weeks now. They were both seen on video more than a month ago. They appear to be wearing wigs, both of them, for some unknown reason.\par \par \uc2 An all-out search is underway for Melodee Buzzard, while her mom faces a felony charge that`s apparently unrelated to the disappearance. Like I said, very bizarre.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans looks at this mysterious case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ATTORNEY: We enter a not guilty plea.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: In the midst of a national search for her missing nine-year- old daughter, Ashlee Buzzard was in a courtroom Wednesday pleading not guilty to a false imprisonment by violence charge.\par \par \uc2 The case stems from a November 6th incident involving a man identified as Tyler Brewer. He told several media outlets that Buzzard was allegedly armed with a box cutter and didn`t allow him to leave her house.\par \par \uc2 The 40-year-old was arrested Friday outside her home. A judge released her yesterday with a GPS monitor.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say the case is not directly related to Melodee`s disappearance. She was first reported missing on October 14th by an administrator at her school. Law enforcement officials say Melodee was seen at this security video at a car rental business with her mother on October 7th. Authorities say they appeared to be wearing wigs.\par \par \uc2 They say it was the start of a road trip for the duo. Sheriff`s officials believe the pair traveled as far as Nebraska and back through Utah, Arizona, and Nevada, to California, where Buzzard appears to have returned without her daughter.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: She should be playing outside with her kids, she should be interacting with her cousins, not hiding somewhere in a wig.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Detectives and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have conducted searches of Buzzard`s home.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Ashlee has not been cooperative with the investigation, has not been able to provide us evidence of Melodee`s well-being.\par \par \uc2 GRANDMOTHER: I want to know with my granddaughter. What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: We`ve reached out to Buzzard`s attorney for comment, but have not heard back.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Incredibly bizarre story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is just very strange, and I want to know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, very strange.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- is there a father involved? Is she going through a divorce? There are so many unanswered questions there. We just hope little Melodee is all right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That she is okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wherever she is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, switching gears now. If you`re on a tight budget, you might want to forget about penny pinching, because the humble penny is no more.\par \par \uc2 After 232 years, the last-ever one-cent coin was minted in Philadelphia on Wednesday on the President`s orders. That`s partly because pennies cost -- well, a pretty penny to make nearly four cents each. That`s according to the U.S. mint, so one penny costs four cents and while some are mourning the end of an era, others just say it`s common sense.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: After a 232-year run of continuous production, it may be that all the penny is good for now is your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON BEACH (U.S. Treasurer): Three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: U.S. Treasurer, Brandon Beech oversaw the minting of the final penny yesterday and said making the coin was no longer financially responsible or necessary.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Grease"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The value of the penny, which has long cost more to produce than it is worth, is a decades-old debate, which then U.S. Mint Director Edmund Moy spoke to "60 Minutes" about in 2008.\par \par \uc2 EDMUND MOY (U.S. Mint Director): It`s unsustainable. You can`t sustain losses on pennies and nickels and expect to be a viable organization that benefits the American people.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The mint says there are now an estimated 300 billion pennies in circulation, but is phasing the coin out and transforms it into a potential collector`s item, some consumers are asking this question.\par \par \uc2 How are they going to handle prices? That will be an interesting discussion.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: We reached out to a sampling of major retailers. McDonald`s said it would round totals to the nearest five cents for cash transactions. The convenience chain, Sheetz encouraged cashless payments, and while \uc2 Kroger\uc2  said its grocery stores would continue accepting pennies, it asked customers to provide exact change.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: An organization representing the retail organization reported yesterday that two-thirds of the nation`s largest companies say they`re rounding cash transactions to consumers` benefits when pennies are unavailable and that practice is costing businesses millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always find pennies so annoying trying to get rid of them.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now that I know they`re going away, I am like --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t want them to go away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, I kind of like it. It also doesn`t seem fair, though, Kelly, that many places are going to round up as opposed to round down when it comes to the penny.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It`s not nearest, it sounds like for most.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will see.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how much do your thoughts cost now? It is not a penny anymore. Penny for your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I need a quarter.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Much more expensive. Inflation.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My thoughts are worth at least worth a nickel.\par \par \uc2 Thanksgiving, as you know, it is almost here, and if you`re hopping on a plane, you may be wondering if all of those recent flight cuts will affect your travel. We`ll ask somebody who knows! There he is.\par \par \uc2 Hello, \uc2 Ed Bastian\uc2 ! Ed, hello.\par \par \uc2 Delta`s CEO.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with Ed and a very good friend.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The end of the government shutdown could soon mean relief at our nation`s airports, but it will probably take just a second to get things back on track.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of flights are being canceled every day and Thanksgiving as we know is coming, two weeks from today actually.\par \par \uc2 For some insight, we`re joined in a network exclusive by -- this is \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Ed Bastian\uc2 . Always good to see you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN (\uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  CEO): Gayle, it is always good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what is new in your world? Anything going on?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen, the shutdown is over. We`re all very relieved about that, but we keep hearing, even though it`s over, it`s going to take a minute for airlines to get back on track. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: I think we`ll be back a lot faster than people think. The last couple of days, actually, were pretty good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: The staffing, as soon as the Senate deal on Monday got approved, staffing levels almost overnight improved dramatically. So last couple of days, the only cancellations that Delta had were the government mandated cancellations for us, which is mostly on our smaller planes.\par \par \uc2 And I expect by the weekend, we`re pretty much going to be full. Full steam ahead.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How bad did it get for you? Give us an example of what a Delta schedule is normally like and what it was like during the shutdown and how you handled all of that?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: Well, one of the big challenges we had which is the mandated shutdowns is that the staffing levels in some of the airports and the control towers were significantly reduced, and listen, I can`t blame them. When you don`t pay people for weeks on end and expect them to do the type of job they do, the stress they have, and the strain they have.\par \par \uc2 So Saturday was a perfect illustration, we had a little storm pass through Atlanta, not anything terribly unusual. The overall level of arrival rates got reduced down to 20 an hour. Normally, that`s 80 an hour. Brought down to 20 an hour. Partly the storm, partly the staffing levels were significantly lower than where they normally were.\par \par \uc2 That`s the equivalent of us having a snowstorm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: And we had, as a result of you know, several hour block, we had hundreds of cancellations. And you think about the thousand, the tens of thousands of people whose plans were disrupted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they said Delta had more cancellations than other airlines. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: I don`t think we have that much. I think everyone had roughly the same, I mean, order of magnitude. I think the Atlanta situation itself was probably indicative of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Sean Duffy, Transportation Secretary, said this was done for safety reasons. We all get that. And I don`t expect you to say, yes, I`m worried about safety too, now. But for many people, who are still very nervous about flying, because it does seem very dicey for a lot of nervous flyers in particular.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to reassure people and let people know that it is still safe to fly.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: It`s incredibly safe to fly. It`s the safest form of transportation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know and people always say that, but --\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: Secretary Duffy did the right thing by getting ahead, when they saw that the staffing levels and the controllers were having a difficult time managing without getting paid, pulling proactively the schedules down was the right.\par \par \uc2 We didn`t like it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: -- but we felt like it was the prudent thing to do. Controllers are back to work now. They`re going to get paid, I understand in the next day or so.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, I think the system should return to normal by the weekend. I really do. And normal for us is an incredibly safe, incredibly reliable, great experience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the airline must have lost a lot of money during this time.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So does that mean, normally when a company loses money, it normally ends up hurting the consumer in some ways. Is that -- will prices be raised now?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: No, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why not?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: Well, it is a competitive marketplace.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not looking for a raise, I am not looking for an increase, but why would it not?\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: No. It`s a competitive marketplace. Everyone went through the same thing. We had, at Delta, we at a little over 2,000 cancellations for that period of time, meaningful, but we`ll still be fine financially. It was a week. It wasn`t -- it wasn`t --\par \par \uc2 If it was an extended period of time, it would be more challenging. If we get back, and we will be back to normal this weekend, yesterday we were over 90 percent on time, systemwide, every flight, all day long.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And no concerns about Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: No concerns.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: Thanksgiving is going to be a great holiday period of travel.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, I`m going to be looking for Delta for Thanksgiving. Thank you so much, Ed. Always good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, the stories we think you`re going to be talking about. We call it "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:44 exactly. I love when that happens.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m going to need you to stick around. We have got a lot coming up in our next hour, including an insider`s tour of Boston with Tony and CBS News Boston anchor, Chris Tanaka. They go to Fenway Park and a street that`s rich in literary history.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Running Man"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, we`ll be talking with Glen Powell from the new action- packed thriller "