 talking all the big Boston news. Rob Gronkowski back on a one-day contract with the Patriots, why that is such a big deal, why it is so special and what he will be doing tonight at the Patriots-Jets game.\par \par \uc2 Also, Pat Patriot himself will be here live and we`ve got an insider`s tour of Boston with Chris Tanaka of WBZ.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Vladimir Duthiers and that`s Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Coming right up in this 8:00 hour, actor, Glen Powell. We`re talking to him from "The Running Man." He plays a man competing in a deadly T.V. game show at one point wearing, well, a towel.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony is in Boston for an insider`s tour from our colleagues there. Did you know there`s a bar at Fenway Park where you can even watch the game during bathroom breaks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve also got some CBS MORNINGS deals for you, including some items to inspire you, taking you an early start on the holiday shopping.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How many days until Christmas? Anybody know?\par \par \uc2 Well, Thanksgiving is two weeks away today.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. It is coming. I know that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s around the corner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is around the corner.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is around the corner.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s begin this hour with the biggest, longest shutdown in government history. The President made it official last night. It`s a big relief for federal workers and most SNAP recipients could see their food benefits restored within about 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 But though the shutdown is over, the blame game is not.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes has more from The White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Vlad, good morning. It`s amazing how quickly things change. Now that that bill has been signed, the lights are back on, the government has reopened, and some federal employees and contractors are returning to work this morning.\par \par \uc2 The bill passed by a narrow margin in the house last night, with six Democrats joining all but two Republicans in voting yes. The final vote, 222 to 209 and just before he signed the new funding bill into law, President Trump blamed Democrats for what he says was an extremely damaging shutdown for the country. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: This cost the country $1.5 trillion, this little excursion that they took us on. Unfortunately, the vast majority of congressional Democrats were happy for millions of Americans to suffer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The bill he signed funds the government through January 30th, so just two-and-a-half months. It includes language to reverse recent staffing cuts to guarantee back pay for federal workers and to protect against additional federal layoffs.\par \par \uc2 A lot of Democrats are really upset that the bill doesn`t extend tax credits for 20 million Americans who are facing higher health insurance costs. Last night, President Trump said he would actually be willing to work with Democrats on health care, but then he said he still wants to end Obamacare. He also said that voters should remember the pain of this shutdown when they head to the polls for the midterm elections next November -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Tony, gets a special insider`s tour of Boston from our friend, Chris Tanaka of CBS News in Boston, including a bar that might as well be on the field at Fenway.\par \par \uc2 Step by step, we are grooving this morning, but first it`s 8:02, it`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Running Man"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Glen, that`s crazy. Don`t murder Glen Powell. He`s starring in the new movie "The Running Man."\par \par \uc2 Ahead, he talks about what it was like to do most of his own stunts and the legend who had the final say in his casting.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We always appreciate that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Actor, Glen Powell stars in the new \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2  thriller. It`s called "The Running Man." It`s based on a Stephen King novel from back in the day.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Running Man"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it takes place in the near future in a society where the contestants participate in a reality game show. It`s called "The Running Man." They`re hunted by professional assassins. So Powell plays Ben Richards, he`s a regular guy competing on the show to win cash. How much cash? A billion dollars with a B to save his sick, young daughter.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Running Man"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How is this going to turn out? Let`s just say Glen Powell has a very packed schedule, but he came by the studio a couple of days ago. Here`s our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hello, Mr. Powell.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL ("The Running Man" actor): How`s it going?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not as good as you. I`m thinking it`s good to be you right now, Glen, but I am so, so, so excited about everything you do.\par \par \uc2 Last time we talked, I said you were on a rocket. Now, I think you`ve taken the whole damn ship and you`re in the stratosphere, but I`m so happy for your success.\par \par \uc2 But Ben Richards, this was tough for me because he`s an angry guy, he has a short fuse, and that`s so opposite of everything you do. So I know it`s called acting.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what did you do to prepare for this guy?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: You know, I think the Ben Richards, yes, his sort of operating emotion is definitely rage, you know, he`s sort of like in a world in a world in which he`s trying to provide for his family, but he stood up for some of his co-workers, and he got blacklisted. So he can`t -- you know, he can`t -- his daughter is incredibly sick, and he can`t provide for she or his wife.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Running Man"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: And so he goes on this unwinnable game show where no one has ever survived.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: So really, it`s a guy who`s just frustrated by the bully.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: You know, he`s really angry at the world and the situation he`s in. So for me, I just, like, kind of thought about my own family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re so good in it. Edgar was here earlier this week, your director, and he said that when it came to casting Ben Richards, he only wanted you. And I said, why? He said because he`s relatable, he`s likable, and I wanted people to see him doing something that`s very different than what he is, but he`s also very believable in this role.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: And, you know, Edgar is just one of the greats. And it`s like, I think the thing I`m so excited about is, like, you`ve seen him do action -- parts of action in some of his movies before, but like, you get to just unleash a whole new part of his, you know, filmic attributes, like he`s just so good./\par \par \uc2 He is a true -- he`s one of our greatest directors, and I feel like you get to see him in a whole new way in this movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But listen, there is a whole lot of action in this movie. And I said, so did Glen do his stunts? He goes, he did 99.9, there were a couple that he couldn`t do because of insurance reasons, not because he couldn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But not because he didn`t want to do it, because it`s extremely physical.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Absolutely. Well, the funniest part is, I realized at the end of the shoot there`s like this bridge jump where, like, there`s a game of chicken, and I jump off this exploding bridge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: And I didn`t realize why they put it at the end of the shoot until later, which is all insurance.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Running Man"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: He dies, you know, yes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just in case.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes, the insurance has more influence over this movie than you think.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did you prepare for something that`s so physical?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because to me, it`s different than anything I`ve ever seen you do. And you`ve had physical roles before. It`s not like you`re a mouse burger sitting around on the set. You`ve done some very physical things.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But this is next level.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Well, the interesting part about this one, when you finish reading the script for the first time, I was like, I`m going to get -- I`m going to take some licks on this movie. I knew that it was going to be --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to take some licks --\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Some licks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: But the hard part is when you go and actually do it. All my friends that have starred in action movies, I just asked their advice on what to do, and every single one of them said, it is going to be a lot more brutal than you expect, just because you know the explosion is happening or the gunfire or the hits, it doesn`t mean it doesn`t hurt, you know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: And you`re doing it 12 times in a row.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a great scene where you`re hanging off -- in a towel, and you`re hanging midair with the towel. And I kept thinking, dear Lord, let the towel fall. And Glen, it never did. What was it? Double stick tape?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes, yes. This person that double stick taped, that strong button, just holding on in the winter of Bulgaria.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But -- that`s the other thing I was wondering, because it`s during the winter, and you had to shoot that scene over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: All night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The other thing I thought was interesting, Glen, is that Stephen King had to approve you.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I`m thinking, has he seen any Glen Powell movies? What was that like waiting because, listen, everybody wanted you, but Stephen King had to have the final sign off.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was it like for you waiting to see? Am I in? Am I out? Was there anything that you were worried about?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Well, I got the news from Edgar that I was his choice to be Ben Richards right before I was going on "The Jimmy Fallon Show." And so I happened to be with my family, and we all just celebrated. I was so excited.\par \par \uc2 And then the next day, I get a call from Edgar. He said, by the way, I forgot to tell you, you don`t actually have the role yet. And I was like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come again?\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: He goes, Stephen King has to approve you. And I was like, well, I wish you would have said that before I, like, completely celebrated. I`m now emotionally invested. So that night he said, Stephen King`s going to watch "Hitman," which is the movie I did with Richard Linklater.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: So -- and there`s some like, disguise -- disguisable elements in "The Running Man" as well. So Stephen King watched it, and I got the word the next day that he loved the movie and that I was -- I was fully approved to be Ben.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The other thing that I like so much about you -- I won`t -- no, I won`t do that. The other thing that is --\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: It weak buttons on towels?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it`s like, you know, I`ve made it my mission to find a girl for Glen Powell. He goes, Gayle, I`m okay. I don`t need your help. But I`d appreciate it if you`d run it by me.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I always appreciate you, wing-manning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I really, I really --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I think you need to be a wingman for me.\par \par \uc2 Going back to University of Texas that you`re getting your degree.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now that`s fascinating to me, because there are people in school now that are trying to do what you are doing.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they would say, why do I need a degree? I`m trying to do -- I`m trying -- they want to be what you`re doing, and you`re going back to school. Why is that important to you? I love that you`re doing it by the way.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: You know, what`s funny is, I don`t know if there`s like, necessarily a logical reason more than like a spiritual reason, which is, I like to just finish what I started. And University of Texas means a lot to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: I didn`t want to get the Honorary Degree where they just sort of say, hey, like, you`re in the club. I`m like, no, I want to earn it, you know? I don`t like to take shortcuts. And I feel like, for me, getting that degree is the end of a wonderful chapter that I started back in 2007.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Cheering you on always, Glen Powell, I can`t wait for people to see you in "The Running Man."\par \par \uc2 GLEN POWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m predicting it`s going to do well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Glen Powell, Glen Powell, "The Running Man" is in theaters this Friday, distributed by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , part of our parent company.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Glen Powell is hosting "Saturday Night Live."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s going to be cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He said that`s a dream come true for him. And the guest -- musical guest is Olivia Dean. That`s going to be good, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I also think the movie is going to kill.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is going to crush.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know Gayle was watching that movie, like, please fall off towel. Please fall off towel.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, we have a special mystery guest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You ain`t wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We don`t know who it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You ain`t wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They won`t tell us what`s going on. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so I guess we have a very special guest, a surprise guest that`s joining us. It`s such a surprise that we don`t even know who it is, but in our control room, I think they have a few clues.\par \par \uc2 Our mystery guest has been called the People`s Superstar, the guest loves a hat and no shoes. This person is now a number one "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best- selling author.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kenny Chesney?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t know --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "The Naked Cowboy"?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kenny Chesney?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who do we think it is?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it Kenny Chesney?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is it? The one and only?\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it only when you said no shoes.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY (Singer and author): Good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kenny, hi!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is he in the building? Kenny, are you in the building?\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY I`m not in the building. I`m in my driveway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Kenny.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is in his driveway, so Chesney is now a number one "New York Times" best-selling author --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- for his new memoir "Heart Life Music" and he joins us now, like he said from his home in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 First off, congratulations, my brother.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know it was our interview, Kenny, I know it. I know it. It just shot you up!\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: Yes. Not only was it your interview, Holly Gleason and I, who co-wrote the book with me, we were talking earlier, the very first interview I ever did in February about this book was with you guys on this show. So you guys have been a huge supporter of this journey. And I just wanted to come on this morning to say the last week has been incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: You guys were so great to me on set and I just wanted to thank you for being a part of all this. It`s been the most amazing thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so nice!\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: I never saw it coming. You know, I`m a singer and a songwriter. I never saw this coming.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, you`re a singer, a songwriter and a best-selling author.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Author.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How does it feel to hear that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The book is so good, too.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: It feels great. You know, I`m very happy. I`m very happy for Holly, who`s a journalist and been doing this a long time, and we have just had a thrill, which had -- it`s just been nothing but joy riding it. It`s been hard work, but it`s just a testament to my audience that I`ve built out there on the road for a while.\par \par \uc2 And we`re just thrilled, guys. I just wanted to say, thank you so much. I just -- I couldn`t be happier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, we thank you. And so I know you`re on the road doing the book tour, but I was reading that you`re also going to be at The Sphere in Las Vegas doing a residency.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I really want to come see you there.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: As long as you wear those glasses.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just for you, but tell us about -- because this Sphere is next level in terms of what you can do in that --\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: Yes. It is really incredible. It`s an insanity thing to do, you know, but it forces me and my road family, especially the band, to create different neural pathways, if you will, to put our show together.\par \par \uc2 And believe it or not, we don`t do our first show till June. We`re already on sale now, but we`re already working on what that show is going to look like here in November.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: So it`s a lot of work, but we love it. We really do love playing there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Kenny, in your book, you are very open and vulnerable about a lot of things, and you write in the book, what makes you, you, is your ability to show up and be present. So how do you remain present? I mean, you`ve sold millions and millions and millions of records, you`ve got a residency coming up in The Sphere. Now, you`re a best-selling author.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: Yes, well, I don`t know. I think that was a lot of just my family, you know, it`s how I was raised, you know. And so I`ve never really thought it was my place to take myself too seriously, no matter what we`re doing, you know?\par \par \uc2 But my mom was that way. My grandparents, we`re all very humble people that try to spread love and joy. So that`s what I try to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Kenny, you have performed with so many icons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I mean, Jimmy Buffett, Bruce Springsteen, Willie Nelson, Taylor Swift, and that`s just to name a few. Is there anyone that you look at now and you`re like, you know what? I want to write a song or hit the stage or perform with this person or this band?\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: I don`t know, it would all depend on the song. You know, like everything that we`ve ever done as far as collaborations was song based. It wasn`t for marketing or to get awards.\par \par \uc2 Like a lot of people today do that, just in a ways, it`s like a marketing ploy. They don`t even know each other.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, they`re kind of strategic with it.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: For me, if the song was great, that would kind of lend to doing something with someone else. But I`ve been very fortunate to -- it`s crazy, not only to have met people that really inspired me and that were heroes at one time, but became friends, you know, and I got to collaborate on stage with them.\par \par \uc2 And that was one of the biggest highlights, you know, to get to know Bruce Springsteen at all on any level was just a huge highlight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love-- the thing I loved about the book is you told so many great stories about the people you`ve met in the relationships you have. Made me want to go to Key West, I`ve never been, but I`m curious about --\par \par \uc2 I`m curious about what you thought when you first found out that your book was number one. So quickly, just take us there.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: Well, it was yesterday. I got the call last night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: And I was stunned. I just couldn`t believe it, because I never saw that coming. I just wanted to write a book about my journey. And to tell a little bit of my story. This book isn`t my whole story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mission accomplished.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: I wanted to pay tribute to the people that have been in the audience all these years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt!\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHESNEY: I wanted to champion all the people that championed me over the years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we`ve got to get out here, Kenny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mission accomplished, Kenny!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well done!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It is time to bring you some of the stories that we call "Talk of the Table." Because this is a table we`d be talking.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are talking.\par \par \uc2 All right, so we`ve been talking a lot about A.I., A.I. singers, right? So my "Talk of the Table" is artificial intelligence and how it is dividing Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 Scarlett Johansson, Ben Stiller, Guillermo del Toro, Whoopi Goldberg and other famous names are condemning the use of A.I. to create synthetic actors. They want the government to tighten copyright regulations.\par \par \uc2 However, there are some like Michael Caine, Matthew McConaughey and Liza Minnelli who are embracing the technology and making deals to allow companies to replicate their voices.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, how close are we to watching an A.I. actor on the big screen? I started checking out these videos by Emmy winning director, Sergio Cilli, who is putting A.I. to the test, what he is doing, just so I set this up, he is doing what are common now in Hollywood, which is \uc2 Zoom\uc2  casting calls, right, where you have a \uc2 Zoom\uc2  with the director and the actor is on the other end, and the director takes the actor through a couple of steps to ensure that they can act.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A couple of scenes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A couple of scenes. So check out what happened when Sergio did it with A.I.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SERGIO CILLI (Director): Let`s start with some really basic emotions. Let`s start with anger. Okay, you`re angry.\par \par \uc2 (A.I. generated actor)\par \par \uc2 SERGIO CILLI: Okay, so that felt like a dog or some kind of animal. So let`s try a little couples improv.\par \par \uc2 A.I. MAN: We are so excited to be auditioning. We absolutely love doing projects around the house.\par \par \uc2 A.I. WOMAN: Yes. We demo, we build. We can`t wait to get started.\par \par \uc2 SERGIO CILLI: Nice. You guys are fun. I like that little hammer trick you did, too.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So what ends up happening is A.I. cannot replicate the emotions that Sergio is asking. For example, when it asked the A.I. to be angry, actors draw on experience. They draw on emotions to bring forth what the director is requiring. A.I. can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Also, a lot of things happen. If you notice, in that last one, the woman`s voice swapped --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A lot of glitches.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A lot of glitches. So I think actors are okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For now.\par \par \uc2 Sergio tells me that he started doing this because he was worried about the industry, and it`s given people so much joy. Someone said it has helped them with their depression.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Eighteen million hits in 14 days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. How about that?\par \par \uc2 All right, so no need to worry yet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, my talk of the table is former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player Reggie Bullock, showing fans that he is still balling. So Bullock spent 11 years with various \uc2 NBA\uc2  teams. Now he may not have been a household name to you, but he is doing just fine, and I mean fine enough to buy himself his own resort island in Belize.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So Bullock Island has a four-room house with a movie theater hidden behind a bookshelf and a pool. There are also six vacation villas that are there, an outdoor Tiki Bar, restaurant as well as a basketball court, spa and beach club. It`s all surrounded by the beautiful Caribbean Sea.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me just give him some advice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the price?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got to check with him. You know, I love this because, you know, athletes think success looks like something else when they first get into the league, but I want to give some advice that I wish somebody would have told me, listen, it`s okay to buy watches and lavish lifestyle with the cars and the clothes, I get it.\par \par \uc2 But those things have been made for centuries and will continue to be man- made forever. Invest in something that they don`t make anymore, which is land.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Land, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so just a little advice, a little game for you guys to have --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wise there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- a little money right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Reggie Bullock, ahead of his time.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table." Really scary red carpet moment at the Singapore premiere for "Wicked for Good." Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from red carpet moment at Singapore premiere for "Wicked For Good.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody goes after --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you can see a fan just jumped the barrier and goes straight towards Ariana Grande, who is walking with Cynthia Erivo, but look at Cynthia Erivo jump into action.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Michelle Yeoh, Jeff Goldblum there too. Grande seemed a little shaken up, but I love how Cynthia just said, oh no!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cynthia got there before the security guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cynthia said, not today.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She jumped in right to shield her co-star Grande, as you know, has been very open about suffering from PTSD. You remember, after a bomb was set off at her concert back in Manchester, back in 2017.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yup. Yup.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But social comments about Cynthia? You go, Cynthia. Cynthia having faster reflexes than multiple grown male security.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How hard is it for people to act like decent human beings? Because this behavior is unacceptable. I just -- why can`t people just behave?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because that was a very scary situation. I don`t if he is overzealous --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It could have been much worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- or what he meant to do. But Cynthia, bravo, Cynthia!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s really, really weird.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Cynthia.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bravo, Cynthia.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Done Cynthia!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You stepped up for your home girl.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s all right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we have one more story, for that, let`s go to Tony, who is out in Boston.\par \par \uc2 Tony, what do you have for us?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One more story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, guys, thanks for including me.\par \par \uc2 Well, I`m in Boston, I`ve got a big Boston news. Rob Gronkowski signing that one-day contract with the Patriots, one of the greatest football players of all time, won three of his four Super Bowls here in New England. That one day contract allows him to officially retire as a Patriot.\par \par \uc2 He talked yesterday at a ceremony about why that is so special to him. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB GRONKOWSKI (\uc2 NFL\uc2  player): I`m a patriot for life. My career started here 100 percent, I need it to end here. There`s no doubt about that. I mean, the whole Gronk persona, everything about myself, was all because of you guys, it was all because of the fans here in New England. It was all because of my teammates accepting me and everyone else here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The whole Gronk persona, so much fun, so much love in the air. \uc2 Robert Kraft\uc2 , the owner of the Patriots, joked that Gronkowski has never had a bad day, and that if the afterlife is real, we all want to come back as Rob Gronkowski.\par \par \uc2 I disagree, though, personally, if I may say so, I want to come back as Pat Patriot. How you doing? Head and shoulders above the competition? 100 percent muscle here is the weight. And can you show us the Gronk spike?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Uh-oh! Uh-oh! Watch out for the patrons in the back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa! I wore a cup for this segment, just for that. Thank you very much. 11 Super Bowl appearances, working on a 12th this year. Six Super Bowl wins, working on a seventh. They take on the Jets tonight at Foxborough. Gronk is going to be ringing the bell. He`s Keeper of the Light. Good luck, \uc2 New York Jets\uc2 . You`re going to need it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You all have the same jawline.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, very, very cool.\par \par \uc2 Up next, coming up next on CBS MORNINGS, CBS MORNINGS Deals, but first time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time now for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, and we`ve got several items, all at exclusive discounts up to 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping now, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com. Knockings lifestyle expert, Elizabeth Werner is here. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER (Knockings lifestyle expert): Hello. Good morning. I feel like you`re so far away today, but this is amazing, so 42 days till Christmas.\par \par \uc2 In a couple of weeks, Thanksgiving, most of us are going to be setting up our trees.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yup!\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Well, I have already set this tree up in my home, and I cannot wait to show you how gorgeous it is. So a couple of years ago, I decided to simplify the holidays, and I went artificial. I don`t have to worry about the needles. I don`t have to worry about watering.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Well, these trees, I tested a ton of them. These two were my favorite. So the one that you`re stand