ings you see.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And they`re so accessible.\par \par \uc2 All right, so now we`re going to move on to Tumblr, and I would love for you to demonstrate this. So this is a way to take all those dull knives in your kitchen, we all have a drawer full, and sharpen them up. So it comes with this magnetic holder. It`s going to put your knife in exactly the right angle.\par \par \uc2 Right now, you`re using the diamond side of the actual roller, that`s going to sharpen and help realign the knife. Then you`re going to flip it over to the stainless steel side. That side, when you roll it is going to get rid of all those burrs, smooth it out.\par \par \uc2 Now, in this set here, which I love, you`re also getting a leather strop. This is something that you`re going to use to then polish the knife when you`re done sharpening it. And it comes with a stand. This is a gorgeous gift. It`s my husband`s favorite \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals item.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they start at $79.99, thirty-eight percent off. That makes an amazing gift. It presents beautifully.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so my dog, we have a lab, and when we brush her, there`s hair everywhere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everywhere!\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: I think, when this came to my house, I was like, I don`t know what is this. I was so skeptical. It`s amazing, right now, it`s on. You don`t hear it when you turn this on. It comes with all these tools. You can clip your dog, you can groom your dog, you can get rid of the undercoat on your dog.\par \par \uc2 And as you`re doing it, there`s my puppy and my husband. She is so chill, relaxing, because it`s quiet. She loved it. It`s massaging. And guess what? There`s no hair all over the place. Everything gets put right into the vacuum, and then you`re going to empty it out.\par \par \uc2 We have friends that have a golden and they take it to get groomed. It`s $130.00 every time they do it. You can do all the grooming yourself at home. This is $119.99. It comes with --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All of this?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: -- all of it, and you`re saving $80.00 so yes, clip, cut, de-shed, and you name it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: No mess!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No mess.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Elizabeth Werner, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 We partner with all of these companies to get these deals.\par \par \uc2 To buy anything you saw, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`ll be right back. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beautiful Boston, revolutionary Boston. We`re so excited to be here.\par \par \uc2 The thing about Boston is, when people think of it, they think of the Boston Tea Party, the Boston Massacre. Maybe they jump to Charles River. They visit Harvard or MIT. But there is so much more to Boston than that.\par \par \uc2 To get an insider`s tour, I brought in Chris Tanaka of WBZ.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My sweater brother. He`s a local strolling around here, co- anchor of the morning show. He showed me the things off the beaten path. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chris, how are you, man?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Long time.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: How are you? How are you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am doing okay. This is supposed to be an insider`s tour.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Correct.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But this is Fenway Park.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everyone knows Fenway Park.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: I`m going to take you to a place that not a lot of people have seen.\par \par \uc2 Bleacher Bar.\par \par \uc2 This is what it`s all about. Look at the view.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing. I feel like I`m playing deep right center right now.\par \par \uc2 Geez, you really did order a thing or two.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: I did tell them to put it on your card.\par \par \uc2 Baseball is your religion. Fenway Park is your highest church. This actually used to be the opposing team`s batting cages. If you`re watching the game and you have to take a bathroom break.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: You don`t have to miss a thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: No, you see that window right there? That`s the men`s stall.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: So people, when they come to Boston, Tony, they think of sports. They also think it`s history -- Freedom Trail, American Revolution. I`m going to take you to a different place, a different time, that`s just as important and very cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 Beacon Hill.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Brooklyn-esque.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Brooklyn is Boston-esque.\par \par \uc2 This is Acorn Street.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It feels even older than Fenway. It is like stepping back in time.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Oliver Wendell Holmes, Nathaniel Hawthorne, Robert Frost, they all lived in this neighborhood. It`s kind of like ground zero of the great literary movement of the late 1800s.\par \par \uc2 And if you pivot around the incline, gives it this Instagramability.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve done some sports.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yup!\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: We`ve done some history.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Next up we`re. Wicked smart kids.\par \par \uc2 City of Cambridge, home to Harvard, MIT -- the wicked smart kids.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All the places I didn`t get into.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: And this Tony, is graffiti alley. It`s the confluence of education and the arts, you may say. Let`s leave our mark.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Let`s have some fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Oh, that is awesome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What can possibly top that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: You`re going to love this. A unique New England tradition you may not have heard of, but you`re going to love.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sounds great.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Candlepin bowling. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: This was invented in 1880, Worcester, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 You get three rolls instead of two.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: And the reason why you pick up one of those.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This feels ornamental. All right, here we go. Get me out of the gutter!\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Warm it up. Warm it up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good ball.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: That looks good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 These pins get knocked down with bowling.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: He picks up this 10. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 Here it comes. Here it comes. Here it comes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Couldn`t be better.\par \par \uc2 You see that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: That`s Boston. You think you got it, you didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am starting to understand.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You laugh every time you see that. Because it was perfect ball. It was a perfect ball.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Oh my gosh. Imagine that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here is the thing. In candle --\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Candlepin.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Candlepin, thank you very much, you don`t remove the pins, so they`re there as an obstacle as you bowl.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: They are scattered, and they can be used to help you, but in your case, hurt you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love Boston. I love candlepin. You`re a Boston guy.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why do you love it?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: I`ve never lived anywhere else so self-assured.\par \par \uc2 Bostonians, New Englanders know exactly who they are, and I feel like part of it is from the history.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: You can touch the house Paul Revere lived in. And there`s something incredibly grounding about all of that. And then, of course, there`s this deeply rooted almost comedic cynicism that New Englanders have. The world view is cynical to the point of comedy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s in part because of the weather. And you`re a guy who lived in Honolulu. Hey, who is this weirdo? Hold on a second. Uh-oh! Hey!\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: And one more thing, of course, the sports.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Celtics, \uc2 Red Sox\uc2 , \uc2 Bruins\uc2  and Pat Patriot, my guy right here. I mean, come on, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Working on a seventh Super Bowl. You think this is the year? You`ve covered sports.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: Yes. Look, they`ve got all the pieces. They`ve come together much quicker than everyone thought they would. But Pat, no one stops us when we`re on a roll, right, bud?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got Drake Maye. He throws deep. Give me this football.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Geeze.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TANAKA: It went to the Nathaniel Eugene --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey! Okay. Using the government.\par \par \uc2 Great stuff, fellas!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nathaniel Eugene Burleson. Thank you guys. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020251113elbd00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 SUNDAY WEEKEND NEWS for October 26, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Holly Williams, Jason Allen, Andrew Kozak, Weijia  Jiang, Lana Zak, Carter Evans, Elise Preston, Itay Hod \par \uc2 3,665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, a big break in the brazen heist at \uc2 The Louvre\uc2 . Suspects arrested over the daylight theft of crown jewels.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: I`m Holly Williams outside The Louvre Museum in Paris, where there`s been a breakthrough in the hunt for the perpetrators of a daring jewelry heist.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, monster storm. Hurricane Melissa powers up.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: I`m Jason Allen in Kingston, where people are using even the shortest breaks in the rain right now to prepare their homes for the hurricane strength winds that are on their way.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: One hundred and forty mile per hour winds, three feet of rain and deadly storm surge. I`m meteorologist Andrew Kozak with the very latest on Melissa and its track.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: President Trump in Asia dancing and deal making.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: I`m Weijia Jiang traveling with President Trump in Kuala Lumpur, where trade talks are the talk of the town.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: While this anti-tariff ad from Canada turns into an international incident.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Government of Ontario\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT (\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary): The Premier of Ontario seems to have come off the rails a little.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Rare beef. Why ranchers are frustrated with the President`s plan to lower record high prices.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: I`m Lana Zak in Toledo, Iowa, with a new stew that`s brewing in cattle country over imports from Argentina.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All of that, plus a national park gets wild with federal workers furloughed.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: I`m Carter Evans at Yosemite National Park, where some adventurers are taking advantage of the government shutdown to break the law.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening, everyone and thank you for joining us on this Sunday. We begin with a-life threatening hurricane churning in the Caribbean Sea and violent weather right here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh (bleep).\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is Gautier, Mississippi along the Gulf Coast, where a tornado surprised this driver in a parking lot today. Severe thunderstorms also pummeled Louisiana, this video from a suburb of New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 And here is Hurricane Melissa seen from the International Space Station. It is intensifying rapidly and could be the strongest storm ever to hit Jamaica.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jason Allen is in Kingston, the capital tonight, and leads us off.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: The wind is really starting to gust now along the shoreline here in Kingston. The rain has been falling in heavy bands on and off, and the waterways, the channels that carry runoff out of the mountains are starting to run faster now.\par \par \uc2 We saw the waves beginning to build in the fishing villages along the shoreline today. The fishermen pulled their boats up out of the water, parking them across the road. In flood prone sections of the city, people shoveled dirt out of broken roads to fill sandbags and during breaks in the rain, we saw some of them climbing onto rooftops to reinforce them and patch leaks.\par \par \uc2 The Jamaican government has opened hundreds of shelters across the city, but in this region of more than a million people, only a few hundred people so far are staying in those shelters. Most people we talked to said they had no plans to leave their homes. They don`t want to leave their belongings unprotected during what could be the strongest storm to ever hit this island.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Understandable.\par \par \uc2 Jason Allen in Kingston. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on Melissa`s path, let`s check in with CBS News Meteorologist Andrew Kozak in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Serious situation tonight south of Jamaica, that is where we are finding Hurricane Melissa, winds of 145 miles per hour, churning there over water between 89 and 90 degrees. That`s all that red color coding.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about rainfall rates two to three inches per hour, a little bit more than that, in two to three feet when all is said and done. And that`s not even the biggest concern, because tonight, we`re also watching places like Kingston caught between the mountains and the bay, a storm surge of nine to 13 feet could push a wall of water in with nowhere else to go, and the interior section of the island lacks the resources and the structural components here to withstand winds of a hurricane anywhere between 140 and potentially 150 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 Middle of the week, it`s Eastern Cuba and then the Bahamas we`re going to look for before this thing turns out to sea.\par \par \uc2 Quick look at the Lower 48, Pacific Northwest, the Atmospheric River continues. Heavier rain and snow will eventually return as we head into the middle of the week. But tonight, Jericka, all eyes and all concerns are on Melissa churning just south of Jamaica, and we can see why.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Kozak, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Paris now, where authorities today made their first arrest one week after that brazen daylight heist at \uc2 The Louvre\uc2 . The robbery at the world`s most famous museum stunned France and riveted the world.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams is in the French capital tonight with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Jericka, the French prosecutor said the arrests were made last night, just as one of the men was preparing to leave the country from an airport here in Paris. "Le Parisien" newspaper reported that two suspects were arrested, both aged around 30, and both from one of the city`s more deprived areas. We cannot independently confirm those details.\par \par \uc2 The theft has caused outrage here in France because of apparent security lapses at one of the most important museums in the world. The daring daylight robbery took just eight minutes.\par \par \uc2 The thieves disguised themselves as construction workers, used a basket lift up the museum`s facade, and then broke in through a window, making off with jewels valued at over $100 million. In terms of their historical value, the jewels were once worn by French Royalty and are considered priceless, but experts have predicted that the stolen items could be dismantled, the gold and silver melted down, and the gems cut into smaller stones, which would be almost impossible to trace.\par \par \uc2 There`s no information from the authorities here on whether they`ve made progress in locating the stolen jewels, but Jericka, the French prosecutor expressed frustration that information about the arrests had leaked to the media, saying it would hinder the work of around a hundred investigators in the search for the stolen treasures.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Holly Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Trump is in Malaysia, the first stop of his five-day Asia trip. Today, a summit with world leaders, with the President seeking to cut trade deals with new friends and old rivals.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang is traveling with the President in Kuala Lumpur, and has more on that -- Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of a high-stakes summit between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping, both sides are signaling optimism for reaching a new trade agreement.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): After a 24-hour journey around the world, President Trump arrived to Malaysia Sunday morning, joining the welcome festivities on the tarmac and setting the stage for his visit during the Annual Summit for the \uc2 Association of Southeast Asian Nations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States of America): The United States is with you 100 percent and we intend to be a strong partner and friend for many generations to come.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Trump signed new trade agreements with Malaysia, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam, but it`s a potential deal with China in the spotlight to avoid a new 100 percent U.S. tariff.\par \par \uc2 Trump plans to meet with Chinese President Xi Jinping on Thursday in South Korea.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re going to have a good deal with China. They`re going to make a deal. It`s going to be great for China and great for us.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Meanwhile, Trump has abandoned trade negotiations with America`s second biggest trading partner, Canada, because of a T.V. spot that used the words of former President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  to criticize U.S. tariffs.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Government of Ontario\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Trump added an extra 10 percent tariff, with the ad still playing this weekend, but set to stop Monday.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Face the Nation)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT: It`s propaganda coming across our border to decry the tariffs. This is interference in U.S. sovereign matters.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  is here for the Asian Summit and also plans to go to that other economic summit in South Korea, where the President will be, but Trump says he has no plans of meeting with Carney while in Asia, although he is leaving the door open with meeting with North Korean leader Kim Jong-un -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Weijia Jiang, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 This is a critical week in the government shutdown and the showdown in Congress over expiring Obamacare credits, and today, new ripple effects for air travelers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston is at LAX, \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2  with more on that part of the story -- Elise.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Jericka, the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  says flights here were halted this morning because of a staffing shortage at a Southern California Air Traffic Control facility. Now, as the government shutdown enters its fourth week, many Americans are already feeling the strain.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Cars lined up in Pontiac, Michigan this week, trunks open.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s helping us out a lot. We need this food.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER IVEY (Forgotten Harvest): If the government shutdown continues, there will be less food out there.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Christopher Ivey works with Forgotten Harvest, which collects surplus food and delivers it to hungry people.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER IVEY: We`re going to really have to lean into the community support that we need to be able to help us get through this difficult time.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Nearly 13 percent of people in Michigan rely on the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program or SNAP. They`re among the nearly 42 million Americans who will see benefits slashed on November 1st if the government shutdown stretches on.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "Face the Nation," Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent called on Democrats to end the gridlock.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Face the Nation)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT: I would urge moderate Democrats to be heroes, come across the aisle like they did in the spring, and pass a clean C.R.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And former Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  weighing in, criticizing the President on the BBC for using private donations to build a ballroom during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from BBC Sunday with \uc2 Laura Kuenssberg\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Former Vice President of the United States of America): Because right now, we have a President in the Oval Office who is building a ballroom for his rich friends while he is depriving working people of health care.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Democrats say they`re willing to meet with Republicans on a plan that doesn`t raise health care premiums. Meanwhile, air traffic controllers will be missing their first full paychecks on Tuesday -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Elise Preston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 On the \uc2 CBS\uc2  "Money Watch," red meat prices are at record highs, but President Trump`s push to import beef from Argentina in an effort to lower costs has created a growing beef in the heartland.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lana Zak is the only network correspondent based in Iowa, and has new reporting tonight -- Lana.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Already, the trade wars have decimated soy prices here, but cattle had been on the rebound, that is until now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BERLIN WILLBETTER (Cattle rancher): Come on. Come on.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK (voiceover): After several years of losing money on cattle, Berlin and Pete Willbetter (sp?) were looking forward to turning a profit. They also grow corn and soy. Those sales devastated by the ongoing trade war with China.\par \par \uc2 How important is it to you that you have cattle when soy and corn is going so poorly?\par \par \uc2 BERLIN WILLBETTER: Well, it`s going to depend on the year, but this year, it is the one thing that is going to make any money for us.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: So the Willbetters were astounded when President Trump announced he wanted to quadruple beef imports from another country.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: If we do that, that will bring our beef prices down. It would help Argentina, which we consider a very good country, a very good ally.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: His words, sent wholesale cattle prices plummeting.\par \par \uc2 In the supermarket, the price for meat has skyrocketed over the past year. Ground beef up nearly 13 percent, steak up 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WHALEN (Iowa Cattlemen`s Association CEO): You have to look at the total supply chain.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Brian Whalen is the CEO of the Iowa Cattlemen`s Association. He says Americans won`t really see grocery savings from increasing beef imports.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WHALEN: It really only is going to be about 2.5 percent of our total beef supply. It`s really not going to make a substantial difference.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: The Willbetters and their neighbors say they`re disappointed that the President`s America First trade agenda doesn`t include them.\par \par \uc2 BERLIN WILLBETTER: The whole point of tariff was to bring production back home, so we are producing beef here, and now he is trying to do something to destroy that production.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLBETTER (Cattle rancher): We`re being used and abused as producers.\par \par \uc2 BERLIN WILLBETTER: It feels like being a pawn in a game we`re not going to win.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: The White House Press Secretary says that the President "loves ranchers," but feels like increasing supply is the best way to decrease prices. Meanwhile, all of the farmers here are waiting for news on that long overdue aid program that`s been put on hold because of the government shutdown -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: So important to hear from those ranchers. Lana Zak, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the death of a former \uc2 New York Jets\uc2  star.\par \par \uc2 We learned today that Nick Mangold has died from complications from kidney disease. The seven-time \uc2 NFL\uc2  pro Bowler was just 41 years old. Mangold`s death comes 12 days after he made a public appeal for a kidney transplant.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, why more thrill seekers are taking flight at this National Park.\par \par \uc2 Plus, one student, one kick equals massive payday.\par \par \uc2 And meet the vampires of San Francisco who is dying to tell you a story.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: As the government shutdown enters its fourth week and federal workers furloughed, the country`s national parks largely remain open to visitors for now, but as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans reports from Yosemite, things are getting pretty wild.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ve never seen that before.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): It`s an unusual sight because base jumping in National Parks is illegal.\par \par \uc2 Charles Winstead (sp?) captured this video while climbing Yosemite`s 3,000- foot high El Capitan, and it`s just some of the unlawful behavior he`s seen here recently.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WINSTEAD (Resident): Car camping, base jumping, drone activities. From what I hear, people are hiking Half Dome without permits.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Even with the federal government shut down, National Parks are still wide open, but most of the staff is furloughed, which may embolden thrill seekers.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It`s in the middle of the (bleep) meadow.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WINSTEAD: We started seeing people base jumping, and not the typical crack of dawn, do it quick and then run away, but middle of the day and just walking off without any concerns.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Here at El Capitan, climbers worry about the danger of drones and base jumpers above them.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE BROWN (Climber): I`ve definitely been climbing and had base jumpers like fly past me.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: What`s that like?\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE BROWN: That`s scary. It`s like, really loud. Like, incredibly loud.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Robbie Brown (sp?) didn`t see any base jumpers this trip, but he did see emergency services.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE BROWN: They did a rescue the other day. There were some people who got hurt, which is fairly regular.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So those sorts of activities are still going on.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE BROWN: Oh, yes, like people are still rock climbing and having a great season.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The warm fall weather and no admission fees are attracting plenty of visitors, including Yosemite regular, Tom Evans, who has been photographing climbers here for decades.\par \par \uc2 TOM EVANS (Regular visitor): There is nobody manning the gates, so people come in all the time now since it`s free. The employees that they need to run this, they`ve all been sent away.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  told CBS News, Rangers are still patrolling high use areas, but their priority is keeping everyone safe and protecting the environment, and now hoping all visitors will be on their best behavior.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, CBS News, Yosemite National Park.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, how a rock climber`s fall sparked a sky high rescue.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Now to more, some of our top stories in tonight`s "Weekend News Roundup."\par \par \uc2 One person is dead, six others wounded after being shot at Lincoln University in Pennsylvania. The gunfire sent people running for cover Saturday night. It happened during the school`s homecoming celebrations. One person has been arrested.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, a sky high rescue this weekend, search teams near Prescott National Park responded to an injured rock climber air lifting her to a hospital. The 30-year-old woman fell about 15 feet.\par \par \uc2 And in Nashville, a Vanderbilt sophomore is cashing in. He made a perfect kick in his socks. Tyler Wang won $500,000.00 by making the 33-yard field goal that`s part of sports analyst, Pat McAfee`s weekly contest.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, an interview with a vampire, but first, a look back at this day in history.\par \par \uc2 Graphics onscreen: This Day In History -\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1881.\par \par \uc2 Shoutout at O.K. Corral in Tombstone, Arizona\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1958.\par \par \uc2 First Commercial Jetliner Takes Flight with Pan Am\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 2001.\par \par \uc2 Patriot Act Signed Into Law After September 11 Attacks.\par \par \uc2 DAN RATHER: President Bush today signed legislation giving the government sweeping new powers to go after suspected terrorists with electronic eavesdropping, searches and detention without charges.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Finally, tonight, Halloween is just days away and vampires are once again, creeping out of the twilight.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s "Weekend Journal," \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Itay Hod reports that San Francisco, not Transylvania, has a lot more bite than you might think.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD (voiceover): To mere mortals, her name is Kitty Nasarow, but around here, she is simply known as the Vampiress of San Francisco. When were you bitten?\par \par \uc2 KITTY NASAROW (Vampiress of San Francisco): 1897.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: She claims it was none other than Count Dracula himself who did it.\par \par \uc2 KITTY NASAROW: That was the night that my life came to an end, but not my existence.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Ever since, she has been on a mission to feed our curiosity.\par \par \uc2 KITTY NASAROW: This is the site of the most brutal battle between mortals and vampires.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: For 20 years now, she has led her vampire tours where history has a habit of coming back from the dead.\par \par \uc2 KITTY NASAROW: Alcatraz is by far the most haunted spot in the entire city.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: She doesn`t drink blood, she spills the tea.\par \par \uc2 KITTY NASAROW: They think if they wear garlic around a vampire, they`re going to be protected. It`s a spice.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Marc Kauffman, noted celebrity sommelier, said her tours were the perfect pairing for his label, Vampire Wines.\par \par \uc2 MARC KAUFFMAN (Celebrity sommelier): We`re on her website. She`s on our website.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Interview with a Vampire"/AMC)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: from AMC`s "Interview with a Vampire" series based on Anne Rice`s classic --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sinners"/Warner Bros Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: -- to this year`s blockbuster "Sinners," our obsession with the undead has never been more alive.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Robin Werner, who teaches vampire literature at the \uc2 University of New Orleans\uc2 , says that while the Big Easy remains the vampire capital of the U.S., San Francisco might just be their summer getaway.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBIN WERNER (Teaches Vampire Literature at the \uc2 University of New Orleans\uc2 ): You`ve got the fog, you`ve got the ambiance.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: For the vampires of San Francisco, it has been the perfect way to make a living.\par \par \uc2 KITTY NASAROW: Everybody loves to get scared.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: A grave reminder that while we, humans, may never achieve immortality --\par \par \uc2 KITTY NASAROW: We don`t exactly make a quiet entrance.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: -- time spent hanging out with vampires is never in vain.\par \par \uc2 Itay Hod, CBS News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Who knew vampires could be so kind?\par \par \uc2 Well, that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Sunday. I`m Jericka Duncan in New York. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020251027elaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS PLUS for October 17, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anna Schecter, Kati Weis, Nikole Killion, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,622 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Welcome to CBS Evening News Plus. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The impact of the government shutdown expands. Thousands of workers that oversee the country`s nuclear weapons and federal court staff are next to get furloughed. Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill, where there`s no sign of progress to end the 17-day standoff.\par \par \uc2 And In Depth tonight. With ICE detention tactics under scrutiny, we examine the rules of engagement for the administration`s illegal immigration crackdown in U.S. cities.\par \par \uc2 Those stories and more right after our news headlines.\par \par \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  pleaded not guilty today to charges of keeping top secret documents at his home and sharing classified information with family members. President Trump`s former national security adviser says the charges are part of the president`s effort to intimidate his adversaries.\par \par \uc2 President Trump confirmed the U.S. military attacked a submarine in the Caribbean. He says it was carrying drugs. Unlike the five previous strikes, this is the first time where there were survivors. They`re now being held on a \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  ship.\par \par \uc2 And a major development in the British royal family. Prince Andrew says he has agreed to give up his royal title of the duke of York amid a fresh wave of headlines and accusations stemming from his friendship with sex offender \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This includes excerpts from a new posthumous memoir from Virginia Giuffre, who says she was trafficked by Epstein and had sex with Andrew when she was 17. He has denied the accusations and settled a civil case with her without admitting guilt.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the deepening fallout from the government shutdown, now threatening America`s nuclear arsenal; 1,400 workers responsible for safeguarding the nation`s nuclear stockpile are set to be furloughed, as the federal government runs out of money.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown is also grinding parts of the judicial system to a halt.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion joins me from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So, Nikole, run us through the vital parts of the government that are being affected now.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, well, the latest update that we have is that the federal court system is due to exhaust its funding come Monday.\par \par \uc2 And while judges are expected to continue to serve, it does mean that some of these federal courts will maintain limited operations going forward there. Meantime, the head of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  warned today that the agency that runs the nation`s nuclear stockpile is also running out of money.\par \par \uc2 So, if that happens, about 80 percent of the staff, or 1,400 employees, could be furloughed, with only about 375 remaining on the job.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The president has repeatedly said he`s going to target Democratic priorities. What exactly is he targeting?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, well, \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , the director of the \uc2 Office of Management and Budget at the White House\uc2 , announced today that the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  would pause about $11 billion in projects, mostly in blue cities, including New York and San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, this comes on the heels of an announcement by the president earlier this week that he would be terminating a major infrastructure project in New York City to rebuild a tunnel between New York and New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Also, you may recall, earlier this month, the Trump administration froze billions of dollars in climate projects in a number of blue states as well.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, finally, Nikole, are there any little gangs working anywhere on a solution or any kind of anything happening that might get this resolved?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Yes, no, no gangs up here.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I mean, there`s frustration on all sides. However, we may see a shift in strategy on the part of Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who did tell some of us this week that, next week, he plans to bring up a bill to potentially pay federal workers who are being required to work without pay during this shutdown.\par \par \uc2 So, he obviously will also try to continue to bring up this temporary funding bill, although Democrats continue to say Republicans need to come to the table on health care before they can provide any additional votes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Nikole Killion on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 At the White House, President Trump remained noncommittal on sending Ukraine the long-term -- the long-range Tomahawk missiles needed to strike deeper into Russia. He met with \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  today after his more- than-two-hour call with Putin about the war on Ukraine on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by \uc2 Charles Kupchan\uc2 , senior fellow at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Charles, good to be with you again.\par \par \uc2 On these Tomahawks, what impact -- let`s say America gave them all to Ukraine. What impact would that have?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KUPCHAN\uc2  (Senior Fellow, Council on Foreign Relations): You know, these are quite long-range weapons that can hit Moscow and beyond. They carry a punch.\par \par \uc2 So, the costs would go up to the Russian population and to Putin. It would change Putin`s cost-benefit analysis. Is this some sort of game changer? No, there is no single game changer. I think it`s an arrow in Trump`s quiver.\par \par \uc2 And my own view is, if Trump`s going to get a deal out of Putin, he`s going to have to apply a lot more pressure than just Tomahawks. I don`t think we had a decision today on the Tomahawks because he wants to hold that in reserve when he meets with Putin in this upcoming meeting in Budapest, saying, hey, I need you to make a deal, or I may well transfer these long- range weapons.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: We had a meeting in Alaska. I was there. And now there`s a meeting in Budapest. What does this tell you about the state of things? And what are you -- what do you think could come out of such a meeting?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KUPCHAN: Listen, John, I mean, you were in Alaska. Trump and Putin have met or talked over a half-dozen times. His special envoy, Steve Witkoff, keeps going to Moscow. They have good conversations.\par \par \uc2 What has Trump gotten out of all of this negotiating? Nothing. Nada. Zippo. Every night, the Russians continue to bomb Ukraine. One can only hope that, in the conversation that Trump and Putin had this week, Trump had some signal that Trump -- that Putin is ready to change his way, to make some concessions here.\par \par \uc2 I don`t yet see it, but let`s hope for the best. Let`s hope that Trump can pull a rabbit out of the hat when he meets with Putin in Hungary.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Charles, bring Europe on stage here, NATO on stage. What role have they taken? And where do they fit in this narrative?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KUPCHAN: You know, they have really picked up the slack that Trump has left by backing away from giving Ukraine more economic and military support.\par \par \uc2 The Europeans are now buying American weapons. They`re increasing economic support to Ukraine. But I think, in the end of the day, if we`re going to get a deal here, Trump has to step up and say, hey, I`m going to help Ukraine until the job is done.\par \par \uc2 Putin did -- Putin isn`t going to stop until he runs into a stopping force. And I think Biden got it right by giving Ukraine what it needed to defend itself, but he dropped the ball on diplomacy. Trump does the diplomacy, but he dropped the ball on helping Ukraine. He`s got to help Ukraine, keep the diplomatic door open, and he may well get across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Charles Kupchan, thank you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors today unsealed a stunning criminal complaint alleging a man living in Louisiana helped plan and even took part in Hamas` deadly October 7 attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 As CBS` Kati Weis reports, the suspect made his first court appearance today.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voice-over): Prosecutors say this is 33-year-old Mahmoud Amin Ya`qub Al-Muhtadi in Gaza, along with other militants, before Hamas` deadly attack on October 7.\par \par \uc2 According to a federal criminal complaint, Al-Muhtadi communicated with a paramilitary group that helped conduct the terror attack. The complaint says the cell phone data shows him near one of the targeted neighborhoods. More than 1,200 people in Israel were killed, including more than 40 American citizens.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say they obtained phone calls that Al-Muhtadi placed the morning of the attack. In one call, he says -- quote -- "Get ready. The borders are open." In another, he tells someone -- quote -- "Bring the rifles and whatever you have."\par \par \uc2 So far, Al-Muhtadi, who was living in Louisiana since at least this summer, is the only accused conspirator expected to face an American jury. He received a U.S. visa last September, nearly a year after the attack.\par \par \uc2 CBS national security expert Sam Vinograd.\par \par \uc2 Who do you believe may have dropped the ball here?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: It does appear that, in this case, the U.S. government was not able to conduct social media vetting on this individual prior to him being granted a visa and entering the United States.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: Vinograd says Al-Muhtadi posed a clear national security threat.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: This individual in the first instance never should have left Gaza, but certainly should not have gained access to the United States.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: And an FBI task force based here in New Orleans is credited with tracking down Al-Muhtadi.\par \par \uc2 Today, in court, he repeatedly said that he was innocent and requested better jail accommodations, so he can practice his religion -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Kati Weis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, here are 3 Things to Know.\par \par \uc2 Where`s the Picasso? Police in Spain are investigating the disappearance of a Picasso painting worth about $700,000. The 106-year-old painting, Still Life With Guitar, is small, roughly the size of a postcard. It vanished earlier this month while being transported from Madrid to an exhibition in Grenada. It was discovered missing when the exhibitor`s curator noticed it was not among the delivered artworks.\par \par \uc2 President Trump`s immigration crackdown could have dramatic effects on the economy, according to a new study from the National Foundation for American Policy. The study finds the mass deportations of immigrants will reduce the U.S. work force by nearly seven million people by 2028 and by nearly 16 million 10 years from now.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss running shoe company On is being sued in a class action lawsuit in which customers complain their CloudTec sneakers consistently make a loud, embarrassing squeaking sound. The shoes in question cost between $140 and $180 a pair. The company had no comments, squeaky or otherwise.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on Evening News Plus in tonight`s In Depth: the growing criticism over the arrest tactics used in the administration`s crackdown on illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: The Trump administration`s illegal immigration crackdown continues to intensify.\par \par \uc2 Federal authorities announced 1,400 immigration arrests across Massachusetts on Thursday. But some of the tactics used in the operations are coming under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s In Depth, Anna Schecter examines the rules of engagement.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER (voice-over): From crashing vehicles to forceful takedowns, these encounters with federal law enforcement captured on cell phone video have gone viral.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just ICE, Border Patrol agents trained to catch drug smugglers and human traffickers in the desert now deployed on the streets of cities like Chicago.\par \par \uc2 What jumps out at you when you`re watching these scenes play out on social media?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANDWEG (Former Acting ICE Director): Yes, what strikes me immediately is how this administration has put these agents in a terrible position.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: John Sandweg is the former acting director of ICE.\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR JOHN SANDWEG: These are units that are designed to address high-risk threats and bring overwhelming force to bear. And I think the big flaw here is not what -- not -- I don`t blame these agents. I blame the administration for putting agents to -- who are trained in this manner in a city where they don`t belong.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: In Chicago, federal agents deployed tear gas on a crowd on Tuesday...\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It`s sad. I -- this is sad. This is very sad.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: ... prompting a judge to express concern and ask agents to wear body cameras.\par \par \uc2 Illinois Governor J.B. Pritzker:\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR J.B. PRITZKER (D-Illinois): I`m glad that the judge is stepping up, doing the right thing. I think we`re going to see more cases brought against ICE and CBP.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: So, what are the rules of engagement for these federal agents?\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR JOHN SANDWEG: Well, generally, the use of force that you use needs to be reasonable to the threat that you face. You are required to de -- try to de-escalate a situation first.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Do you have a warrant? They haven`t asked for a warrant.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: We showed some of the clips to DHS, and they told us the videos show "a growing and dangerous trend of illegal aliens and agitators obstructing and attacking law enforcement."\par \par \uc2 JERRY ROBINETTE (Former Special Agent in Charge, U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement Homeland Security Investigations): It`s very stressful. Both sides are at a very height of stress.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: Jerry Robinette has 34 years of law enforcement experience as a police officer and a Homeland Security Investigations special agent in charge.\par \par \uc2 JERRY ROBINETTE: The agents have a mission. They`re only going to tolerate so much. And when somebody crosses that line, the administration has pretty much given them orders that they are not to back down.\par \par \uc2 ANNA SCHECTER: A new show of force increasing across the country.\par \par \uc2 Anna Schecter, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Since the initial uncertainty surrounding the president`s trade war rattled markets, the stocks have been on a steady climb.\par \par \uc2 The S&P index has seen nearly 15 percent growth year to date, but the ride isn`t infinite. We have seen downturns before. The crash of 1929 was the most notable.\par \par \uc2 Bestselling author Andrew Ross Sorkin examines the lessons from 1929 in his new book. We recently talked to him for tonight`s Interview.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: So, Andrew, just to start, take us back to 1929 and the role that the stock market played in American life.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN (Columnist, "The New York Times"): So, the 19 -- it`s not even 1929. Even the 1920s was when the -- really the first time that Americans, ordinary people, could invest in the stock market.\par \par \uc2 There were brokerages emerging on the corners of streets literally like Starbucks. And you could walk into one of these places, and if you had a dollar, they would lend you $10 so that you could go buy stocks.\par \par \uc2 And, really, business had become almost A part of the culture in a way that it had never been before. These CEOs were on the covers of magazines like celebrities.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Now, there are two sections in this book.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: One is the crash. Let`s start with that.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN: When the crash came, when the stock market fell, it wasn`t just that the stock went from $50 to $30 and they lost $20. Because they had borrowed so much money, they oftentimes lost everything.\par \par \uc2 And so you had people whose whole life was completely devastated. As a result, you had people literally jumping out of windows.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: The second portion of your book is more about the economic devastation that happened afterwards. How was the market crash related to the run on the banks, related to the Depression?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN: So, that`s the thing about the `29 crash that I think is misunderstood more than anything else, which is, the crash in October of `29 was really just the first domino in a series of dominoes and policy mistakes that started then.\par \par \uc2 There was the psychological scarring of America that took place in October of `29. But then there was a series of decisions of, how do you handle this? How do you deal with this in the real economy?\par \par \uc2 And you had President Hoover and President -- and the treasury secretary, Andrew Mellon, both of whom effectively thought -- who thought this was almost a psychological problem. Nobody wanted to pour money into the system at the time. And that was actually the -- economically, the absolutely wrong decision.\par \par \uc2 So, unlike what the Federal Reserve did in 2008, when we had a financial crisis and they poured money at it...\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN: ... this Federal Reserve did the opposite.\par \par \uc2 And then, on top of that, you had President Hoover put a whole series of tariffs in place, Smoot-Hawley, 1930. Every economist in the country was going down to Washington, saying, please don`t do this, in a very similar way, frankly, to what`s happened now.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: They played a bad hand badly.\par \par \uc2 Are -- is there -- there are so many great characters in here. Is there a key villain, or is it a kind of conspiracy of bad choices that led to the...\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN: I like to think it`s a conspiracy of bad choices.\par \par \uc2 I think Charles Mitchell, who ran a bank called National City, which becomes Citigroup, is just this fascinating character. He was really the most famous banker of his time and had really led the way in terms of lending money to lots of people to trade. And he became the face of the crash.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: When you were working on this -- certainly, as a reader, I feel it -- did your -- the hairs on the back here next stand up and go, there`s a little of this going on today?\par \par \uc2 And if you felt that way, where do you see it or feel it?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN: So, the truth is, when I first started the project, I thought I was just writing about history.\par \par \uc2 And as I was continuing on and on and on, and headlines would come about tariffs or regulations or taking regulations away or this or that, I thought, oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 One of the big features of this period was this idea of democratizing finance. It was going to no longer be for the elites. It was going to be for everybody. And I think, right now, that`s a big theme in America.\par \par \uc2 Everybody wants access to the lottery ticket.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Andrew Ross Sorkin. The book is "1929."\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: My Reporter`s Notebook is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: What is work for?\par \par \uc2 A new survey shows managers and Gen Z give very different answers. NYU Professor Suzy Welch and San Diego State sociologist Daniel Davis commissioned the study to examine workplace values, because, when values align, companies thrive. When they don`t, stress, disengagement, turnover.\par \par \uc2 They found that managers want employees who strive for success that others recognize and who see work as central to their decision-making and behavior. Gen Z, on the other hand, wants purpose, balance, and belonging in work.\par \par \uc2 The overall disparity in values between the two groups was huge. Only 2 percent of Gen Z respondents align with the values employers most prize. The boss wants reliability. The applicant wants resonance. They`re not arguing over hours. They`re over what hours are for.\par \par \uc2 For most of the last century, the purpose of work was survival and security. Then came ambition and achievement. Now the new generation adds a new measure, meaning. They want to know that their hours add up to something more than output. That`s not a rejection of hard work. It`s a search for proportion and alignment.\par \par \uc2 Alignment has become a jargon word, a fancy way for the boss to say fit the mold, but real alignment is when everyone, from the intern to the CEO, moves by the same North Star. When people ask for meaning, they`re asking to belong to something larger than themselves.\par \par \uc2 It`s the spark in a teacher`s best lesson plan, the lift a nurse feels after a long shift, a line worker`s pride when the product rolls, a reporter`s satisfaction when notebook scribbles nourish others.\par \par \uc2 The best work carries the hope that what we build or uncover will last beyond the shift, the quarter, the deadline. The next generation isn`t redefining work. It`s reminding us what work was meant to be all along, a job worthy of our hours, a place to build things that last and that are true, and to leave the people in the place better than we found them, whether that place is the office, the community or the world.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s CBS Evening News Plus. I`m John Dickerson.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020260205elah0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For October 16, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Charlie D`Agata, Kelly O`Grady,  Elaine Quijano, Nikki Battiste, Cristian Benavides, Maurice DuBois, John  Dickerson, Lonnie Quinn \par \uc2 3,794 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I just think he`s a bad person.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A former national security adviser accused of a national security breach, \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  indicted today on charges he illegally stored and transmitted national defense information.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also tonight: After a lengthy phone call with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , President Trump says they will hold a summit in Hungary to try to end the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We want to get peace.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH MAURER (Hillsborough County, Florida, Deputy Police Chief): These were predators, and they were sophisticated.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: High-tech crime. Robbery suspects accused of using an air tag to track their victims` car. Then:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY CHIEF JOSEPH MAURER: The wife opens her door, and she`s immediately attacked.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And a movie theater where everyone`s a star.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN EHRMAN (Employee): They see me, they understand me, and they accept me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: For the third time in four weeks, the Justice Department has secured a criminal indictment against a political opponent of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A federal grand jury in Maryland today indicted the president`s first-term National Security Adviser \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  for allegedly storing and transmitting classified information.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane is in Washington with the details.\par \par \uc2 And, Scott, this case is different from the others.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This one allegedly includes top secret defense information.\par \par \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  tonight and his defense lawyer both say no crime was committed, and they look forward to proving that. But, in the meantime, he`s a criminal defendant facing the prospect of prison time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): Two months after federal agents searched John Bolton`s home in Maryland and his office in Washington, a grand jury has indicted Bolton on 18 counts, including transmission of national defense information and unlawful retention of national defense information.\par \par \uc2 The charging documents allege the former national security adviser shared more than 1,000 pages of highly sensitive information about his day-to-day activities in the White House, including classified national defense information, some of it top secret, and some of which he e-mailed in diary- like entries to relatives between April 2018 and September 2019.\par \par \uc2 President Trump responded to Bolton`s indictment this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I didn`t know that. You`re telling me for the first time, but I think he`s a bad person. I think he`s a bad guy. Yes, he`s a bad guy. It`s too bad, but that`s the way it goes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Bolton was a top Trump adviser in the president`s first term, but he departed after 17 months over fierce disagreements over foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BOLTON\uc2  (Former U.S. National Security Adviser): He`s just not receptive to new facts. The intelligence briefings don`t communicate as much information as they should.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The two men have clashed frequently since then. Bolton wrote a book about the Trump White House which described the president as erratic and a stunningly uninformed leader.\par \par \uc2 Bolton is the third Trump rival to face criminal charges in recent weeks. Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and New York Attorney General Letitia James were both charged in Virginia. All three deny wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE (Former U.S. Deputy Assistant Attorney General): This indictment is definitely much more substantial than the indictments we saw against \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2  and Tish James. It paints a picture, allegedly, of a defendant who was careless with classified information, with information concerning the national defense.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The court is expected to schedule an arraignment in this matter in the coming days, which means \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  will face a judge at the federal courthouse in Greenbelt, Maryland. He will likely have his fingerprints taken, have a booking photo taken.\par \par \uc2 And a judge has been assigned, John and Maurice, a 2013 appointee of then- President Obama.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Scott MacFarlane in D.C., thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : After making peace between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , President Trump is turning to the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 He will meet at the White House tomorrow with President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Today, Mr. Trump had a long phone call with Russian President Putin, and, afterward, he announced they will soon be holding a summit in Hungary.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): We hope we`re going to get it stopped.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): Fresh off his peace efforts in the Middle East, President Trump is now turning back to the one deal that has eluded him for nine months.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Because of my relationship with President Putin, I thought this would be very quick.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In a bid to kick-start talks, Trump agreed today to hold another one-on-one meeting with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , like the one he just hosted in Alaska in August.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We have made some headway.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Back then, Trump thought he could coax Putin into sitting down with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I think, if everything works out well today, we will have a trilat.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But Putin never committed to it, and White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt said today that hasn`t changed.\par \par \uc2 Did he say what it would take for him to be willing to sit down with Zelenskyy finally as well?\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): I don`t want to get into that, but, as I just said, the president and President Putin discussed meeting together in Budapest. And President Trump will talk about that opportunity with President Zelenskyy when he`s here at the White House tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Zelenskyy will likely press Trump tomorrow to send him long- range Tomahawk missiles capable of striking deep in Russian territory. Trump said he warned Putin today that he might do it when the two spoke by phone for 2.5 hours.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I did actually say: "Would you mind if I gave a couple of thousand Tomahawks to your opposition?" I did say that to him. I said it just that way. He didn`t like the idea.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Ukrainian officials said today that a massive overnight strike by Russia is a sign of Putin`s disinterest in peace.\par \par \uc2 And on the eve of his visit to the White House, Zelenskyy argued that Putin is only reengaging now in order to discourage Trump from sending those Tomahawk missiles.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Nancy Cordes at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now to the escalating tensions between the United States and Venezuela, whose dictator is under U.S. indictment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We learned today that the U.S. has put on a display of military muscle at Nicolas Maduro`s doorstep.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Charlie D`Agata.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): In a dramatic new show of force, three B-52 long-range bombers flew for hours yesterday off the coast of Venezuela. Late today, the commander in charge of the mission, Admiral Alvin Holsey, abruptly stepped down, a surprise move less than a year into the job.\par \par \uc2 That`s after President Trump told reporters he authorized CIA operations in the country, prompting concern from Democrats on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-Connecticut): Starting wars that may spiral out of control ought to be deeply alarming to the American public.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: There are now 10,000 troops in the Caribbean, including eight warships and a submarine. And new images show military helicopters, which could carry special operations soldiers, 90 miles off Venezuela`s coast.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): We are certainly looking at land now, because we have got the sea very well under control.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The U.S. military took out another alleged drug-carrying boat this week, the fifth strike in six weeks. Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro has fired back against the escalation, saying there will be no regime change or CIA-orchestrated coup.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has not explicitly called for regime change, but the administration has made clear it does not want Maduro to remain in power. There`s a $50 million reward for information leading to his arrest for alleged drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Former U.S. Ambassador to Panama John Feeley said Maduro has long been a problem for the U.S.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FEELEY (Former U.S. Ambassador to Panama): He is the nominal head of a government that has been wholly captured by organized crime in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: On the mission commander`s sudden resignation, Defense Secretary Hegseth released a statement thanking Admiral Holsey for his 37 years of service, but provided no reason for his departure. Sources tell CBS News there was tension between the two men.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: All right, Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge has ordered agents to wear body cameras as they carry out immigration arrests in the Chicago area. This comes after ICE agents deployed tear gas against protesters this week. The Trump administration says the protests have become increasingly violent and that officers have had to use tear gas to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Officials in Alaska say nearly 2,000 miles of the state`s western coastline was affected by the powerful storm that hit last weekend. Remote Native villages were wiped out. Hundreds of residents are being airlifted to cities hundreds of miles away. At least 1,600 people are staying in shelters.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Diane Keaton`s death certificate released today says the Oscar winner died of pneumonia. No underlying causes were listed. Keaton died in Santa Monica, California, last Saturday, and her body was cremated several days later. She was 79.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The price of new cars has hit a milestone. The average sale price in September was for the first time more than $50,000.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And 94,000 vehicles sold for more than $75,000.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady looks at what`s driving car prices higher.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: What are you hearing from consumers when they come in here?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BENSTOCK (General Manager and Vice President, Paragon Honda and Paragon Acura): Prices too high.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY (voice-over): Brian Benstock is the vice president and general manager of Paragon Honda and Acura in Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BENSTOCK: Car prices seem to have been rising ever since COVID, and there`s no turning back on that.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And why is that?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BENSTOCK: Manufacturers during COVID were producing more expensive cars and American consumers wanted those more expensive cars.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: For the first time ever, Americans are paying more than $50,000 on average for a new car. That`s according to \uc2 Kelley Blue Book\uc2 . Part of the price increase is due to wealthier shoppers opting for luxury vehicles and E.V.s, which are more expensive. But features are another driver.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BENSTOCK: Cars have never been better equipped than they are today. Cars have never been safer than they are today. A good portion of the increase in the price of cars is the content that the customers are getting. It`s not strictly due to inflation.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The increased cost for new cars has budget-conscious consumers shopping used, driving up those prices as well.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BENSTOCK: They take a look at the new car prices. They see that they`re higher than they`d like them to be. The alternative would be to go to a certified pre-owned car or used car.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The affordability issue goes beyond just the sticker price. Auto loan interest rates and insurance premiums have drivers struggling to make payments.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BENSTOCK: They try and fit their car payment into their monthly budget. So...\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Benstock says it`s time for manufacturers to shift their offerings.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BENSTOCK: As long as we`re focusing on what the customers want, not what the manufacturers want, I think we will be able to bridge that affordability gap.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Now, car prices are up, but here`s some good news. The national average for a gallon of gas is just about $3, and that`s the lowest it`s been in four years.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Kelly O`Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Coming up next on the CBS Evening News: Police say robbery suspects attached an AirTag to their victims` car to follow them home.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also tonight, Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: I`m Elaine Quijano in Montclair, New Jersey, one of many towns seeing a surge in e-bike sales. What are the consequences?\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And a movie theater staffed almost entirely by people who`ve often got trouble finding jobs.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : As crimes go, this one is particularly frightening for the way the suspects allegedly pulled off a plan burglary.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The police in Hillsborough County, Florida, say the suspects attached a tracking device to the victim`s car and used it to follow them to their home.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides on how it all played out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES (voice-over): The couple had just pulled their car into the driveway one night in August. Newly released surveillance video shows two men walking over and then forcing them into the garage.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH MAURER (Hillsborough County, Florida, Deputy Police Chief): The wife opens her door and she`s immediately attacked by one of our suspects. He physically grabs her and pulls her out of the car. Almost simultaneously, the driver and husband gets pulled out of the car with a gun into his waist.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Police say the suspects, attempting to rob the home, used an AirTag taped under the bumper to track their vehicle.\par \par \uc2 They were lying in wait, but, once inside the house, an alarm went off and they took off. The two men, 26-year-old Luis Charles and 32-year-old Omardy Maldonado-Rodriguez, are now behind bars, booked on charges, including armed burglary, robbery with a firearm and illegal installation of a tracking device.\par \par \uc2 This deputy body camera footage shows the moment one of them was pulled over and taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What`s your first name? Last name is Charles, right?\par \par \uc2 LUIS CHARLES (Suspect): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Luis?\par \par \uc2 LUIS CHARLES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is that your first name? All right, can you just step out of the car real quick for me?\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: What`s so interesting is that the AirTag helped the burglars track their victims, but in this case, it also helped you catch them.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY CHIEF JOSEPH MAURER: Absolutely. They used their name to purchase it. They used their name to register it and their phone number and their e- mail address. So that was the big break in the case because we were able to identify through video surveillance.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Roberto Larreal is owner of a home security company in Miami.\par \par \uc2 What should people look out for?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTO LARREAL (Owner, Inhoome): That`s a good question. You know, the iPhone can let whether a AirTag was found near you. So it keeps you aware.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Authorities say, if you see those notifications and you don`t know where they`re coming from, then that means that you should contact them. Now, \uc2 Apple\uc2  says that it works closely with law enforcement and safety groups and that each AirTag is tied to a specific \uc2 Apple\uc2  I.D. and serial number.\par \par \uc2 This AirTag has a serial number inside the battery compartment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Cristian Benavides, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Up next: danger on wheels.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Eye on America looks at the risks posed by e-bikes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: In Eye on America, we look at issues facing the nation -- tonight, e-bikes.\par \par \uc2 Hard to believe, but electric bikes have been around since the late 1800s. Now they`re more popular than ever. But all too often, a ride on an e-bike ends in the E.R.\par \par \uc2 We caution you here, some of the images in Elaine Quijano`s report are graphic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voice-over): Stunning surveillance video shows a 15-year- old going an estimated 40 miles per hour on an e-bike and slamming into a car in Montvale, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 MIKE GHASSALI (Mayor of Montvale, New Jersey): I see that kid flip over the car, but I hope he`s alive.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: The boy survived. But for Montvale Mayor Mike Ghassali, maybe the most troubling part, the crash wasn`t a complete surprise.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MIKE GHASSALI: We get calls almost every day where these kids are on their bikes doing stunts. And that`s how they get in trouble.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: E-bikes, now a nearly $2 billion market, are exploding in popularity.\par \par \uc2 What have you seen here in your community with respect to e-bikes?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MIKE GHASSALI: This is the first year now that we see an issue with the e-bikes. All of a sudden, the parents are buying these really high- speed bikes.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: But with increased sales come increased risks for pedestrians like this woman in Minnesota, who was struck by a 14-year-old while on a sidewalk, and more and more for younger riders, like this 15- year-old in Massachusetts who survived, and a 13-year-old in New Jersey who did not.\par \par \uc2 In fact, e-bike E.R. visits among children 10 to 17 have more than tripled since 2019.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG CORNELL (Diamond Cycle): So an e-bike has a battery. Typically, they`re in the frame right here.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: E-bikes make up nearly 40 percent of sales at Craig Cornell`s Diamond Cycle in Montclair, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 How fast can some of these e-bikes go?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG CORNELL: Some e-bikes can go 50 miles an hour or more.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: He won`t sell any e-bikes that go faster than 20 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG CORNELL: They`re really not e-bikes. They`re really e-motorcycles.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Nationwide, a patchwork of regulations governs e-bike safety. But New Jersey Congressman \uc2 Josh Gottheimer\uc2  says they`re not enough.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JOSH GOTTHEIMER\uc2  (D-New Jersey): I`m very worried about kids being safe.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: He introduced legislation that addresses helmet use for e- bike riders under 18 and offers grant money to police departments that crack down on unsafe underage riding.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JOSH GOTTHEIMER\uc2 : My son has an e-bike.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: What was your thought process in getting him an e-bike?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JOSH GOTTHEIMER\uc2 : I was opposed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JOSH GOTTHEIMER\uc2 : And then a lot of his friends are riding him. And my process was, OK, let`s talk about what rules you`re going to have to follow.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MIKE GHASSALI: The parents think that they`re doing a favor. They`re making the kid happy. It`s a death trap.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: A new way for teenagers to get around, with increasingly disastrous results.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Elaine Quijano in Montvale, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Well, Shakespeare said the play is the thing -- up next, a theater where it`s all about the work.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally here tonight, this is National Disability Employment Awareness month, and we were made aware of a business that hires people with disabilities.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It`s a multiplex that shows first-run movies.\par \par \uc2 But Nikki Battiste tells us the real story in this theater is what`s happening off the screens.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bring it in.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice-over): It`s the employees at the Ridgefield, Connecticut, Prospector movie theater who make it unique.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: About 75 percent of them live with a disability.\par \par \uc2 BEN EHRMAN (Employee): There`s so many places, especially in the job market, that you can help but feel judged for having a disability or being neurodivergent. But, here, I feel like I`m accepted as I am.\par \par \uc2 It`s about $12.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Ben Ehrman has a degree in paleontology.\par \par \uc2 BEN EHRMAN: And here today I`m going to give you a preview to the upcoming movie Jurassic World Rebirth.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The theater pairs its employees with jobs that highlight their strengths, a practice they call sparkle.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Schultz says she has been living with a disability since a car accident 18 years ago caused a severe brain injury.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SCHULTZ (Employee): Back to feeling useful and able and, like, appreciated and um -- sorry.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SCHULTZ: It`s really a pleasure to be here. It`s been such a bright spot. I mean, of course, having a job feels more productive, feels like a more positive life direction.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: How has working here impacted your life?\par \par \uc2 I actually had some issues and doubts about myself, but after working with everyone here, I feel like I can finally believe in myself again.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Nikki Battiste, CBS News, Ridgefield, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You know, we talk about it all the time. Everybody wants to be seen, wants to be heard. Perfect example. It brings you to tears, right?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes, yes, it does. Thank goodness Nikki was out there bringing that to us to see it.\par \par \uc2 That`s the CBS Evening News. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . The news continues on Evening News Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Thanks for watching. I`m Maurice DuBois. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020251017elag00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump: CIA to Conduct Covert Operations in Venezuela; ICE Agents Clash with Protesters in Chicago; Typhoon Wipes Out Community in Alaska; CDC Scientist Sounds Alarm on Layoffs; \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Struggles to Find Hostages Bodies Amid Rubble; News Organizations Reject New Pentagon Rules; Tale of Two Economies; Kevin Federline on Life with Britney Spears; Victoria`s Secret Fashion Show Dazzles; Breaking Down America`s Protein Craze; Supreme Court Considers Major Redistricting Case; Jeremy Allen White on Playing Bruce Springsteen; A Look at CBS Country Music Competition "The Road;" New Book Teaches How to Learn to be Brave; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Weijia Jiang, Ash- Har Quraishi, Rob Marciano, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Debora Patta, Jill  Schlesinger, Kevin Frazier, Adam Yamaguchi, Anthony Salvanto, Cassie  DiLaura \par \uc2 14,295 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Thursday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is on his way back from Israel. This morning, President Trump says he has authorized the CIA to conduct covert action in Venezuela. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The President confirms that he`s authorized the CIA to operate inside Venezuela after U.S. attacks on boats from that country.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): A lot of the Venezuelan drugs come in through the sea, so you get to see that. But we are going to stop them by land also.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have new details of the clashes between protesters and ICE agents in Chicago. We are there with the local reaction.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Scary. It is so scary.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In a new book, Britney Spears` ex-husband says he is worried about the pop star`s mental health.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FEDERLINE (Britney Spears` ex-husband): I might wake up my sons are going to have to deal with the unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The family of actress, Diane Keaton is now revealing the cause of death. They say the 79-year-old died of pneumonia.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: A moment of distraction would have turned deadly for this woman if it wasn`t for a security guard that pulled her to safety in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: She was pulled away at the last second. See that! Wow!\par \par \uc2 JOE MAZZULLA (\uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2  coach): You`re not playing against each other, you`re playing against the coaches.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Members of the Celtics media team were expecting to play a pickup game versus each other.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Instead of playing each other, they would be taking on the coaching staff.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: And it went about as well as you would expect. It got ugly fast. 57 to four.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: What is that? Two field goals? That`s all they got.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Those poor guys. I couldn`t have done much better.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Me neither.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The coaching staff woke up and chose violence.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s eye opener is presented by Progressive. Making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you feel good about yourself, athlete at the table, if you lose 57 to four?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no. But if you won -- listen, you play to win the game! Hello?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m sure they`re like, okay, there`s a reason why they`re on the media team, not playing professional sports.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s begin this morning with the Trump administration ramping up pressure on Venezuela with some of the most aggressive moves against a nearby nation in more than 40 years.\par \par \uc2 U.S. strikes have sunk five boats the U.S. says were from Venezuela. Officials says they were carrying drugs, though there is no firm evidence of that. Now President Trump says he`s told the CIA to start operating inside Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at The White House.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Vlad, good morning to you. Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 The CIA has not commented so far, but you`re right, President Trump confirmed he authorized the agency to go into Venezuela to conduct covert action. Now, he says, the U.S. military may expand its operations.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Hosting a dinner last night, President Trump joked about going fishing off the coast of Venezuela. In recent weeks, his administration has announced five strikes on boats off its coast, killing 27 people.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We had a lot of drugs coming in by water and that stopped. In fact, nobody wants to go fishing anymore.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Earlier in the Oval Office, Trump told CBS News` Ed O`Keefe that he authorized the CIA to get involved, due to a flood of drugs from Venezuela and criminals.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Why did you authorize the CIA to go into Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I authorized for two reasons, really. Number one, they have emptied their prisons into the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: While hundreds of thousands of Venezuelans have come legally and illegally to the United States in recent years, there`s no evidence they come out of prisons.\par \par \uc2 In Caracas, Venezuelan president Nicolas Maduro responded saying there would be no regime change or CIA-orchestrated coup.\par \par \uc2 (NICOLAS MADURO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The U.S. military has added about 10,000 troops in the Caribbean, including 2,000 Marines aboard the USS Iwo Jima. Trump warned it could launch strikes on land.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We are certainly looking at land now, because we`ve got the sea very well under control.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The administration recently notified members of Congress that the U.S. is in a non-international armed conflict with drug cartels. Democrats warned the strikes risk getting the U.S. into a full-fledged war, and Senator Bernie Sanders says the President needs authority to go to war from Congress.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): The President is acting unilaterally in violation of the Constitution. That`s a pretty big deal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In August, the administration announced a $50 million reward for information leading to the arrest of Maduro linking him to a Venezuelan drug cartel.\par \par \uc2 Asked if the CIA has authority to take Maduro out, the President Trump said it would be ridiculous for him to answer that question, adding he believes Venezuela is, "feeling the heat" -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Weijia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the latest on the immigration enforcement showdown in Chicago. We saw ICE agents throwing teargas at protesters this week, and now there is new video of a deliberate collision as agents chased down to two suspects. People who live there say they are now living in fear.\par \par \uc2 Ash-Har Quraishi spoke to them and he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Ash-Har good morning to you, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, here at the ICE facility in Broadview, which has been a flashpoint for recent protests, the scene was calm last night as demonstrators obeyed a new protest curfew, put into place by the Broadview mayor, but it was a different story elsewhere in the Chicago area earlier this weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: This video shows the end of a -speed car chase in Chicago Tuesday. DHS says U.S. Border Patrol agents driving a white SUV intentionally hit the back of the car to apprehend two undocumented immigrants.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting "ICE go home!")\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Police say a crowd formed and as officers moved in to calm the scene, some people threw objects at federal agents, in response, the agents deployed teargas hitting both protesters and officers and made several arrests.\par \par \uc2 Juanita Garnica (sp?) says her 16-year-old son was detained.\par \par \uc2 JUANITA GARNICA (Mother): Nobody called. Nobody did anything.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: In recent weeks, we`ve seen confrontations with immigration officers escalate across the Chicago area, sparking fear and outrage.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting)\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Federal agents detained this young man outside a \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and pulled this woman out of a car near a school. DHS accused her of stalking an ICE vehicle.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They don`t have a warrant though.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: And this video shows ICE agents slamming a man to the ground. He was carried away on a stretcher.\par \par \uc2 What`s the feeling in the community right now?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So sad, scary.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: On Wednesday, this group canvassed homes and businesses, sharing information about people`s rights during immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANDWEG (Former Acting Director, U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement): I`ve never seen immigration enforcement done in this particular manner with this level of aggression.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Former acting ICE Director John Sandweg who served in the Obama administration argues that immigration operations are meant to be discreet, not played out in public.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANDWEG: I think that the administration has put these agents in a really tough spot. They`ve put them on a very contentious and politically charged mission.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Now, it`s not likely that that`s -- that there is going to be a pull out anytime soon. Meantime, the top judge in Cook County issued an order that stops ICE agents from detaining anyone coming in or out of the courthouse, which went into effect yesterday -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ash-Har, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of Alaskans are being airlifted to safety in what the National Guard there calls one of the most significant evacuations in the state`s history. It comes after the remnants of a typhoon brought a record storm surge to the state`s Southwestern Coast, sweeping away homes and killing at least one person.\par \par \uc2 Emergency Management officials said yesterday that the two hard-hit villages have to be completely evacuated. CBS News meteorologist Rob Marciano has more on this and another storm moving into the middle of the country.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Kipnuk, one of those villages, nearly all of those homes` at-sea level have been destroyed, reports from there. And you know, you had it go way up to get any sort of safety.\par \par \uc2 This is the time of year where these storms come from the Pacific and traverse into parts of Alaska. Over a hundred-mile-per-hour winds here. We had a storm surge of 6.6 feet. A thousand people displaced here. And now they have another storm that`s coming at them today, so they have that to deal with.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in California, the cleanup is underway in Topanga Canyon. That`s where some mudslides were from the rainfall we had there. So they have got the heavy equipment out yet again, and this is certainly early for this time of year.\par \par \uc2 That storm that is now in The Rockies and will be coming out into the Plains as we go through the next couple of days. So what that is going to do is start to pump up the heat and humidity. And that`s what`s indicated by this blue and some of the green areas and by the time we get to Saturday, we`re looking at a potential -- not only see some record high temperatures across Texas, but maybe some severe weather across the South Central spots of the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, over to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Rob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge has ordered the Trump administration not to fire any government employees for now, while the government shutdown continues. The White House Budget Director says they`re looking to cut more than 10,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns spoke with a CDC scientist who was let go and says, people will die because of those cutbacks.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hey, good morning to you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Well, nearly 600 people were fired from the CDC and now they face even more uncertainty all while warning about the consequences for public health.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The Trump administration used the government shutdown to lay off more than 4,000 federal workers before the courts stepped in. Budget Chief \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2  says that there are plans to go much further.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL VOUGHT\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Office of Management and Budget\uc2  Director): I think we`ll probably end up being north of 10,000.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Among those who received a layoff notice over the weekend is this CDC scientist, who asked us to conceal her identity for fear of retribution.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s truly like an episode of "Squid Games," because we don`t know what`s going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: She`s worked at the agency for the past decade, studying chronic diseases like diabetes and heart disease and warns about the impact of the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 For everyday Americans, is their health -- their public health at risk?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Absolutely. The everyday American should be very concerned. It`s going to mean more deaths, more preventable deaths. People will absolutely die from the impact of this administration.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A spokesperson for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  said the layoffs were part of cutting "a bloated bureaucracy" and that the positions were deemed "non-essential by their divisions." But the scientist we spoke to said the cuts at the CDC will impact responses to disease outbreaks, like the measles outbreak in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Five new cases were reported earlier this week, bringing the total to 16 since July with 139 students quarantined.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: These cuts don`t shrink government, they blind, silence, and disarm the nation`s ability to keep people safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And we`ve learned that among those laid off were people who were working on defending the country against biological threats, like pandemics and those who were monitoring natural disasters and cyberattacks on hospitals -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very scary stuff. Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In the Middle East this morning, the ceasefire in Gaza, it`s still holding. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has returned the bodies of two more Israeli hostages while Israel has returned at least 30 Palestinian bodies.\par \par \uc2 New video shows the staggering destruction in Gaza. One group supporting the family of the hostages says the peace process should not move forward until all of the bodies are returned. Officials in the region say that will be very difficult to do.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta is in East Jerusalem with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Debora, on paper, it sounded like a very good plan, please return all the bodies, but the reality has not been so easy.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Indeed, and a very good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well Hamas says he it has handed over all the hostage bodies it can and needs specialized equipment to retrieve the rest. Senior U.S. advisors told CBS News they are not at a point where they believe the ceasefire has been violated.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Israeli soldier Captain Daniel Perez was laid to rest on Wednesday after his family finally received his body held in Gaza for over two years.\par \par \uc2 Perez was killed fighting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  during the October 7 attack. For his family, it brings fresh pain.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It is a future we have to face, it is proof that you`re really gone.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: To date, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has returned nine bodies. Senior U.S. advisors have told CBS News that the level of destruction in Gaza has made it almost impossible to retrieve the rest, even if \uc2 Hamas\uc2  knew where they were buried.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has weighed in.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It`s a gruesome process. I almost hate to talk about it, but they`re digging. They`re actually digging. There are areas where they`re digging and they`re finding a lot of bodies and then they have to separate the bodies.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Israel said it would return the bodies of 15 Palestinians in exchange for every hostage.\par \par \uc2 For the past few days, the Red Cross has been transferring the remains of loved ones back to Gaza but that, too, has been mired in controversy.\par \par \uc2 (SAMER HAMAD speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "We saw with our own eyes clear signs of torture and execution," said Gazan Health official, Samer Hamad (sp?). Their hands and feet were cuffed, their eyes blindfolded.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Now, the bodies of 19 hostages remain in Gaza. Turkey has offered their assistance, given their extensive expertise in recent catastrophic earthquakes -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Debora Patta for us in East Jerusalem, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has joined other news organizations in declining to agree to a new set of requirements that would limit the media`s ability to cover The Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, dozens of journalists including our Charlie D`Agata packed up their things and left the building rather than accept the new rules. Last month, the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  told reporters they would have to sign document agreeing not to disclose classified or controlled information that was not previously approved for release.`\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, CBS News and four other outlets, including Fox News, the former employer of Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said the new policy threatens core journalistic protections. They vow "continue to cover the U.S. military as each of our organizations has done for many decades."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Money Watch," the nation`s largest banks are reporting a big jump in profits from last year. But on the flip side, Americans say that they are struggling.\par \par \uc2 A new report reveals 69 percent say their income is falling behind the cost of living. Five years ago, it was 50 percent. \uc2 CBS\uc2  news business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so the stock market is up 13 percent. Big banks are saying that they`re making money, but many Americans are saying, we are struggling.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what is going on right now in the overall economy?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know, what we know is, despite the stock market, which is just a measure of company`s ability to make money in the future, that the economy is slowing down from last year`s pace. We grew at about 2.8 percent last year. Most economists are pegging two percent for this year.\par \par \uc2 Tariffs, obviously, uncertainty, but we`re still growing and that is important. But we have seen that tariffs have created some price pressures, we`ve also seen that the uncertainty aren`t tariffs has caused a lot of Americans to become unnerved.\par \par \uc2 But overall, again, the big push in this economy so far, is the spending on A.I. Billions of dollars that companies are spending on artificial intelligence. By some measures, about it`s about 40 percent of the overall economic spending going on right now, so that, plus high-income earners who are spending, that has kept the economy afloat right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s been a lot of fear, a lot of talk about a market crash or even a correction. Is there anything that people should do or can do to protect themselves against that?\par \par \uc2 And how likely do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I hate the word "inevitable," but some people believe it`s coming.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I mean, here is what I want everyone to know. "Crash" is a very loaded word. Will the market take a breather at some point? Of course. I don`t know when that`s going to be. I don`t have a crystal ball out here that is going to tell you what is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : If you are an investor, you should know that you usually come in with a game plan. Some money in stocks, some money in bonds. If the stock position has gotten much bigger than what you thought you wanted it to be. If all of a sudden you`re 80 percent in stocks instead 60, go ahead and re-balance your account. Take some money out, get back to your prior allocation.\par \par \uc2 But I want to be clear about why some of the anxiety is popping up among regular Americans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Exactly. Because what about the people that don`t have stock portfolios?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Exactly!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am just talking about people that are living day-to-day, paycheck-to-paycheck.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Exactly! So you know, we had a Bankrate survey that came in and said, how many people have gotten a raise in the last year? And in the survey, just about half of people said, yes, I got a raise over the last year, but that means about half did not.\par \par \uc2 And so the cumulative effect of inflation, prices at the grocery store, prices for day care, prices for housing, prices for health care. That is putting pressure on people. Even if you are lucky enough to have a retirement plan at work, that is for decades in the future. It doesn`t help you for the here and now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what do they do? That`s what the question is.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So what they need to do is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What are we doing now?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Okay, first of all, you`re not alone. You should not feel crazy. That`s very important. Number two, make sure you watch your spending in the holiday season. Number three, work your network. Talk to people. You know how you get another job? You talk to people. Networking is a loaded word, but I need people to understand. One person a week is where you need to go. Find one person a week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, one person a week. I like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good advice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, thank you, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much. A lot to think about.\par \par \uc2 There`s new information about the cause of the death of a Hollywood icon. We`re talking about Diane Keaton. Her family is revealing how she died.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, Gayle talks with Jeremy Allen White and director, Scott Cooper about their new movie "Springsteen: Deliver me from nowhere" and Gayle brought a message for them from The Boss himself. Can`t wait to hear what it is.\par \par \uc2 Plus, some big names hit the catwalk at the new Victoria`s Secret fashion show.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch," we will incredible video of a quick-thinking security guard who saved a life on a train platform. You`re not going to want to miss this.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Diane Keaton, her family has revealed to "People" Magazine that the Oscar winner from pneumonia this past Saturday. Her family is thanking fans for the "extraordinary messages of love and support" that they`ve received. They received a ton. One source told "People," Keaton`s health declined very suddenly.\par \par \uc2 She was a star for more than 50 years in classic movies like "The Godfather," "Annie Hall," and "Something`s Gotta Give." We were all wondering about the cause of death and you hear it`s pneumonia in 2025. That`s so scary.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. It is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, Kevin Federline tells "Entertainment Tonight" that Britney Spears is still having trouble. Stick around. Kevin Frazier is with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A grain silo collapsed in Northeast Illinois yesterday. Workers were seen scrambling to escape the huge dust cloud. Investigators say one of the bins had shown signs of distress just moments earlier. We are pleased to say, no injuries reported, which is great news, although the collapse did knock out power to more than 150 homes and businesses. Yikes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very lucky on that one\par \par \uc2 We all know Britney Spears, she has had a tough time in recent years. We`ve watched it play out. Now her ex-husband is raising questions about her life today.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Federline`s memoir "You Thought You Knew" is out next week, but it is already getting major push back from Spears. The book re-visits their marriage with Federline saying he`s worried about their mental health.\par \par \uc2 In an exclusive interview, he told "Entertainment Tonight`s" Kevin Frazier his main priority is their two sons.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Kevin Federline is opening up about his life with Britney Spears, from their whirlwind romance to parenthood, her mental health struggles, and alleged substance abuse.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FEDERLINE ("You Thought You Knew" author): I kind of have to sound the alarm that I truly feel that somehow, someway, I just wish that their mom would get help. I`m not going to just expose her personal life, but it`s ten times worse than anything that I`ve said in my book.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Spears stunned fans in 2004 when she married backup dancer, Kevin Federline. Three years and two children later, they divorced.\par \par \uc2 Despite not having spoken to Spears in years, Federline wrote in his new book that his sons describe strange moments during visits with their mother. They would awaken sometimes at night to find her standing silently in the doorway, watching them sleep. "Oh, you`re awake," with a knife in her hand. Then she would turn around and pad off without explanation.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Federline also spoke about the Free Britney Movement and the end of a 13-year conservatorship which granted her father legal control of her finances and personal decisions.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FEDERLINE: Everybody deserves to be free, right? But at what cost?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Spears` team fired back, accusing Federline of profiting off of her with the book`s release.\par \par \uc2 There`s an assumption that you are doing this because the child support ended with Britney.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FEDERLINE: Really? That -- you want to go there right away, all right? I would be lying if I said that I didn`t want this book to successful. I really want Britney to be around for her children for a long time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re happy to say that Kevin Frazier joins us now. I have so many questions and thoughts.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So they`ve been divorced 18 years. I always think, if you`ve been in the inner circle, why is he doing it now? If he`s concerned about Britney, and I don`t doubt that, why wouldn`t he work behind-the-scenes to do that? And not profit from it?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Sure. First of all, everyone at this table is a parent. You know that your kids don`t care about how famous you are or who you are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also true.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: He wants his children to have a relationship with their mother. He cares about her. At the same time, he says, hey, look, I want to sell books, but I also want to tell my story, because he became infamous when became famous, because of the situation with Shar Jackson and her being pregnant when he met Britney.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And he has never really escaped that and he wants people to know who he is and what he has done in his life, besides his marriage with Britney.\par \par \uc2 So that is a portion of the book, but there are very damning details about their relationship in this. But at the end of the day, the kids want a relationship with their mother.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Kevin, why not work behind-the-scenes though to do this as opposed to revealing all of this this way?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: The children have told him that it`s okay to write this book. And the children --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The children are 19 and 20, they`re not little kids.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are young adults.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Right. He has shepherded them all the way up through high school and they graduated from high school and they are trying desperately to help their mother.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let me just show our viewers what Britney Spears posted on X last night responding in the claims in Federline`s book.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She posted: "The constant gaslighting from ex-husband is extremely hurtful and exhausting. And those white lies in that book, they are going straight to the bank and I am the only one who genuinely gets hurt here."\par \par \uc2 What`s your take on that?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: My take goes back to the children, who genuinely get hurt are the children. Nobody in this situation, and that`s Kevin included, wants to see anything happen to Britney.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: They want help for Britney, and if people don`t want to help her, then he doesn`t want them involved in the circle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you believe all the things he`s saying? He`s acting like Britney is in big trouble, well, she seems to be in big trouble when you look at those videos. He said, he worries about the day that his kids that they get a call that the unimaginable has happened.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Sure. And let`s go back --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you believe him?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: I do believe him, because let`s go back to 2008, in January, in that 51/50 that happened that night when she was taken to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what is that 51/50.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: I was there at the hospital. Yes, that 51/50 hold, because she was holding Jayden James, a toddler in the bathroom and the police had to come in and get her and she was taken to Cedars-Sinai Hospital that night. I was there at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t know the story behind what happened with Kevin and the children. Kevin had to come to get Jayden James, he had to prove that he wasn`t abusing them. Then they had to go to the house and check Preston out before they could move on.\par \par \uc2 So there have been a lot of things behind-the-scenes that have gone --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That have gone on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That people don`t know about.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: That we`re going to learn. You learn in this book a lot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You learn in the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s very complicated, but she clearly, everybody watching, you can see she needs help.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Kevin. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good to see you, Kev.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a star-studded Victoria`s Secret fashion show last night in New York City. People were on the buzz about this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Victoria`s Secret)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The iconic Missy Elliot, Latin artist, Karol G and singer Madison Beer performed and strutted on the runway along with the fashion superstars like Gigi Hadid and some new famous faces.\par \par \uc2 WNBA superstar Angel Reese, look at her -- earned her angel wings, sporting two lingerie looks. God, she looks good!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wooh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Looks great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was an historic moment for the Chicago Sky player and for decorated Olympic gymnast, Suni Lee. They both became first professional athletes to grace the iconic fashion show`s catwalks. Neither of the athletes are new to modeling. They have both been featured -- both featured in "Sports Illustrated" Magazine.\par \par \uc2 But Ashley Graham was there. It was really star-studded on there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You there, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Were you there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Didn`t want to walk the runway?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was at home in bed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve been -- you`re also a "Sports Illustrated" model.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, exactly, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what I am saying.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I was not there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, stick around. The American diet that has entered the protein boom. But how much protein do we really need? Adam Yamaguchi goes shopping with a dietitian to help make sense of this new trend that is exploding.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. We will be right back. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Popular protein shakes have been in the news recently after a new report found several powders and shakes contain high levels of lead. But that probably won`t keep some Americans from their protein obsession. According to one report, 61 percent of consumers increased their protein intake last year, but how much protein do we really need?\par \par \uc2 Adam Yamaguchi got some expert shopping advice to help us make healthier choices.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: My go-to these days, protein chips.\par \par \uc2 If you`ve walked into a supermarket lately, it`s hard to mis. Protein is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 I can eat a whole bag.\par \par \uc2 YASI ANSARI (Clinical dietitian at UCLA) Let`s be careful, while they have high amounts of protein, they`re also still high in sodium.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Yasi Ansari, political dietitian at UCLA, is helping me making sense of the dazzling, often confusing array of protein-touting labels.\par \par \uc2 Before I even know that there is yogurt, I just see, protein, 20 grams and then it happens to be yogurt.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from EGGO Waffles advertisement)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: This is when you know the craze has really hit. Pancakes and waffles.\par \par \uc2 YASI ANSARI: Some of your favorite childhood foods.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from QUEST advertisement)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: What is actually good for you? Like are protein snacks, protein bars, protein chips?\par \par \uc2 YASI ANSARI: What I encourage people to do is not be relying on these products and consuming them in moderation, instead.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For growing children and adolescents, protein provides the building blocks for muscle, bone, skin, and hormones; while for older adults, getting enough is especially critical. It can help preserve strength, maintain bone density, an reduce the risk of age-related injury.\par \par \uc2 YASI ANSARI: You can use it towards building enzymes and supporting your hormone health and providing a structural component to your hair, skin, nails, and then helping you feel more satisfied and full.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: But how much do we actually need? Government guidelines suggest most adults aim for about 0.8 grams per kilogram of body weight, or about 54 grams for someone who weighs 150 pounds.\par \par \uc2 Americans on average already consume over 74 grams every day.\par \par \uc2 YASI ANSARI: You want to look for high-quality sources. So those are, generally speaking, animal protein. Now, another way to do this is by combining beans and grains and making it a full meal.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: As Americans demand convenience, food manufacturers have been quick to respond. Protein is now a $114 billion industry and growing. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  recently added protein boosters to its drink menu.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  advertisement)\par \par \uc2 (Clips from social media)\par \par \uc2 YASI ANSARI: We`re seeing that influencers, fitness professionals, they`re all encouraging more protein after lifting.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: All of that added protein, though, can come with a bit of sticker shock.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen grams of protein in pancakes. The protein version is more expensive.\par \par \uc2 YASI ANSARI: Then choose the one that fits your budget and add a protein yogurt or an egg.\par \par \uc2 That was fun!\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: That was fun. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Adam Yamaguchi in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Parents will love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, the stories you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up at 8:00, actor Jeremy Allen White plays Bruce Springsteen in a new movie --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- so we asked him, have you ever had a moment where you`re all by yourself thought, and you go, holy (bleep) I`m doing this. Spoiler alert. Guess what he said. That`s a tease.\par \par \uc2 Plus an exclusive early look at life on the road, the new \uc2 CBS\uc2  music competition series with Keith Urban and Blake Shelton.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a one-two punch! So, that`s coming up in our next hour with Bruce in the background. You never go wrong with Bruce Springsteen in the background. Vlad -- not Vlad, you`re Claus.\par \par \uc2 Claus and I are heading over to the table to see you, Vlad and Nate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Loo, we are going to be showing you a little bit of Anderson Cooper with Stephen Colbert. It`s a really touching moment.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, time now for "What to Watch." I`m going to jump right into it, because we have an exclusive clip from tonight`s Season 5 premiere of "Ghosts."\par \par \uc2 In the hit comedy, married couple Sam and Jay turn a mansion into a bed and breakfast only to find that it`s haunted. So last season ended on quite the cliffhanger when Jay was tricked into signing a deal with the devil.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, Sam and the ghosts try to get Jay out of his agreement. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Season 5 of "Ghosts" premieres tonight right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+. We love "Ghosts" around here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always makes me think of Tony, because it is his favorite show. Remember, he and Katy dressed up as two of the characters --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right for Halloween.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For Halloween.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. We love "Ghosts" around here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, now to a very powerful appearance by "60 Minutes" correspondent, Anderson Cooper last night on the "Late Show" with Stephen Colbert. Cooper was there to discuss his podcast, which explores grief and death, and he told Stephen about the profound impact some of his listeners` stories have had on his life.\par \par \uc2 And at one point, his voice was shaking with emotion as he spoke about his own experience with death. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER ("60 Minutes" correspondent): I didn`t allow myself to grieve as a kid, because I was so overwhelmed when my dad died when I was a kid, then my brother died by suicide, that I shut down. And I`ve lived for 40 years without allowing myself to feel the pain of the grief.\par \par \uc2 But the irony is, by not allowing myself -- and you taught me this -- by not allowing myself to feel the pain of it, I also didn`t allow myself ever to feel joy, true joy, because you can have one without the other.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Anderson went on to say that he was deeply affected by Stephen`s own story, in which Stephen`s dad, James, and his two brothers, Peter and Paul, died in a plane crash. Colbert said he "learned to love the thing that he wished that never happened."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I just love -- Anderson has been very -- he has been vocal about losing his dad, Wyatt. His dad died when he was very young, and his brother took his own life -- his brother, Carter, took his own life when Anderson was still just a young man.\par \par \uc2 And I love that he come to reconcile these feelings that he`s had about his brother and his dad and he learned that by listening to Stephen, who was profoundly affected by the death of his dad and brothers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that Anderson talks about it so candidly, because it is going to happen. We will all be in this position one day, if we haven`t already, where you lose somebody close to you, and how do you deal with it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I love how emotionally available they are when they get together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right. Both of them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those two guys, when they sit down, I just love being a fly on the wall listening.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. The notion that you can find grace and redemption of your soul through pain is a very powerful one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Good stuff.\par \par \uc2 All right, stick around, we`ll be right back. We have more for you, so don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in t