 I take these blue voters and I put them into districts and I am going to draw some lines here where the big number of voters are these red figures, right?\par \par \uc2 Now, the Voting Rights Act has said, they are on the basis of race, that would dilute the power of some voters, and you can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 Now, suppose I also try this. I take all of these blue voters right here, and I`m going to draw this very crazy-looking line and I am going to put all of these blue voters into one district.\par \par \uc2 Well, Voting Right Acts has said on the basis of race, you can`t do that, because it violates their voting power in the state elsewhere, okay. So come out here now, back to the national map, and what you`re going to see is that these districts down here, especially in southern states, you`ve ended up with at least some districts where the voters in the district are majority, in this case, Black voters and these are the districts that are really going to be question.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so then, what are the political implications of all this?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: So Republicans control the redistricting process in these southern states and what could happen is that if this is upended, they could redraw these district lines and these districts could go away.\par \par \uc2 Now, the key here is that minority voters, Black voters, vote overwhelmingly for Democrats. They voted 84 percent for Democratic congressional candidates last election. So these districts are blue right now. If those go away, Republicans could net maybe, we think, as many as 12 districts out of this area, which would really up their chances of holding on to the House next year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow! So this has big, big, big political implications.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Salvanto, always good to see you in studio, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, Gayle, over to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right. Always 