End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: White and Cooper tell us about making the movie and Springsteen`s visit to the set. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. No pressure there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at Bruce looking at the monitor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thumbs up!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back. Yes!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Born in the USA"/Bruce Springsteen/Sony Music Group)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nobody listening to that is going who is that singing? That of course is Bruce Springsteen`s anthem, "Born in the USA." He`s the subject of the highly anticipated new biopic, it`s called "Springsteen: Deliver Me From Nowhere."\par \par \uc2 Emmy award winning actor, that would be Jeremy Allen White, who you may know from "Shameless" or "The Bear" -- Jeremy is so fantastic in everything he does -- now, he is taking on the role of The Boss. Scott Cooper is the film`s director, screenwriter, and producer.\par \par \uc2 We got a chance to sit down with both of them together and they shared how this movie breaks the mold of traditional music biopics.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Born to Run"/Bruce Springsteen/Sony Music Group)\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: I don`t remember a time I didn`t know the name Bruce Springsteen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here`s somebody who`s listening to Bruce when you were in high school and now you`re playing Bruce Springsteen. Did you ever have a moment where you are by yourself and you`re going, holy bleep, I`m doing this.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Every day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s no question Bruce Springsteen is a legend. But playing him was not an immediate "Yes" for Jeremy Allen White.\par \par \uc2 What was your hesitation?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: I think, you know, I admire Bruce a lot, and I know how beloved he is by so many. And I think that the relationship we have with specifically, you know, musical artists, it`s so personal, it`s so intimate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: White didn`t know how to sing or play guitar before taking on this role, but that didn`t deter Director Scott Cooper or Springsteen, himself.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: I was on the phone with Scott, and he said, you know, Bruce really wants you to do this. I said , what? He said, Yes, Bruce really wants you to do this. And I said, why didn`t you just tell me that in the first place. I`m not going to stand in this man`s way.\par \par \uc2 And once I had him, kind of you, know, behind me and I knew he`d seen my work, and he thought that I had, you know, what it took. And I said, okay, let`s take a little leap of faith here and try.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That meant intensive voice and guitar lessons.\par \par \uc2 How did you handle the pressure and anxiety of getting through it? And at what point did you know because there had to be a moment, Jeremy, when you thought, God, I`m doing okay.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: I think I found some confidence, strangely, in singing his music. I sang his songs over and over and over again. And you know, now, we have this shared experience and I`ve been very lucky in my last couple of jobs, like, learning a skill has been a part of the job and that gives you a shared experience with the character you`re playing.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Springsteen was also a resource.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: I asked him about you know, why this film? Why this time? And I asked him more specifically about this road trip that he went on with his very good friend, Matt D`Elia and this thing that happened to him at the County Fair, where he had this sort of panic attack.\par \par \uc2 And I said, you know, I think I know what that feels like but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you ever had a panic attack?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Yes, I am familiar with the feeling, that sort of like doubt and confusion, and what can bring that on, but I asked him what it was that brought it on and he said, you know, I felt like I was outside of myself, I felt like I was just an observer in my own life and I was really afraid I was just going to remain an outsider in my own life and just watch it happen and that was the scariest thought.\par \par \uc2 And I was like, oh, wow. I know that feeling. I`ve been contending with a similar feeling in my life during this time. You know, one of the most beautiful things he did with me was really give me permission, you know. I think that was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Permission to do what?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Permission to bring myself to it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Springsteen: Deliver Me From Nowhere" takes a different approach than most biopics only focusing on one chapter of his story, the making of the album, "Nebraska."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We invited director, screenwriter, and producer, Scott Cooper to join the conversation.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER ("Springsteen: Deliver Me From Nowhere" director, screenwriter and producer): People bring their own ideas about who this is and what the movie should be. You wanted "Dancing in the Dark."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: You wanted "Glory Days."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: Right? Bruce put that on the shelf. He put "Born in the USA" on the shelf and he said to Jon Landau, his longtime manager and collaborator, no. "Nebraska" has to come out. It`s going to come out and I`m not going to do singles, no tour, no press, I`m not even going to be on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I don`t even want to be on the cover.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Think about, guys, what it took for him to do that, when Jimmy Ivey what are you and all of those guys are screaming, what are you (bleep) nuts? you`ve got "Glory Days" and "Born in the --" and he says, no.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: It`s a massive creative risk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought that was such a powerful scene --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: It`s a commercial risk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: But that`s who Bruce Springsteen is, and I think that`s what separates him from so many people and that`s why he`s endured now for six decades.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Springsteen was heavily involved in the filming process, paying multiple visits to the set.\par \par \uc2 Was that helpful? Because he said, you know, if I am in the way, let me know. Did either up with one of you ever say, you know, Bruce, you`re in the way, we`ve got this.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: I was too afraid --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: I mean, look -- I would imagine for --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because he said, look, if I was in the way, I`ll go.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: And I think he meant that. Look, Jeremy is so locked in, like the great actors I`ve worked with on set, is that -- and correct me if I`m wrong, of course, you knew Bruce was there.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: But Jeremy is so committed to the moment that I never saw him waiver.\par \par \uc2 Now, as the writer and director, who better than the source, than the subject matter whom you`re making a film --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere"/20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The film delves into what Springsteen described as some of the darkest days of his life. It portrays his struggle with depression and his tumultuous relationship with his father, who suffered from schizophrenia.\par \par \uc2 The masses haven`t seen it yet, but what`s been the reaction that you`ve been getting about mental health?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: I have to say, Gayle, it`s much more than I could have ever hoped. So, the film debuted at the Telluride Film Festival. I had a few psychologists who were there come to me after Q&As and say, Scott, I hope this film reaches beyond Telluride, because I think it can help people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: Especially men.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: There`s a loneliness crisis in America. People don`t know how to give voice to their pain. Maybe they`re embarrassed. For some men, they think that vulnerability --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is a weakness.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: -- is a weakness. And I suppose if there`s anything that I want this film -- for people to take away from the film, it is, if you` struggling, if you`re despairing, and if you don` know how to get help, can`t afford help, ask. Because it saved Bruce`s life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Land of Hope and Dreams (Live at NYFF)"/Bruce Springsteen/\uc2 YouTube\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I asked Bruce earlier today, what would you say about Jeremy? What would you about Scott? And I want to read what he says, well, actually, this is what I want. I want you to read what he said about Jeremy.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Oh, boy!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you read what he said about Scott. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: Jeremy gives a deeply intuitive psychological portrait, flaws, mental struggles, and all of the young man that I was at that time. Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, isn`t that nice? The young man I was at that time.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: God!\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Wow. Bless you, Bruce. I can`t believe it -- I still can`t believe, you know -- I don`t know. I still can`t believe we got started, I can`t believe he gave our blessing in the first place, I can`t believe the way she`s showing up for the film and for us now.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: Supporting the films in ways I never even could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is what he says about Scott. Jeremy, can you read that?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: I am more excited for Scott. Scott`s film honors my experiences, my work, and my family in a way only Scott Cooper could have brought to the screen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s you, Scott Cooper.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Only Scott!\par \par \uc2 SCOTT COOPER: It`s beautiful. Oh, my God, Gayle! I`m so touched by that!\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALLEN WHITE: Wow!\par \par \uc2 BRUCE SPRINGSTEEN (Singer-songwriter): Stay strong. Have a nice evening.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done, Gayle. Well done!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: First before you start, well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Thank you. I love the piece so much, special shoutout to Craig Shea (sp?) who edited it, because it all starts in the edit, I believe and Katie who produced it. Katie Smith (sp?). Bravo to you!\par \par \uc2 But I love Bruce Springsteen so much. He is so thoughtful, so kind, so smart, and even if you`re not a Bruce Springsteen fan, the one person who I`m talking to at this particular time, what`s wrong with you -- this movie will change the way that you perceive him, the way you perceive mental illness, I think that`s important.\par \par \uc2 And shoutout to Scott Cooper, I`ve just met him and Jeremy, fell in love with both of these guys for the first time. His father died the day before the filming of movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He lost his house in the fire. Bruce Springsteen said, leave your hotel and come stay in my home. That`s who Bruce Springsteen is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What I love about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bring your family and come.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- is Bruce going through those difficulties and then putting out this music that ultimately saved people like me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I said, Vlad, I said, it`s heartbreaking to me that the pain that you went through has brought so many people such joy. It`s extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the movie is extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Please, please go, October 24th. "Springsteen: Deliver Me From Nowhere is out in theaters next Friday, October 24th. Mark it down. You will not be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching "CBS MORNINGS." We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, Blake Shelton and Keith Urban`s new country competition show.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It`s time now for a \uc2 CBS\uc2  edition of "Talk of the Table" and \uc2 CBS\uc2  Premiere Week continues.\par \par \uc2 It`s an exclusive. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` new musical competition. Have you heard about this guys?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s called "The Road." It premieres this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 So how does it work? Twelve emerging country musicians compete as opening acts for superstar, Keith Urban. That`s cool!\par \par \uc2 The winner gets a quarter of a million dollars too, and a chance to perform the Stage Coach Festival and a recording contract, and they get money, too.\par \par \uc2 "Entertainment Tonight`s" Cassie DiLaura sat down with executive producer, Blake Shelton and Keith Urban while the show was filming this past spring.\par \par \uc2 And she got a sneak peek of life on the road with the show`s tour manager, that`s Gretchen Wilson.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting "We`re going on the road! Wooh!")\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: These 12 up-and-coming musicians are hitting the road for a 10-week ride with country music star, Keith Urban.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Each performer get the chance to open for the hugely successful Grammy-winning musician at seven different venues across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: How are we feeling tonight!\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Each night, contestants play for a live audience, earning their votes to stay on tour or be sent home.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank all so much.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: It`s all a part of the new \uc2 CBS\uc2  series, "The Road."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Yellowstone"/Paramount)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Which tapped "Yellowstone" creator, Taylor Sheridan, and another country icon as executive producers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stay Country or Die Tryin`"/Blake Shelton/Wheelhouse Records)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: That`s right, Blake Shelton.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON (Singer-songwriter): I came up playing in the bars, and so I relate to the way these artists are trying to get there, because it`s the way I did it.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Shelton told us he helped hand-pick the 12 contestants and the venues for their performances and how it was a struggle at times to stay behind-the-scenes.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: I`m on several episodes.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Couldn`t stay away from being in front of the camera.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: I couldn`t stay away. I was getting jealous. I saw Keith on camera and I was like, he looks so good! I`ve got to get some of that!\par \par \uc2 So, I felt like it was important for me to at least get in there, but we`re just spectators. It`s about those contestants and we just have thoughts and things that we think the audience may want to hear.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: We got a taste of Blake`s and Keith`s on-camera chemistry during our interview.\par \par \uc2 KEITH URBAN (Singer-songwriter): Are we close enough?\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Which was the day they were filming their Season Finale.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: It`s okay, buddy, we`re almost to the finale. It`s almost over.\par \par \uc2 KEITH URBAN: It`s just so sad.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: It`s almost over and we`re almost there.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: What made you say "Yes" when he called you?\par \par \uc2 KEITH URBAN: He had incriminating photos.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: It`s blackmail!\par \par \uc2 KEITH URBAN: So, I mean, for me, it was like, it`s such a great concept. There was something really interesting about watching these performers figure out how to come out and grab an audience. The good thing about the show, too, is you get to do an original song.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEITH URBAN: That`s a blessing and a challenge, because you can play "Freebird" and get them like that, right, but then, you`ve got to do one of your songs and try and get them to love you just as much.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEITH URBAN: It`s very cool watching that happen.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: We also met up with fellow country star, Gretchen Wilson and acts as tour manager and mentor for the contestants.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN WILSON (Singer): It is pretty much like this everywhere you go.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: She gave us a backstage pass to the contestants` home away from home.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN WILSON: After you. Watch your step.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Oh, thank you so much. Hospitality. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN WILSON: Contestants have been traveling on this one. When we started, there were 12 musician up here, so it really -- it wasn`t as spacious as you think. So, this is your only real sanctuary. It is also -- it`s your home.\par \par \uc2 Getting close to each one of these musicians, hearing them speak to their families over the phone, missing their loved ones, all things that I`ve been through.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: And then there is that interim, where you`re traveling. And it`s the grind, they say. And it`s not easy. There`s a lot of financial sacrifices you have to make. Sacrifices time away from your family.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN WILSON: That`s why this show is so unique, because you get to see the real nitty-gritty.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: With cameras rolling at all times.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Viewers will get to buckle up and hop on for the wild ride.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Reporter: That is life on "The Road."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, this is truly going to embody everything --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It looks good!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- that a country star is all about.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am very excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we weren`t allowed to see one episode, but I like it already.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I say, all the time, country music people are some of the nicest people. You have got Blake and Keith, also, they certainly fit in that category, but the payoff for the contestants seems huge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLES