urse.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: They`ve got a flat tire. They are in a parking lot. You don`t want them to be abandoned or need a stranger or have to call for services.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: They have this, they take care of it.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it`s $49.99. Twenty-nine percent off.\par \par \uc2 Okay, the next thing my husband says, everyone must have in the car, you`ve got kids that are driving.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You know every time they get in an accident, people with blame them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Because they are young drivers, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yup!\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: We all need a witness in case we`re ever in an accident. This is amazing. It`s from Minolta. This is 2.5, high definition camera.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: First of all, easy to install. Stick it in the window, plug it in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: It does the work for you. It has got a motion-censor in it. So the minute you start to drive, it`s going to turn on and it is going to start recording.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turn on.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Say you`re in a parking lot and somebody hits your car, it`s going to turn on then, too. You don`t even have to be there. It does the work for you.\par \par \uc2 The fact that it`s high definition means this. I can see the license plate when I watch the video. I can see -- it`s got a wide angle -- the entire intersection.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So this becomes a critical piece of evidence to give your insurance company or the police if you`re an accident. Right now, this is $99.00, savings of a hundred bucks. Call your insurance company, they may lower your rates if you have this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh. Really?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes. So, this pays for itself immediately. Everyone, your kids, your parents, yourself, this is a must have. And a hundred dollar savings. It`s huge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: See, these aren`t wants, they are needs.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: All of them!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I am talking about.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That`s my new desk over there and you`re taking the chair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. Elizabeth Werner, I am getting the chair.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We partner with all of these companies to get these deals, so to get anything that you saw, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Hey, yo! Vlad, what`s up next?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s going on, Nate? I`m here in the greenroom with trailblazing Bishop Mariann Edgar Budde. She`s in our progressive greenroom. She`s got a new children`s book, here it is. It`s what we can all learn and there`s a "Harry Potter" story in here, right?\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE (Episcopal prelate, "How We Learn To Be Brave" author): Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s coming up when we come back. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: I ask you to have mercy, Mr. President, on those in our communities whose children fear that their parents will be taken away, and that you help those that are fleeing war zones and persecution in their own lands to find compassion and welcome here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was the leader of Washington`s Episcopal Diocese, Bishop Mariann Edgar Budde making a plea to President Trump during the Inaugural Prayer Service earlier this year. She writes about that moment in a new book for young readers. "We Can Be Brave: How We Learn To Be Brave In Life`s Decisive Moments."\par \par \uc2 She highlights examples from "Harry Potter" to \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr.\par \par \uc2 The Right Reverend Mariann Edgar Budde joins us now. Good morning! Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Good morning. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, what I love is, you open the book writing that for you, parenting was a master class in courage, this is directed to young people.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Break that down for me.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Well, to watch children grow up and to realize that nearly every day, they asked to do something they`ve never done before, from the beginning and it seems like our Creator has made us so that we can face thresholds of unknowing and things that we`ve never, ever been asked to do, and then we cross them, time and time again.\par \par \uc2 So when they were very young, to when they were teenagers facing really hard things, I watched them, sometimes wanting to protect them and not being able to, seeing how they responded when they failed and got back up again, I think it`s one of the great privileges of parenting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, if they`re asked to do something, and I hadn`t thought about it until you put it in the book this way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whether they are riding a bike or doing something they`ve never done before, young people are asked to do brave things all the time. So do you think that bravery or courage is taught, learned or is it something we are born with?\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: All of the above. All of the above.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All of the above?\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: It`s clearly innate. It`s part of our - - the way we are created to move towards things that we cannot do, and we learn it, and we`re inspired by others.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Certainly, if we have people around us who encourage, whose life examples we follow, it is all of those things and that is one of the things I wanted to say. It`s a lifelong journey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, another thing that you say, you write in the book, accepting what we do not choose and cannot change is one of the most courageous decisions we make.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: And it`s a hard one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a hard one.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Especially hard when we`re young. Because we don`t want -- you know, who wants to accept the things that we don`t want to be true? But, you know as well as I that some of the most courageous people, and the people who made change started by accepting something.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: And then making their life choices from there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, one of the most moving chapters in the book is one where we talk about Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you illustrate what he used to call dangerous unselfishness.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you use that to illustrate the Good Samaritan story. The Good Samaritan, where the two members of the clergy walk by a wounded man, and they say, if I stop to help this man, what will happen to me? But the Samaritan who is one of the most hated classes --\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Hated people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He says what?\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: He says, if I don`t stop to help this man, what will happen to him? And King used that as an example of his justification for going to be part of the sanitation worker strike in Memphis, which is where he was killed, because people didn`t want him to go. And he said, if I don`t go, what will happen to them?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: As opposed to, if I do go, what will happen to me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What happens --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m curious about your own background, though, Bishop, because you became a national figure for many people when you addressed the President that day in the prayer service. Some people looked at that and said, okay, she`s spot-on, that`s perfection. Other people looked at it said, how dare her, that`s very disrespectful.\par \par \uc2 I would imagine that you gave that a lot of thought before did that. It`s not something that you go in, it is sort of off the cuff. What was your intention in that moment? And how did you decide, I`m going to do it here and now?\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: Thank you. My main goal in that moment was to bring the humanity into the room of people who had been spoken of in sweeping, de-humanizing terms and to broaden -- to broaden all of our awareness, although we all know this, that human beings are not all one thing or the other. So I wanted to do that.\par \par \uc2 And I also knew that many people in our country are afraid.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think you made a difference in that moment?\par \par \uc2 RIGHT REVEREND MARIANN EDGAR BUDDE: I don`t think I made a difference in the policies of this administration. That`s pretty clear. I think that there were many people who were grateful that I see them and that I spoke a truth that they were afraid was being consistently denied.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is a powerful message, Bishop Mariann Edgard Budde, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 "We Can Be Brave" goes on sale this coming Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everywhere you like to buy your books. That will do it for us. Stick around for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020251016elag00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 President Trump Threatens to Disarm \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ; One-on-one with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 ; Trump Threatens More Cuts to Government Programs; Record-Breaking Rainfall in Los Angeles; Supreme Court Taking Up Key Redistricting Case; Trump Awards Medal of Freedom to Charlie Kirk; Report: many Protein Powers Contain High Lead Levels; Remembering Grammy- Winning Musician, D`Angelo; Concerns Over South Carolina Measles Outbreak; Guilty Plea in Arson Attack on Pennsylvania Governor Residence; Donnie Wahlberg and Sonequa Martin-Green Live; Michael Lewis on "The Big Short" Audiobook; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Authors on New Book, "All the Cool Girls Get Fired;" \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Tony Dokoupil,  Debora Patta; Caitlin Huey-Burns, Rob Marciano, Jan Crawford, Cristian  Benavides, Scott MacFarlane \par \uc2 14,966 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on your Wednesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is still in Tel Aviv. He`s going to join us in just a moment. He`s got an exclusive interview with Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , and let`s just say he had a lot to say.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s eye opener. It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 President Trump demands that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  disarm after it carried out a wave of killings across Gaza during the ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is still on the streets of Gaza. How can you say the war is over?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli Prime Minister): Well, we agreed to give peace a chance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: On day 15 of the government shutdown, federal workers are living on partial paychecks, and the President promises more cuts.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): We are closing up programs that are Democrat programs, and we`re not going to let them come back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Doctors raise concern over new measles cases across the U.S. with one of the worst outbreaks in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHLEIGH GARDNER (Parkside Pediatrics pediatrician): We have never had this big of an outbreak.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The rare October storm slams Southern California with heavy downpours.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Months` worth of rain in just one day.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: The music world remembers D`Angelo, the legendary R&B artist, and pioneer of modern soul music.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Who didn`t want to be the Angelo.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): I don`t know if you`ve seen this, but Katy Perry, the pop music superstar was spotted smooching the former Prime Minister of Canada, \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2 . Man, oh man! What a couple this. I`ve been wondering how Canada was going to exact their revenge for the tariffs and this is it. They`re taking our women. They`re not just taking our women. They`re taking our astronauts.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They look pretty happy to me. I say, good for them both.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good for them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I say that, too.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to start with something very, very important. We`re going to begin with a fragile ceasefire in Gaza that is holding for now in spite of new challenges.\par \par \uc2 Israel claims this morning that one of the four bodies that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  handed over yesterday is actually not a hostage. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has sent gunmen into the streets of Gaza, and there are images showing executions of people that the group calls collaborators and outlaws.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Trump gave \uc2 Hamas\uc2  a stark warning yesterday. In his words, he says, if they don`t disarm, we will disarm them. We`ll have more on that in just a minute, but first we`re going to go to Tony. He`s still in Tel Aviv, where he got an exclusive interview with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Tony, I know you were due to fly back yesterday, but I say getting exclusive with Mr. Netanyahu is worth sticking around for one more day. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, this was big, Gayle, so we did stick around and we met up with the Prime Minister at Rabin Medical Center just after sunset, that`s just outside of Tel Aviv here, and we watched as he and the First Lady visited with the newly reunited hostages and their families there.\par \par \uc2 But we also looked ahead beyond Israel`s borders and beyond this current ceasefire to Palestinians` future.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who is going to govern Gaza? The only names mentioned are \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Tony Blair\uc2 . Is \uc2 Tony Blair\uc2  going to be the president of Gaza?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : I doubt it, but I think that this is a transitional period, and we want to fashion governance that works, that is not made of people who are committed to our destruction, because if we are, if we put them there, then we will just repeat it again and again and again and we don`t want to have the October 7th massacre repeated.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How do you de-radicalize the population in Gaza that wants to fight? You`ve got thousands of young men who have just spent two years in during brutal war, or fighting in this brutal war and on the back of that generations of struggle?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : I think that you have to understand that Gaza is not uniform. There are Gazans who are fighting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and are saying, we don`t want this anymore. And a lot of people in Gaza now know that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has brought catastrophic consequences to them because of its fanaticism.\par \par \uc2 The most important thing in destroying fanaticism is to destroy a certain hope, the hope that the fanaticism will achieve its results. When people know Israel is here to stay, you`re not going to destroy the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One thing we didn`t touch on is the latest apparent disruption in aid into Gaza, because that bit of news came to us after our conversation, but we talked about a lot, and we`re going to have more of it at the 7:30,half hour, including what it was like working with President Trump and also what comes next in this fragile ceasefire, but for now, more on the situation in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 This morning, food and medical aid is passing through the border, but as we mentioned, there`s a problem with the 28 deceased bodies. These are hostages who died in captivity, Debora Patta in East Jerusalem has more on that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Debora, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is slowly releasing the bodies of hostages still in Gaza, a total of seven so far, which has angered families waiting for their loved ones. The \uc2 Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics\uc2  says more than 11,000 people are still missing in the territory, and we spoke to hostage negotiator, Gershon Baskin, who told us, sadly, some bodies may never be recovered.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Somewhere in this landscape of ruin and loss are the bodies of the remaining hostages.\par \par \uc2 GERSHON BASKIN (Hostage negotiator): Many of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  commanders who are responsible for burying these Israeli hostages are no longer alive. They were killed by the Israelis. There are thousands of Gazans who are unaccounted for, who are believed to be buried underneath the rubble of buildings that Israel bombed.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: First responders are now searching for the dead, but it is not easy when over 90 percent of the buildings have been damaged or destroyed and there are only the most rudimentary tools available.\par \par \uc2 As Gaza starts to think of rebuilding, the U.S. peace deal calls for an interim governing body headed by President Trump to run the territory that ultimately hands control over to Palestinian rule.\par \par \uc2 But that body has yet to be established, and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has stepped back into the vacuum, allegedly targeting Palestinians accused of collaborating with Israel.\par \par \uc2 There are videos of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  executing Palestinians believed to be armed gangs. What`s going on there?\par \par \uc2 GERSHON BASKIN: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is killing them because they can. Israel has empowered with weapons and money, big gangs of Palestinians who were involved in mostly illegal activities in the past, and they`ve empowered them as an alternative to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: President Trump had this to say.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You know, they did take out a couple of gangs that were very bad, very, very bad gangs, and that didn`t bother me much, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: \uc2 The Palestinian Authority\uc2  has condemned the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  executions as extrajudicial killings. President Trump also said yesterday that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  must disarm and warned the group, and I quote, "We will disarm them, and it will happen, perhaps quickly or perhaps violently." -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Debora Patta for us in East Jerusalem. Debora, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Nate, I am going to toss it back to you in New York with a reminder to our viewers, we will have more from Prime Minister Netanyahu at the 7:30 half hour, including the growing problem of everyday Americans across the political spectrum, turning away from Israel.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we`re looking forward to that at 7:30, Tony. See you again in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 It is Day 15 of the government shutdown. Thousands of workers have only received partial paychecks, and soon there will be no paychecks at all. The Senate failed again last night to pass a spending bill to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is tracking the efforts to end the impasse, and the response from The White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The motion is not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: With Congress at a standstill, President Trump is threatening more targeted cuts.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re closing up programs that are Democrat programs that we were opposed to, so we`re being -- and they`re never going to come back.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: His comments came during a meeting with Argentinian President \uc2 Javier Milei\uc2 , whose country has been offered a $20 billion bailout by Trump. Critics slammed the timing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): The overwhelming majority of Americans say we need help with our health care, and they`re busy spending $20 billion because Trump seems to really like the leader of Argentina.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In the past week, many federal workers received their final paychecks. Air traffic controllers were among those who received a portion of what they are owed. They won`t be paid again until the shutdown ends.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Many fanned out at airports across the country urging lawmakers to open the government.\par \par \uc2 DREW MCQUEEN (NATCA Great Lakes Regional Vice President): Most controllers around the country are already working six-day work weeks, 10-hour days. They shouldn`t have the extra burden of worrying about when they`re going to get paid.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Members of the military will get their paychecks due today after the administration rearranged Pentagon funds at the President`s direction.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You got it?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But how long those funds will last is a concern for social worker and military spouse, Crystal Bettenhausen-Bubulka, whose husband has been serving in the Navy for the past 31 years.\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL BETTENHAUSEN-BUBULKA (Social worker and military spouse): For me, it`s more so looking at the mental health side of everything. How much do we need to push before we have military families that just can`t take anymore?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Do you feel like this is putting your life on hold?\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL BETTENHAUSEN-BUBULKA: Oh, absolutely, yes, 100 percent. It`s a holding pattern.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, lawmakers here continue to get paid, as is required by law, but the police officers who protect this capital have missed their first full paychecks, and the union that represents them is urging lawmakers to start talking and negotiate -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Caitlin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to Southern California, where a powerful storm dumped nearly a foot of rain. In Los Angeles area yesterday. Interstate 5 was temporarily shut down as crews in waist deep water cleared the drainage system. In Encino, the Fire Department`s swift water team rescued two stranded adults and two dogs.\par \par \uc2 Rob Marciano is tracking the storm.\par \par \uc2 Rob, it doesn`t look good. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Vlad, this is very unusual for mid-October in Southern California. I mean, normally still the dry season, so a lot of rain coming through this area. The low itself is now over Nevada, but we`re slowly getting some wrap around moisture.\par \par \uc2 But yesterday was extremely wet in areas like Santa Barbara, we had some flash flood warnings, and we had rainfall amounts over three inches. In Burbank 2.37 inches, that not only set a record for the date, but also set a record for the date and the month. And you can see things are winding down there, so that will help actually moisten up the ground as we head towards Santa Ana season.\par \par \uc2 We do have a little bit of leftover snow here. This thing will wind itself up into Canada and kind of weaken, dump snow into the Intermountain West, and then bring a severe threat for parts of the Central Southwest or South Central area. There you go, Little Rock as we head towards Saturday, but we`ll be watching that -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Rob, good to have you back in the studio.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court hears arguments this morning in a case that could undermine one of the last surviving parts of the Voting Rights Act. Now that is one of the country`s most important Civil Rights laws.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The court here today is going to be focused on part of that law that can require states to draw up legislative districts that sometimes zigzag across the state, to group together voters based on their skin color. This is all to increase minority representation, and the court`s decision could have sweeping implications nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SHELTON\uc2  (Power Coalition executive director): It is really troubling for anyone to pretend that race is not an unbelievably loud issue in our country right now.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Civil rights advocates like Ashley Shelton say now is not the time to gut Section II of the Voting Rights Act and end the use of race in redistricting.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SHELTON\uc2 : We cannot pretend that there isn`t going to be considerable loss of minority voice and power if we were to do away with Section II.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Advocates credit Section II with increasing minority representation nationwide by requiring states to draw some districts that are majority Black, like this congressional map in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 But critics say Black candidates are winning in Louisiana and elsewhere in non-minority districts, and grouping people based on their race only fuels divisions.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH MURRILL (Louisiana Attorney General): I think it`s time to give people a chance and to continue to vote for people based on their character and their fitness for office.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Louisiana Attorney General Liz Merle says the case shows how states are in an impossible position. Three years ago, civil rights groups, including Shelton`s, sued under the Voting Rights Act to get a second Black district and won in court, but after Louisiana drew up the new map, a group of White voters sued arguing the new minority district was unconstitutional because it considered race.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH MURRILL: The Constitution has told us to not sort people by race. The courts have told us we can`t sort voters by race, and yet we are being told to go out and sort our voters by race.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now the court has restricted the use of race in other areas, such as when it ended affirmative action and college admissions, and if the justices use that reasoning in this case, that could really change the rules for how states draw up their legislative districts, the political implications could be huge, including in those current redistricting efforts for the midterms, possibly as soon as next year -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Trump honored assassinated conservative activist, Charlie Kirk at The White House yesterday, a day that would have been Kirk`s 32nd birthday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: He loved this country, and that`s why this afternoon, it`s my privilege to posthumously award Charles James Kirk, our nation`s highest civilian honor, the Presidential Medal of Freedom.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The co-founder of Turning Point USA was awarded the Medal of Freedom just over a month after he was killed on a college campus in Utah.\par \par \uc2 Kirk was known as a staunch advocate of free speech, but also echoed some controversial views. His widow, Erika wiped away tears as she accepted the medal in his honor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A new study is casting doubt on just how healthy some protein powders and shakes are. Consumer Reports say it tested samples of nearly two dozen brands, and says about 70 percent contained more lead in a single serving than its expected recommended people in just in an entire day. Two specific products, Naked Nutrition`s, vegan mass gainer and Huel`s Black Edition powder allegedly contained well over 10 times the daily recommended limit.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to CBS News, Naked Nutrition said third party testing "confirmed that no heavy metals exceeded \uc2 FDA\uc2  reference intake levels for adults, including for sensitive groups such as women of childbearing age." A spokesperson for Huel told CBS News its products undergo regular independent testing, and they are completely safe to consume.\par \par \uc2 While there are no widespread federal guidelines for dietary lead limits, no amount of consumption is considered safe.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tributes are pouring in for Grammy Award winning musician D`Angelo this morning. The neo soul singer who was born Michael Archer, died Friday at the age of 51 from pancreatic cancer. His blend of musical genres like funk, jazz and R&B made D`Angelo a pop culture phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Brown Sugar"/D`Angelo/\uc2 EMI Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Considered one of the most influential R&B artists of his generation, D`Angelo upended the genre at 21 with the release of his debut album, 1995`s "Brown Sugar."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Lady"/D`Angelo/\uc2 EMI Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It quickly went platinum with hits like "Lady" and is widely credited with ushering in the neo soul movement, combining classic soul with influences from hip-hop.\par \par \uc2 D`ANGELO (Singer): It means a lot to me, you know what I am saying -- that people, people appreciate what I do, and they appreciate real soul music. You know what I`m saying?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: D`Angelo followed that up with the album "Voodoo" in 2000 which featured the hit single, "How Does It Feel?"\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Untitled (How Does It Feel)"/D`Angelo/Virgin Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The song won the Grammy for Best R&B performance, and the music video, which featured him shirtless, turned him into a sex symbol, a label he largely rejected, choosing to focus more on his music.\par \par \uc2 But at the height of his fame, D`Angelo largely retreated from the limelight. It would be more than 10 years until he released his third and final album, "Black Messiah" in 2014.\par \par \uc2 His influence helped inspire a new generation of R&B artists says "Rolling Stone" senior music editor, Jeff Ihaza.\par \par \uc2 JEFF IHAZA ("Rolling Stone" senior music editor): You know, a lot of people say things like, this is a once in a generation talent, and I think someone like D`Angelo is a very clear reminder of what a singular individual artist looks like and how quickly audiences can recognize that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. When we first met him, he was 21.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Twenty-one, an album came out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was just a success.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Musical genius at the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s also heartbreaking, because Angie Stone died seven months ago in a car crash. They have a son together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They were a couple, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Michael Archer, Jr. He is talking about what it`s like to lose both parents in a very short period of time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Lauryn Hill had a very beautiful tribute to him on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  where she called him the artist of a generation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At one point, he had the music industry in a chokehold, and he had that hiatus, and he often talked about it. In one interview, he said: The time away made me realize it`s not about chasing what you were, it`s about honoring where you are. I had to learn that the silence is part of the music, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he had wonderful videos.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just saying.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All that chocolate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All of that chocolate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And those abs!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead -- that too -- still ahead, a new warning about measles. We will visit the site of a severe new outbreak.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Alec Baldwin tells how he and his brother, Stephen ran into trouble with a garbage truck and a tree, and there is video, but first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, a dog owner`s worst nightmare. We will show you how one mischievous pooch accidentally set the house on fire.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" President Trump is on "Time" Magazine`s cover with the headline "His Triumph." So why does he hate it so much? I`m going to tell you. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . Keep your career moving forward. Visit careers.progressive.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bad dog! Bad dog!\par \par \uc2 This North Carolina canine accidentally started a fire inside a house by chewing on a lithium ion battery. The dog named Colton sees the smoke in flames, and he races upstairs. Colton wasn`t hurt, but the house did have some smoke damage, and the rug was toast. It turns out, everything isn`t a chew toy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Colton said, "What happened?"\par \par \uc2 Coming up, more of Tony`s exclusive interview.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s 7:30 and you`re looking at video of another U.S. strike on a small boat that President Trump says was carrying drugs off the coast of Venezuela. Unclassified footage posted to social media by the administration shows what appears to be a stationary vessel being struck by a missile.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, President Trump says six men on board were killed. This is the fifth known attack by U.S. forces on alleged narcotraffickers in the Caribbean. Critics say the strikes violate U.S. and international law.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have more now of Tony`s wide-ranging exclusive conversation with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . So they spoke at a hospital just outside Tel Aviv, where the Prime Minister and his wife met newly returned hostages and their families.\par \par \uc2 Tony joins us once again from Tel Aviv -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 In a blunt moment, we asked the Prime Minister, point blank, what it would take for him to consider Israel`s many conflicts to be over. And he responded in part: "The way to purchase peace is through strength," and it was military strength, at least as much as diplomacy, that he believes, allowed Israel to win back the hostages that we met face-to-face just last night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everybody in Israel since the last time I was here seems so relieved. Do you feel relieved?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : I feel both relieved and very moved.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We watched late Tuesday as Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  and his wife, Sara, moved room to room, visiting five returned hostages and their families on their first full day back together.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Each described an ordeal of long hours underground, limited sunlight and scarce food.\par \par \uc2 Avinatan Or, for example, the partner of another hostage, Noa Argamani, lost at least 60 pounds in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : I`m learning they didn`t lose hope, that they believed that one way or the other, we will get them out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now it`s a question of whether this moment of hope for Israel and the region can last.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump says the war is over. But clearly, the war is not over. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  is deployed to sections of Gaza still and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is still on the streets of Gaza. How can you say the war is over?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, we agreed to give -- to give peace a chance. The conditions in his 20 plan -- 20-point plan are very clear. It`s not only that we get the hostages out without getting our military out, but that we would subsequently have both demilitarization and disarmament. They`re not the same thing.\par \par \uc2 First, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has to give up its arms. And second, you want to make sure that there are no weapons factories inside Gaza. There`s no smuggling of weapons into Gaza. We also agreed, okay, let`s get the first part done, and now let`s give a chance to do the second part peacefully, which is my hope.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You just mentioned how closely you`re working with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on this. He took some digs at you, as he often does with people during his speech at the Knesset.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: He`s not the easiest guy to deal with, but that`s what makes him great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why does he say you`re so hard to work with?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, I hope he says that because I`m very tough on the matters that pertain to my country`s future. It is always the responsibility of the leader of the Jewish state to make sure that the Jewish state is never imperiled with its very existence. That`s why I did the things I did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There was a poll that came out recently in America finding that just 14 percent of Americans under 30 say their sympathies lie with Israel and with the Israeli people. You are losing young people across the political spectrum in America and across the world. Is there a fix for that?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : I think so. I think the first fix is to finish the war as speedily as possible, something that I have sought to do against all these contrarian propaganda.\par \par \uc2 Of course, I want to end the war. Who wants it to continue? I`ve been to war myself. I`ve been in battles. My brother died leading the historic rescue at Entebbe for the release of hostages.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m aware.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : You have to be crazy to want wars to prolong --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So ending one --\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : So, first, you want to end it. One, end the war speedily, because in the TikTok age and in the television age, letting wars go on too long is going to cost you precisely what it cost you. There`s a real battle on the social media. It`s a big battle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is. It is.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : It`s a battle for truth, really.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s also a battle of messaging, of ideas. And there`s a big idea that the world talks about, and that America talks about, the two- state solution, something that you used to talk about, but not anymore. How come?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : When I talked about it, it wasn`t the proposition that people give. They assume, okay, there are two sovereign states, and a sovereign state has, for example, military power. It can make covenants.\par \par \uc2 The Palestinians should have all the powers in a peaceful day to govern themselves, but they can`t have the powers to threaten our survival. That sovereign power of security must remain with Israel, because otherwise the jihadists take over. Iran takes over immediately, and that`s what happened every time we vacated territory, the most extreme fanatics came in, and people said to me, but that`s not, you know, that`s not a perfect state. And I said, you`re right, but it`s perfectly aligned with reality.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So is that a reality you can imagine existing?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, it`s a reality that if you had Palestinian governance that stopped teaching their kids to destroy the state of Israel, obviously, if you have that and they educate them for peace, then I think you can have a different reality. But even under that reality, this could take generations.\par \par \uc2 Israel has to be in control of the military power to prevent our destruction.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`ll add just one more thing, guys, as I toss it back to you in New York, just before we started rolling on that interview, I mentioned to the Prime Minister that his obituary after the success of the last few days will look very different today than it would have looked like two years ago. And he said quote, in part, "It`s not his obituary that he`s concerned about, it is his country`s" which underscores, once again, for Netanyahu, many of Israel`s problems feel existential, and as we think about what`s coming next in the ceasefire and for the future of the whole region, I think that`s a real insight into his mindset as he looks out onto the world.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, well done.\par \par \uc2 I know that new information is coming out minute by minute, but given the information that you had, very well done. We`ll talk to you a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a new measles outbreak in South Carolina raises urgent questions about the possible impact of misinformation over vaccines. Stick around. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s growing concern over a measles outbreak in South Carolina and whether misinformation about vaccines is making it worse. Health officials have now confirmed 16 cases in that state since July, and dozens of students are quarantining at home.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides has more from South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Measles cases are on the rise, forcing nearly 140 unvaccinated students in two South Carolina schools to stay home for three weeks after they were exposed to the virus.\par \par \uc2 Parents, Chandler and Emma Gordon are expecting baby number three in April. While they and their boys are vaccinated, the recent measles outbreak has made them extra careful.\par \par \uc2 EMMA GORDON (Mother): We`re a little cautious knowing a newborn coming into this doesn`t have the opportunity to be vaccinated or protected against measles.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The highly contagious virus spreads through the air and can linger for up to two hours. Symptoms can include high fever, cough, runny nose, red eyes and a rash. The virus can also cause severe infections, especially in kids under five.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHLEIGH GARDNER: It`s very concerning. We have never had this big of an outbreak, especially in such a short amount of time.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Dr. Ashleigh Gardner works at Parkside Pediatrics in Spartanburg, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Have you seen changes in parents in recent years?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHLEIGH GARDNER: We have. The miscommunications that can happen sometimes lead to a decrease in vaccine rates, and it`s our job as doctors in order to help give truth and point them to hopefully having the best medical decision possible.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The measles vaccine is safe and effective according to the CDC. Nationwide measles cases are at a more than three-decade high, while vaccination rates among kindergarteners have been trending downwards in recent years.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHLEIGH GARDNER: Measles is so contagious that it needs a very, very high level of vaccination in order to achieve that herd immunity. So if it dips down at all, there`s the risk for this highly contagious virus spreading very quickly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Symptoms can take one to three weeks to appear, and so someone could have the measles, be spreading it and not even realize that they`re sick. Health officials say that at least one of the cases was linked to a local gym, and they`re expected to give an update later today - - Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cristian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the stories you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some big things coming up for you at 8:00, including the stars of the highly anticipated new \uc2 CBS\uc2  drama, have you heard about it? "Boston Blue."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Boston Blue"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll talk with the stars, Donnie Wahlberg and Sonequa Martin- Green about the spin off from the beloved show, "Blue Bloods." People love that show.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the authors of a new book called "All the Cool Girls Get Fired" will be here. They know what they`re talking about. It happened to them. They`re going to share the lessons they learned after being pushed out of their very high profile jobs.\par \par \uc2 So all of that`s coming up in our next hour, but right now, Claus and I are heading over to the other side of the room, because Vlad and Nate --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- "What to Watch." You got a brief tease?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have you seen that picture of President Trump on "Time" Magazine?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not happy about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I understand why, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to show you why. We all kind of understand why.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "What to Watch" with Vlad right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR D