ornia. I mean, normally it`s still the dry season.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is Day 15 of the government shutdown. Thousands of workers have only received partial paychecks and soon, there will be no paychecks at all. The Senate failed to pass a spending bill to reopen the government.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: It`s been two weeks and there is no end in sight. Yesterday, speaker Mike Johnson said this.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): We are barreling toward one of the longest shutdowns in American history.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: And government is shut down indefinitely. Yes, so like any building that sits unused this time of year, the capital is now Spirit Halloween.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The government workers, they are not laughing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, they are not.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are not laughing. I hope they fire this out sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Get it done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s begin this hour with disturbing new video showing the moments a man broke into the Pennsylvania Governor`s Mansion in April and set the dining room alight in an attempt to kill Governor Josh Shapiro and his family. It shows Cody Balmer trying to break into the rest of the building. Well, yesterday, Balmer pleaded guilty to charges that include attempted murder, aggravated arson and terrorism.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` justice correspondent, Scott MacFarlane has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Newly released security footage shows Cody Balmer, who pled guilty to arson, breaking a window of the Governor`s Mansion with a sledge hammer and throwing a Molotov cocktail inside setting the house on fire. Pennsylvania Democrat, Josh Shapiro says the incident has been haunting him for months.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): We will forever be changed by this. We know that time will heal, but the scars will remain.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The video also shows Balmer breaking a window and going inside where he ignited another Molotov cocktail. He could be seen moving around the mansion, kicking in door in close proximity to rooms where the Governor and his family were sleeping. No one was injured in the incident.\par \par \uc2 About an hour after the attack, Balmer called 911 himself, criticized the Governor`s stance on the war in Gaza and admitted responsibility.\par \par \uc2 CODY BALMER (Suspect): You all know where to find me. I`m not hiding and will confess everything that I had done.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Balmer appeared in court Tuesday where he was sentenced to 25 to 50 years in prison. The attack to place on the second night of Passover. Shapiro, who is Jewish also had guests in the house. Two State Troopers helped them all escape as the fire tore through some of the rooms.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): I have carried with me this enormous sense of guilt. Guilt that doing this job that I love so much has put our children`s lives at risk. It has been really hard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Overall, threats against public elected officials have skyrocketed. This year, there were already hundreds more investigations against threats of federal lawmakers than all of 2024 -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow! Thank you very much, Scott.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Michael Lewis, he has got a new version of his best-selling account of the 2008 financial crisis. You remember this story, "The Big Short." It was huge back in the day. Well, it`s been 15 years. We`re still talking about it. Why he sees a risk of new financial trouble just ahead. But first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, there`s a lot of buzz about the new \uc2 CBS\uc2  drama, "Boston Blue."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Boston Blue"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A spinoff of "Blue Bloods." The star, Donnie Wahlberg and Sonequa Martin-Green will be here to tell us all about the new show. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There they are!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey! You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are really excited -- this is our excited face -- about our next guest, Donnie Wahlberg, and Sonequa Martin-Green. Thank you very much!\par \par \uc2 So, the duo star in the new \uc2 CBS\uc2  series, it is called "Boston Blue." It`s a spinoff of the popular long running drama, you know it, "Blue Bloods."\par \par \uc2 So Donnie Wahlberg is reprising his role as \uc2 NYPD\uc2  detective, that`s Danny Reagan who finds himself taking a new position with the Boston Police Department.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Boston Blue"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, he is paired with Detective Lena Silver. She is hot, hot, hot and good, who comes from another prominent law enforcement family, played by, Sonequa Martin-Green.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, the pair is meeting for the very first time while investigating a case.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Boston Blue"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Off to a really good start.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let me see your badge!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You really seem to like each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that projection.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN ("Boston Blue" actor): I don`t trust you yet!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it, too. I know. I`ll just say this to both of you. What`s it like to be involved in another hit show, you two?\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Aw!\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG ("Boston Blue" actor): Aw! Honest to God, girl!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gayle stop talking!\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Literally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Keep talking. Keep talking. They only gave me two episodes to watch and I would have watched whatever they sent. It`s already off to a great start. I love that it is multiracial with the families. I love that it is multifaith, and Donnie, I read that you were reluctant to do a spinoff at first. Am I reading it right or wrong?\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: Yes, I wouldn`t say reluctant to do a spinoff per se. It was hard to say good-bye to "Blue Bloods."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: And to process that. It came along so quickly, the next incarnation of the show that I hadn`t really processed it. You know, I was still saying goodbye to the crew members and --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: -- 500 people lost their jobs and it was a lot. I think -- but when I got to the point of not seeing this show, "Boston Blue," as the nail in "Blue Bloods" coffin, but the opportunity to keep family, faith, and tradition on T.V., to tell a story of a law enforcement family and to keep the Reagans alive in this new world with an amazing new cast and amazing new partner.\par \par \uc2 When I saw the opportunity rather than seeing it as a negative --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: I was like, oh, I have to do this, right? So now, we`re just -- we are having a ball. It`s amazing and we are keeping those things on television. It`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is this thing called chemistry, Sonequa.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hundred percent! You guys have it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like we have chemistry here.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you have to have chemistry. For you, you`re going into a very well established show. Did you have any trepidation about that? Not about taking it, but how am I going to fit in here?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But also coming from also a very well-established franchise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Franchises.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Star Trek," "Walking Dead."\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Thank you. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I meant when I said she has -- that`s what I meant, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good job, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I like to -- I talk too much. I talk too much.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: I like the chemistry. I am loving this chemistry.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is an A and B conversation, I`ll C my way out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, go ahead! Go ahead, Sonequa. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: But no, yes, you know, I am still floored by what God is doing, because I just couldn`t have expected it. I didn`t think "Star Trek: Discovery" would come after "Walking Dead," and I certainly didn`t think another beloved T.V. franchise would come after "Star Trek: Discovery." So, I learned a lot about doing that, about building new iterations of beloved franchises, right?\par \par \uc2 And it is exciting to be able to bring what I know, what I have experienced and there`s also so much more hope in me now, because I see what it is like and I know the steps that we should take or at least, I have an idea.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: So it is nice to be here. I`ve learned so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: And I have had so many show families.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: So I know exactly how to help build one and you know, how to keep the connective tissue to the franchise and to keep true to the shoulders we`re standing on, right? I know exactly how to do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So far so good!\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: She really brings so much the table in that experience, but also in her talent. I mean, everything, across the board.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: The show is, you know, it`s a universe show. It`s a spinoff. However you want to refer to it, but it is a new world as well. Danny is a fish out of water and he meets someone that really is one of the reasons he stays. Not that it`s romantic or anything, but he meets an equal.\par \par \uc2 You know, they are from different worlds, different experiences, but they see something in each other. They have -- they share common bonds, like they come from law enforcement families and Sonequa`s experience is so unique and so helpful in this process. You know, having done these shows and you know, she is like, we`re like the dynamic duo like we lean on each other. We talk to each other constantly.\par \par \uc2 We are constantly working to -- for the success of this show and ultimately, unique places.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the multiracial -- the two of you, it like your family, you have a White sister, you have a Black brother.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you all explain that.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I haven`t seen anything quite that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it is a representation of what this country looks like - -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- which is refreshing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, speaking of dynamic duos, you were talking during the commercial break and I have to bring this up. We only have a minute, but every time I see you, I compliment your outfit.\par \par \uc2 Vlad was asking about your watch. Can you please tell us the significance of that watch you have on?\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: Oh my gosh! My wife gave me this watch. We took a little weekend off when we were first dating, just getting, you know, a little rough patch. One of our very rare rough patches.\par \par \uc2 But she gave this watch and we gave a long hug and she said --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said it was about an hour.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: It was a long hug, yes, because we were like, we didn`t know if we were going to make it through this weekend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is after reconnecting after an argument.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: We reconnected and had the weekend off, and this is before we were even married and all of that. And she -- we hugged for like an hour and she gave me the watch and she said, I never, ever -- if we ever have a problem again, let`s just remember this moment in this room when we reconnected and how we feel. So I never set the watch, I left it the time of day that she gave me the watch so I could remember.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To take you back to that moment?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it has been like that ever since.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: And I wear it every day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the time is still the same.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: And I never set the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you picture the hotel room? Can you still picture the hotel room?\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: Right over in Brooklyn, in Williamsburg.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: I know exactly the room number and everything.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you guys are a dynamic duo. What`s the key to longevity? Real quick.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: Putting faith at the center of our relationship and putting the relationship above all else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s yours, Sonequa?\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: I agree. Same! Literally the same.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re the same -- yes.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Because we are married for a similar amount of years, and yes, that`s the key. That`s exactly it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And having a hit T.V. show don`t hurt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amen to that! Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love that!\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I also love a couple of episodes are directed by our friend, Bridget Moynahan.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh! That`s right.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: That`s right. We`ve got a surprise guest coming from the old show popping up here and there.\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: That`s right. There is somebody really special you`re going to see coming up to the show, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay. Okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s called a tease.\par \par \uc2 Donnie Wahlberg, Sonequa Martin-Green, we are cheering them on. The show, guys, is so good.\par \par \uc2 "Boston Blue" premiers Friday at 10/9 Central right here. Where Donnie?\par \par \uc2 DONNIE WAHLBERG: \uc2 CBS\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where is -- streaming on where?\par \par \uc2 SONEQUA MARTIN-GREEN: Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Up ahead, best-selling author, Michael Lewis is here.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, he`ll tell us about a new version of "The Big Short," his book on the 2008 financial crisis and what he sees as the biggest economic threat today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Big Short"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is a scene from the hit film, "The Big Short," about how the 2008 financial crisis was mainly triggered by a U.S. housing bubble. The movie is based on the 2010 book of the same name, by acclaimed author, Michael Lewis. Now for the very first time, Lewis is telling the story in an audio book. We`re happy to say, Michael Lewis joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS ("The Big Short" author): Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you. So what was it like to revisit "The Big Short," for this audio book? And just tell us what your reaction was to just rereading the words that you`ve written?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That you wrote, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That you wrote, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: I was -- you know, I was amazed how good the material still was. Really, that was it. It wasn`t -- sometimes a book, when it gets old, gets dead on the page.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: And this did not feel dead on the page and it didn`t feel dead on the page. I realized about half way into it, because we`re still living in the world the financial crisis created. Like, you can draw a line from that event to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You can draw a line from that event to lots of stuff that is going on in the financial sector.\par \par \uc2 And so it was just sort of oh, that was the big -- I thought of it as the end of a story. Sort of the collapse of these firms that I had written about in my first book, "Liar`s Poker."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: It was the beginning of some other story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the story continues. Does it surprise you that it is relevant today, 15 years later that book came out?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: It was so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fifteen years ago, it came out and we are still --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: I don`t know how much you remember, but it was such anger, right? It generated this feeling of deep unfairness.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: That you have these rich Wall Street people who do all this stuff and money they shouldn`t do. Everybody pays a price for it but them, and they seem to get bailed out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: So it has got this weird situation where it is capitalism for everybody, but the capitalists.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: And that`s when you start hearing people say that things are rigged and this anger, and this -- and the polarization in our politics starts. The Tea Party, and Occupy metastasized into other things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Only got worse, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so Michael, we have the housing market, the stock market, crypto, we have A.I., you throw in politics now that is underlining everything. What do you feel like is the biggest financial threat right now?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: So, there is going to be a crash, because there always is. It`s very hard to know when. There will be some kind of crash.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That it is inevitable.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: Yes, The biggest threat to me isn`t one of those things. It`s do we have the institutions to deal with it once it happens? So that, if you look -- the reason everything could be calmed down, 15 years ago is that the Fed was independent, and the government was trusted. I mean, the government could function like a parent walking into the room.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So how do you feel about the political pressure impacting that?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: Right now, so, the idea of a Federal Reserve that is basically an arm of the President and politicized and you know, we are not managing our finances. It is really -- it should make you really uneasy that the stock market is doing what it is doing and gold is rocketing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: People are getting out of dollars and into gold. Why are they doing that? Because they feel uncomfortable. So, and we have a polarized political process. Can you imagine right now, Congress coming together and doing anything?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS: So, what I would worry 