om Jeremy Allen White, and Scott Cooper, the director, writer, and producer of "Springsteen: Deliver Me from Nowhere" tomorrow right here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 But I fell in love with Jeremy Allen White. That was the first time I met him. There is even a story behind the hat that he was wearing. Bruce! So, he says, Bruce is so important to so many people. It was daunting, I didn`t to disappoint and Bruce Springsteen says this, Jeremy tolerated me and I appreciate that. Isn`t that great? That Bruce would say that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s awesome. I love that. Can`t wait for it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you listen to him, for you Vlad, you listen to somebody and then you are playing them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, you know how I feel about Bruce Springsteen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Probably the singular idol of my life and I can`t wait to see this film and he is perfect for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jeremy is having more than just a moment. It is well- deserved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is a new survey on young people and how they are using artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The Center for Democracy and Technology conducted national surveys of 1,000 high school students. The research found that one in five students surveyed said or someone they know have used A.I. for a romantic relationship. One of the authors of the report says students attending schools that use A.I. were more likely to report that it is used for mental health support, as a companion and for romance.\par \par \uc2 Researchers say teens are using the software to escape reality. So, I wanted to bring that up, because it talks about young people and teens, but Vlad, it seems like you have 