he Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com. We got you.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next two guests want to take shame out of being fired, Laura Brown, Kristina O`Neill are their names. They are former top fashion editors. Both were let go from their editor-in-chief positions at "In Style" and "The Wall Street Journal." You have to read the book to get the back story.\par \par \uc2 The long-time friends met for a drink and posted a selfie on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  with the caption, "All the coolest girls get fired." Yes!\par \par \uc2 That inspired their new book, "All the Cool Girls Get Fired: How to Let Go of Being Let Go and Come Back on Top."\par \par \uc2 The book includes very practical solid advice, along with accounts from remarkable women like Jamie Lee Curtis, Oprah, Mika Brzezinski, Carol Burnett -- lots.\par \par \uc2 So, Laura Brown and Kristina O`Neill are here with us. First on CBS MORNINGS, I was so excited, guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, I love the book.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN ("All the Cool Girls Get Fired" co-author): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL ("All the Cool Girls Get Fired" co-author): Good morning. It looks good with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It gives such great advice. But at one point you said, why should you all take advice from me? Like who are you people that we should -- that you can give others advice?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: Yes. We`re like every woman out there who has been through it and I think that`s really why we wrote this book, because so many people are going through, you know, complete upheaval in the workplace, and hang lived it, you know, we thought we could share some of our experience with the wider world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People are --\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: This is a book we wish we had.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: We had to \uc2 Google\uc2 . We had to \uc2 Google\uc2  lawyers? We had to \uc2 Google\uc2  with, what is COBRA? How long do I have it? And so, we aggregated all of this knowledge because there is so much vulnerability out there, there is so many layoffs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: It is like, this is the road map and a hug.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said take a brief wallow break.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Just a little one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is okay.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lick your wounds for just a second.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you said, you have got to get out there and strike while the pink slip is still hot and the firing is on top of mind. Get out there and get back in.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Exactly. It actually makes you feel better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Easier said than done!\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: It does, but when you know, when you are alone, like and you`re wallowing and after a while, you`re like actually, I feel worse.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: You know, just put it like, we call it like proof of life and also like, if you don`t put your hand up and say this happened to me, who is going to reach for you? You know, who is going to help you? And that`s the real -- it is the real -- if you are spinning and obfuscating and saying, this didn`t happen. It`s like take ago big ball of shame. This is really like anvil landing on your head.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: So, then, it is like, oh, let`s take a heavier thing and carry that around. You don`t need to do it and the minute that you remember that you have your own value and worth and the loss of your job does not affect, then after that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are not your job. You say, you are not your job.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: You are not your job and I think, that`s -- they have taken so much control away from you. Why let them take away the things that you have built? And everything you put into the workplace does not disappear when you are fired. There`s a platform that you have built over years and years --\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: And you stand on it.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: -- of experience and you still stand on that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But how do you detach from that? Because when you give so much to your job, it not only becomes your purpose or your passion, it becomes who you are, or at least that`s what the world tells us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then when things go awry, or they don`t work out or you get fired or let go, all of a sudden, you are struggling to find your identity. What is your advice to people at home that are watching right now?\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: And so it is so difficult, as we know, but do we say do not attach all your value to your job, because if you lose your job, you lose it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You lose yourself.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: You know, and so to have family life, to have interests, to have something on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go. Yup.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Some insurance around your job, so if this happens to you and media, you know, it is such a bellwether for all of this, is that you have something to stand on that is left there for you, and we are lucky we had solid home lives and things outside, but whew!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have advice about what to do.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: And each other.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Yes. Each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you think that it is coming.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Yup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When it comes to get all your doctors` checkups out of the way while you still have insurance. Just make the rounds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The practical advice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, practical advice.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: There are really simple practical things that we, you know, and again hindsight is 20/20, right? So, I had never updated my LinkedIn profile in 10 years, I hadn`t backed up a single contact when I worked at "The Journal." And so these are the things you can do, these like little insurance policies that you can take out on yourself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you can do it while everything is going good.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When you are doing well.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: And keep your eye on the horizon and just be like, okay, let`s read the room a little bit, you know, and like, see where I am. So you`re just always, like again, you are built up and you`re insured and feel like you have agency, because when you are fired, you lose it for a minute.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: But there are of these things you can do to get your control back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So one of the chapters I really love is the chapter than you, when you speak to Mika Brzezinski, because there are two things that struck me. One, how hard it is to continue to advocate for yourself --\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- even when you`re at the top of your game.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the other one is, we all act as mentors, but I often think, well, who are our mentors?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s so hard to ask for advice from other people --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- even though you are dishing it out constantly.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Yes, and Mika, as part of her lure now, you know your value platform is, because she was fired and she then took a step back and she goes, okay, I just want to work. I`m going to do the cut-ins, you know, and she took a job at 10 percent of her original salary. She goes, I just want to be there. I just want to be there. And time progressed and she ended up trying out for "Morning Joe."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And look where she is now.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Mike Brzezinski -- exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like what you say, and get a job quickly, even if it is beneath you, but no job is beneath you. What is beneath you is not paying your rent.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: It is like, you ought to be able to pay your rent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say, the door didn`t hit us on the way, it opened wider.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Laura brown and Kristina O`Neill. Thank you and bravo!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The book is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Cool Girls Get Fired" on sale now.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What a show!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was a great show. Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BROWN: Oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right! Look at that.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: That thing! Look at how it flows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And look at how it flows.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA O`NEILL: Look how it flows.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcrip