board.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ll hear directly from lawmakers on both sides about the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight: Scary moments at New York LaGuardia after two Delta planes collide on a taxiway, damaging the aircrafts.\par \par \uc2 JOEY ANNUNZIATA: We got absolutely smashed by another Delta flight. I don`t know if we hit them or if they hit us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The government shutdown enters its second day as Democrats hold firm on health care demands.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R) (Vice President of the United States of America): Open up the People`s Government and then let`s fix health care policy for the American people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tributes pour in for the legendary wildlife expert, \uc2 Jane Goodall\uc2 , who has died at 91.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2  (Wildlife expert and researcher): No intention of being a scientist because girls didn`t do that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: A partial building collapsed in New York, a dramatic moment caught on camera.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Amazingly, no one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Game 2 between the Reds and the Dodgers, L.A. with a chance to close out the series.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Line drive by Mookie Betts, the Dodgers sweep the Reds two- zip and they`ll take on the \uc2 Phillies\uc2  in the Division Series.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: During this shutdown, employees deemed essential like TSA officers will stay on the job, working without pay until it is.\par \par \uc2 RONNY CHIENG ("The Daily Show"): Wait! TSA officers were getting paid this whole time?\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 RONNY CHIENG: Then why are they so angry?\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 RONNY CHIENG: Look, I`m sorry I didn`t know Kindles were laptops. All right, stop yelling at me.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: TSA agents get really annoyed with some of the passengers and news flash! Some of those passengers are very annoying.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of TSA --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I thought you were going to say some of those passengers are me --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, no, no, no!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are me, I know. Look, I don`t take the laptop out. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You do encounter some personality.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of TSA, let`s stay right there at the airport, and this is a story that hits very close to home here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 . We begin with a scary situation at New York`s LaGuardia Airport last night. Two regional jets crashed into each other on the tarmac, injuring a flight attendant.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 CBS\uc2  news producer who I`m very close with was on board one of those flights, and he took this video showing how the wing of one aircraft hit the front of the other plane.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is at LaGuardia for us.\par \par \uc2 Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hey there, Nate. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, a total of 93 people were on board both of those flights, and as you mentioned, that includes our very own \uc2 CBS\uc2  news producer, Joey Annunziata. He actually captured video of the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 Now, he described a chaotic scene where people were shouting after the impact and asking each other what exactly happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ENDEAVOR AIR FLIGHT 5047 PILOT: It seems as though there`s an aircraft that collided with us.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Just before 10:00 P.M. last night, \uc2 Endeavor Air\uc2  flight 5047 arriving from Charlotte, landed safely at LaGuardia, but as it taxied to the gate, the plane was struck by another departing CRJ-type aircraft, \uc2 Endeavor Air\uc2  flight 5155.\par \par \uc2 AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL: You guys did clip, correct?\par \par \uc2 ENDEAVOR AIR FLIGHT 5074 PILOT: Yes, we did. Their right wing clipped our nose and the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 JOEY ANNUNZIATA: We got absolutely smashed by another Delta flight.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: CBS News producer, Joey Annunziata was on board Flight 5047.\par \par \uc2 JOEY ANNUNZIATA: It was absolutely -- super jarring! Everyone shot forward in their seats, and it was kind of a little chaotic. As soon as it happened, we were kind of shocked at what happened. We`re kind of moving at a pretty decent clip, too.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Annunziata captured the moment right after the collision, which left 5155 swing completely torn off and extensive damage to the windshield and nose of the plane he was on.\par \par \uc2 Endeavor Air\uc2  is a subsidiary of Delta, which in a statement, said that the taxiing planes were traveling at a "low speed."\par \par \uc2 LAURA EINSETLER (Commercial airline pilot): This is extremely unusual. These kind of things do not happen.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Captain Laura Einsetler has over 30 years of experience as a commercial airline pilot.\par \par \uc2 LAURA EINSETLER: There was some very significant force that took place here, so the investigators will look at speed at the time. It can be pretty challenging sometimes at LaGuardia, so it is imperative that pilots are always heads up looking around.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And Delta issued a statement saying they would investigate what exactly occurred. They went on to say, "Safety of our customers and people comes before all else." -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is a standard statement. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I am so glad that Joey and everybody else was on board, but when you`re what -- you know, I`m not a pilot, of course, but you think, why can`t they see each other on the ground that way? That`s what I`m confused about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t get it either. Someone should have hit the brakes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt about it. I was with them. We were in Charlotte interviewing Bubba Wallace. We were sharing a beer just to unpack the interview because it went well, and he took a separate flight. I crashed when I went to sleep. As soon as -- I crashed when I got home and I woke up to news that he crashed as soon as he landed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My first text message to him was, "Are you okay?" Thankfully, he said, "Yes."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to have a news person on board. They immediately take out a phone and start talking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With sweeping shots and everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Joey. Thank you. We`re glad you`re okay.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to Washington, where it is, day two of that government shutdown. President Trump has threatened mass layoffs, and now he says he`s going to take aim at projects that are important to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill with the latest on this.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning. Always good to see you. Is there any sign of progress toward a deal here?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Right now things are at a standstill here on Capitol Hill, the House and Senate will pause to observe the Jewish holiday of Yom Kippur, meaning this shutdown will continue for another day, leaving thousands of federal workers in limbo.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: From the Capitol to the Washington Monument, signs of a shutdown are everywhere.\par \par \uc2 The National Zoo, part of the Smithsonian is open for now, but could run out of funding by next week. No treat for Yael Krigman, who has owned a bakery across the street for more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 YAEL KRIGMAN (Baked by Yael Owner and President): When they don`t go to the zoo, they don`t come across the street to Baked by Yael, and we lose out on sales.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Around the country, the federal fallout is growing with an estimated 750,000 government workers furloughed, and in a call with House Republicans Wednesday, White House Budget Director Russ vote warned mass layoffs could come within the next two days.\par \par \uc2 President Trump claim Democrats would be to blame.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): Well, there could be firings, and that`s their fault, and it could also be other things. I mean, we could cut projects that they wanted, favored projects, and they would be permanently cut.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Trump administration announced Wednesday it would withhold $18 billion in funding for transportation projects in New York City, home to Democratic Leaders \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Do you think you`re personally being targeted?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): The Trump administration is targeting the jobs -- thousands of jobs of hard-working Americans.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: With all sides seemingly dug in.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Does this look partisan? Does this look dirty to you? Twenty-four pages to fund the government.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A glimmer of hope on the Senate floor as Republicans and Democrats huddle together to find a path forward.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JEANNE SHAHEEN (D-NH): We are not that far apart. This is not that difficult to solve.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Krigman hopes they cook up a solution soon.\par \par \uc2 YAEL KRIGMAN: Decisions have consequences. I know that as a small business owner and I think that the government needs to understand that, that their actions have consequences and they hurt real people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, furloughed federal workers will get back pay. Many of these small businesses that rely on them do not when the Senate returns Friday. Republicans plan to push for another clean funding extension, but Democrats insist any deal must address rising health care costs -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have more on this coming up. We`re going to be joined by Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren, and later by Republican senator speaker or Republican Speaker of the House, rather, \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2 . Looking forward to those conversations, but now to some breaking news from England, at least four people hurt after a car and stabbing attack at a synagogue.\par \par \uc2 It happened in the northern city of Manchester, and it happened on Yom Kippur, the holiest day on the Jewish calendar.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has the latest for us now, Holly, good morning to you. What do we know?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The police here say they were called to the Heaton Park Synagogue just after 9:30 this morning, local time, after a report of a car being driven at members of the public and a stabbing. The police arrived within 10 minutes and shot a man who is believed to be the offender. Four other people were injured with both stab wounds and injuries caused by the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to a local media report, the attacker did not get inside the synagogue itself. The mayor of Manchester told our partners at the BBC that the suspect is believed to be dead and that security at the synagogue intervened to keep many people safe.\par \par \uc2 One video appears to show a police officer warning people that the attacker has a bomb. We haven`t confirmed whether that turned out to be true. Now, last year, the \uc2 British government\uc2  committed extra funding, around $100 million to protect synagogues and Jewish schools. There are around 300,000 Jewish people living here in the UK. The British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said that he is appalled by this attack on Yom Kippur, and the police will be deployed to synagogues across the country -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Holly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is expanding efforts to crack down on crime and illegal immigration in cities run by Democrats, Attorney General Pam Bondi and Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  traveled to Memphis yesterday. Hundreds of Federal officers and National Guard troops will soon be there as well.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes is in Memphis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: With a renewed message, vowing to wipe out crime in several major cities across the country, Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  and Homeland Security adviser Stephen Miller touched down in Memphis late Wednesday, joined by U.S. Attorney General Pam Bondi.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI (U.S. Attorney General): we are here to have your backs.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Their message to nearly 700 state, local and federal agents: Rescue Memphis. A directive made despite crime in the city dropping to a historic 25-year low in the first eight months of this year according to Memphis P.D. Still, they say they hope to mirror the efforts in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER (Deputy Chief-of-Staff for Policy and Homeland Security adviser): We will liberate this city from the criminal element that has plagued it for generations.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: On Tuesday, speaking before hundreds of admirals and generals in Quantico, President Trump said this.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): And then I told Pete, we should use some of these dangerous cities as training grounds for our military.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: We asked city leaders for their reaction.\par \par \uc2 J.B. SMILEY, JR. (D) (Memphis City Council member): It makes me believe that he doesn`t care about the people who live here. When you say, training grounds, let me go out there. I hear target practice. I hear some of the most vile treatment that you can imagine.\par \par \uc2 This is not done to bring us any good. This is done to control us whether they collaborate with us, and it`s problematic.\par \par \uc2 J. FORD CANALE (D) (Memphis City Council member): Yes, so training grounds, I would say I would use a different language. They`re coming, whether you want it or not. Why don`t we create the best situation for everybody and work together to create the best possible outcome.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: We know those National Guard troops may not arrive in the city until later this month. This is all happening, though, at the invitation of Tennessee Governor Bill Lee. That`s in stark contrast to the response we`ve seen from other cities like Portland, Oregon and Chicago, which have openly opposed the deployment of troops and in some cases even filed lawsuits to keep them away -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We are learning nearly a hundred people on a Royal Caribbean cruise ship fell ill from norovirus. It`s a very contagious stomach infection. That ship docked in Miami early this morning after the cruise line reported the illnesses to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides has been talking with people on board.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB PETTIT (Sick passenger): I don`t think I`ve ever been that sick in my life.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: It`s been rough seas for dozens of passengers aboard Royal Caribbean Serenade of the Seas after a norovirus outbreak.\par \par \uc2 BOB PETTIT: I got violently sick, and I couldn`t make it to the bathroom, even.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Bob Pettit says he got ill one night after dinner. He is traveling with his son, Joshua.\par \par \uc2 Had there been an outbreak declared already?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA PETTIT (Bob Pettit`s son): We knew it was starting -- it seemed a day or two after the ship visited Mexico, all of a sudden they started at the buffet with enhanced protocols of serving. The staff was serving food.\par \par \uc2 I`ve managed so far to escape, hopefully, if I can make it one more day and get off the ship without being sick myself.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey, we`re coming into Panama.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: The ship has been on a 13-day voyage through Central America and the Caribbean, departing San Diego on September 19th, and arrived this morning in Miami.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , more than 90 passengers and four crew members reported symptoms of norovirus, which include diarrhea and vomiting.\par \par \uc2 Royal Caribbean\uc2  says it has isolated sick passengers and increased cleaning and disinfection procedures.\par \par \uc2 Is this a cruise ship passenger`s worst nightmare?\par \par \uc2 DR. AILEEN MARTY (Infectious disease expert): It is a miserable, miserable three days for most people.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Dr. Aileen Marty is an infectious disease specialist.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re on a ship, you hear something like this happening. Do you avoid the buffet? What do you do?\par \par \uc2 DR. AILEEN MARTY: Absolutely avoid the buffet and just be very, very careful about your hand hygiene.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: There is no antiviral treatment for norovirus. So it`s just about treating the symptoms, making sure that you stay hydrated. Now, this is the 19th outbreak of a gastrointestinal illness on a cruise ship so far this year that`s already outpacing all of last -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Cristian, I love a good cruise. All right, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  is due in court tomorrow to be sentenced for his felony convictions on prostitution related charges. Our national correspondent Jericka Duncan spoke exclusively with a former employee who testified in the hip-hop mogul`s criminal trial.\par \par \uc2 Stylist, Deonte` Nash filed a lawsuit last week alleging Combs physically and sexually abused him for years, which Combs denies.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You say you were strangled on several occasions.\par \par \uc2 DEONTE` NASH (Sean Comb`s former hair stylist): Several.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Was there ever a point where you thought I could die?\par \par \uc2 DEONTE` NASH: The whole time I said he is going to kill us. He is going to kill us.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: You talk in the lawsuit about literally having a to-go bag and cash just in case you had to go into hiding.\par \par \uc2 DEONTE` NASH: It has some underwear, some shorts, a tank top, a hoodie and you know, just some -- and I would just like --\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And you say this so casually, like, this was normal behavior at that time.\par \par \uc2 DEONTE` NASH: Normal behavior. And it would be like, you know, very normal. This was normal, very normal behavior, and everybody pacified it.\par \par \uc2 So who are we going to tell? Like, who exactly were we going to tell? Who are we going to call? Ghostbusters? Like there was no one there to save us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`ll have more of Jericka`s interview tomorrow here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, we are remembering one of the most important animal researchers of modern times. Her name, \uc2 Jane Goodall\uc2 , whose research on wild chimpanzees captured our imaginations. We`re sorry to say she has died at the age of 91.\par \par \uc2 Goodall`s pioneering studies challenged scientific consensus and made her a household name.\par \par \uc2 Seth Doane profiled her for "CBS Sunday Morning" back in 2021.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : You can`t just sit all day. It`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: I can`t imagine that you`ve ever sat all day.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : Not really. No.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: That was her attitude until the very end, the famed conservationist was doing podcasts into her 90s.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : I feel that I was put on this planet with a mission.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: And speaking at events earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : My dream began when I was 10.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: When we met at her home in England in 2021, she was just as you would imagine \uc2 Jane Goodall\uc2  might be. As we set up our cameras, she was more interested in a visit from a robin near a tree she used to climb as a child.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : I read "Tarzan" up there.\par \par \uc2 That`s when my dream began: I`ll go to Africa live with animals. No intention of being a scientist, because girls didn`t do that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: She started as a secretary, then landed a job as an assistant to a paleoanthropologist.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : I clearly was passionate. I clearly had an understanding of animals, and he felt that women might be better in the field, that we might be more patient.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: Crucially, in the Gombe Rainforest of Tanzania, she witnessed chimpanzees fashioning and using tools, something that, until then, was thought to be unique to humans.\par \par \uc2 So these were your earliest observations.\par \par \uc2 Her groundbreaking work as a female scientist in the 1960s made her a "National Geographic" cover girl, and she tried to keep attention on protecting wildlife and the environment through the \uc2 Jane Goodall Institute\uc2 , telling us she did not really want to be this busy.\par \par \uc2 Why do you do it?\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : Because there`s a message to get out and I`m getting older, and there`s less time left ahead of me, and the world is falling to pieces.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: Musing about another adventure in April, she discussed death, saying she believed there was something next.\par \par \uc2 JANE GOODALL\uc2 : I can`t think of anything more exciting than discovering what something is.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: During our interview, she had a little nip of whiskey to soothe her vocal cords and told us that a daily walk sorted out pretty much everything else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, Seth, I met her a couple of times too, and you can`t help but be in awe of her when you met her.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: Absolutely!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is the thing -- to know what you wanted to do at the age of 10, but she was such a kind hearted person.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: She absolutely was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is what struck me about her.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: When we finished the interview, we drove off in the taxi. She came running out of the house. She said, "Stop! Stop the cars!" What`s \uc2 Jane Goodall\uc2  doing? She said, "I want to say goodbye to the taxi driver."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Of course, she did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She lived a good life. Seth, so you said, whiskey and walks. Right?\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: Yes, yes. A little nip of whiskey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A little nip of whisky.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: She actually had it in a little stuffed animal, chimpanzee, and would pull it out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love that detail. It was more than the vocal cords. Seth, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, part of an apartment building in New York suddenly collapses. What we know about the cause.\par \par \uc2 And we will take you inside the Taylor Swift merchandise money machine with her new album just hours away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to go! I want to go!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to --\par \par \uc2 But first, 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, did you know Sarah Jessica Parker is a book publisher? Well, she`s here, first on CBS MORNINGS, along with the author of her latest book.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" the queen of Halloween, Heidi Klum is dropping hints about her costume this year. We`re going to show you. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: -- Austin Wells and right on to the right field. Fair ball!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tie game, bottom of the eighth, Austin Wells connects. Jazz Chisholm from first base on a single comes all the way around and keeps the Yankee season alive. Forty-seven thousand cheering in the Bronx. Game 3 today.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re looking at a terrifying video of a partial building collapse in New York City yesterday. Authorities say a gas explosion caused the ventilation shaft connected to the boiler room to crumble. Remarkably, no one was hurt. A gaping hole can be seen on the side of the 20-story building and a huge pile of bricks on the ground just feet away from a playground.\par \par \uc2 City officials say they are relocating dozens of residents to hotels after 38 apartments were evacuated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s amazing when you look at that video, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And no one was hurt. That that was the case.\par \par \uc2 The clock is ticking. I cannot wait. What is it? Taylor Swift`s new era begins in less than 24 hours, people, this is happening. It`s finally here.\par \par \uc2 Her 12th album, "The Life of a Show Girl," will be released at midnight on the dot. Dozens of different C.D.s and vinyl editions, along with the merchandise and a movie. So all of this follows Swift`s mega popular Eras Tour.\par \par \uc2 Senior business and tech correspondent, Jo Ling Kent, she`s just as happy as I am. She, too is a hardcore Swiftie. She`s in the studio today. She has got more on the business of Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT (Singer): This is my brand new album.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bejeweled"/Taylor Swift/Republic Records)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The world`s most recognizable showgirl is entering a new era.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour (Taylor`s Version)/\uc2 Disney\uc2 +)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: And there are no signs her gold rush is slowing down.\par \par \uc2 Where does Taylor Swift fall when it comes to being a business person?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN EVERS (Author): In terms of business person, Taylor is at the top.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Kevin Evers is the author of the new book, "There`s Nothing Like This: The Strategic Genius of Taylor Swift."\par \par \uc2 How would you describe Taylor`s business strategy for this album.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN EVERS: Taylor has always been really fan obsessed.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour (Taylor`s Version)/\uc2 Disney\uc2 +)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN EVERS: If you look at all the album variants and all this new stuff that she`s doing around this album, she`s really trying to give her fans as much value as possible.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: And as a result, she`ll generate a lot of value for herself as well.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN EVERS: For sure! I mean, she`s definitely going to make a lot of money from this album, and she`s not shy about that. But at the same time, the reason why all of these strategies are working is because her fans are actively engaged.\par \par \uc2 JASON KELCE (Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player): It`s orange!\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS KELCE: Yes, it is and sparkly.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In August, Swift announced "The Life of a Showgirl" on her now fiance, Travis Kelce`s podcast.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour (Taylor`s Version)/\uc2 Disney\uc2 +)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: She said the album was inspired by the record smashing Eras Tour.\par \par \uc2 The first ever to gross more than $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT: This album is about what was going on behind-the-scenes in my inner life during this tour, which was so exuberant and electric and vibrant.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Since then, countdowns on her website have appeared for limited edition vinyls, C.D.s and cardigans, which sold out within minutes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Target is preparing for shoppers expected in stores hours before midnight. Fans are flocking to see her "Taylor Swift: The Official Release Party of a Showgirl" film in theaters this weekend only, and the album has already broken a record with more than five million \uc2 Spotify\uc2  pre saves.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour (Taylor`s Version)/\uc2 Disney\uc2 +)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: I am one of those pre-saves, by the way, and of course, Swift is also entering a new era in her personal life after her engagement to Travis Kelce. Evers told me that that upcoming wedding is playing into this release, as fans are expecting the album to be a behind-the-scenes peek into their relationship as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen, remember when we saw the two of them on the podcast. I love that they were so frank and so candid --\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- and you can just tell they`re two people who are really into each other, and I can`t wait to see how it influences her music.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: They are not only getting married, they are a power couple. They`re so aware of their brands, they`re really trying to deliver for fans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Jo Ling, she is good --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think --\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Oh, she is good. It is the songwriting --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is really good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: -- that really brings us all in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Let`s be real. It`s not just the business and marketing of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It is what she writes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of that though, I think there should be a Taylor Swift Business 101 course taught on college campuses --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or Songwriting 101.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She can do both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like her inner team --\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: We are seeing though across the country --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- they are a machine.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: You can sign up. We went to one at Berkeley. It was fantastic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh really?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am impressed by the business, but the most impressive thing is all of this just came from her mind. I mean, it`s the songwriting that is the head of the river.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: She is a creative genius, no doubt about it. I can`t wait for tonight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a great point.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead any chance of a deal to end the government shutdown, Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is day two of the government shutdown. Still no deal. Democrats say they`re trying to stop a GOP spending plan that could raise health care costs for many Americans, millions in fact.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren joins us now for more on the state of things. Senator Warren, thank you very much for coming back to us. We appreciate having you on as always.\par \par \uc2 I do wonder what the state of things are right now? Eventually, someone is going to have to compromise. Someone is going to have to blink and make a deal. Also, someone is going to get the blame. Are any negotiations going on right now?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): So look, the Speaker of the House just said last night that he didn`t plan to negotiate and didn`t want to negotiate. But here`s the thing to remember, Americans have -- we`ve had a broken health care system for a very long time, but in July, the Republicans, all by themselves, with no Democratic votes voted to cut health care for 15 million Americans, just knock them off their health care coverage, and voted to raise insurance premiums for tens of millions more. And those increases in insurance premiums are occurring literally right now, this month in October, people are getting their notices.\par \par \uc2 so Democrats said at the time, when you pass that bill, it`s going to come due, because when we get ready to do the budget for the year, we`re going to ask that that be rolled back, and the Republicans didn`t want to talk about it, wouldn`t negotiate. So now that`s where we are. Democrats are fighting to try to keep those health care costs down and roll back the Republican cuts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Senator, Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2  of South Dakota says he is ready to negotiate around the subsidies, but they`re not going to negotiate with the government in a shutdown mode. Why not reopen and go to the table to make a deal?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): So I don`t understand that. We`re ready to negotiate. We have had now three months. We`ve been begging the Republicans to come to the table and negotiate. You know how many negotiations there have been? Zero.\par \par \uc2 And the Republicans are saying they won`t negotiate when there`s not a shutdown. They won`t negotiate when there is a shutdown. So far, they won`t negotiate at all.\par \par \uc2 We are only fighting not to expand the money that`s spent on health care, just to roll back the cuts so that what health care coverage people had last June, they will still have in October, November, December and going forward, that`s all that Democrats are asking for and the Republicans won`t even sit down with us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Senator, they`re saying they will sit down now, and you have some Democrats, I`m thinking about Senator Masto of Nevada saying that by withholding -- by shutting down in this particular moment, you`re giving more power to the Trump administration. You`re not taking power away, you`re giving it. Why do you disagree with her?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Look, let`s understand that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to do whatever he wants to do, and \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , the author of Project 2025 is in there trying to make as many painful cuts as he can.\par \par \uc2 That could be stopped if the Republicans in the House and the Senate would just say no more of this. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has already illegally fired tens of thousands of federal employees, and now we have a President of the United States who is saying that what he wants to do is impose as much pain as possible on some of his fellow Americans, just so that he can win a political battle.\par \par \uc2 He is cutting infrastructure spending in places where the money has already been approved because it`s important to build those tunnels and build those bridges, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he doesn`t care. He just wants to impose pain on Americans because he thinks that Democrats actually care and that Democrats will be forced to back down.\par \par \uc2 Look, we`re fighting for people`s health care. That`s what this is all about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, Senator, you know, Republicans say what you`re really fighting for is taxpayer dollars for, as they put it, illegal aliens. I know that`s not strictly true, but there is a provision --\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Excuse me, not strictly true. It`s a flat out lie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is not true directly, but --\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): It is a flat out lie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The way that they frame it --\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): There is nothing that in Medicaid, there is nothing in Medicare that permits one undocumented immigrant to get $1.00 of a system. None. Zero.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator, if you let me finish the question.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Of course, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the Democratic counter offer, the proposal for funding, there is a restoration of Medicaid benefits for certain non-citizens that had been taken away in The Big, Beautiful Bill as Republicans put it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Republicans don`t think those people should be getting health care on the taxpayer dime. I`m not asking on the merits. Politically, putting that in there exposes you to this talking point, this lie as you put it. Why put it in there? Why is it worth it?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): No. What`s happened is that. What we`re saying is that whenever hospitals give care, what is going to be the level of reimbursement? And the Republicans said it`s going to be a low level. The Democrats said, we just want to go back to the level it was before, because you`re going to bankrupt hospitals. You`re going to put rural hospitals out of business.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans and the Democrats plan, nothing changes. The number of undocumented immigrants who still get care if they show up bleeding and unconscious at an emergency room, there is absolutely zero difference. The difference is whether or not hospitals who under laws that date back to \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  may provide emergency care to anyone who is unconscious will actually get reimbursed at a very low level that will put the hospital ultimately out of business, potentially or at a higher level.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans, there is no change, no change in the number of undocumented immigrants who get any help under what the Democrats want.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator --\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Look, what we`re looking for is help for people who are going to get pushed out of nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator --\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): -- help for people who are at home and relying on home health aides. That`s what this fight is about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Senator, I appreciate your expansion on that issue, and I appreciate the conversation. I hope a compromise is reached soon.\par \par \uc2 Senator Elizabeth Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In our next hour, House Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  will join us to talk all about the shutdown, some of the same topics we just covered right now.\par \par \uc2 It`s 7:45, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Star-studded 8;00 hour ahead, just for you! Including actress, Sarah Jessica Parker. She`s just arrived, first on CBS MORNINGS. Did you know she also publishes books? I didn`t know that. She`ll talk about her latest title and the end of her show, "And Just Like That."\par \par \uc2 Plus, the star of the new film, it is called "Regretting You," the lovely McKenna Grace is