n`t do it.\par \par \uc2 JOE MILTON (\uc2 NFL\uc2  player): I would if you want me to.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOE MILTON: All right, set!\par \par \uc2 MAN: Like aim for my chest not my face.\par \par \uc2 JOE MILTON: I gotcha!\par \par \uc2 Should I do it for real?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh! I think some broken fingers there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Came in hot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like how he turned to his friend, he is like, "Should I do it?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Should I do it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the guy is like, "Yes!"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like how he goes --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jammed him!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- I will, if you want me to. He sounded so confident. I will, if you want me too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So funny.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So good! So good!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, you would have done that, but except you would have made the --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I would have soft tossed the throw to him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Would you really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If the fan is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is a quarterback, be careful what you ask.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. That`s right!\par \par \uc2 All right real quick before we get out of here, the queen of Halloween, Heidi Klum, is offering a sneak peek of this year`s costume. The supermodel shared these images on social media.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh boy!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Writing, "This is just the beginning." Of course, Klum is famous for outrageous Halloween get ups. We`ve got the worm. Remember the worm?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s crazy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The worm is wide.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are we thinking, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wild.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s so creepy!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, what do we think that is?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is no telling.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It looks like a "Terminator" or a vision from --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, we have actor, McKenna Grace who will be here to talk about her new movie, "Regretting You."\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, Sarah Jessica Parker will be here to tell us about being a book publisher as well as an actor and the latest book in her lineup. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil. That`s Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Coming right up, first on CBS MORNINGS, actor, Sarah Jessica Parker is here today. But did you know this? She also publishes books. Did not know that she`s here with the author of her latest title.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve also got actor, McKenna Grace in the building, star of the powerful new movie, "Regretting You" about a devastating accident and family secrets.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Adriana Diaz learns the moves behind the hit Broadway show, "Buena Vista Social Club." The show`s choreographers have a fascinating backstory.\par \par \uc2 All right, we begin this hour with a couple of questions, if a tree falls in a forest and no one is around, does it make a sound? How about this one? When a driverless car breaks a traffic law? Who gets the ticket?\par \par \uc2 Police near San Francisco had to deal with that when they say a self- driving \uc2 Waymo\uc2  vehicle made an illegal U-turn. We are seeing more and more driverless cars on the roads these days, and Kris Van Cleave found there is still room for improvement.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 Waymo\uc2  just drove around the school bus.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Cellphone video catches an autonomous \uc2 Waymo\uc2  passing a school bus stopped with its red lights flashing in the Atlanta area Monday, a move that could land a human driver there a $1,000.00 ticket.\par \par \uc2 It`s the latest in a series of \uc2 Waymo\uc2  incidents that have gone viral. Over the weekend, San Bruno, California Police pulled over one after it made an illegal U-turn.\par \par \uc2 Right now, California Police would typically cite a driver, not a vehicle, and they don`t necessarily have a mechanism to cite a car that has no driver, but the state is changing the law. Next year, these driverless cars will be able to get tickets, just like people.\par \par \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , owned by \uc2 Google\uc2 `s parent, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  offers driverless rides in five cities with plans to expand.\par \par \uc2 So we`re getting on the freeway.\par \par \uc2 Last year, we got an exclusive look as \uc2 Waymo\uc2  was testing on Arizona freeways.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s really about never making a mistake, driving defensively and predicting things that can go wrong.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Is that the bar? Never making a mistake.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So the bar is to make as few mistakes as possible.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The company argues its own safety data shows 91 percent fewer serious injury crashes and 92 percent fewer crashes injuring a pedestrian compared to a human driver, but acknowledges the vehicles are still learning, saying: "We continuously refine our system`s performance to navigate complex scenarios."\par \par \uc2 Are the self-driving cars being held to a higher standard?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT\uc2 : I think they need to be held to a higher standard.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  chair \uc2 Robert Sumwalt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT\uc2 : Before we have widespread use of self-driving vehicles, we`ve got to make sure that they are implemented properly.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Waymo\uc2  says it`s looking into the Atlanta incident, and if its vehicle is ticketed, they will address it as any driver would.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come July in California, there will be a process where companies of driverless vehicles will be notified of the traffic violation. It will be up to the DMV to issue any kind of notice or penalties.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, we`ll talk to House Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  to see if he thinks there is a deal in sight to end the government shutdown. But first 8:03, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming right up, Sarah Jessica Parker will be here in the studio. She`ll tell us about the new book she`s publishing. Can I say, she has great taste and why she fell in love with this one.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re in day two of a government shutdown, and President Trump is threatening mass layoffs of federal workers.\par \par \uc2 For more on all this, Republican House Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2 , who`s been blaming Democrats for all of it, joins us now to expand on those thoughts and more.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Speaker, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 My first question is an question is an easy one, is any negotiation going on right now on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): There is no negotiation right now, because the Democrats have dug in. You say, I`m blaming the Democrats. These are objective facts.\par \par \uc2 We passed in the house more than two weeks ago, a clean Continuing Resolution. There`s no partisan bills in this. There`s no Republican priorities. It`s just an extension of current funding, Tony, so that we can keep the government open until November 21st to allow the appropriators in both parties to continue their good work.\par \par \uc2 They`ve been working very hard, in a bipartisan fashion to move the 12 appropriations bills. We just ran out of time because the end of the fiscal year is September 30th.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Mr. Speaker --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): All are asking --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They clearly --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): Wait a minute, this is very important. All we`re asking is that Senate Democrats do what they have always done in the past, every single time and allow the additional clock. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mr. Speaker, they clearly think they have leverage right now, which is why they`re doing this. There`s also things that the Trump administration and Republicans think they can get in a shutdown. One thing is shrinking the size of government, some layoffs. I know you think government is too big. I know you think it does too much and does too much of it poorly.\par \par \uc2 You know about the layoffs that are planned? You`ve got word of them. What departments? How many people?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): I don`t know. I don`t know those details yet. We did have a long conference call yesterday with \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , who directs the Office of Management and Budget, but what was communicated to us is that they are trying is that they are trying to mitigate the damage.\par \par \uc2 The President and Republicans in Congress do not want a government shutdown. We are bending over backwards to prevent it, because real people get hurt.\par \par \uc2 The Senate Democrats, 44 of them, have now voted three times to shut the government down. And here`s what`s going to happen, Tony, real Americans are going to suffer from this. You have veterans benefits, food nutrition programs for women, infants and children that are being stalled.\par \par \uc2 You have health services that will not be provided. We have troops, TSA agents and Border Patrol agents now going to work without pay. There`s real damage. FEMA services are being shut off in the middle of a hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is leading the Democrats off a cliff, and it`s very simple, everyone now knows why. He is trying to fend off a challenge from his far left and his party for his next Senate race. That`s why he`s done it.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  voted for this exact product in March of this year --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mr. Speaker --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): -- and every year that he`s been in Congress, which is more than half my life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mr. Speaker, I`m curious about the implications of there not being a deal. The subsidies that support the Affordable Care Act, also known as Obamacare, used by more than 20 million people, they`ve come to depend on them. If those subsidies go away, health care premiums will spike. Some people will choose not to go on insurance. Bernie Sanders is arguing some people are going to die as a result.\par \par \uc2 Are Republicans trying to topple, or effectively topple Obamacare without outright saying so?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): No, this is an absolute 100 percent red herring. Everyone in Congress knows, and the Democrats know as well, even the ones making this crazy argument that that is a provision that does not expire until the end of December -- December 31. We are in the beginning of October.\par \par \uc2 They`re trying to make a December policy issue part --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): Wait a minute, the argument for a stop gap funding measure in Congress, that is not the issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Speaker people at home, have to make decisions now about what they`re going to be doing next year. They`re looking at how much money they have, how much something might cost, and they`re deciding now.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): Tony, we have three months for Congress to do its deliberate work. All we`re saying is, while that work is done on these and other issues, keep the lights on for the American people.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats have made a wild series of counter demands. What they`re demanding is to spend $1.5 trillion additional dollars in for a seven-week stop gap funding measure. We can`t do that. They want to give health care paid for by taxpayers, hardworking Americans to illegal aliens. We are not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaker, I did ask --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): That they want to give a half a billion dollars back for left leaning media organizations paid for by taxpayers. We`re not doing that. They know that. They`re playing a political game here, and it`s very dangerous for the people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaker, I hear you on that.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): It`s quite sad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hear you on that, and I did ask Senator Warren about some of those subjects in the last hour.\par \par \uc2 I want to get you on the record on this A.I. sombrero video thing with Leader Jeffries. Everyone is talking about it. He says that the video and the images are racist and bigoted. You`ve got to work with this individual. Do you see his point of view here? Can you see how he might feel that way?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): Hakeem is my friend. We have a great relationship of trust. He`s a great colleague.\par \par \uc2 I think he has to lighten up. When you show that you`re upset. I get attacked in memes every single hour of every day. I don`t react to it. That`s what people are trying to get out of you is a reaction.\par \par \uc2 The President was making a joke, okay, and so much has been made out of this. They`re acting as if the issue of the day is a battle over memes on social media. The issue is to get services returned to American people, young mothers who are dependent upon that nutrition assistance right now, veterans who need their services.\par \par \uc2 We have suicide prevention programs that are being halted right now. This is a serious business. We should not talking about sombreros on the internet. We should be talking about the American people`s needs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is serious business. We`ve given it extensive coverage today, and we`ll continue covering it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 One additional topic I do want to get your answer on is Representative Grijalva, who won a special election last week in Arizona. She`s yet to be sworn in. I know you`re not in session, but you`ve sworn in Republicans out of session. Why not her? Does it have anything to do with Jeffrey Epstein?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): Well, the House is not on the floor doing business this week, but we will do it immediately, early next week, as soon as everyone returns to town. We have to have everybody here, and we`ll swear her in. I congratulate her on her win. She is replacing her father, who had a long history here, and she`ll be a productive member of Congress. We look forward to that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Speaker Johnson, I really appreciate your time as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE JOHNSON\uc2  (R-LA): Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle, what have we got next?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do we have next? Sarah Jessica and Victoria, I always say we meet the nicest people in our greenroom. And guess what, Tony? That hasn`t changed. We`re talking about Redel`s brand new novel. Pull up that novel, please, author -- published by this one, Sarah Jessica Parker has an imprint. Those of you who are in the book world, you know this.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about the book -- may I just say, Sarah Jessica, you have great taste, great taste in books.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER (Actor and publisher): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- and shoes. We will discuss.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: And great good fortune in having this author.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We always appreciate that, and thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "And Just Like That"/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talk about life imitating art, just like Carrie Bradshaw fulfilling her dream of becoming an author, actor, Sarah Jessica Parker is making her mark in the world of books and how.\par \par \uc2 The actor is the founder of SJP LIT -- I love that -- which is known for publishing novels with a big heart. Her latest book, her latest pick is "I Am You," it`s by author Victoria Redel. She certainly knows how to turn a phrase. It takes place in 1600s Amsterdam as two female artists navigate their careers and their secret romance. Oh-la-la!\par \par \uc2 Sarah Jessica Parker, we`re happy to say Victoria Redel join us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, girls, it`s good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL ("I Am You" author): Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I believe, Sarah Jessica Parker, you can`t have a good conversation without wearing good shoes. So Kelly, can you please get a shot?\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Oh, Gayle!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I worse these today. These are my SJP -- Nate, do you --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They look --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is our fashionista.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like them, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is no less than the third time she`s taking her shoe off today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve shown everyone around the country. I love me some Sarah Jessica Parker shoes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: And I love all the versions of blue, top to bottom that goes with our delft blue on our cover --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: -- of this magnificent, surprising, extraordinary novel.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know let`s --\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: By this supremely talented author.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, congratulations.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you said you instantly fell in love with this book and decided to publish it right away.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Well, if she would have us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Yes, we had to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you had to woo her. A lot of people wanted her.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: We had to, you know, meet with her, as did many other publishing houses, and throw ourselves in the -- you know, at the mercy of the court, and make a case for the ways in which we would support and love and make sure this book was in many -- as many readers hands as possible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But why did it capture you? Because number one, you`re a voracious reader.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: I think it is the same reasons --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes --\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: I am a voracious reader, and I think for the same reasons, it captured you for, I think, first of all, time and place, which is interesting. There`s not a lot of books about this particular period. The fact that these two women, the subjects of this of this extraordinary story, are, in fact, real people that very little is known about.\par \par \uc2 And you have an author who`s just incredibly skillful at putting -- to taking fact and history and making this surprising, sensuous, atmospheric, dramatic, wholly unexpected story come to life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the thing, because, you know, I heard 1600 -- based in the 1600s, I`m like, I don`t know. It is about a female artist. I don`t know. And then I started reading it, Victoria. And I went, wow, wow, wow. So just briefly tell us the story, give us the backstory, because it`s so good.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Based on a true person --\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: It`s based on a true person. It`s based on Maria van Oosterwijck and Gerta Pieters.\par \par \uc2 Maria van Oosterwijck was a painter who was recognized in her time. Her work was everywhere, all over Europe, although she was never allowed into the Amsterdam guild of painters. No woman was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: And into her life comes a family servant, and she takes that family servant and makes her first her paint preparer and then her assistant, and then they begin to develop an increasingly close and complicated, and -- a relationship that keeps turning with relationships, power and love and devotion.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And complicated is the word, and it`s interesting, because at some point the student surpasses the master, which always adds a very interesting dynamic to the story.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Hence the title.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: "I Am You".\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: But I think also, you know what I think is so special about the book is that, yes, it takes place in 1600s and I`m almost inclined to not say that, because the book is in so many ways and the theme, so contemporary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Contemporary.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: And it feels so fortunately, topical in a way, there is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Very relatable.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Right? There`s so much about ambition and power and seduction and devotion, what that does to the human being, and also just commerce and art and the ways in which people move in the world, when there is prestige and possible gains and deception.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: And a very serious plot twist.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And transformation, which is something if we`re lucky enough, we will go through once or twice or multiple times in our life. In fact, Gerta says "Beauty is a transformation, often a messy one at that." What is the significance of those words?\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: Well, it works both ways in the novel, right? Because, firstly, it`s talking about paint and just how it`s made and what it`s made of, which is an astonishing and strange part of the book, or what I learned.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: But it`s also about what happens to a person as they begin to develop. It`s a story of two women artists, and one of them is really going from maid to painter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: And what happens when you step into your own authority, your own sense of self? And that sounds like a beautiful thing, and it is a beautiful thing --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it is messy.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: -- but it is also a mess.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just like painting.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can we talk about "And Just Like That" for a second.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is still in mourning about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Actually, I have -- go on, go on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, you need a hug?\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: What`s harder to talk about government shutdown or the end of this?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am a completist, and I`ve been watching all the reruns on HBO. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Oh my God!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I find it to be so transporting. So --\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Thank you. Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is so good!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But were ready for the end? Clearly, a lot of thought went into it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: It was a very --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wasn`t ready for Carrie Bradshaw to leave us yet.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: I mean, I`m not certain I understand what that decision means, actually, because I could just be on hiatus, you know. But it was a difficult decision. Michael and I talked a lot about it, and I think it`s out of respect for this really singular professional experience that you have to consider what you want to do, have you done it, and sometimes it`s best to gracefully walk away when things feel really right and sort of energetic versus squeezing and exploiting people`s generosity and hospitality.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also walking away when people are saying, don`t go.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Yes, yes. That is always something that we try to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you find yourself into publishing and reading and the thrill of reading.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: The thrill of reading.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations on that.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. That`s good, Tony.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The thrill of reading, Sarah, Jessica Parker, Victoria Redel, we thank you. "I Am You" is on sale wherever you like to buy books.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA REDEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Buena Vista Social Club")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 That`s from the hit Broadway musical, "Buena Vista Social Club." It`s getting rave reviews here in this city. You might remember the legendary album by the same name, so it`s based on a real Cuban Members Only Club. It opened in the 1930s and they say was a hot spot for Afro Cuban music and dance.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus co-anchor, Adriana Diaz, she knows how to move on the dance floor. Find out how the show`s irresistible dancing has a story all of its own.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 So the Buena Vista Social Club earned five Tony honors this year, and its co-choreographers have a special connection to the music and to each other.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: In Times Square, people are lining up for a portal to Havana, Cuba to a Broadway Dance party that`s contagious.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Buena Vista Social Club")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This is the "Buena Vista Social Club," combining elements of Afro Cuban, Latin, ballet and modern dance, a recipe created by husband and wife choreographers, Justin Peck and Patricia Delgado.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN PECK (Choreographer): And then there`s this mambo step where I lean out, and then we move into this kind of like early salsa idea.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Moves they created in the studio and at home.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN PECK: You never know when an idea is going to strike, and you have to just like dance in the living room.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Peck now, the resident choreographer at New York City Ballet met Delgado when he was a visiting artist at Miami City Ballet, where the Delgado was principal dancer, but out with an injury, so she had time to show him around town.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DELGADO (Choreographer): I always look back, I`m like, that was the best injury of my life. Years later, I feel like, still, I can feel like the butterflies of meeting him from that day.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I can see the butterflies, just looking at you look at him. It`s just the sweetest thing.\par \par \uc2 They each grew up listening to this music, Delgado in a Cuban household in Miami; Peck in California in a family that loved Latin music. Even their first wedding dance was to a "Buena Vista Social Club" song, which now feels like destiny.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN PECK: I joke that that was like the first workshop for the "Buena Vista Social Club" musical.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Buena Vista Social Club")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The musical is a fictional retelling of this unlikely 1996 recording session with all but forgotten Cuban legends. The Broadway take won multiple Tonys, including Best Choreography.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DELGADO: As a Cuban American daughter of immigrants, I would like to thank my entire family for your sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: When your parents saw the show, what was their reaction?\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DELGADO: They can`t watch the show without crying from like the first few notes, and especially my dad and my mom said to me after the opening night, you know, I haven`t been back to Cuba, but you brought Cuba to me, and I, like felt like a gift I could give back to them for the life that they allowed me to have. So --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Buena Vista Social Club")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What is it about the music that connects with so many people? I mean, those opening chords in "Chan Chan" are haunting.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DELGADO: Gosh! It`s like, to me too, it`s just still a mystery. That`s the intrigue of it all. It`s like, how can you describe why people from all over the world feel this music like a lot of what is so powerful is the idea that when they made this album, it was a second chance for them. And I think that is such a like universal idea that you never know when the opportunity is going to come.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But for the show to feel authentically Cuban, they had to go there. Their guide was associate choreographer, Carlos Gonzalez, who showed me the stage and indulged me with a dance.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS GONZALEZ (Associate choreographer): And then you come down the right. Keep going. And then I will come this way.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What was it that you wanted Justin and Patricia to see in Havana?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS GONZALEZ: I mean, to feel, you know, the sweat of it all, and why the movement sometimes it`s not that clean. It`s not about how precise or exact it is, about the heaviness of the island.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: born in Cuba, he left everything he knew at age 11, when his mother won the U.S. visa lottery.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS GONZALEZ: And it was a cultural shock. First of all, we went to Lansing, Michigan when I was 11. I had never seen snow, never heard the language. It was just my mother, my father and I.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The show about love, loss and second chances speaks to Cuban Americans like Gonzalez and speaks to tourists who`ve never heard of the "Buena Vista Social Club" but connect with its message.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA DELGADO: It`s hard to use the word proud, because I feel like, "orgullo" is the better word, "pride" in Spanish, right? Orgullo -- I feel like when I leave the show, and I`ve heard it from so many people, at the end, you feel uplifted, and you feel a sense of joy that is unique, and I hope it just continues to uplift people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s hard to stay seated and not dance watching that piece. It was just so incredible, and the dance is so important in particular to this show because it`s the first ever on Broadway where the songs are sung in Spanish.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Buena Vista Social Club")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So the dancing helps do some of the storytelling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who doesn`t want to feel lifted when you leave the theater? I haven`t seen it yet. It`s been on my list.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really want to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Every time I see a story like that, I think I`ve got to go. I`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I will.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: On \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus, we`re going to have a behind-the-scenes tour by Carlos Gonzalez, the associate choreographer, but they were all wonderful. Patricia and Justin were incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Power of imagination bringing Cuba back to life.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For parents who haven`t been able to go back --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s such a magical thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But also for tourists that know nothing about Cuba perhaps. I mean, the audience is so diverse, and you have a lot of people from all over the country coming to see the show, who may not speak Spanish, may not know how to dance salsa, but still really appreciate it and love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am sucker for a good love story.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Were you dancing for us, or was that an audition?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh, so the end, he was like, she booked it. She booked it. She`s in the show. And I was like, well, if we must.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Adriana, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, if we must.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. Courtney Cason is here with an alternative to a traditional hammer designed for everyday jobs. You hear it and you see it. It is easier to hold. Check it out 