You can split it up and gift them, 56 percent off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Winter is coming. A nice warm mug outside --\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY CASON: It is coming. Maybe a little Hotty Toddy on Game Day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- while it is chilly! Oh, I like the way you think. That is the second time you brought us some drinks.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY CASON: Sure! How about it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Courtney Cason. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything that you saw, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com. We make it easy for you. Stick around. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is actor, McKenna Grace. I love her name. She is fantastic. She`s starring in the new \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2  film. It`s called "Regretting You," so it`s based on the best-selling novel of the same name, Colleen Hoover strikes again -- it follows the Grant family as they deal with the aftermath of a devastating accident -- devastating is the word.\par \par \uc2 McKenna Grace plays Clara Grant. In this clip, Clara`s mother, that`s Allison Williams -- also knock out here -- catches her with a guy from school who, let`s just say, parents do not like\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Regretting You"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s one of those scenes where, if you`re a teenager, you`re just like, strike me dead now, please, please.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Take me now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take me now, Jesus.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, McKenna Grace joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome and congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE ("Regretting You" actor): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did love that scene. I love you and Allison together as mother and daughter. We`ll get to that in just a second.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But okay, the notes say this about you, McKenna Grace, that this movie came about or you had a big influence, because you got a call from an Oscar winning actor. Who is that who called you?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: I did, Miss Octavia Spencer. I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you guys know each other?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Yes, yes, ma`am, she and I worked together whenever I was like, nine on a film called "Gifted." And I just like, I don`t know, all of her advice has always been very, very important to me. And she called me up and was like, hey, like, you`ve got to do this. I worked with Brunson Green, our producer, on "The Help" and he`s such a dream. And I was like, I already wanted to do this project because I was in love with the script, but just getting that call and hearing how lovely Brunson and the whole team was, was like, "I`ve got to."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s nice to be validated when you think I really like it, and then somebody like Octavia calls, who you like and respect.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think, okay, there was no doubt anyway, but it`s -- you have said that this was one of your favorite shoots to date.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How come?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: It was such a blast. I feel like I`m still such a family with this whole cast.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Regretting You"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: I mean, I was taking pictures in the greenroom of me, like, messing with whatever is on Dave`s face to send to a group chat with him and Mason, like I just had such a blast with everybody involved and the entire crew and cast felt like such a family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What did you love about the story itself?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: For me, coming off of being -- it is so weird to be like a former child actor, now I am in high school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We remember you from "Young Sheldon."\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Yes, oh brother! It is like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look!\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Oh my gosh, yes, little me! It`s so weird.\par \par \uc2 For me it was the first time getting to step into a bit more of -- like I`ve done very mature roles and very serious things, but it was my first time playing more of my age and stepping into a role in a romance movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: So that was such an exciting opportunity for me. And there`s also a lot of romance, but so much grit and intensity and sadness --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So much.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: -- to these characters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very layered.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: So it felt like just such an exciting opportunity and I truly loved the story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You and co-star, Allison Williams were co-producers on this project. What did you learn? What was it like being an executive producer, and what was it like working with her?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Well, it`s -- working with Allison, first off was like, so lovely. I adore her. And getting to work with her as a producer as well was very lovely for me.\par \par \uc2 The last week I just spent sitting in the greenroom, talking and talking and talking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And learning, huh?\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Yes, absolutely learning from her. But I mean, Dave, as well, is also a producer on this, so I just got to sit and talk with them all the time about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: But it`s not my first time being producer, but it`s my first time being a producer as an adult in the industry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: So I kind of got to step a bit more into that role behind- the-scenes with, like, all the cuts of the film and, like, my notes, really holding weight.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s nice being an adult in the industry.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Yes. It`s really cool. It`s different.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You had great chemistry with Mason Thames. I love the relationship that you all had, that you built. And then we should say that this is a movie about secrets. Big, big secrets.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t want to give anything away.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Lots of secrets in this one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: McKenna Grace, thank you so much. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are awesome. It`s good to see you as an adult.\par \par \uc2 MCKENNA GRACE: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Regretting You" opens in theaters October 24th.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You wear it well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is distributed by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , which is owned by our parent company.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are proud to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020251002ela200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Sniper Had Anti-ICE Message Written on Bullets; Charges Against Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2 ; Tracking Possible Storm Threats in the Tropics; Rescue Efforts Underway After Typhoon in Asia; Family Sues Over Plane-Helicopter Collision in D.C.; White House: Government Shutdown Could Lead to Job Cuts; \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  on Housing Market and Mortgage Rate; Jimmy Kimmel`s Return Show Lands Big Ratings; Apple T.V.+ Halts Release of "The Savant;" New Paramount+ Documentary "Bodyguard of Lies;" How \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is Using Robots for Holiday Retail Rush; Trump to Sign Order Allowing TikTok Deal; Comedian Kevin Fredericks on Failure and Success; Richard Grenell on Kennedy Center Honors Changes; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Billy Crudup: From the State to "The Morning Show;" \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Omar Villafranca,  Scott MacFarlane, Rob Marciano, Anna Coren, Kris Van Cleave, Ed O`Keefe,  Jill Schlesinger, Carter Evans, Kelly O`Grady, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 14,798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Thursday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Nate is on the West Coast. We`re going to show you what he was up to a little later.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s he doing?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, it`s something good!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot of news today, including new details of that attack on an ICE facility, and we`re tracking growing storm threats in the Atlantic. Lots going on, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say a targeted attack on an immigration facility in Dallas killed one detainee and wounded two others.\par \par \uc2 TODD LYONS (Acting ICE director): There was no way he could have known detainees were in that vehicle. He was just indiscriminately shooting, hoping it caused carnage and mayhem.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Prosecutors face a deadline to file criminal charges against former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE (Former Deputy Assistant Attorney General): Trump has a very long standing grievance against \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A growing hurricane threat in the Atlantic, while a super typhoon smashes into China, causing widespread damage.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: In Bangkok, Thailand, a massive sinkhole has opened up near a hospital and police station. It`s 160 feet deep.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: A tourist on a cruise ship hoped to ride a slide down to the deck pool, but ended up not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: You can see the woman trying to wiggle her way to safety inside the clear tube.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): Oh, man, I would be so embarrassed if people found out I was on a cruise.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): You know, a lot of people watched our show last night. I got so many texts from so many people. It made me realize how many of my friends are never watching the show at any other time.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): He got huge ratings. It did so well that the \uc2 Disney\uc2  just announced, next summer, they`re releasing "Guillermo & Stitch" live action. Live action.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I could see it happening, but the ratings were amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Six-and-a-half million.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Six million viewers, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , guys, it was close to over 19.5 million.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lot of people wanted to hear what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we are going to be talking about it. We are going to be talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have some thoughts on it.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with new information about the suspect in a deadly shooting at an Immigration and Customs Enforcement Office, this one in Dallas. Officials say that the gunman targeted the facility yesterday using ammunition that had anti-ICE messages written on it.\par \par \uc2 The gunfire hit three people who had been detained, killing one of them and critically wounding the others. The suspect was later found dead. Officials are still trying to learn why he did all of this.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is at the scene and joins us with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning to you. What can you tell us this morning?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This small facility behind me is an ICE field office. It`s not one of those big detention centers we`re used to seeing, but it is plenty busy. Detainees come here to be processed. Other immigrants come here for the regular ICE check-ins, and agents are in here day in and day out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE ROTHROCK (Special Agent in Charge, FBI Dallas): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating this incident as an act of targeted violence.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Department of Homeland Security officials say a sniper perched himself on a rooftop and opened fire on an ICE field office in Dallas early Wednesday morning.\par \par \uc2 This woman captured the shots while on a video called nearby.\par \par \uc2 Officials say the gunman shot three detainees in a van just outside the building, killing one. No ICE agents were injured.\par \par \uc2 Acting ICE Director Todd Lyons.\par \par \uc2 TODD LYONS: There was no way he could have known detainees were in that vehicle. He was just indiscriminately shooting, hoping it caused carnage and mayhem and it was at an ICE facility.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Lyons identified the shooter who was found dead from a self-inflicted gunshot wound as 29-year-old Joshua Jahn. Court records in Texas show he pleaded guilty in 2016 on a narcotics charge involving marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement was seen scouring homes in North Texas and Oklahoma linked to the gunman, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  pointed to a possible motive.\par \par \uc2 JOE ROTHROCK: Early evidence from rounds that were found near the suspected shooter contained messages that are anti-ICE in nature.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: As the Trump administration has doubled down on immigration enforcement, DHS says attacks against ICE agents have increased by around 1,000 percent since the President took office, including a bomb threat just last month at this same facility.\par \par \uc2 This violence may also have a chilling effect on immigrants who are pursuing legal status.\par \par \uc2 What does this do to the migrant community in this area?\par \par \uc2 ERIC CEDILLO (LULAC National Legal Advisor): Very scared. I know a number of immigrants are going to be a lot more hesitant to make these check-ins, to go down to the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Lyons says his concern is for everyone`s safety.\par \par \uc2 TODD LYONS: One person being killed is one too many. We can`t have this going on at, you know, ICE facilities.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: We don`t know the victims` names yet, but the Mexican consulate said one of the victims that`s in critical condition is a Mexican citizen. And in a social media post, DHS Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  said that they plan to increase security at detention centers and the processing facilities like this one here behind me, we have noticed that presence significantly this morning -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Omar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In other news, we`re waiting to hear about possible criminal charges against former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2 . Federal prosecutors in Virginia have only a few days left now to decide if they`ll comply with a demand from President Trump to indict Comey.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more now from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Tony. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 President Trump celebrated firing \uc2 James Comey\uc2  during his first term, but now the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  director who opened the investigation into Russia`s meddling in the 2016 election could be Trump`s first high profile rival to be prosecuted since he returned to The White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Multiple sources familiar with the matter tell CBS News, prosecutors are considering bringing a case against \uc2 James Comey\uc2  before a grand jury.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): He was a terrible person, did terrible things.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The decision rests with Lindsey Halligan, a former Trump White House aide and confidant, who was named the interim top prosecutor in the Eastern District of Virginia, Monday, after Trump said he ousted the prior prosecutor who did not press forward with the case against Comey.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, Trump posted suggesting Comey and two other critics of the President, New York Attorney General Letitia James and California Senator Adam Schiff, should be criminally charged, saying that justice must be served now.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We have to act fast. If they`re not guilty, that`s fine. If they are guilty, or if they should be judged, they should be judged, and we have to do it now.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: \uc2 Tom Dupree\uc2  is a former Department of Justice official.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE: What is different about this case is that there clearly has been significant presidential pressure brought to bear, and you don`t want a universe in which that pressure distorts the prosecutorial judgment.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump allies have accused Comey of lying to Congress five years ago this month, when his testimony conflicted with other witnesses.\par \par \uc2 JAMES COMEY\uc2  (Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): I stand by what -- the testimony you summarized that I gave in May of 2017.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Multiple reports say charges against Comey could include a count of perjury, an allegation he`s denied.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The key here is the statute of limitations to bring charges related to that testimony runs out Tuesday. CBS News reached out to the U.S. Attorney in Virginia and an attorney for Comey, and neither has responded. If Halligan proceeds with the charges, this would be the first case she ever prosecutes in her career -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re watching multiple storms gaining strength in the Atlantic, and one of them could pose a threat to the United States.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 It has been quiet, thankfully, this September, but the Atlantic is certainly getting more active. We`ll get to what`s happening here.\par \par \uc2 First of all, Gabrielle, something rare that we haven`t seen since 2019, a hurricane warning up for the Azores. This is going to continue to head that way and towards parts of Spain as we go through the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 But our bigger problems for us here in the states are these areas of concern. This guy right here has got a pretty good -- a decent area of seeing some action, and then we also have Umberto, which will be reacting along this Bermuda high, likely becoming at least a Category 1-2, maybe a 3 as we go through several days.\par \par \uc2 But this one right, just north of Puerto Rico, that`s the one we`re really watching closely. What it does over the next several days, we`ll have to see. We`ve got an area of low pressure here. We`ve got this high pressure here. We`ve got Umberto that might interact with us.\par \par \uc2 And you see these squiggly lines, they`re all our computer models, and about half of them take it into the Carolinas, but half of them take it out to sea. What this does over the next few days, we`ll have to see. We`ll have greater detail on all of this tomorrow, but if you live in the Carolinas, hopefully by now, you certainly have your preparation kit ready to go, and maybe you have to do something with that as we head towards Sunday, Monday, Tuesday of next week.\par \par \uc2 Hot and humid or warmer humid across the East Coast with intermittent showers, kind of a messy day across the northeast. I don`t have to tell you that, if you`re waking up to it. This clears out a little bit as we head towards tomorrow, but another round of moisture coming into the southeast that may play a role in what these potential hurricanes are going to do -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Rob, I know you`ll be watching, and thank you.\par \par \uc2 Typhoon Ragasa, one of the strongest storms to hit Asia in years, is continuing to hammer Southern China, but it has lost its super typhoon status. The storm killed more than two dozen people in Taiwan and the Philippines. It`s now heading towards Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Anna Coren reports from hard hit Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Through rising flood waters, rescuers carried residents out of their buildings to higher ground as Super Typhoon Ragasa continued its path of destruction, hitting Guangdong Province in southern China.\par \par \uc2 Despite losing intensity as it made landfall, it still brought severe flooding and widespread damage. Earlier in the day, it pummeled Hong Kong, bringing hurricane force winds, torrential rain and a storm surge that showed the power of Mother Nature.\par \par \uc2 The owner of this restaurant thought he`d taken necessary precautions.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The pressure of the wave that came right over the wall and directly into the restaurant, and all the restaurants were affected exactly the same. There`s not much you could do.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: In Taiwan, where 14 people were killed when a dam burst and a bridge collapsed, new footage shows the full extent of the devastation.\par \par \uc2 For residents in the region, the cleanup continues before more bad weather hits.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Anna Coren in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now to the first of what is expected to be many wrongful death lawsuits after that deadly mid-air collision over the nation`s capital, this was back in January.\par \par \uc2 The widow of one of the 60 passengers on board an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  regional jet is now suing.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has been following this story from the very beginning and has the latest details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The wrongful death suit alleges \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , the \uc2 FAA\uc2  and \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  "utterly failed in their responsibilities to the traveling public."\par \par \uc2 Casey Crafton died in the January mid-air collision. His wife is now suing. His brother, Dailey spoke Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 DAILEY CRAFTON (Casey Crafton`s brother): Casey was betrayed by the system he trusted, we all were.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Sixty-seven people died when an Army helicopter and an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  regional jet collided in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 BOB CLIFFORD (Clifford law firm lawyer): The crash of American Eagle 5342 was predictable, it was preventable.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: While the crash investigation is ongoing, the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  has found air traffic controllers at Reagan National Airport were doing multiple jobs that night. The Army helicopter was flying higher than it was supposed to, while several of the tower`s radio messages were not fully received by that crew and similar close calls had become common, but went unaddressed before the crash.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also claims \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  manipulated and abused the system at DCA to maximize arrivals at busy times of day.\par \par \uc2 DOUG LANE (Wife and son died in the crash): It was just a situation that was a ticking time bomb waiting to go off.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Doug Lane told reporters Wednesday he`s ready to go to court. Lane`s 16-year-old son, Spencer and his wife Christine were killed in the crash.\par \par \uc2 DOUG LANE: We went from a family of four to a family of two, kind of in a blink of an eye.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  tells CBS News "... it has a strong track record of putting safety of our customers and team members above everything else," adding "It will defend against claims the airline caused or contributed to this accident."\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice would ultimately defend the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  in court, saying "... it will pursue a just resolution of this matter that provides fair compensation to the victims of this tragedy." Many more lawsuits are expected to be filed.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS. I`m Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another year, another possible government shutdown, this one less than a week away. It could mean hundreds of thousands of government workers will be furloughed, and overnight, the Trump administration threatened to cut some of those furloughed jobs permanently if Democrats don`t make a deal.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is in Washington for us once again.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tony, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 This is a major escalation in the stakes over a possible government shutdown next week, because you next week, because usually, during a shutdown, so-called nonessential federal workers are furloughed and then paid back when it`s over. But in a memo obtained by CBS News, the Office of Management and Budget tells agencies to consider laying off or in government speak to issue reduction in force notices to employees and programs that have what`s known as discretionary funding that`s set to run out on October 1st, and are, "not consistent with the President`s priorities."\par \par \uc2 Democrats and Republicans are locked in a standoff as Tuesday`s deadline approaches to keep the government funded. The battle is currently in the Senate, where 60 votes are needed to pass a temporary spending plan, so it requires some Democratic support, and in exchange for their votes, Democrats want concessions like rolling back this year`s Medicaid cuts and extending health insurance tax credits. President Trump cancelled the meeting with Democratic Congressional leaders that was scheduled for today, calling their demands ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 Last night, those same Democratic leaders responded to the threat of layoffs, calling them an act of intimidation and telling the administration to "get lost" -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In this morning`s "Money Watch," there`s unexpected good news in the housing market. The latest government report says new home sales in August jumped 20 percent compared to July. That`s a bigger increase than expected, and also a three-year high. So let`s talk about this with CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dolla` Dolla Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s happening?\par \par \uc2 All right, so what`s behind this big increase?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know, all of these builders have been offering a lot of big incentives, so that`s helped. Mortgage rates have started to slide a little bit. But I also want to not be too much of a buzzkill here, but this is one month of data, and these can be revised, these numbers.\par \par \uc2 So we are happy to have the good news, but we really need to see what happens next, because new homes are only a small portion of the overall market, most are existing homes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What does this mean for affordability?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I mean, look, affordability is still tough because we have prices which are still high. The median prices of a home in the U.S. this summer, about $440,000.00. That`s the median, okay? And although mortgage rates have come down, they`re still high. We`re not at three percent, we are at, you know, six-and-a-quarter percent, and then you add in homeowners insurance, and you add in everything else that comes with a house, it is still quite expensive to carry a home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So if it is a good time for -- if you`re buying a home, what does it mean if you`re trying to sell a home?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think what`s going to really be interesting here is are sellers motivated? Now we talk about mortgage rates, okay. Mortgage rates were at almost eight percent two years ago, seven percent earlier this year. Now we`re at about six-and-a-quarter. We have a new survey from Bankrate that said five percent is sort of the magic number, because five percent is the mortgage rate for a 30-year loan, where more buyers could jump in and feel more affordable.\par \par \uc2 But with a seller, imagine you`re a seller, you`re busting at the seams, you`re a young family. You want to trade up, but you`ve got a three percent loan. At five percent, you might be much more willing to list your home.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s really smart for people to run the numbers, but also to be aware the market is really in flux right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What if you`re having trouble coming up with the down payment? I ask is, because when I tried to buy my first home in my early 20s, I went in because I thought I can afford the mortgage. I didn`t know the down payment part. And I thought, where am I supposed to get that from?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think that that is a huge --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it was 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes, it was a huge hurdle then, it`s a huge hurdle now, because prices are so much higher. So you and I grew up in a time where we said 20 percent down, that`s where we just had to be. Now it`s more like 10 percent down, but if you think about a down payment at 10 percent and you look at that median home, that`s still $44,000.00.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Now there is an interesting company that is tracking incentives. There are about 2,000 different kinds of incentives for home buyers to help with that down payment.\par \par \uc2 So the name of the company is Down Payment Resources, and if you look there, you might have a little bit of a help -- a boost. A lot of people turning to families.\par \par \uc2 By the way, if you`re getting a loan from your families, or you`re getting a gift, make sure you put it down on paper. We don`t want to have uncomfortable Thanksgiving and holiday meals. Loan versus gift, and also put it down on paper, so that your siblings don`t get a little torched.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So people don`t have selective amnesia.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it my turn?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s yours.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s your turn.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am like, there was no Gayle there.\par \par \uc2 Where`s Gayle? Thank you, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was rolled up. I couldn`t see it.\par \par \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel`s return to T.V. after his suspension turned out to be a very good thing for the ratings. The numbers are huge. More than six million people watched Tuesday night`s "Jimmy Kimmel Live," and another 26 million saw it on social media platforms. That`s huge numbers.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Kimmel talked about his return, and he was back to his usual form when it came to the political jokes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL: I did hear from one very special friend, moments after we taped our show last night, the Mad Red Hatter wrote: "I can`t believe ABC fake news gave Jimmy Kimmel his job back."\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL: You can`t believe they gave me my job back. I can`t believe we gave you your job back.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, despite those big numbers, the show is still blacked out in 23 percent of the U.S. households. Sinclair and Nexstar, owners of 71 ABC affiliates refused to air the show because of Kimmel`s comments about the man accused of killing Charlie Kirk, which guys, makes the numbers even more extraordinary. That`s a big chunk of the country that even didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. But the 26 million on social media, most of that money doesn`t go to ABC, and the business is still bad, particularly if you`re offending half the country, the ones that voted for the guy he doesn`t like. So, here we are. Good luck late night.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Apple T.V.+ delays a new drama starring Jessica Chastain after recent political violence. What she`s saying about all of that. But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, Billy Crudup has won two Emmys for the hit series "The Morning Show."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Morning Show"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He tells Anthony Mason how a challenging stage performance helped lead him to that role.\par \par \uc2 And my boy, Nate, goes home to Seattle to catch a Mariners game, and let`s just say there was a lot of pressure on him. We`re going to show you why. It`s pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Check out this surveillance video of a smash-and-grab jewelry store heist east of San Francisco involving more than two dozen people smashing display cases. They stole about a million dollars in merchandise in this incident alone. Seven people have been arrested. Police say the group may be tied to other robberies.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, that may be how people get their down payments.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I hope they catch everyone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not the right way. Not the right way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was going to say, I hope they can catch every one of them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, Apple T.V. and Jessica Chastain, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from XGCTUI/X)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow. Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this massive sinkhole that swallowed a roadway in Bangkok, Thailand yesterday. Another angle shows the moment a second cave in --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at hat car, Vlad on the left.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That took down