ath, they are deciding to, I guess, not fan the flames or try to step back and reassess.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: \uc2 Apple\uc2  hasn`t explained why, but Chastain, who is an executive producer on the series, says she`s "... not aligned on the decision to pause its release," citing a number of violent attacks aimed at both sides of the political spectrum since she began working on the show five years ago.\par \par \uc2 Chastain says, "... while I wish this show wasn`t so relevant, unfortunately it is. `The Savant` is about heroes who work every day to stop violence before it happens, and honoring their courage feels more urgent than ever."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Savant"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 ARAMIDE TINUBU: I think that`s what she`s trying to say, like this is not the time to run and hide or be scrambling or whatever the case may be. Because of all this kerfuffle, it`s probably going to get a much bigger audience than it may have initially.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Savant"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jessica Chastain is so good in everything she does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was really looking forward to it. I like her phrase, though, "not aligned."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Not aligned," yes. We are going to use that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to use that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can use that at home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you disagree -- "I`m not aligned on that decision."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Honey, pick up your socks. I`m not aligned actually on the sock pickup right at this moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I hope it comes back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, to another serious topic here.\par \par \uc2 A new documentary uncovers the hidden truths behind America`s 20-year war in Afghanistan. "Bodyguard of Lies" from See It Now Studios reveals how thousands of lives and trillions of dollars were lost due to failed war policies.\par \par \uc2 The film features testimony from government insiders, never before seen footage and confidential documents that expose the conflicting messages about the war over four different administrations.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bodyguard of Lies"/See It Now Studios/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Bodyguard of Lies" is streaming now on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, how robots are working behind-the-scenes at one of Amazon`s largest facilities and what the technology means for human jobs.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning marks exactly three months from Christmas, and companies are already planning for the holiday retail rush. Forty-three percent of Americans intend to start their holiday shopping before Halloween this year -- that`s their intention anyway, we`ll see how that goes -- according to one study.\par \par \uc2 "Money Watch" correspondent, Kelly O`Grady got a behind-the-scenes look at how \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is using robots to speed up deliveries, but will those robots lead to job cuts for humans?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: When you click "Buy" this holiday season, there`s a good chance a robot like this is helping to fulfill your order, and with online holiday sales expected to grow up to nine percent over last year, major retailers including \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , along with shipping couriers like UPS and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  are investing big in robotics.\par \par \uc2 So what does all of that automation mean for the holiday season?\par \par \uc2 We`re in Shreveport, Louisiana getting a behind-the-scenes tour of Amazon`s most advanced fulfillment center.\par \par \uc2 It`s called SHV1, and at more than three million square feet, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says it`s one of the company`s largest facilities.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER (Amazon`s Vice President of Robotics): We have a number of different types of new robotics that we`re using to fill customer orders.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Is that one of them?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER: Great example of one right here, a very noisy one right here.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Scott Dresser is Amazon`s Vice President of Robotics. He introduced us to that robot in Proteus, one of almost a thousand in SHV1.\par \par \uc2 So does this facility run 24/7?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER: So it can run 24 hours a day, and the robots we designed to run all day long, if they if they need to. Certainly, as we come into our peak holiday season, which we`re already getting ready for, that`s something we think about a lot, because we will have to run the building almost all day round.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: When a customer orders a product, the robots get to work. These ones move the item out of storage, then one of the 2,500 employees sorts it, while another packs it before it travels along some of the nearly 15 miles of conveyor belts to get labeled.\par \par \uc2 Meet Cardinal.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER: Cardinal is lifting packages that weigh up to 50 pounds, and then we can use Proteus to take the container over to the docks, so a lot of it is less about performance, more about making sure that our employees are safe.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The final step here, a human loads the packages onto a truck. Dresser says, SHV1 is 25 percent more efficient than other fulfillment centers.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER: If you`re ordering an order here in Shreveport right before Christmas and you need that by Christmas, robots are going to help make sure we get that product to you fast, safely and accurately.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: He says, while the robots are replacing some jobs, they`re also creating new ones.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER: Every time we`re able to grow and expand the network which robotics has enabled over the last decade, we`re able to add new jobs, like our robotics maintenance engineers, people that we need in the operation now that we have these new robotics to help us run the building.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: While Amazon`s revenue has grown, its number of employees has held steady, around one-and-a-half million since 2022.\par \par \uc2 You are a public company. You have a duty to your shareholders to maximize profit. At what point does that become more important than ensuring that there`s always a human element dealing with something like Proteus?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER: The thing that we think of most when we`re deploying a new fulfillment operation or a new process in a fulfillment operation is, how are people and robots best going to be able to do that together.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So man with machine versus man versus machine.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DRESSER: It is always man with machine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Now, he added that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is offering training for employees whose positions are being replaced and shared those more advanced jobs pay better. When we pressed him on if those jobs could ever be replaced, he said robots, they`re always breaking down. They need assistance, and that humans will be needed for those roles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did you feel being there?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It felt very advanced, but it was a little bit strange. You just have these robots moving around, but they have those googly eyes. They`re kind cute!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Uh-oh! Anthropomorphizing? Is that what they were?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We learned that from the guy yesterday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You saw a face on a robot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Humanizing, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Take those eyes off of there. We`ve got to remind ourselves, it`s a robot.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Good luck to those people planning to shop for Christmas before Halloween.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in "What to Watch" Rihanna has someone new under her umbrella. We`ve got all the details. Right now, it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some big things coming up for you in our 8:00 hour, including Anthony, as in Mason.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Morning Show"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s got a conversation with Emmy winner, Billy Crudup. How "The Morning Show" star got the role after telling Jennifer Aniston, listen to this -- he wanted to play the weird guy. I like that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, comedy star, Kevin Fredericks says failure is one of the secrets behind his success. He`ll be here. He`s got a new book which explains how losing big helped him win, and let`s just say Kevin Fredericks` is doing all right.\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour, but right now, Claus and I are heading over to the table because Vlad and Tony --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you know what I mean to hang the leg? Hang the leg if you`re a pitcher?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does it mean?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is referencing Nate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to be talking about Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are going to get a little tutorial.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to show you what that is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In just a moment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are going to show you next. Claus, you ready?\par \par \uc2 CLAUS STUHLWEISSENBURG: Yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a tease! See you later.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Got to start with some Rihanna. You know why?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why? Why? Why?\par \par \uc2 VLADI