ight? So here`s the triple sabotage part. The first one is the escalator. Then there was the teleprompter, right? The teleprompter did not work during the President`s address, and he joked that whoever is operating it was in "big trouble." Well, the \uc2 UN\uc2  had an explanation for that, too.\par \par \uc2 They tell CBS News that The White House was operating the teleprompter. In other words, they took their laptops, they plugged it into the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  system, and so whoever is working that prompter on that laptop. Busted!\par \par \uc2 And then the other thing was, the President said the audio wasn`t working. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says, yes, that`s because they`ve got to translate for everybody`s language. That`s why you didn`t hear it in the auditorium.\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt says the Secret Service is investigating.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, it is good to hear that it probably wasn`t something so nefarious.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That The White House photographer accidentally, because he wouldn`t have meant to do that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No! He was going up backwards.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He was going up backwards, trying to get the video and probably oops!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what I like is because I`ve been on an escalator when that happens, and it can be very dangerous, I like that the First Lady looked around and just kept right on walking. She didn`t miss a beat.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No. She never missed a beat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She just kept right on going.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s like, let`s keep going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whatever this is, I`m going.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m excited for Nate. Let`s talk about Mr. Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s talk about this.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 Seattle Mariners\uc2  welcomed a hometown hero to throw out the first pitch last night. Of course, we`re talking about former Seahawks wide receiver, Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There he is getting loose.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Getting loose. You`re right, Tony. He`s throwing some balls behind-the-scenes there. Actually, wait, wait, wait, wait, I think this needs some music to warm us up. \uc2 Cue\uc2  that music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, boy!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A "Little Eye of the Tiger" there. Look at him. Looking confident. He`s got that eye of the tiger. Look at the production value on this. I mean, look, he`s striding out there. He`s got his stuff. He`s ready to go.\par \par \uc2 Watch him out, coming out of the dug out there. Look at that, waving to the crowd. What`s up, homie? What`s happening everybody? Yes! Yes!\par \par \uc2 All right --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s see what`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, have you been drinking?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hang the leg, Nate. Hang the leg!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s look at his form.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hang the leg.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What are looking at here, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hang the leg!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I told him -- he`s done this before, so he`s like, I want to do it differently this time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He did a good job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How should I do it? And I say you should soak in the applause, the ovation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then try to hang the leg for effect. And that`s what he was doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So he listened to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So he listened to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was given -- and I applaud --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Vlad, I --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- the effort. It`s very hard to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I applaud that, and I applaud your play by play.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You like that play-by-play commentary?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There`s Nate throwing the ball. We love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think he threw it hard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He looks good. He looks good. He is loose, a little bit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s hard to hang the leg. It`s hard to hang the leg on --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s put it this way. He did it way better than I could.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, you guys are like, yes, yes. I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, I`ve seen you throw. I`ve seen you throw.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a very funny comedian --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- appreciation for that video.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- Kevin Fredericks will be here. Stick around. CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil. That`s Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this 8:00 hour, Billy Crudup is back for another season of "The Morning Show." Yay! As a network executive, this time without a job, he tells Anthony Mason why the role is a perfect gift for him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also comedian, Kevin Fredericks used to work in a bank until he got fired. He`ll stop by to show us how that failure and others like it helped power him to success.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we continue Raking in the Savings at CBS MORNINGS Deals with products from high tech to skin care, all at deep discounts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody likes a deal, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at 8:00."\par \par \uc2 Immigration officials demand answers after a deadly shooting at an ICE office in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 TODD LYONS: One person being killed is one too many. We can`t have this going on at ICE facilities.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Federal prosecutors weigh President Trump`s demand to charge former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  with a crime.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE: There clearly has been significant presidential pressure brought to bear, and you don`t want a universe in which that pressure distorts the prosecutorial judgment.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  is sued by the family of a passenger killed in a deadly mid-air collision over the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 DAILEY CRAFTON: Casey was betrayed by the system he trusted, we all were.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: A California woman has been charged after her dog allegedly voted in two elections. Rut-row!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the California woman faces multiple felony charges. Prosecutors say that her pet, you heard that right, was given mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Which explains why the winner of the 2022 California Senate race was chicken on the floor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have a lot of news to get to.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a jam-packed 8:00 hour.\par \par \uc2 We begin with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  saying a deadly shooting outside of an ICE office in Dallas was "an act of targeted violence." That`s how they put it. Officials say a gunman opened fire from a nearby rooftop, hitting three detainees being held inside a van. One of those detainees was killed, the others were severely wounded. No ICE officials were hurt in the attack, and investigators say the shooter himself was killed -- killed himself.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is on the scene for the latest on all this troubling situation.\par \par \uc2 Omar, good morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DHS says the shooter fired indiscriminately at this ICE facility behind me, which is a processing center for detainees. Now, at about 7:00 A.M. yesterday morning, investigators say there was a lone sniper on a nearby rooftop. He fired at a van, hitting three people inside the vehicle, as you mentioned, they were detainees. One of them was killed, the other two are in critical condition. Police quickly found the shooter, who they identified as 29-year-old Joshua Jahn who died from a self-inflicted gunshot wound.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel released this photo of the suspect`s shell casings that were found near the scene that have anti-ICE messages written on them. Investigators are still searching for an official motive, but DHS Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  said the attack was motivated "by hatred for ICE."\par \par \uc2 Now, according to DHS, attacks against ICE agents have increased by around 1,000 percent since the President took office. Worth noting, there was actually a bomb threat at this facility earlier this year. Now, no law enforcement officials were injured during yesterday`s attack, as you mentioned.\par \par \uc2 As for the victims, we still don`t know any information about them, but of one of them that is injured, the Mexican Consulate says they are a Mexican citizen -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Omar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is expected to sign an executive order today allowing a deal to keep TikTok operating in this country to move forward. It comes more than a year after Congress passed a bipartisan bill giving TikTok`s China based owner, that`s ByteDance nine months to sell TikTok or face a nationwide ban.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has signed multiple executive orders to delay the laws enforcement. Now, sources say the deal would allow a group of American investors, including the software giant, \uc2 Oracle\uc2  to manage TikTok`s data. We should note that \uc2 Oracle\uc2  is owned by billionaire, \uc2 Larry Ellison\uc2 . His son is David, and David is the Chairman and CEO of our parent company, that`s Paramount-Skydance. Got it?\par \par \uc2 It is unclear if users would need to download a new version of this app.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, actor, Billy Crudup plays a scene-stealing network executive in the hit series, "The Morning Show."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Morning Show"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So he`s talking to Anthony, as in Mason, how Jennifer Aniston convinced him to take on this role. What did she say? But first, it`s 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are here in the progressive greenroom. I always say you meet the nicest people in here. This is one of those nice people I was talking about, stand-up comedian -- what`s your name stand-up comedian?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS (Comedian and author): Kevin Fredericks aka KevOnStage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As opposed to Kevon --\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Kevon Stitch -- in you go!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh! Kevon Stitch!\par \par \uc2 KevOnStage is how you know him on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and he`s going to share how embracing failure changes life. Why things can change your -- Kev, nobody likes to fail.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: No, but everybody will. And it`s okay, it`s a part of the process.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we will get through it.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You promise?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Me and you Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Me and you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me and you and your wife.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: And my wife.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from KevOnStage Studios)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like Oreos, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 That`s comedian Kevin Fredericks performing stand-up earlier this year in Dallas. He has built a career making people laugh, and he does it very well, but he says the road to success was paved with a lot of failures. He shares his story in the new book. It`s called "Successful Failure: Lessons Learned Flat on My Face." I love that. I love that title.\par \par \uc2 And we`re happy to say that Kevin Fredericks joins us live and in color in the studio.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is really good to have you.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Thank you, guys. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Because you -- I said Kevin Fredericks is doing all right, a-okay these days.\par \par \uc2 But let`s go back to the early days. One of my favorite lines in the book, as you said, I have failed, but I am not a failure.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that line.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: I think a lot of times people think success and failure are two opposite things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: And the truth is, to me, and what this book is about, is failure is a part of the process to success. It`s a necessary part of the journey -- getting fired, making mistakes, losing this, making bad deals -- all of that stuff makes you a better person and to accept it and embrace it and welcome it, not to do it on purpose --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: But to learn that you might not even know why this is happening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: But usually looking back, you realize, oh, without this, I wouldn`t have done that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wouldn`t have done this. Yes!\par \par \uc2 It is funny how you can go through something, you think this is the worst thing that ever happened, it turns out to be a blessing in disguise. We were talking about you getting fired at the bank.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now that conjures up, what did you do? What did you do? But when I read the book, you got fired, really, for being too honest.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: One thousand percent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what happened in that particular moment?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: My area retail lead manager came to me and was like, because part of this I was South Sound seller of the quarter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Big deal, Gayle. I don`t know if you know about South Sound.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: That`s from Lakewood all the way to Seattle, that was me!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: The number one guy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Let the record reflect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: I was the man that quarter. I had credit cards going, home equity line of credit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was you!\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: That was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were good at your job.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: I was good at my job. So she really saw the potential, but I wasn`t really reaching that potential anymore. So she`s like, what do you want to do? And for the life of me, I don`t know why I told her the absolute truth. I was like, I want to be a comedian. I want be an entertainer. I want to do plays and movies.\par \par \uc2 She`s like, yes, but what do you want to do here? And I told her, I looked her dead in her eyes and said, I am just working here till I blow up. Cool!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So the company said, let`s invest in that guy.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, right!\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Ninety days later, I was walking out the door with a little -- I didn`t even have a box. I just had -- I just had, like a pen, and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A pen! I`m taking this pen.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes, I am taking this pen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is mine!\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: You all robbed me from this. I`m taking this on my way out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But you write, what I like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m curious about -- you learned from that, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, this just sort of leads to that, because in the book you write this, "Failure in life is like Thanos before the snap. Every Marvel superhero nerd will understand, it is inevitable."\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "But what distinguishes us -- what distinguishes those who stay mired in disappointment from those of us who wade through and find success is the willingness to embrace failure."\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why are people unwilling to embrace failure? I know it sucks, but why are people not willing to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s why we don`t like embracing because it does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It does.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It hurts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I get it. I get it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: I honestly think before we don`t embrace failure, I think we avoid trying because of the fear of failure. I think a lot of people are -- they don`t have a lot of failures to point to because they haven`t tried to do anything. They`ve pre-failed, so to speak, because oh, if this doesn`t work out, people are going to say this, they`re going to say that, so they don`t even try.\par \par \uc2 But people who have failed, they`re like, oh, that`s it. I can`t -- I can`t do anything. This didn`t work. If I had stopped when I failed. I wouldn`t be sitting here today in front of you three. I failed at plays, at business, at con -- everything that I`m good at now I have failed and continue to fail, like I wouldn`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but when you`re going through this though, Kevin, you can`t think, oh, I`m failing. It`s okay. I`ll be all right. That`s not the thinking when it`s happening to you.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: No, absolutely not. I don`t want to even put that part out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: The thinking at the moment is like, when I got fired from the bank, it was like, how am I going to pay my bills?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have two children and married to Melissa.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Two children. I`ve got my wife, you know, I`ve got to come home to that Black woman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: I ain`t got no -- I ain`t got no job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: I am not through with this car, and I`ve got nowhere to go tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We tend not to like that.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes. In the moment, it`s just like, what can I do? And I think I picked myself up. You know, I had to -- I had to go down to the labor ready. I had to take my two kids walk across the street. A bus drove by splashed dirty water on me. I was in a movie, you guys, but I made it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, you made it. You made it. I mean, being a comedian is very hard. For comedians who are trying, who are young, for actors, you name it, for creative people who are bombing right now.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And right now their head is under the pillow.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: For sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s your advice to them?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: My advice is to get up. Just get up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Get up!\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: I use this example, Rocky the boxer in the movie, he was actually a terrible boxer, no defense, no nothing. He just wouldn`t stay down. And I think one thing that was really important is like Apollo Creed lost his confidence, because when he should have knocked Rocky down, he kept getting up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: And I think if you can just get up, do a bad version of it, do an okay version of it, do 30 percent -- done wrong is better than not done at all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we should also say you are very funny. You got me in the book where you said --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are. You`re terrific.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- you almost lost your Black people card because you like gas pumpkin pie.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes, I love pumpkin pie. I stand on it. I`m looking at you, America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What is wrong with loving pumpkin pie?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Nothing!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Black people like sweet potato pies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh!\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: And they won`t stop me, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so he said his blackness was humiliated because people said --\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- what`s wrong with you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you for not making me ask that question.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really appreciate that. I gave Vlad the wink, I was like - -\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So now, I am only realizing, you and I are the same person.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Fredericks, always good to see you, man.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Thank you, guys. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very much. And may you continue success even - -\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate would have known.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FREDERICKS: Yes, he would have known.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He would have explained it to me. He would have reached out and touched my hand and go like, it`s because we like sweet potato pies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me and you are the same, Tony.\par \par \uc2 "Successful Failure" is on sale right now.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this year`s Kennedy Center Honors will look a little different. The Center`s new president is here to discuss the changes. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Trump will be hosting this year`s Kennedy Center Honors ceremony in December, which will air right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , we`re proud to say.\par \par \uc2 The honorees are Sylvester Stallone, George Strait, Gloria Gaynor, Michael Crawford and Kiss. We`re joined now by --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good lineup.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a very good lineup.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Awesome lineup.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A very good lineup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re joined now by the President of the Kennedy Center, who is also the U.S. Presidential Envoy for Special Missions, Richard Grenell.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador Grenell, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL (Kennedy Center president): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So clue people into what`s going on with the Kennedy Center, because the President is going to be hosting the ceremony this year, and he`s the Chairman of the Kennedy Center Board.\par \par \uc2 But earlier in his administration, in his first term, he didn`t go to the Honors. He hasn`t been involved. So what`s his role? How did this come about?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: Well, first of all, we are ecstatic to have these honorees, because the public has responded really well. People have been asking for George Strait for a long time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My all-time favorite.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Legend.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: And the people that are coming forward to say, I want to sing, I want to participate to honor George Strait, Gloria Gaynor and others, it is going to be a phenomenal show.\par \par \uc2 I think what we`ve decided to do at the Kennedy Center is confront what I think "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " did earlier this week, which is to say, Broadway is dying. The arts are dying. Every arts institution, most every across the country, are financially in disrepair.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to confront that, and I think "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " piece on how Broadway shows people aren`t going, they`re not participating. We`ve decided to confront that. And President Trump, as someone who really understands corporate America, has decided that we`re going to do big shows, big programming, that fill as many seats as possible. And if we can`t fill enough seats to make sure we`re revenue neutral, we`re going to ask for corporations to step in so that we can get every single show with a sponsor, enough ticket sales to revenue neutral, so that we are not losing money on these shows.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s already proven to be a winning combination, because we`ve never raised more money at the Kennedy Center than we have, for instance, Saturday night, our National Symphony Orchestra Gala has exploded the budget in terms of fundraising.\par \par \uc2 We usually raise about $1.5 million. We`re already up to $2.5 million. We haven`t even started the show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do you think that is Ric, because when it was first announced, many people, not many, but some artists pulled out and said, I don`t want to participate. They were concerned about the political leanings of it. Do you -- can you say to people that there really are no politics involved in the Kennedy Center Honors.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: Look, we want every single person to come. Harvey Fierstein, for instance, came out and said, I`m banned from the Kennedy Center. Not true. No one is banned. I haven`t canceled a single show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have not.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: Not a single show. But let`s be honest about this, many on the left can`t come and perform for conservatives because whatever reason they`re political. We want to play --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re saying they`re welcome. Everybody is welcome.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: Of course. Everyone is welcome at the Kennedy Center. The only change that we`ve done, we haven`t cancelled a single show, "Hamilton" pulled out because they didn`t want to play for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 What I have been clear about is every single show must become revenue neutral. We haven`t cancelled anyone, but if you don`t sell enough seats and you can`t get corporations to pay the bill, then you`re on the hook. And if you can`t be on the hook for that, don`t ask us to go into more debt.\par \par \uc2 When I arrived at the Kennedy Center, we were paying the staff with debt reserves. We were literally having zero money in the bank.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You see that changing already, based on what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: Dramatic fundraising increase. We`ve made some hard cuts. We`ve had to make changes. But again, look at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " piece earlier this week, every single arts institution has been having these problems.\par \par \uc2 What I would say, Gayle is there are a lot of media that all they want to do is focus negatively on the conservatives and let me give you an example.\par \par \uc2 The arts reporters covering the Kennedy Center have never done a story about ticket sales until we got there. Are ticket sales struggling? Of course, they are. Every arts institution. And look at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " said, Broadway is closing and in financial disrepair because people aren`t going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it sounds like you want to change that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which is good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you are --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you are --\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: And President Trump --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you are doing it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ambassador, before you go, really quickly. The President of Venezuela offered to engage --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have 30 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- in direct talks with the U.S. What are you going to do? Are you going to engage with Venezuela over these drug trafficking boats that the President has --\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: So I`ve already been engaging at the direction of President Trump. I`ve spoken to Mr. Maduro. I`ve gone down to Venezuela, and I continue to talk to his team.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love it. All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Richard Grenell, thank you very much. Appreciate you being on. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GRENELL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Be sure to watch the Kennedy Center Honors on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 "Talk of the Table" it`s grand return. It`s back! That`s next.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a minute.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a minute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In a minute, yes --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KI