ut of a job.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: Out of a job and out of ideas.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The 57-year-old actor who`s won a pair of Emmys for his performance calls the part a surprising gift at this point in his career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Morning Show"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: To have a part that is quite complicated and asks a lot of me as an actor and is popular are things that I was not expecting at all.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Crudup fell in love with acting when his mom took him to Broadway shows as a kid.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: New York, to me, was a kind of Shangri La.\par \par \uc2 When I got into \uc2 NYU\uc2 , I came up here in the fall of `91.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He came to get his Masters in acting.\par \par \uc2 What did you get at \uc2 NYU\uc2  as an actor?\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: Tools. You get tools, essentially.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Thinking he was going to be a teacher.\par \par \uc2 Why didn`t you think about it as --\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: Because nobody makes a living as an actor.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sleepers"/Warner Bros)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He landed his first movie role in the 1996 film "Sleepers" with Brad Pitt.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sleepers"/Warner Bros)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But Crudup was wary of playing leading roles.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: I think I was aware that whatever looks I possessed that people were happy about them was going to fade at a certain point, and so I better -- I better get ready for a short career or surgery --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: -- one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Almost Famous"/Dreamworks Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He wanted more challenging parts.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Almost Famous"/Dreamworks Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Like lead guitarist, Russell Hammond in "Almost Famous."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Almost Famous"/Dreamworks Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Cameron Crowe`s 2000 film about a rock band.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Almost Famous"/Dreamworks Pictures)\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: Well, I`ll tell you, it felt difficult because I didn`t play guitar, and not only that, I was supposed to be a prodigal talent, so I was well aware that this was not going to work for some group of the audience.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Crudup says he was constantly breaking guitar strings.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: And who was there to save my ass every time? Peter Frampton.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Almost Famous"/Dreamworks Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The rock star, had a small part in "Almost Famous."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Almost Famous"/Dreamworks Pictures)\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: He`d come in, change the guitar string right away. No, keep after it. Billy, wonderful. Keep at it, you know?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: I had parked his car when I was a valet in high school, and that was my claim to fame at the 15th Street Fisheries in Fort Lauderdale. And then 15 years later, or whatever, he`s changing my guitar strings.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This was actually the first professional stage you appeared on.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: Well, in New York, and I`ve appeared right over there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Here at the Vineyard Theatre, he made his off Broadway debut in 1994.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: It was a kind of esoteric think piece on Japanese internment camps. And at a certain point I entered totally nude and I remember about two weeks into the run backstage, thinking, what exactly am I doing with my life? This is -- I was getting paid hundreds of dollars a week, you understand, and --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: At the Vineyard, Crudup later, took on perhaps his greatest challenge, playing 19 different roles in a one man show about a con man, Harry Clarke.\par \par \uc2 What gave you the courage to take on a part with 19 different characters?\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: It Well, my first response was no, because who would want to do that? That seems like a terrible idea, and after you suffer through the indignities of forgetting your lines, like three minutes into an hour and 20 minute show and breaking out into a flop sweat, calling for a line. Actually, I called for line from here, and the booth is back up there, and from back there, I hear my most amazing stage manager go --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But Crudup says it gave him the confidence to audition for "The Morning Show."\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: It Truthfully, it came up about because of the place that we`re sitting in.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You were here on stage in "Harry Clarke."\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: It I was here on stage in "Harry Clarke" and Jen came and saw it. Jennifer Aniston came and saw the show.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She was here sitting where we are here.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: She was here, right here somewhere, probably, I think right over there maybe.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Morning Show"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Aniston, who plays anchor, Alex Levy, is also an executive producer of the series.\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: It She said, is there anybody that is interesting to you? And I said, I like the weird guy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Morning Show"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Do you have a theory on why people have been so attracted to that character?\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: It I mean, if I did, I think I would have done really well early in my career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Morning Show"/Apple T.V.+)\par \par \uc2 BILLY CRUDUP: Or maybe it`s his hair. Did they ever think about that?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It could be.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, thanks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Too true.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, I was like, we were just talking about Billy Crudup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is such a great character.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s too true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know people like that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Too many people like that, but he is like --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is probably one of those fascinating characters on that show and I just love that Jennifer Aniston was like, but you`re so talented --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- you could play whoever --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He could play anyone. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But he`s always gravitated towards that. From the beginning, he was pushed trying to -- towards leading roles, but he wanted to do the more complicated parts. He just thought they were more interesting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like what he said, you know, my looks seem to be okay. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is great looking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He looks great. Style is great. The hair is great.\par \par \uc2 You too as well, Anthony. Thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 You can watch new episodes of "The Morning Show" Wednesdays on Apple T.V.+.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a great show.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye!\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250925el9p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 16, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson, Lee Cowan, Jericka Duncan, Carter  Evans, Elizabeth Palmer, Scott MacFarlane, Meg Oliver, Lonnie Quinn \par \uc2 3,756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TYLER ROBINSON (Defendant): Tyler James Robinson.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The suspect in the fatal shooting of Charlie Kirk is charged with capital murder. Prosecutors say Tyler Robinson confessed to his roommate and revealed a possible motive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY (Utah County, Utah, District Attorney): Robinson: "I had enough of his hatred."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A judge throws out terrorism charges against Luigi Mangione. He still faces murder charges in the killing of a UnitedHealthcare executive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Has it ever been this bad in the last decade?\par \par \uc2 VALERIE MARTINELLI (Career Counselor): No.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America, the class of `25 struggling to find that first job.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And remembering Robert Redford, the Sundance Kid.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAUL NEWMAN (Actor): What`s the matter with you?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD (Actor): I can`t swim!\par \par \uc2 (SCREAMING)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The suspect in the fatal shooting of the conservative activist Charlie Kirk made his first court appearance today. Tyler Robinson heard the charges against him.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Including aggravated murder. If convicted, Robinson could get the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Prosecutors say Robinson had planned the assassination for more than a week and confessed to his roommate. As for motive, the Utah county attorney, Jeffrey Gray, says Robinson claimed Kirk was spreading hate and told the roommate: "I had enough."\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Gray did not cite any specific example of what Kirk had said that may have set Robinson off.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans has been covering the story since the killing last Wednesday at Utah Valley University and joins us tonight from the courthouse in Provo -- Carter.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Today, we saw suspect Tyler Robinson for the first time in court after prosecutors laid out pages of new details that showed the alleged shooter may have killed Charlie Kirk with what he called grandpa`s rifle.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Shortly before suspect Tyler Robinson made his first court appearance from jail...\par \par \uc2 JUDGE TONY GRAF JR. (Fourth District Court): Mr. Robinson, for count one, aggravated murder.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: ... Utah County attorney Jeff Gray unveiled sweeping allegations of what happened after Charlie Kirk was assassinated on the campus of Utah Valley University last Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY (Utah County, Utah, District Attorney): Police interviewed Robinson`s roommate, a biological male who is -- was involved in a romantic relationship with Robinson.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Perhaps most stunning was this accusation of what happened shortly after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY: The roommate received a text message from Robinson which said: "Drop what you`re doing. Look under my keyboard."\par \par \uc2 The roommate looked under the keyboard and found a note that stated -- quote -- "I had the opportunity to take out Charlie Kirk, and I`m going to take it."\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Court documents included a series of text messages where Robinson seemed to confess.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY: Roommate: "You weren`t the one who did it, right?"\par \par \uc2 Robinson: "I am. I am. I`m sorry."\par \par \uc2 Roommate: "Why?"\par \par \uc2 Robinson: "Why did I do it?\par \par \uc2 Roommate: "Yes."\par \par \uc2 Robinson: "I had enough of his hatred. Some hate can`t be negotiated out. If I am able to grab my rifle unseen, I will have left no evidence."\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The alleged murder weapon was found in a wooded area near campus.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY: Robinson again: "I`m wishing I had circled back and grabbed it as soon as I got to my vehicle. I`m worried what my old man would do if I didn`t bring back grandpa`s rifle."\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And the county attorney said Robinson made a request toward the end of the string of text messages.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY: Robinson again: "Delete this exchange. You are all I worry about, love." That came from Robinson.\par \par \uc2 Roommate: "I`m much more worried about you."\par \par \uc2 I am filing a notice of intent to seek the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE TONY GRAF JR.: You will remain in custody without bail.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The hearing lasted just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Did your investigation uncover any individuals that may have known about this shooting in advance?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY: They are still looking into -- it`s an undergoing investigation.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So is that a possibility?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GRAY: They haven`t ruled that out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Carter, what did you notice about Robinson`s demeanor?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: He was stoic the entire time. He showed no emotion, even when the judge mentioned the death penalty. And, by the way, that green vest he was wearing, that`s to prevent self-harm.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, any remorse, Carter, at all?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: I didn`t see any in the courtroom and not in those text messages that were released earlier as well. It seemed like the suspect was more concerned with getting caught, his partner`s well-being, and losing his grandpa`s gun.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Carter Evans in Provo, Utah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel, who visited the crime scene in Utah last week, was on Capitol Hill today defending his handling of the case.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Defending that and a lot more.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane reports, the Senate hearing got very heated.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHARLES GRASSLEY (R-Iowa): So help you God.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): Seven months into the job, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel insists he`s keeping it.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL (Former Deputy Assistant to President Trump): I`m not going anywhere. If you want to criticize my 16 years of service, please bring it on.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But Patel took heat from Democrats for his handling of the Charlie Kirk assassination probe, including a social media post in which he touted the subject was in custody, who would later be released.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD DURBIN\uc2  (D-Illinois): Take credit.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Illinois Democrat \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD DURBIN\uc2 : That he violated one of the basics of effective law enforcement. At critical stages of an investigation, shut up and let the professionals do their job.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  director made no apologies.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL: I don`t see it as a mistake. I see it as something working with the public to identify that there was a subject.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Patel is also accused by former agents of a series of politically motivated firings of veteran \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, including two more who publicly accused Patel today.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL: That`s a one-sided story. Anyone that has been terminated from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , generally speaking...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD DURBIN: Well, then -- so, tell your -- tell your side of the story.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL: ... failed to meet the needs of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Senate Republicans backed Patel, but Democrats, including New Jersey Democrat \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , predicted Patel himself could be fired.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CORY BOOKER (D-New Jersey): Donald Trump has shown us in his first term and in this term he is not loyal to people like you. He will cut you loose. This may be the last time I have a hearing with you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: He responded in part...\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL: It`s my time, not yours.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: ... with heated exchanges.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ADAM SCHIFF (D-California): Do you think they`re stupid?\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL: No, I think the American people believe the truth.\par \par \uc2 You are the biggest fraud to ever sit in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . You`re a disgrace to this institution and an utter coward.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ADAM SCHIFF: This stinks to high heaven. This stinks to high heaven.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Greg Rosen, who left the Department of Justice as a supervisor of prosecutors in May, said just the perception of politics in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  could hamper the Bureau.\par \par \uc2 GREG ROSEN (Former Department of Justice Supervisor): If you have an agency that is viewed at cherry-picking its subjects so that it can sort of drive a result, it sort of infects the whole institution.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The trust.\par \par \uc2 GREG ROSEN: Yes, I mean, it erodes trust completely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Patel also testified today there was no evidence convicted sex predator \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  trafficked young women and girls to other men. And he says it was the Bureau of Prisons, not him, who recently transferred Epstein co-defendant Ghislaine Maxwell to a lower-security prison.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott MacFarlane in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 A judge today dismissed terrorism charges against Luigi Mangione, who authorities say was caught on camera fatally shooting the UnitedHealthcare CEO, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , in New York City late last year. The judge said there was not enough evidence to prove that Mangione acted with terrorist intent.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan has more now from the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Twenty-seven-year-old Mangione walked into that courtroom. Now, he was shackled and wearing tan clothing. Even though the terrorism charges were dismissed, he`s still facing a second-degree murder charge at the state level. And when it comes to the death penalty, that is still on the table because it is part of the federal case.\par \par \uc2 Mangione is due back in state and federal court in December.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : President Trump arrived in London tonight for a state visit to the United Kingdom. He and the first lady are being hosted by King Charles at Windsor Castle. The president will also meet with the prime minister to discuss trade and other issues.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And CBS Orlando affiliate WKMG reports a man wearing goggles and what appears to be a wet suit robbed a Disney Springs restaurant of as much as $20,000. The police say it happened just after midnight Monday in the morning while workers were putting cash into a safe. No arrests have been made.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now to Robert Redford. He was Hollywood royalty with a crown of golden hair.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Redford died today at his home in Utah. He was 89.\par \par \uc2 Sunday Mornings` Lee Cowan has more about the one-time heartthrob turned Oscar-winning moviemaker and environmentalist.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD (Actor and Director): You don`t like talking about your end. I mean, who does? I can`t do this forever. I have been doing it since I was 21. As you move into your 80s, you say, hey, that`s enough. That`s enough.\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN (voiceover): He was truly a natural. Robert Redford shattered all kinds of norms in what being a movie star really meant.\par \par \uc2 PAUL NEWMAN (Actor): Would you make a jump like that you didn`t have to?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: I have to, and I`m not going to.\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN: Over his career, he had more than 80 films to his credit.\par \par \uc2 MERYL STREEP (Actress): When are you leaving?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: Tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN: Many playing the golden-haired romantic lead.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: Katie, you expect so much.\par \par \uc2 BARBRA STREISAND (Actress): Oh, but look what I have got.\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN: His films had both humor...\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: Are you serious?\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN: ... and heft.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: This is some stuff from the Eisenhower campaign in 1952 that Hunt was connected with.\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN: He took risks many actors wouldn`t. His directorial debut was Ordinary People, a film studios thought was too dark, and his choice for the lead, Mary Tyler Moore, too light. It won Redford an Oscar.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: From the time I was just a little kid, I was always wanting to go away from the rules. I wanted to go -- I didn`t want to break any. I just didn`t want to be held by them.\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS: I don`t even know you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: You know me. I`m just like you.\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN: He was of Hollywood, born in Santa Monica, California, but never really enamored by it. Redford fled to the mountains of Utah instead, where he championed all kinds of environmental causes.\par \par \uc2 It was up here too where he launched his \uc2 Sundance Institute\uc2  and the Sundance Film Festival, focused on those who had something to say, but were rarely heard.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: Whenever there`s an attempt to snuff politically, to use politics to snuff a point of view, then we try as best we can to come behind it and push that back out in the marketplace and encourage the work to be seen.\par \par \uc2 LEE COWAN: Robert Redford died at Sundance surrounded by those who loved him and the mountains he loved so much.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that was Lee Cowan reporting for us.\par \par \uc2 As an actor, Redford was so convincing, I thought he was Bob Woodward for the longest time, right, in All the President`s Men.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Bob Woodward...\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And then The Natural, when it came to playing baseball, I thought he was a baseball player. He was that good.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes. Bob Woodward probably wouldn`t mind people thinking that he was Redford.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I realized, when I started going through the different films, The Sting, All the President`s Men, Butch Cassidy, The Natural, this is how I learned what great acting looked like.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And I didn`t realize he had taught me that until these tributes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes, set the bar really high.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That`s true.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, Lonnie Quinn on storms along the mid-Atlantic coast.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : An independent panel accuses Israel of genocide in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will also have this story:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: I`m Meg Oliver in Hartford, Connecticut, where recent college graduates entering the work force can`t find work. Why Gen Z jobs are disappearing and why it matters, that`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Flooding today in Virginia Beach, Virginia, as a storm hits the mid-Atlantic coast.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn has more about it -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: OK, guys, the worst weather anywhere in the country, you just said it, it is Virginia Beach.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video. I mean, the rain came down so heavily, still coming down heavily. Seven inches have been reported at the airport in Virginia Beach. Look at the winds. The winds are just whipping out there, 50 to almost 60 miles per hour. I mean, trees look like they`re dancing.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the big picture of the entire U.S. of A., your eyes are going to get focused to that storm that`s right around Virginia Beach. Now, tomorrow morning, they still have wet weather in Virginia Beach, but then it pushes up towards New York City. Now, it`s staying offshore. It`s not going to be a repeat performance in New York City. But they will get some rain on it, but not nearly what they have been dealing with right now in Virginia Beach.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I want to talk about, the Tropics. There`s a story here. We`re not dealing with a new name or a new number, but, boy, are we close. This storm that is about 1,000 miles from the Caribbean could very well get a name. It would become Gabrielle if it gets that name. It`s got 35 mile- per-hour winds right now. Could get 39 mile-per-hour winds by tomorrow. The next day, it would become a tropical storm, getting very close, five to seven days, to Bermuda.\par \par \uc2 We will keep an eye on that one. Is there a hurricane in its future? Perhaps. We got to keep a close eye on it.\par \par \uc2 Guys, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now to the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Independent experts commissioned by the \uc2 U.N. Human Rights Council\uc2  accused Israel today of committing genocide in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Israel called the report distorted and false.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has been covering the war since \uc2 Hamas\uc2  launched a surprise attack on Israel in October of 2023.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NAVI PILLAY (United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights): The ongoing genocide in Gaza is a moral outrage and a legal emergency.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER (voiceover): It`s an explosive conclusion about a war that`s now almost 2 years old.\par \par \uc2 Navi Pillay, the head of that \uc2 U.N.\uc2  commission, said Israel`s policy in Gaza met the legal definition of genocide by, among other things, killing Palestinians and imposing conditions intended to destroy them, like restricting aid.\par \par \uc2 Predictably, it ignited a war of words. Israel`s ambassador to the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Daniel Meron:\par \par \uc2 DANIEL MERON (Israeli Ambassador to the United Nations): Israel categorically rejects the libelous -- libelous rant published today by this commission of inquiry.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Inside Gaza, Israel`s military launched a ground defense of today to take Gaza City. It says 350,000 people have been evacuated. They will join the masses already in makeshift shelters simply trying to survive.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said the Gaza City offensive`s goal is to defeat the enemy, that is, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . But critics in Israel from hostages` families to military experts believe that only a cease-fire and tough-minded political negotiation will end the violence and restore stability.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Those genocide charges aren`t likely to influence Israel`s policy inside Gaza, but they will further isolate the country on the international stage.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Liz Palmer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Eye on America is next. Don`t go away.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now Eye on America, our look at challenges and issues around the country and, we hope, solutions, tonight, college graduates looking for work in a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver now on what experts say grads can do to increase their chances of scoring that first job and advice for employers as well.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: So, how organized are you?\par \par \uc2 JAIMEE DEL PIANO (College Graduate): Very.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Twenty-three-year-old Jaimee Del Piano thought her credentials would stand out, a stellar GPA, a degree in psychology, a master`s in public policy at the \uc2 University of Connecticut\uc2 . She graduated in May hoping to land a job as a child advocate.\par \par \uc2 How many applications have you sent out?\par \par \uc2 JAIMEE DEL PIANO: Anywhere from like 10 to 20.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: What`s the response been like?\par \par \uc2 JAIMEE DEL PIANO: I haven`t had enough experience. And that`s, of course, challenging, because I can`t get any experience without a job.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: A recent report shows graduates are entering a turbulent economy with more competition for entry-level positions. Job postings have declined by 15 percent, while the number of applications has increased by 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 JAIMEE DEL PIANO: A lot of the jobs that I was looking for was, like, federal jobs or state jobs.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Some of those jobs have been eliminated by budget cuts.\par \par \uc2 So, you`re competing with people with a lot of experience.\par \par \uc2 JAIMEE DEL PIANO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Del Piano`s frustrations are felt by job seekers around the country.\par \par \uc2 RYAN MCGONAGILL (Business.com): They had each done multiple internships. They were parts of community groups.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Ryan McGonagill is talking about his own interns at Business.com, which helps small businesses looking to expand their reach. As they sent out resumes looking for work, he did a study tracking each one.\par \par \uc2 Of the 311 applications those interns sent out, 87 percent didn`t even get a response. Only five applicants received a scheduled interview, and the number of job offers, zero.\par \par \uc2 Has it ever been this bad in the last decade?\par \par \uc2 VALERIE MARTINELLI (Career Counselor): No.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Valerie Martinelli is a career counselor. She advises taking a personal approach to each application, write different cover letters, network in person, and steer clear of A.I.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE MARTINELLI: Don`t use A.I. for any of your application materials, because what a lot of employers are saying, if they think you are using A.I., they think it`s disingenuous and it will automatically disqualify you.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: As for employers, Martinelli says they need to put the human touch back into human resources.\par \par \uc2 What is your advice to H.R. managers and companies that are hiring right now?\par \par \uc2 VALERIE MARTINELLI: Take the three to five years experience off of your job descriptions.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Del Piano is trying to remain optimistic, but with $15,000 in debt, it`s not easy.\par \par \uc2 What are you learning from this experience?\par \par \uc2 JAIMEE DEL PIANO: I`m learning that you have to have grit.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: But grit alone won`t get it done for this year`s job-hunting grads like Jaimee Del Piano. They`re going to need plenty of patience and resilience as well.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Meg Oliver in Ellington, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Robert Redford once said: "As a director, I wouldn`t like me as an actor. As an actor, I wouldn`t like me as a director."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Americans like him just fine as both.\par \par \uc2 And we leave you tonight with the images of a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD (Actor and Director): Artists and art have the power to effect change.\par \par \uc2 I think change is inevitable. It`s going to happen whether you like it or not. If you try to hang on to not changing, you`re going to be left behind.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFORD: As an artist, I just can`t think of a better life than the one that I have been blessed with. It`s just a great ride.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250917el9g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For September 11, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Weijia Jiang, Scott MacFarlane, Carter Evans, Jason Allen, Maurice  DuBois, Janet Shamlian, John Dickerson, Lonnie Quinn \par \uc2 3,917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Manhunt. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  posts a $100,000 reward for the assassin of Charlie Kirk.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BOHLS (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent in Charge): We`re exhausting every resource to find him, and we will do so.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The bureau releases photos of a person of interest, as the police recover the shooter`s rifle.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The president says he will award Charlie Kirk the Medal of Freedom.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Charlie was a giant of his generation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also tonight, Eye on America: a program aimed at improving communication skills at work.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANN KOWAL SMITH (Founder and CEO, Reflection Point): If we don`t talk together, we can`t work together.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And nearly a quarter-century later, the stories written in 9/11 dust.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This is real.\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ (Chief Curator, 9/11 Memorial and Museum): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This is so striking.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is asking for your help finding this man, described as a person of interest in the assassination of conservative activist Charlie Kirk. CBS News has learned law enforcement believes it has the man`s name. It has not been released.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Bureau is offering a reward of up to $100,000 for information leading to the identification and arrest of the killer.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And while the assassin remains on the run, the authorities have what they believe is his rifle. It was found in the woods near Utah Valley University, where Kirk was gunned down yesterday as he was beginning his Prove Me Wrong debate with college students.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We begin our coverage with Carter Evans in Orem, Utah -- Carter.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Behind me is the stage where Charlie Kirk was speaking when he was shot, and it is now a crime scene. Investigators are also ratcheting up the manhunt for his killer.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has learned from law enforcement sources that \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel is now headed here to Utah.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voice-over): Authorities still have no motive, but they`re now piecing together what happened when a festive moment turned chaotic.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: As Charlie Kirk was speaking in the central courtyard of Utah Valley University, the gunman fired one shot from this building about 400 feet away, according to law enforcement. Video appears to show a person running across the roof just moments after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Investigators canvassed the neighborhood next to campus today, going door to door looking for leads and reviewing video footage from doorbell cameras. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says they found a .30-06 single-shot bolt-action rifle, a gun like this one commonly used by deer hunters.\par \par \uc2 Investigators told us they found that high-powered rifle in a wooded area. Neighbors tell us that`s right over here. And if you look closely, you can see the police crime scene tape.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent Robert Bohls.\par \par \uc2 Is that suspect possibly still in this area?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BOHLS (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent in Charge): No, not in those woods. We walked through those woods and secured it. As to the community, I can tell you that this was a targeted event.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The Utah Valley campus will be closed until Monday. And in a nearby classroom where panicked students fled, you can still see chairs and desks used as barricades.\par \par \uc2 What was your initial reaction when you saw him go down?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN HATCH (Student): Disbelief.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Jordan Hatch (sp?) is one of several students we met still in shock.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN HATCH: It`s crazy to think that the place where I go to school is now going to be forever known as the place where Charlie Kirk was assassinated.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Grace Baird (sp?) and Grace Dishman (sp?) head up a chapter of Kirk`s Turning Point USA at their high school.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: It`s horrible that violence and politics have become almost synonymous in the state of our country right now.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: We were there to just, like, witness, like, both sides of, like, the politics and kind of just, like, see what people had to say. Like, people who don`t usually get to have a say were able to have a say. And that was, like, destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And here`s another indication that the manhunt is now escalating.\par \par \uc2 We have learned the \uc2 U.S. Marshals Service\uc2  has joined the search for the gunman.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Carter Evans in Orem, Utah.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Carter.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The potential evidence, in addition to that rifle, includes footwear impressions.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane in Washington has more on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): In the first 24 hours of the manhunt for an assassin, there was confusing and apparently contradictory information from authorities. Six minutes after \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel posted a subject was in custody for the shooting, local police said something quite different.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He was released from custody after we identified that he did not match the shooting suspect and was not an accurate person of interest.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It took Patel another 90 minutes to announce that person of interest was released. Just after 8:00 p.m. local time, investigators confirmed another person had been taken into custody and also released. Patel has faced criticism for his handling of the response.\par \par \uc2 Sam Vinograd is a former counterterror adviser in the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 The initial hours had some seemingly contradictory information. Is that a problem for anybody?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: The American public relies on law enforcement at all levels of government, federal, state and local, to keep them safe. And part of that is transparently and reliably communicating information, so that the American people can make their own decisions about their safety.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent in charge in Salt Lake City, Robert Bohls, has only been in that job for a few weeks after prior Director Mehtab Syed was moved out this summer.\par \par \uc2 Vinograd says this probe will be uniquely complex and demand better coordination.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: The immediate aftermath of a very public and violent incident is an incredibly heightened threat environment. That`s because bad actors may feel inspired by what they`re seeing across their screens and their social media feeds.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, Scott, is this the first big test of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in this new administration?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It`s a big test, and they have much thinner ranks for it.\par \par \uc2 Not only is the Salt Lake City special agent in charge new, but they have fired dozens of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, prosecutors and professionals in this new administration. And a lot of experience is now on the sideline.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Scott MacFarlane in Washington, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Vice President J.D. Vance arranged for Kirk`s body to be flown home to Arizona this evening aboard Air Force Two.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Weijia Jiang reports that, at a 9/11 memorial service at the Pentagon, President Trump revealed that Kirk would be receiving the nation`s highest civilian honor.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): As President Trump paid tribute to the victims of 9/11, he also took time to honor Charlie Kirk.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I will soon be awarding Charlie Kirk posthumously the Presidential Medal of Freedom.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: CBS News has learned the site of the president`s remarks was moved from right outside the Pentagon building to an interior courtyard out of an abundance of caution after Kirk was shot outdoors.\par \par \uc2 Trump is among many inside the White House who were close to Kirk, who campaigned with the president several times. Vice President Vance, who called him "a true friend," flew to Utah today to visit with Kirk`s family. From the Oval Office last night, with the shooter still at large, Trump blamed the political left.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: For years, those on the radical left have compared wonderful Americans like Charlie to Nazis and the world`s worst mass murderers and criminals. This kind of rhetoric is directly responsible for the terrorism that we`re seeing in our country today.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Kirk`s assassination is the latest in a surge of politically violent acts against people in both parties, including two attempts on the president`s life last year, the arson of a Democratic governor`s home in Pennsylvania, and the murder of a Democratic state legislator and her spouse in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Homeland security expert Donell Harvin says that the hyperpartisan atmosphere has made conditions ripe for violence.\par \par \uc2 DONELL HARVIN (Former D.C. Chief of Homeland Security and Intelligence): Far extremes of the right and left have spent so much time villainizing each other, demonizing each other, they make each other easy targets for those who are radicalized within their bases.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, this afternoon, law enforcement officers responded to a bomb threat at DNC headquarters in Washington, D.C., that turned out not to be credible.\par \par \uc2 Still, political leaders are on heightened alert, with lawmakers in both parties canceling upcoming events.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Weijia Jiang, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 The court heard opening statements today in the federal criminal trial of Ryan Routh. He`s the 59-year-old man from Hawaii accused of attempting to assassinate then-candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last September, weeks before the election. Routh is representing himself. He delivered a rambling statement. The judge cut him off twice, then told him he had forfeited his right to continue.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On Wall Street, the Dow, the Nasdaq and the S&P 500 all closed at record highs. The government reporting today that consumer prices rose last month at an annual rate of nearly 3 percent and had the biggest monthly jump since January. Investors believe this means that, next week, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is sure to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And comedian Nate Bargatze, who`s hosting the 77th Emmy Award Sunday night, rolled out the red carpet today, along with the show`s producers. The Emmys will be handed out Sunday night on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, a look at the largest migrant detention center in the United States and who`s running it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We will also have this story:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: I`m Janet Shamlian in Washington. From factory floors to executive suites, why companies are turning to something akin to a book club to increase productivity.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The west coast of Florida getting some nasty weather today. This is the Fort Myers area.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn has more about that and tropical weather in the Pacific -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Well, guys, Fort Myers, you just showed the video down there. They have been six straight days with rain. They haven`t seen a peek of sunshine. Where`s the sun? Well, it`s not going to be out there, OK?\par \par \uc2 The rain risk, excessive rain is still there tonight. Getting into your day tomorrow, it`s still there. What`s the problem? What`s going on? Well, Florida currently has this stationary front just lagged up on top, and, consequently, it`s just moisture bout after moisture bout coming through the area.\par \par \uc2 When does it change? Saturday night late into Sunday. That should push offshore and we can kind of -- I`d ratchet down the rain chances. I wouldn`t bring it down to zero for Sunday, but the tough stuff, the heavy stuff would be behind us.\par \par \uc2 So now let`s talk about the Tropics. It`s an important day to talk about, because this day, specifically this day, September 10 and the 11th, the peak of hurricane season. And we start off this day without a name or a number in either the Atlantic or the Pacific. But, as of right now, we have a number. We have Tropical Depression Number 13 just offshore of Acapulco.\par \par \uc2 By tomorrow, this becomes Tropical Storm Mario. By the time you get to, say, Monday, it`s a Category 1 hurricane, all the while staying offshore. That`s the best news. The second best thing I can say is, it will offer up some much-needed moisture to places like Arizona and Nevada. It`s not coming onshore, but it`s the moisture plume that will push into that area.\par \par \uc2 Guys, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Much-needed is right.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In the summer heat of the Chihuahuan Desert, a city made of tents is springing up in West Texas. It is being built by a company called Acquisition Logistics to hold as many as 5,000 migrants.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The builder, which is getting more than a billion taxpayer dollars, is also the operator of the facility, though it has no experience running a detention center, much less one of this size.\par \par \uc2 Jason Allen takes a closer look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN (voice-over): The long white tents visible to anyone driving across the East El Paso are intended to grow into the biggest immigration holding facility in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2  has been inside twice.\par \par \uc2 This appears to be a giant tent city. That`s all we can see from the outside. But what is it like inside?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-Texas): That`s what it is, a giant tent city. There are hard floors. There are walls that go up probably about three-quarter of the way to the ceiling.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Escobar said she saw about 1,500 people inside during her last visit two weeks ago. The government awarded Acquisition Logistics a $1.25 billion contract to build and operate the detention center.\par \par \uc2 This house in suburban Richmond, Virginia, is listed as its headquarters and has no public record of running a detention facility before this one. Acquisition Logistics did not reply to our messages.\par \par \uc2 The government has built this on Army property, but it`s nowhere near anything that resembles an active military base. It`s in the middle of sand dunes and scrub brush and on the very edge of Fort Bliss.\par \par \uc2 The Army base and El Paso have a long history with immigration. Unaccompanied children stayed there Presidents \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ricardo Samaniego is the El Paso county judge.\par \par \uc2 RICARDO SAMANIEGO (El Paso County, Texas, Judge): I just would like to be a little bit more vigilant about what`s going on in there. I want to see it. I want to make sure.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: We requested access to the facility and asked to speak to DHS and ICE officials about conditions for detainees, but they declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR\uc2 : One of the things I heard repeatedly from the men who I spoke to was that the food was so bad that it was making them sick.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Escobar said some of the men inside told her they were moved to El Paso from Florida`s Alligator Alcatraz.\par \par \uc2 Are they getting any sense of how long they will be here before they will be moved somewhere else?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR\uc2 : None. They are told nothing. They are given no information. They don`t know if they`re going to be moved to another facility. They don`t know if they`re going to be deported. It`s like a black box for them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that was Jason Allen in El Paso, Texas.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now tonight`s Eye on America, our nightly snapshot of the country, sometimes a story on the news, sometimes beyond the headlines.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s story is more timely than we knew when we planned it. With the country reflecting on how we talk to one another, Janet Shamlian tells us about a program designed to improve communication in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANN KOWAL SMITH (Founder and CEO, Reflection Point): I`d love to kick us off with a question.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voice-over): It`s no fairy tale. These executives are bonding over a book, instead of business.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: He got off the path, pun intended.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: They work for MES Life Safety, an equipment supplier for first responders. But the short story they`re discussing has little to do with their jobs.\par \par \uc2 When you were told there was going to be this group meeting and the book reading, you thought what?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FERRARI (MES Life Safety): I thought, what are we doing? Like, how does this make sense for our everyday business?\par \par \uc2 SHRIDHAR SHAH (MES Life Safety): Kumbaya was the first thing that came to mind. But after the very first session, I came back just a believer.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS FERRARI: What else do you need for us to support you?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Chris Ferrari and Shridhar Shah are on the team taking part in Reflection Point.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Reality is different for different people.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Think of it as a book club redesigned for the modern worker in a neutral space, allowing colleagues to share opinions and practice communication skills they can use later in work.\par \par \uc2 MARVIN RILEY\uc2  (CEO, MES Life Safety): It is a very unique social technology to bring people together.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: \uc2 Marvin Riley\uc2  is the CEO of MES and brought the Reflection Point program when he took over last year.\par \par \uc2 Why did you want to institute this program?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN RILEY\uc2 : It`s a way for us to bring our life experiences and share with each other and to reach a common ground with each other.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Research by the Harvard Business Review indicates there`s a direct link between empathy and commercial success. The most empathetic companies outperform peers in the stock market by at least 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 ANN KOWAL SMITH: If we don`t talk together, we can`t work together.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Reflection Point`s founder, Ann Kowal Smith, chooses the short stories for discussion, focusing on those matching issues and challenges faced by the team.\par \par \uc2 ANN KOWAL SMITH: The reason that this matters is because we give people a chance to practice the skills that enable them to open back up again, right?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: To those who would say this is just woke programming, get in there, do your job, we don`t have to be touchy-feely and share our emotions at work?\par \par \uc2 ANN KOWAL SMITH: If you believe that people who can`t talk to each other are going to take your business to the next level, then, fine, this is not for you. But if you understand that people drive the future, then it`s really important that we invest in those people.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The nonprofit has worked with close to 20,000 people across almost 150 companies, from construction and manufacturing to health care and tech.\par \par \uc2 Have you seen change within your team since you started doing this?\par \par \uc2 MARVIN RILEY\uc2 : I have seen dramatic change. We`re going to build our culture layer by layer using this methodology, because it works.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Turning the page on understanding through the power of a shared story.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: What did he learn from that?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For Eye on America, I`m Janet Shamlian in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Up next, Maurice with the stories written in the dust of 9/11.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : On this 24th anniversary of 9/11, America remembered the victims in services at the Pentagon, in Shanksville, Pennsylvania, and here in New York.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: When the Twin Towers came down, they created clouds of toxic dust that lingered for months. That dust has been linked to the deaths of more than 2,000 people and counting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS (voice-over): The image of a city covered in dust lives forever in the nation`s memory...\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ (Chief Curator, 9/11 Memorial and Museum): These were some items of some of the local residents.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: ... and is on display at the 9/11 Memorial and Museum.\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ: What you`re seeing is the actual dust.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Chief curator Jan Ramirez explains how the dust tells a story.\par \par \uc2 This is real.\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This is so striking.\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ: Dust also was the first writing substance, where urgent messages and motivational phrases...\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Written in dust.\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ: Written in dust.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On walls.\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ: Yes, on walls all around the site, mostly for the first responders to orient themselves.\par \par \uc2 But the shoes kind of say it all.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The museum has collected thousands of items that were covered in dust.\par \par \uc2 JAN RAMIREZ: Here, it`s the things that allow us to empower the human stories.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The dust installation at the museum highlights the stories of those exposed to toxic material and the advocacy that led to compensation and health care for those impacted.\par \par \uc2 Beth Hillman is the museum`s president and CEO.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH HILLMAN (President and CEO, 9/11 Memorial and Museum): The dust is a part of the consequences of that attack. And continuing to understand it, to recognize its consequences on the communities that it most deeply affected is an important part of what we do in the museum.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: What do you hope the public comes away with when people come here and experience this?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH HILLMAN: We hope they realize the courage and compassion with which people responded to 9/11, including fighting for recognition and support for the people suffering from health effects.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I have to tell you, that was my first visit. I had avoided it for all these years, but it`s an incredibly moving experience. Never forget is the whole idea.\par \par \uc2 You see so many items, a fire truck, pictures, mementos, and you will never forget, which is the whole idea.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It pulls you in, having visited it also. And, for me, what I remember is, when the dust and the buildings were coming down, the first responders who were running inside it when everybody else was running away.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Extraordinary, yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It`s extraordinary heroism.\par \par \uc2 That`s the CBS Evening News. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . See you soon on Evening News Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 We will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250912el9b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 U.S. Condemns Israeli Strike in Qatar; Russian Drones Shot Down Over Poland; Trump Denies Writing Note in Epstein Birthday Book; 911K Less Jobs Added from April 2024 to March 2025; Queens Couple Killed, Home Set on Fire; Federal Charge for Ukraine Refugee Murder Suspect; American Kids` Test Scores Declining; Cracker Barrel Scraps Remodeling Plans; New Video Shows U.S. Missile Bounce off UFO; \uc2 Apple\uc2  Rolls Out New Products Amid Global Tariffs; \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  on Pressure Faced by Middle Class; Controversial Immigration Crackdown in Chicago; Emma Heming Willis on Husband Bruce`s Diagnosis; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Life Through the Lens of Sally Mann; Lilly Singh on New Comedy Movie, "Doin` It;" \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Ramy Inocencio, Imtiaz  Tyab, Nancy Cordes, Kelly O`Grady, Scott MacFarlane, Elaine Quijano,  Charlie D`Agata, Jill Schlesinger, Jo Ling Kent, Ash-Har Quraishi, Anthony  Mason \par \uc2 15,017 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Wednesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are following major escalations in conflicts overseas, including Russian drones shot down over our \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally in Poland.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Poland accuses Moscow of an act of aggression after it shot down Russian drones over \uc2 NATO\uc2  airspace during an attack on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The Air Force saying that there was a real threat to the safety of Polish citizens.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Trump shrugs off questions about the birthday letter he allegedly sent to \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): It`s nonsense, and frankly, you`re wasting your time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The latest UFO hearings on Capitol Hill leave Congress with more questions than answers.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE KNAPP (Investigative journalist):That`s a Hellfire missile smacking into that UFO and just bounced right off.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: In Southern California, dozens of containers tumbled off of a cargo ship.\par \par \uc2 MAN: -- wheelchair, always wondered what it`d be like -- this was The Offspring.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, that`s not the way -- that`s not the way we normally do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Something happened there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clearly, we`re having technical difficulties, but we`re all in good shape. We`re glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are glad to be here. We`ve got a lot of news to get to, very busy Wednesday. Major stories overseas that could have a big impact on the U.S. and U.S. policy as well.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has issued a rare condemnation of an Israeli airstrike that happened yesterday in Doha, the capital of Qatar, a key American ally. Israel is saying it was targeting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  officials who are there considering a ceasefire in Gaza. We`ll have more on that story in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with breaking news from Poland, another important U.S. ally, saying it has shot down Russian drones that entered its airspace during an attack on Ukraine. About 10,000 American troops are stationed in Poland, which is a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally, of course.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is following that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Tony, yes, good morning.\par \par \uc2 And Poland`s Prime Minister \uc2 Donald Tusk\uc2 , says the military clocked 19 drones that it says violated Polish airspace and that three or perhaps four drones were shot down.\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  spokesperson said this is the first time that \uc2 NATO\uc2  planes have engaged potential threats in allied airspace.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: The high pitched whoosh of fighter jets over Poland reacting to what the government says was Russia`s violation of the country`s and NATO`s airspace. Poland assessing the damage to homes and land.\par \par \uc2 Poland`s Prime Minister relieved there were no casualties, but said \uc2 Donald Tusk\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER DONALD TUSK speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: "We are most likely dealing with a large scale provocation."\par \par \uc2 The incursion forced Warsaw`s Chopin International Airport to suspend operations temporarily.\par \par \uc2 It was just last week Poland`s President Karol Nawrocki spoke with President Trump at the White House on boosting Poland`s defenses.\par \par \uc2 KAROL NAWROCKI (Poland`s President): American machines opened our meeting. It was great, and we are waiting for 32 F-35s in Poland.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: That U.S. hardware seen as essential for \uc2 NATO\uc2  defense as Russia relentlessly pounds Ukraine next door.\par \par \uc2 This past weekend, Moscow launched a record, more than 800 drones on Ukraine, damaging a government building in the capital.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s latest comment on Russian engagement just last night.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: So the Putin call is going to happen this week.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I think so. I think this week or early next week. Thank you. Thank you. Enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And \uc2 NATO\uc2  spokesperson said, in fact, several \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies or their systems responded to Russia`s incursions. The Netherlands Air Force responded with fighter jets, an Italian early warning aircraft was also launched, and German Patriot missile batteries in Poland were also placed on alert. But one of Russia`s top diplomats in Poland says there has been no evidence that those drones came from Russia -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re going to take you to the Middle East, and President Trump`s criticism of Israel`s controversial missile strike in Qatar. Imtiaz Tyab is in the capital, Doha, which is more than 1,300 miles from Israel.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, Israeli leaders are defending the strikes here on the country capital, even after President Trump said he`s not happy about it, saying, and I`m quoting here, "It neither advances Israel nor America`s goals."\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , meanwhile, has denied any of its top negotiators were among the six who were killed.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I would tell you this, I was very unhappy about it, very unhappy about every aspect, and we`ve got to get the hostages back, but I was very unhappy about the way that went down.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The series of Israeli air strikes on this building in Doha`s quiet Embassy District, sent shock waves through the Qatari capital. Any hopes President Trump had of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , whose political leaders had gathered here, apparently to discuss his latest proposal to end the violence in Gaza and free the remaining hostages now seem up in smoke.\par \par \uc2 It also seems to have deeply rattled the Trump administration who are incredibly close allies with Qatar, who not only hosts the largest U.S. military base in the Middle East, but are also gifting the President a plane to be used as the next Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 Qatar`s Prime Minister, meanwhile, has called the Israeli strike an act of "state terrorism."\par \par \uc2 (MOHAMMED BIN ABDULRAHMAN BIN JABER AL THANI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Saying, "Does the world need a clearer message than this? Who is closing the door to peace?"\par \par \uc2 Qatari leaders also say they were only contacted by U.S. officials about the Israeli strike 10 minutes after it happened.\par \par \uc2 Dubbed Summit of Fire by Israeli leaders, the attack appears to have derailed the talks which Qatar had been hosting. The tiny Gulf Kingdom is one of the few direct channels between the U.S., Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and has hosted countless rounds of negotiations to end the war in Gaza and free the remaining hostages.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now, Qatar has a long history of being considered neutral territory for complicated, rather diplomatic negotiations, including hosting talks between the Afghan Taliban and the previous Trump administration. Given the White House was alerted in advance of the Doha attack by Israel, Qatari leaders are asking why they weren`t informed ahead of time -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Imtiaz Tyab in Doha, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S., the White House is on the defensive again over possible links between President Trump and convicted sex offender \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 . The President dismissed the controversy while talking to reporters outside a restaurant in Washington last night, but his critics point to this infamous birthday note to Epstein and other documents accusing him of failing to answer key questions.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has called the messages in Epstein`s birthday book a "dead issue," but this continues to fuel a controversy that he is eager to put behind him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: During a rare visit to a D.C. restaurant last night, President Trump took questions about documents from the estate of \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 , including this 50th birthday message to Epstein that Trump allegedly wrote in 2003.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It`s not my signature, and it`s not the way I speak and anybody that`s covered me for a long time know that`s not my language. It`s nonsense.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The White House Press Secretary said Trump is open to having a handwriting analyst look at the letter, and she accused Democrats of driving the story.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): Why are the Democrats all of a sudden caring about this? It`s because they are desperately trying to concoct a hoax to smear the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But it was the Republican-led House Oversight Committee that subpoenaed the documents from the estate of the late convicted sex offender. On Tuesday, the committee`s chairman said the investigation should focus on justice for Epstein`s victims.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): None of the victims nor their attorneys were saying anything about, oh, look up this birthday card to see if it`s legit or not. The President says it`s not. I take the President`s word.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Democrats on the committee said they don`t.\par \par \uc2 REP. GREG CASAR (D-TX): When you have a really powerful person like the President saying there`s nothing to see here, that`s when you`ve really got to look into it, because that smells like a cover up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This week, members of the Oversight Committee staff are planning to meet with Epstein`s lawyers in New York to review additional financial records, and the committee is awaiting more files from the Justice Department, beyond what the DOJ has already turned over, which was largely public anyway -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nancy, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We have some surprising economic news showing more signs of a slowdown in the job market. The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  has revealed that employers added nearly one million fewer jobs than originally reported from April of last year to March this year.\par \par \uc2 "Money Watch" correspondent, Kelly O`Grady is here to show us how this happened. Why it`s important.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, great to have you here. So the revisions are normal. Normally, there are revisions.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But not this large, right? What does it tell us?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: That`s exactly right. This happen every year, but this is the biggest on record.\par \par \uc2 So from April 2024 to March 2025, so last 10 months of the Biden administration, first two months of the Trump administration, the economy added 911,000 jobs less than we originally thought. And to put that into context, it`s only 0.6 percent of the overall labor force, but that`s not insignificant, and if you dig into where the biggest changes were, leisure and hospitality, think of your restaurants that was down 176,000; the professional services, think accounting, legal, roughly 150,000; manufacturing, retail. That`s a lot of numbers, but the biggest takeaway is the weakening in this labor market that we`ve seen over the last four months, what this data tells you is actually happening a long time ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Interesting. What does it tell us about the overall economy?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So look, this is a backward looking report. So yes, we`re in a worse spot, but we`ve already lived through that. I think that`s really important context, and so that`s why the market, by the way, didn`t blink yesterday when we got these numbers. But what it does tell you is there are red flags.\par \par \uc2 This is definitely a long-term trend, not a blip, but here`s some good news for those looking at interest rates, they want them lower, this puts pressure on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to deliver an interest rate cut next week and prioritize the labor market. The question becomes, is it a half point, or is it a quarter point?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not a blip. Kelly, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: New York City, police are searching for a man who robbed and killed an elderly couple and then he set their home on fire. Police say a suspect, his name is -- he is 42 years old, his name is Jamel McGriff was caught on camera leaving this couple`s home on Monday after the fire was set.\par \par \uc2 Police say that neighbors reported that a man had been knocking on doors claiming that he needed to charge his cell phone. At least one person turned him away.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say that the 76-year-old victim was seen on surveillance video letting him in to the house. Police say the suspect is on parole after spending 16 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to another tragedy, a man accused of killing a woman in Charlotte, North Carolina, now faces federal charges that could bring a death sentence. The attack on a commuter train was caught on camera last month. The victim was an immigrant from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The rare move to intervene in a local case highlights the Trump administration`s focus on crime.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more on the murder case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Twenty-three-year-old Iryna Zarutska fled the war in Ukraine to find a new home in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say on August 22nd, on her way home from work, she was fatally stabbed three times while seated on the Light Rail System in Charlotte as surveillance cameras rolled. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  said the man who carried out the attack is DeCarlos Brown, Jr., who they say was later seen walking off the train with a bloody knife.\par \par \uc2 RUSS FERGUSON (Western District of North Carolina U.S. Attorney): This is obviously a horrible, horrible situation, but this is why federal statutes exist. They exist to take care of situations like this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Brown already faces local charges, but on Tuesday, U.S. attorney Russ Ferguson of North Carolina announced a federal terror charge is being added, citing the scene of the crime, a mass transit system, raising the possibility of the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 RUSS FERGUSON: It`s a terroristic act to make people afraid to go about their daily lives. That`s what terrorism is.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Court records show Brown has been arrested 14 times prior, including a 2015 conviction for robbery with a dangerous weapon.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We have to respond with force and strength.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: President Trump leaned into the case Tuesday, posting on social media a repeat offender allegedly killing a young refugee justifies stronger federal intervention and deployments in Democratic-led cities.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We cannot allow a depraved criminal element of violent repeat offenders to continue spreading destruction and death throughout our country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: North Carolina Governor Josh Stein, a Democrat who won the seat last year, posted about the case, saying we need more cops on the beat to keep people safe, and that`s why my budget calls for more funding to hire more well-trained police officers. Brown has not appeared in court to enter a plea on these federal charges, he is undergoing court ordered mental health exams -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The latest version of The Nation`s Report Card shows America`s high school seniors have the worst Math and Reading scores in more than two decades, and the first data since 2019 also shows eighth graders falling further behind in Science.\par \par \uc2 Elaine Quijano looks at the message for students, parents and educators.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: The new test scores from the National Assessment of Educational Progress show only 35 percent of 12th graders are proficient in Reading, the lowest in testing history dating back to 1992, just 22 percent were proficient in Math, and 31 percent of eighth graders meet the proficiency in Science.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE ALLEN (Center for Education Reform founder and CEO): Our Nation`s Report Card is abysmal right now.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Jeanne Allen runs the Center for Education Reform.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE ALLEN: States have to give power to parents and to local educators and to entrepreneurs who want to start the kinds of programs in schools that we see are working across the board. We do not change the landscape. We do not change the results.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Comp Sci High School in New York City is defying the nationwide downward trend in test scores.\par \par \uc2 David Noah heads up the charter school.\par \par \uc2 How much do you think the use of cellphones, computers has affected the reading scores in particular?\par \par \uc2 DAVID NOAH (Comp Sci High founding principal): It`s had a huge impact. But I think it`s not irreversible. We have to adapt and adjust to schools as parents to make sure that we are still making the case for the value of reading in a world that is really different than it was 10 or 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Four days a week, students here spend 35 minutes reading outside of English class. That emphasis on literacy has helped raise students` test scores here, Noah says, not only in Reading, but also in Math and Science.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: So David Noah recommends parents ask teachers how much time is set aside just for reading during school, and how do teachers know that students aren`t being distracted by a device? So at Comp Sci High School, cell phones have actually been banned since the school opened in 2018.\par \par \uc2 David Noah said he understood that instinctively, children, because he sees it with adults, right, are going to be distracted if those devices are around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But those --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead, go ahead!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The good news is cellphone bans, it seems to be sweeping the country.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of districts have it now. There`s some recent research finding that when a cellphone is in the classroom and kids have access to it, it`s the equivalent of a great teacher becoming mediocre and a mediocre teacher being bad. So it can really change things.\par \par \uc2 We might actually in these results be seeing the effects that will correct themselves once these phones are gone.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Yes, and modeling behavior is important also, right? If your children see you and you`re not engaged in a conversation, or you`re not engaged in anything other than a screen that is something that sends a message to your children.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So is a solution, pull out the books, keep reading to your kids and keep encouraging them. You know, when I was little, we had the library card, we went to the library.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that was considered fun.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So is it something that we need to really say and emphasize to our children?\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s important to pull out a book and read.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Right and --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Also, as parents like you said, be a reflection of what you want them to become.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Put your phones away.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sometimes easier said than done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, more often. Always easier said than done.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Very true!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I hear the message. I hear the message. Elaine, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, dozens of containers fall from a massive cargo ship into the ocean. I hope you weren`t expecting something that was in one of those, guys. So what happened here? We`ll explain.\par \par \uc2 Plus, there are new questions after a strange video is shown at a hearing about UFOs on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re real!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re real. But first it`s 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Emma Heming Willis will be here in the studio. She is married to Bruce Willis, and now she is talking openly about life with a spouse who has dementia.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch," a big name is back on Broadway. Just you wait!\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hamilton"/Radical Media)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just you wait!\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Edward Jones.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Cracker Barrel is rolling back another part of its attempted rebrand. The company, of course, tossed out a new, simplified logo last month after intense backlash, and yesterday, the company announced it`s also undoing the updated designs you see here, which had already appeared at four locations. It`s going back to this, what the company calls the vintage Americana you know and love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cracker Barrel said, we`re just going to leave it alone, because America has spoken. We`re just going --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Nothing happened!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nothing happened.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s not broke, don`t fix it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do like the new cinnamon roll skillet they have. No one is complaining about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s an innovation we can live with.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Keep that!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead, how a container ship nearly unloaded itself in Southern California. Look at that video. Wow!\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Apple\uc2  under pressure to deliver something entirely new, we`re going to hear what experts think of the newest line of products that came out yesterday. I can`t wait to try one of these, actually, it`s always fresh.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, it`s going on the stacks!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s 7:30, CBS MORNINGS. An unscheduled test of gravity there at the \uc2 Port of Long Beach\uc2 , and gravity worked, an epic fail.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 70 shipping containers toppled off of a cargo ship, crashing onto a smaller barge below. No injuries were reported. We do expect some delays with people getting what they ordered, though. Other boats corralled the fallen containers, keeping them out of the harbor, and the pier was shut down to allow crews to secure the scene.\par \par \uc2 Officials say the containers held mostly furniture, clothing and electronics, so nothing important.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right now to what you might call a close encounter on Capitol Hill. During a hearing yesterday about UFOs, a whistleblower provided some extraordinary video. It seems to show a U.S. military drone shooting a missile at an unidentified object off the coast of Yemen last year.\par \par \uc2 The aerial encounter didn`t go as planned and it is raising a lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata has more on that mysterious mission in the sky.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The newly released video captured by a U.S. Reaper drone shows a glowing orb off the coast of Yemen. Suddenly on the left side of your screen, you see a Hellfire missile zip in, strike that unidentified object and bounce right off it.\par \par \uc2 When we slowed the video down, you see the Hellfire missile continue to travel on its path. That trajectory becoming clearer from this zoomed out angle. Not shown is a second Reaper drone that launched the missile.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Eric Burlison shared the video at a House Oversight Committee on what the military calls unidentified aerial phenomena, better known as UFOs.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE KNAPP: That`s a Hellfire missile smacking into that UFO and just bounced right off, and it kept going.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC BURLISON (R-MO): It kept going, and it looks like the debris was taken with it.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE KNAPP: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC BURLISON (R-MO): What the hell is that?\par \par \uc2 LUIS ELIZONDO (Former United States Army Counterintelligence Special Agent): We`ve never seen a Hellfire missile hit a target and bounce off.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Lue Elizondo is a former senior intelligence officer with The Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 LUIS ELIZONDO: When a Hellfire makes a hit, a kinetic strike on something solid, there`s usually not much left of whatever it is it is hitting. It`s very, very destructive.\par \par \uc2 What seems to happen is that the missile is either redirected or, in some case, perhaps, glances off the object and continues on its way.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: A recent government report revealed the government received more than 750 new UAP sightings in the 2024 fiscal year, leaving lawmakers digging into the mystery and National Security concerns posed by these objects.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Does this video scare you guys? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Wiggins?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Me, too. So there`s a lot missing from that video, like what mission they were on, and as they said, what are we even looking at here?\par \par \uc2 Now, we do know the U.S. military is conducting regular airstrikes against Houthi targets who posed a threat to \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  and commercial vessels. Pentagon officials tell CBS News they have no comment -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Nate, I`m thinking they probably have some comments behind-the-scenes on this one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s no doubt about it. Listen, I`ll quote Charlie D`Agata, "Me, too."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Charlie, this is terrifying, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I haven`t seen an American missile bounce off an object on video since the movie "Independence Day."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, this is some weird stuff, and either it`s an alien not good, or some other country has an amazing new piece of flying machinery that is immune to missiles, also not good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, somebody called Will Smith and Jeff Goldblum, because we`re going to need some help.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. All right.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  has rolled out a new line of products under heavy pressure to show they really are something new. They include the thinnest iPhone ever made, a new version of Air Pods Pro, and a watch that can monitor your blood pressure.\par \par \uc2 This is Apple`s first major product launch since a global tariff war began back in April, and our senior business and tech correspondent, Jo Ling Kent looks now at Apple`s efforts to stay on top of innovation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: \uc2 Apple\uc2  is making moves --\par \par \uc2 MAN: At 5.6 millimeters, it is the thinnest iPhone we`ve ever made.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: With the first ever iPhone AIR starting at nearly $1,000.00, alongside its new iPhone 17 and 17 pros, starting at nearly $800.00 and $1,100.00, respectively. Air Pods Pro 3, with live language translation.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Apple\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: But the reveal comes at a challenging time for consumers. According to WalletHub, nearly nine in 10 Americans think iPhones are overpriced, and nearly 60 percent say inflation and tariffs will keep them from buying a new phone this year.\par \par \uc2 MARK SPOONAUER (Tech expert): For the most part, \uc2 Apple\uc2  is still very much a premium brand when it comes to phones.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: We spoke to tech expert, Mark Spoonauer after Apple`s unveiling on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 MARK SPOONAUER: The \uc2 Apple\uc2  ecosystem is really strong, and I think that some of that shine is starting to come off with the competition that we`re seeing around A.I.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Google\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARK SPOONAUER: Maybe \uc2 Apple\uc2  is a little bit behind the competition, but they`re known for doing that. They`re usually late to market, and they try to be best, so we`re going to have to see how that works out.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jo Ling Kent in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jo Ling, thank you.\par \par \uc2 New research shows the middle class is shrinking and stretched very thin. We`re going to talk to \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  about the financial pressures facing middle class families.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Money Watch," how the middle class is getting squeezed. Rising costs from groceries to housing are outpacing wages and leaving many households stretched very thin.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, Dolla` Dolla` \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to break it all down. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So let`s just cut right into it. Who makes up the middle class and what impact is the current economy having on their bottom line?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So when we look at the middle class, according to \uc2 Pew Research\uc2 , they peg it to household income, which right now is $80,600.00. So it`s a range. It`s $53,000.00 up to $161,000.00, and that`s about just over half of the U.S. population.\par \par \uc2 What`s interesting to me about that is the middle class has been shrinking. That`s down about 10 percentage points from 1971, so our middle class is getting smaller, the big wealthy people, they`re getting wealthier, and a lot of those middle class people drifted into the lower class, as defined by Pew.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so is there any relief in sight? Families are sitting back listening right now. What can they do? Kind I get some relief at home?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So I want to explain that the reason why people feel pressure is that we have big categories that you`re spending money on, and we`re talking about housing, we`re talking about health care, we`re talking about child care and tuition.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yup!\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : These categories have gone up by so much more than your income, that`s why you feel squeezed. So if you`re feeling that way, the first thing is take a deep breath, because you know what, you can`t solve these problems on your own. These are big issues that have been forming for decades.\par \par \uc2 You need to focus on your family. You need to be looking at the money you`re spending, scrape by as much as you can. But everything that you think is like an extra thing, and sometimes I would even put, like, saving for college in that category. Take care of the here and now. Focus on today.\par \par \uc2 Get those numbers every single month together, try to, like, basically, say, automate my payments, stay out of debt when things are relieved.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, those are some of the solutions.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the bigger solutions, there are policy things, you can`t solve them. But when I think about the middle class squeeze, I also want to help people understand the reason why people don`t believe in the so-called American Dream that level of like, hey, I can get ahead.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : The reason is that these big categories are eating up so much of your household take home. So when we think about this, try to make sure that you`re putting money away when you can, staying out of debt, watching how you spend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so a recent survey found that 71 percent of parents with kids under 18 felt overwhelmed by their financial responsibilities. What`s behind that?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Okay, what`s really behind this is child care. Those early years. Not every state has ability to send your kids to school before age five. And when you look at this, look at the amount of money that is being spent on child care.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sheesh!\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : We are talking about six, seven, ten, fifteen thousand dollars a year.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a significant chunk!\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It`s huge if you are working hard. So I think that people really need to look closely at these costs. But also to say, I`m not saying don`t have kids because they`re expensive. I`m saying, take some pressure off yourself. You don`t have to do everything right now. You need to get through these periods. And when the kids go to school, you re-evaluate.\par \par \uc2 But what I really want people to understand, some of the people will say to me, well, aren`t these people invested in the stock market? Yes, some are, but they have to focus on the here and now first. So I almost would say, if you can put some money away in a retirement plan through work up to the match, great. If you`ve got debt, whittle it down. Great! But don`t freak out. Try to just give yourself some grace, because these problems are not going to go away tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Real quick, financial stock market advice, should we jump in the deep end or wear financial floaters?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : By the way, I love the question. Stock markets reaching all-time highs. For everyone who has some money invested, keep it diversified. Now is a very good time to make sure that you have some money stock, some money bond, some money cash. Don`t be a hero, man!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great advice. Don`t be a hero.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up the stories you will be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some big things coming up for eight o`clock, including Emma Heming Willis, the wife of Bruce Willis. You`re very close to me, Tony. The camera is right in my face, people, and it`s scaring me. She`s got a powerful new book about her family`s experience with his illness.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Anthony as in Mason speaks with world famous photographer, Sally Mann. Find out how she`s weathered the ups and downs to maintain a creative life.\par \par \uc2 So all of that`s coming up in our next hour, but first, Tony and I who got right in my face, we`re walking over to the table because Vlad has arrived.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m here!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Getting ready for "What to Watch." You have a big tease?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got a great -- we`ve got a whole bunch of stuff!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to be talking about the 10 best chefs in America.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that! "What to Watch" with Vlad!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That takes time!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right after --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Hollywood Nights. Hollywood Nights." We`re going to begin 