may remember, his family announced that he was stepping away from acting due to health issues that were affecting his cognitive abilities, and since then, they share that he`s been diagnosed with something called frontotemporal dementia, also known as FTD they call it.\par \par \uc2 His wife, Emma Heming Willis is taking us inside their family`s experience in the new book. It`s called "The Unexpected Journey." Such a great title, "Finding Strength, Hope and Yourself on the Care Giving Path." It is a road map for people caring for a loved one with dementia and we`re very happy to say that Emma is joining us in the studio this morning.\par \par \uc2 Emma, hello!\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS (Bruce Willis` wife, "The Unexpected Journey" author): Hello!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am in awe and awe and bowing down to you, and I marvel that you have written this book while you`re in the middle of this journey yourself, still trying to figure it out for yourself.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: Yes, yes, yes! I am, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 You know, I realize that the unexpected was born from this idea that I have the time, the energy, the access and the resources to be able to take the wisdom and insight from this journey that I`m on still and bring in experts and specialists, and it was just a way for me to be able to pay it forward.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I think if I was a caregiver or a care partner, as you call it, I would say, thank you, thank you, thank you, because you really let us know what this takes to do. It`s hard work, it`s very difficult. It`s never ending.\par \par \uc2 And you said the truth of the matter is, there is no easy fix for this. There`s nothing that anybody can say or that anybody can do. But you do give us practical things to help people who are going through this.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: I think that caregivers are so fatigued with making decisions all the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: And I really wanted just to put some actionable, tangible ideas into this book so that caregivers don`t have to think too hard. You know, sometimes I just want someone to tell me, like, tell me what to do. Just tell me what to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: Because, you know, we`re so maxed -- we`re so maxed out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, this is -- there was statistics that really surprised me. Caregivers often and do die before the patient, and this is the thing that got me, they die at 63 percent higher than people their age.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: Yes, I know. That was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you do when you heard that?\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: That was my wake-up call. A neurologist told me that you saw that. They saw that I was burning the candle at both ends, and told me about this statistic, which I never heard of. I was floored by it, and it was the wake-up call that I needed. I needed to hear that so I could understand and start to really take my own health and prioritize that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you feel you were not taking care of yourself? You must have not?\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: No --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: No, I wasn`t. I mean, most caregivers aren`t. I think that the percentage is that 40 percent of caregivers aren`t able to make their own doctor`s appointments because they don`t have the support, they don`t have the help. And they are not thinking about themselves. They are thinking about their loved ones and their family and the household and there`s so many things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But one thing that you stress over and over, which I think is so important, is that, please don`t be afraid to ask for help. Not only be afraid, you must ask for help. It`s essential that you do that.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: I didn`t know I was allowed to ask for help.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: 