 be aligned on, we weren`t. And our -- things just were different. It was very gray.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But then you said there were symptoms that you could no longer ignore, like, what?\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: Yes, I think it was just again, it`s so ambiguous, this idea. But there just came a point where I was, like, enough is enough. There is really something going on there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are going to the doctor.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: And you really have to, like, trust your gut, and then you have to go in, and you have to advocate for your person and yourself. You know, when I do this awareness, the importance is when we really want to get people into the doctor sooner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: So that they can get diagnosed sooner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: So that they can get into trials that are there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That could help them.\par \par \uc2 But this is the thing. You go into the doctor, you get this diagnosis of a disease. Do we call it a disease?\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: It is a disease.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You get this disease that you`ve never heard of before? And what are you told at that point?\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: Well, nothing. Just check back in in a couple of months.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they give you a pamphlet, Emma.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: Yes, as I was leaving --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the point. They give you a pamphlet and say, check back in. We`ll talk to you later. You walk out of the doctor`s office and you think what?\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS: Yes! Yes. I mean, I walked out with nothing, no hope, no direction, no road map, nothing. And I was stunned. And that is how this unexpected, you know, from that traumatic moment was where that book comes from.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`ve also been very candid about in your situation, 