g -- no him.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Oh you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we`re scrubbing the tub.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, I do stuff at the house.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: He cooks. I know he cooks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When was the last time?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: I know he cooks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Get on -- get on out of here!\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Clean her up! Clean her up! Clean that Gayle up!\par \par \uc2 All right, so Cup Cargo. This is great. You`re going to put this right into your cup holder. It actually has a bottom that you rotate, and it`s going to fit into any cup holder. You need to turn that off with a little button up here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You`re going to fit it in. Not only do you now get to put your drinks in here, up to 40 ounces, rather than just a tiny bottle, it`s got a place to put your phone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You are going to put your phone in there. Your hands free. You`re safe. And it`s just simple and easy. I wish I had come up with this idea myself. $32.99, that`s 45 percent off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, Liz, and we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything that you saw, scan the Q.R. code on your screen, or visit \uc2 CBS\uc2  deals.com\par \par \uc2 All right, stick around. We have plenty of more show to go.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back. CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 A compelling conversation now about creativity with renowned photographer, Sally Mann, whose work is sometimes controversial, but it`s been exhibited all around the world.\par \par \uc2 Her latest and newest project focuses on the process behind her famous pictures. Man`s memoir, "Artwork" describes the heartbreaks, challenges and triumphs that come with living a creative life. Mann took Anthony Mason on a tour of her long-time home studio ahead of the book`s release.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How many acres is this?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN (Photographer): Almost 800.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Wow!\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The photographer, Sally Mann lives in Lexington, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: We`re headed down this way.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Okay. So what is this?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: So this is my wet plate collodion studio.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ah!\par \par \uc2 She often works with a 19th Century technique that uses a negative image on glass.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: So you`ve got a plate, you hold it like this.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: You pour the chemistry on like that, and you spread it all around.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s given her acclaimed photographs of southern landscapes, of her family, a painterly quality.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I have horses, tons and tons of horses.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In her life and her work, Mann has never wandered far from Lexington.\par \par \uc2 In terms of having a career as a photographer, did you ever think you needed to leave here?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes, I did. I was sure of it, and I was right. I did need to leave here if I wanted to have a successful career.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But you didn`t leave.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I didn`t and it just took me a little longer. I just stayed home and I made the work, and I just made more work, and I made more work and more work, and pretty soon I had work that people couldn`t ignore.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you know what you were trying to say in the beginning?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I think I knew what I wanted my pictures to look like and what I wanted them to evoke.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: It was a lot about the South and the complexity of the South and the dark underside of the South.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "So often I would feel there was a great picture just there, teasingly close," she writes in her new memoir, "Artwork." Like when she saw her daughter Jessie, holding a candy cigarette.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: And I tried it so many different ways.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: These are her early versions.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: And it builds and builds and builds until finally I had it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "Candy Cigarette" became one of her best known images.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: You just glance over at the work and you`ll get an absolutely perceptible gut flutter. You will feel which one is the right one, and that`s how -- I go by my gut always.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: The second to the last --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mann sold her first print at a college snack bar in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 For 30 bucks?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes. 30 bucks, yes. The key was who I sold it to.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: It was push pinned up on the wall, and who was waiting in the queue to pay for her \uc2 Coca Cola\uc2 , but Mrs. \uc2 Hirshhorn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Art collector, Olga Hirshhorn. The \uc2 Hirshhorn Museum\uc2  in Washington, D.C. was named for her husband.\par \par \uc2 There was one art dealer who actually said to you, in the beginning, these pictures are too domestic.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Right. They`ll never sell. Slammed them back up in the portfolio and threw me out.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Any artist in the beginning has to deal with rejection.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How did you deal with it?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Badly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I took it so personally,\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But she persevered.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I`m a peasant. I mean, I go to work.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: "I believe artists should suffer," Mann writes in "Artwork." "Without character, you will have nothing to say."\par \par \uc2 Do writing and photography give you different things?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: They both give you that wonderful moment of satisfaction. When you write just the right word, it is as if the tumblers in the safe have all slotted into the perfect place when you`re a safe cracker.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: It`s a way more exciting feeling when you get a good picture. It`s ecstatic, almost.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Only briefly, right? Because the minute you feel that, that cresting wave of ecstasy, your insecurities wash in and suck it all out again. It only lasts a minute.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, but you`re willing to chase that feeling again, even though the insecurity comes back.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Right! Right! It`s got to be some kind of drug.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mann published her first memoir, the best-selling "Hold Still" in 2015. A year later, her son Emmett, who struggled with schizophrenia after several brain injuries, took his own life at 36.\par \par \uc2 You dedicate this book to Emmett. Did you find a way to deal with grief in your art?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Just keep doing it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes! Yes! I mean, many people said, are you -- are you going to write about Emmett? No, I`m not going to write. I`m just going to make work.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Do you think it`s in there somehow?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Probably.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes. I do believe that.\par \par \uc2 John Banville is one of my favorite writers and he said there`s really only two kinds of people there are the bereaved and the yet to be bereaved, right?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: And I think that you could tell the difference in people`s work who`ve been -- who`ve had a like a gash of pain go through their lives.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This is your new work back here?\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes, yes, yes. This is what I`m so excited about.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: At 74, Sally Mann is working on a new series of landscapes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: It`s from light hitting the negative during development, and that`s what sent me down, talk about a rabbit hole. It sent me down this. I mean, this is the most recent one I took.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love this!\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Isn`t that amazing?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I can`t figure these out. I can`t replicate them reliably. So not only do I have chance, but I have chance and serendipity. Yes, that`s a --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Wow!\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: That`s the perfect recipe, right?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is a pretty type of combination.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You write that the one indispensable tool that you always go back to is essentially passion.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: Yes, it is a tool, and you can`t manufacture it. It`s -- it has to come up from within you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: All I know is that I can`t not do it, you know? I just take the pictures.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s kind of nice that you have something like that.\par \par \uc2 SALLY MANN: I`m so lucky.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, and we are too, because her pictures are amazing. One of the things that`s really interesting about reading in her book, "Artwork" is how she kept taking pictures when her children were really little. Because as she writes, she says the crying baby will kick the sunshine out of ambition, no matter how maniacal and but she persevered, as she has through everything that she`s been through in her life, and she`s created an extraordinary body of work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It felt so candid to me. It felt like you just sort of stopped by the house and she said --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- come on in, Anthony. Let me show you around. That was the tone to me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, as she says, I`m a peasant. I go to work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Those are her work clothes. She said, that`s literally what I do. I take pictures. She`s really --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anthony, that phrase there, the believed and yet to believed.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The bereaved, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bereaved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bereaved, bereaved. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That came from John Banville, the writer, but yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony, you have been around a ton of artists.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is something that stood out to me. She was very emotionally present.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Do you find that she was a little bit different than artists you have met before?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No, I think -- one of the things that I really admire about her, and she writes it about a lot, is this whole idea that you go to work, that an art -- and I learned this from my stepfather, who was a painter, who went to work every morning at the same time, no matter whether he was feeling inspired or not.\par \par \uc2 She talked also to me about, she said, you know, she said, as soon as I finished this book, she said I was in a panic because I didn`t know what I was going to take pictures of next. That`s what the artist has to deal with. It`s like, what is my next subject? Where am I going now?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right!\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So you have to be present.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You have to be present.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You have to be present. But she said, I found this new body of work, and now I`m -- I`m really excited again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guys, I`m stuck now because I thought. She said, believed and not to be believed. But bereaved is even stronger. Yes! Because we will all be at some point, the yet to be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Those who have had pain --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- and those who have yet to --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, she said that gash of pain --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very deep!\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: -- you`ve got to experience that gash for pain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your body of work is amazing. You`re incredibly present in these interviews. That`s why they`re so good. And I can`t wait to see what you do next.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sally Mann -- Sally Mann`s book is called "Artwork." It`s on sale right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Anthony, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Actor and comedian, Lilly Singh is here in studio. She will share her new movie about a 30-year-old virgin who teaches high school Sex Ed. It is a wild ride.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, I like that music!\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is making her big screen debut in a new comedy film. Actor and former late night host, Lilly Singh is starring in the new movie "Doin` It" which she co-wrote and produced by the way.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Doin` It"/Aura Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lilly plays Maya, a 30-year-old Indian American software engineer who is a virgin and unexpectedly finds herself teaching Sex Ed at a high school. In this clip, Maya meets the principal a concern, and a concerned parent and teacher about her changes to the curriculum.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Doin` It"/Aura Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Lilly Singh joins us right now. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH ("Doin` It" actor): Good morning. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Thank you. Before I get to my first question, the movie is absolutely hilarious.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Grabs you from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Grabs you from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Grabs you --\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Thumb-dump, baby!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is layered. It`s a little bit of raunchy. It`s culturally nuanced. I just want to say, well done.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Appreciate you. Thank you. It`s been seven years of my life, so I really appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And speaking of that, it`s been seven years, you co-wrote and you produce, you star in --\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What was it like to get it off the ground and what inspired the story?\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I can already hear my mom saying, you`re lazy. Why didn`t you direct? Listen, it is an independently financed feature, so it`s been very, very difficult to get made, but I think it`s such an important film.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Doin` It"/Aura Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I didn`t grow up with Sex Ed. I feel like this conversation is so taboo across so many cultures. I think we`d be better if we normalize this subject, which is why this movie so important?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Most of us didn`t grow up with Sex Ed, so we cut from didn`t go up with Sex Ed, to the teacher saying, and this week we`re having Vulva Week.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I was like, uh! That`s the thing. What Nate said about the movie, on one part, you`re laughing, and the other part, you go Lilly! Can`t even put that on the screen.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I know, well, I think --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was your thinking? Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I think comedy is the best vehicle to have some otherwise awkward, uncomfortable conversations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt!\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: And so I think that everyone, at some point in their life is going to deal with the topic of sex.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: And I think education, empowerment and safety is really important when it comes to sex.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: So I think we should be using comedy as a vehicle to just normalize this conversation. Kids and teachers should be talking about sex.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So people now, they`re going to wonder how much of Maya`s experience and your real life overlap? All right, so --\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: If I am a virgin? If I am a 30-year-old virgin?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I will just be really raw --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, speak into the microphone. Yes, Lilly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you have to say about that general topic?\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I will say, listen, I`m going to be very honest and vulnerable. I know so many older women who are in their 30s and 40s who feel awkward about sex still, who were virgins very late into their life, and there`s a lot of shame associated with that, and I don`t think that`s right. I don`t think that sex should be a shameful subject, especially for women, and especially, I`ll say, for women of color.\par \par \uc2 I think that we should feel comfortable being like I like this. I don`t like this, I enjoy this. I don`t enjoy this, and I don`t feel comfortable with this. So I think it doesn`t matter what age you are, you should feel empowered to say what you want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. But I didn`t hear the answers to the question about Lilly --\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Oh, well, I will answer that part. I will -- yes, there are a lot of moments in the movie where Maya says some awkward stuff. It`s from my life. I`m very similar to this character.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But also, the other thing that was interesting to me, it seemed very intentional. The male characters and the female characters in the movie were a switch. What was your -- what was your thinking there? Explain what you were doing.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: You know, growing up with sex comedies, one, we never saw someone that really looked like me at the center of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: But I think that we rarely see female, forward, female centered sex comedies. And so it was important for me to have the female characters be front, center complex.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Doin` It"/Aura Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: There is a male love interest, you know, he says some stuff, and he`s cute and he`s fine, but he`s very accommodating --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To her.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: To her. We rarely see in a sex comedy, it`s usually the woman who is doing all the accommodating.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Saying all the right things. But this man is just like, I`ll be patient. I`ll do whatever you want.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I`m like, we can use that every once in a while.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, and as I mentioned earlier, it is culturally nuanced. It feels like you were very intentional about representation in this film. You even brought on your mom as a cultural consultant.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to hear about that.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: That correct, everyone. My mom helped make my sex comedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: That`s exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shoutout to Mama.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Shoutout, Mama. First time we had the talk at the age of 35 on the set of this film.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what did mom say about this movie to you?\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: You know what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: She has not watched it yet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What?\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I know she helped on set.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: She helped with some of the scenes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So she saw some of the scenes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Yes, so we`re going to watch it together when it comes out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: With my dad on one side and my mom on the other. But I have a feeling my mom is actually going to be thrilled, and my dad will say no words. I am quite sure that he will be watching and be like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just not going to say --\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I am going to be glad for the first time that my dad can`t express his feelings for the first time ever!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dad will be what time is dinner? What time are we doing that?\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Exactly!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, oh, I`ve got a call. Is that the phone in the other room? I mean, you might want to film that reaction.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Oh, absolute content, baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When did you know you were going to be funny?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I was going to ask.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you going to ask?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you`re so quick.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Thank you. I appreciate that. I don`t think I ever knew I was funny. I think I`m just naturally a little weird and outspoken, and then people laugh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No! Uh-uh.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I just really believe in saying what`s on my mind. I think so often we feel alone in our experiences, when really we`re all experiencing the same thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: And so really, when you say, I`m funny, I think I`m just open and I`m honest.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When did you realize there is a career in this? That you can just, you know, be professionally funny?\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: You think I have a career?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think you`re on your way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Can you say it again?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think you`re on your way.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: You hear that, Mom, Dad? I have a career. CBS MORNINGS said it here first.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is on national television right now.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 You know, so I started on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  where there was no gatekeepers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: And that was very important for someone like me. And so I think why I`m so adamant on telling stories through a specific cultural lens is really I believe in the power of representation. I believe in the power of seeing stories featuring different people.\par \par \uc2 Growing up when you have a movie about sex and you don`t see anyone that looks like you, that really messes with your mind.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt!\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: and I really just want young girls to watch us and be like, "Oh! Finally!"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I can feel normal and see myself and learn something, you know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the title "Doin` It" too, all morning, we were going "doin` it."\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: "Doin` it" "Doin` it" Let`s go. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes! LL Cool J.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And could please leave the boots when you leave.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ooh! Okay.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Absolutely, Gayle! You took your shot. You get the boot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve been looking at the boots, and I went woah!\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I get the boots if you watch my movie, September 19th in theaters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wear a size 10.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: You see that plug, I just did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, I was going to plug it!\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Thank you so much!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, well, I was going to plug it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We were just about to say -- but say it again, Nate.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Say it again, baby.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We got you. Well, listen, before I say it, I just want to say, well done. I feel like this is a beautiful, hilariously relevant movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two classics popped up in my mind. I don`t know if comparison works.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Hit me! Hit me!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s like "40-year-old Virgin" meets "Bend It Like Beckham."\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Ah! I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two classics that I love.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: I love both of those references.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lilly, I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Eleven looks fantastic.\par \par \uc2 LILLY SINGH: And I can`t wait in four years, I too will be a 40-year-old virgin, so I am just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much. "Doin` It" premiers in select theaters Friday, September 19th, as Gayle always says, run, don`t walk to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t walk!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m telling you, it is so funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up on CBS MORNINGS tomorrow, that is one of the biggest --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you forget what it was. Coming up on --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tomorrow, I would like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- superstars in the world will be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are talking about the one and only Usher!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There he is!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You don`t want to miss this. That is tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. You don`t have to call just watch us. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Usher is on the show!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes! Live and in color in the studio. I like that.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We ask you please to stick around for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus with this one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doesn`t time fly when you`re having fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It does fly when you`re having fun!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We had Lilly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, what you`ve got coming up?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We had Emma. We had Anthony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, gosh! I don`t even know. You asked me too many questions here. You know what? I`ve been pondering this morning? Teddy is only six years old. You know what he did yesterday? He asked for a phone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Teddy?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what did you say? What did you say?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t -- I wasn`t really pay attention. I was looking at my phone, so I was kind of confused --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My baby is growing up!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re growing up too fast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Teddy, the answer is no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We got him one, of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Spoiled!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye!\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250910el9a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 9, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson, Ash-har Quraishi, Carter Evans,  Adam Yamaguchi, Scott MacFarlane, Weijia Jiang, Holly Williams, Mark  Strassmann, Elaine Quijano, Lonnie Quinn \par \uc2 3,831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A major escalation of the war in the Middle East. Israel launches a deadly attack on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders in Qatar, a U.S. ally.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli Prime Minister): Israel acted wholly independently.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Federal charges are filed against the man accused of stabbing and killing a Ukrainian refugee in North Carolina. He could get the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): This is pure evil on full display.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What`s going on in the stacks?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A mess in the ocean. Dozens of containers topple off a cargo ship and into a California port.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Eye on America: A city reinvents mass transit. And now the buses run on time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TIM BURTON (Bus Passenger): Every 15 minutes is just incredible.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The chances for a cease-fire between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  seem as distant as they have been any time since October 7, 2023, when \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists staged a surprise attack on Israel. Today, the Israeli military launched an attack against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders more than 1,000 miles away in Qatar.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The strike was aimed at buildings housing \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` political wing in Qatar`s capital of Doha.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Qatar, which has been attempting to negotiate an end to the war, condemned the Israeli attack.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has long been reporting from the Middle East and joins us now from London -- Holly.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Israel says that it targeted \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders who it claims were responsible for the October 7 attacks. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  said tonight that the strike killed five members of the entourage of one of its senior leaders.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): The blast in a residential neighborhood was caught on security camera. Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  called it a surgical, precision strike.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli Prime Minister): Israel acted wholly independently.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is designated a terror organization by the U.S., but its political wing operates openly in Qatar, making negotiations easier. We visited a different building used by the group in 2023 to interview another senior leader, Mousa Abu Marzook.\par \par \uc2 MOUSA ABU MARZOOK (\uc2 Hamas\uc2  Senior Leader): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  did their duty.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Qatar is a vital U.S. partner and a mediator in cease-fire talks. It condemned the attack on its soil as criminal.\par \par \uc2 GONEN BEN ITZHAK (Former Israeli Intelligence Officer): Who is going to accept on the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  side any deal?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Gonen Ben Itzhak is a former Israeli intelligence officer. He accuses Israel`s prime minister of deliberately attempting to derail negotiations with the strike because he wants to continue the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Do you think, at this point, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  cares what the United States thinks about this strike?\par \par \uc2 GONEN BEN ITZHAK: Netanyahu feels that, right now, he can do whatever he wants.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Family members of hostages also criticized the attack. Udi Goren says he wants \uc2 Hamas\uc2  punished, but not if it means he never recovers the body of his cousin.\par \par \uc2 UDI GOREN (Cousin of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Hostage): I have no pity for them. I don`t want to see them killed. I want to see everybody signing a deal that brings all hostages back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Holly, as we try to understand this, Israel drops bombs in the territory of a U.S. ally. For our viewers who thought we were all on the same side, this is very striking. What`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Well, so Israel and Qatar are both U.S. allies.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess, what does it mean for American leadership if one of them carries out a strike on the other`s territory, seemingly with impunity? And I think, for some in the Middle East, this will look as if the United States, which for decades has been known as the world`s policemen, no longer has the same power or influence that it once did.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Holly Williams for us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Holly.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The White House says President Trump was notified about the Israeli attack in advance and has since spoken to Prime Minister Netanyahu.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The president posted that he wants the war in Gaza to end now.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang at the White House.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 This strike comes at a delicate time, as the U.S. tries to broker a peace agreement between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . President Trump says the attack does not advance Israel or America`s goals. He posted on social media that: "This was a decision made by Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu. It was not a decision made by me."\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, the president spoke with Netanyahu and Qatari leaders, and he assured the Qataris another attack would not unfold on their soil.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): The president views Qatar as a strong ally and friend of the United States and feels very badly about the location of this attack.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Weijia, the largest U.S. military base is in Qatar, the largest U.S. military base in the Middle East. When did Israel tell the president or the White House that this attack was going to take place?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, a senior U.S. official and a diplomatic source familiar with that strike confirms the U.S. was informed by Israel about the attack in Doha as it was about to happen.\par \par \uc2 Trump said the administration immediately informed the Qataris of the impending attack, but he also said, unfortunately, it was too late to stop it. Qatari officials confirmed today that, by the time they were warned, they were already hearing explosions.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Weijia Jiang at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A 34-year-old man with a long rap sheet was hit today with a federal terror charge in the stabbing death of a Ukrainian refugee on a Charlotte, North Carolina, commuter train. That charge carries the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The horrifying video of the attack is all over social media.\par \par \uc2 Justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane is following this case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): Iryna Zarutska was 23 years old and fled from a bomb shelter during the war in Ukraine to find a new home and new job in North Carolina. Prosecutors say, on August 22, on her way home from work, she was killed while in that seat on the light-rail system in Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says the man who stabbed and murdered Zarutska is Decarlos Brown Jr., who they say was later seen walking off the train with a bloody knife.\par \par \uc2 RUSS FERGUSON (U.S. Attorney For Western District of North Carolina): This is obviously a horrible, horrible situation. But this is why federal statutes exist. They exist to take care of situations like this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Brown already faces local charges, but U.S. attorney Russ Ferguson of North Carolina today announced a federal charge as being added, citing the scene of the crime, a mass transit system, raising the possibility of the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 RUSS FERGUSON: It`s a terroristic act to make people afraid to go about their daily lives. That`s what terrorism is.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Court records show Brown has been arrested 14 times, including a 2015 conviction for robbery with a dangerous weapon. President Trump and his allies have highlighted the incident as proof that his attention to crime is justified.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): We have to respond with force and strength.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The president leaned into the case today, posting on social media that a repeat offender allegedly killing a young refugee warrants stronger federal intervention and deployments in Democratic-led cities.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Brown has not yet entered a plea in his federal criminal case, but, recently, a judge in North Carolina ordered he undergo a competency exam before that local murder case can move forward -- John and Maurice.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 Shipping containers tumbled into the water today at the \uc2 Port of Long Beach\uc2  in Southern California.\par \par \uc2 Adam Yamaguchi is at the port.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: The Coast Guard says more than 60 shipping containers fell off a cargo ship, leaving a mess of boxes in the water here in Long Beach. You can see this fireboat using water cannons to use the force of water to keep those boxes from drifting off into areas of the port where there still is traffic.\par \par \uc2 The vessel carrying the containers arrived at the port from Southern China. It`s not yet known what`s inside those containers or how they fell off the ship. Now, no injuries have been reported and authorities are continuing to investigate the cause of this incident.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : More than 20 people have been killed in two days of violent protests in Nepal. The parliament building was set on fire, along with the homes of a number of politicians, including the prime minister, who resigned. The protests were initially spurred by a ban on social media. That ban has since been lifted.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The Supreme Court has agreed to decide whether President Trump`s sweeping tariffs are legal. The court is fast-tracking the case. It`ll hear arguments in early November.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And there are new concerns about the health of the U.S. economy. The Labor Department reported today that employers added nearly one million fewer jobs than originally reported from April of last year through March of this year.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Job sites have become ground zero for the Trump crackdown on illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We have seen the roundups in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 And Carter Evans reports, other major cities could be next.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Immigration raids are becoming a familiar site across the country. Seven workers were detained by federal agents at this Los Angeles car wash just before Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 BEN FORAT (Business Owner): We saw a bunch of masked men jump out of vans without any badges and with machine guns.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Owner Ben Forat (sp?).\par \par \uc2 BEN FORAT: Customers started running for their lives because they thought it was a -- a shooter.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Some customers hid in a closet with workers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was honestly the saddest I have ever seen, like, to see grown people cry and scared and think they`re going to lose their families, and like that.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: On Monday, the Supreme Court sided with the Trump administration. Homeland Security says it`s planning major operations nationwide. There`s already a buildup in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Border czar Tom Homan:\par \par \uc2 THOMAS HOMAN (White House Border Czar): We`re not out looking for non- criminals. Now, if we run into a non-criminal during these operations, they absolutely are going to be taken in custody.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Amid that threat, local leaders created rapid-response teams to document ICE operations.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ash-har Quraishi drove through Chicago with community activist Maricela Batista (sp?).\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: What kinds of individuals make up the rapid-response teams?\par \par \uc2 MARICELA BATISTA (Community Activist): It`s community members, parents, children of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 ASH-HAR QURAISHI: Do you anticipate larger-scale operations?\par \par \uc2 MARICELA BATISTA: Yes. It`s everyone. They are targeting everyone if you look brown.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Back at the L.A. car wash, Ben Forat is now training new employees. He says he always keeps their paperwork in the office, but that didn`t matter during the raid.\par \par \uc2 Did they allow them to come back and get their documents to show them?\par \par \uc2 BEN FORAT: No, they just grabbed them and took them. One guy was screaming: "My stuff is in my locker." But no one wanted to hear it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: So, the owner here is now telling all of his workers to keep all of their papers on them at all times while they`re working. And, by the way, he says he`s all for law and order. If someone here has broken the law, he says they could come and get them. He just ask that they not terrify dozens of people in the process.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Carter Evans in L.A. tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS: no stars on the latest report card for America`s schoolchildren.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Plus, this tonight:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m Mark Strassmann in Akron, Ohio. Take a ride with us on a transit system rethinking how to get people on the bus on tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And "Got Milk?" is back with a whole new crop of mustaches.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A wildfire that broke out yesterday in San Diego County has now been fully contained, but not before it destroyed a number of homes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn has more about fire conditions in the West and the latest on Tropical Storm Kiko -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: You betcha, guys. There`s a lot of good news to report about Kiko.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you go back to just last week, this was a Category 4, a beast of a storm. Right now, yes, it`s a tropical storm. It has 40 mile-per-hour winds. If it goes on to 38, it`s not even a tropical storm anymore. Forecast to go north of the Hawaiian Islands.\par \par \uc2 The concern that the National Weather Service has is for some big surf out there. And, yes, that can be dangerous for people who are going in. But the surfers are going in all the time right now in Hawaii, because they`re sort of in their element, but you got to be careful out there, because rip currents, they can be deadly.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the situation in California, you talked about the fire burning in San Diego now 100 percent contained. There are plenty of fires in California that are burning right now that are not 100 percent contained, one in particular, the Garnet Fire. It`s only 14 percent contained very close to Sequoia National Park.\par \par \uc2 They`re worried about the sequoias catching fire. Again, it`s only 14 percent contained. And we don`t see any rain coming to this particular fire throughout the end of this week. But, guys, we will give you updates if we get it.\par \par \uc2 And I leave you with a little Hawaiian surf. It`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, thank you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The report cards came out today for America`s high school seniors, and they are not good. Their performance in reading and math has fallen to the lowest level in decades.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Elaine Quijano has the disappointing numbers, and she visited a school that`s setting an example for the nation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): Every day at Comp Sci High School in New York City, students spend 35 minutes reading outside of English class.\par \par \uc2 DAVID NOAH (Founding Principal, Comp Sci High School): Things have really improved over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: David Noah heads up the charter school.\par \par \uc2 DAVID NOAH: All the way through high school, we need dedicated time that is not English class, not history class just to read.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Noah says that novel approach has helped raise student test scores here, not only in reading, but also in math and science, results that defy a nationwide trend.\par \par \uc2 New test scores from the National Assessment of Educational Progress show only 35 percent of 12 graders are proficient in reading, the lowest in 30 years. Just 22 percent were proficient in math, and 31 percent of 8th graders meet a proficiency level in science.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE ALLEN\uc2  (Founder and CEO, Center for Education Reform): Our nation`s report card is abysmal right now.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: \uc2 Jeanne Allen\uc2  runs the Center for Education Reform.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE ALLEN\uc2 : States have to give power to parents and to local educators and to entrepreneurs who want to start the kinds of programs in schools that we see are working across the board.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: David Noah believes opening books and putting away phones is part of the solution.\par \par \uc2 How much do you think the use of cell phones, computers has affected the reading scores in particular?\par \par \uc2 DAVID NOAH: It`s had a huge impact, but I think it`s not irreversible. We have to adapt and adjust to schools as parents to make sure that we are still making the case for the value of reading in a world that is really different than it was 10 or 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: David Noah recommends parents ask teachers how much time is set aside just for reading during the school day. And how do teachers know that students aren`t being distracted by devices?\par \par \uc2 At Comp Sci High School, cell phones have actually been banned since the school opened back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Elaine Quijano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A major U.S. city is getting high marks for mass transit.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : How it reinvented the system is tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now tonight`s Eye on America, our nightly portrait of the country, the people, the issues and solutions.\par \par \uc2 One out of 20 Americans takes mass transit to work each day, and it`s not always a smooth ride.\par \par \uc2 So, tonight, Mark Strassmann takes us to a city that is making mass transit work, the Rubber City, Akron, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): You`re about to learn how Akron reimagined its mass transit system.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER (METRO Regional Transit Authority): What would you change if you could change anything?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This is how, by listening.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER: Are we coming on time?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Dawn Distler rides a different bus once or twice a week, and when she does, she`s all ears.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER: We are here for you.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Nearly 40 years ago, Distler drove a bus here. Today, she`s the CEO of \uc2 METRO\uc2 , Akron`s transit system, serving more than five million riders a year.\par \par \uc2 It`s an old-school approach asking your customer, what can we do for you?\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER: And also getting out and riding. If we`re not using our product, why should anybody else use it?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 METRO\uc2  threw out its dated map of where buses fan out across the city. Routes changed. Frequency improved. Buses started running seven days a week every 15 minutes where they`re needed most.\par \par \uc2 And how far from work do you live?\par \par \uc2 TIM BURTON (Bus Passenger): It`s about four or five miles.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: We rode with Tim Burton on his way to a new job at this grocery store where he stocks shelves. You can call him, without exaggeration, a lifelong \uc2 METRO\uc2  user. As a newborn, his mother brought him home on a \uc2 METRO\uc2  bus.\par \par \uc2 TIM BURTON: The frequency is the biggest thing for me. Every 15 minutes is just incredible.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 METRO\uc2  also started hiring differently. Take Robin Hundley, our bus driver.\par \par \uc2 Were you surprised when they hired you?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN HUNDLEY (Bus Driver): Oh, man, I was. It was the best gift ever.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Hundley applied with no prior experience driving one of these 40-footers. No problem.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN HUNDLEY: I just knew it was something I wanted to do, and I was ready.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER: We can teach you how to drive a bus. I can`t teach you how to be nice.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN HUNDLEY: Oh, you`re welcome. Yes, I just -- but it`s good.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Akron`s transit system reinvented itself in 2023. Over the next year, ridership increased 24 percent, more than triple the national average.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER: All of our maintenance will be done in this space.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Distler walked us through the city`s new $61 million bus maintenance facility, and \uc2 METRO\uc2  won the industry`s Oscar, the 2025 National Mass Transit System of the Year.\par \par \uc2 Basically, you played the cards you had, rather than the cards you wish you had.\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER: Yes. No, no, nobody gave us more money.\par \par \uc2 Being able to get where you need to go, having transportation to get you there, as far as I`m concerned, that`s a civil right.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 METRO\uc2  is riding high on its back-to-basics route. Provide it...\par \par \uc2 DAWN DISTLER: Does that one run enough?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... and they will ride it.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Mark Strassmann in Akron, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Finally tonight: It was one of the most popular advertising campaigns ever, the "Got Milk?" campaign.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It was started by the California dairy industry in 1993 with an ad portraying a man on the phone trying to answer a radio station quiz. He has the answer, Aaron Burr, for the record, but he loses because he has a mouthful of peanut butter and cannot be understood.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The ad`s tagline became a national catchphrase, "Got milk?"\par \par \uc2 And, in 1995, "Got Milk?" introduced the now famous milk mustache.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Over the years, lots of famous people were shown sporting a white stache. Now, to mark the 30th anniversary, the milk folks have started a new "Got Milk?" campaign with a search for a new stash of staches.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They`re traveling the state of California, this time photographing ordinary folks with milk mustaches. So far, 12,000 people have had their picture taken.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: California`s dairy industry milking the mustache for all it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And now I want a cookie.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes, or a piece of cake. How about that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Both.\par \par \uc2 That`s the CBS EVENING NEWS. The news continues on \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS PLUS streaming on CBS News 24/7. We will see you there.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250910el9900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For September 7, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Anthony Salvanto \par \uc2 9,412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: A not-so-hot jobs report leads to more uncertainty about the strength of the economy. With Friday`s jobs report showing an even foggier forecast for American consumers than anticipated, President Trump called on his chief economic adviser, Kevin Hassett, to explain.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT (Director, National Economic Council): Right now, we`re puzzled about the \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers and looking for new leadership there to make it so that the numbers are more reliable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But can those independent agencies, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  and the Federal Reserve, maintain their independence in light of political pressure? We will ask Kevin Hassett and get his take on the state of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Plus, as the circle of critics grows when it comes to \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 `s handling of vaccines and management of the CDC, we will talk to Republican Senator Roger Marshall. And the top Democrat on the Senate Intelligence Committee, Mark Warner, will also join us to weigh in on the news of the week.\par \par \uc2 Finally, as President Trump moves to rebrand the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  as the Department of War and continues to warn the city of Chicago he will soon send in federal forces, we will talk to Illinois Democratic Senator Tammy Duckworth.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with the director of the National Economic Council, Kevin Hassett.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and thank you for being here...\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Good morning. It`s great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... in person.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s a lot to get to with you, but I want to start on what just happened overnight.\par \par \uc2 Russia has launched a very significant attack on Ukraine and -- in Kyiv. They actually even hit a Ukrainian government building. Does the U.S. condemn the assault? And what are you waiting for now in terms of triggering those sanctions on Russia?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, as you know, I`m not one of the foreign policy advisers of the president, but at the National Economic Council, we are responsible for making sure that sanctions get enforced and that people that are helping Russia with their war against Ukraine, as, for example, India has been doing by -- excuse me -- by buying Russian oil, that we`re ready to respond to them economically.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, last night`s news is very disappointing, and I`m sure that there`s going to be a lot of talk today and tomorrow about the level of sanctions and the timing of sanctions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The timing of additional sanctions on Russia?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Perhaps, yes. I mean, it`s up to the president in the end, but, yes, it`s a very disappointing set of affairs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On the U.S. economy, there were 22,000 jobs added last month. The past two months of data were also revised down.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said in interviews this week it suggests there`s less momentum here than you thought. Has job creation stalled, and should the Federal Reserve be concerned about the jobs picture?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well -- well, first of all, the interesting thing about the jobs numbers, right, is that they had the biggest revisions in 50 years over the summer, and we came in with 22,000 in August. But if you look at what they said in `23 and `24, they have two ways of estimating the jobs.\par \par \uc2 There`s something called the payroll survey, where they ask the employers how many people did you hire? Then they have something called the household survey, where they call up people, they say, do you -- do you have a job?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And over those two years, the payroll numbers said that we created 4.5 million jobs and the household survey said about two million fewer, about 2.5 million jobs. And they should have the same answer.\par \par \uc2 And so, for August, for example, the payroll survey said 22,000 and the household survey said 288,000. And so I think that what`s going on is that these old-fashioned ways of surveying -- like, I wonder if you`ve ever gotten a survey in the mail and filled it out and mailed it back in. Maybe you have.\par \par \uc2 But I think that that`s the way they`re trying to do it. And the reason we`re getting these massive revisions and these numbers that don`t make sense is really that we`ve got to modernize the way we do the labor data.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: But if you look at the non-labor data, you know, second- quarter GDP was revised up to 3.3 percent. The \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2 `s GDP estimate for the third quarter right now is 3 percent. And so industrial production is at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you believe all that data? All those models are functioning?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And so -- no, the point is, when there`s dissonance in data, that you have to sort of watch how it all works out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Got it.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And the thing that I`m most suspicious of right now in terms of data quality is the job number, in part because there are other ways to do it. There`s this company called \uc2 Homebase\uc2  that does it. Their August number is 15.\par \par \uc2 But, again, even think about this. If we ask households how many jobs do they have, it`s plus 288,000 in August. If we ask the employers, it`s 22,000. Then that`s -- that becomes a puzzle for economists. Like, what is wrong with the data? And we kind of know what`s wrong with the data. What`s wrong with the data is, people aren`t filling out the forms and sending in the surveys.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because, last month, the president fired the head of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , suggesting there was political bias, not just what you`re talking about, which is a very technical issue on how data is collected and crunched.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did you fix the political bias or...\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well -- there -- there -- there are patterns of the data that look like political bias, but they could be accidental. And that`s why we need to get a new set of eyes in there to make sure that things are more transparent, that we modernize the surveys and we make them so that more people trust them.\par \par \uc2 But think about this. At the Federal Reserve...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you don`t trust these numbers from Friday?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: I think the \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers need to really be improved. They need to be modernized.\par \par \uc2 And I gave a talk at the \uc2 BLS\uc2  next to \uc2 Alan Greenspan\uc2  we had a few years ago where I talked about the necessity of modernizing them, and so I still agree with that position.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, trend line-wise, when we hear from companies like \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 , Deere, Ford, \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  that their costs are going up due to tariffs, that doesn`t suggest there`s going to be robust hiring in those sectors, right, when a company is taking on something that could hurt their productivity -- or profitability, I should say.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, we`ve seen inflation has come down quite a bit. It was in the high 3`s when President Trump took office. The average over the six months is 1. 9.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, even the Fed right now clearly agrees that inflation has been under control if you believe futures markets, because the federal funds futures markets are now saying the Fed is going to cut interest rates three times this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Expected...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And so the Fed -- they`re expected to. And so, therefore, you know, the Fed is expected to feel like it`s comfortable with where inflation is right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But my point being that doesn`t forecast from these companies a scenario in which they would necessarily be hiring. Have you been able to calculate what the immigration crackdown has done to employment?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, yes, there are -- there are a couple of things that we could look at. First of all, the private sector employment is up by about half-a-million this year, and government workers, government employment is down by about 100,000.\par \par \uc2 There`s another thing, is that most of the jobs that have been created in the U.S. are for native-born workers, and they`ve actually -- there`s actually been a reduction in labor supply for non-native-born workers. And the other...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you prove that?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Excuse me?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you prove that?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Oh, we have a -- we have a survey where they actually ask it. Now, maybe we don`t trust the surveys, right?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: And there is actually a thing...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And if you know the corporations are hiring undocumented or have a pattern of it, are you prosecuting those companies?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: There -- there`s technicalities that -- when someone`s released into the country, then, in the previous administration, then they would often get a number that would allow them to work legally until they went to court. And so there were, for sure, people that were undocumented...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But going after the employers?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: ... who were being able to work.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing that you can see in the data, because we get the unemployment insurance claims by, like, county, that about 80 percent of the claims for unemployment over the last few months have come from blue states. And so there are places like Portland and Chicago where people are fleeing the cities, and you`re seeing that in the numbers too.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you don`t plan to prosecute companies who had this pattern you`re talking about of hiring people in the country illegally?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: I don`t work for the Justice Department, but -- but I do see the pattern of native-born versus non-native-born employment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we are also seeing in that data you say you don`t trust necessarily that it`s manufacturing that`s trade-related, that that is where the lack of hiring is happening. Those are the areas that the president had promised there would be new jobs.\par \par \uc2 When do those new jobs in those sectors come?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Right. Let`s think about it again though that in terms of the way that the data are a little frustrating for everyone right now, that industrial production is at an all-time high, GDP is booming, productivity was up 3.8 percent in the last quarter, and it looks like manufacturing jobs are down.\par \par \uc2 And so how is it that industrial production is at an all-time high while manufacturing employment is down? It`s something that we`re looking forward to seeing what happens in the revisions. The revisions have been last -- last year -- there`s a benchmark revision next week.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the revision was more than a million over the last year. This is why we need new and better data.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`ll wait to see if those jobs are created.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about the Fed, because the president, you were standing next to him in the Oval Office, said he might basically make you one of the most powerful men in the world, because he`s considering you to become the next chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Our \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling we just did shows that 70 percent of Americans want the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to make decisions independently from President Trump. But there`s a big partisan difference here; 59 percent of Republicans say the Fed should be guided by what Trump wants, 41 percent should be independent of Trump. Which Republican camp do you fall into?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Oh, if I were in that survey, then I would say 100 percent that monetary policy, \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  monetary policy needs to be fully independent of political influence and...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Including from President Trump?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Including from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 The fact is that we`ve looked at countries that have allowed the leaders to take over the central banks, and what tends to happen is that it`s a recipe for inflation and misery for consumers. And so \uc2 Central Bank\uc2  independence is something that -- as you saw, there was a hearing this week about that, that Democrats and Republicans and the White House all agreed about.\par \par \uc2 Now, the question is, has the current \uc2 Central Bank\uc2  been as independent as we would like, as transparent we would as we would like? And I think that there`s some dispute about that, which we could go into if you`d like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Secretary Bessent went into that in an article in "The Wall Street Journal" this past week.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: He did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He talked about the \uc2 Central Bank\uc2 `s independence being under threat due to mission creep. There should be an independent, non partisan review of the entire Fed, its role in regulation, monetary policy and research.\par \par \uc2 What`s your plan to overhaul the Fed?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Well, I don`t have a plan to overhaul the Fed right now. I`m just happy to do my job.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have to have been thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Look, I have been the president`s -- one of his top economic advisers for nine years. We talk about everything from golf to decorating the Oval Office to monetary policy, but, right now, what I`m focused on is doing my job as Director of National Economic Council every day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you would endorse what Secretary Bessent wrote in that article fully?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Yes, I agree with his article, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you would be prepared to implement that vision for the Federal Reserve if you are chair?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: It`s a hypothetical, that -- we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, it certainly seems, from one of the political appointees, the Cabinet member, to be a vision for the future of the Fed that the Trump administration would like.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Right. Oh, I -- I agree -- I agree with his vision, but I look forward to the president deciding who is going to be the Federal Reserve Chair. And I think that he and Secretary Bessent will do that ably. There are a lot of great candidates.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What will you do if the Supreme Court does rule that the president`s tariff policy, or at least those under the IEEPA, the International Emergency Economic Powers Act, are not legal?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: We -- we certainly don`t expect the Supreme Court to do that. And I think that we`re on pretty high ground. And so it`s a very unlikely scenario.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is that there are other legal authorities that one could use to get the same outcome. 232s, 301s, you know, numbers that we could go into if you like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Other ways to implement tariffs.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: But it might be like, yes, too -- too deep a dive for the show on a Sunday.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: But -- but, yes, there are other things that could happen should it go that way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Kevin Hassett, thanks for coming in this Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT: Thank you. It`s great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by the top Democrat on the Senate Intelligence Committee, Mark Warner.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (D-Virginia): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 You are known for your Intel work, but you`re also on a number of other committees. We were just there talking about the economy. Do you agree that, basically, the economic data that the United States releases is potentially fundamentally flawed?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: No, I think that the \uc2 BLS\uc2  has been the gold standard for years, viewed not only in this country, but around the world.\par \par \uc2 Could there be reforms? Absolutely. But it`s a little ironic that you`re firing the referee, the president firing the head, cutting the staff. I`m not sure how that is going to improve the quality of the data.\par \par \uc2 And the president had promised that -- a golden age from American workers. We`re down 80,000 manufacturing jobs since the beginning of his term. We`re at basically a flat hiring position. And what I think families who are listening, if anybody`s got a family, a family member that`s in college or immediately getting out of college, you know what a tough job market is to find this.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things I would like to have heard from Kevin and, frankly, from more economists is, we`re about to see the biggest job dislocation in our history, I believe, in terms of A.I. job dislocation. And those are many times kind of a -- college-driven jobs.\par \par \uc2 I mean, think back five years ago, we said to every kid, if you want to get a good job, go become a computer programmer.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Those are going to be some of the first jobs ignored and eliminated.\par \par \uc2 And so I just -- I`m fearful that this focus on who`s keeping score, when I think the American public realize the job market is tough, and particularly in terms of manufacturing jobs, there is no golden age.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and there`s also a fundamental remaking of the economy if you`re talking about artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 So, on that point quickly, there were more than a dozen tech titans at the White House this week, including some of these giants in the artificial intelligence space, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 `s founder, \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO, and they were all around the table praising President Trump for the investment in chip manufacturing and A.I.\par \par \uc2 Do you deserve -- do you think he does deserve that praise?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, I can tell you, the chip manufacturing came about because of a bill that \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  and I put in under Biden, the CHIPS infrastructure act...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: ... with the $52 billion, totally bipartisan. President Trump has been very times critical of that bill. Now he is, I think, sending a bit of a mixed message, because some of the investments in that bill were going to Intel, our last meaning -- meaningful fabricator of chips.\par \par \uc2 He took an equity stake there, which is debatable, although I do think getting some of the profits...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Taxpayers own 10 percent of the company now.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 But the -- here`s the weird thing. While he`s trying to support American chip manufacturing, at the same time, he`s taken off any of the restrictions on, for example, \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  H20 chips to sell to China. So, what - - how do we read this? If we want to say we want to build American capacity, Intel, yes, make sure we maintain our lead against China, well, why sell some of our most cutting-edge chips to China?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: It is a complete contradiction in -- in policy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the White House says, oh, they`re not great chips, so, therefore we can -- can sell them. That`s what the commerce secretary has said.\par \par \uc2 But on -- on the Intelligence beat, as the vice chairman of the Intelligence Committee, you have an oversight authority, and under law, the Intel agencies must inform that committee about their operations. The CIA has to tell you about covert actions. This is an important kind of oversight.\par \par \uc2 But, this past week, you said you were blocked from even attending a meeting at the \uc2 National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency\uc2  out in Virginia. Why do you think you were blocked? And has your Republican counterpart promised you that this isn`t going to continue?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: I have done these visits to our intel facilities for the 10 plus years I have either been chair or vice chair of the Intelligence Committee. They are part of the normal course. You get a chance to see the work force where they`re working. You get to see the new -- newest, best, coolest stuff.\par \par \uc2 And, oftentimes, I get to do a town hall meeting, because I`m not only the chair or vice chair, but many of these facilities are in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: So, this -- this meeting had been on the books for literally weeks, normal course of business.\par \par \uc2 This very unusual individual Laura Loomer, who is a right-wing blogger who is so controversial that the Trump administration didn`t even put her in -- in their Cabinet or -- and she has been a 9/11 denier. She`s been a virulent anti -- anti-Muslim individual. She has been a big proponent of working with white nationalists.\par \par \uc2 Yet she seems to have the ear of President Trump. So, his first national security adviser...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: She speaks to Cabinet members, including the secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, his first national security adviser got kicked out because she went after him. The head of the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 , who I saw with -- recently, General Tim Haugh, got fired. She claimed that firing.\par \par \uc2 Recently, we`ve seen 37 senior intel facility individuals lose their -- their security classification, which basically puts them out of a job. And she brags about all this on her blog. I mean, it really raises the question, is she actually the acting secretary of defense or secretary of war as of this morning?\par \par \uc2 Is she the real director of national intelligence? What she went after me on was something unrelated to this, and said, why can we let some anti- Trump person in to an intelligence facility? Strangely enough, Tuesday night, my meeting was canceled.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: No, we`re not going to stand up for that. I have talked to my Republican colleagues. I think you will...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. Is Tom Cotton promising you this won`t happen again?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: I`m going to get those meetings held. I have raised it with Cotton. I have raised it with the other Intelligence members.\par \par \uc2 You want to talk about something that is dangerous, you restrict Congress` ability to do oversight, particularly with this administration, then we are flying blind. And in the intelligence space, in particular, not every member can do this. Those of us who are senior on the Intel Committee, we are the limited numbers. We are the eyes and the ears of the rest of the Senate to do this oversight.\par \par \uc2 They try to restrict this, America will be less safe. And we`re going to -- I`m going to get those meetings.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, tell me about this campaign, it would seem, in Latin America, with the United States taking out this drug boat.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Rubio, your former colleague on the Senate Intelligence Committee says -- quote -- "If you`re on a boat full of cocaine or fentanyl or whatever headed to the United States, you`re an immediate threat to the United States." He is saying that is a national security justification to carry out a campaign with an unclear timeline.\par \par \uc2 Have you reached out to Rubio about this? Do you know anything about how it is being formulated here with the intelligence community?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, first of all, the Maduro regime in Venezuela are bad guys, and we obviously need to do all we can to stop fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 We have not been briefed on this. This was a DOD -- it was not an intel project. It was a DOD project. But my fear is, there are still international laws of the sea about how the process of interdicting these kind of boats -- there`s supposed to be a firing of a warning shot. You`re supposed to try to take it peacefully.\par \par \uc2 My understanding, this boat, none of those procedures were followed. And I`m going to get the full brief this week. But what I`m worried about is, if we put our sailors in harm`s way by violating international law, unless there is the appropriate designations, could this come back and -- and hurt those sailors? The sailors were doing their job.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, as far as you know, this is fully a military operation; it is not using the intelligence assets of the intelligence...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, we have not...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because the administration says they know everyone who was on that boat. They had great clarity and fidelity."\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: Well, I would like to see that data.\par \par \uc2 But this, again, goes to the point this kind of action should be briefed to the intelligence community. And I`m a member of what`s called the Gang of Eight. Those are the folks that are supposed to get the absolute secret stuff that only the president gets. I have gotten no briefing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No briefing on this?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: No briefing on this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are they conducting regular information-sharing with Gang of Eight?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: There are information-sharing. The level of sharing and the timeliness of the sharing, we...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: For example, we were -- on the Iran bombings, we were not notified beforehand. My Republican colleagues were notified.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: When you start making oversight and who gets shared information on a partisan basis, the...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER: This kind of cooking the books and intelligence, how we got into the war in Iraq in the first place.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Warner, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with a lot more Face the Nation, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our Norah O`Donnell spoke recently with Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  for \uc2 CBS\uc2  Sunday Morning.\par \par \uc2 Coney`s new book is "Listening to the Law: Reflections on the Court and Constitution."\par \par \uc2 Here`s part of their conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This court has issued stays or limited injunctions in cases regarding birthright citizenship, foreign aid funding freezes, federal work force reduction.\par \par \uc2 Will you -- can you answer that question? Do these repeated emergency or shadow docket interventions by the Supreme Court risk undermining public trust, especially if they are perceived to be politically motivated and favorable to President Trump?\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT\uc2  (\uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Associate Justice): Well, I think that the point of the book is that justices and judges, I want to include all federal judges in this, are deciding things on the merits.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, to the extent that any decision makes us unpopular, that`s part of the price of the job. We have to make our best judgment about what the law requires. And, sometimes, that might mean that the executive branch succeeds in litigation and sometimes not.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not our job to look across the docket and say, oh, we want to even things out so that sometimes the executive branch wins some, sometimes it loses some. That`s the antithesis of what our job entails. Our job entails making legal judgments about the cases that come before us. And we really can`t -- I mean, we want the court and I want the -- I want the public to have trust in the integrity of the court.\par \par \uc2 But the court can never change its decisions, its judgments about what the best -- what the right outcome is in order to tailor it to public opinion. The court`s supposed to render unpopular decisions sometimes, because, sometimes, the right legal answer isn`t necessarily the popular one.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you can see more of Norah`s interview on CBS Mornings tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation, Senators Roger Marshall and Tammy Duckworth, plus analysis on our new \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Republican Senator Roger Marshall, who joins us this morning from Wichita, Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 So --\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL (R-KS): Margaret, great to with you. I know it`s going to be a great discussion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. I know you were an OB/GYN in practice for, you know, decades before you ran for office. This past week the \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary underwent intense questioning from both Democratic lawmakers and some of your fellow Republicans who were also doctors.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Secretary Kennedy, in your confirmation hearings you promised to uphold the highest standards for vaccines. Since then, I`ve grown deeply concerned.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JAMES LANKFORD (R-OK): I would say effectively we`re denying people vaccine.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean that`s -- that`s the Republican whip and that is the chair of the committee, \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 . Why do you think they`re off base in their concern?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Look, President Trump chose \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  to a disruptor to the CDC. And that`s exactly what he`s doing. He`s focused on making the CDC more transparent, to make it more trustworthy right now. Right now Americans don`t trust the CDC. So, he is literally turning that place upside down.\par \par \uc2 I respect what my colleagues are saying. But I think that, you know, this whole issue today, or in that meeting, was about vaccines. In my humble opinion, not every person needs every vaccine. And I don`t think there is many children out there that need 76 jabs by the time they`re old enough to vote. But before you label me a non-vaxxer person, look, I`ve raised money for polio vaccinations. The MMR is a great vaccine. It saved thousands of lives. Vaccines overall have saved hundreds of millions of lives.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: But not every person needs every vaccine. And we just want to empower parents and the doctors to make great decisions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. I just want to unpack a few things you said there and just very -- because we want to be very careful in this very heated environment.\par \par \uc2 When you say you have a problem with trusting the CDC, it was just a few weeks ago a gunman walked on to the CDC campus in Atlanta and shot the place up.\par \par \uc2 Do you care to respond to that, and do you think that we need to be careful when we are discussing the CDC and public health officials right now?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Well, look, of course I condemn that shooting. But the -- the lack of confidence in the CDC goes back to what the CDC did during Covid. They -- they misguided us, maybe lied to us even, about the origins of Covid and how to treat it as well. And the vaccine, they -- they overpromised what the vaccine could do as well. So, that`s where the distrust is.\par \par \uc2 And now \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  is in there trying to clean up that distrust and trying to give American parents, and grandparents and the doctors the right information, transparent information, to make good decisions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. OK. But just before we talk about other vaccines, specific to Covid and what you just said. With the CDC it was -- the pandemic was during the Trump administration. It began during the Trump administration. And Operation Warp Speed was a presidential directive by -- by Trump, which some of your fellow Republicans say he deserves the Nobel Prize for because it stopped the pandemic. So, did you trust the CDC and Operation Warp Speed under President Trump, or are you saying you don`t think the president deserves the prize for that shot?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Yes, look, President Trump absolutely deserves the Nobel Prize. That vaccine saved millions of lives. But this is such a different time. That`s what my friends --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, because you just said something that sounded very contradictory to that.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: I disagree with you. I think it`s such a different time today than it was five years ago. Five years ago we had a novel virus. None of us had any immunity to it. It was a strange virus made in a lab in Wuhan, China.\par \par \uc2 But today, on average, Americans have had Covid five times. We now have natural immunity to it. And not everybody needs the vaccine. So, both things can be true. And that`s why when you have people that don`t understand science, that don`t understand medicine --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Why they don`t get it. It was a different day then than it is today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. But people do understand that when they go into their pharmacy and have a problem getting a shot, because in many states they are now told they need to go back and get a prescription, that the effect here of the Kennedy policies are making it more difficult, even if they fall into categories where they should be able to get a shot. You would acknowledge that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: I think you`re making this way, way too complicated. That, look, vaccines are a medicine. And just like you need a prescription to get a GLP-1 --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but what you said, he`s disrupting. Right, you said he`s disrupting, but effectively what you were hearing the Republican whip, \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 , and the Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  talk about was the -- the interruption of the availability of vaccines. Cassidy said, "I would say effectively we`re denying people vaccines." So, that`s not Margaret Brennan, that`s \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Yes. Look, I disagree. Respectfully disagree. Think about this. What the CDC is now saying is, if you`re over the age of 65, you can have the vaccine. It`s interesting that the U.K. and France have said 75 and 80 as the age. So, all you need to do is email or call your doctor`s nurse and get a prescription and you should be able to get it.\par \par \uc2 If you`re over the age of six months, you probably -- it`s probably a good idea to talk to your doctor. And all your nurse has to do is look at the chart and says, oh, little Johnny`s got asthma, definitely needs the vaccination as well. So, I think we`re making this way too complicated.\par \par \uc2 Why does everybody lose their minds whether it comes to Covid vaccine? Why can`t we let the doctor and the parents decide. Let the patients decide.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: There`s so many more important things out there we should be talking about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Chronic diseases.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Twenty percent of our adolescents have obesity, mental health problems and -- and -- and are under prescription drugs. There`s so many more things to talk about than just Covid vaccines.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, but I think we`re conflating a few things, including the operational aspect of this. I think people get frustrated with their insurance companies. I think people get frustrated with their pharmacies and the implementation of health policy. And you know that that`s more complicated than just saying we`re -- we`re changing a few things here. There are big implications. And I want to talk to you about it because you said you like the MMR vaccine, you like DPT, polio, smallpox. You don`t like hepatitis b, which is a liver infection, and you don`t think newborn kids me to be vaccinated against it.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cassidy, who is a liver doctor, said he treated poor and underserved patients and that it can be a life-altering condition for the rest of your life. So, why isn`t it a compelling argument to you that this should be available?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Yes. So, I delivered a baby -- I delivered a baby every day for -- for 25 years. We did a hepatitis test on every one of my patients. At the time of delivery, if that mom has a negative hepatitis test, she`s in a stable, monogamous relationship, she`s not doing IV drugs, she`s not letting her baby going to play in a sandbox full of used needles, then there`s zero chance that that baby`s going to have hepatitis.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s other moms that -- or other babies that do need it, OK? We need to be more specific. We can`t be overly prescriptive. If that mom has not had prenatal care, if she`s an IV drug abuser, if she`s not in a stable relationship, a whole lot of reasons, but we need to pick and choose. Not every baby needs the hepatitis vaccine. And especially on day number one. What are these vaccines doing to -- to --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: To mess with the immune system of -- of that particular baby as well?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: So, that`s my concern. So not every baby needs hepatitis b vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. And I understand it can also be spread from -- in a household sharing toothbrushes or razors or, for example, if her partner was not monogamous, the pregnant lady, for example.\par \par \uc2 But -- but I hear your point here, that you want this to be more bespoke, like, more picking and choosing. But others would argue here that -- that in doing that you are going to drive down immunization rates overall if you create broader doubt in vaccines. And that it`s a slippery slope here. Are you at all concerned that that is going to be the takeaway?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Look, \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  did more to create vaccine hesitancy than \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  will do in his four years or eight years, however long he is the secretary. That`s where the problem is. So, don`t blame \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  for the problems created by \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But this -- but I`m just telling you, parents today want more information.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m asking you about what you said about the hepatitis b vaccine.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: I`m sorry. Go ahead again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I wasn`t talking about \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 , or Secretary Kennedy, I was talking about you and what you said about the hepatitis b vaccine and are you concerned that picking and choosing these things like this, this bespoke concept of giving more choice, that it also has the effect of undermining confidence in vaccines?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Yes, I think this is a big difference between Democrats and Republicans. Democrats want a one size fits all prescription for everybody. And I`m telling you, not everybody reads the -- reads the book, does exactly like that. I have confidence in doctors, in nurses and parents and grandparents to make these decisions. I don`t think that we should have one government policy that dictates every one of these vaccines. I think local policy, local schools, if they want to have requirements, what Florida did was a bridge too far.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: But how about just a little common sense. Just a little common sense would go a long ways here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. OK. Because President Trump said he had some doubts about that Florida statement you just mentioned there, too, and that he likes a lot of vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, Secretary Kennedy founded this group that is arguing that women taking Tylenol or acetaminophen might be putting their babies at risk. They claim there`s a link to neurodevelopmental disorders. Would you take -- tell a pregnant woman to take a Tylenol and not feel worried about it?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Look, Tylenol is a category b drug. I`ve given it to lots of patients. It`s on the approved -- approved list.\par \par \uc2 But that being said, I try to tell my patients not -- my pregnant women not to take anything because, you know, we don`t know what we don`t know. I`m very cautious. You know, why --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: Why do 20 percent of our children have some type of a mental health illness right now? Why do we have so many peanut allergies? Why do we have so many people with autoimmune diseases?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: I -- we -- so, we don`t know. What we don`t know, we don`t know. And I want gold standard studies to help us figure this out and sort it out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ROGER MARSHALL: But as far as I know, Tylenol is OK in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you, Senator. We`ll leave it there for today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re joined now by Democratic Senator Tammy Duckworth. She joins us this morning from Schaumburg, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH (D-IL): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you attended, as I understand it, a demonstration yesterday in Chicago where thousands of people were out on the street protesting President Trump`s vow to send in immigration agents. I know he`s also posted on social media suggesting troops will go in. Quote, "Chicago is about to find out why it is called the Department of War."\par \par \uc2 Vice President Vance says there aren`t plans to send in troops. Do you have concrete indication now that there are plans to do so?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: Let`s make it clear, the president of the United States essentially just declared war on a major city in his own nation. This is not normal, Margaret. This is not acceptable behavior.\par \par \uc2 No, we don`t have any indications of them getting ready to send troops into Chicago. In fact, I was out at Great Lakes base yesterday, naval training base, and they were able to confirm that the only assistance they have been requested to provide is just office space for ICE, but that there are no barracks, no detention facilities, none of that is being requested or prepared to -- in order to support troops into Chicago.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So -- so then is this just, you know, social media trash- talk? When you say he`s declaring war, is this tongue-in-cheek?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: I think he`s renaming the Department of Defense the Department of War. And did he not just say that Chicago will find out what it means to be at war? I -- I don`t -- I take what the president of the United States says very seriously because that is the respect you have to give to the office. And if that`s what he`s declaring, then let me make it clear, it would be an illegal order to declare war on major city, any city, within the United States by the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator, I know you just mentioned you went out to the Great Lakes naval training session -- station, I should say, and you said, the military leadership said they`ll only get office space. ICE cannot bring in lethal munitions. And the resources will not be diverted from military training. So, all of that together, did you breathe a sigh of relief and say this won`t have a real impact on the military?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: Well, we`re going to make sure that there`s not an impact on the military. We need to make sure that as, you know, Great Lakes naval training base is where every single recruit in the \uc2 United States Navy\uc2  goes through to get their basic training, and then later on some advanced training. We need to make sure that not a single dollar, not a single penny of the resources that they have there should be -- that should be directed towards training our nation`s sailors and making them ready to do their jobs. None of that money needs to be diverted in order to support ICE or any of Donald Trump`s political, you know, theater efforts. We need to focus on making sure we turn out the very best sailors we possibly can out of the base.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, in terms of how Democrats are responding to this rhetoric, "The \uc2 Chicago Tribune\uc2 " faulted Governor Pritzker for his threats to, quote, "not stand idly by if the Guard went in." They wrote, "what does that threat actually mean? How about a conversation before the action or the response because it`s all potentially harmful to collective health." It sounds like there`s a lot of tension here. Is there an opportunity to work with the administration to avoid making this worse?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: Well, they`ve not -- they`ve not responded to any of our requests. I mean, I`ll give you a very concrete example. Senator Durbin, myself, Senator -- Congressman Schneider sent a request into DHS to ask them to explain what exactly are you going to be doing at Great Lakes, and can we come look at your facility so we have a better understanding of what your operations are, and they replied, no, you can`t, and, in fact, gave their staff the day off on the day that we went to go look at the facilities and locked the doors and left the base. Basically, they fled the base. This is not the action of someone that`s doing something legal or that they are -- that they`re proud of. They literally gave the people the day off, locked the doors and left.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Navy allowed us on to the post. They allowed us to tour the outside of the facility and also where they would be storing the non-lethal munitions. But DHS did not show up.\par \par \uc2 And we certainly have sent the administration multiple inquiry about what they are planning on doing, who are they bringing into Chicago. Are they planning to bring the National Guard in. They`ve none -- none of that. They`ve not even reached out to local law enforcement to try to coordinate. You know, if they were truly, truly interested in fighting crime, then they would work with local law enforcement and ask them, what do you need? What help do you need? And we`ve not gotten any, any communications or feedback from the administration whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you, in your armed services role, about what is a significance geopolitical meeting this past week. You had a lot of America`s adversaries gathering together. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , Xi Jinping, Kim Jong-un of North Korea, all of them in Beijing. And China and Russia seemed to be deepening their work together. What do you think the signal to the United States here is?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The signal to the United States is -- is exactly what, you know, these authoritarian regimes want to send, which is that they are the new world power, that America is losing its ground, and that they have utter disrespect for the United States. I think it was very, very clear. And it, you know, it doesn`t escape me the fact that once this meeting happened, which was humiliating to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he immediately responds by saying, well, then I`m going to rename the Department of Defense the Department of War and declare war in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 This is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does. When something bad goes -- happens to him in the news cycle, or when the Epstein victims come together and have a very emotional press conference, he changes the topic and distracts us by saying things like, I`m going to send ICE into Chicago, I`m going to do this or do that. The American people cannot be fooled. We cannot be -- allow him to distract us from what is truly happening, which is, this is a man who has driven our economy to a point where now there are more unemployed people than there are jobs. You know, we`re not selling our agricultural products overseas. And our adversaries are united against us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the unemployment rate is still low. But I hear you on a weaker than expected jobs number.\par \par \uc2 Just very quickly, there was military action taken in America`s backyard by the Trump administration. Vice President Vance says, "killing cartel members who poison our fellow citizens is the highest and best use of our military." He was talking about a strike on a drug boat -- alleged drug boat off the coast of Venezuela. What do you make of that show of force?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: It was an illegal move by the Trump administration. There is a thing called due process in this country, and that needed to have happened. And the fact that it happened in international order -- in international waters actually opens Americans to a similar action by our adversaries. You just -- we were just talking about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Xi Jinping and getting together and showing a show of force against the United States. Well, there`s nothing now to stop them from doing something like that against Americans in international waters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: And it could be civilians. And they could say, you know what, those were drug boats. We`re just going to go ahead and blow them out of the water.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well --\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY DUCKWORTH: You know, he`s setting a precedence here that puts Americans in danger.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Duckworth, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re back now with our executive director of elections and surveys, Anthony Salvanto, for more insight on today`s CBS News poll.\par \par \uc2 So, Anthony, let`s start on the economy. That jobs report for August was lower than expected. Inflation is down from its post-Covid peak, but it`s still not at that 2 percent level the Fed wants to get to.\par \par \uc2 How are people feeling?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Earlier this week I asked people, pick one word to describe the economy. And the top one they picked was "uncertain." And you see here, I think tariffs are a big part of that. We`ve already seen four in ten people tell us they`re cutting back on spending because of increases from prices that they see as part of tariffs. They think tariffs are going to raise prices in both the short term and the long term. And part of that is support for imposing new tariffs has been ticking down. It is now down again. Increasingly confined just to the president`s base, to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is their view that it will ultimately increase manufacturing jobs in the U.S., that people ought to be willing to pay a little bit more to support President Trump`s policies, but that view has not permeated beyond that base to the wider public, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we might need to wait until we see that job growth in those areas.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, I mean it could be, in some ways, that pull back a little bit of leading indicator as we go forward, right? People spending. The other part of this, too, is more broadly. And we talk about who and where you can impose tariffs. When we talk about influence on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , a presidential influence there, the lens of this is also about presidential power and how much influence a president can have over those levers on the economy.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I asked about this a little. And what we`re seeing is, Republicans very comfortable with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  having those levers. Other Americans, not so much.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that`s unusual for conservatives in particular who have, for so long, trumpeted free market economy, not a centrally planned one. So really liking that there is such control in the hands of a political leader is really unusual. That`s a change.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, it is in the sense that if you look back at polling over generations you would see Republicans, free markets, less government control. Here, so much of it, like so many things, centers around their trust in President Trump for that. But you also see some of those splits within Republicans where the MAGA base, they describe it as being efficient, and many others describing it as, you`re right, just more control.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it is that MAGA base that has reshaped the entire party.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, that`s what we mean really when we talk Republican these days.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about the National Guard. This does seem to unify Republicans, if I understand it, from your polling. The focus on immigration and law enforcement. This surge of federal resources to D.C. He`s talking about Portland and Chicago and Boston and Baltimore. What`s the perception of it?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, the lens on this touches on views of crime and what is effective of fighting crime, but also on rights and freedoms. Yes, for proponents of this, many of them Republicans, they`re saying it`s effectively about fighting crime. They say they`d support deploying the National Guard to other cities, like you mentioned. Even to their own area, whether there`s Democratic-run areas or Republican-run areas.\par \par \uc2 But for the majority, who are in opposition to this, in principle, they say, well, it`s going to impose on rights and freedoms. It`s going to make those rights and freedoms less secure, here again, through the lens of presidential power. The public generally would see sort of to the local leaders, the governor and the president all involved. One thing to note, and I think it`s related, speaking of the Republican base, the president`s overall approval rating is ticked up from July. It`s up two points. And it`s stabilized after having steadily declined over those early weeks and months of his term. That`s really helped buy Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And you look at how people evaluate the president. Republicans say they`re evaluating him more on immigration, deportation, crime, those issues. People who are evaluating him more on the economy, on inflation give him overall lower ratings.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anthony Salvanto, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s a sad morning for those of us here at FACE THE NATION as we learned that the long-time associate director, Mary Frances Sirianne, has passed away. She was here with a smile on her face every Sunday morning for decades before she retired in 2020.\par \par \uc2 For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020250907el9700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 5, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Maurice DuBois, Kelly O`Grady, Ed O`Keefe, Skyler  Henry, Dr. Jon LaPook, Lonnie Quinn, Chris Livesay, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,786 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: The labor market tightens. Unemployment rises. She served America for six years and now --\par \par \uc2 AUBREY CARTER (Military Veteran): I am unable to get health insurance for my kids because I cannot find a job.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A record migrant roundup in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MAN 1: We need construction to cease immediately.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: ICE arrests hundreds of workers at a Hyundai plant.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Carlo Acutis was just 15 when he died. Now Pope Leo is about to make him the first millennial saint.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Steve Hartman with a college senior pursuing a lifelong dream.\par \par \uc2 KENT BROUSSARD (\uc2 LSU\uc2  Marching Band Member): Your only failure is not trying.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From the CBS News Headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Good evening. I`m John Dickerson. \\\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: It is getting harder and harder to find a job because the economy is creating fewer and fewer of them. Only 22,000 last month, most of them in health care. All this according to a report today from the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You may recall the last negative report a month ago cost the Commissioner of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  her job. The President fired her, leaving it to the Acting Commissioner to preside over today`s report, which also shows unemployment ticking up to 4.3%, the highest since 2021.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: The weak jobs numbers mean there is a stronger chance the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will lower interest rates later this month, which the President has been demanding.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Shortly after the report came out today, Mr. Trump lashed out at the Fed Chairman, calling him Jerome "Too Late" Powell. Kelly O`Grady here now to put the report in perspective. And Kelly, the numbers were even lower than expected.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: That`s right. And I think here the takeaway is the labor market is slowing down and the unemployment rate of 4.3%. It is still considered healthy, but we have now seen four straight months of weaker job growth.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY (voiceover): The U.S. economy added 22,000 jobs in August, well below expectations. Some sectors fared better than others. Health care and social services continued to grow while the government and manufacturing sectors lost jobs. Manufacturing is down 78,000 total jobs this year. The report also delivered revisions to past months. The June number was revised to show the economy actually lost jobs for the first time since 2020. July, however, was adjusted up slightly. \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  is the Director of the \uc2 National Economic Council at the White House\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2  (\uc2 White House National Economic Council\uc2  Director): Inflation is low and economic growth is solid. Income grows solid as well, so that the only part of the data that`s disappointing is that the jobs numbers have been a little bit lower over the last three or four months.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY (voiceover): Revisions are common, and August numbers are often adjusted up. But today`s jobs report highlights the growing weakness in the labor market. From January to April, the economy added over 120,000 jobs on average each month. The last four months under 30,000.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: So, Kelly, we know the what, but why is this happening?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Well, if you`re a business and you`re facing uncertainty, you`re not going to go out there and hire a ton. You`re going to pause and you`re looking at trade policy, changing immigration policy, changing consumer sentiment. And I think manufacturing is a really good example of this.\par \par \uc2 As I mentioned, we lost jobs over the past year. That`s something, by the way, that`s been happening since 2023. But you have manufacturers right now faced with tariffs on steel and aluminum, which are big inputs. And so they`re saying, Well, I`m not going to go hire a ton when I`ve got costs going up. I will note, though, we haven`t seen mass firings, and that`s a really important point. I don`t want people to panic right now.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We heard the president blasting \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  once again today. How do we expect the Fed to react to today`s news?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: They get a green light to cut come September, and I think chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  is going to face questions of, "Did you wait too long to cut?" And you saw some of the in easiness in the market today. It sort of shot up when investors were saying, "Oh, we`re going to get a rate cut because of this jobs number." But then the uneasiness, the investors that I`m talking to are saying maybe we should have a bigger cut than a quarter point. Maybe we waited too long.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We shall see. Kelly O`Grady. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Later in the broadcast, a single mom in her own words about her struggles to find a job after serving the country in the military.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump today ordered the name of the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  changed to its original name.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: It was created under George Washington and called the Department of War. And so, it will be again. But why? A question for Ed O`Keefe at the White House.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, good evening. The Department of War was the title used until 1949 when branches of the military were united under the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 . The name President Trump described today is too politically correct as he signed the executive order rebranding the department. The word defense was literally being chisel off the walls of the Pentagon, and Secretary Pete Hegstead`s nameplate was updated to call him the Secretary of War.\par \par \uc2 The president says he`ll seek congressional approval for a formal name change. Today`s moves came as 10 F 35 jets were deployed to Puerto Rico as part of the Pentagon`s growing military operation against Venezuelan drug cartels. Earlier this week, a boat allegedly carrying drugs and 11 Venezuelan gang members was blown up in a U.S. Strike. And after two Venezuelan F-16 fighter jets flew near an American destroyer Thursday, the president today said of Venezuelan jets, try something similar again, they`ll be shot down.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Ed O`Keefe at the White House. Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Hundreds of people were working at a Hyundai plant in southeast Georgia when ice agents showed up and arrested them.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Most are Korean. It`s the largest sweep yet at a workplace in the president`s crackdown on illegal immigration. Skyler Henry is in Ellabell, Georgia, which is west of Savannah.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: All yours.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voiceover): Video filmed inside the plant shows armed homeland security agents executing a search warrant Thursday.\par \par \uc2 MAN 2: We need construction to cease immediately. We need all work to end on the site right now.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voiceover): Officers can be seen outside rounding up hundreds of workers in yellow vests. Nearly 500 people were arrested. The majority were South Korean nationals. U.S. Officials said all had either enter the country illegally, overstayed their visas or were not authorized to work. Steven Schrank is the special agent in charge.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN SCHRANK (Homeland Security Investigations Special Agent in Charge): Together, we`re sending a clear and unequivocal message that those who exploit our workforce undermine our economy and violate federal laws will be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voiceover): The massive $7.6 billion battery plant, a joint venture between South Korean companies, L.G. and Hyundai covers nearly 3000 acres and is the largest economic project in Georgia`s history.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): On behalf of all Georgia`s, I want to thank you --\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voiceover): Three years ago, Governor Brian Kemp welcomed its opening. Authorities alleged the plant had been under investigation for what it said were illegal practices for months.\par \par \uc2 CASEY DEAKIN (sp?) (Production Supervisor): There`s helicopters, Humvees, Georgia State Police --\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voiceover): Production supervisor Casey Deakin (sp?) was at the facility when the raid took place.\par \par \uc2 CASEY DEAKIN: I would assume they would continue, you know, production. However, with that significant loss with personnel, I don`t know how they`re going to go forward.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hyundai and L.G. said that they are cooperating with law enforcement. All of this comes just a week after the South Korean president met with President Trump at the White House to negotiate trade and tariffs. The Korean president pledged $150 billion by Korean companies into the U.S. and that includes 26 billion by Hyundai.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Skyler Henry in Ellabell, Georgia. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now, more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s evening news roundup. The Israeli military has begun blowing up high rises in Gaza like this one in preparation for a full-scale takeover of Gaza City.\par \par \uc2 Israel has accused \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of using the buildings as a base for attacks on its troops. Today marks 700 days since \uc2 Hamas\uc2  launched a surprise attack on Israel, killing 1200 people and taking 251 hostages.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: A landmark class action settlement has been reached, affecting artificial intelligence and intellectual property. The A.I. company \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  will pay $1.5 billion to the authors of half a million books. The authors claim \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  pirated their work to train its A.I. chatbots. The settlement works out to $3000 a book and is the first of its kind in the A.I.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Era and air travelers whose flights are canceled. They`re seriously delayed, will not be getting any additional compensation. The Trump administration is scrapping a rule proposed under President Biden, but never put into effect a story.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: The "Wall Street Journal" is reporting is getting a lot of attention. The journal says Health Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  plans to announce that the use of Tylenol by pregnant women may be linked to autism in Children. Acetaminophen is the active ingredient in Tylenol.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  calls the Journal`s report speculation and the maker of Tylenol, \uc2 Kenvue\uc2  said it believes there is no causal link to autism. Still, though, shares of \uc2 Kenvue\uc2  plunged nearly 10% today. Dr Jon LaPook is here.\par \par \uc2 And Jon, you`ve been doing some reporting on this all afternoon. What are you learning?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I spoke this afternoon to a researcher who was part of a major study published just last year that followed 2.5 million Children in Sweden over 25 years, he said. Use of acetaminophen, which is the active ingredient Tylenol during pregnancy, was not associated with an increased risk of autism in Children. In addition, we reached out to the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , and this is what they told us. They said there is no clear evidence that proves a direct relationship between the prudent use of acetaminophen during pregnancy and fetal developmental issues.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: So, John, people out there are still wondering, ah, what do I do? What do they do?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: \uc2 HHS\uc2  has called the "Wall Street Journal" report speculation, and I think we have to take them at their word. The reason why we`re even talking about this right now, right, is that the news is out there. There`s been a lot of reaction and concern, and we`re trying to help put things in perspective. Of course, RFK Jr. has promised a report on autism, which we look forward to, especially the evidence behind any conclusions and recommendations.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It`s all about showing their work.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Show your work. Show your homework.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Dr. LaPook, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, Lonnie Quinn is tracking two tropical weather threats, one Atlantic, one Pacific --\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: -- and we`ll have this story.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I`m Steve Hartman. However you plan to spend your retirement, I`m guessing it`s not like this. Meet the college senior tuba player when we go on the road to Baton Rouge, Louisiana, coming up on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We are less than a week away from the peak of hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: And Lonnie Quinn is tracking some tropical action in the Atlantic and the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie?\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: You bet. We`ll start with the Atlantic. So, here`s the situation. As of right now, we have this storm, all right? Two little clusters. You see one, two right there. Yesterday, that had a 90% chance to develop into Gabrielle. Today, the chances are down to a 70% risk. Why would it go down? And you`re also wondering, what`s that big brown swath on the TV screen? That is Saharan dust. And Saharan dust plays a big role in limiting the formation of a storm. Storms love moist air, all right? That feeds them. They become stronger. With that super dry air on top, it`s a lower chance to develop.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s still 70% chance, so it`s still likely we will see Gabrielle out of that system, but less of a chance today than what we saw yesterday. Do you want to show you what`s going on in Hawaii? Because they`ve been watching Kiko. Kiko is right now a category four storm. The best news for Hawaii, all right?\par \par \uc2 Right now, the Big Island is not even in the cone. It approaches the Big Island probably sometime on Tuesday, but now it`s a tropical storm. Winds are about 60 MPH, and as it pushes through the Hawaiian Islands, it should be backing down to maybe 40 MPH winds because it`s encountering the friction with the land. But all in all, a couple of different oceans and a couple of positive points to point out.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Okay, Lonnie Quinn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: And we started the broadcast with the Labor Department report showing a slowdown in job creation. We want to go behind the numbers now to look at the real-life impact of a weak labor market.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You will hear now from a \uc2 U. S. Army\uc2  veteran unable to find a job and struggling to support herself and her Children.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AUBREY CARTER (Military Veteran): My name is Aubrey Carter. I am 30 years old. I live in Atlanta, Georgia, and I have not been able to find work since I left the military in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Guys, you guys, let`s do our affirmations before school.\par \par \uc2 KAI: I am Kai (sp?). I am beautiful.\par \par \uc2 AUBREY CARTER: Even after serving six years, I am unable to get health insurance for my kids because I cannot find a job.\par \par \uc2 All right, I love you. Have a good day, okay.\par \par \uc2 KAI: Okay.\par \par \uc2 AUBREY CARTER: I`ll see you later.\par \par \uc2 I do Uber part time. I never thought I would be Ubering after the army for additional income. I do juices on the side. Today my customers are asking for a green juice. I thought that after the military that I was going to have health care for my kids, which is a very common misconception. I have insurance through the V.A. but my kids are uninsured.\par \par \uc2 How was your day at school today? Good. Last year, the kids caught the flu and we had to share medication because the Tamiflu was so expensive. I couldn`t afford it for three. I feel invisible because despite serving my country, despite being a veteran, the job market has still shown no mercy.\par \par \uc2 Good job like that. That`s a perfect P.\par \par \uc2 I want more for my kids than just giving them the bare minimum. But it makes me emotional because I just want them to have so much more in it. It hurts telling them no.\par \par \uc2 There you go. Clean. I would just really like for the people in power to know how much veterans truly have sacrificed.\par \par \uc2 ALL: I pray my soul to keep --\par \par \uc2 AUBREY CARTER: How hard it is for veterans to find employment to truly just keep up.\par \par \uc2 Good night, guys. I love you.\par \par \uc2 Good night, mommy:\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Hope she gets a break soon. My goodness.\par \par \uc2 The Catholic Church is about to get a new saint.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: An Italian teenager. His story is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Carlo Acutis was a teenager who wore jeans and Nike`s. That may not fit the image most of us have of a saint.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: But this Sunday Acutis will be canonized by Pope Leo as the first millennial saint. Chris Livesay has his story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY (voiceover): He wore jeans and Nike`s and love to write computer code. As a preteen, he started building a website cataloging miracles around the world. It went viral, spreading his global reputation as God`s influencer.\par \par \uc2 MAN 3: A genius with the Internet at the same time, this passion, this passion of life, passion for holiness, died tragically at the age of 15 in 2006.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY (voiceover): Just four days after he was diagnosed with leukemia.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA SALZANO (Carlo Acutis` Mother): He likes animal --\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY (voiceover): For his mother, mourning was transformed into a calling to see Carlo Acutis. Her son become Carlo Acutis, the saint, unlike any other saint in history.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: But what does it feel like? I mean, to see the relics -- to see the relics of your son, that`s not normally what a mother can experience when they lose a son.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA SALZANO: But I have likely the faith. So, this -- and I believe.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: But questions remain about whether sainthood has come too quickly. Some Vatican watchers point to the family`s wealth and relentless promotion of his cause. The catholic church says its requirements have been met, starting with miracles attributed to Acutis, like healing a boy`s pancreas in Brazil, reminding the faithful that sainthood can be as ordinary and as extraordinary as the life of a teenager.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You can see his mom`s pride all over her face special.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: When we were there for the funeral of Pope Francis, teenagers had come from all around the world for the canonization. That was what was supposed to be happening. And to hear teenagers talk about Acutis was to -- to watch people in totally enthralled with his message and his story. It was quite something --\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And he earned it clearly.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: It was amazing. Steve Hartman is next "On The Road."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Finally tonight, tuba or not tuba? That was the question. The answer came late in life for the man Steve Hartman met On The Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Now here`s something you don`t see every day or any day for that matter, unless you live in this formerly quiet neighborhood in Baton Rouge, Louisiana, where for the past year, 66-year- old Kent Broussard has been marching toward a dream, a dream he`s had ever since he was a little kid when he first saw the \uc2 Louisiana State University\uc2  marching band. Someday he`d play tuba for them, he thought.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And that day never came?\par \par \uc2 KENT BROUSSARD: It did not.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And at some point you get too old. You`ve got a job?\par \par \uc2 KENT BROUSSARD: Well --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You got a family.\par \par \uc2 KENT BROUSSARD: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You`re not going to go play for the \uc2 LSU\uc2  band.\par \par \uc2 KENT BROUSSARD: No, no, I wasn`t going to do that. I wasn`t going to play for the \uc2 LSU\uc2  band when I was 50. But 66 he thought maybe at least that`s what he told his wife Cheryl.\par \par \uc2 CHERYL BROUSSARD: And at first I was like, do they have like a senior band? I mean, I wasn`t sure what he was exactly talking about. And it was the real -- real baby.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): And so this year, this retired accountant went back to school just in the hopes of making the band. He did play tuba as a kid, but had completely forgotten. So, he relearned and trained hard for what was to be a very competitive audition process. Band Director Simon Holoweiko.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You didn`t cut him any slack.\par \par \uc2 SIMON HOLOWEIKO: No.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He doesn`t get any breaks for being a senior citizen.\par \par \uc2 SIMON HOLOWEIKO (\uc2 LSU\uc2  Marching Band Director): No, there are no breaks when you come here and we follow that process to get the best possible group we can.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): Which is why Kent was never really expecting to make the cut and why he was this excited when he did.\par \par \uc2 Kent is now the oldest person in the band by 41 years. His first game is tomorrow, and he hopes his little dot on the field will punctuate the importance of high stepping out of your comfort zone.\par \par \uc2 KENT BROUSSARD: Try it. The only thing you can do is fail. Your only failure is not trying.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): And your only risk annoying the entire neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman "On The Road" in Baton Rouge, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yeah, that`s fantastic. I love him getting in the gym. You got to be strong, be tough.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Go Kent. Yes, strong and it`s hot out there with that.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That sure is.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: That is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m John Dickerson. See you soon on EVENING NEWS PLUS.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It`s my turn. I`m Maurice DuBois. Have a great weekend. Thanks for watching for the week. We`ll see you on Monday.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250906el9500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For August 21, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Ed O`Keefe, Tom Hanson, Debora Patta, Rob  Marciano, Janet Shamlian, Jo Ling Kent, Lonnie Quinn \par \uc2 3,719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . Maurice DuBois is off tonight.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Erin is still whipping up the weather along the Northeast Coast. The threat of 17-foot waves and 50-mile-an-hour winds prompted the governor of New Jersey to declare a state of emergency and to declare, because of rip currents, don`t go in the water. Many beaches in the New York City area are also closed for swimming.\par \par \uc2 The hurricane hit North Carolina`s Outer Banks with powerful gusts and large swells. Tonight, the storm is pulling away from the East Coast and heading into the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist Rob Marciano leads us off from Virginia Beach.\par \par \uc2 How are the conditions there, Rob?\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Oh, John, the winds are still ripping here along the coastline. Even with Erin over 100 -- 350 miles away, that huge wind field continues to agitate the ocean here, and this surf has just been relentlessly punishing the Eastern Seaboard.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (voiceover): The western edge of Hurricane Erin delivering coastal flooding and strong winds to the shores of North Carolina hours before the massive storm finally began to move away from the U.S., seaside homes enduring the onslaught of ocean water.\par \par \uc2 And despite days spent shoring up important arteries like Highway 12, state transportation authorities kept bridges closed to ensure they would withstand the weather.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We are worried about the next high tide cycle tonight and the tide cycle after that in the morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Five different species of sea turtles visit or nest on the Outer Banks. And this video shows powerful waves pushing through the habitats designed to protect them.\par \par \uc2 And as waves pounded the shores of Virginia Beach, Jeremy Williams (sp?) and his family tried to make the best out of the bad weather.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY WILLIAMS (Virginia Beach): Know before you go, right? Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Prepare for the worst.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY WILLIAMS: Prepare...\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hope for the best.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY WILLIAMS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Thirty-five million people are under coastal flood advisories up and down the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is on New York`s Long Island, where we`re seeing extreme surf conditions.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Even with Erin more than 200 miles offshore, we are still feeling its impacts here at Jones Beach. Take a look behind me. This is what should be a sand walkway, but instead it has been swallowed by coastal flooding from those waves that are reaching as high as 10 feet.\par \par \uc2 And when you factor in high tide and waves that could be as high as 16 feet overnight, this floodwater is expected to rise.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Back in the Carolinas, waves climbed over 15 feet and surfers from across the region were flocking to the beach to ride the swell.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: We have some neighbors that are big surfers, and they said to come out and check out the waves. So we wanted to bring the kids out and kind of see that and experience it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Well, we haven`t seen any surfers either in Kitty Hawk or here in Virginia Beach. I mean, these waves and these swells are just too big. And I`m told it`s not fun and it`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are lifeguards on duty, but not to rescue people, but to make sure that people don`t even get into the water, it`s so rough here, and will continue to be in the next couple of days -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Rob Marciano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now we turn to Lonnie Quinn, who joins us to tell us what we can expect from this storm and maybe some other weather.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: OK, you betcha.\par \par \uc2 So the good news report on Erin, from this point forward, it`s getting farther and farther out to sea. All right, it`s made its closest pass. It`s also in the process of weakening. When you look at a satellite image and you see all those bright colors, the reds, the yellows, the oranges, where you don`t see the bright color, that is the storm ingesting some dry air, and that will erode the storm.\par \par \uc2 This is a weakening storm. It`s kind of falling apart. It`s a low-grade Cat 2, Cat 1 overnight, and downgraded as we push on from there. The story has been the waves. And that did play out. That`s certainly verified. Some of the wave heights didn`t max out at that big number that we thought we might see. But, certainly, they were there.\par \par \uc2 For tomorrow, it`s not entirely gone. So, for the Eastern Seaboard -- and I`m talking about basically the Northeast -- I`d stay out of the water tomorrow, but look at this, good news for Saturday. That`s a key day, this weekend. It`s good to go back in the water, and a lot of folk getting their last weekend in before school starts. So have at that.\par \par \uc2 All right, I do want to talk about that other story. And here it is. It`s the West Coast heat anywhere, top to bottom, Portland, Oregon, going all the way down to Lancaster, California, 100 degrees or hotter for everybody out there. Las Vegas hits 110 tomorrow. And I know you folks in Vegas are used to big-time heat; 110 -- you have been hotter this summer, but 110 for tomorrow, that does set a record for the day.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the very latest from here in the weather department. It`s all yours, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Wet on one coast, scorching on another.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : For the last week, residents of Washington, D.C., have witnessed the accumulation of force on their streets, federal officials and National Guard troops, local and from other states.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the commander in chief reviewed the troops, President Trump traveling into the city to highlight his takeover of the D.C. police.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe joins us with details -- Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: John, the president tonight thanked dozens of federal law enforcement officers working out of U.S. Park Police headquarters in the Anacostia neighborhood of Washington. That`s closer to a part of the city that sees more violent crime than where we have seen National Guard troops deployed in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as a poll shows a majority of Washington residents oppose the federal takeover. On Wednesday, the vice president was heckled during a visit with Guard troops. Since the takeover began, however, federal agents working with D.C. police have arrested at least 630 people and seized 86 illegal firearms.\par \par \uc2 D.C. police confirmed to CBS News there`s not been a reported homicide in Washington since last Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, Ed, I want to ask you quickly about a major decision on the civil fraud case against the president. What happened?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 A New York appellate court threw out the more than a half-billion-dollar penalty the president owed in the civil fraud case against him, with one of the justices arguing the fine was excessive. The president, whose company had been found guilty of criminal tax fraud last year, today declared total victory.\par \par \uc2 But the case is far from over. The decision merely tosses out the fine, not the underlying fraud ruling against him. He`s still barred from doing business in New York state for three years, his sons for two years. The president can appeal the case to the state`s highest court. And the New York attorney general tonight says she will appeal the decision that tossed out the fine -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ed O`Keefe at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 California`s Senate today approved a redrawn political map intended to give Democrats at least five more seats in the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 . Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  hopes to get this on the ballot for the special election in November. The Democrats are doing this to counter the Republican redistricting plan in Texas. Other states are preparing similar redistricting efforts.\par \par \uc2 Overseas, Russia attacked Western Ukraine overnight. At least one cruise missile struck an American-owned electronics plant that President Zelenskyy says makes coffee machines, among other items. Zelenskyy said the Russians attacked -- quote -- "as if there were no efforts by the world to stop this war."\par \par \uc2 And an ominous warning today from Israel`s military, advising medical officials to prepare for evacuation of Gaza City. Israel is preparing an offensive to take over the entire city and move its more than half-a- million residents to shelters in the south.\par \par \uc2 In the Gaza Strip, the U.S. and Israeli-backed Gaza Humanitarian Foundation, or GHF, was established to replace the United Nations as the main distributor of aid. In late June, the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  approved $30 million for the group, calling its work absolutely incredible.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta spoke with a former insider with the GHF and joins us from London.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: According to the \uc2 U.N.\uc2 , hundreds of Palestinians have been shot and killed by \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  and foreign military contractors at or near GHF aid sites.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to a new eyewitness who concurs. He told us, it`s not only the idea of shooting, but also personnel hired through American subcontractors to secure GHF sites.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIKE (Former Aid Truck Driver) (through dubbed voice): I thought I was going there to help people.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voiceover): When this man was hired by an American logistics company to drive aid trucks in Israel, he says he had no idea it meant he`d be working with the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation inside Gaza.\par \par \uc2 He asked us to conceal his identity, dub over his voice and change his name because he fears reprisals. We will call him Mike. He secretly recorded several videos, including this one. What you are hearing, he says, is the sound of gunfire from brightly lit GHF sites fired at Palestinians seeking aid.\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): It took me two or three days to realize that they were actually shooting at people. They weren`t shooting at combatants.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: So did you think these were warning shots?\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): No. It`s indiscriminate.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And who was doing the shooting?\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): The \uc2 IDF\uc2  and the American security personnel. They have got positions on each corner of the sites, and they would often shoot at exactly the same time as the Israeli soldiers.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: We do not see who was shooting on the videos Mike gave us, but he said there was not a single occasion he observed...\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: ... when there was no shooting. And he was at the sites on average five days a week for several weeks.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has seen his work schedule and metadata from his cell phone confirms the dates and times he was in Gaza. CBS News has been reporting on the almost daily shootings and deaths at or near GHF sites since GHF began operating its sites at the end of May. Palestinians would gather near the sites hours before they opened to be the first to grab food.\par \par \uc2 (HEAVY BREATHING)\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): I was breathing quite heavily because I`d never seen a crowd of people behave with such intensity and such desperation.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: What`s the worst thing you experienced?\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): I was brought outside the perimeter and I was tasked with cleaning up the remains of animals and some humans. What had happened is that there had been so much shooting that human remains had been left there, and the smell got quite bad.\par \par \uc2 So the security contractors had complained, and the \uc2 IDF\uc2  said it wasn`t their business. So I was tasked with cleaning it, but I wasn`t warned what it was.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And you were told to clean up everything there, and do what?\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): Just put it in the back of a truck, which took everything away. I struggle to talk about it. I even feel a bit clammy, and I can feel my chest beating harder. I just shut down, really.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Over 1,800 Palestinians have died trying to get food, more than 1,000 near GHF sites, according to the U.N.`s \uc2 Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GHF says allegations that their contractors have shot aid seekers and mishandled their remains are "categorically false" and "utterly baseless." They have criticized CBS News` refusal to provide them with more detailed information regarding Mike.\par \par \uc2 Mike said that some of the Americans hired to secure the GHF sites made him feel uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): They would often boast about how many people they have killed, if they have managed to shoot animals, or how many birds they`d shot because they were bragging about how good their aim was.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: So, they -- if -- they would shoot anything?\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): Anything that was living, even birds.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Mike is home now and won`t be going back.\par \par \uc2 Why are you doing this?\par \par \uc2 MIKE (through dubbed voice): It just wouldn`t sit right with me if I didn`t say something. These atrocities don`t have to happen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: GHF told CBS News that their contractors do not fire on civilians, nor, they say, has anyone been killed by gunfire at a GHF site, not even within sight of a foundation site. And they say they have never encountered any situation involving unclaimed bodies at or near their sites.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IDF\uc2  categorically denied allegations that it deliberately fires at Palestinian civilians, but told CBS News it is investigating recent reports of harm to civilians and any violations will result in further action.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Debora Patta in London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS: Newly unsealed video show TikTok employees voicing some of the same concerns about the app that got the social media platform sued.\par \par \uc2 And we will have this:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: I`m Janet Shamlian near Waco, Texas, where some farmers are changing course. They`re still working these fields, but in a different type of way.\par \par \uc2 That`s ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And later: the historic heist that made the Mona Lisa the most famous painting in the world.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Last fall, the attorney general of North Carolina, joined by 12 other states, sued TikTok, alleging that the social media platform was designed to be addictive, particularly for minors, and that users and their parents were deceived about the risks.\par \par \uc2 Newly unsealed video in that case shows TikTok employees voicing some of those same concerns.\par \par \uc2 Here`s senior tech and business correspondent Jo Ling Kent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Mission accomplished. Some rice.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): As 170 million Americans scroll TikTok...\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hands down, this is the best app.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: ... we`re getting a new look inside the company. This video compilation unsealed by a North Carolina judge and obtained by CBS News shows employees had candid internal discussions about TikTok`s impact on users` well-being.\par \par \uc2 BRETT PETERS (TikTok): We have these expectations and goals and they don`t necessarily -- they aren`t necessarily congruent with, like, good mental health.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The edited video was released by the state of North Carolina, which sued TikTok and its Chinese parent company, \uc2 ByteDance\uc2 , last year, alleging the company knew the social media app was addictive and deceived parents and kids about the potential safety risks.\par \par \uc2 Alexandra Evans worked on safety at the company.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA EVANS (Former TikTok Head of Safety Public): I mean, the product in itself has baked into it compulsive use.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In response, TikTok said the clips are misleading and cherry-picked and called the edited video "a shameful attempt to distort an open internal conversation about making the platform safer when TikTok was just beginning five years ago."\par \par \uc2 The release of this video comes just two days after Minnesota Attorney General Keith Ellison also sued TikTok, alleging it`s violated consumer protection laws.\par \par \uc2 KEITH ELLISON (Minnesota Attorney General): My office is suing TikTok for preying on Minnesota`s young people, for violating Minnesota law with addictive algorithms and exploitative features.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: TikTok told us the new Minnesota lawsuit is based on misleading and inaccurate claims, adding, they have more than 50 features to help young people use the app and offer controls for parents too.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jo Ling Kent, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next, looking at a niche industry that relies on sheep labor.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Texas is not only the top oil-and-gas-producing state in the country. It`s also among the nation`s top solar energy producers. The solar farm boom in the Lone Star State has created thousands of jobs and unique opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Janet Shamlian with tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES (Business Owner): This is the best thing I have ever done.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): In a dusty Texas field, Chad Raines (sp?) surveys his new crop...\par \par \uc2 (SHEEP BLEATING)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: ... sheep.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever think this would be your industry?\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES: Never in my wildest dreams did I imagine that I would be a shepherd.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Raines family had farmed cotton for generations, until two years ago, when they gave it up for good.\par \par \uc2 When did it hit you, you had to get out of cotton?\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES: After about three or four years of just not making any progress, just not even breaking even every year. I mean, we were losing money, and that debt was rolling over, adding to it every year. So I had to look at doing something different. I had to think outside the box.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: So he became part of a growing trend, abandoning traditional farming for what`s called solar grazing, renting out sheep as lawn mowers for solar farms like this one near Waco.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of the largest in the U.S., with more than a million panels, and owned by Enel North America.\par \par \uc2 Adam Sotirakopoulos runs the site.\par \par \uc2 ADAM SOTIRAKOPOULOS (Enel North America): The sheep work when it`s raining. The sheep work when it`s muddy. Sheep work at night.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: These are your moneymakers.\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES: They are.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The sheep do most of the work, keeping vegetation away from the panels, while their owners earn steady paychecks.\par \par \uc2 So this is saving the solar company a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And it`s making you a couple of dollars.\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES: It is. It`s -- and it`s a good living.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Farming has always been a gamble, but the odds have gotten worse. A 2022 \uc2 USDA\uc2  report found more than half of small and mid-sized farms in the U.S. operated at or near a loss.\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES: The small farmer is going away. It`s the big companies. The bigger farmers are the ones that can farm a little bit better, based on the economies of scale.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Between low prices and small yields, Raines says, if he`d grown cotton, he would have lost almost $200,000 last year. Instead, he cleared a profit of close to $300,000, grazing 6,000 sheep across 20,000 acres, a growing business he can eventually pass on to his sons.\par \par \uc2 This is sustainable?\par \par \uc2 CHAD RAINES: Absolutely. One is a college graduate. One is in college. And when he graduates, he`s going to come to the farm as well. And we -- there`s plenty of work for all of us.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Amid a changing landscape, making ends meet while still staying rooted in the farmland.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Janet Shamlian in Riesel, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We end tonight with art history concerning perhaps the most celebrated piece of art in the world, Leonardo da Vinci`s Mona Lisa.\par \par \uc2 On this day in 1911, the priceless portrait was stolen off the wall at the \uc2 Louvre Museum\uc2  in Paris. It was an inside job. Vincenzo Peruggia, a former Louvre worker, spent the night in the museum and then plucked the work from the frame and smuggled it out under a white smock, common dress for museum employees at the time.\par \par \uc2 Peruggia later claimed he wanted to return the painting to Italy, believing Napoleon had stolen it. The theft became a global sensation. The Mona Lisa, La Joconde in French, became the most famous painting on Earth. Peruggia kept it hidden in his Paris apartment for more than two years, until December 1913. He was caught after trying to sell it to an art dealer, who alerted authorities.\par \par \uc2 Peruggia served only months behind bars and was considered a patriotic hero in Italy. The Mona Lisa, now housed in a climate-controlled, bulletproof enclosure, will be moved to its own wing in the coming Louvre renovation to accommodate its millions of visitors, who will also pay an additional fee.\par \par \uc2 That`s the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . Be sure to join us for the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250822el8l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  Reaches Tentative Settlement; Trump and Putin Phone Call; High Stakes Meeting; Skeptical about Future of War; Hurricane Erin`s Coastal Impact; Fallen Utah Police Officers Honored; Deadly Texas Measles Outbreak Ends; \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  Settles Defamation Suit; Labubu Warning; Ketamine Queen Takes Plea Deal; Ozempic`s Slimmed Down Price; Taylor Swift`s Business Mastermind; A.I. in the Classroom; Coastal Emergency; Easy School Lunches for Kids; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Inside Edition`s New Chapter; Surrogate Grandparents; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Tom Hanson, Nancy  Cordes, Holly Williams, Rob Marciano, Kelly O`Grady, Carter Evans, Jo Ling  Kent, Nancy Chen, Ash-Har Quraishi, Lisa Ling \par \uc2 15,483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Tuesday as you welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off. Guess what? It`s his birthday. Wish him a Happy Birthday if you see him walking around the street. He loves hugs.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot of news this morning, including a big development overnight in the Ukraine negotiations. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): The war is going to end, and this gentleman wants it to end, and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants it to end. I think the whole world is tired of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ukraine`s President returns to the White House with European allies. President Trump agrees to back security guarantees.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hurricane Erin churns in the Atlantic, prompting urgent warnings and evacuations along the eastern seaboard.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: It`s all about those choppy seas, rip currents and beach erosion.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Breaking News: \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  reaches a tentative deal to end its flight attendant strike.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We cannot and we will not turn our back on these workers.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: After years of railing against mail-in voting, the President says he`s moving to get rid of it for good ahead of the 2026 midterms.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Concerns for world number one Jannick Sinner, less than a week away from his U.S. Open title defense.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Mr. Sinner cannot continue.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: He retired from the Cincinnati Open Final against Carlos Alcaraz through illness.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Alcaraz, a little sportsmanship here as well as he writes on the camera, "Sorry, Jannick."\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: In the Little League World Series --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: This is a good battle between Nevada and South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Players -- that one`s on the ground in the right. Tillis, he lost the ball. Vargas is coming in. He`s safe. Nevada will walk off for the seventh and win a thriller, one to nothing.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The team is now one win away from the U.S. Championship.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, you`re getting all tingly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Actually, yes, yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony`s face just lit up here at the table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am getting a bit of the feels.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it bringing back memories?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve got some glory days.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lifelong memories are locking them in there. I love it. Those kids were having a great time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of my favorite events of the year.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of news to get to, including breaking news overnight.\par \par \uc2 Flight attendants, just moments ago, say they have a deal to end their strike against \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 . It has forced cancellations of thousands of flights that could affect schedules for you, even today.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is at LaGuardia Airport here in New York.\par \par \uc2 Tom, good morning to you. What are we learning?\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hey there, Tony. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Union says that it worked overnight for as many as five hours with \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  and a mediator present in order to reach this tentative deal. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  says that it hopes to resume at least some of its flights today after three full days of strikes that brought this company to a screeching halt, and I want to get people up to speed on how we got here.\par \par \uc2 That`s when 10,000 flight attendants on Saturday walked off the job after months of failed negotiations over wages. Now, what they agreed to is not clear yet, but what we do know is \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  previously offered a 38 percent increase in total compensation over four years, which the company says would have made its flight attendants the best compensated in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Another major sticking point, at least according to the Associated Press, is so called ground pay, or compensation for work done between flights such as boarding and de-planing, which until now, was unpaid.\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  has not commented on the agreement, but they say "... the suspension of our service is extremely difficult for our customers," adding that they "... deeply regret and apologize for the impact on them and of this labor disruption."\par \par \uc2 Now I walked over to the \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  counter this morning to get a reaction. They were tight lipped. I might have even received what they called to be a Gen Z stare, but if you look up at the board here, you can see that some flights are at least showing that they`re boarding. Other flights are still cancelled.\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  does say that it is going to take some time for things to get back to normal, but people can book with confidence with \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 , because during this ratification process and the negotiation, there cannot be another lockout. There cannot be another strike -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow! People will be glad to have them back in business.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the next steps in the push to end this war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The White House says President Trump again spoke with Russia`s \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  yesterday IN what the Kremlin says was a 40-minute phone call. Now, that came after Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy visited the White House yesterday along with key European allies. The group got the President to agree to security guarantees for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 We understand there is an effort now to have Putin and Zelenskyy meet face- to-face. Do you really think that`s going to happen anytime soon?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I think this is THE greatest chance we`ve seen so far, Gayle. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 During that call with the Russian President, President Trump worked to arrange this meeting between Putin and Zelenskyy, which the German Chancellor told reporters could happen within the next two weeks. This would be the first meeting between these two leaders since the war began three and a half years ago. And a Russian spokesperson, though, would only say that high-level talks would take place and did not confirm that Putin himself would be involved.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And I have a feeling you and President Putin are going to work something out.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Five hours of meetings between President Trump, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  and European leaders yielded two key results. First, a promise from President Trump that the U.S. will help provide security guarantees designed to protect a post war Ukraine from future invasions by Russia.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER (British Prime Minister): The other material outcome was the agreement that will now be a bilateral agreement between President Putin and President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That was British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , one of seven European power players who all flew into D.C. in a show of support for Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian President): Thank you very much for your efforts.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: After Trump appeared to find common cause with Putin last week, following their summit in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We had an extremely productive meeting, and many points were agreed to.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In a revealing hot mic moment yesterday, Trump seemed to suggest to French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  that Putin would end the war as a favor to him.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I think he wants to make a deal for me. You may understand that. As crazy as it sounds.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump did agree with \uc2 European Commission\uc2  President \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  that the return of an estimated 20,000 Ukrainian children kidnapped by Russia at the start of the war should be a top priority.\par \par \uc2 URSULA VON DER LEYEN (\uc2 European Commission\uc2  President): Every single child has to go back to its family.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But Trump remained lukewarm Monday on the need for a ceasefire, even as the Europeans insisted there must be an immediate end to Russia`s daily bombings before serious peace talks can begin.\par \par \uc2 FRIEDRICH MERZ\uc2  (German Chancellor): I can`t imagine that the next meeting would took place without a ceasefire. So let`s work on that, and let`s try to put pressure on Russia.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The group did not discuss what should happen to the 20 percent of Ukrainian territory that is currently controlled by Russian forces? Zelenskyy said that`s a matter he`ll only hash out with Putin himself.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY: All the sensitive things, territorial et cetera, we will discuss on the level of leaders during trilateral meeting.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And so now, the pressure is on Putin to accept and attend this meeting. He has always had a long list of demands that he said needed to be met before he would sit down with Zelenskyy, but he told Trump on Friday he was open to it, so the world will be watching, Vlad, to see if he follows through.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The stakes in all of this could not be higher for Ukrainians. Russia continues to hammer the country with missile and drone strikes, even as talks continue.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has covered this war since the beginning, and she`s once again in Ukraine, speaking with people about the latest developments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: For many Ukrainians, it`s difficult to believe that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  really wants peace.\par \par \uc2 A swarm of Russian drones killed at least five people in an apartment complex yesterday, according to Ukrainian officials. Rescue workers tried to save this baby girl, but she didn`t make it.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  has survived nearly three-and-a-half years of war as Ukraine`s leader, but he may now face his biggest challenge.\par \par \uc2 If Zelenskyy makes territorial concessions as part of a ceasefire deal, permanently giving parts of Ukraine to Russia, some here will see it as weak or foolish.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Too many people have suffered and died," said this woman in Kyiv. "Did they die for nothing and we will give away land?"\par \par \uc2 But if Zelenskyy doesn`t make concessions, he risks alienating President Trump again, as he did back in February.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You don`t have the cards right now.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA (People`s Deputy of Ukraine): So of course, it hurts to see that Russia is not being pressured, that nothing has been done to Russia.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Oleksandra Ustinova is a member of Ukraine`s Parliament.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that President Trump understands Ukraine and this war, or is he somewhat naive?\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA: I think that President Trump still believes that Putin can be negotiated, and we, having all of these years of negotiating \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , do not believe a single word he says because he always lies and breaks his own word.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ukrainians know that international agreements have failed to protect their country in the past, and some fear it wouldn`t be any different this time, even with security guarantees from the U.S. -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Holly, President Trump was pretty adamant that a meeting between Zelenskyy and Putin is imminent. Are we on the brink of peace here?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Well, look, none of the players can afford to simply reject President Trump`s efforts and his optimism, that would be political suicide. So they have to do the diplomatic dance. They have to play along, Vlad, willing or not.\par \par \uc2 Now, for his part, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , I think, is thrilled to have the red carpet rolled out for him by the U.S., but it seems unlikely that at this point he really wants to end the war. After all, Putin has called Ukraine`s democratically elected government, an "illegitimate regime."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, the clock is ticking. Holly Williams in Ukraine for us. Thank you, Holly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To weather now.\par \par \uc2 Forecasters are giving urgent warnings for millions along the Eastern Coast due to Hurricane Erin. The massive storm is not going to make direct landfall, but it is expected to make waves, quite literally; dangerous rip currents as well.\par \par \uc2 Erin brushed by the Turks and Caicos Islands yesterday, bringing heavy rain and causing widespread flooding. And parts of North Carolina have already said, "Don`t go in the water." They banned swimming on beaches there, although it does look beautiful in Kitty Hawk right now, not bad at all. People should be getting out of there soon, however.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is here to show us what to expect in the hours ahead.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, parts of the outer bands have been ordered to evacuate, especially Ocracoke Island. There is our storm. By the way, we got another one right behind it. So even though this one`s going to miss the U.S., we`re going to have some big impacts, and then we`ve got one right behind as we get into the peak of hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is a Category 3 storm. It is going to waffle between two and three over the next 48 hours. The track is going to bring it close enough to where we`re seeing immense amount of rip current. Look, the entire East Coast going under the gun, not just today, but tomorrow, really through the rest of the week, from Florida all the way up through parts of Southern New England, so that`s dangerous swimming for sure, and we`re going to see winds that get very close towards the coastline as well.\par \par \uc2 This storm has really grown in expanse of its wind field. Across, we`re looking about nearly 600 miles of tropical storm force winds. So when it gets abreast of the Outer Banks here, late today, on Wednesday and Thursday morning, we`ll probably see some tropical storm conditions, and then it does peel off.\par \par \uc2 But again, we`re going to see rip currents. We`ll see heavy surf. We`ll see storm surge as well. We`ve got tropical storm warnings that are posted for this area, as well as Storm Surge Warnings with surges expected two to four feet, which doesn`t sound like much. When we`re talking about the waves that are being generated by this that`s going to be very, very intense. We`re talking about nine footers, 10 footers in Florida, 15 to 23-foot waves, potentially across the Outer Banks. That`s not just dangerous, but that`s going to do some structural damage to the beaches and some homes there, so we`ll be watching that carefully over the next 48 hours -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Rob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Northern Utah, where the community is honoring two fallen police officers. Thousands lined the streets last night in an hours` long tribute to Sergeant Lee Sorenson and Officer Eric Estrada. Authorities say they were shot dead while responding to a domestic dispute in Tremonton, Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 A Sheriff`s Deputy and his service dog were wounded, both are recovering. The suspect faces multiple charges, including two counts of aggravated murder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Texas Health officials say the deadly measles outbreak that killed two children in their state is finally over. This is good news.\par \par \uc2 More than 760 cases have been confirmed since January, with two-thirds affecting little kids. State officials say no new infections have been recorded in more than six weeks. That is a threshold used to declare outbreaks over.\par \par \uc2 The Texas flareup was traced back to a Mennonite community near the New Mexico border, which authorities say has a history of low vaccination rates.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The conservative cable channel, \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  has agreed to pay $67 million to settle a lawsuit related to the 2020 election. Dominion, a voting machine company, accused \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  of spreading lies that it used its technology to change votes in the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  did not apologize, calling its coverage fair and balanced. Dominion says it is pleased to have settled this matter. Two years ago, it collected $787 million in a similar settlement with Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In this morning`s "Money Watch," a warning regarding the latest craze in plush toys. You may have heard of them. They`re called Labubus, fun to say, they`ve taken over the internet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. They`re fun!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But there is concern about La-bogus ones, they could be fake. They could be dangerous to