0.00 and despite my own understanding, they are selling out online and in stores.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Now, Labubus, they`re part of the blind box trend, where the specific doll comes inside a sealed package, then you sort of reveal what it is. The frenzy has led to thefts, including this one near Los Angeles, where about $7,000.00 worth of dolls were taken and it has also inspired counterfeits, not La-bogus, but they are called Lafufus.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, I like that! All right.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So, U.S. investigators have identified multiple shipments of look-alike toys sent from China. According to the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2 , the fraudulent dolls can break apart easily and are small enough to lead to choking hazards.\par \par \uc2 This is the important thing here. The watchdog group is warning parents to stay away from the fakes and only buy legitimate Labubus. This, by the way, is a fake. This is a Lafufu.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: that Lafufu looks pretty good!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, it is pretty -- it`s pretty similar. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look, okay, so how can people tell they have Lafufu and not a Labubu.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Right. Okay, so there a few things you can check for. Real Labubus, they feature a holographic sticker with a scannable Q.R. code that links to the Pop Mart website. Also count the teeth. Authentic ones have nine. This guy has 10.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay!\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And make sure you only purchase from trusted stores, right? If it`s too good to be true, the price is too good, it is probably -- it`s not right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whose is that one?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: It is my boss` daughters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh that belongs to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You better watch out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was asking for Celine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That