Sangha, known as the "Ketamine Queen" agreed to plead guilty to five federal charges saying she knowingly distributed ketamine to Perry, a successful actor and author who struggles with drug addiction were well- documented.\par \par \uc2 Sangha`s lawyer, Mark Geragos had previously denied the allegations in a recent Peacock documentary.\par \par \uc2 MARK GERAGOS (Attorney): My client never met Matthew Perry, has nothing to do with Matthew Perry.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, Geragos says his client is taking responsibility for her actions.\par \par \uc2 Sangha is the latest of five people to avoid trial by pleading guilty, including Perry`s friend, his live-in assistant and two doctors who charged Perry tens of thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN ESTRADA (U.S. attorney): These defendants were more interested in profiting off Mr. Perry than caring for his wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: From the investigation`s outset, authorities promised to hold all drug dealers accountable, hoping Perry`s death and these guilty pleas send a clear message.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, Taylor Swift can sing, we know that, but she can also sell. See how a 20-year relationship with her fans is paying off in the huge interest in her newest album.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "New Heights" Podcast/\uc2 Wave Sports + Entertainment\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back, 7:30, CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Our near daily reminder to not pose with or play with dangerous wildlife. Shawn Meuse with the lesson today, found out the hard way as he was taking a photo with a six-foot shark he just caught. The shark lurched and took a big old chunk out of his leg, we are blurring it, but it is a big old chunk. Trust us.\par \par \uc2 CBS Fort Myers affiliate, WINK, spoke with Meuse and the seen-it-all local fire chief. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN MEUSE (Bitten by a shark): I`m one of the fortunate one -- fortunate ones. And, yes, I`m a shark bite victim. Lot of good stories now.\par \par \uc2 C.W. BLOSSER (Boca Grande Fire Department Fire Chief): Now, you`ve got to be careful when you`re playing with Mother Nature.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, you do. Meuse is expected to make a full recovery. Sharks, meanwhile, have been around for hundreds of millions of years.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Humans, not as long.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: May not be forever.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. See you next time. I wonder who`s going to win that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. A lot of people are rooting for the shark.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bets are on the shark. Yes, me too.\par \par \uc2 Oh boy! All right, now to some good news for those of you who pay out-of- pocket for those popular weight loss drugs like Ozempic.\par \par \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 , the company that makes it, has announced it is slashing the price by more than half. It is still not cheap. It will now cost $499.00 a month online with three dose sizes to choose from. The company says it is doing this not just to make the drug cheaper, but also to discourage patients from using cheaper knockoff versions, which may not be as safe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The rollout of Taylor Swift`s newest album -- have you heard, she`s got a new album coming out -- it is making headlines with more surprises for her fans, but it is also a lesson in marketing for other performers.\par \par \uc2 So her website posted another countdown yesterday for a vinyl release of the album with Swift on the cover -- I love this cover -- in a glittering showgirl outfit. "Variety" says the album sold out in just one hour. Marketing giants like \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Spotify\uc2  are now joining the movement.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent has more on the Swift phenomenon, and yes, we`re no dummies. We realize even this segment we`re doing right now is part of her marketing genius. We`re here for it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour (Taylor`s Version)/Taylor Swift Productions)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: For more than two decades, Taylor Swift has mastered the art of storytelling and built a close relationship with her fan.\par \par \uc2 (TAYLOR SWIFT FANS singing)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: As a business woman, she`s been a game changer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour (Taylor`s Version)/Taylor Swift Productions)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @KristenCarey/TikTok)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: On Monday, Swift dazzled her fans again with another countdown on her website, this time to limited time vinyl preorders for her upcoming 12th studio album, "The Life of a Showgirl."\par \par \uc2 @ASHAZURA (TikTok): So you`re telling me, while Taylor Swift was doing the biggest tour in history, she was simultaneously writing an album about doing the biggest tour in history.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT (Singer): This is my brand new album.\par \par \uc2 JASON KELCE (Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player, podcast host): Ah!\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS KELCE (\uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2 ): Ah!\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Even before announcing her new album on her boyfriend, Travis Kelce`s podcast, Swift had been leaving Easter eggs for fans.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @LifeOfAShowgirlTS12/TikTok)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Like this \uc2 Spotify\uc2  billboard in New York City, directing listeners to a playlist called "And, baby, that`s show business for you."\par \par \uc2 But Swift`s biggest boost in visibility comes from all of us says marketing expert Kevin Evers.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN EVERS (Marketing experts): I think she understands that superstars aren`t self-made, they`re created by fans.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: We`re watching Taylor Swift execute all of these essentially marketing strategies. Is she basically the equivalent of an MBA course in how to satisfy your customer?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN EVERS: Yes and no. What separates Taylor is that she has built such a close fan base and a fan base that`s so curious and so willing to create content and to engage with Taylor.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Taylor Swift just referenced one of art history`s most famous paintings on her new album.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN EVERS: Taylor is able to drop hints and clues, but those fans take care of the rest.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: And a quick \uc2 Google\uc2  search of Taylor Swift, you`ll get a little shower of confetti on your screen, and \uc2 Google\uc2 `s spokesperson did confirm it`s partnered with Taylor Swift to launch this fun and interactive Easter egg, as they`re calling it, and they`re trying to celebrate her upcoming album, and it`s just another side of how much influence she has.\par \par \uc2 And this is, by the way, not the first time that \uc2 Google\uc2  has worked with her on this front.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Boy oh boy!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she is genius.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That sure is a sign --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Jo Ling, I did \uc2 Google\uc2  just to see the confetti come down. It`s purple and pink.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It is there!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is definitely there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it`s genius what she does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think, it is too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think it is genius. It is genius.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- look, it has happened before, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she is talented, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Elvis, The Beatles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is also good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly right, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- Michael Jackson, this is not new.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But if she wasn`t this good, though, it wouldn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly, right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It wouldn`t matter. She is just so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The music is what matters first and everything else is built on that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that`s what I love when I see those young fans who are like singing the songs of -- her songs while they`re meeting her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You see the joy in their faces. That`s what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s what it is all about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yup!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jo Ling, thanks again.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, day two of our Back-to-School Week. We are taking a look at how artificial intelligence has already changed the education landscape. What it means for both students and teachers? Are we still doing school with A.I.? I guess so.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, for now, anyway.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back-to-School Week continues here on CBS MORNINGS and a tricky subject today, the rise of artificial intelligence in classrooms.\par \par \uc2 Advocates say it`s going to be a helpful tool for kids and for teachers alike. Others say it discourages kids from thinking on their own, critically, that is.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen spoke to teachers and a tech executive about what`s next, including a new partnership that could launch a fresh chapter in education.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEROME ONG (Fifth grade teacher): I put two books in there, and it puts it into a podcast, and it`s literally 13 minutes of information about the Boston Tea Party.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Jerome Ong uses A.I. tools every week in his fifth grade Ridgewood New Jersey classroom.\par \par \uc2 JEROME ONG: You have to find what works for your students.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Has it changed the way you approach teaching?\par \par \uc2 JEROME ONG: Absolutely!\par \par \uc2 They are reading a book and ultimately listening to it at the same time. You know, so when I play the audio --\par \par \uc2 A.I. VOICE: Have you ever heard of a party where, like, the main event was chucking something super valuable right into the water?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Ong says he teaches what A.I. is and where it falls short.\par \par \uc2 JEROME ONG: Like, if you keep at it and say, no, you`re wrong, you`re wrong, eventually A.I. will say, I`m sorry I was wrong. And doing that with your class can show you that A.I. isn`t really as smart as you think it is.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It almost sounds like you`re teaching A.I. literacy in all of this too.\par \par \uc2 JEROME ONG: Right.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Artificial Intelligence is top of mind in most fields, and many educators have been teaching themselves how to use it. But this summer, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  announced a first of its kind plan to train hundreds of thousands of members of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , or \uc2 AFT\uc2 , the nation`s second largest teachers union.\par \par \uc2 What really drew you to this partnership?\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH (\uc2 Microsoft\uc2  president): I think if we`re going to make A.I. work for students, for kids, we need to listen to teachers.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  President, Brad Smith.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH: It can, I think, change the way teachers work in ways that empower teachers, gives them more information, makes it easier in terms of preparing for classes, thinking about how t