mula that we use, and essentially it`s a carbohydrate source, a protein, a fruit, a vegetable, and then something fun, like dip, maybe a dessert, maybe some chips, something that kind of is exciting when they open up the box.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So we see that here. These are some examples of the Feeding Littles formula.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got veggie wontons, rice cake sandwiches, chicken tender sliders that Tony pointed out.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN MCNAMEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And even a yogurt parfait.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN MCNAMEE: Yes, so the point of the yogurt parfait is to build your own option. So you can kind of pile it -- your child can pile it together if they want, or if they`re not into mixed foods yet, maybe they eat them all separately.\par \par \uc2 So people kind of think they have to do again, that kind of formal lunch, but we can have breakfast for lunch. That`s okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But here`s the thing about packing lunches for kids, though, it`s a kind of hope and pray situation.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN MCNAMEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You make it, you close the lid, it goes off in the little backpack, and then at the end of the day, you`re like, well, let`s see how we did.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN MCNAMEE: And I will tell you, even if your kids are extremely adventurous, we`ve talked about our kids sometimes like things and sometimes don`t. Even the most adventurous kids don`t always eat their lunch, and that is normal. That is okay.\par \par \uc2 If you think about the school environment, the cafeteria is very overwhelming, and it doesn`t mean that you did something wrong or you packed the wrong thing. Even my kids who love their lunch didn`t eat it because they didn`t have time, and a lot of the time is because they`re being sociable, right? They`re having fun talking to their friends, or they want to get out for recess.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So does that 