nside Edition." That`s a clip from her very first broadcast that was last night, by the way.\par \par \uc2 She is the first new anchor in more than three decades -- wow -- succeeding Deborah Norville, who announced her departure earlier this year. We`re very happy to tell you that Eva pilgrim has more than 20 years of experience in the T.V. industry, so she ain`t no slouch.\par \par \uc2 We`re excited to welcome her here to the \uc2 CBS\uc2  family. You might have watched on the other network, but now she`s ours.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning, Eva. Congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM ("Inside Edition" host): Good morning. Twenty years, I can`t believe I`ve been doing this that long.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 So now that the first show was done, I was curious about how you were feeling last night, and what did your mom say? Because moms always have something to say.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Yes, you know, I grew up watching the show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: So there was so much anxiety that I had about doing a good job, because it`s nostalgic for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: I used to watch it with my mom. My mom, of course, was really nice, and then she was like, "Maybe wear brighter color tomorrow."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what she said?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you`re getting advice from mom. I imagine Deborah Norville, after doing it for 30 years and handing off to you, had some advice as well. What did she tell you?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: She sent me the most lovely e-mail, and basically she was like, I can`t believe I was there for 30 years.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: But it`s such a great job. And so it was one of those things where she was like, enjoy it. This is a great moment for you. You`re going to have fun. Have this moment for yourself.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve been -- yes, you`ve been talking about how this is your dream job. And all of us this morning, Gayle kept saying, as you know, it`s her dream job. We teased it to all the affiliates, and we all think that we have our dream jobs too here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What is it for you about this job?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we said, isn`t it nice when your dreams come true?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Look, how often does that happen, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: You know, I love what we do. I love getting to tell stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: But I also like really wanted to be a mom and a wife, and this job gives me the ability to be all of that and really give all of that to each of the people and all of the people involved. Instead of feeling constantly like I`m only having to work, I can only be a mom on vacation. Now, I can actually -- I take my daughter to school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How old is she? How old is she?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: She`s three.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ella, yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: She is going to be four. And I desperately wanted to be a mom. So getting to be able to do -- oh, there she is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Being sweet!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is so adorable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes!\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: But you know, getting to be able to do that is so, so special. And I don`t take it lightly. I know it is truly a gift, that I am made to have this job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a beautiful thing. Yes, so you`re not going to have to be chasing breaking news in the same way as you were in the news business. But Deborah Norville had some tough assignments here. I mean, she spent a week in jail, according to this, starting a music video. She is a train conductor. What are you going to bring to the experiential part of this?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: I hope that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I`m wondering, are you thinking -- sitting here thinking, I am going to do this. I`ve got this idea, this idea, because Deborah was there as we know for 30 years. Are you thinking, I want to make it different. I want to do something else with the show.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Yes, I have a bazillion ideas in my head.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A bazillion. That`s a good start.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: So, you know, I keep telling myself, and the people who know me, who are very close to me, are, like, Eva, just one foot in front of the other for the first several months.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: And then you can, sort of like, work on the ideas. I don`t know that it will all happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: I hope they don`t send me to jail for a week.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, tough assignment.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Maybe something with food would be fun. I love the mysteries. It`s what I had been covering for all of these years. So to get to do more with some of that would be fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: We`ll see. We`ll see where it goes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And when you were done with the first show, when it was done, did you think, okay, now I can relax? Because I know what it`s like. You just want to get the first one done.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Yes. You just get that one under your belt. Today will be easier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Inside Edition")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you`ve got a couple of really interesting stories for tonight`s "Inside Edition," which features an exclusive interview with a boat driver who worked for Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Yes, everyone has been talking about the Jeffrey Epstein story for quite some time. So this was the guy who was contracted to be the water taxi to the island.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh boy!\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: So just imagine --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What he saw.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Who he saw, what he saw.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And who he saw.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: Yes, who he saw is the big question, and he talks about that, and sort of some of the reflections that he has now looking back on it. He didn`t know who he was driving. Now he has a little bit better of an idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fascinating. Congratulations. I`d love to know how conversation went when they first said, "Hey, Eva, would you like to be the next hose of `Inside Edition`?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. "Inside Edition."\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: I was like, are you serious?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t say, let me think about it and I`ll get back to you.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: No. I almost fell on the floor. I could not believe they were asking me that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a huge dream.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you didn`t seek the job. You didn`t seek it, they just came at you.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: It kind of came out of nowhere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very nice.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM: You know, sometimes, God just gives you gifts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go. Thank you, God!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love that.\par \par \uc2 Eva Pilgrim --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very much. "Inside Edition," folks, airs weekdays at 7:00 P.M. Eastern on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Lisa Ling shows us how some families are turning to the internet to find grandparents for their children. Pretty cool story.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Some of our most impactful and influential relationships in life are with our grandparents.\par \par \uc2 Around 70 million people in the United States are grandparents, and around 70 percent of Americans believe it`s important for a grandparent and a grandchild to have a close relationship.\par \par \uc2 But issues like distance, busy schedules, strained relationships, as well as adults opting out of having kids, have left many feeling the loss or absent of these bonds.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, Lisa Ling is exploring this issue and the unique way some are forming unconventional families to fill this need.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Here is your key.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Anteres and Louie Turner are raising twin boys in Southern California. About two years ago they realized something or someone was missing from their family.\par \par \uc2 ANTERES ANDERSON TURNER (Mom): We`re talking about the village, the village that you know, we had growing up. The grandparents would like come and like hang out or just impart their wisdom.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Caden and Cameron`s (sp?) paternal grandparents live across the country. Anteres` Dad died during the pandemic, and her mom isn`t actively involved.\par \par \uc2 ANTERES ANDERSON TURNER: I was like, My God, I wish there was, like, a way to just, like, adopt somebody. I`m like, can you just go trolling like nursing homes and say, hey, like, does anybody need like, a grandchild?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Rather than trolling, they went looking for a grandparent online, and came across the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group, Surrogate Grandparents USA, which has around 12,000 members.\par \par \uc2 When you first were told about this \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page called Surrogate Grandparents, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA (Grandparent): I thought it was great. I thought it was amazing because it was going to fill a need that I actually have.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Less than 100 miles north of the Andersons, Janet Firestein Da (sp?) was also searching for her missing piece, grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: I have five kids, none of which -- none of whom are going to ever give me grandkids, and they were pretty clear about that. I was getting older, and I wanted to get down on the floor and play Legos and trains and read books.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: That`s when she, too, came across the Surrogate Grandparent \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page where those in similar situations have been connected.\par \par \uc2 Tell me your reaction when you saw Anteres and her family, their picture posted on the page.\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: The picture was adorable. I reached out just for yucks, honestly, to see if. There was, could be something there, and we just connected immediately.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: For safety reasons, they planned their first meetup at a public park.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about the first meeting.\par \par \uc2 ANTERES ANDERSON TURNER: The moment that they met her, they gave her a big hug, and it was like, okay.\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: My actual favorite Hebrew word for that is "bashert" which means, meant to be.\par \par \uc2 My boys!\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: That was two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Has it exceeded your expectation?\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 What you got?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Now, Janet, who the boys called "Nana Jay," is an integral part of the Anderson family.\par \par \uc2 What`s your favorite thing about Nana Jay?\par \par \uc2 CAMERON TURNER (Grandson): She plays with us, makes pretend movies and has fun.\par \par \uc2 ANTERES ANDERSON TURNER: Hugs, maybe?\par \par \uc2 CAMERON TURNER: Yes, hugs!\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: Definitely hugs!\par \par \uc2 CADEN TURNER (Grandson): I think she is very kind.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Tell me about what the last two years have been like with your surrogate grandma.\par \par \uc2 ANTERES ANDERSON TURNER: It`s been like, a weight has been lifted off my shoulders. She`s gone on trips with us. We`ve celebrated Hanukkah. She`s gone to the library with us. I mean, just so many different things like that I desperately wanted for my kids, and we get there with Janet.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: In what ways have the boys filled a void for you?\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: It`s indescribable for me, because I haven`t had that experience before, to be that grandparent and I love it.\par \par \uc2 In church, Martin sang hymns --\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: After the first year with her surrogate grandsons, something in Janet`s life drastically changed.\par \par \uc2 Janet, you got news from your youngest child that she was going to have a baby.\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: Yes, I did. That was kind of a surprise.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Janet`s granddaughter was born in March, but I`m sure you`ve already guessed the love she has for Cameron and Caden remains unwavering.\par \par \uc2 JANET FIRESTEIN DA: It hasn`t changed anything. It doesn`t change anything for me. Those boys are mine.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Anteres, what has it meant for you and your family to have Nana Jay in your lives?\par \par \uc2 ANTERES ANDERSON TURNER: It`s like closing a loop, and by adding her in, we feel complete.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: They say you can find anything online, including a grandparent, but it`s so important to mention that when it comes to the Surrogate Grandparent Facebook Group or meeting anyone online, it`s really important for the parents to employ safety and use their safety as a discretion.\par \par \uc2 We also spoke to the founder, Donna, who was amazed at how quickly this group has grown and how many unconventional families were formed as a result.\par \par \uc2 Back to you in the studio.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Lisa, this is great. I`d never heard of this number one, but number two, I love this story on many levels. I love that it`s a mixed couple Black and White, because it shows you love really, doesn`t have any color.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One hundred percent!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was really, really beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Yes and Nana Jay makes it a point of celebrating Hanukkah with the kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get it.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250819el8j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 SATURDAY WEEKEND NEWS for August 16, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Taurean Small, Debora Patta, Manuel Bojorquez,  Andrew Kozak, Ali Bauman, Elise Preston, Dave Malkoff \par \uc2 3,660 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, President Trump reverses course on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Thank you very much, Vladimir.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2  (Russian President): Next time in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: No need for a ceasefire. The President back in Washington now aligning with Putin`s plan to end the war.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: I`m Taurean small at the White House. Ukraine`s President is headed to an Oval Office sit down Monday amid new turmoil about his country`s future.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: I`m Debora Patta in London, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is back in the Kremlin and reacts to the summit.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, Texas showdown.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS shouting "When our rights are under attack. What do we do?" We fight back!")\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Protests nationwide today against Republicans push for more seats in Congress.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: I`m Manuel Bojorquez in Dallas, where demonstrators say redistricting efforts in Texas are a Trump takeover. They plan to keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Hurricane Erin powers up.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: I`m meteorologist, Andrew Kozak. I`ll have the latest forecast and what it could mean for the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Travel trouble.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY BROWN (Canadian flyer): When I got to the airport, I wasn`t able to get a boarding pass.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A strike grounds \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  stranding passengers in North America and beyond.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: I`m Ali Bauman at LaGuardia Airport, with the impact on American flyers and why the busy summer season has U.S. carriers soaring.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And later, the surprise from space. What was that streaking across the Georgia sky?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening and thank you for joining us on this Saturday. Tonight, a new split in the push to end the war in Ukraine as the country`s president heads to Washington Monday for a face-to-face conversation with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 In a major reversal, the President returned to Washington today, dropping his ceasefire demand for the Ukraine war and telling allies that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants territory for a permanent peace deal.\par \par \uc2 Russia invaded Ukraine nearly four years ago, seizing large areas of the country, 400,000 Ukrainians had been killed or wounded, along with nearly a million Russian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Taurean Small is at the White House tonight and leads us off.\par \par \uc2 Taurean, good evening.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the President is reversing course, saying he`s no longer seeking an urgent ceasefire in Ukraine, but now agrees with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that pursuing a peace deal is the best way to end the war.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: President Trump hit the greens one day after rolling out the red carpet in Alaska for Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , the moment marked an end to years of isolation for Putin on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 (VLADIMIR PUTIN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: It is apparent that sooner or later, we have to amend the situation to move on from the confrontation to dialog.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: Following the nearly two-and-a-half hour long closed door talk with the Russian President, Trump touted progress.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: There were many, many points that we agreed on, most of them, I would say, a couple of big ones that we haven`t quite got there.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: But ended their joint appearance after 12 minutes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN: Next time in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: Without questions or any specifics.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy on social media encouraged the U.S. to strengthen sanctions on Russia if Putin doesn`t commit to a meeting between the three leaders.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to Fox News, Trump says the next move is Zelenskyy`s.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Now it`s really up to President Zelenskyy to get it done.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: Sam Vinograd is a CBS News National Security contributor.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: There are certainly concerns that heading into the Oval Office meeting between Trump and Zelenskyy on Monday, that Trump will try to bully President Zelenskyy into accepting the contours of the deal that he hammered out with President Putin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: CBS News has learned President Zelenskyy has been receiving a lot of coaching on how to interact with President Trump since their disastrous Oval Office meeting in February -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Taurean Small reporting from the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, European leaders are moving to renew their support for Ukraine, and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is also speaking out.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta at our London Bureau has new developments.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is back in the Kremlin where he described his meeting with President Trump as frank, substantive and "bringing us closer to making the necessary decisions."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: But the Alaska Summit could not have been more removed from this, the bloody battlefields of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The two presidents met under a banner proclaiming they were pursuing peace. Across Ukraine, it was anything but that, as Russia launched a series of strikes, including in the north eastern city of Sumy.\par \par \uc2 (MIROSLAV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "Whenever peace talks begin," said Ukrainian soldier Miroslav (sp?), "Things get even more terrifying on the front line."\par \par \uc2 After more than three-and-a-half years of fighting, war-weary Ukrainians are desperate for a ceasefire, but summit expectations were low.\par \par \uc2 Most believed it did little more than bring Putin, a man they regard as a war criminal, in from the cold.\par \par \uc2 (PAVLO NEBROEV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "It`s a great diplomatic victory for Putin," said Pavlo Nebroev (sp?). "He has completely legitimized himself."\par \par \uc2 Ukraine also turned up the pressure with drone strikes in the Southern Russian cities of Rostov and Belgorod. The Summit capped a week of intense diplomatic activity as European leaders, including Germany and the U.K. met with President Zelenskyy and held a video call with President Trump in a bid to ensure he did not sell out Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 After the summit, they held back criticism of the meeting, instead signing a statement thanking President Trump for his efforts, but stressing the long-term need for ironclad securities for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: European leaders will meet with President Zelenskyy on another video call tomorrow, Jericka, to discuss the Alaska Summit ahead of Zelenskyy`s strip to Washington on Monday. Yes, a lot to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, efforts by Texas Republicans to redraw the state`s congressional maps got new pushback from protesters at rallies across this country. Nearly 280 events and 34 states, according to organizers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez is in Dallas tonight, with more on what is at stake - - Manuel.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Jericka, good evening.\par \par \uc2 The protests cap a nearly two-week long, long standoff in which Democrats who left the state were threatened with fines and arrests.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "When our rights are under attack what do we do? We fight back!")\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: They call this fight the "Trump Takeover Rallies" in several cities across the country. Here in Dallas --\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "We fight back!")\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: To Austin, to Los Angeles, they protested plans by Texas at the request of President Trump to redraw its congressional maps.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Don`t mess with Texas. Don`t mess with our districts.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Protesters object to the plan that could give Republicans five more seats in Congress ahead of next year`s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 What`s your message today?\par \par \uc2 FENIKA MILLER (Black Voters Matter senior coordinator): Our message is that when our rights are under attack, we have to stand up and fight back.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE (D) (Former U.S. Representative): Then there will be a check on their lawlessness. There will be accountability for their crimes.\par \par \uc2 DUSTIN BURROWS (Texas Speaker of the House): The House will come to order.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But the state`s Republican majority is now poised to make it happen opening a second special session.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): Texans stay and fight. In this case, you have a bunch of cowards who cut and run.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Democrats who left the state to prevent a vote have said they will return now that California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  said voters there will decide on a redistricting measure that could counter the Texas GOP gains.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): We have got to recognize the cards that have been dealt, and we have got to meet fire with fire.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: It`s unclear exactly when all Texas Democrats will return to the state, but as long as there are 100 lawmakers available for a vote, which is quorum in the Texas House, it is expected the bill will pass -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Manuel Bojorquez in Dallas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, West Virginia`s Governor said he was sending up to 400 National Guard troops to Washington, D.C. at President Trump`s request. They will join soldiers already deployed with D.C. Police on the streets of the district. The troops may be armed, that`s a major shift that comes after the President deployed them to "take back the Capitol," even as local authorities report crime falling in the area.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  is moving quickly to shut down a strike that grounded the country`s biggest airline. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flight attendants walked off the job at midnight, stranding thousands of passengers across North America and beyond.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ali Bauman is at New York`s LaGuardia Airport with more on the fallout.\par \par \uc2 Ali, good evening.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 The walkout may be over, but it could take days for service to return to normal. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  operates hundreds of flights each day to the U.S., so this will impact travelers all across North America, and the strain to the system comes as the cost of flying is already skyrocketing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Hey, hey! Ho, ho!")\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: The \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  is directing \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flight attendants to go back to work hours after thousands walked off the job Saturday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: All of us have been affected by this. We can`t use food banks. We can`t be sleeping in our cars anymore.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: But \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  has told the government it will take up to 10 days to restore full service, expected to affect 130,000 customers each day, 25,000 of them outside of Canada.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY BROWN: When I got to the airport, I wasn`t able to get a boarding pass, and they said the flights were all canceled.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Casey Foy (sp?) rebooked with the ticket agent at LaGuardia Airport after checking prices online.\par \par \uc2 CASEY FOY (Traveler): Like looking at a one-way flight to go home was double what I paid for my round trip ticket to come here.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 `s operations were grounded, just as airline ticket prices are soaring, up four percent in July according to the Labor Department, it`s the first price hike in six months.\par \par \uc2 EMILY KAUFMAN (Travel expert): People are traveling in record numbers right now.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA: Travel expert, Emily Kaufman says rising prices will likely be exacerbated by the ripple effects of the \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  strike.\par \par \uc2 EMILY KAUFMAN: It comes to supply and demand, with people traveling in record numbers, it makes it more difficult to find a great deal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: And airlines are cashing in. Along with rising ticket prices, the cost of jet fuel is down, sending airline stocks up this week. But if you`re looking to travel on a budget, Kaufman says ticket prices tend to drop by mid-September -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, and you`ve got to get those tickets early.\par \par \uc2 Ali Bauman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Atlantic`s first hurricane of the season, Erin, suddenly intensified. It is now a dangerous Category 5 storm. Let`s go straight to CBS News meteorologist, Andrew Kozak in Philadelphia, who is tracking the hurricane for us.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yes, good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 A monster storm just yesterday, a tropical storm and now a Category 5. Winds over 160 miles per hour as this moves West at about 15 miles per hour. The island of Puerto Rico, no direct hit, but two to six inches of rain, gusty winds, landslides even a possibility.\par \par \uc2 And new tonight, as the storm moves north and west, we have Tropical Storm Watches in effect for Turks and Caicos.\par \par \uc2 Now, this will continue to move anywhere between 15 and 20 miles per hour, and the track of this will get a little bit closer to some of the islands of the Bahamas, and then it veers to the north. No direct landfall for the United States, but it stays a very powerful Category 4 all the way into early next week and into late next week, it veers off to sea. Why? High pressure that`s across the Atlantic, and it`s a steering mechanism which will allow us not to get a direct hit, but we will feel the effects up and down the East Coast next week, from Florida to Maine, we will have waves and potentially some coastal flooding. A storm that we are watching very closely tonight and into the rest of the weekend -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Andrew Kozak for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, remembering the life of a long time soap opera star.\par \par \uc2 But first this story --\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: I`m Elise Preston in Hollywood at the newest high rise that many are making their forever home.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Los Angeles has a new landmark. This one towers over an unusual location and in a sprawling city where space is at a premium, the sky is the limit.\par \par \uc2 Here \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Los Angeles has no shortage of architectural oddities: The Hollywood sign, the Watts Tower, this space age theme building at LAX now add one more to the list, a newly constructed five-story residence for the dearly departed.\par \par \uc2 TYLER CASSITY (Hollywood Forever co-owner and president): It`s called the Gower Mausoleum. So we`re trying to answer all of our community`s needs for death care.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Hollywood Forever`s new mausoleum towers over the sprawling grounds that are the final resting place of Judy Garland.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wizard of Oz"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Silent film star Rudolph Valentino\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Sheikh"/Paramount Pictures)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: And "Gone with the Wind`s" Hattie McDaniel --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Gone with the Wind"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: -- the first Black American to win an Oscar.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Gone with the Wind"/MGM)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Ricardo Pantrik (sp?) raised his family in Hollywood and visits his mother`s grave every week.\par \par \uc2 RICARDO PANTRIK (Visits his mother`s grave every week): It is a quad, so hopefully I`ll be here, and maybe some children will join me.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: He bought four crips. The mausoleum opened in January, and already two of the five floors are sold out.\par \par \uc2 RICARDO PANTRIK: We are part of Hollywood Forever because I used to bring my family to teach them how to drive here.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Hollywood Forever is changing the definition of cemeteries by making them for the living. There`s festivals morning yoga and movie nights, but space is at a premium.\par \par \uc2 Architects Roberto Sheinberg and Michael Lehrer had to create very permanent housing for about 13,000 people.\par \par \uc2 How do you even begin to design a mausoleum in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTO SHEINBERG (Architect): The way we start all our projects is just we solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 Instead of buying more properties outside of the city where people have to drive, it`s just going vertical.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEHRER (Architect): This is somewhere between parking and housing. Making it beautiful was kind of important.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The mausoleum welcomes visitors with soothing marble, ample natural light and stunning views of L.A. landmarks.\par \par \uc2 You`re right in the middle of the neighborhood that you all grew up in. Yes?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA MORALES (Local resident): Hollywood has been our home for years.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The design brings elements of peace and comfort to Vanessa Morales (sp?).\par \par \uc2 VANESSA MORALES: My Grandma will rest here forever, but we come to take in the moment. It`s nice to know that she can finally rest and that we`re okay with knowing where she`s at permanently.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: In the \uc2 City of Angels\uc2 , a place of sadness now one of serenity.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston, CBS News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, it might be the longest checkout without any fees.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from ABC/\uc2 Disney\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That is actor, Tristan Rogers, a fan favorite on the soap opera, "General Hospital." The Australian born actor joined the cast of the ABC show back in 1980. It was supposed to be a two-day job, but Rogers stayed for 45 years, making a final surprise appearance just last month. He played a mysterious spy who became a crime fighter.\par \par \uc2 Tristan Rogers died Friday at his home in Palm Springs, California after being diagnosed with lung cancer. He was 79.\par \par \uc2 To San Antonio, Texas now, where the public library says someone has returned a book, get this, nearly 82 years overdue. It was checked out in July of 1943. Fortunately, the library stopped charging late fees four years ago.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a rare discovery from outer space, but First a look back at this day in history.\par \par \uc2 Graphics onscreen: This Day In History --\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1896.\par \par \uc2 Gold discovery in Yukon sparks Klondike Rush.\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1954.\par \par \uc2 "Sports Illustrated" debuts on newsstands.\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1977.\par \par \uc2 "King" Elvis dies in Memphis at age 42.\par \par \uc2 ROGER MUDD: Thousands of Elvis Presley fans streamed into Memphis today hoping for a glimpse of the body of the former Rock and Roll King. Presley had died because of an irregular heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We end with a spectacle from space that appeared as a fireball scene in several states, including Georgia. Well, in tonight`s "Weekend Journal," \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Dave Malkoff shows us how the extraterrestrial visitor has an extraordinary past.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: As if fire in the sky over Georgia crashing into a family`s roof isn`t spectacular enough.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re going to take a look here.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Just wait until you see what a shooting star looks like up close.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS (University of Georgia researcher): This is thing I love about these microscopes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Wow! It just changed to color. What I`m seeing is an iridescent brightness shining back at my eyes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: The specific chemistry in those minerals does not occur here. It had to have been formed on somebody out in space.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Those little black specks in there are older than Earth. They formed as melted droplets in space when the sun was just getting its planets in line, more than four-and-a-half billion years ago.\par \par \uc2 You can touch it?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: These are the larger ones.\par \par \uc2 Oh, yes. You can.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: University of Georgia researcher, Scott Harris went to the house and collected the meteorite.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: The pieces that, like the one you`re holding there, those stayed within a few feet of the crater.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: So that hole in the guy`s floor is technically a crater hit by a supersonic space rock while the mystery man was on a \uc2 Zoom\uc2  call.\par \par \uc2 If that \uc2 Zoom\uc2  would have been recording.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: Yes!\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The homeowner let Harris take these photos, but doesn`t want any meteorite hunters or nosy reporters poking around his house.\par \par \uc2 So this thing is traveling through the solar system. It comes through the atmosphere, breaks up and goes directly to this guy`s house.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: It could not have picked a calmer individual`s house.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: If this box is the man`s house. This is how the rock came flying in.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: These were clean shot holes through the roof, through the attic. There`s still debris in their furniture, up on the refrigerator, on the mantle.\par \par \uc2 Months later, they`re still picking out dust from the fabric of their sofa.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The family is now cutting out these holes in the house on every layer so they can eventually go to a museum for everyone to see.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: There`s also one place where there`s almost certainly a little bit of this dust and that`s in his lungs.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: But he`s not going to get any superpowers from that, is he?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT HARRIS: Well, you know, we can hope, right?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Dave Malkoff, CBS News at the University of Georgia in Athens.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Cool stuff!\par \par \uc2 Well, before we go, a warm welcome to viewers watching our new station, CBS Atlanta. Today is their very first day on the air. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 And that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Saturday. I`m Jericka Duncan in New York. Have a great night, and we hope to see you here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020250817el8g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for August 11, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson, Ed O`Keefe, Meg Oliver, Omar  Villafranca, Scott MacFarlane, Ian Lee, Jo Ling Kent, Rob Marciano \par \uc2 3,970 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): This is an emergency.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The numbers show crime in Washington, D.C., is down, but the president claims it`s out of control.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Our capital city has been overtaken by violent gangs and bloodthirsty criminals.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So he`s taking charge of law enforcement in the federal city.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Theodore Roosevelt was once the police commissioner here in New York City, but that was before he was president. Today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the sitting president, put himself in charge of the police force in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The president said, despite evidence to the contrary, that crime in the nation`s capital is out of control. It has actually been declining since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The president`s takeover of law enforcement in Washington will include armed federal agents manning posts near the Metropolitan Police.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And hundreds of National Guard troops are getting ready to hit the streets.\par \par \uc2 Justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane joins us now.\par \par \uc2 And, Scott, this is a big turnaround from just a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It sure is, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 Three months ago, a top Trump administration official announced crime was down significantly in Washington, D.C., an early success for the administration, he said. Yet, today, the president has announced a crime emergency and militarized the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): In the nation`s capital, home to nearly 700,000 people, violent crime dropped 26 percent from last year. But, today, President Trump took charge of the nearly 4,000-person police department, claiming Washington, D.C., is in crisis.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Today, we`re formally declaring a public safety emergency. This is an emergency. This is a tragic emergency.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: He defended his controversial decision by painting a dystopian picture of a community overrun by marauding packs of criminals. And he cited the assault of a young administration staffer earlier this summer.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Our capital city has been overtaken by violent gangs and bloodthirsty criminals, roving mobs of wild youth, drugged-out maniacs, and homeless people.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But the latest data shows homicides are down 12 percent from last year, while robberies declined by 28 percent.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Muriel Bowser defended D.C.`s image.\par \par \uc2 MURIEL BOWSER (D-Mayor of Washington, D.C.): While this action today is unsettling and unprecedented, I can`t say that, given some of the rhetoric of the past, that we`re totally surprised.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Federal agents from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and ATF were already posted on D.C. streets, raising concerns for some, including Tom Warrick, a former Homeland Security official.\par \par \uc2 TOM WARRICK (Former U.S. Homeland Security Deputy Assistant Secretary): Taking \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents off of organized crime cases, terrorism and other kinds of cases to do street patrols, that`s not the best use of a highly trained \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You think this could be a distraction?\par \par \uc2 FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY TOM WARRICK: It really looks as though a lot of this is performance.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The 800 National Guard troops are expected to deploy later this week, and many have little training or experience policing U.S. streets.\par \par \uc2 FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY TOM WARRICK: The best-case scenario is what happened in Los Angeles, which is, these 800 people stand around for several weeks doing essentially nothing, and then eventually most of them go home.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott, the president mentioned he has ideas about what to do with the homeless in Washington. What are those?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: At times, John, he referred to Washington, D.C., as a slum and then said homeless people need to be moved away from the Capitol. He gave no detail of what that looks like or how that happens.\par \par \uc2 We do know both the federal government and the D.C. government regularly clear encampments for homeless people. It`s a painstaking, lengthy and sensitive process. Hard to know, Maurice and John, what that looks like if it`s sped up.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And you talked about the National Guard troops we saw in L.A. back in June. What makes this situation different, Scott?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This is one of a kind, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 The Washington, D.C., district is not a state. The president can unilaterally call up the Guard without permission. In Los Angeles, he used the Guard to protect federal buildings. Here, they can be used throughout the District of Columbia, just a different, unicorn situation in the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott MacFarlane in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Well, the new week is starting off with a new deadly shooting in America, this time in the parking lot of a Target store in Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The police there say three people were killed. A suspect is in custody.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is in Austin.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The shooting happened behind me at this Target parking lot around 2:00 p.m. when officers received calls of a shot fired.\par \par \uc2 Well, they arrived about four minutes later, and that`s when they found the three victims. Now, police say they spotted the suspect as he hijacked a car and then drove away. Well, during that chase, he crashed about a mile- and-a-half down the road, ditched that car and hijacked another vehicle. Well, police chased him to South Austin. They finally caught up with him, Tased him and arrested him.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the suspect is a 32-year-old man with a history of mental health issues. We don`t know any of the victims` names, and we still don`t know a motive.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Omar Villafranca in Austin, Texas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The ground shook in Pittsburgh today, but it wasn`t an earthquake. It was an explosion at a \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  plant 15 miles away in Clairton, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At least one person was killed, at least 10 others were injured as workers risked their lives rescuing others.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The steel plant converts coal to coke and, in the process, creates toxic coke gas.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Meg Oliver is on the scene.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH #1: There`s possible entrapment under the rubble.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): Just before 11:00 a.m., the first calls came in after an explosion inside what`s known as the reversing room, which regulates the massive ovens at the plant, leaving behind a gaping hole in the building. Several people were trapped\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH #2: Reported explosion with multiple injuries at Clairton Works.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: With thick smoke billowing in the air, first responders worked to pull survivors out of the rubble.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  executive vice president Scott Buckiso says one person is still missing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BUCKISO (Executive Vice President, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 ): We think we know the general location of that employee, but we`re not 100 percent sure.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The plant employs more than 1,000 people. It`s unclear how many were inside the room where the explosion happened.\par \par \uc2 Deanna Forkey works across the street from the plant.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA FORKEY (Witness): Our door blew open at the same time. And when I looked up and actually looked out the window to notice, I saw the black smoke. At that point, I realized there was an explosion over there. It wasn`t normal.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: This is not the first incident at this plant. In 2018, a Christmas Eve fire cost the company $40 million. And, in February, two workers were reportedly injured after a buildup of gases exploded, causing minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 Clairton`s Mayor Richard Lattanzi says the mill is a big part of the community.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LATTANZI (Mayor of Clairton, Pennsylvania): Some people are not going to see the same husband or son or somebody that`s working in the mill. And it`s just a sad day for Clairton.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: As far as the air quality, they haven`t detected any contaminants above federal standards. In fact, they just canceled the cautionary order for residents who live within a mile of here to stay indoors.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Meg Oliver in Clairton, Pennsylvania, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has confirmed that the gunman in Friday`s attack on CDC headquarters in Atlanta fired more than 180 shots and broke about 150 windows. A police officer was killed. Health Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  visited the CDC campus today. The police are investigating whether the gunman, found dead at the scene, was motivated by conspiracy theories about the COVID vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder spoke with a number of CDC workers about the shooting, and we will have that on EVENING NEWS Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Paramount, the parent company of CBS News, has reached a seven-year, $7.7 billion deal to become the TV home of Ultimate Fighting Championship events in the U.S. Starting next year, all UFC fights will stream on Paramount+. Select events will air right here on CBS Television.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : There are concerns among U.S. allies that Ukraine`s president will not be part of the Trump-Putin summit in Alaska on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe asked President Trump about that today.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is not invited on Friday?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): He wasn`t a part of it. I would say he could go, but he`s gone to a lot of meetings. He`s been there for 3.5 years. Nothing happened.\par \par \uc2 Having to do with routes, trade.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So, what`s the definition of successful meeting on Friday with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, we`re going to have a meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And at the end of that meeting, probably in the first two minutes, I will know exactly whether or not a deal can be made.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: How will you know that?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Because that`s what I do. I make deals.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ed joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, what does the president hope to get out of this meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, John, the president and other officials now describe the upcoming face-to-face as a -- quote -- "feel-out meeting" to gauge how serious \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is about making a deal.\par \par \uc2 He says he plans to consult Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  and other European leaders before and after their exchange, but said he expects both sides are now going to need to swap territory that they have gained during the war. That`s a deal-breaker for Zelenskyy, as is the face-to-face meeting with Putin, unless the Russian leader agrees to a cease-fire first.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Ed, so much on the line here. What are the risks?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, Maurice, for the president, the risk is, he gets played by Putin. Remember, he`s expressed frustration with the Russian leader several times in recent months about how they have a positive conversation about winding down the war, and then Russia turns around and attacks Ukraine again.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a risk this meeting merely leads to an escalation or that an in- person exchange could lead to more of the same. For the Ukrainians, the big risk is that the president bargains away their territory or makes other concessions. But multiple world leaders today reiterated they expect anything about Ukraine`s future is going to be determined by Ukraine alone.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ed O`Keefe at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, Rob Marciano on flooding in Wisconsin after near-record rainfall.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also tonight:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: I`m Ian Lee in Cleveland, Ohio, where they`re building the next generation of skilled American workers. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (MODEM CONNECTING)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And the end of an era, an ear-splitting era.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Weekend storms brought flooding to the Milwaukee area after a foot or more of rain.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Meteorologist Rob Marciano has more about it -- Rob.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Gentlemen, not raining there today, but the rivers, the Milwaukee River is still rising tonight. And they may get a little bit more rain tomorrow, but not nearly as much rain as what you`re looking at here.\par \par \uc2 This is video out of the Cape Verde Islands, where Tropical Storm Erin was dumping rain before it was even named. So we have got flash flooding here. Unfortunately, there are fatalities. There are six fatalities, four of which were children. And the cleanup is under way.\par \par \uc2 All right, the storm itself obviously just off the coast of Africa, it`s a weak storm right now. We don`t expect it to strengthen very quickly. We have got dry air. We have got cooler water. But, over the next couple of days, we do expect it to become the first hurricane of the Atlantic season, likely to become a major storm.\par \par \uc2 Each one of these lines is one of our computer models, dictates where those computers think the storm is going, across the Atlantic, and then, towards days five, six, and seven, hinting at beginning to recurve it out to sea. This time of year, that`s fairly typical. When storms develop where Erin is, they get to the northern part of the Caribbean or the Bahamas and they either get into the Gulf of Mexico or most likely recurve.\par \par \uc2 How close or do they hit the East Coast? Right now, it`s way too far out to make that call. We have got over a week to watch and prepare, but something certainly to be worried about, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Rob Marciano, globe-trotting tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And now to an unusual deal between two American computer chipmakers and China. In a reversal, President Trump is now allowing \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  and AMD to sell their chips to the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The deal is unusual because the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  will become a stakeholder and get a share of the sales.\par \par \uc2 Senior business and technology correspondent Jo Ling Kent has the details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I said, if I`m going to do that, I want you to pay us as a country something.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voiceover): President Trump`s deal with American chipmakers \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  and AMD to sell two semiconductors to China in exchange for a 15 percent cut of their revenue will generate an estimated $2.7 billion for the Treasury.\par \par \uc2 How unusual is this kind of deal?\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. MARK MONTGOMERY (\uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 ): It`s not unusual if you`re watching The Sopranos, but it`s unusual in national security realm.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Retired Admiral Mark Montgomery is an expert in A.I., China and cyber warfare at the \uc2 Foundation for Defense of Democracies\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. MARK MONTGOMERY: We have had an advantage in emerging technology since the middle of World War II. And the idea that we`re now willing to put some of that at risk for $2 billion in payments, it`s nonsensical.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The president says the \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  chip being sold to China is less powerful than the company`s most advanced semiconductors, which power A.I.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: This is an old chip that China already has.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Is that true?\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. MARK MONTGOMERY: I`m worried that, even if they are slightly second-tier, they`re giving the Chinese a significant leg up and putting our national security and our military forces at risk.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: The deal is also a policy reversal by the president.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: China and other countries are racing to catch up to America having to do with A.I., and we`re not going to let them do it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In his first term and in recent months, President Trump restricted high-end chip exports to China due to national security concerns.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. MARK MONTGOMERY: When you give them to China, you give them to Russia, to North Korea and Iran.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: In a statement to CBS News, \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  did not deny this deal, saying: "We follow rules the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  sets for our participation in worldwide markets. We hope export control rules will let America compete in China and worldwide."\par \par \uc2 AMD did not respond to our multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jo Ling Kent, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A program that`s helping fill thousands of skilled jobs with people who need them.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On one side, there are people who need a job and a second chance, on the other side, employers with thousands of jobs to fill. And now a program is bringing them together like a magnet.\par \par \uc2 Ian Lee has tonight`s Eye on America from Cleveland.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE (voiceover): For the first time in Mary Lamar`s life...\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: ... she`s driving a new car to a job she loves.\par \par \uc2 It was a bit of a rough journey.\par \par \uc2 MARY LAMAR (\uc2 Talan Products\uc2 ): It was. I was in prison. I did 3.5 years for something that I never saw coming.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: So what`s it like?\par \par \uc2 The Cleveland native slipped into a life of crime at a young age, eventually serving time for robbery when she was 50 years old.\par \par \uc2 MARY LAMAR: I chose a different lifestyle, and drugs and alcohol is in my story.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Now 56, Lamar is a first-time homeowner and supervisor at her job, where she leads a team of machinists.\par \par \uc2 MARY LAMAR: Your recovery is what matters, and you have to have that support.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Support she found at MAGNET...\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tell me a little bit about yourself.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: ... an Ohio-based nonprofit that helped retool her life. Its goal is to educate and engage people on the available careers in manufacturing, in part to help fill more than 400,000 jobs in that sector.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN KARP (President and CEO, MAGNET): These are good salaries. These are good careers.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: MAGNET offers year-round career classes for adults who are un- or under-employed...\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everyone, grab a handle.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: ... and, for students, summer camps, field trips and apprenticeships, all free thanks to grants and donations.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN KARP: When they see the machines, their eyes light up.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: MAGNET CEO Ethan Karp wants to fill factory jobs by polishing America`s Rust Belt workers.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN KARP: We can`t just say, hey, pick yourself up by your bootstraps. We can`t say that. You need the grit and determination to do that, full stop, but you also need some help.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: But they do more than just give folks a pair of bootstraps.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN KARP: If somebody doesn`t have childcare, if health care is an issue, if transportation is, if crime is, all of those things affect their ability to get a job. That`s what we`re trying to remove with access, and it gives people the wraparound supports to overcome all of those barriers.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: One of MAGNET`s work force partners is \uc2 Talan Products\uc2 , where they stamp specialty metal parts and have added 40 jobs in the last year. Adam Snyder is the COO and Mary Lamar`s boss.\par \par \uc2 What does made in America look like today?\par \par \uc2 ADAM SNYDER (COO, \uc2 Talan Products\uc2 ): There`s a lot more manufacturing that we would love to have here. It`s going to take a little time to build the work force.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: What advice would you give people?\par \par \uc2 MARY LAMAR: Be open, be fearless, take a chance, be honest. You can be successful at any stage of your life. I`m proof.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Proof that a can-do attitude, along with a little support, can still help achieve the American dream.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: For Eye on America, I`m Ian Lee in Cleveland, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Unfortunately, though, there`s a tragic footnote to this story. A short time after we met Mary Lamar, a fire destroyed her house and killed her partner of 18 years. Lamar was able to jump to safety, but was seriously hurt.\par \par \uc2 MAGNET and her employer, Talan, have set up a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  for her. We wish her the best.\par \par \uc2 And up next: AOL and the dial-up disconnect.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Finally, tonight: AOL says it is ending dial-up service.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The news here is that AOL still has dial-up service.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In the 1990s, it was the way to connect to the Internet with, at its peak, more than 20 million subscribers.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: After downloading the program, you would click on the AOL icon, choose from a series of local phone numbers, and, if you didn`t get a busy signal, you would hear some irritating screeches and then:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MODEM CONNECTING)\par \par \uc2 COMPUTER VOICE: You`ve got mail.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR: Hey, I`ve got mail.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Voila, you were online, and no one else in the home could use the phone.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But then came broadband and Wi-Fi, and most users said goodbye to dial-up.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dial-up always was something of a misnomer. In the 1990s, the touch pad had long since replaced the dial.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS: Notice that I brought my finger around until it firmly touched the finger stop. And now I remove my finger and let the dial go back by itself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: AOL says dial-up will hang it up on September 30.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 COMPUTER VOICE: Goodbye.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Remember, you`re trying to log in. You would have time to go brush your teeth, make breakfast, make dinner, and then come back, and you would finally be in, right?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And if you were on and somebody picked up the phone to make their call, they would knock you off.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Maurice, I feel like we`re describing what it was like when our parents said, and a guy would bring a block of ice to your house for the icebox.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we had black and white TV and all that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes, that`s right. And now we`re those people.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS Plus will be with you in a moment on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250812el8b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For August 4, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Cordes, Ed O`Keefe, Jon LaPook, Jericka Duncan, Janet  Shamlian, Holly Williams, Rob Marciano, Maurice DuBois \par \uc2 3,902 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Control of Congress is on the line, and the president wants Texas Republicans to draw that line.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On Texas, how many more seats do you want the Republicans to draw?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Five.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But Texas Democrats are out to stop them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE DUSTIN BURROWS (R-TX): Members, a quorum is not present.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois. \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  is off.\par \par \uc2 And we begin with the tale of two maps. This is a proposed new map of congressional districts in Texas. It was drawn by Republicans to try to give them five more seats in the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 . And this map shows why that is so critical. Republicans now have only seven more seats than the Democrats in the U.S. House. So control will be up for grabs next year.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans gain five more seats from Texas, it would improve their chances of keeping control, maybe even adding to their majority. But Democrats in the Texas House are preventing a vote on the redistricting by fleeing the state. Late today, the Republican governor, Greg Abbott, ordered them arrested.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe followed the Texas Democrats to the suburbs of Chicago -- Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Maurice, good evening.\par \par \uc2 As you said, the Lone Star State standoff has national implications over the future balance of power in Congress. And it all began because President Trump asked Texas Republicans to act. The Texas Democrats now here in Illinois say they`re going to stay here to try to block the changes, at least temporarily.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: (voice-over): When Republicans gaveled in the Texas Statehouse today in Austin...\par \par \uc2 (GAVEL BANGING)\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE DUSTIN BURROWS (R-TX): Members, a quorum is not present.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: ... there weren`t enough Democrats in town to hold a vote on a GOP-proposed redraw of the state`s congressional map, leading Speaker Dustin Burrows to threaten their arrest.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE DUSTIN BURROWS: This House will not sit quietly while you obstruct the work of the people.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The new map is part of a special legislative session Republican Governor Greg Abbott called mostly to address the aftermath of deadly July flooding.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers we spoke with in St. Charles, Illinois, John Bucy, Ann Johnson, James Talarico, say that`s an issue they wanted to address.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE JOHN BUCY (D-Texas): We had a cabin full of 8-year- old girls get washed away in the middle of the night. That`s an emergency worthy of our time, our attention and our action. Unfortunately, Greg Abbott has answered the call of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and he`s holding this special session hostage.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Trump, aware that, historically, a commander in chief`s party loses House seats in the midterm elections, asked Abbott to redraw the Texas map.\par \par \uc2 On Texas, how many more seats do you want the Republicans to draw?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Five.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE JAMES TALARICO (D-Texas): Democrats and Republican politicians both gerrymander. And it`s wrong in both instances. This is particularly egregious.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And to the Republican charge that you guys leaving the state allows them to turn around and say, they`re playing games, they`re avoiding the flood debate, you say what?\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE ANN JOHNSON (D-Texas): This is kind of like the bully that takes your lunch money every day. At some point, you got to stand up. Otherwise, you just end up broke and hungry. We have told Republicans, enough. You have gone way too far.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Down in Texas, Democrats are urging them on, like the thousands who showed up across the state for recent hearings about the new map, including Vicki Toups (sp?).\par \par \uc2 You drove two hours to be here for this.\par \par \uc2 VICKI TOUPS (Protester): To protest the redistricting.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s how much it bothers you?\par \par \uc2 VICKI TOUPS: Yes, absolutely. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, Ed, how much longer could this go on?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, the Democrats we spoke to today, Maurice, say they`re going to be here for at least two more weeks, the scheduled end of that special session. If it goes that long without them being there, the governor could call another special session. If he takes up issues just related to the flood relief, they say they will come back.\par \par \uc2 But if he tries to bring up the redistricting again, they may leave the state yet again.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At the same time, Democrats in other states are responding too.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s right. And if you look here in Illinois, they have drawn their map in such a way that Republicans barely have any seats. There`s really no more territory to gain. In New York, it would be unconstitutional to do what`s going on in Texas.\par \par \uc2 So all eyes turn to California, where Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  says he`s going to work with the state legislature to hold a statewide referendum asking voters` permission to draw a new map in response to Texas if Texas does indeed redraw its map. It might give Democrats four or five more seats.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, got it. Ed O`Keefe there in Illinois, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, House seats are not the only numbers on the president`s mind. As we have reported, he is not happy with the numbers coming out of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , which led him to fire the Biden-appointed commissioner. Well, today, he said he will name, as he put it, an exceptional replacement.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s a scam, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): President Trump is still lashing out at labor economist Erika McEntarfer, who until Friday led the small, but crucial \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: So, you know what I did? I fired her.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And you know what? I did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: He fired her after the bureau`s monthly jobs report showed the U.S. economy added an underwhelming 73,000 jobs in July. At the same time, the bureau dramatically revised its jobs numbers from May and June downward, making them the smallest months for job growth since 2020.\par \par \uc2 Today, Trump called them fake political numbers concocted to hurt Republicans, an explosive accusation that his aides have yet to back up.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT (Director, National Economic Council): I think it`s a massive failure of our data agencies to be making willy-nilly changes that are difficult to understand and often have apparently partisan patterns.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  revises past jobs reports every month as more data comes in. In the final year of President Biden`s terms, jobs numbers were later revised downward for eight out of the 12 months. In the final year of Trump`s first term, they were revised up eight times.\par \par \uc2 KATHARINE ABRAHAM (Former U.S. Commissioner of Labor Statistics): That`s just part of the process of ensuring that the numbers that we end up with are as accurate as possible.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Katharine Abraham served as \uc2 BLS\uc2  commissioner under Presidents Clinton and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . I spoke to her remotely today.\par \par \uc2 KATHARINE ABRAHAM: There`s no way that I could have rigged the numbers even if I`d wanted to. The numbers are produced using procedures that are specified in advance.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: So what the president is accusing her of is something that it would be almost impossible to do?\par \par \uc2 KATHARINE ABRAHAM: Yes, that`s a fair statement.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: One of Trump`s central accusations is that McEntarfer inflated Biden`s job creation numbers to make them look better before last year`s election.\par \par \uc2 But, Maurice, that is incorrect. The bureau actually scaled back Biden`s numbers repeatedly in the months just prior to Election Day in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Nancy Cordes at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to some statistics that could save your life. They come from the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 , and they serve as a new reminder of how important it is to get screened for colon cancer beginning at the age of 45. A report out today says that, over a four-year period, the number of colon cancer cases among Americans 45 to 49 has nearly doubled.\par \par \uc2 Our Dr. Jon LaPook deals with this every day as a gastroenterologist. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Jon, as an expert in the field, what do you make of this increase in diagnoses?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I`m smiling because it`s good news.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2018, the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  said for the first time, you know what, let`s start screening people at average risk at 45, rather than 50, because we`re seeing an increase in colon cancer in younger people. So you would have expected to see increased diagnosis of colon cancer at the early stage in that group. That`s exactly what we saw, which is great news, because, caught very early, the cure rate is more than 90 percent.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That`s huge.\par \par \uc2 But, still, the study shows only about a third of the people eligible who should be getting screened are actually doing so. That doesn`t sound great.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Yes. I mean, one in three is not great. It`s better than - - as you can see from the graphic back in 2019, it was one in five. We have a ways to go. I should point out that, even though colonoscopy is the gold standard, stool tests can also be pretty good at picking up colon cancer at an early stage.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, what do you tell people who are hesitant to go out there and get this screening? They just don`t want to do it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It can save your life. Almost every colon cancer starts off as a benign polyp that over the years turns into cancer. So, if you have a polyp that`s destined to become cancer and I go and do a colonoscopy and remove it while it`s still benign, instead of getting colon cancer, you don`t.\par \par \uc2 That`s a home run over the center field wall. To me, it should overrule any hesitation that you have.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That`s pretty compelling.\par \par \uc2 Jon, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Boeing\uc2  dealing with its second labor strike in less than a year; 3,200 workers who build and maintain fighter jets walked off the job today. They rejected a four-year contract offer that included a 20 percent pay raise and $5,000 in bonus.\par \par \uc2 A fire broke out this morning aboard a New Jersey commuter train across from Manhattan. A number of passengers were treated for smoking elation. Federal investigators trying to determine what caused it.\par \par \uc2 And actress Loni Anderson died over the weekend. She is best remembered as a sharp-witted receptionist on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  sitcom WKRP in Cincinnati.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD HESSEMAN (Actor): Jennifer, how`d you like to join a union?\par \par \uc2 LONI ANDERSON (Actress): Johnny, I already belong to a union. It`s a quasi-religious group called the International Sisterhood of Blonde Receptionists. If I told you our minimum salary, you would have a heart attack and die. Bye.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Loni Anderson was 79.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead here on the CBS Evening News, Rob Marciano on clouds of smoke moving in from Canadian wildfires, plus this tonight:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: I`m Janet Shamlian in DeWitt, Iowa. They came to towns like this fleeing war and chasing the American dream, but now the fate of Ukrainian refugees is in limbo. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And disappearing butterflies -- why their population is shrinking.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We have been watching this picture all day, an export from Canada, smoke pouring into the Northern United States from wildfires on the other side of the border.\par \par \uc2 Rob Marciano here in New York City with the hazy days of summer -- Rob.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you can see it behind me. I mean, the sun here in New York and New Jersey is being shrouded by the haze and the smoke from those wildfires all the way up in parts of Northern Canada. And we`re not the only Americans that have been dealing with this. Many are dealing with really unhealthy levels of this stuff. You see advisories that are out from Minnesota to Maine all the way down to Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Those individual sites like Elmira and Scranton and Detroit, they have got unhealthy levels of smoke -- of air quality up and over the 150 mark. All right, this is a satellite picture. You kind of see the darker grade right along the Pennsylvania-New York border. That is smoke, and it drifts all the way to Detroit, which was the fourth worst unhealthy area in the world, with 156 for an AQI.\par \par \uc2 And where that air parcel comes from is way up in Alberta and then picks up that smoke across the boreal forests and brings it all down, not a good track for anybody, but we do see some improvement coming not today, not tomorrow, maybe for the Midwest. It gets -- digs in on Wednesday for much of the Northeast before it begins to wane and maybe lighten up as we head towards the weekend.\par \par \uc2 But the next couple of days are going to be dangerous, for sure. Meanwhile, in the West, we have fires there, and we have smoke there and heat across parts of Phoenix and Las Vegas, in the depths of summer right now, Maurice,.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We need a break.\par \par \uc2 Rob Marciano, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, Russia and China are conducting joint naval maneuvers in the Sea of Japan this week, as ties between the two nations grow stronger. At the same time, the United States has increased the number of Marines and sailors in Northern Australia to 2,500 in response to the growing Chinese threat in the Asia-Pacific region.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams went along with the Marines on maneuvers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voice-over): We flew across the Australian Outback with a platoon of U.S. Marines in a pair of Ospreys. We landed in enemy territory, and the Marines fought their way forward..\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Push it! Push it!\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: ... through the forest to stage a raid.\par \par \uc2 This is just an exercise, more than 500 U.S. Marines training alongside their Australian and Japanese allies, over 2,000 troops in total across an area bigger than Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: A group of Australians role-played the enemy combatants. This exercise is part of the U.S. military`s expansion in Asia, an effort to prevent war by deterring China from military aggression.\par \par \uc2 The Australian commander here is Brigadier Ben McLennan.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. BEN MCLENNAN (Australian Commander): Every time you commit to an exercise like this, it is a rehearsal, and you treat it as if it`s your last opportunity to do so before war arrives.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: What kind of a war?\par \par \uc2 BRIG. BEN MCLENNAN: A rehearsal for a war, the likes of which we haven`t seen since the Second World War.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: China`s President Xi Jinping is thought to have ordered his military to be ready to invade the island of Taiwan by 2027 if necessary, raising fears of a conflict that could draw in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Australia`s around 2,500 miles south of China, a so-called unsinkable aircraft carrier.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go!\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The U.S. military presence here is now at its biggest since 1945.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. NICHOLAS FOUST (\uc2 U.S. Marine Corps\uc2 ): This is the 81-millimeter mortar platoon.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Major Nick (sp?) Foust, the Marine commander, told us his men are building personal connections with America`s allied forces.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. NICHOLAS FOUST: Forging those relationships is crucial.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: If it ever came to war, they`d be just vital.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. NICHOLAS FOUST: Whether it is war or whether it is a crisis or contingency, we`re just building relationships.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The U.S. Marines were victorious in this exercise. America`s alliances in this region have perhaps never been more important.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That`s Holly Williams reporting from the Australian Outback.\par \par \uc2 Well, they put down roots in Iowa farmland, but now the future of Ukrainian refugees is in doubt. One thing is for sure, though. Their neighbors don`t want to lose them. Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This is a pivotal time for Ukrainians on opposite sides of the world. President Trump has given Russia until Friday to end the 3.5- year-old war, or face severe sanctions, and 200,000 Ukrainians who fled that war are waiting to learn their fate because their permits to work here in the U.S. are not being renewed.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voice-over): Liana Avetisian and most of her family fled Ukraine in 2023 and have built a new life in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 So, you have really been embraced by this community.\par \par \uc2 LIANA AVETISIAN (Ukrainian Refugee): Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Resettling in rural DeWitt, Iowa, which calls itself the crossroads to opportunity.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA BOELENS (Assists Refugees): This is your new community.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And it`s been just that for more than 75 Ukrainians like herself welcomed by people like Angela Boelens, who has helped many with housing and jobs.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA BOELENS: I get a phone call every week from employers saying, do you have anybody from Ukraine who needs a job? Because we have a work force shortage in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In Kyiv, Avetisian sold real estate. Here, she took a job at a window replacement company.\par \par \uc2 SAM HEER (Business Owner): To find people that want to work and take pride in their work to our standards is not easy. I want more Ukrainians if they want to...\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: You want more?\par \par \uc2 SAM HEER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Sam Heer employed Avetisian, her husband and her cousin before their two-year work permits expired. Most European countries are renewing work permits of Ukrainian refugees. The Trump administration is not.\par \par \uc2 This is a red state?\par \par \uc2 SAM HEER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Who did you vote for?\par \par \uc2 SAM HEER: I voted for Trump.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: If you had the president or the administration`s ear, you would say what?\par \par \uc2 SAM HEER: These people are hard to find and come by. They have been through upheaval just to get here. And then, all of a sudden, what they thought they were building towards is shaken. When people do the right thing and follow the rules, they should be rewarded.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For Avetisian and her teenage daughter, the opportunity of DeWitt could be slipping away.\par \par \uc2 So you bought this house?\par \par \uc2 LIANA AVETISIAN: Yes, we bought this house after...\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: That`s a pretty big deal.\par \par \uc2 LIANA AVETISIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: If you had to sell the house to leave here, how would that be for you?\par \par \uc2 LIANA AVETISIAN: It would break my heart, really.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Some might say they were never promised that they could stay here permanently, so why shouldn`t they have to move on?\par \par \uc2 ANGELA BOELENS: They were promised to stay until it`s safe to return. It`s not safe to return. It`s not even close. And when we make a promise to people, I feel we keep it.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Boelens says many more work permits are about to expire, leaving Ukrainian refugees in limbo. They may not be forced to leave, but without a job, they can`t afford to stay.\par \par \uc2 Would you go back to Kyiv?\par \par \uc2 LIANA AVETISIAN: It`s not safe to go back to Kyiv. Every day, every night, it`s bombing. And I have a lot of videos from my mom.\par \par \uc2 (WEAPONS FIRE)\par \par \uc2 LIANA AVETISIAN: That`s my mom.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Having left everything behind, refugees like Avetisian hope to stay rooted in the American heartland they call home.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Janet Shamlian in DeWitt, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And up next: the disappearing butterflies.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We end tonight with the flight of the butterflies. Just where have they gone?\par \par \uc2 Here`s Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I don`t like things that fly. I will be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): Depending on your tolerance for bugs, walking into this butterfly garden can either be calming or a little creepy.\par \par \uc2 Ooh. Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 But the future of these creatures is up in the air.\par \par \uc2 Jilian Fazio is the director of New Jersey`s Essex County Turtle Back Zoo, which houses more than 1,500 butterflies in this tent.\par \par \uc2 What is happening with butterflies in the United States?\par \par \uc2 JILIAN FAZIO (Director, Essex County Turtle Back Zoo): Butterflies are threatened by habitat loss, pesticides, and climate change, which is causing severe weather events.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: From 2000 to 2020, there was a 22 percent decrease nationwide, and a \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  study released today reveals that butterfly populations in the Midwest are dwindling even faster. No butterfly species has increased in that region in the last three decades.\par \par \uc2 Why is this important?\par \par \uc2 JILIAN FAZIO: Pollinators are responsible for a lot of the food we eat, so, just at the very basic level, humans rely on pollinators for food.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What is it about them that`s so special?\par \par \uc2 BOY: It`s because they`re harmless and they only land on you. They almost look like fairies.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: They almost look like fairies. OK. Oh, there goes one right there.\par \par \uc2 It`s hard to imagine a world without butterflies, but how we respond now could determine whether we see more of them take flight in the future.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan, CBS News, West Orange, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that is the CBS Evening News. I`m Maurice DuBois. I will be sitting in for \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  on Evening News Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7. We will see you there and right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250805el8400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Texas Redistricting Fight; Dangerous Western Wildfires; Air Quality Concerns; 3,200 Boeing Employees Go on Strike; Search for Veteran Who Opened Fire in Montana Bar; Manhunt in Tennessee for Man Accused of Killing Four; "Rigged" Jobs Report; Former Special Counsel Under Investigation; Russia Volcano Erupts for First Time in Centuries; Scary Boat Collision; Benefit Concert Held for Texas Flood Victims; Remembering Loni Anderson; "Rockstar" Moment for Pope Leo; Playground Burn Warning; Josh Brolin Live; Soundtrack for Love; A Love for Lawn Care; Extending Your Joy Span; CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Ed O`Keefe, Jason  Allen, Rob Marciano, Omar Villafranca, Weijia Jiang, Chris Livesay, David  Schechter, Meg Oliver, David Begnaud \par \uc2 14,259 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your week as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Tony is off. We`re following out-of-control wildfires in the West.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, a political showdown in Texas. Why Democratic lawmakers have fled the state. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Texas Democrats flee to Illinois over what they call a power grab by Republicans, a fight with huge implications for Congress and democracy.\par \par \uc2 REP. GENE WU (D-TX): This will occur everywhere, because if one person is going to cheat and no one is going to stop it, why doesn`t everyone just do it then?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Multiple wildfires explode in size in California, burning out of control and forcing people to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is a very rapidly, fast moving fire.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The manhunt continues for an Army vet accused of killing four people in a bar. Police say he is armed and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Like if it`s the Mike Brown I know, it doesn`t surprise me.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Actress, Loni Anderson has died.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Anderson is best known for her role on the hit T.V. comedy "WKRP in Cincinnati."\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: This was at a Las Vegas Aces game. The old baby race promotion. Well, this baby said, forget crawling.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: Go, go. Her parents were just as surprised. Dad saying that those were her first steps. Get her a basketball.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: He drives this one. High and deep to right center. RobertAnthony Cruz, game winning walk off home run.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Redemption for Coach Rac after his viral cable news flip fail --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CNN "News Central")\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: -- lifting the Savannah Bananas to victory with his first ever walk off homer. It looks like he`s still got the moves.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: The ground in Camden Yards going buck wild and crews doing aerials all over the diamond.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are so fun to watch.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve got to go to a game.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But it also just shows that anybody can have a bad day, as he did on CNN.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When he goes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because that looks like that hurt.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is incredible.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They channel their inner Vincent Moon when they do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait to go to a game.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love watching them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It looks -- look at these!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, wow!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: These are the most athletic dudes on the planet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So much fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are making baseball so fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with something that`s not so fun, depending on your point of view, it`s a showdown that could have a big impact on democracy in this country.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, the Republicans want to withdraw the political map in a way that could help them win additional seats. So in response, Democratic lawmakers have just fled the state. Last night, they gathered in Illinois, where they called the move a power grab by President Trump, and now Republican leaders are threatening to expel and arrest them if they do not come back.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is in St. Charles, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning. Things getting very dicey in Texas today.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Texas and now, here in Illinois as well, Gayle. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 At least 51 Texas House Democrats left their home state last night, came to this lovely Illinois town in order to block a Republican written congressional map that was set to pass the State House later today.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Governor Greg Abbott is threatening to expel these legislators for not showing up for work if they don`t today. The Republican Attorney General is threatening to arrest them. They could face big fines, but so far, these Democrats are defiant.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRIS TURNER (D-TX): We are here to fight for the millions in Texas who don`t want Trump`s Texas takeover.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Dozens of Texas House Democrats are now in the Chicago suburbs, leaving their state behind, disrupting plans to vote on a new Republican drawn map to reshape congressional power in the Lone Star State.\par \par \uc2 REP. GENE WU (D-TX): we`re not here to play political games. We`re here to demand, to demand an end to this corrupt process.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Trump has directed Texas Republicans redraw their map.\par \par \uc2 On Texas, how many more seats do you want the Republicans to draw?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): Five.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In hopes of avoiding the losses that usually happen to a President`s party in midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Texas Republicans hold 25 of the state`s 38 seats in the House of Representatives, but the redrawn map would give the GOP an advantage in at least five more seats. Down in West Dallas --\par \par \uc2 REP. MARC VEASEY (D-TX): Marc Veasey, your Congressman.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hi.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARC VEASEY (D-TX): Yes. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I knew that face.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Texas Democratic Congressman Marc Veasey is a familiar face at this community center in a neighborhood he has represented for a dozen years.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARC VEASEY (D-TX): I`m really concerned that they may be left with representation that does not reflect the values and the political beliefs and desires of the people that live in these neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Veasey now wants Democrats in other states like California and Illinois to redraw their maps.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARC VEASEY (D-TX): Absolutely, I have no issues with that.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: What you`re saying is it`s time for Democrats to fight fire with fire.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARC VEASEY (D-TX): Absolutely. You have to match Trump`s energy. If you don`t match Trump`s energy, you get run over.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Texas Republican Governor Greg Abbott says the new map is necessary to address constitutional concerns raised by the Justice Department, but critics across the state call it a power grab. Thousands showed up at field hearings across the state in recent days, some driving hours to be heard.\par \par \uc2 Who drove how many hours?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Four hours.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: To come and speak tonight?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s how much this bothers you.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now, in a statement overnight, the Texas Republican Party calls the Democrats "... stunt to cut quorum nothing more than a transparent attempt to weaponize chaos and rewrite reality while Texas Republicans are working to ensure fair and lawful redistricting that reflects the will of Texans," the Republicans say "... Democrats are choosing obstruction over representation, denying voters their voice to score political headlines."\par \par \uc2 Nate, this has kicked up something down in Texas we`ve never quite seen before.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt. Everything is bigger in Texas, and even the drama.\par \par \uc2 Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to the West, where dozens of wildfires are burning this morning. In California, the Gifford Fire, north of Santa Barbara, doubled in size in just 24 hours. At least three people have been hurt, and it`s one of 14 active fires in California, seven of them that started this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 Jason Allen is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Firefighters faced ferocious, dark orange flames that raced up this steep hillside. Acres of dry brush, some up to six feet fueled the walls of fire.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is a very rapidly, fast moving fire. It takes a lot of personnel to take -- to take on something of this size.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Air tankers dropping fire retardant dealt with winds fanning the erratic flames. Nearly 50,000 acres are scorched across Santa Barbara and San Luis Obispo Counties. About 100 miles north of San Francisco, the Lake Fire spread to more than 300 acres, prompting evacuation warnings.\par \par \uc2 In Reno, Nevada, the fast moving 75-acre Piedmont Fire forced residents to evacuate. Those orders are now lifted after crews were able to contain the fire.\par \par \uc2 And in Arizona, the Dragon Bravo fire near the Grand Canyon`s North Rim has now burned more than 116,000 acres, making it a mega fire. Further north in Utah, the Monroe Canyon Fire is now more than 58,000 acres. The governor declared a State of Emergency after nearly 700 wildfires have ripped through the state this year.\par \par \uc2 The current fire season has crews all across the West enduring brutal conditions with little rest.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The type of fire that is burning right now and what is projected, they probably will be down there for the full for 14 days.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Back here at the Gifford Fire this morning, the containment has actually dropped from five percent down to three percent. Now, it is burning just to the west of the Madre Fire that was just contained last month. So officials here are hoping that burn scar is going to help this fire from spreading in at least one direction -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Jason, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Wildfire smoke is causing trouble for millions across the Midwest and Northeast. Minnesota, Wisconsin, and Illinois had to deal with serious smoke and haze over the weekend from more than 700 wildfires burning in Canada. Right now, Detroit is one of the top 10 cities in the world with the worst air quality.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is here tracking today`s air pollution problems.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Chicago was really bad there for a couple days. They`ve improved a little bit, but look at all the states here in the northeastern quadrant that are under air quality alerts from Milwaukee to Green Bay, Detroit, as you mentioned, real bad; Erie, Buffalo, all of New York State and much of New England here, and we`ve got the smoke that`s lifting a little bit in the Midwest, but then dropping as we go through the next 48 hours into the Northeast. So these numbers may get a little bit worse as we go through time. We`ll talk more about the air quality in the next hour.\par \par \uc2 Talk more about this fire that`s burning out there in the West, you just saw that. Now the containment has gone down. We`ve got relative humidity levels so they can go down this afternoon, so elevated fire behavior is expected today, gusty winds. Winds will die a little bit tomorrow, but the heat will be cranking up, not just across Southern and Central California, but really across much of the Southwest. We`ve got excessive heat warnings here and extreme heat warnings for Phoenix right through Friday.\par \par \uc2 Now we`ve got Tropical Storm Dexter. You may have heard about this over the weekend, our fourth tropical storm of the season. It is north of Bermuda by about 250 miles. Pretty weak one at this one. It is going to be a fish storm, but things are beginning to heat up in the tropics, as we see here. We`ve got this activity in the Central Atlantic, and then a 30 percent chance of something a little bit closer to home over the next seven days is developing. We`ll watch more about that as we go through time.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, over to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Rob. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Breaking now, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers who build fighter jets went on strike overnight. Thirty-two hundred workers across three facilities in Missouri and Illinois, rejected a deal that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  says would have boosted wages by an average of 40 percent. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has already seen major setbacks in recent years, including when a door panel blew out on one of its passenger jets in January last year.\par \par \uc2 And in June, one of its Dreamliner planes operated by \uc2 Air India\uc2  crashed killing 260 people. This is the latest strike seen by the company. Last year, 33,000 workers walked off the job for nearly eight weeks. Just weeks after that strike ended, the company announced a $3.8 billion quarterly loss.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning now to the search for an Army veteran accused of killing four people in a Montana bar. Officials say that 45-year-old Michael Paul Brown fled the scene after opening fire with a rifle. They`ve shared this photo of him taken right after the shooting. He is wearing only as you see in this picture, a pair of black shorts.\par \par \uc2 Brown is considered armed and extremely dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is in Anaconda, Montana with the latest on the story. Omar, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The search for Brown is ongoing. Just behind me, that`s where police have blocked off part of the road and just up the mountain, that`s where they`re focusing their search.\par \par \uc2 Now, Brown served in the Army and in the State National Guard and is an avid outdoorsman.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Tragedy struck the small, tight-knit mountain town of Anaconda, Montana on Friday. Police say 45-year-old Michael Brown walked into The Owl Bar around 10:30 in the morning and shot and killed four people.\par \par \uc2 The victims, 64-year-old bartender, Nancy Lauretta Kelley and three patrons, 59-year-old Daniel Edwin Baillie, seventy-year-old David Allen Leach and 74-year-old Tony Wayne Palm.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN KNUDSEN (Montana Attorney General): From what I have gathered, it sounds like he was a regular at this establishment. He literally lived next door. I think it`s likely that he knew the bartender and these patrons, which makes us even more heinous.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: The Montana Department of Justice released this photo of the suspect they believe was taken after he left the scene -- gaunt, barefoot and barely clothed. Officials say he then fled in a white pickup truck and was briefly pursued by law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN KNUDSEN: There was some confusion, because there were two white vehicles that were involved. There were some federal assets that were involved in that pursuit, but we -- I believe that was the last law enforcement contact.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: We spoke to some local residents who say they know the suspect and claim he has mental health issues.\par \par \uc2 SHANE CHARLES (Local resident): He`d tell us stories that he`s John Wick one day or he was the President`s right hand man.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Shane Charles (sp?) was also friendly with one of the victims, bartender Nancy Kelley.\par \par \uc2 SHANE CHARLES: Nancy was a fun, bubbly person, always happy, had a smile on her face, always the first to say, hello. How`s your day going?\par \par \uc2 All of the victims were great people. Our community is suffering because they`ve lost some great, great people.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: There`s a small army of officers who are looking for Brown. In fact, they were using a helicopter last night looking for him just near the mountain. There`s a $7,500.00 reward for any information that leads to his arrest -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, another manhunt this morning, two men are under arrest, accused of helping the suspect, a man accused of killing four members of the same family near nearly a week ago.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-eight-year-old Austin Drummond is still on the run, considered armed and dangerous. Police say he killed the mother, father, grandmother and uncle of a baby girl, then kidnapped the child before abandoning her in front of someone`s home.\par \par \uc2 Police say he knew the family. The two men arrested over the weekend are accused of helping Drummond after the killings.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A weak Jobs Report rattled Wall Street last week, and so did the President`s response. On Friday, he fired the messenger, the official who delivers the Jobs Report. He claims the whole thing is rigged against him.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House. Weijia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Vlad, good morning to you. Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 That Jobs Report was released on the same day the Trump administration`s reciprocal tariffs were announced. Businesses have long warned that the uncertainty related to tariffs could lead to downsizing and job cuts, but Trump has a different explanation for the numbers, a "scam" within the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Late Sunday night, President Trump defended his decision to fire Erika McEntarfer, the now former Commissioner of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Trump accused her, without evidence, of rigging the July Jobs Report.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`ll be announcing a new statistician sometime over the next three to four days. We had no confidence. I mean, the numbers were ridiculous what she announced.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The economy added 73,000 jobs last month, which was fewer than expected. May and June were revised downward by over 250,000. Asked if there was evidence the numbers were rigged, National Economic Council Director Kevin Hassett said the revisions are the evidence.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT (National Council of Economic Advisers director): There have been a bunch of patterns that could make people wonder, and I think the most important thing for people to know is that it`s the President`s highest priority that the data be trusted and that people get to the bottom of why these revisions are so unreliable.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Trump claims it`s not the first time McEntarfer has manipulated the numbers.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: If you remember, just before the election, this woman came out with these phenomenal numbers on Biden`s Economy -- phenomenal numbers.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But the Jobs Report that came out four days before the 2024 presidential election was revised downward. It showed in August and September of 2024, one hundred twelve thousand fewer jobs were created than initially reported.\par \par \uc2 William Beach served as the head of the BLS during Trump`s first term. He called his successor`s firing totally groundless and a dangerous precedent. Warning, if any weak numbers are released by Trump`s next pick --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BEACH (Former \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  commissioner): Everybody`s going to think, well, it`s not as bad as probably really is, because they`re going to suspect political influence. So this is damaging. This is not what we need to have.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: According to a \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analysis, the July Jobs Report had the largest revision since 1968. Changes to Job Reports, though, are common because the figures are based on estimates that are later updated as additional data comes in -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Weijia, while we have you, let me ask you about this. The Trump administration is now investigating former Special Counsel Jack Smith. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: That`s right. CBS News confirmed over the weekend that Jack Smith, who was appointed in November 2022, and oversaw two federal prosecutions of Trump is being investigated by the Office of the Special Counsel.\par \par \uc2 At issue is whether Smith potentially violated the Hatch Act, a federal law which limits certain political activities of federal employees. Some of Trump`s closest allies encouraged this move, while Democrats argue this is an attempt to turn the Special Counsel`s Office into a political weapon -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Weijia Jiang at the White House. Weijia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Overseas, a volcano in Russia erupted for the first time in at least 400 years. I want you to take a look at this plume of smoke rising from a peninsula in the country`s far east. Now you can see ash soaring nearly four miles into the sky.\par \par \uc2 Scientists say it could be related to the massive 8.8 earthquake that hit the region last week. The volcano is located in the nature preserve. Everyone nearby has been evacuated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s really beautiful, Nate, too bad it is so dangerous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know, right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is really pretty. It looks like something out of a movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, we`re going to remember the Emmy-nominated actor, Loni Anderson from that hit show back in the day, "WKRP in Cincinnati." We`re going to take a look back at her very memorable, memorable career.\par \par \uc2 Plus, look at this, it is a scary encounter on the open water. What do you think causes boat to nearly capsize, tossing one person overboard? There`s a clue. There`s a little clip there in the water. But first it is 7:17, time now to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Oscar nominee, aka Thanos, aka Brand, Josh Brolin, is here.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weapons"/Warner Bros. Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: New horror movie is getting great reviews. He`s going to talk to us about it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, it`s a full circle moment for David Begnaud in this week`s "Beg-Knows America."\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" -- did track star, Noah Lyles go too far when he crossed the finish line against a top opponent? We`re going to show you.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Inspire. No mask, no hose, just sleep.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Loud for Love"/CBS News Texas)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is Annabel Dwyer performing at "Loud for Love" last night`s benefit concert for Texas flood victims.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Shires and Coffey Anderson also performed. The show was hosted by CBS News Texas and Chef Tim Love raised more than $100,000.00 for a cause that is so near and dear to all of our hearts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I Remember that story is so heartbreaking, it`s so good that people are coming out. Remember, we were talking about Amanda Shires being on the show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And CBS Texas, the thing we talked about on Friday is that they`re members of the community. That`s why they want to help.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, coming up, our salute to Loni Anderson, the sitcom star who married Burt Reynolds and became a household name, that`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Stay here. Stay here. Oh, my God! They`re going over. Oh, man overboard!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A scary moment in the water off New Jersey there like something out of the movie "Jaws" when a boat collided with something below, sending one person tumbling. It turns out it was a whale.\par \par \uc2 An investigation is underway. Sadly, the whale did not survive, but all the people are expected to be okay. Sheesh! Scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very scary.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we remember Loni Anderson, one of the great American pop culture figures of the 1970s and 80s. The Associated Press reports she died yesterday at a Los Angeles hospital after a prolonged illness.\par \par \uc2 Anderson`s breakout role on the CBS sitcom "WKRP in Cincinnati" led to other roles and a world famous romance.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "WKRP in Cincinnati"/Shout! Studios)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Loni Anderson rose to fame as the quick-witted receptionist, Jennifer Marlowe in the CBS comedy hit series, "WKRP in Cincinnati" during the show`s four-year run from 1978 to 1982.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "WKRP in Cincinnati"/Shout! Studios)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Anderson refused to play a stereotypical dumb blonde and asked that the character be rewritten before taking on the role. She earned two Emmy nominations and three Golden Globe nods for playing that sexy and smart character on the show.\par \par \uc2 The St. Paul, Minnesota native, also appeared in popular 1970s and 80s shows like "Three`s Company" and "The Love Boat."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Love Boat"/Aaron Spelling Productions)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stroker Ace"/Universal Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On the big screen, Anderson starred alongside Burt Reynolds in the 1983 NASCAR action comedy, "Stroker Ace."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Stroker Ace"/Universal Pictures)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The two married in 1988 and adopted a son. They were together until 1994 when their messy divorce became fodder for tabloids.\par \par \uc2 Her final TV appearance was on a 2023, lifetime special "Ladies of the 80s: A Diva Christmas."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ladies of the 80s: A Diva Christmas."/Lifetime)\par \par \uc2 LONI ANDERSON (Actor): I really enjoyed my career, so I want to be there if it`s funny or like this, if it`s fun.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tomorrow would have been Loni Anderson`s 80th birthday. She is survived by her fourth husband, musician, Bob Flick, a son, a daughter, a stepson, and four grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 She was such an icon of that era that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, her role on that show is so iconic that we`re talking about it even today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the fact that she was a brunette. They wanted her to dye her hair blonde, and she said, I don`t want to do that until they said, we`ll change the character. She didn`t want to do that, boop-boop-bee-doop!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Busty, ditzy blonde, until they changed it to say, we`ll make you the smartest person in the room. I love that even back