ation from around the globe celebrated, the Pope was quick to draw attention to their peers who cannot.\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV (Bishop of Rome, head of the Catholic Church and sovereign of the Vatican City State): We are with the young people of Gaza --\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO XIV: We are with the young people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: It was a rare break from Italian into English for the American Pope, the global language reaching a global audience.\par \par \uc2 (GIRLS cheering)\par \par \uc2 RITA PIENDI (German pilgrim): It`s just amazing to see just how big the World Church is, and how many people are here just believing in God and actually being on fire to see the Pope.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOFER DELANO (American pilgrim): I know there`s going to be a lot of people, I didn`t know it`s going to be this many. So, I`m just so happy to be here. I`m so happy to see Pope Leo.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: It`s all part of a broader Catholic effort to connect with the digital generation. Last week, Pope Leo invited influencers to the Vatican, images at odds with a church in decline in much of the world. In the U.S., just three in 10 Catholics attend mass weekly, according to a study from Pew.\par \par \uc2 As teens and 20-somethings from more than 140 countries made clear, this was no ordinary mass.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The Catholic Church has long been trying to win back young people, many of them disaffected by decades of scandal surrounding the priest sex abuse of minors. According to Pew, 43 percent of people raised Catholic in the U.S. no longer identify as Catholic -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Chris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we have tips on how parents can keep their kids safe in the scorching heat, including on the playground.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With heat warnings popping up across the country, rising temperatures are turning pavements and playgrounds into straight up burn zones.\par \par \uc2 CBS News climate correspondent, David Schechter has more on what parents need to look out for, and here`s a warning for you, some of these images can be jarring.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: You`re just sort of sprawled out down here?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOOLEY (76 years old burn victim): I sprawled out head first over here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: When 76-year-old Robert Wooley lost his balance in his Phoenix backyard, it was blazing hot, and he sustained contact burns on 20 percent of his body.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOOLEY: And I just couldn`t get back up. And I looked at my hands, and the skin had peeled off my hands, like the skin from an onion, and looked like raw hamburger underneath.\par \par \uc2 DR. KEVIN FOSTER (Valleywise Health Burn Center medical director): Heat affects, you know, everything.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Dr. Kevin Foster is the medical director of the Burn Center at Valleywise Health, where they treated a record number of contact burn patients last year, including 15 deaths.\par \par \uc2 What do you attribute, though, that number going up?\par \par \uc2 DR. KEVIN FOSTER: It definitely is related to temperatures. No question about it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: When Dr. Foster and his team analyzed 16 years of data, they found a connection between rising summer temperatures driven by climate change, and more burns.\par \par \uc2 For example, in 2008 when the average summer temperature was close to 107 degrees, there were nine contact burns. By 2023, with the average temperature at 113 degrees, burn cases jumped to 120.\par \par \uc2 DR. KEVIN FOSTER: Once it gets super-hot, then the number of burns goes up, and the acuity, the seriousness of those burns, likewise, goes up.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Can you walk on your feet?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: And that includes children. This toddler suffered first degree burns on his feet after running on hot pavement, but fully recovered after treatment.\par \par \uc2 What should parents be thinking about to reduce the risk of this?\par \par \uc2 DR. KEVIN FOSTER You really have to think about it ahead of time, because you can`t really deal with it when it`s happening, because it happens so quickly, and once it`s happened, you can`t go back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Phoenix is a city grappling with the impact of heat on health, where most playgrounds and bus stops are shaded and Ryan Stevens, an engineer with the city, wanted to show us how on 140 miles of streets, they`ve applied a special asphalt coating that helps reduce surface temperatures.\par \par \uc2 RYAN STEVENS (Engineer): This street was treated, and that street hasn`t.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Okay, so let`s check it out.\par \par \uc2 We go over to the street where it hasn`t been treated, you`ve got 155 here and 144 over here.\par \par \uc2 Cooler, but not cool enough to prevent a burn, as Robert Wooley knows.\par \par \uc2 What would you tell people about being aware that this could happen?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOOLEY: The ground is a lot hotter than the air, and that contact with the hot ground is potentially lethal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: David Schechter, CBS News, Phoenix, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, the stories you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." And Vlad has some good stuff, so stick around, but right now, it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up for you at eight o`clock, including a very special milestone for "Beg-Knows America" as David marks his 50th installment with an update on one of our favorite stories. It`s about a little boy who actually, actually loves to mow the lawn. Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Oscar-nominated actor, that`s Josh Brolin is here today, first on CBS MORNINGS, with a highly anticipated new movie. It`s a very scary movie. If you like scary movies, this is for you. It`s called "Weapons."\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour. But first Klaus and I are heading over to the table because Vlad and Nate have moved over, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey, hey, hey!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you have a preview line for "What to Watch"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve got a preview. We`re going to be talking Noah Lyles and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, that`s a good tease. That`s a good tease.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, war of the words.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "What to Watch" with Vlad when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s you, my brother!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Eye of the Tiger" oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Eye of the Tiger" for "What to Watch" with Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Eye of the Tiger" is also you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m not distracted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s kind of like, "Eye of the Tiger" of Nate? Oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nate is pumping them, and he is like, he is just living the glory days on the --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I haven`t seen you. So, that`s my bad.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, what have you got, baby?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 All right, so you don`t normally see trash talking on the track and field.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yup!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But it happens.\par \par \uc2 At the U.S. championships in Oregon, things got a little tense at the end of the men`s 200-meter final yesterday. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Lyles slightly ahead and looks over and says, "I`m still the world champion" and a little bit of a push there at the end as the