eing in every championship track and field match.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`ve got another race to show you. This one, nothing but nice.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from WNBA/X)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ten little babies lined up at halftime --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- of the Las Vegas Aces game on Saturday. The goal is to crawl to a parent at the other end. The frontrunner stunned the crowd when she stood up and took what appeared to be her very first steps and walked into her mommy`s arms.\par \par \uc2 Her dad said she`d been standing up, but not walking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at that!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know dad is so happy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look, Dad is very proud.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at that little trophy. That little trophy that they`ve got.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It said "champ."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But imagine you take your first steps there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right in front of --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In front of everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But also, the baby is like, I`m not going to let these other little babies beat me, and if I have to, like, do something I`ve never done before, stumble over and get that hug.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When they are first learning to walk, it`s like, they don`t know their ankles and knees work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they do that sort of zombie --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are walking around like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so cute.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But even when they do like, when Celine is walking now, it`s like, you know, a little kids like, they can`t keep a straight line, they`re just like, walking like this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I just think it`s funny, even though she has been walking for like two years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s nice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, here`s a friendly reminder for summer vacationers. If you are eating something, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Beware of outdoor thieves. Watch this TikTok video.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Storyful)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was a seagull that snatched a juicy steak right off a plate at a restaurant in France.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The TikTok user claims that steak cost more than $100.00.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A hundred dollar steak. Seagull is going to be living and check out this sneak attack in Jersey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my goodness!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to know what she was saying to the guy. Do you know what she was saying to the guy before it came?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Victoria Shannon Reed/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does it look like? It looked like she was --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She was talking to him and saying, like "Habibi," which I think means like my little lover, my little lovey, you know, don`t quote me, but that`s what I think it means.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And now we`ve got this other sneak attack in Jersey --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seagull, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Victoria Shannon Reed/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This woman trying to get a seagull on camera. She got her wish. He stooped down and grabbed that sandwich. Do we have the audio on that? She`s mad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As long as they don`t hurt you, it`s okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Protect your sandwiches and your face.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, we have actor Josh Brolin. He has a new horror movie coming out. He`s going to talk about it on the show.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well, hey, y`all, it`s David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`re celebrating the 50th installment of "Beg-Knows America" where you know we find the heart in every story, and to mark the milestone, we`re going back to where it all began, our very first character, the young man who had a lawn mower and two dreams.\par \par \uc2 He was featured in our first story. What`s he doing now? We`ll show you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, actor, Josh Brolin isn`t a scary guy, but his new movie is very scary. He`s here to talk about the movie. It`s called "Weapons." Even the trailer has people at the edge of their seats.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a full circle moment for "Beg-Knows America." For his 50th installment, David Begnaud returns to his first story about a very inspiring young man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Could music be the key to finding love? A new concert series says it`s worth a shot, and there`s actually research to back all of this up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRIS TURNER (D-TX): We are here to fight for the millions of Texas who don`t want Trump`s Texas takeover.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Democratic lawmakers in Texas flee the state to delay a Republican effort to gain seats in Congress.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: How many more seats do you want the Republicans to draw?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Five.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dozens of fast growing wildfires explode in size in the West, forcing evacuations.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is a very rapidly, fast moving fire. It takes a lot of personnel to take -- to take on something of this size.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The manhunt continues in Montana for an Army vet accused of gunning down four people at a bar.\par \par \uc2 SHANE CHARLES: Our community is suffering because they`ve lost some great, great people.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Look at Ledecky go! Look at Ledecky go!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Katie Ledecky continues her dominance with her seventh straight -- you heard that right -- seventh straight 800-meter freestyle world title. You go Katie Ledecky.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: The seven-time champion of this event, the first ever in World Championship history.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Simply the best.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love watching her in the water, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yup!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Simply the best.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That stroke was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Katie La-GOATy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Katie La-GOATy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is the GOAT for sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we are going to start this hour with the wildfire smoke from Canada making it harder to breathe for millions of people across the Northern U.S.\par \par \uc2 It was a big problem in Minnesota and Wisconsin over the weekend. Now all of that smoke is moving eastward. Forecasters expect poor air quality from Wisconsin to New York today.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is here with the information you need to keep you and your family safe.\par \par \uc2 Rob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Hey, good morning again, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, man, that`s when it was really bad. Remember those fires? Well, we are approaching that sort of Canadian wildfire season. Over 700 fires are currently burning, most of which are in Saskatchewan and Manitoba across the Boreal Forest. Most of them are natural, but that smoke amplified by climate change is getting into the northeastern third of the country.\par \par \uc2 So places like Green Bay, Milwaukee, Detroit, you`re in it as far as the Air Quality Alerts go. Detroit is like a top three right now in the world as far as cities that are unhealthy with the air quality there, so that smoke is very dense in there. These numbers will replicate, obviously, green being good; yellow, not so good; oranges is bad; red is even worse.\par \par \uc2 So here is your index, and basically, if you get over a hundred, that`s when you should take notice. If you are a member of the unhealthy group, older people, you should limit your outdoor activity.\par \par \uc2 But like in Detroit right now, it`s over 150, so unhealthy for everybody. So Nate, even a world class athlete like yourself would be suffering in Detroit right now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Rob, that`s my question. If you have poor air quality, what can you do to protect yourself?\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Well, there`s some obvious things, and then there`s some kind of DIY, do it yourself, DIY things that we can talk about as well.\par \par \uc2 Avoid burning. You don`t want to barbecue. You don`t want to have a fire pit outside. You don`t want to add to things. And then maybe a portable air cleaner. If you don`t have a high end filter --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: -- you can make one yourself. Okay, so most of the filters in our HVAC units are kind of lower MERV. MERV is the minimum efficiency rating value, and basically you want a higher MERV when you have smoke around.\par \par \uc2 And you can do that by just getting a plain old box fan, taking a MERV of 13 filter, square one, just pasting it to the back side of this. You want to make it even more efficient. You can kind cut out a circular thing, and that`ll kind of focus things more, even more efficient than that, add two in the back here. If you want to get really crazy, you can do a whole cube here and get things, and this will be almost, you know, to the quality of, say, of a hospital.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: So that would be the extreme thing. But I think just box fan, get one filter, and you`re good to go. And obviously pay attention to some of the symptoms, right? Well, if you can smell smoke, sometimes you can`t smell it, and the particles are small enough to where you should start coughing. You`ll have trouble breathing. Your eyes will start to sting. Boy, when we cover wildfires, that`s the one thing we always get, scratchy throats, runny noses, headaches. I am starting to sound like a doctor here, but you just feel like -- you don`t feel so great when the air quality is poor. And I think we`re going to be into this for the remainder of the summer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, I`m going to take that box fan trick. You`re over there, like my MacGyver. I see you.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Don`t ask me to build it. I am just telling you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But you know what I`ll take with my MERV, some gold and frankincense.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little biblical humor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. We knew what you meant.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did you?\par \par \uc2 Rob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we actually did, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, it`s MERV. All right, ahead, "Beg-Knows America" revisits the story of a boy with a booming business -- I didn`t even get a little clap -- who`s now giving back in a big way. Look here, who`s in our green room? Check it out. It is Oscar-nominated actor and producer, Josh Brolin from the new film, "Weapons." But first it is 8:05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was supposed to be here alone with Josh, but everybody said, I want to go talk to Josh Brolin.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hang out!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I say you meet the nicest people in our progressive greenroom, because Josh, we`re talking about your movie.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN ("Weapons" actor): Yay!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was scary.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Scary AF! That`s all I am going to say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It delivers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And with a plot!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Anything you want to say about "Weapons"?\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: But you all have got your totally different reactions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: You didn`t see the ending. You were very scared by it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I loved it.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: You were laughing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I couldn`t even -- normally a scary ending, I would just go like this. But I was like ugh!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Josh, I think that spells success.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: I mean, if you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right?\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: I hope. I mean, look, we`re all talking about it. We all can`t stop talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all talking about it.\par \par \uc2 Josh, we`re going to talk about it more after the break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re still talking about this movie.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is Academy Award nominee, Josh Brolin, whose new movie is being praised by critics.\par \par \uc2 So Brolin stars alongside Emmy winner, Julia Garner in "Weapons," it is called. He, by the way, is also executive producer, thank you very much. Brolin plays Archer, a dad whose son is one of 17 elementary children from the same class to mysteriously disappear in the middle of the night. Where`d they go?\par \par \uc2 So Archer becomes very frustrated with the police investigation, and he blames the teacher, played by Julia Garner.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weapons"/Warner Bros. Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she is.\par \par \uc2 Josh Brolin joins us first on CBS MORNINGS. Welcome to you.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s just say Dad Archer is a badass and takes things into his own hands.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When he doesn`t like what police are doing.\par \par \uc2 But this is the thing about this movie. We all have all these different perceptions. I personally think you shouldn`t watch it by yourself. It`s scary. It`s scary, but it also is smart scary in that you`re trying to figure out what is happening here.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: It`s a brilliantly designed, I think that`s what people are reacting to most. It`s a brilliantly designed script. And I read it first, I hadn`t seen his first movie "Barbarian." And then I got to -- I wanted to meet him, and then he told me, which I think is interesting, that every character is based off a reaction that he had to a friend of his passing away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh. Who`s the he is you`re trying to --\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: It`s Zach Cregger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Zach Cregger who did "Barbarian" is the writer/director of "Barbarian," which I thought was a really kind of great emotional aspect, and then he took this exhausted genre of horror that we`re all so used to and kind of bored by, and made something super unique.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because Josh, you say, it stays with you, continues to crawl through you and won`t let go.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Well, look, we`re all talking about it. We`ve all been spazzing out talking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: You had one reaction, you had another reaction, you and your wife and your daughter had another reaction.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s incredible. It`s -- I love horror movies.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And this is one of the best I`ve seen in decades. The movie is brilliantly constructed.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The way that it is split into chapters --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chapters.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- following each character.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Yup!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then we find ourselves inside these intersections and how the story lines meet.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done! But let`s talk about you, though, because you say your character represents the current every man.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there are moments in this film where you are talking and I`m saying to myself, if I was in a situation, that`s exactly what I would do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What I would do.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: What you would do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So why do you describe your character as every man?\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Because I was in the middle of the movie and I say, I have nothing to do. I felt really bored, because, you know, you do -- the every man is what? It is somebody who`s a victim of their circumstances, and he wants to have control.\par \par \uc2 And then he doesn`t have his kid, and then he realizes what he wasn`t giving his kid, which was affection and time and presence, and then -- and then, once, maybe he gets his kid back, you see this guy go through the trajectory of being from totally unemotional to emotional and available.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: And I`m like, look, I love the idea of confronting masculinity like that, some kind of manual masculinity that we all live by that says, you know, we don`t do anything and we don`t smile, we don`t laugh and we don`t cry and all this kind of stuff. And you go, have the thing that you care most about be taken away from you, and then see what happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See what you do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Josh, I`m going to fan boy for just a little while.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because you`ve been one of my favorite actors in Hollywood, going back to when I was a teenager and I first saw "The Goonies." I mean, you`ll always be --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Goonies," yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But then let`s talk about Thanos, the MCU. Let`s talk about Gurney in "Dune."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s talk about Tom Cheney in "True Grit." Let`s talk about Cable in "Deadpool 2." You`ve done it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He doesn`t like you much, Josh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, "Wall Street," "Money Never Sleeps."\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Wait, where is my --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Josh is like keep talking.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Keep talking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So in this particular film, though, what you`re doing is you`re channeling all of these different emotions at once. You`ve got frustration, you`ve got anger, you`ve got sadness. Sometimes you`re doing this in a single scene, talk to me about that process.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And comedy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And comedy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Talk to me about that process.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: You know what? It`s -- I don`t -- thank you for saying that. I think that`s really sweet. But the thing is, is you choose these roles and you go, how can I be challenged? How can I continue to 41 years I`ve been doing this. You do a role like Cable. It`s something, you know, I was 50. I was like, I wonder if I can get in really good shape at 50. That was my motivation. You do Thanos. It was supposed to be like a cameo, and it turned out to be a huge thing. So you don`t know how things are going to turn out.\par \par \uc2 With this horror genre. It`s like, okay, this is an interesting confrontation of masculinity, but it`s also a genre I haven`t really been involved with.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weapons"/Warner Bros. Pictures)\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: I watched horror films when I was a kid. I had kids, I stayed away from horror films, and I thought this is a really well-designed script. How can I -- I don`t know if I can do this or not? And I like the idea of not knowing if I can do something.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you like horror films as a kid?\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was your --\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: I mean, I saw the "Friday the 13th." It`s really irresponsible, my mom loved horror films, and she read a lot of like, true crime books, which was totally -- you know, so she`d come up and say, watch this movie with me. And it`d be like, "I Spit On Your Grave."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That one is --\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: That`s horrible.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a deep cut.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: It`s a deep cut. It is irresponsible parenting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How old were you when you first saw that?\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: I`m not going to tell you, but I saw the -- I remember "The Blob." I remember "Friday the 13th." I remember "Poltergeist" later on. I mean, but see, that`s what you and I were talking about. It`s like things that actually have an impact, and you sit around with people and you talk about it.\par \par \uc2 We don`t have a lot of those movies. "Sinners" was one of those.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, you`ve given it --\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: "Get Out" was one of those.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you have one, right!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And now you have one, Josh Brolin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s called "Weapons."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Josh!\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Weapons" is in theaters and IMAX on Friday, like Gail says, run, don`t walk. I`m telling you, I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the big screen, got to see it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tomorrow, Brolin`s co-star, Julia Garner will join us first on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 JOSH BROLIN: Yay!\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: For many, summer is a time when love is in the air, but a rising number of singles are back to searching for a partner the old fashioned way, in person. Yes, in person.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the use of dating apps dropped about 16 percent last year on the top 10 most popular apps. Meg Oliver checked out a new event that uses music to try and spark a little romance.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: When it comes to dating these days, perhaps there`s nothing that`s making heart sing more than music itself.\par \par \uc2 The music event startup group Sofar Sounds has witnessed that firsthand at their new singles night events in six cities worldwide, including London.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Maybe you`ll meet tonight the love of your life.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: And New York City.\par \par \uc2 ALEXA RENEE (Sofar Sounds NYC Crew lead): Okay, so I`m going to introduce our very first artist tonight.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: They are intimate pop-up concerts for young adults looking for love through a common passion.\par \par \uc2 Their quick sellouts have been quite the surprise, just so far as crew lead, Alexa Renee.\par \par \uc2 ALEXA RENEE: Who here is single?\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: What`s the response been like?\par \par \uc2 ALEXA RENEE: The response has been kind of overwhelming. I mean, I think we started implementing this because we knew that there was a desire for it, right? But I think that we`ve all sort of been shocked by the excitement that our guests have expressed.\par \par \uc2 ALEXA RENEE: Here are the Bingo cards, they`re about to --\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: That excitement comes as new research indicates singles are turning to micro communities, like running groups, book clubs and concerts to meet people in person through shared interests instead of online.\par \par \uc2 But neuroscientist Daniel Levitin, who studies music and the brain, says music can strike the first chord in a successful relationship.\par \par \uc2 What is the science between music and love?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LEVITIN (Neuroscientist): Well, it`s a complicated question. We love music and we fall in love to music. Music activates the same reward and pleasure centers of the brain as love does.\par \par \uc2 We get this burst of good feeling chemicals when we listen to music we like, or when we`re in love.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: How can music affect a relationship?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LEVITIN: Your individual views about things correlate highly with your musical taste, not perfectly, but well enough that if you find somebody who likes the same music as you do, 70 percent chance you`re going to hold a lot of the same core values.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Music to the ears of a whole lot of singles, looking for a special spark.\par \par \uc2 What inspired you to come here tonight?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Honestly, I need to just kind of put myself out there, try something new.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Do you feel like music can play a role in helping you find love?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Absolutely. I mean, I can`t be with somebody that doesn`t have similar taste of music than me. That would be like mixing water and vinegar.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: What would your best advice be for singles, looking for love.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LEVITIN: The secret of love, of course, is allowing yourself to be vulnerable, but more importantly, creating a safe space where your potential partner feels they can be vulnerable with you.\par \par \uc2 Shared music is really a good way to do that.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: A refreshing way to find love that hits all the right notes.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Meg Oliver in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looks like a vibe.\par \par \uc2 All right, a new book wants us to find more joy as we age. Ahead, we will talk to the author of "Joyspan." That`s after "Talk of the Table" so stick around. We have plenty of more show to go.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that song, "Walking on Sunshine."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know you do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People say, what would be your theme song? I always pick that because I still happen to be on the planet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It is time to bring you some of the stories that we call "Talk of the Table." Because this is a table we be talking and Vlad, you`re in pole position.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I`m kind of bummed, I`m not happy today --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- because we know how hard it is to say goodbye to friends.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that`s what Carrie, Charlotte, Samantha and Miranda have become since the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And they are my "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We first met when they were single ladies in their 30s on HBO`s "Sex and the City."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Sex and the City"/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love this show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We laughed and we cried --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yup!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- with them through their marriages.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We wanted Manolo Blahniks.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All the Manolo Blahniks, the "Cosmopolitans" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The makeups and the breakups.\par \par \uc2 The recent reboot, "And Just Like That" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "And Just Like That"/HBO Max)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- gave us a chance to reunite with our old friends and meet some new ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course, now the series is ending after the third season wraps this month. We`re not the only ones that were emotional about this farewell.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Jessica Parker posted this on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 : "Carrie Bradshaw has dominated my professional heartbeat for 27 years. The symphony of all those emotions has been the greatest soundtrack and most consequential companion, therefore the most sentimental and profound gratitude and a lifetime of debt to you all."\par \par \uc2 Kristen Davis, who play Charlotte, wrote that she was profoundly sad, and I was too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This new reboot wasn`t as good, of course, as the original, nothing ever is, but it was like catching up with old friends and meeting some new ones.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But can we just talk about how seminal "Sex and the City" was?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve never seen a show like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Kudos to Darren Star, who I got the chance wants to meet at the Paramount up front, one day he was backstage, and I was just like, so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Michael Patrick King.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Darren Star and Michael Patrick King, I`m like, Darren, what you created --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- is seismic in pop culture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It pulled the curtain back on a specific living experience that wasn`t depicted on T.V. --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- at that time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We had never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I still like "And Just Like That," because I was really glad to see them back on screen. Nuts about all of them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sarah Jessica, I love what Sarah Jessica Parker said, they`re going to be missed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I think that what`s interesting, too, for me, is that love them or hate them. There are some people who thought New York City changed profoundly when "Sex and the City" came out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, because, like, okay, all of a sudden the meat packing was a thing, right? Neighborhoods became overrun. You still can`t go in front of Carrie`s original West Village apartment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because of the sheer influence of that show.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s my point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it. I love it. I love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nice one, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, what have you got.\par \par \uc2 The guys are going to be missed.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" -- I love this one, too -- story of one family that got a big surprise after losing their family pet. Anybody who`s lost a family pet, you know how tough this is.\par \par \uc2 So a California family lost their beloved terrier-chihuahua mix, that`s Rufus. So the kids are ages are four, six had never known life without little Rufus. So they did what any family does, they started looking for their next dog, eventually finding Ziggy.\par \par \uc2 Ziggy`s photo on a website that specializes in finding homes for older dogs. They were smitten and also fascinated by the resemblance of the two dogs.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was going to say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They look alike, don`t they?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So they brought Ziggy home.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Whoa!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They discovered other similarities. So somebody in the family said, let`s do a DNA test. They found that Ziggy was Rufus` father.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No way! No way, Gayle!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No way!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Ain`t that sweet? I love that story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What -- Ziggy is Rufus` dad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ziggy is Rufus` dad. So they committed once -- they adopted Ziggy without even knowing the background. Rufus was about 15 when he passed. They think Ziggy is about 16 or maybe even 17, but little six-year- old Maya said they both look like the same colors. That`s how little kids talk.\par \par \uc2 So we`re hoping that Ziggy hangs on a little bit longer. I love that they got to have this connection.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Unbeknownst.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is wild!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that, Gayle. That was good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is a very close encounter with two animals in the wild. I`ll explain. One involves a bear. Okay, so they make a very, very close encounter. Let me show you exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Seattle Kraken)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So some folks were fly fishing. They were on a fly fishing trip in Alaska. This happened back in June. The Seattle Kraken`s mascot was on the trip as well, a six-foot tall blue sea troll named, Buoy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yep! So some say the unique look of the mascot got the bear`s attention. The mascot was fishing with John Hayden for the NHL team and some others, when they spotted the bear. The bear spotted them, and then the group took off, rightfully so, right?\par \par \uc2 The bear actually followed them. The bear even made a charge at Buoy, but the mascot remained calm and the bear stopped. Hayden says he blames it all on Buoy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On Buoy, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The bear seemed to be captivated by his look.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Buoy said time to go!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Talk about a love encounter in the wild.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time to go. I am out!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Feet do your stuff. We`ve gots to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, ahead, David Begnaud checks in with a very young businessman whose story kicked off "Beg-Knows America" as we celebrate a big milestone for the series, but first time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 Today marks the 50th "Beg-Knows America" here on CBS MORNINGS where CBS News contributor, David Begnaud finds the heart in every story.\par \par \uc2 To celebrate, we want to take you back to where this series first started, a story about a 12-year-old businessman with some big, big dreams.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Before most kids were even thinking about a summer job, Quentin Hines, Jr. from Charlotte, North Carolina was running one. He was 10 then, now he`s 12, and he is making moves, and not just with mowing lawns.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR. (Young entrepreneur): I started cutting yards probably when I was about like, probably by myself when I was probably about eight years old, but with my dad, it was probably around like ever since how I could walk.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Really?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: How much do you love to cut grass?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: I really, really love it. I really love it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What do you love about it?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: It makes me feel less stressful and make me feel very like soothed.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I heard you have some dreams about working at the airport.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: So ever since I was younger, I always wanted to get a contract with the Charlotte Airport. And like sometimes when I would go out there, they would have the mowers out there, so I would like stick there for a while.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: His passion was contagious. So we got to thinking, what could we do to help a kid like Quentin get just a little closer to his dream?\par \par \uc2 I got a surprise for you.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I hear too like mowing.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Mind being part of my crew today?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But Quentin`s big day was just getting started.\par \par \uc2 DANNY LOSITO (\uc2 Carolina Panthers\uc2 ` Director of Sports Fields and Ground): I`m the Director of Sports Field and Grounds here for \uc2 Carolina Panthers\uc2 . We`re happy to have you. You want to be part of the grounds crew today?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANNY LOSITO: Yes?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The Panthers gave Quentin the job and a jersey and put him to work, and just when he thought the day couldn`t get any better.\par \par \uc2 What does it say?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, SR. (Quentin Jr`s. dad): Wow, man!\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Mind blowing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We checked back in with Quentin this month, and guess what? He was right where we left him. Like the weeds, business is booming for QJ`s Professional Lawn Care.\par \par \uc2 Quentin doubled his number of clients from last year. Got some new equipment.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Is it another hedge trimmer?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And some new attention?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: I was working on a yard down the street, and someone rolled past. They put their window down. They said, hey, I recognize him from the news. It feels like I kind of became a local celebrity.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And watching a story about yourself? Well, Quentin says that felt surreal.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: I was sitting with my friends and we were watching the air, I did get a little teary-eyed seeing that I did all of that in one day. I was like, I actually did all of this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But the year wasn`t just about success. Quentin lost his grandmother --\par \par \uc2 GRANDMA: Look at QJ driving a lawn mower!\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And his grandfather, the man who ignited his love for lawn mowing. His family found comfort and community in their church.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In Jesus name, we pray. Amen.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP answer "Amen.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And I bet you can guess what Quentin did next.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: I`ve never really cut their -- cut the grass before, but I already felt welcome with coming into the church. And after I started mowing the grass, I felt like, even more welcomed into the church.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He felt so welcome that the pastors asked Quentin to preach to the whole church.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: What that shows us is that God sees our hearts, and he rewards humility.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And then Quentin took it a step further. He donated $1,000.00 from his own earnings to help the church pay off its mortgage.\par \par \uc2 Did we mention he`s only 12?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: When you put things into the world, it always finds a way to come back to you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: These days, Quentin is mowing grass about six to eight hours a week, and yes, his parents are still his only employees. Mom handles the business side.\par \par \uc2 SHIRLEY HINES (Quentin, Jr.`s mom): You get those two done on Friday.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And dad, well, dad still drives Quentin to his clients, and he helps with the bigger jobs. But we had to ask, how`s that working out still?\par \par \uc2 What`s the biggest problem you have with your only employee?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: Oh, sometimes, he takes a lot of breaks.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It turns out, some things never change.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: He`s still taking a lot of breaks.\par \par \uc2 So at first, when I would try and pay my dad, he will always say, "No, keep your money." Now, every time I pay him, he is saying, "Okay, thank you."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Now between balancing homework, friends, and football tryouts, Quentin says his love for mowing is still what drives him.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN HINES, JR.: It mostly feels like it really just opened a lot of opportunities for me, and He really just showed me that this is what He wants me to do on this earth. I love it, and I will never stop never.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, God, we fell in love with Quentin Hines, Jr.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Quentin Hines, Jr., ladies and gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yay!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fell in love with him because that was very funny. At first, Dad used to say, keep your money. Now, Dad goes by, thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see you, Mr. Hines, but David, I think what you do is so important because the news is so heavy. We all need a time to laugh and really reflect.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do you think it`s so important that these stories are so important right now? Maybe that is the answer.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Because it`s the hope we all need to see. It`s the hope I need to see, right? I`m doing what I want to see on television, and I want you guys to know that my inbox is full of stories from liberals and conservatives and people who are MAGA and from Spokane and Charlotte. It brings everybody to the table, the type of stories that we put on here every Monday morning, and it fills my cup.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So David, our viewers want to know, how do you and your team select discover these stories that you feature?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because people are going, pick me, David, pick me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve got a story. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes. Well, here`s what`s interesting. I`d say about 75 percent of them come from viewers like you, people who write into Dear David. DearDavid@CBSNews.com.\par \par \uc2 The Quentin Hines, Jr. came from somebody who said, hey, David, I heard about thought you should know, and that`s how we go and tell. We want you to know that we`re listening and we want to amplify those stories. We are looking for the everyday, exceptional hero. That`s the bread and butter of what we do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love it. Love you, David. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congrats on five-oh!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, what a great, great milestone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So if you`d like to have a little bit joy in your life - -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- we`re going to talk with aging expert Dr. Kerry Burnight for her new book "Joyspan" about how to find joy at every age. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is challenging us to all shift our perspective on aging and find joy in all stages of our lives, because it is true.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Kerry Burnight is her name. She`s a gerontologist, an expert on aging. Her new book is called -- I love this title, "Joyspan; The Art and Science of Thriving in Life`s Second Half.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Kerry Burnight, we`re happy to say, joins us in studio, live and in color, wearing yellow, which I happen to think is a very happy color. Let`s just start with that.\par \par \uc2 So, Kerry, I`ve heard about lifespan, health span, joyspan, which made a whole lot of sense to me when I read your book. Explain "Joyspan" and what it means to you.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT (Gerontologist and "Joyspan" author): Joyspan is the part we`ve been missing. So it`s the quality -- because who wants to live a long life if you`re not loving the quality of your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you can be healthy and still not loving the quality of your life.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Exactly, so "Joyspan" is wellbeing and longevity. It`s about satisfaction, and it`s the opposite of toxic positivity, because growing older is hard, but what we need is to think with the challenges that aging bring, how do we make the most of these bonus 30 or 40 years?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because there are going to be challenges.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There are challenges when we get older.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There are challenges.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: And so, you know, losing loved ones or a chronic disease or a diagnosis you didn`t expect and it is for that reason why joy matters even more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is what I like, because she says guys aging, you could look at it as a decline or continued growth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, that`s right!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I say, continued growth.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yup.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And what I love about this book, it`s applicable to so many different people from different walks of life.\par \par \uc2 I`m a former athlete. Played in the \uc2 NFL\uc2  from age 21 to about 33. Most of the time when guys leave the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , because it seems like a lifetime ago, they can`t find joy. It escapes out of their grips because they`re trying to compare it to this void that they feel over this beautiful game we played.\par \par \uc2 But for you, you say joy isn`t ecstasy, it is not finding the most like impactful and joyful thing in the world, but it`s closer to contentment.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Exactly right. So unlike happiness, which is like this for all of us, joy, even in the very hard times, is something from the inside out, and because of that, it can be cultivated in the same way that we do our physical. We can get this fortification inside to make it all the way to 105.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, Kerry, I found this book incredibly validating, because I remember having a conversation with a younger person a few years ago, and they were saying to me, oh my gosh, I can`t imagine being how old you are. And I said, you know, I can`t imagine being your age.\par \par \uc2 When I was in my 20s, life was okay. My life has gotten better every year that I`ve been on Planet Earth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel you on that, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Every day I woke up and I`m happy to be the age that I am, having lived the things that I`ve lived, and the experiences that I`ve had and that`s what this book is sort of about.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: And you are part of a revolution of stepping into our age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like a revolution.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: And saying -- yes, so, it`s not about how not to age, it is how to age with strength.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With joy, because I like the other thing you say is laughter is so important. Because, you know, little kids laugh 300 times a day. It starts to decline when we`re 23, but laughter is key, and longevity does not determine how you`re going to live your life -- genetics doesn`t --\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Right. It`s easy to say, oh, she`s lucky because of genetics.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: But in fact, there is choice, and there is the effort that it takes to go okay, every single day, I`m going to choose this again, even with the hard stuff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you have always liked older people.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As a little kid, because we are saying we thought that older people are scary and it`s not fun, but you see something differently.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Society makes older out to be the Boogeyman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: It`s a real lucrative business to be anti-aging, but pro-aging is a whole different story, and it isn`t lucrative, but it`s where joy is found.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you say continue growing, connecting and adapt.\par \par \uc2 DR. KERRY BURNIGHT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Kerry Burnight, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Kerry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 "Joyspan" goes on sale tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Looking for joy.\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250804el8400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For August 3, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Debora Patta \par \uc2 9,656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: new economic jitters, as turbulence over tariffs intensifies and President Trump fires the person who delivered Friday`s week jobs report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Trump didn`t like Friday`s employment report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I believe the numbers were phony. So you know what I did? I fired her.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what`s behind those numbers and the back-and-forth with the dozens of countries facing new tariffs later this week? We will talk with Trump`s top trade negotiator, Ambassador Jamieson Greer, Minister for Canadian U.S.-Trade \uc2 Dominic LeBlanc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And for a look at how these developments are impacting Americans, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  CEO \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2  will join us.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what exactly is ahead for Americans who are on Medicare or Medicaid? We will talk to the head of the agency that runs those programs, Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 New Mexico has the highest proportion of Medicaid recipients, and the state`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , will also be here.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report that came out Friday was unexpected and disappointing. There is more uncertainty ahead for American businesses and consumers when it comes to President Trump`s plans for imposing new tariffs on imports from some 70 countries now expected this Thursday.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, we spoke with Mr. Trump`s trade representative, Ambassador Jamieson Greer, and we asked him whether he thought there was any chance those rates would be negotiated down in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER (U.S. Trade Representative): I don`t -- I don`t think they will be in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of these -- well, I know a lot of these, are set rates, pursuant to deals. Some of these deals are announced. Some are not. Others depend on the level of the trade deficit or surplus we may have with the country.\par \par \uc2 So -- so these -- these tariff rates are pretty much set.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said, at some point, the president`s view is maybe a tariff is better than a deal.\par \par \uc2 Are you saying there are countries that just they have no shot of avoiding a tariff?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: Most countries in the world, they just have a tariff assigned to them, right, whether it`s...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`ll be the 10 percent or 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: ... 10 or 15 or the higher-level tariff, because, again, when the president is looking at this, he looks at potential deals.\par \par \uc2 And we bring him potential concessions from countries and the things they might want to do. And he compares that to the potential tariff that might be applied to try to get that deficit down. And then, talking to his advisers, he makes a call on this.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, sometimes, a country will come back and make additional concessions that -- that make it more appropriate. He`s trying to get at the deficit. He`s trying to reshore manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Trying to reshore manufacturing, bring manufacturing jobs back to America.\par \par \uc2 But we just saw in this unemployment data that, while the level is pretty low overall, it`s pretty steady and good, manufacturing, in particular, we saw it contract for the fifth straight month in July. Factory employment dropped to lowest levels in five years. What does that data indicate to you about the impact of your tariffs?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: I think a lot of companies were waiting to see if the tax bill was going to come through with the expensing for capital goods and things like that.\par \par \uc2 And so I think now you know a lot of that data comes pre-One Big Beautiful Bill. Now that we have One Big Beautiful Bill, and we have a better sense of where the taxes are going. I think we`re going to see a much -- we`re going to see more investment. All the -- all the commitments on investment we`ve seen countries making, that`s going to come through.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t -- I don`t read tariff policy into that number. I think that is kind of pre-bill policy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president just announced that the head of labor statistics is being fired because of the weak jobs report, claiming the data was faked.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: You know, even last year during the campaign, there were enormous swings in the jobs numbers. And so it sounds to me like the president has real concerns, you know, not just based on today`s, but everything we saw last year.\par \par \uc2 You want to be able to have somewhat reliable numbers. There are always revisions, but, sometimes, you see these revisions go in really extreme ways. And it`s -- you know, the president is the president. He can choose who works in the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let me ask you about Canada, which is our second largest -- largest trading partner.\par \par \uc2 The president increased tariffs to 35 percent. It applies, though, to just about 10 percent of what Canada sells here. Why bother to do this now in the middle of negotiations?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: So -- so, I would say, first of all, early on, the president imposed a 25 percent tariff on Canada, and that was really about fentanyl and border issues, right? It`s -- it`s a separate regime from the reciprocal tariff.\par \par \uc2 And what did Canada do in response? You know, they talked about helping at the border. And I`m not -- you know, I`m not a -- the drug czar or anything. But what I do know, as the trade guy, is that Canada retaliated. The only other country in the world who retaliated on tariffs was the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 And so if the president`s going to take an action, and the Canadians retaliate, the United States needs to maintain the integrity of our action, the effectiveness, so we have to go up too.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you`re talking about the things that the former Prime Minister Trudeau put in place, not the current prime minister?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: And are -- and are still in place.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are still in place.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the current prime minister has held off largely on retaliation here. That`s the guy you`re negotiating with and his team. So what`s the strategy here? And aren`t you worried that this will hurt the broader free trade deal if you truly do want to renegotiate it next year?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: I`m not concerned that it`s going to complicate things with Canada. Our view is, the president is trying to fix the terms of trade with Canada. And if there`s a way to a deal, we`ll find it. And if it`s not, we`ll have the tariff levels that we have.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So I hear you drawing distinctions when you say, I`m the trade guy. I`m not the drug czar.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m the trade guy. I`m not handling these other things.\par \par \uc2 But the president is kind of blending all these things together, because he cited fentanyl once again when it came to tariff -- to the policy with Canada. He also said on social media: "Canada`s decision to back statehood for Palestine is going to make it hard for us to make a trade deal."\par \par \uc2 How does that have anything to do with financial and trade agreements?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: The president of the United States has his foreign affairs power where he can -- he can manage relations under the Constitution with foreign countries.\par \par \uc2 Congress delegated to the president the ability to take economic action in response to national emergencies in the International Economic Emergency Powers Act. And, for example, the Treasury Department, they have a number of sanctions where they can actually cut off a country`s trade with the United States, prohibit goods, cut them off from our financial system for geopolitical reasons.\par \par \uc2 So the fact that they can do that, almost certainly the president can do something that`s not as expansive and just -- and just put a fee on those goods, which is a -- which is a tariff.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So let me ask you about Brazil, because the U.S. has a trade surplus with Brazil. That means, you know, we sell them more than we buy from them.\par \par \uc2 So it doesn`t seem to be consistent here, when you have the president put 50 percent tariffs on Brazil, one of the highest of any countries. And at the same time, the president is bringing up things that have nothing to do with trade when he`s justifying them.\par \par \uc2 He sent a letter to the current government complaining about the prosecution of his ally Bolsonaro, who is -- who allegedly staged a coup when he lost the last election. The president called it a witch-hunt. This seems politically motivated and not about trade.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: There`s a 10 percent tariff on Brazil because we have a surplus with them. That`s the reciprocal tariff. And then there`s a 40 percent tariff that the president has chosen to do under the International Emergency Economic Powers Act...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: ... like we would do any sanction where we see geopolitical issues.\par \par \uc2 The president has seen, in Brazil, like he`s seen in other countries, a misuse of law, a misuse of democracy, what one might call lawfare. It is normal to use these tools for geopolitical issues.\par \par \uc2 I mean, sanctions, we`ve been using them for years with all kinds of countries, including countries we like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You view tariffs and sanctions as the same?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: They`re just different in degree. I mean, tariffs are actually lighter than a sanction. A sanction, you`re cutting off a country from your financial system. You`re prohibiting trade with them. A tariff, you`re allowing trade. You`re just putting a fee on it. It`s a -- it`s a lesser step than sanctions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But now you have moved -- you have moved far away from dealing with the deficit.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: Well, the deficit has a 10 percent tariff.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Now you are talking about politically motivated trade actions here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the president sent a letter to President Lula saying the tariffs are due in part to Brazil`s insidious attacks on free elections. Why are you trying to influence a criminal trial of an ally of President Trump?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: The president of United States, historically, whether it`s a Democrat or Republican, they have used IEEPA to impose sanctions for all kinds of geopolitical reasons in all kinds of countries. Sometimes, it`s countrywide. Sometimes, it`s specific to certain, you know, individuals, and often foreign leaders and foreign officials.\par \par \uc2 So this is not -- this is not way outside the market.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When it comes to trade, the big deal that is pending out there is, how is the president going to deal with China? There`s an August 12 deadline. And if that deadline is not met, you have said tariff levels could snap back to above 80 percent.\par \par \uc2 Is that deadline going to slide?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: So that`s what`s under discussion right now. I would say that our conversations with the Chinese have been very positive. We have discussions at the staff level, at my level. You know, President Xi and President Trump have had conversations.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They said that it`s sliding. The Chinese said it`s sliding.\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: That`s something we`re working toward. That`s what we talked about. And so...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you`re not there yet?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: And so -- so they want to do that. We`re working on some technical issues, and we`re talking to the president about it.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think it`s going in a positive direction. You know, I`m not going to get ahead of the president. But, you know, I don`t think anyone wants to see those tariffs snap back to 84 percent.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did you get any commitments in those two days of talks in Stockholm?\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR JAMIESON GREER: So, yes, we did. Yes, we talked about -- and I won`t go into detail, because they`re, you know, confidential conversations between two -- two governments, but they really focused on rare earth magnets and minerals.\par \par \uc2 China has put a global control on the world. And so for the United States, we`re focused on making sure that the flow of magnets from China to the United States and the -- and the adjacent supply chain can flow as freely as it did before the control. And I would say we`re about halfway there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can find our full interview with Ambassador Greer on our Web site, FacetheNation.com, and our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re back with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  CEO \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and thank you for being here with us.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2  (CEO, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 ): It`s good to be here again, Margaret. Hope you`re doing well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I`m hoping you can give us some clarity here on what`s going on with the economy.\par \par \uc2 Your Bank of America economists say no rate hikes this year and no recession. Is that still the case after Friday`s jobs report?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Yes, it`s still the case. And that`s a -- less growth than they would have had six, nine months ago, and reflects the impact of the tariff war and the trade and all that.\par \par \uc2 And -- but they still think we continue to grow. And we`re growing at a slow rate, say 1.5 percent this year, a little more next year, and a little more the year after that. But it will take inflation -- for the Fed to get inflation out of the system really through the end of `26 into `27, down to the 2 percent level.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why they have the Fed holding. What they believe is, sort of in the middle of next year, the Fed will start cutting and bring the Fed funds rate closer to what would be a more normal rate, around 3 percent, 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, even though we saw this really kind of astounding dissent by two members of the Fed...\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... saying, we do need to move on interest rates, your prediction from your economists is that that`s not appropriate at this time?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : They don`t think it`s -- they`re going to move. Now, the market says they`re going to move in September, maybe twice this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : The market was at seven times one point this year. Now they`re down to two. Then they`re down to one. Now they`re up to two.\par \par \uc2 This is going to move around, but the reality is, two things people should really keep in focus. One is, until the inflation is out of the system, the Fed is going to be a little -- very careful. And that`s what they said. And then, secondly, the rate we`re going to go to is a rate that is more normal than pre-global financial crisis, more of a 3 percent, 3.5 percent rate, which actually means the American economy is probably functioning better, frankly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, on that point, "The Wall Street Journal," we were reading in, puts the tariff tax increase as costing $360 billion a year. That`s one of the largest tax increases in history, they say.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe the administration`s arguments that it`s really only foreigners who are going to pay the cost of this? Do you think economists are overstating the negative impact?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Well, I think no one really knows, honestly, because this is a different regime than we`ve been in before.\par \par \uc2 And there`s -- so they`re trying to extrapolate from things from 50 years ago, when economies were a different structure. Our team thinks it`s -- has an impact on inflation of about, you know, 30, 40 basis points.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Meaning adding to prices people are paying.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Yes, adding to the inflation rate in the United States.\par \par \uc2 But we need to back up. What the real impact right now is the new Trump administration coming in had four or five policy areas they were really going to go after, having learned in the four years they had to move very quickly. Those were around trades and tariff, immigration and taxation and deregulation.\par \par \uc2 What businesses -- and I just was in the Midwest with a bunch of businesses -- they`re all trying to do is figure out what the answer will be so they can go ahead and make their plans for `26. So the activity that`s slowed down has more to do with people just trying to figure out the answer. It doesn`t mean every answer is acceptable, but most answers are.\par \par \uc2 So what do they have answers on? Obviously, the tax bill getting done. That`s a good answer for business, because it makes the rates permanent. What`s the second thing they have an answer on? They have an answer now on the range of trade possibilities. And so, as they think about the trade possibilities, they sit there and say, tariffs might not be worse than X. They see some deals getting done, all of which is good work.\par \par \uc2 What they don`t have an answer on is deregulation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Yes, new regulations have stopped, but they are hoping for more deregulation, so that will help their business models going forward.\par \par \uc2 And then the last is immigration. What will immigration really settle in like? And that`s what they tell us. So they`re not using their lines of credit. They`re not -- the indications from them are, they`re being a little more cautious, really waiting for some answers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Businesses aren`t hiring either, we saw in this jobs data on Friday. It was the worst three months for job growth since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Your firm, when I was reading the analysis, points to a number of different factors, and one of them is artificial intelligence and the adoption of that impacting hiring. How dramatically is it reshaping the job market?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Well, I think this is sort of a question of almost a glass half-full/half-empty type of thing. So the impact...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No pun intended.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : But the impact of technology on human work content as a percentage of productivity has been huge.\par \par \uc2 In our company, at -- in 2010, when I started with the management team, we had 285,000 people. We have 212,000 people today. That was the impact of technology. We`re bigger, more customers, more transactions, more reports to the government, more data, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 So the impact has always been huge. A.I. gives you a place to go that we`ve never been able to go before. In other words, they`re jobs that take text, think about it, and produce it, many, many jobs in our company, our research team. Now you`re able to maybe use a machine to enhance that activity.\par \par \uc2 So we believe that people who harness A.I. for their benefit are going to be very successful. The -- my teammates who harness A.I. for their benefit are very successful. It`s nervous-making for young kids now, saying, will the jobs be there for me?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Then I say, look back historically. America has a lot more people working here. And think about the amount of technology that came in over the last 50 years, and we have twice as many people working in this country as we did 50 years ago, twice as many.\par \par \uc2 And the population has only gone up by about a third. So think about that dynamic as it finds its way through. That`s the glass-half-full part of it. But it will have an impact. I don`t think it`s impacting a lot right now, because many companies are just trying to learn how to use it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Technology is impacted, and A.I. gives it a place to go it hasn`t gone so far.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So we`re talking about all the unknowns and why it`s kind of hard to model things right now.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Exactly, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Friday, the president fired, as you know, the head statistician that comes up with these jobs numbers and presents them to the public.\par \par \uc2 The former head during the first Trump administration came out in her defense...\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... and said, this is -- this is without merit, and it undermines credibility of data.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned by this firing? And do you feel there is political pressure here?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Well, I think that`s more politics. And I know I`m in Washington, D.C., and that`s what we`re supposed to talk about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Government data is -- is...\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : But the reality -- the reality is, the data...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... hugely important to modeling, as you know.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : It`s 2025. It`s 2025. And the data should be able to be -- they use surveys and things like that, which, frankly, just aren`t as effective anymore.\par \par \uc2 So, if you look at the rate of people who respond to their surveys, it`s down from 60 percent level to 50 percent level. You know, we don`t use surveys as aggressively. We do -- we watch what consumers really do. We watch what businesses really do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : They can get this data, I think, other ways. And I think that`s where the focus ought to be. How do we get the data to be more resilient and more predictable and more understandable?\par \par \uc2 Because what bounces around is restatements. And that was one of the largest restatements going back five or seven years into the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Five years in the pandemic. That creates doubt around it. And so I think the key is, let`s get -- let`s spend some money. Let`s bring the information together. Let`s find where else in the government money is reported.\par \par \uc2 We report millions and millions of data points to the government every day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : The data is out there somewhere.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Finally, back in January, you were at Davos. President Trump talked about \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Many conservatives complained that the banks are not allowing them to do business within the bank, and that included a place called \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This conservative -- they don`t take conservative business. And I don`t know if the regulators mandated that because of Biden or what.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you want to respond to the allegation that conservatives are not being allowed to do business with your bank?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : We have 70 million consumers, and we`re the biggest small business lender.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s not -- the issue they`re focused on is, the regulators` impact on this industry. And you heard Senator Scott talk about this, this week, this reputation, this after-the-fact look that you banked X, and now, after the fact, you`re going to say X didn`t turn out to be what you thought.\par \par \uc2 So we look at it. We look at it based on risk. People may feel those decisions are made for some other reason, but we always make it on what`s best for our company, what`s best for the client.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are there -- are there industries you`re uncomfortable doing business with?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : No, we do business with...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Guns, oil and gas, tobacco, all of it?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : We do business with all those industries.\par \par \uc2 Individual companies, because of credit decision stuff, that`s different. But the reality is, is that, if they gave us clarity for the regulatory thing and avoid the second-guessing, that would be helpful. And I think that`s what the president was pointing out, if you listen to him.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2 , thank you for giving us some insight into the data you are seeing.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a moment. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to the Canadian minister for U.S.-Canada trade, \uc2 Dominic LeBlanc\uc2 , who joins us this morning from Moncton, Canada.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC LEBLANC (Minister for Canadian U.S.-Trade): Good morning, Ms. Brennan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were just here in Washington negotiating. And while the talks are officially continuing, you left town without a deal, and you left town with now what is a 35 percent tariff on goods.\par \par \uc2 How much of a setback was the president`s decision to do that while you are still at the table?\par \par \uc2 MINISTER DOMINIC LEBLANC: So we were obviously -- obviously disappointed by that decision.\par \par \uc2 We believe there`s a great deal of common ground between the United States and Canada in terms of building two strong economies that work well together. That`s been the history of the 40-year free trade agreement that goes back to President Reagan. We were pleased the United States is respecting the terms of the USMCA agreement.\par \par \uc2 That`s vital, we think, to the cost of living and affordability certainly in the United States. It`s true in Canada as well. So we`re continue to do the work. We left always with a better understanding of the American concerns in the trading relationship.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador Greer, Secretary Lutnick engage with us in constructive, cordial conversations. So we`re prepared to stick around and do the work needed. We think, Ms. Brennan, that the economies of both countries are strengthened when we do things together. The trading relationship between Canada and the United States is unlike other partners.\par \par \uc2 One description which I -- which I thought was very apt, we don`t sell things to each other, as much as we build things together. And that`s why it`s difficult in this relationship when so much is integrated.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MINISTER DOMINIC LEBLANC: But we remain very optimistic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But -- but you heard Ambassador Greer say, Canada -- because Canada retaliated to the initial tariffs all the way back in April, when Prime Minister Trudeau was in office, you`re paying the price now, even though you have a new government in place.\par \par \uc2 If that`s the issue, why not make that concession and pull back?\par \par \uc2 MINISTER DOMINIC LEBLANC: So, Prime Minister Carney, our new prime minister, has, we think, built a very businesslike, respectful relationship with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 We think that`s obviously very important to Canada and we think to the United States. We`re dealing with -- take, for example, the steel sector in Canada. It`s a strategic importance to national security in Canada, as it is for President Trump and the American economy.\par \par \uc2 We now have a situation where there`s a 50 percent tariff. We`re the biggest steel export market for the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MINISTER DOMINIC LEBLANC: We have a 25 percent tariff. There`s a 50 percent tariff when we want to sell something into the United States. So, effectively, we`re blocked from doing that.\par \par \uc2 But the national security interests of Canada requires that we have a viable steel and aluminum sector. And my conversations with Secretary Lutnick and others are that therein lies an example where, if we do the right work together -- we have, Ms. Brennan, the toughest rules of any country dealing with Chinese dumping into Canada.\par \par \uc2 We have melt and pour tracing so that products coming from other countries with Chinese steel can`t be dumped into the Canadian market. So we`re looking and advancing ideas where we can do that work with the United States, at the same time ensuring that our economy continues to have sectors vital to the economic future of Canada.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MINISTER DOMINIC LEBLANC: But that`s not in contradiction to President Trump`s national security objectives in the United States, of course.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I want to talk to you more about this idea of the so-called fortress North America to take on China and some of the specifics of the dispute on the other side of this commercial break.\par \par \uc2 Please stay with us. We will have more questions for Minister LeBlanc shortly. We will see you in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation, including an interview with the head of the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 , Dr. Mehmet Oz. And the Democratic governor of the state of New Mexico, \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , will join us.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION. We return to our conversation with the Canadian minister for U.S.-Canada trade, \uc2 Dominic Leblanc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Minister, we were just talking about some of the sectoral tariffs, the metals. American automakers, GM, Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , they have all said that these tariffs are hurting their profits. The 50 percent metal tariffs, which use Canadian aluminum, the secretary of the Treasury was talking about those just the other day, they`re see the impact here in the United States. A bit of a backfire in some ways. Do you see room for maneuver on these? Are they willing to negotiate with you on those tariffs?\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC LEBLANC (Minister for Canada-U.S. Trade): Ms. Brennan, we -- we hope so. And as I say, we`re encouraged by the conversations with Secretary Lutnick and Ambassador Greer, but we`re not yet where we need to go to get the deal that`s in the best interest of the two economies.\par \par \uc2 But your example is a good one. Canadian aluminum companies massively supply the American market. And by putting a 50 percent tariff on aluminum from Canada, you`ve increased the price of a whole series of goods. The automobile sector again is an example where there`s been deep integration.\par \par \uc2 We`re the biggest customer of U.S.-made automobiles. Heavily, heavily importing into Canada, light and heavy-duty trucks. Fifty percent of the cars that we have finished in Canada and sell to the United States are made up of American parts. So, therein lies a perfect example where instead of tariffing one another, or President Trump, for his national security reasons under his Section 232 tariffs, wants to have a strong domestic steel, aluminum, automobile sector. Well, so does Canada. And we -- we understand and respect totally the president`s view in terms of the national security interests. In fact, we share it. And what we`ve said to our American counterparts is, how can we structure the right agreement where we can both continue to supply one another --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC LEBLANC: In a reliable, cost effective way that preserves jobs essential to the American economy, but the same thing is true obviously in Canada as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, are there any plans for the two leaders to speak? I saw President Trump said your prime minister called him Thursday and they just never connected. I mean are tensions that high? And given the changing justification for the tariffs, do you really feel like you`re negotiating with the other side in good faith?\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC LEBLANC: Sure, we do. Of course we do. As I say, the conversations have been informative, constructive, and cordial. I would expect the prime minister will have a conversation with the president over the next number of days. That`s certainly my plan again with Secretary Lutnick, recognizing that we think there is an option of striking a deal that will bring down some of these tariffs, provide greater certainty to investment.\par \par \uc2 We, Ms. Brennan, we passed in Canada our version of the president`s one big, beautiful bill, it`s called the One Canadian Economy Act, which we think will unlock up to $500 billion of investment in Canada for things like pipelines, port infrastructure, mines.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC LEBLANC: All of which offer huge opportunities to American businesses as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well --\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC LEBLANC: So, we think there`s a great deal -- a great deal to work on together.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Minister, we`ll see if you can get one.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Change is coming for the country`s Medicaid system as part of the enactment of the big, beautiful bill.\par \par \uc2 To help us understand what`s ahead, we turn now to the administrator of the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 , Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Welcome to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (Administrator, \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 ): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve got a lot of work ahead. I want to start on drug costs. The president put this 25 percent tariff on India. A big drug producer. The president`s trade deal with the E.U. puts a 15 percent tariff on imported medicines from Europe. How do you stop the drugmakers from passing along those costs to people on Medicare and Medicaid?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Well, the president`s letter on Thursday, for most favored nation pricing, is a good example of that. And he`s been working on this tirelessly since the first administration.\par \par \uc2 And just to put this in context for many of the viewers, about two-thirds of bankruptcies in America are caused by healthcare expenses. About a third of people when they go to the pharmacy, they leave empty handed. They can`t afford the medication. So, the president has said, enough global freeloading.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been covering much of the development costs for new drugs to cure cancer, do other things with lots of other illnesses that are life- threatening. Isn`t (ph) it time for the American public to understand that we should not be paying three times more for the exact same medication in the same box, made in the same factory.\par \par \uc2 So, the president is saying, equalize it out. Let`s use a model that worked, for example, for external threats, that`s what \uc2 NATO\uc2  did. Everyone has to pay a little more. We`ll pay extra too. But we won`t pay a lot more than everybody else so they actually have to raise their -- their -- if you cut contributions, in this case to an internal threat, which is illness, we`ll pay a little less in America that way more Americans can afford these medications and it`s a fair system for the entire globe.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, this was declared in these letters that were sent out to 17 pharmaceutical companies this past week and it calls for extending that to Medicaid drug prices. Is that intended to offset what will be, you know, cuts to Medicaid? And do you know, you know, if the companies are actually going to follow through on this? Like, how do you actually strong arm them into doing it?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Well, just get the numbers correct. We`re putting 200 billion more dollars into Medicaid. So, we`re actually investing in Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: At a time when costs are going up. So --\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Costs are going up, but there`s been a 50 percent increase in the cost of Medicaid over the last five years. So, I`m trying to save this beautiful program, this noble effort to help folks, giving them a hand up. And as you probably gather, if Medicaid isn`t able to take care of the people for whom it was designed, the young children, at the dawn of their life, those at the twilight of their life, the seniors, and those who are disabled, living in the shadows as Hubert Humphrey said, then we`re not satisfying the fundamental obligation of a moral government.\par \par \uc2 And this president has said over and over that he believes that it is the wise thing and the -- and the noble thing to help those who are vulnerable. And every great society does that. And we`re going to as well.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to invest in Medicaid, as is required, but we want an appropriate return on that investment.\par \par \uc2 One thing that Medicaid patients should not face are drug prices they can`t afford.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Because then --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you enforce this with the pharmaceutical companies?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Well, the -- the pharmaceutical companies, if you sit them down quietly, Margaret, and we`ve done that, and say, you went into this business at some point because you cared about people. I know there`s many out there shaking their heads, but that is actually the truth. People go into healthcare, whether they`re pharmaceutical companies or insurance companies or the PBMs or anybody in the space, evidence (ph), the CMS, the most impressive thing to me in my new task that the president`s appointed me to is the remarkable quality of the people within the organization. Just unbelievably talented. They went into this job because they care about healthcare and about people.\par \par \uc2 Somewhere along the lines people forget, they put numbers ahead of patients. And when that happens, then you start running into problems. So, we went to the pharmaceutical companies and we said, you appreciate this is not a fair system. We should not be paying more in America, three times more, for your products than you charge in Europe.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: They get the joke. They understand the reality of this problem. They`re engaging with us. We`re in the middle of those negotiations.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: The president has a unique power to convene. We`ve done it with dealing with prior authorization, this heinous process where patients feel like they`re, you know, trying to get care from a doctor, everything`s being done except all of a sudden the -- the arm of insurance comes in and stops the whole process for unknown reasons, for week, sometimes months.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Your -- the insurance companies representing 80 percent of the American public got together and they said, because we pushed them, we`re going to deal with this. We can do the same, I believe with the pharmaceutical industry, with most favored nation pricing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let me ask you about the changes that are coming because of this new law to Medicaid, which is jointly administered between the feds and the states. There are major reductions to federal healthcare spending here. One of the changes are these work requirements. It`s about 20 hours a week, volunteer or work, to qualify for healthcare.\par \par \uc2 What is the guidance you are giving to states on how to implement this? Because in this economy, things are more complicated. \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver, independent contractor, how do they show they work their 20 hours a week?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Last weekend I was at the National Governor`s Association, with Secretary Kennedy, who has been a big advocate of work as well. And every Democratic president, and Republican president, has said that the foundation of a healthy welfare system, of a social system of support, is work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but I`m -- but I`m asking how you actually implement that and register it so that people who are working do qualify and they don`t get caught up in paperwork because they didn`t file something on time, that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Right. As long as we`re OK that people should work and would want to work, and it`s not just work, it`s community engagement. They can go get education, right?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: They can take care of family members.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: They can contribute in other ways. But work`s a great way of doing it. It can get you out of poverty if you can find jobs and elevate yourself.\par \par \uc2 There have been efforts to do this in the past, but they haven`t been able to achieve what we can achieve because we have technologies now and we have invested already, as soon as the bill was signed, began pilots to try to demonstrate that we can actually do this correctly. We have pilots now in Louisiana and in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: In both cases, within seven minutes, you can click on where you`re working -- you mentioned \uc2 Uber\uc2 . You`re an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver. You click that button on your phone. It just takes you to your payroll provider. Let`s say it`s ADP. We then ask your permission, can we connect with this payroll provider to -- to demonstrate what you`ve actually been able to work and earn over the past month.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: This also, by the way, confirms your eligibility.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a bigger benefit here. Once you do that, you`re in, you`re done. However, what if we take it one step further, Margaret? What if we go beyond just proving that you tried to work, to actually saying, you know what, you didn`t work enough but we can actually help you by connecting you through employment office.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you`re still figuring out the technology, but isn`t there an end of December deadline for a lot of these things to be figured out, and how do you make sure that people don`t get kicked off because, in the state of Georgia, which already had work requirements, they have really struggled to make this work.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Well, a couple of things. It`s not the end of December. It`s the end of December a year from now. And Georgia is apples and oranges. Georgia had a program only for people under the poverty level and, for those people, if they wanted, they could elect to come into a system to help them get jobs. There have been 50,000 reduction in head count of uninsured people in the overall program in the last five years. Overall, Georgia, 2 million less uninsured people. So, Georgia is using a lot of tactics, and they`re going in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 I would argue that if you have confidence in the American people and their desire to take the offer to try to get a job if we challenge you to that, and, remember, if you`re an able-bodied person on Medicaid --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: You`re spending 6.1 hours watching television or leisure time. So, you don`t want that --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the Kafa (ph) Health Policy found 92 percent of adult Medicaid recipients already are working or they have the carve out because they have to have caregivers or they have to do other things.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: They`re fine. All they have to do is -- there will be a simple app. If you`re already carved out, that`s super simple. If you`re supposed to be -- if you`re able-bodied and you`re supposed to be working, we want to help connect you to the job market and get you into work. We have twice as many jobs available in America as people seem to want them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: The foundation of work is not just about fulfilling eligibility. The goal of healthcare insurance is to catalyze action in the right direction, to get you healthier, to give you agency over your future so you recognize you matter and you should have a job there for -- to go out and change the world.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a drug addiction problem in the country. How are those changes going to impact people who are on Medicaid in states like Kentucky, in states like West Virginia?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: In many instances there are carveouts for folks who need -- who have substance use disorder problems. There are programs to support --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do they prove that?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Well, they can either --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that in the app?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Yes, it will be in app. And the app, again, if we`re being -- but this is being developed by the \uc2 United States Digital Service\uc2  led by Amy Gleason, who is a wonderful technologist. She and I were with the president and Secretary Kennedy and the head of the -- the czar for A.I. in this country on Wednesday talking about overall how we`re going to change the use of health technology in America. We`ve got to get into 2025 with health technology, as is true in every other sector.\par \par \uc2 If you`re watching the show right now, you could also have been streaming media. You could take an \uc2 Uber\uc2  somewhere, ride share. You can do an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 . Technology should make the system more efficient.\par \par \uc2 We should have confidence that it will also allow us to do what we all agree is possible. If the whole challenge to a work requirement is that you don`t have confidence in our ability to accomplish it, that`s a separate question, because I do have confidence in the American people and we have confidence we can pull this off.\par \par \uc2 Look at the passport system, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Right now you can go and get a passport in two weeks without having to go to the post office, send pictures in, all that`s gone. It`s fixable. Let`s use technology.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m still confused on how someone who is in the throes of substance abuse is going to use an app to say I`m in the throes of substance abuse every week to file online, but --\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Well, when they go -- when they go in to get their help for their substance abuse treatment, assuming they`re going for help on that, they can also get enrolled in -- and those requirements can be fulfilled.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: We want to talk to them in as many ways as possible. It`s not going to happen just because we put an app out there. You -- you have social workers, other folk -- elements who care a lot about this population who are coming together.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: But they have to have some mechanism to report back. That just has not been done well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and this is incredibly detailed, and that`s why we wanted to have you on. I have so many more questions for you on rural hospitals and -- and some of the other criticisms. I have to leave it there for now but thank you, Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Can I give you 30 seconds on rural hospitals? Because this is important.\par \par \uc2 You have 7 percent of Medicaid money going to rural hospitals. We`re putting $50 billion. The president wants this. The congress wants this. It will make a massive dent (ph).\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are a lot of questions about how you`re going to dole that out and whether you have already made promises. Do you have any specifics for us?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Yes, we -- we -- it`s going to be -- they`ll get the applications in early September.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: The -- the -- the money is designed to help you with workforce development, right sizing the system and using technology to provide things like telehealth that can change the world.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: Imagine if we can change the way we think about the delivery of health and make it more about getting people healthy so you can thrive and flourish and be fully present in their own lives and as Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Dr. Oz, we`ll leave it there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn to New Mexico`s Democratic Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 . She joins us from Santa Fe.\par \par \uc2 Governor, two out of five New Mexicans are on Medicaid. You`ve got a lot of rural hospitals. Have you figured out how to implement everything Dr. Oz just laid out?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D-NM): Absolutely not. There is no real way to implement this. It`s more paperwork for everyone. It`s more paperwork for federal government, for state governments, for county governments, for local hospitals, for independent providers. And you know what Americans really hate, Margaret? When you go to your primary care physician, and you spend 20 minutes sitting at a chair, not even on the exam table, while they are inputting data into a computer. So, this doesn`t make any sense.\par \par \uc2 We should be a society and a country that is connecting people to healthcare providers. I think the one thing that Dr. Oz represents, that`s a fair representation, is, we should be healthier as Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : All right. And we need to be moving out of poverty. We need drug prices, we should talk about that, to come down. So, go after insurance companies. Do manufacturing here. Make sure we can negotiate fair prices. Let states do that. Because I guarantee you, we`ll do a better job than the federal government.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, get people early easy access. Today, more than half, or about half, our small businesses don`t even offer healthcare coverage. So, you can get a job, but now what?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Well, you said, though, in your state, because, as a governor, you`re going to have to figure this out.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have reserve cash from some oil and gas revenues, as I understand it, that have been put aside. Doesn`t that show it is possible for the federal government to shift more responsibility back to the states? That`s the argument conservatives are making.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : They are. And it`s temporary. There is no way any state, including this one, which, frankly, I am really proud of, we are in really good financial shape. That takes planning and effort.\par \par \uc2 You know, our job projections continue to be met and exceed, unlike the federal jobs report, which is going in the opposite direction. So, I don`t know where all these jobs are going to be in this anemic economy. I mean, it`s so bad, with -- the last time it was this bad, I was in college. And let me tell you, that was a very long time ago.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, temporarily, we can do that. But you can`t do it over the long haul. The loss minimally to New Mexico over less than a decade is between $12 billion and $13 billion. And when, not if, rural hospitals and local providers close their doors, I can do this better than any other state. The last governor completely canceled behavioral health.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Six years later, we are still reeling from trying to rebuild. We put a billion dollars into behavioral health just this last legislative session. It is not so easy to rebuild something out of nothing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the $50 billion rural healthcare fund under this Republican law is supposed to give people the -- your -- states like yours the ability to come and say, we need this extra cash. Are you going to have to ask for that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : I`m going to ask for every dollar the federal government has put aside anywhere that benefits a New Mexican. So, you`ve got $50 billion, that`s $1 billion for each state if it was even. Do you know how much money it would take to shore up rural hospitals? More than a billion. And to put that in perspective, let me do this. It`s a billion just for behavioral health. It`s a billion plus just to keep people`s coverage. It`s another billion for prescription drugs. It`s a billion dollars for rural provider delivery investments. And that`s only 50 hospitals. You have hundreds of hospitals, hundreds, 400 rural hospitals across America that will shutter.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : So, that`s the number, at (ph) it is. We are -- how do we pick these rural hospitals? And if you pick a southeastern rural hospital in New Mexico, what about the rural hospital in western New Mexico?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Economies fail. People have to move away. You don`t have any OBGYN care.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : That whole area collapses. And they are reducing rural healthcare delivery by about $134 billion. So, the $50 billion is just to make someone, somewhere feel like they recognize that this is a disaster.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : $900 billion out of Medicaid is catastrophic. Straight up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, we ran through a lot of material here. I have more questions for you. But very quickly, can you tell me, you deployed the National Guard to counter unrest in New Mexico. How is that different from what the president did in California?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Well, they`re not policing. They`re doing the back-end work so that trained community policing and members of that training, right, those local police officers, they`re on the streets. What we have in this country is a shortage of police officers. What I have in New Mexico is a partnership. So, they`re doing all of the -- they answer all of the emergency calls. They handle all the traffic clearance when we`ve got a crash. And it is working. We`re beginning to see more productive fentanyl, drug-dealing high-end arrests than we did without The Guard. And I`m really proud of that work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : This is about partnering and leveraging, not about indiscriminately going after individuals who have not committed serious crimes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Governor, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : You`re welcome, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: FACE THE NATION will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Our Debora Patta went with the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  into Gaza last week. A rare visit for journalists. She filed this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): Hunger has forced an impossible choice in Gaza, risk gunfire or starvation.\par \par \uc2 "They shoot constantly," said this teenager. "This boy was standing next to us, and he was killed."\par \par \uc2 Hundreds have been killed near the U.S.-funded Gaza Humanitarian Foundation. Aid hubs controlled by the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 , which denies it fires on desperate crowds. But under global pressure, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  aloud more \uc2 U.N.\uc2  supplies in this past week, and took us to an aid collection point just inside Gaza to prove they are not deliberately starving Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: The \uc2 IDF\uc2  has brought us here to demonstrate that, as far as they`re concerned, their responsibility for delivering aid stops right here.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): But look at what happens in this \uc2 U.N.\uc2  footage as it tries to deliver that food.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: It` kids. It`s children.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): Shots landing right in front of desperately hungry Palestinians. In this \uc2 World Food Programme\uc2  video, one of their trucks is looted by people so crazed with hunger, they say, they`ll do anything to get something to eat.\par \par \uc2 Inside Gaza, there are still 20 living hostages who have been held for 667 days, including Rom Braslavski and Evyatar David. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  released new propaganda footage of David looking so skeletal, his brother Ilay fears he is on the brink of death.\par \par \uc2 ILAY DAVID, BROTHER OF HOSTAGE EVYATAR DAVID: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is using Evyatar in one of the most horrific and calculated campaigns of cruelty imaginable.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): According to our recent poll, three out of four Israelis want the hostages freed in exchange for an end to this war.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s our Debora Patta reporting from Israel.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching. I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020250803el8300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for August 1, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Ed O`Keefe, Kelly O`Grady, Steve Hartman,  Charlie D`Agata, Paul Heggen, Lonnie Quinn, John Dickerson \par \uc2 4,049 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 August 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A gloomy jobs report. After she says fewer Americans were hired, the president orders her fired.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I believe the numbers were phony, just like they were before the election. And there were other times. So you know what I did? I fired her.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . Maurice DuBois is off tonight.\par \par \uc2 The president`s trade policies are taking a heavy toll on the labor market. The uncertainty caused by his on-again/off-again/up-again/down-again tariffs has employers cutting back dramatically on their hiring. They created just 73,000 jobs in July. Even worse, the numbers for May and June were revised downward today sharply.\par \par \uc2 So, over three months, as the tariff wars escalated, the U.S. economy created only 106,000 jobs. The president himself created one job today at the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . He ordered the firing of the head of the agency behind the bad jobs report, which also includes the unemployment rate ticking up to 4.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 He accused Erika McEntarfer, without evidence, of cooking the books to help \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  before the election and to hurt him now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Before the numbers that came out, I said, who is the person that does these numbers? And then they gave me stats about before the election. I had a similar problem. I mean, she gave out numbers that were so good for the Democrats. It was, like, unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ed O`Keefe is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 But we begin right here with CBS News Money Watch correspondent Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, you talked to one of the consumers of these numbers today.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I did.\par \par \uc2 So, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  looks at the jobs report when making decisions about interest rates. Today, we interviewed \uc2 Beth Hammack\uc2 , president and CEO of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland\uc2 . She`s one of the people in the room for those discussions.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY (voiceover): Many business owners over the last six months have paused hiring because of tariff uncertainty. That contributed to the weaker-than-expected jobs report.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the employment picture, May, June and July, we added less than 100,000 jobs each month. Is that a trend?\par \par \uc2 BETH HAMMACK (President and CEO, \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland\uc2 ): It may be. It`s an emerging trend. It`s something we should be watching.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: \uc2 Beth Hammack\uc2  says, the economy is healthy on paper, just not for everyone.\par \par \uc2 BETH HAMMACK: We definitely hear about a two-speed economy. The top part of the income spectrum is doing incredibly well. At the bottom end of the spectrum, I hear individuals trading down. They go to the supermarket and they can`t buy ground beef. They`re buying hot dogs.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Are you worried about that growing inequality?\par \par \uc2 BETH HAMMACK: What I`m focused on is making sure that we have an economy that`s working for all Americans. Inflation has been above our target for 4.5 years. And with the tariffs that are coming in, it may be likely that those prices continue to rise.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The Fed decided on Wednesday to leave interest rates unchanged because of persistent inflation. That was before this morning`s challenging jobs report. The economy added 73,000 jobs last month, which was fewer than expected. May and June were revised downward by over 250,000.\par \par \uc2 BETH HAMMACK: I feel confident in the decision that we made at this most recent meeting.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: What happens if you wait too long to cut rates?\par \par \uc2 BETH HAMMACK: If we wait too long to cut rates, that could mean that more people are losing their jobs than we`d like to see.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: While the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is meant to be apolitical, President Trump has repeatedly pressured the chairman to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has traditionally and is supposed to be independent from political pressures from the White House. How important is that independence?\par \par \uc2 BETH HAMMACK: Having that monetary independence really allows us, allows me to help set monetary policy in a way that we can support job growth and support stable prices.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Kelly, the Fed is such a mystery. It`s great to hear from someone inside the Fed.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s talk about another government institution, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Normally, we never talk about them when a jobs report comes out. The president wants to talk about the person totalling up the numbers. Why?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Yes, so he does not trust these numbers. And I will note, context-wise, historically, markets, business leaders, financial institutions, they have looked at this institution that has been doing this for decades and trusted these.\par \par \uc2 Now, he`s taking issues with the revisions we mentioned. Look, revisions happen. The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , they get more information over time, and they go back and they say, hey, well, actually, that was higher, this was lower. This was larger than normal of a revision.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Economists look at these jobs numbers and say, well, they`re lower because companies are uncertain about tariffs. Remind us where we are in the tariffs story.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So we have got trade frameworks or deals with a number of our biggest trading partners, including the E.U., but the biggest outstanding ones, China, Canada, and Mexico, we`re all waiting for those three.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : When these kick in on the 7th of August, who`s going to pay?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Three possibilities. The companies and the countries that are sending goods to the U.S., they might lower their prices. The American companies that are buying those goods, they might absorb some of that cost, and they have been doing that, but they can`t do that for much longer probably, which means you and me might pay more.\par \par \uc2 And the Yale Budget Lab said that that could go to the tune of $2,400 per household this year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Now to Ed O`Keefe. He went to the southern side of the U.S.-Canada border to see the impact of the tariffs there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): Resurgence Brewing Company in Buffalo, New York, sits just four miles from the U.S.-Canada border. And owner Jeff Ware has long relied on Canadians crossing over to quench their thirst.\par \par \uc2 But an anti-American boycott north of the border sparked by the ongoing trade war means business is drying up.\par \par \uc2 JEFF WARE (Owner, Resurgence Brewing Company): Probably 15 percent to 25 percent down year over year, based on the lack of Canadian traffic.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Which is a huge hit, especially this time of year.\par \par \uc2 JEFF WARE: It`s huge.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tapping into Buffalo`s history as a beer-brewing mecca during the industrial age, Ware wants to lead a local resurgence.\par \par \uc2 How does the trade war affect that resurgence?\par \par \uc2 JEFF WARE: Well, it doesn`t make it easier. This is just another weight on our shoulders that we have to bear.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: About an hour away...\par \par \uc2 JACK KLAPPER (Farmer): So this one was born about 30 minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: ... Jack Klapper is a seventh-generation farmer with more than 5,000 cows pumping out 13,000 gallons of milk a day.\par \par \uc2 JACK KLAPPER: Definitely have seen some rising costs.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: As he tries to expand, Klapper is dealing with rising costs of Canadian lumber and steel, slapped with higher tariffs. But, beyond Canada, Klapper sees shoots of optimism, thanks to President Trump`s new trade agreement with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JACK KLAPPER: I feel like, as a dairy farmer, we kind of won. E.U. products are now being slapped with a 15 percent tariff fee coming into the United States.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Now he wants Trump to do more.\par \par \uc2 JACK KLAPPER: We can`t ship certain cheeses into Europe because of the name of the cheese. So, if we`re making Parmesan cheese or feta cheese, we`re not allowed to ship that into Europe. But it seems like, when there`s butter that wants to be imported into the United States from Europe, it`s a wide-open door for them.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On the farm and in the taproom, both business owners just want Trump`s tariff war to end.\par \par \uc2 JEFF WARE: Let`s come up with a good plan that might take a while to implement, but everybody knows the plan.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ed O`Keefe joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, I was thinking about this, trade negotiations with the Canadians. And, this week, it`s not just about dollars and cents or economic issues. The president said he`s unhappy with the prime minister`s decision to recognize a Palestinian state and that that might be a part of trade negotiations.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, what started off as a trade dispute mostly about automobiles and agriculture is now hung up on disagreements over Middle East policy.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not just Canada. The president`s also prolonging talks with Brazil. He`s already slapped a 50 percent tariff on that country, in part because he`s concerned with how the current government is prosecuting a former Brazilian president who`s closely aligned with him.\par \par \uc2 And, today, the president was tight-lipped about how trade talks are going with South Korea and India, all of it a reminder that, while this was designed to juice the economy in the eyes of the president, he`s using this tariff or now to try to extract concessions to meet his personal and policy concerns -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ed O`Keefe at White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 The police in Montana are looking for a 45-year-old man suspected of opening fire at a bar today, killing four people. Public records show he lives next door to the bar. There`s no word on what led to the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein`s former girlfriend Ghislaine Maxwell has been moved from a federal prison in Florida to a prison camp in Texas one week after meeting with Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche. Maxwell was convicted in 2021 for helping Epstein abuse underage girls.\par \par \uc2 And nearly eight million student loan borrowers who enrolled in a Biden era repayment plan are now seeing their balances go up as interest accrues again. According to one analysis, the average borrower now has to pay about $300 a month in interest.\par \par \uc2 Now to the Middle East. U.S. envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  and Ambassador \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  visited a food distribution site in Gaza today. Nearly two years of war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has led to widespread hunger there.\par \par \uc2 Margaret Brennan joins us from Washington now.\par \par \uc2 And, Margaret, what was the goal of this visit?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, John, President Trump said twice this week, in fact, that he is moved by the pictures of those starving children in Gaza. He says they`re not fake, which is a gentle rebuke to Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu, who rejects the U.N.`s assertion that there is manmade famine in most of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 So those images released today that you`re seeing now given to us by the Trump administration are meant to convey the president`s sense of urgency to fix it. He sent his personal envoy, \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2 , to see for himself a food distribution center that`s run by that Gaza Humanitarian Foundation.\par \par \uc2 We have told you it`s controversial, backed by Israel and the U.S., and it`s been accused of both brutality and inefficiency. But, still, American taxpayers are going to send $30 million to it in the next few days. That`s notable, considering that otherwise the Trump administration is slashing foreign aid across the board.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. official told me the president does realize there`s great need beyond food, including a shortage of medical aid, water, sanitation, and just very basic services.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Margaret, that`s on the humanitarian, starvation front.\par \par \uc2 There is also the larger question of, what is the U.S. doing, if anything, can it do anything with respect to the larger conflict, a question of a cease-fire and an exchange of hostages? Where does that stand?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the president still wants one. He wanted to be the person who brokered an end to the war, but the cease-fire talks are stalled. The Trump administration says that`s on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At home, the president`s also responding to some outcry from some stalwart MAGA supporters, people like Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , who called it a genocide this week. She`s questioning why the U.S. even supports Israel`s war.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Face the Nation moderator Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the Friday \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, Steve Hartman with a young man whose work ethic is paying off.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER (\uc2 Burger King\uc2  Employee): I knew, like, all the good I put out here is going to come back to me somehow.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It`s amazing how it worked out.\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER: It`s -- yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I`m Charlie D`Agata right along the border with Mexico, where thousands of U.S. troops have been deployed. We will have that story coming up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It was almost gone with the wind.\par \par \uc2 A microburst pulled an empty Delta jetliner away from the gate in Denver. It happened last month. We just got the video. The wind was so strong, blocks around the wheels couldn`t hold the aircraft in place.\par \par \uc2 For more about dangerous weather, we go to Paul Heggen, chief meteorologist at CBS News Bay Area -- Paul.\par \par \uc2 PAUL HEGGEN: John, the big story for the past couple of weeks has been the extreme heat gripping much of the country, but now that heat beginning to subside, the last of the heat advisories for the Deep South going to expire over the next couple of hours.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a problem, a catch, because the cooler air mass that has moved in contains abundant wildfire smoke drifting in from Canada. That shows up in this faint shade of gray on the satellite perspective. And the air quality readings reflect that. In the orange and red dots, that`s where we have unhealthy to very unhealthy air quality readings for much of the Upper Midwest.\par \par \uc2 And the air quality is going to continue to be a problem as we head into the weekend. Air quality alerts remain in effect because the smoke is just going to be drifting around. And we`re also producing smoke from fires burning in the Western U.S., the largest of which has become a megafire. This is called the Dragon Bravo Fire burning in Northern Arizona to the north of the Grand Canyon, only 9 percent contained, over 110,000 acres.\par \par \uc2 And, John, fire crews have their work cut out from them this weekend with temperatures approaching 100 degrees and winds gusting over 20 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Oof, heat on top of fire.\par \par \uc2 Paul Heggen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The president`s crackdown on illegal immigration has border crossings from Mexico down to historic lows. The Border Patrol says about 4,400 migrants were apprehended at the border in July, the lowest number for any month on record. But that`s coming at a price. The Pentagon told CBS News the estimated cost of border security operations since February is $1.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata got a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): We took off for the border in Arizona with Colonel Hugh Jones.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The border is right below you. There`s the wall.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Jones commands a brigade of soldiers guarding that wall stretching across rugged terrain.\par \par \uc2 COL. HUGH JONES (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): The brigade is spread across 1,044 miles of the southern border.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: So that sounds stretched out. How do you figure that out?\par \par \uc2 COL. HUGH JONES: Because we can`t brute-force the border or just occupy it by force, we try to drive that in the smartest way we can, using a lot of the capabilities we would use in warfare.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Chinook helicopters lifted in heavy equipment, while thousands of active-duty troops deployed to reinforce the border.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. SCOTT NAUMANN (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): We span four U.S. states.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Major General Scott Naumann commands American forces on the border and the Army`s quick-reaction 10th Mountain Division.\par \par \uc2 What is this assignment doing to overall readiness of the 10th Mountain Division?\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. SCOTT NAUMANN: Basically, I synchronize all different capabilities and resources to overpower an enemy. What I`m doing here is really no different. I`m not shooting. We`re not firing lethal weapons, but we`re solving a problem.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: These combat units are sealing the border with barbed wire, strategic patrols and surveillance in the air and on the ground, where Sergeant Ana Harker and Specialist Austin Waters keep close and constant watch.\par \par \uc2 Americans might be looking at this and say, what do you guys do all day?\par \par \uc2 SGT. ANA HARKER (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): We look out to the border, and whenever we see somebody cross by, we say, contact Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And how long have you been doing this?\par \par \uc2 SGT. ANA HARKER: Oh, like three months.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And, in three months, you have seen how many people?\par \par \uc2 SPC. AUSTIN WATERS (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): I think just four.\par \par \uc2 SGT. ANA HARKER: Four? The -- four?\par \par \uc2 SPC. AUSTIN WATERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: You must be begging to see somebody out there.\par \par \uc2 SPC. AUSTIN WATERS: Something. A lot of animals.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: No one`s sure how long those soldiers will be deployed there, John. That is a decision that rests with the president alone.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Charlie, I got another presidential question to ask you on a very different topic. President Trump threatened to send what he called nuclear submarines off the coast of Russia. Can you help us understand what`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Yes, so, John, that was in response to threats by former Russian President Dmitry Medvedev. And it`s important to note here that President Trump very rarely pulls the nuclear card. The \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  has a longstanding policy of not divulging the whereabouts of its fast-attack or nuclear armed submarines.\par \par \uc2 Location of those subs is a tightly kept secret. So President Trump`s post about moving them around is highly unusual and a strong message to Russia.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Charlie, for fielding both of those.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Steve Hartman has an update on an On the Road story, a success story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER (\uc2 Burger King\uc2  Employee): You want a Whopper With Cheese?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): At this \uc2 Burger King\uc2  in Dacula, Georgia, 18- year-old Mykale Baker is known for his whopper of a work ethic, shows up early, stays late...\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER: There you go.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... and looks at every shift like it`s a privilege.\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER: I like helping people and not expecting anything back for me. That`s just me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So you would be working at \uc2 Burger King\uc2  whether you got paid or not?\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER: Yes. I knew, like, all the good I put out here is going to come back to me somehow.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It`s amazing how it worked out.\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER: It`s -- yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This is the story of how that karma came to be.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mykale McKenna Quayeric (sp?) Baker.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: As we first reported a couple months ago, it was graduation night. Mykale left the ceremony and dropped by \uc2 Burger King\uc2  for a late meal. And that`s when he noticed his colleagues were absolutely swamped with orders.\par \par \uc2 At this point, most kids would have just been glad they had the night off and been on their way. But, instead, Mykale did what Mykale does.\par \par \uc2 MYKALE BAKER: I wasn`t even clocked in. I just put gloves on and said, "What you all need help with?" basically.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So, with his sash and medals still draped around his neck, Mykale hopped on the line, the whole thing caught on camera by a customer, who couldn`t believe her eyes.\par \par \uc2 MARIA MENDOZA (Customer): There`s not a lot of people who are like that.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This is Maria Mendoza.\par \par \uc2 MARIA MENDOZA: Thirteen years of school. You graduate. The last thing I would think is somebody would go to work.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Maria was so impressed, she posted the video online and started a crowdfunding campaign to reward the kid for his integrity and dedication.\par \par \uc2 Today, two months after our story first aired, the fund is at more than $230,000. Mykale has enrolled at Gwinnett Technical College to pursue his dream of becoming a master auto mechanic and owning his own shop, all proof that his mother, Demeshiah, was right...\par \par \uc2 DEMESHIAH SCOTT (Mother of Mykale Baker): I`m so proud of you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... when she taught him to always work hard even when you think no one is watching, because you never know when someone is.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman On the Road in Dacula, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Up next: the day the music was born.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Ladies and gentlemen, rock `n` roll.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Forty-four years ago today, Music Television, better known as \uc2 MTV\uc2 , was launched.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The first image just after midnight, the liftoff of \uc2 Apollo\uc2  11. If they could put men on the moon, why on Earth couldn`t we have a 24- hour music channel?\par \par \uc2 In its early days, \uc2 MTV\uc2  played music videos around the clock, beginning that first morning with The Buggles.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The heyday of the deejay was over, and in came the veejay. \uc2 MTV\uc2  introduced the nation to artists like Cyndi Lauper and turned stars like Michael Jackson and Madonna into superstars.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Over the years, the format changed...\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : ... music giving way to reality programs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m not going to lie.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mike, get over it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m not going to lie.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Nothing happened.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m not going to lie.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But this is how \uc2 MTV\uc2  was born on a summer day in 1981.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, happy birthday to our sister network.\par \par \uc2 That`s the CBS EVENING NEWS. I will be back with EVENING NEWS Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7. And Maurice DuBois will be back here on Monday.\par \par \uc2 I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . See you soon on Plus.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250802el8100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS MORNINGS for July 15, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Rob Marciano, Jason  Allen, Ian Lee, Ed O`Keefe, Celine Gounder, Mark Strassmann, Adriana Diaz,  Nancy Chen \par \uc2 14,446 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets to start your Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, actually, he is stuck at the airport in Phoenix, but we`ve got a lot of news including serious flooding in the Northeast. We`ll show you the incredible pictures, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Severe flash flooding causes havoc across the Northeast where some areas got nearly 10 inches of rain in a few hours.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It went from maybe just a little stream of water, and then it just started flooding over.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Like a river?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes. It was like that.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: How scary was that?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s pretty scary.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Trump says he`ll use \uc2 NATO\uc2  to get weapons to Ukraine and threatens new sanctions on Russia.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States of America): We are very unhappy, I am, with Russia.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Unreleased music from Beyonce is reported stolen from an employee in Atlanta. Police have a warrant for a suspect.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON: And they ended up with some priceless items.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The Supreme Court ruled that the Trump administration can dismantle the Department of Education and carry out mass layoffs.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Elmo is back to his usual family-friendly content after a hacker posted racist and antisemitic remarks to the Sesame Street character`s X account.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART ("The Daily Show" host): This is what happens when you go too long without tickling Elmo.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: The big dumper is your home run derby champion for 2025.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Cal Raleigh is the new king of swing.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: And he may have predicted it 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 CAL RALEIGH (As an eight-year-old kid): I`m the home run derby champ. (Rapping).\par \par \uc2 I am the home run derby champ.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Cal knew he would be part of the derby way back when he was an eight-year-old kid.\par \par \uc2 (CAL RALEIGH cheering)\par \par \uc2 CAL RALEIGH: What you say? What you say? I`m the man. I`m the man. Oh yeah! Oh yeah!\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive. Making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that video. He knew back in the day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Little Cal became Big Cal and he made big on those promises.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cal said, I`m the man. I`m the man. He was eight. You go, Cal.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with this, though. The aftermath of the dangerous flash flooding in and around New York City.\par \par \uc2 Just take a look at what happened in the biggest metro region in the United States. Some roads, we have to tell you, are still flooded the morning after the heaviest downpours.\par \par \uc2 In some areas, first responders had to rescue people trapped in their cars by the raging floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this, a New York City Subway stop nearly filled up with water. This is not the commute that you want. Wow, that`s scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano is north of the city.\par \par \uc2 Rob, the thunder and the lightning last night was a little unsettling. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: I`ll say that, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Boy, it was a mess. Wasn`t it? Across the New York City metro area, really across much of the northeast. There were 84 Flash Flood Warnings issued by the National Weather Service, two to four inches of rainfall came. It doesn`t sound like much, but it came in about just two hours, and obviously, this side street near the Saw Mill River Parkway is still -- still the water, very, very slow to recede. You see up to the wheel wells of some of the large, large trucks there.\par \par \uc2 The water, it came in fast, it came in furious for sure. Roads turning into rivers and it came right during the evening rush.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: The rainfall came fast and furious, flooding roads and leaving drivers with little time to escape.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Looking like 15 to 20 minutes. It went from maybe just a little stream of water and then it just started flooding over.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: More than half a foot of rain falling on Scotch Plains, New Jersey --\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Leading to dramatic water rescues. First responders using rafts to rescue people from the flooded streets. This video shows rescue crews wading through knee-deep water and using a rope to save a man trapped in rushing water to bring him to dry land.\par \par \uc2 Others were carried to dry land in a front loader.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We couldn`t go any further, so we had to get out and we tried to go up the road and we couldn`t, so they told us just to stay there and they came and picked me up in a loader.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Nearby in Plainfield --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL VARGAS (Trapped in car): I`ve never seen flooding like this.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: -- Michael Vargas (sp?) says he was trapped in his car for more than an hour.\par \par \uc2 On the New Jersey Turnpike, cars pushing through rising water there. The Governor declaring a State of Emergency.\par \par \uc2 And in Westchester, New York, emergency crews rescuing this driver stuck in a busy parkway. The video shows other cars left abandoned as floodwaters reached the roof of their vehicles.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: In Pennsylvania, puddles of murky water swamping the streets in Mount Joy after seven-and-a-half inches fell drenching the area.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is no joke.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: And in Manhattan, more than two-and-a-half inches of rain inundating the city streets. The floodwaters pouring into some subway stations, even into the train cars, forcing some passengers to kneel in their seats to stay dry as they watched the waters surge onto the platform.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When it rains here in New York, it sucks. I don`t know what is going on. The drainage? I mean, look at this.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: I mean, just absolute chaos across New York City. It feels like the past 10 days, we`ve had flash flooding just about everywhere east of the Rocky Mountains, and obviously, it`s not over yet. This is the month for it. It`s going to slide a little farther to the south. No rain expected here, so we should dry out north of, say, Trenton, New Jersey, but south of Baltimore and D.C., towards Roanoke, that`s going to be your hot spot for seeing more in the way of these tropical downpours.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got high moisture, summertime atmosphere, so it doesn`t take much for things to bubble up in the afternoon and that rain certainly comes down heavily.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of tropics, we`ve had tropical rains across parts of Florida, that little area of low pressure. Now, the National Hurricane Center saying we`ve got about a 40 percent chance of it developing in the Gulf of Mexico into something more serious over the next few days. We`ll be watching that very carefully, either way, more in the way of heavy rain across Florida.\par \par \uc2 No rain expected across Arizona where those fires continue to burn. Temperatures still very, very difficult for firefighters today. Winds will be in the 20s and no rain expected there. They certainly could have use some of the rain we had here last night, Tony, but as you know, that`s not the way it works this time of year -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, it is not, Rob. Thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in Central Texas, new flash flooding held up the search for victims of the Fourth of July disaster there. That recovery mission resumes this morning a day after parts of Kerr County were overwhelmed again by heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the same areas that were devastated just 10 days ago. Jason Allen is in hard-hit Kerrville.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: water submerged roadways and overwhelmed rivers in Texas, Monday, forcing volunteers searching for bodies from July 4th`s flash floods in Central Texas to evacuate to higher ground.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We cannot afford to have people in the water and right next to the water that are not swift water rescue trained and capable of at least saving themselves from being near that water.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Nearly 100 people are reported missing along the Guadalupe River.\par \par \uc2 ROB KELLY (Kerr County, Texas judge): We don`t know where they are. We don`t know how many lost. We`ve heard accounts of trailer after trailer after trailer being swept into the river with families in them. We can`t find the trailers.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: This is what Penny Deupree`s Hunt, Texas neighborhood used to look like. Now, rubble is all that`s left behind in the place she and her family gathered for nearly two decades, including on July 4th when it was flooded.\par \par \uc2 PENNY DEUPREE (Escaped flooded home): When I saw that water coming through the front door, it looked like an ocean to me.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Deupree and her eight family members climbed to a loft, but rising water forced them to break through the wall. Dupree`s kids helped her swim on to the roof.\par \par \uc2 PENNY DEUPREE: You don`t think about anything. You just do.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: You just react?\par \par \uc2 PENNY DEUPREE: Mm-hmm, yep. And our family is pretty good at, you know, hanging together. They could have left the old lady there if they wanted to.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: They were not going to do that. They were not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 PENNY DEUPREE: They were not going to do --\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: We spoke to Deupree`s family earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re blessed, right? We have a tight-knit family.\par \par \uc2 PENNY DEUPREE: Family is the most important thing in my life. And God and family. That`s what gets you through.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Those are -- I mean, that`s what the flood did not take away.\par \par \uc2 PENNY DEUPREE: No, it didn`t.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: The Kerr County Sheriff said that they are going to be working hard out here for at least another month or two, but they could be searching for victims for the next six months -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, thank you very much, Jason. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the two massive wildfires that are still zero percent contained near the Grand Canyon`s North Rim this morning. The fires have burned more than 55,000 acres and forced the area to close for the season. Dangerously hot weather is fueling the flames.\par \par \uc2 Ian lee is in Northern Arizona near the fire zone.\par \par \uc2 Ian, good morning.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 We are northwest of the White Sage Fire. Every day, we see thick plumes of smoke on the horizon. Fire is unpredictable, and we met with a cattle rancher who told us the flames threaten his livelihood.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE MCCORMICK (Cattle rancher): These winds can get erratic, and the fire can make its own weather.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: This road carries George McCormick (sp?) and his anxiety.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of uncertainty around here.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE MCCORMICK: Sure, you bet there is.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: From the gates of the McCormick Ranch, we could see the White Sage Fire burning just a couple of miles away.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE MCCORMICK: Yes, that is starting to cook back over there.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: For more than 140 years, McCormick says his family has worked this corner of Arizona, where bone-dry vegetation can spell ruin.\par \par \uc2 Fire hits this, it`s running.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE MCCORMICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: The two fires ripping through the North Rim of the Grand Canyon are growing in size and are still zero percent contained. Together, they`ve burned more than 55,000 acres.\par \par \uc2 Arizona`s Democratic Governor is calling for an investigation into why the federal government decided to manage one of those fires, the Dragon Bravo Fire as a controlled burn when it first broke out. The fire has burned more than 70 structures, including the historic Grand Canyon Lodge.\par \par \uc2 Just since we`ve been standing here, I now see one, two, three, four, five, six puffs of smoke.\par \par \uc2 At the McCormick Ranch, the weather is cooperating for now, but experience has taught this 77-year-old rancher not to get too comfortable.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE MCCORMICK: If it runs to the west, we`re in trouble.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Yesterday, Arizona`s two Democratic senators sent a letter to Secretary \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , requesting an analysis of the decision to treat the fire as a controlled burn, and asking if recent heat trends and dryness were taken into consideration. Meanwhile, McCormick told us he and his son plan to evacuate their herd sometime today -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it, Ian. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has announced a dramatic shift in his policy on the war in Ukraine. He is now giving Russia seven weeks to stop the fighting or face new economic sanctions.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe sat the White House with the latest on this.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning to you. It sounds like the President has had it with Mr. Putin.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It sure does, Gayle. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 The countdown is on with President Trump now saying \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has less than 50 days to make peace or face not only a fortified Ukraine, but also fresh economic penalties from the U.S. that could hurt the few countries left that do business with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We are very unhappy, I am, with Russia.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: President Trump picking sides, saying the U.S. will once again provide firepower to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But instead of directly delivering new weapons, including Patriot Air Defense Systems, the U.S. will take part in the assembly and \uc2 NATO\uc2  will buy them for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re not buying it, but we will manufacture it and they are going to be paying for it.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Sitting alongside NATO`s Secretary-General in the same spot where he famously clashed with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You don`t have the cards right now.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: -- Trump says his anger is now directed at Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: My conversations with him are always very pleasant. It seems that we have very lovely conversation, and then the missiles go off that night.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So Trump says if Putin doesn`t cut a peace deal with Zelenskyy in the next 50 days, he`ll impose 100 percent tariffs on countries that buy oil and gas from Russia.\par \par \uc2 Customers include China and Brazil, already faced to set stiff new sanctions from Trump starting next month.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, this has been the deadliest month for civilians in three years, after relentless Russian attacks.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy met Monday with special U.S. Envoy, Keith Kellogg and later spoke with Trump.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In his nightly address, he thanked the U.S., Germany, and Norway for making plans to deliver new weapon systems, and said his country remains committed to making peace.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Overnight, the former Russian President Dmitry Medvedev responded to Trump`s plans calling them a theatrical ultimatum to the Kremlin, and that Russia didn`t care and in an interview BBC published overnight, Trump said he is not done with Putin, just disappointed a deal hadn`t been done -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In today`s "Health Watch," big news for some popular American snacks amid questions about food dyes. Welch`s is the latest of several major companies to announce it is eliminating artificial food dyes. It says it will only use colors from natural sources by early next year.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder who is editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so this all comes amid a push from the Health Secretary, Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. who has blamed artificial food dyes for many of America`s chronic health problems. So what do we know about how synthetic dyes affect our health?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: There is some evidence that synthetic dyes can affect the behavior of a subset of kids. Red Dye #3, which the \uc2 FDA\uc2  wants to eliminate by 2027 has also been shown to cause tumors in rats. However, the levels at which humans are exposed to these artificial food dyes are exceedingly low, and the health risks really pale in comparison with the health risks of the added salt, sugar, and fat in processed foods.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so then explain this. On Monday, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved a natural blue food dye for use in sports drinks and candy. Help me square that knot.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So this new dye, Gardenia (Genipin) Blue, is a plant- based dye. However, it is made using soy protein. And so the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is still weighing whether this needs to be labeled as a potential allergen. But again, here is the thing, food dyes are not the main threat to our health. That`s really a red herring, and we`re missing the point if we`re not addressing the added salt, sugar and fat.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, I mean, it does seems sort of like that`s an easy target, when we should be addressing the things that really affect obesity rates and other health issues in this country.\par \par \uc2 So what should people be looking for when they look at the label when they are out shopping?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, again, look at the salt, sugar, and fat. Salt increases your blood pressure and the sugar and fat are what are driving the increases in obesity, type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, cancer.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration had -- its \uc2 FDA\uc2  had proposed front of package food labeling regulations. We are still waiting to see if this \uc2 FDA\uc2  will follow through with that, but that would make it a lot clearer for consumers to know how much salt, sugar, fat, and whether those levels are unhealthy in those foods.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you very much, as always.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, over to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I got it. Thank you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Four astronauts are back on Earth this morning after a nearly three-week stay aboard the International Space Station. Their \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  capsule splashed down overnight off the Southern California coast.\par \par \uc2 Now, the private Axiom mission was commanded by the great Peggy Whitson, who has now racked up a record of 695 days in space. You go, Miss Peggy.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is an excellent view.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Streaking across the night sky over the Pacific, the Axiom-4 crew returned to Earth early Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: We have splashdown of the AX-4 crew back on Planet Earth.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Welcome home.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A short time later, the recovery team helped the crew out of their Dragon capsule, their first breath of fresh air after 20 days in space.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Ignition and liftoff.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Launched last month, the private mission managed by \uc2 Axiom Space\uc2  was commanded by veteran astronaut, Peggy Whitson, making her fifth trip to space.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WHITSON: It`s so much fun to be in space again, always fun to be in space, but it is really fun to be in space with three new astronauts.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Her crewmates, these three rookie flyers, were government- sponsored astronauts from India, Poland, and Hungary. Three nations that last sent someone to space more than 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Since Peggy made me enter. It`s a truly amazing place.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Before she launched, we asked Whitson about the mission`s goals.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is the kinds of mission that really puts the international in International Space Station, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WHITSON: It does. It absolutely does, and it opens up access to countries that might not normally get access to space.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Mission accomplished.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Mark Strassmann in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Peggy. She is always an inspiration. I remember when she took off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s very nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a wonderful thing to be back as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead, was Beyonce targeted by thieves? \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  say somebody stole her music, but not in the way you might think.\par \par \uc2 And baseball`s home run derby on the eve of the All-Star Game. See who won it all with some big swings. There he is, but first, time for a check of your local weather, 7:17.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, we catch up with the Jonas Brothers. They`ll tell us about their new album and how their father inspired an important new partnership.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the always entertaining Nick Offerman joins us to talk about his movie, "Smurfs."\par \par \uc2 Also, see the incredible outcome after a plane plunges into a Florida neighborhood. You`re not going to believe the video.\par \par \uc2 And in "What to Watch" we will reveal some of the big names who are up for Primetime Emmys.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Liberty Mutual Insurance\uc2 . Only pay for what you need.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: There he goes. Deep to center! Gone!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The man they call Big Dumper. We will let you \uc2 Google\uc2  it, is your new 2025 Home Run Derby Champion.\par \par \uc2 Seattle Mariners\uc2  catcher Cal Raleigh won it all last night in Atlanta. He is the first catcher and the first switch-hitter to win the derby and he did it in very sweet style.\par \par \uc2 Dad was pitching, 15-year-old brother was catching. His mom was in attendance. He signed a jersey to her at the end. It`s a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I`m still trying to figure out Big Dumper.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is because he has got a big booty, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s got a big butt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Okay. I got it. He has also got a big bat. You go.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a whodunit with Queen Bey as the victim. What thieves are accused of stealing from her and here is the thing, it was something very important.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *********************************************************\par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back, folks.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the moment a small plane crashed into a tree in a Florida neighborhood, and it was all caught by a doorbell camera. All four people, we are lucky and happy to say, survived, thanks to the quick work of neighbors who helped rescue the pilot and passengers.\par \par \uc2 Witnesses say one resident used an ax to break the window and help get them out. This happened Sunday just north of Miami as the plane was trying to land at a nearby airport.\par \par \uc2 Remarkably, none of the homes in the neighborhood were destroyed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is good news.\par \par \uc2 Beyonce`s Cowboy Carter Tour -- this is not good news, is coming to an end very soon, but she has now become the star of a whodunit? What you might call the case of the missing music. Oh, no!\par \par \uc2 There is an arrest warrant out in Atlanta this morning after police say that somebody grabbed a bunch of hard drives containing songs the superstar has never released.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz has been following the story, she joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, this is a travesty. This person must be arrested.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They must be found, Gayle. I agree completely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they must be found.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I mean, the Cowboy Carter Tour is incredible, and here`s what happened.\par \par \uc2 Last Tuesday, a man who police identified himself as Beyonce`s choreographer, 37-year-old Christopher Grant, and another person who said he was one of her dancers, 32-year-old Diandre Blue, they told law enforcement that someone had broken into the trunk window of their rented \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and taken numerous items that belonged to the singer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Texas Hold`Em"/Beyonce/Columbia Records)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Just two days before the start of Beyonce`s four-show run in Atlanta, police say there was a brazen theft of her intellectual property. According to an \uc2 Atlanta Police Department\uc2  incident document, the burglary was reported last Tuesday night in a parking deck near a popular market.\par \par \uc2 A man police say identified himself as Beyonce`s choreographer and another who says he was one of her dancers, told officers someone had broken into the back window of their Jeep Wagoneer while they were away and stolen two suitcases.\par \par \uc2 The men say hard drives containing Beyonce`s unreleased songs, watermarked music, footage plans for her shows, and past and future setlists were also taken. There is an arrest warrant out for a suspect authorities have not yet identified.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  news law enforcement contributor, Rodney Harrison.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON: The next steps are to do as much of a background check, see if he is -- if he or she is in any type of database. See his possible whereabouts, last known address, and any other people that he or she may be affiliated with.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  say investigators were able to recover two very light fingerprints from the scene, and added that cameras in the area captured the crime.\par \par \uc2 Harrison says it is too soon to know whether the burglar knew the items belonged to Beyonce or whether the crime was random.\par \par \uc2 TOMAS MIER ("\uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2 " staff writer): It was really surprising to hear that somebody like Beyonce had this music stolen.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tomas Mier is a staff writer with "Rolling Stone."\par \par \uc2 TOMAS MIER: With somebody of the caliber of Beyonce, you expect everything to be in vaults with a lot of locks and keys. I think it is just going to cause Beyonce to just have everything locked and loaded in case it is released or somebody does leak this footage or music that she has that ready for her own releases at the time that she is ready to release them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Beyonce is moving on from Atlanta. She wrapped her fourth and final show there last night. The Cowboy Carter Tour will end later this month in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Look --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody has lots of thoughts.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve been talking throughout this whole morning.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What I have been thinking is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Inside job.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They were away from the car for about an hour, and police do say they have a suspected model of the car that could have been involved in this theft, the suspect`s car.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But so far, they haven`t I.D.`d him, at least publicly. They think it isa a male. There were Air pods that were also stolen that were pinging to a location, then the pinging stopped.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And, so do we think the person didn`t know that they were stealing something from Beyonce? Then people say, if it is that valuable, why is it sitting in a car?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Luggage didn`t --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Nobody knows. We don`t know if it was intentional or not yet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Also now, if they`re watching this, they know what they have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How many times do you leave your suitcase in your trunk?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the time.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Right. And with your computer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All the time.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It was a computer laptop, other things that were stolen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I don`t have Beyonce music in my --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: If you are Beyonce`s choreographer, maybe you do on your computer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Maybe --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Beyonce has a copy of this --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But unreleased music. That is interesting, a choreographer, unreleased music that is what they are preparing.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They were working on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Working on. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This person will be found. They will be caught. And we hope --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Brought to justice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Justice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All will be known.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tell them, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to go to the last show in Vegas though, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The long arm of the law is coming for you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I think we should all go to the last show in Vegas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to go to that last show.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Just to stay on top of this story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I agree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Co-signed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Shawna says she is not paying for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And \uc2 CBS\uc2  would pay -- yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Gayle, all right, coming up, here we go, simmer down, folks. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, your visit to a national park this summer could feel much different than previous years. How staffing cuts mean big, big changes.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Grand Teton National Park there.\par \par \uc2 This summer, millions of families will explore our great outdoors in our great national parks. But a lot of those trips are going to look different compared to last year.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen is here to show us why.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Hey, Tony. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 In 2024, the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  reported a record-setting year. Nearly 332 million visits. But this year, an advocacy group says federal staffing cuts and policy changes are likely to have some serious consequences for visitors.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Each year, millions of visitors come to Maryland`s Assateague Island to swim, camp, and catch a glimpse of this National Park site`s freely roaming horses.\par \par \uc2 But this summer, families will also see this, warnings no lifeguards are on duty.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ZACK TYNDALL (Berlin, Maryland): Every day that with we don`t have a lifeguard on the beach is putting every one of these swimmers, every one of our local residents, everybody that comes to visit Assateague Island in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Zack Tyndall is the mayor of nearby Berlin, an amateur surfer and former paramedic, he says rip currents here are his main concern.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ZACK TYNDALL: When I came out here for calls, we relied on the Rangers, we relied on the life guards.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: According to the National Park`s Conservation Association or NPCA, the park usually staffs about 13 lifeguards during the summer and one nearby town says those lifeguards were responsible for 24 rescues last year.\par \par \uc2 We asked the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  about staffing, but they didn`t directly respond to our questions. Instead, saying lifeguard shortages are a nationwide concern many communities are facing.\par \par \uc2 This is one of more than 430 \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  sites across the country. The \uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2  says the entire system is facing a full-blown staffing crisis.\par \par \uc2 ED STIERLI (\uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2  Mid-Atlantic Senior Regional Director): This was a policy put into place by DOGE and the current Trump administration that led to a complete hiring freeze on all hiring across the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Ed Stierli overseas the Mid-Atlantic for the NPCA which says staffing overall for the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  is down 24 percent since President Trump took office, not including seasonal hires.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): Let`s do the right thing before we see something bad.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Virginia Senator Tim Kaine represents part of the area the park covers. He says this goes beyond National Parks and only legislation can provide protection.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): In Trump term two, he`s not waiting for Congress. He`s just slashing, burning, firing people, laying folks off, cutting agencies, eliminating contracts.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: According to a Trump administration official, all fire and law enforcement, and public-facing park personnel are prioritized to remain in place. But these concerns go beyond Maryland.\par \par \uc2 At the Grand Canyon, hikers are warned help could be delayed in part because of limited staff. Across the country, the NPCA says visitors should expect longer lines to get inside popular parks and also recommends planning ahead carefully with potentially limited cell reception.\par \par \uc2 Do you think public safety even now is already at risk?\par \par \uc2 ED STIERLI: It`ll definitely be a different experience for many visitors this summer and people need to be prepared, and they need to be vigilant and aware.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Now, since we first started reporting on this story earlier this month, the NPCA says the Interior Department has started providing funding for lifeguards in parts of the park. That said, the overall issue of staffing shortages at National Park sites remain, and visitors should make sure to carefully check each park`s website for alerts before they head out this summer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in "What to Watch," we`ll reveal some of the nominees for the upcoming Primetime Emmy Awards right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , though right now, it is 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got big things coming up for you at 8:00. Actor and comedian, Nick Offerman just arrived and will join us in the studio to tell us about his new movie, it`s the "Smurfs." It is so good. Why he wanted to be part of the film and what it was like working with Rihanna.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we catch up with the Jonas Brothers. Well, Adriana Diaz catches up with the Jonas Brothers. They tell her about working together for 20 years, their new album, and important partnerships close to their hearts.\par \par \uc2 All of that is coming up in our next hour, but first -- did I mention yesterday Klaus is back? Guess what? He came back again today. So, we`re heading over to the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s up?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Day 2. The hip is still working.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Getting ready for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come on, faster, faster.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is still working.\par \par \u