m is number three. Hello Diane von Furstenberg. Ireland is number two, and the country with the best work-life balance, drum roll, please, is New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 All of these countries are way ahead of the U.S., which, by the way, where do you think the U.S., on the list of one to 60?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just like the bottom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where did the U.S. ranked?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A list of one to sixty.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One to sixty.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One to sixty.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like at the bottom. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They ranked 59th. That`s down from 55 last year and 53rd back in 2023.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, who is 60 this year? It`s going to be like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, 60 -- I don`t have it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, it is notable though that is not a --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alison (sp?), tell me who is 60? But the index uses factors like paid vacation leave, access to health care, public safety, and average hours worked per week. That`s right, Nigeria. Because we talked about this is number 60.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People work hard in Nigeria, as somebody who lived and worked in Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But look at New Zealand versus United States. New Zealand, 86 out of a hundred is how their feel. Here, we are 31 out of a hundred. New Zealand, 32 days of vacation. The minimum wage in New Zealand is $16.42. Here it is $7.25.\par \par \uc2 Now, the maternity leave, I think, is great in New Zealand, 26 weeks, 100 percent pay. In this country, women in this country know this, it`s 12 weeks and they don`t have to pay you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is the difference of work-life balance? The definition is -- the definition: Life comes first, and work should exist in service of enriching, not overshadowing it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, it`s funny, I thought that we were moving -- I thought we were getting better in this country about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A lot of companies have embraced work-life balance when it comes to employees, but, clearly we`re not when you look at the hard numbers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not if we are 59th out of 60.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re always working, and that`s my "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The death of partying in these United States. What is going on, people?\par \par \uc2 It`s been noticed by a whole bunch of folks. There is a survey, the American Time Youth Survey found that in the last 20 years, 2003 to 2024, the amount of time that everyday Americans spent hosting or attending a social event declined by half, and it has been particularly bad thing among young Americans.\par \par \uc2 People between the ages of 15 and 24, when you should be partying all the time, as the song says, you`re partying is down 70 percent compared to 2003.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But why is that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it because people are working or because they don`t want to party?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is it because it is expensive to party now?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On any given weekend, only one in 25 of American households are doing something social, either hosting or attending. Isn`t that crazy?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, I would think that hosting -- I`m not sure about that, but you know, going out to restaurants, going out to bars, that stuff is increasingly expensive. Everything is priced way, way high.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you have people over and have some cheese on a plate at home and drink some whatever. I mean, like, it doesn`t have to be expensive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, it doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can have people in the backyard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t think that like hanging out and partying a little bit more is going to, like, solve the world`s problems, but I do think we could do a little bit better connecting with our neighbors.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s an important thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clearly, we`re 59th out of 60.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they said, in this country, we live in an always on culture. You`re expected to be on duty 24/7 and so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The "The Atlantic`s" Derek Thompson says Americans spend more time alone today than in any other period in recorded history.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we wonder why we`re so divided. You`re right, connecting with our community, connecting with people in our life would probably serve us well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Party at Gayle`s house!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Party at Tony`s house!\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ll see you later, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Party at Tony`s house, Saturday at 2:00. Bring something.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everybody is invited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, and your address is?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Jonas Brothers, they are global -- it`s in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 The Jonas Brothers, they are a global sensation. They are celebrating 20 years together with a very busy summer, we have to say. They`re releasing a new album that`s close to home. They`re going back on tour. That`s good.\par \par \uc2 And they are collaborating with Mastercard in support of the charity organization, Stand Up to Cancer.\par \par \uc2 So, to kick off the partnership, the band conducted a very special performance of their new single, it is called "I Can`t Lose."\par \par \uc2 The full video of that will debut tonight during the MLB All-Star Game. That`s Major League Baseball. But we`ve got an exclusive sneak peek and here is the lovely, Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mastercard)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: High above New York City, the Jonas Brothers gave a private concert for some of their bravest fans, cancer survivors and their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Going through chemo.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The fans were told they were just doing interviews and had no idea they would meet their favorite band.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS (Jonas Brothers): I think it`s an amazing opportunity for us to be able to just surprise them and say how much we love them and support them.\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS (Jonas Brothers) Hello.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS (Jonas Brothers): All right.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, my God! Kevin!\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS (Jonas Brothers): Thank you for sharing your story with us.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s personal for Kevin, Joe, and Nick. Their father, Kevin Jonas, Sr., was diagnosed with stage 2 colon cancer in 2017 and went through six months of chemotherapy.\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS: You don`t realize how intense it is until it hits your doorstep and you realize it`s someone who can be close to you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And your dad is okay? He has been in remission?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS: He is doing great.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: Cancer free for a few years now, and it is incredible to be on the other side of that. So proud of the way he handled it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So, he is back. Dad jokes are back and all?\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS: They never left.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS: It never stopped.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: It never left.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mastercard)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: To help others, they worked in support of Stand Up to Cancer on this music video for "I Can`t Lose." An anthem they hope inspires anyone facing a battle.\par \par \uc2 The song is from their seventh studio album out next month, called "Greetings From Your Hometown." It`s the latest chapter of their two decades together as a band.\par \par \uc2 Can you believe it`s been 20 years?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS: No.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: Yes and no.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Because you guys look --\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS: My knees maybe --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: -- good for your 20th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS: -- said yes. Oh, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: Started young.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS: We`ll take it. We`ll take it though.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Year 3000"/Jonas Brothers/Columbia Records)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Nick was just 12 years old when the brothers, sons of former Christian singers, signed their first record deal in 2005, Joe was 15 and Kevin, 17.\par \par \uc2 They`ve since toured the world, pursued solo projects.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jealous"/Nick Jonas/Island Records)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And started their own families.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heart by Heart"/Joe Jonas/Republic Records)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Love Me to Heaven"/Jonas Brothers/Republic Records)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But they`re returning to their roots with their upcoming album and tour, and you can guess where they shot their album art.\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS: Went back to our familiar neighborhood in Wyckoff, New Jersey, where we really started as a band and started writing songs, went to our favorite bagel shop. It was, I would say, a food day. Our favorite Mexican restaurant.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS: Pizza.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wait. Who chose the restaurants? Who is the restaurant chooser?\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: It chose us.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh, really?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: It kind of chose us.\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS: We knew where we were going. We didn`t have to debate.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN JONAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Slow Motion"/Jonas Brothers/Republic Records)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The album`s sound is also an ode to their past.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: Our dad raised us on some of the greats. Carole King, \uc2 Tapestry\uc2 , Bee Gees number ones, Beatles number ones, Billy Joel, Stevie Wonder. We actually -- we even got the opportunity to effectively work with the Bee Gees with our current single, "No Time to Talk."\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Really?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @JonasBrothers)\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: Technically, in the credits, it says, you know, that we have a songwriting credit with them, which is very cool.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What would you say to your younger self at the start of this career 20 years ago?\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: Invest in Uber.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Invest in \uc2 Zoom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE JONAS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NICK JONAS: I think earlier in our career, we were kind of stressed about everything, and you know, we weren`t able to really take a second to look around.\par \par \uc2 I think each night of this tour, we`re just going to look out and try to just take it all in and think about how crazy it is that we went from the little house and minivan in New Jersey to being able to travel the world like this and see our fans. It`s really an honor.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still going strong. That`s great.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Going strong. It`s been 20 years since they had their first album and you heard what Nick said at the end, you know, I can`t believe we went from that little house in New Jersey --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: -- and the minivan to where they are now, and that`s really what they`re trying to do with their new album and tour. It`s all about their hometown, it is all about going back to their roots and they were lovely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go back and listen to the Jonas Brothers` song "Lovebug."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve been talking -- yes, you said it, you love that song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I really, really like that song and really like them. I really like them as people. I think this is great.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I mean, and it just goes to show, they`re doing this to try to raise awareness about cancer, raise funds for cancer and you know, they were obviously affected personally.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But, imagine being one of those fans who had no idea they were about to meet the Jonas Brothers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s always fun.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And then, they`re performing on this roof. It was a hundred degrees that day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It didn`t look it. You guys looked cool as cucumbers.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They did -- it was great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was cool.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, so if you at home would like to donate to Stand Up to Cancer, scan the Q.R. code on your screen and right now, it is time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Summertime is here, which for a lot of kids means days at the beach, the park, maybe the pool, and also some heightened self- consciousness about their bodies.\par \par \uc2 One-third of six to eight-year-old boys and more than half of girls at that age say they want to be skinnier and ono average, girls start dieting at just eight years old.\par \par \uc2 So, how can parents help their kids promote a healthy body image?\par \par \uc2 We are joined by a clinical psychologist, Dr. Sheryl Ziegler, author of "The Crucial Years: The Essential Guide to Mental Health and Modern Puberty in Middle Childhood."\par \par \uc2 Dr. Ziegler, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 This whole segment is giving me flashbacks to me being eight years old, coming out of the family pool and a relative pinched my belly fat, totally innocent, but I didn`t even know I had a belly until that moment.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER (Clinical psychologist and author of "The Crucial Years"): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it changed the way I thought about myself.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: I actually love that you started off with that, because "A," this is not just a girl issue, this affects boys as well. But the other piece is, that`s exactly right. Until you`re about 8-ish, seven or eight, you don`t almost know your body that well.\par \par \uc2 I mean, studies show at age three, a little toddler knows what it means to be fat. But you might not have had any bit of self-consciousness, and then all of a sudden, it is summertime and someone does something like that. Says -- makes a comment or says something like that, and that`s like one of the first tips is, don`t do that. Don`t comment on kid`s bodies, especially when they`re in puberty. They`re adjusting to their new body. They might not know they have any kind of flaw or perceived imperfection.\par \par \uc2 And then when we do that, here you are, all of these years later, you still remember it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you -- go ahead. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say, you don`t comment on it, but then, you know, you also find yourself shopping for middle school clothes, as I did recently with my older daughter and you`re constantly saying, would you like this? Do you not like this outfit?\par \par \uc2 How do you engage in normal parenting interactions without encouraging or ignoring something, you know?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: Well, that`s perfect. So if your daughter was saying to you, do you like this, right? You want to say something back like, how do you feel in it? Do you like it? Is it comfortable for you? Do you think you could sit all day in that? Do you think you could you play in that?\par \par \uc2 So, you really just want to make comments or even have conversations with kids, particularly in puberty, about things like their interests, their new style, the new music they`re listening to. Oh, I love your smile. Remembering a memory. Not saying things like, look at you, you`ve gotten so tall or especially around pool and beach time when they`re so, you know, they feel so exposed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: And kids do things like cover themselves up and wear oversized t-shirts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear what you`re saying, Sheryl, but I was really surprised that boys, girls, six to eight are even thinking about this.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it because they`re being teased? Is it because parents are putting pressure on them? Are they getting it from peers?\par \par \uc2 I had -- when I was six to eight, I wasn`t even thinking about my body.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: Yes. This is such a different world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A: When do you think we should start thinking about it? What should we do to make these kids feel better about themselves?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: So, I think parents do need to know this kind of information, that it is different, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: And so I think, what parents need to do is say things like, you know, even to other people, like, don`t make comments about my kid`s growing body, right, we are making different comments, if they want to wear something, if they want to go bathing suit shopping, let`s say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: And you can tell they`re feeling self-conscious, you can just say to them, if you`re not comfortable in that, you don`t have to wear that, let`s find something else.\par \par \uc2 But kids, what they do is they wind up not going to the party or not going to the beach sometimes because they`re self-conscious.\par \par \uc2 So, parents can also watch what they`re saying. When parents say things like, oh, I shouldn`t eat that, that`s too much or they`re making negative -- that`s one of the biggest ways kids soak in modeling, so that`s really important.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the other thing that`s really, really important is that parents realize that this is happening, that they are adjusting to their new bodies, and then if we are positive about it, the research says they`re more positive about it, so that`s really important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how can parents then promote healthy body image and high self-esteem in their children?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHERYL ZIEGLER: So, one of the things that`s important is actually start with food. Understanding the connection between food and feelings is really important. So not necessarily saying, like, no, no, we`re not going to have that. Demonizing certain food categories is a bad idea. Just really being open and saying, your body is actually for moving and writing and learning, and really not making it about like the toxic beauty culture. That`s part of what the answer is, is that they are exposed to a lot more.\par \par \uc2 There`s so many different sources of media, so if you can focus on your body, we want it to be healthy and want it to be active, those are two really positive messages that really are effective.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really great stuff.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Sheryl Ziegler, always great to have you. Her book, "The Crucial Years: The Essential Guide to Mental Health and Modern Puberty in Middle Childhood" is out now.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always say, time flies when you`re having fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good time today. Good show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nick is always fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is true. That is true. And about the party that we`re having this weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I will bring the paper plates --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What am I bringing?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- and the paper napkins.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re bringing the -- paper plates and napkin? You`re bringing the booze. You know more about alcohol.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ll bring the booze. I`ll make the cocktails. I am your bartender.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, I will bring --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Your bartender.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- some cutlery too.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. Stick around for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS PLUS with this one and we will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250716el7f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Texas Flooding Death Toll Tops 100; Search for Survivors in Texas; Survival Stories; Tropical Storm Chantal`s Deadly Path; Ceasefire Negotiations at the White House; Trump Sends Tariff Letters to Trade Partners; Gunman Opens Fire at Texas Border Patrol Facility; L.A. Mayor Blasts Immigration Raid at Public Park; Philadelphia Trash Strike; Three Saved After Getting Stuck on Oregon Cliff; Change is Afoot at Our Airports; Big, Beautiful Bill Impact; Stories of Survival; Oprah`s Latest Book Club Pick; CBS MORNINGS Deals, Dexter is Back; Going for "Belly Laughs," CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson; Maurice DuBois, Jason  Allen, Janet Shamlian, Rob Marciano, Nancy Cordes, Nicole Sganga, Ross  DiMattei, Kris Van Cleave, Jill Schlesinger, Jo Ling Kent, Lisa Ling \par \uc2 14,912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue the trumpets on this Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got amazing stories of resilience in Texas five days after that terrible flood disaster. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The number of dead climbs in Texas and the search for survivors continues, as many are now asking why this tragedy happened.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. DAN PATRICK (R-TX): If there had been a siren, maybe that would spark people to say, we have a massive disaster like five minutes away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A heavily armed man is killed after allegedly opening fire on a Texas Border Patrol station.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Many, many dozens of rounds fired from by the suspect towards the building.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Israel`s Prime Minister praises President Trump over Iran as fighting continues in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU (Israeli Prime Minister): The letter I sent to the Nobel Prize Committee nominating you for the Peace Prize, which is well deserved.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: TSA is reportedly letting travelers keep their shoes on during screening, quietly rolling out the change nationwide.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: The dog that caught the ceremonial first pitch to the Reds game decided she didn`t want to give it back.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Avoiding groundskeepers and field personnel like we`re Barry Sanders and no respect for law enforcement either right here at all.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #4: This field is mine. I own it all.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #5: This video captured by a Camp Mystic nurse showing the evacuation journey for the girls who survived the catastrophic flooding.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing worship song)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Singing worship songs on the bus, taking as many girls as possible to safety.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing worship song)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Their voices sound so beautiful for something that is so tragic. I was told that camp counselors led them on a sing-along. Very, very, very powerful stuff there, and that is where we`re going to begin, of course, this morning with the aftermath of that catastrophic flooding in Central Texas.\par \par \uc2 At last count, more than 100 people are now confirmed dead, with Kerr County the hardest hit, but all hope, they say, is not lost.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, Gayle, because search and rescue operations are still underway. People have come from all over to help. But in the middle of all this, there are also questions, how could this happen so fast, and why did the warnings come so late?\par \par \uc2 "CBS Evening News" anchor Maurice DuBois is leading our coverage from Ingram, Texas along the banks of the Guadalupe River.\par \par \uc2 Maurice, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 You can`t see right at this hour, but the river is a good hundred yards behind me here. It`s dark this morning still, and in a matter of minutes, Friday morning, as we take you back there, the water surged to about eight- and-a-half feet here, pushing this trailer home behind me to the side, carrying people, pets and possessions along the way.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, we watched from this very spot as first responders pulled another body from a pile of debris across the river, and this is 11 miles downriver from Camp Mystic, that century old Christian summer camp where hundreds of girls were sleeping in their cabins innocently when those flash floods hit.\par \par \uc2 We visited the camp yesterday, where you could see much of the area still caked in debris and mud. The camp confirming 27 campers and counselors died after being swept away.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, officials said they had rescued more than 850 people since the flooding began, but we have definitely seen the mood here shifting over the past 24 to 48 hours, with the grim reality setting in for families waiting for news.\par \par \uc2 There are also concerns that this disaster could be repeated in the future if more robust warning systems, such as sirens are not installed in this flood prone area.\par \par \uc2 Jason Allen has been here covering that and more since the very beginning. He joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Jason, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Good morning to you, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 You know this is really slow and tedious work, right? These rescue crews are having to work in sections, a kilometer at a time, half mile or so at a time, until they can get things cleared. Only then can the heavy machinery come in and clear out all of this debris, and although rain chances are a lot lower than they have been over the past few days, anything that does fall now is falling on ground that is saturated.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Rescue teams have not stopped their around the clock search for anyone who might still be alive along the Guadalupe River. Authorities have already scoured miles of the river, finding people hanging on to tree tops and rooftops.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Kerrville, Texas/Lorena Guillen/TMX)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Officials say hundreds of first responders are involved, some using drones equipped with thermal sensors to try to spot any survivors.\par \par \uc2 DALTON RICE (Kerrville, Texas city manager): This is, if not the worst, one of the worst disasters in this region in a very, very long time.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: As people desperately search for their missing loved ones, local officials are facing questions over why Kerr County, part of an area known as Flash Flood Alley never invested in its own Flood Warning System.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): We know the National Weather Service put out an emergency warning just after 1:00 A.M. and a second emergency warning just after 4:00 A.M.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously most people at 1:00 A.M. and 4:00 A.M. are asleep, and so I think we will have a reasonable conversation about A, are there any ways to have earlier detection and some of that, the limits of a flash flood, they`re very difficult, because they can arise so quickly. But everyone would agree, in hindsight, if we could go back and do it again, we would evacuate, particularly those in the most vulnerable areas.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Lieutenant Governor Dan Patrick told us the state could fund warning sirens, and he thinks legislators can take it up this month.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. DAN PATRICK (R-TX): If they can`t afford to do it then, let us do it.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Is that a possibility?\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. DAN PATRICK (R-TX): Sure, we have a special session starting two weeks from today, and I think we can take that up and do some other things of funding the sirens.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Something he says could make a difference in the next flood.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. DAN PATRICK (R-TX): So had all of those sirens gone off, maybe -- you know, maybe that would spark people to say, we have a massive disaster like five minutes away.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Local officials said there was a private drone yesterday that crashed into one of the helicopters that has been flying in the area. They have been firmly telling people there is a strict ban on flying private drones over the river while they`re still running operations.\par \par \uc2 Now, when asked yesterday, how many people might still be missing in the area? Officials would not give a direct answer to that. When asked, is it dozens? Is it hundreds, Maurice? The only thing they would say it is, "A lot."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Okay, we are going to leave it right there.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jason Allen, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 As rescue workers do comb through the debris, Janet Shamlian has been hearing some truly remarkable stories of survival. She is in Kerrville this morning.\par \par \uc2 Janet, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Maurice, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Despite the tremendous loss of life here, there are stories of resilience and compassion, neighbors braving rising water to help each other, and those first responders working tirelessly to reach those in danger.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The tragedy that claimed so many lives on the Fourth of July was almost 300 miles away --\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE singing)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But at a vigil in Houston Monday night, the sadness was close to home.\par \par \uc2 Camp Mystic alum, Lauren Hamner (sp?) has a daughter who summers there too, and said music is central to the community.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN HAMNER (Camp Mystic alum): I`m hearing reports of the counselors leading the girls in song and prayer at the height of the flood waters inside buildings and at the base of trees on a hill while lightning and storms raged.\par \par \uc2 Lord, have mercy, they understood the assignment.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And you could hear the singing of Mystic campers, even as survivors evacuated Friday.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: a chorus of hymns as they passed over rushing water.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP singing)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Now across the region, people are cleaning up, starting over.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN BATES (Local resident): The water started. It was up here.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Robin Bates (sp?) left her house in the community of Sandy Creek as the flood waters rose. When she returned, she says, two houses had crashed into hers.\par \par \uc2 In the city of Burnett, Dottie Bai (sp?) woke to chest high water. With her sense of humor intact, she recalled her neighbor`s house stripped from its foundation floating into hers.\par \par \uc2 DOTTIE BAI (Local resident): You know, if they wanted to come visit me, I would invite them over. They didn`t have to come that way.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And here in Kerrville, Ricky Ray Robertson (sp?) says he survived because he was watching baseball across the street from his own cabin, which was swept away by the storm.\par \par \uc2 RICKY RAY ROBERTSON (Local resident): \uc2 Houston Astros\uc2  and Jesus Christ saved my life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The \uc2 Houston Astros\uc2  will take that one as a win.\par \par \uc2 The volunteer effort here, the kindness is remarkable, and many times it`s nothing formally organized, people clearing debris from someone`s home that they don`t know, or simply offering a meal -- Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Janet, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 In our next half hour, I`m going to speak with a man who dropped everything to join the search when he found out a friend had been affected by all of this.\par \par \uc2 I just want to show you where we are here. We`re in someone`s yard. This is someone`s trailer, their home just collapsed under those eight feet of water. And here you see all their stuff.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they`re staying with friends trying to figure out their next move, and literally, they have no idea. They`re basically in shock, as we send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got the best of Texas there, neighbor helping neighbor.\par \par \uc2 Maurice, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Maurice mentioned the next half hour, but also be sure to watch Maurice`s coverage tonight on the "CBS Evening News."\par \par \uc2 Maurice, thank you very much. We`ll check back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we`re also tracking deadly flooding in North Carolina, believe it or not, where Tropical Storm Chantel dumped nearly a foot of rain on places like Chapel Hill, that is just west of Raleigh, North Carolina. At least two people died when their cars were swept away by rushing floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 CBS News meteorologist, Rob Marciano shows how far the flooding in this area has spread.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: In Durham, North Carolina, the Eno River spilling out of its banks Monday, rising water swamping cars and reaching the doorstep of several homes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAMONTENERO (Local resident): We looked out the window and said, "Oh my God."\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: John Lamontenero (sp?) was awakened by his son-in-law when their house began to flood.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAMONTENERO: They said, "We`re flooded. And pack a bag just in case we can get rescued."\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: They were among 80 people rescued by the Durham Fire Department.\par \par \uc2 In Chatham County, the Haw River becoming raging rapids, quickly rising to over 22 feet, a new record height.\par \par \uc2 One of the worst hit areas was Alamance County, where flood waters cut off a neighborhood. This health care worker unable to get to work.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My client depends on me to be there, and I can`t even get there because I can`t get across the bridge.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Winston Salem`s Fire Department rescuing people by boat late Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 Rushing water swept by a nearby mobile home park, damaging at least a dozen trailers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I was scared -- shocked. Because I ain`t never seen anything like this before.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: This intense rainfall, once again, coming from the remnants of an intense or a weak tropical storm and here is some of the rainfall tallies. There`s northwest of Chapel Hill, 10 inches plus, and they typically, for the month of July, get five. Those are two months` worth of rain, the remnants now of Chantal, thankfully, is going out to sea. It is now southeast of Cape Cod.\par \par \uc2 But now we`ve got another thing to contend with here in the northeast, a lot of heat, lot of humidity, and we have that persistent severe weather later on this afternoon along the I-95 corridor from Richmond, D.C., Baltimore, Philly, New York, we`re all in it. And boom, five, six o`clock, right around the rush, that`s when the thunderstorms come in. These will be intense. It could have some damaging winds and heavy rainfall as well.\par \par \uc2 And now the Northeast has its own Flood Watch out from D.C. all the way to Southern New England.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, back up to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it, Rob. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 At the White House, President Trump met Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu last night to discuss the next step in ceasefire negotiations in Gaza. Nancy Cordes is at the White House with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, at that dinner meeting last night, President Trump told reporters while he was sitting with Prime Minister Netanyahu that he still thinks there is a good chance that a ceasefire deal can be struck this week. This was the first time the two have met since the U.S. struck Iran`s nuclear facilities a couple of weeks ago. Netanyahu called that a "historic victory," and he also made it clear that he still does not favor a two- state solution in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s what we do know about the ceasefire proposal they are mulling as it stands right now. According to an official briefed on the negotiations, it would involve a 