d Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Very excited about this, as I had to remove my shoes, put him in the bin this weekend. You go, why are we doing this again?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why are we doing this?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why are we doing this?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`ve got holes in my socks, so people are looking.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is going to join us later to talk about this. We`ll go to Texas next.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: So we`re going down. Good position.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Copy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back, it`s 7:30. You`re looking at footage from a nail-biting helicopter rescue, not from Texas, but from Oregon. It happened on Saturday as a Coast Guard helicopter airlifted three people who had been trapped on a cliff side.\par \par \uc2 It was two 14-year-old boys -- right on brand for 14-year-old boys -- stuck after trying to climb up a rock face from a beach on the coast. But then a member of the local Fire Department also became stuck while trying to save the two boys. Fortunately, we can say no one was hurt in the end.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like what you said, "right on brand."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right on brand.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For 14-year-old boys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Trying to explore.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Trying to explore.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Getting outside, what you can do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we love them. We cannot say enough about 14-year-old boys and the first responders all around the country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, first responders and volunteers, they are still searching for many people who are unaccounted for in these devastating floods. In many cases, it really is just neighbors helping neighbors.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Evening News" anchor, Maurice DuBois in Ingram, Texas, with a great example of that.\par \par \uc2 Maurice, you know, in these worst of times, it often shows the best of humanity, and I can say I never get enough of these stories. We need them right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Same here, Gayle. Good morning once again, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Absolutely, there are some really great people out here.\par \par \uc2 One of them is Louis Deppe. He joins me right now.\par \par \uc2 He lives not far from this devastated area. He joined the search effort to help find a friend`s missing daughter.\par \par \uc2 He`s been at it for days.\par \par \uc2 Louis, good morning to you. Thanks for talking with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE (Search and rescue volunteer): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: How are you holding up? How are you doing? You`ve been here since Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Saturday morning. Yes. We`re doing good. We`re doing good that -- you know, we`re tired, but running off adrenaline, it`s for a good cause and trying to find people and bring some closure to those who may not be alive anymore, and so that`s kind of where we`re at in the search.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes, we`re all cheering for you, literally, across the country, around the world, as it turns out.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Take me to the beginning. You see a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post about a friend`s child who is missing.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Yes, I saw a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post of a friend of mine who is missing, a friend of mine since, really, all of our adult life. His wife posted and said, basically, we have an emergency. We need help. We need prayers.\par \par \uc2 And so I immediately called him, and he`s in Beaumont, their family, and I said, what`s going on? And he said, our daughter has been swept away. And I said, what can I do? I`ll be there. And so I jumped in, grabbed my son, 20 minutes later, and we headed over to Kerrville.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: How old is your son?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Twenty-one.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So you know what this is like to have a child that age?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Yes, actually, my son`s 25. I haven`t -- my youngest daughter is 21 and so there was definitely a connection.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You totally get it. So you get out here. You`re 45 minutes away. You get out here. How do you -- how do you proceed? What do you do?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: We -- I met up with him and said, what you need me to do? And he had a few friends, and we started basically right behind the property. The property was the house, it was swept off a hill into the river, so there`s a big pile of debris. And we started looking there for his daughter and the other three young, you know, people in their 20s, to see if we could find anybody.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Did you jump in the water? Were you able to do that. Did you form a grid? How did you go about this process?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Yes, we divided up into teams of like, two or three people, because if you go by a big pile of debris or tree tops, you -- one person can`t really see it. It`s so thick. They`re like a huge bird nest, and it took two or three to surround it. That`s how we divided it up, and just made sure we didn`t miss a -- we didn`t get in the water. We weren`t prepared to get in the water. We stayed out of the water. We stayed safe, but we tried to cover as much terrain as we could.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And what did you see, ultimately? Did you find your friend`s daughter?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: We did find her. She was found yesterday, mid-morning.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: She hadn`t made it. She hadn`t survived?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: She did not survive. But it`s tragic, but you know, in an odd important way, it was -- it brought some closure. And so my friend, Ty Badon found his daughter, Joyce Catherine Badon, and it was -- and one other of the four was found, but there are still two missing.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes. What about the spirit among you searchers? I mean, you don`t even know a number of the people you`re looking for, but you`re looking anyway. What`s that like with the folks you`re with?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Yes, I think a lot of it -- I didn`t really think about that. I thought about, look, this is my friend in need. His daughter is missing. I have a 21-year-old daughter. I can`t imagine -- I think every parent`s worst nightmare, and so I didn`t think it through, just muscle memory, let`s come help and see what`s needed.\par \par \uc2 And so you know the cause keeps you going, running off adrenaline, and you know, that`s where we`ve been the last three days.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: What do you want to tell the world about this community?\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: It`s a great community. You know, I grew up going to summer camp not too far from here, Camp Longhorn. And you know, I have so much respect for the people. The adults in charge of these little kids. I mean, many of these camps have been here a hundred years, and they save so many lives.\par \par \uc2 So many lives were saved. Many didn`t make it, but more and more were saved. And the fact that there`s so many people willing to give everything to try to find these kids, and now it`s probably turning into a different mission, but the spirit of Texas is alive and well and strong, and we`re going to keep looking to everyone is found.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And you`re going to press ahead today.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: Press ahead.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I thank you so much for talking with me this morning. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEPPE: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Louis Deppe, one of the many searchers out here, the volunteers doing heroes work.\par \par \uc2 We are going to hear more stories from survivors of the flash floods here in Central Texas in our next half hour. Until then, I am going to send it back to you in New York -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo to Mr. Deppe. You know anybody who is a parent, or even if you don`t have a parent -- even if you don`t have a child, you have a beating heart, you put yourself in that situation and hope that somebody would help you.\par \par \uc2 Maurice, thank you so much. Bravo to Mr. Deppe again.\par \par \uc2 If you would like to help, please go to CBSNews.com/HelpTexas, which is on your screen below, to get a list of organizations that are involved in this recovery. And of course, we`ll put it on our website, too.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to ask Jill Schlesinger about the President`s massive new tax cut and budget law. There`s a question, who`s going to benefit and who`s going to lose out? Jill knows.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Money Watch," we are talking about the winners and losers in President Trump`s so-called Big, Beautiful Bill now that he has signed it into law.\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, Jill Schlesinger is here with more.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so there is something for some and not the case for others. Outline the big picture for the BBB.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Okay, so the big picture is that the extension of the first Trump administration`s 2017 tax cuts are now made permanent. They were supposed to go away at the end of this year. That`s why you heard about, oh, there`s a big tax increase. It was actually part of that first Trump tax cut. So those extensions are now made permanent.\par \par \uc2 Now, in addition, there are some temporary benefits that are also included to pay for a good portion of this bill. There are huge cuts to Medicaid, which is the nation`s plan for health care for lower income Americans, about 71 million to 72 million of them, and also cuts to SNAP, that is food stamps.\par \par \uc2 So there is a clear winner and loser in that, in that if you pay taxes, a lot of your tax rates are going to be extended forward, but if you rely on the government, you could see big changes in your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There used to be a T.V. show back in the day called "The Biggest Loser," but now this seems to be a reality for some who really loses in this particular case.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: I think it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there are a lot of winners, too.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Oh, absolutely. But I think that if you look at the losers, because I like to kind of get the bad news out up front, which is, we have about the lower 20 percent of earners in the U.S., that`s making maybe less than $18,000.00.\par \par \uc2 Now, these folks don`t pay federal taxes in a big way, so having a tax cut doesn`t help them, but they do rely on benefits. These folks are expected, according to the non-partisan Penn Wharton Budget Model, they are expected to lose 2.9 percent of their income, which amounts to about almost 900 bucks. That`s a lot if you`re only making $18,000.00.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: So the lower end tends to be the big loser potentially in this bill.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What about middle income earners?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Now this is a weird thing, because there`s a nuance in this. Overall, according to Penn Budget Model, there won`t be a big change, but if you are somebody who relies on tips for income, you are happy. There is going to be a change. There won`t be as much of a tax levied on tips; if you rely on overtime.\par \par \uc2 If you are a homeowner in a high tax state like California, New York, New Jersey, those were states that had limitations on their State And Local Tax deduction, those deductions are actually going to be expanded, but these are temporary in nature. So I want to be clear about that. If you`re borrowing money to buy a car, you`re going to have a temporary reprieve. You`ll be able to write off some of the interest.\par \par \uc2 If you have children or dependents, we talked about this, some of those tax credits are made a little bit better, and there is this special credit if you have a baby born in the next few years, where you`re going to get a thousand bucks in an account.\par \par \uc2 So if you fall into one of those categories, great. But if you`re a middle class person and you don`t fall into one of those categories, you sort of like, well, what`s in it for me? And maybe not that much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Always on top of it, Jill Schlesinger, we appreciate you, and there`s a lot to unpack, so Jill will be back on our Plus hour with Tony, of course, that will be in a bit, so make sure you stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the stories that you`ll be talking about today and "What to Watch." That was supposed to be Tony`s read, but I`m a grab it here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Go for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up for you at eight o`clock, including Oprah`s latest Book Club Selection only on CBS MORNINGS, she`s going to reveal her pick, and we`ll hear from the author.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dexter: Resurrection"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, he`s back. Michael C. Hall once again, playing his best known role in the new series, "Dexter: Resurrection." Yep, he`s still a serial killer. And guess what? He`s still popular. He`s going to tell us what fans can expect.\par \par \uc2 So all of that`s coming up in our next hour, but right now, Tony and I are heading over to the table because Jo Ling Kent has arrived.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Good morning again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In for Vlad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got refrigerator wars going on at home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You came back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am filling them in. I`ll tell you in the commercial break. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Can`t take the heat?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Big Brother ain`t Little Brother`s lunch. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Vlad is still on vacation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Fire away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fire away, Jo Ling Kent, in for Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad is off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`re in good hands with Jo Ling.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Oh, thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You know, we had some great news in American tennis. Wimbledon, American Ben Shelton, Big Ben made it to the quarterfinals for the first time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Shelton loves swings. Another corner for Big Ben Shelton.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: That`s just something. I love the energy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: He got a big boost from his family, including his sister Emma, who snagged a few days off of work.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There we go.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: An additional from where? \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , after that viral public plea from her brother, and yesterday, Shelton thanked his dad for coaching him and listen to what he said about another sport that he loves.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: In college, you loved your American football and you were a quarterbacker. What are the comparisons? I hope I got this right. What are the comparisons between tennis and American football?\par \par \uc2 BEN SHELTON (Tennis player): I`m not sure many people in the crowd know what that is. I grew up playing quarterback or a quarterbacker, either way.\par \par \uc2 I`ve always loved athletics, playing a team sport, something bigger than yourself. Now, I`m obviously playing an individual sport, but I still have a team supporting me that I work with every day. So that`s the most important thing to me. I don`t want to be out here by myself. I want to be doing it with people that I love, and I`ve got a lot of people that I love over there\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, wonderful. Thank you very much. Into the quarter finals, Ben Shelton.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Googly eyes as she said it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Googly eyes on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about the girl in the blue, his girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Trinity Rodman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Right. Trinity Rodman, Washington, Spirit, number two star forward. His girlfriend, and she was in the stands there in the blue cheering him on throughout the tournament. So cute.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about a power couple.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: They are such a power couple. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Dennis Rodman`s daughter. That`s nice, but I love \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , say it again. Let his sister take the day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think she may be a good luck charm.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It sounds like there`s a few good luck charms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, he is talented.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Ultimately, he has got a big match with Sinner coming up. We`ll see how that goes, too. A big one.\par \par \uc2 We`re also thrilled to announce that Jane Pauley, our host of "CBS Sunday Morning" will receive this year`s \uc2 Poynter Institute\uc2  Medal for Lifetime Achievement in Journalism. The award recognizes her huge impact on the profession, especially as a pioneer for women in T.V. news.\par \par \uc2 Jane`s career has spanned 50 years, and it`s going strong, and she became the co-anchor of "The Today Show" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Today"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: -- in 1976. Get this -- just 25 years old.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: She has won several awards, many awards, over the years, and is a fierce advocate for mental health awareness.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you imagine having a big -- at 25?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congrats to her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, Jo Ling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And still going -- go, Jane.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Congrats, Jane.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dexter: Resurrection"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, actor, Michael C. Hall returns to his role as a serial killer in "Dexter: Resurrection." Now we all thought Dexter was dead, but he`s back, and he`ll join us in a bit.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, and we love when this happens, Oprah is going to reveal her new book club pick exclusively on CBS MORNINGS. That`s what we like, the exclusive part, and we`re going to hear from the author.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 A comedy fest in L.A. is focused on Asian-American culture, and it`s a very funny festival. Lisa Ling spoke to two of the headliners, Joel Kim Booster and the legendary, Margaret Cho.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Emmy nominee, Michael C. Hall is back playing "Dexter" in a brand new show, and he is killing it as always. He`ll be right here on the set.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see what you did there, but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Rescuers refuse to give up hope after the flooding disaster in Texas, as crews continue their search for the missing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Everyone would agree, in hindsight, if we could go back and do it again, we would evacuate, particularly those in the most vulnerable areas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: President Trump meets with Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu, but there`s still no ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: President Trump told reporters while he was sitting with Prime Minister Netanyahu that he still thinks there is a good chance that a ceasefire deal can be struck this week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The sanitation workers` strike in Philadelphia shows no sign of ending as trash piles up in the city streets.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s outrageous already. So if this thing go another two weeks or so, it`s going to be totally out of hand.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tourists and locals run for their lives as Spain kicks off the iconic Running of the Bulls Festival in Pamplona.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whoa. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Running of the Bulls. Never too late. Never too late.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Too late.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: 2026?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, thanks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You might fall and trip me up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well we do that series --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`re right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, we do that series, "Never Too Late." It seems perfect for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I don`t think so. I`m not that dumb.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No thanks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll leave that alone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, but we`re going to begin this hour taking a tough turn back to Texas. The stories of grief there, of course, but also stories of families who are experiencing immense relief after those catastrophic floods in Texas.\par \par \uc2 More than two dozen people lost their lives. Of course, we`ve been telling you at Camp Mystic, but somehow many children there also survived the raging floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Evening News" anchor, Maurice DuBois has more of those stories, and he joins us now from the state of Texas once again.\par \par \uc2 Maurice, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 All along the Guadalupe River, this -- this is what it`s like -- a seemingly endless trail of destruction.\par \par \uc2 We drove to Camp Mystic along back roads, we passed through ranches and passed more destroyed homes, but nothing, nothing prepared us for seeing what`s left of the camp.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: In the rubble that used to be Camp Mystic, we watched as Fletcher Cordell (sp?) searched for anything left behind by his 11-year-old daughter, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER CORDELL (Searching for daughter, Olivia): She had her charm bracelet laid out, and she`s been asking about that a lot.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Olivia`s cabin is now filled with mud and occasional dead fish as debris litters the campsite outside.\par \par \uc2 Is that hers?\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER CORDELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Olivia survived the deadly flooding, rescued by helicopter. Cordell says she is only starting to process the disaster.\par \par \uc2 But as a dad, it`s like anything for your child.\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER CORDELL: Whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that`s why you`re here.\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER CORDELL: I mean, yes. I`ll stay here as long as I can, till it`s, you know, found, and if I don`t find it, I`ll come back.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Just across the street, down the Guadalupe River, a different kind of search.\par \par \uc2 Loved ones will do anything to find their family members.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-seven-year-old, Alicia Olvera (sp?) used to live right here, up the hill. There didn`t used to be a river here, there is now. Family members are looking through the trees, through the branches, even going into the water.\par \par \uc2 That`s where we found, Olvera`s son, Efrain Torres (sp?) up to his beard. His sister, Juvia (sp?), on the road above.\par \par \uc2 JUVIA (Searching for mother): So we`re just hoping that she`s close by.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So you don`t think she made it? You don`t think she survived?\par \par \uc2 JUVIA: I mean, we want to think, but it was really hard.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The body of Olvera`s husband was found nearby.\par \par \uc2 JUVIA: It`s a really hard time for everybody.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Hard to process?\par \par \uc2 JUVIA: It`s hard to process, yes. We can`t process it. So, I don`t know how to describe it, something no one ever expected, like a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And no one can really process this quite frankly. The image of that young man in the water reaching down for his mom, that`s going to stay with me for a while, as well as so many other images here, you just can`t forget it.\par \par \