ar \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Go. Kick it off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m in pole position. My "Talk of the Table" is a man who is taking some heat for putting his dog ahead of his girlfriend. Let us explain.\par \par \uc2 A Reddit user, a Reddit user, you know they have that section: Am I being a you know what -- posted that before picking up his girlfriend at the airport, he let her know that his dog, Bonnie, would ride next to him. He says Bonnie gets very nervous in the back seat, and she makes a lot of noise.\par \par \uc2 It all seemed good until his girlfriend had to actually sit in the back seat and the dog Bonnie is sitting in the front seat. Now, the girlfriend, the man`s family, most have read it, disagree with his approach. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 I`m thinking it`s a 15-minute ride to the airport. Why don`t you just leave Bonnie at home?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think if there`s a longer term -- well, no, because Bonnie, you know, might be yelping at home, gets nervous being left alone. I think if you tell the girlfriend, look, I really love this dog, and we`re going to try to work through this issue, but this is a temporary thing. She`ll understand.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What am I missing here?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my God, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You want the dog to suffer?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Get your dog in the back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The dog is going to suffer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and let the dog figure it out. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Women like men --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the girlfriend is sitting -- Lauren, Lauren, I`m on your side, Lauren, the girlfriend. The man added -- the man says that his friend sided with Lauren because he thinks they`re a little jealous of our relationship. We don`t know how long he`s been with Lauren or how long he`s been with Bonnie, but I think that that could portend other things.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let me get this straightened out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no. What were you going to say? You`re saying women like men and then what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Women like men that love dogs, except when their love involves them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But -- but women like men who prioritize them over the dog.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Put the dog in the front seat?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So they don`t like women who love -- they don`t like men who love dogs in this particular case.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I would like a guy --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is an expression of love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I would like a guy that loves dogs and treats dogs very well. My ex did not, which I think is a sign.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, shots fired.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I think it is a sign somebody that doesn`t like a dog, I think that says something about you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I really do. But I don`t think that the dog is more important than me sitting in the back seat and the dog gets in the -- I really don`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So leave Bonnie at home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, it is why --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel that. I feel that. Unless you want to be sleeping on the couch, put your girlfriend in the front seat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Patti, what do you say?\par \par \uc2 PATTI: I agree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With what?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Patti agrees.\par \par \uc2 PATTI: You and Nate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No brainer to me, but all right. All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is a big change for flyers. So we hear that passengers at airports across the country will no longer have to remove their shoes at standard security check-ins.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So we had to go to the expert to confirm this.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave landed at LAX a few hours ago. So Kris, is it true?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It is, but it`s not everywhere, at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be a phased rollout that appears to have started yesterday at select airports. We can tell you, at LaGuardia and at LAX, we saw people going through standard security, most of them with their shoes still on, and we understand that several other airports, including BWI in the D.C. area, also has made this switch over with more to come in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kris, it looks like you ran from the airport to your hotel room. So, you okay?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: With my shoes on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, with your shoes on.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: I`m glad --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It`s just very early out here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I can tell. You can lay down on the bed as soon as we`re done.\par \par \uc2 So, why did they decide to make the change now? What happened?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, so this policy is one that gets reviewed every so often. So it sounds like the agency, the TSA, is going to let this policy expire.\par \par \uc2 Already, if you were a PreCheck flyer, you weren`t taking your shoes off, and at peak times when they would bring a dog through the line, people weren`t taking their shoes off even in general security, and there`s been an argument for a while that the body scanner technology that people go through, in general security is sufficient enough to detect threats. TSA is also developing additional scanners that are even better at sensing it.\par \par \uc2 This policy went into effect about 20 years ago after Richard Reid, the shoe bomber, tried to blow up an American Airlines plane with a bomb in his shoe. He was unsuccessful, but it brought up at the time a glaring weak point in security. TSA believes it`s been addressed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I hope they`ll do this with liquids. I hate throwing away --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I want --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- an unopened water bottle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. I want my 64-ounce Big Gulp as I go through security.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hate that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Long term that`s the goal, Gayle. Long term, that`s the goal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, I hope so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I want to keep my belt on and my jacket on. We await news on those fronts. Kris, thank you very much. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead and lie down on that bed. Lights out time for Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nighty night.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There he goes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- is going to be our next guest, actor, Michael C. Hall, who is returning as one of T.V.`s most famous and beloved, if you can say that -- serial killers --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, look at him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- in "Dexter: Resurrection." But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Actor, Michael C. Hall is returning as serial killer, Dexter Morgan in the highly anticipated series "Dexter: Resurrection."\par \par \uc2 Set in New York City, the story picks up right where the 2021 miniseries left off. Spoiler alert, 10 weeks after being shot by his own son, Dexter wakes from a coma.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dexter: Resurrection"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In this preview, Dexter talks to the ghost of his adoptive father as he tracks down a new serial killer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dexter: Resurrection"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the man who will make your soul shiver, the incredibly talented Michael C. Hall joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL ("Dexter: Resurrection" actor): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so in the previous mini-series, "Dexter: New Blood," your character was shot, as we just mentioned, and presumed dead. How did he come back to life?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, he looked really dead. There was a lot of blood.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was gone.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: He is a very resilient guy. He`s like a cat. But, you know, it was very cold on that forest floor. Maybe the sun didn`t hit him as directly as Dexter thought he had, or as we thought he had, but yes, I mean, he definitely was very compromised, went into a coma, but he woke up.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this all stemmed from a conversation that I had with a producing director on the show, remembering how wonderful it was to shoot the show, and just out of my mouth popped, "What if he didn`t die? Is that crazy?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What was their response?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: He`s got a story to tell.\par \par \uc2 Marco Siega, he said, "Call Clyde Phillips. Ask him." Our show runner, and it started a conversation. We all became increasingly compelled by the idea that if he didn`t die, that this would have afforded him a second chance at life, and maybe will allow him to move forward, and I think does in a way that`s unburdened by the past.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: He doesn`t forget his past, but he`s not weighed down by it in the same way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But why do I struggle with liking this guy because --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Because he kills people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because he kills people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But ethically. He`s a code -- there`s a code.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because he is he is a serial killer. You`re right, Tony, he`s got a code.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A code of ethics, if you will.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there`s a line and it says, "When you kill other killers, future innocent victims are saved." So I like that part.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there is a part where he is torturing people before he kills them. Why am -- I am sorry. All morning long, we`ve been saying the popular serial killer?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always seem to trip up the words when I`m saying that. What is it about him?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes, it is crazy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is crazy. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: I mean, when we started with the idea of doing this pilot episode, and I would tell people about it, they would cock their head and say, well, good luck with that. I don`t think that`s going to work. I am not going to watch that. Who`s going to like that?\par \par \uc2 But somehow, the show strikes a tone that gives people permission to identify with this dark guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a great way to put it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Because he is taking unique responsibility for his darkness. He is targeting people that arguably deserve it. I mean, it`s all very morally gray at best, because he is killing people, but, yes, he is killing killers and maybe saving more lives than he is ending.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re also an executive producer on this series, and you and the team have managed to bring in all sorts of amazing guest stars. Uma Thurman --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got Neil Patrick Harris, and the list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes, Peter Dinklage, Krysten Ritter, David Dastmalchian, Eric Stonestreet -- it`s so gratifying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, you`re a name dropper.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes, no, I am dropping names. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But to Tony`s point, though, Tony, they wouldn`t come if they didn`t think it was good or --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, it seems like they all want -- they all want to dabble in their shadow self.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes, that`s right. I mean, it`s gratifying for us, certainly that we`re able to attract people of this caliber.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: But I do think a part of the appeal is people jump at the chance to, yes, play characters who are --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Dark people, people like being called upon to mind their shadow side.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: The side of themselves that in the world they try to suppress, thankfully. But yes, it`s a delicious invitation for some actors.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Michael, I have a suggestion for your show. Did you guys know that he sings? He`s in a band.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did know that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the name of your band? I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Princess Goes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Princess Goes -- and word on the street is that you have a very good voice. Do you envision a storyline where we see Dexter at some point singing? People would love to see that. I think that could be a nice twist. You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Executive producer, Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: I will take that under advisement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Executive producer, Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Princes Goes performance)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: I mean, I don`t know. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if it`s quite in sync with how he thinks of himself. But, you know, I`ve learned to never say never with Dexter, so he could be forced into a situation where he has to do it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It could be a new spin off. Yes, it could be "Dexter: He Sings Too."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But I love the father-son dynamic. Your son, Harrison, is now picking up dad`s business.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you`re also talking to your dead father. The father- son dynamic is very crucial in this series to me.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Yes, Dexter has this internalized father who has re- emerged in this series, with whom he has an ongoing conversation, and that informs his own relationship to his own son.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: His own son is this flesh and blood evidence that Dexter is, in fact, in spite of what he might believe about himself, a human being, and his connection to his humanity, his connection to light, you know, and he`s drawn to that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s very layered and you are very good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, this show connects very fan base.\par \par \uc2 Michael C. Hall, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Michael C -- C stands for what? C stands for what?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL C. HALL: Carlyle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Carlyle.\par \par \uc2 "Dexter: Resurrection" premiers with two episodes this Friday on Paramount+ with Showtime, part of our parent company, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 . We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Just for Laughs")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That of course, is Margaret Cho, a comedian considered by many to be the Godmother of Asian American comedy. She is among the headliners at this weekend`s Belly Laughs Festival, alongside Kumail Nanjiani, Bobby Lee and Hasan Minhaj.\par \par \uc2 The Los Angeles festival is a celebration of Asian culture, and Lisa Ling spoke to Cho and another performer, Joel Kim Booster.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN (Chili co-owner): Hello. My name is Kevin.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Hi, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN: The co-owner of Chili.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: This looks incredible.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN: To start your meal.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And you`re going to be part of Belly Laughs, right?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: What are we having today?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN: So right over here, we have our honey butter chips. We have our tteokbokki, which is a deep fried rice cake, little bit of sweet chili sauce, parmesan cheese and this is our shrimp toast.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: I`ve interviewed a lot of people, from heads of state to drug dealers, but I`ve always struggled with comedians because I am so not funny. So I want to ask you first, like, when did you know you were funny?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO (Comedian): I was really bullied because I`m queer, I am weird, traumatized child. I had very vivid dreams about performing as a comedian, as a child that are really so much like it is to do what I do now.\par \par \uc2 So, I kind of always knew that I would do this.\par \par \uc2 Just seeing Joan Rivers --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Joan Rivers performance)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: I thought, wow, she seems like she has friends and I wanted that so badly.\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER (Comedian): I was also really bullied growing up because I was gay.\par \par \uc2 For a lot of gay people, I think we take the other route, which is be the funny person and sort of turn it around.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Conan"/TBS)\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: When you could make those people laugh, it really did sort of lessen the blows a little bit.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Joel, you were adopted.\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And did you ever have Korean food?\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: Not a ton. I did not meet another Asian person my age until I was 13 years old.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Joel Kim Booster performance)\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: You were the -- probably the most prominent Asian face I saw growing up like regularly, and so -- and it`s so wild to just be sitting here at this table with both of you, because I think you -- both of you, were sort of like instrumental in helping me, you know, learn to love this part of myself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It occurred to me today that your show, "All American Girl" came out 30 years ago.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "All American Girl"/ABC)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And now you`re headlining this incredible festival, "Belly Laughs" --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: -- with this phenomenal slate of Asian comics. How does that make you feel?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: It`s so exciting. You know, I think it`s a really wonderful opportunity for us to also see each other, because we`re always working.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Could you ever have imagined being on this roster of talent?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: No, it`s really incredible and I love it because I definitely, completely take credit for all of it like --\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: -- I really revel in it. So much respect and love there. It`s pretty profound.\par \par \uc2 My first performances of like doing shows when I didn`t have a head shot, so I went to the club and they had put an illustration of a Chinese man from the railroad eating rice with chopsticks. You know, from that to what we have now, where we have this incredible festival with all of these amazing, very different and really unique and exciting comedians coming together --\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: All of whom are selling out venues in their own right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: All over the world.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Which is was also absolutely incredible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: All over the world.\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: "Belly Laughs" is such a great opportunity to finally just be on the same show, but also put to bed that idea that our material is identical, because you`re going to see, if you come to this festival, you know, scores of Asian comics. I`m, like, deeply excited to have that freedom for the first time.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Who should come to "Belly Laughs"?\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: People who like good comedy and like good food.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: It`s as simple as that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: Everyone, everyone. I mean, anybody who wants to have a good time out, anybody who wants to laugh and wants to eat, which is everyone.\par \par \uc2 I remember growing up as, you know, Korean child and having squid in my lunch box, and this being such a very shameful thing. And so going from like food shame to food pride, it`s really -- it`s really remarkable. The shrimp toast is my favorite.\par \par \uc2 JOEL KIM BOOSTER: No, the shrimp toast is my favorite. You know like --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET CHO: It is very good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like good food and like to laugh, sign me up. That`ll do it for us. Good night, Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250708el7800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For July 3, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Debora Patta, Nikole Killion, Kelly O`Grady, Caitlin Huey-Burns,  Charlie D`Agata, John Elliott, Nancy Chen, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): The yeas are 218, the nays are 214.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON: The motion is adopted.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A huge victory for the president. The House gives final approval to his massive spending and tax cut bill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CONGRESS: USA! USA! USA!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . Maurice DuBois is off.\par \par \uc2 President Trump will have more to celebrate tomorrow than America`s birthday. He will sign the signature piece of domestic legislation of his second term, a massive spending package that will extend the tax cuts of his first term, but make big cuts in social programs to help pay for them. Congress passed the bill, barely, and despite opposition and threats from the president`s sometimes friend and adviser, sometimes not, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Musk said the package adds to the deficit. It took a tiebreaking vote by the vice president to get it through the Senate. Today, it passed the House 218-214, as two Republicans joined all the Democrats in voting no.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is in Washington, but we begin with Caitlin Huey-Burns in Des Moines, where the president is holding a rally and claiming the bill will make the economy take off like a rocket ship -- Caitlin.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He signs the Big Beautiful Bill. How about giving the money...\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: John, President Trump will begin selling that bill here in Iowa tonight.\par \par \uc2 And, as the House was going through that marathon voting session, we found people here in Iowa watching it very closely too, anxious to know what it might mean for them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voice-over): As the bill passed in Washington...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): The motion is adopted.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: ... Ben Smith in Iowa, owner of Smitty`s Tenderloins, breathed a sigh of relief. The restaurant has been in Smith`s family for generations. He says the bill`s provision eliminating federal taxes on tips could help his industry.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean for you as a restaurant owner, no tax on tips for your employees?\par \par \uc2 BEN SMITH (Owner, Smitty`s Tenderloins): I look at it as a good opportunity to -- just to see our employees see the fruits of their labor and their hard work.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Deanna Mutchler (sp?) has been working at Smitty`s for nearly four decades.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA MUTCHLER (Employee): We don`t expect a tip. Well, I don`t. But it is -- it will benefit our future waitresses a lot.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The 900-page bill also eliminates federal taxes on overtime pay, extends Trump`s 2017 tax cuts, provides increased spending on border security and defense, and gives businesses tax deductions for new equipment.\par \par \uc2 But the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimates the bill will add $3 trillion to the deficit. To pay for it, lawmakers made deep cuts to some social programs, including nearly a trillion dollars from Medicaid, which could leave nearly 12 million uninsured, something that worries customer Janie Nagel (sp?), who says her cousin relies on the program.\par \par \uc2 JANIE NAGEL (Customer): I`m concerned about her not having the medical care that she needs, that there are so many people. How do we know who gets cut? And I feel -- I feel bad that they`re taking that away.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The legislation also impacts the renewable energy industry and the electric vehicle market.\par \par \uc2 Here`s my colleague Nikole Killion with that.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): Rob Smith has been in the car business for more than 35 years. He manages dealerships in Florida, Pennsylvania and Maryland, where electric vehicles have become a growing part of his business.\par \par \uc2 How many electric vehicles do you have on this lot?\par \par \uc2 ROB SMITH (Car Dealership Manager): On this particular lot, we have got around 100, 110 right at the moment.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But he`s considering scaling back on his inventory. That`s because, come September 30, consumer tax breaks on electric vehicles are expected to end under the newly passed Republican megabill. That includes credits of up to $7,500 towards the purchase of new vehicles and $4,000 for those that are used. The incentives help boost sales.\par \par \uc2 ROB SMITH: Business is very good in the electric vehicle market, yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Are you concerned that that could change if this E.V. tax credit is rolled back?\par \par \uc2 ROB SMITH: Absolutely. People need to know how much things are going to cost, and they want predictability for their -- planning for their family budget.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The issue drove a wedge between President Trump and Tesla`s \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Elon`s very upset that the E.V. mandate is going to be terminated.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But the legislation makes good on the president`s campaign pledge to roll back the Biden era credit and other clean energy provisions like wind and solar credits passed under the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 NICK NIGRO (Atlas Public Policy): We`re in a battle for the future of what we like to call the clean economy.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Nick Nigro with Atlas Public Policy says the move to end the credit and other renewable energy subsidies could be a blow to U.S. automakers already struggling to catch up to China.\par \par \uc2 NICK NIGRO: Fifty percent of all new vehicles purchased in China are electric vehicles. They dominate the solar industry.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. was really trying to play catchup in the last few years. But with this -- enactment of this law, we introduce even more uncertainty in what the future of the clean economy looks like for the U.S. and therefore manufacturing industry and lots and lots of jobs.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Nikole, the president`s sales job on this will start when he signs it or continue when he signs it. So when will that be?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, the president asked Congress to deliver this Big Beautiful Bill to his desk by July 4. And so that`s when he`s going to get it and that`s when he`s going to sign it in a ceremony tomorrow afternoon.\par \par \uc2 A short time ago, he told reporters he made no deals to win over lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Nikole Killion on Capitol Hill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 Seven people are missing still two days after a fireworks warehouse in \uc2 Yolo County\uc2 , California, exploded in flames. The risk of more explosions has slowed the search. The cause of the initial blast is under investigation. The fire has burned nearly 80 acres.\par \par \uc2 The monthly jobs report today is better than expected.\par \par \uc2 Money Watch correspondent Kelly O`Grady breaks down the numbers.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: The economy added 147,000 jobs in June. So let`s look at who was hiring. Half of the jobs were created by state and local governments, primarily in education. That`s the green line here on the bottom, which is growing. At the same time, there`s been a slowdown in jobs in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 So that means, without the government hiring, this snapshot of the labor market would look less positive. Then there`s the unemployment rate, which ticked down to 4.1 percent. But that`s partly because more people stopped looking for work. Overall, today`s report is strong, but economists are asking if this growth is sustainable.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The Florida attorney general`s office says the first group of migrant detainees has arrived at a new detention facility in the Everglades. And the Supreme Court has cleared the way for a group of migrants with criminal records to be deported to South Sudan.\par \par \uc2 The Gaza Health Ministry run by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  for reports nearly 100 Palestinians were killed over the past day by Israeli airstrikes and shootings. Nearly half were killed on their way to get food from an American-backed aid organization.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta in East Jerusalem has more about the risks Palestinians are taking to keep from starving.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): This was the scene at a Gaza Humanitarian Foundation aid site filmed last month by an American security contractor working there and given to our partner the BBC, thousands of Palestinians herded into a long fenced-off corridor and forced to battle it out for food amid bullets and stun grenades.\par \par \uc2 The contractor explained there was machine gunfire from a watch tower and shots from a nearby security guard.\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Then the unmistakable sound...\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: I think you hit one.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: ... of American accents.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Hell yeah, boy!\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: The contractor spoke on condition his identity was concealed.\par \par \uc2 CONTRACTOR: When this guy was shooting into this group, this man dropped to the ground and was motionless.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: GHF said the person heard shouting in the video had been removed from the site, but did not give any other details. The contractor, however, said he brought the incident to the attention of his superiors.\par \par \uc2 CONTRACTOR: And then the leadership said to me: "Well, you have no way of knowing that person was hit with a bullet. Maybe they tripped. Maybe they were tired and they passed out."\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: The U.S. has approved a direct grant of $30 million for GHF, but the group has not disclosed the rest of its funding.\par \par \uc2 We asked director Johnnie Moore earlier this week about reports of more than 600 people killed trying to access aid at their sites.\par \par \uc2 REV. JOHNNIE MOORE (Executive Chairman, Gaza Humanitarian Foundation): And yet, every single day, we wake up to -- in the West, or our team is operating simultaneously in the Middle East, to this piece of disinformation that is just without any scrutiny whatsoever, while we`re facing all the scrutiny in the world.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: But the contractors said they were given explicit rules of engagement.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Deb, aid organizations that know how to distribute aid say that the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation is not doing it correctly. What are their specific complaints?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, John, GHF only has four aid sites. There used to be 400. And this forces aid seekers into a narrow choke hold to collect food. They walk long distances, often returning empty-handed.\par \par \uc2 And other groups like \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  sent registered recipients a text message to say they will be distributing in their area. Critics also say GHF has weaponized food and it`s used as a bargaining chip essentially to force mass displacement, as the sites are all towards the south of Gaza, with none in the north, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Debora Patta reporting from East Jerusalem, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, hot summer weather is driving the biggest wildfire California has seen this year.\par \par \uc2 And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I`m Charlie D`Agata at the Pentagon. The Army is in the field creating a new way of fighting with drones. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I`m Nancy Chen in Assateague Island National Seashore, where millions visit every year and there are no lifeguards this summer -- why locals are blaming federal policy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : This wildfire in California started yesterday and has already grown to 55 square miles, the Madre Fire in San Luis Obispo County.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist John Elliott of our New York flagship station tells us that hot, dry conditions and strong winds are feeding it -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ELLIOTT: I got to tell you John, the weather is not going to help with the firefight.\par \par \uc2 Now, California technically not under a red flag fire warning. Much of Nevada, parts of Arizona, Utah, to the north, they are under that warning. And in the West, it`s just hot and dry with those offshore winds. Where we don`t need the rain, West Texas, already four inches, we`re going to see more, storms Houston up to Dallas.\par \par \uc2 In the Western Lakes, it`s all about the heat and humidity combining to make it feel like 100-plus. In New England and parts of the mid-Atlantic, we have got watches, we have got warnings. Look at this new video from Binghamton, these massive old-growth trees knocked down by hurricane-forest winds with these collapsing storms, a lot of hail with these severe storms as well.\par \par \uc2 Sets it up for a nice weekend, but very tricky tonight. Florida, more rain for you, and the concern is development of a tropical system on the Atlantic side. A lot of the water stays out to sea, but dangerous waves early next week. Stay safe and have a great Fourth -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : You too, John.\par \par \uc2 John Elliott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Among the most popular destinations this holiday weekend are national parks. More Americans than ever are visiting them, but there are fewer rangers, lifeguards and other workers to help them. The Trump administration has cut about 1,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 And, as Nancy Chen reports, critics say that could make parks less safe.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voice-over): The shoreline at Maryland`s Assateague Island is a familiar place for surfer Zack Tyndall. A former firefighter paramedic, Tyndall often helped beachgoers here. Now, as the mayor of nearby Berlin, he says safety isn`t guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 What are you most worried about?\par \par \uc2 ZACK TYNDALL (Mayor of Berlin, Maryland): I don`t want to see anybody drown. We had saves where we were able to bring people out and we had some where we weren`t able to.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Each year, millions visit Assateague Island National Seashore, which is managed by the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 . But, this summer, families will also see this, shuttered lifeguard towers and signs warning no lifeguards are on duty.\par \par \uc2 We asked the Park Service about staffing, but the agency did not directly respond to our questions, instead saying lifeguard shortages are a nationwide concern many communities are facing. According to a new analysis by the \uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2 , permanent staffing is down 24 percent systemwide since President Trump took office, and less than half of seasonal worker positions, including lifeguards, have been filled.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD STIERLI\uc2  (\uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2 ): This was a policy put in a place by DOGE and the current Trump administration that led to a complete hiring freeze on all hiring across the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Ed Stierli is with the \uc2 National Parks Conservation Association\uc2 , which did the analysis.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD STIERLI\uc2 : The reason there are no lifeguards at Assateague is, honestly, a symptom of the chaos and dysfunction that has been impacting this agency since the beginning of this administration.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A Trump administration official told us all fire, law enforcement and public-facing park personnel are prioritized to remain in place.\par \par \uc2 Back in Maryland, though, Zack Tyndall is just hoping for a safe summer.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ZACK TYNDALL: Every day that we don`t have a lifeguard here on the beach is putting every one of these swimmers, every one of our local residents, everybody that comes to visit Assateague Island in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It could get worse with next year`s proposed budget, which calls for a cut of more than $1 billion to the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 , the largest in its history.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Nancy Chen on the Maryland shore.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next with U.S. forces training for drone warfare.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : President Zelenskyy announced today that Ukraine has signed a deal with the American company Swift Beat to jointly produce drones for the war with Russia. Just yesterday, President Trump put a pause on delivery of some weapons to Ukraine because U.S. stockpiles are dwindling.\par \par \uc2 As drones become a key part of America`s arsenal, we decided to give you another look at Charlie D`Agata`s report on how U.S. forces are training to use them in tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): Deep in the backwoods of Louisiana, an experimental unit of 101st Airborne soldiers is setting out...\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Any strike element, this is Chaos 46.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: ... with cutting-edge technology.\par \par \uc2 Sergeant 1st Class Anthony Clark says his unit is exploring a new way of fighting.\par \par \uc2 How much of this are you doing on the fly?\par \par \uc2 SGT. 1ST CLASS ANTHONY CLARK (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): Almost all of it. But it`s good. That`s why we`re here. We`re to create those tactics, we`re to create those standard operating procedures, so that the next generation of soldiers understands from our mistakes and can be even more effective with the systems.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Those systems include both surveillance drones and attack drones, decoys and jammers, directed by laptops or a cell phone app.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Six one six, go (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Many of the innovations come directly from the revolution in drone warfare now taking place in Ukraine. Just last month, Ukrainian operatives smuggled more than 100 drones into Russia, launching them from containers and destroying an estimated one-third of the Kremlin`s long- range bombing fleet.\par \par \uc2 It was a bold attack, offering lessons for soldiers in the 101st Airborne, transforming their own combat operations. The command post, well hidden in the trees, is minuscule, a fraction of the size Major General Brett Sylvia commanded 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. BRETT SYLVIA (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): My command post was 10 different tents, 40 different vehicles. It was easy to see and therefore easy to kill.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The art of deception in this exercise against an opposition force considered the enemy goes beyond face paint and camouflage. It`s running fiber-optic cables to antennae hundreds of yards away from the command center, then spreading out decoys, off-the-shelf circuit boards made to look like laptops, cell phones.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. BRETT SYLVIA: Just the other night, the enemy was completely deceived by that and put an artillery barrage on just a bunch of decoys that were out in a field, and then this command post stayed safe and sound.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I think you said it wasn`t like hiding a needle in a haystack. It was...\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. BRETT SYLVIA: Hiding a needle in a stack of needles.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: They`re also experimenting with robotic vehicles controlled by Lieutenant Parker Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 Is this the future?\par \par \uc2 LT. PARKER MITCHELL (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): Yes, sir, it absolutely is.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And why?\par \par \uc2 LT. PARKER MITCHELL: Why? Because there`s no reason to trade blood for blood, when we can trade blood for steel.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Putting that steel to work on future battlefields, Specialist William Bateman says, will not be without its challenges and rewards.\par \par \uc2 SPC. WILLIAM BATEMAN (\uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): Right now, we`re in the middle of molding the future and participating in history being made. It`s absolutely stunning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And not your grandfather`s 101st.\par \par \uc2 SPC. WILLIAM BATEMAN: No, definitely not.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Now a new generation is putting innovation to the test on the battlefields of the future.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Charlie D`Agata at Fort Johnson, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We will be back with a semiquincentennial minute.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Tomorrow, the United States of America begins the semiquincentennial.\par \par \uc2 Breaking down the Latin and the math, quincentennial means 500th anniversary, which it is not. Semi is half. Half of 500 is 250, which it is. America`s 250th year of independence starts on the Fourth of July, something to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 But, sadly, a Gallup poll finds only 58 percent of adults are very or extremely proud to be Americans, a record low and down from 90 percent at the start of this century. It`s been a quarter-of-a-millennium since the founders declared this land free, and with great pride began making the country what it was.\par \par \uc2 They left it to future generations to use that freedom to make America what it will be, something that would make us and them proud.\par \par \uc2 And that is the CBS Evening News for tonight. For Maurice DuBois, I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The news continues on Evening News Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7. See you there soon.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250704el7300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For June 10, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed O`Keefe, Mark Strassmann, Lonnie Quinn, Jon LaPook, Maurice  DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The Marines move into Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (U.S. Defense Secretary): I think we`re entering even another phase, especially under President Trump, with his focus on the homeland, where the National Guard and Reserves become a critical component of how we secure that homeland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : There are now thousands of National Guard troops and Marines in L.A.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: No ICE in our state!\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And a few dozen protesters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This is what it`s like on the dividing line in downtown L.A.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . Maurice DuBois is in downtown Los Angeles, where 700 Marines are now joining more than 4,000 California National Guard troops. They were all ordered in by President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And his defense secretary told Congress today we`re likely to see more of this around the country, the commander in chief using the military in roles usually performed by civilian police forces.\par \par \uc2 The president has made no secret of his admiration for the military. At Fort Bragg today, he watched a demonstration of weaponry in commemoration of the Army`s 250th birthday. And he will be reviewing a massive military parade in Washington Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The forces on the streets of L.A. tonight far outnumber the demonstrators protesting the arrests of undocumented immigrants. The cost of the deployment, it`s estimated at $134 million. California asked a federal court today to stop it.\par \par \uc2 And Maurice has the latest.\par \par \uc2 Maurice, you`re outside the detention center there which has gotten a lot of the activity. What`s happening where you are right now?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Let them go! Let them go! Let them go!\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Well, John, so striking how quickly things can change here on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we`re about 300 yards away from the federal detention center. Officers are here, the protesters on this side. But that`s the detention center all the way down the street. You can see that heavy police presence. The reason, some people decided to get rowdy after a day of peaceful protests, and the police just weren`t having it.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Let them go! Let them go! Let them go!\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS (voice-over): As a bus believed to be carrying detainees arrived at L.A.`s federal detention center, protesters blocking the entrance were met by pepper balls fired to push them back.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It was a brief moment of tension in an otherwise calm day.\par \par \uc2 This is what it`s like on the dividing line in downtown L.A., peaceful protesters here, part of hundreds, a group of hundreds of people in downtown. And, right here, we have National Guard troops standing at the ready.\par \par \uc2 Most protesters tell us they`re not looking for trouble. Among those we spoke to, Enrico Green (sp?), a Marine combat veteran who served in Iraq and Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 People across the country look at this and say, L.A. is burning, we need Trump to send in the military, send in the National Guard, the Marines, whoever, to get it under control. You say what?\par \par \uc2 ENRICO GREEN (Protester): I say that L.A. is not burning. L.A. is protesting. L.A. is standing up for our neighbors. And L.A. is showing solidarity.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Today, crews attempted to clean up from the latest round of protests, which last night started peacefully...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: There will be a reckoning.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: ... and ended turbulently.\par \par \uc2 While scenes like this are being broadcast prominently around the world, here`s some context. It`s all happening in a small part of downtown L.A. at and around City Hall and the federal building, where detainees from ICE raids have been taken, just a few blocks in a county of nearly 10 million people.\par \par \uc2 As for the skirmishes, police and public officials here say they have it under control. But federal reinforcements keep coming, 700 U.S. Marines to join more than 4,000 National Guard troops.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles Mayor \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  tells us the extra help is not welcome.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to the National Guard, you say they`re underutilized or just...\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS\uc2  (D-Mayor of Los Angeles, California): Well, I`m saying...\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: ... basically not working.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KAREN BASS: I`m saying there`s nothing for them to do. They are guarding a building. OK, that is fine. But why would you bring in more troops, when even the ones that are here have nothing to do?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, Maurice, compare yesterday, if you would, to what`s happening today.\par \par \uc2 And, also, who is doing the actual arresting?\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes, the arresting seems to be being done by the \uc2 LAPD\uc2 . This thing seems to be taking on a rhythm. The day builds slowly, and then things get more rowdy as the night goes on.\par \par \uc2 Police seem to have found their rhythm with that when it comes to pushing people back. And so the difference yesterday, people were near the federal buildings. Today, they`re a good several hundred yards away. So we will see what the night brings. People here aren`t exactly happy.\par \par \uc2 And there are rabble-rousers, as you know, who also joined the crowd. And we will see -- we will see what happens, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All right, Maurice, we will be back to you later in the broadcast. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on the president`s plan to use the U.S. military to protect the homeland, we go to Ed O`Keefe at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): And we have...\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice-over): President Trump warned today the unprecedented deployment of National Guard and Marines in Southern California to quell protests is just the start.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I can inform the rest of the country that, when they do it, if they do it, they`re going to be met with equal or greater force than we met right here.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The president specifically called out people who might protest the first-of-its-kind military parade planned for Saturday in Washington. Preparations are under way for the celebration of the Army`s 250th anniversary that coincides with Trump`s 79th birthday.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And for those people that want to protest, they`re going to be met with very big force.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The warnings come as Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  and other top Pentagon officials told lawmakers today the Guardsmen and Marines in Los Angeles are trained to control crowds and armed.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (U.S. Defense Secretary): Commanders and troops on the field are very well prepared, sir.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK HAGEL\uc2  (Former U.S. Defense Secretary): This situation...\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: \uc2 Chuck Hagel\uc2  is an army veteran and former Republican senator who served as President Obama`s defense secretary.\par \par \uc2 What would be your biggest concern then about having National Guard and Marines deployed in large American cities?\par \par \uc2 FORMER SECRETARY CHUCK HAGEL: Loss of life like we saw in 1970.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But 1970 at Kent State.\par \par \uc2 FORMER SECRETARY CHUCK HAGEL: At Kent State, when the National Guard opened up on students and killed students, partly because they weren`t trained. They panicked.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: When they hear the president say, if there are protests, I`m going to send troops into other cities or to other places, how would officers likely be reacting when they hear that?\par \par \uc2 FORMER SECRETARY CHUCK HAGEL: The concern that our top leadership will have if this continues is, am I going to get to the point where I may have to refuse a direct order to send in or give troops authority to do whatever?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And I would think even contemplating defying an order from the commander in chief is a nightmare scenario for most officers.\par \par \uc2 FORMER SECRETARY CHUCK HAGEL: Sure. Contemplating a scenario like that is a career-ending decision.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Ed O`Keefe joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, the president spoke at Fort Bragg today. What did he say, if anything, about what`s happening in L.A.?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, it was quite a scene, John, to see the commander in chief speaking in front of troops giving such a politically charged address about what`s going on in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 He called protesters -- quote -- "animals." And when he referenced Los Angeles Mayor \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  and California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , he called them incompetent. The crowd, a mix of civilians and uniformed personnel, booed at mentions of the mayor and the governor. Quite a scene at the nation`s largest Army base.\par \par \uc2 We don`t normally see the commander in chief give such political remarks in front of the troops -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 Ten people were killed in a shooting today at a high school in Austria. Officials say the 21-year-old gunman, a former student at the school, took his life. Austrian public media reports that its country has had four school shootings in the past 32 years.\par \par \uc2 British scientists warn that emperor penguin populations may be dwindling near the South Pole. Since 2009, as climate change has caused Antarctica sea ice to shrink, the estimated population of 16 penguin colonies has declined by more than 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  downgraded its forecast for the U.S. economy. Instead of growing 2.3 percent, as the bank predicted in January, the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  now predicts America`s economy to grow by 1.4 percent this year because of the president`s trade war.\par \par \uc2 In a recent CBS News poll, two out of three Americans told us they`re stressed about their own finances. Three out of four said their incomes are not keeping up with inflation. That`s a lot of people struggling to make ends meet. And starting tonight in our series The Cost of Living, you will hear some of their stories in their own words.\par \par \uc2 First, a single mother of three, ages 1, 4, and 8, trying to make ends meet on a teacher`s salary of $37,000.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BOISJOLI (Teacher): Sweet. We`re ready to rock.\par \par \uc2 My name is Michelle Boisjoli. I am 37 years old. I am a teacher in St. Louis County, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Since the price of eggs and bacon has gone up, we have done a lot more cereal.\par \par \uc2 Eat it with your spoon.\par \par \uc2 You got to stay safe in the car, right?\par \par \uc2 I have had to take on a second job just because everything has gotten so expensive.\par \par \uc2 Hi, baby girl.\par \par \uc2 I have been a teacher since about 2008.\par \par \uc2 (singing): I love my brown shoes. I love my brown shoes.\par \par \uc2 It`s definitely not something that people go into for the money. They go into it because they love it, which is why so many of us do have to work two jobs to make ends meet. It takes multiple incomes to survive in this economy.\par \par \uc2 I just left work, and now I am going to go DoorDashing for a couple hours. It looks like our first delivery, we have to go to \uc2 Burger King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So this first one is $4.50 guaranteed, and then sometimes we get tips.\par \par \uc2 I always grew up thinking about the stereotypical American dream, where you own a house and you have a yard to play in. And I think that dream is dying. I try to make every dollar count. A lot of the time, I will just wait to feed myself until the end and then just eat whatever leftovers the kids haven`t touched.\par \par \uc2 Put your cute little bib on.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the people making the decisions for us are wealthy, don`t know what it`s like to work two jobs, don`t know what it`s like to have to pay for gas with quarters, and yet they`re making the decisions for us.\par \par \uc2 If they knew a little bit about the average person who is fighting every day to make ends meet, I think that maybe they would make decisions that were actually helpful for the average person.\par \par \uc2 Good night, sister. Come here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Tomorrow night, you will meet a rookie Connecticut police officer and his wife struggling to find an affordable first house.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, more from Maurice in Los Angeles, and Lonnie Quinn with a stormy weather forecast in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 And we will also have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m Mark Strassmann near Atlanta. Most women behind bars were also victims of domestic violence. Georgia is finally ready to hear their horror stories in court. And that is tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The scientists who recommend what vaccine should protect you and your family have all been fired. I`m Dr. Jon LaPook in New York.\par \par \uc2 After the break, I will explain why.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is dismissing the 17-member panel of experts that advises the CDC on vaccine use. He says they have conflicts of interest. And he`s doing this to, as he put it, reestablish public confidence in vaccine science.\par \par \uc2 Our Dr. Jon LaPook spoke today with a former head of the CDC.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 THOMAS FRIEDEN\uc2  (Former Director, \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 ): Vaccines are one of the greatest accomplishments of the past century and now they are at risk.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK (voice-over): When Dr. \uc2 Tom Frieden\uc2  was director of the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  from 2009 to 2017, he received guidance from the \uc2 Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices\uc2 , or ACIP, a group of scientists who guide the CDC on vaccine policy.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 THOMAS FRIEDEN\uc2 : It`s one of the most respected institutions we have had.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: After announcing yesterday that he`s firing all 17 committee members, late this afternoon, the \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary gave his vision for a new vaccine committee.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR. (U.S. Health and Human Services Secretary): And we`re going to bring people onto the ACIP panel, not anti-vaxxers. We`re bringing people on who are credentialed scientists, who are highly credentialed physicians, who are going to do evidence-based medicine.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: What`s your best sense about what`s behind this decision?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 THOMAS FRIEDEN\uc2 : I don`t think I can read Secretary Kennedy`s mind, but I do know that his stated rationale of strengthening trust is not what he`s achieving. What he`s achieving is the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: He has said that there are conflicts of interest that have not been addressed.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 THOMAS FRIEDEN\uc2 : This is simply untrue. We strengthened the system 15 years ago.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: How has it been strengthened?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 THOMAS FRIEDEN\uc2 : If there`s anyone who has a potential conflict of interest, they have to recuse themselves from all decisions and discussions about that topic.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Kennedy has long been a critic of vaccines. He testified in January that his recommendations would only be based on widely accepted science.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR.: I am not going to go into \uc2 HHS\uc2  and impose my preordained opinions on anybody at \uc2 HHS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Dr. Jon LaPook is here with us.\par \par \uc2 Jon, play this out. What happens next?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: \uc2 HHS\uc2  has said it`s going to replace those fired advisers. It hasn`t said when or with whom.\par \par \uc2 And, meanwhile, as we await that decision, this afternoon, Dr. Frieden said, if a new committee decides they`re not going to recommend a certain vaccine, well, your private doctor may say, no problem, I`m going to recommend it anyway. But an insurance company may say, well, we`re no longer going to cover it. That could be a big, expensive deal for some people.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Right. That`s how this affects regular people.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Sure.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dr. Jon LaPook, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Severe storms last night caused flooding and blew down barns in Western Pennsylvania. Lonnie Quinn tells us, the same storm system has moved east.\par \par \uc2 It sure has, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: You betcha.\par \par \uc2 And if I can show you the picture here on the radar, it stretches anywhere from the Lower Hudson Valley all the way up into Vermont. That will push offshore, much better day forecast for tomorrow in that portion of our country.\par \par \uc2 All right, elsewhere, the area shaded in pink for New Mexico, portions of Texas, tornado watches in effect right now. And look at all the water. It is just raining around the Gulf Coast, particularly Louisiana. Could have some flooding concerns there, but not like the flooding I`m about to show you.\par \par \uc2 That gentleman right there is standing on his roof. And, John, this is South Africa, five-, six-foot floodwaters there for this early season storm. And where you put cold air on top of that kind of available moisture, you get this type of picture. This is also, believe it or not, South Africa. South Africa does get some snow.\par \par \uc2 But to pick up snow this time of the year and to pick up a foot of snow, this is highly unusual for that part of the world. I will say the forecast for them gets much better by the time you finish up this week -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn spanning the globe.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Up next, Eye on America: why hundreds of women in prison could be resentenced.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They`re the last words of the Pledge of Allegiance, three words that ring in our ears, "justice for all."\par \par \uc2 Achieving that is a continuing challenge. And towards that end, some states are rewriting laws for sentencing victims of domestic violence for crimes connected to the abuse they endured.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LATOYA DICKENS (Parolee): I did not intentionally stab and kill my husband.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): In January 1999, Latoya Dickens` fight with her husband turned deadly.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA DICKENS: We struggled over the knife. He was stabbed one time and he died three days later.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Dickens, convicted of felony murder, served 25 years in prison. She`s now on parole for life. But Dickens says at no point did Georgia courts consider her backstory.\par \par \uc2 For years, she says, she was a battered, terrified spouse.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA DICKENS: I will never forget this one day that he got so angry with me, he lift me up waist-high and threw me off of a porch while I was eight months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: How often did it get physical?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA DICKENS: At least about 10 times out of the month.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: How often were you afraid for your life?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA DICKENS: Most of the time.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Georgia`s new survivor justice law says, in court, domestic violence survivors can ask for lower sentences if they can present evidence abuse led them to commit their crime. Under that law, Latoya Dickens` sentence could have been as little as 10 years.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. STAN GUNTER (R-Georgia): This is not a get-out-of-jail-free card. They still have to serve some portion of the sentence.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Republican State Representative Stan Gunter sponsored the bill. It passed overwhelmingly in Georgia`s legislature.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. STAN GUNTER: We weren`t looking at the whole picture. We were just getting bits and pieces of it. And it wasn`t enough to make a good, clear decision on how to treat these people.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: So justice delayed better than justice denied?\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. STAN GUNTER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In Georgia, hundreds of women behind bars could be resentenced. Nationally, more than 70 percent of incarcerated women report being victims of violence from their intimate partners.\par \par \uc2 Three other states have similar laws. Six more are considering one.\par \par \uc2 Ellie Williams directs the legal office of a group advocating against domestic violence.\par \par \uc2 ELLIE WILLIAMS (Legal Director, Justice for Incarcerated Survivors Program): It gives people an incredible amount of hope that they have not had until there`s not only a pathway for me to get out, but what I hear from them too is, it affirms that someone believes me.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Dickens still wants a court to hear evidence she killed in self-defense.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA DICKENS: I felt I deserved to serve some time, but not 25 years.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: What do you want Georgia`s new law to do for you?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA DICKENS: Examine the facts and determine whether I should be off of parole. I want a fair chance at rebuilding my life.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Across the country, thousands of women behind bars may have that same chance.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Mark Strassmann in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Coming up next, an update from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Why are they running?\par \par \uc2 MAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Back in Los Angeles now.\par \par \uc2 We talked about how quickly things can deteriorate and change. Protesters are now blocking the 101 Freeway, major highway here in L.A., blocking it in both east and west directions. The crowd just started moving after we last spoke to you. People didn`t know where they were headed to, but here they are. Police have moved on to the highway to try to get them out of there. It seems that it`s kind of working.\par \par \uc2 At this point, some people are running away. It`s a chaotic situation, probably a good harbinger of what`s to come tonight.\par \par \uc2 I`m Maurice DuBois in Los Angeles, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you, Maurice. Changing by the moment.\par \par \uc2 That`s the CBS Evening News. For Maurice DuBois in Los Angeles, I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 The news continues on Evening News Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7. I will see you soon.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250611el6a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For June 4, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti, Imtiaz Tyab, Celine Gounder, Jo Ling Kent,  Jill Schlesinger, Jonah Kaplan, Maurice DuBois, Lonnie Quinn, John  Dickerson \par \uc2 3,757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Casualties of a trade war.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN (Founder and CEO, Munchkin): We now have 80 percent tariffs on a metal soft-bite spoon for babies.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : As the latest tariffs hit businesses and families.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: American consumers and businesses are caught in the middle of a trade war. New Trump tariffs today doubled import taxes on steel and aluminum to 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That will raise costs for businesses who depend on those metals. And it is likely to force them to raise prices on their products.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This is the day the president set to have America`s trading partners submit proposals to avoid still more tariffs next month. He said China`s President Xi is extremely hard to make a deal with. Canada`s prime minister said negotiations are under way, but, if they fail, he is ready to retaliate.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Today, the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  said tariffs will slow the economy. The president believes they will eventually boost U.S. manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Right now, some U.S. manufacturers say they`re being hurt by the tariffs and their customers will feel the pain.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent is in Los Angeles tonight -- Jo Ling.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 When we think about these 50 percent tariffs on steel and aluminum, you often might think of those big-ticket items, when it comes to cars, trucks, refrigerators, washing machines, but what we found in Van Nuys, California is, these steel tariffs are hitting everyday smaller items too.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voice-over): We went to the headquarters of Munchkin, one of the most innovative baby brands in the country, where steel tariffs are impacting their biggest products and their smallest.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN (Founder and CEO, Munchkin): No one thinks about a baby spoon as a steel product.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Steven Dunn is Munchkin`s CEO and founder.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: Tariffs should be strategic. We need a tariff policy that doesn`t have the unintended consequences of putting 80 percent tariffs on a metal spoon.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Dunn says making their popular soft-bite baby spoons will cost 80 percent more because of the 50 percent steel tariffs, plus the existing 30 percent tax on Chinese imports.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: It`s a tariff on parents and it`s a tariff on small and medium businesses throughout the entire country.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Steel and aluminum tariffs are now the highest they have been since the 1930s. And, this week, the global economic group the \uc2 OECD\uc2  downgraded its forecast for U.S. growth, pointing to tariffs and policy uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Tariffs affect almost all of Munchkin`s 500-plus products. The vast majority are made in China. Dunn says his profits have already taken a hit and after tariffs were announced he had to lay off workers for the first time in 35 years. \uc2 Munchkin\uc2  is also discontinuing some of its most popular items.\par \par \uc2 You have stopped ordering these entirely?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: It just will become unaffordable if we pass on the cost of the tariffs. It will make it too expensive. We don`t believe consumers will pay for it.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Why can`t you make these kind of products in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: There isn`t the manufacturing base, the tooling equipment, the automation.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: So how do you run a business?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DUNN: It`s like being blindfolded, throwing darts at a rotating target.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jo Ling, there`s some new economic data that seems to back up what your reporting is telling you.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: That`s right, John.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to hiring momentum, it slowed down in May, according to the private payroll provider ADP. In fact, small businesses are bearing the brunt. Small businesses lost 13,000 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Jo Ling, what are analysts expecting for the May jobs report? That`s a big number and it comes out on Friday.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Maurice, it`s really all about what this number shows. The market is expecting a slowdown from May compared to April. But since tariffs began, we heard from businesses that it would be 60 to 90 days until we started to see the actual real-world impact on jobs. And we are sitting at that 60-day mark right now.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Jo Ling Kent tonight in Los Angeles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says President Trump`s spending and tax cut package will add nearly $2.5 trillion to the debt over 10 years. The CBO also says nearly 11 million more Americans could lose health insurance.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A U.S.-backed aid group that provides food to Palestinians in Gaza was forced to suspend distribution today.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab in Tel Aviv has more on this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Well, we were able to make contact with the representative for the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation...\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): ... who told us they will not be resuming operations tomorrow. Now, the group had paused aid deliveries today in order to -- quote -- "enhance safety." For three days straight, there were large-scale deadly shootings not far from the U.S.- and Israeli-backed group`s hubs in Southern Gaza, which are staffed by heavily armed American private contractors.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Israeli military said it allowed 157 trucks of aid into Gaza, but our CBS News team on the ground reports, so far, none of it has been distributed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A federal judge today blocked the government from deporting the family of the alleged Boulder, Colorado, firebomber to ensure they get due process.\par \par \uc2 The Egyptian national is accused of injuring 15 people in the attack on a demonstration in support of Israelis held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And a cargo ship is burning off Alaska. The Coast Guard says the ship is carrying thousands of cars and trucks, including 800 electric vehicles. The crew was rescued.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And a man from Washington state is facing federal terrorism charges for allegedly providing chemicals for a car bombing last month at a fertility clinic in Palm Springs, California.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is following this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The bomb that exploded outside the clinic was made with ammonium nitrate, a volatile fertilizer the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says was secured by 32-year-old Daniel Park of Washington state.\par \par \uc2 AKIL DAVIS (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director in Charge): Park was in possession of an explosive recipe that was similar to the Oklahoma City bombing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Park was arrested at New York`s JFK Airport overnight after an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation led to him being detained in Poland. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  discovered he traveled there after the bombing, tracking him down using online chats and travel itineraries.\par \par \uc2 He appeared in federal court in Brooklyn this afternoon, charged with providing material support to terrorists. He waived his right to a detention hearing. Investigators say he connected online with the man he allegedly helped, Guy Bartkus, over their shared nihilistic ideology that life has no meaning.\par \par \uc2 According to an affidavit, in January, Park shipped about 180 pounds of the fertilizer to Bartkus` California home before meeting him.\par \par \uc2 BILL ESSAYLI (U.S. Attorney for the Central District of California): Park spent approximately two weeks visiting Bartkus` residence in Twentynine Palms in late January and early February, running experiments in Bartkus` garage.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says Bartkus then drove the bomb to the Palm Springs clinic, where he carried out what is now being called a suicide bombing.\par \par \uc2 Is there any indication that there were other targets to follow?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT DIRECTOR AKIL DAVIS: No indication that there were other targets, and our analysis is still trying to determine exactly how much explosives were used.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says neither man appeared on their radar before the attack.\par \par \uc2 Park will now be transferred to California to face his charges. If he`s found guilty, he could face up to 30 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The commissioner of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is promising to conduct a review of mifepristone, a medication that can end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dr. Celine Gounder is a CBS News medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, remind us again why mifepristone is the subject of so much attention.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Mifepristone is a medication that is used for medication abortion, which now accounts for over 60 percent of all abortions in the United States.\par \par \uc2 But you have anti-abortion groups that question the FDA`s authority to regulate this, as well as the safety of the drug. Last year, the Supreme Court upheld the availability of mifepristone, but there are still debates about its use and its regulation. And this is despite the fact that the drug was approved some 25 years ago and is also used to manage women undergoing miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You had a look at some of the evidence that the administration cited for this review. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Gosh.\par \par \uc2 Well, the authors are not medical professionals. The study -- well, study - - was not reviewed by experts in the field. This was not published in a scientific publication.\par \par \uc2 And I think also, importantly, they did not reveal the sources of their data or their methodology. And this is important, because Senator -- or -- sorry -- Secretary Kennedy has made a big deal about the importance of being able to reproduce scientific research. And without the data source and the methodology, you can`t really do that.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Real fast, where does this go from here?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Really depends on if the \uc2 FDA\uc2  strictly follows the science and its own extensive safety reviews. We know that this is a drug that is at least as safe as Tylenol.\par \par \uc2 But it depends, science versus politics.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn on the record heat in the Northeast and that spreading smoke from Canada.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Also tonight: His daughter was killed in an antisemitic attack in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM (Father of Sarah Milgrim): Usually, a parent tries to mold their child. Sarah molded us.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: I`m Jill Schlesinger in New York. Economic uncertainties are making many Americans feel queasy as they approach retirement.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Two weeks ago tonight, 30-year-old Yaron Lischinsky and 26-year-old Sarah Milgrim, in love and planning to get married, became victims of antisemitic hate. They were gunned down in cold blood in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They had worked together at the Israeli Embassy. The suspect is charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 Milgrim`s parents, Nancy and Bob, talked to Jonah Kaplan of our CBS Minneapolis station WCCO about their loss.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM (Father of Sarah Milgrim): She was the perfect child. She loved people. She loved family. She loved her community. She loved her religion.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN (voice-over): Sarah Milgrim also loved her job. And that`s where her parents, Bob and Nancy, and her brother, Jacob, told us she met and fell in love with Yaron Lischinsky.\par \par \uc2 On May 21, the couple was leaving an event at the Capital Jewish Museum in Washington when a gunman opened fire, killing them both.\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM: Usually, a parent tries to mold their child. Sarah molded us. I`m a different person now than before. This happened from learning, but from learning so much about what Sarah did and her courage.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: What have you learned from her?\par \par \uc2 NANCY MILGRIM (Mother of Sarah Milgrim): She was really strong. Her desire to reach her goals and to see a better future was stronger than any of the hate that was directed towards her.\par \par \uc2 JACOB MILGRIM (Brother of Sarah Milgrim): This attack was in the beating heart of the free world. For it to happen there, it says that it can happen anywhere.\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: We`re sitting down now about two weeks since the shootings and in the immediate aftermath of another attack on a group of Jews, this one in Boulder, Colorado. How does it feel to be a Jewish family in America?\par \par \uc2 BOB MILGRIM: Threatened, insecure. Because of what happened to Sarah, I think I have been hardened by it, that I now -- I`m not scared, because she gave the ultimate sacrifice. And I can`t -- I can`t be scared.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONAH KAPLAN: According to the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 , 2024 saw the highest number of antisemitic incidents in 45 years, while a survey from the \uc2 American Jewish Committee\uc2  found, 93 percent of American Jews, they said they believe antisemitism is a problem in America, John and Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So important to share their story.\par \par \uc2 Jonah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you, Jonah.\par \par \uc2 Now to some potentially dangerous weather. It`s headed here in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn is following the record heat and the smoke that is wafting its way down from Canada -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Guys, that`s where I want to start because some of that smoke from the Canadian wildfires has made its way here to New York City. Now, it`s upper-level smoke.\par \par \uc2 So what that means is, take a look at our skyline. That iconic view you see right there, there`s just a little bit of haze out there. It`s not like what we experienced two years ago, where the entire sky turned orange. It smelled like a campfire walking down Broadway. That`s not the case. So where is the worst-quality air right now?\par \par \uc2 It`s in the Ohio Valley, stretching over towards the Great Lakes. And where`s it going to go from this point moving forward? It all depends on how the jet stream flows. As of right now, that jet stream has been flowing south from Manitoba into, say, Northern Minnesota, into the Ohio Valley. Then it does a U-turn and it pushes well up to the north, north of Maine, and it exits.\par \par \uc2 Basically, every day, we will get better as you go through the next two or three days. The temperatures are going to be a story, because I told you that jet stream goes well to the north like up around Maine. If you`re south of that, big temperatures. Manchester hits 95 degrees tomorrow. Springfield is 93 tomorrow. New Bern, North Carolina, with some rain cool there, you`re only 77. So that`s a big change right there.\par \par \uc2 I do want to tell you one thing to put this all in perspective. The worst air quality we have had from this event was in Northern Minnesota, and was between 250 and 300 on the AQI scale. Two years ago, and you guys probably remember this, here in New York City, we had the worst-quality air anywhere on the planet. It was between 400 and 500.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was something else. And this is nothing like that event for us here in the city -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes, it was dark in the middle of the day back then, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Something else.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Let`s hope that doesn`t happen again. Exactly. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Will economic turbulence lead some Americans to make a U- turn on the road to retirement?\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Up-again/down-again stock prices, on-again/off-again tariffs, up again and again prices, it`s enough to give current and future retirees motion sickness.\par \par \uc2 Workers in the first quarter of the year saved a record 14.3 percent of their income for retirement, but the value of the average 401(k) fell by 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, Jill Schlesinger checks back with a couple we met last year as they were looking down the road to retirement.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER (voice-over): Dinner at the Gomez home outside Boston provides a textbook image of the sandwich generation.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ (Planning For Retirement): Here you go.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Three sets of relatives living under one roof.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: The club sandwich has a lot of layers, and we have a lot of layers.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: It`s not the easiest way to save for retirement, as they told us when we first met them last year. Back then, their nest egg was healthy and growing. Stocks were climbing, hitting an all-time high by February of this year, but they cratered with the start of the trade war in April, only to climb back and recover most of the losses.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: I feel like I`m on a roller coaster. You just hope that, if we`re going to be on the downturn now, we would be on the upturn when we...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: ... we decide to retire.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Today, like millions of Americans, Alicia, 57, and Chu, 59, are experiencing the waves of an uncertain, seesawing market.\par \par \uc2 It was about almost a 20 percent slide. Did you feel like you could go through that again?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: No.\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ (Planning For Retirement): I don`t want to go through it again.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: If you`re paying attention to the market, you`re going through it every day.\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI (Labor Economist): Forty-three million people are about that age.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: These gyrations can trigger rash decisions, says labor economist Teresa Ghilarducci of \uc2 The New School for Social Research\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: We have a name for living through that kind of volatility, and it`s called scarring.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: How do I help those folks or prevent that scarring from causing them to act?\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: Act in a way that`s...\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: That`s detrimental.\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: In times of financial crisis, do not talk to your friends or your family about what to do. Take a breath, take a minute and rely on expert advice.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Alicia, who holds down two jobs, had thought maybe she`d cut back work at 62. Chu, who works in logistics, thought 65. Now they have adjusted that mind-set.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: It`s probably going to be 67, at least. I think there`s still a lot of unknowns.\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Would you stop putting money in your retirement accounts? Would you pull back on funding something else?\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: The first place would be pull back on the retirement funding, because we are maxing that out.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Adding to their anxiety, fear that the Social Security system could run dry. There`s been a 13 percent jump this year in people claiming retirement benefits early, despite permanently reduced payouts.\par \par \uc2 Would that then argue that you should claim Social Security early, because...\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: No, not yet.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: No? OK.\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: Not yet. Wait for the maximum benefit. Don`t haircut yourself now, anticipating it will be cut later.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: The Gomezes say their retirement investments are up by about 3 percent this year, so they will simply sit tight and work hard to hold on to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: You can only take so many hits. We don`t have due overtime.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: No.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: In the long run, saving for retirement really is more like riding a roller coaster than a merry-go-round.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Jill Schlesinger in Boston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Some people drink coffee as they read their newspaper. In tomorrow`s Eye on America, you will meet a couple who sell coffee to support a newspaper.\par \par \uc2 Now, have you ever had one of those days? Well, Jim Marshall had one on October 25, 1964. His story is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally tonight, one of the most infamous plays in sports.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It`s 1964. Minnesota Viking Jim Marshall carries the ball triumphantly into the end zone.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But, uh-oh, it is the Vikings end zone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Watch Marshall, number 70, pick up the loose ball.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Marshall had recovered a 49er fumble, got disoriented, and ran 66 yards the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Instead of a Vikings T.D., he scored a safety for San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On the way home, his teammates joked with him that Marshall should fly the plane so it would land in Hawaii, instead of Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But it would be unfair to judge Marshall by one gaffe. The two-time Pro Bowler was an iron man. His 282 straight games as a defensive end set a record.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Marshall twice left the hospital to be in a game and once played after accidentally shooting himself.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And about that game, Marshall said: "Think about making your worst mistake in front of 200 million people and getting teased about it every day of your life."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Jim Marshall died yesterday. He was 87. What a career, four Super Bowls with the famed Purple People Eaters defense.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Man, but that many games in a row, extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 That`s the CBS Evening News. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . The news continues on Evening News Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7. See you there.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you right back here tomorrow. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250605el6400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 SATURDAY WEEKEND NEWS for May 31, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Andrew Kozak, Imtiaz Tyab, Ali Bauman, Itay Hod,  Elise Preston, Josh Crabb, Taurean Small, Carter Evans \par \uc2 3,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, the West sizzles in a pre-summer heat wave. Temperatures, extreme and record breaking. The heat shifting East the Midwest, struggling to breathe smoke from Canada, as the wildfire season there is off to a ferocious start.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: I`m meteorologist, Andrew Kozak, I`m tracking how long the smoke is going to stick around, how hot the temperatures are going to get, and what`s next as we begin June.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight --\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab in Tel Aviv. The Trump administration has said negotiations for a ceasefire in Gaza has been set back after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  asked for amendments to the proposed deal, this, as Israeli strikes intensify.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Steel Deal: President Trump touts a new merger while doubling tariffs on foreign steel, but will American consumers be paying the price?\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  getting out of DOGE?\par \par \uc2 Pardon power.\par \par \uc2 TODD CHRISLEY (Former T.V. reality star): And I have no shame.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A convicted reality star now a free man, courtesy of the President.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: I`m Ali Bauman in New York, as some are alleging a flurry of presidential pardons are favored to his supporters.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And later, need a little help around the house? \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Itay Hod shows us how college students are cutting rent in pricey San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE (Dominican University): Some delicious pasta, olive oil and some sea salt.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening and thank you for joining us this Saturday.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight in the West, where several cities could experience record breaking temperatures, and it`s still spring. In the Midwest, some 22 million people this weekend are dealing with air quality issues because of the pictures you see right there, as smoke from more than 200 wildfires push to the south from Canada.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston leads us off tonight from Los Angeles on the extreme weather.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good evening.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Jericka, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 The West is in the grip of a blistering heat wave. The triple digit temperatures are a real danger, and summer doesn`t even start for a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Crews struggle to control a quick spreading fire in San Diego County. More than 300 acres of rugged hillside have already burned with several neighborhoods under mandatory evacuation orders.\par \par \uc2 In Canada, a ferocious start to wildfire season, with wind whipped flames burning in three provinces -- Alberta, Saskatchewan and Manitoba -- roughly 175 fires this week have forced at least 17,000 people to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 WAB KINEW (Manitoba Province Premier): We are now turning to our neighbors from the South to help us in our time of need.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: About 22 million Americans in Minnesota, Wisconsin, Michigan, the Dakotas, Ohio and Illinois are under air quality alerts because of the hovering smoke from the Canadian wildfires.\par \par \uc2 While the West bakes, powerful storms are pounding the East with heavy rain in Maryland and high winds in Florida. In Kentucky, cleanup is underway after a tornado fire killed at least one and injured several others.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, summer does not begin until June 20th, but already, Phoenix has experienced 14 a hundred-degree or hotter days this year -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Elise Preston for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The wildfires in Canada have forced thousands to evacuate in three provinces. In Manitoba, there are 25 fires burning, some of them out of control.\par \par \uc2 Josh Crabb of the \uc2 Canadian Broadcasting Corporation\uc2  is in the town of The Pas. Josh, what are fire crews up against right now?\par \par \uc2 JOSH CRABB (\uc2 Canadian Broadcasting Corporation\uc2 ): Jericka, , they`re dealing with several large fires in remote areas of the province which are posing a danger to crews and multiple communities. Firefighters are using water bombers, helicopters, and crews on the ground are trying to protect cities, towns and indigenous communities.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian military has been called in to rescue people from one remote First Nation where the airport has been closed and the power is out, they`re being brought here to where we are before being sent farther south for safety and shelter.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And I understand, Canada has raised its national wildfire preparedness level to five, which is the highest. It`s a level that you all didn`t reach until last year in July, what`s changed?\par \par \uc2 JOSH CRABB: Jericka, it has been a hot, dry spring with a lack of precipitation and there`s lots of material in the forest which allow these fires to burn fast and spread fast. Crews have put in restrictions to keep people out of the back country, and they`ve banned fires across the province. The fire danger is extreme, and they`re worried about more fire starting on top of the ones they`re already fighting.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, the CBC`s Josh Crabb, we appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And for more on those weather conditions, let`s check in with CBS News meteorologist, Andrew Kozak.\par \par \uc2 Andrew, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking excessive heat warnings, heat advisories, and still triple digit heat. For the remainder of your Saturday across the Desert Southwest, fire danger still an issue. The yellow, the orange, red, indicating that there is going to be some relief, though, for Sunday. Meantime, we have that color all the way up toward Canada, that`s where those fires are coming in, and the smoke billowing down, affecting areas like Minneapolis, Minnesota. The entire state of Minnesota has an air quality alert at least through Monday. If you have some air issues, respiratory issues, you need to avoid being outside for periods of time.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, look at the triple digit heat that we had for today. Triple digits will eventually start to come down as we head into your Sunday, a little bit better. We`re also dealing with some showers and thunderstorms across areas like Oklahoma and Texas that will give us a slight risk for severe weather as we head into the day on your Sunday and then that will focus down into Central Texas as we get into your Sunday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Across the nation on Sunday, we`ve got the heat out west, a couple of storms in the midsection of the country, and cooler weather across the East Coast -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Andrew Kozak, we appreciate you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 To the Middle East now, where tonight, Israel`s military confirmed that it has killed Mohammed Sinwar, one of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` top military commanders in Gaza. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has not responded to the claim. There`s also new doubt about the push for a ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  Imtiaz Tyab is in Tel Aviv with the details.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Jericka, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Trump`s Middle East envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  has said \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has proposed amendments to the U.S.` broker deal had `set negotiations backwards." \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had asked for additional U.S. guarantees on aid deliveries and the withdrawal of Israeli forces, while the original proposal, which Israel has accepted, would see 10 living hostages who were taken captive during the October 7 attacks released and hundreds of Palestinian prisoners freed from Israeli jails. If implemented, the fighting would also be paused for 60 days as the hostages are released in phases.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the situation in Gaza remains catastrophic. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  calls the besieged Palestinian territory the "hungriest place on Earth," because of Israel`s continued harsh restrictions on aid.\par \par \uc2 Over the past week, a new player, the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation, backed by Israel and the U.S. has started distributing food, but critics say it militarizes relief efforts and is against all humanitarian principles as Israeli strikes continue to expand across Gaza, Jericka, killing at least 60 Palestinians the past 24 hours alone.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Imtiaz Tyab in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, President Trump is spending the weekend in the nation`s capital after firing a new shot in his global trade war. This one focused on steel and aluminum. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Taurean Small is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Taurean, the President is once again causing some turbulence there with these tariffs.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: Yes, that`s right, Jericka. He also celebrated at that Pennsylvania rally a manufacturing deal he previously opposed.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering "USA.")\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL (voiceover): President Trump is touting what he is calling a partnership between \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  and Japan`s \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States of America): We`re here today to celebrate a blockbuster agreement that will ensure this storied American company stays and American company --\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: He promised steel workers they would soon see an impact.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: There will be no layoffs and no outsourcing whatsoever, and every U.S. steel worker will soon receive a well-deserved $5,000.00 bonus.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: Trump also announced a doubling of tariffs on foreign imports of steel and aluminum.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re going to bring it from 25 percent to 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: But it`s American consumers who could be paying the price. New tariffs threatening to impact the price of cars, appliances, even construction supplies.\par \par \uc2 This, as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  exits his role as head of DOGE. He sat for an interview with "CBS Sunday Morning" that airs tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (\uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO): It`s not like I agree with everything the administration does. So it`s like there`s -- I mean, I agree with much of what the administration does, but we have differences of opinion.\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: Returning from Pittsburgh, the President dismissing concerns over a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " report that Musk abused drugs during his time in the Trump administration and on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I am not troubled by anything with Elon. I think he is fantastic. He did a great job. And you know, DOGE continues.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TAUREAN SMALL: President Trump left the door open for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , hinting he could return as a special adviser -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Taurean Small, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Trump flexed the power of his office this week, issuing a flurry of pardons. They include convicted fraudsters and political supporters.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ali Bauman has more on that story.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Jericka, presidents usually issue pardons at the end of their terms, but Trump has been on a spree since inauguration. He granted clemency to dozens this week alone, while his new pardon attorney in the Justice Department posted "No MAGA left behind."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN (voiceover): Reality star, Todd Chrisley is already planning his T.V. comeback after he and wife, Julie, received pardons from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 The stars of the cable show "Chrisley Knows Best" were convicted of tax evasion and bank fraud in 2022, accusations they deny.\par \par \uc2 TODD CHRISLEY: I have no shame.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: The President intervened after appeals from their daughter, Savannah, including at the Republican National Convention.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH CHRISLEY (Todd and Julie Chrisley`s daughter): I just appreciate the administration looking into it and seeing the faults within our case.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: The Chrisleys were among a blitz of pardons and commutations this week, including former New York Republican Congressman Michael Grimm, convicted of tax fraud and the notorious Chicago gang leader Larry Hoover, who had been serving multiple life sentences on federal murder and drug trafficking charges, though he still faces 200 years on state charges.\par \par \uc2 The President also said he would consider pardoning the men convicted in a plot to kidnap Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You know, they were drinking and I think they said stupid things.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Whitmer visited the White House last month.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): I said, I think it would be the wrong decision. I would oppose it. He said, okay, I`ll drop it.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: On his first day in office, Trump pardoned 1,500 people either charged or convicted in connection with the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: And when asked if he would consider pardoning \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  if he is convicted in the ongoing sex trafficking trial, Trump did not rule it out, saying the music mogul used to like him, but the two have not talked in years -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Ali Bauman in New York. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, six people soared to the edge of space.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In Texas, \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  launched its 12th human space flight. The capsule, after rocketing up about 66 miles, landed 10 minutes later, after separating from its booster.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, Hollywood on hold. Why California`s biggest industry faces an uncertain future.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a swarm to remember. New details about millions of escaped honeybees in Washington State.\par \par \uc2 And a helpful match making app that`s anything, but romantic.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, there is mounting concern in California about the survival of the state`s vital T.V. and film industry. High cost and labor issues threaten tens of thousands of jobs.\par \par \uc2 In Hollywood, here is CBS Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): For years, Phil Mangano made a good living as a film and T.V. editor in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 PHIL MANGANO (Film and T.V. editor): It was just job after job after job.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Real consistent work.\par \par \uc2 PHIL MANGANO: Very consistent work.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: But after Hollywood writers went on strike in 2023 followed by actors, production ground to a halt. California lost 40,000 film and T.V. jobs that year alone.\par \par \uc2 PHIL MANGANO: When that finally settled like, okay, great. Things will come back, and there has been no significant increase in job opportunity.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Since its peak in 2021, television production in L.A. has decreased by 58 percent, and in just the first quarter of this year, on location productions here fell 22 percent.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY (Puck News): Right now, it`s a triage situation. The patient is dying, and you need to bring it back to life.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Matt Bellamy covers show business for Puck News. He says Hollywood productions and the jobs have gone to other U.S. states and other countries with generous tax incentives.\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: some European countries that are offering up to 40 percent back on these productions, and that`s incredibly influential.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Film and filmmaking, pre and post production, it is on life support.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  wants to stop the bleeding by more than doubling the state`s film and T.V. tax incentives, up to $750 million annually.\par \par \uc2 Is it too little too late?\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: The sad reality is that California has sat on this issue for 30 years.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Can California give enough tax credits to offset the very high cost of working in this state?\par \par \uc2 MATT BELLAMY: Other jurisdictions have done their own aggressive cuts to those -- to that bureaucracy. Is California willing to do that? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: In the meantime, Mangano and thousands of others in Hollywood are looking for whatever work they can find.\par \par \uc2 PHIL MANGANO: I applied for a job at \uc2 Costco\uc2  a couple of months ago.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: How long can you hold out?\par \par \uc2 PHIL MANGANO: Not much longer. I have a little savings left. We`re hoping that will float us for a few more months, and then we have to start making some hard decisions.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And what are those hard decisions?\par \par \uc2 PHIL MANGANO: Whether or not we can keep the house.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For now, he`s just hoping the show will go on once again before he moves on to a new career.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, CBS News, Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It`s real out there.\par \par \uc2 Well, still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, new information about those millions of escaped honeybees after a truck accident in Washington State.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How exciting, tonight, a wipeout in soccer`s Champions League match for the European Cup, Paris Saint-Germain beat Inter Milan five-nil clinching their first title, and 19-year-old French teenager, Desire Doue was the game`s breakout star, scoring two goals.\par \par \uc2 An update tonight on those escaped honey bees in Washington state. A local sheriff has issued a correction. He says 14 million honey bees, not 250 million were involved in Friday`s rollover crash near the Canadian border. That is clearly still a lot of bees. Police also thanked the two dozen beekeepers who rushed to help with the rescue effort.\par \par \uc2 And a chance to own a piece of rock and roll history. Fans turned out in Malibu, California today for an estate sale at Tom Petty`s home. No guitars or gold records up for grabs, just his everyday stuff from rugs to glassware.\par \par \uc2 Petty died in 2017 of an accidental overdose.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, odd jobs in return for rent. There`s an app for that.\par \par \uc2 But first a look back at this day in history.\par \par \uc2 Graphics onscreen: This Day In History --\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1969.\par \par \uc2 John Lennon records anti-war anthem during "Bed-in for Peace."\par \par \uc2 (Clip of John Lennon, Yoko Ono and other people performing)\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1988.\par \par \uc2 President Regan visits Moscow for critical Cold War Summit.\par \par \uc2 DAN RATHER: \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  set foot on Soviet soil for the first time in his life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 2005.\par \par \uc2 Watergate scandal`s secret source, "Deep Throat" revealed.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS: The secret is out. This man is the legendary "Deep Throat."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We end tonight in San Francisco, one of the nation`s most expensive housing markets. This is important, because in tonight`s "Weekend Journal," \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Itay Hod shows us how a matchmaking service is helping both college students and homeowners cut costs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD (VOICEOVER): When most students have called it a day, Nanik Tagore is just getting started, a shooting guard for the Dominican University basketball team, he has got range, control, and a shot that rarely misses.\par \par \uc2 But when it came time to find housing in the Bay Area, let`s just say he was out of his league.\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE: Centerfield is one of the most expensive places to live, so I wasn`t sure how was going to be able to live on my own.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: That`s when he stumbled on a startup called "Joey," a platform that pairs students with homeowners who need a little help around the house. Nanik now pays about $1,700.00 a month for a room, but $500.00 of that, he works off with chores, cleaning windows, taking out the trash, sorting the mail and, yes, making lunch.\par \par \uc2 Did you even know how to cook?\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE: The basics.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: The people he lives with can use the help.\par \par \uc2 Paige Miller (sp?) has early stage dementia. Her husband, Robert, runs a company and works long hours.\par \par \uc2 PAIGE MILLER (Early stage dementia patients): It gives me back my freedom, and he`s great to have around, and he laughs at my jokes.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: With about 60 percent of U.S. homes having at least one spare bedroom, the new housing frontier may already be built and gaining momentum.\par \par \uc2 ALISON DONNELLY (Joey founder and CEO): It just didn`t make sense to me why we couldn`t have something similar to au pairs for older adults.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Joey founder and CEO, Alison Donnelly, says Nanik is the first student to match up, but more applications are already being processed.\par \par \uc2 ALISON DONNELLY: What we can do at Joey Co. is create more bedroom inventory for these students.\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE: So here`s my room.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: As for Nanik, he`s found more than just a place to stay.\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE: It doesn`t feel like a chore to lend a helping hand.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Because sometimes, the most important plays don`t happen on the court --\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE: Here is the salad.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: They happen in real life.\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE: They are colorful.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Or even a small assist could be a game changer.\par \par \uc2 NANIK TAGORE: Is this peas any good?\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Itay Hod, CBS News, San Rafael, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What an awesome idea.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Saturday. Tomorrow on "Face the Nation," Margaret Brennan`s guests include Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent.\par \par \uc2 For now, I`m Jericka Duncan in New York. Thank you for watching. Have a great night, and we hope to see you here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020250601el5v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For May 21, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Skyler Henry, Weijia Jiang, Debora Patta, Nicole Sganga, Tom  Hanson, Ian Lee, Lonnie Quinn, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,821 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: For more than a century, the Oval Office has been the scene of dignified diplomatic exchanges between the president of the United States and other world leaders.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But President Trump has turned the Oval into a ring for sparring with them, including the president of Ukraine, the prime minister of Canada, and now the president of South Africa.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Mr. Trump sprung a video on an unsuspecting \uc2 Cyril Ramaphosa\uc2  designed to back Mr. Trump`s unsubstantiated claims that there`s a genocide under way in South Africa targeting white farmers.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In recent days, the president has welcomed white refugees from South Africa to the United States just weeks after signing an executive order suspending America`s refugee admission program.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Debora Patta is in South Africa and is South African. We will hear from her in a moment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But we begin with Weijia Jiang at the White House -- Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 For months, President Trump has accused South Africa of condoning genocide, even cutting off all U.S. assistance to the country back in March. Today, the South African president was hoping to reset the relationship. But Trump had a different plan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I think we also have a...\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): The meeting started with a friendly jab from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: He is a man who is certainly in some circles really respected, other circles, a little bit less respected.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But when South African President \uc2 Cyril Ramaphosa\uc2  denied Trump`s accusations his administration is allowing genocide, Trump was prepared to push back.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Turn the lights down and just put this on. It`s right behind you.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: On a television brought into the Oval Office for this very moment, the president showed videos peddling unsubstantiated claims of genocide against white South African farmers.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Those people in many cases are being executed. They`re being executed. And they happen to be white and most of them happen to be farmers.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Ramaphosa acknowledged a law that allows his government to expropriate land for public use with compensation, but he insisted white farmers are not being targeted.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Do allow them to take land.\par \par \uc2 CYRIL RAMAPHOSA (South African President): No, nobody can take land.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And then, when they take the land, they kill the white farmer. And when they kill the white farmer, nothing happens to them.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT CYRIL RAMAPHOSA: No. No, there is quite...\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Nothing happens to them.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT CYRIL RAMAPHOSA: There is criminality in our country. People who do get killed, unfortunately, through criminal activity are not only white people. The majority of them are black people.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Trump was asked if he`s concluded genocide is happening.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, I haven`t made up my mind. I hate to see it.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The president claimed without evidence his administration has accepted a tremendous number of people from many places experiencing persecution or genocide.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): I`m Debora Patta in Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has ground all U.S. refugee admissions to a halt, except for one group, the white Afrikaner ethnic minority that once led South Africa`s brutal apartheid regime.\par \par \uc2 The first group of South African refugees in America claim they have been victims of violence and discrimination.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Get the police intelligence.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And there are many more who want to follow who have been attending informal meetings to learn about Trump`s offer.\par \par \uc2 Professional hunter Dolf Grobler has already applied.\par \par \uc2 DOLF GROBLER (Professional Hunter): I`m worried that the genocide, which is currently mainly focused on white farmers, is going to spread.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: So life was better under apartheid?\par \par \uc2 DOLF GROBLER: If I see my -- yes, I can`t say in my heart that we are better off now.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Claims of a white genocide are often circulated by right- wing groups, but are simply not true, said Afrikaner academic Piet Croucamp.\par \par \uc2 PIET CROUCAMP (Afrikaner Academic): There`s no sign of it, never has been. In fact, whites are economically the strongest group. This is the land of milk and honey if you`re white.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: More than half of all farming land is owned by white South Africans, who make up just 7 percent of the population. And while the country has a staggeringly high crime rate, it is not racially motivated, says Afrikaner cattle farmer Nick Serfontein.\par \par \uc2 NICK SERFONTEIN (Afrikaner Cattle Farmer): I feel safe. I don`t -- I sleep with my doors open here on the farm.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: There are also allegations that white farmers are the victims of land expropriation without compensation. But in the 31 years since apartheid ended, there has not been a single case.\par \par \uc2 Serfontein told us he`s trained the next generation of more than 700 black farmers.\par \par \uc2 NICK SERFONTEIN: I`m positive. I`m extremely positive. And so are the young people in South Africa, the young farmers. They`re positive. They want to be here. They want to farm.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: In a country where he says the land must be shared by black and white.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Debora, I`m wondering, do we have a sense of how many of these white farmers have been killed?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, Maurice, there are over, according to the police, 50 farm murders a year, but that includes white farmers and black farm laborers.\par \par \uc2 And that has to be seen in the context of the fact that this country has a shockingly high crime rate, seven times higher than the U.S. Nearly 27,000 people were murdered here last year, but that violence is indiscriminate.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Deb, as somebody who lives in South Africa, what do people say? How do they take President Trump`s view of their country?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, when the story first broke, people were joking that they suddenly woke up and it was too dangerous to go outside anymore, referencing the supposed genocide.\par \par \uc2 But, humor aside, there`s also anger. South Africans say that giving refugee status to Afrikaners makes a mockery of the whole system, when there are countries where there really is genocide. And as for most white farmers, they say, well, they`re quite happy here and have no intention of going to the U.S. -- John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Debora Patta from Johannesburg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon says it has accepted a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  747 as a gift from the nation of Qatar for President Trump to use as Air Force One. Democrats and Republicans have questioned whether this is legal or practical, given the cost of the overhaul required to make the plane meet security and military standards.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A federal judge says the Trump administration violated a judicial order and may be held in contempt for trying to send migrants to the African nation of South Sudan.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Nicole Sganga.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA (voice-over): CBS News has learned these eight deportees originally from six countries, including Laos and Cuba, are in limbo in the East African nation of Djibouti, deported on a privately owned jet leased by ICE.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge believes the men convicted of crimes ranging from armed robbery to murder were not given a meaningful opportunity to object their removals. Lawyers for the men say they were deported after being told they`d be sent to South Sudan, a country plagued by an ongoing armed conflict.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And five inmates have now been captured after escaping from a New Orleans jail last Friday. Five others are still on the run. A lawyer for a maintenance worker accused of helping the inmates says they intentionally clogged the toilet so he would turn off the water, which then allowed them to remove the toilet and escape through a hole in the wall.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : One of the tools the federal government uses to fight misconduct by local police departments is the consent decree. A police agency agrees to undertake reforms and is monitored for compliance.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Today, the Justice Department said it is ending investigations of six police agencies and canceling decrees proposed for Louisville after the fatal shooting of Breonna Taylor and Minneapolis after the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here`s Skyler Henry.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN O`HARA (Minneapolis, Minnesota, Police Chief): There`s still there`s so much hurt here, I mean, in the community, with the cops.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voice-over): When we spent the day with Minneapolis Police Chief Brian O`Hara on Monday, he told us he was expecting today`s announcement.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF BRIAN O`HARA: Consent decrees don`t worry about fighting crime. I do.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The consent decree in question was a court-enforceable agreement mandating federal oversight over the Minneapolis Police Department. It aimed to eliminate racial bias and excessive use of force. It was in the final stages of implementation, until today.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel as if the police department even needs one in the first place?\par \par \uc2 CHIEF BRIAN O`HARA: We need to be building trust. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: He needs to build trust because the community is still hurting from the 2020 killing of 46-year-old \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It feels like at one point this intersection was at the center of the world because of what happened here.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF BRIAN O`HARA: The level of crime in the city skyrocketed. At the same time, the numbers of the police force just diminished. Two years later, half the police department was gone while people in the community were experiencing violence on levels they had never seen before.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Today, the DOJ said: "We are ending the Biden Civil Rights Division`s failed experiment of handcuffing local leaders and police departments with factually unjustified consent decrees."\par \par \uc2 Chief O`Hara`s perspective is more nuanced. His department is still under a consent decree with the state, and he says he will continue with reforms related to arrest practices and use of force, regardless of what`s going on in Washington.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF BRIAN O`HARA: It`s not enough to just do consent decrees. There`s more that we have to do to change the reality of how people experience policing on the street.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, Skyler, given that those state consent decrees are still around, how much will this federal decision actually affect the police departments and how they do business?\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s the interesting thing, John. I think time will tell on that front. But, to your point, because there are still state consent decrees still in effect and other efforts as it relates to police reform in several other of these departments that were being looked into, I think that many in leadership say that they will continue to try to do this work.\par \par \uc2 And that includes Minneapolis, as Chief O`Hara says that it will take time to make sure that things get back to the way they used to be.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Skyler Henry, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn is watching the latest tornado risk and a possible nor`easter.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: I`m Tom Hanson in Westbury, New York, where some of the states formerly incarcerated are getting a fresh start by hitting the road and filling an urgent need in the work force. That`s coming up on tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: I`m Ian Lee in St. Louis. A tornado killed five people and did more than a billion dollars worth of damage, but why did the city`s warning system fail?\par \par \uc2 That`s our story next.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The city commissioner responsible for making sure St. Louis is ready for an emergency has been put on paid leave because it wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Sirens failed to sound on Friday before a deadly tornado touched down.\par \par \uc2 Ian Lee is in St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARA SPENCER (Mayor of St. Louis, Missouri): Let me be clear. CEMA exists to alert the community when severe weather is coming. This office failed to do that in the most horrific and deadly storm that our city has experienced in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE (voice-over): St. Louis Mayor Cara Spencer says that word of possible tornadoes came Friday afternoon, but the city`s emergency officials didn`t push the button to sound the outdoor warning sirens because they were out of the office at a workshop.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CARA SPENCER: There was human error. There was a failure to do testing. There was a host of things that went wrong here that resulted in the failure to get the emergency management system, the siren system working that day.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: The tornado was a mile-wide when it went through residential neighborhoods of St. Louis packing winds of more than 150 miles an hour. Five people were killed.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the more than 3,000 buildings damaged, according to the mayor. The tornado hit the ground and carved a path of destruction over 20 miles long.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CARA SPENCER: This cannot happen when a tornado is ripping through our community.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Mayor Spencer put the city`s head of emergency management, Sarah Russell, on paid leave and launched an investigation.\par \par \uc2 Would lives have been saved if those sirens would have sounded?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CARA SPENCER: It`s hard to say. The siren system is designed to really alert people outside. And it didn`t do that. And, at the end of the day, that was a failure.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: This local failure comes at a time when staffing at National Weather Service offices across the country have decreased dramatically and leave warning systems stretched thin.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CARA SPENCER: I can say that, if there`s a problem there, we will mobilize immediately again to ensure that the system and the backup system are in place, working, and working well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: I have covered wars on three continents, and the destruction here reminds me of what I have seen on those front lines, homes and buildings completely destroyed.\par \par \uc2 The mayor tells me the city desperately needs federal aid to repair and rebuild what`s estimated to be over a billion dollars in damages.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ian Lee in St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Ian.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And a tornado watch went up today in Southwestern Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Lonnie Quinn is tracking a nor`easter as well -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Yes, lots to talk about here.\par \par \uc2 Well, as far as Western portions of Pennsylvania go, you are looking at Pittsburgh right now. That`s not such a bad picture to show you, except, about an hour ago, Pittsburgh was underneath this system. Yes, it did have tornado watches associated with it. It`s shaded in yellow that you see right there.\par \par \uc2 The whole system is pushing to the east. But what`s interesting, this system moves east. It`s got severe weather, but it`s nothing like what we dealt with yesterday for the last seven days. It`s going to merge a bit with what you see offshore. That`s a little low-pressure system gets its act together. And, as they phase together, they become a bigger system, actually one with a name.\par \par \uc2 We refer to these as nor`easters. And I know that sparks fear in a lot of people. We think a lot of snow, horrible storms. Well, this one is a much weaker one. You can see it come together right here, one storm pushing up from the south, another storm coming in from the west.\par \par \uc2 And you will see the phasing taking place here. Here we go. Now you start to see that quintessential counterclockwise rotation. Almost looks sort of tropical. It`s not. It is a nor`easter. It will go to the north, bringing northeast winds into Boston, so maybe two to four inches of rain, I think tops.\par \par \uc2 But you have got wind gusts that are 40 miles per hour. The big thing it`s going to do, lower the temperatures. Look what we`re talking about. Boston and Worcester should be around 70 degrees tomorrow. You`re 46. Mansfield, you should be about 73. Well inland into the Ohio Valley, you`re going to be 50 degrees, so about 20 to 25 degrees colder than you should be for the Northeastern portion of the country.\par \par \uc2 Guys, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All right, Lonnie Quinn.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Former prisoners in need of a job, an industry in need of workers, and a program that brings them together.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That`s tonight`s Eye on America next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now a story about second chances. For people who have served time, being released from prison is just the start of the road back. They also need a job.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, Tom Hanson has the story of a program that matches former prisoners with an industry in need of workers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA (Trucker): You want to release your air brakes.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: OK.\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: And put it on drive.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voice-over): Every time Jorge Badilla shifts into gear, he`s sitting in the driver`s seat of an industry that`s running on fumes.\par \par \uc2 (HORN HONKING)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: The \uc2 American Trucking Association\uc2  says it`s short 60,000 drivers. With each mile, Badilla is filling a critical need and mapping out a future he thought he`d never have.\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: I have an opportunity to do something positive for my life. It feels great to be free. This is my past that I don`t want to remember.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Freedom is especially meaningful to Badilla. He spent nearly a decade in federal prison for dealing drugs from this housing project in Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent had his gun pointed to my face like this.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: That was the moment your life changed.\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: Completely. I didn`t see the daylight for the next nine years.\par \par \uc2 This is when I got my GED in prison.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Getting out of prison might have been the end of his sentence, but it was the beginning of a different kind of struggle.\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: When you come out of prison, you feel like all the doors are locked. Nobody wants to give you a job.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Nationwide, six out of every 10 formerly incarcerated people are still jobless four years after getting out of prison. Close to 70 percent of them will end up back in prison, a problem called recidivism.\par \par \uc2 How often did you see someone who got out and then ultimately was arrested and brought back in?\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: A lot.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: A lot.\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: And me seeing that also helped me wake up.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: So Badilla entered a program with New York City`s Office of Criminal Justice and Emerge Career, a tech company. It trains the formerly incarcerated who are considered low risk for trucking jobs, helping them get a commercial driver`s license and connecting them with trucking companies in states across the country.\par \par \uc2 So far, 260 have completed the program. Over the last fiscal year, 94 percent of Emerge Career students who started the program graduated, and all of those graduates received job offers with an average starting salary of $75,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 For incarcerated people who participate in initiatives that combine education and job training, the odds of returning to prison drop by nearly half.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like to be able to drive anywhere?\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: I mean, if I had to compare it to something, I would say a bird.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: A bird.\par \par \uc2 JORGE BADILLA: A bird. A bird is free.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: The freedom of the open road delivering a second chance for Badilla and other formerly incarcerated people across the country.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Tom Hanson in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In tomorrow`s Eye on America, as we head into Memorial Day weekend, we look at the return of a tradition at Arlington National Cemetery, horse-drawn caissons.\par \par \uc2 Maurice and I will be right back with a judge`s landmark ruling.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally, tonight an update on Conway, New Hampshire`s trial of the century.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Or the trial of February 14 anyway.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At issue, this mural over Leavitt`s Country Bakery, a local landmark. It was painted by high school students.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But an inspector said it violates the town code because the mural is actually a sign. And, as a sign, it is four times larger than the code allows.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: What makes it a sign, Conway contended, is that it depicts donuts and other confections that are for sale inside the bakery.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The bakery sued, claiming its free speech rights had been violated. And, this week, in a 23-page ruling, federal Judge Joseph Laplante, whose other recent cases include birthright citizenship, ruled that the town had indeed infringed on the bakery`s constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Twenty-three pages, that`s a lot of pages. The owner of the bakery asked for damages in the amount of $1.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Clearly, it was never about the dough.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At least this one time.\par \par \uc2 And that is the CBS Evening News. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . The news continues on Evening News Plus streaming on CBS News 24/7. We will see you there.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you right back here tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250522el5l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For May 19, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Manuel Bojorquez, Kris Van Cleave, Jonathan Vigliotti, Ian Lee,  Janet Shamlian, Lonnie Quinn, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,906 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Terror attacks in this country have fortunately been rare, even more rare, a terror attack in which the weapon is a car bomb.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But that is what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating right now, the late Saturday morning car bombing outside a fertility clinic in Palm Springs, California.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The only person killed was a suspected bomber. At least four other people were injured, but, judging by the size of the explosion, it could have been much worse.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : As for the motive, investigators say the suspect appeared to be against procreation. They say his target was a clinic that helped people achieve their dreams of having children.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A source tells CBS News that, in an angry set of writings believed to be that of the suspect`s, he talked about wanting to livestream his suicide and the bombing.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is in Palm Springs tonight -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Maurice, a good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 It`s been two days since this attack and still many questions remain, including what kind of device was used, where did the materials come from, and how was it constructed? We have learned it was a chemical mixture, and the blast was so powerful, it caused damage for several blocks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): In the aftermath of the explosion outside a Palm Springs fertility clinic, what continues to stun investigators is the sheer size of the blast.\par \par \uc2 Akil Davis is with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AKIL DAVIS (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director in Charge): This is probably the largest bombing scene that we have had in Southern California.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The impact of the car bomb could be felt miles away. Destruction was visible for over 100 yards in all directions. The suspect, 25-year-old Guy Edward Bartkus, was killed in the explosion.\par \par \uc2 A law enforcement official tells CBS News that investigators have found bomb-making materials inside the suspect`s home in the community of Twentynine Palms. That`s an hour`s drive from the Palm Springs clinic. They`re also digging through his computers, phones, and online records.\par \par \uc2 AKIL DAVIS: We are treating this as an intentional act of terrorism. We are tracking a possible manifesto out there. The subject had nihilistic ideations, and this was a targeted attack against the IVF facility.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Given the size of the explosion, investigators say this could have been catastrophic. On Saturday, the clinic was closed, but all the embryos were saved.\par \par \uc2 GREG LYLE (Palm Springs, California, Deputy Fire Chief): We were like, OK, well, if we don`t do anything, the bad guy wins.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Palm Springs Deputy Fire Chief Greg Lyle and an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent ran into the clinic, helped restore the power, then salvaged thousands of medical records.\par \par \uc2 You had no idea who you were helping.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY FIRE CHIEF GREG LYLE: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: You were just triggered to act.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY FIRE CHIEF GREG LYLE: Right. Right. I got a text from one of our fire personnel, and he said thank you. It was his family, his wife that was supposed to go in for an appointment this week, and his embryos were in the office.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Jonathan, this attack happened on Saturday, when the clinic was closed.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : What do we know about the timing and why on this particular day?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, John, it`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , we have learned, is investigating several theories, the main theory, that this was a suicide bombing meant to be livestreamed. The second theory here is that the suspect intended on planting this bomb only for it to be detonated days later at another time.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of the scenario, it speaks to a level of radicalization that is very rare and uncommon here in the U.S., John.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Jonathan, do we have any idea why the attacker chose this particular location? And might there have been other plans for other attacks here?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, that is still a critical question, why the attacker chose this location and if the bomb materials that were found at his home were intended for another target.\par \par \uc2 Officials tell us, as the investigation continues, the threat here has been contained.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Jonathan Vigliotti in Palm Springs tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The Supreme Court today allowed the Trump administration to revoke legal protections the Biden administration gave to roughly 350,000 Venezuelan migrants.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: This could clear the way for their deportation.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez reports how this decision affects protected migrants who live and work in Miami and other parts of South Florida.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voice-over): On a recent visit to Miami`s Little Havana neighborhood, we found conversation about temporary protected status heating up.\par \par \uc2 You think it`s bad for them to remove the status.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE) You still approve President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: So you continue to support the president.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: This is iconic Domino Park, where many Cuban Americans come to enjoy their favorite pastime, playing dominoes. And in between games, some have told us that they agree with President Trump on most things, except this.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Cubans, Venezuelans should have a right to be protected.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: "Clearly, yes," she said.\par \par \uc2 Temporary protected status, or TPS, allows immigrants from countries in turmoil to legally stay and work in the U.S. for 18 months or more. A Biden era policy known as humanitarian parole does the same. Trump vows to end both, leaving hundreds of thousands of people in limbo and facing deportation. In South Florida, that includes Cubans, Nicaraguans, Haitians, and Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 It was President Biden in 2021 who made Venezuelans who fled the economic and political crisis in their homeland eligible for TPS. The Trump administration says TPS and humanitarian parole have become back channels for immigrants to stay longer than necessary and question the Biden administration`s vetting of recipients.\par \par \uc2 In Doral, Florida, 40 percent of the population is estimated to have ties to Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 What happens if they have to go?\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN PORRAS (Vice Mayor of Doral, Florida): The city`s not going to be the same.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Maureen Porras is Doral`s vice mayor.\par \par \uc2 VICE MAYOR MAUREEN PORRAS: We need to have some real commonsense permanent solutions to these immigrants that have been coming in that have been contributing, that are law-abiding, that are part of our communities and have been adding value to our city and our communities.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: While the Supreme Court decision potentially allows the administration to move forward with deportations, the justices also appeared to signal that Venezuelans who had received their legal documentation before President Trump ended the program could sue to fight their removal.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Manuel Bojorquez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now more of the top stories from around the world in the Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 President Trump spoke separately by phone with the leaders of Ukraine and Russia and said both have agreed to immediately begin cease-fire negotiations. But no date or place has been announced. The diplomatic push came shortly after Russia hit Ukraine with one of its largest drone attacks of the war.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Federal investigators say the Brooklyn Bridge was not significantly damaged when it was struck by a Mexican navy ship in the East River on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Here`s senior transportation correspondent Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says it is still waiting for permission from the Mexican government to get on board this ship. It`s a bit of an unusual process for the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 , but it`s because it`s a foreign naval vessel.\par \par \uc2 Now, investigators are just getting started, but say whatever went wrong happened within about five minutes between when the ship essentially backed out of its parking spot and when it hit the Brooklyn Bridge, killing two Mexican sailors and injuring at least 19. We`re hearing for the first time, a tugboat assisting the ship radioed for help less than a minute before impact.\par \par \uc2 Now, so far, no word on cause. And because the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  hasn`t been able to get on board, they are yet to confirm any of the initial reports about mechanical or engine failure.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump today signed a revenge pornography law backed by first lady Melania Trump. It bans posting explicit images online without consent, including so-called deepfake nudes made by artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 You may recall our \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  spoke with a teenage victim who had urged lawmakers to take this action after her fake nude photos circulated for nine months on \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The news that former President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has Stage 4 prostate cancer that has spread to his bones has some asking whether it could have been diagnosed sooner.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I talked today with Dr. Dana Rathkopf, a medical oncologist at New York`s Sloan Kettering Cancer Center. She specializes in prostate cancer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. DANA RATHKOPF (Memorial \uc2 Sloan Kettering Cancer Center\uc2 ): I see prostate cancer almost exclusively. It doesn`t follow any one pattern.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Does it surprise you that it is so advanced and moving so quickly, given that he has seen the best doctors possible?\par \par \uc2 DR. DANA RATHKOPF: Cancer cells are chaotic, and they don`t read the guideline rule books. And so our screening guidelines don`t necessarily apply to a cancer cell that`s going to decide to grow or evade a particular screening technique.\par \par \uc2 And so there are many people that have the access to highest levels of care and undergo regular screening and still get diagnosed with advanced disease.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Given what he has been diagnosed as having, how long would he have had cancer, do you think, before this diagnosis?\par \par \uc2 DR. DANA RATHKOPF: Well, I can`t really speak to the specifics of President Biden, but I would say that, in general, advanced prostate cancer presents in many different ways. It`s not really one-size-fits-all.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It`s Stage 4 and in the bones. That didn`t start just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 DR. DANA RATHKOPF: Typically, a more aggressive prostate cancer portends a worse prognosis, meaning that patients are at higher risk of having cancer grow quickly and/or having cancer spread outside of the prostate.\par \par \uc2 And when cancer spreads outside of the prostate to other parts of the body, we consider that metastatic. And so the most common place for prostate cancer to metastasize to is, in fact, the bone.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, if you were presented with a person with this case, you wouldn`t say, oh, they missed it?\par \par \uc2 DR. DANA RATHKOPF: No, I would not say that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And so, John, for men over 70 watching this wondering how does this apply to them, what`s the doctor have to say about that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The doctor said she`s been getting calls the minute the news broke.\par \par \uc2 First, her point is, don`t generalize from a single case. If you are concerned about this, talk to your doctor. She talked about shared decision-making. And she said it`s not necessarily the case, if you`re over 70 that, you get a PSA test, or a prostate test, because, in some cases, there can be false positives,or the treatment can be worse than living a life with a cancer that`s going to be very slow going, and you -- it may outlive you.\par \par \uc2 So, basically, she said, talk to your doctor.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Solid advice.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: All right, thanks, John.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead here on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn on the latest tornado threat in the South Central United States.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: I`m Ian Lee in Middleton, Wisconsin. This used to just be called shop class. Now it`s a pipeline for a potential six-figure career, students trading keyboards for a tool belt, without the college debt. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: I`m Janet Shamlian in Trinity, Texas. Rural hospitals are a small town`s beating heart. And when they close, it`s like pulling life support on the entire community.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Having health insurance is no guarantee that you will get health care, not if you live in rural America.\par \par \uc2 A new report says hundreds of rural hospitals are in danger of closing because they can no longer afford to stay open.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The main reason, insurance pays rural hospitals less than their big city counterparts for the same services.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Janet Shamlian.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voice-over): We were there as workers covered the emergency room signs at Mid Coast Medical Center in Trinity, Texas. The sight of medical workers leaving for the last time signaling this is one of the latest rural hospitals to shut its doors.\par \par \uc2 MARJORY PULVINO (President, Trinity Memorial Hospital District Board): I think how sad it is.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Marjory Pulvino runs the hospital board.\par \par \uc2 MARJORY PULVINO: It has a huge impact on the committee. We will lose lives; 400 people a month come here.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: In the East Texas Piney Woods, Mid Coast was more than a building. It was a lifeline, where babies were born and where the heart of Cathy Courtney`s 90-year-old husband was restarted, saving his life.\par \par \uc2 How many times have you used this facility recently?\par \par \uc2 CATHY COURTNEY (Patient): I`d say about six times in the last 14 months.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The closure, hospital officials tell us, was driven by the same factors that have closed other rural hospitals, low reimbursement rates from elderly patients` Medicare and Medicaid coverage, which made up most of the hospital`s budget.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals in urban areas get significantly more reimbursement from their patients who have private insurance, which tends to pay more. Republican- proposed cuts to Medicaid could leave more than 8.5 million people uninsured and force even more rural hospitals to close.\par \par \uc2 A new report finds almost 750 rural hospitals are at risk of closing. Courtney`s husband is now home and doing well, but the 87-year-old is unsure she could drive that far if she or her husband of 65 years had another emergency.\par \par \uc2 When your husband had a heart attack recently, how long did it take you all to get here?\par \par \uc2 CATHY COURTNEY: Probably 10 minutes, 15.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And if you had to go to the next closest hospital, how long would that have taken?\par \par \uc2 CATHY COURTNEY: Over half-an-hour.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Is that the difference between perhaps life and death?\par \par \uc2 CATHY COURTNEY: I would say it is.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, Janet, when these rural hospitals close and folks end up having to drive a long way, what`s the overall impact on their health as a result?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: They often give up on preventative care, things like regular checkups, and that can lead to the delayed diagnosis of something like a serious illness like cancer.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : What about the effect of these hospitals closing on the community, Janet?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: It is beyond the hospital. These hospital workers shopped in the stores. They ate in the restaurants. If they move to a new area for a new job, that affects the real estate market, the school district, property taxes. These rural hospitals are more than health care. They really are the heartbeat of the community.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Janet Shamlian reporting tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Tornado watches are up tonight for the center of the country from Texas north to Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn once again tracking the severe storm threat tonight -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: You betcha.\par \par \uc2 It`s out there currently. We have tornado warnings, as a matter of fact. Certainly, watches will continue for a bit longer, but warnings out there. And they can do damage like you see right here, Plevna, Kansas, earlier today. And that -- in fact, this was last night, the middle of the night. Tornado touched down, destroyed some homes, didn`t touch other homes. That`s the signature oftentimes of a tornado.\par \par \uc2 If you look where the line is as of right now, it stretches from the Texas- Oklahoma border, stretches all the way up into Iowa. I want to zoom in on one particular area, McAlester, Oklahoma. Get in nice and tight on that, because that cell that you see right there, there is a tornado warning. That is on the ground right now.\par \par \uc2 If you can hear my words and you are in McAlester, you need to get to a safe place as of right now. It has been a tough day. It will be a tough night, at least another hour or so of this. And, tomorrow, it`s back into the Tennessee Valley. And Okla -- excuse me -- and Kentucky could be under the gun again tomorrow. They went through a horrible weekend.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We are in warning season.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: All right, Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Tonight, the growing popularity of trade schools.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The high cost of a college education and the prospect of decades of debt has younger Americans looking for an alternative. Many are turning to the trades. The cost of learning a trade can be much lower and skilled labor is in demand. So there are plenty of good-paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 Ian Lee has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE (voice-over): At Middleton High School in Wisconsin, shop class is back and booming.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN ZANDER (Teacher): And this is the way we`re going to assemble it. We`re going to take...\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: For three decades, Justin Zander has shaped wood and minds.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN ZANDER: My goal is to teach them comfort and skills so they become comfortable with a few skills and those skills will be transferable from many different trades.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: The revival is fueled by the demand for tradespeople. And with some paying six-figure salaries, what were once blue-collar jobs are now gold-collar.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN ZANDER: It used to be kids were interested in it as a sideline. Now the kids are interested in it because it could lead to a good-paying job for the future.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Middleton High built a $90 million campus in 2022, putting trades in the spotlight, part of a larger trend embraced by 27 states that increased spending on tech ed by an average of $182 million.\par \par \uc2 AIDAN DOWN (Student): I think it`s a pretty easy option to go into a trade job, especially if you have some experience in these classes.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Aidan Down is like more than 75 percent of students here who take tech ed classes.\par \par \uc2 AIDAN DOWN: I`m choosing to go into the trades.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: You`re pursuing what you love.\par \par \uc2 AIDAN DOWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Interest in trade schools has nearly doubled since 2017, while university enrollment has declined slightly since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Thalia Madden is a senior taking building construction.\par \par \uc2 THALIA MADDEN (Student): I think that`s really important, is showing kids even just from the get-go that college isn`t the only option.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Here at Middleton High, students can take woodworking, welding and robotics. This robotic arm isn`t just a cool classroom gadget. It`s the real deal. Students here at Middleton High are learning to program the same kind of industrial robots used in factories across the country.\par \par \uc2 CARLO ZWETTLER (Zuern Building Products): Shop classes helped a lot.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Carlo Zwettler once got his hands dirty in these same classrooms.\par \par \uc2 CARLO ZWETTLER: There`s a lot of freedom around here.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: He got a job right away at Zuern Building Products, putting that experience to use.\par \par \uc2 Has there been any discussion about, like, they`re going to get in college debt and you`re straight into the work force?\par \par \uc2 CARLO ZWETTLER: Oh, yes, we always give each other a bunch of crap about that. So...\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: For Justin, his return on investment is introducing kids to a profitable way of life he loves.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN ZANDER: I`m not opposed to having kids smiling when they leave the room.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: I`m seeing you`re smiling a lot by talking about it. You really enjoy what you do.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN ZANDER: I absolutely love teaching. I can`t imagine...\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: There`s no alternative.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN ZANDER: There`s nothing else.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Making it in America starts with more people than ever seeing trades as a ticket to a promising future.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Ian Lee in Middleton, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In tomorrow`s Eye on America: Her two sisters were killed by a tornado. Now she`s dedicating her life to saving others.\par \par \uc2 Maurice and I will be back in a moment with a new location for one of the most famous brownstones in America.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The clouds hanging over "Sesame Street" were swept away today.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The future of the children`s show more than a half-century old was in doubt since late last year, when \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  chose not to renew it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Today, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  came to the rescue with a streaming deal. Later this year, new episodes of "Sesame Street" will start running on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and on its original home, \uc2 PBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Season 56 will feature some updates, including more exploring of the neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It will also take viewers inside the famous brownstone.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, kids, we can indeed tell you how to get to "Sesame Street." It`ll be on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and \uc2 PBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, that reminds me of being a little one.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It takes us back.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Thank goodness it`s still here.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And something to watch later.\par \par \uc2 But that`s it for the Evening News for now. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice...\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : See you on Evening News Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 We will see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250520el5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for May 12, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson, Anna Coren, Tom Hanson, Nicole  Sganga, Kelly O`Grady, David Schechter, Lonnie Quinn, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 3,834 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : They spent the weekend in a secluded Swiss villa, and, voila, a truce in the trade war.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The United States and China announced today that negotiators have worked out a rollback of the triple-digit tariffs each country imposed on the other.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The U.S. will cut the 145 percent tariff on Chinese imports to 30 percent, and China will slash the tariff on U.S. goods from 125 percent to 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The truce will last 90 days. That will give negotiators time to work on a longer-term deal. It is temporary, but still welcome news for consumers facing rising prices and for businesses trying to stay in business amid the tariff uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Wall Street is relieved. The Dow gained 2.8 percent today and is back above 42000 for the first time since the trade war intensified in early April.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Kelly O`Grady is looking at the impact of the truce here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with Anna Coren in Hong Kong -- Anna.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Well, for the past six weeks, President Trump`s trade war has caused havoc to markets and seriously disrupted global supply chains. Well, that turmoil has ended for now.\par \par \uc2 But what`s most surprising is that China made very few concessions in this truce, whereas the U.S. has dropped its tariffs by more than 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN (voiceover): Switzerland`s neutrality proved the ideal setting to de-escalate the U.S.-China trade war. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent made this announcement:\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT (\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary): We want more balanced trade, and I think that both sides are committed to achieving that.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Beijing has also agreed to remove some U.S. companies from its blacklist, but it`s refused to lift its ban of rare earths to the United States.\par \par \uc2 There is a great sense of relief in China that exports can resume to the U.S. Since the trade war began, we have been meeting with business owners inside China. Last month, at the Canton Fair, the largest import-export trade fair in the country...\par \par \uc2 RICHARD CHU (Business Owner): Five thousand.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: ... I met Richard Chu (sp?), who has been selling fabric gift bags to the U.S. for 20 years. His American buyers had told him to halt production and stop shipments, which left him feeling a deep sense of betrayal.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD CHU: I think it`s not fair, so it just make me feel sick, sick about the USA now.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: China`s economy has been vulnerable to President Trump`s trade war. Youth unemployment, lack of domestic consumption, and the fallout from the property crisis have exacerbated the pain at home.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): China, first row.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Economists I have spoken to say, considering this was President Trump`s trade war, he has caved to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL PETTIS\uc2  (\uc2 Carnegie Endowment for International Peace\uc2 ): Trade conflict becomes very painful.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: While world-renowned China expert \uc2 Michael Pettis\uc2  told me that neither side has the stomach for the economic pain of a worsening confrontation, which is why both sides have agreed to back off the now.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY (voiceover): I`m Kelly O`Grady. We first met \uc2 Kenneth Hartman Jr.\uc2  in February. He`s a fifth-generation corn and soybean farmer from Waterloo, Illinois, and China is a key market for those products.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned what tariffs might do to your bottom line?\par \par \uc2 KENNETH HARTMAN JR\uc2 . (Farmer): I`m very concerned about tariffs. I`m concerned with the future of rural America because we have been through some economic problems.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: With the retaliatory tariffs the first time around, has some of that business been permanently lost?\par \par \uc2 KENNETH HARTMAN JR\uc2 .: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Despite a reduction in tariffs on Chinese goods over the next 90 days, a 30 percent import tax will remain in effect. Some business leaders are cautiously optimistic, but warn, consumers could still see higher prices.\par \par \uc2 Shipping activity has already slowed as businesses have canceled orders from China. They have dropped 27 percent year-over-year in the last week of April. China`s not ordering as much from the U.S. either. \uc2 Kenneth Hartman Jr.\uc2  worries the U.S. is running the risk of competitors taking big bites out of U.S. business with China, including his.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH HARTMAN JR\uc2 .: We have our folks from South America, Brazil and Argentina that can still expand their land. So this gives them an opportunity to expand, and then some of the markets that they expand to, sometimes, we never get them back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Kelly O`Grady joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Kelly, if prices might still go up and there`s uncertainty, it sounds like we`re not out of the woods yet.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of optimism, don`t get me wrong, but 30 percent tariff, that is not nothing. And that`s on top of existing tariffs in targeted areas from the previous administrations, by the way, three months for a deal this complicated, that`s a short time.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: If you`re a business, you`re a business owner, what are you supposed to do in terms of making plans for the future?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: I had that same thought, OK? This broke, I immediately grabbed my phone. I said, well, what happened under the first Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 Seven years ago, almost to the day, May 9, U.S. and China, they came to a pause. And then, a couple of weeks later, they were back to retaliating. So, for a business, this is really hard to plan because it`s only a pause, and history teaches us this roller coaster can start up again.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Kelly O`Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Well, in the Middle East today, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  released the last living American among the hostages it took when it invaded Israel on October 7, 2023, 21-year-old Edan Alexander, who is also an Israeli citizen.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : \uc2 Hamas\uc2  said the release is meant as a goodwill gesture towards the United States to try to get peace talks going again.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson now on Alexander`s first day of freedom in more than a year-and- a-half.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It says that Edan has had his first conversation with his mother.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): Seeing was believing in Edan Alexander`s hometown in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Let`s go!\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: hundreds of people watched Israeli TV as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  handed him over to the Red Cross, ending 584 days in captivity.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: In Israel, this was the scene when Alexander`s family witnessed the same moment.\par \par \uc2 When you saw that image on the screen, what went through your mind?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Just happiness. Like, it`s -- I mean, it`s not over, but it feels like we...\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It feels like we have gotten past the biggest hump.\par \par \uc2 (SCREAMING)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hours later, mother and son were reunited.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Ben Esser (sp?) and Michael Leshner (sp?) went to Tenafly High School with Alexander.\par \par \uc2 Is this the Edan that you guys know and have known for so many years? Did he look the same?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: It`s honestly just incredible that he is alive. He`s truly a fighter and he`s an incredible person, but looking at him, he definitely looks much thinner. He looks a lot more pale than when he left, for sure.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Alexander, who holds American and Israeli citizenship, moved to Israel to serve in the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2 . He was stationed near the border with Gaza when \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacked and was taken hostage.\par \par \uc2 What is the first thing that you`re going to do or say when Edan gets back here?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Welcome home and give him the biggest hug that he`s ever received in his life, squeeze him so hard and tell him that we love him.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: The Alexander family is still figuring out when they will return to the United States. Currently, Edan is undergoing medical treatment at a hospital in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 There are believed to be 58 hostages still in Gaza, nearly two dozen of them still believed to be alive.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Tom Hanson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now more of the top stories from around the world in our EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 Pope Leo greeted reporters at the Vatican today. He said, free speech is a precious gift and called for the release of jailed journalists.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Bidding ended today for dinner with the president and a private tour of the White House for the top 25 buyers of the Trump family crypto coin. The top purchaser, with nearly $1.5 million in holdings, is believed to be Chinese billionaire Justin Sun.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And CBS News has confirmed the Trump administration has spent at least $21 million transporting migrants who are in the country illegally to Guantanamo Bay, Cuba. The facility now holds just 69 migrants.\par \par \uc2 Our Nicole Sganga visited a new migrant facility in New Jersey and found it is mostly empty.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA (voiceover): This is Delaney Hall, a privately operated prison less than 10 miles outside New York City. It opened less than two weeks ago. We came here today because it`s the first ICE detention facility to open under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 And it`s the largest center of its kind, with 1,000 beds. But one thing struck us: Where are the detainees? ICE Acting Director Todd Lyons says there are 152. That`s just 15 percent capacity.\par \par \uc2 Director, ICE is overcapacity, but I`m looking at a number of empty beds right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD LYONS (Acting Director, U.S. \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2 ): Correct.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 ACTING DIRECTOR TODD LYONS: The teams out here in New York and New Jersey are focused on the worst of the worst and getting those individuals off the street that have been let go by a lot of these sanctuary jurisdictions. I think, as you see more cooperation with ICE, when we do arrest more criminal aliens in the street, you will see the population here rise.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The cost of the facility, $1 billion over the next 15 years. Since President Trump took office, federal agents have arrested nearly 2,000 illegal migrants in the Newark area, six times the number picked up during the same period in President Biden`s first year.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is calling the influx of migrants an invasion and says that could be grounds for suspending habeas corpus, the bedrock American legal right that allows a person to challenge their detention in court.\par \par \uc2 Migrants here, do they deserve due process? Do they deserve habeas corpus?\par \par \uc2 ACTING DIRECTOR TODD LYONS: Illegal aliens that come into Delaney Hall, they do get due process. I am proud of the work that`s done here because they are being treated in a safe and humane fashion.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Would you be concerned if that due process was taken away?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We got to go.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Before we could get an answer to that question...\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are way past right now.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: ... his team ended our interview.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: First, ICE officials told us our time with the director was simply up. Later, they cited security reasons.\par \par \uc2 As for when the facility will reach capacity, Maurice and John, Lyons says that depends on cooperation from local law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Nicole Sganga, good to have you at the table. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, Lonnie Quinn on heavy rain from South Florida to North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we will have these stories as well:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: I`m David Schechter in California. One way we know the climate is changing is from 67 years of painstaking measurements of greenhouse gases. Scientists are now worried that cuts by the Trump administration could interrupt that gold standard. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: I`m Nikole Killion in Winchester, Virginia. Rural hospitals hope for the best and brace for the worst, as Congress wrestles with potential cuts to Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 What it could mean for patients -- that`s next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump signed an executive order today giving pharmaceutical companies 30 days to lower prescription drug prices. Otherwise, he says he will reduce what the federal government pays them for drugs through Medicare and Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : At the same time, Medicaid, which provides health insurance for low-income Americans, could be a target of Trump budget cuts. Critics say that is a prescription for disaster.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 YOLI DE LEON BELTRAN (Patient): I have been dealing with most of this, like, my whole life.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Twenty-four-year-old Yoli De Leon Beltran (sp?) has been coping with chronic health issues affecting her joints and nervous system since she was a child, which requires a lot of medication.\par \par \uc2 YOLI DE LEON BELTRAN: Overall, it`s about 30.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Thirty medications?\par \par \uc2 YOLI DE LEON BELTRAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: That`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 YOLI DE LEON BELTRAN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: How do you pay for all of this?\par \par \uc2 YOLI DE LEON BELTRAN: I am very fortunate that I have Medicaid. They have been paying 100 percent for most of my medications.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But she worries her coverage could be impacted as House Republicans finalize portions of President Trump`s legislative package, seeking at least $880 billion in savings that could include Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Valley Health CEO Mark Nantz runs six rural hospitals in Virginia and West Virginia and has lobbied lawmakers several times this year.\par \par \uc2 MARK NANTZ (CEO, \uc2 Valley Health System\uc2 ): We need a solution that`s workable. And throwing out a number like $880 billion, I mean, that -- somebody`s going to get hurt in that situation.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: GOP lawmakers argue the latest proposal would strengthen Medicaid and eliminate waste and abuse. While it backs off deeper cuts, it would require recipients work, volunteer or attend school up to 80 hours per month. The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says at least 8.6 million people could become uninsured if the bill becomes law.\par \par \uc2 Would you have to close your doors?\par \par \uc2 MARK NANTZ: We`d have to think about, I mean, can we keep every location open? I don`t think Valley Health would go out of business, per se. But I think that Valley Health would look substantially different.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: For Yoli, who visits Valley Health`s main hospital about once a month, it`s a lifeline.\par \par \uc2 What would you say to lawmakers?\par \par \uc2 YOLI DE LEON BELTRAN: There are people on this program that would not be alive right now if it weren`t for this program.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The president is urging Republicans to unite behind this bill, which would use some of these cuts or savings to help pay for an extension of his 2017 tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Heavy rain is in the forecast for the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Parts of Texas are in for a spring heat wave.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn tracking it all for us tonight -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: You betcha, guys.\par \par \uc2 And when I was speaking on Friday evening, I talked about Northern Florida and Georgia picking up big weekend rain. And, sure enough, Vidalia, Georgia, picked up eight inches of rain. Well, now it`s South Florida`s turn. That is West Palm Beach. And look at the radar picture. From South Florida, that line of rain pushes into the ocean. It hooks around, comes back onshore around the Carolinas.\par \par \uc2 And they will be training showers and storms, meaning they train over the exact same place, like train cars over a track. So, South Florida, where you see the red color, could see four to six inches of rain, and then, as you push up toward the Carolinas inland towards maybe portions of Western Virginia, maybe a three-to-six-inch range.\par \par \uc2 But where it`s not raining, you get to the midsection of the country -- and you guys were referring to this -- it is record-setting heat out there. Look at some of these projections that we`re dealing with. San Antonio hits 104 degrees tomorrow. That would break a record by six degrees. And look at this number.\par \par \uc2 All right, Grand Forks, North Dakota, sees 97. That record of 93 has been in the books since 1893. Maybe they think it`s about time for some fresh numbers in Grand Forks. Going to be a hot one out there.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Trump job cuts could affect scientists who track changes in our environment.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America looks at the possible fallout next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The people of Earth just got a new warning about what we`re doing to the planet. A \uc2 NOAA\uc2  report says more carbon dioxide was pumped into the environment last year than ever before. \uc2 NOAA\uc2  knows that because it monitors CO2 levels, but Trump administration cutbacks could affect the scientists who do that critical research.\par \par \uc2 David Schechter has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RALPH KEELING (Professor of Geochemistry, \uc2 University of California, San Diego\uc2 ): This is the center of the operation that my father started.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER (voiceover): Professor Ralph Keeling is analyzing air samples inside these volleyball-looking flasks that were collected around the world. He`s a professor at the Scripps Institution of Oceanography at U.C. San Diego.\par \par \uc2 And, for him, this is a father-son business.\par \par \uc2 RALPH KEELING: He never encouraged me to go into the field, but he inspired me by what he did.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: That`s a young Ralph in the middle, and that`s his dad on the right. It was Charles David Keeling, who died in 2005, who first developed what`s known as the Keeling Curve. It plots over time the unchecked rise of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, which is warming our planet.\par \par \uc2 Look how closely the curve matches the rise in global average temperatures.\par \par \uc2 RALPH KEELING: The headline, sadly, is the same every year, is, we keep breaking records.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Now this work is in jeopardy because the Trump administration has proposed cuts that would slash climate research, specifically targeting the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2 , or \uc2 NOAA\uc2 , which runs its own larger carbon sampling program.\par \par \uc2 Do you have some concerns that that work is going to stop?\par \par \uc2 RALPH KEELING: It would be a big blow if that work stopped, not just for me personally, but for the community and for the world at large.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Supporting the work that Keeling started is now a combined effort of scientists around the world.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: All right, now we will walk away.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Over the years of our climate reporting, we have stood on a volcano in Hawaii to see samples gathered, and in Norway took a cable car to see how samples are shipped to a \uc2 NOAA\uc2  laboratory in Boulder, Colorado, for analysis.\par \par \uc2 Why do you have to send samples to Colorado?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Because we want to see that we are measuring on the same scale.\par \par \uc2 RALPH KEELING: It`s beautiful data, but it`s also sad. So underlying that sense of, wow, scientific wonder and beauty is also a sadness that this is actually what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: The Trump administration`s plans would eliminate NOAA`s global CO2 program, ending decades of unbroken data collection and degrading the ability to project how climate change will impact our future. The administration has not yet responded to our request for a comment.\par \par \uc2 RALPH KEELING: Turning off a program like this would be like turning off the headlights on a dark street at night. You can`t see where you`re going.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: The story of how our climate is changing has in part been revealed by a father and a son. Now, with that continuous record under threat, who knows where the story goes.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, David Schechter, La Jolla, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: In tomorrow`s Eye on America, from Trinidad, Colorado, how a town that fell on hard times reinvented itself with a few strokes of a paintbrush.\par \par \uc2 Up next: why appearing on American currency is a posthumous honor.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Finally tonight: Among the 5,800 bills before Congress is H.R.1761.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Otherwise known as the \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  $250 Bill Act.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Sponsored by South Carolina Republican Joe Wilson, it would create a new denomination of currency bearing the image of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At the moment, the law allows only deceased individuals on our money, and therein lies a story.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : At the end of the Civil War, Congress authorized a 5 cent note. The Treasury man in charge of printing it was one Spencer Morton Clark. For reasons known best to him, Clark put a picture of himself on the bill.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Members of the House hit the roof. Martin Thayer of Dinwiddie County, Pennsylvania, called it derogatory to the dignity and the self-respect of the nation. And, in 1866, he got Congress to ban images of live individuals from our currency.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The Trump Act would make an exception for a sitting president, but the bill is given little chance of passage.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, for anyone dying to appear on U.S. currency, that is the only way.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And that`s the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 The news continues now on EVENING NEWS PLUS streaming on CBS News 24/7. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . I will see you then.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 We will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250513el5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 SATURDAY WEEKEND NEWS for May 10, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Ramy Inocencio, Willie James Inman, Elise Preston,  Kati Weis, Andrew Kozak, Adam Yamaguchi, Noel Brennan \par \uc2 3,982 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, President Trump praised as a peacemaker amid escalating tensions between India and Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 Today, a surprise ceasefire follows days of cross-border attacks.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: I`m Ramy Inocencio and actions speak louder than words. Hours after Trump`s post on his Truth Social platform, blast rocked at least two cities in Indian administered Kashmir.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, new hope for fired federal workers, a judge ordering a pause to the government`s drastic downsizing.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: I`m Willie James Inman at the White House where President Trump is sending mixed messages about raising taxes on the rich.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States of America): I would love to do it, frankly. I don`t think they`re going to be doing it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Trade war tsunami. U.S. and Chinese negotiators meet for the first time as tariff costs hit U.S. ports.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: I`m Elise Preston at the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 , where workers are feeling the pain. What`s next for consumers?\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Airport chaos, as Newark`s troubles mount, United CEO weighs in.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Face the Nation)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2  (\uc2 United Airlines\uc2  CEO): It absolutely is safe at Newark and in the entire country. And the reason is, when these kind of outages happen, we train for them. We have backup procedures.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Truly American. Leo XIV`s unexpected ancestry.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: I`m Kati Weis in New Orleans, where state records reveal the new Pope`s Creole roots.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And in our Weekend Journal, Kids` Conclave. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Noel Brennan shows us how these Chicago students learned valuable lessons picking their new Pope.\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: I think you`ve got to be kind and respectful.\par \par \uc2 BOY #2: Be Pope of the people.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening and thank you for joining us on this Saturday. Tonight, a U.S. led ceasefire between India and Pakistan is already shattered with India accusing Pakistan of repeatedly violating the truce, adding it has retaliated.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announced the ceasefire on social media. Pakistan praised the President as a peacemaker. India, however, did not.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio leads us off tonight from our London bureau.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Yes, this appears to be a breakthrough, Jericka, but actions speak louder than words, and that full and immediate ceasefire that President Trump posted about does not appear to be holding.\par \par \uc2 This is coming from India`s Foreign Secretary, and reports out from Indian administered Kashmir, blasts have been heard in the cities of Srinagar and Jammu with projectiles and flashes of light seen in the night sky like this.\par \par \uc2 India`s Foreign Secretary says the Indian Army was retaliating and dealing with what it called a border intrusion, similar to last night, when India and Pakistan fired missiles from fighter jets and launched drones at each other.\par \par \uc2 India said that it targeted Pakistani air bases and Indian colonels said Pakistan had earlier fired at health and school facilities. The two have been locked in this latest violence for the past two-and-a-half weeks.\par \par \uc2 On April 22nd, gunmen massacred 26 tourists in Indian administered Kashmir. Most of those killed were Indian Hindus. Delhi accuses Islamabad of being involved in that attack, and that is a charge the country vehemently denies.\par \par \uc2 The two have fought three wars since the 1940s over control of Kashmir in the High Himalayas. It is estimated tens of thousands of people have been killed since then.\par \par \uc2 And dozens more have been killed in the past week, Jericka. This is the worst violence between the two neighbors in the past three decades.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, a story we will continue to stay on top of.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a show of unity in Ukraine. The leaders of Britain, France, Germany and Poland met with President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  in Kyiv. They are threatening to ratchet up pressure on Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  if he does not accept an unconditional 30-day ceasefire in that war.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin called that proposal "confrontational."\par \par \uc2 President Trump backs the new ceasefire demand in Ukraine, the President seen here spending the weekend in the nation`s capital today golfing at his course in nearby Sterling, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Willie James Inman is at the White House, where there is a new challenge to the President`s agenda -- Willie James.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Jericka, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, a federal judge in California is pausing efforts to drastically downsize the government, a move the President says would help reduce spending.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN (voiceover): Firings at, at least 20 government agencies and massive spending cuts are paused for two weeks as a federal judge in San Francisco considers a challenge brought by employee unions.\par \par \uc2 In the court filing, Judge Susan Illston wrote: "The President likely must request congressional cooperation to order the changes he seeks." The case, the latest episode to put the brakes on the Trump agenda with hundreds of pending court challenges nationwide, and comes as the White House pushes for funding of the President`s other priorities, including boosting border security.\par \par \uc2 The President now suggesting taxing the rich as a way to pay for his plans. That is expected to rattle Republicans in charge of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Friday)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I would love to be able to give people in a lower bracket a big break.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: There`s new pushback on the President`s immigration policies. This confrontation at a detention center in New Jersey, the Democratic mayor of Newark, Ras Baraka, arrested for trespassing.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RAS BARAKA (Newark, New Jersey): If I was on their property, I was invited there. Somebody allowed me to go in. I didn`t climb a fence, I didn`t kick the door down. I didn`t bust the window like their friends did at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: A Homeland Security Official calling it a political stunt, warning there could be more arrests coming.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox)\par \par \uc2 TRICIA MCLAUGHLIN (DHS Assistant Secretary for Public Affairs): We won`t stand for it. We`re not playing games in this administration.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: The episode comes as the Trump administration says it is considering suspending the constitutional right to due process for undocumented migrants to speed up deportations.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER\uc2  (Deputy Chief-of-Staff for Policy and Homeland Security Adviser): The privilege of the Writ of Habeas Corpus can be suspended in a time of invasion, so this is an option we`re actively looking at.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Jericka, the last time Habeas Corpus was suspended took place in Hawaii after the surprise attack on Pearl Harbor. It has never been used to handle illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Willie James Inman for us at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Progress in the trade war between the U.S. and China. For the first time, negotiators from the two countries are meeting. It`s happening in Geneva, Switzerland. They`ll be back at it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to the trade war, West Coast ports are ground zero. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston is at the largest one there in Los Angeles -- Elise.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Jericka, the flow of goods from China into the U.S. has slowed dramatically. The drop off in activity could mean job cuts, shortages, and tough choices for consumers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Normally bustling berth sit empty at the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 , facing 145 percent tariffs, cargo coming from China is estimated to be down more than a third, according to port officials. Less cargo means fewer jobs for dock workers, truck drivers, and others in the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All of us, truck drivers, we`re moving less freight, and less freight means less money.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: L.A. officials say the tariffs threaten $500 billion in revenue and put two million local workers at risk. While painful, some say the tariffs are a step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GUTIERREZ (Truck driver): We believe this administration is doing a good job. It`s just that it`s going to take a little time.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The slowdown at U.S. sports is likely to ripple through the economy, impacting transportation, warehousing and other businesses.\par \par \uc2 Consumers may eventually see less inventory on store shelves and higher prices. Experts say savvy shoppers who have been waiting to buy may want to purchase cars, appliances, electronic goods and furniture before July 2nd.\par \par \uc2 California`s Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is pushing back on Trump`s tariffs. He`s running an ad on Fox News saying the trade war is harming the state`s economy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gov. Gavin Newsom`s TV commercial, Fox Business)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GAVIN NEWSOM (D-California): These tariffs punish families and risk ending America`s run as the world`s greatest economy. Take it from California. We`re the ones leading it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Other West Coast ports are also feeling the brunt of tariffs. Officials at docks in Seattle, in Tacoma, say they`re expecting a 40 percent drop in cargo volume this month -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That is significant. Elise Preston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s no end to the travel troubles at Newark`s Liberty Airport -- runway construction, air traffic control shortages, and two communication outages have caused massive delays, alarming passengers.\par \par \uc2 The airport is one of the busiest in the country and a global hub for United, which is cutting flights to Newark.\par \par \uc2 Today, CEO, \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2  spoke to \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ed O`Keefe for "Face the Nation."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: If you`re pruning the schedule then, that means less supply with increasing demand going into the summer, doesn`t that potentially mean the cost of a ticket is going to go up?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : My guess is, if you look at the ticket prices flying out of Newark right now, that in fact, is not what is happening. There`s less demand because there`s concerns about safety and so what we`ve also done is put bigger airplanes on the route.\par \par \uc2 So put a larger airplane, have fewer flights, but with bigger airplanes to maintain the number of seats that we have flying in and out of Newark and air travel remains a pretty strong bargain across the country and around the globe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Watch the complete interview with \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 ` CEO \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2 , tomorrow on "Face the Nation."\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Pope Leo XIV is preparing to address the faithful in Saint Peter`s Square. Sunday`s mass will be his first as head of the world`s Roman Catholics.\par \par \uc2 Today, the pontiff took his first trip outside the Vatican to visit the tomb of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 . As the new Pope lays out his vision for the future, we are learning new details about his past.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kati Weis has been digging into the Pope`s Creole roots there in New Orleans -- Kati.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: Well, Jericka, behind me here is the Saint Louis Cathedral in New Orleans` French Quarter. Records show that the Pope`s great grandmother was actually baptized here at this Cathedral in 1840 and that his maternal family has deep roots in the Big Easy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS (voiceover): Pope Leo XIV, the first American leader of the world`s Roman Catholic faithful embodies the multicultural fabric that is the United States. His connection to New Orleans researched by historian, Jari Honura.\par \par \uc2 JARI HONURA (Historian): It highlights the role that Catholics of color, that Black Catholics have always played.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: Historical records show that all four of the Pope`s maternal great grandparents were free people of color. His maternal grandparents listed as mulatto and Black.\par \par \uc2 JARI HONURA: This just underscores how interwoven all of our families are.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: The family moved with the Great Migration North, away from the Jim Crow South. They settled in Chicago in the early 1900s where Pope Leo grew up.\par \par \uc2 His brother told \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , they rarely spoke about their Creole roots, and Pope Leo has not spoken publicly about his racial identity.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there`s a possibility, then, if you were to go further back, that some of his lineage may have been enslaved?\par \par \uc2 JARI HONURA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: The new Pope`s ancestry inspiring New Orleans Catholics.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think if there was going to be a first American Pope, this is a very good person to take on that role.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KATI WEIS: And New Orleans Mayor \uc2 LaToya Cantrell\uc2  says she`s thrilled to learn about the Pope`s Louisiana connections. She says she hopes he uses them to build a more just and compassionate world -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Kati Weis for us in New Orleans, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, a 4.1 magnitude earthquake jolted parts of the Southern US that originated in Tennessee and was felt more than 120 miles away, including in Atlanta. No injuries or damage has been reported.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the weather, what many of us want to know is, how is it looking for Mother`s Day? Let`s check in with CBS News meteorologist, Andrew Kozak in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yes, Jericka, we`ve got a busy Mother`s Day weekend ahead with active weather on both coasts. We`re talking about more record heat out in the Desert Southwest, but very pleasant conditions in the mid- section of the country. All eyes on the Southeast, where we have flooding rain.\par \par \uc2 In fact, from Florida, the Panhandle over to areas like Atlanta, and then over toward areas like Charleston, we could be looking at several inches of rain the next couple of days. This area of low pressure will continue to stay basically across the same area, bringing waves of moisture in from the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 So for tonight, the rest of your Saturday night, flood risk with the Panhandle tomorrow, that area expands, especially as we head into tomorrow afternoon.\par \par \uc2 If you`re down here, we could be looking at anywhere between four and six inches of rain by the time all is said and done on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Now to the West Coast, a scorcher of a Sunday, where temperatures will be in the triple digits, in many cases, 10 to 20 degrees above average. The good news though, by the time we get into Tuesday, areas that could be near 100 like Vegas drop into the 70s. Some relief.\par \par \uc2 For Mother`s Day at a glance, we`ve got that to deal with, beautiful across the midsection of the country, and if you`re in New England, plenty of sunshine to celebrate mom -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Andrew Kozak, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 With Mother`s Day here or around the corner, so is flower frenzy. This is the busy L.A. Flower Market packed with sellers and buyers. According to the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 , Americans will spend more than $34 billion on Mother`s Day this year, the second highest total in nearly two decades.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, food activists or fear monger? This woman says she has a recipe to make America healthy again. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: This week, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved three new natural food color additives, a big step in the Trump administration`s push to phase out petroleum-based dyes.\par \par \uc2 Helping to lead the charge, food activist and social media influencer, Vani Hari who has millions of followers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adam Yamaguchi reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Natural Products Expo West)\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI (Food Babe founder and Truvani): We`ve awakened the entire country to these issues.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI (voiceover): You may not know her name --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: -- but at the Natural Food Expo, where the crowd is obsessed with how healthy their food is, Vani Hari, also known as the Food Babe, is as big a deal as it gets.\par \par \uc2 You`re treated like a rock star in this space here.\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: I`m just so excited that I`ve been part of this movement to tell people the truth about what`s been done to their food.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Food Babe)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: As an influencer with nearly four million followers, Hari supported Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.`s nomination and now sits on his grassroots food advisory panel.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Natural Products Expo West)\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: At the highest levels of leadership, we are talking about the issues that I have been fighting for, for over a decade.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Hari has no formal nutrition training, but embraces emerging research linking additives like emulsifiers, dyes, and preservatives to obesity and other chronic disease.\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: We`ve allowed the food companies to inundate our food supply with all of these manmade chemicals that I think don`t belong in the human body.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Food Babe Social Media posts)\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: This is a box of Froot Loops from Canada.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Hari first went on the offensive when she discovered that many iconic American processed foods contain dyes and other ingredients that are not found in the same products outside the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Food Babe Social Media posts)\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: That next time you want to go into Chick-Fil-A and get that frosted lemonade, you need to hear my voice.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Next, Hari moved from the grocery aisles to the fast food lane.\par \par \uc2 Skeptics question Hari`s self-taught expertise and her self-interest in selling her own food products.\par \par \uc2 You also have a lot of critics who say she doesn`t know what she`s talking about.\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: You know, I would say, sometimes we`ve just got to use our common sense. And you don`t need to be a rocket scientist to know how to eat.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Growing up in North Carolina, this daughter of Indian immigrants struggled with eczema, inflammation and excess weight. Then she cut out ultra-processed food.\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: The weight came off and never came back. I went off nine prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 Cheers.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 But Hari knows it will take influence from all sides to make America eat healthier.\par \par \uc2 VANI HARI: Our regulatory agencies have utterly failed us. And it is up to citizens like me to hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Adam Yamaguchi, CBS News, Charlotte, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a Soviet spacecraft crashed lands on Earth after a journey that started a half century ago.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Today, a well-known New Jersey landmark is no more. A former hotel that stood out for decades at the crossroads of an interstate and other highways was demolished with a dozen explosions. The 22-story building had stood in Mahwah since 1987.\par \par \uc2 Russia said a Soviet-era spacecraft returned to Earth today after looping through space for -- get this -- 53 years. It was launched in 1972 designed to land on Venus, but the craft never made it, becoming stranded in Earth`s orbit after a rocket booster failed, Russia says it splashed down in the Indian Ocean, west of Jakarta, Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a suspenseful conclave run by kids, but first, a look back at this day in history.\par \par \uc2 Graphics onscreen: This Day In History --\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1968.\par \par \uc2 Peace talks to end Vietnam War begin in Paris.\par \par \uc2 WALTER CRONKITE: The long hope for Vietnam peace talks began here today.\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1980.\par \par \uc2 Chrysler gets $1.5 billion bailout loan from \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: The federal government will bail \uc2 Chrysler Corporation\uc2  out with a massive loan guarantee package.\par \par \uc2 This Day In History: 1994.\par \par \uc2 Nelson Mandela\uc2  becomes South Africa`s first Black President.\par \par \uc2 DAN RATHER: \uc2 Nelson Mandela\uc2  today climaxed his astonishing rise from prisoner to President.\par \par \uc2 NELSON MANDELA\uc2 : The time for the healing of the wounds has come.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We end tonight in Chicago where students at a Catholic school had reason to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s "Weekend Journal," \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Noel Brennan shows us how they were first in electing a local Pope.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOY: Who would you vote for?\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN (voiceover): When the mystery of the Papal Conclave meets a kid`s imagination?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It is time for our Conclave to begin.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: Chicago`s Our Lady of Mount Carmel Academy becomes the Sistine Chapel.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Let the Holy Spirit be happy with you.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: Suddenly, a fourth grader like Cameron Smith --\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SMITH (Fourth grade): What would you do if you became Pope?\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: Feels the weight of a Cardinal in Rome?\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SMITH: Wow. There`s so many good options.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: Cameron has to vote like a Cardinal and think like one, too.\par \par \uc2 BOY: If you want to be a Pope at all, it would be an interesting answer.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: Teachers, parents and their parish helped plan this mock conclave.\par \par \uc2 BOY: We really have no idea who is going to get chosen.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: It`s up to 20 kid cardinals to pick their Pope.\par \par \uc2 BOY: They need to be humble.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SMITH: I think you`ve got to be kind, respectful.\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: Be a Pope of the people.\par \par \uc2 BOY #2: What makes a good Pope is a Pope that`s holy and has a good heart forever.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: How many votes is this going to take?\par \par \uc2 BOY: I think maybe four to six. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: Four to six.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: And we`re on two.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SMITH: We think that`s not fair.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: As Cameron and the Cardinals start campaigning for each other, front runners crop up.\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: Augie up here. It`s going to be him.\par \par \uc2 BOY #2: I`m confident, but I am not confident.\par \par \uc2 BOY #3: Now, we have to eat because we`re so stressed out.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: No consensus on a Pope just yet. The cardinals have finished a snack break and are in their third round of voting.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SMITH: They say we`re getting very close to a Pope, and everyone`s freaking out. It could be anyone.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: The palpable papal suspense finally lets.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Cardinal Augustus Wilk.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: And it is fourth grader, Augie Wilk --\par \par \uc2 BOY: You did it.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: -- who`s gotten at least two-thirds of the vote.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 BOY: You did it.\par \par \uc2 AUGUST WILK (Fourth grade): I learned, let the Holy Spirit guide me through all my voting, and like to make the right choices. I also learned it`s good to have a little bit of fun, but also stay humble.\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: White smoke signals the news to the entire school.\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: Here.\par \par \uc2 BOY #2: Red curtains.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NOEL BRENNAN: And in this moment, you`d swear you were in Saint Peter`s Square.\par \par \uc2 Noel Brennan, CBS News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: A cute Conclave.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Saturday. I`m Jericka Duncan in New York.\par \par \uc2 We thank you for watching. Have a good night, and we`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020250511el5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For March 13, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Holly Williams, Kelly O`Grady, Nancy Cordes, David Schechter,  Lonnie Quinn, Tom Hanson, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 4,097 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We start with a correction.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That is what they call it when stocks are down 10 percent from their all-time highs. The S&P 500 entered that territory today.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The Nasdaq was already there, and the Dow is just 300 points away.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The corrections are part of the confusion over the rapid changes coming out of Washington, on-and-off tit for tat tariffs, federal job cuts, now including the post office, and the possibility of a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Today, the commander in chief of the trade war threatened a Whiskey Rebellion. President Trump said he will put a 200 percent tariff on European alcohol if the E.U. puts a 50 percent tax on American whiskey in retaliation for his tariffs on steel and aluminum.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Kelly O`Grady here now to look at the impact of those metals tariffs here at home -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Well, those 25 percent tariffs on imported aluminum and steel are already in place and could impact industries you wouldn`t expect.\par \par \uc2 So we spent time with one New York-based craft beer maker who was figuring out how to manage these new costs when her expenses were already ticking up.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND (Brewer): So, the three main things that are on our mind with imports right now are steel, which are all these tanks, aluminum for the cans, and the grain that goes into the beer.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY (voice-over): LeAnn Darland is a brewer based in New York. Tariffs are the latest challenge in her competitive industry.\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: We`re still growing and we`re very grateful for that. But margins aren`t wonderful. It`s still a challenge.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And what are some of those challenges?\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: What keeps me up at night most is just the changing consumer preference towards alcohol, other than that, just rising costs of everything.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Darland and her business partner have opened five TALEA Beer Co. taprooms in four years. The cost of aluminum in the last five years has increased by more than 30 percent. Grain costs 20 percent more and steel is up more than 40 percent. And that`s before tariffs.\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: So aluminum costs represent 25 percent of the cost of a case of beer for us. If we were to take that on ourselves, I mean, that`s obviously going to impact our bottom line.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: How much of that cost would you pass on to the consumer?\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: For the beer we sell in our taprooms, we wouldn`t increase our prices. For distribution, I think, again, it would be a conversation with us and our distributor and our supplier to say, like, how can we kind of spread this increased cost across all of us?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: LeAnn Darland later told us her cost could go up by a dime per can with tariffs on aluminum and grain. In an industry with tightening profits, though, she remains hopeful.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling about the economy overall right now?\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: I think we will have a few years of not turmoil, but uncertainty. The great thing about beer is that historically it`s been recession-proof.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Kelly, since we`re at the bar, President Trump has threatened a 200 percent tariff on European champagne and wine. And then, today, he said all of his tariffs, he`s just going to keep going with it.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Well, and that`s exactly why you saw the markets drop so significantly. Investors don`t like tariff turmoil. And, by the way, neither do businesses.\par \par \uc2 So that 200 percent potential retaliatory tariff on wine coming from the E.U. nations, I actually spoke with a small business owner. She imports wine. She sells it. And she says, look, I`m already dealing with the fact that people aren`t consuming as much alcohol. And this is really going to hurt her. Does she stockpile? Does she pass that cost on to customers? Does she rotate out to importing from other regions?\par \par \uc2 It`s just so much uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And speaking of uncertainty, the markets are dealing with that tomorrow. We have got the possible government shutdown and then consumer sentiment data coming out tomorrow as well?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And that feeds into this cycle of uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, the government shutdown, it can rattle markets a little bit, but that consumer sentiment, we already saw it drop. So what happens? Consumers spend less.\par \par \uc2 Then CEOs, they say, well, consumers are spending less. And the \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  CEO said today, customers only have enough money for the basic necessities. That rattles markets. And then consumers have more anxiety, even though inflation is getting better based on the data the last two days. It`s just this vicious cycle.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It truly is.\par \par \uc2 All right, Kelly O`Grady, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The post office says it will eliminate 10,000 jobs through voluntary retirement. More than 11 percent of its employees are military veterans. The post office says it values the skills they bring to the job.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Vets make up 30 percent of the overall federal work force. Many who were new on the job were fired in the Trump-Musk purge.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes reports, they feel their years of service are not being valued at all.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): Jonathan Hairston has served his country for nearly eight years including six in uniform doing counterintelligence in the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIRSTON (U.S. Veteran): I took an oath to protect and serve this country against all enemies foreign and domestic. And, right now, our rights as civil servants, as public servants, it`s all in question.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Hairston worked as a civilian A.I. specialist until three weeks ago, when he was fired as part of a purge at CISA, the agency responsible for government cybersecurity.\par \par \uc2 The form letter Hairston received claimed he was not fit for continued employment.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIRSTON: I feel like I have been dishonorably discharged without any voice of concern, no answers to any questions.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Your service was disregarded?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIRSTON: Service was disregarded.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It`s estimated at least 6,000 veterans have lost their government jobs over the past month.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DOW (U.S. Veteran): Kind of ironic.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Many of them are disabled vets like Stephen Dow (sp?).\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DOW: And then they said: "You have about five minutes. Pack up your personal things.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Dow served in the Air Force for 20 years. A blast in Afghanistan left him with a broken neck.\par \par \uc2 Why did you decide to look for a job with the federal government?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DOW: Because I want to continue to serve.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: He started working as an executive assistant at the Pentagon nine months ago.\par \par \uc2 I mean, your review says you have a mastery of technology. "Steve sets the standard for productivity." You were good at your job.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DOW: I was. Yes, I take initiative for things. I find things that are broken and try to fix them. And that`s ingrained in me from when I was in the military.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Did you vote for President Trump?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DOW: I did three times.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Why?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DOW: Because I thought he was the right person for the job. And I didn`t expect this.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: So what`s your message to him now?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DOW: Take a closer look.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I asked President Trump about that the other day.\par \par \uc2 Are you tracking how many veterans have been fired so far?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And do you want DOGE to avoid letting them go?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We are. We are. And we take good care of our veterans. So we`re watching that very carefully.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But CBS News has found no evidence that the administration is tracking veteran layoffs or trying to prevent them.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HAIRSTON: It`s harmful to the mental health of veterans, right? Some of us have lost friends and family members serving this country. To get treated like this, it`s a disservice to their lives. It`s a disservice to our service.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Nancy Cordes joins us from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, today, a federal judge said the Trump administration has to rehire those probationary employees that they fired. And the judge said basically this is because the Trump administration fired them on false pretenses?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right.\par \par \uc2 He says that, in many cases, these workers were told that they were poor performers, when there`s evidence that was simply not true. Now, this case is going to continue to work its way through the courts. But it underscores the fact that these probationary employees, like the ones that I spoke to, they do have some rights. You can`t just fire them without cause.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why the government claimed they were poor performers. But when it comes to veterans in particular, John, there`s a reason why the federal government has always prioritized hiring them. It`s because they tend to be prompt. They`re experienced. They`re dedicated to the mission. They tend to be among the best performers.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At this point, Nancy, does it seem that they have any hope of being reinstated?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: They told me they do have hope. They want to go back to their jobs. But that puts them in a kind of limbo. They know they need to start looking for new jobs, even as they wait to see whether they will be reinstated.\par \par \uc2 And this is a challenge not just for them, but for so many workers. And they know that more cuts, potentially even deeper cuts, are coming. So even if they do get their old jobs back, they could be fired again.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Nancy Cordes on the story from the White House tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 100 protesters were arrested today inside Trump Tower in New York. A Jewish group led the protest, demanding the release of Mahmoud Khalil. He`s the former Columbia grad student facing deportation over his role in last year`s campus protests against the war in Gaza. The Trump administration wants to revoke Khalil`s green card, saying he spread \uc2 Hamas\uc2  propaganda.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump was forced to withdraw his nominee to run the CDC. Dave Weldon, a vaccine critic and former Republican Congressman, did not have enough votes for confirmation.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The death of a former Columbine High School student wounded in the 1999 shooting has been ruled a homicide. Anne Marie Hochhalter, who was shot twice, spent the rest of her life in a wheelchair. The coroner says her injuries contributed to her death last month from sepsis at age 43. That brings the Columbine death toll to 14.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  showed little enthusiasm today for a U.S.-proposed cease-fire in Russia`s war with Ukraine. Putin said he likes the idea in principle, but details have to be worked out.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is in the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 Now, Holly, you have reported from the region for a good 11 years now. You have seen Putin ignore a lot of agreements. Any reason to believe he would follow this one?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Well, Maurice, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  certainly doesn`t look like a man who`s ready to lay down his guns. We saw him visiting troops in Southern Russia in a video released yesterday, talking strategy with his commanders.\par \par \uc2 Also, Russia currently has the upper hand on the battlefield. It is gaining ground. And \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has essentially tied his political legacy to this war. All of that makes it highly unlikely that Putin actually wants a cease-fire. On the other hand, Putin may not want to anger President Trump by simply rejecting his attempt to achieve peace in Ukraine. So it`s possible we will see some delaying tactics.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Picking up, Holly, on that idea of angering President Trump, President Trump said, if Putin doesn`t go for this deal, that there will be economic consequences. What might he be talking about?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Well, John, we don`t know, but President Trump might be talking about trying to shut down Russia`s so-called shadow fleet of oil tankers. That is hundreds of tankers that use deceptive or illegal means to evade sanctions on Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 And if the U.S. and its allies actually did that, they would do deep damage to Russia`s economy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Holly Williams in Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Holly.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn with dangerous weather as we head toward the weekend.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: I`m Tom Hanson in Waterbury, Connecticut, where a man claims he escaped 20 years of captivity from this home behind me. We will share the details that left us and local police stunned.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: I`m David Schechter in Sebago, Maine. Winter is getting warmer, and that`s having a not-so-sweet impact on the trees that make our maple syrup.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Firefighters in Connecticut responded to a house on fire.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: What they found was a house of horrors.\par \par \uc2 And now Tom Hanson reports, the owner is under arrest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voice-over): This Waterbury, Connecticut, home turned into a prison for a 32-year-old man who says he was held captive for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Chief Fernando Spagnolo has spent three decades in law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 FERNANDO SPAGNOLO (Waterbury, Connecticut, Police Chief): This is the most horrific act of inhumanity that I have ever witnessed as a police officer. And, frankly, in this position, travel a lot around the country, talk to my peers at different conferences and summits. And I have just never really heard anything as terrible as this story.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Police arrested the man`s step mother on Wednesday; 56-year- old Kimberly Sullivan was charged with assault, kidnapping and unlawful imprisonment. She`s denied any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 The victim says he lit the house on fire to escape using hand sanitizer, paper and a lighter.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF FERNANDO SPAGNOLO: We believe that he may have walked through the fire to get out of the room that he was in. But he stands at 5`9`` and he weighs 68 pounds.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF FERNANDO SPAGNOLO: It`s amazing that he`s alive.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: The warrant for the stepmother`s arrest states the alleged victim says he was locked in a second-floor room that measured eight by nine feet. He was only let out for 15 minutes to two hours a day to do house chores.\par \par \uc2 IOANNIS KALOIDIS (Attorney): She genuinely appeared shocked when I told her about these allegations.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Ioannis Kaloidis is Sullivan`s attorney.\par \par \uc2 IOANNIS KALOIDIS: She was not his primary caretaker as he was growing up.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: She wasn`t?\par \par \uc2 IOANNIS KALOIDIS: No.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Who was?\par \par \uc2 IOANNIS KALOIDIS: His father was.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: His father died January of 2024. That still leaves a gap of time before the victim was found by police.\par \par \uc2 IOANNIS KALOIDIS: And during that time, my client is adamant that there was no locks used on those doors, there was no restraint imposed on him.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Tom Hanson joins us now from Waterbury, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Tom, this young man was 11 when this allegedly started. Did the authorities ever stop by the house in any way?\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Yes, well, John, we`re learning that authorities actually did stop by at least twice in 2005, but they didn`t find any signs of neglect. They said the house was in good living condition. They were just as shocked as anyone when they came across this scene back in February.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Unbelievable story.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson tonight in Waterbury, Connecticut, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dangerous weather will be moving across the country as we head into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn here now to tell us it`s in the West right now and will be in the East by Sunday -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 And what it leaves behind in the West are mudslides. We talked about it yesterday. This is \uc2 Riverside County\uc2  in California. \uc2 Zoom\uc2  in tight right here, because you`re going to find these cars, I mean, they`re not just like sort of stalled. They are caked in mud. That`s all the way up through the wheel wells.\par \par \uc2 And that storm is currently now into the mountain area of Utah. You open up the doors. This is Alta, Utah, earlier this morning. You`re going to lose everything here as it whites out, picking up over an inch per hour. They have already got six inches of snow. They`re going to finish up with about two to three feet.\par \par \uc2 So where does that mountain snow go as we push into the weekend? It moves to the east. And by the time it gets to St. Louis, we`re talking about straight-line winds, but St. Louis could very well have some winds that are blowing 60, 70, maybe even 80 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 And then, from St. Louis, I`m focusing on this area. We talked about it yesterday. That area in the Deep South, some of those cells could really percolate and we could be dealing with tornadoes this weekend. I think it`s probably going to end up being a weekend that we talk tornadoes by the time I`m back with you guys on Monday.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, Lonnie, tell us about the show in the sky tonight.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: OK, the full total lunar eclipse, a great thing to see. Where do you want to be? Where the clouds are not. Unfortunately, quite a bit of cloud cover at that hour, about 2:00 in the morning.\par \par \uc2 So, the northern fringe, you got clouds, the East Coast, the West Coast, clouds, and Texas, south of the -- South, in the country, you have got clouds, the midsection, a place like St. Louis, probably one of the better spots to see it early, early in the morning, 2:00 to 3:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lucky them. Lonnie Quinn, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Climate change is making this a challenging time for the people who produce our maple syrup.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: As winter turns to spring, this is the sweet season for maple syrup production, when the weather`s warming and the sap is flowing. But climate change is threatening this $1.5 billion industry.\par \par \uc2 David Schechter has tonight`s Eye on America from Sebago, Maine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER (voice-over): You know you`re in Maine when the pancakes come hot off a 100-year-old wood stove.\par \par \uc2 I love that I`m having maple syrup with a maple syrup farmer.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: But drill into Alan Green`s eighth-generation maple syrup operation and you will find it doesn`t run as smooth as it used to.\par \par \uc2 ALAN GREENE (Maple Syrup Farmer): The last 10 years, we are definitely becoming warmer earlier. We`re not getting the cold and the gradual warmup. We`re getting warmup, warmup, warmup, with deep freezes in between.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: It`s been a cold winter here so far, but across the country over time, climate change has warmed winters by an average of four degrees.\par \par \uc2 The coldest parts of the country are warming the fastest. Compared to 1970, Maine now experiences 22 more warm winter days, when temperatures are above normal, which means the sap flows much earlier and gets boiled earlier than what Alan`s dad recorded in his annual syrup log.\par \par \uc2 ALAN GREENE: So, in 1971, the year I was born, the first time he...\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Me too.\par \par \uc2 ALAN GREENE: The first time he boiled was March 21. We finished our season on March 21 some years now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: And that`s when he started.\par \par \uc2 ALAN GREENE: He was just starting.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Projections show the prime range of sugar maple habitat shifting northward, where it`s cooler.\par \par \uc2 Jason Lilley is a professor of sustainable agriculture with the \uc2 University of Maine\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Are these maple trees under stress from climate change?\par \par \uc2 JASON LILLEY (Professor of Sustainable Agriculture, \uc2 University of Maine\uc2 ): I would say absolutely. I just don`t want producers to be going out of business and to see this industry start to decline. We can`t just sit around and watch this happen.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Instead, Lilley says, some producers are exploring different varieties of trees, actively fitting their forests, giving trees more space to grow.\par \par \uc2 Let`s tap a tree.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: And using plastic tubing and vacuum systems that are always on standby for early flowing sap.\par \par \uc2 ALAN GREENE: The technology has come about while climate change has been pushing us. And they`re a good blend. They`re working together. I think we need to be paying attention and be prepared for it. For those who aren`t, I think it`s going to cost them down the road.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Maple syrup is America`s original sweetener. European settlers learned to collect it from Native Americans. It`s a practice that goes back centuries. Now, within the span of decades, it`s all changing.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, David Schechter, Sebago, Maine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have another sweet story tomorrow, this one from Steve Hartman about a magical moment for a young boy.\par \par \uc2 And we will be back in a moment with a case of wardrobe malfeasance.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : There are many ways to cheat in sports.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You can cork a baseball bat to make it lighter, deflate a football to make it easier to grip.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But how do you cheat in ski jumping?\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: If you`re the Norwegian team, you alter the suits to make them more aerodynamic. Three more jumpers were suspended today. That`s five now, along with the head coach, assistant coach and the equipment manager.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A whistle-blower caught the alterations on camera. A reinforced thread was added to the suits to increase surface area, so the jumpers would fly farther.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It was done so well, the Ski and Snowboard Federation had to rip the suits open to find the extra material.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The coach and the equipment manager who performed the skull-stitchery apologized, saying: "We regret it like dogs."\par \par \uc2 Not clear if the athletes knew about the tampering. At least two said they did not.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The federation seized the suit as it investigates, leaving the fate of the team hanging by a thread. And they need to apologize to the dogs too.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : That`s the Evening News. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I will see you on Evening News Plus soon streaming on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you back here tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Have a good evening.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250314el3d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Major Education Department Cuts; Trump Tariffs in Place; House Passes Trump-Backed Spending Bill; Ceasefire in the Works; D.C. Flight Path Concerns; Dozens of New Measles Cases Across Several States; Ready to Return Home; 130-Year-Old Shipwreck Found in Lake Superior; Surveillance of Missing Spring Break Student; Justice Department Purge; Congressional Hearing Controversy; Higher Costs Brewing; 2025 Grocery Store Awards; Uzo Aduba Live; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Power and Spirit; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Caitlin Huey-Burns,  Weijia Jiang, Holly Williams, Kris Van Cleave, Kate Bilo, Omar Villafranca,  Mark Strassmann, Manuel Bojorquez, Scott MacFarlane, Kelly O`Grady \par \uc2 14,654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on a Wednesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is on assignment, we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration slashes half the Department of Education`s workforce, causing uncertainty over the agency`s future.\par \par \uc2 LINDA MCMAHON, (\uc2 U.S. Department of Education\uc2  Secretary): That was the President`s mandate. His directive to me, clearly is to shut down the Department of Education.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The U.S. says it will resume security support for Ukraine after the country agrees to a ceasefire with Russia.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (U.S. Secretary of State): We`ll take this offer now to the Russians, and we hope that they`ll say yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A student missing in the Dominican Republic is seen in new video the night she disappeared.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: You`re saying you respect the family, but you also have to respect the process of an investigation.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Billy Joel is postponing the next four months of his tour to recover from a recent surgery and undergo physical therapy.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON ("The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon" host): Tesla`s stock is plummeting, so Trump said he`s going to support Elon and buy a new \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States): Thank you all very much. I love Tesler --\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): Tesler rhymes with his daughter`s name, Ivanker, you know. Jesus Chrysler.\par \par \uc2 MAN: When you`re at the gas station, do you ever try to get it to stop at a perfect number?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The \uc2 Sacramento Kings\uc2  turned that into a game during time outs. They called the perfect pump, and this Kings fan, Tom, he`s going viral.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The goal for this fan here was to get exactly $25.00.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: He just got a perfect pump, baby.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: We`re hoping he got some free gas.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: That would be nice.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that`s a fun game. You know, when you`re pumping the gas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pump gas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, it always clicks off before it`s all the way full, and I would do --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. Yes, you`re trying to get that last drop, right? You`re like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, until it spills out --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One more. One more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re not supposed to do that. I don`t recommend it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you`re not supposed to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re not supposed to do that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, that`s why it clicks off.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just trying to get that last, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was me, Vlad, just get one more.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, congratulations to that guy. Exactly 25.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like that.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin though, I know you`ve heard about this, massive cuts to the \uc2 U.S. Department of Education\uc2 . More than 1,300 employees, they were fired last night. The agency`s workforce has now been cut nearly in half by the Trump administration. So you might expect, it is raising a lot of questions about how the government will now handle student loans and services for disabled students.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is following all of this.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, a lot of people looking for answers this morning. Hope you have something.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hey, good morning. Absolutely, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Trump has long said that he wants to get rid of the Department of Education, and yesterday, the administration took steps to do exactly that, but they also insisted that the agency will continue to deliver on key programs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: One by one, employees at the Department of Education exited the building Tuesday after receiving an agency wide e-mail telling them to vacate the office by 6:00 P.M. and that it would remain closed through Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Within hours, the Department announced it was laying off more than 1,300 employees. Among them Brittany Coleman, a Dallas-based Civil Rights lawyer.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY COLEMAN (Dallas-based Civil Rights lawyer): It`s very disappointing to know that the work that we do to try to help our students get great educational opportunities was not valued.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Look, the Department of Education is a big con job.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: President Trump has long complained the agency is ineffective and inefficient, saying he`d like his newly confirmed Education Secretary Linda McMahon to shut it down.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I want the states to run schools, and I want Linda to put herself out of a job.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yesterday, McMahon told Fox News, the job cuts were the first step in what`s likely to be a dismantling of the department.\par \par \uc2 LINDA MCMAHON: What we did today was to take the first step of eliminating what I think is bureaucratic bloat.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Established by Congress during the Carter administration, the Department of Education has a budget of around $80 billion, but its main job isn`t funding public schools. The bulk of the Department`s responsibilities include financial assistance to low income schools, support for students with disabilities, Civil Rights oversight, and federal student loans and grants.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY COLEMAN: Thanks to federal student aid, I was able to get the loans that I needed to help pay for my education, which allowed me to go to Law School and to be able to serve as a Civil Rights attorney to help other students achieve those same goals.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, abolishing the Department of Education completely would require an act of Congress. As Linda McMahon acknowledged in her confirmation hearing, but what we saw happen here yesterday follows a pattern of what we saw through \uc2 USAID\uc2 , which didn`t have the support of Congress, and there really are not the votes in Congress to get rid of the Department -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Caitlin, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Now to new global tariffs that take effect today on orders from President Trump. There is now an additional 25 percent charge on all steel and aluminum imports worldwide, which likely means higher prices here, and it`s got a lot of American companies rattled.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the White House became a car dealership yesterday, with an event on the South Lawn where President Trump appeared to want to know what`s it going to take to get you into a brand new \uc2 Tesla\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, quite a sight yesterday. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Tony. Good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 Quite a show, a staunch show of support for the world`s richest man, as many Americans look for reassurances about their own financial futures. President Trump`s message to them, everything is great, everything is fine, and he is moving ahead with more tariffs, no matter what Wall Street says.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: After Tesla`s stock tanked 15 percent on Monday, taking its biggest hit since 2020.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I want to buy one of these.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: President Trump turned the South Lawn of the White House into a car lot, making a sales pitch for Americans to buy a \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which he pledged to do.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m going to have it at the White House, and I`m going to let my staff use it.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Tesla`s stock climbed following Trump`s endorsement, but the markets were rattled Tuesday, after Trump posted an early morning threat to increase the fee on Canadian steel and aluminum imports from 25 to 50 percent in response to Ontario`s Premier \uc2 Doug Ford\uc2  imposing a 25 percent tariff on electricity going to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 But by the afternoon, Ford reversed course.\par \par \uc2 DOUG FORD\uc2  (Ontario, Canada Premier): We have both agreed, let cooler heads prevail.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Prompting Trump to back down, too, reverting to the planned 25 percent tariff, which begins today.\par \par \uc2 Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick brokered the truce.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK )U.S. Commerce Secretary): The markets are going to learn. Let the deal maker make his deals.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Lutnick says Trump`s policies will take time to implement, but he insisted it`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK: These policies produce revenues. They produce growth. They produce factories being built here.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Despite Wall Street`s reaction to the trade tensions, Trump said he does not think there will be a recession.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I don`t see it at all. I think this country is going to boom.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Just two days earlier, he was not so sure.\par \par \uc2 MARIA BARTIROMO\uc2  (\uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2  host): Are you expecting a recession this year?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I hate to predict things like that.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Tuesday evening, surrounded by the CEOs of America`s biggest companies who have watched their stock slide, Trump defended his economic strategy.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: The tariffs having a tremendously positive impact. They will have, and they are having.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Just a few hours ago, the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the world`s largest trading bloc responded to that increase in steel and aluminum tariffs. The E.U. announced plans to impose counter tariffs on about $28 billion worth of U.S. goods starting in April, saying it must act to protect consumers and businesses -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it, Weijia. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Congress took a major step toward avoiding a possible government shutdown. House Republicans narrowly passed a six month spending bill backed by President Trump with just one Republican voting no.\par \par \uc2 The bill now heads to the Senate, but it will need Democratic support to pass there. The deadline for all of this, midnight on Friday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In other news, Ukraine`s President has agreed to support a 30-day ceasefire in the war with Russia. In return, the U.S. will restore military and intelligence support to Ukraine that President Trump cut off after last month`s showdown in the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 Russia, meanwhile, says it will "carefully study this ceasefire offer." But carefully studying did not stop the Russians from continuing to bombard Ukraine yesterday, including this overnight strike in the country`s north.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It`s a confusing time for American diplomacy. After yesterday`s meeting in Saudi Arabia, the U.S. now appears to be back in Ukraine`s corner and calling on Russia to agree to a 30-day ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO: If they say no, then we`ll, unfortunately know what the impediment is to peace here.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian President): Thank you for invitation.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Yet, just last month, President Trump called Ukraine`s elected President a dictator.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You don`t have the cards.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Before publicly berating him at the White House and then suspending U.S. security assistance and intelligence sharing with Ukraine, both are crucial to this country`s military.\par \par \uc2 That pause has now been lifted, and the President`s Special Envoy Steve Witkoff is expected to head to Russia for talks later this week.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Through three years of war, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has committed well over $100 billion to support Ukraine. Separately, many volunteers have come from the U.S. to help as private citizens.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All right, the line is hot.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: We`ve seen them training Ukrainian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Our bullet is going where we want it to go.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And it`s thought hundreds of American volunteers have signed up to serve with Ukraine`s Army. For some of them, the Trump administration`s recent actions don`t sit well.\par \par \uc2 One American who is fighting for Ukraine`s survival posted this video after that explosive meeting in Washington.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s basically Russian propaganda out of the mouth of an American President. It`s shameful. I voted for that man two times.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Now, the question is whether \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will agree to a temporary ceasefire. His forces currently hold the momentum on the battlefield, but like Ukraine, Russia is thought to have suffered hundreds of thousands of military casualties.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Holly Williams in Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A new report says air traffic around Washington`s Reagan National Airport has been dangerous for years. \uc2 The National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  released its preliminary findings on January`s collision of a military helicopter and an American Airlines Flight.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has got the latest on that report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The force of the deadly mid-air collision over Washington that killed 67 left a two-foot long piece of black hawk tail rotor embedded in the wreckage of the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  regional jet, a horrific accident that increasingly looks preventable.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  found over the last 13 years, airliners coming into Washington Reagan National Airport received at least one collision alert a month due to a helicopter in close proximity. And in the last three years alone, at least 85 examples of extremely close calls. Helicopters and planes were routinely coming within 75 feet of each other during the approach to land.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY (\uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  Chair): The existing separation distances between helicopter traffic operating on Route 4 and aircraft landing on Runway 3-3 are insufficient and pose an intolerable risk to aviation safety by increasing the chances of a mid-air collision at DCA.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Within 90 minutes of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  issuing two urgent safety recommendations, Secretary of Transportation Sean Duffy permanently restricted the airport airspace from nearly all helicopter traffic.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY (\uc2 US Department of Transportation\uc2  Secretary): How did the \uc2 FAA\uc2  not know? How do they not study the data to say, hey, this is a hot spot. We`re having near misses, and if we don`t change our way, we`re going to lose lives. That wasn`t done.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: It makes you wonder what the \uc2 FAA\uc2  was doing. What data were they looking at?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  chair, Robert Sumwalt often landed at DCA as an airline pilot.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: Based on what we now know, I`m going to say this was an accident waiting to happen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says the air traffic controller that was monitoring both helicopter traffic and local airline traffic had been doing both of those jobs for about five hours leading up to the accident. The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  now says it may need to expand its look to other U.S. airports to see if the airplane-helicopter danger exists there as well -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Kris Van Cleave with that important reporting this morning. Thank you, Kris.\par \par \uc2 A powerful storm in the West could affect tens of millions of people as it crosses the U.S. Heavy rain today in California will bring a risk of flooding, and there could be severe storms from the Midwest to the Gulf Coast by the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to meteorologist, Kate Bilo at CBS News Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Kate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KATE BILO: Good morning to you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this is a very powerful storm, another atmospheric river moving into California, and it is going to impact eventually, really, much of the nation. Let`s go ahead and pull up our globe here and just take a look at this storm coming on shore as we go through the day today, and it`s going to move out into the Four Corners region very quickly, then kind of redevelop into a severe threat as we get into the weekend for portions of the Midwest, the Deep South, and eventually the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about the impacts first and foremost in Southern California today. We`re going to be measuring snow in the feet across the Sierra Nevada range, and we`re looking at upwards of two inches of rainfall across Southern California, which, especially for those burn scar areas could lead to the risk of runoff flooding and possible mudslides.\par \par \uc2 Then we shift to the severe weather threat. What we`re looking at here is what we call thunderstorm fuel, available energy for severe weather. And look how this just explodes across portions of the Midwest and the Mississippi Valley by Friday, cities like St. Louis, cities like Little Rock, will be in an enhanced risk for severe weather on Friday, and that threat will then shift to the East Coast as we get into Saturday and Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, I`ll send it back over to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Kate, I got it. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We are learning of dozens of new measles cases around the country, including the first reported case in Southern California. This worsening outbreak is drawing new attention to Health and Human Services Secretary, that`s Robert F. Kennedy, Jr, and his talk of alternatives to the measles vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca spoke to a doctor in Texas who says there is no alternative to vaccination.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: In Gaines County, a small corner of West Texas --\par \par \uc2 DR. WENDEL PARKEY (Seminole Family Medical clinic director): Should we talk to the nurses taking care of the mom?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Dr. Wendel Parkey is on the front lines of the state`s biggest measles outbreak in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 DR. WENDEL PARKEY: It`s something that can kill you, and it`s something that`s very easy to catch. If someone walks into a restaurant, they cough, it`s in there for eight hours.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Measles is a preventable disease that the U.S. considered eliminated in 2000 through vaccines, but since late January, the outbreak has sickened more than 200 mostly unvaccinated people in West Texas.\par \par \uc2 The majority are local Mennonites, a close-knit Christian community, largely wary of Western medicine.\par \par \uc2 You`re a doctor, but you`re also a member of this community.\par \par \uc2 DR. WENDEL PARKEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: What do you tell them about the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. WENDEL PARKEY: Are you going to roll the dice and not get vaccinated? That`s basically what you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Earlier this month, long-time vaccine skeptic and Health and Human Services Secretary Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., told Fox News, vaccinations should be a choice while touting alternate treatments.\par \par \uc2 We were talking about RFK, Secretary Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 DR. WENDEL PARKEY: Right.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: And he`s mentioned steroids and vitamin A.\par \par \uc2 DR. WENDEL PARKEY: I think it may help, but it doesn`t cure it. The MMR, the old vaccines are very well-researched. We know that they`re safe. And this other thing, you can`t be taking vitamin A forever. You take too much of it, it is bad for your bones. A month of vitamin A extra, absolutely wonderful. Five months, no, sir, no.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: For Parkey, there`s a constant balance to inoculate his patients without isolating them.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Omar Villafranca in Gaines County, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can we just give a shout out to Dr. Parkey?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We can, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am just meeting him for the first time, but I like his delivery, and I like the message that he is sending. I like this guy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Straight to the point, with a little personality as well. We like that.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the astronauts who went to space for eight days and are now up there for more than nine months and counting, we`ve got a big update on the mission to bring them home.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the latest on the search for an American student who disappeared while on spring break in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 But first it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, we talk with the star of one of our favorite shows, "Ghosts." Rebecca Wisocky will be here.\par \par \uc2 Plus, thousands of Billy Joel fans will not get to see him on tour. What he is saying at 7:47 in "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 This CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . Save when you bundle auto, home or motorcycle insurance.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tonight, at long last, the \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  rocket is scheduled to blast off from Florida, hopefully clearing the way for the long awaited homecoming of two stranded astronauts.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is at Kennedy Space Center.\par \par \uc2 Mark, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Butch and Suni`s space odyssey is almost over in just a few hours. Their placements will climb aboard a SpaceX Crew Dragon for a 10-hour flight to the International Space Station. Veteran \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut Anne McClain is the mission`s commander. Joining her are crewmates from the United States, Japan and Russia.\par \par \uc2 Wilmore and Williams have been at the Space Station since June. Their stay was extended after their ride home, a Boeing Starliner spacecraft developed propulsion problems during its test flight. \uc2 NASA\uc2  decided to return Starliner empty and keep Wilmore and Williams at the ISS.\par \par \uc2 President Trump and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  founder, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  have claimed Wilmore and Williams were abandoned for months in space by the Biden administration. Musk said he offered to bring the pair home sooner, but \uc2 NASA\uc2  has said it never considered that as a viable option.\par \par \uc2 The astronauts have said they don`t feel abandoned or stuck in space. That is just part of the job, and if tonight`s launch goes as expected, Wilmore and Williams could begin their overdue trip home as soon as Sunday aboard a SpaceX Crew Dragon, appropriately named, Freedom -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very appropriately named, Mark. Thank you very much. Appreciate it. We`re rooting for them.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, new video of the missing American student the last time she was seen in the Dominican Republic. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 A cargo ship that sank more than 130 years ago, after it was praised as one of the safest ships afloat, has been found at the bottom of Lake Superior near Michigan`s Upper Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 The Great Lakes Shipwreck Historical Society found the remains of the Western Reserve split in two under 600 feet of water.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Western Reserve, an all-steel 300-foot freighter sank during a powerful storm in 1892, killing 27 people, including its owner and his family.\par \par \uc2 Now, the news caused a huge public reaction, much like the Titanic disaster 20 years later. And the connection to the Titanic is after that ship sank, it was found that the metal that was used, the steel that was used in the ship had sulfur and sulfur phosphates. It was contaminated. They used a similar type of steel to build a Titanic. It hadn`t been outlawed in Britain yet by the time the Titanic was built.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But that wreckage is pretty clear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can really make out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think, once you hit that deep water and it`s so cold, it is preserved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it gets preserved.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is about a million shipwrecks on the bottom of bodies of water on this planet. We`ve discovered only a fraction of them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, we`ve got new information this morning in the disappearance of the \uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2  student. Her name is Sudiksha Konanki. She disappeared during a spring break trip.\par \par \uc2 Surveillance video shows this 20-year-old was walking with friends towards a beach at a restaurant in the Dominican Republic. It`s been nearly a week now, and that was the last time that she was seen.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is outside the resort in Punta Cana.\par \par \uc2 Manuel, good morning.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people really hoping and praying for good news on this one.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Absolutely, and some new information this morning, this is coming from the department -- the Sheriff`s Department in Loudoun County, Virginia, where Konanki is from, saying that the young man who was last seen with her at the beach is considered a person of interest in this case, but they stress that it remains a missing persons case and not a criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 This is all happening as we are getting a look at the final moments on surveillance video before she was reported missing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Surveillance video appears to show the final moments before 20-year-old Sudiksha Konanki vanished last week, walking with three women and two men towards the Rio Republica Resorts Beach area around 4:15 A.M. on March 6th.\par \par \uc2 Konanki and a man who officials say is an American that she was not traveling with, appeared to walk out with their arms around each other. Sometime later, cameras captured members of the group walking back to the hotel while Konanki and the man she was walking with stayed behind.\par \par \uc2 Then at 8:54 that morning, this video appears to show that man returning to the hotel alone.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything suspicious in there?\par \par \uc2 (DIEGO PESQUEIRA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: National Police spokesperson Diego Pesqueira said authorities are still investigating and cannot yet determine if Konanki`s disappearance is the result of an accident or foul play.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT LUIS ABINADER speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But Dominican Republic President Luis Abinader told reporters that the search for Konanki is focused near the water, because the man who was last with her said a wave hit them while they were on the beach.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Was it always just looking into a potential accident.\par \par \uc2 (DIEGO PESQUEIRA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: All we know is there`s a disappearance.\par \par \uc2 While Dominican and U.S. investigators look into what led to her disappearance, crews are expanding their perimeter searching for Konanki by air, sea, and land.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And as you know, Konanki`s parents have been here in Punta Cana. They arrived shortly after she was reported missing. They have been stressing and urging that officials here should explore all scenarios, including foul play and not just the possibility that she may have accidentally drowned -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, Manny, our hearts really go out to that family. Thank you very much for that report.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to talk with a whistleblower who is warning about the impact of a purge at the Justice Department. Why she is worried about the rule of law in these United States.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gorgeous hair.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re getting a new warning today about the potential effect of recent firings and demotions at the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane in conversation with an 18-year veteran of the Department who suggest independence and fairness could be on the chopping block in favor of loyalty to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Among agents and prosecutors who pursue terrorists, gangs, and drug cartels, there`s a spreading fear coming from inside their own office.\par \par \uc2 You`ve helped hundreds of Justice Department employees. Are they scared?\par \par \uc2 STACEY YOUNG (Justice Connection founder): They`re terrified. They`re being terrorized by this administration. They`re terrified about their careers. Many are terrified about their safety, and they`re terrified about the institution they serve.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Stacey Young, who served nearly two decades at the Department of Justice handled Civil Rights cases and helped author arguments for the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , including during the first Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 She quit the Department in January and has formed an organization Justice Connection to help those recently fired.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds have sought help so far.\par \par \uc2 STACEY YOUNG: There are people who have come to us who are being threatened now, and many, many more believe that they could be.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Including by the January 6 rioters, or just by supporters of the administration?\par \par \uc2 STACEY YOUNG: By January 6 rioters, by supporters of the administration, by others who have believed the Deep State narrative and the idea that DOJ career employees are somehow politicized.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: An ongoing purge of long time employees is growing. Among the newly ousted, the Department`s pardon attorney who alleges she was removed after refusing a request to intervene and recommend actor and Trump ally, Mel Gibson have his gun rights restored. Gibson lost them in 2011 after a domestic violence misdemeanor conviction.\par \par \uc2 And veteran \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent, James Dennehy, who helped lead the Bureau`s New York Field Office, but who defended agents who worked January 6 cases from scrutiny and alleged harassment from Trump allies. Dennehy supporters and colleagues showed up in force to applaud him as he departed.\par \par \uc2 The Attorney General is standing by the purge at the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , saying they`re going after those who oppose President Trump.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI (U.S. Attorney General): We`re going to root them out. We will find them, and they will no longer be employed.\par \par \uc2 But again, we`re starting at every level of the Justice Department, Sean, and getting rid of the worst of the worst.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But Young says many career employees are being impacted.\par \par \uc2 STACEY YOUNG: People at the department take the apolitical nature of the civil service and DOJ very seriously. They stay because their job is to enforce the rule of law, and that`s it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: There`s a growing consensus among our sources inside the Department of Justice, more changes, firings, and demotions are coming. The Department of Justice has defended this purge of employees, saying some can`t be trusted to execute the new administration`s vision and priorities.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve talked to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents and prosecutors, small and big cities nationwide, they warn, it is difficult to focus on their jobs chasing gangs or terror suspects when they worry the policies and norms they followed will nevertheless lead them to lose their jobs at any moment -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And presumably that puts us at risk.\par \par \uc2 Scott, thank you very much for your reporting. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 A congressional hearing on Arms Control and U.S. assistance to Europe ended abruptly yesterday after Texas Congressman Keith Self referred to transgender Congresswoman Sarah McBride as Mr. McBride, and then another member came to her defense. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEITH SELF (R-TX): I now recognize the representative from Delaware, Mr. McBride.\par \par \uc2 REP. SARAH MCBRIDE (D-DE) Thank you, Madam Chair.\par \par \uc2 REP. BILL KEATING (D-MA): Would you repeat what you just said? You introduced a duly elected representative from the United States of America, please.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEITH SELF (R-TX): I will. The representative from Delaware, Mr. McBride.\par \par \uc2 REP. BILL KEATING (D-MA): Mr. Chairman, you are out of order. Mr. Chairman, have you no decency? I mean, I have come to know you a little bit, but this is not decent.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEITH SELF (R-TX): We will continue with this hearing.\par \par \uc2 REP. BILL KEATING (D-MA): You will not continue it with me unless you introduce a duly elected representative the right way.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEITH SELF (R-TX): This hearing is adjourned.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, last night, McBride wrote on X: "No matter how I`m treated by some colleagues, nothing diminishes my awe and gratitude at getting to represent Delaware in Congress."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love how she is handling this, and it made me so sad to see that display. And I love the Congressman that came to her defense. This is a place where we are in this country though.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very, very upsetting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I like, he tried to give him some grace by saying, I`ve come to know you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And this isn`t a decent thing to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Address the person the way they want to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Quick way to come back there with the "Madame Chair" remark as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, "What to Watch" at 7:47. Tiger Woods, he is hurt again. Find out what that could mean for an important date in Augusta.\par \par \uc2 Right now, though 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead in our eight o`clock, actor, Rebecca Wisocky from the hit CBS comedy "Ghosts" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: By the way, one of Tony`s favorite shows, she`ll talk about a big twist for her character, Hetty. Big, big twist.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ll be joined by Emmy Award winner, I just heard she`s arrived.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Residence"/Netflix)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uzo Aduba is here. She`s a detective in the new show, it`s called "The Residence," and boy, she`s knock out in it. Can`t wait to see her, too.\par \par \uc2 So all of that`s coming up in our next hour, but right now, Klaus and I heading over to the table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m doing the pee dance. I`ve got to go pee. So let`s go. Let`s go to commercial. I`ll pee and then we`ll come back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got to be in at 7:47. "What to Watch." We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Tony --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh. I haven`t heard -- this is one of my favorite Elvis songs, "Kentucky Rain" from the 1970 album from "Elvis in Memphis." What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re standing here going crickets. I like "Blue Suede Shoes." I like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Kentucky Rain," my favorite Elvis era, and of course, we`re playing a song that references and talks about Kentucky, because we are remembering a former basketball star who became a billionaire businessman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Junior Bridgeman died yesterday after suffering a medical emergency during a fundraiser in his adopted hometown of Louisville, Kentucky. That`s why "Kentucky Rain."\par \par \uc2 Bridgeman played 12 years in the \uc2 NBA\uc2 , mostly with the Milwaukee Bucs, but he made his biggest impact in the business world. He was one of the largest restaurant franchise operators in the U.S. He owned hundreds of Wendy`s and Chili`s outlets. That`s where you make your money.\par \par \uc2 Bridgeman was also a bottle distributor for \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 . And guess what? He also bought "Ebony and Jet" Magazine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, he`s like a mogul.\par \par \uc2 According to Forbes, his estimated net worth is $1.4 billion. Bridgeman said in 2023, he was determined to learn everything about entrepreneurship for his family`s future. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUNIOR BRIDGEMAN (Former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player): So with athletes they think because I got to be a great player, that I should go in as the CEO or the executive vice president without putting the work in. So that`s the first thing I stress to my kids, is you have to understand, and I have three kids, and all of them had to go to college. They all had to get an MBA, and they all had to go to work for somebody else.\par \par \uc2 The way things worked out, one of them runs the restaurant side, one of them works with the \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  side, and my daughter runs the "Ebony and Jet" side. But be that, as it may --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Generational wealth. That`s what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Building that generational wealth, especially in the Black community, where it has been a struggle since the Jim Crow era to do so.\par \par \uc2 His three children, Eden, Justin, and Ryan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Eden, I know his daughter. Listen, I just saw him in February at a conference.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You did, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I did not know him as a basketball player. I just knew him as a class act, a very successful businessman, but such a great human being. I got the news yesterday. They said, did you hear the news about Junior? Never good when you get a text like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I hadn`t heard it, and when you go and \uc2 Google\uc2 , I felt a punch to the gut. This guy was such a class human being, very smart, very generous, very philanthropic. So his wife, Doris, is three kids, Eden, it was heartbreaking, and he was 71.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seventy-one is way too young.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was at a conference and collapsed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very young.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And maybe his biggest triumph is as a father to have three kids --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- who go the direction they went.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And now work with you is amazing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And who are all successful. Thinking of them today.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He will be missed. Absolutely. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 All right, now to legendary rocker, Billy Joel, he is rescheduling his next eight concerts because of a medical condition. Joel`s Instagram said that he needs to recover from recent surgery. No details were given.\par \par \uc2 Joel, who is 75 said he regrets postponing the shows, but his health must come first. I think we agree. We`re fans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "The Post" said Joel is expected to make a full recovery and resume touring in July.\par \par \uc2 Tiger Woods is also on the mend after surgery, but it looks like he will miss this year`s Masters. He announced on social media that he had surgery yesterday after rupturing his left Achilles tendon. His doctor said the procedure went smoothly, and Woods is expected to make a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 Last September, Woods had his sixth back surgery. We don`t know when the 49-year-old will return to competition.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he still wants to return to competition, it seems.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a shame he`s going to miss the Masters. I`ve been missing the Masters also. In fact, I`ve never been to the Masters, and yet I work for \uc2 CBS\uc2 . We air the Masters.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Talk to Nantz.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who do I need to call?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Call Jim Nantz.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know people, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got a whole list of people. Vlad, have you ever been?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve never been. Never been.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, the price of beer, it is going up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never been either, but thanks for asking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I assumed you had.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hetty is Irish, and actor Rebecca Wisocky will be here to talk all about "Ghosts," the twists and turns. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Emmy-winner, Uzo Aduba. She is here from the brand new show, "The Residence." She`s so good in it. It`s a murder mystery and she is the quirky detective.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 We are also joined by Rebecca Wisocky from one of my favorite series, the \uc2 CBS\uc2  series "Ghosts." She`ll talk about a very big reveal for her character, Hetty.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Save money on groceries with help from the editors at "Simply Recipes," they tested out hundreds of products to help you get a good deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guess what? They brought food.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener" -- I love when that happens -- at eight o`clock.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration begins to dismantle the Department of Education, announcing 1,300 new job cuts.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Abolishing the Department of Education completely, would require an act of Congress.\par \par \uc2 