ery headquartered in Brooklyn, has grown quickly in a few short years despite rising costs.\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: What`s hit us hardest is the cost of construction. We`ve built five locations in four years, and then our payroll costs. We`ve felt that year-over-year.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: And now on top of rent, labor costs, and keeping five tap rooms running, tariffs could trickle down into higher operating costs.\par \par \uc2 So what do you need to import to make the beer?\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: So, the three main things that are on our mind with imports right now are steel, which are all of these tanks. But luckily, we won`t need too much more of that right now. Aluminum for the cans and the grain that goes into beer.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Over the past five years, aluminum prices are up nearly 30 percent and steel up 43 percent, that`s before tariffs. But Darland is hopeful she can share any potential cost increases with suppliers.\par \par \uc2 If we were to see an import tax applied to aluminum, I mean, how big of an impact could that make?\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: So aluminum costs represent 25 percent of the cost of the case of beer for us. If we were to take that on ourselves, I mean, that`s obviously going to impact our bottom line, and we would have to figure out how we`re going to hopefully share the burden of those tariffs.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: If TALEA can`t share the cost of that aluminum tariff, it could mean a five cent increase per can. For food and beverage businesses like breweries, coffee shops, and restaurants, margins are tightening. Any cost increase eats into profits.\par \par \uc2 LEANN DARLAND: Sometimes we get complaints that our beer is too expensive, but everything is expensive now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: While Darland is hopeful TALEA can weather the storm, many other industries rely even more heavily on aluminum and steel. The housing and auto industries, for example,