UBA: Yes, absolutely, I think nerves are good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I saw you on stage with Taylor Swift a long time ago. You didn`t look so nervous then.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: I was nervous then. I was nervous then.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, and thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good work.\par \par \uc2 UZO ADUBA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Residence" can be streamed on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , March 20th.\par \par \uc2 "Talk of the Table" next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. Do you know what that is?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It looks like a turtle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Where`s that story?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For a little while on the third hour, we had a tortoise Thursday thing, but it`s not Thursday, so I don`t know what -- I don`t know what`s going on. Someone is very --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very cute.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Somebody should -- I don`t know what that picture means, but anyway, it was pretty.\par \par \uc2 Time to bring you some of the stories that we call "Talk of the Table." Because it`s a table, we be talking. Who is in pole? You, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is a dramatic discovery down the front of a man`s pants at Newark International Airport.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, this is why.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A man from East Stroudsburg, Pennsylvania, guys, you gave it away. He was caught by the TSA agent. I had a whole set up. He reached out his pants and he pulled out a five-inch turtle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why they showed it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A new meaning to the --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why were you so confused about the --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Guys? Why don`t you just tell him? He found a turtle in his pants. Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Tony, what was he doing with the turtle?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: trying to get it somewhere. I don`t know. It`s his buddy turtle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But is it illegal? Seriously?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, so quote --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it illegal to take a turtle on the plane?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Quote from Thomas Carter, TSA, Federal Security Director for New Jersey: "I believe this is the first time we have come across someone who is concealing a live animal down the front of his pants." In the tweet, though, the TSA added, "You can bring animals through checkpoints."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the guy should have been up front about the turtle in his pants.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and put it in a box.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How is up front?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: However -- hold. Hey. But it`s presumed that one theory is that he was trying to get it through security, because you can`t actually take it on -- many airlines don`t let you take a live animal on the flight itself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So TSA rules may not be the same.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. So did they return the turtle or --?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, they confiscated the man`s turtle, and I have no further information at this time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Jon Tower is going, Gayle, we don`t know where the turtle is. Move on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we have no idea where the turtle is. The man identified the turtle as a red eared slider turtle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Barely very impressive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do whatever Jon Tower says. I am moving on