p with one hand. He just like lobs them up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like the mom`s reaction. Does Katy laugh when you do that? Because I`m like, what are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She was nervous at first, and I was like, Katy, this is my third child, and now my fourth. I got this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the internet. They said, "Now that`s a husband." I like that. "Perhaps the most shareable real on the internet, a baby`s laugh." That`s why I like it, too. Is the best sound ever. I love --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, everybody who is watching this will hear that baby`s laugh --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And feel a little better this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. My "Talk of the Table" is about the growing number of Americans that say that they`re ready to move to another country.\par \par \uc2 Now you guys know, I`ve lived in Africa, I`ve lived in the Middle East, Asia, Europe, so it`s fun living abroad. There`s a new talk, a research poll that says, out of a survey of 2,000 Americans, they found 17 percent of those surveyed want to go. Ireland comes in at number five among the top countries Americans want to relocate to. Ten percent say they`d live in Australia, putting it at number four on the list. England is third, with culture and way of life being the big draw.\par \par \uc2 Food has Americans looking at Italy, which comes in at number two. And you ready for the top foreign country Americans want to live in?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would have thought it was Italy was number one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is Canada.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Canada. Oh wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh Canada. The country`s high quality health care, landscape, and some people say the practical approach to work life balance are given as the top reasons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s i