ony, who do you have?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very excited. We`ve got a spirit here in the greenroom. One of the actors from "Ghosts," Rebecca Wisocky. She`s going to tell us about the latest twists and turns on one of my favorite shows.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Luck of the Irish" takes on new meaning in this week`s episode of "Ghosts." The hit CBS comedy follows a group of ghosts in a mansion who can only be seen by the woman who lives there. Each ghost has a unique ghost power, and this week, at long last, we learn what Hetty`s ghost power is.\par \par \uc2 Hetty played by Rebecca Wisocky is the ghost of a Gilded Age aristocrat who recently learned she is actually Irish. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rebecca Wisocky joins us now. Okay, so that`s a monumental clip.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY ("Ghosts" actor): There it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, not only are you Irish, that was a big reveal a few episodes ago, but now you are able to be seen by a living.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On St. Patrick`s Day.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes, a living that Hetty finds to be quite attractive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Quit attractive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, got a little something. A little spark.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: That is, yes, Jay`s cousin, Sunil, played by Asif Ali, who is wonderful. We had a really lovely time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So for people who have not watched the show or just hearing about it, the musicality of your voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The ensemble cast. Everyone`s in different costumes, how do you describe it, and how did you get into the role?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Well, I was telling Gayle earlier, people comment about Hetty`s voice a lot, and I tend to approach work in a kind of musical way. It seemed really -- first of all, I loved the pilot script. I loved it so much.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ghosts"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: And the way her voice is written, and the cadence and the way she changes her mind in the middle of a sentence, it seemed really obvious to me that she would have this kind of this voice that would go all of these different places, and it`s really a lot of fun and it helps with the comedy, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, I was so surprised the first time I met you, and I said, oh, she doesn`t talk like that. That`s something -- I know, it is called acting. The way you did it, it was so believable to me.\par \par \uc2 And this reveal of the superpower, I think, is so interesting. It`s a testament to the writing, because now it could take you in so many directions.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you heard that that was going to be the superpower on this particular day that you were seen, what did you -- I think it`s such a nice twist, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Well, this kind of character is best when she, you know, gets her comeuppance, when she`s proven wrong. I mean, that`s the most delightful thing for me to play. And she really, she -- she has to -- she does a wood stone 180 on this one, because she -- that was her horror to be, you know, discovered that she was Irish, and, of course, now she`s embraced it and become a Hibernophile, and will be celebrating St. Patty`s Day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Although, so what`s interesting is that on this very show --\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: It`s true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- a few years ago, you predicted, I think. We have the clip. Let`s roll it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have a hatred of the Irish?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Well, she`d rather not speak of it. Yes, Hetty should turn -- actually turn out to be an Irish woman. I think that`d be very funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It would be a funny twist.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And so it is.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did you know at the time that this was coming?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: No, I absolutely didn`t, but I mean, again, they`re so good at giving these characters, you know conflict, and what a wonderful you know, conflict to have.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this show is been picked up for two more seasons, which is kind of a rarity in this day and age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, I love that our bosses at Paramount and at CBS T.V. think that this is the show that -- I mean, it`s so popular, people love it. I`ve been on Reddit forums where people are talking about your appearances.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: I know. You like to scour the Reddit --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He gets little alerts every time you like a page --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, to your point, not just one season, but two seasons.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Two seasons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they have great comedy.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: It`s unheard of, the support that we`ve gotten from \uc2 CBS\uc2  and Paramount+ and also from these fans, people really -- you, in particular.. You were an early adopter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Well, a tip of my hat to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a great thing. I mean, the great thing about the show is, you can watch it with your whole family.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is a limited number of things where you would feel comfortable watching with people age five all the way to, you know, 70, my mom and so we gather as a family, and it brings memories around the -- I mean, T.V., it is T.V., but it`s like, it`s a little campfire for us.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: It`s laugh out loud funny, but you can have interesting, you know, sensitive conversations about some, you know, deeper themes on the show. So we`re really proud of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all noticing your shamrock when you walked in.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Isn`t this sweet?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, you are embracing the Irish side about this.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: There is a story about this, Gayle..\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tell us the story.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: So my father, Ron Wisocky, who just turned 83 by the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Dad.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: He went and enlisted in the Army to get an education -- he grew up poor, and scraped together his pennies, and this was the first present that he gave his mother, my grandmother, Helen Wisocky.\par \par \uc2 So now, he is 65 to 70 --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`ve had it on all this time?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes, 70 years later, here, his daughter sits with you on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`ve had that the whole time?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: I have had this, this whole time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t wear it on a regular basis?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: In fact, I think it`s the first time I`ve actually worn it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s actually really beautiful.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: I`m going to wear it all the time now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, look at that.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes, isn`t that sweet?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is gorgeous. So how does he feel about the twists and turns of your character? He must be excited that you`re Irish.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Oh, they`re my biggest fans, and they`re the biggest fans of "Ghosts" for sure. All of our -- all the casts` parents really are huge fans, and they weigh in all the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would think your husband of 10 years might be a big fan, too.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: I am. I`m married 10 years this year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Congrats. Yes.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: Yes, and I`m going to visit my in laws, the Chus in Boston this weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you have them too -- I tell you, people, who have your parents are so, so, so lucky. I am so envious of that.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WISOCKY: I feel very, very grateful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you for what you do. Thank you for the show, Rebecca Wisocky.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love having you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can watch "Ghosts," Thursdays, 8:30 Eastern right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+. Do it with the whole family. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can watch us tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250312el3c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for March 7, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson, Charlie D`Agata, Celine Gounder,  Carter Evans, Meg Oliver, Nancy Chen, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 4,096 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A lot of the mystery about the deaths of Gene Hackman and his wife has been solved.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Late today, the medical examiner said the Academy Award- winning actor died of heart disease and Alzheimer`s, most likely on February 18. That, the M.E. said, was about a week after his wife, Betsy Arakawa, died of hantavirus, a disease spread by rodents that attacks the lungs.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Hackman was 95. Arakawa was 65. Their bodies were found Wednesday of last week at their home in Santa Fe, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans has the latest on the new developments -- Carter.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Well, what we learned today is more about the timeline.\par \par \uc2 Investigators already knew that Gene Hackman and his wife died in separate rooms of their home, but how and when they died is now becoming clear.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): For more than a week, the deaths of actor Gene Hackman and his wife, Betsy, had been a mystery filled with speculation.\par \par \uc2 Today, Chief Medical Examiner Dr. Heather Jarrell provided answers.\par \par \uc2 DR. HEATHER JARRELL (New Mexico Chief Medical Examiner): The cause of death for Ms. Betsy Hackman is hantavirus pulmonary syndrome. The manner of death is natural.\par \par \uc2 The cause of death for Mr. Gene Hackman is hypertensive and atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease, with Alzheimer`s disease as a significant contributory factor.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Santa Fe Sheriff Adan Mendoza confirmed that Betsy Arakawa Hackman had run several errands on February 11 and, when she returned home, the data trail turned cold.\par \par \uc2 ADAN MENDOZA (Santa Fe County, New Mexico, Sheriff): There was no additional outgoing communication from her or known activity after February 11, 2025.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Based on Hackman`s pacemaker activity, it appears he was alive for a full week after his wife passed away.\par \par \uc2 DR. HEATHER JARRELL: Was he aware that Ms. Hackman had died? And I think that question is difficult to answer, but I can tell you that he was in an advanced state of Alzheimer`s, and it`s quite possible that he was not aware that she was deceased.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, the case is not closed just yet. Investigators are still waiting for a necropsy to help determine exactly how the couple`s dog died.\par \par \uc2 They`re also still trying to gain access to their cell phones to see if it could shed any light on what may have happened in that week between their two deaths.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Carter Evans in Santa Fe tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Dr. Celine Gounder is a CBS News medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, help -- help us understand what hantavirus is.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So this is a virus that is transmitted usually through rodent droppings or urine that have been aerosolized, where someone is breathing those in.\par \par \uc2 There is quite a high case fatality rate, meaning, of the people who we know have hantavirus, how many die, it`s somewhere between 30 and 50 percent. And they die from failure of the lungs and the heart to function.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A lot of people have not heard about it, Celine. So how common is it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Hantavirus is rare. So this is not something everybody at home should be worrying about for themselves. Most of the cases we have seen have been out West, maybe about 30 a year, concentrated in New Mexico, Colorado, and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 You may remember, in the early `90s, there was an outbreak of hantavirus that was discovered by \uc2 Indian Health Service\uc2  doctors in the area, and there were about two dozen or so people who died in that outbreak.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We have about 20 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 Older people are more susceptible or people with weakened immune states?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Presumably. But this is so rare, we honestly don`t have tremendous information about it.\par \par \uc2 The best advice I have for you is, if you`re going to be cleaning in your garden shed or basement, make sure you wear appropriate protective equipment, mask, gloves to protect yourself.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All right, Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Well, Gladys Larke was in her living room in Taylors, South Carolina. Her husband, David, was in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Brad Sigmon walked in, moved methodically back and forth between those rooms and repeatedly beat the Larkes with a baseball bat until they died.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Angry that their daughter had broken up with him. Sigmon showed her parents no mercy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And none was shown to him.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, 24 years after the murders, South Carolina put Sigmon to death in the first execution in the United States by firing squad in 15 years.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver is at the prison in Columbia -- Meg.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: With a hood over his head and a target over his heart, a firing squad executed Brad Sigmon. He was pronounced dead at 6:08, four years after the South Carolina state legislature approved the option for death row inmates.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): In this room behind a century-old electric chair, 67-year-old Brad Sigmon was strapped down here. Hidden behind a wall 15 feet away, three corrections employees fired live rounds at him through a small rectangular hole, a method he chose, instead of lethal injection or the electric chair. New bulletproof glass was installed to shield witnesses.\par \par \uc2 Do you expect more states will follow suit?\par \par \uc2 JON OZMINT (Former Director, \uc2 South Carolina Department of Corrections\uc2 ): No.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Why not?\par \par \uc2 JON OZMINT: This was a political game that the Republicans in our legislature fell for when they voted for that. Nobody really wanted this.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Why not?\par \par \uc2 JON OZMINT: It wasn`t necessary, because we had two methods of execution. We had the electric chair and lethal injection.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Jon Ozmint is the former director of the \uc2 South Carolina Department of Corrections\uc2 , where he oversaw 14 executions. While lethal injection remains the primary method in most states, drug shortages and botched executions have led to a revival of older methods.\par \par \uc2 Sigmon`s attorney, Bo King.\par \par \uc2 Why would your client choose to die this way?\par \par \uc2 BO KING (Attorney For Brad Sigmon): Brad had repeatedly asked for the most basic facts about South Carolina`s lethal injection drugs. He`s like, is it expired? Have the drugs been diluted? Have the drugs spoiled? And he was repeatedly refused that information.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: So, he chose a firing squad.\par \par \uc2 BO KING: He chose the firing squad.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Sigmon was sentenced to death for killing his ex-girlfriend`s parents, David and Gladys Larke, in 2001, beating them with a baseball bat, a crime he admitted to.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe this form of execution?\par \par \uc2 JON OZMINT: Death may be difficult for anybody, but as far as ending a human life quickly, I think this is probably about as good as you can get.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Twenty-three states currently have the death penalty. Five allow the use of a firing squad.\par \par \uc2 BO KING: This was an impossible choice, but between the prospect of dying strapped to a gurney over 20 minutes, drowning in the blood and fluid in your own lungs, as opposed to the firing squad, he made the only choice that he could.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Meg, how are people in South Carolina, state officials, responding to those who are calling this whole thing barbaric?\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Maurice, the state of South Carolina maintains that this is not cruel and unusual punishment because they let the inmate choose the meth -- the way they are put to death, whether it is lethal injection, the electric chair, or now a firing squad.\par \par \uc2 If they don`t make a choice, the electric chair is their default.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Meg, what do we know about the people who pulled the trigger, the executioners?\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: We know that they are part of the corrections staff. We know they volunteered for the assignment, but because South Carolina passed a shield law in 2023, it protects the identities of everybody involved in an execution, so we don`t know who they are.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Meg, just a couple seconds here. Did we get a sense of his last words before he was put to death?\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Yes, his last words, Maurice, he wanted to say: "I want my closing statement to be one of love and a calling to my fellow Christians to end the death penalty."\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Meg Oliver in Columbia, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Meg.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s EVENING NEWS Roundup.\par \par \uc2 The privately backed lunar probe Athena landed on its side near the moon`s south pole. You can see the feet up in the air pointing back toward the Earth. It is the second bad moon landing for Athena`s builders in a year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And a SpaceX Starship exploded during a test launch yesterday from South Texas. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says the second such disaster this year disrupted about 240 passenger flights in the area.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The United States has disabled Ukraine`s access to satellite images, part of President Trump`s ban on sharing military intelligence. At the White House, the president said Russia is, as he put it, bombing the hell out of Ukraine. He is threatening to respond with sanctions.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And two \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  soldiers are under arrest in Washington state, suspected of trying to sell sensitive government information to buyers in China.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata has the latest on that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): This image is alleged to show active-duty Army Sergeant Jian Zhao taking photos of classified material. According to court documents, he told one of his co-conspirators, "Very sensitive document, super difficult to get."\par \par \uc2 Stationed at Joint Base Lewis-McChord in Washington state, Zhao stands accused of selling the materials to agents operating in China, including 20 hard drives, for which he was allegedly offered at least $10,000. Another active-duty soldier and one former soldier were also charged.\par \par \uc2 The classified material is said to include intelligence on High Mobility Artillery Rocket Systems, or HIMARS, Bradley and Stryker fighting vehicles, and America`s overall military readiness in the event of a conflict with China.\par \par \uc2 It`s a case that reflects a growing threat revealed in a CBS News investigation, China`s online recruitment of American active-duty service members as spies.\par \par \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  spoke to Kevin Vorndran, the head of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s Counterintelligence Division.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Are they operating at another level from either -- I don`t -- compare them to Russia or Iran. Is China that much more of a threat in this particular area?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN VORNDRAN (FBI Counterintelligence Division): Based on scale, yes, because they`re -- the scale of their intelligence services is so significant that it outnumbers other play -- other adversaries on the board.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: In a statement, Attorney General Pamela Bondi said the defendants will face swift, severe, and comprehensive justice.\par \par \uc2 Federal law enforcement have told us, in the last two years, they have identified around a dozen cases of China recruiting members of the U.S. military to spy.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Charlie D`Agata at the Pentagon tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS, Lonnie Quinn on the damaging winds in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I`m Nancy Chen. New jobs numbers are signaling possible trouble ahead for the economy, just as workers are being told to get back into the office.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I`m Steve Hartman. For 40 years, this pediatrician has given everything to the families in his care.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER (Family Doctor): You got one good.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Why they`re now giving back.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whatever needs to be done to save Dr. Z, we`re going to do it.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: All right.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That story when we go On the Road to Baltimore...\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: There you go.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... coming up on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The government reported today that the economy added 151,000 jobs in February, even as the unemployment rate ticked up a 10th- of-a-point to 4.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Nancy Chen tells us, the employment landscape in the country is changing as more Americans who`ve been working from home return to their offices.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voiceover): In January, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  joined the growing list of companies mandating a return to the office five days a week.\par \par \uc2 RENA PALUMBO (\uc2 Amazon\uc2 ): Does anybody have ideas for how we can make the summit more effective?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The retail giant allowed us a visit with some of the workers at its corporate headquarters in Seattle, where it`s the city`s largest employer.\par \par \uc2 People back in their cubicles. Is this the new normal?\par \par \uc2 RENA PALUMBO: Yes, this is the new normal.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Rena Palumbo (sp?) has worked on Amazon`s market research team for more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 What was it like being back in the office full time?\par \par \uc2 RENA PALUMBO: It was a bit of an adjustment for some because it`s really nice to be able to have the flexibility to work from home. But it`s also isolating and can be a bit disconnected from your team and your community and the business.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: At one point, the tech-heavy city had the country`s highest ratio of remote workers at more than 30 percent, leaving much of downtown Seattle empty.\par \par \uc2 Now, with those 50,000 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers commuting to the office, local businesses are experiencing a boost...\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: ... a trend seen around the country. The occupancy rate in 10 major cities bottomed out at about 15 percent during the pandemic. That number has since risen to nearly 55 percent.\par \par \uc2 The new Rainier Square Tower is considered a sign of Seattle`s revival, now mostly full.\par \par \uc2 Would that have been unimaginable just a few years ago?\par \par \uc2 JOHN MILLER\uc2  (\uc2 CBRE\uc2 ): For a building that was completely vacant five years ago, yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: \uc2 John Miller\uc2  works for \uc2 CBRE\uc2 , the world`s largest commercial real estate company. Last year, his team in Seattle closed more deals than ever.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MILLER\uc2 : I would say we`re on the edge of a revival. We are seeing the light at the end of the tunnel.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: There are just so many reports about, like, the death of downtown.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MILLER\uc2 : Even in real estate, it`s cyclical. Life is cyclical.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Rena Palumbo says her 45-minute commute is a worthwhile trade- off.\par \par \uc2 RENA PALUMBO: It`s been sad to see Seattle fall asleep over the last five years, and it`s nice to see it wake back up again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Nancy Chen joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Nancy, does the data here include all these federal workers who have been losing their jobs?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Well, this is the first full jobs we have had since President Trump took office. But keep in mind it doesn`t show the majority of the impact we will see from all those DOGE layoffs.\par \par \uc2 That said, it paints a very similar picture to what we saw last month of a strong, yet slowing labor market.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, Nancy, quickly, is Seattle like other cities in the country?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: You know, while there have been major gains in Seattle and nationally, real estate leasing is still far below pre-pandemic levels, which means that there could be some good deals for businesses out there, including lower rents.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Nancy Chen, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Strong winds in the Northeast tore down part of a four- story-tall scaffolding in Boston.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It had been put up to do brick repairs on a building, but no workers were on it at the time and no one on the ground, thankfully, it was injured.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Lonnie Quinn has more about the windy weather -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: All right, let`s get right to it then.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you what the winds are doing right now in our country. So we can pull up the map. On top of the map I want to impose, superimpose the flow of the wind.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you see them shaded in green, it`s a light wind. But just offshore of Boston, where you see that red shading, those are winds that are stronger. But what creates wind in the first place? It`s all about a pressure differential between a high- and a low-pressure system.\par \par \uc2 So the strongest element on the map right now is that low-pressure system way up into the Canadian Maritimes, all right? Now, a high-pressure system has filled in south of it. The low-pressure system will circulate air counterclockwise, all right? So imagine that hand of the clock going backwards.\par \par \uc2 The high-pressure goes clockwise. So imagine where that wind meets. It`s like a pitching machine, right? If you were to drop a baseball in there, phoom, it would fire that baseball out. And, sure enough, that`s what we dealt with today in and around Boston. Thankfully, that low is now far enough away. I don`t see it posing a problem for the night -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie, is that it for the wind, at least for now?\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: For the toughest winds, yes, but they don`t go away entirely because everything`s always moving in the atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 So that high-pressure system, I told you, is going to be pushing into the ocean, kind of fading away, right? But another low comes into the area. It`s got wind with it as well. But you can just tell this is not as strong as that system to the north. See all those isobars so tightly packed around the low?\par \par \uc2 This is a weaker low, so some gusty winds this weekend in the Northeast, but not like what they dealt with today -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On the Road is next.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Steve Hartman with the patients of a saint.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Steve Hartman is just back from the doctor. His office is On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): In one of Baltimore`s poorest neighborhoods, we found one of its richest doctors, rich not in money. In fact, he`s pretty much broke.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER (Family Doctor): All righty.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But 66-year-old family doctor Michael Zollicoffer is flush with job satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: I love people. I love to see a rash. If you say you got a rash, I`m going to find you because I love a bump on your head.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: You see what I`m saying to you? I have got to...\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: No, not really.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: I love -- I will tell you, I love a bump.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Has anybody taken your temperature?\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: No.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: It`s this is to my sweetie.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That infectious spirit is part of what sets him apart. Another is his prices. Can`t pay? That`s OK.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: You make everything sound so easy.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: It is easy.\par \par \uc2 Forget that dollar bill. Now, I`m going to see you no matter what. You walk in that door, you will be seen. You bring your grandma with you, I`m going to see her too.\par \par \uc2 OK.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But here`s what amazed us even more. In the nearly 40 years Dr. Z has been practicing...\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: That one is good.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... he`s never taken a real vacation.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: I think you will live.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Tells every patient they can call him 24/7/365.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: There you go.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I have his cell phone number.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Does everybody have his cell phone?\par \par \uc2 GROUP: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: He`s always available.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This guy seems almost unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: He is unbelievable because you will never meet another person like him ever.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A superhero, but hardly invincible.\par \par \uc2 A few months ago, Dr. Z was diagnosed with cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: I got two separate cancers, one renal, one rectal. But I don`t give up.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And to add insult to injury, Dr. Z didn`t have insurance. There was a whole series of snafus, but bottom line is, he had no way to pay for his radiation treatments, until his patients turned the tables on that ever-giving doctor.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: I`m like, let`s fight. What can we do?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Dr. Z will not give up on you, so we damn sure ain`t giving up on him.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Whatever needs to be done to save Dr. Z, we`re going to do it collectively.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So, collectively, they started a crowdfunding campaign. Nearly 1,000 people donated, raising more than $100,000.\par \par \uc2 Today, his prognosis is good.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He got his insurance back and now plans to funnel any money left over back into the community.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: Open that mouth for me.\par \par \uc2 You know what? I`m going to say something that may seem crazy as heck. I`m thankful that I got cancer, because I am the happiest man on the planet, no matter what the outcome. What we have shown and why we sitting in this table right there to show America, this is what you`re about.\par \par \uc2 We are about giving. I can`t make it without them, and nor can they make it without me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You need each other.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: Let this nation hear this story. Let it hear this story.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And let it follow his prescription for a better outcome.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: If you need something, you just buzz me, OK?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #5: OK.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman On the Road...\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: All right, we will see -- see you on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL ZOLLICOFFER: Good. We good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : As we turn our timepieces ahead Sunday, we got to thinking about clocks.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That led us to Leroy Anderson. In 1945, he was a 36-year- old composer serving in the Army, when he was invited to conduct the Boston Pops.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : He decided to write some music for the occasion, and a clock popped into his head.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: An offbeat clock that didn`t exactly ticktock.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It tock-ticked. The Pops performed the song, and it hit number 12 on the charts in 1951.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But Anderson`s big break came later that year, when a \uc2 CBS\uc2  producer was looking for a theme for a late-night movie show. The name of Anderson`s song caught his eye.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And the music his ear.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that is how The Syncopated Clock became the theme for The Late Show for the next quarter-century.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And part of the American songbook for all time. A look back before we leap ahead.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 See you soon on EVENING NEWS PLUS.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we hope you will join us right here on...\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250308el3700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For March 5, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anna Coren, Scott MacFarlane, Weijia Jiang, Mark Strassmann,  Lonnie Quinn, Maurice DuBois, Lilia Luciano, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 President Trump got two-thirds of the veterans vote in November, but we noticed he never mentioned them in his nearly-100-minute address last night. And now we have learned he is cutting some 80,000 jobs at the VA.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We will have more about that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Today, the commander in chief was prosecuting the trade war he started, when he put out a 25 percent tax on goods imported from Canada and in Mexico and doubled the tax on imports from China to 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: They responded with tariffs of their own. The president spoke by phone today with Canada`s prime minister, but neither side budged.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The president also spoke with Detroit`s Big Three automakers. He gave them a one-month exemption from the tariffs, but urged them to build more cars in the United States so they`re yet -- less dependent on foreign imports.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Wall Street liked the temporary reprieve. The Big Three stock indexes posted gains of more than 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House for us tonight -- Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: We know President Trump pays close attention to Wall Street, but top aides say it does not drive his decision-making as dealmaker in chief.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, less than 48 hours after those tariffs went into effect, we are learning what does, American companies caught in the middle.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): In public, presidential tough talk is fueling the trade war.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Whatever they tariff us, other countries, we will tariff them.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2  (Canadian Prime Minister): Even though you`re a very smart guy, this is a very dumb thing to do.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In private, administration officials are in constant contact with Canadian and Mexican counterparts, with Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick leading negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Just hours after Canada retaliated with tariffs on U.S. exports, he was working the phones, calling Canadian officials. Sources familiar with Lutnick`s call with the premier of Ontario, \uc2 Doug Ford\uc2 , describe it as aggressive and tough. Lutnick told Ford to stand down. Not only did Ford refuse. He said, if anything, his province would go harder.\par \par \uc2 DOUG FORD\uc2  (Premier of Ontario, Canada): To President Trump, don`t force our hand. You underestimate Canadians, you`re making a massive mistake.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Yesterday, President Trump had a call with the Big Three U.S. automakers, Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , and \uc2 GM\uc2 , who have been pressuring him for a carve-out for vehicles built in Canada and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 This morning, Lutnick said Trump was considering walking back tariffs on some sectors. By this afternoon, the White House announced a one-month exemption for cars and trucks. Still, the White House press secretary says Trump is all in on his tariffs plan, despite the risk of rising prices.\par \par \uc2 Why should Americans be OK with that, when so many are already struggling to pay the bills?\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): The American people elected this president to have monumental reform and change, including rebuilding our manufacturing base in this country, standing up to foreign nations who have been ripping off our country for decades. And that requires a little bit of disruption.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Weijia Jiang joins us from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, a little bit of disruption in the relationship with the Canadians. They`re not giving up without a fight.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, Prime Minister Trudeau says Canadians are reasonable and polite, but they will not back down from a fight. That means a 25 percent tariff on more than $150 billion of American product. And Trudeau says they will remain in place until U.S. tariffs are removed and -- quote -- "not a moment sooner."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Weijia, what about Mexico? What are they saying?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, unlike Canada, Mexico did not respond with retaliatory tariffs right away, perhaps to buy a little time for a potential breakthrough. Its president says she will see how things go until Sunday and, if U.S. tariffs are still in place then, not only will Mexico retaliate; she says they will look for deals with other countries.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, finally, Weijia, I want to go back to what we mentioned at the top of the show, 83,000 jobs cut at Veterans Affairs. What do you know about that?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, CBS News has obtained an internal memo from the VA that says it plans to cut 15 percent of its work force to return to 2019 levels. And we asked the VA and the White House, who is going to be impacted? Are we talking about administrators or doctors and nurses?\par \par \uc2 They did not specify, but told us the department is cutting bureaucracy and bloat.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : All right, Weijia Jiang at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 The trade war started as the world`s largest legislature was convening in China.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Nearly 3,000 members.\par \par \uc2 Anna Coren is in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN (voice-over): A show of strength outside the Great Hall of the People, as soldiers from the People`s Liberation Army marched across Tiananmen Square adorned with flags.\par \par \uc2 They`re part of the almost 3,000 delegates who arrived in Beijing for the opening of the National People`s Congress, China`s most important political event of the year.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: But hanging over this highly scripted and choreographed event, the escalating tensions with the United States and China`s defiance.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Excuse me, sir. Are you worried about the U.S.-China trade war?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Do you think that President Xi and President Trump can make amends?\par \par \uc2 Inside the hall, all eyes anxiously waited for the main event. The National People`s Congress is very much focused on the economy, but this year, it has been overshadowed by geopolitical events, with the U.S.-China trade war taking center stage.\par \par \uc2 China`s leader was warmly greeted. Only yesterday, Xi`s government hit back at the U.S. with retaliatory tariffs against agriculture, while the Foreign Ministry spokesman issued a warning -- quote -- "If war is what the U.S. wants, be it a tariff war, a trade war or any other type of war, we`re ready to fight until the end."\par \par \uc2 As President Xi watched on, his deputy delivered his address. While China faces economic challenges, its leadership says it won`t back down, this confident posture defying Beijing`s cold spring weather and the geopolitical storm clouds that are forming.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Anna Coren joins us now from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Anna, with all of those economic headwinds that China is facing, why are they pushing back so hard?\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Well, John, China is basically saying that it won`t be bullied. And this act of defiance that we are seeing from China is because it`s had six years to prepare for this.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the trade war began back in 2018. China has also become a global leader in key industries. It`s less reliant on the U.S. So, despite its economic challenges, China is saying to the U.S., we are prepared.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Anna, the Chinese just announced a big 7 percent increase in military spending. Is that setting off concerns?\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Yes, absolutely, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 Compare that to other countries, and it is significantly higher. And you have got Taiwan, the South China Sea, where China is trying to develop its sphere of influence. So the United States and its allies, they are concerned. And remember that there are members of the Trump administration who believe that China poses the greatest threat to the world.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Anna Coren in Beijing for us.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Anna.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now more of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 U.S. prosecutors call it hacking for hire, and they have charged 12 people in China with that crime today. Some work for a private hacking company that allegedly got paid by the Chinese government. They`re accused of targeting \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  offices, including the \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : CBS News has learned that Trump administration has stopped sharing intelligence that could help Ukraine target Russian forces. Defensive intelligence is still being shared. This follows the cutoff of new military aid as the administration pressures Ukraine to end the war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And the alleged mastermind of a deadly terror attack on the U.S. military was in federal court in Virginia today. Mohammad Sharifullah was arrested in Pakistan; 13 American service members were killed in the suicide attack during the chaotic U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Our justice correspondent, Scott MacFarlane, was in the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 And, Scott, what are you learning about this guy tonight?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes, Maurice, he`s a longtime accused ISIS-K operative with a history of attacks. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says he had been freed from prison for just two weeks in 2021 on a different charge before he allegedly helped plot the Abbey Gate bombing, which killed 13 service members.\par \par \uc2 And the prosecutors say he admitted plotting the route used by the attacker when he was questioned after he was caught in the CIA raid a few days ago, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And so, Scott, what does Mohammad Sharifullah look like in a courtroom?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Well, you really could feel the weight of the moment. I mean, there it is, a suspected ISIS-K terrorist standing in the U.S. court answering for an historic attack, small man, barely 5 feet.\par \par \uc2 He was wearing those blue jail outfits that look like blue medical scrubs. And he spoke in a real meek voice when informed he`d be held in jail until further hearings and faces the prospect of life in prison if convicted, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott, Scott MacFarlane, for us in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn with ice jams in the Northeast and why they are so dangerous.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we will have these stories as well.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m Mark Strassmann. Going to college without taking on any debt. It gets better. Students actually make money. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: I`m Lilia Luciano at the San Ysidro border crossing near San Diego. The president has called border security a national emergency. So what`s happening here now?\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump in his address last night talked up his crackdown on illegal immigration at the Mexican border. Today, to emphasize the point, he sent his vice president, defense secretary and director of national intelligence to the border to the town of Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The vice president, J.D. Vance, said Mr. Trump hopes to have the border wall completed by 2029.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano reports, illegal border crossings are down dramatically.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice-over): This is what the border looked like near El Paso two years ago, as hundreds lined up to claim asylum. We were there at the height of it. Shelters were overflowing with asylum seekers.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: This man we spoke with spent the night sleeping outside in the brutal cold. Now the streets are empty and the shelters quiet.\par \par \uc2 Last week, CBS News went out with Border Patrol agents as they searched for hours for any site of a border crosser. They came up empty-handed. Illegal crossings are down 94 percent across the entire border from the same period last year from nearly 5,000 a day to less than 300.\par \par \uc2 It`s a downward trend that began last year with Mexico`s increased border crackdown and the Biden administration`s executive order to stop accepting asylum claims from people who crossed illegally. Since taking office, President Trump has dramatically cut back the process for legal entry, while promising the largest mass deportation in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 There, he`s off to a slower start. According to the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , at least 55,000 people were deported in the first five weeks of the Trump administration. By comparison, an average of 57,000 were deported every month during the last full year of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 We asked \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  spokesperson Tricia McLaughlin about it.\par \par \uc2 President Trump promised during the campaign that millions would be deported, something like 15 million. When you divide that, it should come down to 300,000 or more people should be being deported; 55,000 is a lot less.\par \par \uc2 TRICIA MCLAUGHLIN (\uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  Spokesperson): I don`t think that the first month is going to be the status quo.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In the first week of the administration, we saw these massive raids. We saw Secretary Noem out there. There was this massive show of force. Have the raids been reduced?\par \par \uc2 TRICIA MCLAUGHLIN: These ICE raids, as they`re often called, are very tactical and they`re very targeted. That`s why we say it`s the worst of the worst. We follow where these individuals are, the low-hanging fruit, so to speak. And I think that they`re going to evolve.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Once you get past the people who have a criminal record, what options do undocumented people have if they want to make a case for staying in this country, working in this country, contributing?\par \par \uc2 TRICIA MCLAUGHLIN: I think that the best message is that they should self- deport and leave now and then come back the right way.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lilia Luciano joins us now from San Ysidro, California.\par \par \uc2 So, Lilia, how else have they been able to get these numbers down so dramatically lower?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Maurice, I`m going to tell you something that I heard today from an immigration attorney dealing with all kinds of cases. Just Trump being in office is deterrence enough.\par \par \uc2 But there have been dozens of policies and actions from the Trump administration, from trying to stop refugee resettlement, to ending the appointments that tens of thousands of people had to make their case for asylum, to ending visa programs, parole.\par \par \uc2 And the impact is felt throughout the hemisphere. Last week, I heard from a Panamanian government official, who told me that now their country is dealing with reverse migration, people heading back down south from those who historically had tried to head up north to the United States.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lilia Luciano on the border for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A winter storm brought blizzard conditions to the Upper Midwest. Driving was extremely hazardous. Dozens of crashes were reported in Iowa and Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In the Northeast, there is a risk of flooding caused by ice jams.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn, help me out here. What are ice jams?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Well, listen, an ice jam happens when you get a warming trend and the ice on the river starts to naturally break apart. They collide, and you just get flooding over the banks.\par \par \uc2 But there`s another element you really need. And you need a lot of rain. That`s exactly what we picked up, this line that you see right here, the same line that pushed through the South into the Southeast yesterday and made its way to the mid-Atlantic today, up into Northern New England and into portions of Upstate New York as well.\par \par \uc2 So, what that does, it raises the level of the river. And now, when that ice is moving along, it has some problems because they start colliding and you get the big flooding. So they break it up into smaller pieces. That boat you see right there, the Edward Cotter in Buffalo, New York, it`s the oldest active fireboat in the world; 125 years, it`s been operating, today being used to break up the ice.\par \par \uc2 So why do you break it up? Because smaller pieces of ice will get through narrow passages on the river. What happens is, when that river starts to melt the ice and here comes those big chunks floating down the river, they will collide when they go around a tight curve or possibly they hit a bridge or something like that. That then causes the water behind it to back up, floods the bank.\par \par \uc2 And this is a problem that we`re going to have in Upstate New York all the way into New England, where we have had record snowpack today. When you break those up into smaller pieces, it will navigate that narrow passage better -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Makes all the sense in the world.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie, here we are in March, early March. Are we looking at more weather to come here? What`s going to happen then?\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Well, let`s take Buffalo, for example, because that`s what we`re talking about right now, Buffalo today 51 degrees. That`s a problem. Tomorrow, you`re 32 degrees, 33 degrees perhaps, back to normal.\par \par \uc2 Get into next week, Buffalo could make a run at 60 degrees -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK. Lonnie Quinn, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It`s a new approach to college education.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Prepping students for specific jobs.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At a time when college is unaffordable for many people, some schools are reimagining higher education, shifting their curricula from general knowledge to providing free training for specific jobs and giving new meaning to working your way through school.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann has tonight`s Eye on America from Merced, California.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): This is Live Oak Farms in California`s San Joaquin Valley, 3,000 acres of tomatoes, peppers, almonds and alfalfa.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Marquez (sp?), a quietly ambitious guy, started here 10 years ago as a day laborer. Today, he`s the operations manager while a full-time college student.\par \par \uc2 Is this your classroom?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS MARQUEZ (Operations Manager): This is it right here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: He`s in a pilot program funded by a federal grant and offered free to students by Merced College. In its competency-based curriculum, students such as Marquez master vocational skills.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Identify the hydraulic controls.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS MARQUEZ: Right here.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Agriculture is only one area. Others include education and health care. It`s real-life, hands-on, rather than the traditional college classroom experience.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS VITELLI (President, Merced College): We know that the traditional model can be antiquated in some ways. So what can we do to upskill them to prepare them for 21st century jobs?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Merced College President Chris Vitelli developed the program for the school.\par \par \uc2 What to you about the program is innovative?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS VITELLI: They come to us, they master a skill, and then they move on to the next one.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You can actually go to school and make money...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS VITELLI: You can.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... in this program.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS VITELLI: You can. I mean, this program is designed so our students can have a part-time job. They can have a full-time job.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: With the soaring cost of college, it`s probably no surprise more than 40 percent of full-time students have a job while they`re in school, but at a cost. They`re roughly 20 percent less likely to finish their degree.\par \par \uc2 As a nontraditional college student, Carlos Marquez can work and study making $1,300 a week, a big help for the 32-year-old father of two.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS MARQUEZ: You get in, get the training, and then you just keep on building from there.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: There are now more than 1,000 of these programs at colleges across the country; 82 percent of them expect the number of those programs to grow.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS VITELLI: It`s incumbent on us to find ways to meet their needs, not the opposite way around.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Marquez started traditional college when he was 18. He didn`t like it and couldn`t afford it when his scholarships ran out. Now he can earn an industry-recognized certificate with added fuel to finish.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS MARQUEZ: I`m first-generation. Being able to accomplish that, get that, and then be an example for my daughters, I mean, that`s where it`s at.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On a farm or off, that`s growth.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Mark Strassmann in Merced, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: When Marquez finishes the program in May, his employer is committed to giving him a 10 percent raise. Other Merced partners have agreed to put graduates on a track to become managers.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s -- or, rather, tomorrow`s Eye on America: two problems solving each other, saving forests and heating homes.\par \par \uc2 And, now, how do you keep a yellow dog from becoming a white elephant? John and I will get into that next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So, you decided to get a dog.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: You go all out, spring for something rare and pricey, and then...\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It doesn`t quite work out. That`s what happened to the Honolulu police.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, meet Spot here. The Happened bought the robot dog at the height of the pandemic with $150,000 in federal COVID aid. Spot was used for contactless temperature checks and telemedicine for the homeless.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : When the city council howled about the price, the HPD said, don`t worry. Spot can be used for years, long after COVID.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Well, the pandemic`s over, but it turns out Spot has not been out for a walk in three years.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Twenty-one in dog years, leaving a spokesperson to do some cleanup. She says the P.D. is assessing whether Spot can be, how should we put it, fixed.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Software programmers may be able to change how Spot runs, so it can, for instance, assist in searches.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : If they can`t teach the old dog new tricks, perhaps they can sell it for whatever Spot will fetch.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Clearly, he`s a good boy.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . See you on Plus.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250306el3500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For March 3, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Kris Van Cleave, Anna Coren, Weijia Jiang, Caitlin Huey-Burns,  Imtiaz Tyab, Maurice DuBois, Lonnie Quinn, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Here come the tariffs. Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is making it more expensive for Mexico and Canada to get their products through America`s front door. And that could increase the cost of the packages left on your front porch.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : At a time when three out of four Americans are telling CBS News their incomes are not keeping up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 At a minute past midnight, a 25 percent tax takes effect on imports from America`s closest and biggest trading partners.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The announcement had investors running for the exits. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , GM and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  led a sell-off that saw the Dow plunge nearly 1.5 percent, the Nasdaq more than 2.5, and S&P 1.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Weijia Jiang is at the White House -- Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, President Trump says Canada and Mexico must face those tariffs because they have failed to stop the flow of fentanyl into the U.S. And, for the same reason, he`s bumping up the fee on Chinese imports.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, economists warn, this could spark a trade war that will cost consumers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): After following through on his threat...\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): The tariffs, they`re all set. They go into effect tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: ... President Trump says there`s a simple solution for countries that want to avoid tariffs.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: What they have to do is build their car plants, frankly, and other things in the United States, in which case they have no tariffs.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: To make his point today, Trump hosted the CEO of a Taiwanese semiconductor chip manufacturing company who pledged to invest $100 billion in new manufacturing plants in the U.S., and the president said Japanese automobiles maker Honda has ditched its plan to open a new plant in Mexico, opting instead for Indiana to avoid tariffs.\par \par \uc2 But auto prices are still likely to increase. One study found a large SUV with parts from Mexico could jump $9,000.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LIPSKY (Former State Department Official): Trade wars are costly, and nobody wins.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Josh Lipsky is a former State Department official.\par \par \uc2 Trump argues that we`re going to slap all these extra fees on imports, so then everyone`s just going to buy America, and that will be great for American manufacturing, American companies. Doesn`t that take time?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LIPSKY: That doesn`t happen overnight. That could take years. And, in the meantime, there`s huge cost of transition.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Another recent study estimates the new tariffs could cost the average U.S. family more than $1,200 a year.\par \par \uc2 I think people at home really just want to know, what does this mean for me? Do companies absorb the cost or am I going to have to pay more?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LIPSKY: You`re going to have to pay more. It doesn`t mean you`re going to have to pay exactly what the tariff is, because there are all kinds of ways which some of this gets absorbed by companies and otherwise, but you as a consumer will very likely pay more for the products that are tariffed. And it could get worse if a trade war erupts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Weijia, we remember candidate Trump promising to lower prices on day one.\par \par \uc2 What has he been saying about the overall economy?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, he hasn`t been talking much about those prices and, in fact, has not talked about the overall economy in terms of specific actions he is taking to immediately lower the prices.\par \par \uc2 Now, his economic team says, look, it`s only been six weeks since he took office, so you have to give us some time for the policies to take effect. And the White House says he will talk about all of this when he addresses a joint session of Congress tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, Weijia, there are even more tariffs coming, yes?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Yes, the president announced that, next month, the U.S. will impose reciprocal tariffs on any country that has a fee on American exports.\par \par \uc2 And a new study from the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  shows that all these tariffs are already having an impact. It shows that consumer confidence decreased by 10 percent since January, and it will likely impact consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, Weijia, has that consumer spending received any kind of pushback from the White House? What`s their explanation for that?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, they say that they are doing everything that they can.\par \par \uc2 For example, when it comes to the price of eggs, they say that the Department of Agriculture has a plan to deal with the bird flu and then deal with the costs. And, so far, they claim that the economy is doing great, and because of all these bigger actions that Trump is taking, whether it`s with DOGE or whether it`s with tariffs, they say it will have an impact and boost that confidence overall.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Just got to wait.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Well, China has already found a way around U.S. tariffs on imports.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Anna Coren reports, China is exporting its factories.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN (voice-over): A two-hour drive outside the Cambodian capital, Phnom Penh, a convoy of semi-trailers passed by.\par \par \uc2 Let`s drive straight through.\par \par \uc2 Moments later, an enormous arch greeted us with signage in two languages, the local language, Khmer, and, beneath, Chinese. But it`s very clear who is in charge of this special economic zone rising from the dirt.\par \par \uc2 Hello, sir? Hello?\par \par \uc2 The manager of this furniture factory invited us in.\par \par \uc2 Yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: OK. OK. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 They moved here from China a month ago to produce ottomans to export to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Most of the companies coming here are Chinese?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: And they`re driving incentive to avoid U.S. tariffs.\par \par \uc2 The U.S.-China trade war is the main reason for this explosion of Chinese investment in Cambodia. In 2016, before President Trump took office, Cambodian exports to the U.S. were roughly $3 billion. Last year, they topped $12 billion, representing nearly 30 percent of the country`s GDP.\par \par \uc2 The Cambodian government says more than half of the factories in Cambodia are Chinese-owned.\par \par \uc2 To America?\par \par \uc2 MR. HUANG (Factory Owner): Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: While Chinese companies are skirting U.S. tariffs, economists we spoke to say they are playing by the rules. But there are fears Cambodia`s economy could become collateral damage.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that President Trump might put tariffs on Cambodia, considering the Chinese investments?\par \par \uc2 MR. HUANG: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Chinese garment factory owner Mr. Huang (sp?) moved to Cambodia 20 years ago, capitalizing on tax breaks and lower wages, while still running factories in China.\par \par \uc2 But when the trade war began in 2018, he moved all operations to Cambodia and now exports 60 percent of his garments to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And who are your customers?\par \par \uc2 MR. HUANG: So, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Costco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: And he said those orders have only multiplied since President Trump announced new tariffs.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Anna Coren joins us now from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Anna, as you mentioned, there are already tariffs on China. President Trump says he`s going to add more tariffs. What`s that going to do to China?\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Well, John, Chinese manufacturers certainly are going to feel some pain. We spoke to one who said she will try to absorb the tariffs. Others won`t be in a position to do so. That`s when you will see those higher costs passed on to American customers.\par \par \uc2 But those retaliatory tariffs we saw from China were mainly symbolic last month. Now they`re going to target U.S. agriculture, and that`s U.S. farmers.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Anna, China`s National People`s Congress starts on Wednesday. That, of course, is their big government meeting. Their economy is not exactly in great shape. So what are they expected to do now?\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: That`s right, Maurice. It`s been hurting from the poverty crisis and high youth unemployment.\par \par \uc2 So this National People`s Congress is about laying out the road map to strengthen the economy. I spoke to an adviser to the Chinese government last night, and he said that, despite all this noise about a trade war, he still is confident a deal will be done and that President Trump will visit China later this year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Anna Coren reporting from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Anna.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We expect the president to have much more to say about tariffs and other issues when he addresses as a joint session of the House and Senate and the American people tomorrow evening.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Maurice and I will bring you live coverage beginning at 9:00 Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Now part -- now more top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup, beginning with a new setback for Pope Francis as he battles double pneumonia in a Rome hospital. He had more breathing problems today. Doctors twice had to clear his lungs. Francis is 88. His prognosis remains guarded.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: In Mannheim, Germany, a man drove a car into a crowd, killing two people, injuring at least 10. The police say the man, who was 40, shot himself as he was being arrested and is listed in stable condition tonight. They say the motive was not political and he may have mental health issues.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And CBS News has learned a number of passenger planes on final approach to Reagan National Airport this weekend received apparent false alarms from their collision-avoidance systems.\par \par \uc2 Senior transportation correspondent Kris Van Cleave joins us.\par \par \uc2 Kris, we have had enough with regular alarms. Now these are false alarms?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Yes, John, we pored through hours of air traffic control audio from Saturday at Washington Reagan Airport and identified at least 12 flights that received these false alarms from their onboard collision- avoidance systems.\par \par \uc2 Six of them, in fact, encouraged the plane to take evasive maneuvers. At least three did go-arounds before safely landing. Now, this is the same airport where that deadly midair collision occurred in January. Fliers are already on edge. Now the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating and looking into the possibility that some kind of external interference may have triggered these alarms.\par \par \uc2 We do know that all 12 of these planes we identified were regional jets, two different makes of regional jets. Other airplanes did not receive these alerts. So it is a mystery at this point. No evidence of nefarious activity, but this many false alarms is highly unusual.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And raises a lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave tonight in Phoenix, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn on wildfires in the Carolinas and a winter storm that could produce a blizzard.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Thousands of \uc2 USAID\uc2  employees fired by the Trump administration this week were given just 15 minutes to clear out their desk. I`m Caitlin Huey-Burns outside of the agency`s headquarters with one federal aid worker`s emotional goodbye.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab in Kyiv. We will take you inside a military trauma hospital, where injured soldiers told us they have no choice but to keep fighting against Russia as U.S.-Ukrainian ties unravel.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It did not end Friday. President Trump attacked Ukrainian President Zelenskyy again today, this time for saying an end to the war with Russia is still -- quote -- "very far away."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Mr. Trump in a post on social media called it the worst statement Zelenskyy could have made and said the U.S. will not put up with it for much longer.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The war, now more than 3 years old, has left three- quarters-of-a-million people dead or wounded.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That includes civilians and military.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is in Kyiv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voice-over): At just 26 years old, Dr. Igor Pryntsypal (sp?) is one of Ukraine`s youngest orthopedic trauma specialists. He took us to an operating group where a Ukrainian soldier was having reconstructive surgery.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: This group of surgeons meticulously removed skin and other tissue from the soldier`s back to rebuild part of his arm that was nearly severed while he fought against Russian forces on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 This is the highest level of reconstruction?\par \par \uc2 DR. IGOR PRYNTSYPAL (Ukraine): Yes. And this is the best surgeons in this operating in Ukraine. It`s real.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: What we`re seeing is one of the most complex reconstructive surgeries that these surgeons here perform. And it really is a race against time to help soldiers like this who have suffered life-changing injuries.\par \par \uc2 Are you afraid Ukraine may lose America`s support?\par \par \uc2 DR. IGOR PRYNTSYPAL: If we have it, we will be more strong. But, today, soldiers said that we know that we must save us.\par \par \uc2 (DR. IGOR PRYNTSYPAL SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Dr. Pryntsypal is also closely involved in the physical rehabilitation of wounded soldiers. Many of them told us they felt a deep sense of uncertainty as U.S.-Ukrainian ties continue to unravel...\par \par \uc2 (DR. IGOR PRYNTSYPAL SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: ... including Alexei (sp?), a grandfather and volunteer soldier in his 50s. He was being treated for shrapnel wounds to his legs and hips from a Russian shelling. Alexei also suffered a battlefield injury to his arm a year ago and still went back to fight.\par \par \uc2 So you`re going to keep fighting no matter what?\par \par \uc2 (ALEXEI SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "Of course, for the sake of my granddaughter," he said. "We must protect ourselves to prove that Ukraine exists," a fight for Ukraine everyone we met here believes in.\par \par \uc2 What keeps you hopeful?\par \par \uc2 DR. IGOR PRYNTSYPAL: We understand that, if we are together, we can fight with everything. And when our friends help us, we are better.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Imtiaz Tyab joins us now from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 You have really extraordinary access there, Imtiaz. So what`s the mood of the people there in Ukraine? Who are they counting as their friends nowadays?\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Yes, well, of the many Ukrainians we have been speaking to, from injured soldiers to politicians, they tell us they believe the U.S. and Ukraine are still friends and that the connection between these two nations runs far deeper than any disagreement between two presidents.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, following President Zelenskyy`s visit to London this weekend, the quite literal warm embrace he received from European leaders, as well as promises of aid and security guarantees, well, that had many here feeling that Ukraine is not alone.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, thanks so much, Imtiaz Tyab, in Kyiv tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Hundreds of wildfires are burning in North and South Carolina, but firefighters battling them got a break from the weather today.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The wind that was helping spread the flames, all -- that`s all died down.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn has the latest now on that -- Lonnie Quinn.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Gentlemen, you`re right. The winds in the Carolinas have sort of relaxed, but not the case off to the West.\par \par \uc2 Every color depicted here shows you some kind of a wind warning. Pay close attention to the bright red in Texas and New Mexico. Red flag warnings stay in effect until tomorrow afternoon. We could use some rain, and there is some rain on the way.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, it could possibly come in the form of severe weather. Overnight tonight, starting at about 4:00 a.m. for Dallas until about 7:00 a.m., the threat is there for severe thunderstorms, possibly even tornadoes. It moves into places like Shreveport, Louisiana, later in the morning, Jackson, Mississippi, Mobile. And then by the time you get to Wednesday, that line has made its way to the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the radar signature, it`ll tell you a lot. When you watch a line form that is vertically stacked, so a straight line, straight up and down, see what was developing right here? This is a strong line of storms. As it makes its trek through the midsection of the country, it`s going to finish and exit the East Coast on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 And, Wednesday we watch anywhere from, say, Washington, D.C., all the way down to Savannah, Georgia. As for the Carolinas, which is what we started talking about, the winds come back to play on Wednesday. But it comes also with a lot of rain, which is a good thing -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: All right, Lonnie Quinn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Federal workers targeted in the mass firings are losing more than a job.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump is giving the heads of federal agencies until next week to submit plans for eliminating more government jobs.\par \par \uc2 The thousands being cut in the mass firings are much more than numbers on a payroll list. And every one of them has a story.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE (Fired Federal Worker): What do you want for lunch today?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voice-over): We joined Kate Scaife (sp?) Friday morning as she made her kids` lunch, dropped them off at the bus stop, and headed to her office at \uc2 USAID\uc2 , where she was a program analyst.\par \par \uc2 Scaife was one of thousands of federal employees who received an e-mail saying they`re being affected by a reduction in force, government-speak for: "You`re fired."\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: It has been one of the hardest months of my life, to feel that I did something wrong, when all we were ever trying to do was the right thing.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL VOUGHT\uc2  (Director, Office of Management and Budget): They are increasingly viewed as the villains.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: That`s top Trump official Russell Vought talking to his right-wing think tank in 2023. Vought has helped billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  carry out the mass firings across the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Vought sent this memo directing agencies to plan for a significant reduction in the number of full-time workers, an effort to shrink the government that could ultimately leave 700,000 federal employees across the country out of a job.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL VOUGHT\uc2 : We want to put them in trauma.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: That trauma was on display Friday as \uc2 USAID\uc2  workers left the building for the last time.\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: \uc2 USAID\uc2  saves lives.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: That`s how you would have described your work?\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: That`s right. We make America safer.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: For the last two years, Kate Scaife`s job was to make thousands of humanitarian programs around the world run efficiently, a role she optimistically believed would match the priorities of the new administration.\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: One of our senior leaders came up to me at one point, passed me in the hall and said: "Be prepared to be the most popular girl in the room in a couple weeks."\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Her illusions faded last month when Musk tweeted about feeding \uc2 USAID\uc2  into the wood chipper. The next day, Scaife couldn`t log on to her work devices.\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: I felt disappeared, right? All of our work was made invisible too.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: We rode with Scaife as she drove to the office.\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: I have got my kids` pictures.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: You leave it there thinking you will get it back.\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: It`ll be there on Monday.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: She was allowed 15 minutes to clean out her desk.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: It just felt really demeaning to be treated like that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Scaife had been the breadwinner in her family.\par \par \uc2 How do you talk to your kids about this?\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: It was so hard to tell them things like, we have made summer plans for these camps, and I can`t afford that.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It feels like work was a big part of your identity.\par \par \uc2 KATE SCAIFE: I feel like I am my best mom when I have this other part of me that helps me to feel engaged with the world. I don`t know how I will find something new that gives me that same joy.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A feeling now looming for hundreds of thousands of federal workers.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Caitlin Huey-Burns in Silver Spring, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: In tomorrow`s Eye on America: The impact of the Southern California wildfires goes beyond the land. We will take a look at the long- term effects of the toxic ash on the ocean.\par \par \uc2 And John and I will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Finally tonight, lest we forget, these are the seven people shot to death at a Fourth of July parade in Highland Park, Illinois, in 2022.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Today, moments before the gunman was to stand trial, he pleaded guilty. He`s 24. He`s expected to spend the rest of his life in prison.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, lest we forget, these are the 14 people killed by a truck-driving terrorist in New Orleans this past New Year`s Day before he was shot dead by the police.\par \par \uc2 Among the victims, Tiger Bech, a 27-year-old former football star at Princeton.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And this is his little brother Jack, a TCU wide receiver, showing the \uc2 NFL\uc2  what he`s got at the weekend combine in Indianapolis. Jack wore Princeton shoes in honor of the brother he looked up to and who he believes now watches over him.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Said Jack Bech: "I know his wings are going to be on me from now until the end of time."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that is the CBS Evening News. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . See you on Evening News Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Have a good night. We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250304el3300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For February 27, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed O`Keefe, Karen Hua, Margaret Brennan, Martha Teichner, Nancy  Cordes, Lonnie Quinn, Imtiaz Tyab, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN (Actor): If you put your effort and concentration into playing to your potential to be the best that you can be, I don`t care what the scoreboard says. At the end of the game, in my book, we`re going to be winners.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: He won Hollywood`s highest awards for dramas and comedies. Now Gene Hackman is at the center of a mystery.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hackman and his wife, Betsy Arakawa, were found dead in their home in Santa Fe, New Mexico. Hackman was 95, his wife a classical pianist in her mid- 60s.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Karen Hua is following the investigation in Santa Fe.\par \par \uc2 KAREN HUA: There`s a growing mystery surrounding two-time Academy Award winner Gene Hackman`s death. His body, along with that of his wife`s and one of their dogs, a German shepherd, was found on Wednesday afternoon lying on the floor in separate rooms. They had apparently been dead for some time, according to investigators.\par \par \uc2 At first, the sheriff`s department here said there were no signs of foul play. But, this morning, we learned from a court document that the deaths are considered suspicious enough to require an investigation. The document reveals that Hackman`s body was found in a mudroom and that he may have fallen.\par \par \uc2 Arakawa`s body was found in a bathroom. There was a space heater by her head and an open prescription bottle on the countertop with some pills scattered near it. The sheriff`s department says there appeared to be no traumatic injuries and no sign of carbon monoxide poisoning.\par \par \uc2 Autopsies are being conducted that may help provide clues on the cause of death.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Karen Hua in Santa Fe, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And \uc2 CBS\uc2  Sunday Morning correspondent Martha Teichner will have an appreciation of Gene Hackman a little bit later in the broadcast.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  is the latest European leader to make a personal appeal to President Trump to continue U.S. support for Ukraine in the war with Russia and to stop his tariff threats.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Starmer was at the White House today.\par \par \uc2 And Margaret Brennan has more on that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER (British Prime Minister): This is really special.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice-over): U.K. Prime Minister Starmer gifted an engraved invitation from the king, an attempt to charm and change the mind of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I think Russia has been acting very well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  made a similar trek to inform and persuade.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Europe is loaning the money to Ukraine. They get their money back.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2  (French President): No, in fact, to be frank, we pay it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: From Brussels, the 27 countries that make up the \uc2 European Union\uc2  dispatched their top diplomat, \uc2 Kaja Kallas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Are all of the European leaders coordinating? And if so, what`s the message?\par \par \uc2 KAJA KALLAS (High Representative for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy, \uc2 European Commission\uc2 ): We are coordinating. And, of course, the message is that the fight that Ukraine is having is not only about Ukraine`s sovereignty, but it`s much, much broader. It`s about freedom of the free world, really.\par \par \uc2 It is clear that Russia attacked Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the Trump administration has decided now not to say that explicitly.\par \par \uc2 KAJA KALLAS: It is certainly a very strong Russian narrative that they want to run to really push, saying that, oh, it`s a conflict between two equal parties.\par \par \uc2 It is very clear one country attacked another country very brutally with force and is bombing Ukraine every day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yet the U.S. voted against a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  resolution that called out Russia for its invasion of Ukraine three years ago. Another battlefield with Europe, President Trump vowed a tariff of 25 percent on their goods, including cars.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: The \uc2 European Union\uc2  was formed in order to screw the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What was your reaction to that remark?\par \par \uc2 KAJA KALLAS: Europe is a peace project. It was created so that we wouldn`t have wars between the members of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , and we haven`t had.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Margaret Brennan joins us now from Washington.\par \par \uc2 So, Margaret, when these leaders come, what`s the one thing or one or two things that they really want to try and convince President Trump about?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, John, they`re basically trying to convince President Trump that Europeans aren`t freeloaders. There`s been explanation that the E.U. has forked over about $145 billion worth of humanitarian, military and financial aid, mainly in the form of grants, over the past three years of this war.\par \par \uc2 America is still the world`s arsenal. It still has tens of thousands of troops in Europe. But the \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  aggression in Ukraine has so terrified European states that they have pumped up spending. Now 23 of the 32 NATO allies spend over 2 percent of their own GDP on their own defense. So there is some persuasion that`s happening to try to keep America committed to defense.\par \par \uc2 There`s also some side dealing on an independent nature. \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  pitched President Trump on a free trade deal as well.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Margaret, ultimately, what do the European leaders want from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Germany`s incoming chancellor openly questioned whether America can be counted on at all. And, today, President Trump said the words they were listening for, which was America`s still committed to come to the defense of \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies.\par \par \uc2 But, really, it is that commitment not just to defense, but also to trade. To be clear, tomorrow, when Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy comes to the White House, no one is making him any kind of security promise. That`s going to be a joint investment deal. And neither Europe nor the United States are promising him defense.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Margaret Brennan in Washington, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 More measles cases being reported around the country after the first death in the Texas outbreak in New Jersey. Two new cases are linked to a patient confirmed to have measles earlier this month. Kentucky just reported its first case in two years. And, in California, an infant with measles traveled through LAX Airport one day last week. Each of these new cases has been connected to travel outside the U.S.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : CBS News senior producer Andy Triay reports, Mexico is sending drug lord Rafael Caro Quintero to the United States. Quintero is wanted in the killing of a U.S. narcotics agent in 1985. Mexico has also agreed to extradite 28 people suspected of being drug cartel members.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, in Washington, \uc2 USAID\uc2  workers fired weeks ago were allowed back in the building today. Some walked out in tears after they were given 15 minutes to collect their belongings.\par \par \uc2 So far, about 100,000 federal workers have been fired or accepted buyouts offered by President Trump and adviser \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Ed O`Keefe talked to one who took the deal and to a former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employee who believes she and others like her got taken by Musk.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice-over): Cole Kracke (sp?) reviews education applications for the \uc2 Veterans Benefits Administration\uc2  outside of Minneapolis and didn`t initially believe an offer that came in late January...\par \par \uc2 COLE KRACKE (Federal Employee): "If you choose to..."\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: ... a deferred resignation plan inspired by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  called "A Fork in the Road." Leave and get paid to do nothing through late September or stay, but risk getting laid off.\par \par \uc2 COLE KRACKE: A couple of my co-workers actually reported it as phishing, because it did not look official.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But Kracke did the math. His 13 years in the Navy, plus five years at the VA, means he vests into the federal retirement system in June. So he sent a one-word reply.\par \par \uc2 Did you think to yourself, maybe this is too good to be true?\par \par \uc2 COLE KRACKE: Once the acting secretary of the VA flat-out -- it said this offer is valid, it`s legal, and it will be honored, what more do I need?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON LISS-RIORDAN (Attorney): Thousands.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Attorney Shannon Liss-Riordan represents more than 2,000 cautionary tales, former employees of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , now X, owned by Musk.\par \par \uc2 When you first saw him offer a fork in the road to federal employees, after everything that went on at X, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON LISS-RIORDAN: Oh, my God, he couldn`t even come up with a new subject line?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Musk offered an early exit to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees in 2022, quit and accept three months of severance or stay employed. Nearly 70 percent of the company opted to quit.\par \par \uc2 Most of Liss-Riordan`s clients, including some who were later laid off or fired, are fighting for severance payments they claim they are owed, which X denies.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We were essentially lied to. We did not get what we were promised, and it left me in a terrible financial space.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: One of those clients is this former employee, fearful of retribution.\par \par \uc2 The situation left you homeless.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It did, yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: How do you explain that to your child?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It was very hard to explain to my child, because he thought \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  was a pretty cool guy.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Would you have taken the deferred resignation plan offered by Musk to federal workers?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Based on what I have gone through, I would be immediately looking for new work and not trusting any of that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Ed O`Keefe joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, it`s so striking to notice all the similarities between what`s happening with the federal workers now and what happened to the workers at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Which is why that former employee is advising federal employees to get out as fast as they can.\par \par \uc2 And she points out that, when DOGE officials started showing up at federal agencies to get a handle on what exactly they`re doing, it reminded her of when \uc2 Tesla\uc2  employees, owned by Musk, started showing up at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  after he bought the company. The buyouts offered, the layoffs that have happened and the further reductions expected to come, she says, all mirror what went on at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , now X, in 2022.\par \par \uc2 We should point out attorneys for the company did not respond to multiple requests for comment. And when asked about DOGE and Musk, the White House today tells us in part -- quote -- "It`s a welcome change to have entrepreneurial giants leading the charge in making government more efficient."\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, by the way, is Cole`s last day working for the Veterans Administration -- John and Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Ed O`Keefe, yes, thank you at the White House tonight. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn with the return of winter.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I`m Nancy Cordes. For kids in foster care, moving from home to home is hard enough, but imagine being given a trash bag to tote all your belongings. One man who lived it is now trying to give children something more. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab at one of Ukraine`s largest titanium mines, part of a potential deal between Presidents Trump and Zelenskyy that could dramatically change U.S.-Ukrainian relations and the course of the war.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Zelenskyy will be at the White House tomorrow to sign a deal that will give the United States access to Ukraine`s minerals in exchange for continuing U.S. aid. It does not include any specific security guarantees.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The deal will set up a jointly controlled fund to pay for postwar reconstruction and, President Trump hopes, to pay the U.S. back for its support of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab got a look at some of Ukraine`s mineral wealth.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voice-over): It was in these Soviet era 4x4s we were taken to one of Ukraine`s largest titanium mines. We traveled over bumpy roads to a remote corner of the country to see the vast reserves for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 To give you a sense of just how big this mine is, it`s so big you can see it from space. According to President Trump, this was just a sliver of Ukraine`s untapped wealth, which includes large lithium, cobalt, graphite, as well as oil, coal and gas deposits, which he wants to stake in as payback for three years of U.S. support in the war against Russia.\par \par \uc2 In theory, the joint fund will invest in Ukraine`s postwar reconstruction, but the country will have to hand over 50 percent of all natural resource revenues, something President Trump insists will earn the U.S. between $350 billion and $500 billion.\par \par \uc2 At Velta, the company which operates the titanium mine, we met with CEO Andriy Brodsky, who said President Trump`s math was wishful thinking.\par \par \uc2 (ANDRIY BRODSKY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "There is no definitive list of mineral deposits in Ukraine, and there are no estimates of the monetary value of these deposits," he said, "so we might as well be transferring 50 percent of the surface of the moon."\par \par \uc2 This may not be the moon, but Brodsky`s concern over the lack of exploration of Ukraine`s natural resources are shared by other experts in the field. Still, if U.S. companies do invest in Ukraine`s resources sector, Brodsky said it could ultimately give President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  what he wants, which are security guarantees.\par \par \uc2 (ANDRIY BRODSKY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "If five to 10 American companies were operating in Ukraine, then the U.S. would suggest placing American troops here."\par \par \uc2 So, really, what you`re saying is that, if the U.S. has more business interest in Ukraine, it will almost automatically give your country security guarantees?\par \par \uc2 (ANDRIY BRODSKY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "Yes, this is how the military and political system has worked forever everywhere."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And when Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  goes to the White House tomorrow to sign the joint fund agreement, he most certainly will not be getting those security guarantees.\par \par \uc2 But what he is likely hoping for is to turn the page with President Trump after an extremely tense few weeks.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Imtiaz Tyab in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: To our weather now, and the meteorological version of spring break is over.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn is here with the return of winter.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Yes, guys, you knew it was not going to last, correct?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you take a look at the radar picture, the first change is all because of this low-pressure system, which is moving through the area. I mean, it puts some rain down anywhere from Chicago to Boston today. It pushes out of the area for tomorrow, but, behind it, temperatures will be five, 10 degrees colder.\par \par \uc2 But the story is Saturday into Sunday. There is going to be a huge change. You`re going to go from feeling like spring to feeling like winter. New York City on Saturday will have a high temperature of 55 degrees. A little bit of wind could make it feel like 53. Not a big deal. Look at D.C. D.C. is 62 feeling like 59. That`s Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Another clipper comes through, changes those temperatures dramatically. You get to Sunday, the high temperature is only 34 in New York City. It feels like 21. It`s 39 in D.C. It feels like 31. And for tourists who are visiting New York City, if they`re walking through Central Park on Saturday, they have a light coat. It feels like it`s about 60 degrees outside.\par \par \uc2 Sunday morning, it feels like it`s about 15 degrees as they`re walking to see the Empire State Building, about a 45-degree feels-like change in 18 hours, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Lonnie, the cold hard reality. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Imagine kids forced to keep the few possessions they have in trash bags.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That would not do for a man who was once one of those kids. Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: There`s a well-known Bible verse that encourages people to bear one another`s burdens. It comes to mind because the man you`re about to meet lives every day in that spirit. He gives comfort to some of the most vulnerable among us because he knows firsthand the burdens they bear.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes has tonight`s Eye on America from Gaithersburg, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Dad wants to take a picture.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): The Scheer family isn`t bound by blood...\par \par \uc2 ROB SCHEER (Founder, Comfort Cases): Your brother doesn`t have anything on his pizza.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: ... but by shared experience. Rob Scheer and his five kids each made their own journey through the foster care system with a trash bag in tow.\par \par \uc2 ROB SCHEER: This is right before I went into foster care.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Rob was 12 the first time a case worker told him to shove his belongings in a trash bag to escape an abusive home. The pattern would repeat until he turned 18.\par \par \uc2 ROB SCHEER: And that`s when they gave me my trash bag and I became homeless.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Years later, when Rob and his husband, Reece, adopted through foster care, they were surprised at how little had changed.\par \par \uc2 Did your kids come to you with trash bags?\par \par \uc2 ROB SCHEER: You know, that was the aha moment for me. When my children arrived and each one of them had a trash bag, I knew we as a society had failed.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That moment drove him to start Comfort Cases, a nonprofit that replaces trash bags with luggage for kids in foster care.\par \par \uc2 ROB SCHEER: Every single child gets a brand-new pair of pajamas. We make sure they have a tag on it. And then our volunteers come in and they actually ribbon all of our pajamas, because we want our kids to know that this is a gift.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: To date, Comfort Cases has delivered more than 250,000 cases to children across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 ROB SCHEER: Being the one most important thing that every child who comes into our foster care system deserves dignity and hope. And how do you do that? Don`t give them a trash bag.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BAKER (Former Foster Child): To carry your stuff in a trash bag, it makes you feel like this is your fault, you`re worth nothing, none of this stuff is worth anything to anyone else.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Ashley Baker grew up in more than a dozen Pennsylvania foster homes and attended seven high schools. Just like Rob, she was given a trash bag each time she moved.\par \par \uc2 What did it feel like to be toting around your whole life in a garbage bag?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BAKER: Definitely, it was very overwhelming to see your things -- sorry -- see your things in that way and not on your dresser anymore or the stuff animals aren`t on your bed anymore and they`re in a bag.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Slowly, things are shifting. In the past two years, Maryland, Texas and Oregon have all banned the use of trash bags in foster care. Similar legislation was recently introduced in New York City.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BAKER: I thought, I will grow up to be the change maker. But to find out there`s others who want to make that change, it`s so heartwarming to know.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The goal is to get laws passed in all 50 states, so that every foster kid gets a backpack, a duffel bag, anything that tells them they matter.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Nancy Cordes, Gaithersburg, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: As they so deserve.\par \par \uc2 And John and I will be back in just a moment with memories of Gene Hackman.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Gene Hackman never considered himself a star, just a working actor who got lucky.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And when you think about the great movies he left us, you would have to say we got lucky too.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Martha Teichner.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN (Actor): Well, I always wanted to be an actor. That`s all I ever wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER (voice-over): He was an unlikely leading man.\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: Feel you don`t want to be on a team, feel free to leave right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: He wasn`t Hollywood-handsome, but he was charismatic.\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: You`re either very smart or incredibly stupid.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: You couldn`t take your eyes off him. In nearly 80 movies, he played every part like the chameleon of a character actor he was at heart.\par \par \uc2 BEN STILLER (Actor): My wife.\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: Oh, that`s right, isn`t it? Well, we will have to swing by her grave too.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: Gene Hackman won two Oscars in 1972 in The French Connection...\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: What about the last of the big-time spenders?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: ... and then in 1993 in "Unforgiven."\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: Well, I ain`t going to hurt no one, but I`m going to hurt you.\par \par \uc2 Cigars.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: He was a Hollywood titan who shunned Hollywood, Living with his wife, Betsy Arakawa, in the mountains near Santa Fe, New Mexico. I met him there in 2000, when he had just written the first of his five novels.\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: I need the diversity.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: He dropped out of school at 16 and lied about his age to join the Marines, but had, in fact, a relentless intellect.\par \par \uc2 You paint. You dive. You write books. What`s -- what else is on that list?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: Well, I`m a pilot, and I have raced cars.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: Gene Hackman`s assessment of his own acting career?\par \par \uc2 GENE HACKMAN: One could say, you know, that I achieved some manner of success.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA TEICHNER: That`s some understatement.\par \par \uc2 Gene Hackman was 95.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250228el2r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For February 25, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Skyler Henry, Ian Lee, Nancy Cordes, Mark Strassmann, Debora  Patta, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,910 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 This time, he means it, maybe.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says he is giving federal workers another chance to respond to his e-mail demand for a list of five things they accomplished last week, or be fired. He did not set a new deadline.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The White House says a million responses were received by the original deadline last night. One response we got to see is from Karoline Leavitt, the president`s press secretary.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Musk faced a revolt today in his Department of Government Efficiency; 21 staffers resigned, saying they would not be part of dismantling critical public services.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Even with that, even with different agencies giving employees different instructions about the Musk demand, Press Secretary Leavitt said Musk, the president and the Cabinet are working as one unified team.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House for us tonight trying to keep track of all of this -- Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Maurice, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Even some Republicans are now saying that Musk and DOGE should be handing some of these responsibilities over to the Cabinet agencies themselves, that they are in over their heads, because now more than a million federal employees are confused about what to do next. And this is coming at a time when even the directions from the president himself aren`t very clear.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s somewhat voluntary. But it`s also, if you don`t answer, I guess you get fired.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): There were new mixed messages from the White House today, with special adviser \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  now threatening termination for federal employees who don`t respond to an e-mail asking what they did last week.\par \par \uc2 The threat comes even after many federal agencies told workers not to respond to the e-mail.\par \par \uc2 RANDY ERWIN (President, National Federation of Federal Employees): We`re being inundated with calls.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Randy Erwin runs the NFFE, a union that represents 110,000 federal workers.\par \par \uc2 RANDY ERWIN: Mainly, they`re asking technical questions. Do I have to respond to this? Why? This is not in my chain of command. They also want to know, what are they looking for?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The confusion is sparking new questions about the power and structure of DOGE,the operation President Trump created to spearhead massive job cuts. At a court hearing yesterday, government lawyers were not able to identify who runs DOGE, nor would White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt today.\par \par \uc2 Who is the DOGE administrator?\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT (White House Press Secretary): So the president tasked \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to oversee the DOGE effort. There are career officials and there are political appointees who are helping run DOGE on a day-to-day basis.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: So is \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  the administrator?\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT: Reagan (sp?).\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thanks, Karoline.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: She later suggested there is someone in charge. She just wouldn`t say who.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT: I`m not going to reveal the name of that individual from this podium.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: CBS News was told late today that the acting administrator is Amy Gleason, a veteran of the first Trump administration. When reached earlier today by phone, Gleason said she was in Mexico and declined further comment.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Nancy joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, I want to ask you about these 21 employees who left and resigned from DOGE. Who are they and why did they say they`re leaving?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: So, John, these are tech experts who were already working to modernize federal computer systems before their operation was kind of absorbed by DOGE.\par \par \uc2 And they say that what they saw DOGE doing was making government less efficient, not more. They wrote -- quote -- "We will not use our skills as technologists to compromise core government systems, jeopardize Americans` sensitive data, or dismantle critical public services."\par \par \uc2 They issued this resignation letter anonymously, but they say they all left senior roles in the tech industry in order to go work for federal government.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: So, Nancy, what about all these other federal employees wondering tonight, do I respond, do I quit, what do I do? What`s the best advice tonight?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: So the advice that unions are giving their workers and members is that, if they have gotten instructions from their agency not to respond, then don`t. Otherwise, if they can`t get guidance from a superior, they should go ahead and answer it, because it`s possible their job could be on the line.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Nancy Cordes at the White House, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The White House won`t say how many federal workers have been or will be fired.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: But Ian Lee tracked down three of them in Wyoming and they told Ian what their firing could mean for the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE (voice-over): Even in the winter, the Shoshone National Forest buzzes with activity, with so many people snowmobiling, riding their bikes, hiking and rock climbing. Maintaining the country`s forests takes an army of Forest Service workers, including Dalyn Grindle.\par \par \uc2 What did you love most about your job?\par \par \uc2 DALYN GRINDLE (Fired Federal Worker): I loved going into work every day and being able to work with my colleagues.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Grindle, along with Jack Corney and Tim Leedy, recently received a letter that said they were being fired for poor job performance.\par \par \uc2 What was your reaction when you saw that the reason you`re being fired was because of poor performance?\par \par \uc2 TIM LEEDY (Fired Federal Worker): You know, I put my heart and soul into that job, and that`s just not true.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Every summer, tens of millions of Americans visit the national forests. But, this summer, they might look a little different.\par \par \uc2 DALYN GRINDLE: There`s a lot of unforeseen consequences that the general public aren`t always aware of.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: What do you fear then? What could happen?\par \par \uc2 JACK CORNEY (Fired Federal Worker): We`re clearing the trails for people to access the wilderness. And that`s just -- that`s not going to happen this year.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Let`s say you just arrived in town and you want some information about the forest, maybe a good place to camp or trail to hike on. Or maybe you`re scared about bears. You come to a place like this, your local ranger station. But because of the recent firings, this one won`t be open any time soon.\par \par \uc2 Terminations at the Forest Service also come as wildfires nationwide grow fiercer and more frequent.\par \par \uc2 Show of hands, how many of you were qualified to fight fires? So, you guys could all have been on the front lines this summer fighting fires if needed.\par \par \uc2 DALYN GRINDLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACK CORNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIM LEEDY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: I spoke with several senior Forest Service officials across the country, and they told me the future leadership of the agency has been wiped out and it could take decades to recover.\par \par \uc2 Another warned that some of these workers monitored bear populations, and, without them, there could be more encounters.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, many thanks, Ian Lee, in Lander, Wyoming, tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 President Trump says Ukrainian President Zelenskyy will go to the White House this Friday. They`re expected to sign a deal for Ukraine to share its mineral wealth with the United States.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Video from Chicago`s Midway Airport shows a \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  jet narrowly missing and avoiding a collision with a business jet that officials say crossed the runway without authorization.\par \par \uc2 Senior transportation correspondent Kris Van Cleave says there were seven runway incursions in the U.S. last year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : A new report finds consumer confidence in February had its largest one-month drop in nearly four years. It was driven by concerns about inflation and the impact of tariffs. And home prices hit an all-time high in November -- in December -- pardon me -- for the 19th month in a row. Case-Shiller says prices year over year were up nearly 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 One thing driving home prices higher is a shortage of inventory.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: There is a shortage of skilled workers as well. The construction industry says it needs to hire more than 400,000 this year to meet demand.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Skyler Henry.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voice-over): At construction sites such as this one in Clearwater, Florida, finding skilled workers is a constant struggle for contractors, including Greg Nichols. His nearly 30-year-old company specializes in excavation and utilities.\par \par \uc2 What is the ideal amount of folks that you would like to have on board?\par \par \uc2 GREG NICHOLS\uc2  (G.A. Nichols Company): Oh, boy. At one point, I had about 120 employees in the field. But the problem with that was, about 80 of them were no good.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: He can`t find enough experienced construction workers, so he had to cut his business in half. In his best years, he was making $14 million a year.\par \par \uc2 GREG NICHOLS: I just made the decision to shrink my company down. I decided I would take the number of men that I thought were competent and capable. And, from that, I would decide how much work we could do. Every day, we turn down offers for work.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Is that a good thing in your business?\par \par \uc2 GREG NICHOLS\uc2 : Not really.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: While the industry has seen an influx of younger workers, it is not keeping up with older workers who are retiring.\par \par \uc2 At 56, foreman Chris Wells (sp?) knows he can`t do this forever.\par \par \uc2 What happens when you decide to hang your boots up?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WELLS (Foreman): I`m afraid for construction, honestly. Everybody down here, I mean, is looking for help. And that`s why I encourage younger guys to come in and get their feet on the ground. Once you get some kind of skill, you will find that it`s very rewarding.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Nichols has tried to retain his workers by doubling their salaries to $40 an hour.\par \par \uc2 GREG NICHOLS\uc2 : I tell my guys, if you just do what you say you`re going to do and show up when you`re supposed to show up, then you`re a superstar in this business.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Now, in addition to offering that pay bump, Nichols has also gone as far as offering bonuses to some of his workers, offering housing to those who have struggled to find a place to lay their head, even digging into his own pocket to bail some of his workers out of jail to ensure that they can show up at a project the next day.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Skyler Henry for us tonight in Atlanta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn with warmer temperatures and melting snow.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m Mark Strassmann in Kenosha, Wisconsin, where talk is growing about government shrinking. How fast is too fast or not fast enough? That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: I`m Debora Patta. President Trump`s aid cuts could not have come at a worse time for Sudan, as it battles the world`s most severe humanitarian crisis ever.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : When President Trump ordered a 90-day freeze on foreign aid, no one on Earth felt the impact more than the people of Sudan. Two years of civil war has left more than 25 million Sudanese starving. That is half the population.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It is the largest humanitarian crisis the world has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 And Debora Patta saw it firsthand.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): This is the city of Omdurman, home to over two million Sudanese, with a soup kitchen on almost every street corner.\par \par \uc2 Well-heeled professionals like Mohammed Hamad line up for food. He used to be an engineer, but has had no work since the war began and the economy collapsed nearly two years ago.\par \par \uc2 (MOHAMMED HAMAD SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "We rely on God and aid," he told us. And, for now, it`s been left to God.\par \par \uc2 Funded largely by the U.S., nearly 80 percent of soup kitchens have already shut down following President Trump`s aid suspension. The kitchens also serve the few hospitals still standing like Al Noa, which has no funds to provide any meals. What you see here is the only food these patients will eat each day, most of it paid for by America.\par \par \uc2 The hospital is overwhelmed and under-resourced. We saw patients being treated on the ground.\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMAL MOHAMMAD (Al Noa Hospital): We don`t have enough beds.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Medical staff under the leadership of Dr. Jamal Mohammad...\par \par \uc2 (DR. JAMAL MOHAMMAD SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: ... are struggling to save the lives of the starving and war wounded.\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMAL MOHAMMAD: You can see how crowded it is.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: It`s very, very full.\par \par \uc2 Prior to President Trump`s aid freeze, they were already running out of painkillers, bandages and lifesaving equipment.\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMAL MOHAMMAD: I don`t know what`s behind that decision of President Trump, but I think it`s going to increase and deepen the suffering of our people.\par \par \uc2 (DR. JAMAL MOHAMMAD SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 (AKRAM ATLAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 (DR. JAMAL MOHAMMAD SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Here, he was consoling 10-year-old Akram Atlan after he was hit by shrapnel. The little boy was terrified he would lose his leg and a long-held dream.\par \par \uc2 (AKRAM ATLAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: You want to be a soccer player?\par \par \uc2 (AKRAM ATLAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: But he was in good hands. Dr. Mohammad was a leading orthopedic surgeon before the war. Now he runs this hospital without any pay. Akram was prepped for surgery with him later that day. Mohammad operated for over four hours and successfully removed this piece of shrapnel that had shattered the boy`s leg.\par \par \uc2 Akram will now have a chance to play soccer again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Debora Patta joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Deb, if the U.S. retreats, who steps in to help the Sudanese people?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: John, other countries are contributing, but the sudden withdrawal of funds has left a huge void that just can`t be filled overnight.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  estimates that it needs $6 billion to prevent people from starving to death.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Deb, supporters of \uc2 USAID\uc2  say their work is not just charity, but in the U.S.` national interest. What do they mean by that?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, U.S. humanitarian aid is the ground game of America`s foreign policy. I spoke to a former U.S. ambassador, Maurice, to the \uc2 African Union\uc2 , who told me that the debate around \uc2 USAID\uc2  has been framed as if it was a charity America cannot afford.\par \par \uc2 She said that`s not true. It`s a strategic investment in U.S. relationships globally.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Debora Patta, thanks so much tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Here in the United States, warmer temperatures this week mean melting snow.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Lonnie Quinn is here now with that for us -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Well, I will tell you guys, a picture is worth 1,000 words. In fact, video is even better.\par \par \uc2 And what you`re looking at right here, this is the U.P. of Michigan. Holton, Michigan. Lookit, time-lapse video right there showing you the snow just melting away. They were about 10 degrees above average. That warmth is going to continue for tomorrow. In fact, take a look at this. Every city that you see listed here is running 10 or 20 degrees above average, even Fargo.\par \par \uc2 I often refer to Fargo as one of the coldest spots in our country, 40 degrees tomorrow. They shouldn`t be getting above freezing. And for those of you who are looking for spring, 23 days away, boy, it feels good out there. But some folks love the snow, skiers, snowboarders. And you always think about the best skiers or the best snow out West.\par \par \uc2 And yet, this year, the Green Mountains of Vermont, Jay Peak with more snow on the ground than any other mountain in the country. They are an inch more than Alta, Utah. That warmth in the East doesn`t last. You`re back below the -- well, say you`re into the teens for windchills by the time you get to Sunday morning. So, winter will be back, guys. It`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That, it will.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : How do some Trump supporters feel about the mass firings of federal workers?\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We visit a state he won to find out. Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: We are now into week six of President Trump shaking up and paring down the federal work force, something that surprised even his closest supporters.\par \par \uc2 We went back to a battleground state Mr. Trump narrowly won to find out how people who voted for him feel about these mass firings.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann has tonight`s Eye on America from Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): At Franks Diner in Kenosha.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Here`s table two.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: ... the T-shirts say it all. "Order what you want, eat what you get."\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I got a table 10.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: We wanted a conversation with supporters of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Do you support the downsizing?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: About the White House drive to shrink American government. We got what we ordered.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They`re spending all our money and there`s too many people in there.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And when they talk about waste and fraud and abuse, you believe that`s going on?\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Oh.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Why Kenosha, Wisconsin? It`s a purple city in a purple state. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won battleground Wisconsin last November, but just barely, by a single point.\par \par \uc2 He won Kenosha County by six points.\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: The war has cost us billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In the back of Franks, we found this reunion of former factory workers.\par \par \uc2 Show of hands, how many of you guys voted for Trump? All but one.\par \par \uc2 Among them, Eric Davidson (sp?), who believes many government agencies and workers accomplish little.\par \par \uc2 ERIC DAVIDSON (Wisconsin Resident): Are you doing your job or are you just making paper airplanes?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And you think there are a number of government employees who are flying paper airplanes?\par \par \uc2 ERIC DAVIDSON: Yes, I`m just going to come in here and put my time in and get my pension and go out.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Bruce Wilson (sp?) is the only one at the table who didn`t vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 When you hear this conversation, do you agree with them, these guys?\par \par \uc2 BRUCE WILSON (Wisconsin Resident): In some respects, I do.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: So the idea of shrinking government, do you support that?\par \par \uc2 BRUCE WILSON: Clearly, if there`s waste, there should be some shrinkage.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: What hits home for the people we met in Kenosha, Trump`s repeated message.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Fraud, waste, abuse. Fraud, waste, abuse.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Fraud, waste, abuse. Fraud, waste, abuse. Just hearing that over and over again seems to have resonated?\par \par \uc2 ERIK NISBET (\uc2 Northwestern University\uc2 ): Yes, and that repetition is very persuasive.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: At \uc2 Northwestern University\uc2 , Professor Erik Nisbet studies public opinion and strategic messaging.\par \par \uc2 ERIK NISBET: You know, a lot people judge the veracity information by how often they hear it. It`s not proof. It`s allegations. But that`s enough for people.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In a recent Reuters/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  national poll,rMD-IN_ 42 percent of Americans support Trump`s push to downsize government. Still, 53 percent oppose it, including Democrat Steve Farrow (sp?).\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that services could be cut back?\par \par \uc2 STEVE FARROW (Wisconsin Resident): I`m really worried about that more than anything else.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And which services are you most worried about being cut back?\par \par \uc2 STEVE FARROW: Mail, for one, Social Security services, Medicare, Medicaid, all of them.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But in this converted railroad car, many of the customers we met simply feel the president is on the right track.\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: He`s getting rid of a lot of corruption.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Have you seen any proof of corruption?\par \par \uc2 MAN #6: Well, not me, myself. But I can see it through all -- through all the news on both sides.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: They`re generally eating up what`s happening in Washington, even if proof is not on the menu.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Mark Strassmann in Kenosha, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That`s a slice of our electorate tonight.\par \par \uc2 John and I will be right back with the man who challenged FDR from the grave.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now an unfamiliar name and why you may be hearing it soon, William Ewart Humphrey.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : One hundred years ago today, Humphrey, a Republican, took a seat on the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Then, in 1933, Democratic President \uc2 Franklin Roosevelt\uc2  fired him, believing he`d be an obstacle to The New Deal. After Humphrey died the next year, the executive of his estate sued for wrongful termination and back pay.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : In the case, known as Humphrey`s Executor, the Supreme Court ruled that the act that created the \uc2 FTC\uc2  barred FDR from firing Humphrey. A commissioner could be removed for inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office, not policy differences.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Which brings us to President Trump`s firing spree. He`s been targeting agencies whose members he appoints.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Last month, he removed Gwynne Wilcox from the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . She is challenging that, which could lead the Supreme Court to reexamine Humphrey`s Executor, the independence of federal agencies, and the power of the president who controls them.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And resurrect William Ewart Humphrey 91 years after his death. And we will be watching.\par \par \uc2 That is the CBS Evening News. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . I will see you on Evening News Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And back here tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250226el2p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For February 24, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Omar Villafranca, Scott MacFarlane, Ed O`Keefe, Lonnie Quinn,  Nancy Chen, Imtiaz Tyab, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  ordered federal employees who have been working from home to report to the office this week.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And ordered them to explain why they should have a job next week.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : By giving him examples of what they accomplished last week. He demanded five examples by midnight tonight.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: President Trump, whose accomplishments last week included firing federal employees, voiced support for Musk today. He claimed a lot of workers are not responding to Musk because, as the president put it, they don`t exist.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But neither does any evidence of that claim. Many workers are not responding because their bosses told them they don`t need to, employees who work for the State, Defense, Justice and Homeland Security Departments.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Education, Commerce and Transportation, on the other hand, told employees to respond. So there`s a split in the administration.\par \par \uc2 And Ed O`Keefe is at the White House tonight to try to sort this all out for us -- Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Hey, guys, the Trump administration now telling agencies they can in fact ignore what Musk said entirely, adding even more confusion in the closing hours before a threatened deadline.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice-over): To reply or not to reply, that was the question roiling the nation`s two million plus federal employees.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BERMAN (Federal Employee): The morale is, of course, abysmal right now. We are being told we`re not worth the money that is spent on us.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Workers, including Alex Berman (sp?) with the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 , were dumbfounded by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who on Saturday told the rank and file they`d have to share what they got done last week. Failure to respond, he said, will be taken as a resignation.\par \par \uc2 It`s something Musk also asked \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees to do when he started layoffs there. A formal e-mail from the Office of Personnel Management asked federal employees for replies by 11:59 p.m. tonight with approximately five bullets of what you accomplished last week.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BERMAN: This is just another step in the demonization and harassment of employees who have only worked to serve the American people. The other thing is, who`s going to read 2.4 million questionnaires?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Adding to the confusion was that decision by several of the largest federal departments to instruct workers not to comply.\par \par \uc2 President Trump backed up Musk`s original demands today.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): I thought it was great, because we have people that don`t show up to work and nobody even knows if they work for the government.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Alex Berman worries this kind of request will further erode the quality of the nation`s public servants.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BERMAN: They are hounding good people and good workers out of the civil service. This will be taken as another anecdotal piece of evidence saying that we`re not the good workers we know we are.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Ed O`Keefe joins us from the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, Ed, when the music stops, what is the final White House guidance on this?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, this, afternoon, it turns out, they told the heads of human resources essentially at all these departments, don`t worry about what Musk sent around. You decide what goes on personnel-wise at your departments.\par \par \uc2 But it was a pretty public split, right? You had the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director. Kash Patel, the defense secretary, Pete Hegseth, two of the president`s most loyal advocates out there for him, saying, ignore \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . You had the commerce secretary, Howard Lutnick, the Treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, saying, no, listen to Musk.\par \par \uc2 So, amid that split today, the White House press secretary tells us -- quote -- "Everyone is working together as one unified team at the direction of President Trump. Any notion to the contrary is completely false."\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And among these workers, there is real fear out there. Is that the whole idea?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It was the whole idea, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the person who`s now serving as White House budget director told \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  last summer that he wanted to -- quote -- he wanted to make sure federal employees were -- quote -- "traumatically affected" by the changes that they would be making across the work force.\par \par \uc2 Whether they have reached the trauma stage or not is unclear, but it is certain federal employees tonight upset and mighty confused by what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Not a bug, but a feature.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Ed.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And now from the firing to the hiring. The president has chosen another supporter in the media for a key position. Dan Bongino will be deputy -- deputy director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , no Senate confirmation necessary.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Neither apparently is \uc2 FBI\uc2  experience. Bongino, a former Secret Service agent and New York City police officer, never worked for the bureau.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane has more about him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): Dan Bongino touts his army of followers, millions of talk radio and podcast listeners who share his devotion to President Trump, his conspiracy theories about the 2020 election, and his pledge to own the libs.\par \par \uc2 DAN BONGINO (Deputy \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Designee): Every kooky lunatic, anti- Trump, swampy, media, left-wing, progressive, AOC, Bernie Sanders-type crazy person loses it. Oh, my God, taking over the world. Listen...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Bongino confirmed today he is trading his army for G- men.\par \par \uc2 DAN BONGINO: I`m committed to service.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Neither Bongino nor the new bureau director, Kash Patel, have ever served in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And both are MAGA loyalists, who critics worry threaten an independent \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bongino`s new position was announced just hours after an association of current \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents had asked Patel to ensure an active special agent would get the post, as has been the case for 117 years.\par \par \uc2 GREGG NUNZIATA (Former \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  Aide): You want someone who can build faith. Instead, he`s chosen someone who is really a radical conspiracy theorist.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gregg Nunziata is a former aide to then-Florida Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GREGG NUNZIATA: You`re going to see career professionals leaving.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You think there will be resignations because of this?\par \par \uc2 GREGG NUNZIATA: No, I do think. I think it`ll lead to that. It`ll be harder to recruit and attract the kind of people that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  requires.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Bongino steps into the bureau amid turmoil, with agents who worked January 6 cases now under scrutiny themselves.\par \par \uc2 DAN BONGINO: If you swore to uphold the Constitution of the United States as an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent and engaged in a tyrannical investigation against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with partisan intent and not the Constitution in mind, you do not deserve your job.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Scott MacFarlane joins us now from D.C.\par \par \uc2 So, Scott, what is Bongino actually in charge of here? What`s the job description?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Oh, Maurice, he can touch any part of this agency he wants to.\par \par \uc2 If there`s going to be a big case, a politically charged case, he gets to approve it, reassigning staffers, promoting staffers. What do they want to spend money on? This is really the blocking and tackling. He could have his fingerprints all over the bureau.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So what do we know in the early days here, Scott, about what this new bureau, what direction it`s taking?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Oh, in just the first few hours, John, a pretty big change. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has ordered more than 1,000 agents and employees who work here in Washington, D.C., to be reassigned to field offices, outposts all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Kash Patel has argued he wants more agents in the community. But, Maurice and John, this could destabilize things, cause more resignations, retirements, and really thin the herd of agents supposed to protect America from terrorists.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Scott MacFarlane in Washington tonight, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 It is one of the most famous images in U.S. history, Secret Service agent Clint Hill leaping onto President Kennedy`s limousine in 1963. Twelve years later, Hill broke his silence about the assassination in a 60 Minutes interview.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CLINT HILL (Former Secret Service Agent): If I had reacted just a little bit quicker -- and I could have, I guess. I`m going to live with that to my grave.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Clint Hill died Friday. He was 93.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A Delta flight to Columbia, South Carolina, was forced to return to Atlanta when smoke filled the cabin of the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  717. Emergency slides deployed, no injuries, though, among the 99 people on board.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And in St. Peter`s Square, cardinals recited the first of what will be a nightly rosary for Pope Francis. He`s fighting double pneumonia and mild kidney failure. The Vatican says he`s critical, but stable.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now to the measles outbreak going on in Texas.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The state Health Department reports at least 90 cases so far this year.\par \par \uc2 And Omar Villafranca is in Gaines County, Texas.\par \par \uc2 And, Omar, tell us how the measles got out of Gaines County, Texas.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Well, the small religious community where it started does interact with the general public, and now this is the biggest measles outbreak in the state more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the hospital behind me has set up a testing site to try to get control of this virus. Now, this outbreak started in an unvaccinated Mennonite community here in Gaines County. And the majority of the cases are children. Now, even though this community is hard to get to, it`s not hard for the virus to get out.\par \par \uc2 Public health officials say a Gaines County woman who later tested positive traveled more than 400 miles to San Marcos and San Antonio, visiting two universities and tourist hot spots while infected. And now officials are warning people in those locations that they may have been exposed.\par \par \uc2 Now, measles is so contagious that, if you`re infected, up to 90 percent of the people who you come in contact with, if they are unvaccinated or don`t have immunity, will get sick.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Omar, talk to us about this being such a remote location, but the concern that this can get out and spread so quickly.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Yes, and that is why they`re trying to test right now.\par \par \uc2 But they`re also working a state system to try to make those counties across the state aware that somebody with the sickness went to their area and be alert and look for cases.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Omar Villafranca tonight in Texas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn with a winter warm-up.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I`m Nancy Chen. As factories around the country have closed down, some communities are struggling to survive. Now one small city is paying workers $20,000 to move there. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab in Kyiv, Ukraine. It was three years ago Russia launched its full-scale invasion. Ukrainians we spoke to today told us they have never been more worried for the future.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The war between Russia and Ukraine entered a fourth year today. Meeting at the White House with French President Macron, President Trump said Russia would be willing to accept European peacekeepers in Ukraine as part of any deal to end the war.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: At the United Nations, the United States joined Russia in voting against a resolution demanding the immediate withdrawal of Russian troops from Ukraine. The vote reflected the change in U.S. policy.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is in Kyiv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voice-over): In the Kyiv suburb of Borodyanka, a long row of prefabricated shelters for those displaced by war. It`s in one of these rooms where Lilia Dyshlyovska (sp?) has lived for nearly three years. Her home was destroyed in a Russian bombing which led her husband, Vladimir (sp?), to volunteer to fight with the Ukrainian army.\par \par \uc2 Every day since, she has been consumed by a conflict which has destroyed so much of her country and her life. We went to meet Lilia in the tiny room she now calls home.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, hello.\par \par \uc2 Where she showed us the art projects she works on to distract herself from what she calls negative thoughts.\par \par \uc2 It`s three years since Russia invaded Ukraine. How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 (LILIA DYSHLYOVSKA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "Pain, just pain that turns you inside out," she says. "I never thought the time would come when my husband would have to fight, but it`s even more painful that it`s Russians."\par \par \uc2 You have already lost so much in this war. You have lost your home. Your husband`s on the front line, and you have lost your business too. How do you make sense of all of this?\par \par \uc2 (LILIA DYSHLYOVSKA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "There is no point to any of this," she says. "There is no point."\par \par \uc2 Three years ago today, Borodyanka was the scene of one of Russia`s worst massacres when it launched its full-scale invasions. Hundreds were killed in the Russian bombings and occupation here. We have visited Borodyanka a lot since Russian forces withdrew. Most of the destroyed Soviet-era housing blocks on its main street have since been abandoned.\par \par \uc2 (LILIA DYSHLYOVSKA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: It`s on this same road Lilia used to live in an apartment with her family. Their home was so damaged in the Russian attacks, it`s unlikely they will ever be able to return.\par \par \uc2 (LILIA DYSHLYOVSKA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "I just want to go home to see my husband, to have my children come visit me," she says. "I want to tell the whole world that this should not be allowed to happen."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Imtiaz Tyab joins us now from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, given what Lilia feels, what`s her feeling about these peace negotiations and ending the war?\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Well, Lilia, like so many Ukrainians, are asking themselves tonight, just what is an acceptable loss after having already lost so much?\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is a country that has seen entire cities destroyed, tens of thousands of Ukrainians killed. And now here we are, three years into this grinding war and the very real possibility that they may be pressured into giving up territory to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Imtiaz, President Trump wants to make a deal for Ukraine`s minerals. What would that look like?\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Well, we`re hearing a deal may be close. And to quote President Trump, the agreement would help to -- the U.S., rather, to recoup what he described as tens of billions of dollars in money and weapons. But for President Zelenskyy, he wants security guarantees, and a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Imtiaz Tyab in Kyiv, Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Here`s some good news, 24 days until spring.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Lonnie Quinn tells us some part of the country are getting a sweet preview.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Yes, and, you know, and a lot of folks are wondering, is this the beginning of spring? Will it just continue? That`s not going to be the case.\par \par \uc2 But, currently, you`re right. You`re dealing with your taste of spring. Take a look at the numbers that we see for portions of the area, all right? High temperatures today, Los Angeles, 78 degrees. You go up to Fargo, 45. Think about this. Fargo, a warm day for you this time of the year would be like around the freezing mark; 45, and it gets warmer for many places around the area.\par \par \uc2 L.A. is up to 84 by the time you get to Wednesday. Fargo still stays above the 40-degree mark, about 41 on Wednesday, so a pseudo heat wave, even for the Northern Plains. And speaking of the Northern Plains, the Northern Tier and the Central Tier, that snowpack that`s on the ground right now, watch it get eaten up by these warm temperatures.\par \par \uc2 Now, that sounds great for a lot of folks out there, but, remember, snow melts and it becomes water. We have some flood alerts in place for portions of Idaho over the next couple of days because that snow is just going to melt too quickly and it will cause a challenge for the folks in that area.\par \par \uc2 But for those of you right now who like these warmer temperatures, you get them for a few more days. It`s not going to stay in place indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Like a head fake here.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: More winter to come.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn with some brief bit of good news.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A city losing population offered a financial incentive to move there.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : If you had a work-from-home job. Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : What does a struggling store do when it wants to attract new customers? Often, it puts on a sale. And what does a struggling city do when it wants to attract new residents? If it`s the city of Cumberland, Maryland, it offers a bonus with a string attached.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voice-over): At the edge of Appalachia and the Allegheny Mountains, the regulars almost always leave Caporale`s Bakery with their hands full...\par \par \uc2 DAVE CAPORALE (Caporale`s Bakery): Thanks, Jim.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: ... just like they have done for the last 118 years in the city of Cumberland.\par \par \uc2 DAVE CAPORALE: That is my grandfather right from the old store. And that`s my dad, Gus.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: While Dave Caporale is the fourth generation to man the counter, his dad, Gus, still pitches in, in the back.\par \par \uc2 But, over time, the city around their bakery has changed. Once Maryland`s second largest community, Cumberland was known as the Queen City. Its crown lost its luster, though, as the area`s three biggest factories all closed within five years.\par \par \uc2 What was it like seeing the city change?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It was like a real busy town. And then they start downsizing, one leave, and the other leave, and we really got hit by it big time.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Can I just talk to you just a second?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: CBS News was here in 1978 when the tire factory began laying off employees.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Are you being going to be affected by the layoffs at all?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: No, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: It`s only going to be a small group.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Unfortunately, they were wrong. Soon, the entire plant shuttered.\par \par \uc2 As companies left town, people did as well. In 1940, the population was nearly 40,000. Today, it`s been halved to less than 20,000. But the city is now hoping to turn a page.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND MORRISS (Mayor of Cumberland, Maryland): Cumberland has always...\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Last year, Mayor Ray Morriss announced an attention-grabbing offer, up to 20,000 bucks each for 10 people willing to move here.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RAYMOND MORRISS: We were looking for people that were bringing their jobs with them. We were looking for remote workers.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Twenty thousand dollars is a lot of money to offer up.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RAYMOND MORRISS: That`s an amount that could make people`s eyes open a little bit.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Cumberland is one of more than 70 places that have paid people to uproot and move in over the past five years.\par \par \uc2 Is it radical to pay people to move to a city?\par \par \uc2 EVAN STARR (Professor, University of Maryland): If you`re a government and you want to bring people to your area, well, before remote work, you had to recruit businesses that were expanding. It could be difficult.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: University of Maryland Professor Evan Starr studies payment programs like these.\par \par \uc2 EVAN STARR: Now, with remote work, what you can do is try to attract workers directly.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: He points to a successful pioneer, Tulsa Remote, which has enticed more than 3,000 participants with grants of $10,000 each. The investment has paid off, raising an additional $11 million in new tax revenue.\par \par \uc2 EVAN STARR: They can use that money to spend on schools and other local amenities. They can make a big difference in the area.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Dave Caporale is looking for a similar boost in Cumberland.\par \par \uc2 DAVE CAPORALE: That was probably in the early `50s. It was just a different time, you know?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Seems like it was pretty bustling then.\par \par \uc2 DAVE CAPORALE: It was, yes. And we will get back to that.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVE CAPORALE: Hope to.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But if hope alone won`t get it done, they`re thinking, here, throw in some dough as well.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Nancy Chen in Cumberland, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The first time many of us saw or heard the great Roberta Flack, she was singing that song made famous in the 1971 film Play Misty for Me.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It was her first number one hit, and in 1973 brought her first Grammy for record of the year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The next year, her signature song, Killing Me Softly, made her the first solo artist to win record of the year two years in a row.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Where Is The Love put her at top of the charts again.\par \par \uc2 Over her career, Roberta Flack was nominated for 14 Grammys and won four.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: A little more than two years ago, Flack revealed she had ALS and could no longer perform. She died today in New York City of cardiac arrest.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Her voice, part of the soundtrack of the 1970s, lives on in 16 top 10 hits on the charts. Roberta Flack was 88.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And what a gift to leave the world.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: That is the news here. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . See you on Evening News Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250225el2o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Pope Francis Health Battle; Federal Workers DOGE Deadline; New Deputy \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director; Three Years of War; Israel Moves Tanks into Occupied West Bank; Deadly Listeria Outbreak; U.S. Measles Outbreak Grows; Deadly Romance Scam; Daring Canyon Rescue; Stars Shine at the \uc2 SAG\uc2  Awards; Trump`s Ukraine Policy; Trump`s Foreign Policy; Conservatives Win German Elections; Trump Bringing Back Title 42; Ripples of Generosity; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Beyond the Gates; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Chris Livesay, Nancy  Cordes, Imtiaz Tyab, Debora Patta, Adriana Diaz, Celine Gounder, Rob  Marciano, Andres Gutierrez, H.R. McMaster, Holly Williams, David Begnaud,  Nancy Giles \par \uc2 14,032 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We begin the week with trumpets as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, but we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, new developments in the health of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , who is hospitalized in critical condition. How the faithful are responding.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I kind of knew it might be time for him to go home. Kind of hope not, I love him. I hope he`s around a little while longer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  demands federal workers list what they`ve done or lose their jobs. Why some Trump appointees are telling their staff say no.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CURTIS (R-UT): We don`t need to be so cold and hard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Deadly romance scam: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says a woman is facing charges after tricking her victims online.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER EVANS (FBI Las Vegas division special agent in charge): Yes, this is technically a romance scam, but this is a romance scam on steroids.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  announcing he is willing to step down for peace in Ukraine or \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Hollywood`s award season is in full swing. Screen Actors Guild awards were handed out in Los Angeles last night.\par \par \uc2 KIERAN CULKIN (Actor): It is funny that the heaviest of all awards is the one that is given by actors, us, what we do -- means so much.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Alex Rodriguez in the house shooting this three pointer from halfway he makes it in, rocking the house.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Former MLB`er, Alex Rodriguez drained a half-court shot to win a Bucknell student $10,000.00.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: That man, he just stormed the court and he is celebrating with A-Rod because that`s what you do as a college student.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Alex Rodriguez. We will have more on that. I`m going to do that for "Talk of the Table." I thought it was great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great athlete.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with the breaking news overnight. I know you`ve heard about this, the health of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 .. He has been in the hospital in critical condition, and according to the Vatican, he`s now showing early signs of poor kidney function.\par \par \uc2 Crowds have gathered outside the hospital in Rome where the Pope has been treated for the past 10 days, and the whole world is watching.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay is in Rome, with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Chris, the whole world is watching, and many of them are praying. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: That`s right, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, a Vatican source just told us that \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is awake and he`s able to get up. He`s also able to take his medicine regularly. He`s also able to eat, and they said that he is in a good mood.\par \par \uc2 But despite that, he`s still in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: For the second Sunday in a row, the Pope`s windows remain shuttered at the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 Around the Catholic world, from Mexico to Massachusetts, the faithful poured out prayers for the Holy Father.\par \par \uc2 In Rome, churches like the Papal Basilica of St. John Lateran held a special mass.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think he is stronger. I think he`s okay with whatever happens. Whether he gets better or not, I think he`s ready for that outcome.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: What started as bronchitis, became much worse. The Pope, now suffering from double pneumonia, required high flow oxygen and blood transfusions after suffering a prolonged asthma-like respiratory crisis on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Then Sunday, blood tests revealed early signs of kidney failure, serious for anyone; critical, for an 88-year-old already missing part of a lung that was removed when he was a young man.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Vatican consultant, Father Anthony Figueiredo.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ANTHONY FIGUEIREDO: He`s a man like you and me, even though he`s the Pope, which means he has his own fears.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Even the Pope is afraid of death.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ANTHONY FIGUEIREDO: Even the Pope and quite recently as well, he indicated that, should he die, he hopes it will be quickly rather than a long period of suffering.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Now, the Vatican is notoriously tight lipped, but the Pope has advised his medical staff to be transparent about his health -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Chris Livesay for us in Rome. Chris, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Back here in this country, over the weekend, an extraordinary message from billionaire, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , a message to every single federal worker, an ultimatum via social media, reply to an e-mail with five things you did last week, or else you`re out of a job.\par \par \uc2 He set the deadline for midnight tonight.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House. We know exactly what she did last week. I can name five things off the top of my head, Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Thank you so much, Tony.\par \par \uc2 This mass e-mail is now causing mass confusion. Federal workers at the Defense and State Departments, DHS, Intel are among those, numerous agencies now being told not to answer it, this whole episode raising new questions about the scope and the limits of Musk`s power as a special assistant to the president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States): Elon is doing a great job.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: After \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  warned it was coming, an e-mail went out to millions of federal workers directing them to list five bullets of what you accomplished last week by midnight tonight. On his social media platform, X, Musk threatened that failure to respond will be taken as a resignation. The ultimatum coming just hours after President Trump urged Musk to get more aggressive as he works to fire thousands of government employees.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re removing all of the unnecessary, incompetent, and corrupt bureaucrats from the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But even some of the President`s allies are taking issue with the approach.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CURTIS (R-UT): If I could say one thing to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , it`s like, please put a dose of compassion in to this. These are real people. These are real lives.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And while a few agencies urged workers to comply with the e- mail, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  led by Kash Patel told agents to pause on responding, so did the State Department led by \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , telling staffers that no employee is obligated to report their activities outside of their department chain of command. The Pentagon also opted out just days after the President made major personnel moves there.\par \par \uc2 He fired the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs, C. Q. Brown and the chief of the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 , whom Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth once derided as inexperienced and suggested she was only promoted because she is a woman.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (\uc2 US Department of Defense\uc2  Secretary): The President deserves to pick his key National Security and military advisory team.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Democratic Senator Jack Reed slammed the moves.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACK REED (D-RI): It`s the beginning of a very, very serious degradation of the military and politicization of the military.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump intends to nominate retired Air Force Lieutenant General Dan "Razin" Caine to replace General C.Q. Brown, he`d be the first three-star general named to be Joint Chiefs Chairman.\par \par \uc2 And in another move, last night, Trump announced that conservative podcaster and former Secret Service Agent Dan Bongino will serve as Deputy Director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  under Kash Patel, second in command at the agency is traditionally a career agent, but Bongino actually lacks any \uc2 FBI\uc2  experience.\par \par \uc2 However, like Patel, he has been a fierce defender of the president -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Three years ago today, despite what you may have heard, Russia invaded Ukraine, starting a war that has cost hundreds of thousands of lives.\par \par \uc2 This morning, a group of Western leaders are in Ukraine`s capital to support President Zelenskyy on the anniversary... Notably absent, President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who has been critical of Ukraine and has reopened relations with Russia. Meanwhile, Russia`s attacks continue.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab reports from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: After three years of war, Ukraine`s capital was targeted in an unprecedented Russian drone attack on the eve of the third anniversary of Moscow`s full scale invasion.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Throughout the years of bitter fighting, Ukrainians have had to flee Russian bombs, see whole cities destroyed, and suffer alleged war crimes from Kremlin backed forces.\par \par \uc2 It also saw the U.S. commit to around $180 billion to support the fight, from ambulances to long-range missiles.\par \par \uc2 We were in the Kherson Region in 2022 when some of the vital shipments arrived.\par \par \uc2 These American made Howitzers shoot further, move faster, and can be hidden far easier than other weapons.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m trying to get the money back --\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: But President Trump upended that earlier this month when he entered into direct talks with Russia and falsely claimed Ukraine had started the war.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Zelenskyy better move fast or he is not going to have a country left. Got to move. Got to move fast, because that war is going in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we`re successfully negotiating an end to the war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The abrupt change in U.S. policy was something Zelenskyy grappled with at a lengthy press conference we attended in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian President): I hope that it will not have influence on other steps of the United States, and they will not cancel or stop or freeze any support.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Crucial support for a nation that has seen tens of thousands killed in the fight for their country`s freedom.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And in that press conference, President Zelenskyy also said he would resign if it meant his country would be at peace and if it were able to be part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , that transatlantic military alliance that Russia is so bitterly against, and something the Trump administration has already said Ukraine likely won`t join as part of any deal it makes with Moscow -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Imtiaz Tyab in Ukraine for us this morning. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In a bit, we`ll talk with President Trump`s former National Security adviser, that is retired General H.R. McMaster, about the Russia-Ukraine War and the shakeup in the top ranks of The Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning to the Middle East, a ceasefire in Gaza is at risk. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  released six more Israeli hostages over the weekend, but Israel then refused to release hundreds of Palestinian prisoners accusing \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of using those hostages for propaganda.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Israel`s Army has stepped up its military operations in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta has more on that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Israeli tanks have moved into the occupied West Bank for the first time in over two decades. The military operation comes amidst fears that the fragile Gaza ceasefire set to expire by the end of the week will not be extended.\par \par \uc2 The last living Israeli hostages to be freed as part of the six-week truce were reunited with loved ones over the weekend, but in Gaza, families waited in vain after Israel said it had delayed the release of more than 600 Palestinian prisoners until \uc2 Hamas\uc2  stops what it calls its humiliating hostage handovers.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  now says it will not engage in any further negotiations until the prisoners are handed over.\par \par \uc2 Families of captives still in Gaza are insisting talks must continue, and Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  was heckled during a military graduation on Sunday, accused of abandoning Shiri Bibas and her two boys after their bodies were finally brought home for burial.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Special Envoy to the Middle East, Steve Witkoff, arrives in the region later this week in a bid to ensure the ceasefire holds for a second phase - - Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Debora Patta in East Jerusalem, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has recalled nutritional supplements tied to a deadly outbreak of listeria. The products were sold to hospitals and long-term care facilities.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus co-anchor, Adriana Diaz is here.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Listeria cases tied to these supplemental shakes have been reported from coast to coast, and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says it is tracking this outbreak, which has left at least 38 people sick and led to 11 deaths so far.\par \par \uc2 The recall effects frozen supplemental shakes labeled under the Lyons ReadyCare and Sysco Imperial brands. They come in four ounce cartons in a number of flavors. These were sold primarily to long-term care facilities like nursing homes and hospitals according to the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cases have been reported in at least 21 states. Of the 38 illnesses so far, all but one were hospitalized. Officials say the outbreak includes cases dating back to 2018, but remains ongoing with 20 reported since last year.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 Sysco\uc2  and \uc2 Lyons Magnus\uc2 , which distributes the drinks, told CBS News they have since recalled the supplemental shakes with \uc2 Sysco\uc2  expressing condolences to those affected -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very much appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 In this morning`s "Health Watch," a measles outbreak in the US has grown to nearly 100 cases, most in the state of Texas. It`s the largest outbreak in that state in three decades, affecting mostly children and teenagers, 16 are in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 There are more cases in New Mexico, meanwhile, and health officials expect that number to grow.\par \par \uc2 So let`s talk about it with CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder, editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 I did hear from viewers when we last had you on to talk about the measles on this question of immunity, if you`ve been vaccinated decades ago, does it last?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So there is a very specific group that does have to worry about their immunity from vaccination. So people born after 1957, but vaccinated before 1968, that group is unlikely to have robust immunity from infection, and at that point in time, they were using less effective vaccines, so that group in particular.\par \par \uc2 Now there are other people, I myself have gotten boosted in the past, because I work in health care, I`ve worked overseas, and I`ve worked in places where there was an active measles outbreak.\par \par \uc2 So there are other groups who would also potentially benefit from a booster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Generally speaking, do you think we should get re-boosted? Because when should you get the measles vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So we typically vaccinate children, so between the ages of 12 to 15 months is your first dose. That`s after the antibodies you get from your mom, through the placenta and through breast milk have faded away, so that the vaccine will actually work. So there`s a logic to when we vaccinate, and then the second dose is given, typically at about four to six years.\par \par \uc2 That for most people, will protect you for life. One dose gives you 95 percent vaccine effectiveness; two doses 97 percent. It`s not perfect, but it`s very close.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But with this outbreak, should you get re-boosted?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It really depends. If you fall into that particular group, again, born after 1957 vaccinated before 1968, I think it could make sense for you. There are certain groups where, unfortunately, we cannot vaccinate so people who are immunocompromised, we cannot boost you safely, because it`s a live vaccine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What if you don`t know? You just -- you know, your parents are dead, you know you can`t remember what you did when you`re an infant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, you don`t know if you`re vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I mean, the first step is still to try to track down those vaccination records. But if you`re somebody like me who moved around a lot as a kid, that might be hard to do. There is no harm in getting an extra dose, a booster if you`re not sure. And that`s actually what we would advise to do, as opposed to getting a blood test to see if there are antibodies.\par \par \uc2 It`s just get -- just get a booster if you`re in doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Dr. Gounder, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Stormy weather continues to hammer the Pacific Northwest after a very soggy weekend. A new storm hits the West Coast later today, and later this week, it will bring rain and the snow to the Midwest. Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go now to CBS News meteorologist, that`s Rob Marciano.\par \par \uc2 Rob, what a good way to start the week, huh?\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Well, Gayle, I`ll tell you what, this week is going to look a whole lot different from last week more broadly, but that storm you spoke of in the Pacific Northwest, look at this. This means business, that big comma shape that tell you it`s a mature storm.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got high wind warnings up for the coast. I-5 from Portland to Seattle will see wind advisory. A very stormy day today, and a stormy night tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also stormy across the Southeast. Let`s pull up the globe. We`re talking about Florida. I mean South Florida, which is unusual for this time of year, heavy rain there.\par \par \uc2 Miami-Dade, Broward Counties could see some potential flooding, certainly some thunderstorms today and tonight. But beyond that, we`re looking at a big warm up for much of the country.\par \par \uc2 This is after that brutally cold week last week, temperatures across the Upper Midwest will be 50 degrees warmer than they were this time.\par \par \uc2 Highs last week were below zero across Minneapolis, they`ll be in the 50s today, so 20 degrees above average, 60s for Oklahoma; Texas, same sort of stuff, and that warm air does bleed to the northeast and the Southeast, so it`ll feel a lot more different this week -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rob, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in a moment, what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  calls a romance scam on steroids. How a Las Vegas woman allegedly conned older men with deadly consequences.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SAG\uc2  Awards/\uc2 Netflix\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, we`ve got all the big winners and moments from last night`s Screen Actors Guild Awards.\par \par \uc2 Plus, David Begnaud brings us an all-new "Beg-Knows America" that spotlights a young girl who`s become a force for good.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Freshpet. It`s not dog food, it`s food, food.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is looking for more alleged victims of a Las Vegas woman it says was involved in a deadly romance scam. The suspect is accused of defrauding older men and causing the death of at least one of them. She is under arrest in Mexico, and U.S. officials want her return for trial.\par \par \uc2 Andres Gutierrez has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SPENCER EVANS: This is a romance scam on steroids.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: On Friday, federal authorities in Nevada revealed a years` long investigation into an international and deadly romance scam.\par \par \uc2 In an unsealed indictment, the Justice Department charged 43-year-old Aurora Phelps with scamming four older men.\par \par \uc2 Between 2021 and 2022, Phelps allegedly used aliases like "Sisy" and "Laura" on dating sites like Tinder and okCupid to lure her victims.\par \par \uc2 After meeting them in person, Feds say she would hit their bank accounts and go on shopping sprees.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER EVANS: Some of them were lonely, looking for companionship. Most of them were, you know, elderly, single men. She befriended them. In many cases, she went on multiple dates with them.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: The 21 counts against Phelps include bank fraud, identity theft and kidnapping. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  said one of the four victims was put in a coma after Phelps drugged him and that the other three died after meeting Phelps. She is charged in one of their deaths.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER EVANS: We believe Phelps kidnapped one of these victims by heavily sedating him and pushing him across the U.S.-Mexico border in a wheelchair.\par \par \uc2 Phelps then took him to a hotel room in Mexico City, where the victim was found dead a few hours later.\par \par \uc2 ANDRES GUTIERREZ: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  also claims there are more possible victims of Phelps` scam in the U.S. and Mexico, including both men and women and dating back to 2019.\par \par \uc2 If convicted on all counts, she could spend the rest of her life in prison.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Andres Gutierrez in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, what the winners of last night`s Screen Actors Guild Awards could tell us about the Oscar race.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 SAG\uc2  Awards/\uc2 Netflix\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: So we`ve located them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re looking at new video in this morning of the dramatic moment a Utah search and rescue team found a father and his 12-year-old boy stranded on a narrow cliff ledge in Southern Utah. They had set out for a morning hike in beautiful Snow Canyon last Sunday, lost their way and then were trapped.\par \par \uc2 Luckily, and this is a wild detail, they found a backpack stocked with a mylar blanket, food and water, very fortunate. That backpack had been left behind by another hiker who had been rescued from the very same area just last month.\par \par \uc2 It helped the father and son survive a cold night before they could be hoisted to safety.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Last night`s 31st Annual \uc2 Screen Actor Guild\uc2  Awards in Los Angeles may have just thrown a little chaos into your predictions for the Oscar season. Some of the biggest stars in Hollywood were there all in one room. A lot of the predicted favorites did not win, a lot of challengers won instead, and that includes the biggest prize of the night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HARRISON FORD (Actor): And the winner goes to "Conclave."\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Conclave"/Focus Features)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The film starring Ralph Fiennes took top honors for Best Performance by a Cast --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Conclave"/Focus Features)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It revolves around the secretive ritual of selecting a new Pope.\par \par \uc2 COLIN FARRELL (Actor): Demi Moore for "The Substance."\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Demi Moore won Best Actress for "The Substance."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Substance"/HUBI)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: During her acceptance speech, Moore held back tears.\par \par \uc2 DEMI MOORE (Actor): I just -- it is just the words are kind of beyond me. So I`m just going to have to just say thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE YEOH (Actor): Timothee Chalamet.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Timothee Chalamet beat out Adrien Brody and others in the Best Actor category for his portrayal of Bob Dylan in "A Complete Unknown."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "A Complete Unknown"/Searchlight Pictures)\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET (Actor): But I want to be one of the greats. I`m inspired by the greats. I`m inspired by the greats here tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Chip from "Shogun"/FX)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On the TV side, Japanese feudal epic, "Shogun" continued its success, nabbing four awards, including Best Ensemble.\par \par \uc2 HIROYUKI SANADA (Actor): This award showing us that acting is a universal language.\par \par \uc2 ZOOEY DESCHANEL AND MAX GREENFIELD (Actors): "Only Murders in the Building."\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was a big shock for the cast of "Only Murders in the Building" which clinched Best Comedy over FX`s "The Bear."\par \par \uc2 SELENA GOMEZ (Actor): We never win. This is so weird.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But the biggest moment of the night belonged to the incomparable, Jane Fonda.\par \par \uc2 JANE FONDA (Actor): Make no mistake, empathy is not weak or woke, and by the way, woke just means you give a damn about other people.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No stranger to political controversy, Fonda was fired up while accepting the Lifetime Achievement Award.\par \par \uc2 JANE FONDA: We must not isolate. We must stay in community. We must help the vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 We must find ways to project an inspiring vision of the future.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jane Fonda still speaking her mind and she looks fantastic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, she also looks great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love Demi Moore winning this award.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve been a Demi Moore fan since she was like in the soaps.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And just to see her get this recognition for being the phenomenal actress that she is, that she`s always been, I`m just really happy, even though everybody in that category and in the Oscar category is deserving.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m hoping for her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does seem like the Demi train is on the tracks, but the big surprise was Timothee Chalamet, because everybody thought Adrien Brody. You can never say it`s a lock, because you never know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You never know. We will be watching that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Five days away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead in a moment, we`re going to talk with President Trump`s former National Security adviser. That`s General H.R. McMaster, who`s got big concerns about the President`s approach to Ukraine and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Today marks three years since Russia launched its invasion of Ukraine, and President Trump is saying he can end this war, but he is making some big demands of Ukraine to do it, which he recently blamed for starting the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant General H.R. McMaster served as Trump`s National Security adviser during his first administration, and he is now a CBS News contributor. He is also the author of "At War with Ourselves: My Tour of Duty in the Trump White House."\par \par \uc2 General McMaster, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Hey, Tony. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Republicans have been shocked by things the President has said the last week about Ukraine and Russia. The National Security community has been shocked. Europe has been shocked. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Everybody should be shocked, because what the President has done is reframe the war in a way that`s just not true, you know, and I think it`s evidence that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is playing him.\par \par \uc2 You know, he had this long phone conversation with Putin. And it also struck me that, you know, the National Security adviser and the Secretary of State, they were overseas before the President made these statements.\par \par \uc2 So Putin plays Trump with, you know, the prospects of a big deal. You know that he could be the peacemaker. He gives him his warped version of history, and so that`s what you heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Those were all the Kremlin`s talking points that you heard him roll out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you think they must be celebrating at the Kremlin right now? You write a lot in your book about how President Putin seems to take great joy and pride in manipulating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Elaborate a little bit on what you mean by that.\par \par \uc2 And now, there are plans underway for a face-to-face meeting between the two. What you think about that?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Yes, so the Kremlin study Trump very closely. They`re trying to use like they would with any president his own character and personality against him. And so you know, you can`t go wrong with flattery with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So you hear Putin always praising President Trump, but then he also criticizes the other people, the stupid people before President Trump who created all of the problems, right?\par \par \uc2 So he blames Americans like previous administrations, rather than Putin`s own acts of aggression. And then he holds out the prospect of a big deal. Hey, President Trump, you can bring peace, but you know, this would be a false peace based on appeasement.\par \par \uc2 So whereas Trump would like maybe to get the Nobel Peace Prize, I mean, he might get the \uc2 Neville Chamberlain\uc2  Award because of appeasement of Putin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does Putin want peace do you think?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Putin wants to dominate Ukraine and he has wanted to dominate Ukraine since he came into office, and so this is what`s really important, Gayle, is to look at the pattern of Russian aggression since he came in 25 years ago, right?\par \par \uc2 And, and so you had a whole range of actions against Europe and, and even what`s going on in Ukraine is matched by a shadow war across Europe, cutting undersea cables, burning warehouses, you know, incendiary bombs on aircraft.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: And you know, Putin is, he`s already annexed Belarus. He`s turned an election in Romania, you could say in Slovakia. He is subverting Georgia, Moldova. He`s not going to stop until he is stopped.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, President Trump says, hey, you know, peace through strength. How about some of that? Because Putin is actually in a really weak position. Putin is in the position that Supreme Leader \uc2 Ayatollah Khamenei\uc2  was in in 2015.\par \par \uc2 President Obama got him up off the mat with the Iran nuclear deal. Trump is doing the same thing for Putin, except even worse, he`s blaming the Ukraine war on the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 So what I would say is, how about a little bit more peace through strength and less of, you know, the false peace through appeasement.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how does \uc2 NATO\uc2  factor into all of this given the concessions the United States is prepared to make with Russia? Can \uc2 NATO\uc2  go at it alone without the United States?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Yes, if the Europeans have the will. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: If -- so, this is something Trump is right on, is the burden sharing from \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies. And so if Europe steps up, they can do it. If you take the defense budgets of the \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries and you subtract the United States, it`s still enough to deal with Russia.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a heck of a lot more that our European friends can do, but the way the message is delivered, it cuts against really, that agenda.\par \par \uc2 You know so don`t blame, you know, the Europeans, for what`s happening. You know, really recognize that they are sharing the burden, but say you`ve got to do more. That`s an okay message.\par \par \uc2 But the way the Vice President went to the Munich Security Conference, for example, lecturing them, you know, that`s not kind of the way to win friends. You know, you can be tough, but you should be respectful with them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about the firing of C.Q. Brown. You know what it`s like to be a general, to be fired by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, what do you make of that? Because you know, C.Q. Brown is a man, they say, of high integrity, four-star general being replaced by someone who doesn`t have the ranking that he does.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you make of that firing and of Admiral Franchetti as well?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Yes. Well, I think it`s regrettable because of kind of the narrative around it, especially you know that these people weren`t qualified.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That they were DEI --\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: They were utterly qualified for their jobs. Now, are these the best people in President Trump`s -- to advance his agenda? If he thinks, no, that`s okay. That`s his prerogative to replace them. But these are true professionals.\par \par \uc2 What you`re going to see with C.Q. Brown is that -- is living in him as an example, the professionalism of our military. What really causes great concern for me is the tendency among both political parties, at times, to drag the military into the partisan politics that`s tearing our country apart, tearing even some families apart, right? That`s destructive for our military. Our military must be seen as above all of that.\par \par \uc2 C.Q. Brown is above all of that. He`s a true professional.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, isn`t it, in fact, politicized?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Yes, there`s this misunderstanding, right, that you know, generals or admirals are woke or that they were advancing President Biden`s agenda. They were actually doing what was expected of them by their civilian leadership.\par \par \uc2 You know, generals and admirals don`t make policy, right? Civilian leaders do. So, if you don`t like the policies in the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , vote somebody else in. That`s what happened.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: What I`m concerned about, and I believe that there`s a lot that need to be changed from the Biden administration in terms of priorities of the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 . You know, the so-called, you know, radical DEI agenda, or these various post-modernist theories that they were advancing, and maybe not enough emphasis on hey, fighting and winning wars, being prepared to do that. That`s okay.\par \par \uc2 But what I`m afraid of is the cure that they`re administering might be worse than the disease itself, and further politicize the military or try to anyway. Our military will not fall for it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope you`re right. I hope you`re right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re going to stick around -- yes, General McMaster is sticking around. Thank you very much. You`re going to be on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus. We`re going to talk about what China is making of all of this, what`s happening in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And we also will have a special birthday celebration that`s coming up in "What to Watch." Right now, it is 7:45 on the dot, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up for you at eight o`clock, including a new "Beg-Knows America." David Begnaud shows us how one family`s fight to get a life-changing device for their little girl turned into a chance, well, to pay it forward, we like that when that happens.\par \par \uc2 Plus Cedric The Entertainer has arrived. He`s here for a special \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. He`s got some very delicious things to share with all of us.\par \par \uc2 That`s all coming up in the next hour, but right now, Klaus and I heading over to the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got a birthday celebration.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A birthday celebration. Whose birthday?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a tease, what we call a tease in television. You`ve got to stick around. Come back after the commercial.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a very good tease.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by the \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  Active Cash Credit Card. Earn unlimited two percent cash rewards on purchases. Terms ap