aby Race, it wasn`t even close.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That might be a record for the fastest baby in all of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It really wasn`t close.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Where were they going to?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Their moms. Their moms were there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, their moms. Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What about the other kids who didn`t even take up --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They don`t love their moms, I guess. I don`t know. No, they love their moms. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh boy. It wasn`t even close is right.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about an election.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We begin this hour with the national election in Germany that could have major implications for the future of Europe.\par \par \uc2 Early results show mainstream conservatives, led by Friedrich Merz, who will have the largest number of seats in Germany`s new Parliament, but the Alternative for Germany or AfD, which is an anti-immigration party backed by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , won a historic 20 percent of the vote. That is the strongest showing for the far right there since World War Two.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It`s the AfD`s best result in national elections since the far right party was founded just 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (ALICE WEIDEL speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: During the campaign, the AfD leader Alice Weidel pledged to carry out the mass deportation of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Many Germans, though, are outraged.\par \par \uc2 Their country spent decades confronting its Nazi history.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We know what happened in Germany in the 30s, and it feels a bit like this is happening again, and this makes me really afraid of the future.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: One of the AfD`s regional leaders used a Nazi slogan at campaign events. German Intelligence has placed the party under surveillance for suspected extremism, and Germany`s mainstream politicians have vowed not to form a coalition with them.\par \par \uc2 But earlier this month, Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  met with the AfD`s leader and urged Europe to stop isolating far right parties.\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R) (Vice President of the United States): If you`re running in fear of your own voters, there is nothing America can do for you.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has gone further and publicly endorsed the AfD.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (\uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO): I think you are really the best one for Germany.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Many AfD voters are angry about the 2015 migrant crisis, when over a million arrived on European shores and nearly half a million claimed asylum in Germany. A series of violent attacks by asylum seekers has fueled their frustration.\par \par \uc2 Faisal Dakar (sp?) arrived in 2015 from war-torn Syria and now employs 27 people at his restaurant in Berlin. He became a German citizen three months ago.\par \par \uc2 He told us he is frightened that the AfD will one day govern Germany and carry out deportations.\par \par \uc2 (FAISAL DAKAR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "They don`t like black hair," he said. "Everyone must be blonde."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: With no single party winning a majority, the pressure is now on to negotiate a coalition, but if Germany`s mainstream parties cannot govern together successfully, that could further drive up support for the AfD -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Holly, I was paying attention until you showed that good food, and then I was like, I didn`t hear anything after that.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In this country, the Trump administration is expanding its immigration crackdown. Sources tell CBS News that a law called Title 42 will be brought back.\par \par \uc2 Now, this allows officials to quickly deport migrants seeking asylum at the border because of "public health risk." The restored order would point to concerns migrants may spread diseases like tuberculosis.\par \par \uc2 Now, the first Trump administration enacted Title 42 because of COVID. The Biden administration kept that policy in place until it expired in 2023. Border officials tell us, the US has recorded fewer than 300 illegal border crossings per day in the past few weeks. Now, that compares with about 4,800 per day this time last year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Big change.\par \par \uc2 We are moments away from "Beg-Knows America." You`re going to meet an inspiring little girl who`s overcome major obstacles herself and now helps others do the same.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Cedric The Entertainer, will join us for a very special \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. He`ll tell us about the barbecue rubs and sauces from his brand AC Barbeque. But first, it is 8:06 time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, we`re going to take you to the set of the new \uc2 CBS\uc2  daytime soap opera, "Beyond the Gates." The creators and cast talk about the drama and the scandal in the show. It is centered on a wealthy Black family, the Duprees. Remember that name.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a Monday, that means it`s time for "Beg-Knows America" where we find the heart in every story.\par \par \uc2 Today, CBS News contributor, David Begnaud meets a nine-year-old girl whose life was changed by an act of generosity, and now her generosity is changing the lives of others.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REMI BATEMAN (Nine years old): I want to do different things, like cut my own food without asking for so much help.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What else?\par \par \uc2 JAMI BATEMAN (Remi`s mother): And doing your own hair.\par \par \uc2 REMI BATEMAN: Yes, as soon as I figure out how to do that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: That would be good.\par \par \uc2 REMI BATEMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She`s nine and a little nervous. Cameras have a way of doing that to you. She`s the heart of this story because of a single act that sparked a ripple of kindness and triggered a chain reaction fueled by the generosity of people she`s never even met.\par \par \uc2 Remi Bateman was born without her left forearm and hand. She`s been using a prosthesis, commonly referred to as a prosthetic arm, since she was six months old, helpful, but it doesn`t give her the ability to independently move fingers.\par \par \uc2 Last year, her mom, Jami came across a social media post for Open Bionics and its 3D printed multi-grip Hero Arm. Each finger has its own motor, making it possible to grip everyday items with precision.\par \par \uc2 Remi and her family from Salt Lake City met with Open Bionics for an initial fitting. The hope was that insurance would cover it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You are doing really good.\par \par \uc2 REMI BATEMAN: Yay.\par \par \uc2 JAMI BATEMAN: The insurance said no, that it wasn`t medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Insurance might say, I understand that would help, but like, she doesn`t need it to live. It`s a cosmetic improvement. What would you say to them?\par \par \uc2 JAMI BATEMAN: I would say, why don`t you try to live with one hand? I do understand that you can live just fine without it, but imagine what you can do if you had two hands. I feel like the whole world is built around having two hands.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Without insurance, the bionic arm was $24,000.00. Like many Americans with medical needs not covered by insurance, Remi and her mom turned to crowdsourcing.\par \par \uc2 Her story made the local news, and they raised $24,000.00 in just four days.\par \par \uc2 When you hit $24,000.00 in the \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2 , did you think we`ll just stop there?\par \par \uc2 JAMI BATEMAN: Initially, yes, but I actually was contacted by very nice man CEO in Texas.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It was Andy Schoonover, the CEO of Crowd Health, a subscriber based crowdfunding platform for medical bills.\par \par \uc2 He called the Batemans after seeing their plea for help on social media, and said, "I want to buy the Hero Arm for Remi." That is where this story takes a turn.\par \par \uc2 JAMI BATEMAN: But then I looked at her, and I said, "Guess what, Remi. We have $24,000.00, but you`ve already got your arm paid for. What do you want to do with it?"\par \par \uc2 REMI BATEMAN: Help more kids.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Why did you want to do that?\par \par \uc2 REMI BATEMAN: So people can actually have a life changing arm like me?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I love you for that. I love your heart.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, in Maryland, nine-year-old Taj Johnson was also trying to raise money for a Hero Arm. He was born with a partial left hand.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN SKINNER (Taj Johnson`s mother): We`ve been looking for different types of like prosthetics or maybe surgeries, something to help give him some type of usage of his left hand.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And like Remi, Taj`s health insurance company said, denied.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN SKINNER: That kind of hurts because you`re telling me that a kid being able to function with daily like tasks as something as small as feeding themselves, getting dressed, you`re telling me that that`s not medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But these are everyday important things.\par \par \uc2 KAITLIN SKINNER: Yes. Yep, things that you need to be able to do to survive.\par \par \uc2 D