re we -- it is a family thing, and so -- and then I love this -- I love the mustard kind of base of the Carolina barbecue. So that`s why this -- this is the one that became very popular, because I just love that kind of bright mustardy taste.\par \par \uc2 So, the Caroline one is one of my favorites.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And you talked a lot about the history of barbecue and how important that is to you.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Yes, you know, I mean, you think back -- it goes back to just how the first way we had to prepare meals out on the open fire. And it just kind of goes back, of course, all of us talk about our families, how we got together, how we would celebrate with each other. And that`s why barbecue is really such a -- you know, a great thing for -- a celebration and good food, family, friends and community.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: And that`s why we love the idea of putting this national brand out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you`re opening up a restaurant.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Yes. We`ve got the restaurant. AC --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Can we all go?\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Yes, come.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Are we all -- we`re all going to go.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Yes, come. We have Century City --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Los Angeles is going to open in April. So we`re doing that. We`re right there by the \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  and the Chipotle we`re doing this. It`s going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And I can`t wait. I watched your show, and I`ve watched you guys go --\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Oh, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: -- from start from the concept, "Kings of BBQ."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, so you got to see it.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You get to see how they come up with the concept, work with food scientists.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You worked with scientists?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, they did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said you worked with scientists.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Really amazing, the process.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we keep -- if you guys want to keep eating. Follow me over here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are going to keep eating. We`ve got to get over here.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Follow me over here, if you`d like. We are going to keep eating.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Because I want you to know that these sauces and these rubs can be used, not just at the barbecue but also in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Right. So I roast a chicken every Friday night. If you look at these pots and pans, they`re from \uc2 Black & Decker\uc2 . This is a nine- piece set, by the way, aluminum, so they heat evenly. They cook evenly. This gorgeous white ceramic is non-stick, and it`s got none of those chemicals that the other non-stick pans have in them, so they`re safe.\par \par \uc2 But look at this, this chicken. You can put these pots and pans on the stove, but also in the oven up to 500 degrees. So we`re going to take all of your yummy rubs and sauces.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Lemon stepper.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, lemon stepper.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: You can look at all of these, right? And you can think to yourself, how we can utilize, but these are great when you`re thinking about that the person, maybe they`re just getting married, they`re just starting, a nice set, a nine-piece set, you get all of these. Or maybe you`re someone like me that`s had your pots and pans for about 40 years.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: And it might be time to update. But I want you to use -- I want you to use all the AC Barbeque cooking inside and outside.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Put those in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s the price of this bad boy?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: $199.99. It is for the full set here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And wow.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: And the eggs are $100.00.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Eggs. Those are not on sale today. We couldn`t bring you those.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Those eggs more expensive.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: We will see, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Than the set.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Don`t ask us for the eggs. Get the pots.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Elizabeth Werner, Cedric The Entertainer, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 We partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw today, scan the QR code on your screen, or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got family memories right here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s cool.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC THE ENTERTAINER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we`re going to get an inside look "Beyond the Gates" as soon as I stop chewing. The first new daytime soap opera in more than 25 years premieres today, two o`clock Eastern on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know it wasn`t that long ago that soap operas ruled daytime T.V. and ruled is the word. The cast of shows like "General Hospital," "Days of Our Lives," my favorite was "All My Children" back in the day, Jesse, oh -- became part of our lives.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jesse and Angie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jesse and Angie, yes. Jesse and Angie.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 CBS\uc2  is doing something that has not been done in more than 25 years. What is that? Launching a new daytime soap opera.\par \par \uc2 "CBS Sunday Morning`s" Nancy Giles takes us "Beyond the Gates."\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: This is the Dupree family.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: They`re the stars of TV`s newest soap opera, "Beyond the Gates."\par \par \uc2 There used to be, I guess, almost a dozen soap operas. Then we went down to five. I mean, it`s kind of a risky thing. You`re taking a chance. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 KARLA CHEATHAM MOSLEY ("Beyond the Gates" actor): Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE ("Beyond the Gates" actor): Right, I got excited.\par \par \uc2 KARLA CHEATHAM MOSLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: Just excited. Just excited.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: The prominent family who lives in a gated community just outside of Washington, D.C. is made up of Mr. And Mrs. Dupree, played by Clifton Davis and Tamara Tunie.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: And a pair of daughters played by Daphnee Duplaix Karla Mosley.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 DAPHNEE DUPLAIX ("Beyond the Gates actor): It`s fresh, it`s innovative. It is -- it`s exciting. They`re not ready for us.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: This is 2025. The writers, they came out swinging. The first episode is just like "phew."\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: Those writers are also breaking new ground.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA DUCKSWORTH (\uc2 CBS Studios\uc2 /NAACP Venture President and "Beyond the Gates" executive producer): It appears to be that we`re the first Black female executive producers in all of daytime drama.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: Sheila Ducksworth is an executive producer and president of this \uc2 CBS\uc2 /NAACP production.\par \par \uc2 Michele Val Jean is an executive producer, head writer and show runner.\par \par \uc2 It`s been a labor of love for the duo.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA DUCKSWORTH: I`d say it`s been a couple of decades in the making that I`ve wanted to actually produce and make a soap opera. What I felt was missing was one that centered on the Black experience.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE VAL JEAN ("Beyond the Gates" executive producer, head writer and show runner): I just wanted to see people that looked like me, right? I didn`t want downtrodden Black people, right? I wanted rich, messy Black people that carried the stories.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: That idea applies to the people behind the camera, too.\par \par \uc2 CLIFTON DAVIS ("Beyond the Gates" actor): I look around and I see African American cameramen. I see costumers and lighting and writers.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: Set designers.\par \par \uc2 CLIFTON DAVIS: Set designers.\par \par \uc2 DAPHNEE DUPLAIX: Yes. Writers.\par \par \uc2 CLIFTON DAVIS: The writers.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: Hair and makeup.\par \par \uc2 DAPHNEE DUPLAIX: The makeup, the hair.\par \par \uc2 CLIFTON DAVIS: "Beyond the Gates" is multicultural from the writers room to before the camera.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: This is the diner --\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: We were on set during filming in December where we saw a crew of more than 200 people --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Back to their original marks --\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: Working around the clock to bring this show to life.\par \par \uc2 WANKAYA HINKSON (Hair designer): I thought I`d bring in the culture to daytime television was very important to me.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: Hair designer Wankaya Hinkson says, historically, hair and makeup departments have often lacked people who knew how to work on actors of color, but not here.\par \par \uc2 WANKAYA HINKSON: I want everyone to be able to identify themselves on daytime television.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: Because we wear our hair many, many different ways.\par \par \uc2 WANKAYA HINKSON: Many different ways.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WANKAYA HINKSON: And I feel like it`s a part of our Black girl magic. So I wanted to make sure that I had different textures.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Up and down stage --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: And it`s not just the look they`re trying to nail. The show`s creators, cast, and crew are trying to deliver daytime drama that soaps are known for, while making something that`s relatable to everyone.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: How are you hoping audiences react and respond to it.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: I hope they fall in love.\par \par \uc2 DAPHNEE DUPLAIX: Y es.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: I hope they fall head over heels in love with everybody in the show --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Beyond the Gates"/CBS)\par \par \uc2 DAPHNEE DUPLAIX: Right.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: And all of the perfection and all of the flaws and all of the humanness and all of -- all of it.\par \par \uc2 Then we have the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TUNIE: Right here among us.\par \par \uc2 NANCY GILES: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Nancy Giles in Atlanta, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you can watch the premiere of "Beyond the Gates" today at two o`clock Eastern, one Pacific on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Years ago, there was a commercial guys where it said, "Try it. You`ll like it." I forgot what it was for, but that`s how I feel about this. Try it, you`ll like it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because, you know, it`s great that it`s an all-Black cast, but you can relate to the issues.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did she say? Messy -- messy, wealthy, Black people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rich, messy Black people.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here for it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here for it. Big time.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow, right here on CBS MORNINGS, your favorite morning show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250224el2o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  in Critical Condition; Musk Ultimatum; Battling for Its Future; Political Shift; Intense influenza; Lights Dim on a Legendary Club; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Seth Doane, Imtiaz Tyab, Holly Williams; Elise  Preston, Ali Bauman. Willie James Inman, Andrew Kozak. Alys Martinez \par \uc2 3,420 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  in critical condition sends a message to the faithful, pray for me.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE praying)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Catholics worldwide on watch, the 88-year-old Pontiff reported to be alert after a respiratory crisis.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: I`m Seth Doane outside the Vatican where we`re hearing tonight that \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 ` prognosis remains guarded. Doctors have been saying it could go either way.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, Musk ultimatum: Federal workers ordered to document their work or resign. New details on the pushback.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CURTIS (R-UT): It`s not unusual in a corporate setting to have people report and explain what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD): What we are witnessing are illegal actions by \uc2 Elon Musk\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Three years after Russia`s invasion Ukraine now battling president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over its future.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab in Kyiv where President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  has said he`s ready to resign if it meant peace in his country,\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Political shift.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: I`m Holly Williams in Berlin. In today`s German elections, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has thrown his support behind the far right alternative for Germany.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Plus, intense influenza.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: I`m Elise Preston in Burbank, California with a closer look at what`s driving the worst flu season in years.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And later, lights dim on the legendary home of the celebrity roast.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: I`m Ali Bauman Bowman in the old Friars Club, where decades of laughs are up in the air.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: The Friars` Club Roasts Johnny Carson.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening, everyone. I`m Jericka Duncan. Thanks for joining us on this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 All eyes are on Rome tonight as the world`s Catholics pray for \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 . The 88-year-old remains in critical condition, suffering from pneumonia in both lungs. The Vatican describing his condition as "complex."\par \par \uc2 Today, the faithful gathered at masses across the country to pray for the Pope, including at New York`s St. Patrick`s Cathedral and in the city of Francis` birth, Buenos Aires, Argentina.\par \par \uc2 The Pontiff has been in the hospital since February 14th.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Seth Doane is at the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  remains in critical condition tonight and blood tests are revealing early signs of kidney failure. We`ve learned that the Pope is also receiving high-flow oxygen through tubes in his nose, and he is continuing to get blood transfusions. Doctors tell us this is not a good sign.\par \par \uc2 On a more encouraging note, the Vatican says he has not had another respiratory crisis since Saturday. That`s when we learned he was in more pain than he had been previously.\par \par \uc2 His doctors have underscored, he is a fragile patient, and this is not an easy case to treat. I asked one of his doctors how many different drugs he`s on and the answer was a lot.\par \par \uc2 He has chronic underlying lung conditions, but despite all of this, Jericka, The Holy See Press Office says he is alert and well-oriented. He even participated in a mass this morning at the hospital, at a small chapel there,\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Seth Doane, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there is confusion and conflicting guidance about an e-mail sent by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to more than two million federal workers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Willie James Inman is at the White House with information on that e- mail and new firings -- Willie James.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Jericka, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the White House is speeding ahead with firing federal workers announcing 2,000 jobs at \uc2 USAID\uc2  will be eliminated and all non-essential hires will be placed on leave. This is just a latest shakeup for federal workers across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: In an ultimatum posted on X, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  demanded federal employees write down what they accomplished last week, adding "Failure to respond will be taken as a resignation."\par \par \uc2 The government`s Office of Personnel Management said in a statement: "Individual agencies will determine the next steps" with no mention of resignations.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, President Trump urged \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to get more aggressive to slash spending.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States): And Elon is doing a great job. We love Elon.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: But not every agency is following through on Musk`s demand. \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel is among those pushing back saying in an e- mail obtained by \uc2 CBS\uc2  news, that the Bureau will conduct reviews adding when and if further information is required, we will coordinate the responses.\par \par \uc2 Trump appointees at several other agencies, including the State Department and Department of Defense are telling employees to disregard the e-mail.\par \par \uc2 The ultimatum is the latest confusion for federal workers who are already grappling with the Trump administration`s aggressive efforts to shrink the workforce.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We are removing all of the unnecessary, incompetent, and corrupt bureaucrats from the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: But some Republicans are calling for compassion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CURTIS (R-UT): If I could say one thing to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , it`s like, please put a dose of compassion in this. These are real people. These are real lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: More job cuts are on the way this week as The Pentagon moves to fire 5,400 probationary civilian workers -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Willie James Inman at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says he`ll give up his presidency in exchange for peace.\par \par \uc2 Monday marks three years since Russia`s invasion. Overnight, Russia attacked Ukraine with a record number of drones.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab is in the capital of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Well, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  made the comments at a lengthy press conference here in the capital, Kyiv. We were there when he said, if his resignation meant peace would be granted for Ukraine and if his country could join the NATO Military Alliance, he would step down.\par \par \uc2 Now, it was not immediately clear whether Zelenskyy was seriously considering resigning or just responding to the latest broad sides from President Trump, who just days ago accused the Ukrainian leader of being "a dictator without elections" and that Ukraine had started the war, not Russia, echoing Kremlin talking points. Now Zelenskyy shot back saying Trump was living in a disinformation space.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, a record 267 drones were launched at 13 Ukrainian regions killing at least one person on the eve of the war`s third anniversary. Now, despite the tensions with President Trump, Zelenskyy also called for a meeting with him as the two countries try to negotiate a deal, which would see the U.S. gain access to Ukraine`s rare earth minerals in exchange for American aid.\par \par \uc2 But the potentially $500 billion agreement so far doesn`t offer any U.S. security agreements, Jericka Rica and Zelenskyy said he won`t sign anything without it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes, still a lot to work out. Imtiaz Tyab, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a major political shift in Germany that could send shock waves across Europe.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams is in Berlin tonight.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 The Alternative for Germany or AfD is on track to win about 20 percent of the vote here. They are far right anti-immigration and want closer ties with Russia and they have the support of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It`s the AfD`s best result since the party was established just 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (ALICE WEIDEL speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: During the campaign, the party`s leader, \uc2 Alice Weidel\uc2  pledged to carry out the mass deportation of immigrants, popular with some voters who believe Germany has let in too many too quickly. Recent violent attacks by asylum seekers have fueled their frustration.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But in a country that`s spent decades coming to terms with its Nazi history, many are outraged.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The far right is taking more and more control of our country and I`m really worried. History is repeating.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: German intelligence has placed the AfD under surveillance for suspected extremism and Germany`s mainstream parties have vowed not to form a coalition with them.\par \par \uc2 Despite that, Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  met with the AfD`s leader in Germany this month and urged Europe to stop isolating far right parties.\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R) (Vice President of the United States): If you`re running in fear of your own voters, there is nothing America can do for you.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  publicly endorse the AfD, making a virtual appearance at one of their campaign events.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (\uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO): I think -- I think you are really the best hope for Germany.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK: I think --\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And Musk has also voiced support for others on the far right in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Rory Stewart is a British conservative and former member of Parliament. He told us Musk`s activities are having an immense impact and like many others, he is worried.\par \par \uc2 Rory Stewart (Former member of \uc2 British Parliament\uc2 ): I think \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is profoundly reckless. He thinks that a country is like building a rocket ship very quickly and it doesn`t matter if it blows up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Following this election, the pressure is now on Germany`s mainstream parties to negotiate a coalition. If they cannot govern successfully, Jericka, that could further fuel support for the AfD.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, important elections.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Israel is delaying the release of more than 600 Palestinian prisoners. They want \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists to stop what it cause the humiliating ceremonies at handovers of hostages.\par \par \uc2 Also today, Israel moved tanks and troops into the occupied West Bank for the first time since 2002. Palestinian authorities call it a "dangerous escalation."\par \par \uc2 On the \uc2 CBS\uc2  "Health Watch," the worst flu season in 15 years has left hundreds of thousands of Americans needing care and putting a strain on doctor`s offices and hospitals.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston is in Burbank tonight with details.\par \par \uc2 Elise, good evening.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Jericka, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 An estimated 33 million Americans have gotten the flu this season according to the CDC. For some, the virus is so intense, it is sending them to the ER.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Severe and even deadly flu cases are surging across the country in what is shaping up to be the worst flu season in seven years. We`re just halfway through flu season, but already 19,000 people have died from influenza.\par \par \uc2 The CDC says more than 430,000 have been hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 What is driving this difficult season?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNE RIMOIN (Professor at Department of Epidemiology, UCLA Fielding School of Public Health): Well, there are probably a number of variables that are associated with this: Lower vaccination rates, lower population immunity and the strains that are circulating that are particularly virulent this year.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: These factors could be to blame for an alarming number of infections in children. Eighty-six deaths have been reported in patients 17 and younger; fewer getting vaccinated this season than last.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNE RIMOIN: We have the tools to be able to prevent further spread and to protect vulnerable populations and we should definitely use those because that will save lives and keep people out of the hospital.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, Rimoin cautions, you can still get the flu if you`re vaccinated, but the vaccine will help you in reducing your severity -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Important information. Thank you, Elise.\par \par \uc2 To the weather now, after a stretch of cold, a dose of spring fever is on the way. CBS News` meteorologist, Andrew Kozak is in Philadelphia --Andrew.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yes, Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Spring makes a little cameo appearance over the course of the next couple of days and into the beginning of March, I`ll have more on that in just a sec. But first tonight, once again, weather top story, the Pacific Northwest. More flooding rain from Seattle, Washington down toward Oregon, Northern California. Take a look at the swirl, wait for it. It`s coming along the atmospheric river. This will be the next piece of energy late Monday night and into Tuesday where we could see an additional two to four inches of rain on top of what they already have, and that of course is also in addition to snow above 7,000 feet.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, spring, yes, the cameo appearance happens this coming week. The next six to 10 days, really a large portion of the country, five to 10 degrees above average. A little taste of that for tomorrow. Monday, we have seventies and eighties in the Desert Southwest, milder across the East Coast. Florida dealing with some rain from the Panhandle to the Keys -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Andrew Kozak, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from FIS/Alpine)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Today, a historic victory for American skier, Mikaela Shiffrin. She won her 100th World Cup Race in Italy. It comes nearly three months after she was seriously hurt in a crash.\par \par \uc2 Shiffrin is the first Alpine skier, male or female, to reach triple digits in World Cup race victories.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, hammering away to preserve history in fire ravaged California.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Penn State\uc2  students dance into the record book this weekend for a good cause.\par \par \uc2 And end of an era at a legendary comedy club.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CLIFF DOUGLAS (Masonry contractor): The important part is you can try to give the owners a little something back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The race is on in Altadena, California to save historic tiles from homes destroyed by wildfires. A newly formed group called Save the Tiles is trying to preserve as many as they can before bulldozers start to clear properties.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s "Weekend Journal," Alys Martinez has more on the work that`s being done carefully, yet quickly.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ALYS MARTINEZ: Piece by piece, masonry contractor, Cliff Douglas is preserving a small part of Altadena history.\par \par \uc2 CLIFF DOUGLAS: Ah, I got it.\par \par \uc2 ALYS MARTINEZ: Douglas is with Save the Tiles, an organization working to rescue these antique tiles, some more than a century old.\par \par \uc2 CLIFF DOUGLAS: These are called batch elder tiles, started in 1910 in Pasadena. They`re a local tile. So that`s why they`re in a lot of the homes up here.\par \par \uc2 ALYS MARTINEZ: In Altadena, chimneys and fireplaces are some of the only things that survived the intense heat.\par \par \uc2 CLIFF DOUGLAS: The important part is you just to try to give the owners a little something back.\par \par \uc2 ALYS MARTINEZ: Douglas and his crew are working against the clock as the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  gets ready to clear out properties. He says there are dozens of homes with these renowned tiles inside.\par \par \uc2 CLIFF DOUGLAS: And we`re trying to beat the bulldozers across town that are moving this way. So, we`d really like to get as many down as we can. It just -- it would just would be a shame to see any of this tile go to the dump.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE ELASHI (Altadena, California resident): They`re unusual.\par \par \uc2 ALYS MARTINEZ: Very unusual.\par \par \uc2 These tiles were part of Valerie Elashi`s 100-year-old home that she soon hopes to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE ELASHI: It`s kind of impossible to build this house because it was so unique, but we could at least preserve the flavor of it by using -- utilizing the tiles on the fireplace.\par \par \uc2 ALYS MARTINEZ: And she says, she is grateful for people like Douglas and Save the Tiles who are doing the all for free.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE ELASHI: These people are going out of their way to help us, homeowners kind of recover and also to save a piece of Pasadena history.\par \par \uc2 ALYS MARTINEZ: Safeguarding the past, one tile at a time.\par \par \uc2 Alys Martinez, CBS News, Altadena, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, it is the king of rollercoaster, why it`s rein has come to an end.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It was dance, dance, dance at \uc2 Penn State\uc2  this weekend. Hundreds of students took part in a 46-hour dance marathon.\par \par \uc2 There was no sitting, no sleeping, all for a great cause -- childhood cancer research. The event raised a record $17.7 million.\par \par \uc2 The world`s tallest rollercoaster is coming down with a nearly 500-foot drop, Kingda Ka has thrilled riders at \uc2 Six Flags\uc2  in New Jersey since 2005, but no more. It`s now being dismantled after often being shut down for repairs, what`s left will be imploded.\par \par \uc2 Next up on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, why it could be last laughs at a legendary comedy club, but first a look back at this day in history.\par \par \uc2 TEXT: This day in history --\par \par \uc2 1896: Tootsie Roll goes on sale for one penny.\par \par \uc2 1945: U.S. Marines raise flag in Battle of Iwo Jima.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: Atop Mount Suribachi, Old Glory, 100 miles near Japan.\par \par \uc2 1960: Demolition begins on Brooklyn`s Ebbets Field.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Finally, tonight, New York`s Friars Club has been the home of comic greats and legendary roasts, but it`s been sold at a foreclosure auction.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ali Bauman.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: The Friars Club Roasts Johnny Carson.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: It`s the club that put comics in the hot seat.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Jack Benny`s life has been about as exciting as the 4th of July in London.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: And stars in stitches.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Four times in the past, the Friars and Kraft Music Hall have saluted the great comedians, and tonight they salute Jerry Lewis.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: From Frank Sinatra to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Friars Club was an exclusive fraternity whose members were mostly in show business.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And they a saying, when you come into the club, you feel okay, but when you leave, you feel great.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: So when did you decide that you wanted to become a member of the Friars Club?\par \par \uc2 CARY HOFFMAN (Actor): As a kid, I wanted to be a star and everybody knew that if you are star, you were in the Friars Club.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Cary Hoffman credits much of his career as a talent manager to his membership.\par \par \uc2 CARY HOFFMAN: And I would lean over and hear television deals being made. It was the center of New York`s show business.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: But the monastery, as it`s known in Midtown Manhattan has been collecting dust now for well over a year, shuttered from financial trouble and forced into foreclosure.\par \par \uc2 MICKEY SALZMAN (Broker): This is that new chapter and that new page for someone to really continue something from old and bring it into a new life again.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: Friars` Steve Beninati says besides its finances, he also watched the club`s popularity wane over the years.\par \par \uc2 STEVE BENINATI (Friars Club): Twenty years ago, everyone knew the Friars Club. Today, I`m not so sure.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: The empty seats they feel are emblematic of a cultural shift.\par \par \uc2 CARY HOFFMAN: Somebody said that in his entire life, Frank Sinatra never had dinner alone. And the Friars was a reflection of that, that camaraderie that doesn`t exist anymore.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: But the club`s former leadership says a small group of old and new members are looking to buy it back from the bank.\par \par \uc2 CARY HOFFMAN: As things get more corporate, this is a throwback that deserves to be in existence now.\par \par \uc2 ALI BAUMAN: While the lights are dimmed, some Friars hope it`s just an intermission and not the club`s closing act.\par \par \uc2 Ali Bauman, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, that is the news for this Sunday. I`m Jericka Duncan in New York.\par \par \uc2 We leave you now with a view of the Iwo Jima Monument in Washington honoring that fierce fight and flag raising by U.S. Marines 80 years ago today.\par \par \uc2 Have a great week. We`ll see you next weekend.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020250224el2n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For February 23, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Charlie D`Agata, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: The president`s mission to shrink the federal work force intensifies and his foreign policy dealmaking reaches a critical juncture.\par \par \uc2 Speaking before a crowd of conservative supporters this weekend, President Trump touted his dramatic efforts to reshape the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s all about common sense. Over the past month, we have confirmed an all-star team of warriors, patriots, visionaries and put the America first agenda into action.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Late Friday, the purge of the federal work force struck the Pentagon at the top during a critical moment for our national security, as the new administration seeks peace talks to end Russia`s war in Ukraine and as a fragile truce between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  hangs in the balance.\par \par \uc2 We will speak with Trump`s top negotiator and envoy Steve Witkoff.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, here at home, the president takes aim at Democratic governors who oppose his agenda.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: You better do it because you`re not going to get any federal funding.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS (D-Maine): We will see you in court.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-New York): New York hasn`t labored under a king in over 250 years.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: We are not -- we sure as hell are not going to start now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will ask New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  how she`s picking her battles with the new administration.\par \par \uc2 Plus:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: the People would like to know, what you, Congressman, and your fellow congressmen are going to do to rein in the megalomaniac in the White House.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Lawmakers facing some heat from voters over Trump`s push to slash the federal work force led by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Maryland Democrat \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  and Utah Republican John Curtis will be here.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, with bird flu measles and other illnesses on the rise and big changes ahead for America`s public health agencies, we will get a reality check from former Trump \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Millions of federal workers are waking up to renewed pressure this morning. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  ordered them late Saturday to describe the top five things they accomplished last week, or risk losing their jobs. That demand was issued formally in an e-mail yesterday from the Office of Personnel Management.\par \par \uc2 But top leaders in agencies such as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the State Department have instructed their staff to avoid responding for now. Just a day earlier, the Trump staff shakeup hit the Pentagon with the ouster of the nation`s highest-ranking military officer, General C.Q. Brown, and other senior leaders at the Navy, Army and Air Force.\par \par \uc2 For more on that impact of the shakeup, we begin this morning with our senior national security correspondent, Charlie D`Agata.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, so it`s the president`s prerogative to choose his advisers, sure, but what`s the impact of firing his top adviser? And when will we get a new one?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Well, Margaret, you, first of all, have to look at what`s going on at the moment right? What`s the immediate impact?\par \par \uc2 This position as the chairman of Joint Chiefs of Staff is in charge, as it sounds, of everybody and planning forward. We have the crisis in the Middle East that`s still unfolding. We have got the crisis at the border where more and more active-duty troops are being sent to. Guantanamo is getting built up.\par \par \uc2 And then you have got Ukraine and the future of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . So this comes at a critical time. And the dismissal of C.Q. Brown will have immediate ramifications. In terms of how long it`s going to take to replace that, well, there will be a sort of Senate hearing. A person has already been put forward, as we`re about to speak about.\par \par \uc2 But, in addition to that, the other top head to come out of that was Admiral Lisa Franchetti. She`s \uc2 CNO\uc2 , chief naval officer, in charge of the Navy. That`s a big one.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The highest-ranking woman.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The highest-ranking woman, and unprecedented in that role. And she was the first to become a fleet commander. There are many firsts in Admiral Franchetti`s career. And we have profiled her when she became fleet commander.\par \par \uc2 Very highly thought of among her -- among personnel at the DOD, including young women who aspire to those sort of levels. C.Q. Brown, again, very highly regarded. They -- there was no reason given for the dismissal of these two individuals.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: We do know that Secretary Hegseth has actually name- checked C.Q. Brown, saying that he`s criticizing him for his DEI programs and saying that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. .\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: ... was he given that promotion because he was black? He says, we will never know.\par \par \uc2 He also took aim at Admiral Franchetti, suggesting she is a DEI hire herself, by virtue of the fact that she is a woman. Also, in addition to that, there`s a -- the state in command of the Air Force who was also removed, so a lot of changes.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned the word purge.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: And that`s exactly what it is at the -- from the very top.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, who is this nominee, or I guess, of -- in the process of becoming the nominee, to step into the role?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It`s a good question. And there are a lot of people inside the DOD who are asking who this nominee is, retired Lieutenant General Dan Caine.\par \par \uc2 He just retired recently as a three-star rank. That`s kind of important, because this is the first time, at least in my knowledge -- it might be corrected -- the first time a three-star general was promoted to that position and kind of...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, and not the head of a combatant command.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Exactly, not the head of a combatant command, like CENTCOM or EUCOM, not in charge of one of the armed services branches.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Big promotion.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Well, yes, some might call it a leapfrog.\par \par \uc2 If he is to be accepted into this role, he will automatically be given that fourth star. But, more importantly, there are certain rules and regulations, that he would have been the first, because he didn`t have that combatant command and because he wasn`t in charge of one of the armed forces.\par \par \uc2 But from -- we have been asking around. And I know people who know him personally. They say, look, he`s a stand-up guy, nicest man you want to meet. There had been some questions about whether or not he was sort of political. I know that President Trump had said that he had met the guy and said that he loves him, as in he loves Trump.\par \par \uc2 According to my sources, that conversation didn`t take place. He doesn`t own a MAGA cap. He didn`t put one on. So he`s been described as apolitical. And he`s somebody who may not have the experience, but has the character to lead the force.\par \par \uc2 But I think what we have to look at here, right...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: ... we have got Defense Secretary Hegseth, and then we have this individual, two relatively inexperienced people at the very highest level of the DOD.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: At a time of global instability and a lot of change here at home.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Trump`s clash with Democratic governors.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from Albany, New York, is the governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , good morning to you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you were just here in Washington, and you met privately with President Trump. In the past, you`ve said the relationship doesn`t have to be adversarial. Was your meeting with him adversarial? And what was your top message?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: It was an adversarial meeting, but I was very clear, especially after I found that the Trump administration had ended a program that was put in place, congestion pricing, by the duly elected members of our legislature, representing the voice of the people, and with a tweet, he claims that he is the king, and, therefore, he has the power to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 And I have a problem with that characterization, because we labored under a king 250 years ago, and, as I said, we`re not going back there. So I wanted to take my case to him directly and let him see the benefits of this program, because our city is paralyzed with gridlock. And we had a path forward to be able to make the city move again, and it`s working.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to just have that opportunity to convey that. But I don`t know that we`re very persuasive on that front, but that`s OK. The people in my state need to know I`m willing to take the fight wherever I have to.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, just to explain for our audience, congestion pricing, you`re talking about this $9 toll you put on people driving into Lower Manhattan below 60th Street.\par \par \uc2 Your fellow Democrat \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  of New Jersey says he doesn`t like this policy, actually asked President Trump to stop it. President Trump`s office says this is discriminatory against working-class people. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: I respond this way. With all due respect to the state of New Jersey, they do not tell us in New York what to do, nor does Washington, when it comes to policies that we believe are going to reduce congestion, move along vehicles. Emergency vehicles are moving faster. Air quality is improving.\par \par \uc2 So I have arguments that are important, but no one else should be able to second-guess us, because that`s not how our system of laws and states was set up. I`m the governor of New York. I will deal with the internal issues before me without interference from New Jersey or, indeed, the federal government.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So this is going to the courts?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: Oh, yes, it`s going to the courts, and I believe we will be victorious in the courts, and this program will continue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, this is not the only standoff between the state and federal government and the tension here.\par \par \uc2 I know you were in Washington with other governors who were meeting with the president this week. Federal funds account for about 40 percent of your state`s budget. President Trump is threatening to withhold federal funds to governors in order to force compliance with his agenda.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what he said to the governor of Maine. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): Is Maine here, the Governor of Maine?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS (D-Maine): Yes, I`m here.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Are you not going to comply with it?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS: I will comply with state and federal laws.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, I`m -- we are the federal law. Well, you better do it. You better do it, because you`re not going to get any federal funding at all if you don`t.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That was about transgender athletes on girls sports teams.\par \par \uc2 Are you Democratic governors going to start to work together on this? Is there legal pushback?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: What he is doing is -- they`ve described it as flooding the zone.\par \par \uc2 They`re attacking California when it comes to FEMA dollars. They`re filing lawsuits in Illinois. They`re going after Maine on this, and they`re coming after programs that have been duly put in place in the state of New York.\par \par \uc2 What they`re trying to do is create this theater of all kinds of activity that is trying to be a distraction to us. And when someone floods the zone in a football game, what you need to have is the defenders be very disciplined, smart, but also stand their ground. And that`s exactly what New York governors, the governor of New York will do, in cooperation with our governors.\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to sit idly by and let our rights be attacked. We`ll work with you when there`s common ground, no doubt about it. Let`s build great projects and infrastructure. There`s areas where we`re going to work with you on immigration and getting the violent criminals off our streets. We do not dispute that. But don`t think that you can just come in and bully us around and not expect a reaction from governors.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know that, when it comes to your state, there`s been a lot of focus on Manhattan and the Department of Justice because of Eric Adams and the ongoing legal issues he has.\par \par \uc2 In the past week, four deputy mayors resigned. Seven federal prosecutors resigned after the Trump Justice Department moved to dismiss those five counts of federal corruption indictments against -- against Mayor Adams.\par \par \uc2 You know the allegation is that the dismissal of the charges was in -- related to a quid pro quo around immigration compliance. Did you discuss this case at all with the president when you were in the room with him one on one?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: No, I did not. In fact, it`s -- we`re waiting for the decision by a judge on whether he`s going to accept those -- the recommendation for a dismissal. So that`s still in litigation.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this. We are not allowing the Trump administration to interfere in the operations of our city. And the legal problems that the mayor is facing because of the Trump administration, and the phrasing they have used, and, indeed, an interview that was done by one of his representatives on national television, saying that he`s basically got the mayor under his control, that`s concerning.\par \par \uc2 And I have to be able to put in safeguards. Of course, everyone says I had the option to remove him. I still hold that option to remove him. It is an extraordinary power. And I know there`s a lot of people disappointed and angry and want something done immediately, but I will always stand on the fact that we are a nation of laws.\par \par \uc2 And one individual, the governor of New York, should not use her voice and her will to override the will of the voters. We have an election in a few months for -- in a primary. I`m going to let the voters decide, but I`m also going to be very cognizant. I need to keep an eye on this situation, especially the way the Trump administration has telegraphed that they want to get into our operations, and I have to be the firewall to stop that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, having said that, does that mean you do believe there was a quid pro quo understanding with Eric Adams?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: I am going by what the perception could be. I cannot let the integrity of the administration in the city of New York be undermined by perception that the Trump administration actually created. They created this perception.\par \par \uc2 The reality, I will never know. But as long as there`s the faith of New Yorkers that has been undermined by what the Trump administration doing is trying to give the appearance of holding the city hostage, those are -- but that`s exactly what they`re trying to do.\par \par \uc2 Again, they`re trying to create chaos. But we have to be smart as Democrats and as leaders and parse through all this and just go do what`s right. That will always be my North Star.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor Hochul, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Joining us now is President Trump`s special envoy, Steve Witkoff.\par \par \uc2 He is in Miami Beach this morning.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 I have...\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF (U.S. Special Envoy to the Middle East): Good morning, Margaret. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have a lot to get to with you.\par \par \uc2 I want to start on the Mideast first. You have said you are headed there this week to work on a hostage deal. I know Prime Minister Netanyahu has changed his negotiating team. Will we get to phase two of this hostage deal? And can we get that American citizen Edan Alexander out any time soon?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: So, I -- we -- we will get to stage two -- or phase two. And I`m very focused on that, and I think it`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 I`m going there probably Wednesday evening. I will spend five days there as soon as I arrive, making -- going to various countries, including Qatar, Egypt, Israel, UAE, and Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 And, as to Edan Alexander, he`s front and center for us. I know his parents. We talk all the time. He`s critical. It`s one of President Trump`s most important objectives is to get all Americans home. And we`re going to be successful in getting Edan home, I believe.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, in that second phase, talking about what comes next for Gaza becomes part of that. For that day after, do \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders need to leave Gaza? Are you discussing that? And, if so, where would they go?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: So, Margaret, the -- the May 27 protocol agreement signed last May 27 sets forth that the phase two negotiation is much about two things, A, a permanent cease-fire, a cessation of all violence, and, in addition to that, the fact that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  cannot be allowed to come back into the government.\par \par \uc2 And I think the way you square that circle is that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has to go. They`ve got to leave.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Physically?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: And we`re going to -- the negotiation will be around that.\par \par \uc2 I would say physically. That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Where would they go? Has any country offered to take them in?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: Well, I think -- I think the devil is in the details, and we`ve had a lot of discussions around it. I`m not at liberty to have that discussion, that specific discussion today, but we`ve got some ideas, and that will be part of the negotiation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve been quoted as saying that when you went to Gaza and you personally saw the devastation there, you couldn`t understand why anyone would say -- stay, but it doesn`t mean you can`t have some sort of right to return, if that`s the policy that works for people.\par \par \uc2 Does the Trump administration support Palestinians` right to return?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: Well, Margaret, first of all, what I would say is this.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is one of the most sensible human beings you`re ever going to meet. He is -- he understands that it makes no sense to endorse old policy prescriptions that have not worked. And what has not worked in Gaza are all of these old policy prescriptions.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s come up with a new notion. And the new notion is, let`s create a better opportunity for these -- for people who have lived in Gaza to have a better life for themselves, better upside, better aspirations for what can happen for their children and so forth.\par \par \uc2 The May 27 protocol talked about Gaza as if it was a five-year reconstruction plan. We`ve been talking in the Trump administration about a 15-year horizon, perhaps even 20 years. And now, all of a sudden, The Wall Street Journal printed an article yesterday agreeing with that math.\par \par \uc2 There is a much -- level-setting the facts suggest that nobody can really live there in a safe environment for probably at least 15 years. So, effectively, we need -- effectively, people have been under this misassumption. When you`re under a misassumption about facts, you`re not going to make decisions correctly.\par \par \uc2 We`re making decisions based on a real set of facts. And so there`s a lot of work that has to be done there. There`s tons of demolition. There`s artillery shells all over the place that could explode at any moment. This is a much longer project, and people don`t belong living there right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But they could return?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: That`s -- that`s -- again, goes to the devil in the details.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Got it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: I`m not sure that anyone has a problem with people -- with people returning. We`ve had these discussions around that.\par \par \uc2 I just think the fundamental issue today is how we get phase two done and then develop a reconstruction plan for Gaza.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: And for that, you`ve got to have a real set of facts. And we`ve been operating prior to the Trump administration coming in under facts that are just not accurate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to also ask you about Russia.\par \par \uc2 You are a very trusted adviser to President Trump, and he sent you to speak face to face with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . FOX reported you sat with Putin for 3.5 hours. You said that you spent a lot of time talking, developing a friendship and a relationship with Putin, and that`s how you helped bring this U.S. citizen Marc Fogel home.\par \par \uc2 Can you take us inside that room? Did you have a translator? Did you have a notetaker? Were you talking in English? What happened?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: There was a translator. And, in fact, there were two translators.\par \par \uc2 And I -- to me, the assignment was trust-building and speaking behalf -- on behalf of my boss, the president of the United States, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . And I had no expectation as to how long the meeting was going to last. I was intent on carrying a message from the president to President Putin.\par \par \uc2 I had been advised that the president -- that President Putin had something for me to transmit back to the president. And the meeting ended, I looked at my watch, and it was close to 3.5 hours that we were in there. And, hopefully, that that suggests that a lot of good things got accomplished.\par \par \uc2 I think that President Trump believes -- and I believe because he believes it -- that trust-building begins with good, proper communication. And we had really good -- we just had a great discussion, me and President Putin. And now it will be up to President Putin and President Trump to work something out. And I think they`re going to be successful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did you have an intelligence officer or anyone else with you? That`s an extraordinary amount of face time with Putin.\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: I had -- it was just me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It was just you.\par \par \uc2 The head of Russia`s sovereign wealth fund -- I know you know him -- Kirill Dmitriev, told Reuters that...\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... afterward, they expect a number of American companies to return to Russia this year.\par \par \uc2 Is he right to expect that? Did you discuss lifting U.S. sanctions off of Russia?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: We -- we did not have that specific discussion at -- at the meeting you`re referring to.\par \par \uc2 But I think that -- that, obviously, there would be an expectation that, if we get to a peace deal, that you would be able to have American companies come back and do business there. And I think that everybody would believe that that would be a positive, good thing to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: After the end to the war and concessions were made by Russia?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: Well, that`s what everybody wants.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF: And what everybody wants, the -- the beginning of that, Margaret, is -- is -- would be a -- would be a temporary cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 But -- but the long-term goal would be an end to this. The -- we`ve had a - - you know, close to a million-and-a-half deaths. And so, you know, President Trump, the -- his -- his agenda is to end this carnage. It just didn`t belong to happen -- it didn`t need to happen.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn`t need to continue, not, you know, another day. So we`re on it, at his direction. And I think there is -- you`re going to see some -- some real positive developments in the near-term future.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Special Envoy Steve Witkoff, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Stay tuned for our next half-hour, including an interview with Utah Republican Senator John Curtis.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION. We return to our conversation with Utah Republican Senator John Curtis.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you here in person, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS (R-UT): It`s nice to be in person. Good morning. And lots to talk about today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I want to get your perspective on the decision by the president on Friday to dismiss not only the chair of the Joint Chiefs but also five other senior military officers. The former chair of the Joint Chiefs, General Martin Dempsey, released a statement saying, "trust should be based on leadership, integrity and performance. To relieve a senior officer not for a lack of one of those qualities but for a real or perceived disagreement in their beliefs harmfully politicizes the military profession."\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that`s what`s happening here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: You know, I think we have to stop and look what happened really last November. And one of my colleagues describe it this way, it was a stop the car moment. Those of us who have had young kids and been on family vacations know what a stop the car moment is. It`s, kids, you`re so disruptive, we`re stopping the car.\par \par \uc2 And I think in many ways the American people said, stop the car. We want dramatic change. We`re unhappy with what`s happening at the border, we`re unhappy with the economy, we`re unhappy with so many things. You keep promising that you`re going to cut spending, and you don`t. Stop the car. And so people really shouldn`t be surprised that this is a stop the car moment. We don`t get to go to Disneyland until we figure this out. And I think that`s what people are seeing right now in Washington.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you think part of the public frustration, or that there was correct to be frustration, with the leadership at the Pentagon or the president`s top military adviser?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Look, we elected a commander in chief last November. This is his discretion. They want change. The people want change.\par \par \uc2 We all knew what we were getting with President Trump, right? Nobody should be really surprised at the - at these dramatic shocks to the system. The system needs a shock. And I think you`re - what you`re seeing as a result of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There is concern, though, at a time of geopolitical risk, as our Pentagon correspondent was suggesting there, that, you know, carrying the institutional knowledge with you through a transition, we`re still at the beginning of the Trump administration, certainly, that there could be a hiccup there or some complication.\par \par \uc2 I mean, General Caine is - or Lieutenant General Caine is a - is a three star. He`s retired. It`s not clear if he needs a congressional waiver or not to take this job because he didn`t run a combatant command.\par \par \uc2 Do you know how complicated it will be to get him in place, or how long?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: So, listen, yes, it - I don`t. And I`m not overly worried about that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: I`ll come back to this, the American people have asked for change. They`ve lost - lost confidence in so many things. They want to see dramatic change. That - that was a stop the car moment. And what happened at the Pentagon was a stop the car moment. Let`s make some change.\par \par \uc2 We`ve tried subtle changes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: We`ve tried the easy changes. We`ve tried working around the edges. And I think the American people are saying, it didn`t work. Let`s - let`s do something more dramatic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`ve certainly seen dramatic. You had the video at the Conservative Political Action Committee of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  wielding this shiny chainsaw.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s celebrating this Department of Government Efficiency idea.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, federal employees also received an e-mail, I`m sure you`ve heard, telling them to reply by midnight Monday to a message with five bullet points of what they accomplished in the last week. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  tweeted, failure to do so would result in firing.\par \par \uc2 Utah has 33,000 federal employees. Is that how they should be treated?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: So, listen, let`s go back to $36 thousand - trillion of debt. And we have to do something dramatic.\par \par \uc2 Now, to answer your question, I don`t believe so. You know I just published an op-ed to DeseretNews.com - deseret.com, and I talk about my experience as mayor and having to do similar things. If I could say one thing to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , it`s, like, please put a dose of compassion in - in this. These are real people. These are real lives. These are mortgages. We - it`s a false narrative to say we have to cut, and you have to be cruel to do it as well. We can do both.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, it`s not just about efficiency, though. I mean the president himself has characterized this as getting rid of bad people.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: You know, I regret that narrative. We all know, you know, thousands, millions of federal workers who are good people, who work hard. But the reality of it is, we have 3 million federal workers. Not all of them do. Many aren`t coming to work. In my work - business -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Working from home, you mean?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Working from home. Let`s hope they`re working from home. We don`t know. That`s why the e-mail. What are you doing? It`s not unusual, in a corporate setting, to have people report and explain what they`re doing, especially if they`re working from home. So, I don`t think this is a request that is that difficult. I would ask my employees to let me know what they`re doing. But I will double down on the fact of, we don`t need to be so cold and hard and let`s put a little compassion and, quite frankly, dignity in this as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. And in that op-ed you said, "sometimes it is the worst day of someone`s life to receive a notice like this."\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: It is. Yes, going back to my experience as mayor, we did have to let some people go. We did a lot through attrition. But every single person we let go, I met with personally. I looked them in the eye. I told them why we had to do it. How can we then help you transition to the next thing. We can do both things.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that`s not happening at all right now, as you know. At these department agencies - some of them, they`re just getting locked out of systems. People don`t even know who to ask. They can`t ask HR. And HR says, I don`t know, if they can`t get ahold of them at all.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Yes. But - but also - I`ll also come back to this, stop the car moment. Like, this is not a normal moment. We have tried. We`ve tried for years to do this gracefully. We`ve tried for years to do this slowly. And we have failed consistently. And the American people said, enough. We`re tired of this. You have to make these changes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, do you believe the president has the unilateral authority to cancel funds appropriated by Congress?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Well, what we`re seeing play out is the wrestle between the three branches of government.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: We`ll find out. And this is the beauty of the system.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You don`t have a point of view?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Well, listen, I - I believe in the Constitution, right? I believe this is how we test the Constitution. And people have said, oh, this is a constitutional crisis. And I say exactly the opposite, it`s proving to work. We have the court`s playing in it. We have Congress who will play in it. We have the ability - I think we hold a lot of responsibility for what`s happening right now. We could solve the budget as Congress, we could solve the border, and we haven`t. And both parties, when Congress doesn`t do their job, and the White House, have a tendency to try to solve it.\par \par \uc2 Let`s let this play out by the Constitution and - and then, Congress, let`s step up, right? We need to do - I`ll be the first to say, we - this is a problem that Congress is, in many cases, has given the American people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The pushback has been relatively quiet. You`re gently doing that by saying, be more compassionate.\par \par \uc2 Senator Murkowski was telling constituents the other day, I can`t be the only one speaking out.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: So, listen -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are Republican lawmakers going to be more forceful?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Well, listen, I don`t know what you want me to be more forceful on. Stop the car. Like, fix this. That`s what Utahans are telling me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, in terms of the congressional role here, the check that Congress is supposed to put on the executive.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOHN CURTIS: Yes. But I also think that`s a stop the car moment. Congress has failed in these - in these things. And the - and the people have said, fix it. So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to fix it with the tools that he has.\par \par \uc2 The courts will push back if he steps out of line. We`ve seen that. We know that. We saw that with President Biden as well. And that`s the beauty of the system. I works.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s very - it`s - the - the people back home are impatient. If you were to walk on the streets of Utah, they would say, stop the car, John. Like, we - this is not good. You have promised, your colleagues have promised, for decades that you would fix these things, and you haven`t.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`ll see if the fiscal problems are fixed in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Senator, it`s going to be up to you and your fellow lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by Maryland Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 I want to refer back to the interview we just did with the president`s special envoy, Steve Witkoff. You watched the Middle East closely. I know you were just meeting with Arab leaders when you were in - in Europe last week.\par \par \uc2 He said that it is not realistic to have Palestinians remain in Gaza while it is rebuilt. Egypt and Jordan are working on plans for that to be possible. Does - does that proposal have any chance of surviving, or is this idea of moving them away for 15 years going to happen?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN (D-MD): So, the good news in what Steve Witkoff said was that he expected us to get to phase two of the discussions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: The very bad news was, he did not disavow this outrageous idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put on the table of cleaning out 2 million Gazans. That is ethnic cleansing by another name. And Arab countries are right now working very hard to put together a plan to show that you can deal with the governance, security and reconstruction, and that you don`t need to, you know, have 2 million people pushed out of Gaza in order to do it.\par \par \uc2 It is tough. It will have to be done in stages. And they have identified areas in Gaza, few areas in Gaza, that have not been obliterated where you can begin to phase the reconstruction and at least get people housing, temporary housing, as you rebuild in other areas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But how does that survive if the United States` most powerful player is saying, that`s not what we want to do?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Well, this is a big problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Because when Trump made those statements, what he did was fueled even more the far, far right in Israel. The Ben-Gvir, the Smotriches who are just celebrating because their idea all along has been to move 2 million Palestinians out of Gaza. They also want to move Palestinians out of the West Bank into places like Jordan and Egypt.\par \par \uc2 So, in addition to being a reprehensible idea for moral and legal reasons, it would also totally destabilize the region, which is why the Arab countries are working so furiously to put together a plan that addresses these issues and has a future horizon that provides security to both Israelis and Palestinians and self-determination for both Israelis and Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We are also hearing that the president is closing in on this deal with Ukraine in regard to its mineral resources that can be used for all sorts of valuable technology. What security guarantees are you comfortable with the U.S. giving to Ukraine as part of any of this?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Well, I think this is all negotiable. But what you should not do, Margaret, is begin your negotiation with Putin by, number one, adopting the Putin narrative, saying that Ukraine started this war, which is just a big lie. And you shouldn`t begin by giving up certain key cards.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, American guarantees or a future for Ukraine in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . However you settle this in the end is a matter of negotiation. But it`s terrible negotiating to give up those cards up front, as - as Trump has done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It was extraordinary to hear Steve Witkoff describe his meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , as he did for us.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: I would just say that this is not America first. This is America in retreat. And China and Russia and our adversaries are celebrating every moment of this as our allies are scared to death. And this is not just rhetoric. This is reality. And so -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is what you heard at Munich last week?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Yes, well, and I think, you know, European allies and others from around the world who believe in freedom and democracy, they`re wondering whether this is just rhetoric or whether this is a real shift.\par \par \uc2 My warning to them is, I think this is reality. I think Trump is much closer ideologically to Putin and other autocrats around the world than he is to democratic countries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: An extraordinary statement to digest.\par \par \uc2 But I want to ask you about what - what`s happening here at home, because you sit on some powerful committees. The administration did have some legal wins this past week. \uc2 USAID\uc2 , a federal judge said that they can put on administrative leave thousands of \uc2 USAID\uc2  employees. Another district court denied a request from unions to stop the firing of federal employees. Are Democrats too knee-jerk to say that all this is unlawful?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: No, this is unlawful. In many of these cases, judges found that the plaintiffs did not have standing, but other parts of their opinion clearly indicated that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  did not have the authority to do what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 So, we just talked about the great betrayal of the Ukrainian people. What we`re witnessing now is the great betrayal of the American people.\par \par \uc2 Canada - Trump said he was going to lower prices. Right now what he`s doing is slashing important public services that help every American in order to finance a tax cut for the super rich, for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and the billionaires.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, there was this e-mail that went out yesterday to federal employees. We know that at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the State Department management inside those agencies has - have told employees, don`t respond to the personnel e-mail saying respond with your five bullet points.\par \par \uc2 Can people working at the State Department feel safe not responding? Are they putting their jobs at risk?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: I don`t think so because what we are witnessing are illegal actions by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . And a number of courts, as you know, have issued temporary restraining orders on their efforts to seize important private information, but also on their overall conduct. So, what the head of management at the State Department said is, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  doesn`t have the authority to require State Department employees to answer these questions. That has to happen in the chain of command.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Even though the president says he wants him to be more aggressive?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: So, the reality is, we don`t - we don`t know what the president meant. But as you pointed out, you`ve got \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  taking a chainsaw to the federal government and important services. And there`s no article four in the Constitution that gives \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that authority. And the way that he`s been sort of, quote, delegating his authority is - there - is illegal. And the actions he`s taking are illegal and we need to shut down this illegal operation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2 , thank you for your insight today.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Dr. \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 . He`s a former \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner and current \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  board member.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back, Dr. Gottlieb.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2  (Former \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner and Current \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  Board Member): Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ve seen these troubling measles outbreaks in Texas and New Mexico. Nearly 100 people sickened. It looks like most of those who have been infected were unvaccinated and the outbreak area has one of the highest vaccination exemption rates in the state. How concerned are you, though, that this could spread?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Very concerned. I think this will spread. There`s been 100 cases that have been identified so far. There`s probably many more than that. So, I think that this is going to get into the hundreds of cases and could take many months to try to fully snuff out.\par \par \uc2 And when you have that kind of a concentration of measles within a community, it`s going to inevitably spread outside that community. When you look around the country, there`s a lot of parts of the country, states that have relatively low vaccination rates when it comes to MMR right now. You look at Idaho, about 80 percent oof children have been vaccinated for MMR. Alaska and Wisconsin at about at 84 percent. Minnesota, 87 percent. States like Florida, Colorado, Oklahoma, Georgia, Utah, around 88 percent. Iowa, I think, is at 89 percent.\par \par \uc2 And herd immunity is achieved at a vaccination rate of between 93 and 95 percent. And even within those states, it`s not like the low vaccination rates are distributed evenly across the states. There`s pockets of under vaccination as communities have relatively low vaccination rates. And so, when you have this much virus spreading in that portion of Texas and New Mexico, I think that there`s some inevitability that you`re going to see cases exported into other states where there`s low vaccination rates. And you could see a continuous spread across the country. Even at a low level we could get some endemic spread.\par \par \uc2 And the risk to the United States right now is that a virus that has been largely extinguished from circulation in the U.S. could return and just continue to spread even at a low level. And the U.S. could be at risk of losing its measles elimination status, which would have more profound implications on things like travel advisories that could be initiated from other countries.\par \par \uc2 And so, beyond just the public health impact of this, which I think is quite substantial, there could be some economic impacts as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : And, finally, when you look at that outbreak in Texas, 27 percent of people have been hospitalized. And there`s been four cases where children have contracted measles that have been vaccinated. So, those were probably kids who only had one dose of the vaccine. But there`s a lot of people who are at risk of this beyond just the people who have chosen to forgo the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s terrifying, Doctor. And kids are usually vaccinated, as any parent knows, around one year old roughly.\par \par \uc2 The MMR vaccine, though, this is the vaccine against measles, is particularly scrutinized. \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , who is now the \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary, has accused it of causing issues in children. He wrote the forward to a book questioning vaccines, saying measles is "a disease that is rarely life threatening and a vaccine that is largely unnecessary but carries real risks."\par \par \uc2 He is now the \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary. How does he restore confidence in a vaccine that he previously denigrated?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, I think it`s going to be difficult. And as best I can tell, he hasn`t spoken to this outbreak. I think you captured the essence of the argument from Mr. Kennedy, as well as people within his anti-vacs movement, that they believe this vaccine isn`t effective. That`s not true. It`s highly effective. And that measles itself isn`t a bad illness. And I think there`s been some statements that actually getting measles has certain long-term advantage, which is just patently false. This is a very dangerous virus. And as I said, about 27 percent of the kids who have been infected in that west Texas outbreak and the New Mexico outbreak have been hospitalized. So, this is going to be a quite serious illness with long-term consequences.\par \par \uc2 Herd immunity is achieved at 93 to 95 percent. We`re well below that in most parts of the country. Texas actually has a high vaccination rate and I think Texas local public health officials are doing a good job to try to get control of this outbreak. But now that it`s stretched into the hundreds of cases, it`s going to be very difficult.\par \par \uc2 This has been a vaccine that`s been studied extensively. The links between - the alleged links between MMR vaccine and autism have been the subject of some of the most - the largest studies ever undertaken. There were two studies done in Denmark, each with about 500,000 children enrolled in those studies. There were also a number of studies that were commissioned by the CDC and the \uc2 NIH\uc2  also looking at this in the United States. And so, this is one of the most extensively studied questions, the purported link between MMR and autism.\par \par \uc2 And I think the reality is sometimes we have to accept no for an answer.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : That there just is no link between the MMR vaccine and autism.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, and those studies were brought up by Dr. Cassidy, Senator Cassidy, in the hearing, and Mr. Kennedy would not affirm them. Senator Cassidy still voted for him, though.\par \par \uc2 But I want to ask you about another issue. Our correspondent, Camilo Montoya-Galvez, has learned the Trump administration is preparing plans to use a health authority, known as Title 42, which you remember was used during the pandemic, to restrict migrant entries across the southern border. Now they want to invoke it on the basis of other diseases like TB. Is that warranted right now? Is TB that much of a problem?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, I - I think from a policy standpoint, the administration has done a good job shutting down flows across the border. So, I`m not sure why they`re reaching for a public health measure to try to provide an additional provision, an additional tool. They seem to have done a good job with the law enforcement that they`re doing at the border.\par \par \uc2 Just from the objective standpoint of TB and the risk of TB being transported across the border, TB incidents rates are high in the countries where immigrants are coming from, but they`re not exceedingly high relative to other countries. So, you look at a country like Mexico. The - the incidents of TB is about 30 cases per 100,000 people. In Venezuela, where there`s a lot immigrants coming from, it`s about 45 per 100,000. That compares to about 10 per 100,000 in a country like England. But you look at a country like India, the incidents is about 200 per 100,000. In South Africa it`s about 500 per 100,000. So, there`s a lot of countries around the world where people come in from all the time where the incidents of TB is much higher than what - what it is in the countries where people are coming across the border from.\par \par \uc2 Now, you can argue that people coming in the country from South Africa or India going through regular check points where they might undergo some screening, but I think just purely from an objective public health standpoint, there`s probably parts of the world where we have immigrants coming in -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : That provide a larger risk of the transmission of TB than across the border.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you give us some perspective on the thousands of employees who are being dismissed from federal health agencies? Should the public be concerned?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Look, I don`t - I don`t think they should be concerned. I think that there have been steps taken to try to re-adjudicate some of those initial dismissals, at least within the public health context when you look across \uc2 HHS\uc2 , and I`m most familiar with FBA where they`re hiring back a number of employees. And I think they could hire back fully half of the employees that were initially dismissed at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . And they`ve tried to preserve things like review functions and inspectors at \uc2 FDA\uc2 . They didn`t do it perfectly because of the way they went about this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : They riffed the probationary employees. So, they did caught some employees that I think are in critical functions. They seem to be hiring them back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : You know, just objectively, they look at this and they say, well, the number of people hired has gone up a lot in recent years and they want to reduce head count.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. OK.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : And so I think we need to be more judicious about who they target.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Dr. Gottlieb, I`m sorry, I`ve got to leave it right there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you for watch. Until next week. For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020250223el2n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For February 20, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Scott MacFarlane, Weijia Jiang, Robert Costa, Seth Doane, Charlie  D`Agata, Debora Patta, Maurice DuBois, Albert Ramon, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Trump today began the second month of his second term as head of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , a government he is determined to shrink.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And he got some help today from a federal judge in Washington. The judge, an Obama appointee, refused to stop the president`s mass firings of federal employees.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The judge said unions representing those workers must take their claims of wrongful firings to the \uc2 Federal Labor Relations Authority\uc2 . The chairwoman of that agency, a Biden appointee, is one of the people Mr. Trump fired.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Since the president was sworn in, he has taken aim at the federal work force of 2.4 million and fired, laid off, or offered buyouts to untold thousands. And we say untold because the White House is not telling us exactly how many as the job cuts continue.\par \par \uc2 But Weijia Jiang has been tracking down the jobs being eliminated.\par \par \uc2 And, Weijia, what have you found?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, we found that there is a group of more than half-a- million workers vulnerable to the administration`s sweeping layoffs and many serve in key positions across the federal government.\par \par \uc2 For some who have already lost their jobs, they`re now desperately looking for answers why.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN (Fired Federal Employee): I`m really not sure what I`m going to do.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): Katie Sandlin is still living out of boxes after uprooting her life in Carbon Hill, Alabama last month, a town of 2,000, to work at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  outside Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN: I wiped out my savings account. I maxed out my credit card. I had to take out a loan.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But she called her job an opportunity of a lifetime, educating communities about \uc2 NIH\uc2  research.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN: People like me, where I`m from, rural Alabama, but these kind of jobs don`t happen to people like me.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Three weeks later, Sandlin became one of thousands of federal probationary workers to be fired. They had less than one or in some cases less than two years of experience and fewer protections from layoffs, making them easier to terminate.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN: Now I`m unemployed. I have a ton of debt and I`m also losing my health insurance.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Did it ever cross your mind that you could be fired in a second?\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN: That part actually never crossed my mind.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Did your supervisor give you a reason for why you were getting fired?\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN: They made it clear that I was not being terminated because of anything that I had done.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: An \uc2 HHS\uc2  official sent Sandlin a letter, saying: "Your performance has not been adequate to justify further employment at the agency."\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN: When I got it, it -- it really upset me because it felt like it wasn`t truthful. My boss, her boss, and even his boss all said that that was not true.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Our CBS News data team found that, at the \uc2 Veterans Health Administration\uc2 , which implements the health care program for vets, probationary workers account for 30 percent of the staff. At the Department of Agriculture, it`s 19 percent of the branch that performs food safety inspections.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to air safety, about one-third of the \uc2 TSA\uc2  may be impacted. So far, 2,000 probationary workers at \uc2 NIH\uc2  have lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 What does the public stand to lose with these widespread layoffs?\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDLIN: We are important, and we do important work. We`re impactful. And if you have questions or concerns about federal workers, ask one.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Weijia joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, what does Katie do now?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, she says she can`t afford to move back to Alabama after shelling out so much money to move here to the D.C. area, so she`s focused on looking for a new job.\par \par \uc2 But she points out how daunting that is, considering there are now thousands of federal workers in the same boat who are competing for those positions.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And, Weijia, you showed us how many probationary workers are potentially vulnerable. Do we have any sense of any of them ending up like Katie, like how many that could amount to?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, just because they are probationary workers does not mean they will automatically be fired. In fact, depending on the agency, some are considered critical, and so they have exemptions.\par \par \uc2 And, remember, they can only be fired for poor performance or misconduct. In Katie`s case, she says it was neither. So, John, that lawsuit you mentioned is just one of several, including class action lawsuits, in the works.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Weijia Jiang.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: One day after the angry exchange between Presidents Trump and Zelenskyy over the Ukraine war, Zelenskyy met in Kyiv today with U.S. envoy Keith Kellogg. A planned joint news conference was canceled, but Zelenskyy said they had a good discussion and it gives him hope of reaching an agreement with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The Ukraine war will be the topic when Mr. Trump meets at the White House Monday with the president of France and Thursday with the British prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Bob Costa is in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Bob, these leaders are coming to Washington to talk to the president because, even though there are diplomats in the region, the president is driving this diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: That`s exactly right, John.\par \par \uc2 There is growing alarm among so many American allies in Europe about President Trump`s position on President Zelenskyy and Ukraine. But there`s an acceptance behind the scenes that they have to come to the United States, have a meeting with him, convince him up close that he maybe could take a bit of a different direction and not try to just focus on the terms of a possible deal between Russia and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So they`re working the phones and getting on the plane.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Bob, a lot of people watching all of this unfold might be wondering what is behind Trump`s embrace it appears of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: When I`m at the White House talking to sources, it`s evident that so many people close to President Trump don`t see it as an embrace, as some kind of hug of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . What they see is a president trying to cut a deal, a president who`s transactional.\par \par \uc2 And as so many people close to Trump put it to \uc2 CBS\uc2 , at the end of the day, Trump sees Putin like he sees President Xi of China, like he sees Kim Jong- un of North Korea, someone who`s a strong authoritarian-type leader, someone he wants to work with one-on-one. And he`s not concerned about post-World War II foreign policy, the usual perception of world order. He wants to do it his way and have his fingerprints all over it.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Robert Costa in Washington tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 An unlikely sight in Virginia Beach, Virginia, snow on palm trees. A winter storm brought a foot of snow and is blamed for more than 500 highway crashes in the state.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The longtime Republican Senate power broker \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  chose his 83rd birthday to announce he will not run for reelection next year. The former majority leader has spent nearly half his life in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: McConnell joined all but two Republicans today in voting to confirm Kash Patel as \uc2 FBI\uc2  director. Patel is a Trump loyalist and a fierce critic of the bureau. Before he was nominated, he vowed to come after those who he called anti-Trump conspirators in the government and the media.\par \par \uc2 It was one month ago today, on his first day in office, that the president made good on his promise to pardon the January 6 rioters.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Today -- today -- excuse me -- some of them gathered -- attended a gathering of conservatives in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane has more about that for us tonight - - Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes, Maurice, a celebratory reunion of sorts for some January 6 riot defendants outside of D.C. They attended this conference in which Vice President Vance and the House speaker appeared and spoke publicly.\par \par \uc2 That includes members of the Proud Boys group, some of whom were convicted of seditious conspiracy, conspiring to overthrow the government January 6. They even posed for a photo.\par \par \uc2 Some of the January 6 rioters told us at first they were turned away from the event, but the Conservative Political Action Committee later issued a statement after getting besieged on social media about it, saying those reports are untrue and that "We support wholeheartedly President Trump`s pardons of January 6 victims."\par \par \uc2 John and Maurice, critics say this declaratively cements the whitewashing of January 6.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott, the Proud Boys were convicted under the Biden Justice Department. The U.S. attorney in Washington is now seeking to take action against someone else. Who`s that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes, that U.S. attorney is named Ed Martin.\par \par \uc2 He was a Stop the Steal advocate, an election denier who defended January 6 rioters in their court cases. In a memo obtained by CBS News, he told his staff that he is going to focus inquiries on Democratic members of Congress and whether they made threatening statements when criticizing judges and Elon Musk`s government efficiency team.\par \par \uc2 That new U.S. attorney will work with the new \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, Kash Patel, who, John and Maurice, when he enters his office, has that 5,000-person \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent employee list of January 6 investigators who are concerned they will be retaliated against.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Justice correspondent Scott MacFarlane in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here: a break from the cold weather for much of the country.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we will have these stories:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I`m Charlie D`Agata in Groton, Connecticut. Building submarines has become the Navy`s top priority. In tonight`s Eye on America, we will show you the surprising place shipbuilders are looking for the next generation of skilled workers.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: I`m Debora Patta in East Jerusalem. A day of mourning across the country after the bodies of four Israeli hostages are finally brought home.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  today returned four Israeli hostages, returned them in coffins.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It was the first release of hostage remains under the cease-fire deal. Among them were two young children.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta is in East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): Under a grim Gaza sky, four coffins were paraded in a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  propaganda spectacle. The fighters handed over the bodies of 4-year-old Ariel Bibas, and his 9-month-old brother, Kfir. Israel says their mother, Shiri, was not among them.\par \par \uc2 The family has become an indelible symbol of the October 7 terror attack. And as the convoy made its way into Israel, mourners lined the streets to pay their respects. The fragile cease-fire in Gaza is approaching the end of its first phase. On Saturday, six more living hostages are expected to be freed.\par \par \uc2 Yael Alexander`s son Edan will not be among them.\par \par \uc2 YAEL ALEXANDER (Mother of Hamas Hostage): Every release, of course, I`m crying and I`m feeling -- it`s very, very emotional. I also cry because it`s not Edan. It`s not my boy.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Originally from New Jersey, Edan is thought to be the last living American hostage. He volunteered for the Israeli army after finishing school and is only due to be released in the second stage of the deal. Negotiations for that have not yet begun.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel that the Israeli government owes you something?\par \par \uc2 YAEL ALEXANDER: Of course. He came to protect Israel. Where is Israel? They need to protect him. They need to bring him home.\par \par \uc2 Edan. (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Her message for Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , you cannot stop negotiating now.\par \par \uc2 YAEL ALEXANDER: Imagine it was you. Imagine you were the father. Your son is held in Gaza tunnels alive and still waiting to be released. Why to postpone it? Do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Debora Patta joins us now from East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Debora, you have reported from the region many times. And with this news today, what are you noticing, if anything, about the mood of the Israeli people?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, Maurice, for the past five weeks, the images of those Red Cross vehicles in Gaza waiting for another hostage exchange have been a symbol of hope here, hostages released alive after more than 15 months in captivity.\par \par \uc2 But today really marked the first time that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has released the bodies of dead hostages. And it signifies the very sobering reality that there will be more hostages coming home in coffins, that, for some families, there will be no longed-for reunion with loved ones.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Debora, what`s the prospects for that fragile cease-fire as it moves into its next phases?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, John, the next round is going to be a lot harder. Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  want very different things. We have heard one \uc2 Hamas\uc2  commander say that they are prepared to release all the remaining hostages in one go in exchange for a permanent truce and a full withdrawal of Israeli troops from Gaza.\par \par \uc2 That`s a nonstarter for Israel, who has said it will accept nothing less than the complete demilitarization of Gaza with no \uc2 Hamas\uc2  presence at all. So we are right back to that thorny question of who is going to control postwar Gaza.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Debora Patta in East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Debora.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It was another day of bitter cold from much of the country, but warmer weather is coming.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lonnie Quinn is off tonight. Filling in, Albert Ramon, chief meteorologist at CBS News Chicago -- Albert.\par \par \uc2 ALBERT RAMON: Gentlemen, we have some really good news, especially for the end of the weekend into next week. Let me show you the arctic air map. And there`s the arctic air tonight.\par \par \uc2 By the time we head towards late this weekend into Monday, that`s going to retreat up towards the north, so, much improved conditions, especially as we head towards Monday and Tuesday. We got to get through tonight though, cold weather advisories in effect from the Outer Banks in North Carolina all the way to Texas, and lows tonight breaking records, in the 20s tonight in Charleston.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures will be in the teens tonight in places like Memphis and Birmingham. But take a look at Omaha, not a windchill, an actual air temperature, 11 below zero. That is his 30 degrees below average for this time of the year. We will break a record set back 107 years ago. And this is one of the coldest winters in years.\par \par \uc2 If you`re wondering, New York City and D.C. and Boston, we got to go back to about 2014 to 2015. Austin, we had that arctic air mass with ice. This is your coldest winter since 2021 -- back to guys in New York.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Albert, with those eye-popping cold temperatures, it`s only going to get warmer. We know that much, but how much warmer?\par \par \uc2 ALBERT RAMON: Let`s talk Monday. How about mid-70s in Austin, Texas, low 70s in places like Tulsa and also in Oklahoma City? Bismarck, though, 50s. That is an 87-degree warm-up from the coldest temperatures you had this week to low 50s as we head towards Monday afternoon -- back to you guys.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Quite warm, Albert Ramon.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, Albert.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : While the president is cutting jobs, a search is on for a new generation of workers to build submarines. The Navy has ordered 29 nuclear subs, and the company that makes them has schoolkids on its sonar.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): At Charles Barnum Elementary School in Groton, Connecticut...\par \par \uc2 MAN: There are a lot of pipes on a submarine.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: ... these fifth graders are learning about submarines.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are going to build some of these periscopes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: It`s actually working. I can see Alexander.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: This is the beginning of a recruitment effort by General Dynamics Electric Boat, Groton`s biggest employer and the Navy`s biggest submarine builder.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY MURPHY (General Dynamics Electric Boat): People sometimes are like, why are you in the elementary schools? In 2033, the people we`re hiring, some of them are in fifth grade right now.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Courtney Murphy is in charge of bringing new workers to Electric Boat.\par \par \uc2 What do you need right now?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY MURPHY: Trade workers, welders, machinists.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: That training is well under way at nearby Ella Grasso Technical High school. Students here work with the same state-of-the-art equipment they use at the shipyard. They learn to work in hot, cramped spaces similar to submarines.\par \par \uc2 Xiamir Fletcher is a senior.\par \par \uc2 What is it about welding that drew you to it?\par \par \uc2 XIAMIR FLETCHER (Student): It makes me focus. Once you start welding, that`s it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 XIAMIR FLETCHER: Dead set on the welding.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It`s part of a national effort by shipyards and the Navy to bring on 100,000 skilled workers over the next 10 years. They`re urgently needed to build a new fleet of nuclear missile submarines and smaller, fast attack subs.\par \par \uc2 To do that, \uc2 Electric Boat\uc2  needs to more than double production.\par \par \uc2 ADM. WILLIAM HOUSTON (\uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 ): It is the Navy`s number one construction priority. That`s how important it is.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Admiral William Houston heads the Navy`s nuclear-powered fleet.\par \par \uc2 What is it specifically about submarines that make them so critical to what you need right now?\par \par \uc2 ADM. WILLIAM HOUSTON: They can go anywhere, any time, and hold an adversary at risk. They can just watch what you`re doing and you don`t even know they`re there.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The problem? The Navy says it doesn`t have enough of them. \uc2 Electric Boat\uc2  is ramping up to meet that national security challenge, a challenge Adam Chioccola and Emma Isbell are happy to take on.\par \par \uc2 EMMA ISBELL (Student): It`s a lot of stress, but the more you do it, the easier, the more natural it gets.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: I know there`s a lot of pressure, but you guys are making it sound like fun.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 EMMA ISBELL: Oh, it`s so fun. It is.\par \par \uc2 ADAM CHIOCCOLA (Student): You got to have fun doing it.\par \par \uc2 EMMA ISBELL: Entirely.\par \par \uc2 ADAM CHIOCCOLA: That`s what takes the pressure off.\par \par \uc2 EMMA ISBELL: There`s not a lot of people in the world that can say that they build nuclear submarines. Like, it`s -- it`s pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: As far as the Navy`s concerned, there aren`t enough people who can say they build nuclear submarines, and the future of the fleet is riding on it.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Charlie D`Agata in Groton, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Romans gathered again outside Gemelli Hospital, placing flowers and candles and offering prayers for \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : He`s been in the hospital nearly a week with pneumonia. The Vatican says he showed slight improvement today.\par \par \uc2 Seth Doane is in Rome.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO (Vatican Communications Director): What is in English? (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: A slight improvement.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Slight improvement, yes.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE (voice-over): At St. Peter`s tonight, Father Enzo Fortunato, a close aide to \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , is encouraged by news of the pontiff`s condition, despite the lack of specifics and just a couple of brief updates a day.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: The mood inside the Vatican is very tranquil.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: Tranquility may be the message from inside the Vatican, but you would never know that from outside Rome`s Gemelli Hospital, where a hungry media circus devours every word.\par \par \uc2 FABIO MARCHESE RAGONA (Journalist): We have little information.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: Fabio Marchese Ragona is the pope`s biographer and a correspondent for Italy`s Mediaset Channel TG5.\par \par \uc2 What is the result of so little information coming out officially?\par \par \uc2 FABIO MARCHESE RAGONA: It`s really hard to work, because, with little information, we have many, many rumors. During the night, fake news, the pope is dead.\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: He believes those rumors are fueled by those who never liked this progressive pope and fanned by skeptics of the institution itself.\par \par \uc2 (FABIO MARCHESE RAGONA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 SETH DOANE: "There`s always suspicion toward the Vatican," Ragona told us, "that they don`t tell the truth."\par \par \uc2 At the center, an 88-year-old with a history of respiratory illness and now pneumonia in both lungs, as the spiritual leader of the world`s Catholics fights a very earthly battle.\par \par \uc2 Seth Doane, CBS News, Rome.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And that is the CBS Evening News. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . See you soon on Evening News Plus.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will see you back here tomorrow. Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250221el2k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Delta Plane Crashes and Flips on Tarmac; Deadly Storm Recovery; Snow and Cold Grips Country; US and Russia Officials Meet; Federal Workers Layoff; Fewer Consumer Protections; Building Back Efficiently; Overlooked No Longer; Oprah`s Book Club Pick; CBS MORNINGS Deals; Road to Recovery; CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Tom Hanson, Kris Van  Cleave, Nicole Valdes, Jessica Burch, Holly Williams, Nancy Cordes,  Vladimir Duthiers, Jill Schlesinger, Nancy Chen, Meg Oliver \par \uc2 14,304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on a Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A plane crashed. It`s upside down. Fire Department is on site. It`s upside down.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A Delta flight with 80 people on board crashes and flips upside down on a Toronto runway, and everyone survives.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The South braces for more severe weather as it tries to recover from devastating floods.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): One of the most significant natural disasters we`ve seen in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: More mass fire rings of federal workers by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and the Trump administration. Some say the reason they were given doesn`t make sense.\par \par \uc2 MONICA (Former \uc2 FAA\uc2  employee): I was in complete disbelief.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Four deputy mayors in New York City have resigned a result of the alleged quid pro quo between Mayor Eric Adams and the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: NASCAR Hall of Famer Jeff Gordon says he wants a sequel to the 1990 film "Days of Thunder." He`s been in touch with Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Days of Thunder.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Stand up straight. Stand up straight.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Wait. Wait. Wait. Wait.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We look -- well, look at us taking a picture with you.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HART (Comedian and actor): You`re so stupid. That`s why I don`t have no clothes with our sizes.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Kevin Hart got roasted when he stopped to take photos with a few WNBA players at the All-Star game this weekend.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`ve got to bend down low. That`s great --\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HART: No.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It should be.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HART: At the end of the day, if you could be this small and this dope, you would be this small, too.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You know, I wouldn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kevin has a very has a very good sense of humor about all the teasing he gets all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He goes if you be the small and dope, he ain`t wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, shoutout to all the short kings out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right. He is all right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking at him roll with that wide angle, anyway.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this morning, though, with the incredible stories of survival after the terrifying crash of a Delta flight guaranteed you`ve never seen anything like this before. Take a look. The video shows the plane as it skids, catches fire and then flips upside down. That`s right, a plane upside down on the tarmac as it lands at Toronto`s Pearson Airport.\par \par \uc2 Eighty people on board when this happened on a flight. It started in Minneapolis. Many were injured, but the big news, the great news, the good news here is nobody -- nobody died in this crash.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is at Toronto`s Pearson Airport with more on this story. Tom, you`ve got 80 grateful people this morning. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Yes, no doubt, Gayle. Good morning to you as well.\par \par \uc2 The two runways involved in this crash are still closed this morning as the investigation continues. We`re learning more about a couple of the people who were more seriously injured, a 40-year-old woman, a 60-year-old man, both in critical condition this morning, 16 other passengers walked away with minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 But still, there are so many questions about how this crash happened in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: New video shows the moment a Delta regional jet crash landed at the Toronto Pearson Airport Monday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 You see it catch fire as it skids across the tarmac before rolling over surrounded by a plume of black smoke.\par \par \uc2 Video obtained by TMZ shows the crash landing at a different angle.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This aircraft is upside down and burning.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Our plane crashed.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Video of the aftermath shows the plane upside down on the tarmac, its wings and tail ripped off and landing gear pointing toward the sky.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everything drop it. Come on.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Flight attendants and crew members rushed to help stunned passengers strapped upside down in their seatbelts escape the wreckage.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON (Passenger): It sounded -- I mean, it was just cement and metal, you know.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Pete Carlson, who was traveling to a paramedics conference, was on the frightening flight.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: All of a sudden, everything just kind of went sideways, and then next thing I know, there is a kind of a blink and I`m upside down, still strapped in.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: He says he knew he had to get off the plane quickly.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: I took my seatbelt off, which I had obviously left fastened for the landing, and as I took it off, crashed on to the ceiling, which had become the floor.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Passengers looked on in shock as emergency responders immediately began spraying down the plane`s charred fuselage.\par \par \uc2 Despite the devastating damage, officials say all 80 people on board, including 76 passengers and four crew members survived.\par \par \uc2 TODD AITKEN (Toronto Pearson Fire Chief): It`s really important that we do not speculate. What we can say is the runway was dry and there was no cross wind conditions.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: At the time of the incident, visibility was down to six miles, and there were wind gusts up to 37 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 Air Traffic Control was heard warning the pilots.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Might get a slight bump in the glide path. There will be an aircraft in front of it.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Billy Nolan, former acting \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator, calls it a remarkable escape.\par \par \uc2 BILLY NOLAN (Former acting \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator): A bit miraculous to wind upside down, and yet you`ve -- we`ve got 80 folks to walk off this plane and with no fatality. So I`ll mark that as a win for us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And I want to read you a statement from \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  that they released. They say the hearts of the entire global Delta family are with those affected by today`s incident.\par \par \uc2 Tony, nothing short of a miracle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it really is.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk more about it with Kris Van Cleave. He is going to talk about the investigation and how this all could have happened.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning to you. You`re a veteran of covering aviation and general transportation. You`ve seen the videos as we have. What do you make of what you`re looking at?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, I think what you can see from the TMZ video looking behind as it is coming into land, this appears to be a very hard landing followed by a collapse of one of the rear landing gear, perhaps because you see the plane sort of shift down to the side right before that engine starts to flame.\par \par \uc2 That will be a good starting point for investigators. You know did the impact the hardness of that landing result in the gear collapsing? Did the gear collapse because of other reasons? That`s not going to be told by the video, but certainly, these new images give us a sense of what appeared to be a very hard landing.\par \par \uc2 You don`t see the typical kind of flare up that you would see as a plane would come in to land, where you bring the nose up just a little bit right at landing to sort of smooth it out.\par \par \uc2 This one looks like they came in, almost like a carrier landing, straight in a hard landing, they were dealing likely with winds at that time. We had heard gusts up to 40 miles an hour when they were coming in.\par \par \uc2 So all of that will be significant. This video does give investigators a pretty good starting point as they make their list of what they want to know and what they need to find out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tom Hanson describe it as a miracle. I certainly believe in miracles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you look at this --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean putting context for us. If you could, Kris, one of the passengers said that they were upside down like bats in a cave, just hanging there with the seatbelts on.\par \par \uc2 How unusual is it in any kind of airline incident for the plane to be upside down at the end?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, it`s certainly very unusual, but clearly something that was thought about when they designed not only the aircraft, but those seats. The seats are designed to withstand many times the force of gravity and if you ever wanted a lesson as to why people tell you to wear your seatbelts on the airplane, this was it. Staying in those seats saved lives.\par \par \uc2 The flight attendants, you know, you`ll hear when you`re coming in to land that they need to prepare the cabin for landing, and they go through and they collect glasses and everything else, things that could become projectiles, they tell you to put your bags away and your laptops away.\par \par \uc2 The reason they do that is for a scenario like this, where things go wrong and those items could be flying about the cabin and injure passengers by securing them.\par \par \uc2 It also means that there`s not clutter on the way out of the airplane. In this case, the exit path was the ceiling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is such a good reminder, Kris.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is great reminder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hadn`t thought of it that way, even though we hear it time and time again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great reminder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really good. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you, Kris. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 In our next half hour of our program, we`re going to talk to a pilot who has flown into that very airport there in Toronto, many times in similar conditions, find out her thoughts on what happened.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, speaking of weather, let`s go to the brutally cold weather in much of the country. It feels like it`s 50 below zero this morning in parts of Montana and North Dakota, Gayle will say out here in New York, but the cold weather is also blasting the South and the East.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky has seen the worst of the bad weather. Twelve people are now confirmed dead after weekend storms caused severe flooding, and another storm is on the way.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes is tracking the recovery effort.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): We just got hit by one of the most significant natural disasters we`ve seen in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Kentucky is recovering from deadly storms that battered parts of the state, flooding out roads and buildings.\par \par \uc2 First responders have been working around the clock, like Mason Michael.\par \par \uc2 MASON MICHAEL (First responder): The water had, at that time, risen to almost a hood level.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: He leads the fire department in Horse Cave. His team rescued a couple from their truck on a flooded street.\par \par \uc2 Since the rain started to fall on Saturday, how many calls have you and your team gotten?\par \par \uc2 MASON MICHAEL: There has been a significant increase.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Communities in Tennessee and West Virginia also felt the wrath of the storms, and now many are trying to see what`s left.\par \par \uc2 This couple got help from family, who went to check on their house with kayaks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Everything in the house is dry.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Back in Kentucky, Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  surveyed the damage in hard hit Pike County, which saw devastating flooding in 2022.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA LUCAS (Kentucky resident): Many have PTSD from the floods that they suffered last time.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Amanda Lucas lives in nearby Letcher County. This year, storms flooded her antique shop`s inventory, but her focus is on her community.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA LUCAS: These are my friends` houses.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people around here have no income, and I just feel like things were just kind of starting to somewhat feel normal again, and now a lot of people is going to have to start all over.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Here in Kentucky, we are expecting a snowstorm to start tonight and last through tomorrow morning and get this, here in Bowling Green, we are not expecting to see any temperatures above freezing for a single hour until Saturday. That is bad news for communities like this that are still dealing with high flood waters -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bad news indeed.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Bay Area meteorologist, Jessica Burch is watching the rest of this winter weather blast.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So how bad will it be today?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA BURCH: You know, we`re taking just a look at the next 48 hours, and we`re already starting to see the storm strengthen, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Over the Central Plains right now, and it will continue to track off into the South and all throughout the Mid-Atlantic as we head into the rest of this week`s forecast, pushing offshore just within the next 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 But it`s important to note a couple of things. It comes just days after that major flooding in Kentucky, and we timed the storm out. Throughout the next 12 hours, watch how it starts to gain strength from Kansas into Missouri, pushing over the Mississippi as we head into the overnight hours tonight, early morning hours tomorrow, making recovery very difficult for our friends in Kentucky as we head into those early morning hours on Wednesday, covering that whole state with snow.\par \par \uc2 That continues to track off into the East and to the rest of this week`s forecast. And the other big story has to do with these cold temperatures that potentially could break records today, like Kansas City at six degrees. You have that wind chill that`s all the way down to negative 12.\par \par \uc2 We`ll keep you updated on that -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jessica, got it. Always good to see you. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 The most extensive talks in years between the US and Russia began in Saudi Arabia this morning.\par \par \uc2 Now, the war in Ukraine is top of the agenda, even though Ukraine is not at this negotiating table.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is following the talks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Secretary of State Marco Rubio sat down with his Russian counterpart this morning, high speed diplomacy aimed at ending the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ignited the conflict nearly three years ago with his invasion, and some say today`s meeting is already a victory for Russia, which has been isolated by sanctions and condemnation.\par \par \uc2 Many Ukrainians feel betrayed.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Ukraine did not know anything about these talks," said Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  yesterday. "We cannot recognize any agreements about it that are made without us."\par \par \uc2 European leaders held an emergency meeting in Paris on Monday. Many in Europe fear that if Ukraine`s allies don`t guarantee its security, Putin could agree to a ceasefire then re-invade in the future.\par \par \uc2 The British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  says his country is willing to provide peacekeeping troops, but cannot protect Ukraine alone.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER (United Kingdom Prime Minister): There must be a US backstop, because a US security guarantee is the only way to effectively deter Russia from attacking Ukraine again.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: He`ll go to Washington next week to discuss that with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: President Zelenskyy says he`ll travel to Saudi Arabia tomorrow. Meanwhile, Russia released another detained American yesterday, a man arrested this month for allegedly possessing marijuana. That was apparently a gesture of good will -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Back in this country, Trump`s acting Social Security Commissioner stepped down over the weekend. According to multiple reports she had resisted letting Elon Musk`s Department of Government Efficiency look inside the agency`s most sensitive records.\par \par \uc2 The White House is saying that DOGE is looking to eliminate waste and fraud. And this morning, Nancy Cordes has the story of one of the thousands of federal workers already fired since Trump took office.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MONICA: And I was in complete disbelief.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Monica, who asked us not to use her last name was hired by the \uc2 FAA\uc2  in December to be an aviation safety assistant at Dallas Fort Worth Airport. She was part of the team that filed inspections and reports on the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  fleet.\par \par \uc2 MONICA: I personally did not feel that our positions were at risk for the simple fact that we are in the Flight Standards Safety Division. It says its name right, flight and safety.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But on Friday, she was let go. With less than a year on the job, she had not yet received civil service protections. The stinging dismissal e-mail blamed her performance and said, "You have not demonstrated that your further employment at the DOT FAA would be in the public interest."\par \par \uc2 MONICA: There have been no negative comment or feedback until I got this letter about performance.\par \par \uc2 MICHAL SHINNAR (Joseph Greenwald & Laake Employment lawyer): What this really does appear to be is just a purely false stated reason.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Employment lawyer, Michal Shinnar says the reason Trump`s team has been citing performance in firings is because, by law, probationary federal workers can only be removed for performance or misconduct.\par \par \uc2 MICHAL SHINNAR: We don`t let an agency get around these congressional directives by just terminating our probationary employees and mass claiming it is performance when it`s not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: She says the situation is ripe for class action lawsuits while Trump officials insist these cuts are just a drop in the bucket.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy posting last night: "The \uc2 FAA\uc2  alone has a staggering 45,000 employees. Less than 400 were let go." We`re still waiting for the exact numbers of cuts at most other agencies like the CDC and \uc2 NIH\uc2  -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, another Trump administration target for cuts is a key consumer protection agency. \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here and will show us how that could affect you. But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Oprah Winfrey exclusively shares her new Book Club Selection, and we talk with the author.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is highlighting some remarkable people whose achievements were overlooked for decades, we meet the writer who is bringing their stories back to life.\par \par \uc2 This CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . Save when you bundle auto, home, or motorcycle insurance.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Money Watch," we are looking at the Trump administration`s push to gut the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 , but what does it actually do?\par \par \uc2 CBS News business analyst, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  is here to break it all down.\par \par \uc2 All right, so why should Americans care? And what are some of the safeguards that exist because of the \uc2 CFPB\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So the \uc2 CFPB\uc2  was created out of the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It was an agency that was very laser-focused on consumers, so everything from mortgages to credit cards to payday lending - - all of that.\par \par \uc2 So some of the things that they have done include really trying to skinny up a lot of documentation, like when you get a credit card bill and all of that gobbledygook and mortgages, so that`s called the Know Before You Owe.\par \par \uc2 Then there is restriction of credit card late fees, that`s in action, that`s been enacted, but I`m sorry -- that has been a rule that`s put in place, but has not been enacted.\par \par \uc2 And then we also have that -- we`ve talked about this, the expunging of medical debt from your credit report, that`s huge it will impact millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 So there are things that really help people on the ground.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So investigations at the \uc2 CFPB\uc2  are on hold. Many staffers are on leave. So where should consumers turn to now?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So there are other agencies that are available for consumer complaints. So one is looking at the \uc2 FTC\uc2 , the Securities and Exchange Commission. They all have consumer arms, right? We also have something for employees and their retirement plan, and states have a lot of insurance and securities arms, as well as consumer divisions that are very helpful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, so you mentioned there are other agencies. If there are other agencies, does the Trump administration have a point in gutting this one?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, this has been a point of Republicans since the beginning of the \uc2 CFPB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Since 2008.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : They said, hey, there are seven other agencies that have consumer arms. Why do we need this?\par \par \uc2 And they really push back that payday lenders actually went to the Supreme Court to defund it. The Supreme Court found against them.\par \par \uc2 Here is what I think is important. There are very few agencies whose sole purpose is to focus on the consumer, and that`s why I think this has been good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha. Consumer protection.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Consumer protection.\par \par \uc2 However, I also want to be clear that when big institutions like \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 `s \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  complain about regulation, I`d like everyone to look back 15 years and see just how much money those banks have made over those years. They`re doing just fine regardless of the regulation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They have been cashing in.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Consumers take action. Be clear, there`s no one agency that is really out there fighting for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love the way you make this all digestible. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much, Jill. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, we`re going to look into the Toronto plane crash through the eyes of a pilot. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Drop it. Come on.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, these pictures still amaze me.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 The crew of that Delta jet that flipped over after crashing on the tarmac in Toronto is being praised for getting everybody out of that plane safely. All 80 passengers and crew on board survived this. At least 18 of them were hurt.\par \par \uc2 Captain Laura Einsetler, she`s a longtime commercial airline pilot who has flown into that area -- flown into that area and that airport many times, joins us now with her expertise.\par \par \uc2 Very good to see you, Captain.\par \par \uc2 I was really looking forward to this because I know you know the airport, the plane and the conditions. So first, I want to get your initial thoughts when you saw this plane upside down.\par \par \uc2 Most of us have never seen anything like this. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER (Longtime commercial airline pilot): It`s very disturbing. This is not something, of course, that we hardly ever see. And now, as the details are coming out and the investigation is taking place, we`ll be able to determine what the mitigating factors were in the situation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How tricky is the airport -- this airport during this time of year? Because it was interesting to me that the pilot made no mayday call to indicate that he was concerned -- he or she was concerned or that something was wrong.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: Basically, on the video, it shows that everything happened pretty fast, flying in and out of Toronto myself many times in the wintertime, as we know in the Northeast, weather can play significant factor. So they`re going to be looking at things like wind gusts, wind shear -- you know, wind shear, things like that; icing on the runways, the condition of the aircraft, and what the pilots were seeing and thinking and experiencing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Captain, can you talk a bit about the training that pilots have? We`ve heard that this is a proven airline design that type of airplanes landed in this area a lot for decades, pilots obviously trained for tough conditions. What kind of prep would have been in place for a landing like this?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: Well, in this kind of winter weather conditions, you`re going to be thinking about again, icing, visibilities, what type of icing might be taking place, what type of wind conditions, wind angles, different types of gusts that might affect the aircraft, cross wind components for those type of airplanes, the capability of the aircraft and the capability of the pilots involved.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Captain, can you walk us through the communication between the pilot and air traffic control, and from what you`ve heard about this incident, could something else have been said that could have led to a safer landing?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: That`s something that the investigators will definitely look at and determine, based on air traffic control, based on the cockpit voice recorder, the flight data recorder, things of that nature, all the different factors involved in this tragic situation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, this is either the fourth or fifth airline/airport mishap in recent months, we should say, recent weeks. Does this rattle you as a pilot?\par \par \uc2 I sit here very rattled, but I`m a very nervous flyer anyway but do you think that the flying public should be concerned? I know they`re all different, too, but should the flying public be concerned?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: I want to reassure the flying public that, again, air travel is the safest mode of transportation. We have 90,000 flights that take off and land safely around the world every day, carrying millions and millions of people.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you want to pay attention. You want to be on apps and things that your airline has for you to keep you updated if there`s any type of delays or weather conditions that might affect your flight, and you always want to listen to the safety briefings of the flight attendants, always, anytime you come on board with us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but I still don`t know how you`re not rattled by this because you simply know more than we do, because you`re the expert here. I just don`t know why it`s not unnerving.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: It is concerning and in aviation, these are the kind of things that we learn from so that we can keep that safety bar as high as we possibly can, and it`s what we`re trained for.\par \par \uc2 We`re experts in compartmentalizing, so you can be assured that we put our lives on the line every day to keep everyone safe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we appreciate the investment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we know the numbers are good. The statistics are in your favor when you get on an airline.\par \par \uc2 Captain Laura Einsetler, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN LAURA EINSETLER: Absolutely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead Americans are showing incredible resilience after the wildfires in California and other disasters.. We visit a company that claims to have a better way to build things back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As we know, national disasters can -- natural disasters can devastate entire communities. More than 16,000 homes and other structures were destroyed just in California`s January wildfires. Rebuilding them will be time consuming and costly.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News analysis found the cost of home construction supplies across the country has increased by 47 percent since 2016.\par \par \uc2 Now, one company says it can rebuild in a matter of weeks for less money. Nancy Chen looks at how it could also help tackle our country`s housing shortage.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CORONADO (Palm Beach, Florida resident): Over here, we have the living room. There`s a little balcony out there.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Jessica Coronado (sp?) has lived in this apartment near Palm Beach, Florida for almost a year.\par \par \uc2 Did you have any idea that this building is constructed differently than others?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CORONADO: I didn`t know at all. I was very surprised. I thought it was just going to be a regular apartment complex.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But this is no ordinary apartment complex. These four buildings contain 96 units and are the first of their kind in the country.\par \par \uc2 The inspiration behind the new technology, \uc2 Lego\uc2  bricks. Just 11 workers constructed the walls, floors and ceilings of each building in only eight weeks.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY (Former US representative): We built that much faster than we would have had if we`ve done it with wood or concrete, sort of a traditional material.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Patrick Murphy is a former congressman and now managing director of RENCO, the company behind this technology.\par \par \uc2 How quickly can you turn a house out of here?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: We can basically produce about 16 homes a day in this facility.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Their factory in Jupiter, Florida is scheduled to begin scaling up operations in April.\par \par \uc2 It`s no coincidence that you`re doing this here in Florida.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: No coincidence.\par \par \uc2 So we probably should have started in an easy area, but we decided to start in the toughest area, and so did years of testing to ensure that it could withstand Category Five hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The company says it can endure 240 mile per hour winds and is more fire resistant than most brick and wood and also water resistant.\par \par \uc2 Will they withstand the test of time?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: They have such a long lifespan that the insurance on our first building is about 20 percent less expensive for that sort of long term insurance.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: It doesn`t rot, it doesn`t rust. Pests don`t eat it, termites don`t eat it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: While the RENCO bricks are more expensive, Murphy says they still save 20 percent because of decreased time and labor.\par \par \uc2 We saw this wall go up in less than nine minutes. What does that mean for the speed in which these buildings can be assembled?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: Basically, it means we can get a lot more product built a lot faster with unskilled workers, right? You saw how easy it was. This entire 2,000 square foot home can be built with two people in one day.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Even an unskilled worker like me can use aerospace glue and a mallet to hammer in a brick.\par \par \uc2 That actually comes together really quickly.\par \par \uc2 Your company calls this the most disruptive innovation in construction in more than a hundred years, which is a big statement.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY: The last big breakthrough there was reinforced concrete that was a hundred, a hundred and twenty-five years ago. So now all of a sudden we have this new material that`s displacing all of that.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Is this a time of change for construction?\par \par \uc2 ILLYA AZAROFF (Architect): It`s a huge time of change.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Illya Azaroff is an architect and teaches sustainable construction at \uc2 City University of New York\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ILLYA AZAROFF: Globally, last year, 114 million people were displaced for natural and man-made disasters. So when I think about that, this has to be a moment of change.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: He said, while products like RENCO have design limitations and tend to be blocky, the structural and time-saving benefits are crucial..\par \par \uc2 ILLYA AZAROFF: When I see innovation in the marketplace like this, yes, we can build to fire resistance, earthquake resistance, to hurricane force winds, tornado force winds. We just have to have the political will to do it, and we have to have the guts to try new ways of doing things.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: RENCO says it costs about $30.00 per square foot to build the walls, floors and roof, including labor and transportation. And the company said it compared the cost of that apartment building in Florida to similar ones made with concrete and saved 20 percent, which goes a long way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what is it made out of?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes, it`s a good question. It`s a composite made of glass, recycled plastic, as well as other materials, including a resin that keeps it all together. But it`s very lightweight, which can save a lot of money in terms of transportation as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really cool. The Lego component is very fascinating.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Teddy is definitely watching right now, and thinking, I want to build a house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dad.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And he will be able to\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I will bring the ante.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead the stories that you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:43 time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re very psyched today about our eight o`clock hour. Why? Because Oprah is on the show today. Only on CBS MORNINGS, she is going to share her latest Book Club pick, and we`ll also hear from the author. We`ll introduce him today.\par \par \uc2 Plus the story of resilience from "Inside Edition`s" Alison Hall. Remember her? She was here last month. Why she has chosen to be so open and so honest about her experience with breast cancer and what she hopes other people will learn from her journey.\par \par \uc2 So all of that is coming up in our next hour, but first, Klaus and I heading over to the table because Vlad has entered the building.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Getting ready for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Talking asteroids and Safari. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am going to put those two together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the connection? "What to Watch" right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 Cancel your plans. We`ve got an asteroid coming.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how lucky are we feeling?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pretty lucky.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony pauses.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t like these odds.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me tell you why.\par \par \uc2 There`s an asteroid that`s heading towards us, and scientists have now doubled its chances of hitting Earth. The asteroid, it`s called, YR4 was spotted by high-powered telescope last year.\par \par \uc2 At the time, \uc2 NASA\uc2  estimated there was a one percent chance of it colliding with Earth in 2032.\par \par \uc2 Now that estimate has grown to 2.3 percent. It doesn`t sound like much, right? But astronomers call that percentage uncommon.\par \par \uc2 Scientists say the asteroid is the size of a building and could possibly wipe out a major city.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which one? I will avoid that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle asking the right questions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which one. Give us that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s why she`s an award winning journalist.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nonpartisan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t want to tell us?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It doesn`t say. I mean, wherever it lands, if there`s a city in its path, that city could potentially be wiped out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on. Come on. Someone`s doing the math. Someone knows the answer to this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is also a small chance it could completely miss Earth and hit the moon, there is that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re telling me, in 2032, there is a two percent chance a city on this planet is gone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By an asteroid.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So here is a couple of things to remember. As --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, you writing down the date?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s only seven years from now. We could all still be here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We most likely will all still be here. Yes. I am hoping that we are all still here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, until the asteroid comes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 So a couple of things to remember. In 2022, \uc2 NASA\uc2  did launch a DART mission that successfully pushed an asteroid --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- out of its orbit. So we do have this technology that exists to move these asteroids, depending on their size, out of orbit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I bit like that movie, "Armageddon."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But back in 2013, there was an asteroid that did hit Earth, and a couple of homes in Russia were damaged by the breakup of that asteroid.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yowzer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Remain calm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All is well.\par \par \uc2 All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s good, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Simone Biles also felt that very same way on a Safari in South Africa. The Olympic gold medalist is celebrating her second anniversary with her husband, Jonathan Owens. They couldn`t go on a honeymoon after tying the knot, so this trip is extra special.\par \par \uc2 Biles documenting their adventures on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  with photos of giraffes and elephants and more. One encounter, though, had a little bit too close for comfort for them, that`s an adult lion. That`s not -- there it is -- an adult line strolling past her vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Biles captioned the video saying she nearly blanked herself. But this is not uncommon. I experienced something very similar in May of 2023. Marian and I took our entire family to South Africa for a Safari. We had a phenomenal time, including -- this is our entire family.\par \par \uc2 But look at this, and I don`t know if we have the sound up. You could hear our guide saying, "Remain calm." Like I don`t know if we have the sound up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is two of them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, there are two and you can see as they approach, and you`ll see how close they are actually to our vehicle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, were you actually calm?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look, I`m filming. You can see, I don`t know if it`s like in a show -- my hand, there we go. There I am. That`s how close we are. Chill. Chill.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Vlad. Coming up, we have actor Nicole Ari Parker joining us for a special CBS MORNINGS Deals.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very impressive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stick around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very impressive.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, Oprah reveals her latest Book Club selection, and the author tells us what it meant to get the call from Oprah herself.\par \par \uc2 Stick around. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, only on CBS MORNINGS, and we love when that happens, Oprah`s latest Book Club pick, and she likes it, really likes it. She`ll tell us all about it, and we`ll meet the author.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 "Inside Edition`s" Alison Hall is here with a very personal story about her recovery from breast cancer. Why she feels it`s so important to share her journey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Ari Parker, the very talented actor and businesswoman is here for special CBS MORNINGS Deals. She wants to help people with their exercise game and Gayle, we think it`s all going to work out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she wants you to look good, too.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everything, drop it. Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All 80 people on board a Delta flight survive after their plane crashes on the tarmac.\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: The next thing I know, it was kind of a blink and I`m upside down.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s just devastation and heartbreak.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another winter storm heads for the South as flooded communities struggle to recover.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m numb. I`m trying to process it all.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Top officials from the US and Russia discuss peace in Ukraine without Ukraine`s involvement.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Meanwhile, Russia released another detained American yesterday, a man arrested this month that was apparently a gesture of good will.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Days of Thunder.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: NASCAR`s Jeff Gordon reveals he has been in touch with Tom Cruise about what? A potential sequel to "Days of Thunder." Yay.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Days of Thunder.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope that happens.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really hope it happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like Jeff Gordon, I like Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I really do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So excited.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let me out of car, Cole. Let me out of the car. One of the only movies I can quote.\par \par \uc2 I love "Days of Thunder." Rooting for it.\par \par \uc2 All right, but we`re going to begin with the news. The tough news out of Toronto, that terrifying plane crash yesterday involving a \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  flight. The video that came in this morning shows the plane making its approach to the runway before crashing in a ball of fire.\par \par \uc2 The good news, 80 people on board, all safely evacuated. Many were hurt, but remarkably, everyone survived this incident.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is at Toronto`s Pearson Airport for us.\par \par \uc2 Tom, what a morning. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hey there, Tony. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the scene here at the airport vastly different than it was yesterday, both of the runways that were involved in the crash, those remain closed as investigators hit the ground to figure out what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 But I want you guys to take a look at some of this jaw dropping video that is coming out of the crash. Take a look at this. This video shows the heart stopping moments when the plane crash lands on the tarmac here at Toronto Pearson Airport, you can see the plane catch fire as it skids across the tarmac before rolling over.\par \par \uc2 We know the \uc2 Delta Airlines\uc2  plane left Minneapolis just before noon, then around 2:15 PM, crashed and flipped upside down here.\par \par \uc2 Miraculously, all 76 passengers and four crew on board made it off the plane. Eighteen people were hurt, two are listed in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 Video of the aftermath shows the plane upside down, its wings and tail ripped off. Flight attendants and crew members rushed to assist passengers evacuate the plane as fire crews went to work immediately spraying the plane`s fuselage. One man who was on the plane, shared his experience.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETE CARLSON: As we made our descent and made touchdown. It was just a very forceful event where all of a sudden everything just kind of went sideways, and then next thing I know, it was kind of a blink and I`m upside down, still strapped in.\par \par \uc2 The most powerful part of today was -- there was just people. No countries, no nothing, just people together, helping each other.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And Monday`s plane crash marks the fifth high-profile plane crash in the last three weeks in North America. And I want to read you a statement from \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 . They say that "... the hearts of the entire global Delta family are with those affected by today`s incident." -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, once you see it, you can`t unsee it, Tom, that`s for sure. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Oprah exclusively reveals her latest book club selection, and then the author joins us in the conversation. But first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, one woman`s years long quest to highlight important figures in our history whose achievements were overlooked.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "Inside Edition" correspondent, Alison Hall will tell us about her recovery from breast cancer and why she`s grateful to actor, Olivia Munn for sharing her experience.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, we honor Black History Month with a story about paying tribute to people who were once overlooked.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, an editor at "The New York Times" noticed a big disparity in the newspaper`s obituaries. They feature mostly men and not many women and people of color.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver introduces us to the devoted obituary writer who is making sure we know more about the remarkable people who blazed a trail.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: What do you find down here?\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI ("The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " obituary editor): This place is absolutely gold for any obit researcher.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Deep below street level in the morgue at "The New York Times," is where obituary editor, Amy Padnani goes digging.\par \par \uc2 From an old school card catalogue to rows of file cabinets, the room is stuffed with more than 170 years of history.\par \par \uc2 How hard is it to get in to "The New York Times" obituary?\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: You have to have done something pretty remarkable, invented something everybody uses, changed the way of life for society at large or a group of people.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Since 1851, "The Times" has published thousands of obituaries from heads of state and scientists to celebrities and athletes.\par \par \uc2 But during a research project in 2017, Padnani realized many trailblazers, especially women and people of color, were overlooked people like Sylvia Plath and Ida B. Wells.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: I thought, yes, why is that? So when I approach my team to ask this question, they said, well, obituaries are a rear view look at society at a time when women and people of color weren`t invited to the table to make a difference. I just didn`t find that answer very satisfying.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: In 2018, she launched "Overlooked," a series in the obituary section to remember notable women who were left out.\par \par \uc2 She eventually expanded the following year for Black History Month to include people of color. Seven years later, it`s still going strong.\par \par \uc2 This month, she is publishing the obituaries for Maria Stewart, the first Black woman to speak publicly about racial and gender equality in the 1830s to Annie Easley.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: Annie Easley, for instance, is a scientist who worked for \uc2 NASA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: And Lena Richard.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: Lena Richard was the first Black person to host a cooking show.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The first Black woman to host a cooking show in 1949 before she could eat at the lunch counter.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: How was she overlooked?\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: We ask this question all the time.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The overwhelming response from readers compelled her to publish a book featuring 66 more stories of remarkable people, including Diane Patrick`s grandfather, Sinclair Clark.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe your grandfather?\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK (Sinclair Clark`s granddaughter): I never knew him, so I can only describe him as a rockstar, taxidermy tanner.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: A rockstar taxidermist.\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Clark`s work is on display at museums around the world, including the \uc2 American Museum of Natural History\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 What did it mean to you to see your grandfather`s obituary printed?\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: It meant that I had something that I could now show to some of the institutions that he worked in.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Patrick discovered her paternal grandfather two years before he died. Even then, she didn`t know that he worked on the iconic Hall of African Mammals in the museum.\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: His methods became the standard for these dioramas in this museum and other museums.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The gold standard around the world?\par \par \uc2 DIANE PATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY PADNANI: It seemed to me he might have been kind of quiet about his own achievements, so I just think he didn`t get the attention he deserved in his lifetime, which is part of the reason "The Times" may not have heard of him at the time that he died.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Overlooked trailblazers finally receiving their due recognition.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Meg Oliver in New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what I love about that? It is that not every trailblazer is loud. They may be proud, but they`re not loud about their achievements.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we don`t know about them.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we don`t know about them,\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But now, we do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is a way to celebrate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it`s a great idea. It really is, a great book as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is, and we will continue to celebrate Black History Month tomorrow. We have someone who is on the frontlines of Civil Rights.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-five years ago, four teenagers challenged a Whites-only lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina. We spoke to one of them, Air Force Major General Joseph McNeil, about the historic protest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Where did you find that courage from to be so young?\par \par \uc2 AIR FORCE MAJOR GENERAL JOSEPH MCNEIL (Member of Greensboro Four): We had strong convictions. We faced adversity. It gives you a strength. I never seriously done much of God, but I suddenly had faith.\par \par \uc2 And I`m sure it made the students the same way. We had all gotten together and determined that we can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Powerful conversation. He was just 17 years old, and you can see more of our interview with General McNeil tomorrow here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really looking forward to that, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, we will reveal Oprah`s latest Book Club selection and hear from Oprah and the author, too.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. We will right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Only on CBS MORNINGS, we are revealing Oprah`s newest Book Club selection. She said to us that this book changed her thinking. We sat down with Oprah and the author, to talk about why she liked it so much.\par \par \uc2 Here`s that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are so excited -- this is my excited face to have Oprah, as in, Winfrey, there`s only one in our studio to announce her latest Book Club selection. Drum roll. Please.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (TV personality): Drum roll. It is called --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , what is it?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : "Dream State" by Eric Puchner. I am so happy to be able to announce this book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so excited to talk about this book, because "Dream State" takes place over the course of 50 years in rural Montana. It follows a young bride who decides to leave her "picture perfect husband," that`s in quotes, after falling in love with his best friend. What?\par \par \uc2 Despite the betrayal the three remain intertwined in each other`s lives. Eric Puchner, we`re happy to say and Oprah is here.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Good morning. Congratulations to you, Mr. Eric Puchner, and this -- this is what I learned for the first time, sitting here with you and Oprah, because I was saying, "Oh, I like your suit." She said, "I picked out the suit --" to what?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Match the book cover.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. It does. Do you approve?\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER ("Dream State" author): I`m so honored. I can`t believe it. I really made it now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am always fascinated by Eric, by anything that involves betrayal, but certainly betrayal among best friends. So I was surprised that you would pick a book about betrayal about best friends. I love reading this kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Well, one of the reasons I really appreciated "Dream State" so much is because there`s this point in everybody`s life where you make a decision and that decision changes the course of your life, right?\par \par \uc2 And what if you had made the other decision? What if you had chosen to marry the other guy? What if you had not married the person that you`re with?\par \par \uc2 Everybody, I think at some point gets cold feet. I remember you telling me when you were many years ago --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: -- getting married that your mother said --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do I know?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: How do I know? And your mother said, oh, you`re just having cold feet. And so I think that this brings that story into full focus about what if you`d made another choice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the thing is, guys, this is more than just cold feet. She actually married the man and then ends up with his best friend.\par \par \uc2 Eric, where does that story come from?\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I think, Oprah, what you`re saying about making a decision and possibly wondering if it was the right decision, and then living in the rest of your life thinking about this sort of ghost life that you could have lived. I think it`s -- it`s very seductive to think that life is elsewhere and to fall through the trap door of regret.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I think.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Oh, that`s a good --\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: But that`s doesn`t mean that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ain`t that good, the trap door of regret.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: -- you would have been happier living this other life. You may, may have been just as happy, or you may have been less happy.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: What it says is that we can make a decision and you can make happiness wherever you are.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Yes, I think part of that happiness is not falling through the trap door of regret and accepting that this is the decision I made, and not dwelling on the other possibilities.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And can I just say up front, your lovely wife is here watching us at this time, you have not fallen through the trap door of regret.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You and your wife are very happy. This is not a personal story for you.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Not at all in that respect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she`s a novelist. Tell us her name.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Katharine Noel is a wonderful, wonderful novelist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I want to read Katharine`s book. Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So this has nothing to do with your own marriage, because whenever I read these kind of books, I think is it something they can relate to in their own personal lives?\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : One of the reasons why I, too was drawn to the book "Dream State," is because it is in a dream state. It`s set in the dream state of Montana, and I know that that`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which is a character in the book.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, which was its own character in the book. I have loved Montana since the river runs through it, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I mean, I adore Montana, and Katharine and I have been going to Montana for the past 25 years and staying at a particular house, which is a house that`s in Katharine`s family.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And that`s the house that`s in the story.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: And it`s the house, it`s not the actual house, but the house in "Dream State" is based on a real house that I adore.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and I don`t want to give the story away, because --\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I can`t replace --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- right up until the end, you keep us -- you keep us guessing right up until the end. Because I kept thinking the book looks like it`s going to about to end, but I still don`t know the answer to fill in the question that I had during the whole book.\par \par \uc2 How were you able to keep them still friends after 50 years? I was really fascinated by that.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I think friendship is really complicated, and I think that one thing I haven`t read enough of in fiction are stories about male friendship. And so I was very interested in this male friendship.\par \par \uc2 Charlie and Garrett were two of the main characters, have been best friends since college, and they form this powerful bond, and then there`s this profound betrayal. And so I was fascinated by how that might play out over 50 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I love this story, because whether you`re Gen Z, a millennial or you`re my generation, there`s something for everyone. I was talking to your producer, who is of the Gen Z generation, who recently married, was saying that she related so much to the story, and I loved having the opportunity to talk to you about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the book. Eric, congratulations, but before you go, I always like to know how people reacted when you got the call. Where were you?\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I was in my office, which gets horrible cell reception, so I had to go out into my car. I was kind of grumbling about it, because it`s this kind of depressing parking lot, and because I was sitting in my car hunched over, and then I get this phone number comes up on the screen that I`ve never seen before, and I answer it, and it`s \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Hello, Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC PUCHNER: I was just so flabbergasted. I was astonished.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, not only that, but I get -- I get the books 