RLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE KERR: You`re going to save up to 27 percent off. So the deal starts at $39.99.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Gabrielle Kerr, we appreciate you. Nicole Ari Parker, thank you for stopping by.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE ARI PARKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything that you saw, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Hey, yo, Tony, what`s up?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s up, Nate? Look what I have here "Inside Edition" correspondent, Alison Hall here with a major health update, a story about breast cancer recovery. This is a happy one.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL ("Inside Edition" correspondent): Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will talk in a bit.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Inside Edition.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know I love this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are so happy to see that that`s "Inside Edition" correspondent, Alison Hall. She is back on the job after undergoing successful surgery to treat breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 Did you hear that? Successful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Successful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Hall underwent a double mastectomy after being diagnosed with stage zero of the disease. She learned of her cancer after reporting on actor Olivia Munn`s own cancer treatment. So she took the same risk assessment tested, Olivia Munn did, a move that may have saved her life.\par \par \uc2 In post-surgery, Hall`s doctor shared some very good news.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So the pathology results were wonderful. We got all the cancer out. There were no surprises. It was exactly as we had expected.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Amazing. So can I officially say I`m cancer free?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You are definitely cancer free.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow, music to my ears.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re very happy to say that Alison Hall joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel as good as you look, Alison? We`re so happy for you.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you. Thank you. I really do. I feel great. I`m so happy to be here. I`m so happy to be back and have this chapter starting to close for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does stage zero mean? I`ve never heard that phrase before, stage zero. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: So stage zero means that the cells have just turned into cancer. They`ve just turned malignant. It has not spread anywhere else yet, and it`s the earliest --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is there.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: It is there. And it is the earliest stage that a cancer can possibly be caught. So, it`s pretty incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So after the cancer was caught, you had some treatment options, some decisions to make. Walk us through those decisions and why you ultimately went with the double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes, absolutely. So I learned about my cancer, and they said that I could do a lumpectomy and radiation and hormone therapy, or I could do a double mastectomy, which would treat the cancer that I had on my right breast, but also dramatically reduce my risk for future cancer.\par \par \uc2 So I took a look at both options, and knowing at the stage of life that I`m in being a young woman, I hope to have a family one day, I wanted to do something that would reduce my risk.\par \par \uc2 My doctor has told me that I now have a less than three percent chance of ever having breast cancer again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. This is -- wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is so good.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Which sounded pretty good to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but take me through the night before the surgery. I don`t want to be inappropriate or impolite, but I`m curious about your feelings, because I was thinking, if it was me, what would I do? I would stand in the mirror and just look at my boobs one more time. Might have a conversation with myself. What did you do?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: That`s exactly what I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you do that?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: That`s exactly what I did. I took a shower, I had a good cry, I listened to my favorite music. I looked in the mirror --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which was what? What? What did you listen to?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: The Head and the Heart, a folk band I love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: And I looked in the mirror and I thanked my breasts. I said, Thank you for your service. You`ve given me 33 good years, and it`s time to move on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I`m ready for this new chapter, and I`m ready to not live in fear.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At the risk of being inappropriate, I`m just asking a follow up, because I was curious too, and as a man, I don`t know how to ask, do you miss your breasts?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was my next question, too.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes. You know what? I really don`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I decided to go the reconstruction route. So I`m doing a two part reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: So when I woke up, I did look down and things looked a little different.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: But I have started the reconstruction process where I have temporary implants currently, and then they`ll replace them with more permanent implants in a few months.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: But I still feel like myself, because this new body of mine is built from a place of strength and control.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: And I don`t look down anymore and think what`s going on in there. Is there a cancer there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re pleased with the results?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes. They look surprisingly great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, you are back to work, and a few people that -- a few people that you may be familiar with, wanted to send you a little message. So check this out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM MORET ("Inside Edition" correspondent): Welcome back, Alison.\par \par \uc2 STEVE FABIAN ("Inside Edition" correspondent): Hey, Hall, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH NORVILLE ("Inside Edition" anchor): Alison, I`m so proud of you.\par \par \uc2 LISA GUERRERO ("Inside Edition): Alison, you are an inspiration. Thank you so much for your courage, your guts. You are a warrior.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH NORVILLE: Thank you for teaching us that we have to be proactive about our health, that we have to ask the right questions, and most importantly, as you did, learn to trust your gut.\par \par \uc2 MARY CALVI ("Inside Edition Weekend" anchor): How you`ve handled this with such courage and such grace is such an inspiration to me and to countless women across the country.\par \par \uc2 JIM MORET: You have grace and empathy, intelligence and tenacity, and you are changing the world one story at a time.\par \par \uc2 STEVE FABIAN: We missed you so much, and we`re so glad you`re doing okay.\par \par \uc2 LISA GUERRERO: We appreciate you, we honor you, and we`re so excited to have you back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We really are. That`s so nice.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those are your colleagues, of course.\par \par \uc2 I know you -- this story came to you through Olivia Munn, and we showed Olivia Munn reach out to you through us, and given a contact, given a message to you. I have heard that you two have communicated with each other?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Yes, she`s been absolutely incredible. After our segment here on CBS MORNINGS, she reached out privately and offered even more words of encouragement and love, and it just meant so, so much to me. I will forever be in debt to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How much time did you take off? Because it seems only yesterday, honestly, that you were here. I know it wasn`t, but how much time did you take off?\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: I was off work for a full month. Four weeks, which felt really good to me. And of course, "Inside Edition" has been incredible saying if I needed more time or if I need to take a day here and there, but after about four weeks, I was really anxious and eager to come back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, and so the takeaway for people is, get that screening.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Get that screening, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Alison Hall, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Alison. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So we`re so impressed, we`re so happy you`re doing better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALISON HALL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you want to see more of Alison`s reporting on "Inside Edition," you can, all you`ve got to do is check your local listings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yup. That will do it for us. We will see you tomorrow right here on CBS MORNINGS. Your favorite morning show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250218el2i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Brutal Winter Weather; Deal to End War; Netanyahu and Rubio Praise Trump`s Gaza Plan; Emotional Reunions; More Federal Workers Layoffs; Texas Measles Outbreak; SNL Celebrates 50 Years; Unsafe Baby Products; Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  Live; Happily Ever After for Trooper the Dog; Plastic Problem; Saddlin` Up with Brandon Sklenar; Saturday Morning at the Shop; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Nicole Valdes,  Skyler Henry, Jessica Burch, Holly Williams, Chris Livesay, Willie James  Inman, Celine Gounder, Nikki Battiste, David Begnaud \par \uc2 13,452 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trumpets on this Presidents` Day holiday, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, but we`re still here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Deadly winter storms carve a path of destruction across the country, bringing heavy rain, intense winds, flash flooding to the south.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): This is one of the most serious weather events we`ve dealt with in at least a decade. We`ve certainly had our share.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ukraine`s president warns not to trust \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as Secretary of State Rubio prepares talks with Russia.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (US Secretary of State): Ukraine will have to be involved because they`re the one that were invaded.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A room full of icons celebrate "Saturday Night Live`s" 50th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MULANEY (Comedian and actor): Some of the most difficult people I have ever met in my entire life.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Three fights in the first nine seconds of a game between Canada and USA in the Four Nations Face Off.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: During the all-star game, a fan beat Damian Lillard in a three- point contest, winning 100 grand.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Adam Sandler expressing our love for SNL over the years through song.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody pass the Kleenex.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Didn`t it just give you a new appreciation for the show and for Lauren Michaels in particular.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For 50 years and still going strong. You may not like every single show, but you can`t deny how good and how solid it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How talented the people over the course of 50 years --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- who have been on that show, have been, the careers that have been made on that show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the sketches that you could walk around this room, around this country and say lines from certain sketches, and people would know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and people know exactly --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you do anything for 50 years, you deserve a congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And guess what, we`re going to talk about it later on, too. That`s how excited we are. Congrats SNL and Lorne Michaels.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with this though, brutal weather weekend for much of the country. The South saw snow, winds, and flash flooding. At least 10 people died, nine of them in Kentucky, which is now under a disaster declaration.\par \par \uc2 In the Midwest and Northeast, harsh winter weather made for chaos at all the airports, nearly 12,000 flights -- 12,000 -- were either delayed or canceled yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have the forecast ahead, but we`re going to start with Nicole Valdes. She`s in Bowling Green, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, good morning to you. How bad is it?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The Barren River here in Bowling Green is still sitting several feet above flood stage after nearly five inches of rain in a single day. This storm left tens of thousands in the dark and without heat for what`s expected to be several days of now, frigid weather.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Torrential rain swelled waterways, creating dangerous flash flooding after a mix of winter weather and severe storms slammed the Southeast this weekend.\par \par \uc2 In parts of Kentucky, water overwhelmed roads and abandoned cars were almost entirely submerged, while anything visible above ground was covered in fresh snow. Sunday.\par \par \uc2 At least nine people were killed since the storm started Saturday. State officials say most of those deaths are from people who attempted to drive through dangerous, fast moving water, including a mother and her seven- year-old child.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Look at that, you look at the number of people we`ve already lost and the damage. This is one of the most serious weather events we`ve dealt with in at least a decade.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: Across the state lines in Tennessee, first responders fanned out over the weekend, rescuing drivers stuck as waters rose to rooftops.\par \par \uc2 Crews had to go door to door by boat in Northwest Tennessee when a levee on the nearby Obion River failed, stranding residents who didn`t immediately evacuate.\par \par \uc2 Severe storm cells also swept through Georgia. Our Skyler Henry was there.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Here in Atlanta, strong winds toppled power lines and massive trees like this one that first responders say slammed into this house, killing a 61-year-old man inside.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: In the Midwest, flurries created headaches for travelers. Snowplows worked around the clock to clear runways at the Detroit Metro Airport.\par \par \uc2 Late Sunday, that system moved East, sending dangerous ice and up to 18 inches of snow to parts of Upstate New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES: And it`s about 25 degrees here now, well below freezing, and those freezing temperatures are going to create a much more of a problem for those crews that are hoping to restore electricity for those who desperately need it here -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In our next hour, we want you to know we`re going to be talking to Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  about all of those recovery efforts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The weekend weather systems are pretty much done, but more rain and snow is on the way. Let`s bring in meteorologist, Jessica Burch from our \uc2 CBS\uc2  San Francisco station.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA BURCH: Oh, it is still cold out there for many of us throughout the Central Plains, and here`s the big story. As we head into the next 48 hours, we`re going to continue to watch that cold surge sweep throughout those areas that just dealt with severe weather over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Over 10 tornado reports in areas like Louisiana, stretching all the way over into Georgia. And we`re not done with that severe weather just yet. Let`s pull up the map and show you what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 Today, tomorrow, we`re talking about this huge cold polar air mass that`s sweeping across the Plains. Today, our feels-like temperatures all the way up into North Dakota top off at negative 53 degrees. That`s a hundred- degree difference compared to our friends all the way down into Dallas this afternoon sitting at 52.\par \par \uc2 Now that`s story number one. Our areas all the way to the south that just dealt with severe weather are getting another batch of it as we head into the next 48 hours. Memphis, all the way over into Atlanta will see more severe weather develop right around the corner, all the way down to the south, where these cold temperatures continue to sweep down there, too. We`ll have more on that coming up in a bit.\par \par \uc2 For now, over to you -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jessica Burch for us. Jessica, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We are going to turn overseas now where President Trump`s America First foreign policy is having an effect on a number of conflicts, including the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, the president spoke about a possible meeting with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to end that war, and it`s drawing a sharp warning from Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has more for us from London. She`s been following this.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 We still don`t have a date for a summit meeting between President Trump and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , but things seem to be moving very quickly. We`re expecting a meeting tomorrow in Saudi Arabia between Russian and US officials, including Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: President Trump is confident he can do a deal with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to end the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States): We have no time set, but it could be very soon.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Like this month?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It`ll be soon. We`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But speaking on "Meet the Press," Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  said he warned Trump about Putin`s intentions in a phone call last week.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy said he told Trump, Russia`s leader, doesn`t really want to end the war, which he ignited nearly three years ago by invading Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian president): He`s a liar. He doesn`t want any peace, but I think he`s really a little bit scared about President Trump, and I think the president has this chance, and he`s strong, but don`t trust him. Don`t trust Putin.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Many in Europe fear that a peace deal without security guarantees to protect Ukraine will see Russia re-invade in the future, but the Secretary of Defense, Pete Hegseth, told his European counterparts last week that US support for Ukraine has limits.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (\uc2 US Department of Defense\uc2  Secretary): Returning to Ukraine`s pre-2014 borders is an unrealistic objective.\par \par \uc2 To be clear, as part of any security guarantee, there will not be US troops deployed to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: That`s been criticized, including by a Republican senator as giving away leverage to Russia before negotiations even begin.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, despite receiving tens of billions of dollars in American aid during the war, some say they`ve now been betrayed.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "I think this is another step towards a world war," says this man in Kyiv. "Trump is trying to pacify the aggressor."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: European leaders are holding an emergency meeting today in Paris, worried that they are being excluded from the negotiations. Meanwhile, President Zelenskyy said today that Ukraine is not involved in the talks in Saudi Arabia and will not accept any agreements made without its participation -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In Israel this morning, three more families are celebrating the return of their loved ones. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  freed those former hostages, including an American citizen on Saturday. Standing next to Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , the Israeli prime minister, vowed to "open the gates of Hell," if all the hostages are not released.\par \par \uc2 He also backed President Trump`s plans for Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The visuals and the words left little room for interpretation.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli Prime Minister): President Trump and I are working in full cooperation and coordination between us.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: On the first leg of his first trip to the Middle East, as Secretary of State, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  championed President Trump`s once unspeakable proposal to turn war ravaged Gaza into the Riviera of the Middle East and to move all of Gaza`s two million people, where exactly remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (US Secretary of State): The president`s thoughts have been very bold about his view of what the future for Gaza should be.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Prime Minister Netanyahu echoed President Trump, who, last week threatened all Hell is going to break out if \uc2 Hamas\uc2  failed to release the hostages.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : We have a common strategy, and we can`t always share the details of this strategy with the public, including when the gates of Hell will be opened, as they surely will, if all our hostages are not released until the last one of them.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: After nearly collapsing last week, the fragile ceasefire holds after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  released three more hostages Saturday, one of them is an American citizen, Sagui Dekel-Chen who heard for the first time the name of his daughter born two months after he was kidnapped.\par \par \uc2 It was a reunion that required sacrifice, said his father.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN DEKEL-CHEN (Sagui Dekel-Chen`s father): Coming to an agreement with a savage terrorist organization that to this day, has not reneged on its pledge to destroy Israel, coming to an agreement with such an organization, alongside pressures, both international and within Israel takes courage.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Seventy-three hostages remain in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity. Today marks the 500th day.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Rubio landed this morning in Saudi Arabia, where he is expected to discuss Trump`s agenda to resettle Gaza. Rubio has indicated that Trump`s proposal was aimed at pressuring Arab countries to come up with alternatives -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Glad some are out at 500 days. Boy. Chris Livesay Israel, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, thousands more federal workers were fired by the Trump administration. The targets included Education, Homeland Security, and Health and Human Services, including the CDC.\par \par \uc2 Willie James Inman has the latest on that story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Less than a month into his term, President Trump took a victory lap at the Daytona International Speedway Sunday.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We brought it back, and it`s been less than four weeks.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: President Trump and billionaire ally, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  have worked to reshape and downsize the federal government, a move his supporters are cheering on.\par \par \uc2 TOM LAVAN (Trump supporter0: I think he came in, pedal to the metal, as they would say here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: But bearing the brunt of the cost cutting are the tens of thousands of probationary federal workers, newer hires, who have been in their roles for a year or two.\par \par \uc2 Close to 1,300 of those employees have been terminated from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 , and we`re learning about thousands more across other government agencies.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MILBURN (Former federal employee and \uc2 AFGE\uc2  union member): I`d say the last couple of weeks, there was a lot of stress, fear and tension.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Chelsea Milburn, based in Nevada, was fired last week from the Department of Education. She started last fall, but before that, she served more than a decade in the \uc2 US Navy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She`s part of the approximately 30 percent of federal workers who are military veterans.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MILBURN: The message right now is that the people making the decisions about whether or not we keep our jobs, how we are treated, and how the termination of our jobs are handled don`t care about our service.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: The Department of Veterans Affairs announced last week, more than 1,000 workers were fired, but the majority of the agency`s 43,000 probationary employees are exempt. Democratic senators say the wider cuts are having a negative impact.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): When 30 percent of the federal workforce is veterans, compared to like two to three percent of the civilian workforce, you do indiscriminate cuts, I`ll tell you it hurts. It hurts veterans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: At least 13 immigration judges who were in training were also affected by the layoffs. Now that might not seem like a significant number, but there`s a backlog of four million pending immigration cases, and President Trump has promised mass deportations, so any change in the number of judges could slow down that process -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lot of tradeoffs to work through. Willie, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in just a moment, we`re going to take a closer look at how the safety of baby products is regulated. One mom shares an absolutely tragic story involving a product that was later recalled.\par \par \uc2 Plus, growing concern about a measles outbreak that`s spreading in the state of Texas. We`ll talk to Dr. Celine Gounder about who`s at risk and how you can protect yourself.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:14, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, yes, it is on a rival network, but we were all watching "Saturday Night Live" celebrated its 50th year on the air last night, we`ll have a little coffee talk about who turned up on Sunday night to honor the show`s amazing history.\par \par \uc2 We`re all for clamp, no big whoop. We`re going to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a new chapter in Trooper the Dog`s incredible journey of survival. "Beg-Knows America" shows how the love of his adopted family carried Trooper through some unexpected challenges.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Nexium, all day and all night heartburn and acid prevention.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In today`s "Health Watch," we are looking at a measles outbreak in Texas. This has some people troubled.\par \par \uc2 Officials have confirmed 48 different cases, most of them in Gaines County, that`s near Lubbock, Texas. According to the CDC, these cases have been found not only in Texas this year, but actually four additional states as well. You see them on your screen, and the list of states is growing after New Mexico`s Health Department recently confirmed a case there as well.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder, who is also KFF Health News editor-at-large for Public Health for more on this.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you very much for being here. What`s behind the spike in Texas in particular?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is what happens when you do not vaccinate. This is a measles outbreak that began in a very close knit rural community, Mennonite community, that has very low vaccination rates. And unfortunately, we have seen vaccination rates -- exemptions rather for vaccination rates really soar in Texas in recent years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So overall, vaccination rates have declined, but only slightly nationally. So you were worried about this pocket of high non- vaccination, but talk to me nationally. Are these declining rates a sign that we could see a wider outbreak?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: With measles, which is what we know of as being the most contagious infectious disease out there, you need very high levels of vaccination to prevent an outbreak. So even a dip from 95 percent coverage to 93 percent coverage can leave you vulnerable to having outbreaks.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s really important to emphasize that the measles vaccine is highly effective. Two doses gets you to 97 percent protection and effectiveness against developing measles infection.\par \par \uc2 It is highly, very safe. We`ve seen hundreds of millions of doses given over the years worldwide. And when you do not get the vaccine, that is when you have the risk of hospitalization and death.\par \par \uc2 And in this particular outbreak, we`ve already seen three people hospitalized out of 48, that`s almost 30 percent. Historically, it was one in five or 20 percent, so it`s even higher than what we`ve seen historically and historically before we had vaccines in the 1960s between 400 and 500 people died every year from vaccination.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People might forget that. Yes, it is MMR on the vaccination sheet. My kids all have it. If you`re looking for it, it might be under that acronym.\par \par \uc2 If people are concerned they`ve been exposed or their children have been exposed, what symptoms should they look for?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So usually, symptoms begin about seven to 14 days after exposure. It starts with cold-like symptoms, but I want to be very clear, this is not just a cough-cold, and then about two to three days after symptoms begin, you get these little white spots inside the mouth, then you can get this rash, which is sort of a flat, blotchy red rash, usually starts around the face and spreads to the rest of the body.\par \par \uc2 And then you can get fevers that are as high as 104.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: That`s when you start to worry, and the infection can spread and cause severe disease in the brain, sometimes leaving somebody with cognitive issues, with deafness, it can spread to the lungs.\par \par \uc2 So this is really not a minor infection, and what we`ve seen recently, KFF polling found that currently, about 17 percent of parents have skipped or delayed a vaccine dose. This is up from about 10 percent just two years ago. So we`re really seeing a lot of exemptions, concerns about vaccination that are not warranted.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a big change. We`ll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gounder, thank you very much for now. Goodbye for now, but don`t go far, because in our next hour, we`re going to be talking to Dr. Gounder about new research on the accumulation of plastic particles in our bodies and in our brains.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, I knew it was something.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Indeed, I was wondering what was going on up there. All right, switching gears here.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, we`re celebrating half a century of "SNL." We will see the big stars and cast members as they bring back many of the show`s iconic moments.\par \par \uc2 But first, your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS live from New York on Sunday night, Adam Driver and other mega stars joined Maya Rudolph and "SNL" veterans to celebrate the show`s 50th anniversary. The TV event, running more than three hours was a nostalgic trip down memory lane, filled with familiar characters, surprise cameos, and, of course, plenty of laughs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It was a night 50 years in the making.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A testament to "Saturday Night Live`s" enduring institution.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "SNL" royalty returned to studio 8H to play classic characters from the show`s different eras in just over three hours.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Will Ferrell played one of his funniest impressions, Robert Goulet alongside some special guests.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Eddie Murphy played Tracy Morgan next to Tracy Morgan in "Black Jeopardy."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Weekend Update" featured "SNL" legend Bill Murray to list his top 10 anchors, none of whom were the man next to him, Colin Jost.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And there was no shortage of A-list actors.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The musical performances were just as star studded, headlined by \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2  with a Beatles throwback.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Adam Sandler sang a heartfelt tribute to the people who keep "SNL" going.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And in a room packed full of stars, the biggest among them was "SNL" creator and executive producer, Lorne Michaels.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Feinberg is the chief TV critic for "The Hollywood Reporter."\par \par \uc2 DANIEL FEINBERG ("The Hollywood Reporter" chief TV critic): That this is the thing that is still going on after 50 years in an industry in which nothing has that kind of longevity, and I don`t think it would have that kind of longevity if it weren`t for Lorne.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know everybody --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If it weren`t for Lorne.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh. You know, the thing is, when you look at the history of "Saturday Night Live," I don`t remember stuff that doesn`t work --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- didn`t work like. I only remember the stuff that did work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. And how that show has become such a cultural touch point for so many people. I mean, 50 years means that almost everyone in your life --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- will know a certain skit or certain sketch as they called them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Isn`t that special?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Isn`t that special. Cheeseburger, cheeseburger, cheeseburger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You look marvelous.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly. Tony, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know that one, but okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You don`t know that one? Oh my God. That was one of the great ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it wasn`t enough because Friday night, they had a huge concert where they went over the history, the music that was known as "Saturday Night Live." Everybody said it was a dream to play there. One of my favorite moments was Cher.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh yes. Everybody loves seeing Cher.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Saturday Night Live"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cher walked -- looked at her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cher is 78.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How old?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seventy-eight.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Seventy-eight. Look at her. Looking amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And then when they asked her yesterday, Cher, you got any questions? She goes, no, but I have a lot of answers.\par \par \uc2 Even Steven Spielberg was like, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never disappoints.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh so good. So good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo. Kudos to "Saturday Night Live."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Honestly, you guys are so freakin` fine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo. Fifty years. gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. Will we be here?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was just thinking, Gayle. I don`t want to be a Debbie Downer, but I am going to predict no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead, we will be here, though, in the next block.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As we hear a story -- this is a tough one, actually -- we`re going to take a turn here. A woman who is talking about baby products through her own prism, one mother`s effort to make them safer after tragedy in her life. You`re not going to want to miss this. Stick around. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next story looks at baby products that critics say are not properly tested before they go on sale and then they take too long to recall if they`re found to be dangerous, not good.\par \par \uc2 Child and baby product recalls recently hit an all-time high. One study found more than 84 million items, think about that number for just a second, were recalled in 2023 alone.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste reports on a mom, who spent nearly a decade fighting for justice she says, for her baby.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN CONNOLLY (Mom who lost child due to faulty baby product): I mean, he was just -- he was perfect.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: In 2015, Kiersten Connolly gave birth to healthy twins, Jameson and Dorothy and says, with the help of \uc2 Fisher-Price\uc2  rock `n Play, she and her fiance James Hatch were actually getting sleep.\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN CONNOLLY: It was sleep saving, like people said it was.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: But on May 16, 2015, Hatch put the infants to sleep in their rocking Rock `n Plays. A few hours later --\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN CONNOLLY: I just -- I heard James scream out. I don`t know, I think I just knew.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Her son, Jameson had stopped breathing in his sleep. He was only six weeks old.\par \par \uc2 KIRSTEN CONNOLLY: It just -- it took my life. It took -- it took everything from me.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: What were you told was the cause of Jameson`s death?\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN CONNOLLY: We waited months, over a year to be told nothing. Nothing. That`s what it said on his death certificate, "undetermined."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It wasn`t until 2019, four years after Jameson`s death, that she says she stumbled upon startling news. \uc2 Fisher-Price\uc2  announced a recall of its Rock `n Play incline sleeper, the same product Jameson died in.\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN CONNOLLY: The anger I felt was -- it was -- it was -- my whole body was palpable. I could not believe.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: According to the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2 `s records, the product has been tied to at least 100 infant deaths since 2012. A study it commissioned found the angle of the incline increase the risk of asphyxiation and suffocation.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the recall, a congressional investigation concluded that \uc2 Fisher-Price\uc2  failed to ensure the Rock `n Play was safe before bringing it to market, and found \uc2 Fisher-Price\uc2  was aware of injuries and deaths in the Rock `n Play as early as 2012.\par \par \uc2 The report also found even after the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  urged the company to disclose safety concerns in 2018, it still took more than a year for \uc2 Fisher-Price\uc2  to recall the product.\par \par \uc2 Mattel\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Fisher-Price\uc2 , did not respond to our request for comment.\par \par \uc2 REGINA CALCATERRA (Attorney): They knew for years that infants were dying and not disclosing it, and during that time, many other infants died.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Regina Calcaterra represents seven families whose babies died in Rock `n Plays, including Connolly. Last year, \uc2 Mattel\uc2  settled with several of the families, but got Connolly`s case dismissed arguing in court that New York`s two-year statute of limitations had passed and she needed to file her case before May 2017, that`s two years before the recall of their product.\par \par \uc2 REGINA CALCATERRA: They`re actually weaponizing the statute limitations. For years, they hid the fact that babies were dying. What they were doing was pushing it down the line. So by the time the recall actually happened, the infants that died early on were not able to file a case because it didn`t fall within the statute limitations.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Delay denies justice.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Connecticut Senator Richard Blumenthal blames a law that requires the \uc2 CPSC\uc2 , the government agency whose job is to evaluate products for safety, to get permission from a manufacturer like \uc2 Mattel\uc2 , in this case, before it is allowed to warn the public of possible dangers, often creating that lag time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): The agency responsible for protecting your children should be unleashed to warn and to recall much more quickly.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Is anyone policing the safety of baby products before they hit shelves?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): There`s really no effective oversight of baby products before they`re used by your infant.\par \par \uc2 Often, safety issues arise, sometimes causing death and years pass before there`s any effective action. This system is broken.\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN CONNOLLY: Say hi.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Connolly says she`s found strength for her two living children, but her loss is immeasurable. Two years after her son`s death, her fiance took his own life, she says, consumed with grief.\par \par \uc2 Connolly is appealing her case.\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN CONNOLLY: I will never stop this fight and the pursuit to bring justice to the both of them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: In a previous report, \uc2 Mattel\uc2  did respond to the congressional investigation, telling CBS News it disagrees with significant parts of the findings. \uc2 Mattel\uc2  maintains the Rock `n Play had extensive research and testing and met or exceeded all applicable regulatory standards.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Connolly lost her appeal, but says she won`t give up and hopes her story saves lives.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s double tragedy for her. I`m so glad that she is talking about it. It`s just really hard to hear that \uc2 Mattel\uc2  apparently knew that the Sleep `n Play was dangerous and still allowed it to be sold.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The system isn`t really set up to protect babies` parents. Safety experts say, if you`re a parent, go to the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  website, check for recalls and warnings, especially if you`re planning to buy on a second hand marketplace where a lot of these recalled products can circulate for years and parents just don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what you should do if you`re concerned, go and check on the website?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Any product, baby products, anything you want to buy, check that website. I looked last night. It`s astonishing, all the recalls and warnings that are there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought Tony said it well during the break, that here`s a mom who did everything right and still had such a tragic loss.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s hard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really unbearable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can`t remember going to wake up my kids from nap time at any point with those first two years without feeling the terror of like, are they breathing? Okay, they are.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  says put your baby to sleep lying flat on its back, nothing in the crib.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, right now it is 7:44, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up for you in our eight o`clock hour, including an update from "Beg-Knows America" on one of our favorite stories. You know this story.\par \par \uc2 Trooper the Dog`s incredible journey, now David Begnaud brings us a new chapter in Trooper`s, inspiring story of survival.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ll meet actor Brendan Sklenar. He`s coming back from the hit show "1923," a "Yellowstone" prequel. He`ll be here to give us some hints about what to expect from the final season.\par \par \uc2 I`m saying hints in finger quotes. You know, they ain`t giving no hints.\par \par \uc2 All of that coming up in our next hour, but first Klaus and I are heading over the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad is getting ready.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Walking on Sunshine," one of your favorite songs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Team USA winning the Cup over the weekend, best nation for hockey. How about that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. We are also talking All-Star Weekend.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All-Star, yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Ancestry. Discover, preserve, share.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Perfect introduction to "What to Watch" this Monday, because we`re talking the All-Star Weekend. First time in 20 years, folks, LeBron James missed out on \uc2 NBA\uc2  All-Star action.\par \par \uc2 The 40-year-old sat out last night`s game in San Francisco because of a foot injury. James was supposed to play in the first ever All-Star tournament where Steph Curry was named MVP for leading Shaquille O`Neal`s team to victory.\par \par \uc2 So no LeBron.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We want his foot better.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know he`s feeling better, hopefully.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, and the fan favorite dunk contest.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dunk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mac McClung proved once again, that some -- I can`t believe they put this in this -- that some White men can jump.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s from a movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s true. That`s true.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 NBA\uc2 /TNT Sports)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Spike.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Spike is like, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at this leap. Look at this leap.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is insane.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Insane.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What? Boom.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, if this were 1994, I`d be pulling mom`s key underneath the basket, lowering it to eight feet trying this exact dunk in the driveway.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at that. That was amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is fun to watch. He is fun to watch.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is his third. This is a threepeat, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Three times in a row that he`s been able to pull this off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, and an 18-year-old fan pulled off a big money shot against a pro. Jaren Barajas had to sink one three-pointer before Milwaukee`s Damian Lillard sunk three to win a hundred grand.\par \par \uc2 The college freshman was struggling, but lucky for him, so was Lillard, and then just as time ran out, this happened.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 NBA\uc2 /TNT Sports)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The crowd goes wild. Barajas said he plans to spend the bucket of cash on college -- good move -- and his family, also a good move.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, you say bucket of cash. Do we have the picture? It is an actual bucket of cash.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. Can we show that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is laying in it like this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still one of the best events in the --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in a moment, Brandon Sklenar are from "1923" talking about the season two. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Hey y`all, it`s David Begnaud.\par \par \uc2 Today on "Beg-Knows America" we have got an update to a story that captured the nation`s heart.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember the incredible journey of Trooper the Dog. He was abandoned then adopted by devoted parents? Well, wait until you hear the new twist in this saga of love, survival, and hope.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil and that`s Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this eight o`clock hour, "Beg-Knows America" brings us an update on Trooper the Dog. Remember him? David shares a very inspiring new chapter for the pup who has already faced so much own on his journey to a loving home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus, a new study shows growing amounts of micro plastic in our brains equivalent to about the size of a plastic spoon. Can`t be good. We`re going to talk to Dr. Celine Gounder.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And actor Brandon Sklenar, who plays a man who`s been through a lot on the hit show "1923," we`ll ask him, what`s next in the upcoming final season.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are going to begin this hour with the latest on the extreme weather in the South. You`ve heard about this. This includes Kentucky, where flash flooding left at least nine people dead there. At least 16,000 people are still without power at this hour. President Trump has approved a Disaster Declaration for the state.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by Kentucky`s Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 . Governor Beshear, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 I know it`s been about 48 hours for your state and your people and you yourself. What can you tell us about the latest rescue and recovery efforts? What`s happening right now?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Well, thank you for having me. It is a tough morning in Kentucky, and for many of our surrounding states. We are still in the search and rescue mode. Thus far, we can confirm we`ve lost nine Kentuckians, but I believe we`ve lost at least two more. So that`s 11 Kentucky families that are hurting this morning, along with hundreds of people who aren`t in their homes. We have about 130 in just one of our state parks.\par \par \uc2 We still have some rescue operations that will need to occur today, potentially evacuating an apartment complex. So while the flash flooding is done, the rivers not haven`t all crested. Some flooding is going to continue, especially in parts of Eastern Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 And then Tuesday night to Wednesday, we`re going to get hit by another snow storm. But we`re tough, and we`re out there working hard.\par \par \uc2 We`re grateful for other states that have sent their search and rescue teams, grateful for FEMA`s help. So we`re working as hard as we can to save as many lives as we can, and then to get people to a safe, dry place as we get the next steps for their recovery in front of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, the pictures tell a very terrifying story in your state. We saw the flash flooding. We saw the high winds.\par \par \uc2 Your big concern, top priority right now is what?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): It is making sure that we`ve accounted for everyone. That means some people who can`t get out of their neighborhoods, we need to make sure we get them to safety.\par \par \uc2 It means any additional nursing homes we`ve already evacuated two, where people need to be safe; making sure people are going to have a dry place to stay for the next couple of days as they dig out, and then it will be turning to the rebuilding effort.\par \par \uc2 But we are still in an emergency situation here in Kentucky. Definitely concerned about our power outages because it`s going to get really, really cold. So in many ways, this is one of the biggest flooding events we`ve had in some areas just since 2022, because we`ve been hit hard in other areas, one of the biggest floods ever, and it`s basically happening in the middle of multiple snowstorms.\par \par \uc2 So we always think we`ve seen it all, and then we get hit by something else, but thank you to everybody out there who`s pouring in resources, who is giving to the Team Kentucky Storm Relief Fund, or who is sending teams to ultimately help our people.\par \par \uc2 We`re grateful for the nation`s help.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, hey, Governor, it`s Tony.\par \par \uc2 We`re so sorry to hear about the loss in your state this morning, and happy to hear about the rescues. We hope they continue. We know your crews, your first responders are out there working right now as we speak. We hope they`re safe, and we hope they get where they need to be.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned FEMA earlier and the Disaster Declaration. It sounds like you have the resources you need right now. I am curious big picture with President Trump proposing eliminating FEMA, if you think your state will have the resources it needs, not only for this storm, but going forward, if there is no FEMA.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Yes, I`ve been outspoken on the need for FEMA. We`ve been through the worst tornadoes in our lifetime. In 2021, it killed 81 Kentuckians. We`ve been through the worst flooding of our lifetime, worse than what you`re seeing right now in 2022.\par \par \uc2 We have suffered loss, and we have suffered damage at a scale that I`m not sure a single generation of Kentuckians have ever seen, and FEMA has been there.\par \par \uc2 Does FEMA need to be fixed in many ways? Yes, but their emergency response, how they surge resources in the search and rescue phase is critically important.\par \par \uc2 Now where we need to see change is on the individual assistance side, especially how FEMA communicates with individuals who need help, but that can and should be fixed.\par \par \uc2 But let me give credit to the Trump administration. We have seen FEMA operating just like they did in the previous storms and tornadoes we faced, we are getting full cooperation right now, and our declaration got turned around by the president in near record time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So before you go, Governor, what would help you most at this time? People are listening to you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Well, we need -- we need help. We need help, especially for our people that will have lost just about everything, and while we believe we`ll get help from FEMA, there are some significant limits there.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve set up a fund, the Team Kentucky Storm Relief Fund, where people want to help and chip in every bit of this will go to survivors. We`ll handle all the overhead costs. We`ve done it now several times.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing is, pray for our people. Call the people you know in Kentucky, hug on your families at home. You know, life is short, our job is to do good things and to be kind to each other in every time we`ve faced one of these.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve come together as a people, but the rest of the United States has turned out to help us and so again, thank you to everybody who is chipping in and helping us in yet another time in need.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Governor, putting it all in perspective for us and showing us the best of the US. Thank you very much for spending some time with us this morning. We wish those first responders the absolute best in your state as well.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, moving on, we all probably remember the story of Trooper the Dog from one disaster to another. This was Hurricane Milton, when trooper was left out there to who knows what was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, "Beg-Knows America" -- David Begnaud shows us how Trooper faced tough new challenges after being saved in Milton and deep in the bonds with his adopted family. This is a story of hope, people.\par \par \uc2 Stick around for it. It`s 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "1923"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is Brandon Sklenar in the season two trailer for the hit TV drama "1923." Ahead, he`ll tell us about the final season and what it was like to act alongside legends Helen Mirren and Harrison Ford.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a Monday. That means it`s "Beg-Knows America" where we find the heart in every story.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, David Begnaud brings us an update today on something that caught our attention, really caught the whole nation`s attention. You may remember it.\par \par \uc2 Trooper the Dog: This story, he was abandoned as Hurricane Milton was making landfall in Florida. Back in October, David traveled there to meet Trooper, and then two months later, David returned as Trooper was settling in with his new family.\par \par \uc2 But shortly after our stories aired, things changed once again for Trooper. So David shows us now how Trooper is living up to his name.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARLA SPINA (Adopted Trooper): Look at this. This is Trooper Dooper.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You are looking at the moment when Trooper the Dog met his forever family.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA (Adopted Trooper): We`re going to be best friends, I can tell you now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Including his new sister, Dallas. The photo of their initial meeting seemed to say it all. It looked like love at first sight.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: Trooper.\par \par \uc2 CARLA SPINA: Hey, it`s Trooper.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: Here is your brother.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And that is where our last story on Trooper ended, at Frank and Carla Spina`s house in Florida, where he seemed like he was going to have a great life.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: Now, he loves you. She gives you kisses.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Two months after our last story aired, we checked back in with the Spinas to see how Trooper was doing, assuming not much had happened.\par \par \uc2 Well, we were shocked by what we found out.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: About maybe two weeks after the original segment aired, and we had been noticing since we adopted him that he had two dime-sized lumps on his left flank. The doctor immediately said, oh no, these are mast cell tumors. These are cancer.\par \par \uc2 So we were in complete meltdown.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Trooper had to have surgery to remove the cancer, then came the painstaking wait for the results on whether it was successful.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: These are our children. We don`t have children. These are our children. And then we got that good news and then we were just so relieved.\par \par \uc2 When we left the doctor`s office that day after he got his stitches out, the doctor said he`s done. He`s cancer-free. We were literally in tears, just walking to the car. Happy tears.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Finally, Trooper`s happy ending, right? Well, no, not quite yet.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: He was out walking, and he just started putting his head down. So I went under him, and he vomited, and a piece of rubber came out of his mouth.\par \par \uc2 So this is actually a freezer bag. This freezer bag weighs 2.1 pounds. It`s a combination of about 110 or 120 pieces of garbage. Could you imagine how starved he had to be to ingest a piece of metal or rubber, pieces of rubber that are the size of quarters.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Our little buddy needed a life-saving procedure. It took vets in Miami more than four hours to remove the metal and the rubber from his stomach.\par \par \uc2 CARLA SPINA: Where are you going, guys?\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: We have a very special bond, me and him, and he`s my best friend, and I think I`m his best friend. I think my wife would agree that I would rather give my life, that she would give her life to save his life.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And we don`t doubt that for one second, because Frank, a criminal defense attorney, is not just fighting for Trooper`s health. He and Carla are also fighting for justice for the abuse they allege Trooper had to endure before he came to them.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: I reached out to the Hillsborough State`s Attorney`s Office. This is their victim witness. This victim witness will be in every single hearing for that gentleman who did this.\par \par \uc2 (TROOPER barking)\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t blame you.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: Every single date, every single hearing, a trial, a sentencing, we will be there, and he`s going to have to look at this dog and account for what he did to him.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Trooper, is more than okay now, or so we think. He is happy and looks to be loving life.\par \par \uc2 FRANK SPINA: He is definitely Daddy`s Boy, right? Daddy -- I love you, too. I love you, I love you, I love you, I love you. I love you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Frank and Carla, this is just for the two of you. Y`all have been the best humans to come into Trooper`s life. Not my opinion, that`s the opinion of the vet who treated him and says y`all have been such angels for him and so we thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gosh, we didn`t hear much from Carla. I know she`s fantastic, but Frank, boy, am I smitten with him.\par \par \uc2 So we do think the Trooper is now going to be okay. Where did he get all that garbage from, David? Where did that come from that he was eating?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Well, Gayle, the vet said, look, his breed, the bull terrier, is known to get into everything, but the vet believes there`s a chance that he was left to live outside, and he probably out of boredom consumed everything he could, or potentially out of starvation ate everything he could find.\par \par \uc2 His previous owner, by the way, a 23-year-old man is facing criminal charges in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good. Good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, amazing --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I love Frank saying they`re going to be there for every court case. I love that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And amazing work by those vets who did the surgery, four hours of work to take all of that out of Trooper`s belly so he`s okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What a beautiful story, David, and beautiful humans that are taking care of Trooper and his sister.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, David, you`re a beautiful human as well, and I want to tell our viewers, thank you very much, David.\par \par \uc2 If you`ve got a story for David, you can reach him and his team at DearDavid@CBSNews.com or contact him directly on social media.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in a moment, new research has found a surprising amount of tiny plastic in our brains. I knew something was wrong with me, and this is it. We`re going to talk to Dr. Celine Gounder about how it gets there all of that plastic and what we can do about it. I have a plastic spoon in our brain.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In today`s "Health Watch," a new study has found an alarming amount of plastic in our brains.\par \par \uc2 Researchers at the \uc2 University of New Mexico\uc2  say they found the amount of plastic in the brain is estimated to be the equivalent of a standard plastic spoon. This standard plastic spoon.\par \par \uc2 CBS News Medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder joins us once again to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Celine --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m not relieved it`s not a big spoon, it`s only a standard spoon. My gosh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So before we even break down, what is potentially damaging us, what exactly are micro plastics? Describe it for us.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So when plastic breaks down, it breaks down to smaller and smaller components. Microplastics are half a centimeter or less, so still pretty significant in size.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So how are they getting into our bodies? And the question is, why is it concerning? I don`t have to have a medical degree to know that it is pretty concerning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I want to know how they`re getting into our bodies.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you saying, if we eat off of the spoon, we`re getting microplastics?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: From the spoon, you might be getting what we call nano plastics, which are even smaller in size. You would see them under the size of -- or under a microscope. But what`s happening is, as the plastics break down, they`re getting into the ground water. They`re getting absorbed by plants, taken in by animals.\par \par \uc2 And so when we drink water, when we eat plants, when we eat animals, we are taking in those plastics as well. We`re finding them all over the body, not just the brain, but the blood, the lungs, the heart, the arteries, the gut, the liver, the kidneys, breast milk, the placenta, even the testes and penis. So like everywhere in the body.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what you`re telling me my brain is 99 percent brain and basically one percent plastic spoon?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: We are finding plastic about the amount of a plastic spoon in the brain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how do you reduce it? I was watching something on TV the other day, eating out of a black plastic container.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, or drinking from plastic water bottles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or drinking from plastic water bottles, and they were saying, don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But is it hurting us? I mean, is there evidence that we`re actually being hurt?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, there are concerns that this could be causing any number of conditions. Could it be increasing in the risk of stroke or heart attack? Could it be increasing the risk of cancer, inflammatory bowel disease, infertility? So there are any number of things, preterm birth. The research is very much in its infancy, but there are some important questions being asked.\par \par \uc2 As to how to prevent or reduce our exposure, switch from plastic bottles to stainless steel or glass bottles. Do not reheat your food in contact with plastic. That is really important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is the big one.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So move it to a glass dish or your plate --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do that all the time.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, not in plastic.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, switch out your cutting boards. Don`t use plastic cutting boards. Use either wood or stone type.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I did that one, too. Yes. Wow.\par \par \uc2 Okay, Dr. Celine Gounder, some really important information. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead "Talk of the Table" is coming up next, including your local news, that`s up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Terrifying.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always like that, "Wow. I feel good."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You nailed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is how I feel, too.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS, time to bring you some of the stories that we call "Talk of the Table." Why? Because it`s a table and we be talking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We be talking and you are in pole position, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, so my "Talk of the Table." is about Serena Williams` Super Bowl appearance --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Loved it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- and the controversial dance --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Loved it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- that people are still talking about.\par \par \uc2 So of course, we remember the tennis legend broke out what is known as the Crip Walk during Kendrick Lamar halftime show. Now the dance is associated with the street gang, the Crips, but it did not start with them.\par \par \uc2 Williams did get some online backlash, like she did after her 2012 Olympic celebration, where she also did the Crip Walk. But you guys know, I`m a history buff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I wanted to look at the actual history of the dance. And I think I`m going to blow your minds here.\par \par \uc2 One of the earliest videos of that dance --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gene Kelly?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Boarding House Blues"/All-American News)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, not. Gene Kelly. It comes from this man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh what?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is Henry "Crip" Heard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And one arm in the 1948 film, "Boarding House Blues." He was a Harlem dance legend. He was already a Harlem dance legend when he lost one leg and an arm in a car accident.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He mostly appeared in Black theaters, in vaudeville, and nightclubs during the 1940s and 50s.\par \par \uc2 What I wanted to highlight is way back in 1948, Crip took that name, that was a derogatory term, and he made -- he challenged people`s assumptions who had disabilities and people thought they were incapable of leading fulfilling lives because they had lost their limbs.\par \par \uc2 He challenged those assumptions, and he turned that term, Crip, which is a derogatory term, into his name and made it his stage name.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that is where that dance originated. It did not originate with the gang in Los Angeles, as some people may say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I wanted to highlight that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m glad you did, because this is what people tell me, I always learn something new on your show. Today, I am learning something new on my show.\par \par \uc2 I`ve never heard of him --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- or the origin of that dance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Me, neither. Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Henry "Crip" Heard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done, Vlad. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" is last night`s British Academy Film Awards, the BAFTAs in London. It`s sort of the equivalent of our Oscars, where the winner of Best Film added a little suspense to the Oscars next month.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2025 EE BAFTA Film Awards/BBC One)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. The Vatican thriller, "Conclave" won the top award last night. Last month, "The Brutalist" was named Best Motion Picture Drama at the Golden Globes, and we`ll have to wait and see what Academy Award voters will choose for the Best Picture.\par \par \uc2 Adrien Brody won the Best Actor BAFTA for his role in "The Brutalist." He also won the Golden Globe, beating out Timothee Chalamet, Coleman Domingo, Ralph Fiennes, Hugh Grant, and Sebastian Stan.\par \par \uc2 Now, breakthrough star, this is big.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is huge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mikey Madison won, lead actress for the hit movie "Anora." I love that movie --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- "Anora" and loved her. Remember, she was here, guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She was here. She was also -- the movie is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all fell in love with her. She`s also nominated for an Oscar. So the whole big thing why everyone`s abuzz, to quote you, "What does this portend?"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. What does it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because everybody thinks that Demi Moore is a lock for her role in "The Substance."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s knock out in that, too, and now Mikey Madison coming in, winning for Best --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Kevin Frazier told me after the Golden Globes, he was like, book it now. Demi Moore, right? And now, what is going to happen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It just got a little interesting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We love them both.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also for Best Picture? Because "Emilia Perez" was in the running for that. There`s a little kerfuffle surrounding that, \uc2 Google\uc2 , and find out --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little bit. Yes, \uc2 Google\uc2  that story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A little bit of a kerfuffle. So, it would be interesting to see how this turns out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, from world of the arts to the world of law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Apparently, in Peru, elite drug task forces sometimes dress up in disguises to get people to answer the door and I`ve got to roll this video of a guy dressed as a giant rodent.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from National Police of Peru)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is a capybara in South America. The term there, it looks like a bear, and he goes right in on the bust.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then, he is tackling a guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a cop?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He has a little bit of a handcuff.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is real?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is handcuffing a guy, maybe --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What is going through that drug guy`s head? Like, he is being handcuffed by a big stuffed bear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you might have noticed the heart on the capybara`s backpack.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a Valentine`s Day bust on top of everything else. And then this elite task force, they did a whole video with the guy in the costume standing there next to him. They`re very proud of this operation here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at how hard --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Book him boys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at the heart, Vlad. Well, that`s one way to catch them off guard.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s so great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is. You know, very effective.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very effective.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Anything to make the bust.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead, we`re going to get a sneak peek at season two of the premier -- season two premiere of "1923," one of my favorite shows on anything, popular Paramount+ prequel to "Yellowstone." It costars Brandon Sklenar. He is here to tell us all the storylines to watch, but first time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest, Brandon Sklenar costars in the hit Paramount+ show "1923." Tony has been raving about all morning, he says, it`s one of my favorite shows.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "1923"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a prequel to the beloved series "Yellowstone."\par \par \uc2 So 1923 follows the Dutton family as they fight to keep their cattle ranch in Montana against very harsh conditions and ruthless rivals, and ruthless is a word.\par \par \uc2 So Sklenar plays Spencer Dutton. We love his character, a Big Game -- look at him -- a big game hunter racing to get back to the ranch to help protect the family`s legacy.\par \par \uc2 In this clip, it`s from the season two premiere, Spencer weighs what he needs to do to get back home. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "1923"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we were saying, he is so freaking cool.\par \par \uc2 Brandon goes "cooler than I am." I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re very happy to say that Brandon joins us in the studio.\par \par \uc2 Before you came, we were all abuzz because your character has been through so much (bleep) stuff. Tony, give the quick rundown on what`s the backstory.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, even attacked by wild animals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve been in an unbelievable shipwreck.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Falsely arrested.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is all from season one. Falsely arrested, put on a labor gang, and you still haven`t made it back to America. The whole first season.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, separated from this life.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Separated the whole time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now the word is that season two, if we can believe this, is even darker than season one --\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR ("1923" actor): It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you like this, tell us why?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes. Well, I mean, I think it`s like season one, Taylor sort of foreshadowed what was possible.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Taylor Sheridan, like the writer, creator of the series.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes, absolutely. And he, you know, you`re kind of giving glimpses into what Spencer is capable of, and what demons he`s sort of running from, and what he`s fighting for.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "1923"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: And I think he really lets the cat out of the bag in season two in a big way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know -- you know, what else I thought was interesting is you already know how this is going to end. Sometimes people come and they legit don`t know how this is going to end, but you do know.\par \par \uc2 And you say the season finale was one of the best things that you`ve ever read.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That it moved you how?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: I mean, I couldn`t even prep it without, you know, tissues in my hand.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what happens? No, no.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: I wish I could tell you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: It`s so beautiful. I mean, the way that he wraps up the second season, and it`s just profoundly beautiful, and the way he ties in the love story with Spencer`s journey and just the culmination of all those arcs, I mean, he does in such a masterful way.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he really writes what he believes in, and you can see just the way he weaves their dedication to the family and how much resiliency they have and their integrity and this like undying love that Spencer and Alex have, and how they just literally do anything. They will go through anything and traverse anything to find each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So we`ve got to figure they`ve got to get back together, but it seems like it`s going to be a bumpy ride.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It doesn`t seem like she`s going to have an easy passage, as you did, neither --\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- to America and then she`s going to get to Montana.\par \par \uc2 All right, but your parents, Harrison Ford and Helen Mirren --\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: And Uncle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Uncle, right? Of course, and Uncle, they are facing the end of the Dutton ranch. They`ve got terrible enemies, and you`re going to arrive at some point, we think, to vanquish these enemies.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you at any point share the screen with Harrison and Helen?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: You know, I can`t confirm nor deny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ugh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ugh.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: If I did, it would probably be pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It would be pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, if you can`t tell us if you shared the screen with them, what`s it like working with them? What are you as an actor, able to glean from these two icons and legends?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: I mean just -- I mean being able to go on set and watch them work, it is so humbling because, I mean the both of them, they`re living icons, and I use that word sparingly, and there`s so few people that have ever been a part of the film world who have accomplished what they`ve accomplished, and the breadth of their work is insane, and they`re still just actors at the end of the day, and they`re the kind -- I mean, you guys have had both of them before.\par \par \uc2 They`re just so sweet and so kind, and they`re human beings before all else, and it`s a testament to why they`ve lasted as long as they`ve had, and people adore them to this day. And yes, they`re just this -- you know, most humble, sweetest people you could ask for.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, let`s talk about you in this role, because when we were chatting in the greenroom, you said that this role was kind of a role you were born to play. So talk a little bit about your background in martial arts and why you would say this is just perfect for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also, he`s a war veteran dealing with trauma.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wonder how you get to duplicate that as well.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Well, yes, I mean, I just grew up super blue collar background in New Jersey, and my dad was a carpenter, my mom was a hairdresser, and kind of had a rough and tumble upbringing, and I`m an outdoorsy guy, and I could ride horses.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it is like kind of all of these things lined up. And, yes, he has a -- Spencer has like a physicality to him, that he uses his body and he`s not afraid to take it to extreme places and I can relate to that for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re also, the last time, you were talking about the movie, "It Ends With Us," which has turned into a big, major story between Blake and Justin.\par \par \uc2 I`m wondering how you`re -- what you`re thinking about that, processing it, navigating it, it doesn`t involve you, but because you`re in the cast, I`m wondering what you`re thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes, I mean, I just -- I just want people to remember why we made the movie in the first place, and what it stands for, and just keeping the focus on that like that, that movie meant so much to me, and I have someone very, very close to me in my life who`s gone through what Lily is going through for a long time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: And I`ve been on the frontlines, helping her navigate that space, so that movie meant a lot to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: And it means a lot to her, this person in my life, and it was one of the reasons that that me doing that film gave her the strength to change her life, so like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say, focus on that.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes, it means a lot to me. So it`s unfortunate that things get taken away from what the ethos of that thing is, and it gets convoluted and I just want to remind people of why that thing is good in the first place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would imagine at this point, you`ve been in it, you said, I`m not in it. But are you Team Blake or Team Justin?\par \par \uc2 He said, I am sticking with Gayle.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: I am Team "It Ends With Us."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And he is Team Taylor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and he is Team Taylor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Team Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s got a little nick on his knuckle there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. No. Congratulations. I didn`t expect you to -- I`m just playing -- messing with you.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SKLENAR: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`re glad you`re here and continue --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is like, "yes, Gayle, yes."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is trained in Martial Arts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s real funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Gayle, you`ve got jokes. Thank you, Brandon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Brandon Sklenar, you are fantastic in this "1923" season two episode. You can watch the premiere this Sunday, on Paramount+, our parent company, we are proud to say, it`s a great series. Start from beginning if you haven`t caught up.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Our next guest says we can all learn wisdom in the unlikeliest of places, even your neighborhood barbershop.\par \par \uc2 Keenan Jones is an educator based in Minnesota, where he worked as an elementary school teacher. He is also the founder of the educational nonprofit, Literacy for Freedom.\par \par \uc2 Jones` debut picture book is called "Saturday Morning at the Shop." It`s about a young boy who visits his local barbershop and discovers the important role it plays in the Black community and Black culture.\par \par \uc2 And Keenan Jones, we`re happy to say, joins us now. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES ("Saturday Morning at the Shop" author): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I love this book.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is such a remarkable book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you write that the barbershop is a place to "Hear wisdom, feel the joy, and share the love." Tell me about that?\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Well, you know, the barbershop, we always say it`s more than a haircut, it is an experience.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Because you have so many people from the community coming in. You`ve got your local police officers, teachers, coaches, all coming in not only to get a haircut, but just celebrating all of the folks in the community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know they talk a lot of smack. There`s a lot of love.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is a lot of -- but I only know because I used to take favorite son, Will, when he was a little boy, and you -- just the conversation I thought was really important.\par \par \uc2 You say the shop is our classroom, lessons about Black leaders, the latest hip-hop buzz. But I like this, hair is a canvas, clippers are the brush creating walking pieces of art on a Saturday morning at the shop.\par \par \uc2 What`s fascinating to me about this? I just assume, well, you must have sons, but you have three daughters.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: I`ve got three daughters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what is the back story and why you wanted to write a book about the barbershop?\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Well, you know, I`m from the Twin Cities, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Minneapolis, yes.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Around 2020, we had some things going on, right? It was a lot of social unrest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: The George Floyd situation and I mentored young Black boys at the time, and a lot of them whom I met, they were very sad, talking about like, why does America treat us this way? Why do we think -- why do they think that we`re just other than anything, other than great? And so that sat with me.\par \par \uc2 I already had these ideas about wanting to write a book, but I didn`t know what it was. And at the same time, the barbershops were closed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that`s nice.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: So I wanted to create something that uplifted young Black men and boys. I couldn`t get into the barbershop, so when I got into the barbershop, all of the Black men were back into the barbershop, and I just missed that feeling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ah.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: And I sat in the chair, and it was like, "This is it. I`ve got to write this book."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Can I ask you really quick, sorry, Tony. Can I ask just really quickly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There is a moment in the book where the protagonist looks at the wall and he sees pictures of Biggie Smalls.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr., and Muhammad Ali.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain to us and to our audience, what`s the connected thread of those individuals and why you chose to depict them?\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Absolutely. Well, you know, I think about growing up, you know, I learned about so many Black leaders and all of their contributions to the country. But most importantly for me, you know, those are folks that really inspired me, you know, not only from hip-hop, right? But to folks who really paved the way to be where I am today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , yes.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Yes, \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Malcolm X, you know, I learned about them, and so it was just -- I had to figure out how to weave them in. But then sometimes at the barbershop, you may look on the walls and you may see the same thing, right? Black leaders, and it`s just to inspire the young people and the folks coming in that, you know, we can do anything that we put our minds to.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, I joked when you came in that you`ve got to write a sequel about the beauty parlor, and you said, everyone says that, well, it`s because it`s a good idea.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that -- are you thinking about it?\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Yes. I`ve got some -- I`ve got some ideas for them. But you know, that`s the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you hope girls will get out of this book?\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: Well, you know, it`s funny that I went to a school visit and I talked about the barbershop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Less than 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN JONES: And a lot of the young ladies also connected with the book. And just said, hey, you know, sometimes I go with my sibling to go to the barbershop as well, so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Y es.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved it, Keenan. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you, Keenan Jones. "Saturday Morning at the Shop" is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. Have a great Presidents` Day holiday. It is a holiday.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Your favorite morning show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020250217el2h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For February 6, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Strassmann, Jonathan Vigliotti, Scott MacFarlane, Lonnie  Quinn, Lilia Luciano, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Uncle Sam does not want you, not anymore.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That is the message President Trump is sending millions of federal workers. Mr. Trump, who spent years firing people on a TV show, is now doing it for real.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: He is firing federal government employees or offering buyouts. The White House says tens of thousands have accepted. But labor unions are suing to stop the buyout. And, today, a judge paused it temporarily.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The president is also targeting thousands of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents and other bureau employees for possible firing, those who were involved in the investigation of the January 6 riot at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Today, another judge blocked the Justice Department from releasing a list of their names.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Scott MacFarlane spoke with an agent on that list, who says the bureau is in turmoil.\par \par \uc2 We are protecting the agent`s identity here tonight, Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Yes, Maurice, this fear of firings is spreading inside the \uc2 FBI\uc2  among agents who try to track down terrorists, child predators and violent gangs, but who also were part of the investigation into January 6.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): The largest criminal investigation in \uc2 FBI\uc2  history has given way to potentially its largest crisis.\par \par \uc2 Do you think this is a distraction?\par \par \uc2 FBI AGENT: Absolutely. I -- personally, I have lost sleep over this.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: This agent, who helped arrest January 6 rioters, fears retaliation from new leadership of the Justice Department for speaking with CBS News.\par \par \uc2 FBI AGENT: We just have a lot of unknowns, and it feels like no one has our backs.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump allies at the Justice Department ordered the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to send a questionnaire nationwide to identify the personnel who investigated January 6 and make a list for leadership to review. The agent is one of approximately 5,000 names on that list.\par \par \uc2 What was it like filling out that questionnaire, saying what you did and when you did it?\par \par \uc2 FBI AGENT: Really demoralizing. Defeated. We all feel defeated, and for not -- and really for nothing and just...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The scrutiny and storm cloud hovering over \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees over their work on cases that yielded more than 1,000 guilty pleas and a 100 percent conviction rate in jury trials sends a chilling message, the agent warns.\par \par \uc2 FBI AGENT: Moving forward, do we pick and choose? Are we allowed to pick and choose what we feel comfortable, based off our...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You think this might scare people off taking certain cases in the future?\par \par \uc2 FBI AGENT: Absolutely. I mean, we -- we`re all humans.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Agents who testified or signed their names to public court documents worry of retaliation from riot defendants.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: For your followers, any messages to them?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Keep the fight. Fight, fight, fight.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Or their sympathizers.\par \par \uc2 FBI AGENT: It -- almost as if these defendants that were all pardoned have been put on a pedestal of being more patriotic and more loyal to this country than the men and women who go out every single day, away from their families, and put their lives on the line.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 FBI AGENT: I really am a loss for words. It hurts. I will say that. It hurts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A Justice Department official in a message to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  earlier this week said agents who were simply following orders or carried out their duties in an ethical manner will not be subject to disciplinary issues.\par \par \uc2 But, John and Maurice, agents and their attorneys say they are not convinced.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Scott, how much of a distraction is this, all of this, to what \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents are paid to do?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A remarkable distraction, according to that agent, checking your e-mail every so often, wondering if that`s the time you`re going to get a termination notice.\par \par \uc2 There`s also this anecdote from an association representing agents. They say there was one who was pulled off the response to that plane crash at Reagan National Airport to go back and fill out the January 6 survey, then returned to the crash site.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Boy, Scott, it is so rare for an agent to speak out publicly like this.\par \par \uc2 I`m wondering, do they feel like they can ever get back to normal and just do their jobs?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The agents are concerned there`s been a fracture, Maurice, in the independence of the Department of Justice, the legendary independence of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , that that is now broken indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, Scott MacFarlane, thanks so much tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave reports, \uc2 FAA\uc2  officials pushed last summer to close one of three runways at Reagan National after two close calls. Ultimately, they kept the runway open. The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating whether airport congestion played a role in last week`s midair collision.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The \uc2 NCAA\uc2  is banning transgender women from playing on women`s teams. This follows an executive order from President Trump that calls for penalties against schools and leagues that allow transgender women to compete in women`s sports.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And the former interpreter for baseball superstar Shohei Ohtani was sentenced to nearly five years in prison for bank and tax fraud. Ippei Mizuhara has to pay back $17 million he stole from Ohtani`s bank account to cover his gambling debts and other expenses, and he has to pay another million to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Southern California Edison admitted today its equipment likely started the Hurst wildfire. That one burned a little more than a square mile and did not cause any deaths or destroy any homes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It broke out the same day as the much larger Palisades and Eaton wildfires. The cause of those fires, blamed for 29 deaths, has not been determined.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: They are 100 percent contained now, but, as Jonathan Vigliotti reports, the cleanup brings new dangers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): More than 9,000 homes and businesses were burned to the ground in Altadena. The \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  invited us to join a team sifting through the ash searching for toxic debris.\par \par \uc2 Harry Allen is the federal on-site coordinator with the \uc2 EPA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What specific materials are you looking for here?\par \par \uc2 HARRY ALLEN (\uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 ): We look for any uncombusted paints or any household chemicals, pesticides. We look for batteries, especially in electric vehicles. And other kinds of hazards, like unburned munitions, we may even remove.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The \uc2 EPA\uc2  also monitors each property for asbestos and arsenic.\par \par \uc2 This is painstaking work as teams respond to what is the costliest natural disaster in U.S. history, and now crews facing mounting public pressure to work faster than ever before.\par \par \uc2 This executive order from President Trump calls for the \uc2 EPA\uc2  to expedite the bulk removal of contaminated and general debris. The deadline for such a large-scale disaster is unprecedented. Consider that, in Lahaina, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  took more than four months to clear hazardous materials from 1,400 homes. In Southern California, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  faces clearing nearly 10 times that amount in just 30 days.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel the pressure in general to expedite this process?\par \par \uc2 HARRY ALLEN: Yes, of course. It`s a lot more to coordinate, and we have to move much more quickly than we had in other situations because of the various pressures.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It`s not just to move this toxic debris out. It`s finding a place for it to go. Last week, the waste began arriving here to be stored and processed. It`s a park 15 miles from the Altadena fire zone. The site is surrounded by four cities.\par \par \uc2 City leaders and residents say no one notified them first.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: We attended a packed town meeting, where community leaders demanded to know from the EPA`s Tara Fitzgerald whether one disaster could unleash another.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think that speed that you`re being asked to work at, it`s risky.\par \par \uc2 TARA FITZGERALD (\uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 ): We are not allowing the materials to -- to seep into the earth, which is how we -- we do know that.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOGAN (\uc2 California Department of Resources Recycling and Recovery\uc2 ): This has everything to do with the mission task and the strict timeline that \uc2 EPA\uc2  must adhere to, which is cleanup in 30 days` time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jonathan Vigliotti joins us now from Southern Oregon, where he`s on another story.\par \par \uc2 But, Jonathan, on this California story, what are your sources at the \uc2 EPA\uc2  saying about this rush?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, John, I spoke with one \uc2 EPA\uc2  official on the ground. She described this cleanup as, and this is her words, bananas.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with another former \uc2 EPA\uc2  official who also said it may be nearly impossible to meet this deadline, describing it as one ultimately about optics. We were specifically talking about the Summer 2020 Olympics. There is concern that corners could be cut in an effort to prepare the city in time for the event.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And then, Jonathan, what about the concerns about rebuilding perhaps too quickly?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, Maurice, and that`s a story that we`re following very closely, because, in the aftermath of these disasters, you had the governor and local officials coming out lifting critical fire code regulations for a swift rebuild.\par \par \uc2 That includes allowing homes to rebuild in the same exact footprint using the same materials as before. There`s been very little mention about burying power lines underground and using fire-resistant materials for the rebuild.\par \par \uc2 I actually spoke with a fire expert here in Oregon who says there is an opportunity here, if you have a plan to build back smarter and safer. He warns that, in not doing so, history could repeat itself again.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Yes, a lot to consider.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn on back- to-back winter storms in the Midwest and the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And we will have these stories as well:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m Mark Strassmann. When one county couldn`t afford pay raises, negotiators tried something else. Instead of more money, what they offered was more time, lots of time. That`s tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: I`m Lilia Luciano. It`s one of the most important waterways for the U.S. economy. We went to the Panama Canal to figure out who actually controls it.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : President Trump has a phone call scheduled tomorrow with the president of Panama to try to resolve a dispute he started over the canal.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The waterway linking the Atlantic and the Pacific is vital to the U.S. economy. Forty percent of U.S. container ships pass through every year.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano gives us a closer look at what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voice-over): We traveled to the canal zone in Panama by land and water to better understand the geopolitical battles brewing here.\par \par \uc2 Luis Rodriguez (sp?) has been driving boat tours for eight years.\par \par \uc2 What does the Panama Canal mean to you?\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Your life.\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The dispute was sparked by President Trump`s accusations that Chinese companies have taken control of the canal.\par \par \uc2 The United States is the primary user of the Panama Canal, with about 70 percent of the traffic that goes through the canal coming to or going from the United States. But a close second is China.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): We either want it back or we`re going to get something very strong or we`re going to take it back.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: What are you hearing about the U.S. wanting to take over the canal?\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: They think...\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: They think that China is operating 100 percent of the canal and you`re saying it`s false.\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The 51-mile-long canal is owned by an independent government agency in Panama, which charges each vessel passing through. There is a Hong Kong-based company that manages two of the four major ports that exist along the canal. They do not charge fees for ships going through the canal, only those that choose to use those ports.\par \par \uc2 ARISTIDES ROYO (Former President of Panama): Here is President Carter.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Former President Aristides Royo was a negotiator of the treaty that gave Panama control of the canal and he also oversaw its operation as canal minister until last year.\par \par \uc2 President Trump says that that is what`s happening, that the neutrality requirement has been violated.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT ARISTIDES ROYO: The -- the harbors, the ports, both Atlantic and Pacific, are under the jurisdiction of the Panamanian laws.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Did you see any evidence of unfair practices against U.S. ships?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT ARISTIDES ROYO: Not a single one.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Today, Panama`s president called the State Department`s claims that \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  ships can pass through the canal for free a falsehood. This comes just days after President Trump vowed to take back the canal.\par \par \uc2 How far do you think Panamanians like yourself would go to fight for the canal?\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: To the maximum.\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Defending your home, because this is your home.\par \par \uc2 (LUIS RODRIGUEZ SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And Lilia joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  actually traveled to Panama to talk about this. Whatever came -- what came of that meeting?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It was his first official visit.\par \par \uc2 One important thing was that Panama will pull out of the \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2 . We`re talking about billions of dollars in infrastructure that now the president of Panama hopes will provide an opportunity perhaps for American investors, who have not paid a whole lot of attention in the region, to go and invest.\par \par \uc2 Second thing is expand collaboration on immigration. This is a president that he says, because of his own crackdown on immigration through the Darien Gap, has reduced the number of people crossing by 90 percent when you compared last year to this year.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2 , that Chinese initiative they`re pulling out of.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Lilia Luciano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, there was some nasty weather on both sides of the country today. In Northern California, dozens of cars and trucks got stuck on Interstate 80 in a blizzard expected to dump several feet of snow on the Sierra Nevada.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And a blast of snow, ice and rain moved from the Midwest to the Northeast. Parts of New England got more than four inches of snow.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie Quinn is tracking the winter weather -- Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: OK, guys, two different storms.\par \par \uc2 On one side, this is \uc2 Bowdoin College\uc2  in Maine, picked up about a fresh four inches of snow, but on the other side of your screen, you`re looking at Tahoe. Lake Tahoe, heavenly ski area, they`re going to pick up a foot, maybe a foot-and-a-half.\par \par \uc2 Two different storms, but they`re following the same path. You can watch across the country. So it comes into the Sierra right now. That`s where it is. Then it`s going to make its way towards the Northern Plains with near- blizzard conditions. It keeps marching on its way towards Chicago by the time you get to, say, I will say it`s Saturday early, early morning.\par \par \uc2 And then, Saturday night it`s making its way into the Northeast. Philadelphia could have an icing problem out there. Anywhere north of Philadelphia, it`s a more significant snowfall than the most recent storm.\par \par \uc2 But I`m telling you, guys, the story here is this storm is opening the door to a lot more storms. The whole pattern is changing. So let`s look at that storm I just showed you. Off it goes. As it exits, there`s another storm right behind it. We will see it come into the area on Tuesday. Behind that, another one comes in on Thursday. Behind that, there`s another one on Saturday, and there`s another one brewing behind that.\par \par \uc2 Again, it`s an old-fashioned winter pattern -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Looks like rush hour out there, Lonnie. When`s the last time we saw something like this?\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Well, I showed you that picture of Bowdoin, Maine. The last time they had this pattern, seven years ago.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK. Thank you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Still ahead: a place where Thursday is the new Friday.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: On this Thursday, we take you to a corner of America where it is already Friday, the final day of a workweek that lasts only four days. Some would call that heaven. They call it Friday Harbor, Washington.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann worked his way there for tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): In the San Juan Islands, off the coast of Washington state, county workers like Brock Polda never got the pay raise they demanded. They have celebrated ever since.\par \par \uc2 As soon as you heard somebody say, how about a four-day workweek, what`d you think?\par \par \uc2 BROCK POLDA (County Worker): Lightbulb, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Polda drives a county truck.\par \par \uc2 Nadine Varsovia, his girlfriend, works in human resources.\par \par \uc2 To get paid the same amount money and work one day less, sounds like everyone`s dream.\par \par \uc2 BROCK POLDA: It really is.\par \par \uc2 NADINE VARSOVIA (County Worker): It really is. I mean, at the end of the day, money really only does so much for you. And what`s the good of having that money anyway if I can`t use it to do anything for fun?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Especially in this magnificent isolation, roughly a 90- minute ferry ride from the mainland.\par \par \uc2 San Juan County had a problem. It agreed that workers were due a raise, but the county had no money. Instead, it offered time, a three-day weekend every weekend.\par \par \uc2 BROCK POLDA: Everyone I see on Friday when I`m in the grocery store running errands, they`re just: "Must be nice. Oh, wow."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BROCK POLDA: "County guy in here." I just smile and laugh.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Forty hours pay for 32 hours` work involves a trade-off. Many county offices are closed on Fridays.\par \par \uc2 Was there griping at the beginning?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON ANDREWS (San Juan County, Washington, Parks and Fair Director): There was certainly some concern from residents in the beginning.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Brandon Andrews, the county`s parks and fair director here, helped negotiate the deal.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON ANDREWS: Do you want us to raise taxes, when things are already expensive? Do you want to cut services, or do you want us to get creative?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In the year-plus since this reduced workweek began, avoiding a big pay raise for employees saved San Juan County nearly $1 million. Job applications are up 85 percent, while the turnover rate is down 43 percent, and employees calling in sick also down by 23 percent.\par \par \uc2 KATIE FLEMING (County Worker): I`m going to pick you up after school, yes?\par \par \uc2 CHILD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KATIE FLEMING: OK.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For Katie Fleming, it`s meant more time for her family and volunteering.\par \par \uc2 KATIE FLEMING: Wow, you guys, good thing for monthly beach cleanups.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: She`s the county`s solid waste coordinator. We caught up with her cleaning storm debris along the beach.\par \par \uc2 KATIE FLEMING: Work-life balance, that is, I think, the biggest -- the biggest thing.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: If people are watching and saying, well, of course, what a great deal for you, you`re saying you`re working just as hard.\par \par \uc2 KATIE FLEMING: Just as productive, yes. I know, in my department, we`re getting the same amount of work done.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Nadine Varsovia agrees.\par \par \uc2 NADINE VARSOVIA: There`s a lot less chitchat in the office, a lot more people just kind of nose to the grindstone getting their work done.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And recruiting for open jobs on this isolated island suddenly has become a breeze.\par \par \uc2 So has your job become a whole lot easier?\par \par \uc2 NADINE VARSOVIA: Yes. Filling positions is great. Now we get our pick of the litter, if you will. What used to be, well, we will take a warm body is now, we get our choices.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: She used the extra time to finish her master`s degree.\par \par \uc2 If you had to work 40 hours a week, but get a pay raise, would you rather have that?\par \par \uc2 NADINE VARSOVIA: In all honesty, no.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: No?\par \par \uc2 NADINE VARSOVIA: No.\par \par \uc2 BROCK POLDA: Yes, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: No?\par \par \uc2 NADINE VARSOVIA: I -- yes, money is really only worth so much. I`d rather have that time for myself.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On this Pacific Northwest gem, today is TGIT, thank God it`s Thursday.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Mark Strassmann, in Friday Harbor, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: They are living right in San Juan County. Maybe a model for other local governments around the country.\par \par \uc2 Brandon Andrews told Mark the county has received about two dozen calls asking about that four-day workweek.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman spends his workweek On the Road, but he will be right here tomorrow on our Friday.\par \par \uc2 And John and I will be right back celebrating a reggae legend.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Reggae lovers not worried about a thing on what would have been Bob Marley`s 80th birthday.\par \par \uc2 Bob Marley and the Wailers introduced Jamaican music to the world in the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Since his death in 1981, Marley`s greatest hits album, Legend, has sold an estimated 25 million copies.\par \par \uc2 Among the celebrations today, Jamaican artists gathered at the Marley family studio in Kingston for a livestream concert.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Marley`s daughter Cedella posted a tribute, saying: "Today, we celebrate your life, your music and the immense love you gave to the world."\par \par \uc2 And that`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 That is the CBS Evening News for tonight. I`m Maurice DuBois. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good night.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250207el2600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For February 3, 2025, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Scott MacFarlane, Weijia Jiang, Kelly O`Grady, Jason Allen, Mark  Strassmann, Lonnie Quinn, Kris Van Cleave, Maurice DuBois, John Dickerson \par \uc2 3,998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2025\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York, this is the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : War and peace, the abridged edition.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 A trade war broke out today between the United States and its neighbors after President Trump threatened Canada and Mexico with new tariffs and they retaliated.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : But, hours later, it was over before a single tariff was fired across any border. Financial markets got rattled, though. More about that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Trump`s new tariffs on imports from China are still set to begin tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Jason Allen in Laredo, Texas, reports, American businesses are getting ready to pass along their higher cost to consumers -- Jason.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Yes, behind me is one of the bridges that carries as many as 20,000 trucks a day between the U.S. and Mexico. Now, it`s quieter tonight because it`s a holiday over in Mexico, but border protection and importers told me traffic has been heavier recently, as retailers try to get ahead of higher prices.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN (voice-over): At the All Tune and Lube in Laredo, Texas...\par \par \uc2 LUIS GUZMAN (Manager): We will get it back to you with pricing, OK?\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: ... manager Luis Guzman (sp?) has been stocking as many oil filters, brake pads and engine parts as he can, trying to beat the price increases 25 percent tariffs could bring.\par \par \uc2 How many of the parts you get are probably coming from manufacturers in Mexico?\par \par \uc2 LUIS GUZMAN: I have honestly a rough estimate. Maybe a good 60 percent, 50 percent parts come from there.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Across town, Rick Laurel`s (sp?) warehouse has been filling up for weeks with products such as makeup, cardboard tubing, cement mixers, all from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 RICK LAUREL (Texas Resident): Now it`s going to get even more full, because now we have a 30-day window that the presidents, both presidents just talked.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: So you don`t expect it to go back to normal. You think they will try to keep moving goods as fast as they can?\par \par \uc2 RICK LAUREL: Well, with an extension of just 30 days, that doesn`t push anything. The only thing it does is pushes the line 30 more days.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: It doesn`t erase the uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 RICK LAUREL: It doesn`t erase the uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Laredo Mayor Victor Trevino says that uncertainty is something new for this city.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR TREVINO (Mayor of Laredo, Texas): That`s what makes it different and that`s what makes it a little bit worrisome. This is what I think. It`s like playing chicken. Who`s going to move first? Who`s going to give way first?\par \par \uc2 We`re the largest port in the United States. About 40 percent of all the merchandise that goes into the U.S. passes through here. So that`s significant.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Even a small impact to a trade corridor where $300 billion in goods move through every year is something that Luis Guzman and his customers can`t afford.\par \par \uc2 LUIS GUZMAN: So, right now, I want to be in a market where I`m going to be competitive with my prices. And I want to be actually good to be good, friendly budget. But with these tariffs, I don`t know if can I even be friendly budget, because we don`t know how much they`re going to be raising up on my price.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Now, leading up to this, local officials told me they were preparing for this really like an emergency. They were talking to their counterparts in Mexico, knowing there isn`t a whole lot they could do about it, but really just wanted to be prepared for what was ahead.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: Jason, what was Luis Guzman going to do if this tariff did happen with prices as he passed them on to customers?\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: You know, what I heard from businesses today, John, is, they weren`t really going to be able to absorb much of this increased cost. For instance, at his shop, if he was able to still offer a car part at the same price, he was going to find somewhere else he could make it up.\par \par \uc2 He was already looking at maybe increasing his hourly labor costs in order to make it up.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Jason, when you say ripple effects, what would that actually look like for Americans living there along the border?\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: So, let`s take Laredo as an example.\par \par \uc2 If goods come through here and they`re higher prices, maybe people start buying less. That means there`s less demand. There`s less shipping that happens. That leads to impacts to jobs, state revenues as well. You can see the ripple effect.\par \par \uc2 And remember back to the pandemic, this is a very complex system, Maurice. It is not something that is unraveled easily.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And, briefly, Jason, is anybody breathing a sigh of relief now that the trade war has been paused?\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Maybe for a day, John, but this really showed them they don`t know what to expect. So, at the auto shop, they have got empty shelves. They`re going to try to fill them because they don`t know what happens in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Jason Allen in Laredo.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jason.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now let`s bring in CBS News money watch correspondent Kelly O`Grady.\par \par \uc2 So, Kelly, let`s take a stroll here through the economic case that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is making for slapping tariffs.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: OK, so a big driver is getting better trade relationships. So let`s look at some numbers.\par \par \uc2 It`s 2023. The U.S. sells $354 billion in goods to Canada, $323 billion to Mexico. That same year, though, they bought $419 billion from Canada and $475 billion from Mexico. So that means there`s a trade deficit of $64 billion with Canada and $153 billion with Mexico.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And you definitely don`t want to be on the short end of a trade deficit.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: No, economically, you want them to rely on you versus you relying on them.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: OK, in practical terms, what does it look like when we slap a tariff on an actual item, an everyday item?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: So let`s look at a car battery, manufactured in Canada. Then a car company buys it, brings it into the U.S. They sell it to a repair shop and then ultimately a customer.\par \par \uc2 But if there is a tariff, that same U.S. car company pays an import tax to bring that car battery into the U.S. Then they have to decide, how much are they going to pass on to that repair shop? And then, ultimately, the consumer says, OK, that car battery costs more. Am I going to buy it? Maybe yes, maybe no.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And this is where potential inflation could happen.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Right. It could mean higher prices. It also could mean that car company says, well, maybe I should manufacture that battery in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And then our trade partners say, well, maybe rather than paying import taxes, I should just do a better trade deal with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Three potential outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Grady, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The prospect of a trade war had investors dumping stocks in the early going today. The Dow plunged nearly 350 points in the first half-hour in trading. But by the end of the day, it recovered a lot of its losses, closing down 122 points.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Weijia Jiang at the White House now, a lot of ups and downs there today as well with the tariffs on and off.\par \par \uc2 Now that they`re off, Weijia, what did the president get after all of that?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, Maurice, President Trump has called tariffs the most beautiful word in the dictionary because he thinks they have such a potent power in negotiating.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, the White House is touting concessions from both Mexico and Canada. So the Mexicans have agreed to send 10,000 soldiers to the border to stop fentanyl trafficking and illegal immigrants. But we should note that, in our reporting, it actually shows the vast majority of fentanyl seized at the U.S. southern border is smuggled in by American citizens and other legal travelers on vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Canada is also sending what they call 10,000 front-line personnel to the border and shelling out about a billion dollars for choppers and new technology to reinforce it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Weijia, OK, these tariffs aren`t happening. So what happens to all of that income that the president said the tariffs were going to be used for to pay all these wonderful things he said they were going to be used to pay for?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And, John, that is a very long list. But the White House says that these Mexico-Canada tariffs had one specific goal, and that was to convince our neighbors to do more to seal the border from illegal immigrants and drugs.\par \par \uc2 They say that is separate from the so-called universal tariffs that would put a 2.5 percent global rate on certain fields, such as semiconductors and possibly even cars. Tonight, sources close to Trump say he is still very much considering those.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Weijia Jiang at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Now some of the top stories from around the world in tonight`s Evening News Roundup, starting with the aftermath of the air ambulance crash Friday in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Four people are still in the hospital, two in critical condition. No word yet on what caused the crash. One person on the ground and all six on the plane were killed, including a girl from Mexico who had been treated at Shriners Children`s Hospital.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Doors were locked at the Washington headquarters of \uc2 USAID\uc2  today at the mutual agreement of President Trump and billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , whom he`s empowered to remake the federal government.\par \par \uc2 USAID\uc2 , established by President Kennedy and Congress, fights starvation and disease in more than 100 countries, including Sudan, scene of the worst humanitarian crisis on Earth. Debora Patta will take us there later this week.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And today was the deadline for thousands of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents to fill out a Trump administration questionnaire asking them what role they played in investigating the January 6 Capitol riot, the largest criminal investigation in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane joins us now.\par \par \uc2 But, Scott, the impact of all of this is being felt across the country tonight.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: An unprecedented day of turmoil inside the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These agents were asked what role they played in the January 6 investigations, if they searched or interviewed or arrested anybody, raising fears of a purge of agents nationwide. Take a look at this. Our CBS News review found some of these lead January 6 \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents are part of Joint Terrorism Task Forces in cities nationwide, including New Orleans, the most recently struck city with a terror attack, and others from California to Colorado to Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 There`s also this concern that this purge would impact human trafficking task forces, gun groups, and, John and Maurice, send a chilling message to \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, be careful what you investigate next and make sure it does not displease President Trump.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So that`s filling out the questionnaire.\par \par \uc2 What`s the long-term impact here, Scott, for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Immediately, there`s a 12:00 p.m. deadline Eastern time tomorrow for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to turn over these names to the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2 , with the possibility that there are going to be cuts.\par \par \uc2 Also possible, lawsuits challenging these firings, if they happen, by attorneys representing \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, but also just a make-or-break day tomorrow. Will the \uc2 FBI\uc2  do it? We know this statement from the assistant director for the New York field office shows that they are trying to rally the troops.\par \par \uc2 He says: "We find ourselves in the middle of a battle of our own, as good people are being walked out of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and others are being targeted because they did their jobs," a demoralizing moment, if nothing else, inside the bureau.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Scott MacFarlane for us in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Still ahead here on the CBS Evening News, Lonnie Quinn with the weather.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And we will have these other stories as well.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: I`m Mark Strassmann in Los Angeles. These migrant day laborers are critical to rebuilding the city, but worried they could be deported.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And I`m Kris Van Cleave at Washington`s Reagan National Airport.\par \par \uc2 The latest on the investigation into that deadly midair collision -- next on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Few images we have seen since the midair collision have brought home the enormity of the loss as this, 14-year-old Everly Livingston and her 11-year-old sister, Alydia, of Ashburn, Virginia. Their mom texted the photo to a friend right before the plane took off, the faces of innocence.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : The sisters and their parents were among the 67 people killed when the plane collided with an Army helicopter near Washington.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has the latest on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): Crews began lifting pieces of the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  regional jet that crashed into the Potomac River after Wednesday`s midair collision, among them, the midsection of the planes mangled and upside-down fuselage, one of the plane`s engines and a wing, as efforts continue to recover all 67 crash victims.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have positively identified 55 sets of remains. During recovery and salvage operations today, additional human remains were located.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigators have accessed the plane`s cockpit data recorder, which shows the airliner was flying at about 325 feet around the time of the collision.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Director of Army Aviation Colonel Mark Ott.\par \par \uc2 Is there any reason why Army helicopters would be flying above that 200- foot line, as it appears in this case?\par \par \uc2 COL. MARK OTT (Deputy Director of Aviation, \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 ): It`s important for us to not speculate because there are all kinds of reasons that you could deviate from an altitude, something as simple as a flock of birds is in front of you, or you may deviate if you see something that`s an obstacle or other threat to your flight.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The black box data also indicates the plane pitched up immediately before impact, perhaps a last-second attempt by the pilots to climb.\par \par \uc2 Airline Captain Laura Einsetler.\par \par \uc2 What`s going on in the cockpit 325 feet up with the runway in sight?\par \par \uc2 CAPT. LAURA EINSETLER (Pilot): The pilots of the regional jet are going to be fully focused on landing the jet. They`re manually flying at that point, focused on the runway, focused on their altitude, their airspeed, their spatial orientation, their descent rate.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The second plane crash Friday in Philadelphia capped the deadliest week for American aviation in more than two decades and has rattled the flying public.\par \par \uc2 What are you telling passengers right now?\par \par \uc2 CAPT. LAURA EINSETLER: We put our lives on the line every day to keep everyone absolutely as safe as we possibly can. So we always want to reassure the passengers and the traveling public that our safety is our absolute number one priority.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And Kris Van Cleave joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Kris, what are pilots telling you about the fact that these two planes -- aircraft, rather, were so close in the first place? And everybody`s wondering, how safe are the skies?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, yes, so, fundamentally, two aircraft, even if that helicopter had been at 200 feet, they were too close together. You want at least 1,000 feet of separation there.\par \par \uc2 So what led up to the circumstances? What is the chain of events that put those two aircrafts so close together? It won`t just be that the helicopter was flying at 325 feet. There will be other factors that led us down the road to disaster here.\par \par \uc2 How safe is the aviation system? It`s as safe as it was before this crash. There are layers of safety built in that are still in effect today, the same way they were two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Kris Van Cleave in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Kris.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Looking at the calendar, we are halfway now through winter.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Looking at the dictionary, winter comes from the old Germanic word meaning time of water.\par \par \uc2 So, Lonnie Quinn, are we in a time of water?\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: You know what, John? With the atmospheric river setting up, it looks like we are in that time.\par \par \uc2 If you take a look at the globe and you sort of spin it around to our country, the atmospheric river is depicted by that sort of, like, speckled blue and black line. So it brings in a lot of moisture right now to Northern California. For example, it`s been in place for a couple of days already, but the next big bout is going to be tonight.\par \par \uc2 So a place like San Francisco could see two inches of rain, but (AUDIO GAP) it`s going to be migrating to the south. So it`s possible for Los Angeles to maybe pick up to an inch of rain. But California`s reservoirs depend on snow. And look at the picture that you see behind me.\par \par \uc2 This is Truckee, California, 6,000 feet. They should be loaded with snow. It`s not in place. Now, the mountain peaks in the background, Northstar, sure there`s snow there, but we just don`t see the snowpack as of right now. Who gets rain? Who gets snow? All dictated by the jet stream. North of it, it`s very cold. South of it, it`s very warm, and it`s just pushed way up to the north right now.\par \par \uc2 So look at this. Houston two weeks ago had four inches of snow. Houston tomorrow sets a record at 81 degrees, every number on this map a record- setter for tomorrow, sort of a taste of early spring -- gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Thank you, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: One of President Trump`s signature policies could make it harder for Southern California homeowners to rebuild after the fires.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : We will have that story in Eye on America next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : \uc2 Cal Fire\uc2  reports, the biggest of the Southern California wildfires, the Palisades, is now fully contained. The fire destroyed thousands of homes.\par \par \uc2 Now, as residents look to rebuild, they could face a problem. The president`s immigration crackdown could worsen what is already a shortage of construction workers.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is in Pasadena and has tonight`s Eye on America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): On the street in North Pasadena, we ran into about a dozen workers volunteering to clear debris, potential kindling just two miles from the Eaton Fire`s burn scar. Many of these workers are here illegally.\par \par \uc2 Bernardo Osserio (sp?) is their crew boss. He migrated from Mexico 36 years ago and in 2021 became a U.S. citizen.\par \par \uc2 BERNARDO OSSERIO (Crew Boss): From every single people, they`re afraid.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: What are they afraid of?\par \par \uc2 BERNARDO OSSERIO: Deportations. People like to work. They want to work. I mean, they want better lives as well.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And there`s lots of work to be done.\par \par \uc2 BERNARDO OSSERIO: Yes. We can`t do it without them. We cannot build America without them.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 MAN: These are the hands that will rebuild Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: We found many of those same workers protesting.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Resist injustice.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This is a rally of migrant day laborers. They`re critical to the rebuilding of Los Angeles, but worried they won`t get that chance because of the Trump administration`s aggressive stance toward deporting anyone without legal status.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (President of the United States): We will begin the process of returning millions and millions of criminal aliens back to the places from which they came.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In California, 41 percent of construction workers are immigrants, many of them staying here without legal documents. Under the Trump administration`s immigration crackdown, deportation officers can remove most immigrants who are here illegally, even targeting refugee programs and birthright citizenship.\par \par \uc2 DAN GATSBY (Founder, Los Angeles Builders Association): They learned a trade. You cannot just bring new people and train them. It`s going to take years. So, I mean, who`s going to do it?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Dan Gatsby founded the Los Angeles Builders Association, a trade group for the local construction industry.\par \par \uc2 Is it realistic that Los Angeles could rebuild what`s been lost without immigrant labor?\par \par \uc2 DAN GATSBY: It`s going to be very difficult. We already have big shortage of home in Los Angeles. I mean, with this on top of it, I mean, good luck.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Gatsby estimates rebuilding Los Angeles could take 20 years and maybe twice as long without the help of immigrant construction labor.\par \par \uc2 BERNARDO OSSERIO: We are essential workers, essential people for the community.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Just like rescuing L.A. from the flames, rebuilding it will take every available hand.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Mark Strassmann in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It`s not just California. The construction industry says it needs to hire 439,000 new workers nationwide this year. According to the latest figures, construction employs a larger share of undocumented workers, at 13 percent, than any other industry.\par \par \uc2 In tomorrow`s Eye on America, from Newland, North Carolina, as the president talks about scrapping FEMA, victims of Hurricane Helene are still waiting for help.\par \par \uc2 Maurice and I will be back in a moment with Beyonce, more than a little bit country.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : It was a big night for Beyonce at the Grammys. She won album of the year for the first time and became the first black artist to win country album of the year.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: She tipped her cowboy hat metaphorically to Linda Martell, the first black woman to perform solo at Nashville`s \uc2 Grand Ole Opry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, later, she told Entertainment Tonight co-host Kevin Frazier how proud she is of her album Cowboy Carter, which puts a spotlight on the banjo, an instrument with African roots.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEYONCE (Musician): I just feel honored to reintroduce some of that history that I think has been lost, and very, very honored that I got the response and the open heart and people welcomed the album. The love I got from the country community tonight really made me feel so seen. And I`m very grateful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : And that`s the CBS Evening News. I`m \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: I`m Maurice DuBois.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020250204el2300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS MORNINGS for December 24, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Adriana Diaz, Michelle Miller, Skyler Henry,  Jordan Steele, Elise Preston, Lilia Luciano, Imtiaz Tyab, Carter Evans,  Chris Livesay, Kris Van Cleave, Kelly O`Grady \par \uc2 13,512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 December 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s December 24th. Christmas Eve. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I`m Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : And I`m Michelle Miller.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle, Tony, and Nate are off, but we are in great hands. Here`s today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, it`s worse than I expected. Slightly panicked.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We showed up an hour-and-a-half really, and it was not enough.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Are we having any regrets?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Rough weather dampens the festive spirit of some holiday travelers in the West and the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Luigi Mangione, accused of killing UnitedHealthcare CEO pleads not guilty to state charges.\par \par \uc2 KAREN FRIEDMAN AGNIFILO (Luigi Mangione`s attorney): We`re going to fight these charges, whether it`s in the state or federal.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : There`s new fallout after the House Ethics Committee released a scathing report on former Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. GLENN IVEY (D-MD): Clearly, everything we talk about in this report is over the line.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A deadly explosion on the water in Fort Lauderdale. Take a look at that.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I saw the top half of the boat fly up about probably 10 feet.\par \par \uc2 MARIAH CAREY (Singer): This is really, really not my thing.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: The queen of the holidays, Mariah Carey is going viral after showing off her not so good wrapping skills.\par \par \uc2 MARIAH CAREY: How do I do this?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Yes, no. Phone a friend.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: I got call out here today about an elf running around causing some mischief in the area.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You heard that right. Someone`s elf is up to no good.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Would you be able to come out and identify him? I`ve got him in my truck.\par \par \uc2 BOY: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Can you see him sitting there?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Is that him?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Is that your elf?\par \par \uc2 BOY: Yes. How -- how he -- how he got out here?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t know. He was running around feeding some reindeer down there.\par \par \uc2 BOY: That was really nice of him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The innocence of children.\par \par \uc2 How did my elf get out?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Cute. And around the holidays, there`s so much magic in the air.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it. What a beautiful way to start the morning.\par \par \uc2 But we begin this morning with millions of Americans on the move this Christmas Eve, and we are expecting a record breaking number of people on the road and in the air, but we`ve been told to expect some nasty weather in the Northeast and on the West Coast. So we`re going to have the forecast in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But let`s begin with Skyler Henry, who is at the busiest airport in the country. That`s Atlanta`s Hartsfield-Jackson.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Vlad, good morning to you, all things considered, things running relatively smoothly here at the airport, the wait time right now to get through \uc2 TSA\uc2  less than 10 minutes, which, considering this airport, is great news.\par \par \uc2 Now, more than 120 million people are expected to be on the move this holiday season today, with the goal of making it to their destination in time for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: At airports across the country, it was hurry up and wait. Long lines greeted flyers at Washington`s Reagan National Airport Monday. Nationwide, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  expects to screen nearly 40 million passengers from December 19th through January 2nd, a six percent increase from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Passengers also packed airports in Denver, Pittsburgh, and in Boston.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Yeah, it`s worse than I expected. So feeling a little slightly panicked.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I`m a full time teacher, so I`m just trying to be on the beach and chill.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: In Seattle, one anxious passenger opened the emergency exit door of an arriving \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight Sunday night, no one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 An estimated 107 million people will travel by car for the holidays, including Tony Ultramori (sp?), who was filling up for a thousand mile drive from Texas to Colorado.\par \par \uc2 TONY ULTRAMORI (Traveler): This is one of those trips where it doesn`t matter how much it`s going to cost, because it`s good to see your family.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Lucky for him, gas prices compared to last Christmas are down an average of eight cents a gallon.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN\uc2  (GasBuddy): Gas prices going into the holidays this year are some of their lowest levels since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Christmas gas prices this year are likely to be at their lowest level on Christmas since 2020.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Now, for those planning on driving today, another bit of good news, less traffic on the roadway. Now, the next holiday rush, if you will, after Christmas, that actually comes on Friday, where they say that it will be incredibly busy at airports like this one -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Skyler, hopefully you`re staying home. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned, there are some storms to look out for on both coasts. So for more on what you need to know, let`s go to meteorologist Jordan Steele, with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jordan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Hi. Good morning, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 Well, we`re going to have travel trouble in several different spots today across the country. The West starting to see a series of winter systems, which could impact San Francisco down to LA. We`ve got storms into Texas and of course, winter weather from JFK to Boston. I`m actually a little concerned about the ice there. So you`ve got to please drive slowly on I- 95 and I-81.\par \par \uc2 How about a look at who gets to experience a classic white Christmas again? Remember, a white Christmas is classified as having at least one inch of snow on the ground, or actively falling snow. You`ve got the elevated West, the Northern Tier, and some of the lucky folks here into the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re curious about where the best snow is, you`ve got to head to the West. This is where you`re going to be seeing powder runs for days. Not one, not two, but five different systems between now through Sunday.\par \par \uc2 So now we`re talking about maybe a half a foot to a foot of rainfall expected along the I-5 corridor. Multiple feet of snow for The mountains into the Intermountain West. We`ve got to be mindful of avalanche danger if you are exploring the back country.\par \par \uc2 And then, on the flip side to all of this, some of us shedding layers for the last weekend of the year, above average temperatures for the majority of us.\par \par \uc2 For more in depth coverage, you guys can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Adriana and the crew at CBS MORNINGS, I hope you guys have a safe and happy holiday.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Happy Holidays to you, too. Thanks, Jordan.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : In Northern California, high waves dozens of feet tall, destroyed a wharf in Santa Cruz, sending workers and debris into the water. At least one person was killed elsewhere by wreckage that washed ashore and the danger on the coast remains.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has our report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: About an hour south of San Francisco, yellow police tape sealed off the Santa Cruz Wharf Monday after intense waves caused parts of it to collapse and send debris into the ocean.\par \par \uc2 BRYSON WONG (Witness): Right when we saw that the whole thing collapsed, we just ran as fast as we can.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Luckily, the structure was closed to the public while under construction, but standing above the wharf`s torn off remains two workers who fell into the water. They were rescued, and a third swam to safety.\par \par \uc2 JAMILAH STARR (Witness): The wharf began to pick up like a wave and come towards us. It was like a movie. I said, "Evacuate."\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Its force shocking witnesses standing just feet away, like Jamilah Starr and her family.\par \par \uc2 JAMILAH STARR: We called the lifeguards. They came. We told them the location of the victims, and then we rushed off the wharf as fast as we can.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The National Weather Service forecast 30 to 60-foot high waves crashing onto the wharf`s coast along Monterey Bay.\par \par \uc2 At nearby Sunset State Beach, one person died at the hospital after being trapped under rubble washed up by large waves. And just south at Marina State Beach, a man went missing after police say strong currents pulled him out to sea.\par \par \uc2 Back in Santa Cruz, Mayor Fred Keeley is warning of a dangerous time near the ocean.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR FRED KEELEY (D) (Santa Cruz, California): We are anticipating that what is coming towards us is more serious than what was there this morning.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For CBS MORNINGS, Elise Preston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In other news, the man accused of killing UnitedHealthcare CEO returned to court yesterday and pleaded not guilty to state charges of murder and terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Luigi Mangione was led into that hearing in New York with his hands shackled to his waist. His lawyer accused authorities of treating this case as a spectacle and making it political.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano was also in the court for the hearing. She`s covered this case since Mangione was arrested in Pennsylvania two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Good morning to you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Well inside the courtroom, Mangione spoke publicly only, two words yesterday, but seemed to have lengthy and focused discussions with his team of attorneys.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LUIGI MANGIONE (Suspect in the killing of \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 ): Not guilty.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Luigi Mangione pleaded not guilty Monday to 11 charges, including first degree murder as an act of terrorism for the shooting death of UnitedHealthcare CEO, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During Monday`s arraignment, Mangione`s lawyers accused state and federal authorities of using the 26-year-old as political fodder as he also faces four federal charges, including stalking and murder, which could carry the possibility of the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 KAREN FRIEDMAN AGNIFILO: He is a young man, and he is being treated like a human ping pong ball between two warring jurisdictions here.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The defense also raised concerns about Mangione`s right to a fair trial, referencing comments by public officials and Mangione`s high profile extradition to New York City last week.\par \par \uc2 KAREN FRIEDMAN AGNIFILO: He was on display for everyone to see in the biggest stage perp walk I`ve ever seen in my career.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Prosecutors vowed to stay in bounds and alleged the evidence against Mangione is overwhelming, citing hours of interviews and thousands of videos.\par \par \uc2 JOEL SEIDEMANN (Prosecutor): I have never seen a case with such volume of evidence, aside from the issue of the quality of the evidence.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Authorities said the two cases will proceed on parallel tracks.\par \par \uc2 The defense attorney has said that what happened last week could potentially pollute the jury pool, and that officials are playing politics. How could this affect a trial?\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: There`s no question that anything in a famous or an infamous case can potentially pollute a jury pool. However, when it comes time to pick a jury, which may be a year from now, that the judge will help the lawyers ferret out those who have been affected by alleged prejudicial pretrial publicity.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The judge has said, while he has little control over what happens outside the courtroom and with those officials like Mayor Eric Adams, he will guarantee Mangione gets a fair trial. His next appearance in this state case, the criminal case, is at the end of February. We might see him before in January for the federal one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So what Rikki Klieman told you was sort of interesting about what could potentially happen when it comes to the jury.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes, I mean, when it comes to the jury, it`s complicated, because a lot of people are resonating, perhaps, with the issues around the health insurance industry. That`s already a problem, and it`s so highly publicized that, you know, it`s going to be very hard to find people who are not connected to this. And I`m seeing on social media a lot of support for the suspect.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes. Everybody has a doctor. Everybody`s been through the healthcare system.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. All right, thank you, Lilia. We know you`ll be there. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Thank you. Happy Holidays.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  is in Washington, DC, in a hospital this morning after developing a fever. A spokesperson says the 42nd president was admitted for testing and observation, and that he remains in good spirits and deeply appreciates the excellent care he is receiving.\par \par \uc2 Clinton, who is 78 has had a history of heart trouble.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Hope he gets better.\par \par \uc2 All right, turning now to the damning report released by the House Ethics Committee yesterday on the alleged conduct of former Florida Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gaetz unsuccessfully tried to block the reports released with a lawsuit. Our Scott MacFarlane has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Just weeks ago, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  was trying to land the job of US attorney general.\par \par \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R) (Former US Representative): I`m looking forward to hearing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Now, the US House Ethics Committee alleges he violated Florida`s statutory rape law, though he has not been criminally charged.\par \par \uc2 The Committee, which first launched an investigation of Gaetz three years ago, issued a 42-page report of its findings Monday, alleging they found evidence Gaetz paid a 17-year-old girl who just completed her junior year in high school $400.00 for sex in 2017 and made payments of more than $90,000.00 to 12 different women likely for sexual activity or drug use.\par \par \uc2 The report says all 12 women testified sexual encounters were consensual, but one woman told the panel the use of drugs at parties with Gaetz may have impaired her ability to know what was going on. Gaetz has repeatedly denied all of the allegations.\par \par \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2 : The person doesn`t exist. I have not had a relationship with a 17-year-old. That is totally false.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Maryland Congressman Glenn Ivey is one of five Democrats on the evenly divided 10-person Ethics Committee.\par \par \uc2 REP. GLENN IVEY (D-MD): From an ethics standpoint, clearly, everything we talk about in this report is over the line.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Committee also says it found evidence Gaetz engaged in illicit drug use, which he also denies.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the report, Gaetz wrote on social media he behaved embarrassingly in the past, drinking and smoking more than he should have, but that he had been exonerated by a separate Justice Department probe.\par \par \uc2 REP. GLENN IVEY (D-MD): We do have some additional information in there that might be of interest to them, and of course, there are state officials who can review this conduct as well and draw their own conclusions about whether they should move forward or not.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Scott MacFarlane in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here in New York, the man arrested in the horrific burning death of a woman on a subway train is now charged with first degree murder.\par \par \uc2 Police say 33-year-old Sebastian Zapeta is a native of Guatemala who was in the US illegally. He`s accused of setting fire to the victim, then watching her burn to death.\par \par \uc2 Police arrested him hours after Sunday`s attack, after a group of high school students said they recognized the suspect from photos of the crime scene. The victim has not yet been identified.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Christmas Eve traditionally focuses on Bethlehem, the historical birthplace of Jesus in the occupied West Bank. Normally it`s a festive time, but not this year.\par \par \uc2 Streets that would normally be packed with pilgrims are practically empty, so are many of the shops.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is in Bethlehem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In the ancient alleyways behind the Church of the Nativity, souvenir shops which have withstood the test of time.\par \par \uc2 The Bethlehem Oriental Store has been run by the McKell (sp?) family since 1818.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS MCKELL (Bethlehem Oriental Store): Here we have some nativity scenes, some figures.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And 73-year-old Elias McKell has worked here his entire life.\par \par \uc2 You`ve had 73 Christmases. Could you imagine having one like this?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS MCKELL: Never, never, never. Two years nothing.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: This is what Christmas looked like in Bethlehem in 2022. A major square full of people, covered in twinkling lights, and this is it now, no trees, no tourists, shops shuttered.\par \par \uc2 Since last year`s October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks, Israel`s assault on Gaza and the surge in violence across the West Bank. Bethlehem, believed by Christians to be the birthplace of Jesus has seen its tourism economy shrink by over 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS MCKELL: Every Sunday I go for prayer. We pray for peace. We pray to hope to stop that war. Enough.\par \par \uc2 (ADA and NADINE JADALLAH speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: It`s a prayer shared by Ada (sp?) and her granddaughter, Nadine Jadallah (sp?), who live in Birzeit in the occupied West Bank.\par \par \uc2 For a second Christmas, Ada`s daughter, Raida Zakaria (sp?), who lives in Gaza, is sheltering in a church.\par \par \uc2 ADA JADALLAH (Birzeit resident): She can`t sleep, she can`t eat, she can`t make anything. She can`t bath, she can`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 NADINE JADALLAH (Birzeit resident): She used to come here every year on Christmas, but now, knowing that she`s there and we are here, we cannot feel the joy of Christmas because she`s living in like these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: For CBS MORNINGS, Imtiaz Tyab, Bethlehem in the Occupied West Bank.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The ripple effects of this conflict goes so wide. You know, all the people affected directly, the hostages, you know, those have been killed, but then also the economies in this area.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Millions --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes, and that further disintegrates any goodwill, any initiation to move forward. It just -- it just stalls everything out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think millions of people are echoing Mr. McKell`s prayers for peace.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, why many \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers are on strike for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Plus Kris Van Cleave takes a driverless taxi for an exclusive first ride as this cutting edge transit marks an important new milestone, but first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Club Cardio is changing the way people work out, creating community and connection. Michelle meets the family that match hip-hop with step aerobics.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fairy Tale of New York"/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, the creative couple behind the eclectic band, Johnnyswim. See how they found their voice making music about everyday life.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll show you how big the Christmas jackpot is for tonight`s Mega Millions drawing. It is one of the biggest in history. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . For a job you`ll love, visit progressive.com/careers.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Christmas seems to be the season to go on strike. In some big cities, hundreds of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  drivers have been walking \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  picket line since last week, and now thousands of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas have walked off the job.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans looks at the demands of the coffee servers who say they need higher pay.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "One, two, three, four, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  we`re going to take no more.")\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Just days before Christmas, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas walked off the job, expanding a five day strike to 12 cities nationwide. According to the Union, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers united.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "No contract. No coffee.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: You should be able to afford rent, food and healthcare.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: It started with just a handful of locations, but the number of striking \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers has been increasing every day.\par \par \uc2 Now, instead of serving coffee, baristas for more than 60 locations are on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 Workers say they are not making enough to be able to live comfortably in the cities where they`re employed.\par \par \uc2 KAI KRAWCZENIUK (\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  shift supervisor): I have many coworkers who are struggling to afford their rent at the end of the month, or end up having to choose between rent, groceries, and their regular bills.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Workers United, which has unionized more than 500 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores, says \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  proposed an economic package without an immediate wage increase and a guaranteed raise of only one-and-a-half percent in future years.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  says Workers United prematurely ended a bargaining session this week. In a statement, the coffee giant says: "The union is calling for an immediate 64 percent minimum wage increase, followed by a 77 percent increase over a three-year period." Adding, "This is not sustainable."\par \par \uc2 KAI KRAWCZENIUK: I`m fortunate enough to have a job where I have a hundred chances a day to make a person smile, but when I`m understaffed, when I`m stressed, I can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : And that is top of mind for a lot of people, including the two of you who get your morning \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , like religiously.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yup.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : It really is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and the folks who are out there and need that wage.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right, living wage is important.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead, as Roman Catholics get ready for a jubilee year, we`ll show you how some of the Vatican`s magnificent features are looking better than they have in centuries.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************************************\par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Earthcam/15th Street Fisheries)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow. Dramatic moment captured on camera here when a small boat exploded while docked in Fort Lauderdale, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Tragically, one person was found dead in the water after this happened, and five other people were hurt. The flames spread to another boat before firefighters could put them out. It`s still not clear why this happened, and the cause is still under investigation.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So scary.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Incredibly scary.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, tonight, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  will celebrate the Vatican`s traditional Christmas Eve midnight mass in a newly restored St. Peter`s Basilica. It`s one of a series of projects all over Rome. Major tourist attractions are getting cleaned up before the New Year, when the Eternal City expects to see many more visitors than usual.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay visited the Basilica to see the historical features that are now fully revealed for the first time in generations, and he joins us from Rome.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 You know, the reason for all of this is that 2025 is a jubilee year. That`s a tradition that dates all the way back to the year 1300. It is a time of renewal and reconciliation, both on the inside and on the outside.\par \par \uc2 To find out exactly what the Catholic Church means by that, we got a close up look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Soaring 450 feet into the sky and spanning more than four football fields, St. Peter`s is the biggest church in the world, and with roots dating back more than 1,600 years, it`s also one of the oldest and was in need of a few touch ups.\par \par \uc2 What better excuse than the jubilee, a year of pilgrimage that traditionally falls every 25 years in the Catholic Church, major renovations on the outside, meant to reflect spiritual renewal on the inside, says Father Enzo Fortunato of St. Peter`s Basilica.\par \par \uc2 But it is sort of a metaphor.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO (St. Peter`s Basilica): Yes. Metaphor is exactly the word you use.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: For the kind of internal --\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: -- restoration.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: One of the biggest restorations was of Bernini`s masterpiece, the Baldachin.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in 250 years, it`s been restored to its original --\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Restored -- before you have the image, I think was very black and the now, we can admire the gold.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The canopy sits atop the tomb of none other than the first Pope ever.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: This is the center of the altar. It`s the center --\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: It`s so stunning.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: -- where is the tomb of St. Peter.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Right beneath her feet.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Also restored the chair of St Peter, one of the most important symbols of Papal power.\par \par \uc2 How many popes has this chair seen?\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: A lot of pope from -- by 87 and five.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: No matter if you can`t come in person, the Vatican has recreated St. Peter`s digitally with the help of AI, allowing you to zoom in on every little niche, nook and cranny from your device.\par \par \uc2 If you can come in person, brace yourself. You`ll be joined by more than 35 million visitors to Rome over the course of 2025 as the Eternal City undergoes its own face lift from the Coliseum area to the Trevi Fountain, where they had to turn the water off, tourists were temporarily left with a small pool where they could cast their wishes.\par \par \uc2 But the main event is at St. Peter`s, where Michelangelo`s tear inducing Pieta is protected by new bullet proof glass. Just beside it, the Holy Door, representing the path to salvation, like at this extraordinary Jubilee in 2015, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  will break its seal this evening.\par \par \uc2 That will be a really special moment.\par \par \uc2 FATHER ENZO FORTUNATO: Yes, it`s a very real special moment for all -- for all Christianity.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  will open the Holy Door or the Porta Santa later tonight, at 7:00 PM Rome time, just before the midnight mass.\par \par \uc2 Local authorities say that security around the entire city has been tightened, this following that Christmas market attack in Germany -- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Chris Livesay in Rome, thanks for the tour.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Kris Van Cleave takes us for a driverless taxi ride. See how \uc2 Waymo\uc2  is expanding the range of its self-driving rides.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : \uc2 Waymo\uc2  touts itself as the future of driverless vehicles, but many Americans find the technology difficult to accept because of safety concerns, and there have been some accidents with \uc2 Waymo\uc2  cars.\par \par \uc2 So how concerned should we be?\par \par \uc2 To help answer that question, the company gave our Kris Van Cleave an exclusive first look at one of its fully autonomous cars operating at a higher speed on a freeway in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: So we`re getting on the freeway.\par \par \uc2 You`re along for a self-driving first. We are the first true passengers invited for a ride on a freeway in one of \uc2 Waymo\uc2 `s fully autonomous electric jaguars, meaning there`s no one behind the wheel.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE KREITMANN (\uc2 Waymo\uc2  principal software engineer): The car stays under the speed limit, which is 65.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Riding with us is \uc2 Waymo\uc2 `s principal software engineer, Pierre Kreitmann.\par \par \uc2 How important is it for \uc2 Waymo\uc2  to be able to operate on the freeway?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE KREITMANN: Yes, freeways are critical for \uc2 Waymo\uc2 . That`s one of the number one requests we get from our riders.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , the nation`s first fully self-driving ride hailing service, is owned by \uc2 Google\uc2 `s parent, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 . It already operates more than 150,000 paid driverless rides a week in Arizona and California, with plans to expand further to Austin, Atlanta, and Miami, but so far, not on freeways. But that`s about to change.\par \par \uc2 After months of autonomous testing in the Phoenix area, gathering data from tens of thousands of miles driven with the goal of adding freeway passenger service in 2025.\par \par \uc2 Freeways are so important because it`s all about time. They`re a lot faster to get from point A to point B. My ride from the airport is about 15 minutes, unless you take only surface streets, and then it`s 30.\par \par \uc2 And competition is coming. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 -backed \uc2 Zoox\uc2  aims to launch in Las Vegas next year, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  rolled out Tesla`s robotaxi concept in October.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE KREITMANN: One very important thing on freeway is that stopping is not the right thing to do, so the car needs to find the right lane to maneuver into.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What`s the biggest challenge to getting the car to work well on the freeway?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE KREITMANN: Part of it is the speed, because the speeds are so high and the mistakes are so costly, so it`s really about never making a mistake, driving defensively and predicting things that can go wrong.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Is that the bar? Never making a mistake.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE KREITMANN: So the bar is to make as few mistakes as possible and be safer than a human driver would be.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Waymo\uc2  cites its data showing in over 25 million miles driven, its cars saw 72 percent fewer injury causing collisions compared to human drivers. But they are not perfect.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Waymo\uc2  runs in a parade.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: As seen in viral videos like this one showing a \uc2 Waymo\uc2  accidentally driving into a parade route before safely pulling over.\par \par \uc2 The vehicles are equipped with cameras, radar and other sensors, allowing them to see a full 360 degrees. The work now is focused on fine tuning how the cars handle freeway driving, like dodging debris in the road.\par \par \uc2 If you were going to give the \uc2 Waymo\uc2  today a grade on this drive. How is it done?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE KREITMANN: I would say six out of 10. I have a bit of a professional bias. I take off points easily.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What do you think it needs to do better?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE KREITMANN: So I think some of these small reactions could be smoother.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Smoothing out the ride to get its passengers in a faster lane for the new year.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s pretty cool, but to me, it would have -- the only way I would be comfortable is if every vehicle was --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Driverless.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- because you can`t compensate for the mistakes that humans make.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, I mean, the statistics were interesting, that it, at least for \uc2 Waymo\uc2  based on their data, that it`s safer than when there are drivers. But there`s just something, for me, still unnatural about seeing no one in the driver`s seat.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : No one in the driver`s seat.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There was that shot where Kris was talking and the driver`s seats is empty, and I`m like, what`s happening? And it turns to him back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I did turn to you. I was like, wait, there`s really no one in that 