c2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : But --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because I don`t know that one.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : But --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Bring back "Santa Baby."\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Michelle, no way. No way.\par \par \uc2 Well, we begin this morning and this hour some potential major challenges for last minute holiday shoppers, including some breaking news on flights as winter weather threatens to disrupt some trips in the Northeast and on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 One airlines technical difficulties are causing major headaches. Skyler Henry is at Atlanta`s \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson International Airport\uc2 , which is the busiest airport in the country.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Michelle, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Yes, passengers on some of those \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flights throughout the country may be spending a bit more time at their respective airports, and that is because the airline says they`re experiencing technical issues and is a full ground stop for all of its flights throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been seeing reactions on social media from passengers leaving from Chicago, Pittsburgh, even reports from our \uc2 CBS\uc2  affiliate, WFOR of some delays down in Fort Lauderdale and Miami, all expressing their frustrations and concerns about what could potentially be happening.\par \par \uc2 We have asked what`s going on here at \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson International Airport\uc2  in Atlanta. Not many delays, but it`s also because this is an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  hub, a bigger Delta hub, if you will.\par \par \uc2 But nearly 120 million people are expected to be on the move this holiday season, and today, many are hoping to make it to their destinations in time for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  expects to screen nearly 40 million passengers from December 19th through January 2nd. That`s a six percent increase from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 For those who plan to skip the long lines at the airport and hit the road, you`re in for a treat. According to AAA, gas prices are down eight cents a gallon compared to last year.\par \par \uc2 But another issue bringing possible disruption, the weather. In the northeast, from Maine to New York to Baltimore, residents are seeing more snow this morning, that could cause delays. Parts of New England could get two to six inches of snow, and further south, some ice is possible around Philadelphia, Baltimore, and Washington.\par \par \uc2 Now the West is getting slammed with heavy winds and rain that could cause dangerous flooding and damage, like this wharf in Santa Cruz, California that partially collapsed as a result of violent waves.\par \par \uc2 Now, a bit of good news for those that are driving today, less traffic on the roadways, but the real Holiday rush, guys, is actually after Christmas. Many airports like this one anticipate a very busy day come this Friday. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Skyler, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In today`s "Money Watch," two of the world`s best known automakers are planning to merge as the industry transitions away from gas powered vehicles. If a deal is reached, \uc2 Honda\uc2  and \uc2 Nissan\uc2  would become the third largest car company in the world, behind \uc2 Toyota\uc2  and \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS News "Money Watch" correspondent, Kelly O`Grady is here to break down what this could mean for your next car purchase.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Good morning to you all.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so the two Japanese car makers, they have formally agreed to hold talks over the next six months, along with \uc2 Mitsubishi\uc2 . The hope is a deal could be reached by June and then executed in August of 2026.\par \par \uc2 So the deal is seen as a lifeline for \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , which recently cut 9,000 jobs and reduced its production by 20 percent with declining sales in China.\par \par \uc2 Now, given any deal, it is 18 months out. I mean, drivers won`t see changes immediately, but a merger could mean more affordable vehicles in the long run.\par \par \uc2 So combining development efforts could mean cost savings that are passed on to the driver. We could also see enhanced EV technology and new models brought to market faster.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, automakers, they have struggled with the transition away from gas cars. This merger would be a play for \uc2 Honda\uc2  and \uc2 Nissan\uc2  to kind of stay alive in the EV race.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, I wonder how it would affect jobs.\par \par \uc2 So Kelly, how much is this being driven by competition from \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and the Chinese automakers?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: A big part of it.\par \par \uc2 Look bigger is better when you`re going to invest in new technology. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has that first mover advantage with EVs. The Chinese car makers, they have government subsidies, right? So combining forces, \uc2 Honda\uc2  and \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , they could really make a run at those.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes, I`m curious what will happen with \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , but that`s a whole other story.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`GRADY: Right.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Kelly O`Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, ahead, I tried a workout class at Club Cardio, the North Carolina gym behind viral videos, and why they decided to pack up their steps and tour the country with the help of their close knit gym members.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we revisit one of our most delicious Dish recipe segments, peanut butter cookies.\par \par \uc2 I`m told they make -- you can make them in 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They say.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : That seems like a long time. It is 8:07, time to check your (LOCAL WEATHER).\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, one heck of a Christmas tip from author, James Patterson.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Alchemy"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus, we visit the singer-songwriters behind the band, Johnnyswim to see how they`re always creating together, and I joined them to help them write a new song.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Our next story is sure to inspire you this holiday season. This is Club Cardio, where hip-hop meets step aerobics.\par \par \uc2 It`s easy to see why workout clips from the gym Step Reloaded Class have been viewed tens of millions of times on social media.\par \par \uc2 The group has gained some big name fans like Grammy-winning singer Ciara. She hired the instructors to choreograph part of a music video back in 2022.\par \par \uc2 So we went to North Carolina to try a class and find out what makes the family business so special.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Club Cardio is a step up from your average gym.\par \par \uc2 The energy doesn`t quite match the location, a largely abandoned strip mall in Raleigh, North Carolina, but at least the house is packed these days.\par \par \uc2 What was it like in the beginning dancing to empty rooms?\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: It was very empty. You can hear echo.\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Yes. It was quiet. And when no one would show, we`d just kind of do it for ourselves. It kind of drew us closer, like we`re not there yet, but y`all were not going to give up.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : In 2018, Sherry Ringer was struggling to find a workout she liked, so she pitched an idea to her daughter, Trinity and the rest of the family.\par \par \uc2 Hip-hop meets step aerobics.\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: I`ve seen the videos with the little headbands on. I don`t know if that`s really my vibe, but --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Are you saying Jane Fonda songs?\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jane Fonda Step Aerobics and Abdominal Workout)\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Now that. No, let me see what I can do with it. I think in our culture, we just dance at anything. So for us to actually be able to dance and include fitness was important to me.\par \par \uc2 I was cheerleader dancer, two-time Dance World champion.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : What?\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Look at you.\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: So, yes. It was --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : The Simone Biles of cheerleaders.\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Ain`t it? Okay.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : The Ringers opened their gym in February of 2020, but the pandemic had other plans. They took the business fully online.\par \par \uc2 How many employees do you have on staff? I mean, how many people --\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: You`re looking at them.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : -- does it take to run this operation?\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: It takes all of us and --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Who is all of us?\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: All of us in this room.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : That`s it?\par \par \uc2 SHERRY SINGER: That`s it. And we look at each other every day and say, we`ve got to find a new gig.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : With Sherry`s son El (sp?) behind the camera, and Trinity`s co-instructor, Peyton Silver on the step. The video started to pick up seen.\par \par \uc2 Fans online started asking --\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Hey, can you come to Chicago? Or hey, can come here? Like, sure. I mean, if we can find something there, let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : So they took the show on the road, lugging their steps to any gym that would host them.\par \par \uc2 Some of their members joined in, including one man who the internet has dubbed --\par \par \uc2 White chocolate.\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Oh yes. He`s a fan favorite.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : I love people who are comfortable in places you wouldn`t expect them to be comfortable. I just love that. Have you seen some of these comments?\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: We see some of them like, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : He is invited to the cookout, the barbecue, the family reunion and spades night.\par \par \uc2 When I ask for a white mocha at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , this what I mean.\par \par \uc2 Have you helped to bring him out more?\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: Absolutely,\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: When you come in here, we`re going to hype you up. We don`t care how you look.\par \par \uc2 JOHN WARD (White Chocolate): As soon as the music starts, all your troubles are gone.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : We caught up with John Ward, aka White Chocolate at Club Cardio`s tour stop in New York.\par \par \uc2 JOHN WARD: I am telling you, just like that. As soon as you walk in. I mean, people pump you up.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Shaniqua Lassiter (sp?) says she lost more than 40 pounds at Club Cardio.\par \par \uc2 SHANIQUA LASSITER (Club Cardio member): Non-traditional fitness can be fun. You don`t even realize you are burning calories.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Self-admitted groupie, Cinda Adams (sp?) has traveled to tour stops in three different states.\par \par \uc2 CINDA ADAMS (Member): I can love my body, enjoy myself, have fun, meet great people. And this is just to me what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : I have a quick little video.\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: Oh gosh.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : From a fan.\par \par \uc2 CIARA (Singer): It is Ciara here, and I just want to take this moment to shout you out, to big you up and tell you I am so proud of you.\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 CIARA: You know, when I first met you and Peyton at the dance studio for my video "Jump," I knew that I was experiencing something very special. So don`t stop. Don`t stop dancing. Don`t stop doing it all. Okay. Bye, guys.\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 TRINITY: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Oh no. He is crying.\par \par \uc2 SHERRY RINGER: Let me tell you something. This place and this space, and what we do have has changed people`s lives. Some of these people have never even picked up a weight before. To see that same person go from learning how to do something simple, like a bicep curl to train at 5:00 AM in the morning, it`s indescribable.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : With connection at the heart of its business model, Club Cardio has built a movement, we should all just try to keep up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Practice, practice, practice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How did you know those steps already?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Practice, practice, practice. You know, they are cool, and they are so engaged, and they`re on tour. They`re going to Miami, Charlotte, and Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : In the new year. So if you live in any of those cities. Go for it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They`re all heart. They are all heart. All soul.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love it.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead, our series "The Dish" shares an easy peanut butter cookie recipe. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Now, to a special holiday installment of our series, "The Dish: Recipe." It is a spinoff of our James Beard Award winning series, "The Dish" Where we focus on just one easy to follow recipe.\par \par \uc2 Today, our friends at New York Times Cooking share a sweet and salty cookie to get you in the holiday spirit. These peanut butter cookies have a fudgy center and crispy edges and they come together in less than 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GENEVIEVE KO (NYT Cooking deputy editor): Good morning. I`m Genevieve Ko deputy editor at NYT Cooking, and today we`re making peanut butter cookies. They`re definitely the first cookie that I ever baked.\par \par \uc2 They`re super fun to make with kids, or even just to let kids make on their own.\par \par \uc2 The ingredients you need to make the dish are all-purpose flour, baking powder, unsalted butter, granulated sugar, brown sugar, salted peanut butter, egg, vanilla extract, peanut butter chips and roasted peanuts.\par \par \uc2 So the first step is to whisk all-purpose flour, baking powder and sea salt. This actually doesn`t use that much flour, because you really want to get the peanut butteriness to come through.\par \par \uc2 So once all the dry ingredients are mixed, then we`ll start with the rest of the ingredients.\par \par \uc2 Half a cup of unsalted butter, half a cup of granulated sugar, half a cup of brown sugar, and you`re just going to smash the butter into the two sugars. This is nice and creamy. Everything is all mixed up. You can`t see the distinction between the sugar and the butter, and that`s when you know you`re ready to add your next ingredients.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to add a whole cup of peanut butter. I like crunchy peanut butter, but you can absolutely use creamy too, and I do like to use salted peanut butter.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to add an egg and two teaspoons of vanilla extract, so this is nice and smooth now, so we know that it`s ready for the flour.\par \par \uc2 Once you add the flour, you want to actually not stir too hard. The more you stir it with the flour, you might end up with too tough a cookie.\par \par \uc2 And at this point your cookie dough is done. But I have two different add ins for these peanut butter cookies that make them really extra special. The first thing is peanut butter chips, and these give the cookies this amazing, almost candy like texture.\par \par \uc2 And then if you want extra crunch, you can also throw in lightly salted, roasted peanuts.\par \par \uc2 So all you`re doing is folding them in really gently. So you can see, this dough really just came together in five minutes. It`s super-fast. We`re just going to scoop the cookie out onto the sheets, you can see, and it just plops right on there.\par \par \uc2 You can get anywhere from 40 to 50 cookies out of this dough. So the cookies are ready to go into a 350-degree oven, and they ideally bake on the middle rack for 10 minutes or so. So the first batch is done, and I`m so excited, because they look so perfect.\par \par \uc2 Oh my God, when you taste this warm, it is just a total taste of the holidays. It`s just the most comforting, cozy cookie. And I love the balance of salty and sweet in these cookies. It just really hits all the notes for holiday baking.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. Knockings lifestyle expert, Gabrielle Kerr is here with a portable charger that can power a number of different devices at an exclusive discount.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************************************\par \par \uc2 (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Love this music. The Boss.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Boss. Bruce Springsteen.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes. I am the boss right now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey boss. You`re the boss now.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What`s the latest here?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re the boss.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the talk of the table this morning. 