rcent. You`re going to save $80.00. Two times more suction than comparable hand vacs and a larger dust bin and a HEPA filter so you don`t have to empty as often.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Gabrielle Kerr, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We partner, of course, with all these companies to get these deals. So to buy anything you saw today, scan the QR code on your screen, or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 All right ahead, Vlad shows us how the duo behind the band, Johnnyswim went from writing songs together in Nashville to creating music and touring as a married couple. They are such a cute couple. You`ve got to see this. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fairy Tale of New York"/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is the band Johnnyswim`s rendition of The Pogue`s classic holiday song "Fairy Tale of New York."\par \par \uc2 Members, Abner Ramirez and Amanda Sudano-Ramirez blend genres like folk, rock, blues and pop to create eclectic sounds and soulful anthems. They`ve been writing and singing relatable music for more than 10 years, and I got the chance to visit the duo in Los Angeles at the beginning of the summer to find out the secret to their success.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Home"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ (Johnnyswim): Started writing songs and the next you know, we made out and then the next thing you know, we have a mortgage and three kids and here we are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And they are Johnnyswim.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Home"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I saw comment on one of the videos that you did: "These two are clearly so in love with each other."\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: I think what people pick up is that we really like each other.\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ (Johnnyswim): We did not want to be the cutesy couple band.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A songwriting session brought Abner Ramirez and Amanda Sudano-Ramirez together nearly 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Take me back to the first writing session for both of you together.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: I think the first time, there was like a group of people over.\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: We wrote a song with a group of people?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: There was a group of people. It was very Nashville.\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: Very Nashville.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Abner couldn`t help but grab his guitar and reminisce about that day.\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: It was like a really sweet love song and writing with like the girl I am absolutely enamored by.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In 2008, the singer-songwriter duo released their self- titled EP. The following year, they got married. And in 2014, Johnnyswim released their debut album, Diamonds.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Diamonds"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What was it about his music that made you think to yourself, I want to write with him. I want to create with him.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: He was just a great storyteller. He wrote songs that weren`t just trying to be cool.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Adelina"/Live at Rockwood Music Hall/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Storytelling is in this band`s DNA.\par \par \uc2 Where did this fountain of storytelling come from?\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: My father was the ultimate storyteller. He passed 13 years ago, but Dad was always great at putting vocabulary to the groan of a soul, whether it was his own or someone else`s and I thought that was one of my father`s many superpowers, but that`s one that I hope to emulate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Was there ever any question that you were going to take the kids on the road and have them be a part?\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: Uh-uh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: None at all?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: No. Honestly, our son, he never missed a show.\par \par \uc2 And he just turned seven and he lost his first top tooth.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a life Amanda was familiar with. Her parents are the late disco Queen Donna Summer, and music producer, Bruce Sudano.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Last Dance"/Donna Summer/The Midnight Special/1978)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A fact she would hesitate to bring up as the band`s popularity was growing.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: We were starting out and that was the first thing that always came up. It was like, so you`re Donna Summer`s daughter? So you`re going to do dance music?\par \par \uc2 And so for us, we were just made the choice of like, limit the conversation of that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Instead, they found a voice of their own. Everyday life shows up in their music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Alchemy"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: I feel like a lot of our songs, our diary entries, like a lot of it lyrically are just stuff that like we`ve jotted down.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Devastating"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like their song, "Devastating," which reflects on relationships` ups and downs, and even therapy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Devastating"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: One thing that I learned in therapy together, we weren`t going to try to find a solution to a massive wound, right. We were trying to find a fire extinguisher, make sure we were equipped for if things ever did get wonky.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "In The Kitchen with Abner and Amanda"/Magnolia Network)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Home is a safe space and also their creative outlet. It`s where they produce a cooking show.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "In The Kitchen with Abner and Amanda"/Magnolia Network)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Make their own merchandise.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "In The Kitchen with Abner and Amanda"/Magnolia Network)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And record their podcast, "Songs with Strangers."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Songs with Strangers")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When I heard about "Songs with Strangers," I was like, let me see what they think.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The story is what would it be like to be Black in the 1960s driving down a lonely road down south and see a police car behind you.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They got to work, writing a song all within 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 What`s fascinating to me is that your primary art is music, but you seem to be able to create art of almost every kind at a moment`s notice together.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: Yes, like our stabilizing factors in life, I think are creativity and community.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We witnessed their community as family and friends came over for pizza night.\par \par \uc2 Did you put some garlic, Mama is asking?\par \par \uc2 (ABNER RAMIREZ speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They say family is their responsibility, just as important is being the artists they were meant to be.\par \par \uc2 Do you wonder why aren`t you bigger?\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: I really don`t think there`s anybody on Earth that can do what I -- what I do. I think I`m married to the greatest songwriter on the Planet Earth. These are things I believe in how I see the world.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Frank Gehry"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 ABNER RAMIREZ: Ultimately, my goal isn`t to be huge and famous. My goal is to help people. My goal is to build community to affect change. It`s for people to feel seen like they would if they`re having dinner with my dad.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Frank Gehry"/Johnnyswim/Mr. and Mrs. Swim LLC)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA SUDANO-RAMIREZ: We probably don`t appeal to a lot of people because our music is our music, but the people that it appeals to were meant for it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : What a dynamic duo.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are favorite band, they`re so -- God, the creative force that compels them to create this art, to bring people together and to feel seen, is incredibly powerful.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : And listening to it, I`m exhaling.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So good. Love them so much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Merry Christmas, everybody. That does it for us.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Happy Birthday, Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Happy Birthday, Tony.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241225ekco00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For December 20, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Imtiaz Tyab, Rob Marciano, Kris Van Cleave, Elaine Cobbe, Nikole  Killion, Steve Hartman, Carter Evans, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 3,679 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Breaking news: deadly attack on a busy Christmas market in Germany. A car drives into a crowd of people. Why officials say it appears deliberate. The new information.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): We will not have a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Shutdown averted? The 11th-hour House vote to keep the government open.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TRAVELER: I usually travel on Fridays, and this is the worst it`s been for me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: With about a third of Americans expected to travel over the holidays, how bad weather is already impacting the rush.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Are you guys serious?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we go On the Road in this season of giving.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: Their joy, that`s the gift to you.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: This was really a life-changer for me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROSEMARIE HERNANDEZ (Gift Recipient): Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good evening. I`m Margaret Brennan, in for Norah tonight.\par \par \uc2 We begin with an attack at a Christmas market in Eastern Germany, an event that could impact security measures at holiday events around the world. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  just announced that it will deploy additional resources in New York City out of an abundance of caution.\par \par \uc2 In Germany, at least two people are dead and dozens injured after a suspect plowed a vehicle through a crowd. Video from the scene shows first responders and police swarming to the area. The driver is in custody, and we are learning new information about the suspect.\par \par \uc2 It happened in Magdeburg, a city of about 240,000 people roughly 100 miles southwest of Berlin. Today`s attack came eight years and a day after a man drove a truck through a Christmas market in Berlin, killing 12 people, and \uc2 ISIS\uc2  claimed responsibility.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Cobbe leads us off with the details on today`s attack.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE (voice-over): A trail of death and destruction in Germany as a car plowed into a crowded Christmas market in the town of Magdeburg. At least two people are dead, one of them a small child.\par \par \uc2 Emergency services rushed to help the injured, at least 60 people, authorities say. Hospitals in Magdeburg and neighboring cities have cleared beds for an influx of seriously wounded people.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: Police arrested the driver on the scene. His motivation is still unconfirmed.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: "We have caught the perpetrator," said the premier of Saxony- Anhalt. "He is a doctor from Saudi Arabia who has been working here and has been in Germany since 2006."\par \par \uc2 German officials had warned people to be vigilant at Christmas markets, which are popular for holiday gift shopping and family outings. And it`s eight years almost to the day since a terror attack on the Berlin Christmas market, which left 12 people dead.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE COBBE: German police believe the suspect acted alone and they suspect he had an explosive device in the car -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Elaine Cobbe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to this breaking news from Capitol Hill. The House voted a short time ago to approve a stopgap plan to fund the government until mid-March. It appears a partial government shutdown will be averted. But what does this indicate about how hard it will be for Republicans to govern after president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  takes office next month?\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion has the late developments from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): With hours to go...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MARK ALFORD (R-Missouri): `Twas five days before Christmas, and all through this House, not a lawmaker was resting, not even their spouse.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: ... the House rushed through another plan to keep the government open before a midnight deadline.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): This is a good outcome for the country. I think he`s certainly is happy about this outcome as well.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The latest version would extend government funding through mid-March, retain more than $100 billion in disaster aid, and keep $10 billion in economic assistance for farmers.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ANTHONY D`ESPOSITO (R-New York): I would hope soon. Christmas is right around the corner, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Congressman...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ANTHONY D`ESPOSITO: We know two things are happening. We`re going to make a decision and Santa`s coming.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But it no longer includes a key provision pushed by president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to suspend the debt limit, which would have provided more leeway for his agenda by giving the U.S. more borrowing authority.\par \par \uc2 Have you briefed the president-elect, yes or no, on this plan?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE STEVE SCALISE (R-Louisiana): The president`s very interested in how his administration will start in January, so we want him to be on a footing for success.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Earlier, the president-elect said: "If there is going to be a shutdown of government, let it begin now, under the Biden administration."\par \par \uc2 The White House fired back.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): That Republicans blew up this deal. They did. And they need to fix this, period.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In the event of a shutdown, hundreds of thousands of federal workers could be furloughed. Essential employees like active-duty military and federal law enforcement would be forced to work without pay, and national parks and museums would close. But mail would still be delivered, Social Security checks would still go out, and the upcoming inaugural ceremony would proceed as planned.\par \par \uc2 TED KENDALL (President, Gaddis Farms): So, we have been following the deliberations.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: For Ted Kendall, a fifth-generation farmer from Bolton, Mississippi, who`s counting on additional farm aid, his livelihood is on the line.\par \par \uc2 TED KENDALL: We need this assistance in farm country. If we don`t get some help, a lot of farmers are not going to be able to survive.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The last government shutdown was back in 2019. It lasted more than a month and cost the U.S. economy roughly $11 billion. Tonight`s vote comes as lawmakers are set to recess for the holidays -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And perhaps narrowly avoiding one.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 A record number of Americans are expected to be on the move over the holidays. AAA estimates nearly 120 million people will pack the nation`s airports and highways between now and New Year`s Day.\par \par \uc2 Our senior transportation correspondent, Kris Van Cleave, has everything you need to know about the holiday rush.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): Tonight, dreams of trouble-free travels dance in the heads of fliers from coast to coast, as an estimated 2.8 million deck the halls of the nation`s airports. The airlines are expecting record crowds, leading to these long lines at Seattle`s airport starting around 3:30 this morning.\par \par \uc2 ANNMARIE STEINOBRUN (Traveler): That was definitely the busiest I have ever seen any airport.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Annmarie Steinobrun (sp?) missed her flight to Orlando last night after getting stuck in an airport traffic jam.\par \par \uc2 How frustrating is that? You`re sitting at the airport, but you can`t get to the terminal.\par \par \uc2 ANNMARIE STEINOBRUN: It`s super frustrating. And it`s like -- it`s just like watching the minutes tick down. I was crying at the desk. The poor gate agent.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This guy`s not going to make it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And millions more are watching the weather. Snow and frigid temperatures mean treacherous driving in parts of the Midwest and Northeast, and it`s also delaying thousands of flights.\par \par \uc2 TRAVELER: We left our house around like 9:00 exactly, and so it was supposed to be like a 30-minute trip, but it turned into an hour.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: AAA expects Sunday will be the busiest on the roads, just as the East Coast is hit with an arctic blast. An estimated 107 million will be driving for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Sunday and the Friday after Christmas are also expected to be very, very busy at the airports, but the travel rush runs all the way through Monday, January 6 -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we know you will be tracking it all, Kris Van Cleave, in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Well, major airports in the Northeast could be impacted by bad weather tonight. Winter weather advisories are in effect from the Great Lakes into the mid-Atlantic region.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist Rob Marciano is tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 Good evening to you, Rob.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO: Good evening, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 No doubt they were de-icing planes across the Northeast. It was a snow globe at times, with the snow coming down hard, accumulating on the grass. But the roads were mostly OK. This was supposed to be a fast clipper system, but it kind of got some juice from the ocean, a coastal low developing. On the radar, you can see still very active.\par \par \uc2 Matter of fact, that will continue to fill in overnight tonight, especially across Eastern New England, Boston, Eastern Mass. You may wake up to two to four inches of snowfall there. And then it all clears out. The winds are going to pick up tomorrow afternoon for everybody in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 And windchills Sunday morning will be in the teens, single digits, even below freezing in through parts of New England, warming up a little bit heading into Christmas Eve. That`s a problem on both coasts, strong system coming into the West Coast,and that traverses the country, also in the Southern Plains, up the Northeast, rain and another wet snow event here in the Northeast come Christmas Eve.\par \par \uc2 Reminder, tomorrow is the winter solstice. So it`s the shortest day of the year, also the coldest so far this year for a lot -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Stay warm, Rob. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now we will go overseas to Syria, where today the top U.S. official in charge of freeing Americans held overseas visited Damascus for the first time since the fall of the Assad regime. Roger Carstens is leading the search for answers about Austin Tice, a Marine veteran and American journalist who vanished in Syria more than 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with Carstens in an exclusive interview for this Sunday`s Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You told reporters that you were amazed at the number of secret prisons that Bashar al-Assad had, more than 40 of them. Have those prisons been searched yet, and do you know if journalist Austin Tice was held in them?\par \par \uc2 ROGER CARSTENS (U.S. Special Presidential Envoy for Hostage Affairs): So it`s not going to shock me to find out that there are prisons that have yet to be discovered and searched. I would say, if I`m stunned by anything, it`s just the amount of prisons.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you would almost think that, if you were running a country, and you wanted to jail your enemies, you would have one prison and it wouldn`t be secret. But to have like 35 or 40 secret prisons, I find that just a horrifying disturbing and yet, in a way, fascinating.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ahmed al-Shar`a, the head of HTS, his group and he himself are still technically designated as terrorists under U.S. law because of prior affiliation with al Qaeda.\par \par \uc2 Do you trust that they are being helpful to the U.S. now?\par \par \uc2 ROGER CARSTENS: Today, we conducted a joint search of a facility that we thought -- we all thought would have some sort of relation to Austin Tice. So, I understand, of course, their past.\par \par \uc2 What I can tell you right -- is that, right now, they`re being helpful in the search for Austin.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you conducted a joint search with HTS of a prison today. Did you believe that that is where Austin was being held?\par \par \uc2 ROGER CARSTENS: You know, Margaret, we had a lot of information over the last 12 years that pointed to a variety of facilities.\par \par \uc2 And in nicking that down over 12 years, we came up with a priority list of about six sites. And of those six sites, we felt that this had the highest probability of having held Austin at one time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you can see more of our interview this Sunday on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Austin Tice is just one of the thousands missing in Syria.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab spoke with people desperate to find their loved ones who disappeared during Bashar al-Assad`s brutal reign.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voice-over): They come looking for answers, scrutinizing what has become a wall of pain outside a hospital in Damascus, hoping in these photos to find some trace of their loved ones disappeared into Syria`s notorious prison system during the brutal 13-year civil war.\par \par \uc2 Among them is Syrian-American Sana Mustafa, posting her own agony on the wall, missing posters of her father, Ali Mustafa, who hasn`t been seen or heard from in 11 years after he was detained by Bashar al-Assad`s secret police.\par \par \uc2 What are you hoping to find?\par \par \uc2 SANA MUSTAFA (Syrian-American): Answers. We deserve an answer. Like, the most simple, basic human right is to know if someone alive or dead.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Under Assad`s long and oppressive rule, even the slightest criticism could land someone in prison, where they were tortured and starved, sometimes to death.\par \par \uc2 What do you miss the most about your dad?\par \par \uc2 SANA MUSTAFA: I miss having a dad, you know, having someone to call baba.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: While we were talking, one person...\par \par \uc2 SANA MUSTAFA: This is his friend.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: ... after the other...\par \par \uc2 SANA MUSTAFA: Eleven years, 12 years. Her husband and her brother have been disappeared.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: ... showed us photos of their disappeared loved ones, faces etched with grief.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Everyone here has a story of pain and loss, and the numbers are staggering. According to Syrian human rights groups, at least 100,000 people have been disappeared in Assad`s prisons.\par \par \uc2 But many believe that that number is far, far higher. The fear is, many of those disappeared are buried here in newly uncovered mass graves.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling right now? I mean, you`re here with so many people who hold your pain.\par \par \uc2 SANA MUSTAFA: I feel -- I -- you know, I feel in community. I feel grateful. I know we`re all in this together and we`re searching for our loved ones together.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: A shared strength found at this seemingly endless wall of pain.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab, CBS News, Damascus.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And workers at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  are staging pro- holiday labor strikes.\par \par \uc2 What you need to know is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Employees at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores started a five-day strike today in several U.S. cities, including Chicago and Los Angeles, a protest that could soon spread to hundreds of stores.\par \par \uc2 And workers are also on strike at more than a half-dozen \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facilities, with more locations expected to join.\par \par \uc2 We get details now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #1: No contract!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: No coffee!\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voice-over): The year is ending the way it began, on the picket line. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers say trouble`s been brewing over pay raises.\par \par \uc2 What are union members looking for in terms of wages?\par \par \uc2 CASSIE PRITCHARD (\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Employee): Fair wages that are livable. You should be able to afford rent, food and health care.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Cassie Pritchard (sp?) says the offer of a 1.5 percent yearly raise won`t even keep up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE PRITCHARD: It`s a pay cut in reality. It`s a nominal pay raise, but it`s only in name.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER #2: If we don`t get it!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Shut it down!\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  started picketing at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  delivery hubs on Thursday, supported by almost 10,000 frustrated drivers and warehouse workers.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VALDEZ (Driver): The workload has progressively gone up. The algorithm sees you and says, OK, she can do this many in this many hours. I`m going to give her more.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: After six years, Vanessa Valdez (sp?) still doesn`t get any sick days. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says drivers like her are not staff, but outside contractors.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VALDEZ: It`s an easy way to wash their hands of us, so we end up voiceless.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We shut it down!\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: 2024 has been a year of strikes, including autoworkers, longshoremen and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  machinists. Union members say they`re struggling with the cost of living, while corporate executives bank record high salaries.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ` new CEO, \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , stands to make more than $100 million in his first year.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE PRITCHARD: Our CEO is the face of corporate greed in America right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, the strikes aren`t expected to have a big impact on overall operations. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says most packages should still arrive on time.\par \par \uc2 And, as for \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , well, employees only shut down a handful of stores like this one behind me. But they do plan to expand the walkout to possibly hundreds of stores through Christmas Eve -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Carter Evans in California.\par \par \uc2 Well, there is news tonight about America`s largest chain of party stores. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A 52-year-old Indiana man was sentenced to 130 years in prison today for the murders of two teenage girls who vanished during a winter hike near their hometown of \uc2 Delphi\uc2 , Indiana in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Richard Allen was convicted last month of killing 13-year-old Abigail Williams and 14-year-old Liberty German. Allen was spotted in a blurry \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  image posted by one of the victims after they hiked across a bridge, and that became a key piece of evidence in the case.\par \par \uc2 Well, according to CNN, the party is over for this company, Party City, which is the largest retail chain of its kind in the U.S. Their CEO reportedly told workers all of the stores are closing and today was the last day of work for corporate employees. CBS News cannot confirm that story at this time, but because Party City was in business for 40 years and filed for bankruptcy last year, we`re letting you know.\par \par \uc2 And stick with us. There`s more on the other side of this break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A group of kids in Phoenix shared some holiday kindness.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voice-over): The red caps were the only clue.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: Yes!\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The only hint that something Christmas was afoot.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Something that would soon strike straight to the heart.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Are you guys serious?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: As we first reported last year, the kids responsible for these moments of joy are students and former students of Derek Brown.\par \par \uc2 DEREK BROWN (Teacher): Also, lights, please.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A Phoenix elementary teacher who uses our On the Road stories to teach kindness and character.\par \par \uc2 A perennial favorite?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Secret Santa, that wealthy businessman who every year gives out hundreds of $100 bills to random strangers.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #3: I was, like, shocked, because, well, who does that?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: I have never seen anyone, like, give -- just give money away like that.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Could do you imagine that someday it would be you?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: No, not ever.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And so, with guidance from Mr. Brown...\par \par \uc2 DEREK BROWN: I sent everybody an itinerary.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... the kids started a Secret Santa club and began fund- raising, calling friends, family, and businesses. They raised $8,000 without any help from their school or district, just so they could turn around and give it all away...\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: It`s OK.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... to people like Rosemarie Hernandez (sp?). Rosemarie had been out of work for a week.\par \par \uc2 ROSEMARIE HERNANDEZ (Gift Recipient): It will give me a lot of relief. Thank you, thank you, thank you. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #5: You get so many feelings in your body that just makes you like want to do it again.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Which is why, a few weeks ago, they did it again, this time with even more students handing out even more money.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: They gave out $10,000 this season, but received something priceless.\par \par \uc2 CUSTOMERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #6: Their joy, that`s the gift to you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Exactly the realization Mr. Brown was hoping for.\par \par \uc2 DEREK BROWN: I want this memory to be so strong that it now drives them every day in everything they do.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Did today change you?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: Definitely. I never felt this way in my life. So this was really a life-changer for me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Whoever said money can`t buy happiness obviously never gave it away.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman On the Road in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: God bless you too. Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that is tonight`s CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Margaret Brennan, and I will see you Sunday on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241221ekck00001\par }