train derailment in West Texas caught on camera.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Stop. Stop.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Police in South Carolina catching a burglar, green handed.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Footage posted by police shows the moment that officers took the Grinch into custody.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: The effort to bring two Boeing Starliner astronauts home is delayed once again.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): A fresh crew needs to launch before Wilmore and Williams can return, and there`s a delay in launching the replacements. Oh, gee. I wonder why.\par \par \uc2 Okay, everybody. Listen up. Who wants to replace the crew that we stranded in space for a year? Go. Hands up where I can see them. Come on. Nobody gets Tang until somebody volunteers.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It doesn`t sound very attractive, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, it doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hands up where I can see them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The next crew is going to have a very big roller bag. It could be a long journey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 Listen, we have a fantastic show for you today and some great stories coming up. Gayle talks with the amazing, Alicia Keys. I`ve got a sneak preview, and I have to say that you are going to love the surprise at the end, so stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 And I visit the iconic Apollo Theater, as it`s honored by the Kennedy Center.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nothing like the Apollo Theater.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with the breaking news overnight, there`s been a surprising development in connection with Monday`s school shooting in Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Officials now say that the 15-year-old girl who killed two others and then herself had been talking online to a 20-year-old man. He allegedly told her that he would also carry out an attack thousands of miles away.\par \par \uc2 Police have now searched this man`s home. He lives in Carlsbad, California, and a judge has ordered him to turn over any weapons that he has.\par \par \uc2 Ian Lee has more from Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Ian, good morning to you. This story gets stranger and stranger.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: It does.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday night, a judge in California granted police permission to seize the weapons of a 20-year-old man, according to the court order obtained by the \uc2 CBS\uc2  affiliate in San Diego. This comes as authorities continue to look into Natalie Rupnow, the 15-year-old responsible for Monday`s shooting and her alleged messages with that man.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Court documents obtained by \uc2 CBS\uc2  affiliate KFMB, say 20-year-old Alexander Paffendorf admitted to \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents that he was plotting a mass shooting with Natalie Rupnow, and told her that he would arm himself with explosives and a gun and target a government building. He didn`t specify which building or when he planned to launch the attack.\par \par \uc2 The alleged connection between the two comes as Madison`s police chief told The Associated Press Wednesday they do not know if Monday`s school attack was planned in advance, but are investigating 15-year-old Rupnow`s social media for a motive that led her to enter the school with two handguns and kill 43-year-old Erin West and 14-year-old Rubi Patricia Vergara before killing herself in a study hall classroom.\par \par \uc2 According to court records, the shooter was enrolled in therapy in 2022.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We saw police officers look like a SWAT team, some snipers across the street.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: A neighbor said he saw police enter the shooter`s father`s home Monday.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Really, really shocking, the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: So there was no indication from this family --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Not that I know of.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: -- that something like this could happen.\par \par \uc2 MAN: No, definitely not.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Tributes poured in for the beloved Abundant Life Christian School substitute, coordinator and ninth grade student.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL BRUBE (7th grade student): They always cared about others, and they were also very playful and very joyful and they always like carry positivity around.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Seventh grade student, Angel Brube said Erin West was someone he knew well.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL BRUBE: She was always caring, always spread positivity around, and just made everybody feel happy.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: And she sounds like she was a very important person to you as well.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL BRUBE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: It`s important to note that the order filed against Paffendorf is a civil action, not criminal. When asked to comment about the about Paffendorf, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  declined to comment -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some breaking news that could affect some holiday shopping and shipping.\par \par \uc2 This morning, thousands of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers are on strike in a push for better pay and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Derick Waller of CBS New York is in Queens at one of the facilities that are impacted by the strike.\par \par \uc2 Derick, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DERICK WALLER: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Very active outside this \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facility here in Queens, and we`re really about an hour into this strike that really could halt \uc2 Amazon\uc2  production lines just a week before Christmas, during this busy holiday season.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  says this is "the largest strike against \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in our country`s history." The union says it represents 10,000 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers who are demanding better pay and working conditions. It says the company refused to negotiate with it on behalf of its workers.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  tells us: "The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  claim to represent thousands of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees and drivers. They don`t." Adding the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have actively threatened, intimidated, and attempted to coerce \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees, which is illegal.\par \par \uc2 Now strikes like this are scheduled to pop up in Atlanta, Southern California, the Chicago suburbs, San Francisco later today. I do also want to add that we did recently get a statement from the \uc2 Teamsters Union\uc2 , basically saying that this is really \uc2 Amazon\uc2  intimidating workers and trying to stop them from unionizing.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, these strikes happening all across.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you are worried about your holiday deliveries, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says that this should not impact operations, but the union says that if your deliveries are delayed, you can blame \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . It`s important to note, though, that this union represents just about one percent of Amazon`s workforce.\par \par \uc2 I`ll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Derick, thank you very much. Hope they resolve things.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, end of the year holiday travel, the rush is on. Airports are expected to be the busiest that they`re going to be today, with airlines expecting to carry more than 54 million passengers between now and January 6th. That would be a new record, if they reach it, and some of those flights could be disrupted today by a storm across the Northern US. It threatens more than 11 million people with heavy snow and strong winds.\par \par \uc2 And so for more on all of it, let`s go to our meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 A fast moving clipper will race on the Plains to the Northeast. This afternoon, snow will stretch from North Dakota to Wisconsin. Tonight, it slides into Chicago and Detroit, and tomorrow, we`ll get flakes in the Northeast with a few lingering snow showers into our Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 Now several cities could use the snow, like Madison, Wisconsin, which is over half a foot below average. Minneapolis, Minnesota is down nearly a foot, and these storm totals will chip away at those deficits.\par \par \uc2 Winds will be strong as today, gusting to 60 miles an hour. We could get a brief interruption in power, bumpy air travel, and cross winds could make driving difficult for high profile vehicles. The winds will improve as we head into tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s the temps, over a hundred million will feel above average today. Enjoy it while last, because behind our clipper, cold air drains south, and by Saturday, nearly 200 million in the East will be feeling the chill.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, this will be the coldest air of the season for several spots, including New York City.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, I will get my coat ready. Steph, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to a major step taken by the government in response to those recent drone sightings in the Northeast. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  has issued a ban on flying drones in parts of New Jersey until January 17th without special permission from the government due to "special security reasons."\par \par \uc2 The rule applies to areas now deemed National Defense airspace. Despite this new rule, officials have said there is no evidence the drone spotted recently posed any kind of threat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On Capitol Hill, a bipartisan budget deal to keep the government running in the new year has just collapsed. Republican members backed away yesterday when President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came out against the plan after his close ally, that`s billionaire, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , spent much of the day denouncing it.\par \par \uc2 And now, House Speaker Mike Johnson is facing new questions about his future. Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you. A lot to unpack here.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hey, Gayle. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 When Congress returns here this morning, they`ll have to come up with some plan at the last minute to avert a government shutdown and fund the government after a well-crafted plan collapsed quite quickly last night after \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  intervened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Chaos reigned on Capitol Hill Wednesday, just days before a potentially paralyzing federal government shutdown, which threatens paychecks for millions of federal workers and contractors.\par \par \uc2 REP. THOMAS MASSIE (R-KY): It`s just a train wreck, but it was a train wreck you could see coming.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Congress was on the verge of passing a sweeping 1,500- page plan to fund the government through March offering tens of billions of dollars to homeowners in flood devastated communities and the first pay raise for members of Congress in 15 years until President-elect Donald Trump`s billionaire ally, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  began a relentless series of posts on social media calling the bill "pork," declaring it "dead," and saying any lawmaker who supported it should be voted out of office.\par \par \uc2 Followed by Trump himself, writing that the bill, which will require potentially dozens of Democratic votes, should eliminate Democratic giveaways, sparking this response --\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): House Republicans will now own any harm that is visited upon the American people that results from a government shutdown or worse.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But as CBS News has reported, areas devastated by Hurricane Helene, including flood-ravaged Damascus, Virginia, where Ralph Wilson lost his restaurant were counting on this week`s congressional spending bill to finally start rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 RALPH WILSON (Damascus, Virginia business owner): There`s got to be a light at the end of the tunnel, you know.\par \par \uc2 But it seems like things just keep happening that, you know, set me back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): I cannot imagine how I could go back to the folks in Southwest Virginia and say we`ve kicked the can again on their future because Congress didn`t do its job.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And this uncertainty causes political problems for the US House Speaker. Mike Johnson will begin next year with the narrowest of Republican majorities, with zero votes to spare at times, and he has to win a vote to be the House Speaker January 3rd.\par \par \uc2 And Gayle, one of his Republican colleagues told us last night that Johnson has already lost his support.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, things are getting very dicey.\par \par \uc2 Train wreck and chaos, never words you want to hear in this type of conversation. But Scott, before you go, we keep hearing that there are developments in that ethics investigation concerning Matt Gaetz.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about that? We all know he was Donald Trump`s first pick for attorney general.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The bipartisan House Ethics Committee has voted to eventually release its investigation into allegations that Gaetz denies of sexual misconduct and illicit drug use. Gaetz has criticized this decision, saying he was exonerated by a Justice Department investigation, and this is really a surprise, because typically, the House Ethics Committee drops its cases when somebody resigns, which Gaetz did last month.\par \par \uc2 This will likely come out after members of Congress leave town, but Gayle, Gaetz has acknowledged in a statement last night the investigation will include embarrassing details, and that he was playing hard in his 30s, but again, that he says he was fully exonerated when the Feds investigated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, a lot of people want to read this report still.\par \par \uc2 Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: An attorney for the accused killer, Luigi Mangione tells CBS News he will agree today to go to New York to face charges of murdering UnitedHealthcare CEO, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The 26-year-old suspect, faces a court hearing in Pennsylvania this morning. This means he could be in New York as soon as this afternoon, that`s where he faces 11 charges, including first degree murder as an act of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Breaking news from France, a verdict in a trial that`s gotten worldwide attention. Dominique Pelicot was sentenced to 20 years in prison for drugging his ex-wife Gisele, and then offering her up to other men for sex. Fifty accused men were -- 50 men, rather, were accused of raping her. They were also convicted in this verdict.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Kiniry reports now on the woman at the center of this trial who refused to be anonymous.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY: This is Gisele. Her head held high.\par \par \uc2 At 72 years old, this grandmother from a Paris suburb has become a feminist hero. The trial saw her ex-husband, Dominique face charges for drugging and raping her for nearly a decade and recruiting at least 70 other men to do the same, all while Gisele was unconscious.\par \par \uc2 Police presented evidence in 20,000 videos and photos, the men in the videos, dubbed "Mr. Everyman" by French media from all walks of life.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 LEIGH KINIRY: The case sparked fury and debate about misogyny and consent in France.\par \par \uc2 The case gained worldwide attention. Gisele`s face was on the cover of magazines, and for months, her supporters lined up outside the courtroom day after day, applauding her bravery.\par \par \uc2 "It isn`t bravery," Gisele said in court, "But will and determination to change society. I want to tell all women, when you`re raped, there is shame, and it`s not for us to have shame, it`s for them."\par \par \uc2 Her impact is clear. This sign says, "Gisele, the women thank you."\par \par \uc2 For nearly 10 years, Gisele Pelicot suffered at the hands of her husband and those he invited into her nightmare. Now, that nightmare is over.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Leigh Kiniry.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There will be people who say 20 years is not enough for this man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. I was thinking that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the only reason he was caught is because he was taking up skirt videos of women in a supermarket, and then police looked at the videos and noticed all the other ones of his wife.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After confiscating his phone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: After confiscating his phone and going through his computer. She was snoring because she`s on sleeping pills 10 times the recommended dose.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is what the crime was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is such a disgusting story. And when they say, "Gisele, the women thank you," the bravery for her to speak up under these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, ahead, America`s first severe case of bird flu in a human. We`re going to talk to Dr. Celine Gounder about the risks, but first 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, Gayle`s exclusive interview with global superstar, Alicia Keys and her high school music teacher. How their powerful connection shaped Keys and continues today in her hit musical "Hell`s Kitchen."\par \par \uc2 Plus, from amateur night to Kennedy Center honoree, the Apollo Theater is a center point in American culture. Nate shows how the Harlem landmark became one of the most famous and influential stages in the world.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2 . For a job you`ll love, visit progressive.com/careers.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Health Watch," California has declared a state of emergency after America`s first severe human case of H5N1 bird flu.\par \par \uc2 The Louisiana patient is in critical condition with severe respiratory symptoms following exposure to sick and dead birds. This is the 61st confirmed human bird flu case this year.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is editor-at large-for Public Health at KFF Health News joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so what do we know about the condition of this patient in Louisiana, and how did they get it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So this patient we know is over 65, has chronic medical conditions. They are in critical condition at this moment, and in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 They appear to have been infected by a backyard flock of birds, not commercial poultry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON:. So is it the same strain as what`s going on with dairy cattle?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It is not the same strain. It is a strain that we have seen in wild birds, and also the strain involved with the teenager who was hospitalized in critical condition from H5N1 in British Columbia, Canada.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And so people at home hearing this are going to say, well, so he was handling birds. Is there a concern now though that people could pass it to other people?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Very good question. So yes, his exposure was sick and dead birds. So for those of us at home, do not handle sick or dead animals. Call Animal Control.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of human to human transmission, the concern is, the more we allow spillover into humans, the more likely at some point you might see a mutation that would allow for human to human transmission.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because most people don`t come in contact with sick or dead birds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how big of a concern should all of us feel watching and listening to you?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Right, so for the general public, the risk is still exceedingly low. The people who are at risk are farm workers, people who handle domesticated animals, livestock, and really don`t drink raw milk. It`s really not a good idea, given everything that`s happening.\par \par \uc2 And again, if the more people get exposed to this virus, the more likely you are to see a mutation that will allow for human to human transmission.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So California has reported a state of emergency. Should other states follow suit?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So what the state of emergency allows for is flexibility in hiring contracting. California has really taken the lead here with the amount of testing that they`ve been doing.\par \par \uc2 Many states have not been testing as aggressively, and now there`s a USDA mandate for testing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Dr. Celine Gounder, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey, this little boy is stuck right here on the tracks. Boom.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Yes, that is incredible footage. That`s a Union Pacific train caught on video as it ran into a tractor trailer in West Texas yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Then the train derailed and hit the Chamber of Commerce Building in Pecos City. At least one person was killed and four others were hurt. Officials say three cars on the train carried hazardous materials, but everything was contained.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, that video, very scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, very scary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Reality TV star, that`s Paris Hilton, is using her platform to help thousands of children in residential centers.\par \par \uc2 She was in Washington yesterday as lawmakers overwhelmingly approved a bill to regulate the kind of youth treatment facility where Paris Hilton says she was abused as a teenager.\par \par \uc2 She`s been working on this issue for years now. Ed O`Keefe has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PARIS HILTON (Reality TV star): It is just something that I`ve been praying for every night for years.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: As lawmakers voted to protect kids in residential treatment facilities, the bill`s biggest celebrity supporter cheered on its passage.\par \par \uc2 PARIS HILTON: I just had tears flooding down my face. I was just so excited. It felt like a dream.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Paris Hilton, the hotel heiress, socialite and reality TV star, has tirelessly lobbied lawmakers for more than three years after revealing in a 2020 documentary the alleged abuse she suffered when her parents sent her to troubled youth centers as a teen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "This is Paris"/YouTube Originals)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But she continued to talk about it and took her message to Washington.\par \par \uc2 PARIS HILTON: I was violently restrained and dragged down hallways, stripped naked, and thrown into solitary confinement.\par \par \uc2 My parents were completely deceived, lied to, and manipulated by this for- profit industry about the inhumane treatment I was experiencing.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: While there`s no central way to track these incidents, Hilton and other advocates say abuse at these privately-run facilities is rampant due to a lack of oversight.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): I`m convinced her bill is going to have these standards now so that congregate care facilities know they can`t be abusing kids. They can`t be mistreating kids.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The new law improves tracking of these youth centers and creates a federal database to better share information.\par \par \uc2 PARIS HILTON: We hope this is a message to this industry that people are watching now and they won`t be able to get away with all the abuse they have for so long.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Hilton said more still needs to be done, but this is an amazing first step. The bill is now headed to the president`s desk to be signed into law -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the delivery challenge to get presents under the tree in the farthest regions in America. Up next, someone who is always checking if you are naughty or nice, Kris Van Cleave, that`s right. He went to the North Pole, Alaska for the next story.\par \par \uc2 There he is. Oh, there he is. I see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kris is ready.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Kris, you`re ready to go. Stick around. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that was his list.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Working on my list.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With Christmas and Hanukkah now less than a week away, believe it or not, you are facing some critical deadlines to get your holiday gifts out on time. Today, in fact, the very last day for 