ans live in rural areas, but Alaska is unique. Some communities, delivery can only come by air.\par \par \uc2 This year, \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  is carrying 85 million pounds of cargo, including \uc2 FedEx\uc2  packages to its 19 in-state destinations, including three above the Arctic Circle.\par \par \uc2 From fresh food and milk to supplies, in this time of year, gifts by the plane load.,\par \par \uc2 Many of those gifts stop first at FedEx Anchorage hub, 80,000 packages a day are sorted, 60,000 international, 20,000 going to and from Alaska.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEWIS (Alaska FedEx Senior Manager Surface Operations): We play a vital role in making sure that Santa Claus gets all the packages to where they need to be, whatever can`t fit on display, we kind of take the ball and run with it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Even to North Pole, a town where the street lights are candy canes and this big guy looms large.\par \par \uc2 At the Santa Claus house, it`s beat the shipping clock.\par \par \uc2 They`ve checked their list twice, knowing it`s far more naughty than nice for their gifts to arrive late. These are among the very last boxes shipping out.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BROWN (Order processer): People are used to waiting until the very last minute to shop online, which presents a challenge for us having to process that order and ship it out from Alaska.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It`s Paul Brown`s (sp?) job to make sure that happens.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BROWN: Just being in Alaska is almost like being in a completely separate country, just because you have to add so many days to that shipping timeline.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And I imagine that works in the same way with you getting things here.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BROWN: Very much so. Everything that comes here has to be either flown in or barged in over the ocean. There`s not even a railroad connection.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But there is an airfield about 20 miles down of frozen road from Santa