ATE BURLESON: Guys, it is right outside.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is it? You saw it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, right around the corner.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, all right. Well, we`ll take a trip.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No? Did you really see it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I saw it when I drove in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, I didn`t see it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, all right, we`ll get to it.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are going to deal with the news, though in Syria actually this hour and the continuing fallout from the end of the Assad regime.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab recently toured a facility there allegedly used to manufacture one of the most popular street drugs in the Middle East, yet another way the now exiled dictator apparently profited from other people`s pain.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In a remote corner outside Damascus, a now abandoned potato chip factory that has shined a light on the ousted Bashar al-Assad regime`s many dark, but open secrets.\par \par \uc2 In this storeroom, we found what are effectively the key components of making synthetic drugs. Things like hydrochloric acid and acetic acid -- and there`s lots of it.\par \par \uc2 Industrial quantities of precursor chemicals needed to make one of the most popular street drugs in the Middle East and beyond, Captagon.\par \par \uc2 Wow.\par \par \uc2 Ahmed Abi Yukin (sp?) is with Syria`s ruling Hayat Tahrir al-Sham. He told us this massive haul of Captagon was found just days after the rebel group`s takeover.\par \par \uc2 So these look like essentially coils of wires, but when you open it up inside, it`s hollow and full of Captagon pills.\par \par \uc2 The highly addictive amphetamine is often referred to as poor man`s cocaine. For years, neighboring countries accuse Assad`s Syria of being the world`s main supplier of the illegal drug.\par \par \uc2 Under Assad, Syria is considered a narco state. How does that make you feel as a Syrian.\par \par \uc2 (AHMED ABI YUKIN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "We feel bad for the young people who were addicted to it," he says. "The Assad regime was destroying a generation. They only cared about making money."\par \par \uc2 And the money is staggering. The bankrupt Assad government reportedly raked in up to $5 billion a year from the illicit trade.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re looking at millions of dollars` worth of Captagon here. Are you just going to destroy all of it?\par \par \uc2 (AHMED ABI YUKIN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "Yes, we will destroy it all," he says. "We will eliminate anything that has to do with the criminal, Assad."\par \par \uc2 Just last year, the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  sanctioned two of Bashar al-Assad`s cousins and other Syrians for their involvement in the trade.\par \par \uc2 Now this wildly lucrative drug business appears to have been crushed along with his brutal and corrupt regime.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Imtiaz Tyab, Damascus.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, Nate visits the iconic Apollo Theater. It`s now being recognized by the Kennedy Center. Well-deserved about that, but first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (GAYLE KING and ALICIA KEYS singing)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Your eyes or ears are not deceiving. That is Gayle King singing a duet with Alicia Keys. That`s right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is singing the word, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are singing. You`re doing your thing, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: More like bleeding.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead only on CBS MORNINGS, she visited Keys` old high school. You`re going to stick around for this and love it, I guarantee.\par \par \uc2 All right, this is CBS MORNINGS. We`ll see you in a bit.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Girl on Fire"/Alicia Keys/\uc2 RCA Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she is.\par \par \uc2 That is the great Alicia Keys and there is no denying she is hot right now. She`s been hot a very long time. Hot, hot, hot, hot, hot.\par \par \uc2 She`s a 16-time Grammy winner, thank you very much and now, the original cast of her Broadway musical, "Hell`s Kitchen" is nominated for a Grammy, too. You go, Alicia Keys.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS and we like that, we caught up with the superstar at her alma mater, the Professional Performing Arts High School, right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We met the music teacher who inspired her back then, and still inspires her today and then we surprised an unsuspecting group of students.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I walked in here and I said, I wonder if Alicia remembers this room. Does Alicia remember this room?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Yes, look at this school. This is my school, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you a social butterfly? Were you a wallflower? Were you a quiet kid? Were you athletic? What were you like as a kid?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: I`ve always been pretty social. I`m definitely a troublemaker.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Like, in the sense of like, I test people and I like, challenge people. I was definitely challenging the teachers, for sure.\par \par \uc2 When I walked in those doors, I walked in kind of the same doors that I have a picture of my mother and I in front of after I graduated at 16 from this school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Class valedictorian, Alicia Keys was a student here at the Professional Performing Arts School when she signed her first record deal.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fallin`"/Alicia Keys/J Records)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She released her debut album when she was 20 years old. It won five Grammys, including Song of the Year for "Fallin`."\par \par \uc2 Alicia`s musical journey began in a Manhattan neighborhood named Hell`s Kitchen, and she says her experiences inspired the Broadway show.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Hell`s Kitchen on Broadway.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like one of the taglines is, "Remember where dreams began."\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As we`re sitting here, do you remember what were your dreams as you`re sitting out there in those seats?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Remember where dreams begin.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Remember where dreams begin is so important, because we all have them. We all have that moment, those times throughout our life.\par \par \uc2 And for me, sitting in these seats with my little bad self, and just like being, you know, against the grain and wanting to challenge every single thing, for sure, in these rooms, on this stage, being able to perform, cultivating my practice, my craft, my gift with one of my most incredible teachers, Miss Aziza, who --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: You know, is a part, a really big part of this "Hell`s Kitchen" story, as well, as you know, my journey in the school.\par \par \uc2 I remember like this was -- everything was possible. Everything was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Okay, you mentioned Miss Aziza. Let`s bring out Miss Aziza.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Come on, Miss Aziza.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Linda Aziza Miller was her music teacher here, and she has spent 50 years as a performer herself.\par \par \uc2 You`re the only one in the room who could tell us what she was like as a student at this school. I`d like to know.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: I`d like to know that.\par \par \uc2 LINDA AZIZA MILLER (Pianist and Alicia Keys` high school teacher): Okay --\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: You can be honest.\par \par \uc2 LINDA AZIZA MILLER: All right, okay, ready? Very intelligent, very energetic, very talented, very driven.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you think this girl is going places, or you just thought she`s very talented. We`ll see where she ends up. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 LINDA AZIZA MILLER: No, I thought she was going places.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did.\par \par \uc2 LINDA AZIZA MILLER: Yes. Good places, successful places.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: She taught me so much about how to arrange music, how to arrange a song. We would learn like these clusters and these harmonies that were so incredible.\par \par \uc2 They perked my ear. I never -- I wanted that. And as I went forward in my life, those are the things that I took and I put into the music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you go from Alicia in the class, learning from Miss Aziza to Alicia now on Broadway, saying, you know who would be good on stage? Miss Aziza.\par \par \uc2 How did that come about, Alicia?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: You know, it was -- it was -- it`s fascinating. One of the most dynamic characters is named Miss Liza Jane. Miss Liza Jane is a mixture between my grandmother and Miss Aziza, believe it or not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, wow. Okay.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: And as I was creating the piece, we`re starting to think about, how do we play the piano on stage? But we want to have a musician that can be visually seen, that reflects Miss Liza Jane, so this is a woman. This is a beautiful Black woman who is powerful. She`s incredible. She plays everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you said, I know somebody.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: And I was like, wait a second, guys, and I don`t even know if she will want to do it, by the way, but what if, if it was Miss Aziza? Could that be?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Miss Aziza has been an orchestra pianist for "Hell`s Kitchen" at the Shubert Theatre since March.\par \par \uc2 LINDA AZIZA MILLER: It`s a moment. It`s really a moment that I never saw coming between a former student and a teacher. You know, so this is precious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Goosebumps. So what does it mean to you? What does this mean to you? This moment in your life?\par \par \uc2 LINDA AZIZA MILLER: It`s -- okay, what it means to me is another opportunity to level up, another opportunity to grow, because I believe you learn until you die. So I`m very grateful, very happy. Thank you, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Thank you, Miss Aziza.\par \par \uc2 (LINDA AZIZA MILLER playing the piano.)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 Next, this former student turned global superstar played for us too.\par \par \uc2 (GAYLE KING and ALICIA KEYS singing.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the fun was just beginning. Upstairs, we had a surprise for a classroom full of theater students.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All right, who wants to go next.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How are you guys?\par \par \uc2 (STUDENTS say "Hi.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m from \uc2 CBS\uc2 , your favorite morning show.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Earlier this year, a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  page to save the school`s theater program from budget cuts caught the attention of actor and alumni, Jeremy Allen White and Alicia Keys.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Jeremy posted on his story promoting the \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2 , and he donated. And then we got a big response from Alicia Keys and \uc2 ROC Nation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alicia Keys.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Donated $60,000.00 to our fund raising.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Is she tied to the school?\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Yes, she is. She came to this school, too.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Professional Performing Arts High School)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The students filmed a thank you video for Alicia Keys, putting a spin on one of her biggest hits.\par \par \uc2 (STUDENTS singing)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Hello.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alicia Keys.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Surprise.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Oh, my gosh. You sound so beautiful. I`m so happy to see you guys. I`m really honored to be here to talk to you and get a second to just tell you that I`m proud of you and that you know, I really recognize you and I see that you`re unique, that you`re different, that you`re you.\par \par \uc2 I want you to always be yourself. I want you to always be different from everybody else. Like that is that`s -- your superpower. Actually, I want you to never forget that the more unique you are, the more you stand out and you guys are all extremely unique and extremely beautiful and incredible, talented, gifted, hopefully kind, because kind is the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alicia, look what she has on. Stand up there.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: I see it as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s been to "Hell`s Kitchen."\par \par \uc2 GIRL: I`m seeing the show tonight for the fourth time.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: The fourth -- go. Great to see you. So happy to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guys, Alicia said she`ll play "This Girl is On Fire." You want to gather around? We could all sing with her.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (STUDENTS singing with ALICIA KEYS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, bravo.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA KEYS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I got such goosebump, guys.\par \par \uc2 That was Greg Parente`s eighth grade theater class. Thank you, Mr. Parente for letting us come.\par \par \uc2 But clearly the kids were so blown away. And this wasn`t just a little photo thing that she did a drive by. After we left, she was still there. She sat and talked to them, answered their questions, spent more time, sang more songs. It just speaks to who she is and how she does what she does.\par \par \uc2 But I was so blown away by what Alicia has accomplished over the years. I remember when she -- when we first met her, when she first arrived on the scene, and now this Broadway play, "Hell`s Kitchen," which she also has a foundation, \uc2 Keep a Child Alive\uc2 , that a proceed from every ticket goes into her foundation.\par \par \uc2 So at last count, they had already raised for \uc2 Keep a Child Alive\uc2 , a million dollars for a nonprofit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And if you haven`t -- have you guys seen "Hell`s Kitchen"?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I haven`t seen it yet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have not, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s another one of those, run, don`t walk. I`ve seen it three times. And every time somebody comes to town, I`d say, you`ve got to go see it, because it takes her musical catalogue, inserts it into the play. But it`s not just plopped in, it moves the story along.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is knockout, too. Just the way Alicia is, a knockout.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, I`ve got to say, that might be my favorite piece you`ve done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I loved it too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I got emotional.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because kids --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I thought that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two things that want growing up is to be seen and heard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And their little voices are so powerful, and they`re being heard in the school. But for her to walk in that room --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And stay, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And to see them, and the way that they responded.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That -- that hit me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She said, the more unique you are, the more you stand out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And keep being you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Man, that was good, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But imagine what it was like for us and the crew because we were thinking --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whew.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- they don`t know what`s about to happen. I can`t wait. I can`t wait. I can`t wait. We were so excited.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great. Good job. Good job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And thank you to Alicia Keys and that school, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, thank you, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, staying in New York, we go inside the legendary Apollo Theater, now being honored by the Kennedy Center as the soul of American culture.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, some cozy, comfortable deals just in for the holidays, including one that might help you sleep better.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next. We got you.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table." this morning. And Gayle, you are kicking it off for us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, my "Talk of the Table." is 6888 Battalion. It was one of the only Women`s Army Corps unit of color to be stationed in Europe during World War Two. Their mission was to deliver roughly 17 million pieces of backlogged mail to soldiers and their families.\par \par \uc2 Now, a film directed by the great Tyler Perry and starring Kerry Washington, is telling their story. So we sat down with Tyler Perry and Kerry Washington and read letters written by an actual soldier back in 1942.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The USO has provided letters. This is one from Louis Speedy Weber, June 19, 1942: "Darling, as I`m writing this letter, I still have not received word from you or from home. I guess the mail takes longer than I expected, but as soon as I get the first letter, I suppose I get one every day after that. feel much better today than I did yesterday. I don`t feel as blue as I did yesterday."\par \par \uc2 I mean, it just shows that these are real people and their lives.\par \par \uc2 TYLER PERRY (Actor): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what these words mean. That`s why this work was so important, and I don`t think people understand that.\par \par \uc2 KERRY WASHINGTON (Actor): I mean, we get freaked out when a guy doesn`t text us back in 10 minutes these days. You know what I mean? Like imagine that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You give 10, I give five.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s been five minutes.\par \par \uc2 TYLER PERRY: You`re sitting there watching, waiting for the three bubbles to come up, the three bubbles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For the three bubbles, yes.\par \par \uc2 And don`t let the three bubbles stop and no message comes.\par \par \uc2 KERRY WASHINGTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TYLER PERRY: Oh, Lord.\par \par \uc2 KERRY WASHINGTON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But you`re right.\par \par \uc2 KERRY WASHINGTON: So, that level of need to be connected, to be seen, to be loved, to be able to communicate with the people that we care about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 KERRY WASHINGTON: Imagine not having that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we`ll have our full interview with Tyler Perry and Kerry Washington tomorrow. They`re going to share some behind-the-scenes stories from the set and visit with the service members. But what`s so extraordinary about the story?\par \par \uc2 Number one, I never heard of these women.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So imagine giving -- sorting out 17 million pieces mail, and they were given that job thinking there`s no way they could accomplish it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not only did they do it, they did it in record time, and what the mail meant back then, reading those letters, what the mail meant back then to the people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, the significance of it, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It takes on a whole different meaning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it is an extraordinary movie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like that mail is essentially keeping people alive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because of hope, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was your lifeline to all of your friends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, it is about morale. It`s about keeping the soldiers in the fight and keeping -- the people back home committed to winning it as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But guys, the mail was just piled up in these warehouses, and not, you know, with addresses. It didn`t make sense. Part of the mail had been destroyed. They would, they would get the scent of the letter and track down the scent. They did extraordinary work to get this letter to the receiver.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s an amazing story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON:. All right, my "Talk of the Table." is the type of honesty that can only come out of the mouth of a child.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Take a listen to a four-year-old story had to say after a Christmas program at her Colorado School.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STORI (Four years old): I am very proud of myself and all of my other friends inside my class. My class is really sweet, except one little boy.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STORI: And I need to tell you --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Teacher grabbed that microphone. So listen, she`s proud of everyone in her kindergarten class, except one little boy, as you heard.\par \par \uc2 Her mother, Brenda (sp?) says that moment highlights Stori`s true personality. Now Brenda says Stori loves people, love performing. And as you can tell, she isn`t afraid of being on the big stage and telling her truth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But online, guys, the comments are really very funny. They say, "Give her back to mic. What does she need to tell us?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like we`re here for the tea, huh?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We want to hear it. But I love A., that the teacher took the mic. I thought that was a very good move, but the mother said she had no idea, of course Stori was going to do this, and this was a little boy from last year, so clearly this little boy made an impact on Stori.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And everybody was laughing, so everybody knew the little boy she is talking about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That class knew. I think the message was received.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are saying, Nate, sit down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is funny when the teacher got the mic, and Stori is like, yes, you`re right, I`m tripping. I went too far.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I know. She did --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She put her hands up and she`s like, you`re right. That`s on me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the way she turned around and walked off.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s like, you`re right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so cute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh man, I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We like Stori.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in a moment, Nate takes us behind-the-scenes at the iconic Apollo Theater now Kennedy Center honoree to look at its defining impact on American culture. But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For nine decades, the legendary Apollo Theater in Harlem has helped shape American culture. The theater has launched the careers of numerous icons, like James Brown and Aretha Franklin, that`s just to name a couple. It also has served as a center of innovation.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Apollo Theater is a respected nonprofit presenting concerts, performances, film screenings and so much more. This year, it became the first institution ever to be honored by the Kennedy Center.\par \par \uc2 We sat down with some people who can speak on the significance of this theater and this award.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Motown Productions)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If the walls of the Apollo Theater could talk, they would tell a story so rich in American culture. Its iconic red marquee can be seen from up and down its block in Harlem.\par \par \uc2 BILLY MITCHELL (Official historian and tour guide at the Apollo Theater in Harlem, New York City): These are people who have performed here over and over again from the 1940s, 1950s --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Young James Brown.\par \par \uc2 Historian, Billy Mitchell, is known as Mr. Apollo, and knows the theater better than most.\par \par \uc2 BILLY MITCHELL: When Mr. Schiffman who let me run errands, introduce me to The Temptations, I thought I was going to melt.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Motown Productions)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He started working here at 15 years old, running errands for some of Motown`s biggest acts.\par \par \uc2 BILLY MITCHELL: When you walk into the main auditorium, the Apollo Theater, this is -- there`s something that physically happens to you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`s been giving theater tours since 1992.\par \par \uc2 Who had the best crowd control?\par \par \uc2 BILLY MITCHELL: Oh, man. The one that could have the audience eating out of their hands was James Brown.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Apollo Theater Archives)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The theater says James Brown may have started his career at Amateur Night at the Apollo, its talent competition nearly as old as the Apollo itself, along with countless greats like Ella Fitzgerald --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Apollo Theater Archives)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lauryn Hill and Jazmine Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Apollo Theater Archives)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Legendary artist, Leslie Uggams began performing here as a child in 1952.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 1966)\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS (Actress and singer): This was where we came to see all these of incredible artists perform on that stage, from the Louis Armstrong to the James Brown. And so for me, this was my playground --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: -- starting at nine years old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you performed here from nine till about 15, 16?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: Sixteen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What was that like?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: The Schiffmans who ran this theater, had a radio show, a contest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yup.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: And I kept winning every week at this contest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: And so finally, they said, you know, we`re going to put an act together for you. We want you to play the Apollo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But the 40s and 50s were a contentious time in America.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: When I was starting here, there was a department store that you couldn`t go into because you were Black. There was a famous restaurant that you couldn`t go into because you were Black.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: During an era of segregation, many black entertainers could appear only at the Apollo when they came back to New York.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: And so I had seen the changes, and my career was changing, and my life had started here, and so I still carry that with me, and people all over the world, when you mention the Apollo, oh, my God, they just go crazy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Apollo Theater has been a catalyst for social and civic advocacy, from protests to presidential campaigns, but as more venues became integrated, the Apollo Theater struggled financially and closed its doors more than once.\par \par \uc2 It`s been established as a nonprofit since 1992.\par \par \uc2 It is marked as a historical landmark now.\par \par \uc2 How does that play a role in what the longevity could be for the Apollo Theater?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE EBANKS (Apollo Theater president and CEO): As a nonprofit? You know, it is an opportunity to create another level of, you know, sustainability.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE EBANKS: Because the Apollo belongs to the people of Harlem, the people in New York.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Michelle Ebanks is the theater`s president and CEO.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE EBANKS: There`s a promise that`s being made for continued service, and we are excited to take on this challenge.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The 2000s launched the Apollo Theater Walk of Fame, honoring the legendary figures behind its success on 125th Street in New York.\par \par \uc2 BILLY MITCHELL: We started recognizing those that made a contribution to the history of the Apollo Theater and the first name that was inducted into the ground was Quincy Jones.\par \par \uc2 So since then, we`ve had Baby Face, you know, which we just inducted; Mary J. Blige, all the Motown acts, Aretha Franklin. You know, all of these people, their names were inducted into our Walk of Fame right under the marquee of the Apollo Theater.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So there`s still some room.\par \par \uc2 BILLY MITCHELL: Oh, absolutely, absolutely. One day there will be Nate Burleson, you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts/The Honors Inc.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Apollo Theater added another achievement to its long list of accolades, a Kennedy Center Honor.\par \par \uc2 QUEEN LATIFAH (Rapper and actor): The Apollo becomes the first institution to receive a Kennedy Center Honor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Celebrating their contributions to the arts as an iconic American institution.\par \par \uc2 All right, last question, and I`ll just let you finish this sentence.\par \par \uc2 The Apollo Theater is --\par \par \uc2 LESLIE UGGAMS: Magic. It`s the Oscar of theaters, as far as I`m concerned. This is the Oscar.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I have to say. Thank you to the Apollo Theater, to Billy Mitchell, of course, Leslie Uggams and Michelle Ebanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They were awesome and inviting.\par \par \uc2 Apollo celebrating its 90th anniversary, and there are renovations to come. They`re not going to change it, but they do want to upgrade it and make it more appealing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Michelle Ebanks is the right person for the job. She`s such a powerhouse, so I love that she`s in that position.\par \par \uc2 Looking at Leslie Uggams, who is 81 years old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another good example of Black don`t crack. And it`s so important that and so appropriate is what I mean, that the Apollo would be the first institution to be honored by the Kennedys.\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yup. Yup.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Leslie Uggams said it right, magic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You walk in that building and you do feel something every time you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there is a feeling now that that magic will always be here because of its structure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and it is a safe haven of performance, and has been and still will be for people of color.\par \par \uc2 But let`s not get it twisted.\par \par \uc2 All races, all cultures, have always wanted to grace that stage because of what it means to people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And all are welcome there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All are welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. All right, you can watch the 47th annual Kennedy Center Honors this Sunday at 8:30/7:30 Central, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , of course, and streaming on Paramount+. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time now for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. We`ve got several items, all at exclusive discounts available today. To start shopping, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com. It`s really easy.\par \par \uc2 Knockings lifestyle expert, Elizabeth Werner is here.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER (Knockings lifestyle expert): Hello.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: I`m excited to be here. All right, this I`m going to hand you this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is this?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: This is called Memori.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: So this time of year, we`re taking a lot of photos, right? The kids are opening gifts. We`re getting together with family. What happens when you go to take that photo and your phone says nope --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s full.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Storage is full.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yup.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Well, we are going to take care of that. This is a universal device. It`s going to fit into your phones, your tablets or your laptops. It`s going to actually pull off your photos and your videos, giving you all that storage space back on your phone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: B