one of this. But here`s what else they are. They`re a lot healthier because this tends to have a lot of chemicals in it. The actual detergents.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: This has no dioxin, chlorine bleach, no parabens, no phosphates, no synthetic dyes. So you just simply rip their little sheet --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Detergent is messing with my kids` skin when they were younger.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Okay, So that is a big thing, and you don`t know what it is. It takes time to figure it out. This is the answer. This dissolves in water. You can even use this if you`re washing in the sink. The other thing that they provide us with are these dryer balls. These are made of wool. You don`t need that fabric softener, also full of chemicals.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: This will get the static out. It will get the wrinkles out. It literally shortens your dry time by 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Towels, sheets, those things that take a long time. Right now, starting off at $18.15.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Okay, I would love for you to have a little rest here. This is called EasyNest. Now, this is a pillow that actually has three different pieces to it. It`s modular.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that for my neck right now.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: All right, you`re feeling that, and this helps a lot of people with back pain --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, soft.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Shoulder pain. You were just talking about people recovering from surgery.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: They use something like this. The nice thing about it being modular with the three different pillows, is that you can take them apart, put them under your knees, elevate your feet. $69.99.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re the best.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, sir, $60.00 savings.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, thank you, Liz.\par \par \uc2 We partner with all of these companies to get these deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To buy anything that you saw --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it comfy, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- scan the QR code -- yes, it is -- anything you`ve seen, go to CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow. Nate will get out of the bed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I won`t.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241220ekcj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for December 4, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ramy Inocencio, Lonnie Quinn, Nikole Killion, Meg  Oliver, Kris Van Cleave, Jan Crawford, Elise Preston \par \uc2 3,883 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: Person possibly shot in front of the \uc2 Hilton Hotel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the urgent hunt for the gunman who murdered the CEO of an insurance giant on the streets of Manhattan. What police are learning from this disturbing new video of the brazen attack.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Why are you meeting with House Republicans?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Pete Hegseth goes on the offensive, as Donald Trump and his team look at possible replacements for the role of defense secretary.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT (\uc2 Boeing\uc2  Whistle-Blower): They`re not going to listen to me until somebody dies.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And tonight`s Eye on America: why a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  whistle-blower says the aerospace giant is boosting its satellite production at the expense of worker safety.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: I don`t think they care at all.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us on this busy night.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin with the intense search for the gunman behind what New York City police are calling a targeted attack caught on camera this morning in the heart of Manhattan as the morning rush hour was getting under way. The victim is 50-year-old \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , the CEO of UnitedHealthcare, part of the largest private health insurance company in the country.\par \par \uc2 The gunman`s face was covered by a mask and hood. He fled to Central Park on an electric bike. And this new video obtained by CBS News shows him leaving the park at 6:59 a.m. That was 15 minutes after the shooting. And he was captured by a surveillance camera on the Upper West Side.\par \par \uc2 Now, the shocking attack took place early in the morning. And there are reports that the victim`s widow said her husband had previously received threats.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Meg Oliver is going to lead off our coverage from the scene of the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Meg.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Security cameras outside this Midtown Manhattan hotel captured what the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  described as a brazen, targeted attack.\par \par \uc2 We want to warn you, the video is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK POLICE DEPARTMENT: You have a male shot coming over under north.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): Just after 6:45 a.m., surveillance video captured a man wearing a mask and carrying a gun with a silencer approaching UnitedHealthcare CEO \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  from behind and shooting him in the back and calf.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has edited the video to crop out Thompson stumbling, then crumbling to the ground. The gunman can then be seen trying to unjam his gun, before shooting again, then calmly walking across the street.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH KENNY (\uc2 NYPD\uc2  Chief of Detectives): The shooter arrived at the location on foot about five minutes prior to the victim`s arrival.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Officials say Thompson was alone and did not have a security team with him. He was rushed to the closest hospital, where he was later pronounced dead less than a half-hour later. Today, his wife told NBC News their husband had been receiving threats before the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Police Commissioner Jessica Tisch:\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TISCH (New York City Police Commissioner): Every indication is that this was a premeditated, preplanned, targeted attack.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Police released these images of the suspect, who they say took off on foot down an alleyway, then fled on a city bike.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH KENNY: We`re still tracking video. The last we see with him on that bike is in Central Park.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Is there a way to track that bike?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH KENNY: There are GPSes on those bikes. We will be working with the company.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Investigators say they recovered a cell phone near the scene and are conducting forensic analysis of the device to determine if it belonged to the suspect.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of him using a silencer?\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON (Former Suffolk County, New York, Police Commissioner): It`s concerning that a silencer was used, because using a silencer kind of means that the person might have been hired to kill Mr. Thompson.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Thompson had been with \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2  for two decades and was made CEO of UnitedHealthcare in 2021. The company issued a statement saying it is "deeply saddened and shocked at the passing of our dear friend."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: A law enforcement source tells CBS News that the suspect went to a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  right before the shooting and left behind forensic evidence that`s being tested.\par \par \uc2 And surveillance video there captured enough of his face to run it through facial recognition. Thompson leaves behind a wife and two sons -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Crucial new information.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Donald Trump`s embattled pick to run the Pentagon says he`s looking forward to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  background check, adding it`s an opportunity for a real investigation into allegations of sexual misconduct and excessive drinking. Pete Hegseth also saying today that, if he`s confirmed as secretary of defense, there won`t be a -- quote -- "drop of alcohol" on his lips.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion reports from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Tonight, president-elect Donald Trump`s Pentagon pick is not backing down after multiple sources told CBS News Trump could be seeking to replace the embattled designee with Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Have you had any conversations with the president-elect about him replacing you with Governor DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH (U.S. Defense Secretary Nominee): I spoke to the president- elect this morning. He said: "Keep going. Keep fighting. Behind you all the way."\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: So, you`re in this all the way?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: Why would I back down? I have been always been a fighter. I`m here for the war fighters. This is personal and passionate for me.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hoping to combat a flood of negative headlines, Pete Hegseth went on a full-court press Wednesday, starting with a "Wall Street Journal" op-ed defending his conduct at two veteran nonprofits, where he was accused of financial mismanagement.\par \par \uc2 And in a new interview, he addressed accusations of excessive drinking.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: First of all, I have never had a drinking problem. I`m -- no one`s ever approached me and said, oh, you -- you should really look at getting help for drinking, never.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: His mother, Penelope, also took to the airwaves and appealed to lawmakers, walking back a scathing 2018 e-mail reported by "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," where she called her son an abuser of women.\par \par \uc2 PENELOPE HEGSETH (Mother of Pete Hegseth): I wrote that in haste. I wrote that with deep emotions. Pete is a new person. He`s redeemed, forgiven, changed.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Are you a changed man?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: I love my mom. And I appreciate her. And by the grace of God, all of us change, and I`m grateful for that.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Still, some senators seemed unconvinced.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KEVIN CRAMER\uc2  (R-North Dakota): The allegations are very, very serious. They can`t be trivialized.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hegseth`s meeting today with Iowa Republican \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2 , a survivor of sexual assault, was considered critical.\par \par \uc2 How was your conversation with Senator Ernst?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: We had an engaging, constructive conversation.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JONI ERNST (R-Iowa): It was a frank and thorough conversation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Senator Ernst, who is also a combat veteran, has been mentioned as a potential defense candidate, but, tonight, when asked whether she`s under consideration, she told me: "Mr. Hegseth is the nominee."\par \par \uc2 As for Governor DeSantis, sources tell CBS News he is interested in the job and considered confirmable -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion, with all that new information, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight millions of Americans are bracing for a blast of wintry weather, with snow moving into the Upper Midwest and Northeast. This is time-lapse video from Wisconsin. Watch as I-94 fills with snow and driving conditions deteriorate in just a matter of minutes. It`s not just snow, but wind and frigid temperatures are also on the way.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lonnie Quinn is with us now and following it all.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Lonnie.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I think it`s going to be the combination of some snow combined with the big winds, that are probably going to make for some pretty tough days out there for the next one -- say, one or two days.\par \par \uc2 If you take a look, currently, right outside of New York City, there`s some snow showers falling. And when we look at the map from high up above, I want to show you what it looks like, all right? The system, it`s currently not even being displayed here, but it -- OK, there we go.\par \par \uc2 It`s right now going to be pushing from the Great Lakes into the Northeast. We`re seeing some of those leading snow showers currently for the Northeast. It puts down a pretty good blanket of snow up around the New England ski areas. But how much snow are we talking about? Is there any lake-effect snow? You bet you there is.\par \par \uc2 When you look at this map, anything shaded in white is like an inch of snow. New York City could see maybe a couple of tenths. But where we see that darker blue, even that yellow color, like around Watertown, New York, lake-effect snow could be six to 12 inches.\par \par \uc2 Even some of the ski resorts up in New England see the same sort of thing. The winds are whipping out there, driving those temperatures down. Watch the invasion of the arctic air. And the numbers, hey, pretty impressive out there. Which one jumps out at you? How about Minneapolis? Tomorrow morning, it`s eight degrees. It feels like eight below. Fargo is one, feels like 13 below zero.\par \par \uc2 Chicago`s 14, feeling like five below zero. Even in the Northeast, that cold air will stretch, sort of setting up camp out there. So, Cleveland 32, feels like 17, New York City 41, feeling like 30 degrees. And, again, for the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 , going to a little bit more festive in our area here in New York City, perhaps a little bit of a coating of snow.\par \par \uc2 Norah, it`s all yours.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: I`m smiling, but I`m getting the shivers just listening to you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LONNIE QUINN: Ain`t that right?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Thank, Lonnie. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in South Korea`s opposition parties moved to impeach their nation`s president today, the day after he stunned the world and temporarily declared military control over the country. South Korea is one of America`s closest allies in Asia, and the U.S. is now closely watching the political turmoil.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio reports tonight from Seoul, South Korea.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voiceover): Tonight, thousands of angry South Koreans marched to the office of embattled President \uc2 Yoon Suk Yeol\uc2 , chanting demands he resign, be arrested or impeached...\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT YOON SUK-YEOL SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: ... after he U-turned on his shock declaration of martial law from late Tuesday night, which saw soldiers and police block access to the legislature and civilians fighting back; 35-year-old Ahn Gwi-ryeong grabbed the barrel of a soldier`s rifle.\par \par \uc2 "I didn`t think," she said in an interview. "I just knew we had to stop this."\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers fought through the security cordon to vote to unanimously block the president`s stunning action.\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 DUYEON KIM (Center for a New American Security): His presidency right now looks like it`s in the hands of his own party. It depends on how many of them might vote for impeachment.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And South Korea`s reputation as a democratic stronghold in Asia tainted too, but Washington`s support is not expected to change, with a deeply vested interest in stability in South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 28,500 American troops are stationed here, in part to deter a nuclear-armed North Korea and its backers, China and Russia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And lawmakers could vote to impeach South Korea`s president as soon as this Friday. If his political party sticks together, and they say that they will, then President Yoon will stay president -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right, an important story. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  hears arguments in a case that could have major consequences for America`s transgender youth. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There`s breaking news tonight from Northern California, where police are investigating a shooting at a Christian elementary school.\par \par \uc2 This is happening in Palermo, California, about an hour north of Sacramento.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston has late details -- Elise.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Norah, the details are still coming in, but, as you can imagine, a very dramatic and terrifying afternoon for those students and parents who rushed to a reunification center.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Units responding, medical, criminal violence.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Another community in shock, two students at an elementary school hit by gunfire. It happened at the Feather River Adventist School, their entire enrollment just 35 students.\par \par \uc2 This photo appears to show the gunman on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, that gunman died of an apparent self-inflicted gunshot wound.\par \par \uc2 At this hour, there`s no known connection between the gunman and the school. Investigators say they are coordinating with several other agencies, including the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . The school remains on lockdown -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In one of the most anticipated cases at the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  this session, the justices heard arguments today over Tennessee`s ban on gender-affirming care for minors. Similar bans have been enacted in nearly half the country.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford has more on a case that could have sweeping consequences.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): Competing rallies made clear the conflict, but, in arguments, a majority of justices appear to agree laws passed by Tennessee and 23 other states banning hormone treatments and puberty blockers for children who identify as transgender do not violate the Equal Protection Clause.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (U.S. Supreme Court Associate Justice): If the Constitution doesn`t take sides, if there are strong, forceful, scientific policy arguments on both sides in a situation like this, why isn`t it best to leave it to the democratic process?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Conservative justices pointed to growing international concerns that transition medications are unsafe for children.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2  (Chief Justice of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 ): I think that would be very troubling to say that, in such a evolving situation, we are going to decide what the right approaches are.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Liberal justices pushed back. One compared the bans to racist Jim Crow era laws...\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2  (U.S. Supreme Court Associate Justice): I`m worried that we`re undermining the foundations of some of our bedrock equal protection cases.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: ... and said the medication could be lifesaving.\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR (U.S. Supreme Court Associate Justice): The evidence is very clear that there are some children who actually need this treatment.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: L.W. is one of the children at the center of the case. She started medication to stop male puberty at 13 and female estrogen a year later.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA WILLIAMS (Mother of L.W.): I can`t imagine her not on her medicines. She`s a different kid. She`s a healthier, happier kid. All we`re trying to do is make the best decisions for our kid.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But a California mom who refused to allow the medication when her teenage daughter identified as trans and has since detransitioned says the risks are too high.\par \par \uc2 ERIN FRIDAY (Mother of Detransitioned Teen): Anybody can go back into their youth and think about decisions that they made at 13, 14 or things that they thought: I will never have kids. I will never get married.\par \par \uc2 But it changes. It changes over life. And once you medicalize a child, they don`t get to change their mind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, you know, as you can see, both these families want the same thing. They want their kids to be happy. They want to protect them. And they both believe that the other side`s approach would cause terrible harm.\par \par \uc2 And as significant as this case is, the question of transition treatments, the decision could go well beyond this issue. For example, questions today, if the court strikes down these state laws as unconstitutional, how could that bolster transgender arguments for participation in girls and women`s sports, Norah?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jan Crawford, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next. A \uc2 Boeing\uc2  whistle-blower opens up to CBS News about his safety concerns, saying lives are at risk.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In tonight`s Eye on America, our investigative team has been speaking with whistle-blowers at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  who say the company is putting profits over safety.\par \par \uc2 While initial concerns were raised after the Alaska Airline blowout in January, we have now learned that similar issues may be plaguing \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s space division. We spoke with an employee in the factory that makes military and commercial satellites.\par \par \uc2 He tells \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave workers` lives are at risk.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT (\uc2 Boeing\uc2  Whistle-Blower): I`m afraid with \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in the hands that it`s in now down here, they`re not going to listen to me until somebody dies.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): For nearly three decades, Craig Garriott has worked at this sprawling \uc2 Boeing\uc2  satellite factory. He`s also the head of the local carpenters union, overseeing about 600 hourly technicians.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: Our people are responsible for the building of the satellites, the testing of the satellites.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But Garriott says, since 2022, executives running the Los Angeles area plant have increased pressure to get product out the door, leading to what he calls a toxic culture that has put his workers in danger.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: It`s been basically me standing in against \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , right?\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Why keep doing it?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: Because I told everybody that I would protect them.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: It`s a concern multiple whistle-blowers who have worked on \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s commercial airliners, including the 737 MAX, told us. Corners were cut as the company sought to boost production.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: They have taken the focus off quality, the focus off the people on the floor. They have put it completely on profit and going fast.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Garriott says, last year, a four-ton satellite estimated to be worth hundreds of millions of dollars crashed to the factory floor after it wasn`t properly secured.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: When it happened, one person was underneath that satellite and they barely got out.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: How significant is it to that satellite to have it fall like that?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: It`s the equivalent of a plane falling out of the sky. It`s the worst thing that can possibly happen on the site.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In the last year, Garriott says he`s raised between 300 and 400 safety violations with management.\par \par \uc2 One employee that we spoke with said he believed management views safety as a -- quote -- "afterthought."\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: I don`t even think it`s an afterthought. I don`t think they care at all.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Liftoff!\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: His concerns come as \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s space program has suffered setbacks, the most high-profile failure in September. The company`s Starliner spacecraft returned to Earth without its two astronauts.\par \par \uc2 NEIL ARMSTRONG (Former NASA Astronaut): That`s one small step.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , which helped put a man on the moon, is reportedly exploring a sale of parts of its space business, as new CEO \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  hinted at on an October investor call.\par \par \uc2 KELLY ORTBERG\uc2  (CEO, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 ): We`re better off being -- doing less and doing it better than doing more and not doing it well.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: There is such a disconnect between the floor and the ivory tower of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  right now.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In April, Garriott filed this lawsuit against \uc2 Boeing\uc2  alleging the company retaliated against him for raising continuous safety complaints aimed at protecting the lives of the workers under his supervision.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: When I first started at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , the guys, the hourly people that spoke up and said, hey, this doesn`t feel right, those guys were revered, right? You speak up now, you`re a troublemaker.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: In a statement, a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesperson said the company "has completed an investigation into this issue and we dispute the allegations made in the lawsuit," adding, "\uc2 Boeing\uc2  did not retaliate against Mr. Garriott."\par \par \uc2 Is it worth it?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GARRIOTT: Yes. I`m not going to stop until I know that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  understands that these people mean something. They matter. And make them feel like they matter. Make them feel like their safety matters.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A good first step he`d like to see, top \uc2 Boeing\uc2  management come to the factory floor to tell them that it does.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Kris Van Cleave, El Segundo, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A split-second decision may have saved a police officer`s life. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 A truck driver is honored for running into danger to save a police officer. \uc2 John Lally\uc2  received the Carnegie Medal for Heroism at Houston City Hall on Tuesday. In November of last year, Lally was driving on the Southwest Freeway when a man in a stolen car crashed nearby.\par \par \uc2 Hearing gunfire, Lally saw Officer Jonathan Gibson get shot in the leg. Then he and another officer pulled Gibson 40 feet to safety. Lally says he hopes others would do the same.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN LALLY (Carnegie Medal for Heroism Recipient): I just want to continue to inspire change. And I want to inspire people that, if you see somebody in need that you can help, if you can help them, help them. And let`s just keep that going.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We are inspired by \uc2 John Lally\uc2 , an unexpected hero. And so, tonight, he`s our Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 And we will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241205ekc400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for November 3, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Nancy Cordes, Janet Shamlian, Kris Van Cleave,  Nikole Killion, Scott MacFarlane, Holly Williams, Andrew Kozak \par \uc2 3,737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, deadlock to the finish.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, Current Presidential Candidate): It is now or never. This is it.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D-Vice President of the United States, Current Presidential Candidate): The vote of the people will determine the outcome of this election.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The presidential candidates focused on the battlegrounds and the undecided in campaigns of stark differences and rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: It is so good to worship with you today.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : It`s a very demonic party.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And a surprise appearance.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday Night Live/NBC)\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH (Comedian): What do we always say?\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH AND \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Keep Kamala and carry-on-ala.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I`m Nancy Cordes in East Lansing, Michigan where Harris supporters started lining up early this morning to see the woman they hope makes history in just two days.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: I`m Nikole Killion in Macon, Georgia where former President Trump tours a trio of states who takes a turn with his closing message.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, last stand in the Treasure State.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: I`m Janet Shamlian in Bozeman, Montana where critical race here in Big Sky Country could flip the script on the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Plus, angry flood survivors throw mud at Spain`s King and Queen.\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma, tornadoes tear up the state overnight. The new threat ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, the end of an era at SFO.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: I`m Kris Van Cleve in San Francisco`s airport. Its bouncy, moving sidewalks are about to take the leap into retirement.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening and thank you for joining us this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 In just over 48 hours, Election Day will finally be here as voters select the 47th president of the United States. Many Americans are not waiting. As of today, more than 77 million Americans have already cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, the candidates rallied supporters and made final pitches, hoping to reach the few undecided voters in states that will determine who is the next president.\par \par \uc2 Our CBS News polling updated overnight shows the contest in the seven battleground states remain deadlocked. We have correspondents traveling with both campaigns. We begin tonight with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes in East Lansing, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Jericka, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Harris campaign is touting its ground game with 16 million doors knocked on to date in the seven battleground states. And all that work, they say, is paying off with an early voting electorate that is younger and more diverse than ever before.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Crowd cheering)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): On this final campaign Sunday, Vice President Harris hit as many parts of Michigan as she could, including a predominantly Black church in downtown Detroit.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Church, in just two days, we have the power to decide the fate of our nation.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Her campaign`s goal this weekend was twofold -- to knock on millions of doors in battleground states --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Have you already voted for Harris?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: -- and to dominate the airwaves everywhere with a surprise appearance on "Saturday Night Live."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday Night Live/NBC)\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH: The American people want to stop the chaos --\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And end the drama-la.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And a closing two-minute ad that played during Sunday football.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` campaign ad)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: As president, I`ll bring a new generation of leadership.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Today, Harris called out her opponent for spreading false new claims about voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I would ask in particular, people who have not yet voted to not fall for his tactic, which I think includes suggesting to people that if they vote, their vote won`t matter.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A November surprise is sending shockwaves through the race. A new Des Moines Register poll that finds Harris now leading in Iowa, a supposedly Red State.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I`m thrilled -- thrilled.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The news quickly made its way here to East Lansing, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We think things are turning very optimistic. I think the state, the country is energized and waking up. And we`re like, we are not going to be lemmings for his Project 2025.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Harris says she has now filled out her own mail-in ballot and sent it off to her home state of California. She`ll be staying the night here in Michigan before spending her final full day campaigning in the largest battleground state, Pennsylvania -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Nancy Cordes for us on the campaign trail. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion in the battleground state of Georgia, with Trump`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good evening.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening to you, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is making his last visit to Georgia here in Macon, which is typically blue territory. And while his campaign looks to chip away at Democratic margins, Trump`s rhetoric on the trail may once again be distracted from his closing message.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Supporters greeted former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as he capped off a three-state sprint in Georgia, Sunday, following a return to North Carolina --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Hello to North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (Crowd cheering)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And Pennsylvania, where he went off script.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Isn`t this better than my speech?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The former president renewed false claims about election fraud, telling the crowd he shouldn`t have left the White House after his loss in 2020.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I shouldn`t have left. I mean, honestly, because we did so -- we did so well.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Trump also went on a riff about the bulletproof glass surrounding him, and appeared to take aim at the press.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : To get me, somebody would have to shoot through the fake news --\par \par \uc2 (Crowd laughing)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : -- and I don`t mind that so much because --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Trump campaign quickly responded: "The president`s statement about protective glass placement has nothing to do with the media being harmed or anything else."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m not down in Iowa --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The campaign also dismissed a new poll showing Trump trailing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Iowa and in the overall race, they asserted the gender gap between both candidates may not be as wide as it seems.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Face the Nation)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (R-Florida): \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a bigger problem among men than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does among women.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The latest CBS News Battleground Tracker shows Trump with a slight edge over Harris in Georgia, but within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 KEITH HOOGSTEAD (Trump supporter): Honestly, I don`t -- I don`t think it`s as close as they`re showing. I think, you know, I think the reality will show itself on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: More than four million votes were cast in early voting, which just ended here in Georgia. Now, the focus is on turning out the vote on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s running mate, J.D. Vance, will be in Atlanta, Monday, while the former president heads back to North Carolina and the Midwest -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All right, Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This campaign isn`t only about who will occupy the White House, but also control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is here to break down that part of the story for us - - Scott.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Another cliffhanger, the next president will need a Senate to confirm his or her Cabinet and potential Supreme Court nominations and help get all of those promised policies passed.\par \par \uc2 The final days of the race for control of the narrowly divided U.S. House has shifted heavily to New York. Republican House Speaker Mike Johnson rallied through three competitive districts there, trying to salvage Republicans` narrow majority and three of his embattled first term New York Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, the House Democratic Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , is expected to make a last minute push through a district for a two-term Democrat trying to preserve a seat near West Point.\par \par \uc2 The playing field is so small, our CBS News Review shows roughly 30 battleground districts in the U.S. House nationwide, that`s fewer than 10 percent of all races. And you can see a group of potentially vulnerable Republicans in California and three competitive races in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 In the race for control of the Senate, Democrats can lose a net of just one, maybe two seats, risk losing their narrow majority helping or handcuffing a future President Harris or future President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And those blue wall battleground states have tens of millions of dollars in spending for Senate races, too -- Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: All very important states, lot of moving parts. Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 Watch CBS News on election night for real-time results, analysis, and exclusive polling. Coverage begins at 4:00 p.m. Eastern on our CBS News 24/7 streaming channel, and at 7:00 p.m. Eastern on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  television network.\par \par \uc2 Now, the outcome of Tuesday`s election could have major implications beyond our borders, including in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams is in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): In the West Bank, many Israeli settlers who have built their communities on occupied Palestinian territory believe a second Trump presidency would be good for Israel.\par \par \uc2 TOMER MARSHALL (Tribe Effects co-founder): We have a nice university up at the end of the road.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: One of them is Tomer Marshall.\par \par \uc2 TOMER MARSHALL: The former administration had a much stronger bond between the U.S. and Israel. Right now, the bond is more questionable. It`s not -- it`s not clear. You know, are you with us? Or you with us up until a certain line?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Israeli settlements in the occupied West Bank are widely regarded as illegal under international law. But in a major shift in U.S. policy, the Trump administration declared that Israeli settlements were not illegal.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2020)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And our alliance with the state of Israel has never, ever been stronger than it is today.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: With Israel now at war in Gaza and in Lebanon and led by the most right-wing government in its modern history, a recent poll here found that 66 percent of Israelis would prefer \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to win the election. Only 17 percent prefer \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  understand the Middle East, do you think?\par \par \uc2 ALON PINKAS (Israeli diplomat and writer): No. He`s a very transactional man.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Alon Pinkas is a former adviser to four Israeli foreign ministers who tried to negotiate peace with the Palestinians. But he told us Trump`s isolationist America first approach is dangerous, making \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  a better choice.\par \par \uc2 ALON PINKAS: I think her understanding of America`s global responsibilities and commitment is different -- qualitatively different than that of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t mean he`s not a friend of Israel. It just means that in the long run, I`d rather, as an Israeli, have her president.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Holly Williams, CBS News, Tel Aviv, Israel.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Today in Spain, an angry reception for the country`s King and Queen as they surveyed damage from historic flash floods. Take a listen and take a look at that -- crowds tossed mud and insults at the Spanish Royals and government officials touring the eastern region of Valencia.\par \par \uc2 The country`s worst floods in decades killed more than 200 people.\par \par \uc2 Well today, tornados battered Oklahoma, leaving at least six people hurt. Mountains of debris piled up in Oklahoma City, where storms damaged several homes, tossed cars, and downed trees along with power lines.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there`s a new threat for more severe weather.\par \par \uc2 Let`s check in with CBS News meteorologist, Andrew Kozak with more on that -- Andrew.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yes, good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of storm reports, including the tornados overnight, 2:04 in the morning, very dangerous to have that overnight. And we`re once again going to be tracking that for your Sunday into your Monday and again, even Monday, we could be looking at more severe weather and enhanced risk for Northern Texas, Tulsa, Arkansas, and up toward Southwest Missouri.\par \par \uc2 We`re also keeping an eye on the tropics. Potential, Tropical Cyclone 18 has now formed. It will move across Cuba, potentially becoming a Category One hurricane, and it may pose a threat to the Gulf States by the end of the week, we`re going to keep an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 For Tuesday, Election Day, nice across California to get to the polls. We have some mountain snow. For the mid-south and areas from Chicago down to the Gulf, we`ll have a couple of showers and storms and no excuse not to get out there and vote across the East Coast, beautiful on Tuesday with plenty of sunshine all the way up from Florida to Maine -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Andrew, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, Senate showdown in Montana`s changed political landscape.\par \par \uc2 Plus, why these astronauts still stuck in space are on the move.\par \par \uc2 And later, a beloved bounce coming to an end in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Montana is having a moment with the election less than 48 hours away. The Treasure State could determine control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The race pits an incumbent Democrat against a newcomer Republican. The big state is home to just over a million people. We sent \uc2 CBS\uc2  Janet Shamlian there to meet voters in a changing political landscape.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Montana`s majestic views, one of the few things some here say that stayed the same.\par \par \uc2 How much has this state changed last few years?\par \par \uc2 ERIC RAILE (Political Science professor): The state has changed tremendously.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Political science professor, Eric Raile says he`s never seen a race like the state`s \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  contest pitting the Montana-born farmer and three-term Democrat --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from political ad)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER\uc2  (D-Montana): I`m \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: -- against a Republican political newcomer, a businessman from out-of-state --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from political ad)\par \par \uc2 TIM SHEEHY (R-Montana U.S. Senate candidate): I`m Tim Sheehy --\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: -- who has come under recent scrutiny, accused of fabricating the story of a war injury. Sheehy maintains he was wounded in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 The race, a study of contrast, not unlike Montana itself.\par \par \uc2 With control of the Senate potentially hanging in the balance, spending has shattered records, more than $275 million. That`s about $350 per registered voter.\par \par \uc2 LORRIE MERRILL ("The Big Sandy Mountaineer" owner): Everybody is complaining about the ads. I am not complaining about the ads. It`s like harvest for me to have political ads.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Lorrie Merrill owns "The Big Sandy Mountaineer" in rural Big Sandy, population 600, Senator Tester`s hometown.\par \par \uc2 No political coverage of the paper, even though you`re at the heart of this Senate race.\par \par \uc2 LORRIE MERRILL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 I do not want anyone to use this paper as a tool to divide this community.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Merrill says Montana has changed as thousands moved during the pandemic from states like California in search of wide open spaces, lower taxes, and more conservative policy.\par \par \uc2 That boom has spiked the cost of living here, and it`s most evident in housing.\par \par \uc2 In Bozeman, new construction abounds, and housing prices have skyrocketed. The median home is up $200,000 since 2020. Property taxes have jumped 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 MURIEL KELLY (Montana resident): Who doesn`t want to move here? It`s the great outdoors.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Muriel Kelly (sp?) is a lifelong Montanan.\par \par \uc2 MURIEL KELLY: On one hand, it`s amazing that you have more community being in a rural area, but on the other hand, a lot of people don`t realize the respect that you need to have for such a pristine place.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Tester is now the last statewide Democrat in what`s become a solidly red state.\par \par \uc2 ERIC RAILE: The state turning red is a function of the demographics changing a little bit and the people moving to the state, but also the nationalization of our politics. It doesn`t look that different from other states at this point.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Thomas Dilworth owns an organic snack food business in Big Sandy with his wife Heather, and says he can`t get workers because there is no housing for them.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DILWORTH (Business owner): We`ve had four years of this kind of regression, lax economy, whatever the circumstance that`s causing it, you can debate about that all you want, but you can`t debate about the fact that people are hurting and they want change.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Amid concerns about the population fueled economic changes and a cultural shift, a recognition, change is inevitable.\par \par \uc2 LORRIE MERRILL: We have to change with the times. It`s just a matter of defining what our values are and working towards those values.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The vista, still stunning, amid a landscape undeniably changed.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, CBS News, Bozeman, Montana.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, why the fun may be over for a popular casual dining chain. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Self-capture complete.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Today, the two Boeing Starliner astronauts stuck on the International Space Station since June were on the move. Suni Williams and Butch Wilmore helped relocate the SpaceX Dragon capsule to an unused docking port to make way for a cargo ship that is set to dock on Tuesday. The astronauts are now expected to return to Earth in February.\par \par \uc2 Well, thousands of striking \uc2 Boeing\uc2  machinists vote on a new contract Monday. The improved offer includes a 38 percent pay raise over four years. The seven-week strike has crippled \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s production at a time when it`s also dealing with quality and safety issues.\par \par \uc2 Well, for nearly 60 years, TGI Friday`s has sold fun. But the party may soon be over. This weekend, the chain filed for Chapter 11 Bankruptcy Protection. The company has already closed more than 100 U.S. locations over the past year.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, beloved bounce, why time is running out on this 395 feet of pure fun.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We end tonight in \uc2 San Francisco International Airport\uc2 , where time is running out on a relic that`s literally been under countless flyers` feet. So we asked \uc2 CBS\uc2  Kris Van Cleave to hop on, and he didn`t hesitate.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): It`s not every day you see flyers with pep in their step bouncing through an airport.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It`s soft, so when you walk on it and it`s a little jumpy --\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Unless you`re in Terminal 3 at SFO, where, for decades now, the race to the gate comes with a bit of a springboard.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It`s walking on a cloud.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: I know it seems silly that people are excited about a moving walkway. You`ve been on them, certainly at an airport, but this one is different. There`s a spring in your step, a bounce when you move. You feel lighter because you`re walking on a rubber band.\par \par \uc2 A nearly 400-foot-long moving rubber band made from recycled tires and sitting on rubber rollers, which is why --\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: They`re just like super bouncy.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The three moving walkways have gone viral on social media.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from United)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Jennifer Garner even showed off her ballet moves to nearly three million viewers.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jennifer Garner)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But if you want a ride, better hop to it, because on Tuesday, these moving rubber bands come to a halt as part of a $2.6 billion terminal renovation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It is bouncier.\par \par \uc2 DOUG YAKEL (\uc2 San Francisco International Airport\uc2  public information officer): I did not believe that I would get calls on social media to save them.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Airport spokesman, Doug Yakel says he too is a fan. But just finding parts to keep them running gets harder and costlier every year. Fixing them can take weeks or even months.\par \par \uc2 DOUG YAKEL: So this is one of those times where, as an airport, you kind of have to make a tough decision. And it`s really focused on offering a reliable, consistent airport to your passengers.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Moving walkways started to take off at airports in the late 1950s. San Francisco`s airport added what at the time were the longest in the world, back in 1964, and by the time Terminal 3 opened in 1979, they were as common as these ticket counters and lines of passengers.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to miss it?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Yes. I prefer the bouncy light feel than walking on hard whatever.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: When the work is done in 2027, Terminal 3 will add 325,000 square feet and boast new energy efficient, but not bouncy, moving walkways.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It`s sad to see it go.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: So jump on one last time before this rubber meets the road to retirement.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, CBS News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Rubber band man, no more.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Sunday. I`m Jericka Duncan in New York. We thank you for watching. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020241104ekb300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For November 3, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Weijia Jiang, Kris Van Cleave, Nikole Killion, Caitlin Huey-Burns,  Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,878 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: It`s down to the final hours of campaign 2024, and election night is shaping up to be an epic cliffhanger.\par \par \uc2 Barnstorming the battlegrounds on Saturday, both presidential candidates stumped for every last vote, with their arguments ranging from the serious...\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): This is not someone who is thinking about how to make your life better.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And this is someone who is increasingly unstable, obsessed with revenge, consumed with grievance.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: And the man is out for unchecked power.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We got to put America first.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And we will stop illegal immigration once and for all. We will not be invaded. We will not be occupied. We will not be overrun. We will not be conquered.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... to the silly.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH (Actress): I`m going to vote for us.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Great. Any chance you are registered in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH: Nope, I am not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Well, it was worth a shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The day capped a wild week on the campaign trail filled with distractions that drew America`s attention to two key voting blocs, Latinos and women, first the fallout from a racist joke that fell flat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY HINCHCLIFFE (Comedian): A floating island of garbage in the middle of the ocean right now, yes, I think it`s called Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democrats jumped on the Trump campaign misstep, but when the current president bungled his party`s message of inclusion...\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The only garbage I see floating out there is his supporters.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... the former president played up Biden`s unforced error and sharpened his plea for the hotly contested Latino vote.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I love the Hispanics. I love them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The battle of the sexes, at least in the voting booth, is also considered crucial in these final days.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m going to do it, whether the women like it or not. I`m going to protect them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Harris is already running well ahead when it comes to women. Will the gender gap widen with Trump`s sexist comments and his violent rhetoric, like this mention of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: She`s a radical war hawk. Let`s put her with a rifle standing there with nine barrels shooting at her, OK? Let`s see how she feels about it when the guns are trained on her face.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk about it all with two key senators, Florida Republican \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Nevada Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, reality checks on the state of the economy and election security.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 More than 76 million Americans have voted already. That`s almost half of those expected to. Our CBS News polling updated overnight shows what we have known for some time. The contests in the seven battleground states are all essentially tied, as they`re within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 We have reporters in all seven states, and we will hear from four of them today, starting with our Caitlin Huey-Burns, who is in Greensboro, North Carolina -- Caitlin.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Margaret, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  campaigned here in Greensboro last night and is returning to the state today and tomorrow, all a sign that this state is up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We can`t take a chance of losing the great state of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The Tar Heel State has been reliable for Republicans. No Democrat has won it since 2008.\par \par \uc2 But of all the states Trump won in 2020, his victory in North Carolina was the closest. And both campaigns are competing for voters on the same turf. In fact, Trump and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  touched down here at the same time yesterday. Their planes literally parked next to each other on the tarmac.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats hope the state`s changing demographics and voters` views on abortion access work in their favor. Republicans, meanwhile, are encouraged by the number of their voters who have already cast ballots. Early voting ended here yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: I`m Nikole Killion in Georgia, where both candidates are making last-minute stops this weekend. The Peach State has been in place since President Biden flipped it in 2020.\par \par \uc2 According to the secretary of state, over four million ballots have been cast. That`s more than three in four who were expected to vote this year. Some of the highest participation totals are coming from Republican strongholds outside of Atlanta, something GOP sources tell us they`re encouraged by.\par \par \uc2 But top Democrats in this state believe they`re also competitive. Plus, more than a million black voters have cast ballots. That is a critical demographic, particularly for the vice president, although the former president is trying to siphon off support.\par \par \uc2 This weekend marks the last time that Harris and Trump will likely be in Georgia, but their running mates will be here. And both campaigns have events planned through the election, with the goal of capturing this state`s coveted 16 electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: I`m Kris Van Cleave in Scottsdale, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Already statewide, nearly half of registered voters have cast their vote either in person early or by mail. And that comes after a push by both parties to get people out early, and includes almost everyone at this Vance rally in Scottsdale, on the ballot, a closely watched Senate race and abortion.\par \par \uc2 Voters leaning towards Trump tell us immigration and inflation are key issues. Voters leaning Harris see different solutions for the border and are focused on women`s rights and protecting democracy. One place where both sides agree is concerns over voter fatigue due to a longer-than-usual ballot that could lead to delays for those voting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Local leaders say security will be tight.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: I`m Weijia Jiang in Philadelphia, where the Harris campaign says volunteers have knocked on nearly one million doors in Pennsylvania in the last 24 hours alone.\par \par \uc2 Her team believes that an aggressive ground game will drive up Democratic turnout among her base, while the Trump campaign has also worked to expand support from voters of color, especially black and Latino men.\par \par \uc2 Another top line here in Pennsylvania, the battle of the sexes. A Trump campaign senior adviser says they are banking on a big boost from men and going after those who usually don`t vote, especially in rural areas.\par \par \uc2 But the Harris team is confident that women, including moderate Republicans, will seal the deal for a victory. One group of Harris supporters we spoke with made up of women who identify as lifelong Republicans say they have talked to thousands of so-called silent Harris voters in the suburbs who plan to vote for the vice president, even though they won`t admit it, even to their husbands.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We have had a lot of women express a genuine discomfort or almost a fear of telling other people in their lives that they`re going to vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But a senior Trump campaign official tells us they do not believe there are enough of those voters to make a real difference.\par \par \uc2 Both nominees plan to hold multiple events in Pennsylvania before Election Day. In fact, Harris will spend all tomorrow storming the state, with several stops ending right here in Philly for her grand finale campaign rally -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s Weijia Jiang in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 We go now to Florida Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (R-Florida): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In recent days, the Harris campaign has said they have seen favorable movement towards them with Latinos and with undecided voters, particularly following that New York rally we referenced there, with the insult comment, making remarks about Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been focused on outreach to Hispanic voters for the Trump campaign. Are you concerned that this unforced error hurt outreach?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Well, there was no unforced error. I mean, a comedian made a joke, and it was tasteless, but it wasn`t Trump that said it.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t -- this guy is not going to be in his Cabinet. I mean, this comedian is not going to be a member of his government. He`s certainly not running for anything. It was a comedian who tells tasteless jokes all the time. I encourage everyone to, well, I guess watch segments of the Tom Brady roast, and you`ll see the kind of jokes he tells. He`s an insult comic.\par \par \uc2 Look, there`s no new outreach to...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: At a Trump rally.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Yes. I mean, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Hired by the campaign.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: He spoke two hours before Trump.\par \par \uc2 Well, again, I think what`s more relevant, if you want to say -- so, it had to be done in hindsight, the guy probably doesn`t get invited, OK, especially with a joke like that.\par \par \uc2 But I think more relevant is the fact that the sitting president of the United States said out loud what most people in the Democratic Party and hierarchy believe, and that is that anyone who votes for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is garbage, is a racist, is a hater, is a Nazi, as -- as the vice president candidate of theirs has accused anyone who attended the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC was running images of Nazi rallies from Germany side by side with images from \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  a week ago. So, there -- you asked me, why are they so confident?\par \par \uc2 If you`re so confident about victory, the way that the Harris people say they are, then why would you be out there attacking not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , attacking anyone who is thinking about voting for him by labeling them as, if you vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you`re this evil, horrible human being?\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t seem to be like the actions of a confident campaign. It seems to me like the actions of a scared one.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I was speaking specifically about that -- that outreach that you`ve been doing to Hispanic voters. It sounds like you think this is negligible.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about women voters, because we`re seeing in our data that the Trump campaign may have an issue there.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what former Trump administration \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  said this past week about that \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR NIKKI HALEY (R-South Carolina): This bromance and this masculinity stuff, I mean, it borders on edgy, to the point that it`s going to make women uncomfortable. You have got affiliated PACs that are doing commercials about calling Kamala the C-word. Or you had speakers at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  referring to her and her pimps.\par \par \uc2 That is not the way to win women.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you share the former governor`s concerns that this is a vulnerability?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Well, first of all, I want to point out, and no one talks about this, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a bigger problem among men than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does among women.\par \par \uc2 Are there women that are going to vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? Of course. Are there women that don`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Of course. Are there women that are going to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Yes, there are, a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 And I think you`ll see that bear out on Election Day. Ultimately, the bottom line is that what this election is going to be about, when people go vote, they`re not -- they`re going to be voting on three things that are critical to them. How much does it cost to live in this country, and can you afford the things that people work hard to be able to afford? Because over the last four years, they have not.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel safe and secure? And the answer is, no, people do not face -- feel safe and secure, when you have dangerous criminal elements entering this country across the open border that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  allowed. And number three is, how close is this country to being dragged into some sort of global conflict?\par \par \uc2 We now have troops from North Korea side by side with Russians on the ground in Ukraine. We`re edging closer every single month to some broader global conflict that could involve the United States. And guess who`s going to have to go fight in those wars? The sons and the daughters and the children of the people who are going to decide this election, and that includes over half the country, which is women voters.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going to be on people`s mind, and that`s what should be on their mind, because that`s what`s at stake in this election.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, in terms of safety and security, that sound bite a moment ago from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  talking about training guns on the face of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the Trump campaign...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: That`s not what he said.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... says that was about foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: It was...;\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we just played it for our viewers and listeners who heard it.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Isn`t it possible to make the case without using rhetoric like that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t talk like someone who`s been in Washington for 30 years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Training guns on her face?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: He doesn`t say it the way I would have said it. No, but that`s not what he said, Margaret. You guys know that. Come on.\par \par \uc2 I mean, everybody knows exactly what he was saying. What he was saying is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We played the sound bite.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: ... you`re so much in favor of war.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you -- no, you played a piece of the sound bite, because, in another piece of it, he said he would give her a gun to go stand in conflict as well. You don`t normally give a gun to someone that is going to be facing a firing squad, which is what much of the media made it sound like.\par \par \uc2 The point he was making is not a new point. It is a point that has been made by people in both parties for decades. And that is, you`re all for war, and it`s easy to be for war when you`re in some fancy building and you`re safe and sound in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Let`s see how much you are for war when you yourself get deployed into combat. That`s the point that he was making.\par \par \uc2 That he uses language that maybe is not what we typically hear from someone that works at a think tank...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: ... well, fine.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s truly, not just unfair, it`s egregious to see that reported the way that it was, along with other things that are out there. I have never seen such a concerted effort.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I have always believed there`s bias, because no one`s unbiased, but I have never seen such a concerted effort like what I have seen, especially in the last two weeks, among multiple media outlets in this country to, in some cases, breathlessly distort and lie about what`s being said and to create and manufacture these gotcha moments against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I have never seen it before.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: It`s over the top.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator, you are the ranking member on the Senate Intelligence Committee. You also know that former commander in chief is not just some guy off the street, and words matter.\par \par \uc2 U.S. intelligence has specifically warned...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: They do matter. They do matter.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... specifically warned about the risk of political violence in this country, some of it stoked by foreign adversaries, Iran trying to do that, Russia trying to do that, in the days after the election.\par \par \uc2 Are you confident in the security and the integrity of the election?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Well, I`m confident in the security and integrity of the elections, and especially because we have strong systems across this country.\par \par \uc2 It`s spread across 50 states. That doesn`t mean there aren`t going to be problems. We saw problems earlier in the week in Pennsylvania, and they were resolved by a court, where people were not allowed -- being allowed in one county from accessing their on-demand mail ballot at the spot. There are going to be issues like that.\par \par \uc2 They`re being confronted at the front end, and those things are being looked at. Look, in the state of Michigan, we know now -- and this is not me making it up. I can`t believe it hasn`t received more coverage. A Chinese national registered to vote and voted. And now he`s being charged for doing that. But guess what? His ballot is going to count.\par \par \uc2 We know at least one noncitizen`s ballot is going to count in Michigan because they don`t know which ballot it is and they can`t retrieve it. These are facts. Now, whether that`s widespread or not, I hope not, but these are things that are happening, and they need to be watched at.\par \par \uc2 As far as foreign adversaries, sure. I mean, Iran wants to kill \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Iran -- this has been fully documented. They are trying to kill \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You talk about violent rhetoric. He`s the only candidate in this race, and not because I wish this on anybody else, who`s had not one, but two assassination attempts against his life.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. So...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: So, of course we`re all concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the case you talked about in Michigan, we have tracked that. The secretary of state of that state has actually brought charges, has made that public. That individual may actually be deported...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Yes. But that vote`s still going to count.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... may actually be deported because there are laws in this country that punish people...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: They are. But the vote still counts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... when they are illegal, and they try to vote in federal elections. That 19-year-old is going to face some consequences.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Correct. But the vote still counts.\par \par \uc2 He is. And he`s going to -- but his vote still counts. His vote will still be counted.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood. I`m just trying to make clear that there are systems...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: But that`s a big deal. I think that`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... as you pointed to, as the courts are also playing a role here.\par \par \uc2 I need to ask you about China, though. This massive and pervasive hacking by China of U.S. telecom companies is significant. Your colleague Mark Warner said it`s the most significant breach he has ever seen in his entire time on the committee.\par \par \uc2 Do you know if China has been able to access the audio of Americans` phone calls?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: Well, I`m not going to comment on what they access, but I agree with Mark Warner`s statement.\par \par \uc2 It is an egregious, outrageous and dangerous breach of our telecommunications systems across multiple companies. I will leave it at that. I think, as time goes on, we`re going to learn more about it. Some of it will be made public. I think there`s more that`s still being gathered.\par \par \uc2 It`s a very serious situation that we face and I think one that is quite threatening, maybe not directly to the election...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: ... per se, but certainly to the national security of our country moving forward. It`s a vulnerability that no one imagined or anticipated.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO: But here it is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Understood.\par \par \uc2 Senator Rubio, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a minute.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined by Nevada Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , who joins us from Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2  (D-Nevada): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re going to be tracking your state very closely and all six of its electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 In this early vote, we`ve seen half of Nevada`s active registered voters have cast ballots, and there`s some anxiety among Democrats because Republicans currently have a lead statewide. Are you confident the remaining ballots will close that gap and deliver the state to Harris?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2 : Well, Margaret, I can tell you a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 One, I am optimistic that we are going to win, not only for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , but for \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2 , in this state. I am absolutely confident in our ground game, in our canvassing and knocking on doors and talking to our voters, because that`s literally what matters here, is making sure that our voters, who are paying attention now in a swing state like Nevada, that somebody is talking to them, because there`s so much information out there.\par \par \uc2 And now is the time for us to connect with our voters and talk to them about the issues that they care about. So, yes, I am very, very confident.\par \par \uc2 And the only other thing, let me say this, Margaret, is, you know, I was reelected just recently, and there was a Republican advantage in my race, in my midterm, and I won. Not only did I win. Our congressional delegation won. And so we also won our -- our state seats in our state House.\par \par \uc2 So what we know in Nevada, it is really about that organization. It`s knocking on those doors. It`s phone-banking. It`s talking to our voters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO: We also have a large population of our voters who are nonpartisan. Over a third are nonpartisan. And those are the independents in other states, and they lean Democratic. They leaned my way.\par \par \uc2 And so I am very, very confident that, as we are working our ground game, we`re going to organize our way to victory here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You -- you mentioned your victory in 2022. That was just by 8,000 votes. So this -- this could be a tight one as well this time around.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign talks about making inroads with the working-class population, in particular, on the issue of the economy. Your state has the second highest unemployment rate in the country. The majority of voters do not have a college degree.\par \par \uc2 Why shouldn`t the Trump campaign be confident that they are making inroads with this key demographic?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2 : Well, a couple of things, Margaret. You talked about my -- my tight race. They`re always tight in Nevada. It`s a swing state. They`re always close. It`s always within the margin.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why it is important you get out and talk to voters and you don`t take anyone for granted. And you`re right. A large part of our population, our service industry, those are high school-educated individuals. That`s my family. They worked in this industry.\par \par \uc2 And these are the individuals we want to talk to. But we also have been engaging them on the issues that matter to them. And if you look at our strong organized labor, which is the culinary, which is made up most of the service industry, along with our building trades, they`re knocking on those doors in favor of the Democrats, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So there is this outright concerted effort by everyone to make sure our voters understand who is -- what`s at stake here. And there`s a stark difference. I mean, you know, let me just say...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2 : ... because this is important for voters to know this, that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has already talked about, she wants to represent everyone. She wants to lift everybody up.\par \par \uc2 No matter who voted for her, she`s going to represent them. She`s going to start day one with a to-do list to how she works on the issues that she knows that many voters are challenged with. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to walk in the Oval Office with an enemies list. There`s a difference between the two.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO: And when you talk to voters about it who are expecting that in Nevada, in a swing state, that makes the difference in whether or not we are going to win. And we are going to win.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But does your party -- we played what the current president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , said about Trump supporters, and some have compared that, Republicans have compared that to what Hillary Clinton said about a basket of deplorables.\par \par \uc2 Does your party have a disdain problem in talking down to working class voters in particular?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2 : Well, I can tell you here in Nevada in general -- first of all, let`s just say, but -- we know this, that President Biden clarified his statement, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has made it very clear she wants to represent everyone, no matter...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, he quibbled about an apostrophe in the transcript.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2 : But -- but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made it very clear moving forward that she wants to represent everyone, right, no matter whether you voted for her or not.\par \par \uc2 And whoever you vote for, you shouldn`t be criticized for that. And she`s made that very clear.\par \par \uc2 What -- what I hear from voters in Nevada -- and this is where the -- where it`s relevant, not the spin rooms, not the polls, not what you`re hearing on the national news, but what are voters saying, and what do they care about? And they`re listening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2 : And I can tell you, my voters in Nevada that I talk to, which is really a large population of not only our blue- collar, but minority Latinos, a -- excuse me, Asian American Pacific Islanders, a vibrant Black community, Native Americans -- they are listening to, what are the issues?\par \par \uc2 Who`s bringing the solutions, right? Who has the solutions to address the affordable housing issue? That`s \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO\uc2 : Who has the issues to address how we lower health care costs and keep them low for all Americans? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Who has the idea about how we protect the Affordable Care Act and not repeal it? That`s \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So those conversations matter to my voters in Nevada, and that`s what`s relevant at the end of the day and how we`re going to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And your state also will be key to watch. You do have abortion on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Even though it`s already legal in your state, there is talk about enshrining it in the Constitution, based on what happens Tuesday. We`ll be following that and what happens with your party.\par \par \uc2 Senator, we have to leave it there for time. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back with a lot more Face the Nation, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Be sure to tune into CBS News on Tuesday night, when we will be live from our election headquarters in New York City. I will be there, along with Norah O`Donnell, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , Gayle King, and the rest of our political team for what is likely to be a very long night.\par \par \uc2 Our coverage starts at 4:00 p.m. Eastern on CBS News 24/7 Streaming, 7:00 p.m. Eastern on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  television network.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION, and our panel of election law and security experts.\par \par \uc2 David Becker is the executive director of the Center for Election, Innovation and Research. \uc2 Chris Krebs\uc2  is the former head of \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2  under the Trump administration. And Samantha Vinograd was most recently a counterterrorism official at the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you all.\par \par \uc2 Setting the table for a consequential week.\par \par \uc2 Chris, I want to start with you.\par \par \uc2 We`re in that 48-hour window that \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  warned was going to be rife with attempts to hack people`s minds. We`ve seen at least four fake videos the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and U.S. intelligence have warned are meant to manipulate. Yesterday, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  said there were two out there that are fake. Before that we had disclosed that Russia was behind a Bucks County, Pennsylvania, fake video and one targeting voters in Georgia, falsely claiming that Haitian migrants were - were voting.\par \par \uc2 How do these tactics that Russia is using now compare to what you saw in 2020 and back in 2016?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS\uc2 : Well, they`ve evolved their techniques from generating and launching content from, for instance, St. Petersburg. That`s what we saw in 2016. You had the internet research agency operating out of an office building, almost like, you know, a normal business.\par \par \uc2 Over time, though, given the improvements in the intelligence community, cyber command, and our own awareness of the activities and what they`re doing, they`ve started to push the boundaries out. And so they`re actually generating content and then they`re laundering it effectively through other platforms, like Telegram and even now what we`re seeing is a significant amount of laundering through X.\par \par \uc2 And then U.S. citizens pick up this information and pick up these videos, and then they promote it on their own accounts and on their own social media platforms, like \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . So, they are moving it out. They`re avoiding the techniques that U.S. law enforcement intelligence has deployed and developed. And so they`re not as good though. So, a lot of these videos that we`re seeing, they`re actually quite amateurish. That`s not to say they can`t have better content coming forward, but really they`re just kind of flooding the zone and trying to distract.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And confuse and sow - and sow distrust of our institutions.\par \par \uc2 Sam, U.S. intelligence has been briefing and disclosing. That`s part of what they`re doing to try to counteract this.\par \par \uc2 We also saw early on warnings about Iran and what they are doing. The assessment Iran favors Harris, Russia favors Trump.\par \par \uc2 What form of influence are we seeing?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Well, Iran has two main goals in this election cycle. First, to denigrate President Trump. They have expressed present - excuse me, a preference for Vice president Harris based upon Trump`s record in office and their perception of what Mr. Trump would do if elected. That`s objective one.\par \par \uc2 Objective two is to sow discord. And they view undermining perception about thein integrity of our election as an effective tool. So, they are using influence operations to denigrate Mr. Trump and to sow discord using AI- generated content really focused in particular on, for example, allegations of voter fraud. We should expect Iran to continue increasingly sophisticated influence operations related to perceptions of a lack of election integrity. That will last well after polls close, Margaret. This is the most attenuated election security related threat environment we have likely seen in history.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because it`s not about just changing votes. It`s about not trusting our democracy.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: David, 76 million Americans already voted. So, can we say those numbers show the adversaries are failing, or are the election workers you`re speaking with really concerned about what`s to come?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Well, it`s - it has increased our ability to be resilient against the disinformation. The more people who vote early, they`re effectively inoculated themselves against disinformation that might be inclined to be intended to help them self-suppress their vote or confuse them about how to vote. So, the fact that we`ve had over 76 million vote already, and that`s going to increase by several million more once all the mail ballots are in, once there`s even early voting going on today, still in about 27 states and D.C. So, that`s very good. That means the number of people that still need to vote on Election Day, when disinformation might be very focused on them, trying to get them confused with very little time for them to be resilient against that disinformation, that`s going to affect fewer voters. That`s a very good thing. And I think we`re seeing, especially in states like Georgia and North Carolina, well over 4 million votes in each of those states has been cast. Millions of votes in many other - of the swing states. And so far, very few problems despite efforts to create problems perhaps from our adversaries overseas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you would point people to the secretaries of state themselves, their websites, for reliable information?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Yes. Whenever you need information, go to your state election website. That`s usually the secretary of state`s website. Sometimes, in North Carolina, Wisconsin, there`s an election commission or an election board. Go to your local election website if you know your country or locality where elections are taking place. And if you need help, go to vote.gov. That will navigate you there eventually. Go - just rely upon those sources solely for information.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Chris, when you were in government, we did see, back in 2018, disclosure by the general at the head of then Cyber Command and the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2  say that - that the government at that time set up a small group to basically knock Russia offline, to physically stop them from some of these influence operations.\par \par \uc2 Can the U.S. undertake offensive operations like that now?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: Absolutely. I do think though that the infrastructure that the adversaries or the Russians and the Iran have put into place may not be where it once was. It has moved. Keep in mind that earlier this year the Department of Justice took offline a disinformation group that we track as Doppelganger, they took their various websites offline. And they did it in a way that was timed so that they could not rebuild those websites and reestablish the engagement with those websites in time for the election. So, there have been steps taken to ensure that the bad guys can`t execute their strategies.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, you have organizations, like SISA, that are doing more with more jurisdictions on election security than at any other point previously. So, they have a lot of confidence around the resilience of the system.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what you said about using Americans to spread disinformation.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: People are becoming unwitting agents of a foreign adversary essentially by re-tweeting or reposting some of these fake videos.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: We have seen that, right? So, there was another Department of Justice announcement that a media outlet, based out of Tennessee, was receiving funding from the Russians, from RT, a cutout (ph) -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Tenet Group.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS: Tenet Group.\par \par \uc2 Now, to David`s earlier point, that is why it is so critically important that American voters, when they`re looking for information about the election, they go to their relevant local or state official. They know what`s going on. Go - if you`re in Georgia, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  and Gabe Sterling are probably running the tightest ship when it comes to elections in the country right now. They`re incredibly effective. They`re out there. They`re putting information in the hands of the American people so they understand what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 And so when Russian operations like the Haitian voters pops up, they were on it immediately and they knocked it down. That is hugely effective in maintaining trust of the process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sam, we have seen concerns about physical security, though, as well at voting locations. The Democratic governor of Washington state has activated the National Guard, put them on stand-by, citing general and specific information and concerns about violence and unrest. You did have that drop box in Vancouver that damaged hundreds of ballots. There was an incendiary device there. Are there specific plots the federal government knows of at this time?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Well, it is a generally heightened threat environment, both in terms of complexity and the length of time that terrorism related threats, criminal threats and influence operations by nation states will last related to the election. It is just prudent planning, Margaret, in this threat environment for officials at all levels of government, federal, state and local, to engage in detailed operational planning. State and local officials are in the lead for the security in their jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 Now, in my experience, particularly post January 6th, those operational plans must include a full sense of all available law enforcement resources. National Guard is the last resort. You have local resources, state resources and then, as necessary, National Guard, as well as a clear sense of who to call when so that there aren`t communication failures.\par \par \uc2 Federal officials have invested a lot of money and time in doing exercises, hundreds them with state and local officials, sharing intelligence and information, and trying to share best practices. But at the end of the day, the state and local officials are in the lead and should have clear operational plans for a range of scenarios that may arise, in particular large scale security incidents that may arise with little or no warning. We learned lessons from January 6th. And from my conversations with former colleagues, that operational planning has been underway for years, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: David, I want to quickly get you to respond to what Senator Rubio was raising during our interview. He was talking about the charges brought by the secretary - or I mentioned the charged brought by the secretary of state against the individual he highlighted had cast a vote despite not being a citizen.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How is it that someone who`s not a citizen could register and cast a ballot? How did that even happen?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Well, we have several protections in place to make sure it`s very, very unlikely this is going to happen. We`ve seen places like Ohio, which has documented only six cases of non-citizen voting over a decade, dating back to 2014. We have places like Georgia, that has documented nine cases of non-citizen voting since 2008. So, it does happen, but it`s extremely rare. And I`m sure Michigan is going to go through the process of evaluating exactly what went wrong in this one particular instance.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also important to note, this is one case. It was very swiftly dealt with. This individual is going to lose his administration at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 . He`s going to be prosecuted. He might go to jail. And he`s almost certainly to be deported.\par \par \uc2 This is a cautionary tale. It`s one of the reasons we know that non-citizen voting is extremely rare. It`s not zero, but it`s very close to zero because the potential benefit to the person who perpetrates that crime, one vote in an election with 160 million ballots is far outweighed by the potential risk that they`re facing of deportation and other jail time, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 I wanted you to explain the process, because I think explaining how our courts work -\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How the systems are set up to deal with these things is important.\par \par \uc2 One of the things disclosed in the U.S. intelligence report put out by the Office of the Director of National Intelligence was that Russia`s amplifying these claims of non-citizen voting.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, that`s what happens.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Right. That`s what happens. And there are plenty of protections in place to make sure that this is extremely rare. As close to zero as it can be.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you. Thanks for your perspective. I know we`ll keep you on speed dial throughout the week.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS:\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To dissect some of the new economic indicators we`ve been seeing, we`re joined by Mohamed El-Erian. He is the chief economic adviser at \uc2 Allianz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the program.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: (Chief Economic Advisor, \uc2 Allianz\uc2 ): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, we saw on Friday this jobs report that indicated job creation stalled a bit in October. There were some complicating factors, like hurricanes and a strike at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . But what`s your perspective on what this indicates for the next president of the United States?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: So, most of the report, as you pointed out, was distorted in a really important manner by the strikes by the hurricanes. The bottom line, Margaret, is we have an economy that has been growing robustly. Inflation is coming down. And the main challenge for the next administration is not just to maintain what is called economic exceptionalism, because we`re outperforming all other advanced economies, but also to continue to reposition it for the engines of tomorrow`s prosperity. And that`s absolutely critical.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we know the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is meeting later this week, may move on interest rates again since inflation is around or getting close to that 2 percent target. But for average people, they see housing prices are high, they see grocery prices are still high. Where`s the scenario where those prices actually come down?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: Yes, and that`s what everybody`s expecting, but it`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 Look, the good news is interest rates will continue to come down. The good news is inflation, which is the rate of increase of the cost of living will come down. But it`s very hard to bring down prices. And that`s one political problem is, when you tell people inflation is coming down, in their head they think prices are coming down, not the rate of increase of prices. So, it`s a misunderstanding, unfortunately. But you`ve to be careful what you wish for, because if prices come down significantly, we are then in something much worse economically.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that misunderstanding being used quite a lot on the campaign trail, Mohamed, as you know.\par \par \uc2 I know the stock market is not an indicator of what`s happening with the economy, but it is forward-looking. This week what should we be expecting as a reaction to the outcome of this election?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: So, believe it or not, a lot of investors are just on the sideline. They see so many possibilities, be it for the presidential race, be it for Congress, or even the extent to which what is promised by presidential candidates will become policy. There stands to be a big gap between the two. So, quite a few investors have said, you know what, there`s way too much uncertainty. I`m just standing on the sideline and waiting to see what emerges.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A gap between what`s promised and what`s delivered is a very diplomatic way of putting campaigning there, Mohamed. But neither candidate is even making a gesture towards reducing the debt burden in this country. And in terms of what`s being promised, the plans that the vice president has put forward is estimated by this Committee for a Responsible Budget to increase the federal debt by nearly $4 trillion through 2035. Donald Trump`s plans would increase it by nearly $8 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Congress, as you suggested there, they can put the brakes on anything here. But does this continued promise of spending and no promise of reducing it concern you?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: It does. It does in a big way.\par \par \uc2 Look, it was unthinkable that we would have almost 30 months of unemployment at 4 percent or below and we`d run fiscal deficit of six to eight percent. Remember, John Kennedy said, when the sun is shining you fix the roof. Instead, the sun has been shining on our economy and we`ve created more roofs. Our debt to GDP is 120 percent of - our debt is 120 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Look, both candidates, whether they like it or not, are going to have to find a way to moderate deficits and debt and critically, Margaret, to create operational flexibility in the budget. The budget is becoming more and more rigid, which means that it`s harder to respond to unanticipated shocks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Unanticipated shocks.\par \par \uc2 In terms of one of the things that we have heard from the Trump campaign quite a lot is this pledge to use tariffs to create income for the government somehow. That`s how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  suggests using them.\par \par \uc2 Do you view tariffs as a regressive tax that will hurt poor people by raising prices for them, or do you view them as somehow, you know, a rising tide that floats all boats?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: So, tariffs are a dangerous tool. They have limited use, but if they are overused, they become counterproductive. It is - it is not a good idea to promise the use of tariffs to deliver all sorts of things. Be it a lower deficit, be it protecting domestic producers, be it putting pressure, leveraging influence on other countries. Tariffs have a role to play, but not to the extent - and similar for the other side. Industrial policy and regulation, they both have a role to play.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: But be careful of overreach.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. Fair point.\par \par \uc2 Mohamed El-Erian, thank you very much for your analysis. The economy consistently the number one issue for American voters in this election.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Joining us now for a preview of what we`ll all be looking for on Tuesday is our executive director of elections and surveys, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, this is neck-and-neck. You`ve been telling us that for weeks now. What is it that the campaigns are focusing on and what we should be looking for?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Hey there, Margaret. So, start with this. Here is the map. The states in white are all those razor-tight battleground states. But let`s get beyond the margin of error for a second and look at some of the political possibilities. And we can show you that in our polling models.\par \par \uc2 Start with this, what I think is one of the key dynamics in this race. There are voters who say they did not vote in 2020 and many of them, in fact about four in ten, say that they definitely will this time. Among them, especially the men among them, are for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the Trump campaign is trying to turn them out.\par \par \uc2 So, we wondered, what happens if he does? And I`ll show you. On this map we`re going to run that scenario through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  models. And if he does turn out a lot of those non-voters, those men, they boost him, he would do well across the sun belt, Arizona, Georgia, North Carolina and flip Pennsylvania, and that would be enough to put him over the top and give him the win. That`s a hypothetical, but it shows you how one of those campaign dynamics could play out.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the other side, there`s another important dynamic, and that is the gender gap. We`ve talked about it a lot. And I`ll show you a slide from one of our recent polls that illustrate that. So many more women voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Well, you`ve seen the Harris campaign out there trying not only to get women turnout, but also some crossover from Republicans, and in particular Republican women. So, we said, what happens if she can get those Republican numbers to match closer to what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got in 2020, driven in large part by women and Republican women. If I put that scenario into the map here, our models show that she would hold the blue wall states across the upper Midwest, keep Georgia in her column, even flip North Carolina, and that would give her an Electoral College victory.\par \par \uc2 All of this, again, hypothetical. But it puts it in political terms, some of the dynamics that could play out, and we`ll be watching, on election night, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`ll be watching in real time as the data comes in to the desk.\par \par \uc2 What is it that we should be tracking throughout the night?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: So, we`re going to show you everything as it comes in. And the first thing I`m going to show you is where the votes are, because sometimes, and I`ll pull up the 2020 map here in Georgia just as an example, you`re going to see red areas, Republican, you`re going to see blue areas, Democratic. But I`m always going to show, there are more people in some of these place than others. If I take this map and I rotate it, you`ll - what you`ll see in the example of Georgia is, the higher bars reflect more votes, and there`s more voters in Atlanta and metro Atlanta. And Democrats need to do well in places like that. Really get a lot of turnout there. Whereas across rural areas, you see it`s more flat on the map here. Not as many votes but Republicans very consistently kind of add to their margins there. That`s one way we`re going to show you the state in full.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I`m going to watch is patterns, shifts from the last election to this one. Again, as an example, if I were to show you the shift that happened from 2016 to 2020, in Georgia, Democrats, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at the time, got a lot of those counties around metro Atlanta shifting blue, those rural areas shifted red, Republicans. We`ll see if something like that happens again. So it`s where the votes come from, how they shift and the last thing, very quickly, Margaret, I`ll remind everybody is, patience. If I were to take that same Georgia timeline and show you what it looked like at say 9:00 p.m., just after poll closed, a couple hours, you see so much uncounted votes still in Georgia, Metro Atlanta hadn`t counted yet, et cetera. If this pattern comes out again on election night, we`ll want to say, hold on, let`s wait for all the votes to get counted before we know the winner.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Patience is a virtue.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching. Until next week. For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020241103ekb300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for October 26, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 David Wade, Ed O`Keefe, Nikole Killion, Holly Williams, Ramy  Inocencio, Andrew Kozak, Shanelle Kaul, Natalie Brand, Itay Hod \par \uc2 3,703 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Tonight, race to the finish. With 10 days to go, the presidential candidates hit the battlegrounds, bringing out the star power.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I`m Ed O`Keefe in Michigan. Vice President Harris calls in a Queen and a First Lady for help.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: I`m Nikole Killion in State College, Pennsylvania where former President Trump is barnstorming the battleground. Why he is making a viral appeal to young men.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Also tonight, Iran assesses the damage after Israel`s high stakes attack.\par \par \uc2 New Russian strikes in Ukraine with new worries about the flow of American firepower to the war`s frontlines.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Is it a Browning?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, it is Browning M2, .50 cal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Weather shift. The Northwest gets wet, while the East wonders if it will ever rain again. The forecast ahead.\par \par \uc2 On the \uc2 CBS\uc2  "Money Watch," the latest on McDonald`s E. coli trouble and turbulence at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (GROUP chanting "One day stronger.")\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Smithsonian National Zoo)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Plus, having a ball. The first video of the pandas` new habitat at the National Zoo.\par \par \uc2 And later, it is the little library that got really big. Hundreds of titles found in this California front yard.\par \par \uc2 JODEEN PERCEY (Not So Little Library): I knew I needed to change the name from "A Little Library" to "A Not So Little Library," because it`s just not a little library.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Good evening. Jericka is off, I`m David Wade.\par \par \uc2 With time ticking in the tightest of races, tonight, both presidential candidates are making their pitch to the ever-dwindling group of undecided voters.\par \par \uc2 More than 35 million Americans have already cast their ballots in person and through the mail. We have two reports from the campaign trail tonight. We begin with Ed O`Keefe in Kalamazoo, Michigan where Vice President Harris is bringing in some political star power. Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: David, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  has been reluctant to engage in partisan politics over the last eight years since she left the White House, but tonight, she made a direct explicit appeal, especially to men, saying they have to vote for Vice President Harris because the lives of women are at stake.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2  (Former First Lady of the United States): I am asking y`all from the core of my being to take our lives seriously.\par \par \uc2 So please, please do not hand our fates over to the likes of Trump, who knows nothing about us, who has shown deep contempt for us, because a vote for him is a vote against us, against our health, against our worth.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): For the second day in a row, Harris is focused on access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Because of Trump and what he did with the Supreme Court, we are looking at a health care crisis in America, which is affecting people of every background and gender.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Today in Michigan, she met with doctors who provide such services.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What is at stake is not just what we`re doing with respect to abortion care, but what reproductive justice means at large.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: While Friday night in Texas, dozens of other medical providers also spoke out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Harris campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 MAN: As a physician, I cannot describe the anguish of not being able to help my patients and the fear of spending the rest of my life behind bars.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But the biggest star of the night was Beyonce, who didn`t sing, she stumped.\par \par \uc2 BEYONCE (Singer): I`m not here as a celebrity. I`m not here as a politician. I`m here as a mother.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: From here, the vice president heads to Philadelphia on Sunday, the biggest city in that key battleground state where she is expected to go neighborhood to neighborhood, trying to find votes at the beginning of the last full week of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, she is coming right back here to Michigan. David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Ed O`Keefe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now from one battleground to another.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is in Pennsylvania, fresh off a visit with a powerhouse podcaster that left Trump apologizing. Nikole Killion is there in Pennsylvania for us tonight with the latest. Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 Whether it is here at \uc2 Penn State\uc2  or in a podcast with Joe Rogan, former President Trump is targeting his message to young voters, particularly young men.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made back-to-back blue wall visits stumping in Central Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We love \uc2 Penn State\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Following a stop in suburban Detroit, where he mocked Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` latest superstar endorsement.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : So Beyonce went up, spoke for a couple of minutes, and then left, and the place went crazy. They booed the hell out of everybody. They have to use people to get people to come.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Despite being interrupted by a protester --\par \par \uc2 MAN: Don`t do it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The former President appeared to receive a warmer reception today after leaving supporters in Traverse City, Michigan, waiting in the cold.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from October 25, 2024)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Trump, nearly three hours late, sent in a video apology.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : But it is all about winning and we had to spend some extra time with some people.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The former President flew from Texas after recording what may be the lengthiest interview of his campaign with podcaster, Joe Rogan.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from October 25, 2024, The Joe Rogan Experience)\par \par \uc2 JOE ROGAN ("The Joe Rogan Experience" podcast host): Your weave is getting wide --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The highly anticipated interview aimed at a mostly male audience.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : So I always got more publicity than other people, and I didn`t -- it wasn`t like I was trying. In fact, I don`t know exactly why. Maybe you can tell me why.\par \par \uc2 JOE ROGAN: I could definitely tell you. You say a lot of wild (EXPLETIVE DELETED).\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The former president and Rogan veered on and off topic for about three hours on everything from debunked election fraud claims to whether there is life on Mars.\par \par \uc2 The episode has racked up more than 18 million views on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  alone, and the former president is banking on another big audience when he rallies in Madison Square Garden, Sunday. David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: All right. Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, President Biden says he hopes Israel`s strike against Iran is the end, and the chief of the \uc2 UN\uc2  says he is deeply alarmed by the escalating tensions.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio has the latest developments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voiceover): Explosions over Tehran as Iranian missile defenses reacted to Israel`s projectiles.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI (Israel`s chief military spokesperson): The \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  is conducting precise strikes on military targets in Iran.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Israel`s military specified its war planes targeted surface-to-air missile systems and missile production sites.\par \par \uc2 Israel strikes were in response to Iran`s direct attack on October 1st, when Tehran launched nearly 200 ballistic missiles, but Iran has seemingly played down Israel`s latest attacks and with reports of only minor damage, the hope is that Iran will not respond, says Brigadier General Pini Yungman, who spent decades advancing Israel`s missile defense systems, including Iron Dome.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PINI YUNGMAN (RET) (Air and Missile Defense Systems Division, \uc2 Rafael Advanced Defense Systems\uc2  EVP and general manager): But if they will continue to launch, and attack Israel, we will hit Iran in a way that they will not be able to launch against us again.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Israel is already fighting wars on two fronts, in Lebanon and in Gaza after storming Northern Gaza`s last functioning hospital, Kamal Adwan, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  pulled back today, leaving behind destruction and death.\par \par \uc2 CBS News spoke to the hospital`s director, Hussam Abu Safiya earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 HUSSAM ABU SAFIYA (Kamal Adwan Hospital director): We don`t have medicine, medications, medical supply, food, water, blood unit, so we have catastrophic situations.\par \par \uc2 (HUSSAM ABU SAFIYA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: That situation, now a personal tragedy. An Israeli sniper shot his 16-year-old son, Ibrahim in the head.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Safiya today buried him, just 16 years old.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And tonight, President Biden said he hopes Israel`s strikes on Iran will be the end of this round of fighting. The US warned Iran not to strike back at Israel, David, to avoid blowing this conflict up into a regional war.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: So many concerns there.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To the war in Ukraine now.\par \par \uc2 Today, Russian attacks in two cities claimed the lives of at least five people, including a teenager. We have video here that shows the moments after a Russian drone strike hit an apartment building in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams reports there are new concerns about the future flow of American weapons to that country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The US has supplied Ukraine with over $60 billion in military assistance since Russia invaded in 2022, including these striker fighting vehicles used by Ukraine`s 80th Air Assault Brigade to launch an incursion into Russia in August aimed at weakening Moscow`s attacks in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Is it a Browning? Is that right?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, it is Browning M-2, .50 cal.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (VIKTOR ZAKAROV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Viktor Zakarov (sp?) is a truck driver turned striker driver who told us he is deeply grateful to Americans for the strikers and other aid.\par \par \uc2 Have you seen them save Ukrainian lives?\par \par \uc2 (VIKTOR ZAKAROV speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Seen it, heard it and felt it," he told us.\par \par \uc2 In Central Kyiv, \uc2 John McCain\uc2  Street was named by Ukraine in the late senator`s honor. But with this US presidential election too close to call, the mood has turned gloomy and anxious.\par \par \uc2 (HELENA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Helena`s (sp?) husband is serving in the military, and told us, "Ukrainians will hold on." She hopes Americans won`t abandon them.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could cut off the supply of weapons to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 (HELENA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Yes," she told us, "For us, it`s a matter of survival."\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, CBS News, Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, tonight, a \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut hospitalized after returning from space has now been released.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 NASA\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: They were part of the four-person crew who splashed down Friday morning after an eight-month stay on the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 The astronaut, who has not been identified, was kept for observation following the "medical issue."\par \par \uc2 Well tonight, the \uc2 US Navy\uc2  is apologizing for destroying a native village of Alaska nearly 150 years ago. Rear Admiral Mark Sucato formally apologized for attacking the village of Angoon.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Sealaska Heritage Institute)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The attack killed six children and caused such dire food shortages that villagers starved themselves so that children could eat. Angoon had been seeking an apology for decades.\par \par \uc2 Now to the weather and a big shift in the West: Rain and snow are in the forecast, while the East hasn`t even seen a drop. Let`s check in with CBS News meteorologist, Andrew Kozak. Andrew.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOZAK: Yes, good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 We are starting on the West Coast tonight where an atmospheric river is going to finally bring in some much-needed rain for much of the Pacific Northwest, Northern California, several rounds throughout the week, and of course, mountain snow.\par \par \uc2 We do have to watch for some flash flooding, as we are going to look at quite a bit of rain coming our way. Along with that, some cooler temperatures, already cooling down in California, Palm Springs in Southern California, Desert Southwest, 90s. But by Tuesday, we`re going for 70s in Phoenix, 60s in Vegas, and 70s in Palm Springs, so a nice cool down finally, feeling fall.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the East Coast, not a drop of rain in New York City all month for October; and Philadelphia, about to shatter the old record with over 29 days without rain.\par \par \uc2 David, a tale of two very different scenarios on either side of the country tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: No doubt. Andrew, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, it`s game two of the World Series taking place in Los Angeles after an historic start.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MLB/\uc2 Fox Sports\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Cortez delivers. Freeman hits the ball to right field.\par \par \uc2 She is gone.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: The Dodgers` Freddie Freeman, with a severely sprained ankle, hit the first ever, game ending Grand Slam in World Series history. It happened in the bottom of the 10th with the bases loaded and two outs. Final score, six to three.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MLB)\par \par \uc2 FREDDIE FREEMAN (Dodgers): Those are the kind of things when you`re five years old with your two older brothers and you`re playing with wiffle ball in the backyard. And those are the scenarios of -- scenarios you kind of dream about, bases loaded, two out in a World Series game.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: I had the same dream. It just didn`t happen for me.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, the highs and lows on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 And healthy food going to families in need, instead of going to waste.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Tonight, on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  "Money Watch." The NASDAQ closed this week at an all-time high amid a rough week for stocks.\par \par \uc2 Shanelle Kaul is on Wall Street outside the New York Stock Exchange with the details. Shanelle.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: And David, it`s been a mixed week for stocks, amid a flurry of corporate news, including big problems for one fast food giant.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL (voiceover): McDonald`s dealing with an E. coli outbreak. One person dead and at least 75 people sick across 13 states. The CDC warns that number is expected to rise.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It made me think twice about my afternoon snack.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: The fast-food chain stock saw its worst weekly loss in more than four years, a price drop of more than $22.00 a share.\par \par \uc2 MAN: How can you trust a place that allows this to happen?\par \par \uc2 (GROUP chanting "Strike. Strike.")\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: New turbulence for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . The jet maker reported a $6 billion third quarter loss this week as the strike by machinists enters its seventh week.\par \par \uc2 Workers are demanding the return of their pension plan.\par \par \uc2 JAKE ROSENFELD (Washington University professor): It would be pretty unprecedented for \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to agree to this particular demand, but \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is in a pretty unprecedented state as well, right, bleeding cash.\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: But \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , a bright spot. The EV maker reporting better than expected earnings.\par \par \uc2 Mortgages keep climbing, now topping six-and-a-half percent. Sales of existing homes fell last month to their lowest level in 14 years.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SHANELLE KAUL: And investors are also bracing for a potential disruption on the horizon with the October US jobs report due Friday and a tight presidential election. David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Shanelle, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead here on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, keeping excess food out of the landfill to fight hunger in the US.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  based in Washington, DC is now helping with the transfer of a crucial currency: healthy food.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Natalie Brand shows us how perfectly good food is going to people in need, instead of the landfill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE BRAND (voiceover): On a busy week at the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , the kitchen cranks out up to 8,000 meals. What`s not consumed, Chef Nick Cavaretta and his team carefully package and prep for donation to organizations in need.\par \par \uc2 NICK CAVARETTA (Chef): We love statistics here at the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , so we just get a weight on everything so we can monitor exactly how much food gets donated.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE BRAND: Cavaretta estimates up to 900 pounds any given week, 25,000 pounds over the past year.\par \par \uc2 You showed us all of these beautiful food. It would have been painful to throw it away.\par \par \uc2 CHEF NICK CAVARETTA: Yes, of course. I mean perfectly edible.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE BRAND: Volunteers from the organization Food Rescue US-DC pick up the leftovers weekly and deliver them on this day to a facility that provides housing to veterans who have experienced homelessness.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Harris, who served in the Air Force, says when her food stamps run low, this is what fills the fridge.\par \par \uc2 LISA HARRIS (Veteran experiencing homelessness): You may not think it, but you never know when you might be in this position, and I`m telling you, not knowing where your food is going to come from, not knowing when you`re going to eat, it`s hard. It`s very hard and we are appreciative.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE BRAND: Kate Urbank of Food Rescue US-Washington, DC chapter says her group serves a dual mission, fighting hunger and climate change by keeping the food out of landfills and redirecting it to nonprofits.\par \par \uc2 KATE URBANK (Food Rescue US-Washington, DC chapter): When you think about the fact that 30 to 40 percent of food is wasted and over 45 million people live in households experiencing food insecurity, it just makes a lot of sense to do the match.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE BRAND: For institutions like the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , where ending poverty is the mission, it is a win-win.\par \par \uc2 CHEF NICK CAVARETTA: If you can just help one person, it would be like a shame not to do that.\par \par \uc2 LISA HARRIS: When you`ve got food like this, people, please donate.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE BRAND: Lisa Harris hopes more organizations heed the call.\par \par \uc2 Natalie Brand, CBS News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Coming up next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, we head to the National Zoo where the new pandas are letting the good times roll.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Smithsonian Zoo)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: The National Zoo in Washington, DC has released the first video of its two new giant pandas.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Smithsonian Zoo)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: And here they are settling into their new home and well, having a ball. They`ve been playing in the pool and generally doing adorable panda things.\par \par \uc2 The pandas are currently under a standard quarantine. They arrived from China earlier this month, and they`ll make their public debut on January 24th, you know they`re going to be popular.\par \par \uc2 When we return, it may be a little library by name, but this California collection of books is booming.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Finally, tonight, have you ever seen those little free library boxes, take a book, leave a book.\par \par \uc2 Well as Itay Hod reports, one woman in California may be starting a whole new chapter in the growing movement.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD (voiceover): When Jodeen Percey of Concord, California, first started her little library. It was never meant to be a big deal, until it was.\par \par \uc2 JODEEN PERCEY: We started with just the "Little Library", and as time went on, we needed to add more shelves.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Now her front yard holds a collection that`s outgrown its title.\par \par \uc2 JODEEN PERCEY: I knew I needed to change the name from a "Little Library" to a "Not So Little Library," because it`s just not a little library.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Neighbors say this is the Goldilocks of book selections that feels just right.\par \par \uc2 LYNN MILLER (School bus driver): The public library, they`ve got all of those different genres and so many to look through, you don`t see anything, because there`s just so much.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: It`s overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 LYNN MILLER: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Percey took a page from the Little Free Library Movement which began in 2009 in Wisconsin. That`s where \uc2 Todd Bol\uc2  created the first ever dollhouse size structure on his front lawn, igniting a book sharing revolution.\par \par \uc2 Since then, his little free library has grown into a sprawling nonprofit with nearly 200,000 lending sites in more than 120 countries.\par \par \uc2 Greig Metzger, Little Free Library`s executive director says the organization has seen steady growth as people rediscover traditional books.\par \par \uc2 GREIG METZGER (Little Free Library`s executive director): I think that maybe the demise of a printed book might have been overstated a little bit.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Seven hundred and sixty-seven million physical books were sold last year, up from 697 million in 2019.\par \par \uc2 As for Percey, she says she`ll continue stocking these shelves, as long as people keep coming.\par \par \uc2 JODEEN PERCEY: That is what can bring us joy, it is putting a smile on somebody else`s face.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Lending a hand and much more, the generous outcome of a novel idea.\par \par \uc2 Itay Hod, CBS News, Concord, California.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WADE: Something tells me, her story is not over yet.\par \par \uc2 That`s the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Saturday.\par \par \uc2 First thing tomorrow, "CBS Sunday Morning" and Norah O`Donnell`s exclusive interview with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I`m David Wade in New York. Have a goodnight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020241027ekaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Workers Reject Contract Proposal; Trump Campaign in Georgia, Lashes Out at Kelly; Harris Appeals to Undecided Voters in Town Hall; North Korean Troops Training with Russian Forces; Doctor in Lebanon Hospital Compares Chaos in Gaza; DOJ Warns Musk $1 Million Check Giveaways May Be Illegal; Arizona Campaign Office Attack; Remembering Dodgers Great, Fernando Valenzuela; \uc2 FAA\uc2  Probing Close Calls at Our Nation`s Airports; High Levels of Lead Found in Syracuse Water; Trump and Harris Make Last Push to Key Voters; The Backbone of Broadway; Heart Health in the US; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; "Shrinking" Grows; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Kris Van Cleave, Nikole  Killion, Weijia Jiang, Imtiaz Tyab, Debora Patta, Jo Ling Kent, Jarred  Hill, Lilia Luciano, Vladimir Duthiers, Nancy Cordes, Anthony Mason, Celine  Gounder \par \uc2 14,299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets on a Thursday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers reject another contract offer extending their strike. What it means for the company and the economy.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Do you feel like \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has heard that message now?\par \par \uc2 JON HOLDEN (IAM Union District 751 president): Members wanted their pound of flesh, and they`re getting it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With less than two weeks until Election Day, Vice President Harris seizes on reports former President Trump praised Hitler.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (CNN host): Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a fascist?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: North Korea sends thousands of troops to Russia amid the war with Ukraine, prompting a warning from the White House.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): They will become legitimate military targets.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The Justice Department warning \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that his daily million dollar lottery for swing state voters may be against the law.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: LaGuardia airport can now boast about being named the best airport in the country by Forbes Travel Guide.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): For New Yorkers who want to check LaGuardia out, the easiest way to get there is to drive to Newark Airport and then fly there.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  dropped this diss track about Elon Musk`s recent appearance at a Trump rally.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN) (2024 vice presidential nominee): Elon is on that stage jumping around, skipping like a dip (bleep).\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: That is top tier Midwestern dad slam.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I believe the scale goes, Mister, Pal, Knucklehead, Real Piece o` Work, Bozo, Dip (bleep) and silently stands up and walks to garage.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s pretty funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m very familiar with that scale, even though I am not from the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think we all are familiar with that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us. We begin with a breaking story that could have a big impact on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Striking workers at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  voted to reject the company`s latest offer. It means the strike goes on at America`s largest exporter.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave is outside a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  facility in Renton, Washington.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Certainly not the way \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executives were hoping they`d be starting the day today, after 64 percent of striking workers rejected their latest labor offer.\par \par \uc2 It was a four-year deal. One perhaps bright side here, it did fare far better than their first offer, which was nearly unanimously rejected here. But nonetheless, this is Day 42 of the strike, 33,000 workers will stay on the picket line, shutting down the bulk of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s airplane production.\par \par \uc2 The rejected deal called for a 35 percent wage hike up from \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s initial offer of 25 percent. It also included increased health and retirement contributions, but the major sticking point for workers remains the loss of their pension. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has said that pension is not coming back.\par \par \uc2 Before the vote, we talked to the union president, Jon Holden about this impasse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Is it realistic to think a pension could come back? The auto workers said they wanted their pension back, it didn`t happen. I don`t know that I can point to a single union that`s won a pension back.\par \par \uc2 JON HOLDEN: Yes, it is the direction society is going, and it`s not right, it`s not okay. But our members are dealing with that, and some want that defined benefit pension, and I understand that fully.\par \par \uc2 We haven`t been able to achieve that, and I can`t say that we will by staying out on strike.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 Boeing\uc2  declined comment on the vote, but it came just hours after the company announced dismal third quarter earnings that included a loss of about $6 billion for the quarter alone. The company is preparing to lay off 10 percent of its global workforce to cut costs. There is no timeline for when negotiations may resume -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now taking you to politics and two very different strategies in these final days before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  appeared to be courting the moderates last night in a CNN town hall in Pennsylvania, calling her opponent unfit and dangerous, while former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appears to focus squarely on his own base at two campaign events in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion and Weijia Jiang are following the candidates. We`re going to start with Nikole with the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent much of his time here in Georgia, courting the evangelical vote and firing up conservatives appearing at an event last night alongside fire brands like \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ignited a packed Duluth, Georgia arena Wednesday night with fireworks ablaze.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We will fight, fight, fight and win, win, win.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Blighting into his opponent --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She takes some time off. I`ve gone 52 days in a row, and I`m not taking any days off, that I can tell you.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But he saved his more fiery rhetoric off stage for his former White House chief-of-staff, \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , who this week revealed then President Trump spoke favorably of Adolf Hitler and warned the former president could rule as a dictator if he wins a second term.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2  (Former White House chief-of-staff): He certainly falls into the general definition of fascist, for sure.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In a post on Truth Social, the former president slammed Kelly as a "total degenerate," a "low life" and a "bad general whose advice in the White House I no longer sought."\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: At a town hall Wednesday night, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was asked if she thinks Trump is a fascist.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Yes, I do. Yes, I do, and I also believe that the people who know him best on this subject should be trusted.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Trump hit back by falsely claiming the vice president called him Hitler. Many rally goers said they still stand with the former president.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD YATES (Trump voter): Well, he may say things he doesn`t mean sometimes, but you know what, we all say things we don`t mean sometimes.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH MANN (Trump voter): Did he run this country as a dictator the four years he was in? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: As early voting surpasses the two million mark here in Georgia, the Secretary of State`s Office confirms that it was the target of a cyberattack that was unsuccessful.\par \par \uc2 Later here in the Atlanta area, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will hold her first joint campaign event with former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and "The Boss," Bruce Springsteen -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 At that town hall in the Philly suburbs, Vice President Harris tried to appeal to persuadable voters, and she was pressed on her positions on immigration, the war in Gaza, and also grocery prices.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is in Philadelphia for us. Weijia, good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Tony. Good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 The Harris campaign tells us they believe that 10 percent of battleground voters are still up for grabs, including right here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 At that town hall, the vice president took questions directly from several of those voters who say they`re not convinced who to choose.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In an appeal to undecided voters, Vice President Harris vowed to bring change to the country.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: My administration will not be a continuation of the Biden administration. I bring to this role, my own ideas, and my own experience.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But at times, Harris struggled to provide specifics when asked about policy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So my question is concerning groceries. What would you do to bring prices down for Americans?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We will have a national ban on price gouging. There are a number of issues that we need to address in terms of bringing down the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: What would you do to ensure not another Palestinian dies due to bombs being funded by US tax dollars?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Far too many innocent Palestinian civilians have been killed. It`s unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Harris was pressed on why she now supports a border wall she once ridiculed as stupid.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Let`s talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that border wall.\par \par \uc2 So remember, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said Mexico would pay for it. Come on, they didn`t.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: But you`re agreeing --\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: So come on.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER -- to a bill that would earmark $650 million to continue building that wall.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We -- I pledge that I`m going to bring forward that bipartisan bill to further strengthen and secure our border.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: CNN`s town hall was held in Delaware County, 25 miles outside of Philadelphia. The campaign says it represents former GOP strongholds that could turn blue, where they are focused on finding Independents and moderate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Back in the city, volunteers are canvassing Philly, making sure voters cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Black Men Vote.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Billy Miller (sp?) is the state director for the non-partisan group, Black Men Vote.\par \par \uc2 Can you put into words how important turnout is?\par \par \uc2 BILLY MILLER (Black Men Vote state director): It is the most important thing in this election.\par \par \uc2 If every Philadelphia voter came out and voted one way or another, it wouldn`t matter what the rest of Pennsylvania did.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: A senior campaign official says next week, the vice president will make what they are calling a closing argument speech on the National Mall, the very same spot where Trump delivered his January 6th remarks right before the assault on the Capitol -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Weijia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We have a disturbing update about what could be the next war in Ukraine. The US and \uc2 NATO\uc2  say they have confirmed that troops from North Korea have started training with Russian forces. They say if the North Koreans joined the fighting in Ukraine, that would be a dangerous escalation of the war.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab time has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: It`s a scene that has set alarm bells ringing from Washington to Kyiv. North Korean soldiers, around 3,000 reportedly on the ground in Russia dressed as Russian military personnel.\par \par \uc2 The images not independently verified, apparently show the recruits at a training exercise near the far eastern Russian city of Vladivostok, just four hours from the border with North Korea, according to a White House assessment.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): After completing training, these soldiers could travel to Western Russia and then engage in combat against the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In June, when the autocratic leaders of Russia and North Korea, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Kim Jong-un held these direct talks, THEY also signed a Mutual Defense Pact, deepening ties that stretch back beyond the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 Since then, South Korean intelligence officials have said North Korea has sold more than a million artillery rounds to the Kremlin, bringing in desperately needed foreign currency.\par \par \uc2 While President Putin in dire need of more arms and men, is attempting to contain domestic anger at the estimated 600,000 killed or wounded Russians fighting in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): The question that we`re asking ourselves, and we don`t have an answer for right now, is, what does Kim Jong-un think he`s getting out of this?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And the fear is North Korea wants Russia to help modernize its outdated weapons systems, including its nuclear missiles, nuclear missiles, Kim, just this month, warned he would not "hesitate to use" -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imtiaz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In Lebanon, overnight, Israeli forces attacked more targets around the capital Beirut, while \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fired rockets of its own into Israel. Now, the fighting across the region is putting more civilians at risk.\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta spoke to one of those eyewitnesses who accuses Israel of using the same tactics in Lebanon as it used in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Starved, besieged, and now forced to move again, Israeli drone footage shows thousands of Palestinians under military orders to evacuate Northern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Israel says it is for their own protection, but residents say there is no safe place to go, fearing they will never return home again.\par \par \uc2 (YINA ABU NADA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "We can`t take it anymore," cried Yina Abu Nada (sp?). "We are being exterminated and no one is helping us. We are innocent."\par \par \uc2 Speaking from the north, Dr. Abu Safieh (sp?), told CBS News, the situation is catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 DR. ABU SAFIEH: Actually, we don`t have medicine, medications, medical supply, food, water, blood unit.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: On Israel`s second front in its war with \uc2 Hezbollah in Lebanon\uc2 , Israeli jets pounded the suburbs of Beirut overnight.\par \par \uc2 American emergency doctor, Mohammad Subeh did two missions in Gazan hospitals and is now volunteering in Lebanon, where he told us, it feels like the early days of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 DR. MOHAMMAD SUBEH (American emergency doctor): We`re seeing kind of the same playbook that was played out.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: He told us ambulances transporting patients to hospitals are being targeted, and that just like Gaza, they are treating an overwhelming number of civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 Israel insists it operates within strict international rules of engagement, but for Subeh, it is the faces of the children caught up in both these wars that haunt him.\par \par \uc2 DR. MOHAMMAD SUBEH: If you ignore everybody else, think about the child, and if that doesn`t move you, I feel like you`ve kind of lost that nucleus of what it means to be a human being.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has said that due to the intense bombardment of Northern Gaza, they`ve been forced to postpone the vaccination of more than 100,000 children against polio -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Debora Patta in Beirut, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been telling you that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is offering $1 million prizes to voters in battleground states. The mega billionaire and his political action committee are backing former President Trump`s 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 Now CBS News has confirmed a Justice Department warning to Musk`s super PAC. CNN was the first to report this.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent has the latest.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Nate, good morning. We`ve confirmed that the DOJ has sent a letter in the last few days to Elon Musk`s America PAC, basically warning that the $1 million checks he`s giving to registered voters may violate federal law.\par \par \uc2 The world`s richest man is randomly selecting registered voters in seven major battleground states who sign a petition supporting the First and Second amendments, and so far, the PAC says it`s given out four sweepstakes-style checks like those, three in Pennsylvania and one in Holly Springs, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 This DOJ warning reportedly comes from the section of the agency that investigates election related legal violations, and the DOJ itself has declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 And now the nonprofit advocacy group, \uc2 Public Citizen\uc2  has also weighed in. They filed an \uc2 FEC\uc2  complaint requesting an investigation as well.\par \par \uc2 Now the federal election law says it is a crime to pay anyone to vote or register to vote, and legal scholars that we`ve been talking to say this appears to be a clear violation, but it could be interpreted in a gray area, since Musk is asking people to sign a petition.\par \par \uc2 Now, Musk himself has already poured $75 million into his own PAC to get Trump voters to the polls. We expect to see more money poured in in these final two weeks. And of course, if you`re on X, you`re seeing it.\par \par \uc2 Musk, who owns what is formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , has been promoting Trump to his 200 million plus followers, and of course, was promised a prominent role in a second Trump White House last month.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve reached out to Musk and his PAC for comment on the DOJ`s letter, and they have not gotten back to us -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jo Ling, thank you very much. Very interesting story there.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, more interesting stories. New effort to stop near collisions at America`s airports. A pair of pilots tell us how they almost faced disaster. But first, it is 7:16, let`s take a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, we celebrate the one thing everyone gets at a Broadway show. "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " has been around for 140, years. Anthony Mason will show us what the theater guide means to audiences and actors. I collect mine.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Brett Goldstein talks about his Apple TV+ show "Shrinking." He co- created the acclaimed series, and he is part of the new season.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll show you Olivia Munn`s revealing and courageous photos for a new ad campaign.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A man is under arrest this morning, accused of shooting at a Democratic Party campaign office in Tempe, Arizona that`s just outside of Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 According to police, it`s the 60-year-old Jeffrey Michael Kelly who had a huge arsenal of guns, I mean, really big stockpile; ammunition, too, inside of his home. They say he may have been planning what the authorities call a mass casualty event.\par \par \uc2 Jarred Hill reports now, the suspect is accused of targeting that campaign office more than once.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KEN MCCOY (Tempe, Arizona Police Department chief): Justice does not have a political party. There is no place in the city of Tempe for political threats or intimidation.\par \par \uc2 JARRED HILL: On Tuesday afternoon, police surrounded Jeffrey Michael Kelly`s car during a traffic stop before hauling him away in handcuffs.\par \par \uc2 He was arrested on terrorism charges in connection with a string of shootings at a Democratic Party campaign office. Officials say the three shootings started back in September, and all happened around midnight.\par \par \uc2 During one incident, Kelly allegedly fired BB pellets and then gunshots at the glass front door and a window.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference, police said that after five weeks of investigation, it was tips by people in Kelly`s neighborhood that led police to him.\par \par \uc2 During his first court appearance Wednesday morning, prosecutors told the court that back in July, Kelly did a \uc2 Google\uc2  search for the DNC office`s address, and officials said that during a search at his home, they found more than 120 guns, over 250,000 rounds of ammunition and .22 caliber casings in his trash can, along with scopes, silencers, a grenade launcher and body armor.\par \par \uc2 Officials say Kelly was seen hanging suspicious bags containing a white powdery substance from several political signs lined with razor blades just a day before his arrest. They say at least one of those bags was found to just contain baking powder.\par \par \uc2 Kelly was released on bail and ordered under house arrest. His attorney says Kelly is a retired aerospace engineer with no prior record and legally owned his firearms.\par \par \uc2 Following their discovery, officials say they believe Kelly was planning a much bigger mass casualty.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jarred Hill.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead in a moment, what the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is doing to address a string of scary close calls at our nation`s airports. Yikes.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to want to see this story.\par \par \uc2 Local news, though, coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MLB/October 23, 1981)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 LA Dodgers\uc2  are remembering one of their heroes from the past as they prepare for the World Series this year. On your screen right now, Fernando Valenzuela beating the \uc2 New York Yankees\uc2  40 years ago plus, 1981 World Series. That, of course, was the year of Fernando Mania.\par \par \uc2 Valenzuela won Rookie of the Year and the Cy Young Award for the Best Pitcher in Baseball, pitching the Dodgers to their fifth overall championship. No one has done what he did ever again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He died at the age of 63 on Tuesday. Very sad news, dying way too soon.\par \par \uc2 We asked former big leaguer turned commentator, Harold Reynolds about Valenzuela`s outsized impact on the sport.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HAROLD REYNOLDS (American former baseball player): You look at Fernando coming out of Mexico, he turned Los Angeles upside down.\par \par \uc2 You had people coming from all over to Southern California to come see him pitch, and then wherever he went, he sold games out. So I think he was the first really true international star in our sport.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Major League Baseball will honor Valenzuela during the World Series, when the Dodgers, once again, face the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it wasn`t just Southern California. I was in love with him as a kid in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you remember him well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I could throw left-handed a little bit to this day, because as a kid with a tennis ball in the yard, I would do his wind up, which was distinctive.\par \par \uc2 He would look to the heavens before he dealt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you remember him, Tony on the thing --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh, of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was so huge. Yes, so huge. He was a guest at the White House that summer in `81. That`s how big of a deal he was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At 63, he is so young.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Legendary career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So young.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a legacy he has left us with.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has begun an audit of 45 major airports aiming to reduce close calls on the nation`s runways. Now, just days ago, in San Diego, air traffic controllers briefly put two Southwest planes on a potential collision course. Yikes for that.\par \par \uc2 But they caught it just in time.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave talked about the dangers with two pilots who survived another close call.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Southwest abort.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: As this \uc2 NTSB\uc2  animation shows, a \uc2 FedEx\uc2  767 came within 150 to 170 feet of landing on top of a Southwest 737 at the Austin, Texas Airport last February.\par \par \uc2 It was an extremely foggy morning. Air traffic controllers in the tower couldn`t see the runway, but cleared the Southwest flight to take off and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  to land.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  Captain Hugo Carvajal was at the controls.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN HUGO CARVAJAL (\uc2 FedEx\uc2 ): As soon as the tower cleared them to take off, we knew that there was an issue because we were about three miles, which is too close at that point.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Just moments before possible disaster, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  First Officer Robert Bradeen saw something through the fog below.\par \par \uc2 FIRST OFFICER ROBERT BRADEEN (\uc2 FedEx\uc2 ): I saw a light that turned out be the position light on the Southwest airplane, and quickly saw the silhouette following that and called for a go around.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: What goes through your mind as you`re seeing an airplane that shouldn`t be there?\par \par \uc2 FIRST OFFICER ROBERT BRADEEN: Obviously, there`s a moment of disbelief: Am I really seeing this? Is this really an airplane right there? And then training and experience kick in.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Last Wednesday, another close call near the Austin airport, when the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says a Cessna unexpectedly flew within hundreds of feet of an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  737 on approach to land from Chicago, forcing the pilots of American Flight 2587 to take an evasive maneuver before landing safely.\par \par \uc2 And earlier this month in San Diego, controllers briefly put two Southwest planes on a potential collision course after clearing one plane to taxi across the runway the other was using to take off.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Southwest 1478 cancel takeoff clearance.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Former \uc2 NTSB\uc2  chair, Robert Sumwalt was part of an \uc2 FAA\uc2  committee reviewing close calls that made 24 safety recommendations.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: I certainly think things are moving in the right direction. It`s important that those of us in the aviation profession, we not lose vigilance, and that we continue to push for better procedures, better technology, better staffing, and that we don`t get complacent.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says close calls, especially those happening on runways are rare and on the decline.\par \par \uc2 In a new audit, the agency will look to identify any additional gaps in procedures, equipment, or processes at the nation`s 45 busiest airports.\par \par \uc2 Recommendations to further reduce the risk of runway close calls are expected by next year.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Kris Van Cleave. It`s good to know that they`re really looking into this.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever been on a flight where it`s coming in for a landing, and then all of a sudden, the pilot guns it, and you go right back up in the air?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, whatever it is, it isn`t good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whatever it is, it isn`t good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I know. We all want our planes to, you know, get there on time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And do it quickly, but not so quickly that they`re on top of each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that is the problem.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I love this report, because it`s some of the things we don`t think about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know, we just want to get to our location, destination, and land.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yet another example of a quick thinking pilot seeing something --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- and making an adjustment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Experience, experience, experience.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So thank you to them.\par \par \uc2 All right ahead, alarming amounts of lead found in the water of yet another American city, this time, Syracuse. Why other communities also have cause for concern?\par \par \uc2 Parents are going to want to watch this in particular.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When you hear the name Flint, Michigan, chances are good, you think of the city`s problem with lead in the drinking water, and now testing has discovered a big problem in yet another American city, Syracuse, New York. Advocacy groups say it has some of the highest levels of lead found in drinking water in decades.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano went to Syracuse, and she joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Lilia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Look, city officials are pushing back on claims that this is a crisis, saying that the latest water testing this month showed that the levels had improved, but some advocates are questioning those results and methodologies, and residents we spoke with say the city needs to be doing a lot more to protect their families.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Thank you. How was school today?\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: When five-year-old Ella showed traces of lead in a blood test, her grandmother, Oceanna Fair was terrified, knowing the effects of lead poisoning, starting with her brother more than 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR (Families for Lead Freedom Now): And is essentially an eight- year-old that me and my siblings care for.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In 2020, Fair fought for federal funding to fully remove lead paint from her home. She was confident her home was lead-free, but as recently as 2022, tests showed her granddaughter was still exposed.\par \par \uc2 How did you feel?\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: Helpless. Helpless because we couldn`t put our finger on it. We didn`t know where she was coming into contact with it.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She says the culprit had been hiding in the basement.\par \par \uc2 How did you find out? Do you have to hire somebody?\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: It`s pretty simple. You can scratch your line and that silver that you see underneath.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: That means it`s --\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: That`s lead.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She only checked this August, after a letter from the city warned of high lead levels in nearly 30 percent of water samples taken from homes with lead service lines. More confirmation --\par \par \uc2 OCEANNA FAIR: We eventually brought a new fridge that had a filter, her lead levels went down, and we assumed it was from the diet we were feeding her.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: This is a community plagued by lead issues, where nine percent of children have suffered from lead poisoning. In some Black and Brown neighborhoods, it`s one in five.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BEN WALSH (Syracuse, New York): You have the public side of the service.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Syracuse Mayor Ben Walsh has called the results from the spring outliers, stressing the latest testing showed far lower lead levels.\par \par \uc2 But he says the city is working to replace all lead service lines faster than the EPA`s 10-year deadline, and providing filters to families with small children.\par \par \uc2 We`ve heard from people in this community who are extremely alarmed, whose children have tested positive. This is being compared to Flint. What`s your response to people who are -- who are that worried?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BEN WALSH: Well, I don`t think the facts support those comparisons. The specific challenges that we`re dealing with, again, are limited to the properties that had have lead service lines. So if you don`t have any lead service lines, there`s no reason to be concerned.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But he acknowledges, even the city does not have a full inventory of all pipes made of lead.\par \par \uc2 This is a community that is 70 percent renters, meaning people don`t know whether those pipes are made out of lead, bringing water into their homes.\par \par \uc2 Darlene Medley (sp?) says the city is failing her two twin boys who suffered from developmental and behavioral issues from lead poisoning.\par \par \uc2 DARLENE MEDLEY (Syracuse, New York resident): When you tell people, when their kids are poisoned by lead that they will never make it to what they were fully supposed to be, you don`t think that deserves a damn apology? That deserves an apology and a whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The twins had been poisoned because of lead paint, and then to find out that this is in the water, and there are millions of buildings across the country that have these lead lines.\par \par \uc2 This year, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  mandated that states and municipalities must change every lead pipe that delivers drinking water within the next 10 years. The Biden administration, it`s a part of the bipartisan infrastructure law provides $15 billion in funding to replace these let lines.\par \par \uc2 In Syracuse, it is going to take about five years, because there are at least 14,000 of these lines to change. Of course, the community says it needs to happen a lot faster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You feel her passion and her anger about what is happening.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems like this could be avoided.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It can be avoided.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does not have to be.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And there are chemicals that are used to control the corrosion, but clearly in those samples, they didn`t seem to have worked. And the fixtures within the house in some homes are also an issue. It`s not just the lead pipe, so even if you change them, it`s important to get them tested.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel that parent`s pain there at the end of your story though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: It was so painful to hear.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Lilia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a stowaway scares dozens of airline passengers. We will tell you what happened in just a bit. But right now, it is 7:43 -- forty-four, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some great things ahead for you at eight o`clock, including an inside look at the iconic Broadway publication, "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ." You`ve seen one of those from our very own Anthony, as in Mason.\par \par \uc2 It`s existed for 140 years. It still looks good. Big things are planned to mark the occasion.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to be joined by the one and only John Legend for special \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals today, we always feel good when he visits the studio, because we`re just ordinary people.\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour, but right now, Scott and I are heading over the table because Vlad has arrived in the building.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Groovin` to a little George Benson there on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Such a good song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Such a good song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a very good song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that how you begin with "What to Watch"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, it has, to do with Anthony Mason and "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, it is Broadway.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is time now for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get into it because we`ve got a couple of really, really good stories that we want to share with you this morning.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with Olivia Munn, who is bravely showing a new side of herself after undergoing a double mastectomy. The 44-year-old reveals her surgical scars in a new campaign for SKIMS. That`s the shapewear brand.\par \par \uc2 Munn revealed her breast cancer diagnosis back in March, and she says it feels liberating to show the scars. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN (Actor): When I look at the scars, or how my breasts look different, or how clothes fit on me differently, I just feel a lot of gratitude. The way my body looks is just a representation of how hard I thought.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Munn says she hopes embracing her scars will help others with breast cancer and breast cancer survivors feel beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I so admire that`s she is doing this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very brave.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And she looks good in the ad, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she great. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ten percent of US bra sales from SKIMS going to breast cancer, Susan G. Komen.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Susan G. Komen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a big number, actually.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a big number.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Congratulations to her. They just had a new baby, her and John Mulaney.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about Ted Danson, right?\par \par \uc2 You remember "Cheers." We all love "Cheers."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Danson is burying the hatchet with his co-star from "Cheers," Kelsey Grammer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Cheers"/Paramount+)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know they needed a hatchet to bury.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me neithe