ESON: The US confirms North Korean troops are in Russia as the war in Ukraine grinds on.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): If Russia is indeed forced to turn to North Korea for manpower, this would be a sign of weakness, not strength on the part of the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): I notice my palms are sweating, knees weak, arms are heavy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  channels his inner Eminem in front of the rapper during a campaign event.\par \par \uc2 How`d that go?\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA: I`m a nervous, but on the surface, I look calm and ready to drops bombs, but I keep on forgetting --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA ("The Daily Show"): Did he just come up with that on the spot? I mean, that was so good, it should be a song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, let`s just say he just came up with that on the spot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the fly, Nate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, I do think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- he wasn`t sure how deep he was going to go, and then he realized, I actually know this song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every bar of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He does know it. And he did it very well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the words of Eminem, will the real US president, please stand up. Please stand up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please stand up. Please stand up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Please stand up.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are going to begin this hour --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- with the -- with the campaign for being the next president. Two different strategies out there 12 days before the election.\par \par \uc2 We`ll start with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , two rallies in Georgia yesterday, and they seem designed to appeal to his base. He doubled down on insults. He called Democrats stupid at least six different times and promised his supporters they`d be part of something great if they voted.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  spoke to undecided voters at a CNN town hall near Philadelphia, taking questions about the economy and then responding to reports that Trump had praised Hitler while in office, calling her opponent, "unfit and dangerous."\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the White House for us to help break all of it down for us.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got these two different events. What do they tell you about the state of the campaigns?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: I think what it tells you, Tony, good to be with you, is that this has become a race in these closing weeks that in some ways resembles a normal election, and in some ways borders on the surreal.\par \par \uc2 On one hand, we watched Vice President Harris last night get grilled about her policy plans on taxes, on the Middle East, on immigration, and she`ll be judged based on how specific, how workable her proposals are on those issues as any candidate for president should be.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, you have this larger conversation going on now about just how wedded \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is to the democratic process in the first place. He suggested just this week in an interview that all the battleground states may have cheated in elections. You`ve got former top aides coming forward to warn that he`s drawn to fascism. He`s musing on the campaign trail about using the military to go after the enemy within, and so all of that is kind of drowning out the policy conversation right now -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Nancy, you know, we`re getting down to the nitty and the gritty here, and both campaigns are talking about making their closing arguments.\par \par \uc2 But here`s a question, do you think this has become more about voting against the other person rather than voting for the candidate you want?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Absolutely, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 I mean, let`s just take the Harris campaign, which I cover day-to-day. Not only is Harris spending a great deal of time warning people about Trump in these closing weeks, but so are her top surrogates on the campaign trail, like former President Obama, who has been dedicating most of the time in his speeches to mocking and criticizing Trump, and yes, Trump is giving them a lot of new material to work with.\par \par \uc2 But the challenge for the Harris campaign is that candidates tend to generate excitement and win when voters view them as the candidate of change. That was certainly the case for Obama back in `08, and if you`re really focused on defining your opponent, that leaves you with less time to define yourself, and so that`s the balancing act, and the Harris campaign is acutely aware of that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy, you have been traveling all over this country covering the campaign, what are you watching for in these final days?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Nate, I`m really curious about that closing argument. We learned yesterday that Harris is going to be giving a speech on the National Mall next Tuesday that her campaign is billing as the big final argument. She`s going to be speaking from The Ellipse, which will inevitably lead people to draw parallels to the speech that Trump gave to supporters on January 6, 2021 at The Ellipse, right before they stormed the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 And that is not an accident. The campaign planned it that way. They want that contrast, and so I`ll be watching to see how she makes the case against Trump, while ensuring that her case for her own candidacy doesn`t get overshadowed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, the final countdown. We`ll check in with you later.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, John Legend will join us for a special \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals. He`s going to share some products from his skin care brand.\par \par \uc2 But first it is, 8:06, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Tony Awards Presents Broadway`s Back/\uc2 CBS\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can, can, can. That`s Danny Burstein, who won his first Tony for "Moulin Rouge." Ahead, Burstein looks back at his Broadway career with Anthony Mason through his "Playbills." Do you save yours, too? As is the iconic magazine celebrates its 140th anniversary -- they`ve been around a long time.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We always thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s no business like show business, and today we`re looking at a business that`s the backbone of Broadway.\par \par \uc2 No matter what show that you see, you`re guaranteed at least one souvenir, a "Playbill." The iconic magazine turns 140 this month, and Anthony Mason shows us how "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " is celebrating this special anniversary.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It is delivered almost every day on Broadway, the magazine that`s the guide to the Great White Way.\par \par \uc2 As any theatergoer knows, it wouldn`t be Broadway without a "Playbill."\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So I can look back and remember.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You provide a magazine to every Broadway theater?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH (\uc2 Playbill, Inc.\uc2  vice president and chief operating officer): Every Broadway theater, most off Broadway theaters and the biggest classic arts venues in the city.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex Birsh, whose family has published "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " since 1973 supervises the printing plant in Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 How old is this machine?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: This machine here is actually, I believe it has its 40th birthday.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s a beast.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: It is, and it`s our beast, and we love her.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: When did "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " actually start?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: 1884.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Which makes it the oldest surviving theater program company in the world.\par \par \uc2 When I think about "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ," I mean, it`s really the history of American theater on its covers.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: I like to think that, too.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: From "Death of a Salesman" --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Death of a Salesman")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: To "Damn Yankees."\par \par \uc2 "West Side Story" to "Wicked."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wicked")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: For over a century, the cast bios and production details have been featured in "Playbill."\par \par \uc2 So this is how you`re celebrating your anniversary.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: This month, every Broadway show has four different "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " covers.\par \par \uc2 And these are meant to mark different eras.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: That`s right. We have the Golden Age, 30s, 40s.\par \par \uc2 So this is the 50s design, and then we have the 60s, with the first showing of the yellow banner. And then we have the groovy 70s.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love the groovy 70s.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: Which was a -- it`s actually kind of a rare design.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s when Alex`s grandfather, Arthur Birsh bought "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ." A printer by trade, he later brought in his son, Philip.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BIRSH (\uc2 Playbill, Inc.\uc2  CEO): We had a basic debate. I thought "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " was a brand, and he thought it was magazine.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Philip pushed for "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " to go online.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BIRSH: And in 1995-`96, we actually were the first company on the internet for the theater industry.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What is "Playbill`s" place on Broadway?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BIRSH\uc2 : "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " is really a treasure box. It could be a souvenir. It could be a guide. The actors, it means a lot, too, because that`s their goal, is to be in a "Playbill."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The 66th Tony Awards/\uc2 CBS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN (Actor): The first time was very, very special.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Danny Burstein made his Broadway and "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " debuts in 1992.\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: I do remember writing my first bio and thinking, oh, my God, holy cow, it`s going to be in a "Playbill." See those two pictures. Holy cow. That is a young man. That is an old man.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: How many Broadway shows have you been in?\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: This is -- "Gypsy" is my 20th.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Burstein stars with Audra McDonald in the revival that opens in December.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Lincoln Center Theater)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He has also starred in revivals of "South Pacific," "Cabaret."\par \par \uc2 You were Tevye.\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: I was Tevye in "Fiddler on the Roof." And that was the hardest role I`ve ever done, and the most fulfilling.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fiddler on the Roof.")\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And this is the one you won the Tony for.\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Tony Awards Presents: Broadway`s Back/CBS)\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: After seven times being nominated.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But who`s counting?\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: But who`s -- well, my mother. She counted.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Your whole history is just right in this pile of "Playbills."\par \par \uc2 DANNY BURSTEIN: I know, this is your life, Danny Burstein.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex Birsh can also track his life through "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Two things you notice right away when you come in here, the sound and the smell.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: Yes. That`s right,\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The smell of ink.\par \par \uc2 You grew up with both.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 Each time I walk in here, that smell makes me feel like I`m, you know, eight years old.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You actually painted these walls as a kid.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: I did, when I was 16. Sadly, the last time they were also painted and I feel embarrassed by that, but it was -- it was a fun summer, and it really taught me the value of an N-95.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex would need an N-95 mask again during COVID, when "Playbill`s" presses shut down.\par \par \uc2 That had to be really painful.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: It was extremely painful. It was the only time in our a hundred and at that point, a hundred and thirty-five plus year history, where we did not know when we would print again.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But the beast came roaring back. "Playbill`s" bright banner has become a symbol of Broadway. And if you were wondering --\par \par \uc2 Why did you choose yellow?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: Well, it`s kind of embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Alex says they were experimenting with different looks when his grandfather asked the ink salesman --\par \par \uc2 ALEX BIRSH: What`s the cheapest color? Yellow. Great. And there we go.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Broadway shows may open and close, but "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " has become a tradition.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The sometimes it`s so simple, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What`s the cheapest color? But it`s the color everybody knows.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is so distinct and it really stands out.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I love the back story of "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, it`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The way you told it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All the 140 years, who can believe it that it`s been publishing continuously since then.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I find I save mine, and then after about five or 10 years, I go, "why am I saving it?" You take yours?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I think that`s exactly -- that`s what most people do. Dave, our audio guy, on the way in, he says, I have a whole drawer full of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You know? But it`s the one souvenir you always walk away with.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can think --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It could be temporary or forever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I can think of five I`m definitely going to save on our table right here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at this. Look at this.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The "\uc2 Playbill\uc2 " folks were kind enough to put the show on the cover. So there you all are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am definitely keeping this.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s the contemporary version, and then you have each of the four period versions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Isn`t that cool?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Honestly, this is fantastic, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much. I`m definitely saving those.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was very nice you guys to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Anthony, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, an important conversation about your health. Dr. Celine Gounder has got some tips on how you can prevent cardiovascular disease. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In today`s "Health Watch," we`re talking about cardiovascular disease. It is the leading cause of death in this country. Did you know that? Nearly 120 million adults in the US live with the disease. That`s nearly half of adults, and it is expected to get worse.\par \par \uc2 A recent \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  report projects heart disease and stroke will affect more than 60 percent of Americans by 2050, that`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 We are joined by CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder. She is editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 I`m so glad you`re here. I mean, heart disease is one thing I always worry about. Both my parents died of heart disease. I`ve outlived both of them. As I sit here today, I`ve outlived both my parents.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is something I think about constantly. I`ve got high blood pressure, high cholesterol, they say it`s all part of your DNA, and even on medication, the numbers are still going up.\par \par \uc2 Why is that? Even when you`re doing the right things.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is a very concerning trend, that by 2050 it`s estimated that six in 10 adults will be living or dying from cardiovascular disease. And so why is that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: This is really a factor of we tend to emphasize prioritize sick care in this country, not preventive care. If you look at how we spend relative to other high income countries, we spend far more and get far worse health outcomes, and we`re not prioritizing primary care, public health, which are the things that would prevent cardiovascular disease.\par \par \uc2 And so why is that? Well, we don`t have enough primary care providers, and some of this is driven by how we compensate different kinds of doctors.\par \par \uc2 So specialists are paid about 40 percent more than primary care providers. Of course, if you`re in school trying to decide, what am I going to go into, you know that is going to influence your decision making. And then on the public health side, of the $4.5 trillion we spend on health in this country, only four percent of that is for public health.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s amazing. I mean, you make such a good point. We call it health care, but really it`s sick care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It`s not even care.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s not even care. It`s not even care.\par \par \uc2 Could weight loss drugs like Wegovy help turn around these numbers?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So that`s an interesting question. For people who can afford them, access them, and tolerate the side effects, they have been very useful. But not everybody can, and these drugs remain in relatively short supply, and they`re quite expensive.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, I was at the annual meeting of the National Academy of Medicine, and there was a session all about GLP-1 drugs with the top researchers in the space, and there was a lot of enthusiasm, but also a lot of skepticism and caution, and really the acknowledgement that we are not going to be able to treat ourselves with these drugs out of the obesity epidemic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what are the -- what health factors are there that will drive up this increase in cardiovascular disease?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, so we`ve made progress on a couple. So smoking and high cholesterol, we have made progress on smoking. It was really the result of public health educational campaigns, taxes, regulations on how they`re sold. So we`ve made progress there.\par \par \uc2 But the things that we are seeing go up are obesity, diabetes, and high blood pressure. And that is being driven by high calorie, high salt, high fat, high sugar foods that are ultra-processed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, it is good to be preventative.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the good stuff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Dr. Gounder, thank you. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back, 8:30 CBS MORNINGS and it is time to bring you some of the stories that are talk of the table this morning. Was that "Candy Girl"?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I used that as my walk on song for a short -- for a short period. Didn`t get a lot of hits. I don`t know why I picked it, but yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you were hitting on the girls though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that was a big year in a variety of ways.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Candy Girl." Okay, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great song. Okay, Tony. White chocolate over there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" you can call it Hair Love 2.0 but I`d like to -- I like to think of this as kindness and the importance of kindness. Listen to this mom offering her daughter words of wisdom during their morning hair routine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MADDIE: Some people are not so nice.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN VEAL CONWAY (Mom): This is absolutely true.\par \par \uc2 MADDIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN VEAL CONWAY: What you can continue to do is just continue to be a kind person and hopefully inspire other people to be kind, too.\par \par \uc2 MADDIE: Maybe we should not be sad at school so you don`t be a little sad at work.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN VEAL CONWAY: No, you`re not responsible for mommy`s feelings, okay.\par \par \uc2 MADDIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN VEAL CONWAY: Just your own. It`s nice for you to be concerned about my feelings. I really appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 MADDIE: But I am.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN VEAL CONWAY: I know you are.\par \par \uc2 MADDIE: I like being concerned because I like caring about you.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN VEAL CONWAY: You do.\par