t was like -- I was like, oh, I look like my 12-year-old nephew if he was an old lady.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know when you looked in the mirror, Brett, what did you see without a beard? Because I would think that it would be very jarring.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes. How long have you had this? Always?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no, no, no. I got it like two years ago.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: All right, cool. All right. Yes, I hate not having a beard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you didn`t want to shave it off? You didn`t want to.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: No, it`s very uncomfortable. Also, I discovered, sadly, that it turns out women don`t like me for my personality.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They like you for your beard?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Studies show, but you did take the role.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You played the role so powerfully.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a very, very difficult role to play. We just saw that. It`s very intense. What kind of preparation research went into it?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Well, if we`re being serious, you know, it`s a playing a drunk driver who`s been responsible for this thing, and I did loads and loads of research as writers in the writers` room, we read lots of accounts of people on both sides of this thing happening.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: And listened to lots of sort of people`s stories, so you`re taking it very, very seriously. And then -- and then, kind of, in terms of playing it, we`d already done all the research writing it, you feel like you`ve acted it when you write it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Shrinking"/Apple TV+)\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: You know what I mean? Because it`s the same process of empathizing with the character. And then when I was playing it, I just thought about PTSD. I know what it`s like when you`re in a car accident. It sort of loops in your head.\par \par \uc2 So I would just kind of loop it before a scene, and it would make it very sad -- you know, it`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tough.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, there are some strong themes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- in this -- forgiveness, confronting demons.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s these patterns and triggers. Do you have personal history with therapy?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes. I have a poor therapist who has been stuck with me for some years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m an advocate for therapy.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Did any of your experiences through therapy help you with this role?\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: With this particular role? That`s a good question. I don`t know. I think -- I think so much of the show, you know, we have consultants who are therapists, and we -- you know, as much as the show kind of plays with the boundaries of therapy, we also take it seriously, believe in therapy, think it`s a very important thing, so --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Having that vulnerability, I`m sure helped.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, I think -- yes, you have to be very -- for this part, you had to be very vulnerable. And having Lukita Maxwell --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who plays your daughter, yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: In that scene, I have lots of --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plays the daughter.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: She is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so good just to see you back on TV after "Ted Lasso." You know, now there`s all the rumors about Season Four.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Please share with the class what you feel like, because, you know we all wanted to come back and there are rumors, rumors, Brett, you`ve heard them, too.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes. I`ve heard these rumors.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING:, Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: You got any info?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s here, he`s there. He is there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do we dare to dream, or should we just let it go and be gone?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said you`d play Roy Kent forever.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Yes, well, dare to dream. Always dare to dream. I would never stop anyone from dreaming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, the bearded -- the very bearded Brett Goldstein.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am going to take that as it is coming back. Thank you, Brett.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GOLDSTEIN: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Shrinking" is streaming now on \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV+. A new episode is on Wednesday. We`ll see you --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241024ekao00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For October 23, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Kris Van Cleave, Weijia Jiang, Major Garrett,  Nikole Killion, David Schechter, Karen Morfitt, Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: Donald Trump`s longest-serving chief of staff says, if reelected, the former president would rule like a dictator.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2  (RET.) (Former White House Chief of Staff): He commented more than once that -- you know, that Hitler did some good things too.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): This is a window into who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really is.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Did he run this country as a dictator the four years he was in? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: North Korea sends troops to Russia to fight in Ukraine. Why defense officials are alarmed.\par \par \uc2 And tonight`s Eye on America. We look at why the nation`s barrier islands are eroding and how to stop it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PULLEN (Photographer): It is difficult to live here, but, at the same time, it`s one of the most beautiful places on the planet.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Less than two weeks to go to Election Day, and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is calling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  increasingly unhinged and unstable. That comes after the former president`s own former Chief of Staff \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  said on the record and on tape that Trump repeatedly praised Nazi dictator Adolf Hitler. He also said his former boss fits the definition of a fascist.\par \par \uc2 Kelly is a retired four-star Marine general who also served as Trump`s secretary of homeland security. Vice President Harris warned today that, if Trump is elected again, people like General Kelly will not be there to serve as guardrails and keep him in check.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign vehemently denies Kelly`s remarks, but this is all playing out with voting already under way. More than 24 million Americans have already cast ballots either in person or by mail.\par \par \uc2 We have a team of correspondents covering both campaigns, with tonight analysis from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion on the trail with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in battleground Georgia and some late-breaking news.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening to you, Norah.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hit back tonight, saying that \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  made this story up, calling him dumb and a low -- and a lowlife, and that is despite reports on Trump`s comments that have been cited by multiple sources.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made no mention of this stark new warning from his longest-serving White House chief of staff, \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , who told "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " Trump could govern as a dictator if elected.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2  (RET.) (Former White House Chief of Staff): He`s certainly an authoritarian, admires people who are dictators. He has said that. So, he certainly falls into the general definition of fascist, for sure.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The retired four-star general also corroborated reports that the former president spoke favorably about Adolf Hitler.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2  (RET.): He commented more than once that -- you know, that Hitler did some good things too.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: That provoked a direct and blunt response today from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): It is deeply troubling and incredibly dangerous that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would invoke Adolf Hitler, the man who is responsible for the deaths of six million Jews and hundreds of thousands of Americans.\par \par \uc2 All of this is further evidence for the American people of who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really is.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Both Harris and Kelly also pointed to the former president`s recent rhetoric about the enemy from within, suggesting he might use the military against domestic opponents.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): It should be very easily handled by -- if necessary, by National Guard or, if really necessary, by the military.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Something Kelly said Trump was repeatedly told by advisers not to do.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2  (RET.): Originally, the conversation would be: "You know, Mr. President, that`s outside your authority. You really don`t want to do that inside the United States."\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: We know what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants. He wants unchecked power. The question in 13 days will be, what do the American people want?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Those here say they`re still with Trump.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Did he run this country as a dictator the four years he was in? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Tonight, CBS News has learned that the Justice Department has sent a letter to a super PAC run by one of Trump`s biggest backers, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The DOJ says that a recent $1 million giveaway to registered voters to sign a petition could violate federal law -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion on the trail with Trump in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to the Harris campaign and its door-to-door effort in battleground states to get every vote they can. In Pennsylvania specifically, the Democrats have 50 offices with more than 450 staffers, and they say they have knocked on more than a million doors since the end of July.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Weijia Jiang is in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voice-over): Popping by a popular Philadelphia deli for lunch.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: We`re going to win.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Vice President Harris visited with campaign volunteers.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): We have 13 days to go, and I am so deeply thankful to you.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Some Harris supporters have no doubt about their choice, including this father voting with his daughter in mind.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Her future, that`s all that matters.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Others are not as motivated.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Just calling to see will you be voting on November the 5th Election Day.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Phone bankers with the nonpartisan group Black Men Vote are making 50,000 calls a day, pushing people to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Can you put into words how important turnout is?\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: If every Philadelphia voter came out and voted one way or another, it wouldn`t matter what the rest of Pennsylvania did.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: In South Philly...\par \par \uc2 MAN #5: Hello. Hi. How you doing? My name is Matthew from Black Men Vote.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: ... canvassers are targeting disengaged voters too.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A lot of the doors I have went to, they say: "I don`t know. I don`t even know who to vote for."\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The Harris/Walz team believes 10 percent of battleground state voters are still persuadable, especially in the suburbs. That includes Delaware County, Pennsylvania, about 25 miles outside of Philadelphia, where Harris is participating in a town hall tonight.\par \par \uc2 Campaign spokesman Ian Sams:\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS (Harris/Walz Campaign Spokesperson): In these counties where Trump is likely to win, if you gain ground and you erode the margin, it really helps you win statewide.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: A key part of that strategy is courting moderate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Harris` town hall is in a county where nearly a quarter of Republicans voted for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  in the GOP primary. But Haley had already dropped out of the race almost two months earlier -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Weijia Jiang, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s bring the race into sharper focus with CBS News chief Washington correspondent Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Major, everyone`s trying to figure out, how is this election going to go, and, of course, this late-breaking news. How do you think the comments of General Kelly might affect these persuadable voters?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So we`re all looking for keys to the final outcome, Norah.\par \par \uc2 And to pick up on Weijia`s point there, one of the keys will be, how solid is that Republican base for Trump? And we know from our own internal \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling that people who are Republicans who think of themselves as Haley Republicans or Reagan Republicans are torn. And they might reasonably ask themselves, these comments from General Kelly and others in the military, have we heard anything like this before?\par \par \uc2 Well, the answer is yes. Three days before January 6, in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," 10 defense secretaries wrote an op-ed saying they were fearful Trump would use unconstitutional means to try to overthrow that election. The Capitol riot three days later vindicated their concerns.\par \par \uc2 So, these Haley, Reagan Republicans might look back on that and these comments now and say, wait a minute, there is something I`m worried about. And the Harris campaign focus groups and internal data tell them that is a persuasive message, that could reach them, which is why you got the ferocious Trump denials this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Major Garrett, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the war in Ukraine and an ominous new development. The Pentagon has confirmed that North Korea is sending thousands of troops to Russia in what Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  calls a very, very serious escalation. And Ukraine`s president believes he knows exactly where those troops are ultimately heading.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` chief foreign affairs correspondent, Margaret Brennan, has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice-over): Video released by the Ukrainian government shows North Korean troops receiving military gear in Russia.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  cannot independently verify the footage, but, today, Defense Secretary Austin confirmed that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Kim Jong-un have their troops training side by side at three specialized military sites inside of Russia.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of Defense): There is evidence that there are DPRK troops, you know, in Russia.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ukrainian President Zelenskyy warned him that Russia may send those forces to Ukraine, proving Putin seeks to escalate, not negotiate.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And South Korean officials say the number may soon climb to 10,000, revealing that Russia is already training 3,000 North Korean special forces how to operate equipment, including drones.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials said the North Korean soldiers traveled in mid-October by ship from North Korea to Eastern Russia and could end up on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (NSC Coordinator For Strategic Communications): If Russia is indeed forced to turn to North Korea for manpower, this would be a sign of weakness, not strength, on the part of the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Russia has already lost roughly 600,000 soldiers, according to the Pentagon, and has turned to hired mercenaries from Cuba and other countries for manpower.\par \par \uc2 An axis of U.S. adversaries are now helping Russia. Iran sent personnel and drones. China lent much-needed financial support. And North Korea sent munitions. The U.S. is still trying to figure out what Kim Jong-un thinks he`s getting out of this deal and fear it could include Russian expertise to help build out his nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY: It`s been concerning, certainly this development, this -- this willingness of Kim to literally put skin in the game here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Margaret joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, you mentioned this axis of adversaries. How significant is it?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s very significant to see America`s adversaries working together, basically to fill in all the gaps for Russia created by those Western sanctions and potentially to help it win in this land grab in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 This is going to set, Norah, a precedent, one that the next president of the United States will have to deal with. In the short term, North Korea is already the most sanctioned country in the world. The White House plans more of them and to surge more weapons to Ukraine in these final weeks of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Margaret Brennan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The strike of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers may be close to an end after more than a month. Tonight, the machinist union is voting on a new contract, and the company`s new CEO is optimistic. But how are the workers feeling?\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): It`s deal or no deal, the high-stakes vote with \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s future on the ballot. The 33,000 striking machinists are voting whether to accept a new four-year deal that comes with a 35 percent pay hike, as well as improved health and retirement benefits, but no pension. Its passing is not a guarantee.\par \par \uc2 MAN: While this is a historic offer from the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  company, that doesn`t necessarily make it good.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s terrible.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Why do you think it`s terrible?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Well, first of all, there`s no pension.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Union president Jon Holden negotiated the new offer.\par \par \uc2 They wanted to send a message. Do you feel like \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has heard that message now?\par \par \uc2 JON HOLDEN (President, International Association of Machinists): Oh, they`re feeling it now. I mean, members wanted their pound of flesh, and they`re getting it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The vote comes at a critical time for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . The company reported a staggering $6 billion loss last quarter as it prepares to cut 10 percent of its global work force after years of losses stemming from production problems, including a door panel that flew off a plane and two 737 MAX crashes linked to a design flaw, killing 346 people.\par \par \uc2 KELLY ORTBERG\uc2  (CEO, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 ): We have got a deal that the employees can feel good about and the company can be successful going forward.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: New \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 KELLY ORTBERG\uc2  : This company has had a good culture. We just need to recover that, get back to what -- what everybody wants \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to be, this iconic company building great aircraft and defense systems.\par \par \uc2 Everything I see, we can do that. We just got to focus on getting back to our roots.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: We should get results later tonight. But, even if it passes, it could be days or perhaps weeks before production can ramp back up.\par \par \uc2 And, if it doesn`t pass, that means more negotiations. But \uc2 Boeing\uc2  says that pension is not on the table -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such an important story.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The Quarter Pounder is causing a ton of headaches for McDonald`s. The latest on the E. coli outbreak, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: McDonald`s spent the day reassuring consumers about the safety of its food following an E. coli outbreak affecting the chain`s Quarter Pounder hamburgers. The most visited restaurant chain in America removed the popular item from menus in 12 states until it can determine the source of the contamination.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Karen Morfitt is in Colorado, where dozens of customers got sick.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAREN MORFITT (voice-over): McDonald`s says the sliced raw onions topping its Quarter Pounder burgers could be the culprit behind the E. coli outbreak.\par \par \uc2 The onions, sourced from a single facility, are being scrutinized, along with the Quarter Pounder beef patties. But a McDonald`s spokesperson adds, the beef comes from multiple suppliers and is cooked well above the 160- degree temperature that kills the E. coli bacteria, noting there would have to be preparation failures at several restaurants.\par \par \uc2 McDonald`s stock was down more than 5 percent today with the news. For now, 20 percent of restaurants nationwide have temporarily stopped serving Quarter Pounders.\par \par \uc2 JOE ERLINGER\uc2  (President, McDonald`s USA): Other beef products at McDonald`s including the cheeseburger, hamburger, Big Mac, McDouble and the Double Cheeseburger, are not impacted.\par \par \uc2 KAREN MORFITT: The CDC says 49 cases were found in 10 states; 26 of the cases were in Colorado and one death; 10 people have been hospitalized, including a child.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KAREN MORFITT: Now, the CDC says cases may go up because of the time that it takes for people to get sick and then be diagnosed.\par \par \uc2 We also know the first lawsuit was filed today -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Karen Morfitt, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next, looking into a growing problem along America`s shorelines, where rising sea levels are threatening homes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Millions of people who live along the ocean have had their lives upended by hurricanes this month. Forty percent of Americans live near the coast and are experiencing how climate change is fueling more intense storms and faster erosion.\par \par \uc2 For tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Schechter takes us to North Carolina`s Outer Banks, where the question of how to keep the ocean at bay has been weighing on residents for decades.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER (voice-over): Living on North Carolina`s Outer Banks, photographer Daniel Pullen has seen the thin line between living on the ocean and falling into it.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PULLEN (Photographer): If you could bring some of the old-timers back from the dead and drive them around, they would just be like what -- why did you build your house there?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: In the last four years, the Atlantic has toppled 10 homes here on Hatteras Island. Pullen captured this one crashing down just last month.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PULLEN: I can`t believe I`m watching a house disintegrate into the ocean.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: And while less than 70,000 people are permanent residents, to make their living, many of them count on the more than five million people that visit each year.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PULLEN: I think people are meant to live on a barrier island, but I don`t know to what extent do you have a massive economy on it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: This narrow strip of sand is called a barrier island, and it buffers the mainland against the force of the ocean. In the Eastern U.S., barrier islands cover 2,300 miles of seashore. And, naturally, they`re always shifting.\par \par \uc2 Out of the water, we met Reide Corbett, an oceanographer who runs the Coastal Studies Institute. He works with the community finding ways to adapt.\par \par \uc2 The way we are developing on barrier islands and living on barrier islands, like, how does that change the nature of what the barrier island actually wants to do?\par \par \uc2 REIDE CORBETT (Coastal Studies Institute): Large dunes, houses, the infrastructure, that stops the transport of sand across these barrier islands, which leads to a more vulnerable barrier island.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: One solution, protecting waterfront homes by rebuilding the beach, pumping sand out of the ocean onto the shore. But that can cost upwards of $25 million. And with rising sea levels and more intense storms driven by climate change, the new sand that used to last five to seven years is now being washed away in less than two.\par \par \uc2 REIDE CORBETT: The economics won`t work always.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REIDE CORBETT: And I think we are -- on the Outer Banks, I think we`re getting to that point.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: What`s unique about the Outer Banks is, almost all of the beachfront belongs to the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2 . It bought this home and another and demolished them.\par \par \uc2 Dave Hallac ran that pilot program. And unlike the 10 properties that fell into the ocean, the buyouts prevented homes from littering the beach and ocean with dangerous debris.\par \par \uc2 DAVE HALLAC (\uc2 National Park Service\uc2 ): We consider this a mitigation program. None of these owners built their houses in these locations. When they were built, there was a backyard, dunes, maybe even a back dune area and a large white sandy beach. And it`s just that erosion and the ocean has caught up to them.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PULLEN: Take somebody that grew up in their small little hometown their whole life and just, like, hey, go live somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Is there another way that this should be or could be managed?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PULLEN: There`s a -- I mean, there`s a way it could be managed. And it`s to let a barrier island exist a way a barrier island is meant to exist.\par \par \uc2 In order to do that, it means things will look a lot different here. I probably would have to move.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHECHTER: Barrier islands, where people hold tight to a patch of sand that`s slowly escaping their grip.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, David Schechter, Buxton, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we have got a final salute to a World War II hero. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America honoring U.S. Marine John Kinsel Sr., one of the last remaining Navajo code talkers, who transmitted messages during World War II in his tribe`s native language.\par \par \uc2 Kinsel died Saturday and was remembered today as a symbol of bravery and pride. A procession of more than 120 vehicles escorted his body to his final resting place in Arizona. During the war in the Pacific, the code talkers` messages about troop movements and battlefield tactics were indecipherable to enemy code breakers.\par \par \uc2 They also fought in major assaults conducted by the Marines in the Pacific. After the war, Kinsel returned to Arizona, raised a family, pushed for the rights of indigenous people, and helped to preserve Navajo traditions.\par \par \uc2 With his death, only two Navajo code talkers are still living. So, for tonight`s Heart of America, John Kinsel Sr. is our choice at 107 years old.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s CBS Evening News. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 We will see you right back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241024ekan00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for October 16, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Debora Patta, Jonathan Vigliotti,Robert Costa,  Nikole Killion, Nikki Battiste, Jill Schlesinger \par \uc2 3,738 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Twenty days until Election Day. Tonight, the candidates aim to broaden support.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I`m the father of IVF.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, Vice President Harris slams \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after he appears to take credit for a decades-old fertility procedure.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): I found it to be quite bizarre. Let`s not be distracted by his choice of words. The reality is actually something very harmful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Family members stand behind the Menendez brothers, calling on prosecutors to release the men from prison after 34 years.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Lyle and Erik acted out of fear.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ (Massachusetts Resident): Here you go.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And tonight`s Eye on America, how the sandwich generation is taking a hit by financially supporting their children and their parents.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ (Massachusetts Resident): The other day, she says: "You`re going to work until 70, right?"\par \par \uc2 I`m like: "I guess so."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and thank you for joining us. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin tonight with the race for president and new questions about the mental health and behavior of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Biden, campaigning for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on Tuesday, said Trump has become unhinged, referring to a campaign event this week where Trump swayed to music for 40 minutes. Trump, now 78, would be the oldest person to become president, but he is refusing to disclose his medical records, which is something presidential nominees traditionally do.\par \par \uc2 In a town hall aimed at courting women last night, Trump made numerous false and strange statements, including claiming to be the father of in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 We also have new reporting tonight on the Harris campaign, including her new outreach to Republicans looking for an alternative to Trump.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion is going to lead us off with more on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what his team is saying tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening to you, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Even as former President Trump tries to expand his base, he continues to double down on controversial comments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We really are the party for IVF. We want fertilization. And it`s all the way.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): Tonight, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  under fire after making this strange claim about in vitro fertilization to an all-female town hall:\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Oh, I want to talk about IVF.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So do I.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m the father of IVF. So I want to hear this question.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: He made the claim less than 90 seconds before admitting Alabama Senator Katie Britt had explained it to him.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I said: "Explain IVS very -- IVF very quickly." And within about two minutes, I understood it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The procedure has been around for nearly 50 years. An official with the Trump campaign told CBS News the former president was simply reaffirming his support and made the comments in jest.\par \par \uc2 But it was no laughing matter for his opponent, the vice president.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I found it to be quite bizarre, actually. He called himself the father of IVF. And if what he meant is taking responsibility, well, then, yes, he should take responsibility for the fact that one in three women in America lives in a Trump abortion ban state.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In another town hall geared toward Latino voters, the former president also resurrected debunked claims about Haitian immigrants eating pets, but took them a step further.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I was just saying what was reported. That`s been reported, and eating other things too that they`re not supposed to be. But this is -- all I do is report. I -- I have not -- I was there. I`m going to be there, and we`re going to take a look. And I will give you a full report when I do.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: That, along with his recent behavior, including dancing on stage, has led Harris to question what she calls his unstable behavior.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, pressure is mounting on Trump to release his medical records after Harris released hers this weekend. Trump told \uc2 CBS\uc2  two months ago he would make them public.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You are now the oldest candidate in this race. Will you release your medical records?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Sure. I do. I just had a medical exam. And I had a perfect score.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Trump campaign recently released a vaguely worded letter and other information from his doctors.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the former president is getting a big fund-raising boost tonight, pulling in about $220 million over the last quarter from a trio of billionaire donors, including \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion in Atlanta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to battleground Pennsylvania, where Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  launched a new push today to win the support of Republicans, including with an interview on FOX News, where she said, if elected, her presidency would not be a continuation of Joe Biden`s.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): I am joined today by over 100 Republican leaders.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voice-over): With current and former Republican officials on hand, Vice President Harris lambasted former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for saying, if elected, he would take on the -- quote -- "enemy within."\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: He says that, as commander in chief, he would use our military to go after them. Honestly, let that sink in. Use of the American military to go after American citizens?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Harris also asked the crowd to consider comments from former Joint Chiefs Chairman \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Donald Trump`s top general, he has called Trump -- and I quote -- "fascist to the core" and said -- quote -- "No one has ever been as dangerous to this country."\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Today`s rally was in Bucks County, a key suburban Philadelphia battleground, where the campaign hopes to peel off Republican voters, especially women.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-Illinois): I urge you to make the conservative choice. Vote for our bedrock values and vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: We spoke to longtime national GOP strategist Jennifer Horn, now backing Harris.\par \par \uc2 If there is an issue that`s flipping women towards Vice President Harris, what is it?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HORN (Former Chair, New Hampshire Republican Party): Well, it`s definitely the overturn of Roe v. Wade, and not just because it`s about abortion, but because it`s an attack on women`s rights, on women`s autonomy -- autonomy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: On a nearby main street, Harris` visit was embraced by some.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s a good idea. It`s better people know what the choices are and what she can offer and what they could vote for if they wanted to.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Republican Ray Chappaca`s (sp?) vote was not in question.\par \par \uc2 You don`t think she has it?\par \par \uc2 RAY CHAPPACA (Republican Voter): Not at all. No, she`s part of the Biden administration. She will just continue that, and nothing will change. It will only get worse.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: While there is energy here in the suburbs today, some Democrats privately tell CBS News they`re concerned about the energy in the city of Philadelphia and want to make sure those core Democratic voters are ready to turn out in November -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa with that new reporting tonight from Bucks County, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tragic news tonight from Argentina. Officials there say Liam Payne, a former member of the boy band One Direction, has died after falling from the third floor of a hotel in Buenos Aires. It`s unclear if it was an accident. His body was found on a patio. Payne had several hit singles as a solo artist. Liam Payne was just 31 years old.\par \par \uc2 Another big story that we are following tonight, could the Menendez brothers, Lyle and Erik, be let out of prison? They are currently serving life without parole for murdering their parents in 1989 in their Beverly Hills home. But now the DA is examining new evidence.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti reports, a number of family members spoke out today for their release.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): A family once torn apart made a unified plea.\par \par \uc2 ANAMARIA BARALT (Niece of Jose Menendez): It`s time to recognize the injustice they have suffered and allow them the second chance they deserve.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Asking a crowd of more than 100 reporters to understand, Lyle and Erik Menendez were abused by their parents, and petitioning for their release from prison for killing Jose and Kitty Menendez three decades ago.\par \par \uc2 ANAMARIA BARALT: Over time, it became clear that there were two other victims there on that day, my cousins Lyle and Erik.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Lyle and Erik Menendez had always claimed the shotgun killings were self-defense, arguing they were subject to constant physical, emotional and sexual abuse. The first jury deadlocked. In the second trial, the judge severely limited claims of sexual abuse and jurors voted to convict.\par \par \uc2 JOAN VANDERMOLEN (Sister of Kitty Menendez): They were just children, children who could have been protected, and were instead brutalized in the most horrific ways.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: This letter revealed a new court documents was allegedly handwritten by Erik to a family member just months before the murders.\par \par \uc2 "It`s still happening," he wrote. "Every night, I stay up thinking he might come in. I`m afraid. You just don`t know dad like I do."\par \par \uc2 Last year, a former member of the boy band Menudo claimed he was sexually abused by Jose Menendez in the 1980s. L.A. County district attorney George Gascon cited that account and the letter as reasons to review sentencing in the case.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE GASCON (D-Los Angeles County District Attorney): I don`t think that they deserve to be in prison until they die.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The current \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series "Monsters" has also revived interest in the story.\par \par \uc2 JOAN VANDERMOLEN: It`s time to give them the opportunity to live the rest of their lives free from the shadow of their past.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And the DA will hold a hearing on this case next month and could hand it over to a judge.\par \par \uc2 If so, a number of options here, a new trial, reduced sentences, or upholding their current life sentences. Norah, it`s a process that could take several months.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jonathan Vigliotti, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the Middle East, the Biden administration said today Israel is taking steps to address the humanitarian crisis in Gaza. It comes after the U.S. warned Israel it was at risk of losing access to weapons funding. Israel, meanwhile, resumed its attacks on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  in the suburbs of Beirut, Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta is there. And we warn you that some of the images that you are going to see are graphic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): Israeli jets pounded Southern Lebanon today. And despite U.S. concern over Israel`s bombing campaign in Beirut, there were a series of strikes around the capital.\par \par \uc2 This comes a day after \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  vowed to strike deeper inside Israel. The Israeli military says it`s targeting \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s strongholds. But there have been more than 2,000 casualties and nearly 11,000 wounded, many of them civilians.\par \par \uc2 At Lebanon`s only burn unit, the hospital has tripled its usual number of beds. In this ward, it even hurts to breathe. But it`s the war`s youngest victims that are particularly difficult for nurse Ali Humayda (sp?).\par \par \uc2 (ALI HUMAYDA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "It`s terrible to see children in pain," he told us, "especially when there isn`t much we can do."\par \par \uc2 Already, 21-month-old Ivana (sp?) has learned to dread the men and women in blue scrubs.\par \par \uc2 (ALI HUMAYDA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "I`m almost done," Humayda reassures her. She`s covered in burns from head to toe. The slightest touch is excruciating. Her mother, Fatima Zayun (sp?), told us their house was hit by a rocket over three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 (FATIMA ZAYUN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "I saw my daughter on fire," she said. Since then, she`s been inconsolable.\par \par \uc2 What do you hope for now?\par \par \uc2 (FATIMA ZAYUN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "I don`t care about anything," she said. "I just want my daughter to get better."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Around 1.2 million people have fled their homes because of the war.\par \par \uc2 Aid organizations are having to deal with a massive influx of displaced people looking for food and shelter -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s a late development in a first-of-its-kind case. A father faces the death penalty for a death linked to shaken baby syndrome. That story is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A Texas man is facing execution tomorrow evening after his appeal for clemency was denied today.\par \par \uc2 Robert Roberson be the first person in the U.S. to be executed for a murder linked to shaken baby syndrome.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Battiste reports, some medical experts say his conviction was based on faulty and outdated scientific evidence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voice-over): The lead detective in Robert Roberson`s case told a panel of Texas lawmakers this morning he`d gotten it wrong.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WHARTON (Former Palestine, Texas, Lead Detective): He`s an innocent man, and we are very close to killing him for something he did not do.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Brian Wharton first met Roberson in the emergency room when he brought his 2-year-old daughter, Nikki Curtis, to the hospital, where she later died. He told Wharton his daughter was sick and had fallen off the bed while sleeping.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WHARTON: He wasn`t behaving the way we thought he should. And it was odd. His child was in the emergency room. She was in desperate circumstances, and he was not particularly emotional.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Roberson was diagnosed with autism after his conviction. The condition can sometimes make it difficult to express emotions in ways that are socially appropriate. Wharton says he`s ashamed he misunderstood the behavior.\par \par \uc2 How did that change your feelings about what you saw that day?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WHARTON: Well, it made everything make sense. Now I can understand it. It would have been nice to have known back then, sure.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Roberson was immediately accused of shaking his daughter to death, but his defense team says an autopsy later revealed his daughter died from pneumonia, and the credibility of shaken baby syndrome is now in question.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN SWEEN (Attorney For Robert Roberson): Robert was instantly judged with suspicion.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: On Tuesday, Gretchen Sween, Roberson`s lawyer, asked the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to halt his execution. Roberson has two other children.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN SWEEN: Robert is this very sweet, simple soul. And what he has always said is that we have to keep hope. And he refers to this letter he got from his other daughter when he was sent away to prison, which said: "Daddy, don`t ever give up hope."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The one person who can still pause Roberson`s execution is Texas Governor Greg Abbott. He could issue a 30-day reprieve.\par \par \uc2 Since Abbott became governor, more than 70 inmates have been executed. Only one has been granted clemency. Roberson is scheduled to die by lethal injection in 24 hours -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikki Battiste, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And Eye on America is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Workers in their 50s are generally at peak earning potential, yet more than half worry they will run out of money in retirement.\par \par \uc2 So, in tonight`s Eye on America, the latest installment of our series on retirement planning.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  visits one couple in their 50s who want to be saving more for later, but they have got a lot on their plate.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER (voice-over): Dinnertime at the Gomez residence in suburban Boston means feeding three generations.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ (Massachusetts Resident): Here you go.\par \par \uc2 It`s crazy. We`re raising my niece and nephew. My mother lives with us. And my sister lives with us.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: So how many people total in this house?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: Seven.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Alicia, 56, works for a nonprofit, and her husband, Chu, 58 works in logistics. They`re part of the sandwich generation, workers supporting young relatives and aging parents.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: I equate it to a turkey club sandwich, because the club sandwich has a lot of layers and we have a lot of layers.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Intergenerational arrangements like this account for roughly five million U.S. households. They can easily wreak havoc on retirement plans.\par \par \uc2 How do you think about the future?\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ (Massachusetts Resident): And we were thinking 62. We can retire at 62 and still be young. And the other day, she says: "You`re going to work until 70, right?"\par \par \uc2 I`m like: "I guess so."\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI (Labor Economist): Fifties is a good time to save.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: People in their 50s, says labor economist Teresa Ghilarducci, need to put away as much of their earnings as possible.\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: In your 50s, you may be pressured to help your adult children. You may be pressured to even help your older parents, but don`t sacrifice your own retirement savings.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: The Gomezes are saving, but they are more than a half-a- million dollars in debt, including house, car and college loans for their two daughters.\par \par \uc2 How long before those are paid off?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: He`s going to be 71 when they`re done.\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: Yes, it`s -- yes, we will have those for a long time.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: The Gomezes do have a nest egg, but not a big one.\par \par \uc2 If you`re in your 50s like them, here`s what to consider. Have honest conversations about how long any assistance for relatives, like any older children, will last. Temporarily reduce retirement contributions to pay down any high interest debt, like credit cards. Then, importantly, don`t forget to boost your savings.\par \par \uc2 Is the money coming in covering the bills that you have?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: We have enough, but it`s not where we should be. God forbid if one of us gets sick or we are laid off. What will that do to us financially?\par \par \uc2 TERESA GHILARDUCCI: Well, people in their 50s have a really high chance of losing their career job. So watch your back. Keep your job.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Both Alicia and Chu were in fact laid off in their 50s. Alicia picked up consulting work, then was rehired. But it took Chu six months during the pandemic to find a new job.\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: When you get laid off, you can`t do the 401(k). So there were six months of 401(k) that was not being put in there.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: Chu missed $13,000 in 401(k) contributions, which would have been worth roughly $40,000 by the time he retires and could have helped cover some retirement costs, like health insurance.\par \par \uc2 On average, a retired 65-year-old should expect to spend about $165,000 on health care throughout retirement.\par \par \uc2 Do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 CHU GOMEZ: It`s funny. Until you bring it up with somebody, oh, yes, that other thing that`s always there.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: Take your time.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: The retirement pressure never lets up.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: We didn`t know we were going to have to care for so many family members. So the unexpected was a wakeup call, I think, for us.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: For the sandwich generation, saving for the unexpected is the best answer to that call.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA GOMEZ: That sounds good.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER: For Eye on America, \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2  in Dracut, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s a lot to think about.\par \par \uc2 Now to a consumer alert about those pesky subscriptions. Today, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  issued what it calls the click-to-cancel rule. Businesses have to make it as easy to cancel a subscription as it is to sign up. They will also have to get your consent for those auto renewal plans and free trials that automatically become paid memberships.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ethel Kennedy was remembered today here in Washington as a champion for human rights and as a fireball of continuous energy.\par \par \uc2 Three Democratic presidents attended her memorial, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . The widow of Robert F. Kennedy died last week at 96 years old, the head of a family that includes 34 grandchildren and 24 great- grandchildren. President Biden spoke of how Mrs. Kennedy reached out when his first wife and daughter were killed in a car accident and then again when his son Beau died.\par \par \uc2 Ethel Kennedy will be buried next to her husband at Arlington National Cemetery.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241017ekag00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For October 4, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Manuel Bojorquez, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Nikole Killion, Jo Ling  Kent, Steve Hartman, Imtiaz Tyab, Scott MacFarlane, Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: the strong jobs report exceeding expectations at a crucial time in the presidential campaign. Will the economy be the deciding factor as America votes?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA WHITNEY (California Resident): The cost of living is a lot.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Hey, folks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: President Biden touts the good economic news in a surprise White House briefing.\par \par \uc2 Americans stuck in Lebanon, as violence continues. We speak to a Marine veteran who says he hasn`t gotten an update from the U.S. Embassy in days.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Do you feel abandoned by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And on this Friday night, we go on the road with the compassion of community in the wake of Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GARLAND (Mountain River Family Campground): We`re so strong, and I know that we will make it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin with new signs of a robust jobs market with Election Day fast approaching and the economy and inflation critical issues for voters. Tonight, the Labor Department reports an unexpectedly large surge in new job creation, with employers adding 254,000 jobs in September. That is far above what was projected by economists. It`s also the largest monthly increase since March.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment also ticked lower to 4.1 percent. The numbers were so strong that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did something he`s never done before as president. He spoke to reporters at the press briefing to take a victory lap.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent is breaking down the numbers for us.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jo Ling. So good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So what does the surge in new jobs tell us about the economy and what did we find out about who`s doing all the hiring?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Norah, this was a blockbuster jobs report, blowing past expectations. The most jobs were added in hospitality, bars, and restaurants, health care, government, and social assistance.\par \par \uc2 But not every single voter is feeling this good news.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (BELL RINGING)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT (voice-over): It was a welcome October surprise for the economy. The September jobs report added the most jobs since March, dramatically beating Wall Street`s expectations, with the unemployment rate ticking down.\par \par \uc2 Hourly wages jumped 4 percent over the last year, outpacing inflation. President Biden today taking credit.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We were told time and again that the polices we were pursuing, we`d put forward weren`t -- weren`t going to work, make things worse, including some of the other team who are still saying they`re going to make things worse. But we`ve proven them wrong.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: While former President Trump has been campaigning on his administration`s economic record.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Inflation has devastated our economy. It`s one of the big problems we have.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Overall, the average unemployment rate has been lower in the Biden administration, while the average inflation rate was lower under Trump.\par \par \uc2 LISA WHITNEY (California Resident): Well, I have applied for unemployment.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Voter Lisa Whitney (sp?) in Southern California was laid off from her job in July.\par \par \uc2 LISA WHITNEY: I`m left paying with my health insurance and my other utilities and my car payment now, which is basically, when you add those all up, is what I get from unemployment. But then I`m still paying my mortgage and my property taxes and my home owner`s insurance.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: How has all of this impacted your family and what you`re able to do?\par \par \uc2 LISA WHITNEY: It`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Do you think you`re better off now than you were when President Biden first took office?\par \par \uc2 LISA WHITNEY: I`m not better off now, no.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Whitney plans to vote for Trump in November as she goes into the fourth month of her job search.\par \par \uc2 LISA WHITNEY: I would like to see a president that really supports employees. I want to be working. I want to care for my daughter. There`s a lot of expenses.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: It just looks like the hits keep coming.\par \par \uc2 LISA WHITNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Looking ahead, the next jobs report will come out just four days before the election. And there`s a lot of global uncertainty on the horizon. The escalating conflicts in the Middle East could push oil prices higher, as well as the economic impact of Hurricane Helene -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jo Ling Kent with all that news, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, CBS News has obtained a bulletin from the FBI and Department of Homeland Security warning domestic extremists with election-related grievances could turn violent ahead of Election Day. Now, that comes as both President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were asked about the peaceful transfer of power today.\par \par \uc2 We have team coverage beginning with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion in Georgia, where Trump was today.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This is former President Trump`s second visit to hurricane-ravaged Georgia this week, but the political storm surrounding his fraught relationship with Republican Governor Brian Kemp garnered much of the attention, although both men appear to have put aside their differences for now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice-over): In a rare joint appearance, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Georgia Governor Brian Kemp were side-by-side for a briefing on Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): You have done a really good job, Brian.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: It was the first time Trump and Kemp have appeared together since 2020, when the former president tried to pressure state officials to overturn the results of the election and was later indicted by a Georgia grand jury.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I just want to find 11,780 votes.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: This summer, the former president lashed out at Kemp at an Atlanta rally.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: He`s the most disloyal guy I think I have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But, today, he had nothing but praise.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We have always worked together very well.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The fence-mending comes amid renewed concern about the November election, with President Biden issuing this warning:\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I`m confident it`ll be free and fair. I don`t know whether it will be peaceful.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I don`t know anything about what he said. I only can hope that it`s going to be free and fair, and I think in this state it will be, and I hope in every state it will be. And I think we`re going to do very well.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Meantime, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  stumped in battleground Michigan.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): We will not be gaslighted. Donald Trump`s track record is a disaster for working people.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Her campaign announced former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  will be part of a campaign blitz across battleground states in the final month, as both campaigns ramp up their efforts.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: I`m Caitlin Huey-Burns in Butler, Pennsylvania, where security is high because former President Trump will be here for a rally, at the same site where he survived an assassination attempt back in July.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2  has expanded the security perimeter and will have personnel stationed inside and outside of that zone, including on the roof of the shed where the shooter fired on July 13. Those shots killed Corey Comperatore, who died shielding his family from the bullets. He will be honored here on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Supporters are already on site.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is Butler. We don`t stand down. We stand up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And the rally site right behind me is already locked down. Authorities say they`re expecting over 25,000 people to be in attendance here, including the billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Caitlin Huey-Burns in Pennsylvania and Nikole Killion in Georgia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the death toll from Hurricane Helene keeps rising. At least 225 people are dead now across six states. And the full scope of the destruction is only now coming into focus. In the mountains of North Carolina, entire towns are swamped, buried under mud and debris. And now a big question is how to start cleaning up.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voice-over): Downtown Marshall, North Carolina, was swallowed by catastrophic flooding. Now what remains is covered in a thick coat of mud and debris that`s considered hazardous.\par \par \uc2 Ben Phillips is shoveling it out of his home.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like to see all your things out here like this?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BEN PHILLIPS (Marshall, North Carolina, Resident): It`s heartbreaking to - - everything we had in that house.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: This area is not known for flooding. The North Carolina flood map shows Marshall with only a 1 percent chance. Out of more than 8,000 properties in the county, only 98 had flood insurance.\par \par \uc2 You have flood insurance.\par \par \uc2 BEN PHILLIPS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But I think you`re probably in the minority.\par \par \uc2 BEN PHILLIPS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: A lot of people did not.\par \par \uc2 BEN PHILLIPS: They were saying that businesses downtown, only about two of them had flood insurance and that almost nobody else did.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Just decimated this whole area.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Fifty-five miles east in Old Fort, this satellite image shows the water`s disastrous path. We got a closer look on the ground.\par \par \uc2 These manufactured homes were not originally here. They were pushed by the sheer force of the water. And it`s not just people`s homes. You can see this shed, even vehicles, and based on the water line there, the water would have reached up to my head.\par \par \uc2 Down the street, Pastor Kimberly Baker and volunteers are cleaning up mud by the barrelful at the United Methodist Church.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR KIMBERLY BAKER (Old Fort United Methodist Church): And you can see how deep it is to the altar.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: For 24 hours, four to five feet of water flowed through the church before receding.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR KIMBERLY BAKER: We didn`t have flood insurance because we have never....\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Never flooded.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR KIMBERLY BAKER: Never needed it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Now there is nothing to do but clean up and count their blessings.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR KIMBERLY BAKER: We`re still here.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: You`re still here.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR KIMBERLY BAKER: And we`re going to be here. And we`re going to come back, not just the church, but the people.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR KIMBERLY BAKER: We`re going to build this little town back.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: This year, out of the 4.3 million properties across the state of North Carolina, only about 115,000 have a flood insurance policy.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, are the reason people here in Marshall are wearing masks and hazmat suits is that there is a sewage treatment facility and a PVC plant both upstream, so the mud and the dust could be toxic.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manuel Bojorquez, thank you for being there and telling their story.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was asked by CBS News today whether Netanyahu was holding up a peace deal to influence our election. Well, the president said he doesn`t know. But, in the meantime, the fighting rages on against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to the south and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to the north in Lebanon, where thousands, tens of thousands of Americans are tonight.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab spoke to a U.S. veteran who is having a hard time getting out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voice-over): The last time Iran`s supreme leader led Friday prayers in Tehran was nearly five years ago. With the barrel of a machine gun in hand, he had a message for Israel.\par \par \uc2 (AYATOLLAH ALI KHAMENEI SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "We did it once," he said, "and we will do it again."\par \par \uc2 Ayatollah Khamenei\uc2  was talking about Iran`s ballistic missile attack on Israel Tuesday night, retaliation for the killing of his close ally \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  in this massive strike in Beirut one week ago. Since then, Israel has been targeting the group`s strongholds relentlessly, devastating civilian areas and displacing more than 1.2 million people.\par \par \uc2 SHANIYAT CHOWDHURY (American in Beirut): And I sat in the living room floor with my wallet, my phone, my passport just in case in any moment I had to run.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Shaniyat Chowdhury eventually did have to run. The \uc2 NYU\uc2  grad student and Marine Corps veteran was in Beirut visiting friends when this happened.\par \par \uc2 SHANIYAT CHOWDHURY: The building right across from the one that I was living in was struck.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Was struck?\par \par \uc2 SHANIYAT CHOWDHURY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The U.S. and other countries have started evacuating citizens, but Chowdhury has had a hard time getting in touch with the American Embassy in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Have you actually spoken to an actual person at the embassy?\par \par \uc2 SHANIYAT CHOWDHURY: No, absolutely not. I`m constantly going on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , looking at the embassy of Beirut`s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  page, and they haven`t updated anyone the last four or five days.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Four or five days?\par \par \uc2 SHANIYAT CHOWDHURY: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: So you`re really in this information black hole?\par \par \uc2 SHANIYAT CHOWDHURY: Yes. I just feel like they just don`t care if you are an American or not.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now, Chowdhury still has no idea how he will get home, as Israeli strikes across Lebanon only intensify.\par \par \uc2 So far, over 2,000 people have been killed in the attacks, a figure the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  is calling totally unacceptable -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Imtiaz Tyab, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Nearly one year has passed since the October 7 attacks that sparked the catastrophic war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . To mark the important milestone, a new documentary, We Will Dance Again, tells the story of October 7 through the eyes of survivors of the Nova Music Festival and through the bodycam and \uc2 GoPro\uc2  footage from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists obtained by filmmakers.\par \par \uc2 One survivor, Etan (sp?), describes how \uc2 Hamas\uc2  threw grenades into the crowded shelter where he was hiding and then how he threw them back out over and over again. But then \uc2 Hamas\uc2  opened fire.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ETAN (Survivor): There was a -- they came back in, and then they started shooting everyone. They left. I looked left and right. And -- and I thought everyone was acting or playing dead, so that they wouldn`t get kidnapped.\par \par \uc2 But after a minute or two, where -- when people weren`t lifting their heads or waking up, I realized that I was sitting in a pile of bodies.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We Will Dance Again from See It Now Studios is now streaming on Paramount+. That is part of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  corporate family.\par \par \uc2 Up next: Country superstar Garth Brooks responds to the stunning allegation of rape made by one of his former makeup artists.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Country music legend Garth Brooks is claiming he was being extorted by a former makeup artist who has accused him of rape. The woman`s name has not been revealed.\par \par \uc2 She claims the attack happened five years ago in a Los Angeles hotel room. She also says Brooks repeatedly appeared naked in front of her, groped her and sent her sexually explicit text messages. In a statement, Brooks maintains over the last few months she has threatened to tell -- quote -- "lies and tragic tales" if he didn`t write her a check for millions. He filed his own lawsuit last month claiming extortion.\par \par \uc2 Now to a disturbing story from Minneapolis. A 10-year-old boy has been arrested, accused of driving a stolen car through a crowded school playground. Police released this surveillance video showing the car speeding on the grass while children play nearby. The car gets dangerously close as school staff scramble to get the children to safety.\par \par \uc2 The incident happened back in September. The boy`s name has not been released because of his age.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we take a close look at the polarizing debate over fracking in Pennsylvania and the impact it could have on this presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: One of the tightest races this presidential election is the battle for Pennsylvania and its 19 electoral votes. It`s a state with nearly nine million registered voters.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won Pennsylvania by just over 44,200 votes. In 2020, President Biden won by a margin of about 80,500 votes. So any issue could make or break either candidate. And in Pennsylvania, fracking is divisive.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane digs into the issue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): In a new home near her grandkids, Mary Kalivoda thought retirement would be worry-free in West Deer Township, Pennsylvania, near Pittsburgh, until she discovered the new neighbor.\par \par \uc2 MARY KALIVODA (Pennsylvania Resident): There are a lot of issues with fracking.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A planned hydraulic fracturing, or fracking, facility nearby. It`s a big industry in Pennsylvania, where they drill and extract a gas gold mine underground.\par \par \uc2 MARY KALIVODA: There have been many health studies that have shown that fracking is not safe for children and perhaps for seniors.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But she acknowledges it`s lured money.\par \par \uc2 MARY KALIVODA: We are not against fracking at all.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You don`t want it banned.\par \par \uc2 MARY KALIVODA: I don`t want it banned completely, no. I think it can exist in a municipality if there are safe regulations put in place.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trevor Sammis` company has 150 employees that provide services used in the fracking process.\par \par \uc2 You can get this job high school degree?\par \par \uc2 TREVOR SAMMIS (Manager, Costy`s Energy Services): High school degree, $100,000-a-year position.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Six figures.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR SAMMIS: Six figures first year in.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The industry estimates fracking has created tens of thousands of jobs here, with a bright future.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR SAMMIS: We are planned through 2025 as far as drilling programs go.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: All the way through next year.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR SAMMIS: All the way through December of 2025.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In a race this close in a state so important, neither candidate wants to alienate fracking supporters. And the issue emerged on the debate stage this fall.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): I was the tiebreaking vote on the Inflation Reduction Act, which opened new leases for fracking.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Vice President Harris has pivoted away from a previous stance opposing fracking. Trump has urged drilling to expand.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Pumping, fracking, drilling and producing like never before.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But even here in Pennsylvania, the politics of fracking are complicated. Only some of the state has gas available to be fracked. And our latest CBS News polling shows voters are split; 56 percent of Pennsylvania voters support it; 44 percent do not.\par \par \uc2 Further complicating the politics, presidents can`t ban hydraulic fracking in communities. Power resides with local leaders. Six states have banned it. In the heat of a close election, candidates are drilling down for every vote they can.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane CBS News, West Deer Township, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Up next, Steve Hartman`s On the Road bringing us hope in the face of Hurricane Helene`s destruction.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight we bring you the story of one family trapped in the path of Hurricane Helene. Their land was their livelihood, a campground, but the memories will never be washed away.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman is On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voice-over): At the Mountain River Family Campground in Western North Carolina, the river isn`t the only thing that gushes. Visitors do too.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It`s beautiful because you`re kind of nestled in a valley at this campground.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It`s a whole little sense of community.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: So friendly and open.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: There`s just no other place like it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: There was no other place like it. This week, the remnants of Hurricane Helene turned this slice of heaven into a hellscape.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GARLAND (Mountain River Family Campground): Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Suzanne Garland`s family owns the campground. First time back, she couldn`t believe her eyes.\par \par \uc2 (CRYING)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Every building, every bit of infrastructure erased.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GARLAND: It was total shock.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Standing by her side, her husband Jared.\par \par \uc2 JARED GARLAND (Mountain River Family Campground): Yes, because it`s just mud, trees and...\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GARLAND: It just looks like a tornado just ripped everything up and spit it back out.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Like many ravaged by this storm, the Garlands` first inclination was to move. But then, like many, they saw the sunrise, and the morning light worked its magic.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GARLAND: It was like a little reminder that like, in all the darkness, the light will always find a way to push through.\par \par \uc2 And I just knew right then and there that I couldn`t -- we couldn`t walk away from it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And the day was about to get brighter still. On their Web site, old customers and total strangers began reserving campsites, commenting: "I know we can`t come. Just booking a night to help out."\par \par \uc2 Dozens did this, paid for vacations they cannot take.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE GARLAND: And I just feel like we`re so strong and I know that we will make it. I don`t know. It just warms your heart.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Like a Carolina sunrise.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman, CBS News, On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: OK, crying again for the second night in a row. Great story, great people.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241005eka400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Dockworkers Agree to Deal To End Strike; Unlikely Political Allies; Election Interference Warning; Thousands Dead in Lebanon Amid Israeli Bombing; Three Former Officials Convicted in Tyre Nichols Case; Helene Death Toll Rises; Falcons QB Kirk Cousins Make History; Garth Brooks Accused of Sexual Assault; Menendez Brothers Case Revisited; \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  Accuser Speaks; Cheney Backs Harris; The Real Grandpa Shady; Planning For The Unknown; "Countdown: 1960"; Diving Into His Fear; A Lesson On Love; Top Hispanic Heritage Reads; Soul Pop Artist Olivia Dean, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Nancy Chen, Nancy  Cordes, Imtiaz Tyab, Skyler Henry, Natalie Morales, Nikki Battiste, Elise  Preston, Jamie Yuccas, Adriana Diaz, Jill Schlesinger, Steve Hartman, Meryl  Hartman, Emmett Hartman \par \uc2 13,750 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Friday to you as the trumpets blare. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, we`re still here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Former Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  joins Vice President Harris on the campaign trail. Why a staunch Republican is stumping for a Democrat?\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (Former US representative): Not an ounce of compassion in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is petty. He is vindictive and he is cruel.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A mixed verdict in Tennessee for officers charged in the beating death of Tyre Nichols.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2  (Attorney): Tyre Nichols death won`t go unaccounted for.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Country music legend, Garth Brooks faces sex assault allegations in a new lawsuit, claims he denies.\par \par \uc2 JEM ASWAD ("Variety" executive music editor): This is off brand in just about every way you can imagine.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: For the first time since 2015, the \uc2 New York Mets\uc2  are moving on in the postseason.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What a performance.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The Falcons in a Thursday night overtime thriller, winning on a dramatic walk off touchdown.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has recalled 27,000 cyber trucks because of a rearview camera issue.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS ("Late Night with Seth Meyers" host): Issue with the rear view camera. Apparently, whenever it`s on, you can see all the people laughing at you.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A federal appeals court cleared the way for the first fully regulated election betting market in the US.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA ("The Daily Show"): It`s shameful. Why are we betting on congressional races when this country could collapse into a fascist dictatorship? Because that`s what I want to bet on, you know.\par \par \uc2 If I could parlay a fascist takeover with the over, under on the Jets game. Oh, baby, I`m in the money. I`m in the race.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive. Making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are going to start with the breaking news this morning. We`re talking about, that dockworkers strike that threatened to cause severe damage to the US economy. It has come to a temporary end.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen was with the workers in New Jersey when they heard the strike was over. Nancy is in the studio today.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning. This is good news on many levels.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It is Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. There was a lot of cheering as that announcement came down. At least 25,000 dockworkers at 14 ports along the Eastern Gulf Coast can now return to work.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, the dockworkers union and their employer group said a tentative agreement had been made on wages and that they would extend their current contract until January 15th.\par \par \uc2 Those briefed on the deal said it includes a 62 percent raise, an increase from their previous offer of nearly 50 percent. All other remaining issues will be addressed when the two reconvene with that January deadline, which includes protections against automation and semi automation that has been a major sticking point in this.\par \par \uc2 Now, there have been major concerns also about supply chain issues because of that strike, with one economic estimate putting it as much as $4.5 billion a day. However, everyone can come back to work now, and President Biden applauded both sides, releasing a statement saying: Collective bargaining works, guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That automation fight in particular, Nancy. We all have a stake in it. It`s coming, and how to deal with it is a big question. Thank you very much for that report. We`ll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to politics and the presidential campaign, to Michigan last night, where former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  went back to the well, repeating his false claims about the 2020 election, just after the release of a new criminal filing alleging that he has always known, that he has lost and not really cared.\par \par \uc2 Across the lake in Wisconsin, Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  campaigned with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , a bipartisan scene that would have been basically impossible to imagine in previous elections.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes has been following all of this. She is joining us now from Milwaukee, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you know there are a bunch of Republicans who have announced they`re not voting for Trump, and some of them have even endorsed Harris, but \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  really took it a step further last night, campaigning with Harris in a battleground state and warning that the nation faces, in her view, an unprecedented threat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY: I am proudly casting my vote for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Former Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  may be conservative, but at a rally in Eastern Wisconsin, she argued voting against the Republican nominee is the patriotic thing to do.\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY: There is not an ounce -- not an ounce of compassion in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It was a major moment for the Harris campaign, which is hoping that Cheney can help them win over even a sliver of Republican voters like former Sheriff Steven Micheck, who was for Trump in 2016, but not anymore.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN MICHECK (Former sheriff): I`m voting for Harris and it`s to prevent again, the chaos and all of the diversions and all of the fear mongering that goes on with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is a stupid war hawk.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In an interview in Saginaw, Michigan, Trump was asked about his wife, Melania`s announcement that she, unlike her husband, supports abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We spoke about it, and I said, you have to write what you believe. I`m not going to tell you what to do.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: On stage, he spread a new baseless conspiracy theory about hurricane relief funds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : They stole the FEMA money, just like they stole it from a bank, so they could give it to their illegal immigrants that they want to have vote for them this season.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And he insisted once again that he won in 2020 despite all the trouble that lie has caused.\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : He is petty, he is vindictive, and he is cruel, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not fit to lead this good and great nation.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Trump is going to be returning to Butler, Pennsylvania this weekend for the first time since the assassination attempt on him this summer. Vice President Harris is in Flint, Michigan today, and we learned last night that former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is going to be hitting the trail for the final month of this campaign, starting in Pittsburgh on Thursday -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another sign we are getting very close.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And as Nancy just mentioned, we are just over a month away from Election Day, and there are new warnings, Iran and Russia are once again trying to interfere with our election.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Olsen, who is the head of the \uc2 Justice Department`s National Security Division\uc2 , sat down with "CBS Evening News" anchor and managing editor, Norah O`Donnell, here is some of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has long claimed that Russian interference is a hoax, in his Word, has that had an impact on the Department of Justice`s public perception?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2  (\uc2 Justice Department`s National Security Division\uc2 ): First 2016 Russian interference in the election was not a hoax. It was actually happening then, it is actually happening now. There is no question about it. It is more aggressive than ever this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Who does Russia want to win?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : Russia has been seeking to boost the candidacy of the former president and to denigrate the candidacy of the vice president.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So Russia is trying to boost \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and denigrate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : We`ve seen that very clearly.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What about Iran?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : The intelligence community, with respect to Iran, has also identified efforts that Iran has made to compromise the former president`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was briefed on real and specific threats from Iran to kill him. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : We have been intensely tracking Iranian efforts, Iranian lethal plotting, efforts targeting former and current \uc2 US government\uc2  officials, and that includes the former president.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But has it gotten more worrisome or more dangerous in the last several months?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : I would say that we are very concerned, gravely concerned, about Iranian plotting.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A programming note, "60 Minutes" will air a primetime election special Monday, October 7th, at 8:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 For more than half a century, "60 Minutes" has invited the Democratic and Republican tickets to appear on its broadcast as Americans head to the polls. Vice President Harris will speak with correspondent, Bill Whitaker.\par \par \uc2 After initially accepting "60 Minutes" request for an interview with Scott Pelley, former President Trump`s campaign has decided not to participate. The original "60 Minutes" invitation to Trump stands.\par \par \uc2 All right, turning now to the growing conflict in the Middle East. Huge fireballs rose over Beirut overnight, as Israel continued its relentless airstrikes against the militant group \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . This morning, Israel also bombed a road used by thousands of civilians trying to flee Lebanon, leaving a massive crater. Imtiaz Tyab reports from Beirut.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Israel`s overnight bombing campaign near Beirut`s main airport was so intense it shook the city.\par \par \uc2 By morning, the devastation was clear to see. The Israeli military hasn`t said what it was targeting, but Lebanese media suggests it was a potential successor to \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , the long-time \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader, who was assassinated in a similar Israeli strike here exactly one week ago.\par \par \uc2 In that week, at least 417 people have been killed in Israeli ground and air attacks, according to Lebanese health officials, including American Kamel Ahmad Jawad from Dearborn, Michigan, a volunteer who was killed in an airstrike in Southern Lebanon according to his family.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the White House said it was deeply saddened by Jawad`s death.\par \par \uc2 The skyrocketing casualties are pushing Lebanon`s already frail health care system to the brink.\par \par \uc2 At least 40 paramedics and firefighters have also been killed in Israeli strikes in just the past three days, said the country`s health minister, raising fears the system could collapse, says emergency doctor, Ziad Abu al Anaim (sp?).\par \par \uc2 DR. ZIAD ABU AL ANAIM (Emergency medicine): Our greatest fear is to receive a very big number of the patients, and you cannot deal with them.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: as a doctor. That must be very painful/\par \par \uc2 DR. ZIAD ABU AL ANAIM: Yes, because we saw what happened in Gaza, and we don`t want it to happen here.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Are you afraid that that`s what`s going to happen?\par \par \uc2 DR. ZIAD ABU AL ANAIM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In Southern Lebanon where Israeli strikes are only intensifying, Israel issued evacuation orders to residents of two dozen towns and villages, raising fears a full scale ground invasion is imminent.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said more needed to be done to avoid a wider war from spreading across the Middle East after Israel about to retaliate against Iran`s Tuesday ballistic missile attack, which was triggered by the assassination of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader, \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  one week ago -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Imtiaz Tyab reporting from Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Here in this country, three former Memphis police officers faced prison after a mixed verdict in their federal trial over the beating death of Tyre Nichols.\par \par \uc2 The officers were convicted of obstructing justice, but acquitted of the most serious charges from that deadly encounter back in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston has reaction to the verdict.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting "Justice for Tyre.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Tyre Nichols`s mother RowVaughn Wells exited the federal courtroom to the sound of supporters chanting her son`s name.\par \par \uc2 ROWVAUGHN WELLS (Tyre Nichols` mother): All of them have been convicted of something, and they`re all going to jail.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Wells` son died nearly two years ago, days after he was repeatedly kicked and punched by to Tadarrius Bean, Demetrius Haley, Justin Smith and two other former Memphis police officers.\par \par \uc2 TYRE NICHOLS (Victim): Hey, I didn`t do anything.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Prosecutors argued the officers did not tell medics the extent of force they used, yet the brutal assault and Nichols` cries for his mother were caught on police bodycams.\par \par \uc2 TYRE NICHOLS: Mom.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Former Memphis police officers, Emmitt Martin and Desmond Mills pleaded guilty in the beating death and testified to the culture of secrecy and loyalty within the now disbanded Scorpion Street Crime Unit.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting "No justice. No peace.")\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Nichols` death ignited nationwide protests, drawing widespread attention and prompting Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to attend his funeral.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): Tyre Nichols should have been safe.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: In total, the jury found all three officers guilty of obstruction for witness tampering.\par \par \uc2 Haley was found guilty of several other charges, including violating Nichols` civil rights by causing bodily injury. The judge ordered the men be taken into custody. They still face state and civil cases.\par \par \uc2 ROWVAUGHN WELLS: We`re happy that they all have been convicted.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For CBS MORNINGS, Elise Preston, Memphis, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all remember that story.\par \par \uc2 Just to hear him yelling for his mother 80 feet away from his home. I don`t understand, though, guys, how you are acquitted of the most serious charges, but found guilty of the coverup.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t understand that legal distinction either.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But I can tell you, everyone is always afraid of a conspiracy of silence when it comes to officers involved in situations like this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And it was really powerful to hear testimony from two of those officers. One of them said at trial about the kicking and the punching, "I knew they weren`t going to tell on me," meaning his fellow officers and "I wasn`t going to tell on them."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s interesting, too, is the mother and father of Tyre Nichols are pushing for the legislation, the George Floyd Police Intervention Act, which requires officers to step in if they see other officers doing something like this. Terrible story.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about something else that`s really terrible in the southeast. The confirmed number of dead from Hurricane Helene is now more than 200.\par \par \uc2 President Biden went to Florida and