d, faithful fan base, so if people start attacking him on social media, so what? It`s not going to hurt him like it would a pop star.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CBS Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 JEM ASWAD: The way that it would harm him in the most is ticket sales.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jamie Yuccas in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We do have a statement from Garth Brooks. He says: "I have been hassled to no end with threats, lies and tragic tales of what my future would be if I did not write a check for millions of dollars." He went on to say, "I do not fear the truth, and I am not the man they painted me to be."\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the Menendez brothers, maybe a new look at some evidence.\par \par \uc2 Local news though coming at you next. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOWIE ROSE (Sports commentator): -- two to nothing, swinging a fly ball to the right field. Pretty well hit. Reel it back at the wall. He jumps. It is not. He did it. He did it. Pete Alonso with the most memorable homerun.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is no joy in Milwaukee today. That`s the call by Howie Rose on Mets radio as Pete Alonso, who will never have to pay for a meal again in this New York City, hit a homerun to put the Mets on top.\par \par \uc2 It was looking totally hopeless for them until that ninth inning homer. They barely had a hit in the game at that point, but that game winner caused pandemonium for Mets fans watching the game on the Jumbotron at Citi Field.\par \par \uc2 Here they are going nuts. That`s what it looked like in my bedroom as well. That is something we`ve been waiting for a long time I will say.\par \par \uc2 The Mets are going to play the \uc2 Phillies\uc2 . You got that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Mets are going to play the \uc2 Phillies\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, we got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We got it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am a married, man, everything`s going very well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We got it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Mets are going to play the \uc2 Phillies\uc2  next week, and I`m doing all I can to make sure that I`m going to be there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you`re the first person I thought of.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I thought, did you think that, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me, too. I was thinking the same thing, even when I came in, I was like, look at this picture on the "Daily News."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, you have to be there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And "The New York Post."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have to be there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look, the reason Mets fans are so excited is because halfway through the season, they were 28 and 37 since Grimace threw out the first pitch, you know, the McDonald`s mascot and since me and Nate were there taking batting practice, the Mets have the best record of baseball.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ho, ho, ho. So you and Nate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here we go Mets. Here we go Mets.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to turn now to an update on one of the most talked about murder cases of the 1990s. You will recall Erik and Lyle Menendez were convicted of the 1989 murder of their parents at their Los Angeles home. They have been behind bars for 34 years.\par \par \uc2 But there is new evidence that is leading LA`s district attorney to take another look.\par \par \uc2 Our Natalie Morales has done extensive reporting on this, and has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yesterday, the Los Angeles County District Attorney George Gascon revealed that the emergence of new evidence in the case of Erik and Lyle Menendez could lead to a resentencing.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE GASCON (Los Angeles County district attorney): We have been given a photocopy of a letter that allegedly was sent by one of the brothers to another family member talking about him being the victim of molestation.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: In 1989, the Menendez brothers made headlines for the murder of their parents, Jose and Kitty. Their trial was must-watch TV at the time.\par \par \uc2 They were convicted in 1996 and have been serving life sentences ever since.\par \par \uc2 The brothers now 53 and 56 have never denied killing their parents, but say they were repeatedly sexually abused by them and committed the crime because they feared for their lives.\par \par \uc2 Late last year, I spoke to Lyle Menendez about the new evidence in their case.\par \par \uc2 What did you think when you heard about these new claims and evidence?\par \par \uc2 LYLE MENENDEZ (Convicted for killing parents): I mean, for me, I just was happy. Because it`s a burden to be telling what happened to you and just have so much doubt in the public air.\par \par \uc2 CLIFF GARDNER (Menendez brothers` appellate attorney): If the judge finds this evidence credible, I think it is sufficient to give them a new trial.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Cliff Gardner is an appellate lawyer for the brothers. He says the new evidence includes a letter that was recently found in storage that Erik reportedly wrote to his cousin eight months before the murders.\par \par \uc2 CLIFF GARDNER: "Every night I stay up thinking he might come in. I`m afraid. He`s crazy. He`s warned me a hundred times about telling anyone, especially Lyle."\par \par \uc2 No one knew about it at trial. It was never presented.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Another attorney for the brothers, Mark Geragos also spoke saying the reviewing of the new evidence is long overdue.\par \par \uc2 MARK GERAGOS (Attorney for Menendez brothers): We`ve got a much more robust and evolved understanding of abuse. I think it`s time, their family thinks it`s time.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And their appellate attorney, Cliff Gardner told us: "I will take George Gascon at his word that he is taking a close look at the case. I`m pleased by it, because I think that anyone that takes a look at that evidence is going to walk away with the understanding those boys were molested as children."\par \par \uc2 Now there is a November 26th deadline for the DA`s office to submit an informal response. But guys, needless to say, this is going to be a long legal process. This was a habeas petition that was filed a year-and-a-half ago with the district attorney`s office, so now they`re just getting to reviewing the case.\par \par \uc2 And George Gascon, we should mention, he is up for reelection at this time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And back then, Natalie, you certainly didn`t talk about child molestation the way that we talk about it.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, it`s so surprising to me that a letter that crucial is just coming to light now.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes, this is a letter that was in storage for many, many years, and this is a letter that Erik wrote, which you know would show that, you know this -- this is a letter that was written eight months prior to the murders.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And it would add to more evidence, perhaps, that they were -- that they were abused.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and I just heard that 23 of the family members also want - - we keep calling them "the boys" -- but the Menendez brothers out. They said they`ve spent enough time in jail.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be following this case. Thank you, Natalie. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Absolutely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a CBS News exclusive, we sit down with a woman who says that she was assaulted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ally, \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2 . You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trump aide \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  was ousted from the former president`s inner circle after a woman accused him of assaulting and stalking her. This was back in 2021, but in August, Trump rehired Lewandowski to help with his presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Now in a CBS News exclusive, Lewandowski`s accuser sat down with our Nikki Battiste.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Trashelle Odom, then the wife of a Trump donor was seated next to \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  at a charity dinner in a Las Vegas hotel in September of 2021. She sat down with me speaking out on camera for the first time about what she called a terrifying night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM (Accuses \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  of sexual assault): It felt like he could just do whatever he wanted and get away with it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Trashelle Odom says she didn`t really know who \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  was when she met him at a fundraiser in Las Vegas in September of 2021.\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: He said, "I`m very powerful. I rule like politics, who goes in, who goes out; I`m the one that makes all those decisions."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: A loyalist and dogged defender of the former president, Lewandowski is now back at candidate Trump`s side as a senior adviser.\par \par \uc2 COREY LEWANDOWSKI\uc2  (Trump-Vance campaign senior adviser): In 2016, when I ran the campaign \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won, I`m here to help him win again. So that`s my goal.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Lewandowski faced scrutiny during that 2016 campaign for his treatment of women after CCTV footage showed him grabbing a reporter`s arm following a press conference.\par \par \uc2 Then in 2017, a woman told police Lewandowski slapped her buttocks against her will and without her consent during a holiday party. She didn`t press charges.\par \par \uc2 Four years later, Odom was seated next to Lewandowski at a charity dinner.\par \par \uc2 Did you feel like he was targeting you specifically that night?\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: A hundred percent. Yes. He was targeting just me. His eyes were just on me.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Then, 32 years old and new to the world of politics, she says, Lewandowski quickly made her uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: He was saying how large his privates were and saying how long he lasts in bed because of how much he works out. He started touching me.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: He was touching you?\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: Yes. Putting his hands like on my lap and like trying to touch my butt and I kept pushing his hand away.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: She later told police she felt scared alone and like she could not do anything, because she believed Corey was someone who everyone at the dinner respected.\par \par \uc2 She says she even tried asking another woman at the party for help.\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: And I was like, "I really need help." I kind of explained. She`s like, "Yeah, Corey does this all the time." Like, this is so weird. Why would -- how is this okay?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Some people watching will think, why didn`t you just leave?\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: Well, I did. I didn`t think he would keep following me, though.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: She says, when she got up from the table, Lewandowski stalked her through the hotel and then threw a drink on her. She says she tried to stay in a crowd until she could get to her hotel room.\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: I was his prey. He didn`t care what he did to someone, especially to a woman. He was just like, I say it, he is just evil.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Las Vegas police charged Lewandowski with misdemeanor battery. He later cut a deal to get the case dismissed, which included paying a $1,000.00 fine and apologizing to Odom for any discomfort he may have caused.\par \par \uc2 The incident led to Lewandowski`s removal from Trump`s super PAC, a spokesperson for the former president, vowing he would no longer be associated with Trump world.\par \par \uc2 How did you feel?\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: I felt like, thank God. A person like that should never have that much power.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Corey`s lawyers publicly denied your allegations. What`s your response to that?\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: Well, if they`re going to deny it, I think it`s funny that they try to pay me to keep my mouth shut.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: How much did they offer you?\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: It was like around thirty thousand. I denied the money. I didn`t want any money from it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Separate from the claims against Lewandowski, a recent legal filing revealed text messages suggesting the 2020 campaign settled multiple lawsuits with women who accused another staff member of discrimination or sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 After speaking to police, Odom says she received an unexpected phone call from someone else familiar with hush money payments, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: I appreciated his phone call. He was kind. He told me that Corey was drunk.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It was a surprise in August.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  is coming in.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: When a friend called to tell her \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  was back on Trump`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 TRASHELLE ODOM: I had no heads up. I just broke down, and I was very, very upset. And if I can get just a little bit to one person, me, spea