in the house.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You could have storm damage. You don`t want to start moving things around. You don`t want to start to do anything until you have documentation. So you`re taking videos, you`re taking pictures. You want to try to find the list of assets that have been damaged in this storm. It could be televisions, it could be dressers and beds, and you want to compile this massive list. This is incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 And just one other thing. A lot of people think that they should start making repairs immediately, only temporary repairs. Nothing actually long- term because you`ve got to submit a claim to the insurance company. You`ve got to contact the adjuster. You`ve got to agree to what they come back with.\par \par \uc2 Remember, when the insurance company comes back and says, "Hey, Tony, we`re going to pay you $5,000 for that," don`t sign anything. This is a negotiation. This is an agreement that you will have. Only when you have confirmation that you`re done, that`s when you start the rebuilding process.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jill, here`s the sad reality and something that`s a warning for everybody in this country. Most people there in the storm zone did not have insurance for flooding.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yeah, you`ve got to go to the FEMA website immediately and get the resources you need right there. This is incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 For everyone else who has insurance, please, please, please do this work before a storm hits. Locate your policy. Know what`s in your house. I love to have people take pictures of every item in their house when everything`s fine. And then you`ve got that list. God forbid something horrible happens later. You know what you have.\par \par \uc2 Remember, when you`re shopping for homeowner`s insurance or renter`s insurance, apples to apples. Do not just base your bid on the price of that policy. Don`t just go for that. It`s important that you know what you have. Please review this before something bad occurs. It can be very helpful in the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jill, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good advice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always good advice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, always. Ahead, "Kindness 101." Steve Hartman and his kids introduce us to a couple who got married not once, but twice. It`s an invaluable lesson about love. that is sure to touch your heart, touch your mind.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Wizard shooting guard Jared Butler is conquering one of his biggest fears, what it is, and what he hopes to inspire in others along the way. But first, 8.05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You meet the nicest people in the progressive green room. I`m sitting here now with a journalist. What`s your name, journalist?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE (Author "Countdown 1960"): Chris Wallace.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is Chris Wallace. You know that voice. He`s here to tell us about his new book on the 1960 presidential election. Can I say about this book, it`s thrilling, it`s interesting, it`s entertaining, and I learned a lot. Is it okay to say that? May I hold it up?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE: Yes, you may hold it up, but look at all these notes. You really read this book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I really read it, Chris, and it`s really very good. And you wanted to take this on why in one sentence.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE: First, it`s a great story, and two, it has a lot of relevance to what`s going on right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What he just said. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Chris Wallace joins us right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Journalist Chris Wallace has covered American politics for five decades now. His "Countdown" book series takes on some of the biggest events in world history. His new book is "Countdown to 1960" It is knockout. The behind-the-scenes story of the 312 days that changed America`s politics forever.\par \par \uc2 He takes us inside the campaigns of John F. Kennedy and \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  in the 1960 election. We`re very happy to say that Chris Wallace joins us in studio today.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Chris Wallace.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE: Well, thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I`m so glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 I love how you set up the two candidates. Nixon, an enigma, ambitious, paranoid, no-buddy-buddy boy.\par \par \uc2 John Kennedy, good-looking, articulate, connected with the voters. But he was young and inexperienced. So that`s how you set the scene between these two candidates. What do you want us to know off the bat about the two of them? And then I want to hit the countdown.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE: Well, it`s one of the most interesting elections. I was 13 years old when it happened, and it was the first election I was personally engaged in, watching the debates. It really revolutionized American politics. I think Kennedy ran the first modern election. He had his own personal pollster. His very rich family bought him his own private plane, The Caroline, named after his daughter, that he flew around the country. He used TV in ways that nobody ever had.\par \par \uc2 Nixon, a much darker, more complicated figure, but he had been the vice president for eight years to \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2 , who was a huge war hero and very popular politically. First presidential debate that was on television. 70 million people watched.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: 70 million. Listen, we would all kill for those numbers today. I mean, so you take us through the 312 days, and then we get down to the nitty and the gritty. 11 hours and 45 minutes. Landslide no longer possible for Kennedy. Nine hours and 30 minutes. Nixon says, "Doesn`t look good." One minute, Nixon concedes in a telegram.\par \par \uc2 This is where the fun begins, because many people thought that that -- and you say may have been election irregularities, and they encouraged him to challenge the results, and he did not.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE: Yeah, and that`s-- there are two reasons I wrote this book. One is it`s a great story. But two, I think it has tremendous relevance to the controversy over 2020 and to really the controversy that we`re living with right now.\par \par \uc2 I believe that the 1960 election-- all I`ll say is may really have been stolen. Tremendous election irregularities in Illinois. Richard J. Daley, the boss of the Democratic machine. Texas, Lyndon Johnson, who was the running mate. And Nixon was under considerable pressure to contest the election and decided not to.\par \par \uc2 So what you had is an election that may really have been stolen, and the "loser" sitting there deciding, "You know, we`re in the middle of a Cold War. We can`t not have a president while this is being adjudicated by the courts. For the good of the country, if not for me personally, "I am going to concede the election."\par \par \uc2 And, of course, that just sets everything that happened in 2020 on its head where there was no evidence of fraud and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , four years later is still talking about the election being stolen.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re also in a different media environment than we were in 1960. One of the values of studying history is to understand the range of what`s possible, and that informs what to expect going into 2024 here.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , according to a filing that came out just yesterday or two days ago, didn`t seem to care whether there was credible fraud in the election. He wanted the fight no matter what. And he`s talking about a fight that will continue after 2024`s election day. What are you prepared for now that you`ve dug into the history and you know what`s possible?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE^ Well, you know, I just think, you know, I`ve been covering this a long time and I`ve been following it even longer, and you know, the Republican Party I grew up in, watching the American political scene that I grew up watching, there were certain rules. The winner won, even if they won somewhat unfairly, the loser lost. For the good of the country, they observed the peaceful transition of power, and we went on.\par \par \uc2 And since 2020, that basic assumption has gone out the window. And you know, I think that real damage to the country because, I mean, what was it, just a year or so ago, there was a poll of all Republicans and 70 percent in 2023, three years after, saying still that they didn`t think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the legitimate president.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE^ What does that do to the fabric of a democracy when almost half the country doesn`t think the president really is the president?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So the media landscape, as Tony pointed out, has changed dramatically. You write in the book that in 1950, only 10 percent of the nation`s families had televisions. A decade later, 50 million.\par \par \uc2 Just talk to us about the dynamics of that debate. The first debate that was televised, the first television presidency, the makeup issue with Nixon and Kennedy, the look, the fact that Nixon was sick. There`s so much going on there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE^ Well, that`s right. And Nixon was absolutely confident. I mean, he`d been on the world stage. He had actually had a kind of informal debate or argument with Nikita Khrushchev, the head of the Soviet Union, back in 1959 at a trade exposition in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 So he thought, Jack Kennedy, this isn`t going to be a problem at all, and probably didn`t prepare enough for it. He also had suffered a serious staph infection. He was 10 pounds underweight. Kennedy comes in, he`s gotten rest, he`s gotten a tan, he looked robust. He also was wearing a dark blue suit, which contrasted with the gray walls. Nixon was 10 pounds underweight. When he saw that Kennedy wouldn`t take makeup, he didn`t need it. Nixon, who had a five o`clock shadow, refused to take makeup and looked terrible. And was wearing a gray suit that blended into the background.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, during the debate, we reported in the book Countdown 1960, that Richard J. Daley, who`s the mayor of Chicago and the head of the Democratic machine, looks at the TV in the middle of the debate and says, "My God, they embalmed him before he died."\par \par \uc2 So there`s no question that that debate may have lost the election for \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, and the radio audience had a different reaction than the TV audience. And now, of course, we have the social media audience, which is a whole other complication. You`ll have to come out with another book on that one.\par \par \uc2 Chris Wallace, thank you very much. "Countdown 1960.: I hope there`s not a Countdown 2024.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s available today wherever you like to buy your books. It`s a great one. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE^ Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Get excited because the return of the \uc2 NBA\uc2  season is right around the corner and players have been putting in a lot of hours working out during the offseason.\par \par \uc2 24-year-old Jared Butler of the \uc2 Washington Wizards\uc2  is among them, but you may be surprised to learn that his exercise goals stretch beyond the court.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Washington Wizards\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: On the court, \uc2 Washington Wizards\uc