at you`re talking about it.\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: Yeah, yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The Wizards have an employee engagement office which helps players achieve personal goals. And when they learned about Butler`s dream to learn to swim.\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE (Swim instructor): Nice to see you again.\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: So good to see you. They set him up with veteran swim instructor Dana LaPierre.\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: Down, down, down.\par \par \uc2 He was very hesitant.\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: I was like in the water and I was like, "Dana, my neck, like it`s just like tensing up. Like what is that?" You know, she`s like, "Oh, that`s anxiety. That`s fear right there."\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How do you feel at the beginning of a lesson when you first get here versus the end?\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: When I get here, I`m like, "Dana, I do not want to do this."\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: People in the water, they`re looking at us.\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: And I`m just sitting over there.\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: I`m like, "Can you stop saying that? Can you stop that?"\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: And by the time I leave, I`m like, "Dana, let me show you. Look, look, I can do this."\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: Right, right, move it, come on.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And he knows how to push forward through fear. At 18, he was diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a genetic condition where the heart walls can be thicker than normal, which he`s spoken about on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: I just have a big heart. I mean that figuratively and literally. When I finally felt like I got to a place in my career where I could and I felt confident enough to share about it, I was just like, "I got to." And I`m glad I did because people still to this day come up to me, asking me all the time about what to do, who to see, who to talk to.\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: Straight down to the hip bone. Touch the hip bone.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And now he`s speaking out about learning to swim as an adult in hopes of inspiring others.\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: I can`t believe it, honestly. I`m so proud.\par \par \uc2 I have all three girls and I say kudos to the Butler family because, yeah, amazing. He really is, honestly.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: He`s like the son you never had.\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: That`s so sweet.\par \par \uc2 DANA LAPIERRE: And if I have anything to do with it, I`m going to make it the best.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Michael Phelps, watch out.\par \par \uc2 JARED BUTLER: Oh, no. Do not do that. Nice.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, got the body for it.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yeah. Jared is just such a delight. And I really commend him for talking publicly about this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This is something that not a lot of people admit, even though it`s true that only a third of adults can swim the length of a pool. So, you know, there`s plenty of people who would like to learn to swim as an adult, but hopefully--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`re embarrassed to come forward. That`s why I agree with you. It`s so important that he`s doing it.\par \par \uc2 Somebody sitting at home right now says, I`m going to do that.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to learn, too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And he has one lesson left. Next lesson, he`s going in the deep end. So go, Jared.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, we`ll be cheering for you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Jared.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I love his teacher, too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yeah, she is awesome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I commend you for showing your basketball skills on camera. I love to work on those. If you like Adriana, see more of her in the third hour. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Time for our series, we call it "Kindness 101." I love this series. It`s where Steve Hartman and his adorable children share stories about, built around kindness and character and the people who`ve mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 We could all use a big old dose of that. Today`s lesson is about love. Steve and his kids introduce us to a couple who are proof that a full heart can help us weather even the stormiest of times.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. This is Meryl.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: And this is our lesson on love.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Emmett, scholars, poets, and preachers have all struggled to define this word, but I`m confident you`ll nail it. Just don`t say it`s the opposite of cooties.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: I`m defining love as that indefinable emotion that makes humans human.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That`s good. But is it a feeling or is it a connection? Is it a choice you make or is it something that just kind of happens to you?\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: It`s a connection that you have with someone that makes you feel like you can really trust them.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: It`s kind of a feeling that just comes naturally. You know, it`s not like you can control it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes, love is amorphous, but you know it when you see it. And I`ve got one story that I think illustrates love pretty well. It`s a bittersweet tale, but it`ll tell you everything you need to know about true love.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL (Alzheimer`s advocate): Oh, my gosh. Let`s find the picture.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Peter and Lisa Marshall of Andover, Connecticut, are paging through the most memorable day of their lives.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It looked like a lovely wedding.\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: It was. It was unforgettable.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But he`s forgotten it.\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: He has forgotten it.\par \par \uc2 Who`s this?\par \par \uc2 It`s the saddest part because you want to reminisce and you`re alone in the memory.\par \par \uc2 Redwing Blackbird.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: At the age of 53, Peter was diagnosed with early onset Alzheimer`s. Eventually, he not only forgot his wedding day --\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: He`s pretty, isn`t he?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He forgot his wife. Lisa became just another nameless caretaker, their entire history together permanently erased. And yet, a whisper of their love must have remained because Lisa says all of a sudden, he began courting her, as if they`d just started dating.\par \par \uc2 Until one day, a wedding scene came on TV. Peter pointed to the screen and said, "Let`s do it."\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: And I said, "Do what?" And he pointed again.\par \par \uc2 I said, "Do you want to get married?" And he got this grin on his face, and he said, "Yeah." So he fell in love with me again.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Lisa accepted his proposal and staged a wedding for her already husband.\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: I can`t even describe to you how magical it was. He was so present, and he was so happy. And it was very touching.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Peter, you may kiss your bride.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Lisa says Peter hadn`t been this lucid for weeks. Unfortunately, it was a Cinderella moment. The clock struck 12, and by the next morning, this wedding, too, was lost to the fog.\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But Lisa says she fully expected that.\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: I`m the one who`s going to remember that, and that`s going to help me heal later, because it really is a true love story.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Alzheimer`s can take away so much, but fortunately, love is almost always the last to go.\par \par \uc2 It`s been three years since we first told that story. Peter has since passed, and Lisa is now on a mission to help other couples living with Alzheimer`s. Lisa, welcome to "Kindness 101."\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: In wedding vows, you promise to stay together in sickness and in health. Why do you think that`s in there?\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: Because you don`t want to be a fair-weathered partner, and by loving someone and giving your full, whole self, you`re giving yourself a gift, because it feels so good to love someone else.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: There`s a saying that it`s better to have loved and lost than to never have loved at all. But as you know, loss can be really hard. Why is that saying true?\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: I believe that 100 percent. I`m very grateful for all the time that I got to spend with Peter, and I try to cherish those memories rather than the grief that I felt afterwards, and that`s really what helped me to stay kind and to stay loving and patient.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: When I meet someone someday, how will I know it`s love?\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: Oh, my goodness, you`ll be all twitterpated. Your heart will feel really full and happy and peaceful. I think peaceful is the right word.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Well, Lisa, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 LISA MARHSALL: Thank you. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Like Emmett said at the beginning, love is kind of indefinable. But if someday you find yourself caring for someone so deeply, it seems like there`s no word in the English language that could possibly explain your feelings. The word you`re looking for is love.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: And until then, stay kind.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No one can tell these stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always love these pieces.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No one can tell these stories like Steve Hartman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, it`s funny when they said got married twice, I thought it was going to be a story about a divorce and then they find each other again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Me too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is so much better than that. But I love the question about love. Sarah Blakely, who we all know of \uc2 Spanx\uc2  fame and now Sneex fame, when she was getting married and she said to her dad, "How do I know?" Her dad said, "Who you marry will be one of the most important decisions you`ll make in your life, but it should be the easiest."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, it shouldn`t be the, is it right? Is it not right? Do I have to hold you?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, it should feel right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I think what`s remarkable too, about Lisa. The story is incredible, but the fact that Peter fell in love with Lisa again - -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It says a lot about the soul that Lisa is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That you would make that same person who fell in love with you at the first time fall in love with you over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wasn`t prepared when Steve said, "Peter has since passed away." I was like, "Oh."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know, that hurts. That hurts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, that got to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Their love was so very real.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, cheering you on Lisa always. So here`s a question. You wish your kids could see these lessons in their school? Yeah, you do. Tell their teachers about Kindness101.com.\par \par \uc2 It`s a new site just for educators where they can access all the "Kindness 101` videos and professional lesson plans to go with each one. These things are knockout.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ahead, Amazon`s Sarah Gelman is here with her top book picks. There she is.\par \par \uc2 We`re honoring Hispanic Heritage Month. Stick around for that, but first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are celebrating National Hispanic Heritage Month here at CBS MORNINGS and today we are highlighting books that explore Hispanic culture and stories with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Books editorial director Sarah Gelman who`s joining us with her top recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, always a pleasure to have you.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN (Amazon Books editorial director): Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much for being here. So up first is a romance book with characters reuniting after decades apart. What do you like?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes, this is "A Dish Best Served Hot" by Natalie Cana. It`s actually the second in a series. It`s called "The Vega Family Series" but you do not need to have read the first one. But the third one is coming out later this month. So when you read this book and you love it, there`s more.\par \par \uc2 So this is two people that have been together in high school, Lola and Saint, and they`re reunited decades later because Saint`s family`s construction company is unknowingly going to shut down an unhoused youth center for LBGTQIA+ youths where Lola works.\par \par \uc2 And so they reunite. Obviously there`s friction. There`s a great cast of family characters that are along the side. And one of my favorite parts is there`s a buffalo wing eating contest, which I think more books should have this.\par \par \uc2 It`s a great book. It`s great if you love Lucy Score. I think a lot of people have read her books. Definitely check this one out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Next up, a memoir. A guest we had on the show, Wilmer Valderrama. I love this book.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad he made your cut.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you like about it? I thought this book was such a good read and such a great story.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yeah, the book is great. I mean, he is best known for his role as Fez on "That `70s Show" a huge hit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s called "An American Story."\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: "An American Story and Everyone`s Invited."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I don`t think a lot of people know when he was cast on that show, his family was on the verge of being evicted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: They had to borrow gas money to get him to his audition. And his career just exploded. And he talks about his family`s immigration from Venezuela and how hard it was. Even though he was a promising young actor, he had this thick accent.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: And no one wanted to cast him because he had the accent. So we learn about his screen career. He talks about "That `70s Show" "Encanto", "NCIS." But he really comes to life. And I think you probably saw this when you talked to him. When he talks about his work as a global ambassador for the US.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It is his passion. And the book is like, the title suggests, it`s a love letter to this country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And don`t say that he`s lucky. He worked really hard for this.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yeah, he did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a great moment where he`s standing on the steps of Cannes. And he goes, how did I go from Venezuela, not really speaking English, to here I am doing this?\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s really, I really, really enjoyed his work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s an incredible actor. But I think that the love letter to America in this book, everybody should read this to understand what the American dream really means to people like Wilmer.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: It`s a surprising book.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Let`s talk about "The Curse That Haunts a Family Living Near the Texas Border."\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: I love that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That sounds really, I don`t know, is it creepy or is it like --\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: No, it`s great. And it`s fun, too. So this is "Malas." It`s Marcela Fuentes. And this is one of my team`s favorite books so far this year. It made our list best books of the year so far. And it stars two women. Lulu is a rebellious teenager. She`s on the eve of her quinceanera. And then Pilar is an older, mysterious woman who shows up in the town.\par \par \uc2 And I love -- these are both very strong women. You mentioned they`re connected by a curse and a family secret. And I love -- this book is like an homage to Tejano culture, especially its music. You will read this book and want to put on Selena right away. You were warned.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t even need -- I love Selena anyway. But yes, okay.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: There you go. You should definitely read this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Malas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love a good thriller.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, last we have this thriller involving the fashion industry. Not usually a setting for thrillers.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Well, I think it is ripe for thrillers. Same thread, Lilian Rivera, This is her first adult novel. And it is spooky.\par \par \uc2 So Samara is a Cuban-American living in New Jersey. And she decides to leave the nest and follow her dream to an industrial town outside of L.A. to work at her dream job in the fashion industry. And she gets there. And there`s the stench of the slaughterhouse next door. There`s noises keeping her awake at night. And dreams that are haunting her. And it becomes clear that this is actually quite a nightmare and not her dream.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it every time you come. I want to read everything you recommend. And I just marvel how you do this with no freaking notes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, I posted on my \uc2 Instagram\uc2  a list of books --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know how you do it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I would say like 80 percent of them are Sara`s recommendations.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN: Well, now you have a couple more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She gives me flashbacks to book reports in school. It`s like you do every single student`s book report perfectly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Sarah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sarah Gelman, thank you very much. We love it. We`ll see you again soon.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dive" \uc2 Universal Music Operations Limited\uc2 /Island Records)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, "Mixtape Moments."\par \par \uc2 We`re going to introduce you to a soul pop artist named Olivia Dean. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Monday on CBS MORNINGS, it`s "Beg-Knows America" with an American foster care success story. Get ready to be inspired by\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Everybody`s favorite twins.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: She said when you make 2,5 nobody going to care that you are a foster child. So no one owes you anything so that sense of entitlement got to go.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And then she said foster care is a chapter not the story, remember that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Amen.\par \par \uc2 They turned adversity into advocacy for others using the life-changing lessons they learned to make a twin size impact on their community. I cannot wait for you to get to know everybody`s favorite twins, oh and by the way, we had a surprise for them. We will see you Monday.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now to our series "Mixtape Moments" that`s where we share a song from an emerging artist. I want to introduce you to soul pop artist her name is Olivia Dean the 25-year old released her debut album last year and this is her single "Dive."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dive" \uc2 Universal Music Operations Limited\uc2 /Island Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Dive" Dean says the song is about letting yourself love and be loved, check out more of her Olivia Dean and other songs and artists we feature here on our \uc2 Spotify\uc2  playlist, just search for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Mixtape on \uc2 Spotify\uc2  or scan the QR code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 I was at a conference in Italy this summer and she was at the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was wondering how you could like picked her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s how I said are you making a presentation, she said no I`m singing tonight. She sang that night. I said whoa, I went got everything she`s done. She`s a knockout. You go, Olivia Dean!\par \par \uc2 That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you on Monday. Let`s take a look back at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Did he lose the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH) Tim I`m focused on the future, did \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  censor Americans\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): That is a damning non-answer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The vice presidential nominees clash over former President Trump`s actions after the 2020 election at their only debate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Israel targets \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  with a new round of airstrikes after the Jewish state was hit by a barrage of missiles from Iran.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: This was an entire building now completely gone and all that`s left is this massive crater.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Breaking news this morning, we`re talking about that dock worker strike that threatened to cause severe damage to the U.S. economy. It has come to a temporary end.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: 25,000 dock workers at 14 ports along the eastern Gulf Coast can now return to work.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is a 500 pound bomb that was dropped by the United States during World War II and later buried underneath Miyazaki Airport. So like it`s one of those bombs that was dropped you know during the war and never detonated, and all of a sudden you`re sitting there looking out your window.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 RIVERS CUOMO (Vocalist, Weezer): His records were made here and a lot of classic rock albums were made here so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was just thinking about that as I was using the bathroom. I was like who`s used this urinal before me?\par \par \uc2 RIVERS CUOMO: That`s the thought I had too. It`s like he`s freely sat on this toilet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe the greatest regular season Mets victory in the history of baseball, the history of the \uc2 New York Mets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you like the Mets, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m a huge Mets fan. So the Mets entering yesterday had won just one game and in the eighth inning they were losing 3-0 and then lost the lead because the Mets always try to lose. They lost the lead in the bottom of the eighth and then they won it back. Greatest game of all time in a regular season.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: New York Liberty moved one step closer to the finals. The \uc2 Liberty\uc2  are now up two games to none. Toward the end, it looked like they were going to go into overtime and all I could think of was please don`t go into overtime. I got to get home to watch the debate, please.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the game the women come over and actually thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well not everybody.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not all 25,000 people --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Those people who got them good seats. Yeah, maybe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This 81-year-old man, his name is Arthur Schubarth. He imported sperm, tissue and testicles from a Marco Polo sheep and then successfully took that material and made a full living sheep out of it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like how you said he imported. I would love to see the image, the TSA image of the importation of the testicles.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Charlie Puth Show")\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: The project is an exaggerated take on my life and the constant pressure to stay in the spotlight or as they say in the zeitgeist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they said you are a zeitgeist adjacent.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Often times artists are maybe a little reluctant to let their music be used for a, I don`t know, erectile dysfunction. But if you are, you know, now you own it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that top of mind.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that comfortably numb?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not this guy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241004eka400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump Criminal Scheme; Trump Responds to Special Counsel; VP Candidates Hit the...\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Scott MacFarlane,  Robert Costa, Imtiaz Tyab, Skyler Henry, Holly Williams, Jo Ling Kent, Adam  Yamaguchi, Nikki Battiste, Manuel Bojorquez, Lisa Ling, Gabrielle Kerr \par \uc2 14,419 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Trump Criminal Scheme; Trump Responds to Special Counsel; VP Candidates Hit the Trail; Israel Continues Strikes Against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  ; Bank of America Glitch Causes Accounts to Show Zero Dollars; Biden Tours Hurricane Damage in North Carolina; Russia Using Glide Bombs to Attack Ukraine; How the Port Strike Could Impact You; Guilty Plea in Connection with Matthew Perry`s Death; The Depraved Heart Murder; Pink Floyd Sells Music Catalogue; Filings Unsealed; Are You Ready Kids?; Company Investigated Over Helene Deaths; Faith in Transition; "Priceless Facts About Money"; New Oprah Special About The Presleys; Should Kids Be Allowed at Weddings?; Charlie Puth Live, \uc2 CBS\uc2  ; Bank of America Glitch Causes Accounts to Show Zero Dollars; Biden Tours Hurricane Damage in North Carolina; Russia Using Glide Bombs to Attack Ukraine; How the Port Strike Could Impact You; Guilty Plea in Connection with Matthew Perry`s Death; The Depraved Heart Murder; Pink Floyd Sells Music Catalogue; Filings \par \par \uc2 Unsealed; Are You Ready Kids?; Company Investigated Over Helene Deaths; Faith in Transition; "Priceless Facts About Money"; New Oprah Special About The Presleys; Should Kids Be Allowed at Weddings?; Charlie Puth Live,\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love a sound of a trumpet on a Thursday, don`t you? Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: and I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today. We`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A judge unsealed Special Counsel Jack Smith`s latest case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , accusing the former president of committing crimes to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT FREDERICKSEN (Former Federal prosecutor): The exception is if the conduct is private and not in his capacity as president.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Israel launches more airstrikes against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  as fighting on the ground intensifies. We`re in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: An entire building now completely gone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A doctor pleads guilty to supplying actor, Matthew Perry with ketamine before his death.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: The chances of him prevailing at trial were not high.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  visiting parts of the southeast devastated by Helene.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Heading to Georgia and Florida today to see the damage in those states.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Voting is underway in the Annual Fat Bear Week contest.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): I have a dog in this bear fight, and it`s 747 Colbert. His nickname was inspired by the ear imperfection he shares with me.\par \par \uc2 The similarities don`t end there, because he also wrote a cookbook with my wife Evie.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA ("The Daily Show"): Both parties put so much effort into spinning the debate result, because it`s effective.\par \par \uc2 MARIA CARDONA , CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: JD Vance was very slick.\par \par \uc2 DONNY DEUTSCH, MSNBC POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: He was too slick.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2  (D-IL): He is a slick debater.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO): He looked shifty. He looked slick.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC GUEST: Yes, he was slick.\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: Slick, slick, slick, slick.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA: Thank God we have all these sharp political pundits to give us a broad array of opinions.\par \par \uc2 They called him slick so much that I couldn`t tell if \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  was debating JD Vance or Moo Deng.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I get it, because Moo Deng is slick.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is slick.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is all slick.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I got it, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, I got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, you got that, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I missed it. I was doing other things.\par \par \uc2 Actually, I`m into the news right now because we`re going to begin with what really are some stunning new quotes and material in Jack Smith`s criminal case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , involving his alleged scheme to stay in power after losing the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 We have new reporting on how it could affect this year`s presidential race and the aftermath as well. These newly unsealed filings, again by Special Counsel Jack Smith, include extensive evidence of a desperate effort to overturn the election and charges the former president denies all this, but the testimony is pretty striking.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is in Washington. He`s dug into it for us. He`s got a full report this morning.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump calls these filings pure election interference, but the special counsel argues, Trump launched a series of increasingly desperate plans to overturn the election, with only then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  standing in his way, a stance which put Pence`s life in danger.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The newly unsealed filing from Special Counsel Jack Smith alleges former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  resorted to crimes, focusing on his alleged pressure campaign on then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 According to the brief, at least nine times in the days after the election, Pence tried to convince Trump to give up his fight to prevent the certification saying he could run again next cycle, Trump allegedly replied "2024 is so far off."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I hope Mike is going to do the right thing. I hope so. I hope so, because if \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  does the right thing, we win the election.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And when Trump was warned during the attack on January 6th that Mike Pence`s life was in danger, Trump allegedly responded, "So what?"\par \par \uc2 Smith also alleges when Trump`s advisers urged him to issue a calming message to stop the riot, he refused.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  .")\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But instead, Trump issued a tweet at 2:24 PM attacking pence. A minute later, Secret Service evacuated Pence to a secure location.\par \par \uc2 The document also alleges Trump allies sought to create chaos, including one unnamed Trump supporter who, shortly after the election, was encouraging protests, even to "make them riot."\par \par \uc2 Throughout the 165-page brief, the special counsel argues Trump was not acting in an official capacity.\par \par \uc2 The new filing comes as Smith tries to salvage the case and keep it headed toward trial following the Supreme Court`s presidential immunity decision in July.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT FREDERICKSEN: The brief attempts to outline all of the evidence the prosecutor has to show that the actions of the former president were in his capacity as a private candidate for president, not in his capacity as president.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump responded to this filing saying the prosecution`s argument is falsehood-ridden, as well as unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 This case will not go to trial before the election, and it will likely be shut down if Trump is elected in November -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Scott, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on this, let`s bring in our chief election and campaign correspondent, Robert Costa who is on the trail in Michigan today.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you. What are you? What are you now hearing from the Trump campaign about these new revelations?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good to be with you. Sources close to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tell me he is furious about Jack Smith`s latest filing, and he firmly believes, and he is telling this to confidantes privately, that if he is defeated in November, he will believe the Justice Department and Democrats have been aligned against him and steal the election from him.\par \par \uc2 That raises serious questions about American democracy and about what could happen in the post-election period if there`s another contested fight.\par \par \uc2 Trump, for now, says he as ever, that he has done nothing wrong in the 2020 election and how he fought that election. That`s likely a refrain we will hear later today here in battleground Michigan. But yesterday, it was his running mate making the case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH) (2024 vice presidential nominee): We`ve got a hell of a crowd in the state of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Fresh off the vice presidential debate, Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  turned his attention back to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): It`s always good when you work in sales to have a good product to sell. And unfortunately for \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  , he has got the crappiest product to sell in the entire United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And during a bus tour of Pennsylvania, Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  raised fundamental questions about the Republican ticket.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): You can`t rewrite history and trying to mislead us about Donald Trump`s record. That`s gaslighting. That`s gaslighting.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trying to capitalize on what they saw as a key moment in the vice presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 The Harris walls campaign unveiled this new ad.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Did he lose the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Tim, I`m focused on the future.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): That is a damning non-answer.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Walz says Vance`s response is an alarm for democracy.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I think in the midst of this, you saw something very clear. It is disqualifying to not acknowledge that the 2020 election was won by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . It`s as simple as that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Vance pushed back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): The media is obsessed with talking about the election of four years ago. I`m focused on the election of 33 days from now because I want to throw \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  out of office.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: As for Harris, she surveyed the impact of Hurricane Helene in Georgia on Wednesday, passing out food in Augusta.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): These are very difficult times, and in a moment of crisis, I think that really does bring out the best of who we are.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Vice President Harris will be back on the campaign trail today in Wisconsin, in a town known as the birthplace for the Republican Party. She`ll be joined by former Wisconsin Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  , who has endorsed her as part of Harris` effort to reach out to traditional Republicans who are wary of Trump -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the Middle East, where the Jewish holiday of Rosh Hashanah has not meant a break in the violence. An Israeli airstrike on Beirut killed seven \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  members, and the Israeli military says it killed 15 of the group`s fighters in Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab has more from Beirut.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Israeli soldiers on the ground in southern Lebanon in this military video. At least eight were killed during an incursion on Wednesday, the deadliest day in nearly a year of battles with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters.\par \par \uc2 It follows Iran`s ballistic missile attack on Israel that has the region bracing for further escalation.\par \par \uc2 (Explosion)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Overnight, another devastating Israeli airstrike in Central Beirut, where at least seven people were killed, and in Dahieh, a suburb that is the seat of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  `s power, more massive Israeli bombings.\par \par \uc2 Some parts are now in total ruins following Israel`s assassination of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader, \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  here nearly a week ago.\par \par \uc2 This is all that`s left of one residential building.\par \par \uc2 The scale of the devastation caused by this Israeli airstrike is just breathtaking.\par \par \uc2 This was an entire building now completely gone, and all that`s left is this massive crater.\par \par \uc2 A once bustling neighborhood and home to hundreds of thousands, Dahieh is now a ghost town.\par \par \uc2 We were only given access as part of a large media tour organized by the militant group. At one point, this man started shouting --\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "We will not surrender. We follow Nasrallah."\par \par \uc2 Of the few residents who remain, there is a sense of fear and defiance.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: This man told us, "We are all Nasrallah. If Israel is fighting one person, they are now fighting an entire nation."\par \par \uc2 And in Gaza where the fighting has raged on for nearly a full year, more death. At least 80 Palestinians were killed in multiple Israeli strikes, including many children.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The fear now is the humanitarian catastrophe in Gaza is potentially on its way here to Lebanon, where the death toll here overnight is at least 46 people killed in Israeli strikes as President Biden appeals rather as President Biden`s appeals for a ceasefire appear to be getting nowhere -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Imtiaz Tyab in Lebanon, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got an update today on \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  `s very frightening online glitch that panicked thousands of customers yesterday. This morning, the bank says the problems have all been fixed.\par \par \uc2 There were more than 20,000 individual reports of customers having trouble with their accounts. Many of them say that the bank`s website showed they had a zero balance. Yikes. Imagine that for just a second.\par \par \uc2 The online issues began around noon, Eastern Time, and spiked about one hour later. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  has since apologized to the customers for any inconvenience. It is still unclear what caused the outages. The good news is, guys, that they`ve worked it all out.\par \par \uc2 I was at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  yesterday for something. I saw a lady in the lobby going, "What`s going on here?" And the guy was saying, "Your money is safe. Your money is safe." And I thought, "God, that`s a strange conversation."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now we know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, we know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That she probably looked at her ATM and was like "uh."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Your money is safe."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t want to be nosy nelly and go what`s happening here? But I was thinking, "Wow, that`s weird."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Wow. That is some scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, we know. Everything is good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, to the cleanup around Hurricane Helene, some scary stuff there as well.\par \par \uc2 President Biden visited parts of the Carolinas yesterday to see the scale of the destruction in that part of our world. The storm hit nearly a week ago, now blamed for more than 180 fatalities across six states; and survivors, they are running short on just about everything.\par \par \uc2 Skyler Henry is in Asheville, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: During his visit to the southeast Wednesday, President Biden surveyed some of the worst hit parts of western North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Hurricane Helene has been a storm, literally, of historic proportions.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: In the aftermath, survivors continue to cope with limited electricity, food, and water.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to Adam Norton (sp?), who is bottling river water to use at home.\par \par \uc2 ADAM NORTON (Local resident): You`ve got to do what you you`ve got to do. I mean, use this to flush your toilet, drink a bottle of water.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Business owners in the area have also been blindsided by the scale of the disaster.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN KING (Local business owner): It`s just, this is catastrophic --\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Bryan and Angela King are the owners of the now heavily damaged 12 Bone Smokehouse in the once vibrant river arts district of Asheville.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN KING: I can`t overstate how much worse this is than anything that`s ever, ever happened.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: So far, nearly 90 people have been confirmed dead statewide, with many still unaccounted for.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Meidinger`s mother, Kim Ashby is still missing after her home in Elk Park crumbled into a nearby river during the storm.\par \par \uc2 She and her husband, Rod, used debris to float down the river until they got knocked off.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MEIDINGER (Mother is still missing): They hit some sort of patch of trees or debris or something, and that`s when they actually collided, and they separated at that point and fell into the river, and that was the last that he saw of her.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Rod was able to escape.\par \par \uc2 Brownie Newman, chairman of the Buncombe County Board of Commissioners, says the region has been shaken by so much loss.\par \par \uc2 What`s that process been like in terms of working with search and rescue crews?\par \par \uc2 BROWNIE NEWMAN (Buncombe County Board of Commissioners chairman): Very painful, you know, it`s just like we have -- you know, we have these first responders who are working so hard and, you know, and they all live here, too.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: President Biden has deployed up to 1,000 active duty troops to help reinforce North Carolina`s National Guard. He also added that dozens of those Starlink satellite services have been deployed in the region as well to help restore communication services impacted by Helene -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Skyler Henry, our eyes on the ground there in Asheville, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In our next hour, we`re going to continue this coverage and visit a factory in Tennessee, where survivors say they were told not to leave, even as the water rose all around them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Turning now to the war in Ukraine, Russia is making new gains in the east of the country, helped by a weapon called a glide bomb.\par \par \uc2 Local officials say one of those bombs hit this apartment building in the city of Kharkiv last night, they`re also being used on the battlefield. Holly Williams visited a center where Ukraine`s military is trying to track them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The Russians have started using cheaply produced glide bombs to terrorize Kharkiv and other Ukrainian cities. They use old Soviet era ammunition, some with over a ton of explosives retrofitted with wings and GPS.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: At this monitoring center, a Special Forces detachment of the Ukrainian Border Guard is scouring the skies for the Russian aircraft that launched the glide bombs and warning those in the line of fire.\par \par \uc2 At a different location, Colonel Maxim Balagura told us the glide glide bombs have changed the war, sapping the motivation of Ukrainian soldiers on the front line.\par \par \uc2 So, the Russians have got smarter?\par \par \uc2 (COLONEL MAXIM BALAGURA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "Of course," he told us. "One can never underestimate the enemy."\par \par \uc2 Russia is now occupying around 20 percent of Ukraine, capturing Avdiivka in February, Vuhledar this week, and closing in on Pokrovsk, a Ukrainian logistics hub in the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: By some estimates, the Russians are gaining ground faster now than at any time since the early stages of the war in 2022.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Around 100,000 Russian soldiers have been killed in the invasion, according to US officials.\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  doesn`t seem to mind, with the population over three times the size of Ukraine`s, Russia is planning to draft more than 130,000 extra soldiers by January.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: This is the apartment building that was hit last night, apparently, by a relatively small glide bomb. The edge of this city is now only around 12 miles from the frontline -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, thank you, Holly Williams reporting from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, how the port strike could affect how much you pay for your groceries if it drags on.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:16, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, this morning in our "State of Spirituality Series," Lisa Ling meets a pastor who likely wouldn`t be allowed to preach in many US churches.\par \par \uc2 Plus, singer songwriter, Charlie Puth says becoming a pop star can be kind of a joke.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "See You Again"/Charlie Puth/Atlanta Records)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ll talk with him about his new mockumentary TV series and the real music stars who are part of it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Charlie Puth Show"/ROKU)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS sponsored by \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2  . \uc2 Progressive Insurance\uc2  offers 24/7 protection when you bundle home, auto, and motorcycle.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- effects of the dockworkers strike now in Day 3. Twenty- five thousand dock workers have shut down ports from Boston to Houston. No deal in sight.\par \par \uc2 Senior business and tech correspondent, Jo Ling Kent is tracking what this could all mean for shoppers.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 There has been some relief on inflation over the last few months, which has been good for consumers, but now there are new concerns that this port strike could drive the prices of food, cars and other goods back up if it goes on for weeks.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about bananas, cherries, and chocolate. Those could be the first items affected. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says if this strike lasts more than a week, retailers that sell clothing, furniture, and home appliances could be hit as well.\par \par \uc2 And you could also see price increases and shortages for that critical holiday shopping season. And some big retailers have been preparing for this basically.\par \par \uc2 Costco\uc2  , for example, said on its recent earnings call that the company has pre-shipped to get holiday goods into the United States ahead of the strike. But you know, small and medium sized businesses may not have the resources, and they may not be as lucky -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The union workers seem pretty dug in. The president said, no automation while he`s alive, basically over my dead body. So how close are they really to resolving this?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Unfortunately, they are not close to a resolution. The union president has said he`s willing to shut down the ports as long as necessary, even at the cost of the US economy.\par \par \uc2 Now, the group representing the ports and ocean carriers said earlier that it increased its offer, but apparently hasn`t met all of the union demands, which include more money, a bigger slice of shipping company profits, as well as commitments to limit that automation we`ve been talking about and guarantee jobs -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jo Ling, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, in just a moment, a new guilty plea in the investigation of the overdose death of "Friends" star, Matthew Perry.\par \par \uc2 Local news, though, coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 This is amazing video of a rare type of solar eclipse known as a ring of fire, that`s from --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- Johnny Cash --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Johnny Cash.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- where the moon doesn`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Johnny Cash.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- where the moon quite block out the sun. Some people went to watch it on a remote, Easter Island that`s in the Pacific Ocean, west of South America.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday`s eclipse could be seen in in the South Pacific, Chile and Argentina. Only about 175,000 people lived in the path of the eclipse.\par \par \uc2 Here in the United States, \uc2 NASA\uc2  says there won`t be another solar eclipse for more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kind of looks like a dragon eye out there like, how do we know it`s not a dragon eye?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is so cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It could be a dragon eye as far as I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Might be, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so exciting to see it, if you ever get the chance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to turn now to new developments in the Matthew Perry case.\par \par \uc2 One of the doctors charged in connection with the overdose death of the very popular "Friends" star has now pleaded guilty.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors allege that five people were involved in taking advantage of Matthew Perry before he died in October of last year.\par \par \uc2 Adam Yamaguchi was in the Los Angeles courtroom and has our report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Dr. Mark Chavez left court in a hurry Wednesday after pleading guilty to illegally obtaining the powerful anesthetic, ketamine that was administered to Matthew Perry close to one year after the beloved actor was found dead in his home`s hot tub.\par \par \uc2 Chavez admitted he used a fraudulent prescription and sold 22 vials of ketamine, as well as ketamine lozenges.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Matthew right here first.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Perry was open about his battle with addiction, and had been using ketamine off label legally for depression, but prosecutors allege when Perry`s doctor refused to provide more, the actor turned to Dr. Salvador Plasencia, who allegedly asked Dr. Chavez for the drug.\par \par \uc2 Court documents show Plasencia texted Chavez in the month before Perry`s death, saying, "I wonder how much this moron will pay."\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: Dr. Chavez is not at the top, so what the government wants from him is ultimately to cooperate against the female defendant, who is called "the ketamine queen."\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Investigators allege, Jasveen Sangha run a Los Angeles drug ring and supplied the doses that killed Perry.\par \par \uc2 Both Plasencia and Sangha have pleaded not guilty and are awaiting trial.\par \par \uc2 Perry`s assistant who found him dead and a messenger have already pleaded guilty. They, along with Dr. Chavez will be sentenced next year and could face prison time.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: Only in recent times that we have come to understand that addiction is truly a disease, and if doctors take advantage of the vulnerable, and they are responsible, people whose oath is "do no harm," those people are going to be punished severely.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Adam Yamaguchi in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know what, guys, it is that "moron" line that gets me every time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The doctor saying, "I wonder how much this moron would pay," considering how highly thought of Matthew Perry was and what he was going through and they knew this. So many people let him down.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right, and as Rikki said --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s really upsetting to hear.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- their first oath is "do no harm."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s unbelievable what they did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Exploiting what appears to be a very vulnerable human being.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they knew that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, coming up, "48 Hours" investigates the death of a former beauty queen. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: I`m Nikki Battiste, "48 Hours."\par \par \uc2 A young woman dies after overdosing on surgical drugs. Her doctor boyfriend faces an unusual charge, depraved heart murder.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This week`s "48 Hours" digs into the case of a former beauty queen who died with drugs in her body.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Sarah Harris was found unresponsive at the Maryland home she shared with her boyfriend, Dr. James Ryan. Ryan, an oral surgeon, told police it was an overdose, but her mother believes Sarah was murdered and the scene looked suspicious.\par \par \uc2 "48 Hours" contributor, Nikki Battiste explains why.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It was January 26, 2022 about 7:00 AM when police with body cameras discovered 25-year-old Sarah Harris at home with her 48-year-old boyfriend, Dr. James Ryan, who told them he`d last seen her the night before.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: All right, you just found her unresponsive?\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMES RYAN (Sarah Harris` boyfriend): Yes. I pulled her onto the floor to start CPR.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Ryan said Sarah struggled with depression and drugs, that she had worked for him and sometimes stole from his office, where he kept the surgical anesthetics, ketamine and propofol, both found in her body.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Did she do any drugs yesterday while you`re around her?\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMES RYAN: Not when I was around her.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Authorities at the scene accepted Dr. Ryan`s account, but Sarah`s mother, Tina Harris soon arrived, already suspicious Dr. Ryan was behind her daughter`s death.\par \par \uc2 TINA HARRIS (Sarah Harris` mother): And I went towards James and attacked him. I started kicking and hitting him.\par \par \uc2 I kept screaming at him.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Sarah`s sister, Rachel soon unearthed text messages between Sarah and Dr. Ryan, suggesting he brought her the drugs, and just weeks before she died, even dosed her himself.\par \par \uc2 "If you wake up, I just went to change after I gave you ketamine just now," he writes.\par \par \uc2 IAN IACAVELO (Retired detective): He is injecting her while she`s asleep.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Detective Ian Iacavelo (sp?), who specialized in pharmaceutical cases before retiring last year, says first responders may not have understood what they were looking at.\par \par \uc2 He says cops usually see street drugs, not surgical medication and supplies like Sarah had.\par \par \uc2 IAN IACAVELO: Usually what we see is burnt spoons, tin foil, not medical grade drugs.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: On March, 22, 2022, James Ryan was arrested and prosecutors charged him with a crime known as depraved heart murder. He pled not guilty, but assistant state`s attorney Jennifer Harrison says Ryan showed a reckless disregard for human life.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HARRISON (Assistant state`s attorney): The act of giving her the drugs is handing her a loaded gun.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nikki Battiste joins us now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Nikki, why would Dr. Ryan have killed Sarah Harris? What`s the motive?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It`s a good question. There really is no obvious motive in this case, and there`s really no conclusive evidence he even wanted Sarah Harris dead, but as a doctor, he`s an oral surgeon, he had to see that she was wasting away, and prosecutors argue he wanted to use those anesthetic drugs to control her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What can you tell us about Sarah`s sister, who the authorities say was very critical to this case?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: It is remarkable. Rachel Harris really was the first investigator on this case. Everyone told us that without her, there wouldn`t have been a murder charge, and this probably would have never gone to trial.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mom seemed very suspicious from the word go. Did she ever try, prior to that moment, to get her daughter away from Dr. Ryan?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: She tried a couple times to get Sarah away. She kept asking her leave, and Sarah threatened to cut her off. Like any mom, she didn`t want to lose her, but in the end, that is exactly what happened, sadly.\par \par \uc2 I`ve covered many, many criminal cases. I`ve never covered one like this. It`s incredibly unique, using these anesthetic drugs. And this charge, I`ve never heard of depraved heart murder.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all looked at each other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all had a reaction to that, yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Everyone should be tuning Saturday night to find out what exactly that means, how it applies in this case. It`s really fascinating.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, yes, you got me hooked, Nikki. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 You can catch Nikki`s report, "The Depraved Heart Murder" on "48 Hours" this Saturday night at 10/9 Central right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and, of course, streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, the stories you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." But right now, it is 7:41, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot coming up at eight o`clock, including the "State of Spirituality" with Lisa Ling. Today, she meets a pastor who has not lost faith, even though many churches preach that he shouldn`t even exist.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Charlie Puth Show"/ROKU)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, Grammy nominated musician -- nobody sounds like this guy -- Charlie Puth is here to talk about his new job in reality TV, except all is not as it seems. He`ll be here to explain. He sings and -- is this an act? We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 Also, \uc2 Mellody Hobson\uc2  has just arrived talking about a children`s book about money. We can all learn something from that.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up next, but right now, Klaus and I heading are over to the table.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming over to the Dark Side of the Moon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, a little Pink Floyd reference. I got it. I got it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, yes, Klaus. You know that. I saw you. I see you, Klaus.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a clue.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a clue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." What band is this?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my gosh, one of the greatest rock and roll bands of all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is it, Vlad? Who is it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am talking about Pink Floyd.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the record, Dear Viewers at home, I knew that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, he did. He actually did know that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re all "Comfortably Numb" here as we begin to talk about one of this great rock bands that are very comfortable right now after selling their music rights for guess what? $400 million.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Pink Floyd Pulse Live at Earls Court/Sony Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, Gilmore./ "Variety" reports, Pink Floyd has agreed to sell the rights to its recorded music, name, and likeness to \uc2 Sony Music\uc2  . The band`s albums include of course, "Dark Side of the Moon," "If She Were Here," "The Wall."\par \par \uc2 Pink Floyd, folks, has sold more than 250 million copies worldwide. This follows a trend of other artists selling their catalogue or publishing rights for big bucks.\par \par \uc2 Sony Music\uc2  has spent more than a billion dollars purchasing rights from other stars like Bruce Springsteen and Bob Dylan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Must be working for them if they keep spending that much money for it. It must be paying off, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just think about it, right? They are now able to use the music and the likeness and all of what they`re purchasing in any capacity that they choose. And oftentimes, artists are maybe a little reluctant to let their music be used for a, I don`t know, erectile dysfunction, but if you are, you know now you own it --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is that top of mind?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that "Comfortably Numb"?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, for some people, not this guy. Not this guy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Alive and kicking. Alive and kicking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pelvic interruption.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Alive and kicking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The screen goes to Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, the bars come up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The cuckoo clock. Technical difficulties.\par \par \uc2 All right, that`s good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, here we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING:. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: An unexpected explosion caught on camera. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 This is a 500 pound bomb that was dropped by the United States during World War Two and later buried underneath Miyazaki Airport in Southwest Japan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Airport officials say no one was hurt, but more than 80 flights had to be canceled.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Officials say the explosion left a crater 23 feet wide and more than three feet deep. So like it`s one of those bombs that was dropped, you know, during the war and never detonated, and all of a sudden you`re sitting there looking out your window.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have some news from our friends at Nickelodeon. Voting is now open for Nick`s Kids Pick the President. It`s an election year tradition that started way back in 1988. Kids can go online and decide who they think should be in the White House. They can submit their vote at KidsPickThePresident.com and can even download an "I Voted" badge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always like this content.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is very cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s so cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It ends on October 28th where the Kids Pick the President`s Special on Nickelodeon, which will be hosted by our very own, Nate Burleson --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- and his 14-year-old daughter, Mia.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is knockout, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, we are so excited. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s great. I love it when they do things together.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is so cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are so good together.\par \par \uc2 Nickelodeon, like \uc2 CBS\uc2  , is part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 So this is really interesting. I mean, kids have a very, very strong track record.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, so Gayle, seven out of eight times since 88 they have picked the popular vote winner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really? Seven out of eight?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Seven out of eight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Popular vote.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The popular vote.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Popular vote, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Popular vote.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I`ll be watching. We will be watching Nate and Mia. Can`t wait to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, a judge in Boston is set to decide the future of a young beaver. The two-year-old named Nibi was rescued from a roadside as a baby. Look at little Nibi. My gosh, she`s so cute.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is cute.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Rescuers are trying to keep her at a wildlife rescue center. A state agency says it`s time for Nibi to be released in the wild. A court hearing is set to be held tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know I cared about beaver so much, but this is so upsetting to me, because the preserve is arguing, if she goes back to the wild, she will not survive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they`ve raised her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They have raised her the whole time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she is very happy where she is. I don`t understand A -- why the courts are involved and why this has become an issue. Leave Nibi alone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So they have raised petitions and all sorts of things.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. I`m all for wildlife being in their natural habitat, but I think this is a circumstance where she`s been in captivity for since she was a little baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Keep in mind, they didn`t kidnap her. She was found on the road.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That she was found on the side of the road.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She was found --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On a busy street and was going to get killed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rescued.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think there are competing claims of what humane treatment is here, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because the folks who -- the zoo folks or the captivity folks are saying they`ve tried to find her a mate or a playmate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they haven`t been able to.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is lonely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, they have tried.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They have tried to find her mate because they do mate beavers for --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you remember when Anderson Cooper did the piece about trying to rewild gorillas?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I forget. Did not go well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It didn`t --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is very hard to reintroduce --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In fact, people who say that she should stay in captivity believe she`ll be dead within a few days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that`s a legitimate concern.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Leave Nibi alone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Leave Nibi alone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, Lisa Ling talks spirituality with a pastor trying to expand inclusivity in the Christian church.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "We Don`t Talk Anymore"/Charlie Puth/\uc2 Atlantic Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know that song? That`s Charlie Puth`s hit song "We Don`t Talk Anymore." Luckily for us, the pop star is talking to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, he`ll tell us about his new TV show and his upcoming album.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King coming up in this eight o`clock hour. The state of spirituality with Lisa Ling.\par \par \uc2 She meets a pastor whose devotion has not changed even as others preach hate against millions just like him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil, businesswoman \uc2 Mellody Hobson\uc2  says it is never too early to learn financial literacy. Why she is sharing that message with kids.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. We`re so excited because we will be joined by Grammy nominated musician Charlie Puth, who`s starring in a new reality show with a twist with some big name cameos.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Big name cameos. What a great voice has got. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from January 6, 2021)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  : We will never give up. We will never concede.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A newly unsealed filing accuses former President Trump of resorting to crimes in a desperate effort to stay in power after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump responded to this filing, saying the prosecution`s argument is falsehood ridden as well as unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Israel targets \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  with a new round of airstrikes after the Jewish state was hit by a barrage of missiles from Iran.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: President Biden visits the Carolinas to survey the hurricane devastation and pledge more support.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Hurricane Helene has been a storm, really, of historic proportions.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Spongebob Squarepants" Paramount)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And get ready. The makers of Spongebob Squarepants celebrate the show`s 25th anniversary with a chance to eat the most sought after recipe in Bikini Bottom. You know it. It`s that Krabby Patty.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Spongebob Squarepants" Paramount)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who doesn`t love the Krabby Patty?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know the Krabby Patty?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Even I know it. Teddy probably should not be watching it already, but he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Already he is. We`re going to begin this hour, though, with a shocking story from workers who survived Hurricane Helene`s flooding in East Tennessee. The rising waters overtook a plastic factory and swept away nearly a dozen employees. At least two of them have died, and four are still missing.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez reports an investigation is now underway because the survivors say they were told to stay on the job as the dangers grew.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Landon Duckett, Storyful)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Robert Jarvis)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: This is video Robert Jarvis says he took last Friday as water started to rise outside the Impact Plastics Factory in Tennessee, where he worked.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT JARVIS (Worker, Impact Plastics Factory): Within, like, three minutes. It went from here to about here.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Despite the threat of flooding, Jarvis says his manager initially told employees they could not leave. He says it wasn`t until the plant lost power and the water kept rising they were finally told to go home.\par \par \uc2 What were the conditions like?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Jacob Ingram)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT JARVIS: It was water was up my waist. Everybody was in pure panic mode, not knowing what to do. We had no evacuation plan whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The AP reports four of his co workers are missing after a truck they were in tipped over in the raging floodwaters. Two employees have been confirmed dead. This video shows one person holding onto spools of yellow pipes waiting to be rescued. Days after the flooding, families of the missing are furious.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The families of Lydia and Rosa still have no answers. Search and rescue has not been clear. They are Tennesseans, too. They need answers. Their families deserve respect.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT JARVIS: Innocent people, they shouldn`t have died and died. I mean, these people were like my family. We work together every day, and I just it was senseless.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: In a statement, Impact Plastics said "At no time were employees told that they would be fired if they left the facility."\par \par \uc2 The company has said that no one was told they couldn`t leave.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT JARVIS: That`s a lie. That`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And the company said yesterday it not yet been contacted by authorities, but that it would fully cooperate with the investigation. As for the families of those who are still missing, they are so desperate for answers. Yesterday, they begged if they themselves could become part of the search. But authorities say, given the debris and the road conditions, it`s simply not safe. Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A powerful set of interviews and reporting there, Manny. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The company saying we didn`t tell people they`d be fired if they left. But it seems like people felt that pressure nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Robert Jarvis saying that people shouldn`t have had to die in this scenario is very damning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They felt some kind of pressure. Otherwise, they would have left.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Awful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Ahead, our spirituality series meets a pastor who`s on the leading edge of changes within Christianity, how he`s creating a more welcoming church community. Plus Charlie Puth. He`s here to talk about his new and very funny TV show, which includes cameos from some of the biggest names in entertainment.\par \par \uc2 Which one`s Charlie in that shot?\par \par \uc2 But first it is 8.04, 8.05. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up, our friend \uc2 Mellody Hobson\uc2  is a pioneer in the financial world and she`s here to share a powerful message for kids. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back this morning with a new installment of our series, "The State of Spirituality" with Lisa Ling, which highlights Americans on unique journeys of faith.\par \par \uc2 Many traditional religions are trying to make progress toward being more inclusive of the LGBTQ+ community. But for some groups, it`s been a very slow journey on that front.\par \par \uc2 Today, Lisa introduces us to a pastor in California who wouldn`t be welcome to preach in many American churches.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER (Hope Lutheran Church pastor): Each player spins a spinner.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Drew Stever says his life is pretty average.\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: We play board games and we garden and we make art and we have to remind our kids 12 times to pick up the socks in their bedroom.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: He and his partner, Hazel, live in Southern California with their three kids and two dogs.\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: Hazel always tells me like I`m her boring white guy.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: But you haven`t always been a boring white guy.\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: No, I haven`t always been.\par \par \uc2 Jesus is asking the people around him, where do you stand?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Stever is a transgender man and he`s also an ordained minister in the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America.\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: Do we stand on the side of fear and hate or do we stand on the side of curiosity and awe? -\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Some Christian denominations have evolved to be more inclusive of the LGBTQ+ community, but the acceptance of trans clergy has been much slower. According to \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  , the majority of Christians still feel whether a person is a man or a woman is determined by their sex assigned at birth.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that the Lutheran Church is a safe place for queer people?\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: It is trying so hard.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: In 2015, the Lutheran Church ordained its first trans priest. Trans clergy also serve in other denominations, but most still forbid LGBTQ+ clergy.\par \par \uc2 Because there are a lot of people who say that according to the Bible, God made man and woman and that couldn`t be more clearly defined. How do you respond to them?\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: It`s hard to relate it to modern day times because it wasn`t written for 2024, it was written for then. When we read in the scripture that God created man and woman, yes, and God created everyone else as well.\par \par \uc2 MAN (Churchgoer): Pastor Drew is a good pastor and I think he focuses on Jesus` commands that we should love God and love our neighbor as ourselves.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: At Stever`s home church, Hope Lutheran in Hollywood, congregants told us when he says all are welcome, he really means it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Churchgoer): Pastor Drew just brings such a light in life and just encapsulates everything we`ve always believed in and learning to really spread a message that is just love.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Born and raised Lutheran in Minnesota`s Twin Cities, growing up, Stever also went to Catholic school, attending church several days a week.\par \par \uc2 Was there a certain point in your upbringing where you just didn`t feel right in your body?\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: I grew up female and in the female context and I just never felt like that worked for me.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Throughout his childhood, Stever says, he felt a lot of anger due to his gender dysphoria.\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: There were definitely times where like, if I can`t exist in one way, I don`t want to exist at all. I would just be better off not alive.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Stever credits a homeroom teacher for first introducing him to a more compassionate Christian theology, accepting of everyone. This eventually helped him realize he was being called to become a pastor.\par \par \uc2 You went into seminary as a woman. How did you come out of seminary?\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: Not a woman.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It was summer 2016 and leading up to the election, anti-trans rhetoric was ramping up. But Stever says he could no longer deny who he`s always been.\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: There was a moment where I woke up in the morning, and the words that came to my mind was, you don`t have to be angry anymore. And I almost felt like God just took a snowball and just threw it at my face.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: So you think that voice was God?\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: I think so, yeah.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: But after Stever transitioned, he began receiving hate, mostly online.\par \par \uc2 Do you have concerns about going public in this way?\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: I mean, yeah, I have concerns. My family`s safety is probably my top priority. And I believe that for those who need to hear, will hear.\par \par \uc2 HAZEL REBECCA SALAZAR-DAVIDSON (Hope Lutheran Church pastor): Folks want to just cookie cutter everything.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Stever`s spouse, Hazel, who is also a Lutheran pastor, knows firsthand the discrimination many can face in church.\par \par \uc2 HAZEL REBECCA SALAZAR-DAVIDSON: There`s a lot of people in the Christian faith who don`t even believe women can be pastors.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And you two are kind of a double whammy.\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: I know.\par \par \uc2 HAZEL REBECCA SALAZAR-DAVIDSON: I know, we don`t mean to be. We`re actually really quite boring.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And Stever plans to keep being boring. But along the way, he also hopes that just by being himself, he can help more people feel seen and safe in church.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to those who may have felt burned by church and by religion?\par \par \uc2 DREW STEVER: My first response is I`m so sorry. I`m so sorry that the church missed you. I would say specifically to folks of color, to people living with disabilities, people who are LGBTQ, you are good. Nothing is wrong with you. You are so good. And you don`t need the church to tell you that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Pastor Drew has had a pride flag hanging outside of Hope Lutheran. And shortly after we filmed there, someone ripped down the flag and damaged it. The flag, though, is back up. And he told us he is, quote, "not deterred by one person`s small worldview."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that`s the way to look at it. I mean, one person did that. And the community, though, says otherwise.\par \par \uc2 It`s interesting. The story`s about being seen, I think you allow people to be seen in such a particular way. Because when you sit in front of them, you`re seeing them. You have such presence in these stories. And it really comes across.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Thank you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you so much for this series. We think it`s fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love how he said those who need to hear will hear.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Yeah, and you are good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Which is the message that the church should be preaching to congregants.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love your neighbor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we`re going to talk more about the "State of Spirituality" series in our next hour. \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Plus. Although Vlad says there`s no S in there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re excited.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also pronounced "plus."\par \par \uc2 Look who`s in our progressive green room. Business leader in overall. There she is. There, you`re looking at a badass in person, live and in color. \uc2 Mellody Hobson\uc2  . Hello and welcome.\par \par \uc2 She wants to educate your children and us, too, about money. She`s joining us next. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We always appreciate that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 Hold it up, Mellody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Mellody Hobson\uc2  , the co-CEO and president of \uc2 Ariel Investments\uc2  , has been on a lifelong mission to teach us all about financial literacy. Now she`s focusing on your children. Her new children`s book "Priceless Facts About Money" I love this title, focuses on educating kids about finances through beautiful illustrations and fun facts.\par \par \uc2 The book helps kids and adults too, I have to say, learn about money`s history and how it all works. We are very happy to say that Mellody joins us in the studio this morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hello.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mellody, your book is so beautiful. Normally I mark it up. I didn`t even want to mark it up. And I learned so much myself and it made me kind of sad because I thought I wish I had it when I was younger. I wish I had it when Kirby and Will were younger because I realized all the things I`ve done wrong.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  (Ariel Investments Co-CEO and president): But you have it now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, but I have it now.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : It`s not too late.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much. But you know, I was raised in a house where we don`t talk about money. It`s just very impolite to do. And you say, number one, stop that because what you need to do, we`re reading about dinosaurs, books about that, that kids will never see, but we don`t talk about money. And you say that`s very important.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : It`s considered very taboo in a lot of families. Wealthy families don`t want kids to be entitled. Families that struggle don`t want kids to be burdened. And both are robbing children of the opportunity to really anchor themselves in a issue that is going to be a part of their entire life, no matter where they live on this planet, which is money.\par \par \uc2 And so I think we have to bring money to the forefront for children, especially since these days, money is very mystical since it`s spit out of machines and we pay for things on phones and cards.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Mellody, it`s personal for you because you were raised in a house where you didn`t have a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great way to learn about money, by the way, is not to have any.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You learn the value of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there was a lot of shame about money. You talk about your utilities being cut off and you thought, I want to know everything there is to learn about money. Put that in context, your background to how it affects you today.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : Well, I think it made me who I am. I tell people there`s no accident. I work in the investment business. I was desperate to understand money as a child because money was such, brought me such a source of anxiety. We would get evicted. Our phone would get disconnected, lights turned off. And I said to myself, I don`t want to live like this.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t about having a lot. It was about understanding how it works. And that`s been why I`ve been so passionate about this subject from all the years I was on television talking about it, because I said, if we could unlock the mystery of money, we could help people and the trauma that they have around money.\par \par \uc2 And it starts in childhood. You learn all your money habits from your parents.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. It starts in childhood. I mean, you can prevent some of that trauma, too. I love this book. I think the illustrations are great. I think the sense of humor is great. The material is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The story-telling is so great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s really, it`s really well done. It`s really valuable for a lot of different ages, because even younger kids will want to look at these pages.\par \par \uc2 Your mother showed you bills growing up.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great advice, right? Why did that work for you? What`s the value?\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : So she really wanted me to know what things cost. I understood the cost of living. I knew our light bill, our phone bill. I knew that water cost money. It wasn`t free. I knew what our rent was.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, some of this was a bit overwhelming, but I didn`t go into the world not knowing. There are kids today that don`t even know what their cell phone bill costs. And I say to parents, they need to understand the, that plastic goes with payments.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON: And it`s very, very important to understand that everything actually cost something. And to start to put that in context over time.\par \par \uc2 Now, my mom started when I was really young. Just pay at the cash register. I was five years old. She said, put the money on the cash register. We`re at a restaurant. I got older. Calculate the tip.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : Count the change.\par \par \uc2 I remember her going to the bank and saying, look at how a bank deposit works. Check these numbers against what`s on the receipt. Do they match? She was making me pay attention to money.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let`s talk about why parents, if you`re watching this segment, will want their kids to read this book because you it`s sort of a history lesson, but a fun one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So take us, for example, to the creation of credit cards and what that was all about back in the Middle Ages.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : Two answers. So the first credit card really was in the Middle Ages. It`s one of my favorite stories. The Knight`s Signet ring that the king would give the Knight`s Signet rings because when they were on the road, they didn`t want them carrying cash because they didn`t want them to get robbed.\par \par \uc2 So they would put their signet ring into a bell with wax seal at an inn or a sword keeper or a pub. And then that person would take the receipt to the castle and get paid.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : So I say it was --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s just one of the nuggets. There are so many.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. And can I say the blurbs, Kate Capshaw, Steven Spielberg, Jamie Dimon, Lewis Hamilton, Kerry Washington, Reese Witherspoon. They all like this book, too. Congrats, Mellody.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wonderful book.\par \par \uc2 MELLODY HOBSON\uc2  : Thank you. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Priceless Facts About Money" is on sale now wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 Local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Spend your money on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning. Ms. King, I nominate you again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, I`m in pole position.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is Oprah`s new exclusive primetime special. It`s called "The Presleys." Elvis, Lisa Marie, and Riley. So she`s talking with Riley Keough. She`s a granddaughter of the king in her first in-depth interview since her mother, Lisa Marie Presley died last year very unexpectedly.\par \par \uc2 And before her death, Presley recorded hours of stories from her life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "An Oprah Special: The Presleys, Elvis, Lisa Marie, and Riley")\par \par \uc2 RILEY KEOUGH (Actor): I think having two family members who have passed away, a lot of that time and grief has been spent wondering about where they are. And I think that the perspective that I choose to have is that they`re on to a sort of new adventure.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Great unknown.\par \par \uc2 RILEY KEOUGH: The Great Unknown.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY : The Great Unknown.\par \par \uc2 RILEY KEOUGH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 And he would travel with it everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Keough is sharing those stories in a new memoir. "From Here to the Great Unknown" is the name of it. Oprah also gets an inside tour like none other of Graceland.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oprah`s special, "The Presleys, Elvis, Lisa Marie, and Riley" will premiere Tuesday night here on CBS Streaming and Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 I know Oprah was very excited about this interview. Is very excited to talk to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is Oprah an Elvis`s fan?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. We did a road trip where we got to go to the house to Graceland back in the day and had actually lunch with Lisa Marie. Riley was a little girl then, and they took us into a vault.\par \par \uc2 But now this is a whole next level of detail of what we`re seeing for the first time. And in the book, Lisa Marie writes that her father, Elvis, was like a god to her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She actually thought that he could change the weather.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh my god.\pa