ife.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why did you want to make it as a mockumentary?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Well, I actually, some of my friends are actually my friends. Like Mike, who you`ll see in the show --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Is my friend from college of like 10 years. And I actually can`t believe how many people I tricked into being into my show. The first person I asked was Will Ferrell and he said yes right away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, and what was--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s in the first episode, he`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s great. What was your pitch? Because you`ve got Courtney Cox, you`ve got Sarah Silverman, you`ve got--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: John Legend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: John Legend, John Legend was great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: John Legend`s episode is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Isaac Mizrahi.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You stole his Grammy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was your pitch to them? And it sounds like a lot of it is, is it scripted, is it improv? How does it work?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: I`d say it`s 95% unscripted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you don`t know what the person`s going to say? You guys are just vibing off of each other.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: I had no idea what Will, the show starts off with me not having any idea what Will Ferrell was going to say to me. He`s a genius with coming up with stuff on the spot. But people like Courtney Cox doesn`t do improv every day, but she`s so good at it. And it became fun.\par \par \uc2 And it`s like writing a song for me, making a show, making an episode. It`s like taking a jazz solo in a way, because you`re coming up with stuff on the spot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So then what`s the actual conversation with these folks like? I mean, when they say, "Sure. I love you Charlie, happy to help, tell me about the project." What do you say?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: The project is, I would say that the project is an exaggerated take on my life in the music industry, in the entertainment industry, and the constant pressure to stay in the spotlight, or as they say, in the zeitgeist.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, but they said you are a zeitgeist adjacent.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re a zeitgeist adjacent, which is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, the lines are hilarious.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I love how your agent is trying to pitch John Legend, "Do you need representation?" That was a really great scene in the movie. In the film, in the series.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the series.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re trying, you guys are on piano together --\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And you`re battling, and you`re trying to come up with stuff --\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: All on the spot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All on the spot.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: All on the spot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Charlie, I think it`s got to be fun to be you these days. Number one, the mockumentary is great. Taylor Swift name checked you in a song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you think when you heard, obviously you didn`t know she was going to do that, I`m assuming.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: No, I did not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So when you heard it, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: I thought it was AI. I thought it was a robot. But then I listened back to it again, because she`s so clever in her writing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: I was like, there`s no way that she just rhymed chocolate with artist, and Puth with a, it`s just too good to be artificial.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you and Oprah have something in common too, which I think is cool. What is it?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: We are neighbors, and I did not know that when I moved to my house with my now wife. But she`s a pretty cool neighbor, and now a cool friend to have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very cool friend to have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s cool. So you`re working on a new album as well, in addition to the show.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: "In That House."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "In that House."\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Yeah, very musical house.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I understand you`re working from a storytelling perspective. You want to focus more on that part of your writing. Talk to me about that.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: I just want to be in this next project that I`m working on, super specific with things that I put into my music because it turns out that people care about that stuff. It doesn`t have to just be girl, world, dog, smog, whatever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I`m curious about your eyebrow, if you don`t mind me asking.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it a, because I thought, is it a trademark of yours?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Well a lot of people ask me this, and a lot of people still don`t know. And I`m happy to answer it. I was bit by a dog when I was two years old, and my eyebrows is kind of like this now. You can do the \uc2 CBS\uc2  zoom in. It`s very HD.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll bet it bothered you then, but I`ll bet it doesn`t bother you now.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: No, I feel pretty cool. It makes me look, I just lie and say that I got in a big...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh they`re zooming in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are zooming in on that eyebrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m sure it was the scariest moment of your parents` life - -\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As a parent to you.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Not a great day at all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All turned out well though. It`s a great --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All turned out well. Because I was thinking, that`s a hell of a trademark to have. But you know, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charlie, it`s great that you`re working on a new album. I love your music. Even the songs you don`t like of yours, I like.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We love everything you`re doing. We love this show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Charlie Puth, thank you very much. "The Charlie Puth Show" premieres tomorrow on Roku.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE PUTH: Tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hoping there`s more to come. That`ll do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241003eka300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For October 3, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Imtiaz Tyab, Skyler Henry, Nancy Cordes, Elise Preston, Adam  Yamaguchi, Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,935 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The grim milestone, with more than 200 killed by Hurricane Helene, as hope fades for finding the missing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MANHEIMER (Mayor of Asheville, North Carolina): We are still in a crisis mode. We are still in the search-and-rescue mode.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The battleground blitz. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  hits the campaign trail with Vice President Harris, while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Plus, our interview with the top Justice Department official tasked with protecting the U.S. from foreign election interference.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Iran, Russia and China are trying to strike at the heart of democracy?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2  (Assistant U.S. Attorney General For National Security): I think that`s a very fair statement.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And tonight`s Eye on America, the major city that`s seen an unprecedented drop in homelessness. How the mayor is combating the crisis with a possible long-term solution.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS\uc2  (D-Mayor of Los Angeles, California): We will be able to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We want to start with some breaking news tonight out of Memphis. There are verdicts in the federal trial of three police officers accused in the fatal beating death of Tyre Nichols during a traffic stop last year.\par \par \uc2 Elise Preston was in the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 And, Elise, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Norah, all three officers were found guilty of trying to cover up what happened the night Tyre Nichols was beaten, but not guilty on other crimes, including the most serious ones, like violating his civil rights. All three officers were taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voice-over): Twenty-nine-year-old Tyre Nichols worked for \uc2 FedEx\uc2 , was an avid skateboarder, lived with his parents and wore a tattoo with his mother`s name on his shoulder.\par \par \uc2 ROWVAUGHN WELLS (Mother of Tyre Nichols): My son loved me to death and I loved him to death. We just had a special bond. He was the baby, you know?\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: But after police stopped him, bodycam footage shows what happened next. Nichols runs off. But then officers apprehend him. This took place less than 80 yards from his mother`s home. He called out for her to help him.\par \par \uc2 TYRE NICHOLS (Victim): Mom! Mom!\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: For some three minutes, Memphis police officers punched, kicked and are seen hitting him with a baton even after he was restrained.\par \par \uc2 (SCREAMING)\par \par \uc2 MAN: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, two other officers pleaded guilty before the trial even began and actually testified.\par \par \uc2 Now, Nichols` family just walked out moments ago. They said, even with the mixed verdict, they feel like the officers, all five officers, have been held accountable, and they feel like they have received justice for Tyre Nichols` death -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston with that breaking news, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the race for the White House runs through an unusual stop for the Democratic nominee, actually the birthplace of the Republican Party. That`s Ripon, Wisconsin, where Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  appeared with former Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Remember, Cheney is a lifetime Republican and former member of the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, there was a campaign surprise from former first lady Melania Trump signaling her support for abortion rights, in spite of her husband boasting many times he proudly overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): A big moment in tiny Ripon, Wisconsin, former Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the daughter of a former vice president, making her first appearance with the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (R-Wyoming): But I will be voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Cheney first announced her support last month, but she`s been a critic of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for years and was vice chair of the January 6 Committee, which examined Trump`s role in the Capitol insurrection.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : He is unfit for any office.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Harris campaign has been working to highlight the growing list of Republicans who now oppose Trump.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE (R-Presidential Candidate): I cannot in good conscience endorse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Those defections rattled Steven Michek, a former Wisconsin sheriff who is switching to Harris after voting for Trump in 2016.\par \par \uc2 STEVE MICHEK (Former Iowa County, Wisconsin, Sheriff): All of the changes in administrative staff, just constant turnover of people, the just unusual things of having all of your children employed in the White House was -- I would not do that in a sheriff`s office.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In a court filing released yesterday, special counsel Jack Smith alleged that Trump ignored threats to his V.P.`s life during the Capitol riot, allegedly telling an aide: "So what?"\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We won. We won. We did win. It was a rigged election.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In Saginaw, Michigan, today, Trump repeated his false claims about 2020 and warned of an apocalypse if Harris wins next month.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Instead of a golden age, America will instead be plunged into a dark age. Your family finances will be permanently destroyed.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: He did not address the new comments from his wife, Melania, who unexpectedly came out in favor of abortion rights in a video promoting her upcoming book.\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP (Former First Lady): There is no room for compromise when it comes to this essential right.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In an interview after the speech, Trump said he actually had spoken to his wife beforehand and told her she should share what was in her heart, even if they disagree.\par \par \uc2 Here in Wisconsin, Cheney just said that Harris is standing in the breach at a critical time in this nation`s history, and she urged conservatives to vote for her -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So interesting that Melania Trump is saying that now.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now the latest on the destruction from Hurricane Helene. Tonight, the death toll continues rising, now past 200. Nearly half of those are from North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Skyler Henry is in devastated Asheville.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Skyler.\par \par \uc2 And I know you spoke with the city`s mayor. What did she say?\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Norah. Good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 We`re here at one of the distribution centers getting out supplies to people here in this region. The mayor told us that the city is still in crisis mode and that she expects the death toll to climb.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MANHEIMER (Mayor of Asheville, North Carolina): We are still in a crisis mode. We are still in the search-and-rescue mode.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voice-over): Six days after the flood, the search for the missing continues, as the death toll rises in Asheville, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ESTHER MANHEIMER: There are people that died in the storm that lived in homes in areas that had never before experienced flooding. So, when you`re thinking about, do I need to evacuate, there were a lot of people that thought, well, I`m probably fine where I am.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: We met Mayor Esther Manheimer in the city`s decimated Biltmore Village. She surveyed the damage with President Biden Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ESTHER MANHEIMER: He understands that this is going to cost billions and it`s going to take a long time and a sustained effort to rebuild our community and Western North Carolina. He understands that, and that commitment is there to stay with us until the end.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: FEMA has more than 1,500 people on the ground in the Southeast distributing supplies. The president has deployed 1,000 soldiers to help, and there are countless volunteers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: There are people saying how many people they have, what kids they have, what ages they are, and so then they come and pack them up. So, it`s basically just like a little grocery store.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: We are in the Shiloh neighborhood here in Asheville at one of the distribution points where the community is coming together to bring all sorts of goods here. You see the essential water, food, supplies. There`s even dog food down here to try to make sure that everyone and even all the pets have everything that they need.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Yes, take whatever you need. Take what you need for your neighbors.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The nonprofit BeLoved Asheville is brimming with donations.\par \par \uc2 Of course. You guys just getting water and paper goods?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Yes?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Water.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: How bad is it -- how bad is it where you all are?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Well, we still don`t have no lights or water, but it`s a blessing for the help.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Come on.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Do you see how the community come together?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ESTHER MANHEIMER: We are a strong community, and we`re being put to a test, but I think we`re rising to that challenge.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #4: Ooh.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Take what you want.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: And -- and the mayor also told us that even the newer structures that were built above the flood stage were flooded anyway.\par \par \uc2 She says, as the city considers rebuilding, climate change should be a factor -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Skyler Henry doing double duty, not only reporting, but helping out the people there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Skyler.\par \par \uc2 Well, the United States is now organizing flights out of Lebanon for American citizens trying to escape the intensifying bombardment, as the conflict between Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  ratchets up. The State Department says at least 250 Americans and their family members are now out, catching a ride on two flights to Turkey.\par \par \uc2 There are thousands of Americans that still remain in Lebanon, including those staying by choice, as Israel masses more troops along its northern border.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab reports from a strike zone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voice-over): Israeli warplanes are striking Beirut`s southern suburbs relentlessly, reducing entire buildings to smoldering mountains of rubble.\par \par \uc2 But in the city`s central Bachoura neighborhood, a rare overnight Israeli missile strike, one floor of a building blown to pieces, killing at least nine.\par \par \uc2 To give you a sense of where we are, Lebanon`s Parliament is just down the road. And for Israel to strike the center of the city like this, so many people are afraid that more is to come. In Southern Lebanon, Israeli strikes are only intensifying, as Israel continues to mobilize its forces along the border and as the number of dead and injured across Lebanon continues to skyrocket.\par \par \uc2 At this hospital in the Southern city of Sidon, we met Abdulaziz Hamoud (sp?), who suffered a traumatic brain injury from the force of an Israeli airstrike. His friend Karim Karim (sp?) shows us his own head injury.\par \par \uc2 (KARIM KARIM SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "Israel is killing women and children who are innocent," he says. "Children are not carrying weapons and rockets. It`s wrong."\par \par \uc2 (KARIM KARIM SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: According to the \uc2 WHO\uc2 , at least 28 health workers have been killed in Israeli strikes across Lebanon over the past 24 hours alone, as the country`s already frail health system strains from so many casualties, says emergency doctor Ziad Abu Al Enaen (ph).\par \par \uc2 DR. ZIAD ABU AL ENAEN (Emergency Physician): Our greatest fear is to receive a very big number of the patients, and you cannot deal with them.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: As a doctor, that must be very painful.\par \par \uc2 DR. ZIAD ABU AL ENAEN: Yes, yes, because we saw what happened in Gaza, and we don`t want it to happen here.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Are you afraid that that`s what`s going to happen?\par \par \uc2 DR. ZIAD ABU AL ENAEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And, tonight, those Israeli evacuation orders are stoking fears its military will move from limited ground incursions to a full-scale invasion and potentially occupy large areas.\par \par \uc2 The last time Israel occupied Southern Lebanon, it lasted for 18 years -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Imtiaz Tyab, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next: why top U.S. officials are warning we are approaching the most dangerous time in this election season and the biggest threat to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 Plus, there`s breaking news on the port strike.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There`s more breaking news at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell CBS News the strike impacting the East and Gulf Coast is over for now. Both sides have reached a tentative agreement, effectively -- effective immediately lasting through January 15.\par \par \uc2 Our crews caught dockworkers celebrating as the announcement was made. At least 25,000 dockworkers in 14 ports along the East and Gulf Coast walked off the job Tuesday in search of higher wages and protections against automation.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, top U.S. officials warn we are entering one of the most dangerous phases of the presidential campaign. And the biggest threat is foreign from countries ruled by authoritarian leaders. They want to weaken our democracy.\par \par \uc2 So, we went to the Justice Department to hear from Assistant Attorney General \uc2 Matthew Olsen\uc2 , who is in charge of the \uc2 National Security Division\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We are just now 33 days until Election Day. Already, voters in nearly two dozen states are voting early. What is your biggest concern?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2  (Assistant U.S. Attorney General For National Security): Really an onslaught of foreign election interference. So we`re seeing Russia, Iran and China targeting our elections. They see this as a period of vulnerability for us.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Iran, Russia and China are trying to strike at the heart of democracy?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : I think that`s a very fair statement. This is a clear and present danger.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How would I know that`s happening?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : Right. You may not see it. We`re seeing sophisticated efforts by our adversaries to hide their steps. They, for example, used fake Web sites, fake Web sites of real news outlets, like "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and FOX News.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Disinformation to make it look like there`s more disunity out there than there really is.\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : Absolutely, to make it look like these issues that are important issues in our society, but that there`s less common ground than there actually is.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): They`re bringing up Russia, Russia, Russia again.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has long claimed that Russian interference is a hoax, in his word. Has that had an impact on the Department of Justice`s public perception?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : First, 2016, Russian interference in the election was not a hoax. It was actually happening then. It is actually happening now. There is no question about it. It is more aggressive than ever this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Who does Russia want to win?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : Russia has been seeking to boost the candidacy of the former president and to denigrate the candidacy of the vice president.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So Russia is trying to boost \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and denigrate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : We have seen that very clearly.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What about Iran?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : The intelligence community with respect to Iran has also identified efforts that Iran has made to compromise the former president`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was briefed on real and specific threats from Iran to kill him. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : We have been intensely tracking Iranian efforts, Iranian lethal plotting efforts targeting former and current \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  officials, and that includes the former president.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But has it gotten more worrisome or more dangerous in the last several months?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : I would say that we are very concerned, gravely concerned about Iranian plotting.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Are you worried about violence in America after the election regardless of who wins?\par \par \uc2 ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 MATTHEW OLSEN\uc2 : In the Justice Department, we are involved in prosecuting the cases that arose after January 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 I would say to anyone who is considering violence, they should look at the hundreds of prosecutions that have been brought here in Washington, D.C., and the long prison sentences in some of those cases. So, I think that we have taken that lesson to heart.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Revealing interview.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next, how one of the biggest cities in the country is finding success tackling homelessness.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A Supreme Court ruling earlier this year gives local governments the authority to ban people from sleeping and camping in public places. Critics say the ruling does nothing to solve the homelessness crisis.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Adam Yamaguchi takes us to Los Angeles, where the mayor has launched a program that could provide a long-term solution.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI (voice-over): This is Coley King`s mobile exam room.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLEY KING (Director of Homeless Health Care, Venice Family Clinic): We want to be there for them.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Four days a week in Los Angeles, the street doctor with the Venice Family Clinic makes house calls to those without homes.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLEY KING: We saw you, and really the last time was 2021. And then, after that, we kind of...\par \par \uc2 MELISSA (Homeless): Lost track.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLEY KING: Lost track. We lost contact. So, I -- here you are.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Fifty-three-year-old Melissa is one of more than 45,000 people experiencing homelessness in the city of Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLEY KING: There are patients that suffer from very serious medical illnesses. There are patients that have schizophrenia, have HIV. So, I think there`s urgency from everyone to solve this.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Nearly half of all Americans living on the streets are here in California, where there was roughly one shelter bed for every three people who needed one last year.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GAVIN NEWSOM (D-California): It`s time to move with urgency at the local level to clean up these sites.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Earlier this year, leaders like California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  were empowered to take action in clearing encampments after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruled that cities could ban people from sleeping outside.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GAVIN NEWSOM: There are no longer any excuses.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Easy to say, perhaps, but the reality is more complicated.\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS\uc2  (D-Mayor of Los Angeles, California): The goal has got to be to solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  is the mayor of Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KAREN BASS: Our problem here is, we don`t have enough housing. We need to build. We need to purchase buildings.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: And that`s where she`s focused, getting people off the streets and into shelter that up until now did not exist. She`s doing it by leveraging empty motels and city-owned properties, with the goal of moving people indoors urgently and clearing encampments permanently.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KAREN BASS: You don`t solve anything by clearing an encampment and just moving them to the next street.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: So far, since 2022, her Inside Safe program has brought more than 3,000 people indoors from over 16 encampments. And the city has seen a drop in street homelessness.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KAREN BASS: Well, this area right here many months ago was filled with tents. And you can imagine, what family wants to bring their kids here right there if this is filled with tents and you`re afraid of the people that are here? And look at it now.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Jessica has been unsheltered since 2019. Just days after we met her, she was placed in interim housing.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA (Homeless): I should be able to get some more rest, but this has been very stressful.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN (Homeless): Oh.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: Others, like Brian, are still waiting.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN: It`s hard to be on camera showing this.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KAREN BASS: We`re dealing with massive numbers, so it is going to take a while. I wish I could tell you that, on this date, this problem would be ended. I can`t do that. But what I do know is that, if we continue along with the trajectory that we`re headed on, we will be able to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: It`s an approach that could also solve a problem for Dr. King.\par \par \uc2 You may lose contact with people unless there`s a plan in place.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLEY KING: Absolutely. We may lose them, and we may lose them forever, because they are that sick. I have faith that, here, we`re already on that pathway to try to do this the right way.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: A crisis decades in the making changing one day, one life at a time.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Adam Yamaguchi in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good people trying to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next, proving you can`t underestimate the power of determination and love.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight`s Heart of America shows us strength, determination and love in the midst of destruction from Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 David Jones was driving from South Carolina to Tennessee for his daughter`s wedding, when the highway shut down. A two-hour drive became a treacherous 12.5-hour journey. Get this. David walked about 17 miles through water and mud up to his knees and climbed through piles of debris, but he made it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID JONES (Father): My daughter`s getting married at 11:00 this morning, and I`m going to be there to walk her down the aisle.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That`s so emotionally moving that my dad loves me that much that he would come and go through all of that to get to my wedding.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: He picked up a reflective stake along the way to keep him safe in the dark. He gave it to the couple, telling them to use it as a reminder to protect each other.\par \par \uc2 So, the power of a father`s love. David Jones is tonight`s Heart of America. I don`t know. It just makes me so emotional.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s Evening News. Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241004eka300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 VP Candidates Square Off in Debate; Voters React to VP Debate; \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  on Takeaways from VP Debate; Iran Launches Missile Attack on Israel; US Reacts to Iran Missile Attack on Israel; Dockworkers Strike Impact; New Diddy Lawsuits; \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  Turns 100; DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  Weighs in on VP Debate; Remembering John Amos; Rascal Flatts Reunion; Searching For Survivors; Vance and Walz Comparison; VP Candidates Square Off in Debate; Ed O`Keefe on Takeaways From VP Debate; Politics In The Peach State; "Barefoot Contessa" Live; NY Liberty Near WNBA Finals Return; Gayle "The Queen" Would Win Survivor; Cloned Sheep: A Baaaad Idea?; A Focus on Fertility; "We Are Free, You & Me", \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Jericka Duncan, John  Dickerson, Ed O`Keefe, Kris Van Cleave, Chris Livesay, Weijia Jiang, Lilia  Luciano \par \uc2 14,773 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets on a Wednesday, hump day, as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, but we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN) (2024 vice presidential nominee): So the rhetoric is good. Much of what the senator said right there, I`m in agreement with him on this.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH) (2024 vice presidential nominee): And I think that Governor Walz and I actually probably agree that we need to do better on this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The vice presidential nominees strike a respectful tone in their debate while they sharply disagree on policy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Israel vows to hit back after Iran launches its largest ever missile attack against the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2  (\uc2 US State Department\uc2  spokesperson): We have been clear that there must be consequences for this attack.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A massive port workers strike enters its second day, threatening major damage to the US economy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Those greedy companies are making billions of dollars and they don`t want to share.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Tributes are pouring in for actor, John Amos, his family revealing he died from natural causes back on August 21st.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): The most vice important night of the entire campaign.\par \par \uc2 The vice-excitement was palpable. These two men were both a heartbeat away from being interesting this evening.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I believe Senator Vance wants to solve this.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): I agree with you. I think you want to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I agree with a lot of what Senator Vance said.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): I actually agree with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA ("The Daily Show"): Wow. Maybe you two should run together. Yes, maybe a date?\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KOSTA: It seems like you have a lot in common. Hello, \uc2 CBS\uc2 , it`s Tim. We`re going to share an Uber home.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It almost did feel like they could share an Uber home.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There was decorum.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We call it civility.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, civility. I like civility.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s where we`re going to begin. As Senator Vance and Governor Walz held their debate, we got a fresh reminder that whoever wins the presidential election is going to be tested by multiple crises here and abroad.\par \par \uc2 Just in the last 24 hours, Iran unleashed its largest missile barrage ever on Israel in response to Israeli strikes targeting \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders.\par \par \uc2 And in the United States, searchers found dozens more victims of Hurricane Helene, a storm that has left thousands cut off from food, water and electricity.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Vlad it is against that backdrop that Senator Vance and Governor Walz offered very different views of American leadership last night at the only vice presidential debate of this election.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News poll shows viewers split evenly on who won, but most did really like the tone. Eighty-eight percent thought it was generally positive. Ed O`Keefe was in the spin room for the debate here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 , and right now he joins us in the morning.\par \par \uc2 Long night for you, too, Ed. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Sure was, Gayle. But it was worth it, because for 90 minutes, the vice presidential nominees made their case, and while it was a mostly civil affair, their differences were still on full display.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The vice presidential debate began with a handshake, but then just hours after Iran fired missiles into Israel, the candidate sparred over national security.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  recognized that for people to fear the United States, you needed peace through strength.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Iran is closer to a nuclear weapon than they were before because of Donald Trump`s fickle leadership.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On domestic issues, the economy consistently ranks as a top concern for voters, and both men made their pitch.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has said to do the things she wants to do, we`ll just ask the wealthiest to pay their fair share. When you do that, our system works best.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Honestly, Tim, I think you`ve got a tough job here, because you`ve got to play Whack-a-Mole. You`ve got to pretend that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t deliver rising take home pay, which of course he did. You`ve got to pretend that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t deliver lower inflation, which, of course, he did.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The future of abortion services has been a major focus for Democrats, but Republican, JD Vance shared a personal connection to the issue.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): I grew up in a working class family in a neighborhood where I knew a lot of young women who had unplanned pregnancies and decided to terminate those pregnancies because they feel like they didn`t have any other options. And you know, I -- one of them is actually very dear to me, and I know she`s watching tonight, and I love you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And made this admission.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): My party, we`ve got to do so much better of a job at earning the American people`s trust back on this issue where they frankly, just don`t trust us.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Around the same time, in a social media post, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said for the first time he would veto a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 But Democrat \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  criticized Republican arguments that the issue should be decided state by state.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): The fact of the matter is, how can we as a nation say that your life and your rights as basic as the right to control your own body is determined on geography?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Walz also got personal when asked about gun control.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): All the parents watching tonight, this is your biggest nightmare. Look, I`ve got a -- I`ve got a 17-year-old and -- and he witnessed a shooting at a community center playing volleyball. Those things don`t leave you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Tim, first of all, I didn`t know that your 17-year- old witnessed a shooting, and I`m sorry about that and I --\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Christ, have mercy. It is -- it is awful.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And while the debate was overwhelmingly civil --\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Well, first of all, Tim just said something that I agree with.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I agree with a lot of what Senator Vance said.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It ended on the divisive issue of what might happen later this year.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I will tell you this, that when this is over, we need to shake hands, this election, and the winner needs to be the winner. This has got to stop. It`s tearing our country apart.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): It`s really rich for Democratic leaders to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a unique threat to democracy when he peacefully gave over power on January the 20th, as we have done for 250 years in this country.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But then Walz pressed Vance on Trump`s decision not to accept his loss to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Did he lose the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Tim, I`m focused on the future.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): That is a damning non-answer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Of course, Trump had urged supporters to challenge the results and then pressed then Vice President Pence not to certify the Electoral College results.\par \par \uc2 Also last night, Vice President Harris reiterated her call for a second presidential debate. Trump repeated that he is "not looking to do it again" -- guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Ed, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan watched last night`s debate in a very interesting place, Grand Rapids, Michigan. This is one of those towns that voted for Trump in 2016 then flipped over to Biden in 2020. Everyone is battling for it once again.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you. Good morning to everyone.\par \par \uc2 It is a very competitive district here in Kent County, Michigan. About 350,000 people voted in the last presidential election. But following last night`s debate, we asked the voters, who do they think won? They said JD Vance. Most of them said that he won this debate.\par \par \uc2 But take politics out of this for just a moment. They said they were most impressed with the civility that we saw on full display last night. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA LEDESMA (Harris voter): This debate that I saw made me happy that again, the politics is going back to us, treating each other with respect.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I think that my biggest takeaway from Vance is that he is able to articulate himself, his feelings, his policies, in a respectful way. That`s one of the things that I dislike about Trump.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY PANIWOZIK (Trump voter): They both complimented each other when they needed to, and gave respect to each other. They got along as two gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Another issue that was top of mind for those voters was gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELLI BERKEY (Undecided voter): They both agreed that they need that something needed to be done, but there really wasn`t a plan to do, to fix anything. The only thing that I heard was Vance saying that he wanted more security in the schools.\par \par \uc2 If someone were to have access to a gun in my kid`s school, I have no idea what I would do.\par \par \uc2 ARICK DAVIS (Harris voter): I got asked more questions when I was getting a dog from the shelter than when I was getting my gun. And we`re just now getting to a point where it seems like maybe there`s going to be like, actually, like, real support on both sides to do like, basic gun safety things.\par \par \uc2 But I feel like the Republican Party has not prioritized or taken like, some of these basic common sense gun things, like seriously.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Before the debate started, two of the voters said that they were going to vote for Trump. Two said they would vote for Harris. Two said they were undecided, but by the end of it, those two undecided said they would vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 So clearly, you know, these voters didn`t see eye to eye on all the issues, but there was one thing that they said they do all agree on. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Is democracy on the line in this election, Bill?\par \par \uc2 BILL (Voter): Yes, sure is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Arick, why are you laughing?\par \par \uc2 ARICK DAVIS: Because it absolutely -- it just absolutely is. And I had to catch myself, but it`s like, oh my God, is it? Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Holly?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY PANIWOZIK: Same. I feel the same way. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA LEDESMA: Yes, definitely.\par \par \uc2 MAN: More than ever in my life. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: "More than ever in your life?"\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Elli?\par \par \uc2 ELLI BERKEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Nice discussion that we had there. We`re going to put the full interview eventually on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . So check it out.\par \par \uc2 With 34 days left, though, all of these voters said that they want to see another debate, that they wouldn`t mind, I should say. Some of them said that they felt like they could get a little bit more clarity if they could see one more debate between Harris and Trump -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really interesting stuff, Jericka. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to our wisest of all wise heads of all things political --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- our chief political analyst, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 . He also hosts "The Daily Report" with \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  on CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : You`ve got to keep the bar low, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our burbling fountain of all wise things. Please tell us. What were your takeaways last night?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Well, it was a kind of a, you know, basically everything that that`s been reported so far, it was civil. It was -- you know, and that`s notable mostly from JD Vance, because you look at who`s running at the top of the ticket, so it`s like you work for a boss who comes in, in cutoff jeans every day, and then guy comes in wearing a suit and tie, and you think, wow, it`s like the old days. It`s like it used to be, you know, where people believed in the norms of civility, they debated politics.\par \par \uc2 That isn`t to say that there wasn`t a lot of traditional stuff that irritates people about politics, but it was so much different than what we`ve heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out on the trail. So that was the biggest thing, I think, probably though it kind of passes through and we move back into the race. That doesn`t really change anything too much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, to me, one of the most talked about moments of the night was January 6th and the difference of opinion between the two.\par \par \uc2 JD Vance seemed to say, look, I want to move forward. Stop looking back, accusing the media like we always bring it up all the time.\par \par \uc2 Can you still say that January 6 is in the past?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Yes. JD Vance said he`s focused on the future. So a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 One, when you spend a lot of the debate talking about how great things were in the Trump years, you can`t then say, wait, but I`m focused on the future. You can`t just remember the stuff you say is the good stuff.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is, you know, who`s focused on this a lot and built his whole campaign around it? It is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So he`s quite focused on this.\par \par \uc2 But then there are two reasons I think this is really important, that that Senator Vance couldn`t give a simple yes, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the election. Simplest question in the world. Still can`t do it. And we interviewed Senator Tom Cotton, Republican from Arkansas, couldn`t do it either.\par \par \uc2 This is why it matters, two things. When you talk to national security experts who are worried about violence around the next election, they say the biggest thing fueling the possibility for violence around the next election is the lie that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the last one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : So that fuel is out there in real time now, and then when you look at the answers from Vance and Senator Cotton, democracy exists by common agreement. Everybody agrees that elections matter and that the votes should be counted and that the power given to the people who win the election then allows you to make laws, and everybody will agree to that. If that agreement doesn`t exist, you have violence.\par \par \uc2 Well, that agreement only exists if everybody chooses to agree to it. So when you see Senator Vance and Senator Cotton making excuses, not saying easily, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost, they are creating room for the lie to keep living.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : This is not a small -- this is not like a debate over tax cuts. This is about a fundamental idea of whether 81 million people should be disenfranchised because a guy didn`t like that he lost.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON\uc2 : Who said that? Not me, but the Republican leaders in the House and Senate at the time who said the lie from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  led to the violence. That`s real and alive right now. It`s not in the past.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. That`s what worries me. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, John.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you call him? A burbling --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A burbling fountain of wisdom?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a programming note for you, too. "60 Minutes" will air a primetime election special, Monday, October 7th, at eight o`clock.\par \par \uc2 For more than half a century, you should know that "60 Minutes" has invited the Democratic and the Republican tickets to appear on its broadcast as Americans head to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris will speak with correspondent Bill Whitaker. After initially accepting "60 Minutes" offer for an interview with Scott Pelley, former President Trump`s campaign has now decided not to participate.\par \par \uc2 We want you to know the original "60 Minutes" invitation to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still stands.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s turn to our other top story.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from Iran`s largest ever missile attack on Israel. Iran fired nearly 200 missiles at targets inside Israel last night, saying it was in retaliation for its assassination of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders. Israeli officials say the bombardment caused only minor damage.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay has more from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Some 180 missiles tear through the night sky as Israel shoots, most of them down, shrapnel falling like embers, some snuck through Israel`s defenses and falling debris killed a man in the occupied West Bank and lightly injured several others.\par \par \uc2 From the Western Wall in Jerusalem to Tel Aviv, the entire population was told to move into bomb shelters, including us.\par \par \uc2 You can see everybody is just piled into this parking garage that`s doubling as a bomb shelter.\par \par \uc2 There are children here. There`s dogs here.\par \par \uc2 Above ground, our colleague Elizabeth Palmer watched the onslaught from a balcony.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The alarms have sounded again for the second time this evening for people to take shelter. We`ve had one volley of Iranian missiles. There`s a second one coming in now, and you can see flashes of light in the sky where they`re being blown up in flight by Israel`s interceptor system.\par \par \uc2 (REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: The Israeli military says the attack will have consequences, and that Israel will act at a time and place of its choosing.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the damage was still being assessed.\par \par \uc2 A ballistic missile left a massive crater in this residential area outside Tel Aviv, just smashing buses and cars like this one and leaving twisted metal.\par \par \uc2 Violence brought to the doorstep of Israel`s largest city against the backdrop of a widening war. Israel has launched what it calls a limited ground offensive into Southern Lebanon, targeting \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rocket positions and uncovering a network of tunnels and stashed weapons.\par \par \uc2 The fighting here has reached an intensity not seen since the last Israel- Lebanon War in 2006 when Israel launched a full ground invasion.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Chris Livesay, Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two \uc2 US Navy\uc2  ships in the Middle East shot down some of those Iranian missiles last night. President Biden`s National Security Adviser calls this attack a significant escalation.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang change at the White House with more on that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Weijia, good morning. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to everybody. You`re absolutely right, and now the US vows severe consequences for Iran, but what those are remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was watching the strikes from the Situation Room alongside Vice President Harris and his National Security team. The Pentagon says it was Biden`s order that those two \uc2 US Navy\uc2  destroyers fired roughly a dozen interceptors to take down Iranian missiles.\par \par \uc2 After the attack, both presidential candidates weighed in. Harris said she unequivocally condemned the attack and said the US would continue to work with allies to hold Iran accountable. Former President Trump declared the world has been "unraveling" since he left office and accused Biden and Harris of leading the US to the brink of World War III.\par \par \uc2 Biden has pushed for a ceasefire in Lebanon to avoid a wider conflict, but he stresses the US is fully supportive of Israel -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it, Weijia. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Another issue that could have a big effect on the 2024 election, the dockworkers` strike, something people weren`t talking about two weeks ago. Now, it`s in its second day.\par \par \uc2 The economy was already top of mind, and it could become an even bigger issue for voters because of these images on your screen.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has everything you need to know.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: For a second day, 14 ports from Boston to Houston shut down after dockworkers walked off the job. Those ports handle about 80 percent of the coffee, tea, spirits and beverage imports, as well as about 60 percent of medical and surgical instruments coming into the US. Also halted, about 76 percent of vehicle exports.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mondi is among at least 25,000 union workers on strike demanding better wages and job protections against automation.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MONDI (Union worker on strike): We`re looking ahead into the future, going to work now at the risk of losing our jobs five or 10 years down the road is very shortsighted.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Morton Williams grocery stores in New York City say they stocked up on extra perishables to buy time.\par \par \uc2 MARC GOLDMAN (Morton Williams Supermarkets produce director): If it goes a few days, there won`t be much of an effect. If it goes weeks, a month or more, there could be a very big effect. We`ll definitely have higher prices, and we might have empty shelves.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The strike could cost the economy up to $5 billion a day.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Apparel, footwear, consumer electronics, home goods, decorations, food products. You know, anything you can think of is coming through these ports, so they are vital to the economy, and it is critical that we get them back up and running.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Still, New York`s governor, again, urged people not to rush the stores.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): It`s not time to be panic buying groceries or other products.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kris Van Cleave reporting for us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in a moment, dozens of new sexual misconduct allegations against \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2 , and we`re going to hear from a lawyer who says he personally represents 120 victims, but first, 7:18, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, TV`s Barefoot Contessa, Ina Garten talks with us about her candid new memoir about her life`s highs and lows.\par \par \uc2 And our series "Three Meals" goes to the battleground state of Georgia. Tony asked voters what it would take for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to get their support.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by Verizon.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  is facing another long list of sexual misconduct allegations. He is in jail awaiting trials on charges of trafficking women for sex parties. He has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 An attorney in Houston says he represents 120 people who say Combs abused them as well. The allegations are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY BUZBEE (Attorney for Combs` accusers): Her youngest victim at the time of the occurrence was nine years old. We have an individual who was 14 years old. We have one who is 15.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-five of the 120 individuals who are plaintiffs in these cases were minors at the time of the acts complained of.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Combs` lawyer said he denies any claim that he sexually abused anyone, including minors.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we ride with the National Guard troops in North Carolina searching for hurricane survivors.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CBS News exclusive video shows former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  -- there he is -- and his family celebrating his 100th birthday, yesterday. Long, long life.\par \par \uc2 Four military jets flew over his hometown of Plains, Georgia. Carter is the first US president to live a full century.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He has been in hospice care, as you know, at home for the past 19 months. He keeps defying all the expectations.\par \par \uc2 According to his son, Chip, Carter`s next move, his next goal is to make it to Election Day so that he can vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is super interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is very moving.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I said yesterday how \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  likes to win. Somebody who profiled him once told me that when he was entered into hospice care, his goal was to break the record for longest stay in hospice care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He just wants to keep he wants to see what happens. He cares about this planet and the people of the United States.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the people care about him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People care about him. Exactly. I think he`s going to make it, too. I think he`s going to be able to do that goal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because he`s never not hit his goals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, President Biden goes to North and South Carolina today to inspect the terrible, horrific damage from Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 Officials now say more than 160 people have been found in six states, from Florida to Virginia. North Carolina`s National Guard is helping many of the survivors.\par \par \uc2 And Lilia Luciano shows us how they`re carrying out that mission.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: In a helicopter high above North Carolina`s remote Blue Ridge Mountains, Tuesday, CBS News rode with first responders as they combed the devastated landscape, searching for survivors.\par \par \uc2 We`re embedded with the state`s National Guard, following several missions. Some are delivering supplies, some are able to land and bring people to safety, and some have to hoist survivors.\par \par \uc2 We saw them in action over the small town of Burnsville, where the National Guard rescued seven people.\par \par \uc2 All right, the clear signal was given, the person is now being hoisted into the helicopter slowly.\par \par \uc2 And 60 miles south in Hendersonville, three survivors waved from the ground, grateful as the helicopter descended to bring them in.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling right now?\par \par \uc2 MARIYA KARPENKO (Rescued from Hendersonville, North Carolina): Grateful to be alive. Grateful that everyone is safe.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But not all rescues have had a positive outcome.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Helene is now blamed for more than 160 deaths across six states.\par \par \uc2 And in the area where you were, were people safe? Are people injured?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Everybody is safe. Everybody is safe and healthy. They desperately need fuel, things to start rebuilding the road so they can get more food and water in there.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Damaged roadways, heavy debris and unreliable phone service has made it particularly hard to reach some of the mountain communities her\par \par \uc2 MAN: There`s still hundreds of people that were getting reports are stranded.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And hundreds of thousands remain without power and running water.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D-NC): If you need help, we are working around the clock to reach you. We`re working together to open more major routes to push supplies forward.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And Governor Cooper added that there`s now about 1,100 people who are housed in 29 shelters across the state -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Lilia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 People all over the US are reaching out to the victims of Helene with offers of help. You can go to CBSMornings.com to learn what you can do or check out CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  or X.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in just a moment, DNC chair, \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , we`ll talk about last night`s vice presidential debate and what`s next in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Last night`s vice presidential debate, likely the last debate before the presidential election, Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  were largely civil to one another, but there were stark contrasts between their views on everything from immigration to the economy and who is the larger threat to American democracy.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  for more on the debate and his reaction.\par \par \uc2 It was civil. It was cordial.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2  (\uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  chairman): It was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But there is daylight between the two parties.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : A lot of daylight.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , who I`ve known for almost 20 years now, was definitely Minnesota Nice last night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a little wobbly in the beginning, though, Jaime, were you worried?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : But, you know, listen, on any debate stage like that, when you get on for the first time, you can -- you can have the butterflies.\par \par \uc2 But when it really counted, when he really started getting into the issues, we saw the differences between him and JD Vance. And JD Vance was as slick as an eel last night.\par \par \uc2 Line -- every time he opened his mouth, a lie fell out. I mean, on abortion, on climate change, on all of these issues, gun violence, right?\par \par \uc2 But what was really, really important? There are two moments that stick out for me. The first moment is on the discussion on abortion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : And the governor basically saying, you know, for a woman to have control of her body should not have to be dependent on which state she lives in. Right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Men can control their own bodies. It doesn`t matter -- the three of us can do whatever we want with our bodies. But why can`t Gayle? Why can`t women have complete control over their bodies?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Why should they have to ask \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or some right-wing Supreme Court in order to do so?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, Jaime, we can sit here and talk about the policy differences --\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- and political scientists think about , strategists too. But when I go out and talk to voters, a lot of people are just picking up on tone, style, presentation, and you`re right, JD Vance was a smoother operator here.\par \par \uc2 JD Vance or \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  came in, he was the most liked candidate, arguably on any ticket, most favorable. But do you think he stuck the landing on that?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Well, listen, when looking at the \uc2 Google\uc2  Trends last night, they weren`t looking at who was smoother on the debate stage? They were looking at abortion.\par \par \uc2 It was the number one searched topic every, every state, all 50 states, number one searched topic, because that is what really matters to people. Can you control your own bodies? Do you have freedom in this country?\par \par \uc2 There`s only one party that is fighting for the freedom of all Americans, and that`s the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s talk about the campaign. I expect you to say it`s going well.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think she`s going to win. I expect you to say that, but recent polling shows that there`s a disconnect with many Black men. I actually know some of them that say they`re going to go for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Is the party doing anything to reengage that part of the electorate?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Well, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And are you worried about it?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : I`m not worried about it. But you know, you always worry about every election, but you make sure you`re not taking anybody for granted. You make sure that you`re talking to people about the issues that are important.\par \par \uc2 For the last three-and-a-half years, I`ve been going crisscrossing this country, having discussions with Black men, with Latino men about the issues that are important to them, about security, making sure that they can protect their families, economic security, but also physical security.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What about voter suppression? Many people are worried about that.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Voter suppression is a huge issue and we should worry about it, because it`s something part of the tactics that the Republican Party has.\par \par \uc2 I, as Democratic Party chair want everybody to vote, and I want them to vote Democratic, but I believe that fundamentally everyone should have the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 We have built, at the DNC, the largest voter protection program that we`ve ever had in our history, because we know part of the tactics, we see it in Georgia, we see it in a lot of other states, of the Republican Party to put up barriers to make it more difficult for people to vote.\par \par \uc2 If you -- and I want to say to -- I don`t know where the camera is, but --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All over.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : All over, all over.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just look at Vlad.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : But I want folks to understand this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : I don`t care if you`re a Democrat, Republican, or Independent. You run into any trouble at the ballot box, you go to your precinct and they say that you can`t vote, text VOTE to 70888. We have lawyers on the ready.\par \par \uc2 And again, I don`t care your party affiliation. Every eligible American should have their right to vote count.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jaime, let me just ask you. Vance was factchecked on the mistruths he was spreading about Haitian immigrants eating pets in Springfield, Ohio. Do you think the damage, though, has already been done with that type of rhetoric, and how do you in the last couple of weeks of this race combat that?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Well, it`s racist, it`s bigoted. Think about this, to lie on people who are your constituents, these people are legal in Ohio, and to lie about this? I don`t think there`s a bigger -- a bigger slight than you can put on somebody that is supposedly a public servant.\par \par \uc2 And Vance stood on that stage and lied about those people and there are consequences to that. When little kids have to go to school and are scared to go to school because of bomb threats, when businesses have to shutter because, you know, they`re scared of people attacking them and the Republican mayor said, that`s a lie. The Republican governor said that`s a lie, said Senator, stop saying it and he continued to do that.\par \par \uc2 And looked in the camera and said, you know what? Yes, I`m lying. I`m trying to make a point. No, we don`t do that in America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , thank you very much for coming by.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 : Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have Governor Walz come by as well. I think that`s imperative, which Vance has been on TV a lot. We`d love to see the governor as well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He doesn`t need to be nervous. He can come by.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Come on by.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to have the stories you`ll talking about and "What to Watch." Right now, though, it is 7:43, time to check a local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a lot going on in our eight o`clock hour today, including the Barefoot Contessa. That`s author Ina Garten, who`s known for her recipes that most people she says can make. Oh, we will see.\par \par \uc2 Now, she is sharing some very personal stories in a new memoir about her life, and she would join us in the studio to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our series, "Three Meals" takes us to the battleground state of Georgia. Tony hits the road for some good old southern cooking and great conversation with voters about the election. Two of my favorite things. Good food and good conversation.\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour, but first, Klaus and I are heading over to the table --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Head on over. You`re walking again. I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- where Vlad and Tony have already --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Georgia is my fallback state by the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- taken up residence.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, your fallback state from --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The food is great. It`s affordable. You can buy a home. You`re going to see it.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All night long.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Who doesn`t love this song?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all love this song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rascal Flatts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we`re going to be talking about Rascal Flatts --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love Rascal Flatts.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But I want to begin by paying tribute to a 1970s, 1980s, 1990s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: -- forever TV icon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ah, yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Roots"/Wolfer Productions)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the most iconic roles in television, played by that man, John Amos, famous for that role in "Roots," "Good Times."\par \par \uc2 He has died at the age of 84. His family revealed that he died of natural causes back in August, but let`s just break down this incredible career.\par \par \uc2 He earned an Emmy nomination for his powerful portrayal of the adult Kunta Kinte in the groundbreaking 1977 miniseries. You can go on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and find clips of him if you haven`t watched the entire thing, the scenes when Fiddler dies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And he expresses that Fiddler is finally free, one of my favorite scenes.\par \par \uc2 His career spanned more than five decades. He broke barriers though first as James Evans, Sr. on "Good Times."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Good Times"/Tandem Productions)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of the first programs showing a two-parent Black family on television.\par \par \uc2 He advocated for positive African American representation in entertainment throughout his career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Roots"/Wolfer Productions)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: James Evans, Sr. represented something that most people have never seen on television before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A father who was very involved in the life of his kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it changed the narrative of what people thought a Black family in America was at the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A Black family was.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And he wasn`t just an actor on that show. He`s talked about how the all-White writing team would have scripts, and then he`d have disagreements, and he would say,. "How long you been Black?"\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 And finally --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kind of shuts down the conversation, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`ve been watching Mary Tyler Moore. He played Gordy -- of course cast her on that show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I forgot about that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Remember about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And King Jaffe Jafar -- James Earl Jones just died a few weeks ago, and now, Mr. McDowell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. We saw --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Big Mick.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was surprised here he died in August.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He died in August.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m glad they`re letting us know, but it was sad to hear that news yesterday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He will be missed. What an icon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s talk about Rascal Flatts reuniting to mark its 25th anniversary. The group`s "Life is a Highway" Tour will hit 21 cities next year.\par \par \uc2 Rascal Flatts had planned a farewell tour in early 2020, but the COVID pandemic sidelined it. Band members said they took time to focus on themselves, and now they`re back together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tickets will go on sale Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay. Yay. Yay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, every year, we highlight Fat Bear Week, a head-to-head competition that celebrates Alaskan bears preparing to hibernate.\par \par \uc2 This year, the start of online voting was delayed due to a different head- to-head battle, one with life and death consequences. Horrified fans watched a live stream video as a male bear known as Number 469, killed a female bear called 402. The fight happened in the waters of Alaska`s Brooks River.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Katmai National Park and Preserve says, "National parks like Katmai protect not only the wonders of nature, but also the harsh realities." The statement said, "Each bear seen on the webcams is competing with others to survive."\par \par \uc2 So online voting begins today and goes through next Tuesday. Like you said before, Tony, what are bears?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re wild animals.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They are wild.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They appear to be wild animals.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They be bearing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Were you watching the webcam live?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t think I would be able to watch that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, \uc2 NFL\uc2  star, Colin Kaepernick and his wife, TV and radio host, Nessa Diab will be in studio talking about a new children`s book.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN (Barefoot Contessa): Perfect. Browned on that side, you can tell if the chicken bounces back, it`s perfectly done. Onto the sheet pan.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, I`m hungry. That`s Ina Garten on her hit TV show, "Barefoot Contessa."\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ll talk with her about her new memoir and the secret to developing a successful and tasty recipe.\par \par \uc2 Nam.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Tony takes a road trip for our "Three Meals" series, He ate well, we like when that happens. And he talked about what matters to voters in the Great State of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Speaking of eating well, author and chef, Ina Garten is here. She`s known as the Barefoot Contessa. And her new memoir, she writes about overcoming the sour notes in her past to build a very sweet present.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab will also be here first on CBS MORNINGS. Their new kids` book is all about activism and the importance of education.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Something they know about, but first here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): The winner needs to be the winner. This has got to stop. It`s tearing our country apart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The vice presidential nominees clash over former president Trump`s actions after the 2020 election at their only debate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): It`s really rich for democratic leaders to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a unique threat to democracy when he peacefully gave over power on January the 20th.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Iran launches its largest ever missile attack on Israel, pushing the Middle East closer to all out war.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: CBS News flies with the North Carolina National Guard during a mission to rescue survivors stranded after Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 MARIYA KARPENKO: Grateful to be alive. Grateful that everyone is safe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Stephen Colbert highlights the differences between JD Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: It was the ultimate showdown between two very different Midwestern vibes. Hillbilly versus Minnesota nice. Attack dog versus folk hero. America`s dad versus America`s dad. Could you come pick me up? JD Vance just walked in here. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A lot of material for the late night comics. A lot of material.\par \par \uc2 So let`s begin with that debate last night with what`s likely the last debate before election day. Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  clashed on policy and about the character required to lead the country. But the exchanges were largely civil.\par \par \uc2 On gun violence, for example, they`re miles apart on how to address the issue, but they agreed that it`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): We know, unfortunately, that a lot of kids are going to experience this terrible epidemic of gun violence. And of course, our hearts go out to the families that are affected by this terrible stuff. And we do have to do better.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I 100% believe that Senator Vance hates it when these kids, it`s abhorrent and it breaks your heart. I agree with that. But that`s not far enough.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ed O`Keefe was there watching the debate and he`s here now with us. Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Good morning, how are you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You live to tell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So it`s interesting, the CBS News poll found 88% of debate watchers said it was generally positive. And that sort of gives me a little insight into this world where people say that, oh, they like to see the WWE aspect of politics. They don`t, if you believe this kind of polling.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Yeah, and look, we fully anticipated that this could become a slugfest. Both sides had channeled that they were prepared for that. But instead it became like two Midwestern dads cracking a beer in the middle of the street between their houses discussing the finer details of gun control policy.\par \par \uc2 And that`s kind of what, frankly, after so many years of acrimony, I think you`re right. A lot of Americans were kind of okay with that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I took solace in it.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My blood pressure was falling. Key issue, they did get into abortion. \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  from the DNC was here saying that was a big issue for them. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tweeted or truthed on his platform during the debate and he said he would veto a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s something new from him. And JD Vance also tried to talk about this issue in a new way. What stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Well, what he was saying is, while we don`t necessarily depart from our current viewpoints on the idea that this should be a state by state concern, we as Republicans have to do a better job of discussing this issue and embracing and appreciating the concern and the risks that women face.\par \par \uc2 That, to me, was a white flag that says, yeah, we`re not winning on this issue anymore. We have the potential to lose a lot of voters over this. Sorry, we`re going to try to do better in at least discussing it.\par \par \uc2 But the position didn`t change. State by state, Roe v. Wade is gone. Let the states decide. The bigger deal to me was that he now, on social media at least, and let`s see if he ever verbalizes it, says that he would veto an abortion ban. The reason why, and we`ve discussed this before, if that`s now his position, go ask his evangelical base what they think of that and whether there`s any potential they stay home or become less enthusiastic about supporting him and Republican congressional candidates that might agree.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Super interesting. I can`t get that image out of my mind of the two dads cracking a beer and talking to his neighbors, but then what really matters is when they go back into their own houses and they shut the door, what do they say about one another?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why I ought to --\par \par \uc2 ED O` KEEFE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know, they like each other. Ed, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in our series, "Three Meals" I hit the road in the battleground state of Georgia to talk with voters about the presidential candidates and the issues. And I also enjoy some southern cooking and a lot of sweet tea, too much in fact.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8.04, time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player Colin Kaepernick and his wife Nessa Diab share their new children`s book. This is their first TV interview together and we`re looking forward to this.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every week until the election, we are driving through key states to talk with voters for our series "Three Meals." And this morning, I go to Georgia, where voters backed a Republican in every single presidential race dating all the way back to 1996 until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  edged out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But the margin of victory there essentially amounted to an overflow crowd at a minor league baseball game. I played in front of bigger crowds in this margin. The latest CBS News poll shows Trump and Vice President Harris, it is very close. They are in a statistical tie in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Top issues in that poll, the economy and rising prices. But as I found out, the people of Georgia have a whole lot of other stuff on their minds.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Peaches")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t we all, Justin Bieber? But what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  most want out of the peach state.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I`m thrilled to be back in the great state of Georgia. I love Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is votes.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D) (Vice president of the United States): Good evening, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A Harris victory here depends on running up the margins in and around Metro Atlanta, where the population is booming. And starting the day over coffee at West Egg Cafe in the city`s West Side neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We just moved to Georgia from California.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We learned a lot of newcomers like Marion and Brian Smith (sp?), they tend to vote blue.\par \par \uc2 Would \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  be better for you economically?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN SMITH (Georgia voter): I wouldn`t vote for him even if, you know, if it meant another hundred grand in my pocket.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Hello, Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won this state in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So you`re voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 There are warning lights for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ISAAC SIMPSON (Georgia voter): I feel like he aligns more with America and American values than she does.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Isaac Simpson (sp?) explained those Trump values while I made some gains on a delicious potato biscuit and omelet.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC SIMPSON: I just know that once you start doing away with things like the second amendment and altering it to your own, what you think is sensible, then it`s a problem because then who`s the arbiter of that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yet over by the counter.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WALKER (Georgia voter): A lot more of the country became more hopeful once Kamala became a nominee.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some counter points from Christopher Walker (sp?).\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WALKER: Democrats are focused on actual issues that are of concern to Americans and solving the systemic problems that we all face.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Afterward, we hit the road and made some U-turns along the way.\par \par \uc2 Heading into the suburbs of Forsyth County.\par \par \uc2 Part of a Republican stronghold that the Trump campaign is counting on. And sure enough, as we settled into the station house off US 19 for lunch.\par \par \uc2 Oh thank you.\par \par \uc2 Over a chef`s salad and shrimp and grits.\par \par \uc2 MAN (Georgia voter): To me, he`s more for the people, the hard working people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We heard a whole lot of Trump support.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Georgia voter): I did like my lifestyle when Trump was elected. I liked that groceries were much more affordable, gas was much more affordable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mixed with general exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Georgia voter): I can overlook some of the nonsense rhetoric that comes out of his mouth. I mean, ultimately I feel like he`s worried about our country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: His talk of border control is what motivates Republicans like Butch and Judy Harrell (sp?).\par \par \uc2 BUTCH HARRELL (Georgia voter): You`re paying for all these immigrants, you`re keeping them up in a hotel, you`re giving them food, you`re giving them health care, free.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You feel like the border has been too open under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JUDY HARRELL (Georgia voter): Oh, absolutely. It`s just a mess.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, how are you?\par \par \uc2 A few tables away and with a serious sugar buzz starting to get the best of me.\par \par \uc2 I should not have gotten the full sweet tea.\par \par \uc2 MATT HUTZ (Georgia voter): Half and half, you got to always go half and half.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We met Matt Hutz (sp?).\par \par \uc2 MATT HUTZ: We had a great economy with the person that I voted for.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who says there`s very little the Harris campaign could do to scoop away his or his mother Sharon`s vote for the man they`ve supported since 2016.\par \par \uc2 So he was like, I think I`m gonna vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and you were like, honey, me too, and then you guys brought it in for a hug.\par \par \uc2 MATT HUTZ: Honey, me too. This is my mom.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yeah, you`re right. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 In need of a little time for the sugar to find its way out of my system, we headed south.\par \par \uc2 Down to Peach County, one of the so-called pivot counties in Georgia, that voted for Obama twice before voting for Trump twice. They love their two for ones here.\par \par \uc2 Did you know two people can eat for $13 today?\par \par \uc2 As we found an irresistible deal at the 478 Country Buffet in Byron. As you might expect in this purple county and in this restaurant.\par \par \uc2 What`s good here?\par \par \uc2 Quite a few folks still mulling their choices.\par \par \uc2 So you`re undecided. What more do you need to know?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN (Georgia voter): I don`t know yet. I just kind of want to hear like all the options, like everything that both of them have to say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Marcotte Anderson (sp?) knows who she wants.\par \par \uc2 What is the biggest issue that makes you support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MARCOTTE ANDERSON (Georgia voter): Right now, abortion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 MARCOTTE ANDERSON: Yeah, Because I`ve been put in scenarios that if I didn`t get a choice, it would not work for me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But she doesn`t always feel like she can express that support in public.\par \par \uc2 I can`t put nothing Harris related on my car. If not, it might be vandalized.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Has that happened before?\par \par \uc2 MARCOTTE ANDERSON: No, but I don`t want.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You don`t want to tempt anybody.\par \par \uc2 And yet.\par \par \uc2 Hold on to your Biden-Harris hat.\par \par \uc2 Consider the story of Greg Hall (sp?), proudly sporting his support for Harris. He`s a Democrat who gave his Republican neighbors a ride to the polls last election, raising an interesting hypothetical this time.\par \par \uc2 You, Greg, a Democrat, drive a Republican to the polls, thereby putting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the top and making him president again. If you knew that that was what you would be doing, would you still do it?\par \par \uc2 GREG HALL (Georgia voter): I would make sure they get to the polls because I believe in the right to vote and the responsibility to vote more than I believe in my own candidate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo to Greg there. Greg`s a great American. You missed a great little sound bite at the end there.\par \par \uc2 So I met a voter in Georgia who drove his Republican neighbor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I saw it back there. Oh yeah. Yes, yes, yes, I saw it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I just feel, you know, we`ve talked about civility and people being cordial again. I think that`s good too. We`re all Americans, we`re still neighbors. We have political differences, but drive each other to the polls.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah, well done, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Ahead --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done, Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah, well done, Tony. Oh, well that`s always a given. I don`t even --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Remember, half and half on the sweet tea.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Half and half on the sweet tea, brother.\par \par \uc2 Another great conversation. The barefoot contestant, Ina Garten, is here to talk about her new memoir. We`re coming back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Barefoot Contessa" Food Network)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She makes it look so easy. That`s Ina Garten decorating her famous flag cake on her Emmy award winning cooking show, "The Barefoot Contessa." I love that. The self-taught cook is the author of 13 bestselling cookbooks. Did you hear that? Number 13.\par \par \uc2 Now she`s opening up about her life. The new memoir, "Be Ready When the Luck Happens." She reveals details from a very difficult childhood, her love story with her husband, Jeffrey. I love that part. And burnout working in the White House, which, by the way, led to her very successful career change.\par \par \uc2 We`re very happy to say that Ina Garten is alive and in color in the studio this morning. We`re so glad to have you here, Ina.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN (Author, "Be Ready When the Luck Happens"): I`m so happy to be here. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Set the scene for us. You`re at this job at the White House, a little doll. And you see an ad in the paper that says what? No cooking experience, no nothing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was just a tiny ad. It was the first day it was advertised. And it`s in the business opportunity section of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " where they like advertise like dry cleaners in the Bronx and frozen yogurt stores. And it said especially food store in the Hamptons. And I thought, oh, that sounds really interesting.\par \par \uc2 And I went home and Jeffrey said to me, I said to him, I just I`m not a government person. I don`t like working on policy. Nothing happens in my job. And he said, just do what you love. If you love it, you`ll be really good at it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. But you have --\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: You know, I saw this ad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes,\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: And said, let`s go look at it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s go look at it. But you have no experience cooking, no experience running a store.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: I`d never had employees. I never worked in the food business. I mean, I knew how to make 12 brownies for my friends, but I had no idea.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Brownies, by the way, you say the homemade brownie is a culinary equivalent of love. Isn`t that nice? I so believe that food is love for me.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: It really is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, it`s true.\par \par \uc2 You also talk about your childhood here, which involved physical abuse, verbal abuse. Whenever I ask people questions like this, I know you write it down in a book and you pick the words you wanted to use to express it. What do you want to get into here? I mean, and why did you want to get into that?\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: You know, I didn`t tell the story of my childhood. Certainly people have had worse childhoods than I did. But I just wanted people to know that you could come from a really difficult childhood and rewrite your own story. You know, that doesn`t have to be the rest of your life. That my parents were really critical. They were really harsh. My father was, would hit us. It was it was really rough. And I kind of tried to disappear.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know it was more than critical and harsh. Your mom said you`ll never amount to anything. Your dad said no one will ever love you.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: Can you imagine telling a teenage girl, nobody will ever love you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: But under this great thing. I walk up Madison Avenue and somebody leans in almost every block and says, I love you. And I think it`s this great cosmic joke. And I think say to myself, I guess he was wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. He was wrong.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the things I loved about your story, and this is a page in the book where you write about being your authentic self and what you know works. I think anybody who`s in front of a camera will have those feelings where some marketing exec will say, you need to do this.\par \par \uc2 And you say, no, I know what connects to the viewers. You write this that while the cameras were rolling, I picked up and took a bite, actually chewed it. "Delicious" I said. Director yell "Cut. You can`t talk with your mouth full. Martha would never do that."\par \par \uc2 What did you say to yourself in that?\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: I said, I`m not Martha.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: I`m Ina. And I want to do it my way. And there was this push pull of doing it. I`m just making sure that I that it was who I am. And I just I mean, you know this better than I do. It`s the only thing that works is if you if you don`t try and act like somebody else on TV.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One of my favorite work, one of my favorite parts, it was Jeffrey, because the love story that you and Jeffrey have, I want to know what he taught you, what you`ve learned from him, because you`ve been married a gazillion years. And at one point you decided to separate because you thought this isn`t working for me right now.\par \par \uc2 Tell me what you`ve learned --\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: You know, in my 20s, because I`d had such a difficult childhood. I always think that he kind of brought me up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: He taught me how to be in the world. He`s happy. He`s grounded. He`s smart. He`s funny. And he really taught me that if we the two of us want two different things, it`s not like he gets to choose or I get to choose.\par \par \uc2 Let`s figure out how we can both do what we want to do. And he`s just extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You found love in your life.\par \par \uc2 I`m with you, Gayle. I do think food is love.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: Yeah, I do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s so amazing going back to that comment in your childhood, no one will ever love you. It`s like not only have you found love in your life, but by teaching people to cook, they can say I love you to the people in their life.\par \par \uc2 And it just it goes on and on. It`s a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN: And that`s what I love about writing cookbooks. It`s not like I`m giving people something. I`m giving the tools to do something for themselves. And when they put down a coconut cupcakes in front of their friends.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your Boston cream pie is on my bucket list.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Ina Garner. The name of the book, "Be Ready When the Luck Happens" is in store now. "Talk of the Table" is next. The commander is looking to add to their winning streak this Sunday when they take on the \uc2 Cleveland Browns\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning. Ms. King.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. My "Talk of the Table" is the WNBA playoffs. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I was there last night to watch the New York Liberty move one step closer to the finals. The \uc2 Liberty\uc2  are now up two games to none against the Las Vegas Aces in that playoff series. Now this is a rematch of last year`s WNBA finals where the Aces beat the Liberty to win their second title in a row.\par \par \uc2 League MVP A`ja Wilson, adore her is hoping for game three win at home on Friday to keep Las Vegas alive in the best of five series. And as much as I love the lovely A`a, A`ja I have to go with the hometown team. It was so exciting.\par \par \uc2 At one point toward the end, it looked like they were going to go into overtime and all I could think of was please don`t go in overtime I got to get home to watch the debate please.\par \par \uc2 Are you aware that you can watch \uc2 CBS\uc2  Live in the car?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes I am.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re aware. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On the CBS News App.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 CBS\uc2  Live in the car.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I finished watching the debate, got home and then watched, finished the debate and all the analysis, so I was up very late last night. But it`s worth it staying up late on a school night for that --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For the for the women`s team, I love these guys and for the debate last night.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m really, really hoping that this is the year.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think a lot of people are hoping along with you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also one more thing at the end of the game the women come over and actually thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh no way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never seen that before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not everybody.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not all 25,000 --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People who got good seats. People who got them good seats.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe the first row there. How long is the thank you line?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up in the air going A`ja! A`ja!\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve never seen anybody come over and thank you for coming to the game that`s the only point I make it. Vlad!\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s celebrity row down there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My "Talk of the Table" is about one of the favorite shows in America not just around here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m talking about "Survivor." It`s been on \uc2 CBS\uc2  for almost a quarter century and so we decided to do something fun. We posted a video on social media asking Jeff Probst, the host of Survivor, out of Gayle, Nate, and Tony, who would win Survivor?\par \par \uc2 People sounded off in the comments. Don`t underestimate Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, you can.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s from your producer Katie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh Katie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who I guess still has a job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you talking about Willis?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Another said my man Nate he sees everything from every angle.\par \par \uc2 Another comment said Tony would outsmart them all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this morning we can finally reveal Jeff`s opinion. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PROBST (Host, "Survivor"): While Nate and Tony and Gayle would make a great final three, sorry fellas, there is absolutely no question who wins in a version of Survivor. The Queen, Gayle, always.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Jeff is dead wrong. When I played that game, and I`m not just saying that because Tony`s here, I told everybody Tony would definitely win.\par \par \uc2 And Nate is very fit too. I get it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not about fit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate is very smart.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You got to be smart, you got to be intuitive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who would you pick? I`d definitely think Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As much as I would love to bask in this, I think a former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player is going to do better than me. You know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I appreciate the confidence.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know though Gayle, the game, Survivor is so popular because it`s the people who you don`t think would win.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it`s more about the athleticism. It`s more than just the athleticism.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t think, I wouldn`t even say it`s about the athleticism.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s the strategy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New season of Survivor, 47, right?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Season 47, tonight, 8/7 central, right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And surprise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thank Jeff for the vote of confidence.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Surprise, none of us are competing in it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is an only in America wild story. Something out of a sci-fi film.\par \par \uc2 An 81 year old man in Montana who`s been charged with federal felony wildlife crimes for cloning a giant foreign sheep here in the US for hunting purposes.\par \par \uc2 Let me try to explain this. So this 81 year old man, his name is Arthur Schubarth. He imported sperm, tissue and testicles from a Marco Polo sheep, the largest in the world. And then successfully took that material and made a full living sheep out of it. Then he crossbred that sheep with a Rocky Mountain bighorn sheep and created a hybrid sheep that he called the Montana Mountain King.\par \par \uc2 And the plan was to sell those, which he actually did, to people who wanted to stock preserves exclusively for hunting. The authorities were not fans of this move and they`ve given him a six month sentence.\par \par \uc2 The judge said this is to deter anyone from "changing the genetic makeup of the creatures on this earth."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like how you said he imported. I would love to see the image, the TSA image, of the importation of the testicles and the semen going through that machine. What it would show up on the screen. The guy, when I look at him, he`s like, "What the?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do you want to see that, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m just curious. Imagine what it would look like to that person.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well now that you have that image in your mind, here`s the other complication here. These are living animals now on the earth. I mean, he successfully created the clone. So people would be wondering, that would be the clone of the Marco Polo sheep that he created here, which is usually native to Kazakhstan, is going to go to a zoo. So that`s going to be living.\par \par \uc2 The other hybrid sheep are not going to be used for hunting. He`s got until the end of 2025 to sell them and the meat will be eaten by somebody. That`s what they say anyway. Just a crazy story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is a wild story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s very apologetic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Baaaad idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s what it said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s very apologetic, by the way, contrite.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re going to meet the founder of a group that helps give young women hope when they face a major health challenge that can affect their fertility.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for your local weather.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I just got that.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our series "Changing the Game" highlights extraordinary women. Did you hear that part? Extraordinary women making a difference. It`s Breast Cancer Awareness Month. We want to introduce you to one woman trying to make a difference for those fighting the disease. And boy, is she working overtime on this.\par \par \uc2 Her name is Amanda Rice. She`s a founder and CEO of Chick Mission. Think of that name. That`s going to be very relevant in just a second.\par \par \uc2 She`s a two-time breast cancer survivor and a skin cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two time, wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right, Tony, two-time.\par \par \uc2 Chick Mission helps young women who have been newly diagnosed with cancer preserve their fertility.\par \par \uc2 So this is when a person`s eggs are removed and saved before treatments that can cause infertility. Chick Mission works with 35 fertility partners in eight states, has helped families save more than $7 million in fertility preservation costs.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Rice, bravo, bravo to you and welcome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE (Breast cancer and skin survivor): Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did your own experience, number one I just I bow to you, three cancers before the age of 40. Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody`s looking out in your life for sure.\par \par \uc2 How did your own experience though lead to this?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Yeah I mean when you`re diagnosed with cancer as a young adult it is very isolating.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes and devastating.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Most of the people you know are postmenopausal, they`ve already lived these long lives thus far and it doesn`t mean it`s right to be diagnosed with cancer, it`s just very unique as a young woman especially.\par \par \uc2 I was a working woman in New York City working in finance and all of a sudden stopped in my tracks by those three words and ultimately I was one of the lucky ones. I was a woman who worked in finance, a white woman, a privileged woman who had access to great care and phenomenal doctors who told me about the risk of infertility.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately that`s something you have to think about before you even start treatment if you want to have biological kids one day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then when you went to freeze your eggs you found out that it was not covered by insurance, you had to pay for it and it`s very costly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Incredibly costly and I actually, I had fertility benefits at the time but when I went to freeze my eggs the insurer told me I wasn`t infertile yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How old were you then? How old were you?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: I was 38.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well so only 18 states offer to pay for fertility coverage. You`ve advocated for more states to require coverage for cancer patients but you`ve also been instrumental in changing the law in one state.\par \par \uc2 Tell us about that because that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Yeah I mean we there is a broader coalition across the country, different organizations that have band together. This past year we worked on Oklahoma with a group of survivors and in honor of somebody that we lost, Corinne.\par \par \uc2 And we worked in Oklahoma, found some authors and ultimately were able to change the law this year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was gonna say talk to us directly about this mission of Chick Mission where the name comes from.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the name.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah the name and then the future of it.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Yeah so we, we`re a triple threat mission. So we give need- based grants as soon as you`re diagnosed it`s a whirlwind of information. So one of the things hopefully you are told is that there may be an impact on your future fertility.\par \par \uc2 So we cover these grants for women that are aged 18 to 40. Any type of cancer but predominantly given the prevalence of breast cancer, those are those are our patients. They are given a small window to freeze their eggs. We don`t have them go out of pocket. We reimburse the practice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow Amanda.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: So we can just take that burden off of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So someone listening to you right now is sitting in tears going oh my gosh maybe she can help me. Who`s eligible? How do they get in contact with you? How does that part work?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: So we, we receive DMs on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . We get contacted on a daily basis from those that have just been diagnosed or have completed treatment. So we say reach out to us. There are a ton of resources.\par \par \uc2 They`re not all in one place but if you live in the eight states where we currently work, you`re diagnosed, you haven`t started fertility threatening treatment yet, you make under $150,000 per year. They`re need-based.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: We`re gonna we`re gonna cover your fertility preservation.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And your health today before we go?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: I`m great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Six years in remission.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can see how important this mission is to you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now we understand why. Amanda Rice, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA RICE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guests are Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab. We`ll talk to them about their new children`s book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There they are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "We Are Free, You And Me." We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Activist and former \uc2 NFL\uc2  quarterback Colin Kaepernick and TV and radio host Nessa Diab are here with us now for their first TV interview together. As a 49ers player Kaepernick protested police violence by kneeling during the national anthem, the couple co-founded the Know Your Rights Camp in 2015 with the mission of advancing the well-being of black and brown communities through education and self-empowerment.\par \par \uc2 Now they are releasing their new children`s book. It is called. "We Are Free, You & Me" Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab, welcome, so good to have you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK (Co-Author, "We Are Free, You & Me"): Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Best-selling authors in --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 So I was just saying to you before during the commercial as a new dad, a girl dad, I absolutely love this book. Why did you choose to write it together? What was that impetus?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Yeah --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whose idea was it? What Colin?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: No, it`s collective conversation. We, at this point I had written another children`s book, a graphic novel. We just had our daughter.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB (Co-Author, "We Are Free, You & Me"): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: And we`re also looking at the work we were doing at Know Your Rights Camp. We`re like, how do we bring this message to a younger audience that they can feel that they have agency, that they can be courageous like they really get to take control of what their future and destiny looks like.\par \par \uc2 And we created this book around the ten points of Know Your Rights Camp.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: So that`s what led to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, Nessa, you said you would have loved a book like this when you were younger.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: well, I just feel like it`s so the message is so easy to understand and it`s just a reminder that wow I do have the right to be happy, to be healthy, to do what I want to do and just to have that confidence.\par \par \uc2 And not saying like the other books I read, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That it didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: But in a different way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: But this is, it`s a lot of fun and you know, I just, I love that a lot of everything we symbolize within the Know Your Rights Camp, you get to see it. You know, all of our volunteers come from different parts of the country in the world to come and give back and you know, it`s the community feeling which you get to see throughout the book and that`s really important.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The artwork is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was gonna say that, the illustration.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The outwork is so great. I just want to read very quickly. The Know Your Rights Camp ten points. You have the right to be free, healthy, brilliant, safe, loved, courageous, alive, trusted and educated. And you look at this list and you go. Well, that`s pretty self- evident, but it`s not in many communities around this country.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: Right, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: And that that`s been our experience and as we work with youth, we`ve seen that show up with them where they may not feel like they have the right to be safe or the right to be healthy based upon circumstances or environment or situation.\par \par \uc2 And we want to reiterate that message to them so they can take that and be like, you know what? I can look at this differently, I can go out and try to accomplish and achieve these things and pursue them and do that with the best of intentions of how we make our community better, how we make the people around us better and to be able to have a show up with this, like this, with the amazing artwork from Gladys, it is incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I appreciate you earlier trying to keep the peace in the children`s literature world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like how she did that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you know kids are honest and kids are frank.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So your little one, when you read this to your daughter, how`d she feel about it? What`d she like?\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: Okay, so Colin won`t say it, I will. So she loves to see Dada. So anytime there`s Dad, that`s her favorite page. So actually there`s an Easter egg in the back towards the end in the spread. Actually what`s in the backdrop right now, you see Colin and myself and our baby in a stroller --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yeah.\par \par \uc2 NESSA DIAB: A background which we absolutely prefer, saying hello to other family and friends, part of the Know Your Rights Camp, but she loves seeing her dad. Her face lights up, it`s all about Dada.\par \par \uc2 I mean I --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And her mom too, who`s a radio superstar.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who`s been on the air 20 years, she`s a legend in this town when it comes to radio, and I know you`re both very involved in your advocacy work.\par \par \uc2 And Colin, it says that you were still getting up every morning, you were still working out, you were still practicing, and when I read that I went "Really?" and then I thought "Why?" Is it because you do really want to play football again or are you trying to make a point?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Both.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Both.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: I think it`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: I mean, when I look at my career, it was 15 years of work and dedication before I ever stepped foot on an \uc2 NFL\uc2  field. And to have that passion and chase it for that long, you don`t just give that up, you don`t just walk away. You chase that, you pursue it, and to your point on "Do you make a point around that?" There will never be an instance where I`m not ready. And there will never be an instance where "Oh, it`s because he doesn`t want to anymore."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You want them to know that you want to.\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But it`s such a sad situation in so many ways because you clearly could do it, and this is what you worked your life to do, and then to have it taken away because you feel it`s important to express yourself on this other issue, which is protected by the way, and which you have a right to express yourself on.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever feel sadness when you pick up that football and wish you could have done two things at the same time?\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAEPERNICK: Well, I think that`s part of the point of like continuing to pursue and chase is, I actually had one of the youth at one of our Know Your Rights Camp ask me that. It was like, you know, I love playing football, but I also really care about my community, and I saw what happened. How do I do both?\par \par \uc2 And for me, I`ve had so many conversations like that, and my response is always, "You don`t have to choose one or the other. You can do both." And I think that`s the environment that we should be pushing forward with.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You both are amazing. Colin Kaepernick and Nessa Diab, thank you both for being here. The book is called "You Are Free, You & Me" on sale now wherever you buy your books.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Best-selling authors in the house.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020241002eka200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For September 30, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Imtiaz Tyab, Hassan Nasrallah, Chris Livesay, Nancy Cordes, Scott  MacFarlane, Skyler Henry, Jon LaPook, Kris Van Cleave, Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,816 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANITA CROWDER (North Carolina Resident): It breaks your heart.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: Hurricane Helene`s catastrophic toll in the Southeast, more than 100 killed, homes, businesses and entire towns destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It really hurts to see such devastation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Israel warns of an imminent ground invasion in Lebanon, as the United States sends thousands more troops to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER (\uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  Spokesman): We want to ultimately see a diplomatic resolution to this conflict.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And with just one day until the CBS News vice presidential debate, how the candidates are preparing to face off.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (D-Minnesota) (Vice Presidential Candidate): Looking forward to -- to tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The CBS Evening News starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and thank you for joining us from the CBS News debate stage. This is where vice presidential candidates J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will meet in their only debate before the election. We will have much more on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But we want to begin with the human tragedy caused by Hurricane Helene. More than 120 people are dead across six states; 600 are still missing. And, today, the president`s homeland security adviser said the death toll could climb dramatically.\par \par \uc2 But the full scope of the 600-mile disaster is still not clear. Helene crashed into Florida as a Category 4 hurricane with storm surge above 15 feet. Then it was onto Georgia, where the governor compared the destruction to a bomb going off. But the highest death toll is in North Carolina, hundreds of miles from landfall, where the emergency is still unfolding tonight.\par \par \uc2 Many communities need food and fresh water. And with roads cut off, some volunteers are actually using mule teams to bring supplies to Asheville, North Carolina. You heard that right.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Skyler Henry is just outside Asheville.\par \par \uc2 And, Skyler, what is the situation like on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Norah. Good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 We were here as military personnel airdropped supplies to Swannanoa. It`s another small community just outside of Asheville that saw significant damage following Helene`s destruction. The people here say they will need all the help they can get as they work to recover.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voice-over): The Southeast is suffering. Days after Hurricane Helene`s hit, nowhere appears to be hit harder than Western North Carolina, where some areas received more than two feet of rain. Roughly 600 people are still unaccounted for.\par \par \uc2 LONNIE EASTRIDGE (Ashe County, North Carolina, Resident): But this is just hard to understand. I mean, this is just massive. This will take years to clean up, if it`s ever.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The city of Asheville and the area around it has been largely cut off since Friday. Cell service is mostly out. Electricity won`t be restored for days and help has been slow to arrive.\par \par \uc2 AARON HAYNIE (Mayor of Marshall, North Carolina): You have got a lot of family businesses that have been here since the turn of the century. They`re devastated. And they`re -- they`re just trying to salvage anything that they can -- they can get.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Ten miles east, the tiny mountain town of Swannanoa is named for the river that runs through it, the same river that washed it away.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And this town is devastated. But everybody focuses on big cities, not here. And we need help. We need food. And I know people are coming. I know they`re coming. And I know God`s got control. It really hurts to see such devastation.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Anita Crowder`s (sp?) husband had to use a chain saw to get into her family`s home. Her father died a week ago. And now they say the flood took what little they had left.\par \par \uc2 I can`t imagine that this -- it must be tough.\par \par \uc2 ANITA CROWDER (North Carolina Resident): It is to see everything you worked so hard for destroyed.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANITA CROWDER: It`s hard. These people worked hard for their -- what they have got. And it breaks your heart.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Now, federal officials still say that they are working to coordinate how to deliver the supplies. And that also includes 40 Starlink systems, an additional set of generators, around 30 of them.\par \par \uc2 They also say that they are trying to get some 60 pallets of water for those who may need them around the community -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It does break your heart to see what people are going through.\par \par \uc2 Skyler Henry, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is calling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a liar after the former president said the current one isn`t doing enough to help storm victims. Trump visited the storm zone in Georgia today, and Biden will be in North Carolina on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane is on the ground in Valdosta, Georgia, with more on the federal response and how this storm is affecting the presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): Helene`s path of destruction has forced the presidential candidates to change course. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  returned this afternoon from a stop in Nevada for a briefing at FEMA headquarters in Washington and spoke to both North Carolina and Georgia`s governors.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): We will continue to do everything we can to help you recover and to help you rebuild, no matter how long it takes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: As a subdued \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  visited a ravaged city in a battleground state, Valdosta, Georgia, where the power is still out, schools are closed and roofs, trees and signs litter the streets.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Helene turned out to be a big one, like just about the biggest that anyone`s seen.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Trump falsely claimed Georgia`s governor, Brian Kemp, couldn`t reach President Biden by phone, even though Kemp and the White House spoke this weekend.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has accused both of you of ignoring the disaster.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): He is lying. Let me get this straight. He`s lying. And the governor told him he was lying.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And Trump suggested \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  provide satellite service to help restore Wi-Fi, even though FEMA is already doing so.\par \par \uc2 We asked him if this historic storm had convinced Trump to consider a new approach on a key issue.\par \par \uc2 Do you think these storms may make you rethink climate change, get more aggressive about climate change?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: No, not too much.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Just down the street from Trump`s location, downtown is shuttered. With generator power, Candy Balmer`s (sp?) restaurant is serving meals for those who need them street-side.\par \par \uc2 CANDY BALMER (Georgia Resident): Our focus is on our community, not any celebrity or politician that comes in.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meanwhile, President Biden said he was commanding direct federal response, first from his home in Delaware, then back at the White House this weekend.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : My first responsibility is to get all the help needed to those impacted areas.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: With absentee ballots already being distributed, there`s another recovery issue to our north in North Carolina, where CBS News has learned the state Board of Elections had an emergency meeting about the damage, and said they will have a successful election no matter the damage -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you for being there.\par \par \uc2 At this time tomorrow, both vice presidential candidates will be right here at the CBS Broadcast Center in New York for their only debate.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes has new reporting on \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and J.D. Vance`s tactics with one day to go.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): Ohio Senator J.D. Vance arrived in New York at midday today, as Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  spent one last day prepping in Northern Michigan.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: How are you feeling about the debate?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This is where they will face off for 90 minutes tomorrow night. The Trump campaign says Vance will try to turn the focus to immigration.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE (R-Ohio) (Vice Presidential Candidate): We`re going to build the wall. We`re going to reimplement deportations.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Vance also plans to challenge Walz on his 24-year military career.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 TOM EMMER\uc2  (R-Minnesota): These were my \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  glasses.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Republican Congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  has been playing Governor Walz in Vance`s mock debates.\par \par \uc2 Have you urged him to stay away from this story about Haitian migrants eating cats and dogs in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 TOM EMMER\uc2 : He`s going to concentrate on the issues that matter most. We need to focus on the economy. We need to focus on the border. We need to focus on peace and stability around the world.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The latest CBS News poll finds more than half of the electorate considers Vance competent. And the same goes for Walz. But Democratic voters are more enthusiastic about their party`s pick for V.P. than Republicans are.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  has been playing the role of Vance in Walz`s debate prep. Democratic Senator Amy Klobuchar is Walz`s longtime Minnesota colleague.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-Minnesota): Whenever he can get in, I think, a little bit of his personality, I think that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It`s been reported that Governor Walz is nervous about this debate. Is that really true?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR: I think anyone that goes out on a national stage and says, oh, I don`t even think about it at all, if -- I think that that`s -- they`re not being honest.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Nancy Cordes is here with me.\par \par \uc2 So how are each of these candidates feeling about the debate that`s going to be right here?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Well, they are, of course, setting expectations sky-high for the other guy.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, today, the Trump campaign said, J.D. Vance has only been a politician for a couple of years, while \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has been in politics for 20 years, so he`s got the edge.\par \par \uc2 Then, you have got \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  saying, I`m simply a humble former public schoolteacher. J.D. Vance is a Yale Law School debater. He`s got the edge.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, Norah, that both of these men are very comfortable speaking extemporaneously about policy, which is part of what makes tomorrow night so interesting.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you very much for that preview.\par \par \uc2 And I will moderate tomorrow`s CBS News vice presidential debate, along with Margaret Brennan. Live coverage begins at 8:00 p.m. Eastern right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . And for special streaming coverage, head over to CBS News 24/7. That`s starting at 4:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Pentagon said it is sending thousands of additional American troops to the Middle East to bolster security. And CBS News has learned Israel has told the U.S. it intends to launch a limited ground offensive in Lebanon to follow up on its aerial assault that wiped out most of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s leadership.\par \par \uc2 We have two reports tonight from the region, beginning with Chris Livesay in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has confirmed Israeli commandos are conducting limited raids into Southern Lebanon, gathering intelligence on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s troop positions and its tunnel systems ahead of an expected ground invasion. We witnessed some of the buildup.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY (voice-over): The roar of engines is the beating drum of war. At this staging area in Northern Israel, soldiers prepare to put Israeli boots on the ground in Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Dozens and dozens of tanks, armored fighting vehicles and bulldozers, everything you would expect for a ground invasion, and the clearest sign yet that Israel is preparing to expand the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Defense Minister Yoav Gallant tells his troops: "We will employ all that is needed from the air, from the sea, and also from the land."\par \par \uc2 The mission? To push back \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and its onslaught of rockets that`s forced around 70,000 Israelis to flee their homes. Two reserve brigades have been called up to join the thousands of soldiers already deployed on the border, as the almost yearlong conflict is poised to enter a new and potentially much deadlier phase.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  was assassinated right here. Since then, Israel has intensified its attacks across Lebanon. We were on the edge of Dahiyeh, the seat of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s power, following a strike there.\par \par \uc2 Well, this was once a five-story building and it`s pretty much all but obliterated.\par \par \uc2 (BOOMING SOUND)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: OK, we don`t know exactly what that was, that clearly a very loud strike in the distance, and it really just underscores the situation across Southern Beirut as Israel continues to strike it heavily.\par \par \uc2 Over 1,000 people have been killed in just two weeks, and nearly one million people displaced, according to Lebanon`s prime minister, including 14-year-old Mustafa Al-Musso (sp?)...\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA AL-MUSSO (14 Years Old): This is birds.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: ... who shows us his pet birds he couldn`t bear to leave behind.\par \par \uc2 (MUSTAFA AL-MUSSO SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: "A rocket fell so close to us," he says.\par \par \uc2 You must have been so scared.\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA AL-MUSSO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: You were?\par \par \uc2 MUSTAFA AL-MUSSO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And, tonight, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is in disarray following Israel`s killing of so many of its leaders, but the militant group`s number two insist his fighters are ready to confront any Israeli ground invasion -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Imtiaz Tyab in Beirut and Chris Livesay in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 On the outskirts of Atlanta, around 90,000 people are still being warned to shut their windows and stay indoors. Fumes from a chemical plant fire created a plume of smoke and noxious chlorine gas that`s been visible for miles. The blaze broke out on Sunday at the plant, which makes swimming pool and spa supplies. No injuries have been reported.\par \par \uc2 A labor dispute may affect some of your everyday household purchases. That`s beginning tomorrow. That story is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A dockworkers strike is threatening America`s Atlantic and Gulf ports tonight. And if you`re looking for a safe harbor, there may not be one left. If the walkout goes on for more than a few days, consumers could start feeling the impact.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): Just hours from a potential strike that could idle ports from Maine to Texas, Miranda Sommer (sp?) is once again worried about her trailer and storage business.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA SOMMER (Business Owner): If the ports close, that means that our drivers aren`t working, our trucks are sitting, our warehouse employees aren`t going to have freight to pull for much longer than a week.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Her warehouse holds goods from the Port of Baltimore, which is one of the 14 ports that could see longshoremen walk off the job at midnight. We first met Sommer in April.\par \par \uc2 Would this normally be full of stuff?\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA SOMMER: Absolutely. And we would normally see people in and out, picking and packing and loading up trucks.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: When her business was nearly halted after the port was shut down for more than two months following the collapse of the Francis Scott Key Bridge.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA SOMMER: I think this was our final week. We said, oh, we`re getting everything in back. We had ships coming in. And now it`s just another -- another hit.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Negotiations have been stalled for months, primarily over wage and automation concerns. At least 25,000 dockworkers could strike, potentially impacting nearly half of the nation`s imports and 68 percent of containerized exports.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SAMUEL (Partner and Managing Director, \uc2 AlixPartners\uc2 ): There`s a little bit of buffer in the supply chain, but if it goes on for longer than days and it goes for weeks, the consumer could feel an impact.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Businesses have been preparing for months, bringing in extra inventory.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): People do not need to rush out to the grocery store and stockpile goods.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: New York`s governor urged calm.\par \par \uc2 Was this avoidable?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL: My job as the governor of the state of New York is to deal with the hands were dealt. You prepare for it. You hope it doesn`t hit. But when it does, you will at least say, we were ready.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Now, negotiations continue. And late today, the group representing the ports says both sides exchanged counterproposals today that may bring them closer to a deal.\par \par \uc2 The union for the longshoremen hasn`t commented. That deadline remains midnight, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kris Van Cleave, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next. How a former president is helping to change the way Americans think about end-of-life care.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: He`s already the oldest former president in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 But, tomorrow, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  will make more history when he turns 100. For the last 19 months, the 39th president has been in home hospice care.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, CBS News` chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook, examines how home hospice is evolving to serve America`s growing elderly population.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK (voice-over): This past spring, Joan Prum`s (sp?) advanced age and increasing frailty ushered in a new reality.\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM (98 Years Old): Well, at 98, it`s all new territory. So I think I`m doing pretty well.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Now, when you heard the word hospice, what did that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: I thought that, unless you were really infirm and bedridden, you wouldn`t be a candidate for hospice, but it turns out not to be true.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Health care teams provide comfort to hospice patients expected to live no longer than six months. As with all palliative care, trained professionals offer medication and treatments to decrease pain an increase quality of life.\par \par \uc2 But with hospice, attempts to cure a person`s illness are stopped.\par \par \uc2 Some people are afraid of that word. They think, oh, hospice, it means I`m...\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: Oh. Oh, indeed. Indeed. I just thought, well, any ment -- mentioning hospice, I says, that is the beginning of the end.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Founded 50 years ago, Connecticut Hospice was the first- in-the-nation and oversees Joan Prum`s care.\par \par \uc2 Barbara Pearce is the CEO.\par \par \uc2 So it`s really hard to predict how long somebody has to live. Who makes the decision?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA PEARCE (CEO, Connecticut Hospice): The patient. The patient and their families.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Routine hospice services average about $200 a day and are covered by Medicare, Medicaid, and most private insurance plans. But only about half of Medicare patients use it.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA PEARCE: Hospice care involves sitting down at the very beginning and saying, what are your goals of care? What do you want your life to look like in the next few months? And how can we help?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Hospice care can be given in nursing homes, assisted living facilities, and places like Connecticut Hospice. But about 99 percent choose to receive services at home, just like former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  and Joan Prum.\par \par \uc2 Did the fact that you had heard about hospice through President Carter help you there?\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: Oh, yes. It certainly made it the thing to do.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: What do you think President Carter and his family going public, saying he`s entering hospice, meant for the hospice movement?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA PEARCE: He`s given everybody permission to consider that as a reasonable option that doesn`t shorten their life, but does increase their comfort and fulfillment.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Joan Prum still cooks and can mostly navigate her Connecticut apartment on her own.\par \par \uc2 So you`re going to show me what`s in the refrigerator. Oh, it`s pretty stocked. Got fruit. What else is in here?\par \par \uc2 But she depends on hospice staff and other visitors to keep her safe and engaged.\par \par \uc2 Are there things that you want to stay around for?\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: Oh. Oh, mostly my grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: That`s it. How many grandchildren do you have?\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: Eight.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And...\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: And they`re spectacular.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Initially, you thought it was the beginning of the end?\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And now?\par \par \uc2 JOAN PRUM: I`m not over yet. I`m not through yet.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And with the help of home hospice, she is savoring every precious moment.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, I`m Dr. Jon LaPook in New Haven, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a great story.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a close-up look at the stage for tomorrow`s vice presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We want to give you a look at our debate stage from a different angle. This is where the candidates will be speaking from tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Governor Walz will be over here at the lectern on my left, meaning the right side of your screen. Senator J.D. Vance will be on this side.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, live coverage starts right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  at 8:00 p.m. and on streaming on CBS News 24/7 at 4:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s CBS Evening News. I`m Norah O`Donnell in New York.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020241001ek9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS WEEKEND NEWS for September 29, 2024, CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Chen, Skyler Henry, Jason Allen, Imtiaz Tyab, Tim  McNicholas, Natalie Brand, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Mark Strassmann, Itay Hod \par \uc2 3,717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Tonight, catastrophic damage. Scenes of destruction across 10 states following Hurricane Helene. Millions without power, the death toll still adding up.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: I`m Skyler Henry in Western North Carolina where flooded roads are slowing the recovery effort following Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: I`m Jason Allen in Cedar Key, Florida, where people are now pushing their homes and all their belongings out to the curb.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Also tonight, new fears of all-out war in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab in Beirut, where nearly one million people have been displaced as Israeli strikes across Lebanon and here in the capital intensify.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: On the campaign trail, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  escalates his rhetoric and insults.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became mentally impaired, sad, but lying \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , honestly, I believe she was born that way.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Plus, why disruption looms for the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: I`m Tim McNicholas in Elizabeth, New Jersey, how a possible strike by dock workers could trickle down to your store shelves.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And later, in a world overflowing with coffee choices, CBS` Itay Hod in San Francisco shows us a place grounded in tradition.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO REPETTO (Coffee roaster): Usually the term is said, "Keep your eye on the ball," but we talk about keeping our eye on the bean.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Good evening. I`m Nancy Chen in for Jericka.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight in the southeast, a region facing a historic disaster. In the wake of Hurricane Helene, millions are still without power, complicating recovery efforts, gas shortages, and spotty cell phone service.\par \par \uc2 The death toll still rising. At least 105 now known dead.\par \par \uc2 The monster storm cut a devastating path stretching more than 600 miles across several states. We have two reports tonight, beginning with Skyler Henry in hard hit, Marshall, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, good evening.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Nancy, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Communities all across this region, including small towns like Marshall, North Carolina, just outside of Asheville are getting their first looks at the damage.\par \par \uc2 And as you can imagine, folks who live and work here, their emotions are high, as they say, it will take years to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY (voiceover): Mountain towns across Appalachia are cut off. Nearly 300 roads are impassable in Western North Carolina alone, there has been no power or cell phone service in the city of Asheville since last week, leaving panicked relatives unable to reach loved ones.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That`s my neighbor`s house. It`s gone. That`s crazy. I just -- I literally just looked. I cannot believe that. There was a whole house right there.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: We spoke to one homeowner in Marshall, North Carolina, who didn`t leave until flood waters were waist deep. When she returned today, the neighborhood was swept away.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Their house is literally completely gone. My neighbor`s house is -- his trailer is up against my house.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Today, authorities rushed to airlift supplies and restore communications with devastated communities.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D-NC): People are desperate for help, and we are pushing to get it to them, a massive effort. Many people are cut off because roads are impassable.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Long lines for gas are snarling busy roads in hard hit South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Miami-Dade Fire Rescue)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: In Florida, where the storm made landfall on Thursday, the recovery is just getting started.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: I`m Jason Allen in Cedar Key, Florida where there`s damage from one house to the next to the next, and you will find this extending for hundreds of miles to the north.\par \par \uc2 Heather Greenwood has lived on Cedar Key for the last five years and manages the town`s bed and breakfast.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER GREENWOOD (Cedar Key, Florida resident): So many of these island people worked so hard, and they`ve just lost everything.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from August 2023)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Touring this afternoon, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  said the damage from Helene appears to be greater than the combined effects of Idalia and Hurricane Debbie in August.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): The thing is, Idalia was a major hurricane, but you had homes that didn`t even have major damage with Idalia. Some of those homes were totally destroyed. I mean, it was really, really catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: The governor said most Floridians impacted, except for people in places like this, could have their power back by tonight.\par \par \uc2 And we talked to the owner here. She wasn`t even going to come back here today, because she said there`s really nothing left, as you can see, to come back to.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the only thing they have is the memories that they are finding in the mud. Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Skyler Henry and Jason Allen. thank you.\par \par \uc2 To the Middle East now, new fears of a wider war. Today, Israel took aim at what it called military targets of the Houthi terrorist regime in Yemen. The attacks come as \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Lebanon confirmed the assassination of a seventh senior leader.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Imtiaz Tyab is in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Well, Israel`s offensive against the Iranian-backed militant groups is spreading across the Middle East tonight. The strikes in Yemen come just a day after it was confirmed the leader of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  was assassinated here in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voiceover): In a video released by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the bodies of \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  and two others killed in Israel`s massive Friday night strikes are wrapped in burial shrouds, as Israeli war planes continue to pound the same Southern Beirut neighborhood he was targeted in.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: At least 19 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commanders have been killed over the past few months, including one today. But the deadliest Israeli strike was on this building in Southern Lebanon, where displaced people have been sheltering in the predominantly Christian village of Ain Ed Delb, killing at least 30.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In this video posted online, a young man says he is trapped under the rubble and this, the same street and building where the strike took place, as he pleads for help.\par \par \uc2 A humanitarian crisis is now rapidly unfolding across Lebanon, with nearly one million people internally displaced, according to the country`s prime minister.\par \par \uc2 On this quiet street in Beirut, a school has now become a safe haven. It`s here we met Mohammed Hamoud (sp?), who is helping hundreds of families and tells us the U.S. needs to pressure Israel to stop striking Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 What is your message to President Biden?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED HAMOUD (Helping Lebanese families in school now safe haven in Beirut): Help us to get out of the war. Enough is enough. People are tired, but we can never surrender. We will never surrender. We need peace.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And tonight, peace seems a long way off with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , now a shadow of itself, still vowing to keep firing rockets at Israel and with the Israeli military massing forces along its northern front of here now as it will soon launch a ground invasion on Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Imtiaz Tyab, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, President Biden is renewing his call for diplomacy to defuse the crisis. CBS`s Natalie Brand is at the White House with that. Natalie.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE BRAND: Good evening. President Biden says all-out war in the region has to be avoided, and that he will be speaking to Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As relations between the two leaders have been strained, the Biden administration did not receive advance notice of the Israeli strike that killed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s leader. But National Security Council`s John Kirby says that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s leadership structure being decimated works to the Israeli`s advantage and is good for the region.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials tell CBS News that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s missile capabilities have also been significantly degraded. Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  says the U.S. is determined to prevent Iran or its proxies from exploiting the situation and expanding the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Austin warned that if Iran or its partners target American personnel or interests, the U.S. will take every measure necessary, Nancy, to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Natalie Brand, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Central Command said today that the two air strikes in Syria have killed at least 37, quote, "terrorist operatives" including members of \uc2 ISIS\uc2  and an al-Qaeda affiliate. The U.S. has around 900 troops in Syria, part of an international coalition against terror groups.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the campaign trail. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  rallies tonight with supporters in Nevada. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in Pennsylvania, where his rhetoric is drawing new scrutiny and backlash including from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CBS Caitlin Huey-Burns was at his rally in Erie.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good evening, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 Well, this is Donald Trump`s second visit to Pennsylvania just this week, which underscores that this state could help determine who wins the presidency, and Erie County, where we are now, is a key bellwether backing the last four winners in the past four presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : But if we win in Pennsylvania, we will win the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In battleground Pennsylvania, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rallied supporters with a familiar focus, immigration.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : This is an invasion and these people are mean, tough.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: It comes after Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  visited the southern border during a campaign stop Friday in Arizona where she criticized Trump for opposing a congressional border bill earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from September 27, 2024)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): He prefers to run on a problem instead of fixing a problem.\par \par \uc2 I reject the false choice that suggests we must either choose between securing our border or creating a system of immigration that is safe, orderly, and humane.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In Pennsylvania tonight, Trump doubled down on personal attacks against Harris.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Trump campaign rally)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became mentally impaired, sad, but lying \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , honestly, I believe she was born that way. She`s a stupid person, stupid person.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The comments echoed the harsh rhetoric he used yesterday in Wisconsin, and today, some allies urged him to focus on Harris` policies.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from State of the Union, CNN)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I just think the better course to take is to prosecute the case that her policies are destroying the country. They`re crazy liberal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And this week, the spotlight will be on the running mates, JD Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  have been off the campaign trail today, prepping for the first and only vice presidential debate right here on CBS. Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Caitlin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And do join us, Tuesday, for a live coverage of the CBS News Vice Presidential Debate co-moderated by Norah O`Donnell and Margaret Brennan at 8:00 PM Eastern, 5:00 Pacific right here on CBS. And for special streaming coverage, go to CBS News 24/7.\par \par \uc2 We learned tonight of the death of Kris Kristofferson. Kristofferson is best known as a superstar of country music, both as a performer, as well as songwriter.\par \par \uc2 He also had deep roots in Hollywood, starring opposite Barbara Streisand in 1976`s "A Star is Born."\par \par \uc2 Kristofferson was also a Rhodes Scholar and former Army Ranger. His family says he died at home in Maui. No cause of death was given. Kris Kristofferson was 88.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, a looming strike by dock workers could threaten the U.S. supply chain.\par \par \uc2 Also, Jimmy Carter`s grandson updates how the former president is feeling ahead of his 100th birthday.\par \par \uc2 And on this National Coffee Day, the search for the best bean.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Thousands of dock workers are set to strike as early as Tuesday. The walkout would impact ports from Massachusetts to Texas, snarling commercial shipping on a massive scale and disrupting the national supply chain.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Tim McNicholas has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS (voiceover): The looming strike would mean the biggest disruption to the flow of goods in and out of the U.S. since the height of the pandemic affecting building materials, fresh fruit, and more.\par \par \uc2 And this shelf could be empty, you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 ABDUL JAWAD (New York City grocery store owner): The bananas, yes.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: New York City grocery store owner, Abdul Jawad says his customers could notice changes within a week of the strike.\par \par \uc2 If it happens, how will that affect prices?\par \par \uc2 ABDUL JAWAD: Well, prices will go higher, without a doubt.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: A White House spokesperson tells CBS News, President Biden does not plan to use his authority to pause a potential strike. The Biden administration is urging the U.S. Maritime Alliance and the \uc2 International Longshoremen`s Association\uc2  to try to settle their differences regarding wages and automation.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 International Longshoremen`s Association\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 HAROLD DAGGETT (\uc2 International Longshoremen`s Association\uc2 ): When my men hit the streets from Maine to Texas, every single port will lockdown.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: That worries, Anthony Serafino, who is stockpiling more inventory at his New Jersey global distribution center.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SERAFINO (Exp. Group president): Stopping the supply chain is one thing, but getting the supply chain moving again is even more difficult.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: Industry insiders say a strike appears imminent, and economists say even a two-week walkout could cause ripple effects into next year.\par \par \uc2 ABDUL JAWAD: I`m always concerned, I`m not panicky concerned.\par \par \uc2 TIM MCNICHOLAS: And a memo obtained by CBS News shows terminals are extending their operating hours here at the Port of New York and New Jersey this weekend, trying to move as much cargo as possible in anticipation of a potential work stoppage.\par \par \uc2 Tim McNicholas, CBS News, Elizabeth, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Still ahead on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  turns a hundred this week. We`ll get a unique glimpse into his long life.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: On Tuesday, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  will become the first former U.S. president to turn 100.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Mark Strassmann in Atlanta has more on an extraordinary milestone.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER (Jimmy Carter`s grandson): That is a replica of the Resolute Desk that was in the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Jason Carter says this is a television first.\par \par \uc2 You`re looking at his grandfather, Jimmy Carter`s private office at the \uc2 Carter Center\uc2  in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 What jumps out at you?\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER: This was not to be a museum. This was to be a place where you got work done. But you can also see, he`s got his Bible there. He`s got his buttons. There`s a Grammy.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Carter is famously the peanut farmer from tiny Plains, Georgia who became America`s 39th president. He turns 100 on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The question that everyone wants to ask is, how is he doing?\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER: He`s very limited in what he can do. He can`t talk on the phone. He`s following the news.\par \par \uc2 When we asked him, was he excited about his 100th birthday? He said, I`m excited about that, but I`m really excited to vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: How does he spend his days?\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER: He gets up most days, and he`ll spend a little bit of time with family. When he`s up and at them, he`s up and at them.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 1977)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CARTER\uc2  (39th President of the United States): I, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , do solemnly swear --\par \par \uc2 MAN: That I will faithfully execute -\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CARTER\uc2 : That I will faithfully execute --\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Most Americans alive today were born after Carter`s one - term presidency ended in 1981.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from the \uc2 Carter Center\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: They may remember him more as humanitarian-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 He and his wife, \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2  globetrotted for four decades. They built habitat houses. They helped eradicate guinea worm disease. Time and again, they championed the underdog in forgotten corners of the globe.\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER: Those 40 years where he and my grandmother worked together on the issues that they cared about really are the fundamental part of their legacy.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Roselyn Carter died last year at 96, but you can feel her presence in this room.\par \par \uc2 Do you have a favorite artifact in this room?\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER: For me, that rocking chair over there, it is this huge double rocker. It`s just a beautiful piece. And they both loved it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Remarkably, the Carters were married for 77 years.\par \par \uc2 That painting was intentional.\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER: Of course. That`s his inspiration, right? He looks at my grandmother, and if you go to her office, you`ll see that she has a similar portrait of him. They didn`t want to be too far apart.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Living to 100, a record for ex-presidents, beats all odds. Carter beat cancer too, telling this to Norah O`Donnell back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2018)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CARTER\uc2 : Well, I called all my family together and told them that I was going to die quite soon, so I was prepared to go, but things turned out for the better.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: How does he want to be remembered?\par \par \uc2 JASON CARTER: As someone who was honest, as someone who did what he thought was right, who stood up for human rights and who respected people fundamentally.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: His 100th birthday will be America`s turn to stand up for \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 Next on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, thousands gather in New Zealand to break a world dance record.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Whakaata Maori)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting and dancing)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Today, more than 6,000 people gathered in Auckland, New Zealand, to reclaim the world record for the largest mass Haka. The traditional Maori war dance involves feet stomping, chanting, hand slapping, and exaggerated facial expressions. France was the previous record holder.\par \par \uc2 Starting tonight, Earth`s sky will have a temporary visitor, an asteroid known as a Mini Moon will enter our planet`s orbit for two months, but it is just 37 feet wide, so you will need a powerful telescope to see it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 NASA\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And tonight, the \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  capsule chosen by \uc2 NASA\uc2  to carry two stuck astronauts back to Earth arrived at the International Space Station. The original spacecraft was deemed too risky. The pair will return to Earth in February, at the earliest.\par \par \uc2 When we return, an iconic West Coast roaster in hot pursuit of the perfect bean.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: We end on this National Coffee Day with what starts the morning for so many of us.\par \par \uc2 On average, Americans drink three cups of Joe a day.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s "Weekend Journal," CBS` Itay Hod shows us what`s brewing in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD (voiceover): In a world overflowing with coffee choices, finding the perfect cup can be a tall order, but there`s one place in San Francisco that`s grounded in tradition.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO REPETTO: Usually the term is said, "Keep your eye on the ball," but we talk about keeping our eye on the bean.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: For nearly a century, Graffeo Coffee, one of the oldest roasters in the country has been a Bay Area institution. But don`t expect to find a latte or any cup of coffee here. In fact, this is their entire menu.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO REPETTO: One blend roasted, a dark roast or a light roast, and then a water processed decaf.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: What you will find says owner, Luciano Repetto, is a perfect bean, so you can brew the perfect cup right at home.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO REPETTO: This is our dark roast.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: A fourth-generation roaster, he ships his specialty blend to customers all over the world.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO REPETTO: It is called a fluid bed roaster.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: The secret, he says, is this machine which allows him to roast the coffee at lower temperatures.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO REPETTO: A difference of two degrees Fahrenheit can change the taste of the coffee.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: This kind of precision doesn`t come cheap. A pound of coffee here will set you back $23.00.\par \par \uc2 But for loyal customers like Ken Soleil, it`s a small price to pay for perfection.\par \par \uc2 KEN SOLEIL (Customer): I come here because, because I think it`s the best.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Melissa San Miguel is the spokesperson for the National Coffee Association. She says consumption of specialty coffee in the U.S. is on the rise.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA SAN MIGUEL (National Coffee Association spokesperson): This specialty coffee category surpassed traditional coffee with the general coffee drinking population for the first time just this year.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: As for Repetto, he has no plans to retire anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 LUCIANO REPETTO: This is what I`ve always wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 ITAY HOD: Until then, he`ll be keeping his eye on the bean while roasting the competition.\par \par \uc2 Itay Hod, CBS News San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: That`s the CBS WEEKEND NEWS for this Sunday. I`m Nancy Chen in New York. Goodnight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020240930ek9t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Helene`s Destruction; Helene`s Path and Conditions; NYC Mayor Adams Expected in Court Today; Harris and Trump Battle Over Immigration Policy; Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Clash as Ceasefire Talks Stall; Major Port Strike Looming; Search for Granddaughter of Medgar and Myrlie Evers; Exclusive Look at "The Summit;" 2024 People`s Choice Country Awards; A Lesson in Admiration; The Bathtub Murder of Kendy Howard; Blood Drive Kickoff; Boosting the Blood Supply; Jeremie Albino`s Time Has Come; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Kristine Johnson, Stephanie Abrams,  Dave Malkoff, Tom Hanson, Skyler Henry, Nikki Battiste, Ed O`Keefe, Imtiaz  Tyab, Vladimir Duthiers, Nancy Chen, Jason Allen, Reynolds Wolf; Steve  Hartman; Meryl Hartman, Emmett Hartman, Peter Van Sant \par \uc2 13,528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey there and welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday, September 27th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: And I`m Kristine Johnson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but we`re in great hands. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." Your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Scenes of utter destruction along Florida`s Northern Gulf Coast after Helene barrels into the state threatening millions further inland.\par \par \uc2 METEOROLOGIST: The National Hurricane Center actually has Helene as a tropical storm over Atlanta. You just don`t see that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New York Mayor Eric Adams is appearing in court today after prosecutors laid out corruption charges.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D) (New York City): This is not surprising to us at all.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Israel`s Prime Minister Netanyahu addresses the United Nations today after rejecting calls for a ceasefire in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN (US Secretary of Defense): We now face the risk of an all-out war.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: The end of an era. After 57 seasons --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: The Oakland A`s won their final home game at the Coliseum.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: Former New York City Mayor \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  has been disbarred in Washington, DC for his efforts to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  try to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): To which Rudy responded, I`ve been disbarred, but I can still go in this bar, right?\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON ("After Midnight" host): Mayor Eric Adams, he is known for a lot of things, from clubbing downtown to clubbing in Midtown to clubbing during work hours.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Adams is resisting calls to step down after federal prosecutors laid out explosive corruption charges against him.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR TOMLINSON: To be fair, Adams is, of course, innocent until proven guilty, although it would be a lot easier to believe he wasn`t doing crimes if he didn`t dress like a gangster from "Guys and Dolls."\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: CBS MORNINGS, we`re going to have a whole lot more on Eric Adams and the implications for national politics.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to start with the hurricane, that massive and deadly storm, a Category 4 as it hit Florida, and right now, Helene has weakened to a tropical storm, but it is still dumping huge amounts of rain all across the southeast, and it`s headed toward Atlanta, where more than six million people live in and around the city.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Helene made landfall late last night in Florida`s Big Bend with winds up to 140 miles an hour and storm surge up to 20 feet. At least three people were killed in Florida and Georgia. Its outer bands nearly swallowed up cars, you can see it driving along a bridge in Tampa.\par \par \uc2 Our correspondents are tracking the storm all along its path. We first go to Dave Malkoff, who is in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Good morning, Nate. Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a situation going on here in Atlanta, and think of how far that is from where the storm made landfall. We`ve got an entire apartment complex here that is underwater. Now these are the cars that are faring pretty well in this storm. There are some, and I can see right down there that the water is right up to the top of the windows.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people live in here. We had people who had to be rescued from their homes, not just in the rescue boats, but also with neighbors coming down, knocking on their doors and saying, you`ve got to get out of here.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a man named Marcus and his wife, Natasha, and they have a three-month-old baby, and they actually got out of their apartment complex, which is down there, and went to another apartment complex.\par \par \uc2 What you`re looking at right now are swift water rescues that were going on just about an hour ago, right here in this neighborhood and in another neighborhood not too far from here.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, there have been three deaths in this, there are two in Georgia. That was when a trailer overturned, and that`s just an unfortunate thing that happened about three hours from here.\par \par \uc2 As for that swift water rescue, Kristine that happened just a little bit, from here, they actually had two people in the car with their pets, and the swift water rescue team had to go in with ropes and pulled them out of there.\par \par \uc2 This is just happening right now and is still pouring here -- Kristine.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Well, Dave, the threat still clearly there. Thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 And Helene may be weakening now, but it was a monster storm as it rolled through North Florida overnight.\par \par \uc2 Tom Hanson is in Tallahassee, where state officials are now assessing all the damage.\par \par \uc2 Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hey there, Kristine. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 While the winds here were ferocious, 70 miles an hour and I want to show you what 70 miles an hour can do, up in the drone, if you want to take that shot, you can see this giant tree that has toppled over on this apartment complex, crushing essentially the side of it and the car below, making Tallahassee just one of many cities now grappling with the aftermath of this monster Category 4 hurricane.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Hurricane Helene barreled into Florida`s Gulf Coast overnight, delivering with it powerful storm surge over a distance spanning hundreds of miles, from Fort Myers to the Panhandle.\par \par \uc2 In Bradenton, just south of Tampa, high water overtook roads, even washing this sailboat on shore.\par \par \uc2 All night, first responders conducted multiple rescues, including these three residents and their dog in Hillsborough County. In Fort Myers Beach, the Lee County Sheriff`s Office competed with fierce winds and rushing water on flooded streets as they deployed two water rescues by boat.\par \par \uc2 The hurricane`s extensive winds slammed communities all over the state. Our Jason Allen was reporting in Crawfordville, south of Tallahassee around the time Helene made landfall.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: The worst of Helene`s winds are probably going to feel that storm surge, which covers a broad area, it will make it difficult to get into the worst hit communities.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And those winds proved deadly. Authorities in Tampa say a falling highway sign hit a car, killing the driver, and strong winds toppled trees in Martin County, one crushing this house, nearly killing the family inside.\par \par \uc2 MAXIMILIANO VELACQUEZ (Martin County, Florida resident): It was horrifying, like I was sleeping in the living room and I was just laying down, sleeping. Next thing I know, I got hit in the head with a piece of drywall.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Even bridges weren`t high enough to dodge Helene, drivers risked passing the Matlacha Bridge near Cape Coral as the storm surge crashed onto it, and before Helene reached its full fury, rescue workers in Treasure Island said they were unable to reach some neighborhoods completely cut off by rising floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: And guys, I`ve got to say, when we were driving around assessing the damage, it was a dark drive. That`s because Helene has knocked out power to over a million Floridians, and power companies say it could take days for the lights to come back on -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tom, thank you very much. The storm is now moving north, and North and South Carolina, both getting historic amounts of rain at this hour, it`s causing life threatening flooding, especially in some of the mountainous areas, including Asheville, North Carolina, which is currently under a flash flood emergency, and Skyler Henry has made his way to Asheville.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, good morning. What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Tony, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 As always, we have been put in an evacuation alert area in this county right now. Just in the last 30 minutes or so, there was an evacuation just to the west of us, where folks living in low-lying areas have had to move as quickly as possible. But really we expect to see the intensity from Helene to pick up in the next couple of hours.\par \par \uc2 Already in this parking lot that we have been in. This is the art district here in Asheville. Water has risen up already to right about the bottom part of my calf. Debris is already here behind us.\par \par \uc2 We have seen wind really jostle us back and forth. We anticipate the wind miles per hour to pick up somewhere to around 70 to 80 miles per hour in the later part of the morning.\par \par \uc2 As for power outages, more than 700,000 people in South Carolina are waking up without power. More than 200,000 here are waking up without power.\par \par \uc2 Guys, all of these elements, some experts say, could potentially lead to landslides and mudslides. We`re also paying attention to some of the historic flooding happening in some of the rivers around this area as well in the nearby French Broad River, which is about 600 feet from here, already seeing historic levels, and it will get even higher as the morning moves on.\par \par \uc2 So we will anticipate what the latest will be. Obviously, us hunkering down here in Asheville, but we will continue to move as the storm does as well - - Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Skyler, thank you for braving the elements. Get inside and get dry.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s find out where Helene is going next. Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking the storm for us.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Helene is now an inland storm, but is still quite potent with significant impacts. There`s a threat for tornadoes from the central Appalachians to the East Coast. It is still a large system stretching from Missouri to Virginia down into Georgia, and there`s a chance we could still see more power outages and downed trees as gusts could be as high as 80 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 It`s getting pulled into an upper level low. It will slowly weaken throughout the day, and together, these will linger into Saturday, bringing clouds and light rain from the Midwest to the south.\par \par \uc2 A lot of cities in its path actually need the rain because we have extreme to exceptional drought. The problem is, we`ve already seen double digit rainfall in western North Carolina, and we could still get another half foot or more. That means there`s a risk for catastrophic and life- threatening flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 It still exists with the possibility, by the way of flooding stretching all the way to the Mississippi River.\par \par \uc2 All of this water will cause rivers to rise, some of which could crush their records by several feet.\par \par \uc2 Nate, this is the fourth hurricane to make landfall on the Gulf Coast this year. This has happened only five other times in history, and I have to tell you, the models are actually picking up on something in the Gulf next week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: In other news now, New York City Mayor Eric Adams is due to be arraigned this morning on corruption charges. Prosecutors accuse the Democrat of taking illegal campaign contributions and bribes from officials in Turkey in exchange for favors dating back a decade.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste is at the federal courthouse in Lower Manhattan now. Nikki, good morning,\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Kristine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Adams will appear here in this courthouse behind me, where he is expected to enter a plea of not guilty. If convicted of the five charges against him, Adams could face up to 45 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS (United States Southern District of New York attorney): This was a multi-year scheme to buy favor with a single New York City politician on the rise.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Federal prosecutors allege New York City Mayor Eric Adams was at the center of a decade long plot to take bribes in exchange for favors to foreign officials.\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS: These are bright red lines, and we allege that the mayor crossed them again and again for years.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Adams is facing five counts of bribery, fraud, and soliciting illegal foreign campaign donations in an extensive 57-page indictment. US attorneys say Adams used straw donors or third parties to accept illegal campaign contributions, some of which helped his campaign qualify for more than $10 million in public matching funds.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors also alleged the 64-year-old received and failed to disclose more than $100,000.00 of luxury travel benefits. In exchange, attorneys say Adams used his influence to help Turkish officials, including pressuring the fire department in New York to allow a newly built Turkish skyscraper to open without inspection.\par \par \uc2 What do you think the biggest challenge for prosecutors will be?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA ROTH (The Southern District of New York former federal prosecutor): One challenge is to prove the quid pro quo. It`s not enough to prove that an official received payments and that that official also used their influence at some point to assist another person. It has to be shown that there was an understanding, even if it was not explicit, that one was for the other.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: We do not participate in straw donors. We do not participate in foreign donors.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Adams denied the charges and continued to stand defiant Thursday, vowing again to remain in office.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: My attorneys will take care of the case so I can take care of the city.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The only person who can remove Adams from office is New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hochul, who is also a Democrat, said Thursday she would be deliberative and thoughtful as she comes to a decision about what to do -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikki, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to the presidential race, where the candidates have been attacking each other`s policies on border security and Ukraine. And today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will be in Arizona, where the plan is to visit the US-Mexico border, something she has not done in more than three years.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the border himself. He`s in the town of Douglas, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Tony, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 You know, early in the Biden 