eck your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, a potential strike in many of America`s ports could have a big impact on consumers. How it could lead to shortages and higher prices.\par \par \uc2 And Steve Hartman and his kids share a lesson in admiration for our series "Kindness 101."\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More time. Let`s go Oakland. The last of those loyal Oakland A`s baseball fans turned out to see their team win one yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The Athletics beat Texas in their final game in Oakland after 57 seasons. They won four World Championships there in California, and now they`re saying bye-bye on their way to Las Vegas by way of Sacramento, after they tried and failed to build a new ballpark there in the East Bay.\par \par \uc2 Tough time for sports fans in that part of California. The Warriors and the Raiders have also left the city in recent years, and there`s no crying in baseball, but I actually teared up just a little.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes, a lot of fans did, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Here`s to you Oakland. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, the next threat from the remains of Hurricane Helene. Your local news is coming up next. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, and as we track the remains of Hurricane Helene, this is the third time in just over a year that a tropical storm system has hit Florida`s Northeastern Gulf Coast, and Helene is now a tropical storm over Georgia, blamed for at least three deaths so far.\par \par \uc2 It is likely to cause even more damage as it moves through the Eastern US, so let`s go back to Dave Malkoff in Atlanta, which is directly in Helene`s path right now.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Kristine, 273 miles, that is how far we are from landfall and look at this. Look at that car back there, completely submerged.\par \par \uc2 In this apartment complex, people had to get out of here in rescue boats this morning. We were here as the swift water rescue teams were actually moving people. I see one person actually coming out of their apartment right here, so they`re actually inside these apartments, not able to get out.\par \par \uc2 There was one swift water rescue that happened not too far from here, where we actually had two people inside the car with their pets. They had to get on top of the car, and then the team, the USAR team, Urban Search and Rescue, they had to get into the car and then pull those people out with ropes. And remember, this is in the middle of the night with this tropical storm pushing down on us.\par \par \uc2 Now, Helene is now a tropical storm. This is the system we have on top of us. Look at this car that was completely submerged just a little bit ago.\par \par \uc2 We still have the chance of having 70 mile an hour wind gusts. Now remember, hurricane strength starts at 74 miles an hour, so we`re talking almost, almost hurricane force wind gusts and this is all the way on the other side of the landfall.\par \par \uc2 We have so much land between us and where the storm came on shore in Florida. It usually kills a hurricane, and that`s what it did, and that`s what it`s going to do through this. But it`s still got so much power. It`s so wide, it`s so widespread. The storm is just so widespread. It is moving so fast, and it has so much energy in it that it`s not just going to push through here in Atlanta, but wow, look at that coming down right now.\par \par \uc2 You can feel this rain that just started like a fire hose just a second ago. So not only are you going to have all of this rain coming down, but you will have flooding -- fresh water flooding coming up from river beds. We are not too far from a creek over here. So we have the water coming on top of us, fresh water. Then we also have the creek spilling over and we have people trying to drive around and the school is closed, Kristine, and that is a good thing. They don`t want people out there, especially kids trying to get on the bus through all of this.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: All right, Dave, thank you for that update. It`s tough out there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tough situation down there, and it was tough further south, actually, in Florida where Helene made landfall, came in hard and fast overnight. In fact, we`ve got some pictures we want to show you.\par \par \uc2 This is Cedar Key, Florida, not far from where Helene made landfall. Now take a look at this video, which was taken by this very same home doorbell camera, and you can see the rising water inside that same house.\par \par \uc2 And then this next picture you`re about to see is about two hours after that photo was taken.\par \par \uc2 Jason Allen has made his way to Perry, Florida and he is surveying the damage. Just arrived, in his car still. Good morning -- Jason.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Yes, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been making the drive this morning here to Perry, just about 10 miles or so from where the storm made landfall, saw some of the eye wall as that storm came ashore last night.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you a little bit of damage that we found, structural damage, obviously, a lot of tree damage in this area. But you can see, there is roof damage here. There is damage to the bricks as well on this shop building.\par \par \uc2 You know, there`s video that we have that shows the night sky as the storm came through, really being lit up by the power lines that were sparking, the transformers blowing, the metal siding ripped off the building in a shopping complex, there was water flooding the parking lots, a lot of debris fell into the streets, and people who were out last night kind of brave enough to go out in these unclear conditions, it was sketchy for them.\par \par \uc2 There were some structures that were bent, flipped out of place. We`ve seen some sheds on the side of the road and a lot of downed power lines on our way here to Perry this morning, all evidence of the 115 mile per hour winds that were recorded.\par \par \uc2 The storm surge was estimated to be up to 16 to 20 feet. I haven`t seen the giant debris piles that we often see with that big storm surge, but that may be in a little bit closer to the coast where we are -- where we were. We have seen waste deep water and flooding as well.\par \par \uc2 As we get back on the road here, we`ll be looking for more damage this morning in Perry, which again, took a direct hit from this storm last night.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Jason, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Many of the country`s ports could see a halt coming to them next week because of a potential dock workers strike. Coming up, how it could impact prices on what you are buying in the stores. We will have all of the information you need.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Money Watch," at least 25,000 dock workers at East End Gulf Coast ports could walk off the job next week, impacting nearly half of the country`s imports and possibly sending prices higher for consumers.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations between the workers union and the employers face a Tuesday deadline.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen shows us how the ripple effect could affect what you buy in the stores.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SERAFINO (Exp. Group. LLC president): Well, we`re stacked to the brim, produce all the way up to our ceiling to make sure we have what we need. I`ve never seen it like this.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Anthony Serafino is running out of space at his company`s global distribution center in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 If there`s a strike happens, what happens immediately in this facility?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SERAFINO: So we are stockpiled with some inventory for a few days, hopefully a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It`s one of the many preemptive measures his business and others are taking to prepare for a potential walkout.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SERAFINO: Day one of the strike, the supermarket shelves will be filled. But if the strike lingers on, we could see a position where we start to see empty shelves, so that`s every retailer`s nightmare.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Dock workers from Boston to Houston and 14 ports total along the East and Gulf Coast could be walking off the job in the union`s first strike since 1977. The sticking points, right now workers make between $20.00 and $39.00 per hour. According to a source familiar with the negotiations, the Union wants annual raises of $5.00 an hour, while management has countered with half that.\par \par \uc2 The union is also fighting the increasing use of automation, which they say could take away jobs.\par \par \uc2 What will the impact be on consumers?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We could see price increases to offset the supply chain cost increases. Also, I think we could see longer delivery times.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Can even one day make such a huge difference?\par \par \uc2 MAN: One day, not so much, especially with some of the preemptive measures that`s been taken. A week, several weeks, in fact, that`s where we really see the ripple effect.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: That`s especially true for items like produce.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SERAFINO: I always say our stuff does not get better with age. It`s not wine. It gets worse. So there`s only so much inventory you can stack.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Now the White House does have some authority to step in and pause this strike or potential strike. A Biden administration official tells CBS News, the Department of Labor is monitoring the situation and has been in touch with both sides, but there are no plans to get involved with negotiations at this time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, so the holidays are coming up. How should shoppers prepare for this potential strike?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes, this is not something you need to run out for and start stockpiling items. A lot of the holiday merchandise is already in store warehouses right now, but the supply chain expert we spoke to said, if the strike happens and it goes on for a few weeks, that`s when there could be an impact.\par \par \uc2 So maybe just be a little vigilant in ordering items early if there are some things specific you want to get.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Another reason to start early, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I try to start early every year. Never do. Another reason.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: There`s always next year.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Now you must.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Thank you, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the stories that you`ll be talking about today in "What to Watch." Right now, it is 7:42, it is time to look at your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ve got a lot coming up at eight, so don`t you go anywhere, including our series "Kindness 101." Steve Hartman and his kids shows us the importance of admiration with a story about a 70s music icon and the super fan you might not expect.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a preview of this week`s "48 Hours." Did a former State Trooper stage a crime scene. Peter Van Sant will be here with a story you won`t want to miss.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of a 70s music icon, Vlad is right over here, and he will have the story that you`ll be talking about today in "What to 