ic now sounds like the Eagles in the 70s. And it`s true. It`s got this nice, easy listening, storytelling, nice writing kind of feeling.\par \par \uc2 You`re skeptical.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I love the Eagles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re going to tell me you met the Eagles and they told you that --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No. No.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, look, the Eagles did sort of bring about that alt-country, that early, alt-country vibe. They were from California, but they had that country vibe that I think a lot of artists --\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: There is still a little twang though in today`s country music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, you`ve got to remember, this is the 70s music icon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up in our series --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am of the 70s and proud of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- "Kindness 101," a lesson in admiration --\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Hey, right over here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right with you, babe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- comes with an emotional surprise for a young girl.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Just ahead, "48 Hours" investigates the mysterious death of a woman in Idaho. Her husband said she had taken her own life, but to investigators, things just didn`t seem right.\par \par \uc2 Peter Van Sant joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, the latest on Hurricane Helene. We`ll check on the conditions in and around Atlanta, bracing for its biggest hit from a tropical system in decades.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: I`m Kristine Johnson.\par \par \uc2 "48 Hours" looks at the case of a former State Trooper accused of killing his wife in the tub and making it look like a suicide.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hartman and his kids have a "Kindness 101" lesson in admiration from a girl who couldn`t get enough of a singer from the 70s.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Have I told you how much I admire you guys?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re so sweet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: We`ve got an entire apartment complex here that is underwater.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Helene hits Florida as a Category 4 hurricane, causing severe flooding and leaving many people without power.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Helene is now an inland storm, but is still quite potent with significant impacts. There`s a threat for tornadoes from the Central Appalachians to the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: My attorneys would take care of the case so I can take care of the city.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: New York City Mayor Eric Adams heads to federal court to plead not guilty to corruption charges.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: If convicted of the five charges against him, Adams could face up to 45 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Israel`s prime minister vows to use full force against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  ahead of his speech to the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN: Israel and Lebanon can choose a different path, a diplomatic solution is still viable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Stephen Colbert looks at the charges against New York`s mayor and he smells a rat.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: The timing is a bit suspicious, because the Feds announced these charges just days after Adams held the city`s first National Anti-Rat Summit.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: What`s that about? What`s that about? Hmm. And I`m being told we have a photo. Is this true? We have a photo of the federal prosecutor announcing the indictment.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Something about that -- something about that guy is suspicious. I can`t put my finger on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Colbert has the jokes, "Ratatouille." That`s that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, "Ratatouille" is the movie. One of my favorite movies.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: "Ratatouille."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do support the anti-rat efforts, I will say.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But --\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got some news to get to though, Hurricane Helene now a tropical storm, but still causing flooding and major damage in the southeast. The system is slamming Atlanta this morning, bringing a lot of heavy rain with it, and it made landfall late last night in Florida`s Big Bend region as a Category 4 storm, winds up to 140 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, Reynolds Wolf, with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is in Atlanta right now tracking things for us.\par \par \uc2 Reynolds, good morning.\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS WOLF (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): What a time it has been. I`ve got to tell you right now, you`ve got two-and-a-half million people under flash flood emergencies, and Helene is the reason why.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go right to the graphics, and it gives you the idea of what has been taking place. We`ve got this pinwheel of moisture that continues to make its way across parts of the Deep South, but it`s so much more than just the moisture.\par \par \uc2 On top of it, you`ve got the winds that have been quite destructive, and we expect that duration to be, well, very difficult, no doubt, as we go forward over the next several hours and several days.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the tornado threat, and there certainly is one, you`ll notice that in terms of the TORCON, the Tornado Condition Index, it ranges from a two to as high as a four, especially out towards the Outer Banks of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps, the biggest issue may be the sledgehammer effect of the heavy rainfall. Now, you`ll notice in Western Carolinas, we`ve got the brighter colors that indicates the heavy rainfall.\par \par \uc2 Remember, we did have an event that came right through the region where you had that boundary that get around 10 to 15 inches of rainfall. So Helene is going to bring even more on top of that.\par \par \uc2 We go from bad to dangerous to catastrophic, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 Then, as we make our way into the weekend, know that this is going to continue to be a huge issue, possibly seeing this moisture make its way across into Tennessee with more issues of rising water and lives that could be threatened.\par \par \uc2 Let`s send it back to you in the studio.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right. Reynolds Wolf, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Crews in Florida are assessing the damage today as Helene moves away. I want you to take a look at this incredible video. This is in South Pasadena, Florida, not too far from Tampa.\par \par \uc2 Responders say this home caught on fire amid the storm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. That is some incredible footage.\par \par \uc2 The flow waters there were so strong, dumpsters were floating down the streets.\par \par \uc2 Jason Allen is in Perry, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Jason, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JASON ALLEN: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 We have moved now into downtown Perry, a city which took a direct hit from this storm overnight. This damage that you see back here, this is a siding. It looks like pieces of a roof. There`s a lot of insulation here.\par \par \uc2 And looking at the adjacent buildings, we can`t tell exactly where it came from, because this building still has a roof. The one that it`s in front of has a roof, and it`s clear that this traveled.\par \par \uc2 We can see some other roof damage to some of the other buildings here in the older downtown area. But even with this, after talking with fire crews that have been out throughout the night, they have been looking throughout town, what they told me is we fared pretty well, again, from a city that took a direct hit.\par \par \uc2 Now this morning, we made the long drive from the west side of the bay near Crawfordville, where we rode out the storm, all the way here to Perry. We did not have power the entire way there, didn`t see anybody with lights on.\par \par \uc2 There were trees down the whole way. There were power lines down the whole -- the entire way, that is going to be the big thing, Kristine, that these crews are out now to deal with today and that people are really going to need back as they start to recover from this storm.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes, that could take days, even weeks, possibly.\par \par \uc2 Jason, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, our series "Kindness 101" shares a lesson on admiration. Steve Hartman has a big surprise for a girl who is a devoted fan of a surprising musical icon. But first, it is 8:05, and it`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: I`m Peter Van Sant, "48 Hours."\par \par \uc2 Did a former Idaho State Trooper use his law enforcement skills to stage his wife`s death in their bathtub?\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to one of our favorite series, "Kindness 101" where Steve Hartman and his kids, they share stories built around kindness and character and the people who have mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 Today`s lesson is admiration. Steve and his kids introduce us to a young girl with a surprising idol.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning, everyone. This is Meryl, and this is our lesson on --\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Admiration. And we begin with my brother, Emmett at the Dictionary Desk.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Good morning, Meryl.\par \par \uc2 I`m defining admiration as a feeling of deep respect or approval.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Who is someone you admire?\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: My math teacher knows how to do literally everything.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: People who just have the courage to stick with something that they are really interested in and keep going at that thing, because that`s what they want to do.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good.\par \par \uc2 It`s important to have role models you admire, because it helps you aspire to greatness, too.\par \par \uc2 For an example, we turn now to our library of stories past for this tale of true, undying admiration.\par \par \uc2 While most tweens in America are fawning over Bieber, Swift, and Styles, 11-year-old Paisley Gardner of Des Moines, Iowa has a different idol, a singer she kept hearing on the radio.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER (From Des Moines, Iowa): It sounds like an angel, somehow.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sounds like an angel.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GARDNER (Paisley`s mom): What?\par \par \uc2 TONY GARDNER (Paisley`s dad): Huh? Okay, well, that`s kind of odd.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Parents, Tony and Jessica say they didn`t know what to think when their daughter became obsessed with that buttery smooth voice of that 70s soft rock legend, Michael McDonald.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Michael McDonald performance)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: They say Paisley was smitten.\par \par \uc2 TONY GARDNER: Without an image of who this person was.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GARDNER: So one day, she Googled a picture of Michael McDonald. She came running up the stairs and flailed herself on the bed and was like, "No."\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: No.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Her pop star turned out to be a Grand Pop.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: I just had to deal with it. But it`s okay.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: She really didn`t care. So one day, her dad surprised her with concert tickets.\par \par \uc2 TONY GARDNER: You want to go see him?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY GARDNER: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: And I almost screamed.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You did scream?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Yes. I did.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 McDonald is on tour with The Doobie Brothers.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: I love you, Michael.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Paisley says she was the youngest fan in the audience by a generation, and the only one who actually got to talk to McDonald.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Michael, you`re my favorite.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sort of.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: He said thank you to me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That was it.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: He said thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And that was enough for her.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: He looked at me in the eye and said thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: After my visit, I reached back out to Paisley, and we talked about how cool it would be to have a real chat with him.\par \par \uc2 The odds of that happening would be very, very slim --\par \par \uc2 Or maybe not.\par \par \uc2 What`s that?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Wait a second. What?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCDONALD (Singer): How are you, darling?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: What?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: It`s okay, sweetie.\par \par \uc2 Eventually Paisley recovered for a nice conversation and an even nicer invitation.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCDONALD: We`ll have you see the show from backstage, maybe. How`s that?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Uh-hmm.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: A sweet gift for the girl who learned there`s so much more to music appreciation.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: I love you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Then hair color.\par \par \uc2 Paisley Gardner joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Paisley.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Hi.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Do you still admire Michael McDonald or have you fallen for someone new?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Okay, so here`s the thing. I kind of have fallen for someone new.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Is it Taylor Swift?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: No.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Who is it?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: This person named Melanie Martinez.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Have you told this to Michael McDonald?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: No.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He`s going to find out now.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: He is?\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Did he keep his promise to let you go backstage?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCDONALD: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 We said hi. I gave him a couple of hugs.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCDONALD: How are you?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: It was pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: How do you think it made Michael McDonald feel to know that he had an admirer as young as you?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Because most of his fans are like my age, right?\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Does she even know how old you are?\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Can I take a guess? Forty-two.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: No.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: He is 60.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: No, you hit it right on the number.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Nope.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: All right, thanks so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 PAISLEY GARDNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Paisley admired a famous person, but a lot of non- celebrities deserve admiration, too, like all of those people out there who resist negativity and divisiveness and carve out a few minutes of their day to join us here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, and we`ll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: And don`t forget to stay kind.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: These are so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not so much the part -- listen, this was like a good movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It pulls at all the heart strings.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes, so real.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I cried, I laughed, I giggled. That was great, Steve.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: It is just a reminder, everything that`s going on around us, there is good in our world.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely. And all the wisdom you can have, Steve Hartman, at the tender age of 42.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. At that tender age of 42. Good stuff.\par \par \uc2 All right, so you wish that you could see these lessons in school? I know you do. Then tell your teachers about Kindness101.com. It is something very cool that we have built.\par \par \uc2 It is a new site for educators, and they can access all of the "Kindness 101" videos and professional lesson plans to go with each of them.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead. Go ahead and go there right now.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Utilize that for sure.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, a preview of this week`s new "48 Hours." When investigators arrived at a crime scene, something just didn`t add up.\par \par \uc2 Peter Van Sant joins us on set with a look at this week`s story.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: This week`s "48 Hours" digs into the investigation of a former Idaho State Trooper suspected in the mysterious death of his wife.\par \par \uc2 Dan Howard says he found his wife dead in the bathtub in February of 2021 and he told deputies she took her own life. But investigators decided to take a closer look at him after finding certain evidence at the crime scene.\par \par \uc2 Peter Van Sant reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: This 911 call --\par \par \uc2 DAN HOWARD (Kendy Howard`s husband): My wife.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Sent deputies from Kootenai County in Northern Idaho to the home of a distraught former State Trooper, Dan Howard.\par \par \uc2 DAN HOWARD: It was a gunshot.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: With their body cameras rolling, deputies captured Dan telling them what happened on that cold February night in 2021.\par \par \uc2 MAN: She`s in the bathtub.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Upstairs, deputies found 48-year-old Kendy Howard`s body in the bathtub with a gunshot wound to her head.\par \par \uc2 Dan Howard told them she had taken her own life, but investigators were suspicious.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Miranda Thomas says too many things seemed out of place.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA THOMAS (Deputy): I went into the house and found a packed duffel bag. Was someone planning on leaving the house?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: In the laundry room, the dryer was still turning, full of clean bath towels and mats.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA THOMAS: Having a dryer running at midnight, very odd, because I don`t do laundry at midnight, sure you could.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: And despite the shock of finding his wife dead, Dan Howard appeared to have recently showered, changed his clothes, and applied fresh deodorant.\par \par \uc2 Detective Sergeant Ken Lallatin said Howard seemed to have a story he planned to share.\par \par \uc2 Almost scripted in a way.\par \par \uc2 KEN LALLATIN (Detective sergeant): Yes, definitely scripted.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: And there were other challenges. Authorities did find the gun in the tub, but there were no prints or DNA connecting it to Howard. There wasn`t enough on the scene to arrest him.\par \par \uc2 Still, authorities were not about to give up.\par \par \uc2 KEN LALLATIN: Dan knows things that most normal people, ordinary people don`t know.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Things like what?\par \par \uc2 KEN LALLATIN: Things like killing someone and staging it to work like a suicide.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: And Peter Van Sant joins us now here in studio.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Peter.\par \par \uc2 Talking about that dryer, you mentioned that it was going at midnight. What did investigators find there?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Well, what was fascinating about that? So you saw the hysterics, the 911 call, and also when deputies first arrived, imagine going inside that house and what you hear is the sound of a spinning dryer. How odd that would be?\par \par \uc2 But they had the perception to take a photograph that had six minutes left on it, and that became significant, because sometime later, detectives were able to determine that at the time he made that 911 call with all of the emotion and hysterics, he was actually doing laundry.\par \par \uc2 So it called into question whether that was a performance that they were listening to on that 911 call.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What else made them suspicious about Dan Howard?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Well, Kendy was shot in the head and she was in a bathtub, but there was surprisingly little blood there. That undermined this notion of a suicide, and also raised the possibility for investigators, was she possibly killed somewhere else and placed in the bath tub.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. So they weren`t able to make an arrest immediately. I think it took over two years for them to make an arrest. How come?\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: Well, you know, he`s a former Idaho State patrolman. They had to get all their ducks in a row. But the biggest obstacle was the medical examiner did not rule this case a homicide. The ME said it was an undetermined manner of death.\par \par \uc2 So they had to go out and find medical experts to actually challenge their own medical examiner in a trial, which is fascinating.\par \par \uc2 You have the defense attorney saying, well, if the medical examiner says this isn`t a homicide, you members of the jury, you shouldn`t either.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: And Peter, you made another observation during that video when police arrived, the husband didn`t seem that upset.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: No. We were told by the people who actually went into that house with all that sobbing, there wasn`t a single tear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. So revealing.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Lots of signs, and we`ll find out how this all ends.\par \par \uc2 PETER VAN SANT: It`s a dramatic trial. It`s a terrific, terrific tale.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Good to see you, Peter. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And you can watch Peter`s report "The Bathtub Murder of Kendy Howard" on "48 Hours" tomorrow night at 10/9 Central here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up in "Talk of the Table" Tony talks about one of his greatest loves. Sorry, Katy. It`s the New York Mets.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She knows. She knows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not an ex.\par \par \uc2 Your local news next.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: It is a different kind of love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning. And Tony, it`s on you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m excited.\par \par \uc2 So after this show, I go upstairs. I`ve got my 