grammar czars in my life that are always pointing out when I make mistakes.\par \par \uc2 So a stationary supplier, Southern Hospitality Company, posted this guide to pluralizing your name for your upcoming holiday cards. And the big takeaway here is, please forget the apostrophes. They simply say that an S to your name, adding it there at the end. For example, my family`s card would say "The Johnsons."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, interesting.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Well, that would be my mom and dad`s, you know, "The Johnsons." My married name is actually Poulin. So sorry, honey, it would be Poulins.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, okay.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: But if your last name actually ends in S, CH, SH, X, or a Z, then you have to add the E-S to make it plural, which can be a bit of a tongue twister.\par \par \uc2 So in other words, the Bush become the Bushes. The Schwartz become the Schwartzes; now the Walters, the Walterses.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Walterses.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Walterses?\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes, Walterses, is that how you do it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It doesn`t sound right.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes. But you know what? If you want to impress all of the people this holiday season, just --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No apostrophe.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: No apostrophe, or you could just keep it simple as The Poulin Family.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Or The Johnson Family.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or the family, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s good. The Johnson --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is good.\par \par \uc2 All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is an unforgettable wedding speech. All right, so Kayla Lee says that she and her groom, Spencer, did not know what to expect when her eight -year-old son, Lincoln, who was the ring bearer, asked to speak at their wedding in California this summer.\par \par \uc2 Let`s see how he did. Grab your tissues.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LINCOLN (Bride`s son): These two people in my life have just been so good with me. They have made my life better. I`m just saying that without these two, my life wouldn`t be my life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so you can see the groom holding his head --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- and crying throughout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LINCOLN: Anyway, to wrap it up, I love everyone here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Then he wraps it up at the end.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So this video exploded online. One person calling Lincoln an angel in a mullet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Another person said, "This kid has been here before." One other person said, "Emotional intelligence, but also the public speaking skills are insane."\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I would say so. I have a wedding of one of my best friends that I`ve known pretty much my entire life, and I`m going to have to grab the mic at some point, so I`m going to copy what Lincoln did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just say what he said.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Exactly. Just say what he said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, he does feel like he is reincarnated. There`s some wisdom there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And some ability, too.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: It`s tough though for kids to verbalize exactly how they`re feeling, but he nailed it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He did a great up.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Thanks, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Up ahead, our "Mixtape Moment" series features an artist who has come a long way from performing on the street as a teenager, but first, let`s get another look at your local forecast, it is 8:34.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Big Ten football is back on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . You can watch Wisconsin face off against USC tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And this season, thanks to the global health care company, Abbott, there`s more than just school pride on the line for the 18 schools in the conference. It`s teaming up with the Big Ten for a new competition to boost the nation`s blood supply at a time of critical shortage.\par \par \uc2 They have drafted a former \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  QB that you might know to help tackle the problem.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Who is that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. We caught up with the one and only, the legendary Tom Brady at the Big Ten headquarters outside of Chicago to talk about "The We Give Blood Drive" and there`s a million dollar prize on the line.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM BRADY (Former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player): It`s obviously great to be here today at the Big Ten campus.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT FORD (Abbott chairman and CEO): We`ll be turning up the volume on Big Ten campuses to see which school and its nation of fans can generate the most blood donations.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, everyone wins.\par \par \uc2 The schools build enthusiasm and engagement in their communities. The donors win by contributing to an important cause and helping others.\par \par \uc2 Blood centers win by rebuilding their blood supplies, and of course, the people who will benefit the most are those that will receive the donations.\par \par \uc2 TONY PETITTI (Big Ten commissioner): We`re really excited about the idea. We have so many kids doing really great things. I think this is going to bring people together.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT FORD: The winning school will not just get the bragging rights, but also a $1 million prize from Abbott to advance student and community health.\par \par \uc2 TOM BRADY: I was giving blood in high school as a student athlete at Serra High School in San Mateo. It`s always been something that I saw as very important. Giving a little bit can save a lot of lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. To find a blood donation center near you, and to learn more about "The We Give Blood Drive," you can visit BigTen.org/abbott.\par \par \uc2 The winning school will be announced at the Big Ten championship game in December and to learn more about the benefits of giving blood, we are joined by Dr. Jennifer Ashton,\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON (OBGYN): Good morning, you guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is a board certified OBGYN. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: I`m doing great. Isn`t that a great idea?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a great idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wonderful idea.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Love it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So Doctor Jen, in your line of work, you have given many patients blood transfusions.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For people watching home and for us, how common are they?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: Every two seconds common.\par \par \uc2 So every two seconds in this country, someone --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, you mean that literally.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: -- literally --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: -- needs a blood transfusion. The list of indications is long. After trauma, car accident, surgery, orthopedic surgery, after childbirth, delivery, people undergoing cancer treatment, people with sickle cell disease -- the list goes on and on and on, lifesaving.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just red blood cells, it`s platelets, plasma, something called cryoprecipitate, all that comes out of a single donation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So a drive like this thing can really make a difference, because there`s a shortage of blood supply in the country right now. Why is that and how does it affect patients?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: It`s probably complicated. There are a lot of reasons that go into shortages -- weather issues, like what we`re seeing right now in Florida with the hurricane, there will be a shortage there in that region.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people think they`re not eligible. So only three percent of age eligible people actually donate, which is why I think this initiative is so great to increase awareness amongst college students, but really, it is very important that people understand that this could literally affect any of us today. So it`s truly a way to save a life.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Let`s talk about eligibility.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: You recently donated blood.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: So who exactly is eligible and what is the process like?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: You know, there are so many myths and misconceptions. Most people don`t think they`re eligible. Age wise, you have to be 16 or 17. Depending on your state, you may need parental approval. You need to be at least 110 pounds. You can`t be actively sick with something like the flu, influenza or COVID.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s other basic screening questionnaire that everyone has to go through. People can go to the Red Cross website and look at the eligibility list, and the process is super easy, you guys.\par \par \uc2 Again, basic questionnaire. The process itself takes about 10 to 15 minutes. All in, you`re there for about an hour. You get a little light snack before you leave, and you`re good to go the next day, and you can re donate basically two months later.\par \par \uc2 So look, there are a lot of ways to save lives. I always tell people, you can learn CPR, you can sign up to be an organ donor. You can give blood multiple times and save multiple lives.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Well, I saw Herbie Husker out there from my University of Nebraska. Come on, so let`s get out there and do this.\par \par \uc2 Dr Ashton, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER ASHTON: You bet.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Monday on CBS MORNINGS, it`s "Beg-Knows America" where we were able to grant a wish for one of our viewers who wanted to reunite with and thank a nurse who saved her life.\par \par \uc2 Here we are outside that nurse`s home in Temecula, California.\par \par \uc2 You flew 1,600 miles for this moment. Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes. I`m so ready.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m really nervous to see her, but I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She has waited 35 years to do this.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And you`ll see what happens Monday on CBS MORNINGS. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And our series "Mixtape Moments," where we take turns sharing a song from an emerging artist and today, it`s my turn. I want to introduce you to Jeremie Albino. He`s a Canadian singer-songwriter, got his start as a teenager singing on the streets of Toronto. They call it busking.\par \par \uc2 Now, he is out with his fourth album. So here`s a listen to "I Don`t Mind Waiting."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "I Don`t Mind Waiting"/Jeremie Albino/Easy Eye Sound)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We like it. We like it.\par \par \uc2 And the rest of Albino`s new album will be released November 1st. Looking forward to it. He goes on tour as well. That`s going to happen early next year.\par \par \uc2 And if you want to hear more from Jeremie Albino and other songs and artists we feature here on our \uc2 Spotify\uc2  playlist, all you`ve got to do is search for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Mixtape on \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or scan the QR code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, I`ve got one more thing to talk about.\par \par \uc2 I want to remind all of you and everyone at home that there`s a big launch coming on Monday CBS MORNINGS Plus.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Whoa. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Aw. Adriana Diaz and myself. You can watch it weekdays on \uc2 CBS\uc2 -owned stations in LA, Chicago, San Francisco, Detroit and Miami, and of course, streaming on CBS News 24/7, our streaming network.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m pumped about it.\par \par \uc2 Adriana is a journalist I`ve respected for so, so long. She`s been a friend of my wife for longer than I`ve known her. And to get together and do a show that is now a little bit more elbow room.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Digging in to what we are curious about, I`m really excited for it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m excited to see you guys do this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s going to be awesome, because you`re going to be able to flex some of those muscles that you maybe get in our ear when people say, you know, wrap it up. You might not have that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everything that grinds my gears, Nate.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Like wrap it up like right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everything that grinds my gears.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wait. Somebody tells you to wrap it up? Nobody tells me that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nobody said wrap it up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, that does it for us. We will see you Monday on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a look back at the week.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINE JOHNSON: Break a leg.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: We`ve got a situation going on here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Scenes of utter destruction along Florida`s Northern Gulf Coast after Helene barrels into the state, threatening millions further inland.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Debris is already here behind us. We have seen wind really jostle us back and forth.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New York City Mayor Eric Adams faces a federal indictment for alleged corruption.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: I will fight these injustices with every ounce of my strength and my spirit. If I am charged, I know I am innocent.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Launching down the middle, intercepted.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Damar Hamlin snagged his first interception of his career during Buffalo`s blowout. Hamlin returned to the \uc2 NFL\uc2  after suffering a cardiac arrest on the field less than two years ago.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Billionaire philanthropist, Bill Gates and award winning musician, Jon Batiste are here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Am I going to see you in New Orleans during the Super Bowl halftime show?\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE (Musician): Oh, you --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh. Wow.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: You`ve got to go with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To be continued. To be continued.\par \par \uc2 JON BATISTE: You caught me off guard.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know I did.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Players can now make big money for their name, image and likeness. Now a college football hero from 20 years ago wants a little bit of that money. We`re talking about 2005 Heisman Trophy winner, Reggie Bush.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you go back to pay him, you have to pay everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So I hope he wins so I can get paid too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You have to pay Nate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re all rooting for you, Reggie, because my boy, Nate, needs to get paid.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two things related to our government that get me misty eyed is voting and when they take the trash away from the curb.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like that`s our government. Goodbye trash, hello democracy. If you don`t pick the trash up, I`m voting you out.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I was preparing to be with you guys this morning at home, I was doing a little homework, and my almost nine-month-old son, Leo, started doing this to me. This is not a crawl, because the belly is not lifted from the floor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I believe it`s crawling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is crawling.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whether it`s a scoot or a crawl. That`s great.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That kid is going places.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But you know what`s impressive? That hair. Oh my goodness. He got a 1970s fro.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood stylist, Law Roach is here with some advice, though, we can`t all look at stylish as Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, stop. Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You guys don`t know this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is a little pelvic disruption here on a Tuesday with Tom Jones.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "It is not Unusual." He know what I was talking about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Pelvic disruption?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Pelvic disruption. Ever see Tom Jones?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When he said that, I was assuming --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s how you ended up with Celine, man. Be careful. Come on.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Frozen"/\uc2 Disney\uc2 +)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: "Frozen" is how you are best known in my household. So many amazing projects.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you the voice of Anna from "Frozen"?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL (Actor): I am.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Get right out of my head. Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And you sing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kristen Bell.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN BELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It is also the singing voice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You`re so talented.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was today years old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony and I are going to go upstairs and watch "Frozen" for the first time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let it go.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240927ek9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 13, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Carter Evans, Elizabeth Palmer, Mark Strassmann,  Caitlin Huey-Burns, Steve Hartman, Nancy Cordes, Carolyn Gusoff \par \uc2 3,677 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): It`s like an invasion from within.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  vows a mass deportation of migrants starting in Springfield, Ohio, a town now seeing bomb threats after the former president spread an unfounded rumor about Haitian immigrants.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`re going to have the largest deportation in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 (POPE FRANCIS speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the pope weighs in on the U.S. election, why he`s criticizing both candidates.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers go on strike. Could it impact commercial travel?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONNY LOUSBERG (Boeing Employee): We just want a fair share of the pie.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS (Mississippi Resident): All these people were talking about me, making fun of me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we go On the Road with a story of redemption.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: What was meant to destroy me actually was the stepping-stone to rebuilding me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell. And thank you for being with us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 There are just 53 days to Election Day, and some mail-in ballots have already gone out.\par \par \uc2 Today, former President Trump spent an hour of what little time is remaining in this campaign to hold a press conference defending his close relationship with a 9/11 conspiracy theorist, threatening also to deport Haitian immigrants from Springfield, Ohio, who have temporary protected status, meaning they are here legally, and then downplaying bomb threats there after he repeated an ugly conspiracy theory about the town during Tuesday`s debate.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  traveled to the battleground state of Pennsylvania, where she said she feels good about her chances of carrying the must-win state.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Los Angeles, where she asked the former president about the time he`s spending with the far right provocateur.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  refused to distance himself from racist comments made by conspiracy theorist Laura Loomer, who has traveled with him this week.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Laura is a supporter. I don`t control Laura. Laura has to say what she wants.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Do you disavow those remarks? And why...\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I have to see what the remarks...\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And why do you associate with her?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, I have to see what the remarks are. I -- you`re telling me for the first time. We`re here about -- we`re here discussing the destruction of San Francisco and California by a person that`s running for president. So I don`t even know what you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But other Republicans have raised alarm bells about Loomer, who called 9/11 an inside job, but accompanied Trump to 9/11 memorial services this week and has posted that the White House would smell like curry if Harris was elected.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  from the battleground state of North Carolina said Loomer utters "disgusting garbage" and will "hurt Trump`s chances of winning. Enough."\par \par \uc2 Trump also doubled down on debunked claims about Haitian migrants causing havoc in Springfield, Ohio, even as some schools there were evacuated today for bomb threats.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And we`re going to have the largest deportation in the history of our country, and we`re going to start with Springfield.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: At Tuesday`s debate, Trump drew attention to the thousands of Haitian migrants, most of whom are living there legally, spreading the conspiracy theory that they are eating people`s pets.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They`re eating the dogs.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: President Biden today voiced his disgust with Trump`s comments.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s simply wrong. There`s no place in America. This has to stop, what he`s doing. It has to stop.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Meanwhile, with just 53 days to go until Election Day, Vice President Harris made her 12th trip to Pennsylvania. Its 19 electoral votes offer more than any other battleground state.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): I feel very strongly that got to earn every vote, and that means spending time with folks in the communities where they live. And so that`s why I`m here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In a new social media post tonight, Trump said that he disagrees with Loomer`s comments, but that she is a private citizen and a longtime supporter of his cause.\par \par \uc2 Trump will campaign tonight in a rally in Las Vegas -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The pope tonight is weighing in on the upcoming election after he was asked by CBS News about what advice he would have for a Catholic voter on who to vote for. Pope Francis made it clear that he doesn`t agree with Trump`s tough immigration policy, nor Harris` stance on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (Leader of Catholic Church) (through translator): They are both against life, the one who throws away the migrants, as well as the one who kills children. Both are against life.\par \par \uc2 Who is the lesser of two evils, that lady or that gentleman? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The pope made those comments on the papal plane on his way home from a 12-day tour of Asia and the Pacific. The pope`s comments hold weight. There are about 52 million Catholics who live here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to this breaking news. CBS News has obtained 911 recordings from parents of Apalachee students desperately trying to get information in the moments after last week`s deadly school shooting in Georgia. A 14-year-old is accused of killing two students and two teachers. And these harrowing calls are difficult to listen to.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): Midway through second period class, chaos.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Sir, my daughter goes to the school next door to Apalachee. Is -- is there a school shooter?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Gunshots, an active shooter at Apalachee High -- 10:23 a.m. last Wednesday, police streaked toward the school.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: We do have an active situation at Apalachee High School right now. We have a lot of calls coming in, and we do have units on scene.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Inside, armed school resource officers confronted the alleged gunman. They say he gave up right away.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My daughter calling me crying, somebody, but everybody is -- boom, boom, boom, boom.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The AR-style weapon used in the attack lying on the floor, killed in the carnage, four people, two teachers and two students, both 14, the same age as Colt Gray, the Apalachee High freshman now charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 His father, Colin Gray, is charged with second-degree murder. He allegedly gave his son the weapon as a Christmas gift. The \uc2 Georgia Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , or \uc2 GBI\uc2 , now says the teenage suspect snuck the assault-style rifle into school in his backpack. The \uc2 GBI\uc2  says a teacher allowed him to leave class to go to the front office. He took his backpack and hid in a bathroom.\par \par \uc2 Minutes later, shooting began. School officials released this video message to the Apalachee community.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA REHBERG (Principal, Apalachee High School): For my Apalachee family, we are planning to have a thoughtful, gradual return to school starting the week of September 23.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: There will be funeral services for two of the shooting service -- two of the shooting victims, rather, tomorrow -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mark Strassmann, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, CBS News takes you inside Gaza and down into the underground tunnel network beneath the strip, including the section where six hostages were found last month, one of whom was American Hersh Goldberg-Polin.\par \par \uc2 Our Elizabeth Palmer traveled with the Israeli military into the corridor bordering Egypt. Those officials reviewed our video, but they had no say in this report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER (voiceover): As far as the eye can see, the wreckage of war.\par \par \uc2 We`re being taken by the Israeli military on a very, very rare trip into Gaza itself, in the southern part along the Egyptian border. This was Rafah City, where \uc2 Hamas\uc2  dug in, literally. A lot of effort has gone into the construction. It`s cinder block. All the blocks are nicely connected with concrete.\par \par \uc2 The Israelis have destroyed eight miles of tunnels in Rafah, and just two weeks ago discovered another one that contained the bodies of six hostages executed just days before.\par \par \uc2 MAN: One of the things that is most devastating for the warriors and the commanders here is when the hostages are being murdered, executed, and we need to take out their bodies out.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: There`s been trauma in Rafah on both sides. Before the offensive against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  started, soldiers ordered more than a million Palestinian civilians to leave. They took refuge elsewhere in Gaza in so- called safe zones, but where there are still daily military strikes.\par \par \uc2 In Nuseirat camp, just yesterday, little Mohammed Hamduna (sp?) survived one. His mother and sister did not.\par \par \uc2 We have seen civilian deaths in areas that should be safe zones. How come?\par \par \uc2 MAN: You know, when \uc2 Hamas\uc2  commanders are using safe zones -- so, every precise attack that we conduct is being thoroughly reviewed in order to minimize the civilian harm and maximize the way that we hit only the terrorists.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: But your -- there is going to be a civilian price. You know that.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We know that because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is a ruthless enemy that embeds inside the Gaza population.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: In Rafah, the enemy \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been defeated by the Israelis, but this is what victory looks like.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: It was a hard-fought victory, Norah, but a limited one, because everybody loses until there`s a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elizabeth Palmer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, 33,000 \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers have walked off the job and onto the picket line after overwhelmingly rejecting the latest contract offer. The aerospace company`s first strike in 16 years is expected to halt production at plants in Seattle and Portland, Oregon.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans reports from the picket line.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STRIKERS: Strike! Strike!\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): The vote was overwhelming; 96 percent of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  union workers want to walk the picket line.\par \par \uc2 I`m from CBS News.\par \par \uc2 At the Renton, Washington, plant, where they build the 737, we met Jerry Tomlinson, Donny Lousberg, and Thomas Doyle (sp?).\par \par \uc2 DONNY LOUSBERG (\uc2 Boeing\uc2  Employee): We`re going to be doing it as long as it takes to get a fair contract.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The union is demanding a 40 percent increase in wages and restoration of their pensions. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  offered 25 percent and extra 401(k) contributions. The company says machinists, who make about $75,000 a year, would be making six figures by the end of the new contract.\par \par \uc2 But workers on the picket line say those increases are based on wages that were negotiated 16 years ago.\par \par \uc2 DONNY LOUSBERG: We just want a fair share of the pie.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: In the Seattle area, where the median cost for a home is more than $800,000, these union workers say \uc2 Boeing\uc2  just doesn`t pay enough.\par \par \uc2 DONNY LOUSBERG: Some people in my shop have to -- they work 12 hours a day, seven days a week to make a living.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: At a conference today, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  chief financial officer \uc2 Brian West\uc2  said the company is "focused on restoring our trust with our people and the union," but added, "The strike will jeopardize our recovery."\par \par \uc2 The production halt of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s bestselling planes comes as the company is already facing a string of safety issues, including two 737 crashes, a door ripped off mid-flight, and a spacecraft that stranded astronauts.\par \par \uc2 JERRY TOMLINSON (Boeing Employee): I absolutely love working for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . I mean, it`s a phenomenal company. We just need to make some adjustments.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, the strike will not affect commercial air travelers, but it could definitely impact \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s bottom line.\par \par \uc2 The company already has about $60 billion in debt, and one analyst suggests a lengthy strike, Norah, could cost another $3 billion.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s a big story.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you for being there.\par \par \uc2 Pop star Justin Timberlake makes a public apology after appearing in court on drunk driving charges. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Justin Timberlake was back in a Long Island court today to accept a plea deal in his drunk driving case. The pop star pled guilty to a lesser charge of driving while ability-impaired.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carolyn Gusoff was there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TIMBERLAKE (Musician): I found myself in a position where I could have made a different decision.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GUSOFF (voiceover): Justin Timberlake appeared in the chic Hamptons` Village of Sag Harbor to face the music, pleading guilty to a reduced charge of driving while impaired, sparing him jail time.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TIMBERLAKE: I try to hold myself to a very high standard for myself. And this was not that.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GUSOFF: As part of the agreement, he was required to issue a public safety announcement.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TIMBERLAKE: Even if you got one drink, don`t get behind the wheel of a car. There`s so many alternatives.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from June 18)\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GUSOFF: Timberlake was arrested in June after running a stop sign just after midnight and charged with driving while intoxicated. He pleaded not guilty last month. Police say he refused a Breathalyzer test and showed signs of intoxication and failed sobriety tests. Police said he admitted to having one martini.\par \par \uc2 In a surprise move, during today`s proceedings, the judge asked Timberlake to add 25 hours of community service to the plea deal, saying the prosecutor`s proposal was too favorable.\par \par \uc2 CBS News legal analyst Jessica Levinson says the judge was mindful of Timberlake`s celebrity status.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: The judge wanted to make sure that this agreement looked like the agreement that would be offered to Justin Smith. They plead to a lesser offense and they get community service.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN GUSOFF: Timberlake must also pay a $500 fine and has one year to perform community service at a nonprofit of his choice -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Carolyn Gusoff, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  want to put more money back in parents` pocket. We break down both of their plans for the child tax credit in America Decides: The Issues.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Being a parent isn`t cheap. Government data shows it now costs more than $300,000 to raise a child from birth to age 18.\par \par \uc2 Since 1997, the federal child tax credit has been helping parents save a little bit of that money. And now both the Harris and Trump campaigns are proposing supersized versions of the tax break.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes reports on the issue from outside Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): If anyone knows how expensive it is to raise a child, it`s Raegen Selden.\par \par \uc2 RAEGEN SELDEN (Mother): Crab`s in the oven.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: She has raised six. Her oldest is 25. Her youngest is 12.\par \par \uc2 RAEGEN SELDEN: I have two sons who are hungry every second every minute of the day. I have daughters who would like to think that they eat dainty, but they don`t.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: She`s an office manager from Norristown, Pennsylvania, and she has always relied on the child tax credit to help make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, the credit was boosted temporarily from $2,000 per child to $3,000, even $3,600 for families with little kids. The move helped cut U.S. child poverty by more than 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 RAEGEN SELDEN: It was a comfort in knowing that you had this extra income.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: One of the few things that both presidential candidates agree on is that the child tax credit should get a big boost.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from August 19)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): I do support it, and I want to have it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from August 16)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate): We will provide $6,000 in tax relief to families during the first year of a child`s life.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Trump campaign is proposing a $5,000-per-child yearly tax credit, while Vice President Harris is proposing a $3,000-to-$3,600- per-child credit with a bump to $6,000 for newborns.\par \par \uc2 Trump wants the credit to go to all families, regardless of income, while Harris would restrict the credit to low- and middle-income families only. She would also make the credit refundable, meaning even parents who don`t pay taxes would get the credit in cash.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA KEARNEY (Professor of Economics, University of Maryland): This is a really exciting moment.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Economist Melissa Kearney.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA KEARNEY: We have tons of evidence that supplementing the income of low-income families with children really meaningfully improves those children`s lives. We see they do better in school. Their health outcomes are improved.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The cost has always been a sticking point. A bill to expand the credit failed in the Senate just last month.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The motion is not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Why do you think people with kids should get a tax break?\par \par \uc2 RAEGEN SELDEN: Because we want them to grow up to be great, productive citizens. And it`s like you can`t do that if they can`t eat.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Especially when the cost to raise a child is only going up.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, CBS News, Norristown, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s a great piece on the issues.\par \par \uc2 And On the Road is next with the story about friends being our real-life superheroes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: The measure of a true friend is someone you can count on even at your lowest moment.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman On the Road.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): You may not recognize him out of costume, but this is David Hobbs. Five years ago, back in his hometown of Clarksdale, Mississippi, David was arrested in this alley for breaking into a backyard shed. He was dressed as Captain America.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, the next day, pictures of the handcuffed superhero went viral. Cable networks and newspapers around the world made him into their laughingstock.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS (Mississippi Resident): I truly felt as if my life was over. All these people were talking about me, making fun of me. And I`m like, man, what an embarrassment I am to my family.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Aside from family, David says one of the few not laughing was childhood friend Trey Lewis.\par \par \uc2 TREY LEWIS (Owner, Good Landing Recovery): After I got my mind around the details of It, I mean, it was just sadness. I mean, obviously, this is drug-induced.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: He was right about that. David had been an addict his entire adult life. Fortunately, his old friend Trey, who he hadn`t seen in 20 years, now owned Good Landing Recovery, a treatment program based in Dacula, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: I came here with a suitcase full of dirty clothes and a-half- a-cigarette. That`s all I had when I got to rehab, bro.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Trey let him go through the program for free.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: And I have five years clean, you know?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Which is why David now looks back at that infamous picture fondly, the worst day of his life now his best.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: Yes, what was -- what was meant to destroy me actually was the stepping-stone to rebuilding me.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Is there a lesson in that?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: I think so. If you still have breath in your lungs and you`re still alive, there`s a chance. You can turn it around.\par \par \uc2 My man.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And you can help turn it around for others.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: (INAUDIBLE) treatment, man.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: OK, get it, baby.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Today, David works at Good Landing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: Bro, you`re amazing, bro.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: How many people do you think he`s helped?\par \par \uc2 TREY LEWIS: Hundreds.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Wow. Really?\par \par \uc2 TREY LEWIS: I mean, that`s conservative, by this time next year, probably thousands.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: David Hobbs --\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOBBS: All those things matter.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: -- superhero after all.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman On the Road in Dacula, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There is hope if we support each other.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240914ek9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for September 6, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mark Strassmann, Elizabeth Palmer, Jo Ling Kent,  Jericka Duncan, Nikole Killion, Nancy Cordes, Steve Hartman \par \uc2 3,755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): These cases are disgraceful.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Will former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spend time behind bars? Voters won`t find out until after Election Day. The judge in his hush money case delayed sentencing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Everyone realizes that there was no case, because I did nothing wrong. It`s a witch-hunt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What it means for the race for the White House.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We will have more on Donald Trump`s delayed sentencing in just a moment, but we do want to begin with some breaking news. The Justice Department says a planned terror attack in New York City has been thwarted. The suspect allegedly wanted to target Jewish people on October 7, one year after the attack in Israel.\par \par \uc2 The suspect was quoted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as saying: "If we succeed with our plan, this would be the largest attack on U.S. soil since 9/11."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes is at the White House with new reporting.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nancy. It is chilling to read what this suspect was planning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: It really is, Norah.\par \par \uc2 And it took the \uc2 FBI\uc2  working with Canadian law enforcement to identify and apprehend this suspect, who is now accused of plotting to carry out a mass shooting at a Jewish center in Brooklyn, which authorities say he wanted to do just over a month from today.\par \par \uc2 Now, the suspect`s name is Muhammad Shahzeb Khan. He is a 20-year-old Pakistani citizen who lived in Canada. He allegedly began posting on social media in supportive -- support of \uc2 ISIS\uc2  last November and eventually, without his knowledge, began communicating with two undercover law enforcement officers. That`s what led them to him.\par \par \uc2 According to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the defendant is alleged to have planned a terrorist attack in New York City around October 7 of this year, with the stated goal of slaughtering in the name of \uc2 ISIS\uc2  as many Jews as possible. October 7, of course, will mark one year since the brutal surprise attack by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on Israeli citizens that left nearly 1,200 people dead.\par \par \uc2 Now, Khan was apprehended just a couple of days ago in Quebec. It`s believed he was headed towards the U.S. border. A resident in the area said she saw police officers outside her home put a man in handcuffs on her front lawn.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH HENSHAW (Neighbor): They said that it was confidential and they couldn`t tell me what was going on. I just saw the SWAT team vans and the army and the -- or the guys all in camouflage with the M16s.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This suspect allegedly told the undercover officers that he chose New York City because it has -- quote -- "the largest Jewish population in America" and -- quote -- "We could easily rack up a lot of Jews."\par \par \uc2 He is charged with one count of attempting to provide support to terrorists -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good work by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 All right, Nancy Cordes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the sentencing delay in that hush money conviction of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . America was supposed to learn whether or not Trump would be sentenced to jail before the end of the month. This all has to do with that Stormy Daniels case. But, today, a judge pushed that back to Thanksgiving week, well after the election.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion is on the campaign trail in Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the self-proclaimed law and order candidate, earns an endorsement from the Fraternal Order of Police in battleground North Carolina just hours after learning his own criminal sentencing was delayed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Everyone realizes that there was no case because I did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The appearance comes after justice Juan Merchan ruled the former president will be sentenced November 26, three weeks after the election, for his conviction for falsifying business records to cover up payments to adult film star Stormy Daniels.\par \par \uc2 Merchan said he wanted to avoid any influence on the election, writing: "This matter is one that stands alone in a unique place in this nation`s history."\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: I`m disappointed in my legal talent, I will be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And just down the street in New York City, Trump chose to attend a hearing on his appeal of a $5 million civil judgment now owed to E. Jean Carroll for abuse and defamation. Afterwards, he spoke for nearly an hour took no questions, and strangely brought up another woman who has accused him of sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: She would not have been the chosen one. Now, I assume she will sue me now for defamation, like I got sued by E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Vice President Harris, who is hunkered down in Pittsburgh to prep for next week`s debate, courted Latino voters in a radio interview.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Uforia)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D-Vice President of the United States and U.S. Presidential Candidate): I come from a middle-class background, unlike my opponent, who had a rich daddy who helped him start his career.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And she picked up another unexpected endorsement from neoconservative former Vice President \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  after his daughter Liz announced her support this week.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (R-Wyoming): \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  will be voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Former Vice President \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  called Trump a threat who can`t be trusted with power again. And he said that we have a duty to put country above partisanship.\par \par \uc2 A Trump campaign official played down the Cheney support -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We will have complete coverage of the presidential debate on Tuesday starting at 8:00 p.m. Eastern right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . It will be followed by a live simulcast of the debate and \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` expert analysis. We hope you will join us.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to that school shooting that took the lives of four in Georgia earlier this week, for the first time, the 14-year-old suspect facing felony murder charges appeared in court today. The suspect`s father, who police say brought him the gun used in -- bought him the gun used in the shooting, I should say, is also now facing two counts of second-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jericka Duncan was at the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (voiceover): With his long blond hair partially covering his face and his hands shackled at the waist, Colt Gray showed no emotion in court today.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Good morning. Sir, are you Mr. Colt Gray?\par \par \uc2 COLT GRAY (Defendant): Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: This morning, victims` family members sat quietly just feet away from the 14-year-old who allegedly killed their loved ones. Moments later, his father, 54-year-old Colin Gray, entered the courtroom handcuffed with his head down and visibly shaken.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is your first appearance hearing.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The first signs of trouble came in May of 2023. That`s when sheriff`s deputies questioned the father and son at their rural Georgia home.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from May 21, 2023)\par \par \uc2 COLIN GRAY (Defendant): We talk about it quite a bit, all school shootings. He`s getting picked on at school.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: They were investigating a tip from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  linking the teen to threats of a school shooting made on the social media site \uc2 Discord\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip from May 21, 2023)\par \par \uc2 COLT GRAY: Like I like told him, I promise I would never say something --\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATOR DAN MILLER (Jackson County, Georgia, Sheriff`s Department): So, you haven`t used \uc2 Discord\uc2  in a few months?\par \par \uc2 COLT GRAY: Oh, no, uh-uh.\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATOR DAN MILLER: And you`re -- you`re -- you`re saying you didn`t say anything like that?\par \par \uc2 COLT GRAY: No. The only thing I have is TikTok. But I just go around there and watch videos.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The teen`s father said there were several unloaded guns in the house and expressed concern about the threats his son allegedly made online.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from May 21, 2023)\par \par \uc2 COLIN GRAY: I`m going to be mad as hell if he did. And then all the guns will go away.\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATOR DAN MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLIN GRAY: And they won`t be accessible to him.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But authorities say that, seven months later, for Christmas, he gave his son the weapon that would be used to commit the worst school shooting in Georgia`s history.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And, tonight, the community gathers at this park behind me not for the scheduled Friday night football game that was canceled, but for a vigil to honor those four innocent victims.\par \par \uc2 As for the suspects, the 14-year-old faces life without or with parole behind bars, his father, Norah, up to 180 years if convicted.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, Jericka, thank you for being there.\par \par \uc2 The White House tonight is demanding an investigation into the killing today of an American woman who was protesting against Israeli settlements in the occupied West Bank. That`s a Palestinian territory much larger -- larger than the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elizabeth Palmer is in Israel and spoke with witnesses to the shooting.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER (voiceover): Ayla (sp?) Eygi was rushed to the hospital, but it was already too late.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They killed her. They shot her in the head.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: She was with activists in the occupied West Bank demonstrating against an Israeli settlement expanding in the middle of Palestinian lands.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, you see the two jeeps, the white car next to it.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: There were clashes with demonstrators, but few details.\par \par \uc2 We do know Israeli soldiers fired live bullets. Jonathan Pollack, an Israeli activist, says one hit Eygi in the head.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN POLLACK (Israeli Activist): I saw her lying on the ground under an olive tree bleeding to death. I put my hand behind her back, where the bleeding came from, to try and stop the bleeding. We called an ambulance, evacuated her to the village`s medical center.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Aysenur Eygi was 26 and raised in Seattle. She`d come to Israel to volunteer in the West Bank, which is a war zone. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says more than 600 people have been killed here since last October.\par \par \uc2 Ayla (sp?) Eygi had no experience of this kind of violence.\par \par \uc2 Pollack is with the \uc2 International Solidarity Movement\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Did she know how dangerous this activism is?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN POLLACK: We make a point of explaining to people the risk.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Essentially, they`re being human shields.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN POLLACK: No, they`re participating in the struggle for liberation.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: An ambulance carrying Ayla (sp?) Eygi`s body left the West Bank tonight for the journey home less than a week after she`d arrived.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The Israeli military has said it is reviewing the circumstances around Eygi`s death -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elizabeth Palmer, thank you for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to the economy. Stocks took a hit today on Wall Street, with all three major indexes ending the day in the red. The Dow was down more than 400 points. The Nasdaq was off more than 2.5 percent, and the S&P had its worst week in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jo Ling Kent is our business correspondent. She joins us now.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jo, this comes after that disappointing jobs report. Tell us what it means.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Norah, big picture, while solid, this jobs report fell short of expectations and confirms once again that this labor market is weakening; 142,000 jobs were added in August, many in construction and health care, while manufacturing lost jobs.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate ticked down to 4.2 percent. Also important, hourly wages rose 3.8 percent over the last year. That`s faster than inflation, which is good news for how far your paycheck can go.\par \par \uc2 Now -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yes, I mean, there`s a lot too of questions about interest rates. What`s the latest on that? Is it going to be a quarter- percent or half-a-percent -- half-a-point, I should say?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: I wish I had a crystal ball for you, Norah, but economists expect a quarter-point cut out of the Fed`s September 18 meeting.\par \par \uc2 But a half-point cut could be more likely with this jobs report. Now, while predicting exactly how much mortgage rates will drop after a rate cut is pretty tricky, savings on a $400,000 30-year fixed mortgage could be substantial. If it`s a quarter-point rate cut, you could save $65 a month.\par \par \uc2 Double that with a half-point cut based on a mortgage rate of about 6.35 percent. Now, looking ahead, there will be one more jobs report before the election, of course, a lot riding on how the economy performs -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Those numbers really do explain the difference.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And there`s breaking news now from space.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s troubled Starliner capsule is heading back to Earth after undocking this evening from the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann joins us now with the latest on this remote-controlled return home.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Almost three months late, Starliner just undocked and is now on its way back to Earth. No one is on board, but a lot is riding on this flight home.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Separation confirmed. Starliner is now backing away from station and starting its return to Earth.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): With no astronauts on board, flight controllers in Houston remotely undocked \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s Starliner from the International Space Station. Left behind, astronauts Butch Wilmore and Suni Williams.\par \par \uc2 Concerns over Starliner`s helium leaks and engine issues have kept them there since June.\par \par \uc2 SUNI WILLIAMS (Starliner Test Flight Astronaut): You have got this. We`re -- we have your back and you have got this. Bring her back to Earth. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 NASA\uc2  decided Starliner will head home without its crew, despite \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s confidence in the ship`s safety.\par \par \uc2 JIM FREE (\uc2 NASA\uc2  Associate Administrator): We view the data and the uncertainty that`s there differently than \uc2 Boeing\uc2  does.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Starliner, flying autonomously, must perform the critical breaking burn on the return to Earth, then separate from its service module and reenter the Earth`s atmosphere for a touchdown under parachutes.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH BOWERSOX (\uc2 NASA\uc2  Associate Administrator for Space Operations): We just don`t know how much we can use the thrusters on the way back home before we encounter a problem.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut Frank Rubio can relate to delayed homecomings. In 2022, his Soyuz capsule sprung a leak. A six-month mission turned into more than a year.\par \par \uc2 Have you talked to Suni and Butch about it?\par \par \uc2 FRANK RUBIO (NASA Astronaut): Yes, we have spoken. They`re doing great. Certainly, there`s a little part of you that`s disappointed. You just have to kind of dedicate and rededicate yourself to the mission.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mark, you`re asking the same question everybody is asking. What about those astronauts? How are they doing and how does \uc2 NASA\uc2  plan to get them home?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Norah, they seem to be doing fine. Space X, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 `s rival, is going to -- scheduled at least to bring them home next February.\par \par \uc2 That`s eight months later than planned. But, Norah, Starliner`s future is much less certain.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mark Strassmann with those updates, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 There`s more brutally hot temperatures out West and heavy rains and flooding across the Gulf Coast. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There were no survivors when a small plane with four people on board crashed into a cornfield and went up in flames today. That`s about 45 miles northeast of Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 Police say the pilot was trying to land at a nearby airport, and a witness said the plane flipped over before nosediving into the ground. Federal officials are investigating the cause.\par \par \uc2 Parts of Southern California are under evacuation warnings after a wildfire broke out near the San Bernardino National Forest. So far, no word of any injuries, but more than 55 million people in the West were under heat alerts today. Burbank was among the city`s topping 100 degrees, while temperatures neared triple digits in Oregon and Washington.\par \par \uc2 And along the Gulf Coast, it`s another stormy night with flood watches posted from Louisiana to the Florida Panhandle. Three to six inches of rain could fall overnight.\par \par \uc2 A little girl is found safe and sound after spending a night alone in the woods. We have got that story next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A Florida woman is recovering after being bitten on the leg by an alligator. It happened yesterday in North Fort Myers while the woman was walking her dog.\par \par \uc2 Neighbor Mark Burlingham ran to help her.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK BURLINGHAM (Neighbor): She was laying on the ground with her dog, and said that an alligator had grabbed -- he came up to get the dog. And she pulled the dog out of the way, and the gator got her leg.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wildlife officials say an alligator 7 feet, 3 inches` long was removed from the scene. The woman is in the hospital and she is said to be doing OK.\par \par \uc2 Police in Washington state released video showing the rescue of a 7-year- old girl who got lost while camping with her family last month. Search teams looked for her all night. She was finally found the next morning, stuck on a steep hillside surrounded by thick brush and sharp rocks. Slowly and carefully, her rescuers got her to safety. The little girl is doing fine now and she just started the second grade.\par \par \uc2 A rare and important piece of America`s history is going up for auction later this month in Asheville, North Carolina. Get this. It`s an original copy of the U.S. Constitution, one of about 100 printed in 1787. There`s only eight of them still remaining. It was found in 2022 in a filing cabinet in a home once owned by a governor of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 The minimum bid is $1 million, but it is expected to sell for much more than that. Oh, I bet it`ll sell for a lot. That`s pretty interesting.\par \par \uc2 On the Road is next with the story about two strangers and the conversation that changed both their lives.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: You never know what event or person will change your life.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Steve Hartman goes On the Road for a story about a warning that turned into a blessing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN (voiceover): When those blue lights appeared in her rearview, then 20-year-old Abbie Rutledge knew she`d done the wrong thing at the wrong place in time. But now, two years later, she sees that run-in differently.\par \par \uc2 ABBIE RUTLEDGE (Surgical Technician): I think it was the right person, right time, and the right words said.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: The cop who pulled her over for speeding that day was Alabama State Trooper J.T. Brown. Abbie told Trooper Brown she couldn`t afford a ticket, that she was broke and in a dead-end job.\par \par \uc2 J.T. BROWN (Alabama State Trooper): And I told her, I said: "Well, how about we talk about it then?"\par \par \uc2 ABBIE RUTLEDGE: It went on for about 10, 15 minutes just talking about different career paths.\par \par \uc2 J.T. BROWN: You be safe.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Their conclusion? That Abbie would make a really good nurse. So, Brown gave her a warning, on this one condition: "Promise me you will go to scrub or nursing school and slow down, and I won`t give you a ticket."\par \par \uc2 Could you have ever imagined that she would take it as seriously as she did?\par \par \uc2 J.T. BROWN: Never.\par \par \uc2 ABBIE RUTLEDGE: As soon as he left and as soon as I got to where I was going, I started pushing myself toward that career, and now I`m here.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And now she`s here, working as a surgical technician at the University of Alabama, Birmingham, Hospital. Abbie says she loves her new job and gives full credit to Trooper Brown, who was at her graduation.\par \par \uc2 ABBIE RUTLEDGE: I wanted him to see the impression he made on me. Five minutes talking to anybody, even if you don`t know them, can make the largest impact of their life ever.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And you never know when.\par \par \uc2 ABBIE RUTLEDGE: You never know when it could happen, no.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Trooper Brown went above and beyond license and registration to guidance and motivation.\par \par \uc2 ABBIE RUTLEDGE: I still got this.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And by taking the advice written on that warning, Abbie not only helped herself. She returned the exact same favor.\par \par \uc2 J.T. BROWN: She made my entire career worth it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman...\par \par \uc2 J.T. BROWN: She did.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... on the Road...\par \par \uc2 J.T. BROWN: I`m proud of you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: ... in Birmingham, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good reminder how our words of encouragement matter and can change lives.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. A good way to end Friday. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240907ek9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for August 12, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Omar Villafranca, Jon LaPook, Bill Kelly, Robert  Costa, Ramy Inocencio, Elise Preston; Dave Malkoff, Carter Evans \par \uc2 3,792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tonight, Israel on high alert, as U.S. warships rush into the region. Our new reporting that a retaliatory attack from Iran and its proxies could come with little to no warning.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (NSC Coordinator For Strategic Communications): We have to be prepared for what could be a significant set of attacks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What the White House is saying tonight, as hopes for a cease-fire deal between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  dim ahead of a critical summit.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Tony Dokoupil, in for Norah, on what is becoming a busy Monday night.\par \par \uc2 We are watching the Middle East, as the White House says Iran and its proxies could launch a significant set of attacks inside Israel any time this week, which is why the U.S. is now rushing additional troops and equipment into that region, including this guided missile submarine and a straight group of F-35S`es aboard the U.S. as Lincoln.\par \par \uc2 The world has been bracing for Iran`s response to the recent assassinations of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leaders, including one attack inside Iran. President Biden, meanwhile, spoke to European leaders by phone today, calling for de- escalation and pushing once again for a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is following this from Tel Aviv for us, where Israelis tonight are waiting for whatever comes next.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voiceover): With the USS Abraham Lincoln aircraft carrier strike group and its F-35 fighters ordered to sail faster to the Mideast, U.S. officials tell CBS News that a limited attack from both \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Iran could come with little to no warning.\par \par \uc2 The White House says it could be this week. The Pentagon also revealed the USS Georgia submarine with its guided Tomahawk cruise missiles is headed to the region, joining two additional U.S. jet fighter squadrons that arrived last week.\par \par \uc2 VEDANT PATEL (Principal Deputy State Department Spokesperson): We are committed to helping Israel defend itself, but, at the same time, we continue to work diplomatically to prevent any major escalation of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: The hope, to deter Iran, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and its allies from attacking Israel, but, failing that, then to defend, like four months ago, when Iran launched an estimated 300 drones and missiles.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. said it shot down about 80 drones and six missiles, Israel and allies the rest.\par \par \uc2 (REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Just hours ago, Israel`s military spokesman tamped down fears of an imminent attack, stressing there was no change to safety advice and that it would warn the country quickly if there was.\par \par \uc2 EHUD OLMERT (Former Israeli Prime Minister): If there is a war between Israel and Iran, it`s going to be a war between Iran and the United States of America and Great Britain and France and maybe other European countries and Israel.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: \uc2 Ehud Olmert\uc2  is a former prime minister of Israel.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRIME MINISTER EHUD OLMERT: All will probably convince the Iranians to think twice.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And renewed cease-fire talks between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are still set for this Thursday. Israel has said it is attending.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  hasn`t committed yet. But if Iran and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  do attack, those negotiations could be derailed -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ramy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are also following breaking news here at home tonight. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is now looking into suspected hacking attempts by Iran against both a Trump associate and the Biden/Harris campaign, the paper reporting that the bureau began investigating earlier this summer, believing that Iran was sending phishing e-mails to Biden campaign officials.\par \par \uc2 And this news drop says \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  returns to X, the site formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , tonight.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  teased his dramatic return to X, the social media site that was crucial to his winning bid for the White House in 2016.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States, and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): We will take back our country and we will make America great again.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trump allies tell CBS News that he is now seeking to counter the momentum of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` campaign, which has been drawing packed crowds across the country.\par \par \uc2 Trump has been falsely accusing Harris of cheating by claiming large crowds that he says didn`t exist. According to the Harris campaign, an audience of more than 15,000 turned out for a rally last week in Michigan. All this comes as Harris works to hold together President Biden`s coalition.\par \par \uc2 And in an interview with \uc2 CBS\uc2  Sunday Morning, President Biden voiced his support.\par \par \uc2 Will we see you out on the campaign trail for Vice President Harris?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Yes. Yes, you will.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Harris is also rolling out one of the first policy proposals of her campaign.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D-Vice President of the United States and U.S. Presidential Candidate): We will continue our fight for working families of America --\par \par \uc2 (Cheering and applause)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: -- including to raise the minimum wage --\par \par \uc2 (Cheering and applause)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: -- and eliminate taxes on tips for service and hospitality workers.\par \par \uc2 (Cheering and applause)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The elimination of taxes on tips echoes a similar effort raised by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We are going to not charge taxes on tips.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He is calling Harris a copycat.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Trump`s running mate, Senator J.D. Vance, is touting his version of a family-oriented tax policy, as he keeps facing questions over past comments about childless Americans.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR J.D. VANCE (R-Ohio, Vice Presidential Candidate): I would love to see a child tax credit that`s $5,000 per child. But you, of course, have to work with Congress to see how possible and viable that is.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The political map also shows this is now a real race. New polls show Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  leading in the battleground states of Wisconsin, Michigan and Pennsylvania -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The Los Angeles area was rattled today by a magnitude-4.4 earthquake centered south of Pasadena, where there was another quake back in June.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: As we have a bit of an earthquake here in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Captured on live television, the earthquake rattled ESPN Studios, jolting Southern California right around the lunch hour.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Is that an earthquake?\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: That`s an earthquake.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Oh, wow, that is a real earthquake.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Whoa. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The shaking so intense, it shook homes and sent items crashing to the floor. This afternoon`s 4.4-magnitude earthquake is the second quake to hit Southern California in less than a week. The epicenter was north of Los Angeles in Highland Park, and it was felt as far away as San Diego.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MERRITT (Resident of Pasadena, California): There was a slow building of energy and then a big jolt and then it was over.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The earthquake is to blame for bursting a water main pipe at Pasadena City Hall.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, despite the intense shaking, no injuries have been reported.\par \par \uc2 The ShakeAlert app did push out a warning about four seconds before the rattling began -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Elise, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, a new tropical storm system taking aim at the Caribbean, Tropical Storm Ernesto gaining strength as it nears Puerto Rico and the U.S. Virgin Islands.\par \par \uc2 Bill Kelly, chief meteorologist from CBS Philadelphia, is tracking this threat for us.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL KELLY: Yes, good evening. Tony.\par \par \uc2 All last week, of course, was Debby. Now we already have our E named storm, Ernesto, as you mentioned, wind sustained at 40 miles per hour. It`s about 300 miles away from Antigua, but it`s racing along at basically 30 miles per hour or so.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to focus in on Puerto Rico, where we could see eight inches to 12 inches of rain come down in a very short period of time. And, of course, that would cause widespread flooding there. And then we shift the focus to the end of the week and into the beginning of the weekend, where Bermuda could see a Category 2, maybe a Category 3 hurricane.\par \par \uc2 That hasn`t happened since 2020, when Paulette made landfall as a Category 2. Back here in the States, still dealing with the remnants of Debby, where we have major river flooding in places like South Carolina. And while Ernesto doesn`t look to have a direct hit on this country, the hurricane season is young. That doesn`t peak out until the mid-part of next month -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will keep that in mind, Bill. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now once again to Uvalde, Texas, and a trove of newly released videos, e-mails and text messages that provide still more terrible clarity into the botched response there to a school shooting that left 19 children dead, along with two of their teachers.\par \par \uc2 This new material follows a legal battle brought by news organizations, including this one. And, as you might expect, some of the new material is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from City of Uvalde Police Video; May 24, 2022)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Uvalde police bodycam video newly released over the weekend shows the chaos and confusion --\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: How do we (EXPLETIVE DELETED) get this guy?\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: -- in response to the May 2022 Robb Elementary School shooting.\par \par \uc2 The city released roughly 600 videos, documents, texts and 911 calls --\par \par \uc2 ARMANDO RAMOS (Uncle of Shooter): The thing that`s happening at Robb right now, he`s my nephew.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: -- including several from the shooter`s uncle begging first responders to let him help.\par \par \uc2 ARMANDO RAMOS: Maybe he could -- he could listen to me, because he does listen to me. Maybe he could stand down and -- or do something, just turn himself in. Oh, my God, please, look, I mean, don`t do nothing stupid.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Where`s the suspect?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: He`s dead.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Where`s the suspect?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: He`s dead.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But by the time of the uncle`s first call, his nephew, the shooter, was already dead, shot by law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT: Please hurry. There`s a lot of dead bodies.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: In the aftermath, also in the document, text messages show officers concerned about the public`s criticism of the police response.\par \par \uc2 In one text, an officer asked: "Is there a way to get our pictures off the P.D. Web site for our safety?"\par \par \uc2 Former Uvalde School Police Chief \uc2 Pete Arredondo\uc2  remains defiant, saying his actions and the officers` actions were not cowardly.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CNN, August 9)\par \par \uc2 PETE ARREDONDO (Former Uvalde, Texas, Consolidated Independent School District Police Chief): I strongly disagree. And, again, opinions vary, sir, and they`re going to. but I can tell you that probably 100 percent of those people hadn`t been in a situation like that before.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s Omar Villafranca reporting for us.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to new researchers warning that cancer in men is expected to skyrocket over the next 25 years, with new cases projected to rise as much as 84 percent, and deaths could nearly double, all this according to a new study.\par \par \uc2 Our CBS News chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook, is here.\par \par \uc2 Dr. LaPook, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 What is going on?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Tony, this study specifically looked at men.\par \par \uc2 We know from previous research in 2020 that cancer death rates around the world are about 43 percent higher in men than in women. So this study today looked at, OK, what do we expect over the next 25 years? And it turns out that it translates to about five million more deaths per year in men in 2050 compared to today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So why an increase in certain types of cancer for men, but not for women?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: It`s complicated, but one reason is a difference in exposure. So you have certain cancer risk factors. For example, cigarette smoking and alcohol consumption is higher in men than in women. There are other possible culprits like exposure to environmental toxins, genetic factors and willingness to participate in cancer screenings.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that many of these cancers, about 40 percent in a recent U.S. study, are preventable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, that does raise the question that some people don`t often like to think about. What can they do to change their life to lower their risk?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: By far, not smoking is the single most important thing.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that about 30 percent of cancer deaths in the United States are from smoking. And after that, exercising, maintaining a healthy body weight, limiting red meat and processed foods, as well as alcohol, these all can help reduce your risk.\par \par \uc2 So, Tony, it is under our control.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, tomorrow`s a new day. People can make a change.\par \par \uc2 Dr. LaPook, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Stick around, because, ahead, we are looking at 2028, the Summer Games in Los Angeles. And we`re also looking back at the amazing results of Team USA in Paris.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The Olympic flag is now back in the United States ahead of the 2028 Games in Los Angeles. And there`s a whole lot of new hardware that came home as well. Team USA easily won the overall medal count and tied China for the total number of golds.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans reports, there is still one medal being fought over.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NBC Olympics)\par \par \uc2 MAN: And the Olympic flame extinguished.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): The flame in Paris may be out, but an Olympic- sized controversy rages on, all about this floor exercise by U.S. gymnast Jordan Chiles.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what`s not in dispute. Chiles should have scored enough points to win the bronze medal, but the judges initially put Romania`s Ana Barbosu in third. The U.S. appealed, and Chiles` score was corrected. But Romania countered, that appeal came four seconds too late.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IOC\uc2  ruled in favor of Romania. Even though \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2  submitted a time-stamped video claiming it proves they made the deadline, late, today, the \uc2 IOC\uc2  said rules do not allow for reconsideration, even when conclusive new evidence is presented.\par \par \uc2 What remains is this indelible moment with Chiles and Simone Biles on the podium and this lasting image of Brittney Griner after all she`s been through, the emotion when Team USA won basketball gold. That victory caps an incredible run of America`s 40 gold medals, nearly two-thirds of them won by women.\par \par \uc2 If U.S. women were a separate nation, they`d be third in the gold medal count.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NBC Olympics)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The Closing Ceremony went full Hollywood, with Tom Cruise leaping into the stadium, taking the Olympic flag, roaring out of Paris and skydiving into L.A., home of the 2028 Olympics.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: L.A. Mayor Karen Bass brought that Olympic flag home with her to L.A. today.\par \par \uc2 One change she`s promising for 2028, a car-free Olympics, though, Tony, I can tell you a lot of people stuck in L.A. gridlock right now might be a little skeptical of that plan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I don`t know how I`m going to get there, but my wife is from L.A. and I will be there. I`m excited.\par \par \uc2 Carter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, in a moment, Eye on America, with a look at the effect on a community when the town`s main employer disappears.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here`s a number for you, 7.2 million Americans out of work right now. That`s 7.2 million people looking for a job and not finding one. And it`s the kind of hurt that can affect entire communities.\par \par \uc2 Our Dave Malkoff reports our Eye on America tonight from one such community, Perry, Iowa. That is a place where a factory town shut down.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: So how many years at the factory for you?\par \par \uc2 JOE SWANSON (Resident of Perry, Iowa): It was just under 14.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): Joe Swanson is no longer working in the town he loves, where his kids go to school. That`s because Perry, Iowa`s, largest employer, this \uc2 Tyson\uc2  pork plant, just shut down.\par \par \uc2 JOE SWANSON: None of us picked this. And I just want everybody to be OK, because I know how hard this is going to be for a lot of -- a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: If it`s this hard for Joe, think of the nearly 1,300 other employees, most of them living in, but no longer working in this city.\par \par \uc2 If you`re going to find a new path forward, sometimes, it`s not here.\par \par \uc2 JOE SWANSON: Yes, absolutely. You have the power. Make sure that you find the right opportunity that`s going to benefit you and your family.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The reality in Perry is that the right opportunities left a long time ago. The meat processing plant is not modern enough for the company. Upgrades would simply cost too much.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Wacker runs the County Development Alliance.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL WACKER (Executive Director, Greater Dallas County Development Alliance): Maybe we were hoping for a miracle at first, where we can just turn off the lights on June 28 and turn them back on with a new user. And that`s simply not the case.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: And they`re not alone. This is one of those one-factory town stories; 25 percent of the people in Perry who are old enough to work in the pork factory do work in the pork factory. If you add in their family and the people who they support, that`s about 60 percent of the town impacted.\par \par \uc2 This is not new. Youngstown, Ohio, was once a thriving steel city of 140,000 in the `70s.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Five thousand employees at one time were gone.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The mills closed. And, today, the population is less than half of what it used to be. Ohio was hit hard again in 2008, this time in Wilmington, when a shipping hub closed; 42 percent of the working-age population lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Then, in 2009, down in Louisiana, a chicken plant which employed more than a third of the town shut down in Farmerville. Back in Perry, Iowa, people like Nacho Calderon are learning from history. His last day driving to work may lead to his first day driving for work.\par \par \uc2 NACHO CALDERON (Resident of Perry, Iowa): Especially concrete or garbage trucks.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Trying to be a truck driver or garbage driver or something like that?\par \par \uc2 NACHO CALDERON: Si, yes. Si, man, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Driving the garbage trucks this town still needs requires a commercial driver`s license.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The community college is giving these trucking classes a way for free to give the workers a shot at staying in town.\par \par \uc2 How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 NACHO CALDERON (through translator): Sad not just for me, but for other people.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: It`s not just you. It`s the people that you work with as well.\par \par \uc2 NACHO CALDERON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NACHO CALDERON (through translator): And they don`t have a car.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: They don`t have cars.\par \par \uc2 NACHO CALDERON (through translator): Or much money.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Nacho is still looking for work. Joe has this advice:\par \par \uc2 JOE SWANSON: And take control.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: He found a job running maintenance for an apartment complex out of town.\par \par \uc2 JOE SWANSON: What I feel like is a great opportunity, and I just -- I want that for everyone.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: A hopeful wish for friends who lost their jobs, but, against all odds, refused to quit on their city.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Dave Malkoff in Perry, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Dave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we travel to Hawaii for a story about a man who lost nearly everything in the Maui wildfires, but is carrying on now for others. That`s in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America. We head to Hawaii for a story of perseverance in the aftermath of last year`s Maui wildfires.\par \par \uc2 Nate Kahaiali`i is a schoolteacher from the town of Lahaina, where, just one year ago, he was training for the Chicago Marathon, when he and his family had to evacuate as flames approached their home. As things turned out, the home would be destroyed, along with everything inside it, except for the running shoes that he grabbed as he ran right out the door.\par \par \uc2 He has since worn those shoes not only in the Chicago Marathon, as he planned, but in every single major race since then. And now, this November, he will wear them once again in the famed New York City Marathon, not for himself, but to raise money for the people back home, those representing Lahaina Strong.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATHAN KAHAIALI`I (Marathon Runner): I continue to think about the people of Lahaina and everyone who`s lost homes, who`s lost loved ones. Every time I bring the shoes with me, it`s like bringing Lahaina with me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Lahaina goes on. The heart of Lahaina beats on.\par \par \uc2 Nate Kahaiali`i is tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Tony --\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240813ek8c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 New Video of Hostage Rescue; Americans Stabbed in China; Raging Miami Apartment Complex Fire; Torrential Rain and Treacherous Heat; Hunter Biden Jury Deliberations; Port of Baltimore Fully Reopens; Detour Planned for Wyoming Road Collapse; \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  Team Up; Dangerous Southwest Heat; Summer Job Spike; Dangerous Southwest Heat; Oprah`s Newest Book Club Selection; A Taste of Childhood; "Swan Song," CBS MORNINGS Deals, CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Chris Livesay, Roxana  Saberi, Teri Hornstein, Stephanie Abrams, Scott MacFarlane, Jo Ling Kent,  Kris Van Cleave, Jill Schlesinger, Nancy Chen \par \uc2 15,548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets on this Tuesday as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A US-backed ceasefire resolution for Gaza is adopted by the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  as Israel releases new video of the hostage raid.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN (US Secretary of State): Does \uc2 Hamas\uc2  want to end this conflict? End this war that it started or not? We`ll find out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A man is in custody accused of shooting someone and intentionally setting an apartment complex on fire. We`ll show you the dramatic rescues.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR FRANCIS SUAREZ (R) (Miami, Florida): We have the suspect who fired the gun in custody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Apple\uc2  announces a new major partnership with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  raising new privacy concerns.\par \par \uc2 TIM COOK (\uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO): It makes your most personal products even more useful and delightful.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: \uc2 UConn\uc2  basketball head coach Dan Hurley has turned down a Lakers` $70 million deal to try and chase down a third straight national title.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Ain`t no party like a Juneteenth party because the Juneteenth party don`t stop.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A Juneteenth concert at the White House. President Biden and Vice President Harris hosted the event.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The White House lawn has never seen anything like this before.\par \par \uc2 ROGER FEDERER (Former professional tennis player): Most of the time, it`s not about having a gift. It`s about having grit.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Roger Federer delivered the commencement address at \uc2 Dartmouth College\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: He served what he called tennis lessons.\par \par \uc2 ROGER FEDERER: And there is another reason I`m here and I can sum it up in two words -- beer pong.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ROGER FEDERER: I`m glad to work on my shots with some of you these past few days. I`m actually thinking about turning pro.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well played.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Well played. You go, Roger Federer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s how you cater to your audience.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And by the way, welcome to the league.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was having a good time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to the league, because I`ve got the beer toss down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like -- I like that he did it that way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We might have to play.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll have to play.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His speech was also really good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin with the new details on the raid that brought four Israeli hostages home from Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Israel released this news footage of the moment the commandos burst in and found three of the four captives inside an apartment building in a crowded refugee camp.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says this operation cost the lives of hundreds of Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 Chris Livesay is in Israel where US officials are now applying new pressure for a ceasefire deal to bring the rest of the hostages home safely.\par \par \uc2 Chris, that`s what everybody wants, bring the hostages home safely, but at what cost? Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Hey, Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State Antony Blinken is visiting Israel amid signs that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is willing to negotiate a deal to release the hostages in exchange for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile Israelis are celebrating the rescue of the four hostages, hoping it was a turning point.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: That daring hostage rescue and new footage from the eyes of the commandos who pulled it off.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: They stormed the apartment building holding the three male hostages.\par \par \uc2 (MEN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "We came here," they shout in Hebrew "To save you."\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Before a rattle of gunfire to cover their harrowing escape.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: For the four hostages now home after eight months kidnapped, it was nothing short of a miracle, says the mother of Almog Meir, Orit.\par \par \uc2 ORIT MEIR (Almog Meir`s mother): You know, last night, I slept all night. It was a relief sleeping. That`s what I want that all the families will feel.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: More than just a boost of morale in Israel, that rescue has offered new hope that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will agree to release hostages in exchange for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 For Israeli commandos to infiltrate so deep behind \uc2 Hamas\uc2  lines, plucking the hostages from the deadly maze of well-armed fighters and human shields, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  might be losing its grip on Gaza and fear their own leaders could be taken out says former special ops commander, Doron Avital.\par \par \uc2 So, does this intelligence mean that there are Palestinians in Gaza talking to Israel?\par \par \uc2 DORON AVITAL (Former special operations commander): No question. We captured a lot of prisoners.\par \par \uc2 (Explosions)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: In the ensuing battle for hostages to escape, the Hamas-run Health Ministry says nearly 300 were killed, Israel disputes that number.\par \par \uc2 (KALUD SHALAK speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "All of my family is did," says Kalud Shalak (sp?). He is the only one that`s left.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We trust you, Biden, seal the deal.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: In Tel Aviv, these are families of hostages who weren`t rescued over the weekend, calling on Secretary of State Antony Blinken, visiting the families of American abductees to push for a deal now.\par \par \uc2 US pressure is the last hope for Yehuda Cohen who have lost all faith in Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 YEHUDA COHEN (Israeli hostage`s father): He is looking for every excuse to refuse a deal. So we will use this operation so we can say, we don`t need a deal, we are releasing them for every eight months, I don`t know for every four months, so by 50 years, maybe my son will be released.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Blinken`s visit comes as five living Americans remain among those in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity. CBS News has learned the US is considering dealing directly with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  for their release, circumventing the government of Israel -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Chris Livesay in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 US lawmakers are working to bring home four Americans from China after they were wounded in a stabbing attack. Now we should warn you, the video from the scene is graphic.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has verified this social media video. I want you to take a look showing the aftermath. The victims are affiliated with \uc2 Cornell College\uc2 , a small liberal arts school in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 A congresswoman briefed on the matter says at least two are hospitalized in critical condition. Chinese officials call it a random attack.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Footage verified by CBS News shows at least three of the injured American victims Monday, one soaked in blood on the ground in Northeast China`s Jilin City after the reported stabbing.\par \par \uc2 The president of \uc2 Cornell College\uc2  in Mount Vernon, Iowa said all four victims were instructors in China as part of a partnership with the university there, and that no students were involved in the program.\par \par \uc2 Cornell has not released the victims` names, but said they were injured during a daytime visit to a public park while accompanied by a faculty member of the Chinese school.\par \par \uc2 Iowa State Representative Adam Zabner told CBS News one of the victims is his brother, David. He said the group was visiting a local temple when they were attacked by a man with a knife adding that David is doing okay and recovering at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Three of the victims including Zabner, have family in Iowa, Congresswoman Mariannette Miller-Meeks` district.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARIANNETTE MILLER-MEEKS (R-IA): Our understanding is that this is a random attack. We don`t know the motivation. Most important is that they receive the proper amount of care and that we can get them back to the United States when you`re medically able to travel and in stable condition.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Police in Jilin confirmed this morning that a 55-year-old suspect is in custody. They say the man stabbed all four Americans after one of them bumped into him.\par \par \uc2 The State Department told CBS News it is aware of the reports about the stabbing and is monitoring the situation -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Roxana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to a wild scene in Miami. This unfolded yesterday where a man is accused of shooting a worker at an apartment complex and then setting fire to the whole place. The flames engulfed several additional apartment buildings west of downtown during morning rush hour yesterday and the shooting victim, and dozens of residents as well all had to be rescued.\par \par \uc2 CBS Miami`s Teri Hornstein has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TERI HORNSTEIN: Big plumes of smoke billowed from this Miami apartment complex Monday morning, as first responders rushed to evacuate more than 40 people inside. Some were rescued from their balconies.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR FRANCIS SUAREZ: There was an explosion right after that evacuation, and our firefighters have demonstrated a tremendous amount of heroism battling this fire.\par \par \uc2 TERI HORNSTEIN: Fire rescue said flames were coming out of the third floor when they arrived around 8:15 AM. As they battled against the inferno, officers found a man with gunshot wounds also on the third floor. He is now in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR FRANCIS SUAREZ: We had 126 approximate firefighters, men and women of our fire department that fought this blaze for approximately eight hours. We`ve got seven ladder rescues.\par \par \uc2 TERI HORNSTEIN: Police arrested 73-year-old, Juan Francisco Figueroa for allegedly shooting the man, a building employee and then intentionally starting the fire. It grew so large the building`s roof collapsed.\par \par \uc2 LT. PETE SANCHEZ (Miami Fire Rescue): We fought from the exterior because it was too dangerous for firefighters to still be inside.\par \par \uc2 JOEL ENRIQUE (Resident): We reached down now.\par \par \uc2 TERI HORNSTEIN: Joel Enrique (sp?) and his family were helped down the stairs by first responders and taken to a reunification center along with other evacuees.\par \par \uc2 JOEL ENRIQUE: There`s some who have never been in a fire, so, my eyes were burning, my nose was burning.\par \par \uc2 TERI HORNSTEIN: One resident taken to the hospital for smoke inhalation along with three firefighters suffering heat exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Teri Hornstein in Miami.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s turn to weather also in Florida, millions of people are under a flash flood risk with heavy rain starting today and expected to last for days. There`s also record heat in the southwest affecting 20 million people.\par \par \uc2 For more on this, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with the widespread rain across Florida today. This will be a multi-day event with heavy rainfall rates, slow moving storms, and that will lead to flooding.\par \par \uc2 We also have to look out for lightning, thunder, and gusty winds within these thunderstorms. We`re going to have waves of rain that will pour over Central and South Florida. It`ll blow from west to east over the state today, tomorrow, and through the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 Now, we actually need this rain. The drought has gotten worse and it looks like the showers will be perfectly placed. But these double digit totals are well over a month`s worth of rain of course giving us that flood threat.\par \par \uc2 Heat is going to be the story for the country for the week here. The jet stream pushing north, allowing for the warmth and humidity to spread far and wide. The only spot that is cool is the Northeast, but that will change as the 80s will be making a comeback as we head into our Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 Nate, good news, if you love the heat, it is going to last through much of the country as we head into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is fantastic news. \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is fantastic news. I love the heat.\par \par \uc2 The jury in Hunter Biden`s criminal trial starts its first full day of deliberations this morning. The president`s son as you know is accused of lying about his drug use when he bought a gun back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is at the courthouse in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It`s been a week filled with emotional testimony in this trial, but it`s the arguments on the gun charges that are expected to decide this case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN (Joe Biden`s son): I think it went well. Can`t wait for the jury to come back. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Hunter Biden`s fate is now in the hands of a Delaware jury. After the president`s son decided not to take the stand in his own defense, Monday, closing arguments started with the prosecution arguing the evidence was personal, ugly, and overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 Adding, jurors should ignore those attending the trial as the First Lady and multiple members of the Biden family looked on.\par \par \uc2 MISTY MARRIS (Trial attorney): Oftentimes, the mannerisms of the defendant who is there to support them, it can be something that sticks in the juror`s mind. So prosecutors tried to get ahead of that.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is charged with three felony counts of illegally possessing a gun while being a drug user and making false statements about his drug use when buying the gun in October 2018.\par \par \uc2 During the trial, prosecutors used Biden`s own words against him, including audio excerpts from his memoir.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN: I used my superpower, finding crack anytime, anywhere.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And texts including messages sent by Hunter to Hallie Biden, widow of his brother, Beau and a woman with whom he later had a romantic relationship.\par \par \uc2 Two days after he purchased the gun, Hunter told Hallie, he was sleeping on a car smoking crack.\par \par \uc2 Biden`s defense attorney, Abbe Lowell said that was a false story Hunter used as an excuse to avoid seeing Hallie and recognize it was not the best way.\par \par \uc2 Lowell said \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  acknowledges buying the gun and having once been an addict, but might have considered himself in recovery and no longer a drug user when he got the firearm.\par \par \uc2 MISTY MARRIS: What the defense wants the jury to conclude is that he might not have actually been using drugs during that time, or at least that prosecutors did not fulfill their burden and prove that he was.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The six-woman six-man Delaware jury deliberated for about an hour yesterday. They resumed this morning in a case that could send the president`s son to prison -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Think about that for a second. We`re all just waiting and watching.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Scott.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some good news out of Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 The Port of Baltimore shipping channel is now fully reopened as of this morning nearly 11 weeks after that deadly collapse of the Francis Scott Key Bridge.\par \par \uc2 The Fort McHenry Channel is one of the busiest in the country and it had been blocked for all those weeks in March after a cargo ship lost power and rammed the bridge knocking it down and killing six road workers who were on it at the time.\par \par \uc2 Salvage workers how to remove an estimated 100 million pounds of debris from the water.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to the latest on the collapse of a key highway in Wyoming that took out a popular route for tourists to Yellowstone National Park.\par \par \uc2 State officials say they hope to have a detour open within the next few weeks as the peak summer travel season heats up.\par \par \uc2 They plan to create two new temporary lanes and fill in some of the collapsed roadway. Wyoming`s governor has declared a state of emergency.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right ahead in a moment, a big 