\par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It is 7:30.\par \par \uc2 There is another stretch of record heat that is making life very dangerous in the Southwest right now.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a look at Phoenix for example. This city is under an excessive heat warning today and looking at temperatures over 100 for the next two weeks straight with highs above 104. Look at all of that orange and red. That is not a good sign. It`s the third hottest June on record since 1895.\par \par \uc2 So with all of that in mind, Kris Van Cleave shows us how Phoenix is taking new steps to fight all of this heat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: This summer scorcher season has started in Phoenix. Temperatures approaching record highs are again in the forecast for much of this week.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: It`s been crazy.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: The last three days was brutal, brutal, brutal.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: It`s been super-hot like I can`t even be outside anymore.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The Phoenix Fire Department even taking to social media, urging people to be heat smart and not hike during peak temperatures, hoping to avoid paramedics needing to use these bags. They`ll put a heat patient in and then fill it with ice to rapidly cool them off.\par \par \uc2 Last summer saw a record 54 days at or above 110 degrees, including all 31 days in July. Maricopa County alone reported 645 heat-related deaths up 50 percent and nearly equal the statewide total for 2022.\par \par \uc2 On average, last July, 13 people a day died in the county.\par \par \uc2 DR. EUGENE LIVAR (Arizona chief heat officer): We look at every heat related death as a tragedy and preventable.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Arizona now has the nation`s only statewide chief heat officer, Dr. Eugene Livar will oversee the rollout of a new extreme heat preparedness plan.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead to this summer, what`s keeping you up at night?\par \par \uc2 DR. EUGENE LIVAR: I think what keeps me up is just is there anything else we can do? Is there more that we can do.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The first of the state`s 18 pop-up cooling centers has been deployed in downtown Phoenix. It`s a solar powered modified cargo container that can be quickly deployed even as cooling centers are open across the region.\par \par \uc2 Some now stay open later into the evening.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WRIGHT (Escalante Human Services manager): It starts in May, so we actually opened most of our cooling centers on May 1st to get started and keep them open until September 30th.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But at \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2 , there is a team welcoming summer surging temperatures.\par \par \uc2 He`s really sweating.\par \par \uc2 KONRAD RYKACZEWSKI (School for Engineering of Matter, Transport and Energy associate professor): Oh, he is.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And he is hot.\par \par \uc2 Researchers use this sweating test dummy they call "Andy" to study how heat impacts the human body.\par \par \uc2 KONRAD RYKACZEWSKI: We`re pushing this technology to a new level and learning a lot about while at it.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Costing more than $600,000.00, Andy is filled with sensors to monitor heat, and is one of only two such mannequins in the world built to go out of the lab and into the heat of the day.\par \par \uc2 Professor Konrad Rykaczewski is an ASU senior global futures scientist.\par \par \uc2 KONRAD RYKACZEWSKI: We wanted to understand how these realistic conditions impact the human body.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: One clear lesson so far --\par \par \uc2 KONRAD RYKACZEWSKI: We really see that from day-to-day as the heatwave continues, your core temperature starts off half a degree higher every day, and then you are so much closer to a heat illness.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And you may not realize that.\par \par \uc2 KONRAD RYKACZEWSKI: You may not realize that.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN C