 exactly.\par \par \uc2 So we`re talking for teenagers and even college students.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So is it better to take a low paying or non-paying internship or a regular summer job?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I have been wrestling with this question, because I know there are some industries --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why are you wrestling? Because I always think an internship, you can get a lot from internship.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You can.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Depending on the intern that you are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Except for --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People want money right now, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Exactly right. That`s exactly what I am getting --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They want more money right now.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : That`s why I am wrestling with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Because I think there are some really good things about getting paid, because you`re now understanding the value of work, but you`re right, there are some industries that are very difficult to break into and an internship can be an entry part.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : But, gang, listen, we know how much student debt is a burden and we know how much kids are really trying to help themselves get a foot forward. So, I think paid jobs can really help that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just to chip away at that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For you`re going ixnay on the internship?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : No. I`m just saying that if you`re -- look, if you don`t need the money, great, but if you need the money, I`m very happy with a paid job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So as you know, I learned the hard way with a summer job that you have to pay taxes on your earnings.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Darn it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What happened? What happened?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was watching police cars in Columbus, Ohio, and I didn`t pay a dime of taxes on the checks I was getting. I didn`t know I had to report it. But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How old were you?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was 20. Gayle --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING:; Okay. I am not judging, just asking.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I know, right, you still -- you have to pay taxes. It`s a great opportunity.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The no judgment zone here. We are fine here.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : We are all safe.\par \par \uc2 So, listen, I think --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are some advantages, right?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes, I mean, listen, I think this is one of the best parts about getting a paid job because you can talk to your kids about paying taxes. Who is this person FICA? Why is less money being deposited in my account?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You can also explain to them, hey, if you`re a 1099 worker, no one is withholding taxes for you. Taxes will be owed.\par \par \uc2 I also think it`s a great time to really instill, I`m looking at you because I know this is right up your alley, automatic savings. You can say to your kids, hey, let`s put away 10 percent of every dollar you make this summer, put it into a savings account. Great.\par \par \uc2 And if they`re over the age of 18, I`ve got a great idea. You say open a Roth IRA. You`re saving for decades in the future. I know it`s retirement, but parents, here`s how you can get them to do what you say.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to do the parental matching program. For every dollar you put into a Roth IRA, we`re going to put 25 cents or something and you show them, you teach them --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Better than 25 cents.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I mean, for every dollar 25 percent match is a pretty good match.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, oka