t and his other dogs and brought them to safety and Garrett was airlifted to a hospital.\par \par \uc2 Blue, of course being hailed as a hero. They are saying that Blue deserves a very nice treat and maybe a good belly rub after all of this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll say. That`s the thing about dogs, unconditional love.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s why I want one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s funny the other dogs were like, Blue, you`ve got it.\par \par \uc2 (Barking)\par \par \uc2 All right. Blue is the real super -- he was the MVP.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my God. That`s good stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very much so.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, ahead, we will talk with the queen of the summer beach read, Elin Hilderbrand about her new book "Swan Song," and a major shift in her writing career.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We do have fun on this show. Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, we`re going to share Oprah`s latest pick for her Book Club, talk with the author about the epic novel. See here`s the novel right here.\par \par \uc2 Any guesses? Any guesses?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour only on CBS MORNINGS, you know we love when that happens -- we are going to reveal Oprah`s next Book Club Selection and the author, guess what, is here in studio.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of authors, Elin Hilderbrand is also here. She`s the queen of the summer beach read. We`re going to talk to her about her latest book. It`s called "Swan Song." And yes, that title hints on some big news.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We`re going all the way back to our childhood in today`s "The Dish: Recipe." It`s a homemade hamburger helper that everyone and I mean, everyone can make.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everyone?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everyone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everyone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll be the judge of that.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s -- they`re making fun of me -- here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN: It is on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to move forward with this proposal or not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Secretary of State Antony Blinken visits Israel to push for a US-backed ceasefire deal for Gaza, which neither side has agreed to.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Five living Americans remain among those in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity and CBS News has learned the US is considering dealing directly with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  for their release.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Four American college instructors wounded in a stabbing attack in China.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARIANNETTE MILLER-MEEKS (R-IA): Our understanding is that this is a random attack. We don`t know that the motivation. Most important is that they receive the proper amount of care and that we can get them back to the United States.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A suspect is in custody after a shooting in a massive building fire that police believe was started on purpose.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR FRANCIS SUAREZ: We had 126 approximate firefighters, men and women of our fire department that fought this blaze for approximately eight hours.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN: I think it went well, but you know, got to wait for the jury to get back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jury deliberations continue today in the criminal trial of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BOY: T-rex.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And nearly two years after it was found by a family in North Dakota, an adolescent T-rex skeleton that scientists called Teen Rex will go on display in Denver.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): To determine that it`s an adolescent partly from its size, but mostly from the fact that even when you were talking directly to it, it wouldn`t take off its headphones.\par \par \uc2 (Laughs)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. Pretty much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Spot on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Uh-huh. Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Headphones are important.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, son. Son? Pay attention.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are you listening?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we`re going to begin with the brutal heat in the southwest of this country, including highs in the triple digits. It`s supposed to last for several days, the second year in a row actually with record heat. It`s all part of a trend of extreme weather all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 , following all of it for us.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 I know it`s supposed to be hot in the southwest this time of year, but even these temps are running 10 to 15 degrees above average. It does look like there`s a break in the heat next week for the West.\par \par \uc2 But Tony, we`re going to keep sizzling across much of the East.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. I don`t like that purple on the map and Steph, numbers published just yesterday show there were a historic number of reported tornadoes in the month of May, all across the US. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Okay, so last month, 570 reported tornadoes struck nearly two dozen states from the Plains to the Great Lakes to the Gulf Coast, including a pair of violent EF-4 tornadoes snapping a record streak of four consecutive Mays without one.\par \par \uc2 Based on preliminary data, that 570 reported tornadoes was the most on record for any May and the highest in over 20 years. Also on the rise, the number of tornadoes per outbreak.\par \par \uc2 Now back in the 50s and 60s, the most intense outbreaks commonly produced 10 to 15 EF-1 tornadoes or stronger, but within the last decade, that number has jumped to more than 20.\par \par \uc2 And climate experts blame warmer temperatures and higher humidity for largely driving these changes and giving us the potential for more thunderstorms in the Southeast and Ohio Valley.\par \par \uc2 The results of all of these big severe storms, it carries a financial cost. Last year, the US experienced a record $19 billion severe weather events, so far this year, we have seen nine.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, that number is sure to rise as we have more than half of the year still to come.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This all feels extreme to people. It is.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead and only on CBS MORNINGS, Oprah`s newest Book Club Selection. We`ll talk with the author in studio, about what it was like to learn when they, he or she, were picked. But first, it is 8:04. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re looking at the queen of the summer beach read, Elin Hilderbrand on the Nantucket Beach where she has written 27 novels.\par \par \uc2 She will join us in just a bit to tell us about her new book, "Swan Song." Also why she says now is the time to retire from writing Nantucket-based novels.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are very excited to reveal Oprah`s newest Book Club Selection. Only on CBS MORNINGS, this has never happened to us before, to reveal it without Oprah. There`s some stuff going on, I will explain a little bit later on, but it`s called channeling my inner Oprah.\par \par \uc2 "Familiaris" --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Did you warm up your voice for that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Familiaris." That`s how she would say it David Wroblewski if she was here. That`s a bad imitation, I know. But Oprah says this: "David takes us on an extraordinary journey that brilliantly interweaves history, philosophy, adventure, and mysticism to explore the meaning of love, friendship, and living your life`s true purpose."\par \par \uc2 "Familiaris" -- that would be the last time I`ll do it -- is a prequel to "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best-selling novel, "The Story of Edgar Sawtelle." Book Club fans may remember that Oprah selected it as one of her picks back in 2008. That`s how long she`s been singing the praises of David Wroblewski.\par \par \uc2 His new book follows Edgar Sawtelle`s grandfather, John Sawtelle and his new wife, Mary, as they start a life together with their friends and dogs in the north woods of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 David, we`re very happy to say, joins us right now in studio. I`m so glad you are here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI ("Familiaris" author): Oh, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I`m so sorry that Oprah can`t -- yesterday, she said I`m going to rally, I`m going to rally. She had has some kind of stomach thing -- stomach flu where stuff was coming out of both ends, I won`t get too graphic.\par \par \uc2 But needless to say, she ended up in the hospital, dehydration --\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- to get an IV. So it was a very serious thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So it had to be --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I hope she`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is -- she will be okay. She will be okay. I hope she`s not mad at me for sharing that detail.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, you didn`t go and tell us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I wanted to make it clear, though, that it mattered to her and it really bothered her that she couldn`t be here for you today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we will pick up the slack, David.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay. Got it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll celebrate you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 She`s so, so love this book. I know Jon is going, let`s get to it. I`m getting to the book, Jon.\par \par \uc2 But this is the thing, you were first selected back in 2008.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You get a phone call.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For "Edgar Sawtelle."\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what was the difference between then and then now?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: I think I expected it even less now than I did then. I was floored back in 2008, and I was just completely dumbfounded at this point, because I don`t -- I just didn`t expect this to happen twice. What a privilege.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is like hitting the lottery twice.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes, or being struck by lightning --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lightning.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: The best kind of lightning. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The best kind of lightning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she loved Edgar`s story so much and now, you`ve gone back, you`ve done a prequel, which was surprising to her.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was there something about the Sawtelle family that you just could not let go. Because it took you 15 years.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: It took me 15 years. I`m a slow writer, but what happened was while I was writing "Edgar," this character of Edgar`s grandfather kept showing up in the book, and wanting to play a bigger role than he deserved to play in that book.\par \par \uc2 So this happens in writing where you have to say, yes, that`s interesting, but I`m going to have to set that aside, because it`s not good for the book itself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: But that kept happening, and I finally said, when I finish "Edgar," if John is still interesting, then he gets his own book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s fascinating.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: That`s and he was -- he was -- I was completely taken with him, partly because the way he is introduced in "Edgar" is having been born with an extra share of whimsy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: And there was a part of me that wanted to know what did I mean by that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Explore that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: By writing that, because --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you mean by that? But you`re the one that wrote it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Well, at the time, it was meant to be, you know, a flashbulb or, you know, an instantaneous portrait of who he was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: So that people would have a sense of his personality.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But that --\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: But I felt like that there was a -- there was just so much behind that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s also so interesting here when writers talk about how characters kind of walk into the scene even if you don`t intend to have them there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And this is what happened in this case, it sounds like, but you also drew on some of your own experiences growing up on a farm in Wisconsin --\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- to write this book. How did what you went through as a kid become something in this work of fiction?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Well, in part, when I wrote "Edgar," what I decided to do is take our family farm and evacuate all the real people and put in all new people to play out a story that I had in mind.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So interesting.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: So I used our farm as a setting, but also relocated it north by about a hundred miles and sort of exaggerated its qualities.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: But the barn in this story and the barn in the first -- in "Edgar" -- that`s the barn I remember crawling around in as a little kid and playing in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s sweet.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: And the house is a variant of the house that I grew up in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You say this book is focused on five quests.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that you? Is that you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is that you right there?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: That is me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Little David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You said this book is based on -- is focused on five quests. Kind of unpack that for us?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: \uc2 Sure\uc2 . I wanted to tell John`s entire life story, and I knew I was going to cover 40-some years. So I didn`t want to try and do that just chronologically, and I decided to divide his life up into what I called the five great quests that his life goes through.\par \par \uc2 Because to me, it seems like that`s the way life works.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: You --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say suppose you could do one impossible thing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you had him some quests.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: And that`s sort of his key phrase that he says to his friends because he tries to convince his friends to come with him on this - - the first of these five quests -- to go to this farm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is also the stories about dogs, which also resonates with Oprah -- farms, dogs, people, ordinary people doing extraordinary things --\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- living their life, and seeing how their life turns out together. But the thing that struck me is about the love story. Because at the heart of everything is a love story, and Oprah and I went back and forth because the title "Familiaris," I said it was Latin for family. She said it has to do with canines.\par \par \uc2 What is it?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Well, you`re both right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re both right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Yes, yes. It`s the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you mean, Gayle, you`re right. NO, but go ahead. We`re both right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: You`re both right. You`re a little writer. It is from the Latin binomial for dog, Canis familiaris, but also the reason why I liked it as a title is there`s the word family is embedded in there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: And familiars, people you know who are close. And the root of the word family is people around the house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The other thing that we went back and I`m not saying argue, but we had a discussion about, the dogs. What did the dogs look like? Because in my mind, I see a dog with long ears, not quite a beagle type, but a lab type. She sees like a German Shepherd type. What does a Sawtelle dog look like to you?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: I`m not going to tell you that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: Here`s why. There is a certain division of labor that I tried to put in "Edgar" and I tried to carry through here that I want readers to be able to imagine how the dogs --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, their own version.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: -- look themselves.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Allow them to paint the picture.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID WROBLEWSKI: And one of the really fun things about talking to people after "Edgar" came out is, people would come along and say, "My dog is definitely a Sawtelle dog," you know, it`s a chihuahua or you know. It is a mastiff or a --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: David, nicely done. Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, bravo. Oprah thanks you. We all thank you.\par \par \uc2 "Familiaris" is on sale now. Check out CBSMornings.com for a reader`s guide.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time for another quick meal you can make any night of the week as part of our series, "The Dish: Recipe." It`s a spinoff of our James Beard award winning series, "The Dish."\par \par \uc2 Today, we take you back to your childhood. Our friends at New York Times Cooking share a homemade hamburger helper recipe that was first created by the culinary director of \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2 . I like the sound of that.\par \par \uc2 The entire dish cooks in one pot.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRIYA KRISHNA ("The New York Time" food reporter): Good morning. My name is Priya Krishna and I`m a food reporter at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Today, we are making my homemade hamburger helper.\par \par \uc2 The ingredients you need to make the dish are: Onion, garlic, bacon, ground beef, white wine, chicken stock, heavy cream, hot sauce, smoked paprika, bay leaf, elbow pasta, American cheese, cheddar cheese, and chives.\par \par \uc2 The first thing we`re going to do is cut up our onion and our garlic. You just need one large onion, and you`re going to dice it into about one-half inch pieces. So you just cut three cloves.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get our onion sauteing with a quarter cup of vegetable oil and season with salt and pepper. We`re going to cook these on medium low heat until they become caramelized and brown, and that takes about 20 to 25 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re going to add our minced garlic and really just let that toast for a couple of minutes. I`m going to add a pound of ground beef, five strips of bacon chopped up.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to spread it into a layer and I`m going to let it brown on the bottom. What you`re looking for in this step is for the bottom of the meat to get this like beautiful, charred, crusty bottom, and to do that, you kind of just have to set it and forget it. I`m going to set my spoon down and I`m going to walk away for 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 This has been cooking for about 10 minutes. Look at that absolutely gorgeous char. And we`re going to just de-glaze the pan with some white wine. You`re just going to let it reduce until it`s basically dry. The wine is what gives it that sort of fancy note of complexity.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re going to add three cups of chicken broth, three-quarters of a cup of heavy cream, anywhere from a quarter cup to a third cup of hot sauce. I like it spicy. So we`re going to go with the full third cup, and two teaspoons of smoked paprika, and then a bay leaf just to balance everything out.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to bring this to a boil and then we`re going to cook our pasta. What`s nice is that you`re cooking the pasta directly in the sauce. So the pasta is releasing its starches. So it`s sort of thickening the sauce as it is cooking the pasta.\par \par \uc2 So while the pasta, it has got about five minutes left, we`re just going to chop our chives. If you don`t have chives, you could totally use scallions.\par \par \uc2 My pasta is cooked. It`s nice and al dente, and now, we`re just going to add our cheese. So you`re going to tear up your five slices of American cheese. You`re going to add a cup and a half of cheddar cheese.\par \par \uc2 Last step, stirring in our chives for a little hit of green. Give them a nice stir.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll just serve myself a little portion. Yum. Like this dish is unbelievable. It is good for people who like creamy. It is good for people who like hearty. It is good for people who like complexity.\par \par \uc2 This dish is everything for everyone. It is a party trick in a bowl.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That looks rich and delicious. I am definitely going to try that.\par \par \uc2 All right, but here`s a question for you. In just a moment, we`re going to talk about what you would put on the line as part of a fantasy football bet. Nate, you got anything?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll put my eyebrows on the line, you want to bet?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No. But a tattoo. Too bad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got the story of a guy who lost big in "Talk of the Table" -- stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. I don`t know that song.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Shaboozey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Shaboozey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, didn`t you pick him for one of Mixtape -- that`s where I first heard him, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is talented.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And now, I went and got the album.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Music is fire.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Heading over to \uc2 Tower Records\uc2 . Pick it up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Shaboozey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is on the Beyonce album, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. Time now for "Talk of the Table" -- it`s a table, we be talking, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, while you were, I think at Will`s wedding, we broke the table. So this is the replacement table. You know we had that glass --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You broke the table?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we had that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I came out here just for the record and it was --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was shattered.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was shattered.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It wasn`t us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And nobody knows how it got shattered.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have an unbreakable table.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It wasn`t the boys being boys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It wasn`t playing ball in the house. I promise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But guys, tables just don`t break themselves. But I`m in pole position today, I`m going to kick it off. Very excited about this story.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got an update from Isabella Strahan on her cancer journey. She, of course, is the daughter of pro-football Hall of Famer and "GMA" co-host, to us, Michael Strahan, he`s a friend.\par \par \uc2 The 19-year-old Isabella was diagnosed with brain cancer back in October. She has had three brain surgeries in her battles with this disease. She has been through it.\par \par \uc2 Well over the weekend, she released a video announcing -- look at this -- that she has finished chemo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s her twin sister. Isabella wearing a gold crown, dancing with her twin sister, Sophia. This is really great, because, guys, after two months, she was supposed to have six rounds of chemotherapy, but because of the progress that she`s made, she`s only had to have four. She`s a student at USC.\par \par \uc2 But we are also pulling for her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are competitors, but all of that doesn`t matter when one of your children is sick.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well said. Well said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are pulling for Michael Strahan and his family, and of course, Isabella. This was great news when I saw this this morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some of the best news we`ve heard all year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that`s great news. That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is Jay-Z`s ROC Nation and his new mission to help low income students of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 The company announced a lobbying campaign to support legislation that would help students secure $300 million in scholarship money --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- to attend private schools. ROC Nation says it aims to empower youth and families with the knowledge to pursue their scholastic dreams, make their voices heard, and become the leaders of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The voucher plan is pending in the state legislature and the student`s eligibility for the new campaign will depend on household income in relation to the poverty level and some of the things will be available, $2,500.00 for half-day kindergarten students; $5,000.00 for grades K through eight. $10,000.00 for grades nine through 12; and $15,000.00 for students with special needs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will make a huge difference.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. It will.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Jay-Z.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The families that needs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" at a restaurant in Charlotte, North Carolina recently, a server noticed a customer come in and sit down with an unusual table guest.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have some video of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, what do we got here?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Okay, so I`m not supposed to be making TikToks at work, but this calls for it.\par \par \uc2 I work at a nice restaurant in Charlotte and I need you guys to see what just came in the door.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, the fella -- the fella is out with --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She doesn`t seem that interested.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, the fella is out with an inflatable doll.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Blow up doll.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A blow up doll. So, but one thing led to another and it turned out that -- so that went totally viral.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But then the gentleman came forth on his own \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account and said, look, what happened is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What happened?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What had happened was?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What had happened was, he lost a fantasy football bet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that was the payoff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, oh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Which raises the broader question, I think, have you -- what would you put on the line? What have you put on the line in a bad bet? I`m curious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think I`ve put a tattoo on the line. The actual bet never went through, but that was the boldest thing I`ve ever said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In getting into it with a friend.\par \par \uc2 But a fantasy football gets wild when it comes to the champ and the loser.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But would either one of you do that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If you go into a restaurant --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh no. No. No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- with a blow up doll? Even if you lost in a bet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t think that`s that embarrassing if people know what`s there for you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but they don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But people don`t know. People don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, you can`t let people know. Yes, that`s a different --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the deal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a whole different ballgame.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Only after the fact that you let people know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let me ask you this, was the girl fine? She said I know I can`t do this at work.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, no, she did lose her job. So the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, she did?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. I mean, that`s a separate -- we can have a separate part two "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She lost her job because she posted the video?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, that`s a separate part two "Talk of the Table" -- I don`t know if we have bonus talk time. But the point is, yes, that`s a question.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t think she should have lost her job.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I mean, you go to a nice restaurant. Let`s say the guy was genuinely out there doing things a little bit differently. Coming at things from a different angle. Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Doing what he needs to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay. I mean --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To get the job done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t need to do a blow up doll.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no. No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She should not have lost her job.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are going to hear from their association, I am sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Moving on. The queen of the summer beach read is here. We should say she`s back. We`re talking to the best-selling author, Elin Hilderbrand, there she is, Elin -- about her new novel. It`s called "Swan Song." And why it`s the last of her Nantucket books. Say it ain`t so, Elin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Retirement?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Say it ain`t so. Let`s see what she means by that.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:35, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh boy, don`t you want to be there? That is a beautiful picture of Nantucket --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- home of our next guest who has been called the queen of the summer beach read. If you can`t go to Nantucket, you can read one of these books We`re talking about best-selling author, Elin Hilderbrand, 27 of her books --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at that, Tony. Look at that wall.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- have been based -- look at all of those books, all based in Nantucket. And now after all of those books and almost 25 years, she is starting a new chapter.\par \par \uc2 Hilderbrand just published what she says is her very last Nantucket-based novel, appropriately enough, titled "Swan Song."\par \par \uc2 It`s about a mysterious wealthy couple and their new assistant who moved to the island and caused drama for the locals with a series of parties, one ending with a missing person. That is drama.\par \par \uc2 Elin Hilderbrand joins us now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, goosy stuff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what`s it like here after 25 years and all of these novels and people love them so much? My mom already texted me she said, Elin`s on tomorrow, bring home a copy of the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND ("Swan Song" autho4): Oh I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: I could not be more excited. I mean, I have been on a one book every summer for 24 years and for seven years, I wrote two books a year. And so, I`ve always been on deadline, and it will be very nice for me to just -- I will keep writing, but I`m going to write on my own timeline.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, but I`ve read this -- to Tony`s point, with a lot of sadness, maybe because I knew that this was going to be the last one of this series.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been here. For the past 11 years, you`ve been here, and you call it "Swan Song," but it also parallels what`s happening in the book, someone else is having a swan song. So I`m assuming that that was deliberate.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Exactly. So my recurring character, Police Chief Ed Kapenash is retiring. He`s at his retirement dinner, he gets a phone call, there`s a house fire and a missing person. He can`t let it go. So he takes on one more case, and I have one more novel.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is like 48 hours to retirement. It`s not just --\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is close to retirement. This is his closing weekend.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he still gets involved.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My prediction is you`ve now retired from the Nantucket-based novel, but you`re going to be traveling around that island. And now that you don`t need the ideas, you`re going to see them everywhere.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Everywhere.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ll still be writing though, Elin, that`s your game plan?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Definitely. Yep, still writing. I`m writing two novels with my daughter, Shelby. We are setting two books at a New England boarding school, so no shortage of drama.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What -- and how is that writing with your daughter? Eighteen?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: She`s 18. It`s been very humbling, Nate because she has her own language and she reads my dialogue and says, no one my age would ever say this. You`re such a boomer. I`m Gen X.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is also -- go ahead. It is also a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, so then, it actually raises the question of like, is the book for that generation or this generation or some generation in between?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: It`s for both. I always try it like in "Swan Song," like I always try and have a mix of generations. So there are younger people in "Swan Song," and there`s drama with them and then there are the people our age.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Every time I read your book, I read it and I \uc2 Google\uc2  to see if these places are real because I`m so captivated by the food.\par \par \uc2 She describes one steak dinner where they said, they drool over the steak the way some people drool over Margot Robbie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The writing pulls you right into the restaurant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The writing -- it does, Nate. I can physically smell it --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You feel like you`re sitting at the table.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- tastes it. There`s one character you describe as a cross between Michael B. Jordan and John F. Kennedy, Jr. Yes, I`ll take two, please.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But your writing is so descriptive and I just can`t imagine that you are stepping away from all of it at this particular time.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Right. So I`m going to keep going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re just going to take a break.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: I`m going to keep going and like in the boarding school novels, like I try and be really specific, but just in a different milieu, in a different arena.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the book --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When I was reading the acknowledgments -- can I just jump ahead to that -- because you did a special dedication to your ex-husband. I love this though. You said: "I will end where I began the book for Chip Cunningham with friendship and profound gratitude." Why did you want to include him?\par \par \uc2 Number one, I love it, because I give my ex a hard time, but at the base of it, he`s the father of my children. I will always wish him well, always.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But for a lot of people, I mean, that is the ultimate mystery of the book is how can she dedicate this to her ex?\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you did. But you did.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it was very sweet.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So glowingly.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Yes --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So glowingly.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: Well, we were together when I started -- like before it was ever published and I went to graduate school and Chip came with me and worked at the Oral-B toothbrush factory so that we would have money.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIN HILDERBRAND: And he always believed in me. He not only wanted me to succeed, he wanted me to shine, and he gave me the time and space to do that. 