oo in 2013. She was one of the four surviving panda cubs that made the program a success for half a century.\par \par \uc2 The zoo`s first pair of giant pandas arrived in 1972, a gift from the Chinese Premier to then First Lady Pat Nixon.\par \par \uc2 PAT NIXON (Former First Lady of the United States): I think they`re adorable endearing creatures.\par \par \uc2 (CHILDREN shouting "Panda. Panda.")\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Since then, they`ve captured zoo goers hearts` tumbling in fresh snow, flopping from heights and chomping down on their favorite snacks, all while welcoming new cubs along the way.\par \par \uc2 Thousands watched in awe online as Xiao Qi Ji was born in 2020, but he and his parents were sent back to China in November, after their loan agreements lapsed amid heightened tensions with Beijing.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Really sad. Heartbreaking to see them go back to China. And hopefully, we can work something out in the future for more pandas.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That hope now a reality after productive communications between President Biden and Chinese President Xi Jinping in recent months, a sign panda diplomacy is alive and well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The new pandas are two years old They will be on a 10-year loan, which means they`re going to be here through at least 2034, and while it`s a win for all of us here in Washington, obviously, it`s actually a boon to China`s panda program as well, because some of the most cutting edge innovations in panda fertility have taken place right here in DC at the National Zoo.\par \par \uc2 And pandas are notoriously difficult breeders, Michelle, so send them here to DC. We`ll get them straightened out.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes, get them straightened out in Washington. That`s back to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Virginia is for lovers, but DC is for pandas.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go. Panda diplomacy is back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is back.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Got to love it.\par \par \uc2 Well, Nancy again, thank you. We love those pandas.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, CBS News investigates the care and nursing homes owned by for-profit companies. Lisa Ling shows as her family`s experience at one of those facilities, but first it is now 8:05, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, we`ll talk to an expert from \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  about how grads can kickstart their careers and also where to look if you want to change your job.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now to a CBS News investigation into the cost of caregiving. More than 70 percent of the nation`s nursing homes are now owned by for-profit companies, often companies with backing from private equity and real estate investors and there is new research raising some troubling questions about the quality of the care at these facilities.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, Lisa Ling first encountered those questions through her own family`s experience, but she soon discovered they are not alone.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, good morning.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Good morning, Tony. Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 Last month, we introduced you to my 92-year-old mother-in-law Grace Song in our "Cost of Caregiving Series."\par \par \uc2 After she was hospitalized for an infection earlier this year, Grace spent a month in a skilled nursing facility, part of an American Nursing Home sector that we found has become increasingly profit driven.\par \par \uc2 This is Berkeley East, a for-profit skilled nursing facility in Southern California, where my 92-year-old mother-in-law Grace Song spent a month earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 GRACE SONG (Lisa Ling`s mother-in-law): When you go through all the stress. I`m just so deadly tired.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: I began recording her during my visits after she told me she struggled to get the attention of staff and medication she said she was told to take promptly at 8:00 AM routinely came hours late.\par \par \uc2 So then you`re starting to panic because so much time was passing --\par \par \uc2 GRACE SONG: Yes, so much time passing by. So I was pushing the buzz to find out what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: One night, my mother-in-law who was a fall risk was left alone in the bathroom and fell. She hit her head and was sent back to the ERROL BARNETT:.\par \par \uc2 GRACE SONG: This is where you feel, how did I get to be so, so helpless.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: More than 70 percent of US nursing homes are now run by for- profit companies, and there is growing evidence patients are paying the price.\par \par \uc2 Harvard professor, David Grabowski is an expert on nursing home policy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID GRABOWSKI (Harvard professor): The vast majority of quality problems in the US are centered in for-profit nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Most US nursing homes rely on taxpayer money from Medicare and Medicaid. Grabowski told us as profit seeking investors have poured in, it`s become more difficult to track where the money goes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID GRABOWSKI: We really don`t know kind of how they`re using public dollars and whether they`re actually going into direct resident care.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: New research suggests within the nursing home industry, extracting profit through inflated rents and management fees is widespread. Meanwhile, many of these facilities have been found to have lower staffing and worse quality of care.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, Berkeley East was taken over by a for-profit company called Aspen Skilled Healthcare, a midsize chain of more than two dozen nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 How often were you exposed to examples of gross negligence.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It felt like on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: This nurse who asked to appear in shadow left Berkeley East after working there more than a decade. She was one of five current and recent former employees who told CBS News patient care had been compromised after Aspen took over.\par \par \uc2 What would you say the biggest changes were?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When we tried to call upper management, they wouldn`t even pick up the phone or call us back. They would just leave us short on the floor.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Berkeley East has racked up so many serious quality issues that regulators say it requires special oversight to protect residents` health and safety and dropped it from an overall four-star rating to one.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Labs weren`t being reported in a timely manner. Patients weren`t given their medications on time.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: While Berkeley East operated at a loss, data from its most recent financial records show overall, Aspen`s facilities reported more than $27 million in annual profits.\par \par \uc2 MAISHA MORRIS: I just feel like she suffered.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Maisha Morris` grandmother, Ruby Frazier spent a month in this Aspen nursing home in Oakland, after a fall sent Ruby to the hospital in November 2022.\par \par \uc2 Maisha and her mother, Yolanda say they watched as Ruby`s condition quickly deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA MORRIS: My mom would push the button for help and they wouldn`t acknowledge her.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: How often was she telling you this?\par \par \uc2 MAISHA MORRIS: Every time we went there.\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA MORRIS: All the time.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: One visit, Ruby who was bedbound began gasping for air.\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA MORRIS: I said, would you all please call the ambulance. No, we`re not going to call. I said, yes, you are.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Emergency room doctors diagnosed Ruby with pneumonia and severe sepsis. She`d also developed these bed sores on her heels. She died six months later.\par \par \uc2 The Morrises are now suing Aspen alleging it knowingly and deliberately understaffed and unfunded the facility to maximize profits. Aspen denies the allegations.\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA MORRIS: They just didn`t care about my mom.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: The Biden administration has been trying to crack down on nursing homes by forcing them to disclose more information about their owners, but David Grabowski told us there needs to be further scrutiny of where taxpayer money is flowing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID GRABOWSKI: Does Aspen have particular investors? There is no ability to peel that back with the federal data.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: My mother-in-law wasn`t the only one who felt ignored. On one visit, her roommate said she couldn`t find staff to change her.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I have no idea when she`s coming back.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Your neighbor said she has to go to the bathroom, but the nurse said she had to go to lunch.\par \par \uc2 GRACE SONG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Are you surprised?\par \par \uc2 GRACE SONG: Yes. That`s the typical, typical answer.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Aspen Skilled Healthcare declined to comment for this report. It recently replaced managers at Berkeley East and has reported staff levels that exceed state and federal requirements.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, an industry lobbying group said: "We support financial transparency and accountability," that says ". these issues distract from others like chronic government underfunding and a growing worker shortage."\par \par \uc2 As for my mother-in-law, she is doing better now. She says she stayed at Berkeley East as long as she did, because the physical therapy which was contracted out was very good. Back to you in the studio.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lisa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We need to be very careful how we respond to the investigation because they are allegations, but that was painful to watch.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lisa, that was a powerful, powerful report, $27 million in profit from Berkeley East for one star service, according to the government rating. I mean, I`m afraid to get old in this country, Lisa. Does it change the way you think about it aging?\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It certainly does, which is why the Biden administration has been cracking down. I do want to report though, that since Berkeley East changed management, we spoke with several employees there who say that they believe things are headed in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : That`s good to hear.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s good to hear. All right, you know, I just talked to my mom last week about getting older in this country and she says I don`t want to go to a home like that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Any kind of home like that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : None of us do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, no one does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. You don`t want to put your parents in a place like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, you don`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up. Vlad has a very personal story from one of our colleagues for Asian American and Pacific Islander Month.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nate, Weijia Jiang shares her immigrant upbringing and says that that immigrant upbringing coupled with her roots in small town West Virginia prepared her to report from the White House.\par \par \uc2 She takes us to Buckhannon, West Virginia, population 5,500 where she grew up, one of the only Asian American families there. Folks, I`m so proud to share her story with you. Stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Some four million students are graduating college this year and many are entering a challenging job market.\par \par \uc2 Government numbers show hiring slowed down more than expected in the US last month, and the unemployment rate ticked up to 3.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 There is some good news for grads, 32 percent of the US jobs listed on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  are entry level positions.\par \par \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert, Catherine Fisher is here with tips for new grads and a few others anyone else looking for a job?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (\uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert): Yes, many graduates.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Good morning. And I know quite a few of them, some recent grads, some grads from 2023, some folks who want to change.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Mill What advice would you have for them, especially these xennials and millennials who sometimes think there is like the culture disconnect.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, first thing is have an open mind. You know, the job market, as you mentioned, is going to be a little bit tough. So what you may have thought your career path was going to be right out the gate may change. So think, be really creative in terms of location, industry, and job type.\par \par \uc2 You also want to really work on that network. Networking should be your full time job until you get a job, but never give up on networking, even when you get that job. So you may think, oh, I don`t have a network.\par \par \uc2 Well, you actually do -- your professors, your teachers, your coaches, et cetera. Tap into them.\par \par \uc2 You also really want to think about what all the firsts you`re going to have. So this is going to be tough. You`re going to have to write your first resume, you`re going to have your first interview, you`re going to have so many firsts, it`s going to be challenging, get used to it.\par \par \uc2 The last thing, which is I think, probably the most important is to have a growth mindset. What we`re finding is that, you know, because you may get a job that you didn`t think you`d be in, this is not your end all be all job. This is a stepping stone.\par \par \uc2 But what we`re seeing is that if you want to have that edge, you need to have AI skills, we hear it all the time.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  just published a report on the state of AI, they found that 66 percent of hiring managers are looking for people with AI skills. But the really great news for entry level is that they were saying that more than 70 percent of hiring managers will hire someone who has AI skills, even if they`re less qualified.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you`ve got to be technologically savvy. You mentioned location.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Are there parts of the country that might be hiring a little bit more?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: There are. So you may have thought, oh, I`m going to end up in New York or San Francisco Bay Area.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dream job.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, dream job. Maybe you`re going to end up in Arizona. Tucson is hiring a lot. So we`re seeing lots of entry level jobs there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: \uc2 Raytheon\uc2 , \uc2 University of Arizona\uc2 , \uc2 Banner Health\uc2 , those companies are hiring.\par \par \uc2 We are also seeing Florida as being a hot spot. Gainesville and Tallahassee are big numbers there for entry level.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We just have about 15 to 30 seconds and our final question here. Are there particular jobs where there`s a lot of demand for people?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: There are. So investment banking analysts, medical assistants, and behavioral health specialists. So you may think, again, I don`t have those skills, a lot of these companies will give you the training and your skills are probably transferable.\par \par \uc2 So make sure that you`re really playing up the skills that you have that they`re looking for.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : I just have to ask this. Are there any things you advise folks not to do in an interview? I mean, what shouldn`t they do?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER: Yes, don`t focus so much on what you don`t have, focus on the skills that you do have. So look at the job description. These are the skills that you`re looking for, these are the skills that you have, and you may not think you have those skills, but you probably do. So be really creative in terms of that storytelling.\par \par \uc2 Get your story down pat. Practice with friends. Think about how do you communicate that you`re a great communicator.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and don`t say you want to worry about work-life balance just yet.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes. Big no-no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Also, watch the movie "Stepbrothers." You don`t do what they do.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Fisher, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, "Talk of the Table" -- it is going to be good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is the Jordan commercials we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Those --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If you see us in the streets, don`t be acting real shy.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you the "Talk of the Table" this morning. And Tony, you`re going to start it off for us, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m very excited. Yes, so you had a Gatorade two weeks ago?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes, yes. Dream house for the kids.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re a drinker of Gatorade.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So as a 90s kid, like yourself, man, I had my first Gatorade in like 15 years at one of Teddy`s T-ball practices.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They had a vending machine, a little lemon lime. Loved it. Took me right back and speaking of that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nineties, baby.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, that was the glass bottle Gatorade.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No recycling in my neighborhood. Just throwing that in the trash. All right, but talking about nostalgia, Gatorade is also going back to the 90s with a new set of TV commercials with that old tagline, "Is it in you?" And you remember who said it Michael Jordan. He`s back, too. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL JORDAN (Former \uc2 NBA\uc2  player): Everything about the game has changed, except for the most important thing. What you have inside? It is what drives the game.\par \par \uc2 The game will always change, but it never will.\par \par \uc2 Gatorade. Is it in you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love it.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Is that him now?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, no.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I just thought it was an AI stuff.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : He looked good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was like an AI special effects.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : It would have to be.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He looks good though. I like bringing MJ back.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because he has the most classic Gatorade commercials.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It gets me totally hyped. I feel like young again. I want to get out there and then I stand up and I`m like --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : I wonder what brought him back? You think you know, he needs the money?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no. But 