e cannot reach until he`s 18 years old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, to the people who paid seven grand for that kid`s art, I have also some drawings at home that my kids made. I can show them to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I see you working, Tony. I see you working.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is a new survey that found dream scrolling, looking at out-of-reach purchases that you`d like to own one day might actually be useful.\par \par \uc2 That poll of 2,000 Americans found the average person spends about two-and- a-half hours a day doing it. Pollsters say that amounts to about 873 hours a day, around 36 days. Half of the respondents say they dream scroll while at work.\par \par \uc2 The good news is 71 percent of those polled said it motivates them to reach their financial goals.\par \par \uc2 Now this is Gen Z and that younger generation that are saying that they just do that constantly. Boomers don`t really do it as much, maybe an hour a day on the phone.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : What is dream scrolling?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dream scrolling, when you`re just looking at stuff --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Like this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: -- that you might want to buy --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Stuff that you might want to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You might want to buy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Look at that. Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For 36 days a year, if you were to take like say half of that time and use it to work on your dreams as opposed to look at your dreams, you might get there quicker.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, Tony with the motivation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Tone it down, oh yes. Okay. Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here we go. Good stuff.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : All right.\par \par \uc2 Well, ahead, Weijia Jiang takes Vlad to her small West Virginia town. See how it helped pave her path to the heights of American journalism.\par \par \uc2 First, a check of your weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All this month, we`ve been profiling members of the Gold House A-100 List, highlighting the most influential Asians and Pacific Islanders of this year.\par \par \uc2 And I am excited to say one of those is my dear friend, Weijia Jiang. You viewers, of course, will know her as CBS News` senior White House correspondent. But did you know that next year, she will become the first woman of color to serve as president of the White House Correspondents` Association.\par \par \uc2 But her story begins in an unexpected place about 300 miles west of Pennsylvania Avenue in a small town far from where she was born in China.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: You picked the best time of the year to Buckhannon, West Virginia. It is the West Virginia Strawberry Festival.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How did your parents find themselves here in Buckhannon, West Virginia, population 5,500?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Yes, this is a question that I grew up asking myself.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The answer to that question is part of her family`s American Dream.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Both presidential candidates --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And one that propelled her to the front lawn of 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Weijia Jiang, CBS News, the White House.\par \par \uc2 Having that perspective of growing up in rural America has been so valuable because I get it. You want a press corps to reflect America.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When Weijia was two, her father moved their family from Xiamen, China to West Virginia, where he attended graduate school.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: So this is where I did my homework. This is where my friends would come hang out with me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Jiangs opened the only Chinese restaurant in Buckhannon, in this building.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: My parents worked 15 to 18 hours a day and it is backbreaking work. The gift that they gave me was my work ethic and my understanding of what an opportunity it is to be able to do work that we love.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is your house.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: This is our house.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Their hard work allowed them to fulfill another element of the American Dream when they bought this home.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: I handmade thousands of efreeti (sp?) flash cards and they`re all still intact.\par \par \uc2 My dad used to always say you don`t know it right now, but you`re making money while you study. And then I got a full scholarship to college.\par \par \uc2 LIYA WEI (Weijia Jiang`s mother): You miss dumpling?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Her parents still live Buckhannon and still make dumplings with love.\par \par \uc2 What does it feel like for you whenever you see Weijia on television?\par \par \uc2 LIYA WEI: Well, I`m so proud that. Yes, of course. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But not every moment is easy to watch.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, go ahead, please.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like this charged exchange with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Why is this a global competition to you --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: At the height of COVID.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And maybe that`s a question you should ask China.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Sir, why are you saying that to me specifically?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I am not saying it specifically to anybody. I`m saying it to anybody that would ask a nasty question like that.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: That`s not a nasty question.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Please go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But Weijia had developed a thick skin growing up in an area with few people of color.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Well, I think the fearlessness came from having to learn how to defend myself. People used to always ask if I could see because my eyes were so small.\par \par \uc2 By the time I was in high school, you know, come at me. I will give it right back to you.\par \par \uc2 DIANNE WILLIAMS (Weijia Jiang`s Middle School teacher): How are you?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She credits her middle school video journalism class with Mrs. Dianne Williams for inspiring her career.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Mrs. Williams is the whole reason why I became a broadcaster.\par \par \uc2 DIANNE WILLIAMS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: You are.\par \par \uc2 DIANNE WILLIAMS: I don`t see why not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Mrs. Williams encouraged Weijia to apply to a contest.\par \par \uc2 DIANNE WILLIAMS: And she already is doing a tremendous job.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hosted by a student-geared broadcast called Channel One News.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And if you won, they flew you to Los Angeles for a week-and- a-half.\par \par \uc2 Hi, I am Weijia Jiang from Buckhannon, West Virginia --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Out of 13,000 applicants, she was one of 20 winners.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The actual anchor and reporters who were working that I got to shadow were Tracy Smith of "CBS Sunday Morning" and Lisa Ling who is a CBS News contributor, and I was only 13 years old and to me they were goddesses, and still are, I should say.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But I got to see what it meant to be a broadcast journalist and I couldn`t unsee it.\par \par \uc2 I hope to be the next Connie Chung, and better. That`s kind of hard, but that`s my goal.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still, at the time, Weijia couldn`t see many people who look like her on the news.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: I wasn`t taking a hairbrush and pretending to sing, I was taking a hairbrush and pretending I was Connie Chung.\par \par \uc2 There were still just less than a handful of prominent Asian American broadcasters.\par \par \uc2 WBUN, our news.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: We have to recreate this.\par \par \uc2 DIANNE WILLIAMS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: At Buckhannon-Upshur Middle School, Weijia and Mrs. Williams guided us to the small basement room used for that video journalism class nearly 30 years ago,\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: I`m Weijia Jiang.\par \par \uc2 DIANNE WILLIAMS: And I`m Dianne Williams.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In a final full circle moment. Weijia had her chance to share a message with students imparting big wisdom on her small town.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: When I was sitting in the same seats that you`re sitting in right now, I dreamed of becoming a TV news reporter, and now, I cover the White House for CBS News and I don`t think I`d be there had it not been for BUMS.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And it is my pleasure to bring in my dear, dear friend, mom and wife extraordinaire, our senior White House correspondent, Weijia Jiang.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to cry because --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m already crying.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : I know you are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very emotional.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, Weijia, this story, I know that you were reluctant to share it because you like all great journalists don`t want to make the story about you. But we talked about how this is while being your story, it is bigger than you. It`s bigger.\par \par \uc2 Your journey is for all of the dreamers out there, all of the first generation and immigrant children who in themselves, see your story and see themselves reflected back in it.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 You know, when you asked me to do this, I was really hesitant because we never want to be the focus of any story. But in this context, I realized it`s not my story. It`s the immigrant story for anyone who comes to this country with nothing but a dream, especially for younger generations who have to see it to believe it, and now they can see me here at the White House.\par \par \uc2 It is an American story --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: -- because you can grow up in a tiny town in West Virginia, and because of the endless opportunities in this country, you can be here at the White House.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s why it was so meaningful to share it and thank you, Vlad, and thank you, CBS MORNINGS for this gift and thank you to my parents forever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Weijia, congrats.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, thank you to your parents. It`s very emotional seeing them and I`m sure they`re so proud of you and seeing your teacher that made such a difference in your life. It`s a powerful, powerful tale.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : And you are courageous and brave every single day and you know what I`m talking about. I`m so proud of you, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you`re one of the best in the business.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Weijia, we talked about how being from small town West Virginia was so important for us to share.\par \par \uc2 We could have done this story in Washington, DC, but I often think that people think that we who cover politics, who cover news in America are elitist from the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They don`t realize that we are from the small towns across the --\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re from small town USA as well.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, that`s the perspective that we bring.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love you, Weijia. Thank you very much for letting me be the one to share your story for you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love you, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Love you, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Love you, Weijia.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we`ll be right back. I`m just caught up in the moment right now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, we all are.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER\uc2 : Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: See you in a bit. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s time now for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals where we share some items all at exclusive discounts.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping now, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Lifestyle expert, Gayle Bass is with us right now.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS (Lifestyle expert): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 And our deals on CBSDeals.com are great for Father`s Day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, that`s right. It is right around the corner.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Great for Father`s Day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And speaking of great fathers, we`ve got the brand from John Legend. We`ve shared it on \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals before, so go there right now because it was very, very popular.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: But we`ve got four bundles today and this is his Loved1 brand.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And what`s great about it is because he is -- you know he`s always on camera.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He always looks good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: He always looks good, but he was looking for a product that was for melanin rich skin and there wasn`t anything out there.\par \par \uc2 So he worked with a dermatologist.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON