 Settles with Nassar Victims; Bird Flu Found in Milk; Pig Kidney Transplants; Anything for Love; Caitlin Clark Ties of \uc2 Nike\uc2  Deal; Lennon`s Long Lost Guitar; Playing Fetch with The Thermanator; Turks and Caicos Travel Warning; Sandwich Generation Squeeze; Blake Shelton`s Life Off Stage; Deesha Dyer`s Unexpected Journey; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Natalie Morales, Nancy Chen, Scott  MacFarlane, Kris Van Cleave, Jo Ling Kent, Jan Crawford, Robert Costa, Jim  Axelrod, Vladimir Duthiers, Lisa Ling, Drew Barrymore, Ross Mathews, Cassie  DiLaura \par \uc2 14,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue those trumpets and welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And I`m Natalie Morales.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, but you see we`re in good hands. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 More demonstrations over Israel`s war in Gaza, but protesters at Columbia say they want amid allegations of antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Our first demand is complete divestment from anything related to Israeli settler colonialism, apartheid, and genocide.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tough testimony for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from his longtime friend, David Pecker, the former publisher of the "National Enquirer," how it could help the prosecution`s criminal case.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I am not allowed to talk, but people are allowed to talk about me.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: First on CBS MORNINGS, we talked to the first woman ever to receive a pig kidney transplant about her experience.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: How sick were you?\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO (Patient): I was pretty much done.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: A photographer who worked for Megan Thee Stallion is suing the rap star claiming, he was forced to watch her have sex with another woman.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER: Clippers and \uc2 Dallas Mavericks\uc2  game two winds up.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #2: Luca Doncic is lethal.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #3: Dallas gets the win. That series is now tied at one game each.\par \par \uc2 SPORTSCASTER #4: It goes. He is the guy.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER ("The Daily Show"): The second day of Trump`s hush money trial, or they could even get into that they had to hold a separate hearing to find out if Trump violated a gag order. Basically, the prosecution`s argument is that a juror has the right to feel safe while serving on Donald Trump`s jury, while Donald Trump`s argument is that that juror lives at 34 West 52nd Street and maybe someone should pay that juror a visit and straighten him out.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually think would be a very interesting case to be on, but I wouldn`t want anybody to know--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- I was on there.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Very tough to be on there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would not want to be on it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know nothing about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Count me out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. To be continued.\par \par \uc2 We are going to begin though with the tensions across this country over Israel`s war in Gaza that stemmed from that October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack. We continue to see the demonstrations in multiple cities now including here at \uc2 Columbia University in New York City\uc2  where the school says students have 48 hours to remove an encampment.\par \par \uc2 In Brooklyn, hundreds of Jewish people were arrested near the home of Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , after holding a Passover Seder, which doubled is a call for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen is at Columbia for us this morning to bring us the latest.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Gayle, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 There were protests overnight here on campus, but it is overall calm right now as conversations with students continue. A representative from \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  tells CBS News there has been some progress made with pro- Palestinian demonstrators, but tensions remain high.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Outside of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 `s campus, demonstrators continue to rally.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: After the school`s administration warned students on campus to dismantle their camps or face consequences, some are seen breaking them down while others remain defiant.\par \par \uc2 From coast-to coast, California to Massachusetts, demonstrations expressing anger over Israel`s bombardment of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 NYU\uc2 , plywood barricades were erected yesterday around a campus plaza.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: After unruly demonstrations Monday night, 120 people were arrested. New York City Mayor Eric Adams blamed the unrest on outside agitators.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D) (New York City): People who peacefully protest for an issue, they are not throwing bottles and chairs.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Earlier in the day at Columbia, protesters reiterated their demands, including an immediate ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in demonstration)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And one of the most crucial components, cutting off any financial interests connected to Israel.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Our first demand is complete divestment from anything related to Israeli settler colonialism, apartheid and genocide.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The words "apartheid" and "genocide" are loaded, but the idea of divestment is a refrain echoed over and over by protesters we spoke to on campus.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Financial divestment, financial transparency --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Financial divestment from any companies that profit off of Israeli apartheid.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: According to Kenneth Stern, who is studying pro-Palestinian campus protests for decades, calls for divestment in Israel have gone nowhere on campuses for more than 20 years, because the issues in Israel are not politically simple.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH STERN ("The Conflict Over the Conflict" author: The campus has decided to divest from investments in Israel or companies that do business with Israel. And part of the reason is that unlike some things that have gotten them to divest, whether it is tobacco, stocks, or things related to climate, this is a political debate where people have strongly divergent views.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And on top of that, divestment isn`t practical.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH STERN: I`ll be very difficult because there are so many companies that deal with Israel. There`s so many mutual funds and other things, it would be very difficult to segregate.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But the practicality may not be the point. The point may just be the protest.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And Speaker of the House Mike Johnson is expected to visit Columbia`s campus later today to meet with Jewish students as we now enter day eight of protests here -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very dicey situation. Thank you very much, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here`s something for you, words turning into action in Washington. Some big changes there that could have an effect on millions of Americans. First tucked into that foreign aid bill that passed the Senate last night, a potential ban on the social media site TikTok, also new rules for airlines from the White House that could save you money when you fly, and finally, the government is deciding an important question for some workers, should your employer be able to stop you from going to a competitor?\par \par \uc2 We`re going to break down each of these stories with our team of correspondents starting with Scott MacFarlane who has got the latest on that possible ban on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 Scott, what do we know?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tony, TikTok is now on the clock. They have a year to be sold or divested by their China-based owner or face the prospect of abandoning the United States within that year, shutting down access to 170 million American users and millions of small businesses.\par \par \uc2 In a Congress that seemingly can`t agree on anything, this passed with overwhelming bipartisan support amid concerns China could use TikTok to exploit and access private Americans` information.\par \par \uc2 One lawmaker called it a spy balloon in your phone, another said it`s a possible tool for propaganda, a big issue as we enter an election season.\par \par \uc2 TikTok has indicated they will challenge this in the courts and file a lawsuit. They argued this is an infringement of First Amendment rights, but Tony, President Biden is expected to sign this bill into law today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Promising, Scott. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are going to turn now to our next story out of Washington. Kris Van Cleave looking at those new rules for airlines that could save you some money.\par \par \uc2 Kris, Chris, that sounds like good news, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The next time you run into travel troubles, you could be eligible for a cash refund. These new Department of Transportation rules that will be going into effect say you can get a refund for a canceled flight, but if you`re delayed more than three hours for a domestic flight or more than six hours internationally, you can also get a refund if you choose not to fly.\par \par \uc2 Also bag fees must be refunded if a checked bag is significantly delayed, and refunds must be given if any services you paid for aren`t provided like broken Wi-Fi.\par \par \uc2 Airlines are also required now to disclose what fees they charge upfront for things like a checked bag, a carry-on bag, a cancel or a change fee. Now most airlines in the US already do that. These new rules will be phased in over the next 12 months --Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Broken Wi-Fi, that`s the fine details. Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now if you signed a contract with your employer, parts of it could be null and void under some new rules that Jo Ling Kent is going to explain for us. It is a change that could affect millions of workers.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning to you. What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Hey, Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  wants to make it easier for you to get a new job. It plans on banning non-compete clauses and those are parts of employer contracts that bar workers across industries from leaving for a competitor or starting a competing business, as some say non-competes help employers protect their trade secrets and allow them to invest in training for employees without fear of losing them, while others argue the clauses are overly restrictive on the workers.\par \par \uc2 The FTC says its ban would translate to 524 more dollars each year for the average worker, yield as many as 29,000 more patents and create at least 8,500 new businesses, but of course this is not set in stone yet. The \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  called the ban an unlawful power grab and said it would sue the FTC to block it -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be following it. Jo Ling, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Well, this morning the Supreme Court here`s a high profile case over abortion access. The case from Idaho could affect laws in other states. It`s the first time the justices are reviewing a state restriction on abortion since they overturned the Roe vs. Wade ruling almost two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court for us.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning. So you know the Biden administration is challenging Idaho`s pretty strict restrictions with a really creative legal argument. They`re saying that that state law which bans almost all abortions actually conflicts with an existing federal law that requires hospitals to provide emergency care for pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Two years after the Supreme Court sent the issue of abortion back to the states, the justices will take up a near total ban in Idaho that some doctors there say is putting pregnant women at risk.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB (OBGYN): It feels like everything has gotten so extreme.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Dr. Stacy Seyb is one of only five high risk pregnancy doctors left in Idaho. He says the law is compromising care because doctors are afraid to perform abortions, even in cases where the state laws seemingly would allow it.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB: Pregnancy is really complex and there are things that can go wrong. We need to be able to help take care of people at this point.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And you know, you could face criminal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 DR. STACY SEYB: Yes, yes and jail time.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Biden administration sued to block Idaho`s law, which bans almost all abortions with narrow exceptions to save a pregnant woman`s life or in cases of rape or incest, arguing it conflicts with a federal law that requires hospitals to provide necessary stabilizing treatment for pregnant women, treatment that may include abortions.\par \par \uc2 RAUL LABRADOR\uc2  (Idaho attorney general): They continue to say that this is putting women`s lives in jeopardy, and it is not.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Idaho Attorney General \uc2 Raul Labrador\uc2  says the state`s law already allows an abortion if the woman`s life is at risk and accuses the Biden administration of trying to find a backdoor way to broaden abortion access in states like Idaho.\par \par \uc2 RAUL LABRADOR\uc2 : There will be more abortions happening in hospitals, because they will create an exception that will allow any type of abortion to happen in the state of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, this case could have far-reaching implications. A ruling for example that blocked Idaho`s law could undermine abortion restrictions in more than a dozen states -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Jan.\par \par \uc2 Now to revealing testimony in former President Donald Trump`s criminal trial from former "National Enquirer" publisher, David Pecker.\par \par \uc2 As Robert Costa reports, Pecker`s testimony could -- could -- spell trouble for the former president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made his frustration known leaving court.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : It is a very unfair situation.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: After hours of testimony by the former "National Enquirer" publisher, David Pecker, who described his strategy ahead of the 2016 presidential election to buy stories and kill them to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , while promoting fake stories about his political opponents, including Senators Ted Cruz and \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors zeroed in on an August 2015 meeting at Trump Tower, where Packer says he met with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his former fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  two months after Trump announced he was running for president.\par \par \uc2 At the meeting, Pecker said Trump and Cohen asked him how he could help the campaign and Pecker told them he would act as the eyes and ears for any negative stories on Trump.\par \par \uc2 The catch and kill plan was the origin of Cohen`s $130,000.00 payment to former adult film star, Stormy Daniels according to prosecutors, to keep her quiet about an alleged sexual encounter with Trump, who denies it all.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: The most damaging part of David Pecker`s testimony so far is that he actually had a meeting with \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that this catch and kill scheme would go forward to affect the results of the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pecker described other stories he bought in never published including a $30,000.00 payment to Trump`s doorman for a false story, as well as paying "Playboy" model Karen McDougal, $150,000.00 who claimed to have had a 10-month long affair with Trump in 2006, which he denies.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: By the time we get to Stormy Daniels, that is really story number three in this catch and kill scheme, and the prosecution is talking about a conspiracy to violate the 2016 election by keeping information from the voting public.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The New York trial will resume Thursday and David Pecker will be back on the stand. That same day here in Washington, the Supreme Court will hear arguments in Trump`s presidential immunity case that could have a big impact and whether he can be prosecuted in the federal January 6 case - - Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We will be following it.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We want to tell you now about a major update on the lawsuit against the \uc2 US government\uc2  by survivors of abuse by former \uc2 USA Gymnastics\uc2  doctor, Larry Nasser.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice has agreed to a settlement of $139 million, all stemming from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s mishandling of this investigation into Nasser`s sexual abuse. It involves 139 claims including from Olympic stars like Simone Biles, McKayla Maroney and Aly Raisman.\par \par \uc2 A watchdog report had found that agents failed to follow up on the allegations for over a year allowing Nasser to continue to prey on athletes. He is expected to spend the rest of his life behind bars.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has announced a potentially troubling discovery. Traces of bird flu in some samples of pasteurized grocery store milk. Health officials say they think it is unlikely the particles could infect people, but they`re conducting more tests just to be safe.\par \par \uc2 This could mean bird flu is more widespread than previously known in US cows, but they say the milk we buy is still safe to drink.\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up, a woman makes history with experimental surgery including a transplant from a very unlikely donor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jon LaPook has the story. But first, it is 7:15 and time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead this morning, new details about an American family`s legal nightmare during a Caribbean vacation. What the State Department is telling tourists.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our new series on the "Cost of Caregiving" focuses on the so-called sandwich generation. We`ll look at the challenges of caring for older parents who are raising young kids.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  is back with another installment of our series "Anything For Love" on the rise of romance scams, Jim.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Vlad, you remember yesterday, we brought you the story of Laura Kowal. Remember she was that mother in Illinois who found what she thought was love online only to be robbed of her life savings.\par \par \uc2 Today, we look at whether dating sites do enough to protect users and even ask one CEO if they invest enough in trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This portion of CBS MORNINGS is sponsored by booking.com.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: This morning we look at an experimental procedure that is giving hope to people who need organ transplants.\par \par \uc2 More than 89,000 Americans are waiting for a donor kidney. So far this year only 6,900 have gotten one; just over 27,000 transplants were performed last year.\par \par \uc2 Well, 12 days ago, a New Jersey patient became the first woman and the second living human to receive a kidney transplant from a pig.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jon LaPook shows how a strategy never attempted before is giving her a second chance at life.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: When you came into the hospital, how sick were you?\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: I was pretty much done.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: At age 54, Lisa Pisano`s heart and kidneys were failing.\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: I couldn`t go up the stairs. I couldn`t drive. I couldn`t play with my grandkids. So when this opportunity came to me, I said, I am taking it.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY (Transplant Institute at NYU Langone Health): When we admitted her to the hospital, she had two cardiac arrests. So that`s how close she was to death.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Dr. Robert Montgomery heads up the Transplant Institute at NYU Langone Health.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 FDA\uc2  permission, he and his team decided the only way to save her life was with a first ever approach, giving her both a mechanical heart pump and a kidney transplant from a pig.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY: -- about to do something that no one has done.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The human immune system rejects organs from animals, but Dr. Montgomery and his team used a pig kidney with one gene altered to make it more compatible.\par \par \uc2 How did you know that the kidney was working?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY: So you release the clamps and Lisa`s blood goes into the kidney and it suddenly turns this beautiful pink color. It is a healthy organ. This isn`t just about keeping somebody alive, it is restoring them to their lives.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: How are you feeling today?\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: I feel great today, compared to other days.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: As she recovers from surgery, Pisano dreams of playing with her two young grandchildren for the first time in years.\par \par \uc2 What`s the scene look like? Draw it for me.\par \par \uc2 LISA PISANO: My granddaughter is, g`ma, g`ma, come on. Running around in the backyard. My grandson is getting into anything and everything you can get into, you know, trying to do it all, and it`s a wonderful scene.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Wow. Dr. LaPook, you`re a professor of Medicine at NYU Langone, let`s start off.\par \par \uc2 I mean, she looks great right now. What is the long-term prognosis for? Is this viewed as a permanent fix?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Well, fingers crossed. I mean, she was very sick.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: You heard, she had a couple of cardiac arrests when she came in. This was her last option. So I spoke to Dr. Montgomery. He thinks she is doing very well right now of course.\par \par \uc2 You know, she is only the second person to get this last month. Nancy Chen reported about up at Harvard, they did the first male who got this, but you know, fingers crossed, in Medicine, you`re just always going step by step. This was used under \uc2 FDA\uc2  Compassionate Use Protocol, so it is not approved yet. There`s ways to go but you know, what an amazing, amazing technological tour de force.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so great that it goes from I`m pretty much done to, g`ma, g`ma, come and play.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And about a hundred people at NYU Langone were involved in this care.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Great story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Jon LaPook.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: The team -- it is a team effort.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, more of our CBS News Investigation about romance scams.\par \par \uc2 Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It is 7:30.\par \par \uc2 Our CBS News Investigation, we are calling it "Anything For Love" about the wave of online romance scams turns now to the role of dating apps.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve reported that these romance scams are costing tens of thousands of Americans more than a billion dollars -- that`s with a B -- a year. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  tells us that scammers have used online dating apps to broaden their reach.\par \par \uc2 Chief investigative correspondent, \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  is back with that part of the story. I`m enjoying this series so much, Jim, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: So much heartbreak, so much heartbreak.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Three in 10 Americans say they have used a dating app at some point. In the case of Kelly Gowe`s mother, she believed it was the safest place to find love.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE (Lauren Kowal`s daughter): My mom was actually very specific about wanting to do online dating.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Online dating felt safer.\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : It`s ironic.\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: I know.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Ironic, because after Kelly Gowe`s mom, Laura Kowal found a partner on the dating site, match.com, she was scammed out of one-and-a- half million dollars by someone posing falsely online as Frank.\par \par \uc2 Did she show you her profile on match.com?\par \par \uc2 KELLY GOWE: She never did. I thought she could navigate that very well and would never put herself in any position where she would get hurt.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Laura was like millions of Americans who have logged on to a dating app looking for love. But according to a Pew survey, more than half the people using dating apps believe they`ve been approached by a scam account. Take the profile picture of Frank who Laura met on match.com.\par \par \uc2 We tracked the stolen photo. It`s actually a doctor who lives in Chile.\par \par \uc2 How is it possible that romance scammers can create these fraudulent profiles on online dating sites?\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES (Garbo): I mean, it`s quite easy. They don`t want friction on these apps.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Kathryn Kosmides` nonprofit, Garbo, partnered in 2021 with match.com`s parent company to provide users with background checks. But after a leadership change at Match, the partnership broke down.\par \par \uc2 Kosmides says the entire industry shortchanges safety.\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES: So we`ve talked to every online dating platform out there from the largest to the smallest. They all have similar thoughts and feelings around trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: And what are those feelings?\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES: That trust and safety is not generating revenue enough for them to invest in it.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Kosmides is one of several people who worked on security with Match, who told us dating apps don`t ask a lot of background questions of new users. The idea, she says is to keep signups easy and the numbers up for these subscription-based businesses that`s essential to boost revenue.\par \par \uc2 So they don`t do enough in your view to combat the fraudulent profiles that are being established.\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN KOSMIDES: Correct.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : In 2019, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  filed a lawsuit alleging from 2013 to 2018, as many as 25 to 30 percent of match.com members who registered each day, were using match.com to perpetrate scams.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Match Group\uc2  told us: "We believe the FTC allegations have no merit."\par \par \uc2 Three former executives who were at Match as recently as last year, told us that fake accounts were a serious and persistent problem for overmatched and underfunded Trust and Safety Departments, the corporate offices that handle customer security.\par \par \uc2 Match told us it spends $125 million every year on trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 JAMES BARNACLE (\uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , Financial Crimes Section chief): Prior to 2017, we didn`t have a significant number of romance fraud complaints in the United States.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: What changed?\par \par \uc2 JAMES BARNACLE: The proliferation of the dating sites.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : James Barnacle is the head of the Financial Crimes section of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JAMES BARNACLE: We think it`s really important for the tech companies to step up and curtail the abuse of American citizens.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: After six months of asking for an interview.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kim, how are you?\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2  (\uc2 Match Group\uc2  chief executive officer): Hi. How are you?\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Good \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  from CBS News.\par \par \uc2 We finally approached \uc2 Bernard Kim\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 Match Group\uc2 , which operates at least a dozen dating platforms, including Hinge, OKCupid, and Tinder.\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2 : Look, we invest a tremendous amount of capital, incredible talent on trust and safety.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: We talked to several former Match employees including former high profile executives. It`s funny you bring up trust and safety because they said that it never gets adequate resources.\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2 : I mean if you look at our investment over the last several years, we`ve only increased the number of talent to people, leadership that`s focused in this area. So it`s existential to our business to remain safe.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Match told us, they removed 96 percent of improper accounts within a day, and use both AI and human moderators to remove bad actors.\par \par \uc2 We tracked down a former romance scammer in Ghana, and shared with him what the Match CEO told us.\par \par \uc2 How hard was it for you to set up a fake profile on the sites like match.com?\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL (Former romance scammer in Ghana): or us, it is easy.\par \par \uc2 BERNARD KIM\uc2 : We`ve really shifted the way that we`ve addressed people that can come into our platforms. We`re working really, really hard every single day to make sure that people are authentic.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL: I think maybe he`s not aware of what is really going on, on match.com. So maybe he might think he has that security on match.com, but people have their way around it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: So what can law enforcement do to tackle this growing threat? We`ll have more on that on the "CBS Evening News" tomorrow and you can see the whole story all in one place on Sunday night in our streaming documentary, "\uc2 CBS\uc2  Reports."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Number one, I appreciate Mr. Kim talking to you as opposed to 