par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNING. I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil and that`s Natalie Morales.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, Lisa Ling looks at the challenges of the so-called Sandwich Generation. That`s people caring for aging parents and kids at the same time. That`s a lot. It`s part of a new series. We`re calling it the "Cost of Caregiving."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And country megastar, Blake Shelton takes us to Las Vegas to show us his new music venue there. He`s also getting personal in a conversation with Cassie DiLaura of "Entertainment Tonight."\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And our good friends, Drew Barrymore and Ross Mathews, they are here from "The Drew Barrymore Show" for a special "Talk of the Table."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we`re going to begin this hour with a warning for Americans traveling to the Caribbean. The territory of Turks and Caicos has enacted strict new laws with a mandatory minimum prison sentence of a dozen years for carrying guns or even merely ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Watson from Oklahoma is currently behind bars there and has a bail hearing today. The Oklahoma man was arrested at the airport when security found bullets in his luggage, something he says were in there by mistake.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Valerie Watson`s return to Oklahoma was emotional. The mother of two is home after 10 days in legal limbo, but without her husband, Ryan.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE WATSON (Ryan`s wife): Just to get to hold my babies.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: He is in jail in Turks and Caicos facing a potential mandatory minimum sentence of 12 years behind bars over four rounds of hunting ammo airport security found in his carry-on bag as the couple were set to travel home April 12th.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: This went from what was supposed to be a dream vacation to a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: We spoke with the Watsons Sunday before they learned Valerie would not be charged and could return home.\par \par \uc2 How do you forget that there are bullets in your bag?\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: The best of my knowledge, I have to assume that it happens while taking a trip to Texas to hunt deer.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Bryan Hagerich from Pennsylvania is awaiting trial after ammo was found in his checked bag in February.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN HAGERICH (From Pennsylvania): I subsequently spent eight nights in their local jail, some of the darkest, hardest times of my life, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Possessing ammunition is prohibited in Turks and Caicos. However, tourists were often able to pay a fine until a February court order mandating even tourists leaving the country are subjected to prison time. That includes at least eight Americans.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GRIM (In prison for ammunition): The judge`s sentencing was completely predicated on the fact that I was an American.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: That judge found Michael Grim from Indiana had exceptional circumstances when he pleaded guilty to accidentally having ammo in his checked bag last year, but sent them to this prison for more than five months anyway, hoping to send a message to other tourists.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GRIM: No clean, running water. You`re kind of exposed to the environment 24/7. Mosquitoes and tropical illnesses are a real concern.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Last September, the US Embassy warned check your luggage for stray ammunition because we will not be able to secure your release from custody.\par \par \uc2 RYAN WATSON: I just so desperately want to be back with my son and my daughter.\par \par \uc2 I want to hold them and let them know that this is just an honest mistake.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: A mistake landing American tourists visiting an island paradise in a potential prison hell.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Kris Van Cleave.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh man --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This story is eye opening and heartbreaking, too.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s hard to believe that -- and the government says our hands are tied here. We have to hope that there is going to be some resolution for this guy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. You really have to check the fine details of the rules of the country you`re traveling to it. I mean, people are going to say, I don`t know how this could happen. I can understand how it could happen if --\par \par \uc2 You know, I once went through airport security and they found a knife in my bag that I`d forgotten from some other trip where I needed it. So if you`re in the business of going on hunting trips, you might have it in carry-on bags, some stray bullets, really will cost you there.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes. Check the luggage before you travel for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the meantime, I hope that they can work something out. This is a very, very sad story to me.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Lisa Ling, she shares a very personal story for our new series we`re calling it the "Cost of Caregiving," how her family is caring for an aging parent while also raising young daughters.\par \par \uc2 Part of the so-called Sandwich Generation is what it is known.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our friends, that would be Drew Barrymore and Ross Mathews from "The Drew Barrymore Show." They are here today for a very special edition of "Talk of the Table" and we always love it when they drop by, but first, it is 8:04, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "God`s Country"/Blake Shelton/Warner Music Group)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is country music superstar, Blake Shelton`s hit, "God`s Country."\par \par \uc2 Ahead he talks about his relationship with wife, Gwen Stefani stepping away from "The Voice" and his new bar and music venue in Las Vegas. What I want to know is who names the babies and who names the dogs? A great Blake Shelton song as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is CBS MORNINGS on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re launching our new series today, we call it the "Cost of Caregiving" about how we care for our country`s aging population.\par \par \uc2 So listen to this number, between now and 2030, about 10,000 people in this country will turn 65 every single day. Many experts say the country is unprepared to care for them, and that care often falls on their adult children. As Americans have children later in life, many end up taking care of their parents and their young kids at the same time.\par \par \uc2 They are referred to as the Sandwich Generation.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, Lisa Ling understands this very personally and she is diving into this issue for us.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, good morning. Glad to have you here.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 So you are about to meet my family who are part of that Sandwich Generation. And sadly, we are not alone.\par \par \uc2 About a quarter of US adults fall into this category, and in this series, we`re going to introduce you to some of them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Mornings in our home are pretty hectic. She said there was a mistake on the study guide. My husband, Paul, and I travel a lot for work, so usually just one of us gets our two girls off to school.\par \par \uc2 But today, Paul has to rush back to take his 92-year-old mother, Grace, to doctors` appointments.\par \par \uc2 GRACE (Paul Song`s mother): Is it cold outside?\par \par \uc2 PAUL SONG: No.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It`s been a tough couple of months for her with multiple trips to the ER and rehab facilities.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SONG: That`s the main hospital.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: We are part of the nearly 80 million Americans taking care of children, as well as our elderly parents at the same time.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SONG: Step up.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And we have a name, the Sandwich Generation.\par \par \uc2 At a certain point, your parents become like kids.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Shin (sp?) is dealing with something similar, until recently, she had both of her parents living with her and her husband while raising two young kids.\par \par \uc2 When did your parents move into your home?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN (Both parents living with her): They moved in in 2017 when my first child was born. They came to offer to stay with me for a year to help raise them and then their health started to deteriorate, so I said, you`re staying I`m going to take care of you.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: It was during the pandemic that she realized it wasn`t just her mom`s physical health that was starting to deteriorate, it was her mind.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about how her behavior changed and how the mom that you had always known really started to change?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: In the first few months, it was still okay. It was just -- it seemed like a lot of nonsense talking from her, but then there was a switch. She would tell me to shut up with profanity words sometimes. She would look at me as if she could kill with her eyes.\par \par \uc2 And I would say something and she`d be like, you don`t know what you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Three years after moving in, Shin`s mom was diagnosed with Alzheimer`s disease. Alzheimer`s disease can lead to delusions and sometimes violent behavior. This makes care very difficult.\par \par \uc2 At first, it was Shin`s father who took it on.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: He was spoon feeding her. He was sitting next to her. He was talking to her all day. There was not a moment that he wasn`t by her side.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: After two years, her mother`s condition continued to decline, but it wasn`t until Shin gave birth to her second child that she knew something had to change.\par \par \uc2 What were those first few months like when your daughter was born and your mother was still living here and going through the height of her disease?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: It was hard. I was trying to heal and take care of a newborn and a six-year-old, so it was a lot of nights of screaming and crying into my pillow.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Hi.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: Lisa.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Shin`s family decided that it would be better and safer for everyone if her mom could move into a memory care facility. They found what they were come for an hour away in Anaheim, California where the majority of the residents are Korean American like her mom.\par \par \uc2 Do you know how she`s been doing here?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: She has actually adjusted a lot better. The first month or two was hard. She -- I heard she was screaming for my dad, and then a shift happened, and they said this would happen and she met some friends that she always hangs out with and sits with and eats with.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Shin was able to pay for her mother`s care through California`s Medicaid program called Medi-Cal. It`s for individuals with little to no income.\par \par \uc2 But in many states, costs for memory care facilities can exceed $10,000.00 a month out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JORWIC (Caring Across Generations chief of Advocacy and Campaigns): Medicaid is a state and federal joint program. It`s complicated, which I sometimes think is by design.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Nicole Jorwic is with the organization, Caring Across Generations. She says most Americans should not expect that Medicaid will cover facilities. She says its waitlist is over 750,000 people long.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JORWIC: States can limit how many people they are going to serve, and also the federal government has limits about how much money people can have in order to receive those services.\par \par \uc2 So Medicaid really requires people to spend down their assets and remain in poverty in order to access long-term care.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: So far, we`ve been lucky that Paul`s mom has been able to stay in her home with our kids keeping close eyes on her.\par \par \uc2 My own father spent his final days in a facility that he paid for with a government pension and what he had in the bank. He passed last November, but had he lived, his entire life savings would have lasted him a maximum of two years.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Shin and her family are grateful to have found a safe place for her mom. But as a sandwich caregiver, Shin still has young kids and an aging father at home.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything you would want to say to all of those out there who are struggling and juggling, taking care of their kids and taking care of elderly people as well in their lives?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: It does get better. The kids will get older, they will become a little bit more self-sufficient and they won`t need you as much.\par \par \uc2 And the elderly, I just want people to know, they don`t want to be the ones needing help either.\par \par \uc2 (LAUREN SHIN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SHIN: And this is my turn to give back for all their hard work.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: While Lauren Shin made the difficult decision to move her mom into a facility, for many, that`s not an option due to not being able to afford it, their loved one doesn`t qualify, or there`s not enough room at these facilities to take them in, and we will have more on that tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, what Lauren said right there just really resonated with me where you go, you don`t want to be the ones needing help either.\par \par \uc2 But I feel for the sandwich generation because on one hand, you want to do right by your parents. On the other hand, you now have these young children of your own. It just seems like a vise coming out on both sides, Lisa.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Absolutely. I mean, there`s such an emotional toll.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: But also, the financial toll is so overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: I mean, over 26 percent of Americans are supplementing care for their parents while trying to take care of kids themselves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am so glad we`re covering this. There`s no shortage of love for the kids or the parents, but there is a shortage of so many other things. It`s very difficult.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Curious about the reaction you`re going to get from this because you`re here for the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: Yes. I mean, I`ve been posting about it on social media.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LISA LING: And I`ve been inundated with people because I think that people are just hungry for information.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, because a lot of people do not know what to do.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Ling will be here tomorrow. She is going to introduce us to a family taking care of a loved one at home and show us the challenges that they face as sandwich caregivers.\par \par \uc2 And for more on caregiver resources and organizations like Caring Across Generations and the \uc2 National Council on Aging\uc2 , we invite you to head to CBSMornings.com, it is all there.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Well, ahead, country music star, Blake Shelton takes us inside his new restaurant and music venue in Las Vegas and why he says he wants Beyonce to play there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good luck.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Neon Lights"/Blake Shelton/Warner Music Group)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: That`s country superstar, Blake Shelton performing at his new restaurant and music venue. Shelton has taken his trademark bar, Old Red from Nashville to Las Vegas. It opens up on The Strip at a time when the singer has taken a step back from a featured role on primetime TV.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment Tonight correspondent, Cassie DiLaura spoke with Shelton at the bar about his life with Rock Queen, Gwen Stefani and her sons.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Happy Anywhere/Blake Shelton/\uc2 Warner Music Group\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: For Blake Shelton, it`s all about family.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON (Singer): They`ve changed my life a lot.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Since becoming a stepdad, the country star says he is loving life with his rockstar wife, Gwen Stefani and her three sons.\par \par \uc2 I just think of the beautiful bond that you have and the role that you have taken on as a stepfather. How have they changed your life?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Well, in every possible way.\par \par \uc2 I get to teach them the country, whether it`s music, whether it`s literally, you know, getting out and getting their hands in the dirt or, you know, being out on the ranch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Blake Shelton/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: The couple first met 10 years ago as judges on the singing competition, "The Voice." It`s the seat that took Shelton from Nashville to Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Voice"/NBC)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: A year later, Shelton and Stefani both went through high - profile divorces. The couple opened up to Gayle King about how their relationship started.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were both very vulnerable in your lives at that particular time. What happened between the two of you that this has worked so beautifully?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Strangely enough, I didn`t feel vulnerable with Gwen. I felt like I had met somebody that had my back.\par \par \uc2 If you would have told me the first season that we met what was going to happen, I would have been like no, we actually don`t even have anything to even talk about, you know, what are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 It`s crazy how things work out.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Right? Did you ever imagine a love like this?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: No. Oh my God. But I don`t know how you can. I don`t know how you can imagine when you find that life partner, that missing piece.\par \par \uc2 It is like, golly, you can go ahead and kill me if this ever, ever goes away or end.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Jimmy Kimmel Live"/ABC)\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Shelton left "The Voice" last year to focus on new projects, not just pairing up with his wife with her new song "Purple Irises" but also on the newest location of his honky-tonk empire, Old Red Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 How the heck is this tractor staying up there?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: You know it`s a very like a thin cotton rope.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Oh, great.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: It really does feel like you`ve stepped in through a portal and you`re in Nashville when you come through the doors.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Who would you want to see grace the stage?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Dolly would be great. I was hoping to get Beyonce to do a little something.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Texas Hold `Em"/Beyonce/Columbia Records)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: She wants to make it in the country music industry, and I think she must. It ain`t happen until she plays Old Red.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: Shelton did land a major booking for Old Red, his wife. In the middle of headlining Coachella with her band, No Doubt.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Coachella)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: The greatest concert I`ve ever seen like it -- it was just - - I`ve never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: You told me a couple of years ago that everything takes a backseat to Gwen and the boys.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: What do you hope the next 10 years looks like for you two?\par \par \uc2 BLAKE SHELTON: If I could have a repeat of these past 10 years would be perfect.\par \par \uc2 CASSIE DILAURA: For CBS MORNINGS. I`m "Entertainment Tonight`s" Cassie DiLaura in Las Vegas. (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He seems like a very happy man with some great music.\par \par \uc2 All right, clear your schedule, you`re not making plans, you`re sticking around because our friends Drew Barrymore and Rossi Ross Mathews from "The Drew Barrymore Show" will join us for a very special "Talk of the Table" -- it doesn`t get better than them.\par \par \uc2 Local news, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are leveling up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I love that song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s time to bring you a very special extra-large edition --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Level Up" -- level up, Drew and Ross. Level up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- of "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: We are here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Featuring our lovely friends, Drew Barrymore Rossi Ross Mathews.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Hi. Rossi Ross.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hosts of "The Drew Barrymore Show." You will always be Rossi Ross.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: You better believe it.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes. Good morning, everybody.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: He has been Rossi Ross since he came and rescued me at that news desk on our show. And now we`re families with your show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. We love it when you guys come.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Had been since day one, so thrilled to be here, and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, you`re up first, Drew.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I am.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: You are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: All right my "Talk of the Table" -- I`m sharing an early look at my eye-opening interview with happiness expert and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " best-selling author, Arthur Brooks.\par \par \uc2 Also, I want to give credit to your bestie, Oprah Winfrey, for really helping bring him to the forefront with the extraordinary book that they wrote. My copy is annotated to the nines. He is sharing tips for worrying less, including keeping a worry journal, which was something I had never heard of or thought of, but take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS (Author): Write down your worries, write them down. You will make them specific. And then when you come back to them, you`re going to see progress and say, yes, I used to be worried about that, but that resolved itself. And that means that the thing I was really worried about, wasn`t as big a worry as I thought. And maybe the thing I`m worried about now isn`t as big as I thought.\par \par \uc2 Write it down, own it. You know, I promise you that as your kids grow up, you`re going to treasure that. You`re going to go back to that. You`re going to look at it when they`re 20 and say, I`m so worried about college. You go back, it`s so funny, because 15 years ago, I was worried about them, you know, falling down on the playground on the first day of kindergarten, or whatever it happened to be.\par \par \uc2 And worries are natural. But the more they`re specific, the better off you`re going to be.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh my god, I keep a gratitude journal. I need to keep a worry journal.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS: Totally. Totally.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I`ve never heard that suggestion in my life, Arthur.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: He also just wrote an article in "The Atlantic," which actually suggest that you write down the things that you have to do, say it`s about 20, knock off the bottom 15, because you`re more likely to meet the top five and then feel more successful.\par \par \uc2 He`s just so brilliant, and what he and Oprah did with their book, "Build The Life You Want," which I love so much, because it is so full of true, honest takeaways that you can really apply to your life. He`s a genius, and I think your bestie for really pushing him out to the forefront.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We can`t wait to see this interview.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes. It is interesting, I got to be in the audience for that with people asking questions and just like you were all during that clip, and maybe people at home --\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: All understand that. We all worry. There`s something that affects each and every one of us. It`s a really fabulous conversation.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I`m a big worrier, too.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, who is next, guys?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s wonderful. What`s up?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Oh, it is me. Okay, I am ready, so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You, Ross.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: My "Talk of the Table" is a new study that found the older people are, the later they think old age begins.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Interesting, right? Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know this is true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is confirmed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who needs a study for that?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I now think 85 is old.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Well, that`s what happened to the study published this week in "Psychology and Aging" surveyed nearly 15,000 adults aged 40 to 85 and the researchers found that 64-year-olds believe old age starts now at about 75.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: And at 75, participants said old age starts at 76.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: So, on average, every four to five years of actual aging, the participants` perception of old age increased by about one year. Remember when you were like 20 and you thought 40 was old.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Well, they all --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: George Clooney came on the show, and it goes, you know, when I was young, I used to think like 50, if I lived till 50 or 60, I`d have a great life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Now I`m 50 or 60 and I`m thinking wait, what`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: You know, it`s so true. It is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kick it up to 90.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I`m going to be 50, I think 50 is the new 30.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Absolutely. Well, that`s what we tell ourselves.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: But it is the new 30.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: There you go.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Even the way we dress and act and behave just doesn`t feel like we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They did back -- because Drew, I remember when my mom turned 50 and she seemed so old and I said, God, I hope I make it to 50. And now for many people, 50, you are just getting started.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that`s right.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes, that`s facts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But I will sometimes -- in my mind, I`m young, I`m walking around, I`m feeling young, I`m feeling good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But then I`ll catch a look at myself in the mirror and I`ll be like who`s that old guy?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Oh, Tony, how --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who is that father figure?\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Come on, Tony, you`re the young one --\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: He looks -- if you look at yourself in the mirror, you don`t like it, there is no hope. There is no hope.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you. Thank you. That was the intended effect of my comment. I appreciate the compliments.\par \par \uc2 Arthur Brooks, you recommend that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Needy much?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: No mass --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony`s turn.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- is a very good thing going on at the Barclays Center. If you`re not doing this in stadiums where you are, they should already be doing this.\par \par \uc2 They take recipes from local businesses and scale them up so that people inside the stadium can have fantastic food.\par \par \uc2 So I am going to spotlight three places that I put on social media here.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Likkle More Jerk. It`s a chicken place. Jamaican patties, beef patties, and also marinated hickory smoked chicken. It is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yum.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Gosh. That looks amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Next up is Nay Nay`s Taqueria, and again, these are local businesses in the New York City area that the Barclays Center, a big stadium, has decided to put in the arena for tens of thousands of fans.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Paizano`s. By the way, that green sauce, I drink it like it`s a green juice. It`s so, so tasty.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: This is a brilliant business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Brilliant idea.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes, can I volunteer to go to the restaurants and pick which ones --\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: -- to highlight, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can apply to Chris Giacalone, who is the head of the taste program.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Okay, good that you said that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Always looking for scouts, I`m told.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Chris, hi, call me.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: How often do they switch them out then?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a good question. I don`t know the answer. But there`s about two dozen of them right now. And so like they sign a little nondisclosure. They give the secret recipe over and then they take it from a local thing to a big national thing.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It`s brilliant. More of this, please.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My "Talk of the Table" -- I just adore this guy. I`m very smitten -- country superstar, Jelly Roll. He is quitting social media. Why? According to his wife, Bunnie Xo, Jelly Roll, has faced relentless online bullying about his weight.\par \par \uc2 On her recent podcast, she spoke about how the comments have affected him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Geez.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BUNNIE XO (Jelly Roll`s wife): My husband doesn`t show it to you, guys. But I`m going to have a very vulnerable moment here. It hurts him.\par \par \uc2 The internet can say whatever the (bleep) they want about you and they say, well, you`re a celebrity, you`re supposed to be able to handle it. No, the (bleep) we`re not.\par \par \uc2 Don`t bully people because you never know where they are mentally.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. Thank you, Bunnie for saying this. Jelly Roll is 39, recently lost weight. He is training for a 5K race in May. And you know, when you meet him, you just fall in love with him and you fall in love with his story. You fall in love with the person that he is.\par \par \uc2 And people always think that celebrities don`t have feelings, that you all can take whatever it is that is thrown at you, but it does.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m glad that she`s speaking up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And saying that he is very vulnerable and that it hurts his feelings and that everybody, we live in a society where people are so mean and they are encouraged and rewarded to be their most mean selves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whether it`s a celebrity or a kid you don`t talk to in your math class.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Or an -- your right, your right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you wouldn`t say to their face--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: -- or at a table where they`re sitting, then don`t say it in public on social media.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Drawing the line saying I`m not going to subject myself to this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You`re so right.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: I love your friendship with him. I saw you on at the awards show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I do. I really do.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: You`re so cute together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is called groveling. Please, come on our show.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he knows I really, really, really care about him.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Well, his story is incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was great.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: All right, my "Talk of the Table" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Natalie.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I am going to give you all a good laugh, okay. It`s an interesting contest in Belgium. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Niel Van Herck/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Seagulls. Yes, seagulls.\par \par \uc2 Drew was like, is that a lobster? No. It is a gull screeching championship. It happened last Sunday. Competitors did their very best impressions of a screeching seagull and listen to this year`s winner.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Niel Van Herck/Storyful)\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: That is nine-year-old Cooper Wallace from Britain, taking home the junior title.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seagull boy.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: He says he picked up the talent after being pecked by a seagull at the beach. He says he actually has a lot of respect for seagulls. He`s not afraid of them. But apparently you have to get very much in character.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to be the gull to win the prize.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I see.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: And he did.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Also have to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that falls into the --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have so many questions.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: How did everyone --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: They really want to be a seagull, you`ve got to stare at everyone`s food, like you want it really part badly.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Oh, that`s part of the competition. He pecked his sister`s French fries.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: So, that put him over the top.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am sure, you don`t know the answer to this, but there was a dangling banana for some reason?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I saw that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is happening with that?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Don`t ask questions, honey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That falls into the different strokes for different folks.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Too much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we should all take a trip to Belgium.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Drew Barrymore and Rossi Ross Matthews, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lovely to see you, guys.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: We love you.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Thank you, guys, so much.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tune in to "The Drew Barrymore Show" later this morning for a very special segment featuring Nate, Vlad, and me.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yay.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Yay.\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How about that?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Look at us together.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: It was so good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I need more compliments. Yes, I did great.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: The boys are here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Drew, how did Tony do?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Always the best.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Check your local listings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Oh, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Drew. Thank you, Ross.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MATHEWS: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad said, he did great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a tie, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is someone who had one of the most sought-after fun jobs in Washington. Her name is Deesha Dyer. She became the White House social secretary for President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Deesha will tell us about her unexpected journey. There she is. Hello, Deesha Dyer, looking pretty in blue.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:39. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our next guest is sharing her inspiring and unexpected path to the White House, and unexpected is the word here.\par \par \uc2 Deesha Dyer was 31 years old, a hip-hop journalist and part time community college student from Philadelphia when she landed an internship at the White House in 2009.\par \par \uc2 So listen to this. Over six years, she rose from intern to White House Social Secretary for President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 --\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- and First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  -- drop the microphone, Miss Dyer. You`re right now, wow.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER ("Undiplomatic" author): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was in charge of every White House event from state dinners to concerts and visit from world leaders and the Pope. She shares her journey in her new book. It`s called "Undiplomatic: How My Attitude Created the Best Kind of Trouble." We`re very happy to say that Deesha Dyer is with us first on CBS MORNINGS and welcome to you.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes, thank you so much. It`s so great to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved reading this book, Deesha because I would see you at the White House and you were always such a badass, so poised so in control, so on top of everything.\par \par \uc2 So to read that you in that position felt like an impostor even when you reach that level, I didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Help us -- explain that.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: The thing about impostor syndrome really, is that you have it and it comes and it goes at different times. And I think that I was in his new environment at the White House, not even with a college degree.\par \par \uc2 So I walked in and I was like, everyone else is more qualified than I am.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But let`s back up, June 2009, you get a call to interview for an intern. You`re 31 years old.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re in community college. You end up getting that job and then after you finish that job, they call you for another job, which says to me, they saw something in you that you didn`t see in yourself.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes, they did and when they called me, I was -- it was late one night and I was so shocked and I said are you sure?\par \par \uc2 Because for me when you`re an intern, you can -- you know, you`re in college or you just graduated. So that`s acceptable not to have a college degree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: But a full-time job?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: And when I said to her over the phone to Alyssa (sp?), I said, "Are you sure?" And she said, Yes, we are very sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: And so, you know, I was very shocked and I couldn`t even believe that they offered me the job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But they did.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I know people really connect with you over the impostor syndrome. It`s something you talk about a lot, you write about a lot in the book. In fact, when you were applying for the internship, when you got the job, you show up first day wearing fake glasses.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: I did.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: To make yourself look smarter.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: I did.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: How did you get over that? You know, what did it take?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. I think I looked around and I said to myself, no one here knows what qualifications I have or what I don`t have. And so why am I making this the burden of this great internship at the White House for Barack Obama? Why is this leading my life?\par \par \uc2 And so I really got over it by realizing that I am able, I am capable. I was getting promoted. And I was like, well, they`re not going to promote me, if I`m an impostor. They see what I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: They see how great I am, and I decided to also dig up the root of why do I even feel this way? Like where does this even start? You know, so that`s really where it came from\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One of the other complexities for people coming up in their career is you get feedback.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`re not sure what is valid feedback that you should take on and what you should reject, and so some of the things you write about hearing are that when you give your opinion, your voice is too loud or too harsh and then, that brushes up uncomfortably against not being yourself, right?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So how do you do -- how do you handle that kind of feedback?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: I think how I handled it is I just said, you know what, this is who I am, I can`t change that. And there`s a good way to use my voice.\par \par \uc2 And so I think I had to figure out how to use my voice to be loud about things that weren`t right about injustice and about things I saw that I didn`t agree with. And I said, I have to deal with the consequences of people just saying, you`re just a little too loud.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but then --\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Or you`re just a little too bold, I had to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But then, you also get very poignant and very personal in this book. I mean, here you are, the Pope is coming to visit.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The first Papal visit.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You also have the first State Dinner with the Chinese Prime Minister --\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- President. And you were in the bathroom throwing up because you just discovered that you`re six weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so you have to put on the face that despite all of the morning sickness that you`re having, you still have to do your job.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take us how you did that, and then at the end of the day, president -- end of the night, President Obama says you did a really good job.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not knowing all that was going on.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Oh, they had no -- they had no idea. And I think for me, the impostor syndrome made me feel like I have to push through this, like no matter how sick I am, no matter how I feel, because I was like, if I just mess up one small thing, I`m going to go back to thinking I`m a fraud, and so will everybody else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said, there`s no room for error or for mistakes in the White House.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Oh, there is no room for error. I mean, the Pope does not visit weekly, you know? I mean, like, you know, China State Dinner does not happen, you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: -- every month and so there was not room for error. And I think I also put myself on a very high bar. You know, there was a newspaper article that said, you know, is she up for this? Is she up for this week? Can she do it? We`re not sure. And so I said I`ll show you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You almost didn`t apply for the internship in the first place. We`re so glad you did because it led to everything else.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Including this book, which is perfect.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, she ends with this: "You`ll never be able to see your light standing in your own shadow, so move out of the way."\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo to you, Deesha.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: I love this.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Thank you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now, you have your own company.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes. Hook & Fasten, my social impact agency.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you feel --\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you still feel like an impostor? I`ll go first. No.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: No, I don`t know. No, I feel great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, and your company?\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Hook & Fasten is my company and we work with corporations and communities on authentic partnerships.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, Bravo, Deesha Dyer, Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: Deesha, woo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are cheering you on always.\par \par \uc2 DEESHA DYER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Undiplomatic" is the name of the book. It`s a great read. It`s on sale now wherever you`d like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In case you missed it yesterday, I didn`t, but if you did on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we showed you some wireless earbuds.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS (Lifestyle expert): These are Groovz. This is all of that sound quality and beautiful connectivity of the big guys but at a fraction of the price, and I think you might notice as we`re panning over this gorgeous floral print.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that floral one. That looks nice.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS: Yes, we`re having fun with colors. Okay, I love that for Mother`s Day, by the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS: Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANE KISS: So they`re fun with colors. You can stock up on these, given that price. You get the same technology, crystal clear sound is the big guys, but like I said they are a \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deal today.\par \par \uc2 I would definitely run not walk towards these because they sell out every time they`ve been presented on air.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can get the Groovz wireless earbuds for $39.99, that is 50 percent off the retail price. Nate`s probably sleeping on a plane somewhere with them in right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To the shop for this and other items, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com and be sure to follow \uc2 CBS\uc2  Deals on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and text \uc2 CBS\uc2  to 65000 to get early access to some of our most popular deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And while he is enjoying himself, we`re having a great time, but tomorrow Jerry Seinfeld and Jim Gaffigan will be here in studio to talk about their new movie, "Unfrosted" about the race to create the Pop Tart.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See you tomorrow on your favorite show. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE MORALES: See you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240424ek4o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for April 3, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Omar Villafranca, Scott MacFarlane, Alex Wilson,  Dave Malkoff, Imtiaz Tyab, Nancy Cordes, Holly Williams, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 3,716 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the new allegations surrounding the Israeli strike that killed aid workers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOSE ANDRES (Founder, \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 ): We were targeted deliberately, nonstop, until everybody was dead in this convoy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Strong words from philanthropist and chef Jose Andres accusing Israel of purposely killing seven \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  team members.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOSE ANDRES: This looks like it`s not a war against terrorism anymore. Seems this is a war against humanity itself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The growing outrage, as we learn about the victims, including an American citizen.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the mounting questions and worldwide outrage after that Israeli airstrike in Gaza that killed seven humanitarian workers from the global aid charity \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 New tonight, the group`s founder, chef Jose Andres, is speaking out with a scathing rebuke of Israel`s -- quote -- "indiscriminate killing" of civilians, urging officials to begin the long journey to peace. Andres is also accusing the Israeli military of knowing of the aid group`s movements in the region and then targeting the three-car convoy of armored vehicles, in his words, systematically, car by car.\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning more about the victims, including 33-year-old Jacob Flickinger, a dual U.S.-Canadian citizen. The American father was reportedly planning to leave Gaza at the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 An Israeli military spokesperson called the killings a grave mistake and said the convoy of three vehicles was wrongly identified as hostile targets.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOSE ANDRES (Founder, \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 ): So this was not just a bad luck situation where, "Oops, we dropped the bomb in the wrong place."\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voiceover): The seven \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  aid workers killed by Israel`s military in Gaza were targeted systematically, car by car, according to the group`s founder the award winning Spanish American chef Jose Andres.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on Israeli television, he broke down.\par \par \uc2 JOSE ANDRES: I have been in Gaza myself. I met -- and some of the people that died were -- were my friends, and I served with them.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The bodies of the six foreign team members were transported out of Gaza today. \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  has identified one of them as Jacob Flickinger, a 33-year-old dual U.S.-Canadian citizen.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s military has vowed to investigate what it called a -- quote -- "grave mistake."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. HERZI HALEVI (Chief of Staff, \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 ): It was a mistake that followed a misidentification at night during a war in a very complex conditions.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: That suggests the aid workers were mistaken for militants.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  says it was coordinating its movements with the Israeli military. Andres says his team tried to call the military even as they came under fire, with team members moving from the first car after it was hit to the second, which was also hit, and then the third, leaving all seven dead.\par \par \uc2 All three cars appear to have been targeted with precision strikes. CBS News has geolocated the wreckage to positions several hundred yards apart.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said yesterday he`s outraged by the deaths of the aid workers, and U.S. relations with Israel appear increasingly strained, but he didn`t answer this question today from CBS News` Weijia Jiang.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: How will you hold Israel accountable for the \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  strike, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: It`s not just \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  that suspended its operations in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Another American nonprofit, Anera, says it`s also pausing its work there. And the United Nations says it`s temporarily suspended its movements at night -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So much aid not getting to the people there.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, we learned today that President Biden will speak to Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  tomorrow. It is their first phone call since the horrific \uc2 IDF\uc2  airstrike on those aid workers.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 And, Nancy, what can you tell us about what`s going to happen in this phone call tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Well, Norah, President Biden just said last night that Israel has not done enough to protect aid workers like the ones from \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is deep frustration here about what the White House sees as a lack of coordination by the \uc2 IDF\uc2 . And the president is expected to convey that frustration and anger tomorrow. Beyond that, the U.S. has been very publicly, Norah, trying to talk Netanyahu out of a full-scale invasion of the city of Rafah.\par \par \uc2 Officials here have been proposing alternatives. So that is sure to come up as well.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And, Nancy, the administration has used the word unacceptable, outraged to describe these airstrikes against those aid workers, but there haven`t been any consequences. Why not?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Not only have there been no consequences; the U.S. is actually preparing to send more weaponry to Israel, including 2,000-pound bombs, possibly fighter jets as well.\par \par \uc2 White House officials tell us there are no plans to cut off aid because Israel is still the closest U.S. ally in the region and because the security of Israel has been a top U.S. priority for decades.\par \par \uc2 Now, we are seeing rising anger in Congress, particularly from the left, after this latest tragedy, Norah. So, could that make it harder to get Congress to approve aid to Israel in the future? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A massive search-and-rescue effort is under way in Taiwan following its most powerful earthquake in 25 years. The magnitude-7.4 quake hit during the morning rush hour. At least nine people are dead, with about 1,000 people injured. Dozens more are trapped by debris.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab has the late breaking developments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN #1 speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voiceover): The moment the earthquake struck was captured as a morning show was live on air --\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN #1 speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: -- shaking heavy studio lights, as panic jolted through the newsroom.\par \par \uc2 Rush hour commuters were also caught off guard by the 7.4-magnitude quake. So too were passengers in this packed subway car.\par \par \uc2 YOLIE SAEBUNG (Felt Earthquake): It was definitely a shock when it happened.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Yolie Saebung (sp?) is originally from California.\par \par \uc2 YOLIE SAEBUNG: We were actually sleeping, and we were definitely woken up by it, because the whole room was shaking. We tried to find, like, somewhere to hide, like, under a table.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The epicenter of the quake was near the eastern city of Hualien, where the shaking was so intense, some buildings crashed to the ground.\par \par \uc2 Across Taiwan, dozens of other buildings have also been badly damaged, as some of the smallest survivors had to be carefully rescued.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN #2 speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Elsewhere, hundreds remain trapped in badly damaged mines and on roads that have been cut off, thanks largely to landslides, which have also blocked a number of tunnels.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan is no stranger to powerful earthquakes, but the public is kept informed by TV and cell phone messages, and school drills saw some children put on protective head coverings as they waited for the all-clear.\par \par \uc2 And while this quake isn`t as devastating as the last major one before it, the destruction it`s caused is clear.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab, CBS News, London.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, more than 40 million Americans are still in the path of dangerous weather from tornadoes to flooding. This storm system that started in California on Saturday is now in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  Dave Malkoff reports from the heartland, where residents are still picking up the pieces.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF (voiceover): This is what the inside of an EF-1 tornado looks like. Ethan Williams` dashcam captured the moment when high winds spun his car around and flipped over another vehicle. His two children were in the car.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN WILLIAMS (Filmed Tornado): My kids, they had their heads tucked on my center console, and I was leaned over on top of them, just covering them, and had my foot on the brake.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: From Indiana to Kentucky --\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have cars flipped over. What a mess.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: -- where five tornadoes touched down in the state, killing one person.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ANDY BESHEAR (D-Kentucky): But what I know and what I have seen is that, every time we`re hit, we will get through it, and we will get through it together.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: Further south, in Georgia, a tornado just missed this church in Conyers.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR ARCHIE SKIPPER (Church of God Christian Fellowship): Even though it laid the tree down, but God still was blessing us, because he didn`t let it fall on the church.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: In Columbus, Ohio, the city is on alert after heavy rain inundated the Scioto River. The river has risen 14 feet from last week.\par \par \uc2 The storm created turbulence for travelers. At its peak, thousands of flights were delayed and hundreds canceled along the East Coast, Atlanta, Orlando, and Chicago airports with the most delays, all part of a coast-to- coast storm system that spawned at least 14 reported tornadoes in six states yesterday, including Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS HAMBY (Tornado Survivor): We really about lost our life.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: In the town of Sunbright, Alexis Hamby feels lucky her family is alive after a possible tornado hit their home.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS HAMBY: Our neighbor grabbed all four of us and pulled us in his basement door, I mean, like a real-life superhero.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVE MALKOFF: The first time these next-door neighbor families met each other was during that mid-tornado rescue.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, it is raining on and off here. That`s not so great when some homes don`t have a roof.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 Dave Malkoff, thank you so much for being there.\par \par \uc2 For where the storms are headed next, let`s bring in meteorologist Alex Wilson from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WILSON (Meteorologist, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Well, Norah, a very soggy and snowy end to the week for the Northeast and New England.\par \par \uc2 We start with the rainy side, flood watches posted from the mid-Atlantic into the state of New York. But look at the snow, amounts as high as two, even three feet for parts of New England, including New Hampshire and Maine. It`s going to be a heavy, wet snow, that heart attack snow, so very difficult to clear.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, the snow really begins to fall in earnest, continues during the day on Thursday. As that low strengthens, winds will be gusty. That could cause travel problems even in areas that don`t see the snow. Winds along the coast could be gusting as high as 50, even 60 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 We look towards next week`s total solar eclipse, cloud cover looking likely across the South Central, with more clearing likely in the wake of the storm, Norah, in New England.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here in Washington, another convicted January 6 rioter was sentenced to more than seven years in prison today for his role in the attack. This comes amid growing concern over former President Donald Trump`s use of words like hostage and patriot in defense of those accused of taking part in the insurrection, including those who attacked law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane has more from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): As \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  increasingly champions January 6 defendants --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from March 2)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): They`re the J6 hostages, I call them.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- and talks of issuing pardons for the attackers of the Capitol, a CBS News review of three years of January 6 criminal cases shows nearly 500 of the defendants are now accused of assaulting police, more than 120 of doing so with dangerous or deadly weapons.\par \par \uc2 And we found those held in the January 6 wing of the Washington, D.C., jail, which has also been mentioned by Trump, include Jonathan Pollock, a Florida man who`s pleaded not guilty, accused of punching and dragging an officer down the steps.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Government Exhibit)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Get out!\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And it recently housed Brian Mock of Minnesota, convicted of four assaults against police, including this one.\par \par \uc2 Trump argues the defendants are persecuted and unfairly treated hostages.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from March 2)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: They`re put in jail for extended periods of time.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You told the judge the other day it`s like a dagger when you hear the term hostages for Jan. 6 defendants.\par \par \uc2 SGT. AQUILINO GONELL (Former \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  Officer): And the reason being is because it`s triggering.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Former Capitol Police Sergeant Aquilino Gonell says the hostage rhetoric adopted by some Trump allies disrespects police and could incite more violence.\par \par \uc2 Homeland security analysts are concerned, too.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA VINOGRAD: Rhetoric that encourages a misperception of what violent extremism is, is only going to encourage more violence and ultimately put the American people at greater risk.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: There are now more than 1,300 total January 6 defendants, including a Texas man allegedly aligned with the Proud Boys arrested today.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, there are more trials to come, including one the week of the Republican National Convention and more straight through Election Day.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the largest egg producer in the U.S. says some of its chickens have tested positive for bird flu at a Texas plant. The highly contagious virus has also been detected at the biggest poultry farm in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca reports from Plano, Texas, on what this could mean for food prices at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): The bird flu outbreak forced egg producer Cal-Maine to stop production and cull nearly two million chickens at its plant in Parmer County, Texas.\par \par \uc2 The number represents less than 4 percent of the company`s flock and less than 1 percent of chickens in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 SID MILLER (Texas Agriculture Commissioner): All the eggs were destroyed. The chickens are going to be destroyed, so it is completely safe.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: This comes as six states are reporting outbreaks of avian flu among dairy cows. This week, a Texas dairy worker had pink eye after testing positive for the virus, becoming only the second known human case in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Texas Agriculture Commissioner Sid Miller says there`s no reason for people to be alarmed.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER SID MILLER: The good news is, this is carried by migratory waterfowl. It`s -- it`s migratory season, but we`re on the tail end of it.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Despite the outbreaks, Texas A&M agricultural economist David Anderson says prices should remain the same.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ANDERSON (Agricultural Economics Professor, \uc2 Texas A&M University\uc2 ): Less than 1 percent of the egg-laying chickens, that`s not a lot of effect on kind of the broad market that folks are going to notice at the store. We have plenty of milk production. I don`t expect to see much effect of this for -- for us consumers either.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: You can`t get bird flu from eating thoroughly cooked poultry or eggs, according to the \uc2 USDA\uc2 , who also advises people wash their hands after handling raw meat and eggs.\par \par \uc2 COMMISSIONER SID MILLER: The message to the consumer is, you know, just go -- go about business as usual, no spike in prices in either egg prices or dairy products. It`s completely safe.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Anderson says the price of a dozen eggs is actually down about a dollar compared to last year and that egg production is up, so consumers shouldn`t have to worry about their morning omelet -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Got it. You heard the rules. Wash your hands.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating a close call between a Southwest plane and a control tower at a major airport.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating a terrifying incident at a -- New York`s La Guardia Airport where a \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  jet with more than 150 people on board may have flown dangerously close to a control tower.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL: Go around. Go around. Fly runway heading, climb and maintain 2,000. Climb and maintain 2,000, 2,000.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The flight was trying to land in bad weather last month and came possibly as close as 67 feet from hitting the top of the control tower.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to CBS News, Southwest says the crew was dealing with turbulence and low visibility on approach to the runway.\par \par \uc2 LSU\uc2  basketball star Angel Reese said today that she`s entering the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  draft, and she did it in style through a photoshoot with "Vogue." Reese says she was inspired by Serena Williams, who announced her retirement in "Vogue" in September of 2022. The 21-year-old is expected to be one of the top players in the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  draft later this month, along with Caitlin Clark of Iowa, who`s moving on to the Final Four of the NCAA Tournament this Friday.\par \par \uc2 There were big ratings for that game.\par \par \uc2 All right, Monday`s total eclipse is expected to rake in the big business for cities and towns along its path. Our report on the eclipse economy, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The countdown is on for Monday`s total eclipse of the sun.\par \par \uc2 The most dramatic show is expected to be in cities and towns along the path of totality through more than a dozen states, where many business owners are looking to cash in on the celestial event.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): In the scenic Texas Hill Country, Kerrville is preparing for its moment in the sun, the blocked sun.\par \par \uc2 How ready is Kerrville?\par \par \uc2 JUDY EYCHNER (Mayor of Kerrville, Texas): Absolutely ready. We have been getting ready for two years.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Mayor Judy Eychner says the city of about 25,000 could swell to as many as 100,000, prompting a change usually reserved for hurricane evacuations.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JUDY EYCHNER: We will have one-way traffic right up here along this bridge.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: So you`re changing the traffic flow of the city?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JUDY EYCHNER: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Kerrville will have one of the nation`s longest views of the moon obscuring the sun at four minutes, 25 seconds.\par \par \uc2 And the clock is ticking for businesses.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I got a chicken, fried chicken, gravy all over.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: At Billy Gene`s Restaurant, owner Crystal Smith doubled many of her food orders.\par \par \uc2 And you will go through most of this?\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL SMITH (Owner, Billy Gene`s Restaurant): Yes, we will definitely go through most of this, yes.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Calls for reservations coming in as far away as Germany.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, spending on the eclipse, like dining and travel, is expected to top $1.6 billion. This coveted track of land has been turned into a campsite and long been sold out, some traveling extreme distances.\par \par \uc2 JENNYTH PETERSON (Hill Country Arts Foundation): We have got visitors from New Zealand, Israel, Finland, Germany, Great Britain.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: That travel expense a high cost for a priceless experience.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And the question now, will Mother Nature cooperate with the solar system?\par \par \uc2 There is cloud cover in the forecast for a good portion of the eclipse path. But, Norah, we can remain hopeful and the weather can change quickly.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a moment.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And we will have live coverage of Monday`s total eclipse beginning at 2:00 p.m. Eastern, 11:00 a.m. Pacific right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and streaming on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Heart of America is next with the national teacher of the year, who gets extra credit in kindness.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 Meet the 2024 national teacher of the year, Missy Testerman. Testerman has been molding young minds for more than 30 years as an elementary teacher at Rogersville City Schools in East Tennessee. She currently teaches English as a second language for kindergarten through eighth grade.\par \par \uc2 The news was announced on "CBS Mornings," where Testerman spoke about what this prestigious award means to her.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN (2024 National Teacher of the Year): There are 3.5 million teachers in this country.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MISSY TESTERMAN: So to be named as the teacher who gets to advocate for our teachers and our students is just an incredible honor.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: First Lady Dr. Jill Biden, who has spent decades as an educator, surprised Testerman in person, and then she announced the first ever Teachers of the Year State Dinner at the White House. That`s on May 1st.\par \par \uc2 Missy Testerman and all of our teachers, tonight`s Heart of America.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240404ek4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Delays Some Tariffs on Canada and Mexico; China Responds to Tariffs; SpaceX Rocket Explodes During Test Flight; Flooding Prompts Rescues in San Diego; Second U.S. Death Thought to be Linked to Measles; President Trump`s Ultimatum; Reining in Musk; Former CFO of FEMA Speaks About Firing; Former Olympian on \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s Most Wanted List; Teen Arrested for Boarding Plane with Gun; Key Evidence Revealed in Kohberger Murder Case; Remembering Bloody Sunday in Selma; Inside the Opioid Crisis; Gene Hackman Death Investigation; Surging Layoffs in America; Ringo and Friends at the Ryman; Measles Immune Amnesia; Wickedly Good Fashion; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Adriana Diaz, Ed O`Keefe, Jessica  Burch, Debora Patta, Dana Jacobson, Anna Coren, Mark Strassmann, Nancy  Cordes, Andres Gutierrez, Jonathan Vigliotti, Cecilia Vega, Carter Evans,  Kelly O`Grady, Anthony Mason, Celine Gounder \par \uc2 13,677 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The sound of a trumpet as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And I`m Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is off today, but we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Trade war whiplash. President Trump now postpones tariffs on a growing list of products from Mexico and from Canada.\par \par \uc2 DOUG FORD\uc2  (Ontario, Canada Premier): He has to understand that he can`t attack our country economically and expect us to roll over.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The White House reins in \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  while fired workers speak out in protest.\par \par \uc2 MARY COMANS (Former FEMA Chief Financial Officer): I was fired illegally by the Trump administration for doing my job.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: A \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  starship rocket breaks apart on a test flight less than two months after a similar failure.\par \par \uc2 BILL HARWOOD: Two failures in a row for \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , obviously a significant setback.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: A former Olympian added to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s 10 Most Wanted List.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: Ryan Wedding accused of running a billion dollar cocaine trafficking operation.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #3: The Flamin` Hot Cheeto that`s shaped like the Pokemon Charizard sold at auction for almost $88,000.00.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): People will spend money on the dumbest things. I mean, isn`t that right, Picklechu?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #1: Halftime at the Lakers game. This fan has a chance to win $100,000.00 if he can make it from beyond the half, and he rattles it in. Cash that check.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER #2: A hundred thousand reasons to flex.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You are a hundred thousand dollars richer, how does it feel right now, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE (\uc2 LA Lakers\uc2  fan): Oh my God. Go Lakers. Come on. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This morning`s "Eye Opener" is presented by Progressive, making it easy to bundle insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you go to see a basketball game and you come out with a hundred thousand dollars.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is not a bad night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not a bad night at all.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin though with President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is hitting the brakes on a number of big agenda issues. He announced just yesterday there will be a temporary pause in hefty tariffs on many imports from Canada and Mexico. That comes just days after imposing the border taxes.\par \par \uc2 Now he also took action to limit \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  ability to fire federal workers after tens of thousands were let go.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is in Toronto, where tariffs are the main issue.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning to you. A lot of changes coming from the White House these days.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They sure are, Gayle. And just after pulling back 25 percent tariffs on the U.S.` closest trading partners. Canadians we`ve spoken to here feel they`re being unfairly punished.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) (President of the United States): One for Canada, one for Mexico.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: A partial reprieve on some tariffs, but the threat of them has already rattled Mexican 