: Every day, Canadians are also taking action.\par \par \uc2 At the Royal Canadian Curling Club, members voted to stop serving American liquor like bourbon and California wine to protest Trump`s tariffs.\par \par \uc2 MAN: As soon as the tariffs became serious, everyone on the board had members calling, saying, we need to take them off of our shelves.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m a new grandmother, and I don`t know what I`m going to tell my grandson about this time in our history.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And you know, President Trump has disrupted American politics, but all of this is a reminder that he is disrupting politics all over the world. This weekend, for example, the ruling Liberal Party of Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  is picking his successor ahead of a general election later this year.\par \par \uc2 Now, polling had shown that that Liberal Party was likely to lose that election, but the race is now tightening because of this tariff war and the growing pro-Canada movement -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ed, thank you so much. The President did not say anything about rolling back the increased tariffs on imports from China, and this morning, that country`s chief diplomat accused the US of, "two-faced" acts.\par \par \uc2 Anna Coren has more from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: With an escalation in the U.S.-China trade war earlier this week, China`s Foreign Minister Wang Yi addressed the media today in his annual press conference, vowing that Beijing will "resolutely counter" US tariffs.\par \par \uc2 In a highly choreographed session with local and foreign media, the Foreign Minister without mentioning the U.S. President by name, made a point of America`s lack of sincerity in its behavior towards China, and Trump`s public remarks of wanting a good relationship with President Xi.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WANG YI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 TRANSLATION: No country can fantasize that it can suppress and contain China on one hand while maintaining relations with China on the other.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA COREN: Well days ago, China announced ambitious plans for growth during its National People`s Congress meeting, not only in response to Trump`s tariffs, but to strengthen its economy, where youth unemployment, deflation and the property crisis remain central issues.\par \par \uc2 But Beijing has been preparing for this moment since the first trade war began back in 2018; now, the global leader in key industries and reducing its reliance on the United States.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Trump is now dealing with a very different and defiant China.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Anna, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We have stunning video of space junk in the sky from a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  rocket that broke apart during a test flight from South Texas yesterday. Ground controllers lost contact with the starship rocket after liftoff, then went into an out of control spin, and the debris forced a temporary halt to flights at two South Florida airports.\par \par \uc2 Now, this was the second time in two months that something like this has happened to a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  rocket.\par \par \uc2 As Mark Strassmann shows us, it`s a bit of a setback for efforts to put humans back on the moon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: What is going on right now?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Those sparks over the sky Thursday night sparked many questions.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my gosh. It`s like lighting up the water.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The bright lights seen in the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, it`s going down.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Over the Turks and Caicos Islands, and in Florida, where one spectator had the right answer for clusters of fire flying.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Hey, guys, Starship.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: That would be \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 `s uncrewed Starship rocket.\par \par \uc2 It was the eighth test flight of the world`s largest and most powerful rocket, but it didn`t fly far after lifting off from Boca Chica, Texas.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And separation.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It did fly its first stage booster back into the chopstick like arms of its launch tower, as planned and seemingly perfect, but nearly two minutes later, a malfunction. The rocket started to spin out of control while in space.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You can see we`ve lost several engines and we`ve lost attitude control of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  said the vehicle experienced a rapid unscheduled disassembly and then lost contact with Starship. That`s when people saw the spectacular show of debris in the sky, a very similar sight --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is crazy.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: To what happened in January when another starship also broke up over Turks and Caicos.\par \par \uc2 BILL HARWOOD: Two failures in a row for \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , obviously a significant setback.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A setback in getting American boots back on the moon, says CBS News space analyst, Bill Harwood.\par \par \uc2 BILL HARWOOD: The company has to put together a string of successful flights to convince \uc2 NASA\uc2  it is reliable enough and is safe enough to put astronauts on board, and how long that`s going to take is anybody`s guess.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  founded by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , has billions of dollars in \uc2 NASA\uc2  contracts to use this rocket to return astronauts to the moon through the agency`s Artemis Program by 2027, and before China puts their own on the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 But Musk and his new boss also have big plans for the rocket to go beyond.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Elon, get those rocket ships going, because we want to reach Mars before the end of my term. We want to do it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For now, all Starship flights are on hold while the \uc2 FAA\uc2  orders \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  to investigate.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Mark Strassmann.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And weather news, heavy rain caused flooding in parts of Southern California yesterday. High water submerged vehicles in San Diego where several people had to be rescued by the Fire Department.\par \par \uc2 For more on this wet weather out West and the rest of the outlook, let`s go to meteorologist, Jessica Burch from CBS News Bay Area.\par \par \uc2 Jess, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA BURCH: Good morning to you too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Along the West Coast, they are expecting to see more rain right around the corner, but let`s head up to the northeast real fast, where I want to talk about some heavy winds that we`re dealing with right now, because from Maine all the way into Maryland, we can see wind gusts up to around 50 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 The National Weather Service has already issued wind advisory throughout the afternoon hours today in this exact same area.\par \par \uc2 We advance the clock all the way into our Saturday and Sunday forecast. It`ll still be breezy at that point, too. And another thing to mention at the same time, we`re also seeing a little bit of a warm up as we head into this weekend throughout the Northeast, jumping from the 40s to the 50s in areas like New York all the way over into DC, while our friends down south are actually experiencing a little bit of a cool down.\par \par \uc2 Let`s head back over to the West Coast real fast, because there are some changes right around the corner for us as we head all the way into this weekend and into early next week. We actually will see more heavy rain in the forecast throughout California and throughout the Pacific Northwest from Monday into Wednesday. We`ll have more on that as we get closer.\par \par \uc2 For now, over to you, Gayle, Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Friday to you, Jess. I still just love your voice. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Second death has been linked to the growing measles outbreak. Authorities in New Mexico say an unvaccinated adult tested positive for measles after the person died. That individual, we`re told, did not seek medical care. The official cause of death still under investigation today.\par \par \uc2 This follows the first death in a decade from the measles. That`s when a child died in West Texas last week. The CDC says there have been at least 164 measles cases just this year.\par \par \uc2 In our next hour, Dr. Celine Gounder will help us look into the damage that measles can cause to your immune system, even after the patient has recovered.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And this morning, a top \uc2 Hamas\uc2  official spoke to CBS News about talks to extend the ceasefire in Gaza after President Trump`s Chief Envoy tried to explain what the President meant by his so-called last warning to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Debora Patta has more on Steve Witkoff`s comments to reporters at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF (U.S. Special Envoy to the Middle East): I think there`s going to be some action taken. It could be jointly with the Israelis. It`s unclear right now, but I think \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has an opportunity to act reasonably, to do what`s right, and then to walk out.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: But senior \uc2 Hamas\uc2  official, Mushir al-Masri says they have no intention of leaving; while they don`t want to rule Gaza after the war, they will not disarm, he said, until there is a Palestinian state.\par \par \uc2 (MUSHIR AL-MASRI speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "\uc2 Hamas\uc2  is a legitimate resistance movement," he said, "And we won`t abandon our weapons."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has designated \uc2 Hamas\uc2  a terrorist organization. Al-Masri says the only way to free the remaining hostages is through negotiations