evealed evidence in the murder of four college students.\par \par \uc2 Your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 So this was the scary moment a 17-year-old with a shotgun and ammunition was subdued by passengers and crew members on a plane in Australia. He was wrestled to the ground once they realized he was carrying a gun.\par \par \uc2 Police say the teen entered the airport near Melbourne yesterday through a hole in a security fence wearing a yellow safety vest. The flight was headed to Sydney with about 160 passengers on board. No one was hurt.\par \par \uc2 Police say it`s too early to establish a motive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, we are seeing key evidence in the case against Bryan Kohberger. He is the man accused of killing four University of Idaho students more than two years ago now.\par \par \uc2 The evidence is a chilling series of texts sent by two other people who were inside the house at the time of the murders.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti has more on the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The unsealed texts shared between the two surviving roommates reveal 17 frantic messages.\par \par \uc2 According to the text, one of them feared a man with a ski mask was in their home around the time of the killings of Madison Mogen, Kaylee Goncalves, Xana Kernodle, and Ethan Chapin took place.\par \par \uc2 The two who were in separate bedrooms texted between 4:22 and 4:24 AM and placed unanswered phone calls to all four victims between 4:19 and 4:27 AM.\par \par \uc2 "No one is answering," texted one of the roommates, identified as DM to BF, adding, "I`m really confused."\par \par \uc2 DM then texted the phone of roommate Goncalves saying, "Kaylee, what`s going on?" DM sent another text to BF writing about the intruder`s appearance, saying "Like a ski mask, almost, and that he had something over his head and mouth." She goes on to tell BF, "I`m not kidding. I am so freaked out."\par \par \uc2 Then BF tells DM, "Come to my room. Run. Down here."\par \par \uc2 The messages come as a 911 call from the morning of the killings was also made public for the first time. The call made by BF came about eight hours after the text message exchange. It`s unclear why they waited so long to call for help.\par \par \uc2 During it, she tells the operator that she found one of her roommates, Xana Kernodle, unconscious, and "believes she got drunk last night." She also tells the dispatcher that a man was in their home.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Kohberger was arrested six weeks later in connection with the case, and a judge entered a not guilty plea to all charges on his behalf.\par \par \uc2 CBS News legal contributor, Jessica Levinson.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: The text messages are really about painting a broader picture for the jury.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The trial is expected to begin in August, where both survivors are expected to be critical witnesses.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jonathan Vigliotti.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That story was so horrible from the very beginning, but to see that they were aware that something terrible was happening at the time is heartbreaking, and I`m with you, Nate. I always think about the families during this time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They have to relive everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They have to relive all this stuff over and over again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And to know they were terrified at home that night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is an awful, awful story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, Selma, Alabama is remembering the day 60 years ago today that is known in Civil Rights history as Bloody Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of marchers demanded the right to vote were met by police officers and State Troopers who pushed them back, brutally beating some of them. The violence was condemned around the country and led to the passage of the Voting Rights Act later that year.\par \par \uc2 Today, there will be a reenactment over the bridge, the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma and the 50-mile walk to the state capitol held in 1965.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it changed the world that day.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, surprising revelations about who gets the payouts in the \uc2 Purdue Pharma\uc2  opioid settlement.\par \par \uc2 Cecilia Vega of "60 Minutes," you hear the clock ticking -- talks with an insider about this. You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Earlier this year, members of the Sackler family and \uc2 Purdue Pharma\uc2 , the maker of the painkiller, OxyContin agreed to pay up to $7.4 billion to settle lawsuits over their involvement in the opioid crisis.\par \par \uc2 For this Sunday`s "60 Minutes," correspondent Cecilia Vega sat down with Ryan Hampton, who served for two years as co-chair of the official creditors committee, representing the victims in \uc2 Purdue Pharma\uc2 `s bankruptcy case, and in this interview, Hampton, who had also struggled with addiction to the drug, speaks about what he learned.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA: You were on the inside. You were privy to a lot of information that had never been seen publicly. What did you learn that was most surprising?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMPTON (Former co-chair of the Official Committee of Unsecured Creditors): The most surprising fact for me, was that victims were not first in line, but last in line because there were larger corporate and government interests that wanted to get paid first.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA: Court records show that lawyers, mediators and others, have already pocketed nearly $1 billion paid by Purdue. Forty-one firms have so far been paid more money than victims are expected to get in the $7.4 billion deal with senior lawyers representing the company charging as much as. $2,645.00 an hour.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMPTON: It`s sickening.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA: Do you not see this as something that could help families? This could be the only recourse they have.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMPTON: Absolutely, but do I think they`re getting a fair shake? Do I think that they`re getting anywhere near what they`re owed, knowing that you can`t put a price tag to that? No way.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA: Is your sense that a lot of those folks might actually not receive payment?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HAMPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh boy, you can catch the Celia Vega`s full report this Sunday on "60 Minutes" right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Authorities in New Mexico are getting ready to share new details today of the investigation into the deaths of Oscar-winning actor, Gene Hackman and his wife and one of the couple`s dogs.\par \par \uc2 The bodies were found in their Santa Fe home last month. Carter Evans is there with the latest on this investigation.\par \par \uc2 Carter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Can`t wait to hear the latest on this story.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: All right, now we`re looking forward to getting more information, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 So far, investigators aren`t giving many details about what they may reveal in today`s news conference, but we do know the autopsies are complete, and we anticipate learning more about the results this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Still, many questions remain, including how they died? So here`s what we know.\par \par \uc2 Their bodies were found by a caretaker in their gated community on February 26th and that`s the person who called 911. Now, investigators say it appears the couple died in a similar time frame, about a week before they were found.\par \par \uc2 We now know that on February 17th, Hackman`s pacemaker stopped working. That`s according to the Santa Fe Sheriff, and we`re expecting to learn a little more about that today.\par \par \uc2 Now the Sheriff`s Office says Betsy Arakawa`s body was found in an open bathroom with prescription pills scattered on the counter, while Hackman`s body was found in the mud room with his walking cane nearby.\par \par \uc2 Now, authorities found blood pressure and thyroid medications, a monthly planner and two cell phones that they had planned to analyze, and they`ve also checked into carbon monoxide poisoning and they say right now, it is unlikely, but we will find out much more from toxicology reports, which could take a lot more time to complete, but hopefully we`d get more information later this afternoon -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hope so.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, looking forward to that information. Carter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead in "What to Watch" Dolly Parton`s emotional musical tribute to her late husband, Carl Dean, but right now, it is 7:42, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have a lot coming up for you at eight o`clock, including Anthony as in Mason`s conversation with Ringo Starr at Nashville`s legendary Ryman Auditorium. Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 The former Beatle talks about his life-long love of country music -- who knew -- and how it shaped his latest album.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`re going to meet the Oscar winning designer behind the magical costumes of "Wicked." His name is Paul Tazewell. He`ll be here today talking about the designs and how he hopes to inspire young people in this industry. Can`t wait to meet him.\par \par \uc2 All of that`s coming up in our next hour, but first Klaus and I are heading over because Dana J