oth.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re learning more about how measles can cause lasting damage to our immune system, even after you`ve recovered. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Celine Gounder is here to explain what that mean exactly, but first, it is 8:04. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, Ringo Starr is showing off his country music chops with his latest album. He`ll talk with Anthony Mason about his long time love of country ahead of his all-star concert airing Monday right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, costume designer, Paul Tazewell just made history of the Oscars for his work on "Wicked." He will tell us what that recognition means to him.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS. Stick around..\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Grand Ole Opry\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Act naturally, yes, that`s right. That is Ringo Starr on your screen, making his Grand Old Opry debut in Nashville recently, where the country classic "Act Naturally" which he and The Beatles first covered back in the 60s.\par \par \uc2 So you might think that Ringo is a little more Abbey Road -- I love that album -- than Music Road, but he has always had a soft spot for country music. Bet you didn`t know that\par \par \uc2 The new concert special airing Monday on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , Ringo celebrates his long time love for the genre at the iconic Ryman Auditorium. Our Anthony, as in Mason, was there to meet up with him.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You`ve played here before.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR (Musician): I have played here several times, and it`s always a thrill.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Is it?\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: Well, yes, it`s -- you know, the home of country music.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: When I was a teenager, Hank Williams and people like that, this was the aim of the game, is to get here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gunpowder & Sky)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In Nashville, Ringo Starr played the mother church of country music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gunpowder & Sky)\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: This is really cool.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 This is Elvis.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: Oh. Elvis is here, okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And then Kitty Wells is up there.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: I love Kitty Wells.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: I just love Kitty Wells.\par \par \uc2 I have a great band.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: He was at the Ryman to celebrate his latest record, "Look Up," an unexpected album of country songs.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Look Up"/Ringo Starr/UME)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It came about after he bumped into producer, T Bone Burnett in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: Peace and love, everybody.\par \par \uc2 T BONE BURNETT (Music producer): Ringo came over and said, "Hey, you want to write me a song?" And I said, yes, I do want to write you a song. So knowing that his name is Ringo Starr, because he wanted to be a cowboy singer when he was a kid, and you know, it sounds like the Sheriff of Dodge City or something, "Ringo Starr."\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 T BONE BURNETT: And so I decided to write him a Gene Autry kind of song, singing cowboy, kind of song.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That song, "Time On My Hands" would be the catalyst for the album.\par \par \uc2 So as a kid, what was country music?\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: It`s emotional.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: As a teenager, Richard Starkey applied to emigrate from Liverpool, England to Houston, Texas.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: And it was because of Lightning Hopkins, the blues singer.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, that you wanted to be in Houston.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: Well, that`s where he lived, so I wanted to be by him, because I looked him, too, and, you know, it didn`t work out.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But one of his first lead vocals for the Beatles would be a country tune. Carl Perkins` "Honey, Don`t."\par \par \uc2 I mean, from the beginning, you were kind of the country guy in The Beatles.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: I was.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, and the first song you wrote for them was basically a country song.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: It was. Yes, yes. It is just how I started, right?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: You know, waiting for your footsteps coming down to the cry.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: The song, "Don`t Pass Me By" would appear on The Beatles "White" album.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gunpowder & Keg)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Jack White performed it at the Ryman.\par \par \uc2 JACK WHITE (Singer): It`s a treat for me, because the first vinyl album I bought when I had cash in my pocket when I was 14.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACK WHITE: On my own, I had skipped school and went downtown Detroit and I went into Harmony House Records, and I bought the "White" album, so to be able to sing a song from that record is pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gunpowder & Keg)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Did you ever think you`d be playing the Ryman with Ringo?\par \par \uc2 JACK WHITE: No. Oh, neither of those things.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gunpowder & Keg)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Neither did Molly Tuttle, who also sang on the album.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY TUTTLE (Singer): I don`t know when I`ve ever worked on like practice something more. I was like, I have to do a good job.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Roccabella Inc.)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She played at the Ryman with Ringo and Billy Strings.\par \par \uc2 BILLY STRINGS (Musician): He walked in the door and was like, hey, man and started kind of dancing and saying hi to everybody.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes..\par \par \uc2 BILLY STRINGS: And he came up and gave me a big squeeze on the shoulder and thanked me for being there. And it`s like, man, it`s my honor to be here, you know?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I was spoon fed that music. I remember my mom showing me the "White" album when I was just a little kid and playing it backwards for me and stuff, you know.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s a good mom.\par \par \uc2 BILLY STRINGS: She is.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gunpowder & Keg)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s easy to forget when you are where you are, that you`re getting to play with those people you used to listen to.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW (Singer): It is never lost on me.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Sheryl Crow was another of the friends Ringo got a little help from.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Gunpowder & Keg)\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROWS: Just running through stuff and being this close to him, I wanted to -- it was like Jesus, I just wanted to touch the cloak. You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: Let`s go and see the t-shirts.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ringo played just two shows at the Ryman.\par \par \uc2 I like that the Look Up Tour is two days.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: Yes, yes. I mean, some tour. You wish it was two days.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: His country tour may have been a limited engagement.\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: Okay, well, we`re seeing that now.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, it`s been great talking to you --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But he left his fingerprints in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 Whose hand is this?\par \par \uc2 RINGO STARR: That`s mine. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s a cast of his hand. It is a cast of his hand. Actually, he says he has one at home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, he seemed to have a lot of jokes, that`s why I was --\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ringo has always had jokes, Gayle, from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s