 to ask all about it, and also who`s at risk.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In today`s "Health Watch," the measles outbreak is showing no signs of slowing down. Get this, last year, there were a total of 285 cases. At least 164 cases of the virus have been detected so far this year. Two unvaccinated people who were diagnosed have died, a child in Texas and an adult in New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 We know measles is a severe and potentially deadly virus, right? But a lesser known fact is how the virus can damage the immune system, raising the risk of other infections even after the person has recovered it. It`s called immune amnesia.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is editor-at-large for Public Health at KFF Health News.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 So let`s jump right into it. How exactly does measles wipe out immunity to other viruses people have already been exposed to? That confuses me.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, we know of other viruses that infect the immune system. HIV is probably the best known one. Measles infects immune cells, and when it does so, it damages immune cells and it wipes out partially your memory, your immune system`s memory to certain infections, so that leaves you more susceptible to getting some of those infections.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have an animation here to help you understand how this works.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So let`s say you have two babies, as they grow, their immune systems mature, and they both gain immunity to different infections, which is represented here by the different colored marbles.\par \par \uc2 One baby gets vaccinated against measles at age one, and then later age four, that baby gains immunity to measles through vaccination, which is what you`re seeing here with the red marbles.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Which, there we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There we go.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: The other baby doesn`t get vaccinated and is not immune to measles. At age five, both kids will start school, and the unvaccinated child gets measles.\par \par \uc2 So if that kid is lucky, it`s a mild case, and they don`t need to be hospitalized, they develop immunity to measles from the infection, but immunity to other infections is weakened, and that leaves them at higher risk for other infections. That`s because the measles is damaging your immune system and erasing that memory to other viruses.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was digestible. Thank you for putting it like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So if somebody has been infected with the measles, is there anything they can do to regain the immunity that they`ve lost?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: No, it`s just time. Time for your immune system to build back up and recover. The best thing is not to get measles in the first place, and the best way to do that is to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we were always told that once you get it, it lasts your lifetime.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Once you get vaccinated, yes, your covered for your lifetime. One dose gets you to about 95 percent, two doses to 97 percent coverage.\par \par \uc2 So pretty much if you`ve gotten your measles vaccination, you are set, you are immune for your lifetime.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Should people try to get boosters just because of this outbreak?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, there`s a very specific age group where you may want to consider getting a booster. So if you were born after 1957, but vaccinated before 1963, you may not have immunity from measles infection or vaccination, because that`s an age group also where the vaccines that we had available were not as effective.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re in that very specific age group, again, born after 1957 vaccinated before 1963 you may want to get a measles booster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I had never heard of the phrase "immune amnesia." How long did the effects of that last?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, from studies, it can last months to a couple of years. So you know it really does set a child back and back before kids were being vaccinated for measles, it is estimated that half of the deaths were actually from other infections they got later because of that weakened immune system.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s so much information out there, but it`s different when it comes from the White House. Earlier this week, in a Fox News interview, \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary RFK, Jr. supported some unconventional treatments for measles, including vitamin A and cod liver. Are these effective tools? What does science tell us?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But look at your face.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So there were three you mentioned.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is like you`re struggling to hold your face in a straight face.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I saw her --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your lip curled up a little bit. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So you have cod liver oil, which has vitamin A.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What is cod liver?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Cod liver oil is oil from the cod --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Cod -- so from a fish`s liver.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. That`s been around for centuries.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: But it is a good source of vitamin A in very malnourished kids in developing countries. Studies showed that it helped reduce certain complications for measles, in particular, blindness, but that -- like you don`t see that in the U.S. Less than one percent of kids have that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 So you know, unfortunately, this is not very good advice that`s coming.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Dr. Gounder, thank you so much. Coming up next, "Talk of the Table." How some of the youngest survivors of the wildfires, what they chose to take home.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, stand by me.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Stand by me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the talk of the table this morning, and Adriana, you are going to kick it off for us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Okay, so this is really sweet. My "Talk of the Table" is "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " for Kids. That`s a special section that runs in the newspaper once a month asking some young victims of the California wildfires what they took with them when they had to flee their homes.\par \par \uc2 So five-year-old Maris Premvree (sp?) three says she took her red velvet dress. Look at that beautiful dress. It was a gift from her neighbor. Both of their homes in Altadena burned down in the fires.\par \par \uc2 Ten-year-old, Nico Chavez says he took his pet turkey named Apricot. His family ran a mobile petting zoo, and they packed all the animals into a van and a truck.\par \par \uc2 Twelve-year-old Ngozi (sp?) says that while her family was grabbing essentials, she had to save her plastic samurai sword, because it`s quote, "super-important to me." I just love that she has a Samurai sword. I love her for that.\par \par \uc2 And then eight-year-old Felicity Dale (sp?) took her stuffed Mickey Mouse. She says it makes her feel safe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it is interesting what they choose.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes. Parents are probably running around grabbing photos.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But they want that one thing that`s going to make them feel safe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t want to present a hypothetical to people out there, because, you know, this was something that actually happened to a lot of families, but it does make you think, what would you grab if you had to grab one thing from your house?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes. I would take pictures.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, me too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, and my kids and my dog.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: My husband, too though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He gets to go too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: My husband, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good choice. Good choice.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a travel and lifestyle influencer. In a note she says, she received while she was waiting for a flight. She is 23 years old. Her name is Steph Bohrer. She posted this video of a napkin with a handwritten note saying, "I have seen the whole world, and you are the most beautiful human in it."\par \par \uc2 I love that.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says a pilot --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A pilot?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: -- placed it on the table where she was sitting, and then he just walked away. She says she didn`t even see his face.\par \par \uc2 Now, when I heard that, I thought that`s such a nice, nice gesture. I was so touched by it. But you know, there`s always cranky \uc2 Yankees\uc2  and haters online. Some of them say he`s probably married. I wonder how many napkins he`s written with the same message. So others say, pilot was just being kind. He obviously wasn`t trying to arrange a hookup. He didn`t leave his name or his number. I mean, it was just a nice message to a stranger with no intentions.\par \par \uc2 I just thought it was so sweet.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I have been around the world, and you the most beautiful human being --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t understand how you can see a negative out of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He didn`t leave his number, his \uc2 Instagram\uc2  handle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: He didn`t try to talk to her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is just serving a compliment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But if I was Steph, I`d be trying to find him.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For real.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I think she --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hello?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I think she is, because she put that on TikTok, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For real.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: No, but it is so sweet. I mean, I got a little warm, just like while you read it, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We think things sometimes about other people, and you never bother to tell them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But how nice it is that he actually took the time to write to her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, I am hearing Shawna in our ear, she is saying, he was spitting game. Of course, he was spitting game. He was quite poetic.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But he didn`t stop. No, if he had stopped to try to talk to her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but I like it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it, too.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: She was --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She better buckle up. It`s about to take off.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is this year`s 12 finalists for the World Video Game Hall of Fame. All right, so they include some all-time favorites.\par \par \uc2 The winners will be honored May 8th at The Strong National Museum of Play in Rochester, New York.\par \par \uc2 "Angry Birds" is one of the finalists.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from ROVIO Entertainment)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The 2009 phenomenon was downloaded billions, I mean, billions of time and launched movies and merchandise.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Activision)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Call of Duty." People also love, it is also on the list. The military first person shooter game is still going strong, more than 20 years after it was first released.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from SEGA)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The ever popular basketball game, "\uc2 NBA\uc2  2K" is also in the Hall of Fame. This was old school footage of the Lakers, but it just came so far since then, the graphics. People would download this game and play it.\par \par \uc2 Look at it now, compared to what it looks like. If you just watch this from a distance.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I thought that was real for a second.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You will think it is real.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Take Two Interactive)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did. I did, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But it`s one of the best games. Some of the other games that stand out to me --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s Steve Kerr. Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, "Golden Tee." I mean, listen, any guy, a couple of beers at a bar, "Golden Tee" is an epic game.\par \par \uc2 "Golden Eye" is one of the best games ever, in a game that popped in my head the first time I ever visited New York, when I was trying to cross the street, "Frogger."\par \par \uc2 Got to shout the "Frogger."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you were trying to cross the street?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I was so rattled. I was like, oh my goodness, I`m about to get hit. But I was like, "What did I do on Frogger?" All right, just try to avoid the cars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t you just pay attention to the lights and cross on green and stop on red.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, it`s harder than that for a West Coast transplant.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Even New Yorkers don`t do that. New Yorkers have been like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I remember, I was crossing the street and somebody behind me was like, "Never stop moving your feet." Because the hand was blinking. I didn`t know what to do. I froze. I was like, who is this man yelling at me? It was an old lady. She passed me. I said, I`m not built for this. I am going back to Seattle.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re glad you stayed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Glad you stayed.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The costume designer behind Glinda and Elphaba`s magical looks is here, ladies and gentlemen. WE will talk with Paul Tazewell about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did he bring the Oscar?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There it is. There`s the Oscar.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There it is. All right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And the fashion of Oz.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is a flex.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But first a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is a flex.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wicked"/Universal Pictures)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go ahead. Goose bumps again and again and again. That is Ariana Grande and Cynthia Erivo`s powerhouse duet "Defying Gravity" from the mega hit movie, "Wicked." You`ve seen it.\par \par \uc2 The world of Oz was re-imagined through more than 1,000 costumes all designed by Paul Tazewell. Drop the microphone, Mr. Paul. Glinda and Elphaba`s story is told through the shapes and colors of their fantastical wardrobe.\par \par \uc2 With more than three decades in the industry, this is not his first rodeo. Tazewell has quite a resume. He`s won a Tony Award for his costumes on "Hamilton," an Emmy for his work on "The Wiz Live." And don`t forget you, when you saw "West Side Story" the movie, that was Paul.\par \par \uc2 He one step closer to EGOT status after making his -- shall I say it again?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, say it again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One step closer to EGOT status after making history at the Academy Awards on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL (Designer): I`m the first Black man --\par \par \uc2 (Cheers)\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: To receive a costume design award for my work on "Wicked."\par \par \uc2 My Ozian muses, Cynthia and Ariana, I love you so much. All of the other cast, thank you, thank you, thank you for trusting me with bringing your characters to life.\par \par \uc2 This is everything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is everything. And we`re so happy to say that Paul Tazewell is here, and he brought Oscar. We`ll look at Oscar in just a second, but Paul, I was in the room that night, and I hope you felt the love.\par \par \uc2 The minute they said your name, people stood up and started applauding before you even open your mouth.\par \par \uc2 I felt that everybody was pulling for you. Did you feel that too?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You had to.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: It was remarkable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: It was really remarkable. I mean, just the synergy -- just everything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are you still floating?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: Of course, of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: With Oscar, right by my side, yes. It means so much to be recognized for this film, for everything that I put into it, and then everyone else that had a hand in creating the costumes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But did you dream of this moment? Did you dream of you being on stage, winning an Oscar as a little kid? Because you`ve been doing this a while. Did you dream of this?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: You know, I have spoken with my brothers and my cousin just about, you know, we would sleep outside behind our house in Akron, Ohio, and dream about what our lives would be as adults. And --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What was your dream?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: It was this. I mean, it was becoming famous, and, you know, leading a glamorous life, and, you know --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: But I didn`t know that it would give me the full, rich life that I`ve had, you know, which has been being a costume designer working with amazing talents, being able to tell really important stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: I had no idea that it was going to look like this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, but look like this. Number one, I gasp when -- we`ve all seen the movie, when she came out in the pink and Cynthia --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m curious about your process when you`re designing when you - - because it`s fantastic -- it is amazing to me that it starts as a drawing on a piece of paper.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, it starts in the mind first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It starts in the mind first.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Then hits the paper.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The drawing, and then that. What`s your process?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: Well, I start with the text, I start with the script, or a director`s idea or a writer`s idea, and then I start to do research.\par \par \uc2 I`m pulling together images that feel reflective of how I envision this world. But it stays -- I like to keep it abstract. You know, I like to keep it in a place of, you know, talking about qualities of color and shapes. And, you know, that was where the idea of the spiral came into play for "Wicked."\par \par \uc2 And the idea of the texture of mushrooms. And you`re -- I`m working to align those elements which viscerally feel appropriate or right for the world of Oz, but then I want to create a connection with the characters, because all of it is about telling stories through what my medium is, which is clothing and making those characters visually more interesting or more specific, because, as with each of you, you choose what you`re going to wear every morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: You get up. You know what you have to do for the day, and you dress appropriately to whatever you have coming your way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is fascinating.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: So you know when you`re telling a story that -- I`m also creating that. I just happen to be creating, you know, an arc of what those clothes might be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You really see that in the movie, because you see the evolution of both of the main characters` clothing. Somebody pointed out to me, I`m not sure who it was. I don`t know if it was Gisele, who`s a huge "Wicked" fan right here, our floor director, but Glinda`s clothes start to look a little bit more like Elphaba later in the movie. Elphaba has her own transformation.\par \par \uc2 I`m so curious about the hat because I recently saw "Wicked" on Broadway, and the hat on Broadway is a simple kind of witch`s hat, but your hat had all this personality. What was the inspiration and the process?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: You know, it was a number of things. One was that -- there`s the moment when Glinda gives the hat to Elphaba, which is given as a joke, and it`s been created by her grandmother, and it`s stored in her luggage.\par \par \uc2 So it was important for it to open up like a top hat would. So, you know, so, having it -- you know, giving it that kind of crooked shape was an element of the story. Also the way that Jon Chu wanted to start the film.\par \par \uc2 He was describing it from the very beginning, that it would be, you know, you would see it from a distance, and it would look like a mountain, and then you`d come in close and you realize that it was a hat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shoutout to Jon Chu. We have pictures of you as a little kid, because you`ve been sewing. You were 16. You were a young boy. You worked on "The Wiz" in high. I mean, it is great to see you back in the day, and I love that you said, "I`m the first Black man," because that struck me. I hadn`t thought about that before.\par \par \uc2 How you were able to do this when there was nobody that looked like you, and now people can look at you and say, I want to be like Paul. How did you do it, Paul, when there was nobody who could be an inspiration to you that looked like you?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: You know, I think that it was developed very early in my life, and being an inspiration for just all of the young people who, you know, find themselves in the world of costume and want to express themselves creatively.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s exactly where you know, I came from, as well, developing as a creative person, going from wanting to be a performer to wanting to be a costume designer, and finding my power in that. And you know, I`m so grateful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve got a superpower, all right.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you joining us. The incredibly talented and gifted.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we didn`t look at Oscar. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You just walk around with him? Hello, I am Paul Tazewell.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Do you check him in your luggage or did you bring him on the plane?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: You know, I actually carried him on to the plane.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`ve got to carry that.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He actually deserves his own seat.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you so much. You are a living legend and incredibly gifted.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good luck in the future and Part Two coming soon, and even bigger and better and brighter?\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, Paul Tazewell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TAZEWELL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Paul Tazewell just told us the costumes in Part Two are going to be what, Adriana?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: They`re going to be elevated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes. Building off of part one. We wouldn`t let him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That it is even more glam.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`ll see you Monday on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, here`s a look back at all that mattered this week. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Get ready for an incredible future, because the Golden Age of America has only just begun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Trump`s speech to Congress was the longest in the modern era. Celebrated his actions over the first six weeks of his new term.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hefty new tariffs on imported goods from Canada, Mexico and China. Canada already hitting back.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2 : We don`t want to see you hurt, but your government has chosen to do this to you.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Academy Awards)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All the great moments and some genuine surprises at last night`s Oscars. The independent film "Anora" walked away with five awards, including Best Picture.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I want to thank the Academy for recognizing a truly independent film.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad has something to say to all the internet haters out there, folks who say he does too much of this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Kapow. I am not creepy.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, y`all.\par \par \uc2 I`m very serious here.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Vlad, you do not have to defend yourself against those haters.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY CASON (Knockings lifestyle expert): We are going to be talking about bras and panties, this is where we leave you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, I can`t stay around here with you?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY CASON: I know you can. But Adriana, would you agree with me that like you go your --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wait, Vlad, you don`t actually have to go. Come.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY CASON: We`re kidding. Come on back. Because honestly --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Bing.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY CASON: You should tell your wife.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There was a big celebrity in the field at the Tokyo Marathon on Sunday. If you look quickly, you might miss it. So somewhere in there is pop superstar, Harry Styles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was reading. He did a mile guys in seven minutes plus.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m up to 14-and-a-half.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: At least you`re out there running.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there you go.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That`s fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On a treadmill.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Yes, you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You impressed, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: I am.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You can take the man out of Florida, but you can`t take the Florida out of the man. So this one`s for you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 So welcome to the Second Annual Florida Man Games.\par \par \uc2 This Florida man says it`s not a game, it`s a lifestyle.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Well, where else are you going to find anything like this? Like, do I really believe a true Florida man doesn`t actually prepare for this event? They`re just born ready to be great.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Born ready to be great. First of all, that is elite mullet. Elite. So shoutout to your mullet gang.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Actor Nick Offerman is his name. He played Master woodworker, Ron Swanson of "Parks & Recreation." Now, he is using his carpentry skills to help others.\par \par \uc2 ADAM YAMAGUCHI: What have you taken from this program and what do you think you poured back into it?\par \par \uc2 NICK OFFERMAN (Actor): Well, it`s a place like I see you. I care about you. Now, this is a hand plane.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I have a great regret that I didn`t learn carpentry from my stepfather while he was still alive, and so I see that, I wish.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Yamaguchi made me want to go work out. Sheesh.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The guy sizzles.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Adam, you look good. You look good. Gayle King to Human Resources.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about a snack.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020240307ek3700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for February 13, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Omar Villafranca, Nancy Chen, Scott MacFarlane,  Carter Evans, Ed O`Keefe, Chris Warren, Louisa Moller, Lana Zak, Vladimir  Duthiers \par \uc2 3,855 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 February 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: snow emergency.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: This nor`easter will be quick, but it will pack a punch.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Heavy snow brings dangerous driving conditions, closes schools, and cancels more than 1,000 flights, the major storm bringing the most snowfall cities like New York have seen in years.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We actually got on the last flight yesterday. That was canceled. We were pushed to the first flight this morning, and that was canceled.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS starts now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Norah O`Donnell, and thank you for being with us on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a fast-moving nor`easter and the 45 million Americans impacted from West Virginia to Massachusetts. In some places, 15 inches of snow fell today alone, leading to dangerous road conditions, crashes, and many tonight are without power.\par \par \uc2 More than a million students were out of school. New York City is digging out, and this is the biggest snowstorm since 2022. Snow-covered roads caused havoc during the morning commute, causing more than 1,200 accidents in Pennsylvania alone. Air travelers faced their own problems with more than 3,000 delays and cancellations at the nation`s airports.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the major concern is coastal flooding. More than 11 million along the shorelines are under alerts tonight.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lana Zak starts us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK (voiceover): A near-miss in the Northeast, as the fast-moving storm dumped snow from Maryland to Massachusetts. New York City saw the highest daily snow total in more than two years, just over three inches, but far less than the eight that was predicted by the National Weather Service.\par \par \uc2 THORSTEN MANTHEUI (New York City Resident): I wanted more. I wanted way more. And I wanted it to snow all day.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: While parts of Connecticut and Pennsylvania saw more than a foot of snow, the wintry white quickly turned slushy and slick, police responding to more than 1,000 accidents in Pennsylvania and hundreds more in New Jersey and Connecticut, according to State Police.\par \par \uc2 Air travel was also a mess, with thousands of interruptions at major airports, including New York`s LaGuardia, where more than a third of flights were canceled.\par \par \uc2 LILIANA RAMIREZ (Traveler): I looked at the weather, and it seemed like it was going to be OK. I -- I made plans, but it definitely affected me. We couldn`t even drive out.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: The Massachusetts coast is under a flood advisory.\par \par \uc2 Louisa Moller with CBS Boston is there.\par \par \uc2 LOUISA MOLLER: Downtown (INAUDIBLE) low-lying areas often flood, but northerly winds and an astronomical tide make this parking lot look like a swimming pool.\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: As a safety precaution, many school districts closed today. New York City Public Schools went remote, but the computer network had a meltdown when nearly a million students tried logging in at the same time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BANKS (Chancellor, \uc2 New York City Department of Education\uc2 ): So, to say that I am disappointed, frustrated, and angry is an understatement.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LANA ZAK: Well, there was one great benefit to that online learning snafu, Central Park today filled with kids who actually got to bring those sleds out of their closets for the first time in more than two years.\par \par \uc2 But, Norah, now that the sun has gone down, all of that slush is refreezing as ice. So, authorities warn you have to be careful out there right now -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That is an important warning.\par \par \uc2 Lana Zak, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 , as there are more storms on the way.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This winter storm dumped more than a foot of snow for some areas in the Northeast. But many of the big cities saw far less than that, including Boston, just a tenth-of-an-inch at the airport. Main reason for that, the overall track of the system shifted a little bit to the south, but also the northeast winds bringing in the relatively mild air from the Atlantic, keeping Boston in the rain quite a bit longer and keeping down the snowfall totals.\par \par \uc2 Any melting happening today could refreeze by morning, with temperatures well below freezing, teens and twenties. Watch out for black ice. Couple more chances of snow going through this week and into the weekend. Fast- moving systems will bring maybe a few to maybe a half-a-foot of snow for some.\par \par \uc2 But it is going to be the cold that millions will feel by Saturday morning, 190 million with temperatures below freezing, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chris Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, President Biden today came out swinging against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , calling his comments about Russia invading \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries shameful and dumb. The president says Trump`s remarks raise the stakes for Congress to approve new funding to support Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 CBS`s Ed O`Keefe details from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The former president has set a dangerous and shockingly, frankly, un-American signal to the world.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voiceover): President Biden leveled a blistering attack today on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for recently saying he wouldn`t defend \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies from a Russian attack if they don`t pay full dues to the alliance.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Saturday)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R-Former President of the United States and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate): Let`s say that happened.\par \par \uc2 No, I would not protect you. In fact, I would encourage them to do whatever the hell they want.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got to pay. You`ve got to pay your bills.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Can you imagine a former president of the United States saying that? The whole world heard it. And the worst thing is, he means it. No other president in our history has ever bowed down to a Russian dictator. Well, let me say this as clearly as I can: I never will.\par \par \uc2 For God`s sake, it`s dumb, it`s shameful, it`s dangerous, it`s un-American.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The president spoke after an all-night marathon debate in the Senate led to early morning passage of an emergency national security spending bill.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TIM KAINE (D-Virginia): The bill, as amended, passes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It would provide billions of military assistance to Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan, but says nothing about U.S.-Mexico border security. And that`s why House Speaker Mike Johnson says he won`t bring it up for a vote.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE JOHNSON (R-Louisiana): National security begins with border security. We have said that all along.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: House Republicans are increasingly taking cues from Trump, who has said they shouldn`t support any border security deal that could benefit Democrats and President Biden.\par \par \uc2 The president today said the stakes are bigger than that.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : And I say to the House members, House Republicans: You`ve got to decide. Are you going to stand up for freedom, or are you going to side with terror and tyranny? Will we stand with America or with Trump?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Meanwhile, tonight, House Republicans are planning to vote again to impeach Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas amid opposition to his handling of border security.\par \par \uc2 A similar vote failed last week. And if they succeed this time, Mayorkas would be the first Cabinet secretary impeached since 1876 -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ed O`Keefe at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon tonight says that Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin is now home from the hospital and has resumed his full duties. Secretary Austin underwent treatment for a bladder problem stemming from his surgery for prostate cancer in December. He`s expected to return to the Pentagon later this week.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of flight attendants walked the picket lines at dozens of airports in the U.S. today. Most of the nation`s flight attendants, represented by multiple unions, are in contract negotiations, seeking better pay and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 We get details from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STRIKERS: Hey, hey, ho, ho, corporate greed has got to go!\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Outside airports across the country today, flight attendants walked the picket lines, several thousand representing two dozen airlines at more than 30 airports, all with the same message.\par \par \uc2 JULIE HEDRICK\uc2  (National President, \uc2 Association of Professional Flight Attendants\uc2 ): We have been fighting for contracts for probably two-and-a- half years.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The picketing comes at a crucial time for flight attendants, who say they have been dealing more frequently with unruly passengers --\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- and feel underappreciated for the times they perform potential lifesaving measures, like when the door recently blew off this \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  jet.\par \par \uc2 STRIKERS: Hey, hey, ho, ho!\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Tim Green (sp?) has been an Alaska flight attendant for more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 When was the last time you got a raise?\par \par \uc2 TIM GREEN (Alaska Flight Attendant): Last significant raise for us was back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Before all the increase in prices and everything?\par \par \uc2 TIM GREEN: Before everything started to skyrocket, and, yes, inflation went nuts.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Most flight attendants have a starting salary of just $27,000. The median salary is $63,000. The demand for a pay raise comes after pilots negotiated between 35 and 50 percent salary hikes and as airlines last year posted a combined $10 billion profit.\par \par \uc2 And most domestic flight attendants don`t even earn their hourly wage until the boarding door closes.\par \par \uc2 TIM GREEN: About 45 minutes to an hour working with the passengers, getting everybody seated, dealing with all the problems.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And you`re not getting paid for that?\par \par \uc2 TIM GREEN: We`re not getting paid. But we`re required to be there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Since no one walked off the job, no flights were delayed because of today`s protest.\par \par \uc2 Now, contract negotiations are under way, so any strike would be months away at this point. And we reached out to several airlines. They all said that flight attendants have the right to picket, and they`re optimistic that deals will be reached -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Carter Evans, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to today`s special election to replace disgraced former Congressman George Santos in New York`s Third Congressional District.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane reports the tight race is being seen as a trial run for the general election for both parties.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): They plowed ahead with a special election on Long Island in a driving snowstorm --\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE GEORGE SANTOS (R-New York): Yes, OK.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: -- to replace a man who regularly produced his own political storm, former Republican Congressman George Santos, who fabricated his life story and allegedly used his campaign to fleece donors and enrich himself.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel like you`re finally turning the page on George Santos?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: How`s that feel?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Good. You know, he had to go.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Republican seeking this seat, Mazi Pilip, is a county legislator born in Ethiopia, a mother of seven who once served in the Israeli military.\par \par \uc2 Is there any concern that the George Santos experience made people less enthusiastic about coming out today?\par \par \uc2 MAZI PILIP (R-New York Congressional Candidate): No, absolutely. George Santos is absolutely the past. The issue is really the -- securing the borders, controlling illegal immigrants coming to our country.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Democrat is former Congressman \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 , whose late flurry of campaign ads sought to neutralize Pilip`s claims Democrats are responsible for a border crisis that`s brought thousands of migrants to New York.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REPRESENTATIVE TOM SUOZZI (D-New York): Well, the bottom line is, I want to talk about immigration, because this is what the people care about, and that`s what I have been talking about for a long, long time.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Thousands voted early here, but the campaigns worried how many prospective voters would trudge through today`s mess.\par \par \uc2 Does this make you less inclined to come out?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Nah. We -- we headed out sledding, and I said, let`s do this now since we`re out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The stakes are so high. Whoever wins can either shrink or expand the Republicans` narrow U.S. House majority.\par \par \uc2 And both parties spent millions of dollars trying to win this seat, but so many of the advertisements, Norah, focused on immigration.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Frustrating news about inflation sent stocks spiraling today. The Dow had its worst day in nearly a year, dropping almost 1.5 percent. It was no better for the S&P 500 or the Nasdaq. Those losses were triggered by a new report showing inflation came in higher than expected at 3.1 percent last month, meaning a cut in interest rates may not come for several months.\par \par \uc2 With inflation still stubbornly high, raising the cost of food and shelter, millions of student loan borrowers are stuck deciding between having to pay their debt or provide for their families.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s MoneyWatch, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Chen has tips to help you save.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (voiceover): With two master`s degrees, Racquelle Perry has always prioritized education, and she has the bills to prove it.\par \par \uc2 How much do you owe?\par \par \uc2 RACQUELLE PERRY (Borrower): Three hundred and seven thousand dollars.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: When you see that amount written out, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 RACQUELLE PERRY: How am I ever going to pay this back?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The 30-year-old mother teaches financial literacy to high schoolers in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. But when it comes to supporting her family, the numbers don`t add up.\par \par \uc2 RACQUELLE PERRY: If I pay this $500, $600, $700 this month, how am I going to afford to buy food for the family for the month, for the week?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Perry avoided her payments, along with 40 percent of borrowers, after federal student loan bills were resumed last fall, following a pause for the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Borrowers won`t face late fees for a one-year grace period, but student loan adviser Betsy Mayotte warns, interest is still building.\par \par \uc2 What are the consequences or risks in not addressing this issue?\par \par \uc2 BETSY MAYOTTE (President, The Institute of Student Loan Advisors): If the loan ends up defaulting, it`s going to be a big hit on your credit. Future debt that you need to take on a mortgage, a credit card, a car loan is likely going to have a much higher interest rate.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: While everyone`s situation is different, all federal loans offer an interest rate discount for autopay. For some, up to $2,500 in annual interest could be written off on tax returns.\par \par \uc2 And the government has constantly evolving programs that can eliminate or reduce loans, with options available through the online loan simulator tool.\par \par \uc2 BETSY MAYOTTE: I would love to see every consumer with student loans get in the habit of reevaluating their student loan strategy and checking in on things once a year.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Lessons for all, including those at the head of the classroom.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen, CBS News, Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Turning now to the migrant crisis impacting major American cities, about 40,000 asylum seekers have been bused to Denver, Colorado, over the last year.\par \par \uc2 That`s more per capita than anywhere in the nation. The city is sending -- spending millions of dollars every week to house, feed, and educate them.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca goes in depth to show us how one place has become a safe haven for hundreds.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Yong Prince wakes up early every morning making breakfast for the hundreds of people staying at her packed Denver motel.\par \par \uc2 YONG PRINCE (Motel Owner): I don`t want people to go work on empty stomach.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But this motel is different. The rooms are free, and the guests are all migrants, mainly from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 YONG PRINCE: I`m financially ready to do this kind of job.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: You wanted to help?\par \par \uc2 YONG PRINCE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: This motel is closed, but it is full of people. And residents tell me that there`s sometimes eight people per room, sometimes a dozen, and, during the busiest times, more than 25 people per room; 25- year-old Carlos took us into the crowded room that he shares with more than a dozen people, including his wife.\par \par \uc2 There`s barely any room to walk. He`s worked as a roofer. And when he can`t find a job, he washes windchills for tips.\par \par \uc2 (CARLOS speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: "I`d rather work hard outside," he says, "because, with this, I can make money."\par \par \uc2 Like Chicago and New York, the migrant surge is driving Denver to the financial breaking point. The Mile High City expects to spend $180 million this year on migrants, forcing the city to cut some services.\par \par \uc2 MIKE JOHNSTON (D-Mayor of Denver, Colorado): We will start to have to green-light a set of hard decisions about budget reductions.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Prince has received help and donations, but says she has spent more than $300,000 of her own money since October. After the recent deaths of her husband and son, the 73-year-old daughter of North Korean immigrants feels this is her mission.\par \par \uc2 Is helping them helping you --\par \par \uc2 YONG PRINCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: -- get through --\par \par \uc2 YONG PRINCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: -- the loss of your son?\par \par \uc2 YONG PRINCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But time is running out. She sold the property, and everyone has to leave by the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 Prince says she`d like to lease another property with her own money to help the migrants as long as she can.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca, CBS News, Denver.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Heart-stopping new video from that deadly plane crash in Florida shows it plummeting from the sky.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Terrifying dash-cam video has surfaced showing the moment a private jet falling from the sky and crashing onto a busy interstate in Florida.\par \par \uc2 The plane carrying five people lost both engines and was heading to the airport in Naples when it slammed to the ground on Friday, killing both pilots. Another crew member and two passengers actually survived that wreck.\par \par \uc2 A 45-year-old man in Wichita, Kansas, is under arrest for allegedly stealing and burning a statue of baseball icon Jackie Robinson, the first black player in Major League Baseball. Police do not believe the theft last month was race-related. They say that the suspect intended to sell it for scrap metal. About $300,000 in donations have now poured in to replace the bronze statue.\par \par \uc2 Well, "The Daily Show" with Jon Stewart is back with his critical and funny takes on politics, the news media and, yes, even himself. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: After nearly nine years, Jon Stewart`s highly anticipated return to "The Daily Show" made it feel like he never left.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Vladimir Duthiers reports, Stewart roasted President Biden, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and even himself as only he can.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART (Host, The Daily Show): Welcome to "The Daily Show."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS (voiceover): Jon Stewart`s comeback to Comedy Central`s The Daily Show was greeted with thunderous applause.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART: I`m excited to be back. I`m very excited to be back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The 61-year-old comedian dived right in, tackling fringe conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART: \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  are world champions, which means the decades-long plot in which Travis and Taylor brainwash America into getting -- into getting routine vaccinations, is complete.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Age was a running theme, with Stewart first taking on President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MAN: Jason Kelce or Travis Kelce?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Mama Kelce. I understand she makes great chocolate chip cookies.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART: How do you go on TikTok and end up looking older?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And then his likely Republican contender, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-Arizona Senatorial Candidate): Well, first of all, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not an old man.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART: He`s an old man!\par \par \uc2 He is objectively an old man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Stewart even turned the tables on himself.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 DULCE SLOAN (The Daily Show): We need more than just the same show with an older, yet familiar face.\par \par \uc2 Like, let someone else run the show.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART: You`re talking about the election, right?\par \par \uc2 DULCE SLOAN: I said what I said.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little older, but he hasn`t missed a beat.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Daily Show, \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Glad he`s back.\par \par \uc2 And we should note that \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 , like \uc2 CBS\uc2 , is part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Major League Baseball is making history, not on the field, but in the broadcasting booth. We will tell you who next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight, history is being made in Major League Baseball, with the Oakland A`s naming Jenny Cavnar as its lead play-by-play announcer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from MLB Network)\par \par \uc2 JENNY CAVNAR (Oakland A`s Announcer): Fire up the fountains. She`s gone.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The veteran broadcaster is the first woman to be the primary voice of a Major League Baseball team, after spending the past 12 seasons as the backup play-by-play announcer for the \uc2 Colorado Rockies\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She`s a five-time Emmy Award winner and was named Colorado Sportscaster of the Year in 2020 year. Cavnar, the daughter of a former baseball coach, said in a statement: "It is a dream come true."\par \par \uc2 Tell you what. It sounds like a home run to me. So awesome. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240214ek2d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for January 1, 2024, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Major Garrett, Tom Hanson, Carter Evans, Astrid Martinez, Ian Lee,  Elise Preston, Ben Tracy, Kris Van Cleave \par \uc2 3,520 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 January 2024\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2024 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Tonight: A 7.5-magnitude earthquake hits Western Japan, triggering widespread destruction. Aftershocks, a tsunami warning, and fires add to the chaos.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Run.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Then it was just a violent shake. I mean violent shaking.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Search-and-rescue teams race to find survivors trapped in the rubble. Thousands try to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 Aerial assaults on Gaza intensify, as Israel prepares to withdraw some troops from the territory.\par \par \uc2 Unprecedented border crisis: 300,000 migrants were processed at the border with Mexico in December, putting pressure on the White House to step in.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s Eye on America: how one aerospace company is working to get more pilots in the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BROOM (Vice President of Commercial Training Solutions, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 ): We need people to be able to get on an airplane, see someone that looks like them, and go, I can do that, too.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And, finally, a class on media literacy showing students that seeing isn`t always believing.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Is there any part of you that`s like, if that`s fake, then what`s actually real?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #3: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDY MILLER (Student): Yes, all the time.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Good evening, everyone. Thank you for joining us on this first day of 2024. I`m Major Garrett, in for Norah.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with a powerful 7.5-magnitude earthquake striking Japan, destroying homes, buckling roads, and forcing thousands to flee to higher ground due to tsunami warnings. Fires broke out in the aftermath. Authorities say at least six people are dead.\par \par \uc2 Rescue crews continue to search through the rubble looking for survivors. The nation issued its highest-level tsunami alert immediately after the quake, but lowered it hours later. Residents along the western coast are still being told not to return to their homes.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has been in touch with Japanese officials. President Biden said the U.S. is -- quote -- "ready to provide any necessary assistance."\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ben Tracy will start us off tonight with the latest details.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY (voiceover): At a train station in Kanazawa, people ran for cover as the large earthquake struck around 4:10 local time Monday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The quake had a magnitude of 7.5, according to the \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2 . It violently shook buildings across Western Japan and rocked several religious shrines where people were paying traditional New Year`s Day visits. Roadways buckled and large fires broke out as dozens of buildings collapsed. The extent of the death toll and how many may still be trapped is not yet known. There have been dozens of aftershocks.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY WU (Tourist From Taiwan): Suddenly, it get pretty strong earthquake, and so everybody was panicked at that time.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Alarms blared as nearly 100,000 people were told to immediately flee to higher ground. The government issued a major tsunami warning, fearing waves could reach as high as 16 feet. That warning was later downgraded, as smaller waves hit the coastline.\par \par \uc2 This was Japan`s first major tsunami warning since an 8.9-magnitude quake hit back in 2011. It caused a devastating tsunami that killed thousands and triggered a meltdown at the Fukushima nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Japanese officials say there are currently no signs of any problems at the nation`s nuclear power plants, including the Shika nuclear plant, which is closest to the quake`s epicenter -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Ben Tracy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to rising tensions in the Middle East. President Biden was briefed by his national security team this morning about the escalating conflict between Iranian-backed rebels and the \uc2 United States Navy\uc2 . Meanwhile, Israel announced it`s withdrawing thousands of troops from Gaza, signaling a new phase in the fighting.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ian Lee reports, the war shows no sign of ending soon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (SIRENS BLARING)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE (voiceover): Sirens rang in the new year in Israel as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fired rockets. The country`s Iron Dome missile defense system illuminated the sky with interceptions.\par \par \uc2 But, in Gaza, 2024 looks a lot like the previous year. Men rescued an injured child, as the sound of explosions chased them in Khan Younis. But hospitals in Gaza are so full, doctors treat injured children on the floor. In Rafah, at this cramped tent city, everyone hopes the new year will bring peace.\par \par \uc2 (GIRL speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: While this 11-year-old simply states: "I wish not to die in 2024."\par \par \uc2 Israel has also experienced loss in this war. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  says more than 170 soldiers have been killed since the ground invasion in Gaza. On Sunday, Israel began demobilizing thousands of troops, the first drawdown since the war began with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` murderous attack in October.\par \par \uc2 It comes amid mounting pressure from the White House to scale back the offensive. But Israeli officials warn, the end of this war is still months away.\par \par \uc2 (REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: "We will allow the soldiers to gather strength for the next activity," says the Israeli military spokesman.\par \par \uc2 They could be needed on Israel`s northern border. Over the weekend, militants attacked from Syria and Lebanon. Israel retaliated, striking \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets. The threats to Israeli, as well as U.S. interests also extend to the Red Sea. Iran sailed a warship into the waters today, a day after the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  engaged four Houthi boats from Yemen that attacked a commercial ship, sinking three.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: And over the weekend, Tel Aviv once again saw protesters demand the resignation of Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, Major, he was dealt another blow. The country`s Supreme Court struck down a controversial law that would have limited the judge`s powers.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Ian Lee, thank you.\par \par \uc2 U.S. border officials processed an estimated 300,000 migrants in December. That is a record high.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Astrid Martinez explains how this influx of migrants is straining resources across the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MARTINEZ (voiceover): A new year and a new set of buses carrying migrants to various cities across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, 350 asylum seekers flown in from the Southern border boarded buses to Chicago after landing in Rockford, Illinois, their transportation north arranged by Texas Governor Greg Abbott. Chicago Mayor Brandon Johnson said on "Face the Nation," city officials were given no notice.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON (D-Mayor of Chicago, Illinois): The rogue buses that are being dropped off across this country in the middle of the night, leaving people with -- with no real support at all, no coordination with the local municipalities, that type of chaos is -- it`s certainly dividing our country.\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MARTINEZ: Chicago is just one city dealing with the influx and now cracking down on buses bringing in migrants without advance notice.\par \par \uc2 Last week, New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  issued an emergency order to restrict bus arrivals. But that hasn`t stopped busloads of migrants now being dropped off at train stations in New Jersey with easy access to the city. Adams says the migrant crisis will cost New York $12 billion by 2025.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the U.S. Border Patrol manages a record migrant surge. Agents are processing a quarter-million new arrivals each month, in December hitting an all-time high of 300,000 migrants.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-South Carolina): We`re full. Don`t come.\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MARTINEZ: CBS News has learned that the White House is open to immigration reforms, including an authority to expel migrants and suspend asylum law, in exchange for aid for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 : Start deporting people here who should be deported. Then you will turn things around pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MARTINEZ: And the state governor of Texas signed a new law that could go into effect in March that would allow state officials to arrest and prosecute migrants if they come into the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 However, the Justice Department said it would sue Texas if that law is implemented -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Astrid Martinez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight: COVID-19 cases are on the rise coast to coast, with the Northeast and Midwest seeing the biggest increases. The CDC reports hospitalizations are up nearly 17 percent. That`s based on the most recent week of data.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston is in Los Angeles County, where some health care facilities have reintroduced mask mandates.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): New year, new concerns about a spike in COVID cases.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELIQUE CAMPEN (Providence Saint Joseph Medical Center): Our volume has really surged. And I attribute that to not just COVID, but influenza, RSV. I`m not seeing as many people fully immunized as in past years for flu, for COVID boosters. And so it makes them much more vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The rise in infections has prompted at least five states to reinstate masking requirements at health care facilities, as a new variant spreads across the country.\par \par \uc2 Cases are up at least 25 percent in Los Angeles County, where emergency room physician Dr. Angelique Campen says she`s happy to see the mask mandate return.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELIQUE CAMPEN: It really helps to protect the vulnerable people that are sitting out there in the waiting room right now.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: The CDC says the new COVID vaccine is the best tool to stay protected against serious illness. But vaccination rates are at an all-time low. Less than 20 percent of adults have received the updated vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, there`s also a growing number of flu and RSV cases, with 31 states seeing high or very high levels of respiratory infections, leading to worries that a triple virus threat could strain hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that these numbers will continue to get worse?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANGELIQUE CAMPEN: We`re not at the peak yet this winter. I`m still seeing numbers increase right now. It seems like every other patient I`m seeing in the emergency department is one of these viruses.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Here in California, the first child flu death was recently announced.\par \par \uc2 Doctors say it`s not too late to get vaccinated for both the flu and COVID to help protect your family -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Elise Preston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A Colorado woman faces extradition to the U.S. after being arrested in England over the weekend on suspicion of murdering two of her children; 35- year-old Kimberlee Singler appeared in a London court today on charges of killing her 9-year-old daughter and 7-year-old son and attempting to kill her 11-year-old daughter nearly two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Singler disappeared after an arrest warrant was issued last week. Court records show she has been in a long-running custody battle with her ex- husband.\par \par \uc2 Officials in the San Francisco Bay Area are investigating a commuter train derailment today that sent at least nine people to hospitals. The train partially derailed near the city of Orinda, east of Oakland, causing at least two cars to catch fire.\par \par \uc2 As the nation welcomes in a new year, that means new laws nationwide, from minimum wage to new gun regulations.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans takes a look at the changes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): New year, new laws that include pay raises for millions of Americans; 25 states and dozens of municipalities plan to increase minimum wage this year.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Hello. May I help someone?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: That means at least $20 an hour for certain food service workers in California.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Who wouldn`t mind getting an extra $4 increase in their pay?\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And California companies also now cannot ask employees about using marijuana off the job. A win for workers in Alabama, they will no longer owe state taxes on overtime pay.\par \par \uc2 Guns are now banned from most public places in California. And Michigan has a host of new gun laws, including universal background checks.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BATES (End Gun Violence Michigan): These laws represent a sea change in Michigan`s gun safety regime.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Texas is targeting diversity, equity and inclusion programs, banning them from public colleges and universities, while restrictions in Kansas will prevent transgender people from changing their assigned sex on birth certificates.\par \par \uc2 POLICE OFFICER: Stop and pull over, please.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: New rules of the road in California require police to tell drivers why they`re being pulled over before they begin asking questions.\par \par \uc2 And, in Illinois, videoconferencing behind the wheel is banned, but hanging fuzzy dice on your mirror is now legal.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Seinfeld, West/Shapiro Productions)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: A new law for telemarketers in New Jersey includes providing a callback number.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Seinfeld, West/Shapiro Productions)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: It`s inspired by this scene from the `90s sitcom "Seinfeld."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Seinfeld, West/Shapiro Productions)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And another new law here in California, first through sixth graders will now be taught cursive writing in school, a lost art in the digital age -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I remember cursive, though altogether not so fondly.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 A new Harvard study found social media companies have made nearly $11 billion advertising to children and teens in the U.S. in 2022. And, according to one survey, social media is also the main source of news for 51 percent of teens. But misinformation there is widespread.\par \par \uc2 In a CBS News investigation, Tom Hanson looks at what`s being done to help teens find the truth.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON (voiceover): Behind the doors of Highland Park High School in Dallas, Texas --\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JACKSON (Teacher): Raise your hand if it`s real.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: -- this group of students is learning that seeing shouldn`t always be believing.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JACKSON: This is a fake.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Brandon Jackson leads this media literacy course, a counteroffensive to the information war playing out on social media.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JACKSON: Now, how do you know that it`s accurate?\par \par \uc2 ISABELLA SWEENEY (Student): Usually put it into a reverse image search.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Is there any part of you that`s like, if that`s fake, then what`s actually real?\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #1: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #3: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 STUDENT #4: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDY MILLER (Student): Yes, all the time. I thought I was smarter than that.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: As an experiment, we posed as three gender-neutral 17-year- olds on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and TikTok. We searched Israeli terms, Palestinian terms, and both. Each account also liked several posts.\par \par \uc2 Today, we`re going to see what the algorithm gives us back. Graphic videos became a pattern on the accounts.\par \par \uc2 It`s hard to watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @Mindovermatters23, TikTok)\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: As did misinformation, like this video reporting to show an Iranian warplane landing on an Israeli aircraft carrier and this debunked video --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @lilach_elgrably, TikTok)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I have to fix a fracture.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: -- of someone claiming to work in a Gaza hospital.\par \par \uc2 LAURA GELLER: If I am searching for information about this conflict, that still pops up.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Yeah. No matter if the video gets debunked, it gets reposted.\par \par \uc2 Dan Evon at the News Literacy Project says one way to determine fact from fiction is pre-bunking.\par \par \uc2 DAN EVON (News Literacy Project): Look for authenticity, look for the source, look for evidence, and to look for the context.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Tools these students believe more schools need.\par \par \uc2 MADDY MILLER: I hope that people in our generation start to want to become more educated about issues. And, like, it can just be really dangerous if we don`t seek out the real information.\par \par \uc2 TOM HANSON: Tom Hanson, CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: CBS News reached out to TikTok and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Both companies said their fact-checking teams are working to fight misinformation on their platforms.\par \par \uc2 There is important news tonight about infant formula for babies with allergies. What parents need to know -- that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Arson investigators are expected to arrive at the scene of a deadly house explosion in Michigan tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The home was blown to pieces on Saturday, killing four and injuring two. It happened in Northfield Township, about 45 miles west of Detroit. "The Detroit News" reports, debris from the blast was scattered across two acres and the explosion was heard miles away.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, an important consumer alert about infant formula and a recall. More than 675,000 cans of Nutramigen hypoallergenic powder are being recalled because of possible bacterial contamination. This formula is designed for infants allergic to cow`s milk. So far, no illnesses have been reported.\par \par \uc2 For more information about the batch numbers to look out for, please go to CBSNews.com.\par \par \uc2 A very lucky someone could kick off 2024 with an entirely new financial outlook. Tonight`s Powerball jackpot has climbed to an estimated $810 million. That`s the fifth largest grand prize in the history of the game. The cash payout, in case you`re curious, would be a little bit more than $408 million before taxes.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next with a look at a scholarship program aimed at getting new pilots in the air.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: In tonight`s Eye on America: easing the turbulence caused by the nation`s pilot shortage. This year alone, airlines will come up short by about 17,000 positions.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Kris Van Cleave shows us how \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is handing out millions of dollars in scholarships to get people off the ground and into the cockpit.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE HYLTON (\uc2 Boeing\uc2  Scholarship Recipient): (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Danielle Hylton spends a lot of her time in the air. Now the \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  flight attendant is taking the leap into the pilot seat.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE HYLTON: So, the first time I flew, I was 17 years old. A friend of mine who said she was into aviation and she was flying, and my mind was blown that a woman could fly planes. And, immediately, I wanted to fly planes.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Training to be an airline pilot takes years and can cost over $100,000. Flight school is typically not eligible for most student loans.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE HYLTON: I have had some delays. I have had major delays.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Some regional airlines are already experiencing a shortage of pilots. And plane maker \uc2 Boeing\uc2  estimates North America will need 127,000 new pilots by 2043.\par \par \uc2 Over the last three years, the company has given out $8.5 million in flight school scholarships.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BROOM (Vice President of Commercial Training Solutions, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 ): We need more women. And we need people to be able to look -- get on an airplane, just like I did this morning, and look left, see someone that looks like them, and go, I can do that, too. It`s something I can do.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Women make up about 9 percent of pilots, and women of color are less than 1 percent. This year, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  handed out 25 scholarships for $20,000 apiece, one of them to keep Hylton`s dream flying high.\par \par \uc2 How important is the scholarship in making it possible for you to be a pilot?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE HYLTON: It`s a huge deal. It has afforded me to take time off to get my flying done.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: And now, 15 years after her first flight, Hylton`s plan to be an airline pilot is a big step closer to taking off.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Kris Van Cleave, Hampton, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Some notable pieces of published work are now in the public domain.\par \par \uc2 We will tell you the big names next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Finally tonight: The start of the new year has brought some of the best known books, films and pieces of music into the public domain.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Walt Disney Productions\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That includes the earliest depiction of Mickey Mouse from the 1928 short film Steamboat Willie. \uc2 The Walt Disney Company\uc2  still retains copyrights over the modern version of Mickey Mouse, but people are now free to copy and share the 1928 character.\par \par \uc2 Other famous works now open to the public include the original German edition of "All Quiet on the Western Front" and Charlie Chaplin`s silent film "The Circus."\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. For Norah O`Donnell, I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Good night, and happy new year.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIV CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020240102ek1100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Deadly Jerusalem Shooting; Temporary Truce Extended; Henry Kissinger`s Legacy; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Lashes Out; Chocolate and Child Labor; President Biden`s Climate Change Record; Navigating Holiday Travel; A Little Help from His Friends; Lily Gladstone Live; CBS MORNINGS Deals, CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Chris Livesay, Holly  Williams, Margaret Brennan, Jo Ling Kent, Debora Patta, Vladimir Duthiers,  Ben Tracy, Anthony Mason \par \uc2 13,480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking News: Multiple people are killed when a gunman opened fire at a bus stop during rush hour on the outskirts of Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): We`re reminded yet again of the threat from terrorism in Israel and Israelis face every single day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The US works to further extend the truce between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as more hostages are released, including an American teacher.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Things are moving so she`ll soon be home.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has a message for advertisers jumping ship from X after he endorses antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK (X CEO): Go [bleep] yourself. Is that clear?\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 , one of the most influential and controversial foreign policy figures in American history has passed away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: King Charles starts now. Welcome, welcome.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The premiere of Gayle King and Charles Barkley`s new series, "King Charles" --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the name was --\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY: Stop it, Gayle. We love you, Gayle. We don`t fight on our first night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no. We`re not. But it is a great story.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: \uc2 Spotify\uc2  released its 2023 wrapped, which shows you which songs and artists you listened to the most this year,\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL ("Jimmy Kimmel Live" host): They also added all of the listening up from all of us and who is the top streaming artist on \uc2 Spotify\uc2  for the year 2023. Is it A. Taylor Swift? That`s it. It`s Taylor Swift. Yes. There`s no B and there`s no C or D. It is just A. Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is not kidding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I was going to say Jimmy Kimmel, you are absolutely correct. I thought he was going to say A. Taylor Swift; B. Taylor Swift; C. Taylor Swift. But it is the same thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is all Taylor Swift. In fact, she was my number one artist, a little preview of my "Talk of the Table" there. My top songs though, none of them involve Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to hearing that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we are going to begin with the news, breaking news, it is not good, out of the Middle East: A deadly attack outside of Jerusalem. At least three people dead, several more wounded after a gunman opened fire at a bus station during morning rush hour. It happened as talks to further extend this fragile truce between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  continue.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, another story we`re following, an American teacher among the latest batch of hostages to be freed as part of that agreement. So, we are covering all sides of these developing stories this morning, but we are going to begin with Chris Livesay, he has been gathering information about that attack. He`s in the West Bank city of Ramallah for us.\par \par \uc2 Chris, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 A fragile peace continues in Gaza amid a tenuous ceasefire there, but the bloodshed between Israelis and Palestinians continues here in the West Bank and in Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 And a warning, these images are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Gunshots)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Deadly shootings and shocking reminders of an entire region and snarled by violence. CCTV tape shows the moment \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist armed with an M-16 and a handgun opened fire on civilians waiting at this bus stop outside an entrance to Jerusalem according to Israeli Police.\par \par \uc2 Several were injured and at least three were killed. A 24-year-old woman, another in her 60s, and a 73-year-old man. Both of the shooters had a record of terrorism and were shot dead on the scene, police say.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: "Two soldiers and a civilian near the stop reacted quickly and neutralized the attackers," says the Jerusalem police chief, all of it following another deadly encounter. This time in the Palestinian refugee camp of Jenin.\par \par \uc2 Here in the West Bank, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says it killed two senior terror operatives in a raid late Tuesday. But the Palestinian Health Ministry says two boys ages 15 and eight were also killed in the ensuing clashes yesterday morning, the youngest reportedly showed here on CCTV footage the moment he is shot.\par \par \uc2 According to two aid organizations, Israeli forces temporarily cut off two hospitals from receiving or accessing any of the injured.\par \par \uc2 Here in Ramallah, locals raising awareness for Palestinian victims amid a scheduled visit by US Secretary of State, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now since the October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack, Israel says it has detained 2,000 suspects here in the West Bank, more than 1,000 of them affiliated with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Chris Livesay, Ramallah.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was Chris Livesay in Ramallah.\par \par \uc2 Although dozens of hostages have been released, many more remain in Gaza. Israel is demanding the return of all of those who have been taken, but a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader who spoke with Holly Williams says that may not be possible.\par \par \uc2 Holly is in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Ninety-seven hostages have now been released since the temporary ceasefire began on Friday, but I`ve been speaking with a senior \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader who claimed some of the hostages are dead.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD (\uc2 Hamas\uc2 ): We are ready to give all evidences --\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ghazi Hamid told us \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is willing to provide evidence that three hostages, four-year-old Ariel Bibas, his 10-month-old brother, Kfir, and their mother, Shiri were all killed in the Gaza Strip by an Israeli airstrike.\par \par \uc2 Israel says it is assessing whether that claim is true.\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD: They paid the price because of the occupation.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But that was a 10-month-old baby and a four-year-old boy.\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD: They have to impose pressure in their government to tell them that you pushed us to the hell.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But Dr. Hamad, a 10-month-old baby and a four-year-old boy can`t put pressure on the Israeli government. Why did they have to pay for the Israeli occupation?\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD: The Israelis, they have a bigger problem. That they occupy the Palestinian people. They have to exert pressure in Israel, their government, in order to tell them that you are going in the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Last night, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  handed over another 16 hostages, including an American, Liat Beinin Atzili, a mother of three whose husband remains in captivity.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Israel held up its end of the bargain by releasing 30 Palestinian prisoners bringing the total to over 200.\par \par \uc2 Hamad told us \uc2 Hamas\uc2  wants a permanent ceasefire to end the suffering of Palestinians in Gaza, where \uc2 Hamas\uc2  officials say over 15,000 have been killed by Israeli airstrikes and fighting.\par \par \uc2 So what would it take for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to hand over all the remaining hostages?\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD: We can have a comprehensive deal that we can release all the prisoners, the Palestinians in detention. At the same time, we can release all the hostages.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: There were thought to be around 6,000 Palestinians in Israel`s prisons and by Israel`s count, around 150 hostages are still being held in the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 How many hostages do you have left in your hands that are still alive?\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: You don`t know?\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD: The number is not so important.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But what do you mean the number isn`t important? People in Israel want to know whether their loved ones are still alive or not.\par \par \uc2 GHAZI HAMAD: We have released 70 people now, and we are continuing to release the civilians.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: We`re expecting more hostages to come out tonight, but we do not know if this ceasefire will extend beyond 7:00 AM local time tomorrow -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Holly, it`s unbelievable, that exchange you had with him. Yesterday, you reported about the little children, the 10-month-old and the four-year-old with the red hair and I was so hoping, hoping against hope that maybe they were still alive.\par \par \uc2 It makes it clear and listening to him -- and he doesn`t seem to have any regard for the human life. He uses hostages like they`re not even that -- like they`re not even human beings. Were you struck by that or that`s just the way it is in wartime?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Look, it was quite a combative interview, Gayle, but I want to make it clear that we`ve had no official confirmation that those members of the Bibas family have in fact died.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. All right. We will still hold out hope then.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams reporting from Tel Aviv, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s president and other world leaders are paying tribute to former US Secretary of State, that`s \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 , who died yesterday. He was 100 years old.\par \par \uc2 Kissinger led US foreign policy through many critical moments in the 1970s and continued advising US and foreign governments for the rest of his life. People sought out his advice.\par \par \uc2 He received the Nobel Peace Prize, but many critics called him a war criminal.\par \par \uc2 Margaret Brennan takes a look at his very complicated legacy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2  first arrived on US shores as a Jewish refugee from Nazi Germany. Following a brief \uc2 US Army\uc2  stint, he became a Harvard scholar, which paved his way to the highest echelons of power.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: "Face the Nation" --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In 1958, Kissinger appeared on "Face the Nation" to discuss the issue that would dominate his career, the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 HARRY KISSINGER (Former US Secretary of State): Over the long term, that Russia is much more likely to be a threat to its security than we are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He caught the eye of President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 , who appointed him National Security adviser in 1969, as well as Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 Kissinger eased relations with the Soviet Union and led secret talks to reopen relations with China after a two-decade estrangement.\par \par \uc2 In a "60 Minutes" interview, Kissinger spoke about advising the most powerful man in the world.\par \par \uc2 HENRY KISSINGER\uc2 : When you`re in this job, you`re not conscious of working with the most powerful man in the world, you`re much more conscious of the problems that have to be solved than of the power you exercise.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Kissinger also spearheaded highly controversial policies, including the brutal bombing of Cambodia, yet was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1973 for helping to extricate the US from the Vietnam War.\par \par \uc2 HENRY KISSINGER\uc2 : Well, this meeting didn`t take very long.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He remained loyal to Nixon throughout the Watergate scandal, and was one of the few inner circle members to escape virtually unscathed and served under President Ford.\par \par \uc2 Kissinger`s pragmatic policies, including support of dictatorships in Latin America are darker parts of his legacy. Yet, US presidents continued to seek his counsel across nearly five decades.\par \par \uc2 In an interview on "Face The Nation" in 2014, Kissinger argued for American leadership.\par \par \uc2 HENRY KISSINGER\uc2 : Every part of the world is changing simultaneously, but it cannot change creatively without a major American contribution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Amid global power shifts, Kissinger`s own views were subject to change. In recent years, he controversially suggested Ukraine cede territory to Russia, only to then call for Ukraine to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This year, he visited a rising China one last time counseling President Xi Jinping amid cooling relations with the US.\par \par \uc2 Henry Kissinger`s strategic decisions will continue to shape the globe for generations to come.\par \par \uc2 Margaret Brennan, CBS News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joining us now to talk about Kissinger`s legacy and his continued influence on American policy even today is presidential historian and author, \uc2 Douglas Brinkley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Professor Brinkley, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 I want to get to how Kissinger continues to influence both the terms and the strategy of American foreign policy right now up to this minute, but also first address this dual legacy hated by some, celebrated by others, and sometimes for the very same action around the same instant. Could you explain these two sides to Henry Kissinger`s legacy?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY (Presidential historian): Well, absolutely. And we all want to send our condolences to his wife, Nancy, and to his two children. You know, they are obviously mourning.\par \par \uc2 Look, \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 , he has more enemies than you can count. It comes out of the academic left. Many people blamed him for the continuation of the war in Vietnam, and the expanding of the war into Cambodia and Laos. The anti-war campuses were filled with anti-Kissinger rhetoric, add to that the fact that Kissinger had a bad anti-democratic record dealing with countries like Chile. Writers like Christopher Hitchens would, you know, put Kissinger on trial.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, you can`t study diplomacy in the United States without grappling with \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 . He invented really, the modern concept of realism or with a hyper pragmatism if you`d like in foreign affairs, realpolitik, as it was called.\par \par \uc2 He was a great believer in superpowers that the United States had to be the most powerful country in the world, and he invented terms we just use, like shuttle diplomacy, like we need somebody now to do shuttle diplomacy in what is going on in Israel and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 , who really orchestrated the biggest breakthrough imaginable going to China with Nixon in 1972, and opening up relations between the two countries and in, you know, the Yom Kippur War, you know, Kissinger is the one really getting a peace settlement out of that. He won a Nobel Prize, so it is a duality to \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There was a duality, Douglas, and I know we`ve all heard about the professional side, but I`m curious about the personal side. Before he married Nancy, he was considered quite the ladies` man back in the day and I was always very fascinated by that.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you talk about that side of \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY: Absolutely. He became a pop sensation. He`d love to flirt with pretty women at cocktail parties. He had that Teutonic accent to where he just would say hello, and people would be enraptured by him.\par \par \uc2 They became, you know, rumors that he was seeing Hollywood starlets and the like. Magazines would put him on a Super K almost like a Superman like figure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY: And so he cut quite a large swath of our culture in the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, he called himself a secret swinger and that power was "the ultimate aphrodisiac."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Aphrodisiac. Yes. Hello, Douglas, is that when you have to say, just say hello. Thank you, \uc2 Douglas Brinkley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, some extremely blunt language from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and blunt is the word here. How the billionaire lashed out at his critics in a blistering tirade in the controversy over his endorsement of an antisemitic post.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:15, exactly. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up: CBS News investigates chocolate that comes from West Africa. Debora Patta shows us how cocoa is harvested by children and the big questions over efforts by America`s largest chocolate maker to end child labor.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up in a bit. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the world`s wealthiest man has a message for companies who don`t want to advertise on X, and I can`t repeat it on morning television, or else this will be my last day.\par \par \uc2 Now, it all came out in a conversation with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " when asked about companies who dropped advertising after Musk endorsed an antisemitic post.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent has more on all of this.\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT: Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It was a jaw-dropping moment on stage at the New York Times DealBook Summit. Right after \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  expressed regret for endorsing an antisemitic tweet, he then stunned the audience with his answer to a question about fleeing advertisers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN (New York Times Dealbook Summit): There was advertisers leaving. We talked to \uc2 