he must know that.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Debora Patta on --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. It is 7:30.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News investigation has revealed very troubling information about how we get our chocolate, in particular Mars products like M&Ms and Snickers that are some of America`s favorites. Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 Debra Patta traveled to Ghana for this story and found children some as young as -- listen to this -- five years old that were harvesting cocoa.\par \par \uc2 Debora is here with us now in the studio.\par \par \uc2 Debora, it is good to have you in person. Good to see you. I am sorry it is for a story like this.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: A very good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, Mars did over $45 million in annual sales last year in large part from selling chocolates like those M&Ms and Snickers, it vowed to have systems in place to eradicate child labor from its supply chain by 2025, but we saw little evidence of this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Laboring in the blistering heat here was young as six, slicing the grass with lethal ease, their machetes nearly half the size of the smallest among them.\par \par \uc2 These Ghanaian children are harvesting the cocoa that ends up in America`s best love chocolates like M&Ms and Snickers. Instead of going to school, they are learning that sharp blades cuts deep and big corporations make promises they seldom keep.\par \par \uc2 We traveled across Ghana`s remote cocoa belt, visiting small subsistence farms that supply US chocolate giant, Mars and found children working on every one of them.\par \par \uc2 Mars boasts about rescuing thousands of children who are listed as beneficiaries of what it calls their robust monitoring system that keeps them off plantations and in schools.\par \par \uc2 CBS News obtained copies of these lists from a whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to try and find some of the children on these lists and see if the information checks out.\par \par \uc2 Our first stop, 15-year-old, Manira (sp?).\par \par \uc2 And your name I think is on this list. Is that you?\par \par \uc2 Here she is toiling away on her family farm, her life since she was just five years old.\par \par \uc2 School is a luxury, she has hardly ever been.\par \par \uc2 MANIRA (Fifteen years old): I feel sad. I want to be a like medical doctor, but they don`t -- they don`t have money to support me.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: The family harvested only one bag of cocoa the entire year. A 140-pound sack fetches around $115.00.\par \par \uc2 Last year, field supervisors contracted by Mars --\par \par \uc2 And this is all they gave you?\par \par \uc2 Gave Manira a backpack and schoolbooks with the slogan "I am a child, I play, I go to school." In the nearly 18 months since then, nobody has checked to see if she is still in school.\par \par \uc2 This cocoa field supervisor for the past 13 years spoke out on condition we hide his identity.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Personally, I`ve made lists before. I`ve made up lists before. And I can say on authority that almost every data, almost every data is cooked.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Or incorrect.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Or is not correct. Nobody has come back to check as to whether it`s true or not.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: CBS News spoke to nearly a dozen children on that list used by Mars. None of them were in school nor had they been regularly monitored that to ensure they attended classes.\par \par \uc2 No one came here ever?\par \par \uc2 The few children that do go to school, instead of pencils are carrying machetes.\par \par \uc2 Put your hands up if you work on the cocoa farms.\par \par \uc2 All of these students told us they harvest