ehind an effort that would eradicate the problem?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Frankly, Tony, not a lot. If they spend some of the money that they use to fight lawsuits, this isn`t the first one, $200 million could be going a long way to changing it. One mother said, all we need is bicycles so our kids can cycle to school, it`s an hour away. They can`t afford that. And more importantly, pay the farmers more for their cocoa.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure consumers would be thrilled.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I`ve heard --\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: To know their money is going to help kids go to school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, fighting the lawsuits, I hear they`re quite litigious. But I`m sitting here with two packs of M&Ms in my purse right now, even as we speak.\par \par \uc2 Now that we see a story like this, what should we do with this information?\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Well, I think what we need to do is pressurize these cocoa companies who signed a declaration more than 20 years ago to eradicate child labor to actually do it. This is a fixable problem.\par \par \uc2 We don`t often go to places where we feel that, but this is. Use that money, build schools. Get these kids an education.\par \par \uc2 Look at the potential there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Debora, that was eye-opening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very eye-opening. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And disturbing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great to have you here on set as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very disturbing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First in a long time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good to see you in studio.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead in just a moment, the US is producing more oil and natural gas than ever before while the Biden administration promotes cleaner energy.\par \par \uc2 Ben Tracy shows us why all that`s happening.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The world`s largest climate summit began today in the United Arab Emirates at a moment when this is true, 2023 set to be the hottest year ever recorded on Planet Earth. It is also a year when the United States broke records in oil and gas production thanks in part to the Biden administration`s policies.\par \par \uc2 As senior national environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy shows us, the president`s climate response is at best, complicated.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In Colorado, Wednesday, President Biden visited the largest manufacturer of wind towers in the world.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : What`s your name?\par \par \uc2 EDDY (Employee): Eddy.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: Eddy, I`m Joe. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Touting record levels of federal funding for clean energy projects across the country.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Building a clean energy future, made in America.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: But he didn`t mention that the US is also producing record amounts of crude oil. Planet warming emissions from fossil fuels are the main driver of climate change. 2023 is expected to be the planet`s hottest year on record.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Drill baby drill.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Compared to former President Trump, Mr. Biden actually approved more permits for oil and gas drilling on public lands in his first two years in office, and the US is now the largest oil and gas producer in the world.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MANN (Climate scientist and "Our Fragile Moment" author): We can`t be building new fossil fuel infrastructure --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Michael Mann is a climate scientist and author of "Our Fragile Moment."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MANN: We will see the missions come to a peak this year. They will no longer climb as they have in the past. The bad news is reaching a plateau isn`t good enough, those emissions have to come down to zero and they have to do that pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And so those emissions are not going to go down like that if we continue to burn fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MANN: That`s right. We need to literally bring carbon emissions down to zero in a matter of decades.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Phasing out fossil fuels is a heated debate at the \uc2 UN\uc2  Climate Summit in Dubai. Many climate activists fear the process has been compromised by being held in the oil rich UAE and presided over by the head of its state-owned oil company.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MANN: It looks bad, it smells bad, and it`s probably bad.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: But he hopes world leaders will rise to the occasion and cut planet warming emissions before it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, the next step for \uc2 NFL\uc2  superstar, Aaron Rodgers recovering from an injury that should have ended his season. My dude is already back on the field. We`ll talk about that in a bit, but right now, it is 743, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got some very talented people in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 Actor, Lily Gladstone has just arrived. She will be here in the studio to talk about her breakout role in the movie "Killers of the Flower Moon." She says, she almost quit acting before Martin Scorsese called her.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Killers of the Flower Moon.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The character, well, changed her life.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we all know Anthony Mason is fantastic in all things. You know, he is a great storyteller. Now, he is adding songwriter to his resume. In our "Never Too Late" series, he teamed up with Grammy-winning expert and 