uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Physical talent, and putting that in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Nate, does it make sense to even take this kind of risk to re-injure yourself --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For him --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Three games?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For three games, and you know, it doesn`t seem like they have a shot at the playoffs, right, at this particular point?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I don`t think the Jets are going to make the playoffs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, does it make sense to take this kind of risk?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It makes sense for Aaron Rodgers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because he has always walked by the beat of his own drum. He has an ego that has propelled him to be one of the best to ever play this game, and I think he`s leading with that.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the thing. It is something kind of sick with athletes that we do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Guys who come back from injuries faster, we praise them. I remember Adrian Peterson, a running back, he tore his ACL, came back in like five months. We`re all like, oh, man, this guy`s an alien, which is kind of sick and twisted.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now Aaron Rodgers is doing the same thing. If this does work, he will get praised by athletes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But if it doesn`t?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . because it is superhuman.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If he doesn`t, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It could injure him permanently.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but that`s the risk you take.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Man, it is like kind of crazy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a great gamble, it`s classic. Classic man thing as well. Like I got this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What were you saying about trying to lift something?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. It`s like me trying to take something in the garage and it`s heavy and someone says, do you need a hand? No, I got it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I got it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what Aaron Rodgers saying to the world, I got it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think he should be glad I`m not his girlfriend because he would not be coming back and maybe that`s why I am not his girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Would you like to be though, Gayle? I am sure Nate could hook you up. I mean, he has talked about it before.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Going a little vanilla? I`m just saying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am not saying no.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve got to get to this next story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A little shaker.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t say to vanilla.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the show between the show, this hookup.\par \par \uc2 Also, also in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , an amazing touchdown celebration last month that went viral has brought about a suspension. Here`s what happened.\par \par \uc2 Miami`s Tyreke Hill grabbed the phone of an \uc2 NFL\uc2  cameraman then did a backflip while taking a selfie. Here it is. Watch this, right, takes the phone. Does this backflip.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CBS/\uc2 NFL\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Here is the thing though. This is against the rules. Players are not allowed to use props during touchdown celebrations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, come on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So guess who got suspended?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The cameraman.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know, because people credentialed with the No Fun League are not allowed to celebrate or essentially be a fan on the job.\par \par \uc2 This young man, his name is 20-year-old Kevin Fitzgibbons who says he was suspended from his dream job for the rest of the season. That`s not good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s not fair.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fitzgibbon says he understands the NFL`s position. But that he and Hill are close friends.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, was it a pre-approved -- he can`t undo it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No. it was not planned at all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, it wasn`t planned.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He got the touchdown and he just took the phone from him. Did the backflip.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on, \uc2 NFL\uc2 . Do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that`s not --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hire Kevin back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Give his job back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That wasn`t his fault.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on, Roger Goodell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on, Rog.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on, Roger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you, Vlad. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, actor, Lily Gladstone. She has been called the heart and soul of Martin Scorsese`s "Killer of the Flower Moon." She`s here to talk about the film`s message and how does it feel to get Oscar buzz.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Thanksgiving travel was the busiest ever. Could it be a sign of things to come?\par \par \uc2 Well, we`ll talk to the CEO of \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  about that, and also what`s up with the industry`s rising bag and seat fees?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Anthony Mason loves to profile songwriters, but he`s never actually written a song. Hear what he comes up with in our series, "Never Too Late."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know it is going to be good. We all know this. But first use today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Breaking News: Multiple people are killed in an attack outside Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More hostages and prisoners are released amid efforts to further extend the temporary ceasefire between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: We`re expecting more hostages to come out tonight, but we do not know if the ceasefire will extend beyond 7:00 AM local time tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Former Secretary of State, \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2  dies at 100, leaving a complicated legacy.\par \par \uc2 HENRY KISSINGER\uc2 : Every part of the world is changing simultaneously, but it cannot change creatively without a major American contribution.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2 , that`s Sir Paul to you, puts on a surprise last minute concert for the fans in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nothing better than a good concert, but we`re going to begin this hour with what you can expect in the sky now that the holiday travel season is underway. Maybe you`re flying to a concert.\par \par \uc2 The TSA says that a recorded -- get this -- the busiest day ever on Sunday afternoon, this was the Sunday after Thanksgiving -- busiest travel day ever. Nearly three million passengers took flights and were screened by the TSA.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  says it welcomed more than four-and-a-half million travelers on United alone during the holiday week. That is up nine percent from the same time last year.\par \par \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2  is the CEO of \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , and he joins us now from Houston, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kirby, good to see you. Thank you for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 I want to begin with two timely bits of information. One, the new airplanes United is announcing about, 230 of them coming online.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2  (\uc2 United Airlines\uc2  CEO): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to hear about that, but I also want to hear about this letter that you received from the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , a subcommittee reviewing a steep multibillion dollar rise in seat fees and baggage fees. So take it away, sir.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : Sure, thank you. And thanks for having me today.\par \par \uc2 I`ll start with the new airplanes. We are starting with -- we are announcing today our first flight on A321neo, which is all of our new airplanes are coming with seatback entertainment, huge screens, big enough bins for every customer on the airplane, to put their bag on board, Bluetooth WiFi. They are great.\par \par \uc2 I flew on one of them last night actually, and two-thirds of the customers were watching TV. "Mission Impossible" seemed to be the most popular show on the TV, but we`re really, really excited about what that means for customers.\par \par \uc2 And as to the letter that we got, you know, at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , we`ve actually been doing the opposite. We`ve been reducing fees. And the biggest change that I think has happened from a fee perspective was United at the beginning of the pandemic, permanently eliminating change fees for customers and it`s led to great, you know, NPS scores really, really improvement for our customers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Mr. Kirby, your fees are going down, you`re saying. The letter here from the Senate, they`re looking for information on that very subject and just to give people a sense of the numbers, what they`re experiencing at the airport overall from the industry, baggage fees up nearly $2 billion in the last four years and in 2022 alone, the industry taking in $4 billion not from change fees but from seat selection fees. Is that not going on at United?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : So -- well, at United, our fees for baggage, take baggage for example, haven`t changed in years, though it is more volume, so the total dollar amount goes up.\par \par \uc2 There are some other airlines that that have added up lot of fees, some of which I think are egregious and -- but we all got the same letter. But at United, you know we`ve focused on being transparent with customers on any kind of fees, but also eliminating those fees like change fees that really were the biggest source of friction and complaints that we had from customers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: United also implemented a new boarding system.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s for economy passengers with window seats that they board before people with middle and aisle seats. What prompted that decision?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : So we`re constantly trying to make things better for customers. In fact, you talked about Thanksgiving was really strong for United and the industry.\par \par \uc2 We had the best NPS scores we`ve ever had in our history over the weekend. But one of them, you know, one of the things that we did, which just kind of normal course of business for us is changing the boarding process to make it a little more efficient, make it go faster, less conflict with customers on airplane.\par \par \uc2 To me, the remarkable thing about that is we really thought of that as normal course of business and it turns out, because air travel is such an impactful moment for people. It is taking them to their biggest and most important moments in their lives, that everything about air travel, you know, is magnified, and is meaningful to customers and to people and it is a newsworthy story, and so we`re excited about that.\par \par \uc2 But really, it`s just a recognition of what we`re trying to do with that, or with the new airplanes, the new terminal that we`re building here in Houston today and announcing here in Houston today. All of that is about trying to improve the customer experience and make travel something that`s a part of your journey, your trip that is good, and it really changed what has been like in the past.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all, Scott, want a good travel experience. You know, I`m a big, United frequent flyer. The best thing is you board it from the back and then come to the first, but people in first class would probably start squawking, you know, how those people are.\par \par \uc2 But anyway, I know.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : Well, thank you for flying us, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, who you talking about there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I know. I know. But Scott, here`s a question. We recently did a story about pilots and their mental health and that many pilots, we`ve had a couple of incidents -- very scary -- involving pilots. That pilots are not seeking mental health treatment because they`re worried about losing their jobs, which is very scary as a passenger, because we rely on the crew, as you know, really for our lives.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s being done to help the mental health of your pilots?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : Yes, so we spend a lot of time on all health related things for pilots, and also on training. You know, I don`t think people realize that our pilots at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , at least, every nine months, go back to our training facility in Denver to train on simulators and to do in a simulator things that will never happen, hopefully, to them in the real world so that they are prepared for anything that happens.\par \par \uc2 And at least at United, you know, we have all kinds of policies in place where people can if it`s -- whether it`s a mental health or substance abuse, anything that`s going on in their lives, illness, even fatigue, that they can call off and not come to work without penalties, without repercussions, and they have really good protections to ensure that that doesn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 So I feel very good about what we do and the time that we spend for all of our employees, making sure that all their health, including their mental health is in a good place when they`re going on to get on an airplane and make sure it`s safe for our customers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate it, Mr. Kirby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always, Scott. Yes, we do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We really do.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My only request is that you put TVs on all the planes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If we could get live television.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : We are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s my only request.\par \par \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 : Live TV is a little harder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You`ve got one of my favorite names. Scott was my dad`s name, Kirby is my favorite daughter`s name, so I`ll never forget you. Thank you, \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2 . Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Time now to check their local weather. It`s 8:09.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Our Anthony Mason goes from acclaimed journalist to songwriter.\par \par \uc2 In the latest edition of our series, it`s "Never Too Late," ahead, hear the song that Anthony wrote with the help of his Grammy Award winning pals.\par \par \uc2 This CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In our series, "Never Too Late," we bring you stories that prove it`s never too late to try something new. That is so true.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Mason has spent decades interviewing some of the world`s greatest musicians about the art of songwriting. This morning, Anthony shows us what happened when he set out to write his own song, oh, he got a little help from two Grammy winning friends in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I don`t even know how to start.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY (Singer-songwriter): Well, you know, especially since it`s going to be your first song you ever write.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: It should be something very personal.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Natalie Hemby has co-written hits with Kacey Musgraves, Miranda Lambert and her own group, The High Women.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: This is a great rule of thumb when you`re writing.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: You`ve just got to dare to suck.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We met in Mike Elizondo`s studio outside Nashville.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO (Singer-songwriter): I printed out the ideas that you --\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: You`re so kind.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Mike who has worked with Dr. Dre, Eminem, and Carrie Underwood, passed out some song ideas I`d scribbled down and we quickly settled on a theme.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: This idea of sort of like a love letter to the sea or a love letter to the ocean.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: I had like a little melody. My soul is in the city, my feet are on the sand, but my heart belongs to the sea.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s real pretty.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: That`s really cool\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Natalie and Mike began massaging the melody.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: Okay, I gotcha. Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: Also if you don`t like something, tree of trust.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: We don`t care, it will not hurt our feelings.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: Never.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s what I should be saying to you.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We started by building a chorus.\par \par \uc2 There`s a slight tension in the fact that you live in the city, but you`re pulled to the sea, which is the way I feel.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: But it`s the sea that keeps pulling me.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: Yes, yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: You can be like, the city is in my bloodline.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, I was born in the city.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: I was born in the city. Sometimes I do that too. Like, you`ll say a lot. And I`ll just sing it out.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: Yes, yes, yes, right.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: I`m part of all its rhythm, magnetic energy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`m wrapped up in its rhythms.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: Yes, I like that, too.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: Would it be too weird to say like this -- this love affair runs deep.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Not necessarily.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: I love that. I think that`s gorgeous. To me, that`s what great songwriting is like.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: It`s poetic, it is personal, but it is also universal.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: You know? I remember the shows, patterns in the sand.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: You said that earlier I thought that was so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Different verse, but there`s something also about the beach after a hurricane, the sea after a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: Just like the calm after a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We`re really good through a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: Yes, that`s the last two lines, really?\par \par \uc2 MIKE ELIZONDO: The last two lines.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But they proved the most elusive.\par \par \uc2 NATALIE HEMBY: It`s