ey, Gayle. It`s Shaq here from Atlanta. I have a question. I`m just wondering why a smart, sophisticated person like yourself would agree to sit next to a dummy like Chuck all alone for an hour every week. Anyway, let me know, Gayle. Love you. Bye- bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love you, too, Shaq.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I said that`s Shaq`s way of saying Charles, good luck. I love you so much and I`m really cheering you on. That`s why he was --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Immediately, Charles starts shaking his head.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When I saw that moment, Charles` response was, Shaq has got a lot of time on his hands. He only works one day a week, so he`s got time to call in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly. I know. That was a nice surprise. We didn`t know that Shaq was doing that for us. So thank you, Shaq.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, make sure you check out "King Charles."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. Next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a pint-sized math genius taking the internet by storm. But before we play the tape, get your ears adjusted because he`s only two years old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`ll just take a second or two to lock into what Devan is saying, but he`s knockout. Here you go.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from DevanLovesNumbers/TikTok.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What I like is that he`s two years old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he can do the problems, but what attracted my attention, he`ll go eight times two "ekes." Three plus three "ekes." So he can divide. He can multiply. He can add. He can subtract.\par \par \uc2 His parents noticed when he was months old, that he was very good that he liked numbers. His first word was seven and he started working on writing numbers on a tablet when he was just one years old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I fell in love with this little kid. You should see him on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . It`s called Devan by Numbers or Alison, is it Devon by Numbers?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Devan Loves Numbers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Devan Loves Numbers. D-E-V-A-N. I highly, highly, highly recommend.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He does math like my two -- or he talks like my two-year- old, but does math like my 14-year-old. Pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is fun. He`s fun.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest has been -- she is good at numbers, too, I`ll bet. Our next guest has been described as the beating heart of one of this year`s most highly rated movies. Have you seen it yet? Martin Scorsese`s "Killers of the Flower Moon."\par \par \uc2 It tells the true story. This is the thing, this is a true story. The manipulation and murders of members of the Osage Native-American tribe in the early 1920s over their oil rich land.\par \par \uc2 Her name is Lily Gladstone remember this?\par \par \uc2 She plays Mollie Burkhart, the wealthy wife of Leonardo DiCaprio`s character, his name is Ernest. He`s a war veteran who takes part in the murders and in this clip, Mollie learns yet another of her loved ones has died a mysterious deaths.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Killers of the Flower Moon.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he does know. He is lying to you, Mollie. He is lying.\par \par \uc2 Gladstone`s work as Mollie has received rave reviews and is getting major, major Oscar buzz. "Killers of the Flower Moon" is distributed by \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , part of CBS parent company and we are very happy to say that Lily Gladstone is here in the studio.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Lily.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE ("Killers of the Flower Moon" actor): Hi, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All I can say about this is Lily is going to the Oscars. Lily is going to the Oscars, Lily is going to the Oscars, and I can`t wait. Golden Globes, too.\par \par \uc2 So what are you thinking about this? How are you processing all of this? You`re an actress, of course. You`re working with Martin Scorsese and Leonardo DiCaprio and people are talking about you in this movie. I want to know what you`re feeling, what you`re thinking.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: I`m just grateful that if I`m getting attention, I know it`s because people care about Mollie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: I mean, like you said, the beating heart of the film that is about these murders. And the story has been done before. Jimmy Stewart was in "The FBI Story," which was funded by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and it focused on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and this case was the formation of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as we know it. So historically, it`s very relevant.\par \par \uc2 But you know, the fact that we care about Mollie so much, we care about her sisters, her family, her nation. That just tells me audiences are drawn to where we were hoping.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You care about that, but I care about you working with the two of them and what that means to you at this point in your career? Because at one point, you were frustrated with your career thinking maybe, is this the right thing for me to do?\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Should I continue doing this? That`s what I want to know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we -- I read that you might be -- you might actually take a data analytics course.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A very different life.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: I mean, I was great at math when I was a kid, not like the little boy that we just saw, but --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: I was, you know, I was encouraged by my math teacher in high school. And, you know, I liked -- I kind of like, there was a bit of a storytelling element to it, all of that. But yes, I think a lot of actors feel the same way at some point in their career, and especially being a native woman. I`m selective about the roles that I want to do.\par \par \uc2 So the ones that I`m able to do and plus the ones I think I`m well suited for, don`t come by that often. Once every year, every couple of years was the pattern, and this was also coming out of COVID. I was a caretaker for my grandmother late in her life, and you know, my -- just spending time with my parents through quarantine, all of it, I was terrified of bringing something home to them.\par \par \uc2 Data analytics and studying really -- I live in northern Washington. This is where the --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, now, you`re in the rocking chair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m always fascinated by how a role can impact someone, and you`ve said that this role has changed you, not as someone who is a performer, but as a human. How did it change you?\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: I feel like when you`re telling stories like this that are about human suffering, but also human resilience --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Killers of the Flower Moon.")\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: That was something that we really tried to focus on that I think has moved me so much. And I mean, just surely, I came in as an outsider, I`m Blackfeet and Nez Perce, but I`m not Osage.\par \par \uc2 We all have somewhat similar histories in regards to, you know, federal policy and you know this, but the reign of terror is very specifically Osage.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I came in with wanting to handle it the way you would expect somebody to come in and tell the story about my great grandmother and now, I have a whole other community, you know. Some of the people that I love most in this world are Osage that I`ve met during this process that let me into their family, their lives, their stories, their history. And, you know, it`s part of doing a project like this that you can`t just cut and run.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And most of us didn`t even know this story.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Most of us didn`t even know this story. That`s why I`m so grateful for the movie, because I learned so much and what does this mean to you now putting it out in the world for people to know this?\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: I think it`s a wonderful opportunity for people to see natives in a way that we haven`t. You know, it is a hundred years ago, but really, it`s a modern story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, oh yes.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: It`s like you see these images that are really exciting, like native people driving around in Rolls Royces.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Killers of the Flower Moon.")\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: You know, it`s -- so it changes our image, something that`s more real in that way. But also, it shows people that we`re still here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: You know, ending the film -- ending this little glimpse into one nation because we all have, all of our tribal nations have some big adversity, some big attempt to eradicate us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is a common thread.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . of pain.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: And yet, we`re still here. We continue forward together. And --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re still here and I`m so glad you`re telling this story. Do you want to go the Oscars, Lily Gladstone?\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Oh, sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, she`s not going to take you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, no, she`s not taking me. I think her parents will probably trump me, but I`m very happy for you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, we all are.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are cheering you on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I know you`re going to the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And by the way, you`re going to the Golden Globes, too. Cheering you on, Lily Gladstone.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We all -- yes.\par \par \uc2 LILY GLADSTONE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Killers of the Flower Moon" is in theaters right now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up next, more of our 12 Days of Gifting. Gayle Bass will show us the heated back wrap she is wearing right there at an exclusive low price. She can move in it and she can dance in it, but first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Time now for CBS MORNINGS Deals powered by \uc2 Shopify\uc2 . It`s Day 4 of our special 12 Days of Gifting just in time for the holidays where we are showing some items with exclusive discounts up to 70 percent off the retail price.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping now, scan the QR code on your screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 lifestyle host, Gayle Bass is here back in the house. All right, so we have a toy that can keep the family entertained.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS (Lifestyle host): Yes, 12 days of gifting. We`ve got great gift ideas and for the kiddos, your kids will stay off their devices and start playtime with endless hours of fun with Zip Linx.\par \par \uc2 It`s a chain reaction game, it`s like domino, but better.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Watch this. I want you to do the countdown. Get ready for the fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh. I was not expecting that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You`re right. Nobody is. It`s great. Zip Linx are like dominoes, except here`s the thing, they don`t trip when you bump into them. You know, when you do dominoes and somebody walks through and they all go down?\par \par \uc2 NATE B