ar \par \uc2 Gayle, you`re the best.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More Anthony Mason.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a perfect way to end. We were just sitting here talking about Anthony, as in Mason, about how good it was. We`re not surprised.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s terrific. Not surprised at all. Also this just in, Phish fans, they are going to be live at the sphere in Las Vegas. Will I see you there? We already booked tickets.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020231130ejbu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for November 19, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Maurice DuBois, Norah O`Donnell, Imtiaz Tyab, Skyler Henry, Reed  Cowan, Astrid Martinez, Nancy Chen, Elizabeth Palmer \par \uc2 3,659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Tonight, remembering \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2 . The former first lady who wielded influence during a turbulent presidency, and championed humanitarian causes worldwide, dies at age 96.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Could \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  have been elected president without \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ROSALYNN CARTER\uc2  (Former First Lady of the United States): Well, I don`t like to think he could.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Also, tonight, fighting rages in Gaza. Caught in the crossfire, Palestinian civilians, the territory on the brink of collapse. These premature babies moved out of the besieged Al-Shifa Hospital today.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I`m Imtiaz Tyab in Jerusalem. Tonight, the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has described Gaza`s main hospital as a death zone.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Israel releases new video of what it calls a terror tunnel under the complex as hope grows for a hostage deal.\par \par \uc2 Plus, holiday head start. Travelers try to beat the Thanksgiving getaway crush.\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: I`m Reed Cowan at \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2  where long lines and a high price to get in the sky may have you speeding up your turkey trot this Thanksgiving holiday.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: `Tis the season to shop, so why are seasonal job hunters feeling a big chill?\par \par \uc2 And later, we head to Japan for a spin in the vehicles of the future, not all of them built for the road.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: It`s not a bumper car.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Good evening. Jericka is off. I am Maurice DuBois. And we begin tonight with the death of former First Lady, \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2 . She died today at her home in Plains, Georgia after a life committed to public service long after leaving Washington.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Carter was 96 years old. No other first couple lived longer after leaving the White House than Jimmy and \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2 , nearly 43 years and no first couple was married longer, 77 years.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Norah O`Donnell looks back at the life of a groundbreaking American woman.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jimmy and \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2  had a partnership that was extraordinary for a President and First Lady. The Carter`s bond was deep, both in private and in politics.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Could \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  have been elected president without \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ROSALYNN CARTER\uc2 : Well, I don`t like to think he could.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: She spoke her mind to her husband, and he valued her advice. She sat in on Cabinet meetings, a first, and took the heat for it.\par \par \uc2 ROSALYNN CARTER: It doesn`t matter what I do. It doesn`t matter what he does. We`re going to be criticized. You just have to have confidence.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Born in Plains, Georgia on August 18th, 1927, the oldest of four children, she started dating \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  when she was 18.\par \par \uc2 ROSALYNN CARTER: The first time I had a date with him, I came home and mother said, you know, I like Jimmy. He has the nicest smile.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: They married a year later. Jimmy was in the Navy which allowed them to see the world. But his father died in 1953. So with their three sons, they returned to Plains to run the family peanut business.\par \par \uc2 In 1970, Jimmy was elected governor of Georgia. Roselynn campaigned by his side and on her own. That continued on the 1976 presidential campaign trail, where their fourth child, Amy often joined them.\par \par \uc2 As First Lady, she continued to be a sounding board for her husband.\par \par \uc2 ROSALYNN CARTER: I look forward to consulting closely with him on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: She also traveled as his emissary, especially to Latin America.\par \par \uc2 After leaving Washington in 1981, they then created the \uc2 Carter Center\uc2  in Atlanta, where they promoted peace and where she continued her work as an advocate for research and treatment of mental illness.\par \par \uc2 She also became a prominent voice for caregivers, and for women and children. And she joined her husband in the work of Habitat for Humanity.\par \par \uc2 And as the world faced the deadly COVID-19 pandemic, the couple used their voices to encourage Americans to get vaccinated to help save lives.\par \par \uc2 Their long tradition of helping others led President Clinton to award the Carters the Medal of Freedom in 1999.\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON (Former President of the United States): Jimmy and \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2  have done more good things for more people in more places than any other couple on the face of the earth.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: President Carter not only called Rosalynn his wife, but also his best friend and chief adviser. A small town girl who didn`t plan on a public life, \uc2 Rosalynn Carter\uc2  understood the power of high office and used it to help make this a better and kinder world.\par \par \uc2 Norah O`Donnell, CBS News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: And tonight, Habitat for Humanity called the late First Lady, quote, "A compassionate and committed champion of the organization." Adding, "She worked fiercely to help families around the world."\par \par \uc2 Now to the Middle East, and today, fighting raged in Gaza, even as Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  appear to be close to a deal to free some hostages.\par \par \uc2 The conflict is now in week seven with no end in sight. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab in East Jerusalem with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Maurice, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Well, 31 premature babies had been safely evacuated from the Al-Shifa Hospital, as the Israeli military released its new video of what it says is a tunnel system used by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that ran below the sprawling medical complex, as well as what it says is video evidence \uc2 Hamas\uc2  took captives from Israel to the facility following the deadly October 7th attacks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: They are the tiniest of survivors born into a brutal war, now transferred here to southern Gaza from the besieged Al-Shifa Hospital where a visiting team from the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  described it as the death zone, and praised Palestinian health workers who continue to work there under dire conditions.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m absolutely humbled by the work of you and your teams, the heroic efforts that you`ve made. I mean, I have no words.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Tonight, the Israeli military released this video it says is of a complex \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnel that runs below the sprawling complex, as well as footage of what it says are hostages from Israel, who are of Thai and Nepali origin, forced into the hospital by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters on the morning they were captured.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI (Israel`s chief military spokesperson): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is holding the elderly.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: CBS News cannot independently verify the claims, but as Israel continues to widen its military campaign from northern Gaza to the south, killing dozens, the first anti-war protests since the start of Israel`s assault on the Palestinian territory was held in Tel Aviv this weekend, forbidden by police until now.\par \par \uc2 Why is the ceasefire so important to you?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Because people are being killed at an alarming rate in Gaza, and because I don`t think the military solution -- there is no military solution to this problem.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Protesters carried signs in Hebrew, Arabic, and English calling for an end to the war and the occupation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: I think that if you lose hope, so there is nothing more to struggle for, there is nothing more to fight for.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: But few across Israel feel this way, this man shouted curse words in Hebrew at the anti-war demonstrators before being led away by police.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: And tonight, Israel`s Intelligence Minister has said the international community should not fund the rebuilding of Gaza after the war, and instead promote what she called the, quote, "voluntary resettlement of Palestinians from the territory to other countries." A suggestion that is deeply controversial given the dark history of Palestinian displacement, Maurice, that is something the Biden administration has said will not happen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Imtiaz Tyab and our CBS News crew, many thanks to you tonight.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden spent the day in Virginia. The President and First Lady traveling to Norfolk, joining military service members and their families for dinner ahead of Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 But the president is also focused on a possible deal aimed at freeing hostages, including Americans held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Skyler Henry at the White House tonight with more -- Skyler.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Maurice. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Biden administration says getting those hostages, including dozens of women and children out of the region, is an extraordinarily high priority for the president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Face the Nation)\par \par \uc2 JON FINER (U.S. deputy National Security adviser): On an issue as sensitive as this and as challenging as this, the mantra that nothing is agreed until everything is agreed really does apply.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Deputy National Security adviser, Jon Finer says the U.S.` attempt at brokering a hostage release deal between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is delicate.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Face the Nation)\par \par \uc2 JON FINER: We`re going to continue working this directly and intensively behind the scenes with the goal of getting as many of these people home including the Americans who are held hostage there as we can.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: President Biden said in an op-ed published in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " Saturday, he and his team are working hour by hour to get the hostages released.\par \par \uc2 He also added, as long as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  clings to its ideology of destruction, a ceasefire is not peace. Going on to say that ceasefires would allow \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants to rebuild, restock rockets and reposition fighters.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Biden does think a two-state solution is the way forward. Some Democrats on Capitol Hill agree.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Face the Nation)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-Maryland): You can be both pro-Israel as I am, and also pro-Palestinian and support Palestinian rights and aspirations to self-determination and a homeland of their own.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: New polling, however, suggests 70 percent of young voters between the ages of 18 to 34 disapprove of Biden`s handling of the war.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Looking ahead for the week tomorrow, President Biden turns 81. He is the oldest sitting president. And he is also running for re- election.\par \par \uc2 A recent CBS News poll shows only a third of registered voters think that the president would stay through a second term -- Maurice.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Okay, Skyler Henry at the White House tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, Thanksgiving is just four days away and the travel rush is on. A cross country storm is expected to impact some of that travel with rain and severe weather from the West Coast impacting the Midwest and East Coast as early as Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 CBS`s Reed Cowan is at a busy LAX tonight with more for us -- Reed.\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: Good evening, Maurice.\par \par \uc2 Many Thanksgiving travelers behind me working hard and working early to beat the storms and the crowds. Fifty-five million people expected to travel and that means the holidays are off to a hectic start.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: Call it a sprint before Thanksgiving dinner, only this rush won`t burn calories as passengers wait in long lines, \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2  and all across the country.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It is so busy and I just want to make sure that we have an easy travel.\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: This weekend, the number of scheduled flights is up 13 percent according to the \uc2 FAA\uc2  in what could be the busiest holiday travel period in nearly 20 years. AAA`s Doug Shupe.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SHUPE (AAA spokesperson): A lot of people are trying to get out of town early to avoid the heavy, heavy congestion at the terminals on Tuesday and Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: Flyer, Michael Devine (sp?) got the warning and arrived early.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DEVINE (Traveler): You`re always warned, get to the airport. It`s going to be crazy, and for us, it was just kind of like, well, we will just keep our fingers crossed.\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: Fingers crossed and flyer`s wallet stressed.\par \par \uc2 How much did you pay for your ticket?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DEVINE: $700.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: $600.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It was close to $700. Yeah, just for one way.\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: The average cost of a domestic airline ticket, $681. That`s up five percent from last year.\par \par \uc2 For travelers not willing to pay for pricey flights, gas prices nationwide are down from last year averaging $3.31 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 DOUG SHUPE: For these big holidays, taking the automobile is the number one mode of transportation. It`s also the most economical for families.\par \par \uc2 REED COWAN: No matter how you make it to the Thanksgiving table, the cost of the traditional dinner is 4.5 percent lower, which takes the sting out of getting there.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: A hundred percent worth it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: So get this, the busiest day to fly is Wednesday and if you`re going to drive on Wednesday, here is a tip, leave before 11:00 a.m. to avoid bumper-to-bumper traffic.\par \par \uc2 Maurice, that means if I leave right now, I`ll be on the East Coast in three days. I like extra turkey with my gravy.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: If it all works out. Reed Cowan, thanks so much tonight.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, good news for commuters in Los Angeles. Interstate 10 will fully reopen tomorrow more than a week after a fire severely damaged part of one of the nation`s busiest roadways.\par \par \uc2 Construction crews worked to combine 10,000 hours to stabilize the freeway.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead here on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  facing an advertising backlash after an antisemitic endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Plus, why retailers are bracing for a ho-hum hiring holiday season.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: It has been a tough weekend for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . The \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  co- founder watched the most powerful rocket ever built explode during a test flight over the Texas coast on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Musk also faces growing backlash from advertisers over antisemitic posts on his social media site, X.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Astrid Martinez has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MARTINEZ: \uc2 Comcast\uc2  and \uc2 Sony\uc2  are some of the latest to say they`re leaving X. They`re joining a growing list of advertisers pulling money from the platform formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 , and \uc2 CBS\uc2  parent company, Paramount are all fleeing after owner \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  endorsed antisemitic conspiracy theories online.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Musk liked the post on X that claimed Jewish communities have a dialectical hatred against Whites. "You have said the actual truth," Musk responded.\par \par \uc2 Now, he is promising a, quote, "thermonuclear lawsuit" against the media watchdog Media Matters for reporting that X has been placing ads next to pro-Nazi content.\par \par \uc2 Is it bad for Musk?\par \par \uc2 PETER KAFKA (Vox senior correspondent): That`s an easy answer. It`s not good for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MARTINEZ: Senior correspondent for Vox, Peter Kafka says the company, X, is still struggling with a heavy debt load.\par \par \uc2 PETER KAFKA: His real issue in the near future is he`s got about a billion dollars of debt that he`s got to pay off every year, a billion dollars in interest payments he has to pay off every year. That`s a real cost he has to figure out how to pay.\par \par \uc2 But he is the world`s richest man. This stuff is available to him if he wants to just sort of eat it, he can.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MARTINEZ: Other social networks like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and TikTok also struggle with monitoring false and hateful content. However, Maurice, Musk has drawn personal condemnation for posting his controversial views to his more than 163 million followers.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Astrid Martinez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead here on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, holiday hiring, why it`s not so jolly.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Tonight, Paris known as the City of Lights is sparkling even brighter. Thousands of Christmas lights on their Champs-Elysees were switched on. They will adorn the iconic avenue until January 7th.\par \par \uc2 In this country, it`s a dim outlook for job hunters seeking holiday employment. Retailers are expected to add 410,000 jobs this season, that`s the lowest level since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Chen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: At this seasonal job fair in New York City, the holidays are already well underway.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m really successful. I ended up getting hired.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Oh, you already got hired.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: On the spot?\par \par \uc2 MAN: On the spot.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Retailers, however, are proceeding cautiously. Employment site, Indeed reports that while there are more seasonal job seekers this year, listings are down.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT DOBROSKI (Indeed career trends expert): It`s still a shaky economy in many ways. So companies are proceeding with caution. That means opening up fewer roles and really looking critically at what roles have to be filled.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Online giant, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  stands out by adding more positions than last year. But many major companies say they`re staying about the same or hiring fewer workers.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  hasn`t announced seasonal plans at all, saying it is already fully staffed.\par \par \uc2 Still, while holiday sales growth is expected to slow from recent years, industry experts are predicting another record-breaking spending season.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  survey estimates consumers will spend on average more than $1,600 on holiday shopping, up 14 percent from last year, even as shoppers face inflation, higher living costs, and resuming student loan payments.\par \par \uc2 STEVE ROGERS (Deloitte Consumer Industry Center managing director): People are still pricing inflation into their holiday. Shoppers are gearing up to spend more to give less.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: How do all of these factors impact how you`re going to be buying for your families?\par \par \uc2 STEVE ROGERS: Regardless of the economic conditions, when it comes to putting gifts under the tree for kids, parents make it happen.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Resilient shoppers and an enduring holiday spirit.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen, CBS News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a fashion statement at national parks. We will tell you why signs have red scarves.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Today is red shawl day and National Parks have taken note from Sleeping Bear in Michigan to Grand Teton and Wyoming, red shawls have been placed at sites to honor missing and murdered indigenous women.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department says indigenous women are 10 times more likely to be murdered or to go missing.\par \par \uc2 At a time when space and astronauts held the world`s attention, \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  Captain Don Walsh made the first ever descent to the deepest place in the ocean. The mission in 1960 reached the seabed more than 6.7 miles down in the Pacific`s Mariana trench and opened frontiers in deep sea exploration.\par \par \uc2 We learned this weekend that Don Walsh died in Myrtle Point, Oregon. He was 92.\par \par \uc2 When we return, the biggest motor show in Japan gets charged up for the future.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: Finally tonight, after a four-year hiatus, one of the world`s most prestigious auto shows return with a bang, a rebranding and a focus on the future.\par \par \uc2 Our Elizabeth Palmer in Tokyo took a spin.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: All the Japanese big names were there with their latest models, and so were some upstart newcomers.\par \par \uc2 This used to be called the Tokyo Motor Show, but it has been renamed. This year, the first year one has been held since the pandemic. Now, it`s known as the Tokyo Mobility Show to take in vehicles like this, the wheelchair of the future.\par \par \uc2 You get it to raise up first and then it`s just controlled by balance, by the way you swing your body -- very smooth.\par \par \uc2 Honda\uc2  calls it the Uni-One. The rest of us just call it amazing.\par \par \uc2 It`s not a bumper car.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere there were customizable vending trucks, robotic exoskeletons and even remote-controlled diggers. But the meat and potatoes of this business is still practical cars for busy people.\par \par \uc2 (KOJI SATO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Powerhouse \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 Koji Sato\uc2  took the stage to introduce the company`s brand new e-vehicles. His company is pouring billions into the Holy Grail of batteries, so-called solid state to have in its cars by 2028.\par \par \uc2 Recharge time, 10 minutes. Range, more than 700 miles.\par \par \uc2 Honda`s vision of the future includes the tiny CI-MEV.\par \par \uc2 You actually designed it with your mother-in-law in mind?\par \par \uc2 YUSUKE HASEGAWA (\uc2 Honda\uc2  design chief): Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Design chief, Yusuke Hasegawa showed me how easy to read buttons replace the gearshift.\par \par \uc2 YUSUKE HASEGAWA: Drive, reverse, and then neutral.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: And on the drawing board is a driverless option.\par \par \uc2 YUSUKE HASEGAWA: The car is automatically coming by itself, yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Comes to you and then you tell it where to go.\par \par \uc2 YUSUKE HASEGAWA: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: And then once it drops you off, you have to tell it where to park.\par \par \uc2 YUSUKE HASEGAWA: Yes, that`s right. Go away (sp?).\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Look out world, here comes the future.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer, CBS News, Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MAURICE DUBOIS: The future is now.\par \par \uc2 And that is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 I`m Maurice DuBois in New York.\par \par \uc2 Goodnight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020231120ejbj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for November 16, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Debora Patta, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 3,658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this busy Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin tonight with several developing stories that impact America`s national security, including our exclusive interview with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , who we spoke with earlier today. We asked him about the ongoing negotiations with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on the release of hostages in exchange for a temporary cease-fire, while Israel is facing tough questions from around the world about the conduct of the war. Tonight, he answers those questions.\par \par \uc2 Also today, the Israeli military says it found tunnels dug beneath Gaza`s Al-Shifa Hospital and released video of its discovery.\par \par \uc2 We also speak with America`s top diplomat, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , about that historic summit between President Biden and China`s President Xi Jinping, the new deal now to crack down on the chemicals that make fentanyl, the drug that has caused so much pain and heartbreak here in America.\par \par \uc2 With all of the biggest developments tonight, we`re going to start with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta in East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voiceover): With every bomb, every strike, families of the more than 200 hostages abducted by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on October 7 are reminded that, nearly six weeks on, their loved ones are still being held in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, more agony, the body of 65-year-old grandmother Yehudit Weiss, abducted last month, found in a building near Al-Shifa Hospital. The Israeli military continues to search that hospital, where, today, it found more proof, it says, that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  was using this as a command center, including a tunnel shaft and a vehicle with weapons.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: As you can see, they were very well prepared. And where they`re hiding all of this equipment is in a hospital, a place that`s supposed to be for humanitarian aid.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Al-Shifa can no longer receive new patients, and so they flee in their thousands, streaming southwards, setting up temporary shelter, only to be told today by Israeli soldiers that they must evacuate again.\par \par \uc2 But, if they stay, they risk becoming part of the soaring death toll, which the Hamas-run Health Ministry puts at around 11,500. That is one out of every 200 Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It`s not about the numbers. It`s about eliminating evil.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And CBS News has learned that Israel is considering a deal that would see \uc2 Hamas\uc2  release some women and children in exchange for a three-to-five-day cease-fire and possibly some Palestinian women and children, Norah, being held in Israeli prisons.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to our exclusive interview with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  about those hostage negotiations and Israel`s long-term plan for the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Prime Minister, thank you for being here on the CBS Evening News.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli Prime Minister): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: CBS News has learned that there is a deal on the table to free hostages in exchange for a three-to-five-day cease-fire. How close are you to bringing some of the hostages home?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: Well, we`re closer than we were before we began the ground action, because the ground action has put pressure on - - on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to achieve a cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 We will have a temporary cease-fire if we can get our hostages back. I don`t think it serves that purpose for me to elaborate further on that. We`re doing every effort.\par \par \uc2 It`s part of our -- you know, our three war -- war goals, one, to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , second, to bring back our hostages to Israel, and third, to ensure that that threat, that terrorist threat, never arises again from Gaza, and that Gaza has a different future, better for us, for the Palestinians, and for the entire Middle East.\par \par \uc2 So, bringing back the hostages is an integral part of our war goals, and we`re doing everything we can to achieve it.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: But, to be clear, are you considering a limited cease- fire?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: I said that we would have a temporary cease-fire only if we have our hostages back. And I haven`t changed what I said.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And would Israel agree to release Palestinian prisoners in exchange for hostages?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: Norah, I don`t think it helps. You can ask me, and that`s your job. You`re a journalist. And you should inquire.\par \par \uc2 But I think there are certain things that we -- we`re holding as confidential until we have something to tell. And when -- when we do, if we do, you and the entire world would know about it, including the hostage families.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: This is the first chance to hear from you since the targeted raid on Al-Shifa Hospital.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is using a hospital as a command center, that is a war crime. Do you have concrete evidence?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: Of course.\par \par \uc2 First of all, we -- we had concrete evidence that there were terrorist chieftains and terrorists, their terrorist minions, in the hospital. And, in fact, they fled. As our forces approached, they fled. That`s why we had no firefight.\par \par \uc2 We entered that hospital with Arabic-speaking Israeli doctors, with incubators, and we had no firefight. But \uc2 Hamas\uc2  was using the patients in that hospital as a human shield. And on level minus-two, we found a command-and-control center, military communications, coded equipment.\par \par \uc2 We found bombs. We found weapons. And we also found terror tunnels in the hospital compound. Now, we`re doing this very gingerly, because we`re -- we`re trying to do the moral thing, the right thing, to deprive \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of having this safe zone in a hospital, but at the same time to neutralize it -- its use as a command center for terrorists.\par \par \uc2 And, so far, we have achieved it. I`m glad to say that we have been able to do that. We talked to the hospital administration. We told them, we`re coming in. Make sure that no one is there, no one is hurt.\par \par \uc2 And, so far, we have achieved that. And I hope we will continue to achieve that as we finish the job.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Where are the hostages being held? Do you know?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: We had strong indications that they were held in the Shifa Hospital, which is one of the reasons we entered the hospital. If they were, they were taken out.\par \par \uc2 We have intelligence about the hostages. But, again, as to your first question, I think the less I say about it, the better.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mr. Prime Minister, you have noted that one goal of the war is to deradicalize Gaza.\par \par \uc2 But does the violence and the killing of thousands of innocent civilians fuel a future generation of hatred?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: First of all, any civilian death is a tragedy. And we shouldn`t have any, because we`re doing everything we can to get the civilians out of harm`s way, while \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is doing everything to keep them in harm`s way.\par \par \uc2 So, we send leaflets. We call them. We call them on their cell phones and we say, leave. And many have left, but \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tried to stop them at gunpoint and fired at the safe corridors that we provided for the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 So, they don`t give a hoot about the Palestinians. They think they`re safe and the underground, this messianic, theological mad cult that is fighting below. And they`re saying that, you know, damn the Palestinians above.\par \par \uc2 We don`t say that. We do everything we can to have them leave. That`s what we`re doing. The other thing that I can say is that we will try to finish that job with minimal civilian casualties. That`s what we`re trying to do, minimal civilian casualties. But, unfortunately, we`re not successful.\par \par \uc2 I will give you the -- you know, I will give you a parallel, because the German --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mr. Prime Minister, I know we don`t have much time, so I do want to ask you about the future of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: Right.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: President Biden has made it clear to you that it would be a big mistake to occupy Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Do you disagree with President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: We want overall military responsibility to prevent the reemergence of terror. We`re not seeking to occupy. That`s not our goal.\par \par \uc2 But our goal is to make sure that what happens there is different. To do that, we have to demilitarize Gaza and we have to deradicalize Gaza, just as you had to have a different future. You know, after the -- the conquest of Germany, you de-radicalized, denazified Germany. After the conquest of Japan, you made sure that the aggressive policies of imperial Japan are -- are different.\par \par \uc2 There was a cultural change. We need a cultural change here. We can`t have a civilian administration enter Gaza that will not fight terrorists, that is committed to funding terrorists, as opposed to fighting terrorists, that uses pay-for-slay, that teaches its children, the way \uc2 Hamas\uc2  teaches its children --\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Are you --\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: -- that Israel has to be dissolved.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Are you --\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: That can`t work.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Are --\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: We can`t go back to failed strategies.\par \par \uc2 There has to be a different regime and a different future for Israelis and Palestinians alike. That`s what will give hope to the Palestinians. We liberate them from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , liberate them from this tyranny, give them a real future. And that requires the destruction of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And move forward on a two-state solution?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: And move forward to make sure you don`t have a terrorist state. Make sure that you don`t have a state that teaches its children that you have to destroy Israel.\par \par \uc2 And I say, let the Palestinians have all the powers to govern themselves, but none of the powers to threaten Israel. And that means not only make sure -- making sure that Gaza is demilitarized, but also that Gaza is deradicalized.\par \par \uc2 And, if you ask me, we need a different, different civilian leadership than the one that has been offered to the Palestinians today.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mr. Prime Minister, President Biden also says that extremist settlers attacking Palestinians in the West Bank is pouring gasoline on fire.\par \par \uc2 Will you hold those settlers in the West Bank accountable for violence?\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: We hold anyone taking the law into their own hands or committing vigilante violence. That is out. We cannot accept that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that is -- I have said that in Hebrew. I have said that in English. I have said that in broadcast, and I`m saying it to you again. I think people -- I have said that to the community leaders in Judea and Samaria.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, they agree with me, because 99.9 percent of the settler community are law-abiding, solid citizens. They -- they pay their taxes. They serve in the army. These are families that are law-abiding families. You have a tiny fraction, and most of them come -- are teenagers that actually come outside -- outside of Judea and Samaria.\par \par \uc2 And they should be held accountable. But I wouldn`t make a false symmetry between the hundreds and hundreds of terrorist attacks, including today -- including just today, right at the outskirts of Jerusalem, of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  sympathizers and terrorists who come and shoot people, you know, innocent people.\par \par \uc2 And we take care to fight against them. So, the degree of -- of this extremist violence is tiny, but even a tiny amount is unacceptable to us. That`s the difference. And you know how it`s tested? Norah, this is it. This is the big one.\par \par \uc2 I condemn these actions. But the Palestinian leadership, the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  leadership, today, President Abbas, 40 days into this horror, the worst horrors perpetrated on Jews since the Holocaust, the rape and murder of women, the slaughter of children in front of their parents and parents in front of their children, the burning of babies, the beheading of women, he has yet to condemn it.\par \par \uc2 I condemn any kind of violence that is perpetrated, any kind of extremist violence. He not only does not condemn it, but his officials laud it. That`s the difference. There is no symmetry between Israel and its foes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mr. Prime Minister, I understand our time is up.\par \par \uc2 I hope you will join us again on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: Thank you, Norah. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: President Biden spoke to CEOs and posed for a class photo with world leaders today at the Asia-Pacific Economic Summit in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 This follows his high-stakes meeting on Wednesday with Chinese leader Xi Jinping. Xi pledged to crack down on the production of fentanyl ingredients and to restore military communications with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 For more on the historic summit, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  joins us from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken, thank you for joining us on this busy day. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): Good to be with you, Norah. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: You attended the meeting with Chinese President Xi Jinping.\par \par \uc2 And, after the summit, President Xi said China is ready to be a partner and friend of the United States. Do you see China as a partner and friend of the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Norah, this is one of the most consequential relationships we have, one of the most consequential relationships between any two countries in the world.\par \par \uc2 And we have an obligation to try to responsibly manage that relationship. Yesterday, we agreed that our militaries would start talking again at the most senior levels and at the operational level. And this is a very important way of trying to avoid a miscalculation, a mistake that could lead to conflict.\par \par \uc2 Second, in terms of actually making a difference in the lives of the American people, the number one killer of Americans aged 18 to 49 is fentanyl, not car accidents, not guns, not cancer. It`s fentanyl. And what`s happened is, the chemical precursors, the ingredients used to make fentanyl, have been coming from China, going to the Western Hemisphere, turned into fentanyl, then coming to the United States.\par \par \uc2 We now have an agreement with China to take concrete action against the companies that are engaged in this practice.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, how can we trust that China is going to carry through with that crackdown on those chemicals that are sent to Mexico that then are turned into fentanyl?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: As the president said yesterday, trust, but verify. And that`s what we`re doing.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Secretary Blinken, when President Biden called President Xi a dictator last night, there were cameras on you, and you looked visibly uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 China today called that wrong and irresponsible. What was going through your mind, and is that the position of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Well, it`s not exactly a secret that we have two very different systems. And the president always speaks candidly, and he speaks for all of us.\par \par \uc2 Look, Norah, it`s clear that we will continue to say things and continue to do things that China doesn`t like, just as, I assume, that they will continue to do and say things that we don`t like.\par \par \uc2 But what`s so important about the meeting yesterday, about all the work we have been doing over the last six months to make sure that we are engaged diplomatically with them, is precisely to make sure that, for the things that really matter, pursuing this competition in a way that doesn`t become conflict, managing our differences, and also looking for areas of cooperation.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mr. Secretary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And the other thing we learned from the meeting is, the pandas may be coming back to the United States.\par \par \uc2 All right, moving on to the day`s other top stories: a scathing report on Congressman George Santos and the verdict for the man who attacked Nancy Pelosi`s husband with a hammer.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, a blistering report from the House Ethics Committee on New York Congressman George Santos is reviving efforts to remove him from Congress.\par \par \uc2 The committee, led by a fellow Republican, says there is clear evidence that Santos blatantly stole from his campaign and deceived donors, and referred its findings to federal prosecutors. Santos is still refusing to step down, but now says he won`t run for reelection next year.\par \par \uc2 A federal jury in San Francisco today found David DePape guilty of attempting to kidnap former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and attacking her husband with a hammer. The 43-year-old Canadian national says he was fueled by right-wing conspiracy theories when he smashed his way into the Pelosis` home. He now faces up to 50 years in prison, in addition to another trial on state charges.\par \par \uc2 Eye on America is next, with a job training program that mines for new careers in coal country.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The recent boom in renewable energy has come at the expense of the coal industry, which has lost half its jobs in the last 10 years.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Eye on America, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann reports on a training program that restores both land and labor.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): In West Virginia`s hollers, deep in Appalachia, jobless coal miners now find a seam of hope.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAMRON (Former Coal Miner): I wasn`t 100 percent sure what I was going to do.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A mine laid off James Damron two years ago.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAMRON: I did know I didn`t want to go back in the deep mines.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Instead, Damron found Coalfield Development and its incoming CEO Jacob Israel Hannah.\par \par \uc2 JACOB ISRAEL HANNAH (Incoming CEO, Coalfield Development): Hope is only as good as what it means to put food on the table.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The program is a community-based nonprofit, teaching a dozen job skills and personal ones -- construction, agriculture, solar installation.\par \par \uc2 JACOB ISRAEL HANNAH: They are going through this process here.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Someone can get paid up to three years to learn all of them.\par \par \uc2 That`s a good deal.\par \par \uc2 JACOB ISRAEL HANNAH: We want to make sure that you have all the tools in your toolkit to know, when you do interview with an employer, here`s the things that you lay out that you`ve learned.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It is working, training more than 2,500 people, creating 800 new jobs and 72 new businesses, a program delivering with roughly $20 million in federal grants.\par \par \uc2 JACOB ISRAEL HANNAH: Instead of waiting around for something to happen, we are trying to generate our own hope.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This isn`t pie in the sky. This is nitty-gritty.\par \par \uc2 JACOB ISRAEL HANNAH: Meeting real needs where they are at.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Steven Spry is a grad. He is helping reclaim an abandoned strip mine, turning throwaway land into lush land.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN SPRY (Recent Graduate): Now I have kind of got a career out of just I can weld. I can farm. I can run excavators.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: You can always find a job doing something.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN SPRY: Yeah, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: With the program, James Damron now works only above ground.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAMRON: That was a big part of my identity, was being a coal miner. And leaving that, like, I kind of had to find myself again, I guess.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And now you have?\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAMRON: I absolutely have.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Appalachia is mining something new, options.\par \par \uc2 For Eye on America, Mark Strassmann, Mingo County, West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a great organization.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  just declared loneliness a global health threat.\par \par \uc2 And for our next episode of Person to Person, we sat down with Harvard Professor \uc2 Arthur Brooks\uc2 . He just wrote a book with Oprah Winfrey about the art and science of getting happier. We asked him about self-care in the journey to happiness.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS\uc2  (\uc2 Harvard University\uc2 ): The whole idea that I am going to find happiness if I focus entirely on myself is completely misguided.\par \par \uc2 That is the short road to true misery, because only to love and be loved is going to give you the happiness that you seek. And only other care will actually bring that to you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Love is the answer.\par \par \uc2 And you can hear more of our conversation about happiness and what it`s like working with Oprah. That`s tonight on Person to Person.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231117ejbg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for November 6, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Ben Tracy, Carter Evans, David Martin, Debora  Patta, Robert Costa \par \uc2 3,471 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  takes the stand in his New York state fraud trial, clashing with the judge and acknowledging he inflated the value of at least two of his properties. Why it all matters?\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The trial tonight that could crush Donald Trump`s New York business empire and end with the former president paying a $250 million fine. Why the judge at one point asked Trump`s attorney to control the former president.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military surrounds Gaza City, as its soldiers battle \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants in the streets. Plus, shuttle diplomacy. What Secretary of State Blinken is hoping to accomplish as he visits the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: There is growing anger against the United States here in Ramallah in the West Bank, with crowds chanting: "Blinken, you`ve got blood on your hands."\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Our new reporting about the type of weapons Israel is using, plus the American submarine now in the Middle East, why it`s rare we know it`s there.\par \par \uc2 Last, best and final offer. What`s holding up a deal between major Hollywood studios and the actors, as the strike hits day 116 and your favorite shows stay off the air?\par \par \uc2 The Earthshot Prize kicks off, with Prince William honoring innovators trying to battle climate change. We will introduce you to one of them.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Only 1 percent of the clothing in the world ever gets recycled, but now a U.S. company has found a remarkable way to turn old clothes into new clothes.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Major dino nugget recall. Nearly 30,000 pounds of \uc2 Tyson\uc2  chicken could contain bits of metal. What to look for in your freezer.\par \par \uc2 And the emotional way Damar Hamlin honored the medical professionals who saved his life.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TIRICO: The most significant person in the stadium tonight is Hamlin. He`s not active for the game, but his presence is part of another chapter in his remarkable and inspiring story.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 And we begin tonight with fireworks inside a New York City courtroom, as former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took the stand in the civil fraud trial against him and his company. Trump clashed with prosecutors and the judge when he refused to answer questions with a simple yes or no, and instead went into long speeches.\par \par \uc2 At one point, the judge even threatened to remove Trump from the witness stand due to his outbursts. Trump and his company are accused of committing fraud by intentionally adjusting property values and inflating his personal wealth to obtain better terms on bank loans and insurance. The judge has already found Trump and his company liable for fraud, and this trial will determine how much Trump will have to pay in penalties.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general`s office is seeking $250 million and to bar Trump and his children from doing business in New York. His daughter Ivanka will testify Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa was inside the courtroom and will start us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Robert.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 A dramatic, intense day, with voices raised and insults exchanged, as former President Trump assailed the judge in this case. Trump also acknowledged a role in the valuation of his properties days after his sons put the responsibility on accountants.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Mr. Trump, how`s it going today?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): Former President Trump zipped his lips in front of cameras, but, on the witness stand, he was anything but quiet.\par \par \uc2 Trump repeatedly lashed out at state Supreme Court Judge \uc2 Arthur Engoron\uc2 , the man who will decide the fate of his business empire. As for yes-or-no answers by the New York state attorney, Trump instead gave winding monologues, prompting the judge to tell him, "This is not a political rally, this is a courtroom," even pleading with Trump`s lawyers to control him.\par \par \uc2 Trump shook his head from the witness stand, saying, "This is a very unfair trial."\par \par \uc2 Trump and other defendants, including his children, have already been found liable for fraud in the case as part of a $250 million lawsuit brought forward by New York Attorney General Letitia James. She accused Trump of inflating his assets, including Mar-a-Lago, Trump Tower, and his Seven Springs family estate, by over $2 billion in order to gain better financial terms.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: That, by overvaluing his properties, that he was going to get a better deal from the banks or a better deal from the insurance companies. That is illegal. The normal person cannot inflate value of properties in order to get a benefit.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trump and his sons have blamed their accounting firm, \uc2 Mazars\uc2 , for any errors.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : This is a case that should have never been brought.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: When asked about the charges today, Trump erupted, calling questions about the valuation of Mar-a-Lago disgraceful. "She should be ashamed of herself," Trump said of James, who spoke outside the court.\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES (New York Attorney General): The numbers don`t lie. And Mr. Trump obviously can engage in all of these distractions. And that what -- is what, exactly what he did.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But amid the clashes, Trump notably acknowledged that he has a hand in his property`s evaluations, saying, "I would look at them, I would see them, and I would maybe on occasion have some suggestions."\par \par \uc2 Asked by the state attorney whether he ever thought the values were off, Trump said, "Yes, on occasion, both high and low."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Robert Costa is back with us after that dramatic testimony.\par \par \uc2 I do have to ask you, since you`re our chief election campaign correspondent, since we are less than a year now before the 2024 election, where do things stand with President Biden and the presumptive Republican nominee, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Norah, CBS News` latest polling shows former President Trump has the edge over President Biden, with 51 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, another poll over the weekend showed troubling signs for Biden. Trump is leading Biden in five of six key battleground states. Those states were won by the president in 2020.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , the Israeli military says it`s cut off Gaza City, essentially splitting the Palestinian territory in two, now, this as the number of people killed in Gaza has topped 10,000. That is according to the Hamas-run Health Ministry.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Debora Patta reports tonight on how the past 24 hours have seen some of the most intense airstrikes since the war began.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voiceover): Day after day, Israel`s fiery bombardment of Gaza continues, as calls for a cease-fire grow louder, with people around the world horrified at the staggering civilian death toll.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  wrapped up his diplomatic tour of the region today after facing open hostility in Turkey with an attempted storming of a U.S. Turkish military base yesterday and protests in the West Bank during his meeting with \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  President \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is growing anger against the United States here in Ramallah in the West Bank, with crowds chanting: "Blinken, you have got blood on your hands."\par \par \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  say they have circled Gaza City, splitting the enclave in two, and claim they have taken control of a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  military compound that contains underground tunnels. Hundreds of thousands of civilians remain in the north, unwilling or unable to leave.\par \par \uc2 (MAN #1 speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: When they do, this is what they are running from.\par \par \uc2 (MAN #2 speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: One-point-five million Palestinians have been internally displaced, says the United Nations, and they are escaping any way they can.\par \par \uc2 Israel has ordered civilians in the north to evacuate, but the journey south can be just as perilous, people so terrified of being caught in the crossfire that everyone, young and old, this man says, walks with their hands in the air in a universal gesture of defenselessness.\par \par \uc2 (MAN #3 SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "We saw bodies just lying around," this man screams. "Please, have mercy on us."\par \par \uc2 But mercy is in short supply here. Death comes without warning. Grief overwhelms the living. This hospital worker was on duty when the bodies of his wife and four children were brought in. This is a crisis of humanity, warns the United Nations, as those trapped inside Gaza wait for a cease- fire that never comes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: And the United Nations once again condemned violence on both sides, warning that no party to an armed conflict, Norah, is above international humanitarian law.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are learning some new details about a phone call between President Biden and Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  today. We`re told that the two discussed the possibility of tactical pauses to get civilians out, help get aid in, and to allow the possibility of a hostage release.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` David Martin is taking a closer look at the weapons that Israel is using as the White House is urging the Israelis to protect civilians.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN (voiceover): President Biden called Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu again this morning, telling him it is imperative to reduce civilian casualties in Gaza, which, by U.S. count, are skyrocketing.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (NSC Coordinator For Strategic Communications): Many, many thousands have been killed and many more injured or -- and/or wounded.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: U.S. officials say the majority of bombs Israel has dropped on Gaza are unguided so-called dumb bombs, which are accurate, but less precise than satellite-guided weapons. These officials say the primary cause of civilian casualties is not the missiles, but Israel`s willingness to strike targets \uc2 Hamas\uc2  located in civilian neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HERZOG (Israeli Ambassador to the United States): This is tragic, but we cannot allow \uc2 Hamas\uc2  immunity just because they hide behind civilians.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Israel claims it has killed more than 60 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  operatives, but U.S. officials question whether the military value of the strikes is worth the civilian harm.\par \par \uc2 The Israelis have dropped leaflets warning civilians to flee Northern Gaza. Even so, said one U.S. official, civilian casualties are not a priority of theirs.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): How Israel does this matters. We will focus as well on steps that need to be taken to protect civilians who are in a crossfire of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` making.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The U.S. is already shipping Israel precision-guided 250- pound bombs, which have a much smaller blast radius compared to some of the bunker busters that have been used to go after the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnel system.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Israel says it needs more white phosphorus shells, which can be used to create smokes screens, but also cause horrific burns.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. official says the Biden administration is unlikely to grant that request -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Interesting reporting.\par \par \uc2 David Martin at the Pentagon tonight, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State Antony Blinken`s shuttle diplomacy in the Middle East isn`t the Biden administration`s only attempt at avoiding a wider war. We learned that a second aircraft carrier, the USS Eisenhower, just arrived in the Red Sea over the weekend. It is headed to the Persian Gulf. That is a clear message of deterrence, and it is directed at Iran.\par \par \uc2 And then, just yesterday, the Pentagon revealing that a U.S. nuclear submarine crossed through the \uc2 Suez Canal\uc2 . The fact that we can see these pictures is very rare, but it is deliberate, a message. These vessels operate mostly in secret, even called the Silent Service. This submarine can carry 154 Tomahawk cruise missiles. That is a major threat to any adversary.\par \par \uc2 All right, tonight, one of the longest strikes in Hollywood history could be nearing its final act. The actors unions responded to what`s described as the last, best and final offer from the major studios and streaming services.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Carter Evans takes a look at the high-stakes negotiations.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Young Sheldon, \uc2 CBS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): The season finale of Young Sheldon ended with a cliffhanger, a major disaster and all its fallout.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Young Sheldon, \uc2 CBS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: That was supposed to be resolved this fall, but the drama on screen is now playing out in real life on the streets.\par \par \uc2 It`s day 116 of the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  strike.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We want a fair deal.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And, tonight, added urgency, the studios giving actors what they call a last, best and final offer.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELLONI (Founding Partner, Puck News): If there is no deal in the next week or so, we`re looking at 2024 movies really being impacted.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: That includes films like Dune: Part 2 --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Dune: Part 2, \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- and the latest versions of Spider-Man --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse, \uc2 Sony Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- Deadpool --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Deadpool 2, 20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- and Mission: impossible.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Mission Impossible, \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And on the small screen:\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELLONI: We`re looking at the full television season being impacted.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Abbott Elementary, ABC)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: That could end any hope of shows like Abbott Elementary --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Euphoria, HBO)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: -- Euphoria or Blue Bloods returning anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Blue Bloods, \uc2 CBS\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: And while writers have settled their strike, what`s holding up this deal is the use of artificial intelligence to manipulate what`s on screen.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELLONI: Because they don`t actually know what they`re fighting over in many ways. They think they know.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from A.I. generated video)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW BELLONI: But we have no idea how this technology is going to evolve over the next three years.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: A.I. is already able to recreate famous faces, a big concern for working actors like Jeri Ryan.\par \par \uc2 JERI RYAN (Actress): They can use your image and your voice and put you into a project that you would not have agreed to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, this afternoon, the actors union delivered its response to the studios. Another counteroffer could drag out these negotiations even farther.\par \par \uc2 And if a deal isn`t hammered out this week, well, that means cameras may not still start -- start rolling until after the holidays -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to another major development tonight. A jury in Colorado found a police officer not guilty of reckless manslaughter today in the 2019 killing of Elijah McClain. McClain died after police put him in a choke hold and medics injected him with the powerful sedative ketamine. Nathan Woodyard, acquitted today, was the last of the three Aurora police officers to stand trial in the case.\par \par \uc2 One was found guilty of criminally negligent homicide. The two paramedics who administered the ketamine go on trial later this month.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to a royal gathering in Singapore, where Prince William is handing out the annual Earthshot Prize. Five winners will be awarded more than $1 million for their innovative solutions to the world`s environmental challenges.\par \par \uc2 In our series Protecting the Planet, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ben Tracy takes a look at how one U.S. finalist is solving one of the clothing industry`s biggest problems.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CONOR HARTMAN (COO and Vice President of Business Development, Circ): We need to protect the planet from the cost of clothing.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY (voiceover): Conor Hartman is chief operating officer of Circ, a climate tech start-up in the former textile mill town of Danville, Virginia. It`s trying to fix the unfashionable problem of clothing waste.\par \par \uc2 Where does most of our used clothing end up?\par \par \uc2 CONOR HARTMAN: Most of it is ending up in landfills or incineration. There`s a garbage truck of fashion waste that is dumped every second of every day.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That`s because much of our clothing, especially inexpensive and trendy fast fashion, is a blend of cotton and polyester, a form of plastic. It`s been nearly impossible to recycle, until now.\par \par \uc2 It`s like a little mad scientist.\par \par \uc2 Circ invented a way to separate the two through a chemical process.\par \par \uc2 CONOR HARTMAN: Our process, for lack of a better term, is a pressure cooker, water under heat and pressure with some responsible chemistry.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: So it`s a very fancy instapot.\par \par \uc2 CONOR HARTMAN: It`s a very fancy instapot.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Both materials can then be used again to make new clothing, like these shirts retailer Zara created with Circ`s technology.\par \par \uc2 So this is the first recycled polycotton clothing item.\par \par \uc2 CONOR HARTMAN: You got it.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The impact could be huge. The fashion industry is responsible for about 10 percent of annual planet-warming greenhouse gas emissions. That`s more than all international air travel and maritime shipping combined.\par \par \uc2 Right now, less than 1 percent of textile waste gets recycled back into textiles.\par \par \uc2 PRINCE WILLIAM (Prince of Wales): We can overcome our planet`s greatest challenges.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Prince William is giving Circ a big boost, naming it a finalist for one of his Earthshot Prizes.\par \par \uc2 When you guys found out you were a finalist, what was your reaction?\par \par \uc2 CONOR HARTMAN: It was pretty emotional, honestly. And to get this level of recognition for a solution that we know is going to be the future is really inspiring.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Circ plans to open its first of several industrial-scale factories by 2026 and refashion the way our clothes are made.\par \par \uc2 CONOR HARTMAN: Because we have all the clothes we need to make all the clothes we will ever need.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Ben Tracy, CBS News, Danville, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The father of a mass shooting suspect pleads guilty to charges connected to the gun used in the attack.\par \par \uc2 We have got the details next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A father who helped his teenage son obtain a gun used in a deadly mass shooting last year pleaded guilty today to reckless conduct.\par \par \uc2 Robert Crimo Jr. sponsored his son`s underage gun license, even after he threatened to kill people. Investigators say his son, Robert Crimo III, killed seven people in the shooting at a Fourth of July parade in Highland Park, Illinois. The gunman`s father will serve 60 days in jail and two years of probation.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there is a major chicken nugget recall. What to look for in your freezer -- next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now this consumer alert.\par \par \uc2 Tyson`s Foods is recalling nearly 30,000 pounds of frozen, fully cooked chicken nuggets after several people found small pieces of metal inside. The recall impacts dinosaur-shaped Fun \uc2 Nuggets\uc2  sold in 29-ounce bags with the best-buy date of September 4 of next year. The recalled nuggets were shipped to distributors in at least nine states across the country.\par \par \uc2 Buffalo`s Damar Hamlin makes an emotional return to Cincinnati months after his near-death experience. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight, we wanted to leave you with a poignant moment from the NFL weekend.\par \par \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  safety Damar Hamlin --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NFL/NBC Sports)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- returned to Cincinnati`s \uc2 Paycor\uc2  Stadium for the first time on Sunday since suffering a cardiac arrest during a game in January. Hamlin, who didn`t play yesterday, was spotted after the game at midfield taking a moment to reflect on the 10 months since his near-death experience.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, he tweeted that he had dinner with his heroes, 10 staff members at the University of Cincinnati Medical Center, saying he wouldn`t be here if it wasn`t for them. And he surprised them by announcing scholarships in their names to help young people in the city chase their dreams.\par \par \uc2 That`s a wonderful way to say thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231107ejb600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 America`s Diplomatic Offensive; Conflict Draws Outrage; Biden versus Trump; High-Stakes Testimony; Strike Action; A Wedding, Finally, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Ramy Inocencio, Kenny Choi, Marwan Al-Ghoul,  Skyler Henry, Naomi Ruchim, Cristian Benavides, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 3,373 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, America`s diplomatic offensive as the wars toll in Gaza rises.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  making unannounced visits in the Middle East this weekend, as Arab states demand an immediate halt to Israel`s attacks.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): We`re working very hard to make sure that the conflict in Gaza does not escalate, does not spread.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The conflict drawing outrage around the world. Inside Israel, rising anger at the failures that led to last month`s assault by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  gunmen.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: I`m Ramy Inocencio in Tel Aviv. Israel has launched a massive bombing campaign on Gaza while anger and protests are growing against Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, Biden versus Trump. New CBS News polling on a possible rematch one year from Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Plus, high stakes testimony. The former president set to take the stand with his business empire under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 Strike action: California in the spotlight as unions flex their power.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHOI: I`m Kenny Choi in Los Angeles where studios have given striking actors a best and final offer to get Hollywood back up and running.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And later, a wedding, a long time coming, 56 years. But who`s counting?\par \par \uc2 MAN: You may kiss the bride.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening and thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We begin this Sunday with Israel`s siege of Gaza and its war on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, CIA director, William Burns is in Israel, part of a new push by the Biden administration to engage leaders in the region and to keep the war from spreading amid rising outrage over civilian deaths in the Palestinian territory.\par \par \uc2 Burns joined Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  already there. Today, Blinken made stops to Baghdad, as well as the West Bank city of Ramallah where he held what`s being described as a tense meeting with Palestinian leader, \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And tonight the Israeli army said its "significant airstrikes" and land assault had essentially split the Palestinian territory in two.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio is in Tel Aviv tonight and has the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Ramy, good evening.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Jericka, good evening.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve had \uc2 Hamas\uc2  retaliate with rockets of their own tonight here in Tel Aviv. As for Gaza, Israel keeps shattering buildings and lives in its mission to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . A viewer warning: The images you are about to see are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Shock in Gaza City, an Israeli fighter jet struck this ambulance convoy claiming \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters were inside. Men and women laid in their own pools of blood outside the Strip`s biggest hospital, Al-Shifa.\par \par \uc2 Israel also hit Gaza`s Jabalia refugee camp, the third time in five days targeting more militants and infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 (BOY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: In the aftermath, this anguished boy said he carried out dead bodies, including one decapitated.\par \par \uc2 And in the Maghazi refugee camp, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says another Israeli strike killed at least 38 people and injured 100 more.\par \par \uc2 This photo journalist carried his dead child, one of four he lost along with four brothers and their children.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Israel claims \uc2 Hamas\uc2  uses civilians as human shields today releasing video purportedly of rocket launcher pits in Gaza next to a children`s swimming pool and playground.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And a new video of Israeli police on October 7th, finding only dead bodies on the grass and behind the bar, riddled with bullets at the Nova Music Festival.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: In Tel Aviv, new protests this weekend against Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , as anger grows in Israel at political and security leaders for last month`s deadly attack by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Many in the crowd marching toward his house chanting "Jail now."\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting "Bring them home now.")\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Outside the Defense Ministry, thousands demanded that all be done to bring the 200-plus hostages held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  home.\par \par \uc2 Why has he not taken responsibility?\par \par \uc2 TAL SCHNEIDER ("The Times of Israel" diplomatic and political correspondent): He thinks what will people watch when they will be, you know, 50 years from now?\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Tal Schneider is political correspondent for "The Times of Israel" newspaper.\par \par \uc2 TAL SCHNEIDER: For Netanyahu, it always has been about his reputation.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Now his reputation is at all-time lows. In a new poll, 76 percent of Israelis want him to resign.\par \par \uc2 TAL SCHNEIDER: He is very good at propaganda, very good at explaining himself, but never good on follow up and taking care of the people.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Should Netanyahu resign?\par \par \uc2 TAL SCHNEIDER: Yes. Netanyahu should resign.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And tonight, the prime minister again stresses there will be no ceasefire, Jericka, until all hostages are freed.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Ramy Inocencio tonight in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Gaza and \uc2 CBS\uc2  news producer, Marwan Al-Ghoul. Just as in the last report, we caution that some of the images are disturbing. The numbers of casualties cited are from the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  by Gaza Health Ministry.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARWAN AL-GHOUL: Today, at the nth day of the month of the war, the life becomes more and more miserable as Israel keep bombing Gaza around the hour, which caused more than 11,000 deaths and more than 24,000 injuries. Half of them or more are children and women.\par \par \uc2 And don`t forget that more than 1,500 killed are missed under destruction still now.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals almost collapse. The healthcare system in Gaza can`t apply its service to the patients, to the deaths and injuries. That`s a matter of life. It`s collapsed. People don`t know where to go.\par \par \uc2 I can tell that more than one million of the Palestinians in Gaza, I mean, half of the population are displaced with no electricity, lack of food, lack of water, and a lot of destructions everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Today morning, I was driving in Rafah City and I saw thousands line and stand on lines waiting for bread. A woman told me that she had been standing for six hours to get some bread, might not be enough for her family.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Marwan Al-Ghoul reporting in Gaza, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well today, pro-Palestinian protesters marched in several cities around the world. Thousands took to the streets of Toronto calling for a ceasefire in Gaza, while in Madrid, demonstrators expressed support for the Palestinian cause.\par \par \uc2 Here in the US, new \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling shows most Americans have sympathy for both Israelis and Palestinians. A majority however, also support providing military aid and weapons to Israel.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Skyler Henry is at the White House with more on how this war could impact the 2024 presidential election now, one year away --Skyler.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Jericka, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Well, there is still ways to go in this race, but that new polling suggests that if there were a rematch between President Biden and former President Trump, it is Trump with the slight edge, and voters say issues on both the domestic and international fronts are fueling the support.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. President, any progress on a humanitarian pause?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Yes.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: President Biden over the weekend signaled that there could be a break in the fighting from Israel`s war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to get more aid in and more hostages out of Gaza as top administration officials overseas look to tamp down concerns about a wider conflict in the region.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 : The humanitarian pause or pauses could make a positive difference.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Lawmakers on Capitol Hill are expressing their concerns about Israel`s deadly siege.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): They are an independent country, but we have a right to say, sorry, you need a new military strategy. Go after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but that means -- but do not kill innocent men, women, and children.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Free, Free Palestine.")\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Thousands of pro-Palestine demonstrators converged on Washington on Saturday, demanding a ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 New CBS News polls show that while Democrats and Republicans think the US should send weapons and military aid to Israel, a number of far-left leaning Democrats are in opposition of the support.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA SCARPO (\uc2 University of Florida\uc2  student): Everybody needs help. There are innocent lives at stake everywhere.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Voters` thoughts on foreign policy along with several hot button issues including the state of the economy and abortion all help former President Trump and the 2024 race for the White House.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We`re going to defeat Crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and we are going to Make America Great Again.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: If he were to receive his party`s nomination, polls show Trump with a three percent lead over President Biden in a possible head-to- head.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Now, the polls also show that the number of Americans who think things are going badly in the country has hit a high for the year at 73 percent -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That`s not good.\par \par \uc2 Skyler Henry for us at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected to take the stand tomorrow at his civil trial in New York. Trump is accused of fraudulently inflating the value of his properties to obtain loans and other benefits.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Naomi Ruchim is outside the state court in Lower Manhattan with what we can expect.\par \par \uc2 Good evening.\par \par \uc2 NAOMI RUCHIM: Jericka, this high-stakes case could cost the former president control of some of his marquee properties, including the Trump Tower here in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NAOMI RUCHIM: Tomorrow`s testimony will help the judge determine how much the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  owes after executives were found liable for inflating Trump`s net worth and the value of his assets in order to get more favorable financing.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Trump sons, Eric and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Jr. testified saying they relied on an outside firm and accountants working for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . The former president testified in a deposition in April, he never felt his financial statements would be taken very seriously and warned people doing business with him to do their homework.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s oldest daughter, Ivanka, is set to testify in the case on Wednesday, and the civil trial is expected to last into December.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NAOMI RUCHIM: And Jericka, a reminder. In addition to this civil case, Trump has been indicted in four criminal cases that will play out as the 2024 election is in full swing.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Thank you, Naomi.\par \par \uc2 Well, there is a new movement on the actors` strike against Hollywood Studios. It`s one of several job actions taken as workers flex their "labor muscle."\par \par \uc2 As CBS` Kenny Choi reports, even more of them could soon be walking a picket line.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHOI: Jericka, major wins for auto workers have given momentum to other labor movements, and here in Hollywood, one of the longest ongoing negotiations could soon come to an end.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Union. Power.")\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHOI: It`s been 115 days in screen actors walked off the job. On Saturday, studios presented \uc2 SAG AFTRA\uc2  members with what they`re calling the best and final offer. The proposal includes more residual bonuses for popular streaming shows and protections on AI.\par \par \uc2 The actors aren`t alone. So far this year, more than 450,000 American workers went on strike up from over 140,000 just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting.)\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHOI: In Oregon, the Portland teachers union is striking for higher pay and smaller class sizes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The district wants to make it sound like it`s really, that we`re out here, you know, fighting for ourselves. We`re not. This is about our students.\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHOI: If no deal is reached by tonight, district public schools will remain closed tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And in Las Vegas --\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting.)\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHOI: Tens of thousands of culinary workers are ready to join the picket line at the end of this week if deals aren`t reached with the casinos.\par \par \uc2 TED PAPPAGEORGE (Culinary Union 226 secretary-treasurer): We will bargain very strongly, but no worker is willing to accept to be shortchanged in this economy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KENNY CHOI: The Biden administration has touted itself as the most pro union in history and amid rising and spiking inflation, workers are leveraging this moment to get their demands met.\par \par \uc2 And here in Hollywood, California, Jericka, the state is seeing the most strikes than anywhere else in the country.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: That ought to tell us something.\par \par \uc2 Kenny Choi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, the high tech race to save Florida`s citrus crop.\par \par \uc2 A record win in the New York City Marathon.\par \par \uc2 And why two seniors waited more than a half a century for this moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Florida`s citrus industry is in crisis, besieged by disease, severe weather, and lost acreage. This year`s crop is expected to yield less than half of what was produced just two years ago. That explains why orange juice prices at the store are up 10 percent from last year, and are now at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 CBS`s Cristian Benavides reports from Groveland, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Glenn Beck is a fourth generation Florida farmer his grandfather started growing citrus in 1887.\par \par \uc2 GLENN BECK (Fourth generation Florida farmer): Never before has there been a series of events that led to a downfall of an industry such as what citrus has experienced.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Beck says virtually all of his 5,000-acre citrus groves are infected with a bacterial disease carried by the tiny Asian citrus psyllid. Citrus greening has plagued Florida farmers for almost two decades.\par \par \uc2 GLENN BECK: The leaves and fruit don`t receive the nutrients that they should until eventually the tree dies.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Research in genetic therapy was applied to help the trees tolerate the disease from within, and individual protective covers known as IPCs provide safekeeping from without.\par \par \uc2 Those efforts are bearing fruit, just in time for this upcoming season.\par \par \uc2 GLENN BECK: If we get some solutions in there, we can start to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: While Beck expects to pass this on to the next generation, he believes he`ll still lose about half the acres of orange groves that he owns by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Matt Joyner with the trade group, \uc2 Florida Citrus Mutual\uc2 , says at its peak, 300 million citrus boxes were produced.\par \par \uc2 MATT JOYNER (\uc2 Florida Citrus Mutual\uc2 ): Last season, we ended it right at 18 million boxes of citrus, so about a 90 percent decline.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: And many Florida packing houses now sit empty, down to only 25 this year from over 100 in the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Hurricanes and a December freeze both wiped out groves which take about four years to recover before harvest. And consumer consumption is down for OJ while land development is up, which helps accelerate the decline.\par \par \uc2 This season, however, could be a turning point.\par \par \uc2 MATT JOYNER: I think that our production is going to be somewhat flat, but that`s actually a victory.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: A victory shared by \uc2 University of Florida\uc2  researchers because science has slowed the decline. They are among many scientists who study what makes citrus trees more tolerant to greening and they`re tolerant to greening.\par \par \uc2 FLAVIA ZAMBON (\uc2 University of Florida\uc2  assistant professor): They are tolerant, so they support the bacterial growth inside of them without dying.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: You think there`s hope.\par \par \uc2 MATT JOYNER: Absolutely. I know there`s hope.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIAN BENAVIDES: Growers need the therapies to work with their livelihood at stake.\par \par \uc2 Cristian Benavides, CBS News, Groveland, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a popular snack for kids has been recalled.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Today in New York City, Ethiopian, Tamarat Tola set a course record to win this year`s New York City Marathon. He finished in two hours, four minutes, and 58 seconds beating the old mark by eight seconds.\par \par \uc2 The Women`s Division was won by Kenya`s Hellen Obiri who ran the course in two hours, 27 minutes, and 23 seconds. Obiri also won the Boston Marathon earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Tyson\uc2  is recalling nearly 30,000 pounds of chicken nuggets. The recall affects its pre-cooked dinosaur shaped fun nuggets. Some customers reported finding small pieces of metal in the chicken. At least one person sustained a minor injury to their mouth.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham, Alabama is mourning the loss of Leroy Stover, a pioneer who paved the way for police officers. The city didn`t hire Stover, its first Black officer until 1966.\par \par \uc2 Stover rose through the ranks and was a deputy chief at the time of his retirement in 1998. Leroy Stover was 90 years old.\par \par \uc2 His legacy surely lives on.\par \par \uc2 Well next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, the wedding of their dreams more than a half a century in the making.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Finally tonight, weekends are for weddings, right?\par \par \uc2 Well, here`s your invitation to a special one involving a couple who waited more than a half a century to walk down the aisle. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann explains.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEREMIAH COFER (\uc2 Atria Senior Living\uc2  executive director): As a symbol of my love.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCMICHAEL (Groom): As a symbol of my love.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Terry and Joyce McMichaels` big day was a long time coming.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCMICHAEL: We met in San Francisco in --\par \par \uc2 JOYCE MCMICHAEL (Bride): The summer of `67.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCMICHAEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And did you know right away he was the one?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE MCMICHAEL: Oh yes. I mean, it was yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: He was 19, she was 23. Smitten, married right away, but no wedding.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCMICHAEL: We went to the Justice of the Peace and got married.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And why did you do it that way?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCMICHAEL: Easiest thing to do and we didn`t have much money back then.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Fifty-six married years later, the McMichaels` live at this Atlanta retirement center. They`re now 75 and 79 and still smitten.\par \par \uc2 JEREMIAH COFER: I asked them about their wedding and they said they never had one. She said it was something that she would want to have, and I said hey, let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 A wedding coordinator helped plan the big day. There`s a cake, a videographer, a stylist, and of course, her wedding dress -- all professionally done, all donated by a touched community.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE MCMICHAEL: We laugh a lot. I guess that`s the secret.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCMICHAEL: We`re still the same people that we were before the wedding.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: What if she says, I don`t?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE MCMICHAEL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCMICHAEL: She might. You never know. She wants to remain my first wife.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE MCMICHAEL: Yes, yes. First and only.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Look at you.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE MCMICHAEL: Very special --\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This time with a dress, the guests --\par \par \uc2 JEREMIAH COFER: We gather here today in the presence of friends and family --\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And all the magic of a proper wedding.\par \par \uc2 JEREMIAH COFER: You may kiss the bride, Mr. McMichael.\par \par \uc2 Fifty-six years is a long time and why not celebrate that? We celebrate everything that goes. Let`s celebrate love.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Terry and Joyce McMichael, newlyweds again and proof that love is worth the wait.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How could you not love that story?\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Coming up tonight on "60 Minutes," when Social Security overpays you, prepare to pay up.\par \par \uc2 For now, I`m Jericka Duncan in New York. From all of us here, thank you for watching. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020231106ejb500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for November 1, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mike Bettes, Kris Van Cleave, Lilia Luciano,  Nancy Cordes, Charlie D`Agata, Carter Evans \par \uc2 3,653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, American citizens and other foreign nationals are allowed to leave Gaza through the border with Egypt, after being trapped for weeks in the war-torn territory, the race to evacuate, as fighting continues between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: We came here hoping to live.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We are there the moment the gate opens to let some people escape Gaza, among them Americans, and some of the critically injured. Plus, the heavy battles in Northern Gaza, as the largest refugee camp is hit with another airstrike.\par \par \uc2 An engineering student at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  is under arrest, accused of threatening to kill Jewish students, as antisemitic threats right here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Despite an arrest having been made over the violent threats, students are still concerned about their safety and speech.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . testifies in that New York fraud trial. What the former president`s oldest son said on the stand.\par \par \uc2 The scary details after a Delta co-pilot is accused of threatening the pilot with a gun that he had in the cockpit. Why he was allowed to have the weapon on the plane.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: Welcome to Novem-brr.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It is starting to feel like winter a bit too early. We have the frigid forecast.\par \par \uc2 School`s out for tens of thousands of students in Portland, as teachers go on strike. What they are demanding.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Portland Public Schools, you can do better, and you must do better.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Selling your home could soon get a little cheaper, thanks to a new court ruling that could reduce the commission fees paid to real estate agents and radically change the way homes are bought and sold in this country.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Some good news as you start thinking about Thanksgiving. The price of turkey is down a lot, but what about other parts of the meal?\par \par \uc2 And Celine Dion`s first appearance in nearly four years. The advice she gave to a hockey team in Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the rush to escape Gaza for hundreds of foreign nationals, including American citizens who`ve been trapped in the war zone for more than three weeks. Today, the border crossing with Egypt was open for the first time since the start of the war, allowing hundreds of foreign passport holders and some critically injured Palestinians to leave Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Diplomatic sources tell CBS News that this first batch of evacuations was negotiated by Qatar, in coordination with the U.S. The news comes as the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  escalates, with a second airstrike on the Jabalia refugee camp in as many days.\par \par \uc2 Israel says fighter jets hit a terrorist command-and-control center and killed several members of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., the human rights office, says the strikes on Tuesday could amount to war crimes due to the high number of civilian casualties, all this as Israeli ground forces push deeper into Palestinian territory.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  commander says troops have broken through \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` front lines and are now at the gates of Gaza City.\par \par \uc2 We have team coverage from Israel to Washington.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata is going to start us off tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden said we will see more from those negotiations in the coming days and that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is working nonstop to get Americans out of Gaza as soon and as safely as possible.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voiceover): For a fortunate few, the nightmare of living under the constant fear of bombardment is over today, 500 people, mostly foreign passport holders, the first to cross from Gaza into Egypt since the conflict began, but only a handful of Americans so far.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  says around 400 U.S. citizens are still trying to get out of Gaza, but more Americans are expected to leave tomorrow, plus their families.\par \par \uc2 U.S. citizen Fathi Abu al-Hassan, is still trapped.\par \par \uc2 FATHI ABU AL-HASSAN (U.S. Citizen): No water, no food, no shelter, nothing, nothing. We open our eyes on the dead people, and we close our -- our eyes on dead people.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Today brought another deadly reminder of the chaos and carnage they`re fleeing. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  said another Israeli airstrike hit the Jabalia refugee camp this morning. More planes had already pounded the area the day before, devastating surrounding apartment buildings, rescuers emerging from the dust and debris cradling small children.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  say it was targeting and killed a senior \uc2 Hamas\uc2  commander and several militants. But civilians are among the dozens of people who died, including children, according to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  health officials. We can`t independently verify those numbers.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "I lost my whole family, 15 of them," says this man holding a list of their names. "They were innocent. What did they do?"\par \par \uc2 Fierce clashes have been reported from both sides in several locations along the Gaza Strip. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  released this new video said to show fighters emerging from tunnels and firing rocket-propelled grenades at Israeli tanks, and this video claiming to show a drone dropping a bomb on a group of Israeli soldiers gathered in a circle.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military reported today at least 16 of its soldiers have been killed in the past 24 hours of fighting. And rockets continue to get through Israeli defenses. A dashcam captured a rocket slamming into a highway near the border city of Ashdod. Miraculously, paramedics said only three people were lightly wounded.\par \par \uc2 But, for now, it`s the other side of the border that`s bearing the brunt of this war and why everyone is so desperate to escape while they can.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Charlie D`Agata is back with us.\par \par \uc2 And so, for those who were able to leave Gaza today, why did \uc2 Hamas\uc2  agree to this deal?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Norah, it`s not entirely clear.\par \par \uc2 We know a number of badly wounded Palestinians were able to cross into Egypt for medical care. But \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  spokesman \uc2 Matthew Miller\uc2  made clear today the U.S. is not making any concessions to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on the aid front -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is calling the opening of the Rafah Border Crossing for foreign nationals a significant breakthrough and the product of weeks of high-level negotiations.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 So what do we know about who was released?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Norah, so far, we have been able to confirm the names of five American women who were escorted safely into Egypt today. Most of them were serving in a humanitarian capacity in Gaza and simply got trapped there when the war broke out.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Barbara Zind, for instance, is a pediatrician from Grand Junction, Colorado. She arrived in Gaza just one day before \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacked Israel last month. She made it out alongside Ramona Okumura from Seattle. She`s an expert in making prosthetics for children.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials said a handful of Americans were able to get out today, no numbers, but they said they`re hoping to get the rest out over the next few days. And we know that, between the Americans who want to leave and their family members, it`s about 1,000 people, Norah, that the U.S. is trying to help evacuate.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That is a significant number.\par \par \uc2 And, Nancy, what have we learned about the role that President Biden played on this? I know he`s been working the phones.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was speaking in Minnesota today. He said that these new evacuations are the product of -- quote -- "urgent diplomacy" between himself and the leaders of Israel, Egypt and Qatar. He also made a point, Norah, of saying that Israel needs to follow international law as it continues to wage war on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House highly aware of mounting frustration in the Arab-American community, in particular over the civilian casualties in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 From the war in the Middle East to the rise in antisemitic incidents here at home. A 21-year-old junior at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  is facing federal charges after allegedly posting threats to kill or injure Jewish students at the Ivy League school.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano is on campus, where she spoke with several students about the ongoing fears.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): New York State Police are now standing guard outside the Cornell Center for Jewish Living, after authorities say Patrick Dai, a junior engineering student at Cornell, threatened to kill Jewish students, including those who eat at this kosher dining hall.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN BRADLEY (Cornell University Student): It was very tense on campus, a lot of students definitely impacted by it.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Today, the 21-year-old was in federal court after his arrest Tuesday and charged with making a string of antisemitic threats online, which included threatening to stab and slit the throats of Jewish students.\par \par \uc2 Police say they tracked him using his I.P. address and that he confessed.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (U.S. Attorney General): As this arrest shows, we are focusing our efforts on confronting and disrupting illegal threats wherever they arise.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: But Dai`s father told The New York Post via text that his son is innocent and that he`s suffering from severe depression, adding the family feared his son was suicidal.\par \par \uc2 Vigorous debate and protests have erupted on college campuses since the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war began, with some leading to violence and intimidation. \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2  (\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director): But the ongoing war in the Middle East has raised the threat of an attack against Americans in the United States to a whole `nother level.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Back on campus at Cornell, students say, despite an increase in police presence and an arrest, students are still concerned about safety and free speech.\par \par \uc2 DILAN MINUTELLO (Cornell University Student): I think people are a little bit more afraid to -- to, I guess, speak out, especially Jewish -- Jewish students, you know, because this is a threat against, you know, their -- their identity.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: I also heard from a group of Muslim students here on campus who condemned those horrific threats against Jewish students and said that they are a mockery of their religion.\par \par \uc2 They also pointed to messages that are Islamophobic and hateful on those message boards and said that they too feel unsafe in this increasingly tense environment -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Very tense.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, we`re learning more tonight about a terrifying confrontation in the cockpit of a \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  flight, where officials say a co-pilot threatened to shoot the plane`s captain mid-flight.\par \par \uc2 As CBS` Kris Van Cleave reports, that co-pilot is facing a felony charge that could land him in prison.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voiceover): Details remain limited, but, while working as the first officer on an unspecified \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  flight in late August 2022, Jonathan Dunn allegedly threatened the captain they would be shot multiple times if the captain diverted the flight.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Transportation Inspector General`s Office says the incident was prompted by a disagreement over a passenger medical event. Dunn of Marysville, California, was indicted by a Utah grand jury last month. Court documents allege he used a dangerous weapon in assaulting and intimidating the crew member.\par \par \uc2 He was authorized to carry a firearm through the TSA`s Federal Flight Deck Officer Program. TSA says Dunn has since been removed from that program. It was launched after 9/11 as an additional layer of security on domestic flights.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Drop the weapon!\par \par \uc2 JOHN PISTOLE\uc2  (Former Administrator, \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2 ): It serves as a last line of defense in case everything else goes bad.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: \uc2 John Pistole\uc2  is a former TSA Administrator.\par \par \uc2 FORMER ADMINISTRATOR \uc2 JOHN PISTOLE\uc2 : I think it`s worth having for the bad guys to know that, even if they can get the cockpit door open, that might be the last door they ever open.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The volunteer violets are vetted, which Pistole says includes cognitive and psychological aspects. They also complete a training program overseen by federal air marshals and are recertified twice a year.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Get back! Get back!\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-Connecticut): We need to take a hard look at the oversight program relating to the people who command airplanes in cockpits.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: This newly revealed incident follows another cockpit confrontation last month, when an off-duty \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  captain allegedly attempted to turn off a plane`s engines mid-flight.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT SUMWALT: I think, if you look at the big picture, our safety system is very good in this country.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Dunn is set to be arraigned in federal court on that felony charge later this month.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  requires pilots to have a valid medical certificate. We understand Dunn`s certificate has been suspended. Delta says he`s no longer employed by the airline. Our efforts to reach Dunn have so far been unsuccessful -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s quite a story.\par \par \uc2 Kris Van Cleave, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . took the stand today at the civil fraud trial that could determine the fate of the Trump family`s New York real estate empire. Trump Jr., an executive vice president with the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , said he was not in charge of accounting details at the company. The Trumps are accused of financial fraud, grossly overexaggerating the values of their properties.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the weather and the November chill that`s in the air over most of the country. More than 150 million Americans will see temperatures below freezing overnight. That`s from Mississippi to Maine.\par \par \uc2 For the cold forecast, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hey there, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 That fall chill is sticking around, but not for that much longer, OK? We have got about one more day to get through the really cold stuff, and then a gradual warm-up will move in. Notice, the temperatures tomorrow morning really cold, even down across the South, places like Montgomery. Alexandria, Austin, Texas, will have temperatures near freezing, if not below it, in Nashville, 28 degrees, temperatures freezing once you get across the Ohio Valley.\par \par \uc2 Now, the cold air is all courtesy of the jet stream. It`s taking a big dip to the South here. All that cold air from Canada has come southbound. However, that jet stream flattens going into the weekend. That means a lot of places here, temperatures rebound, a lot of places at or above average across dozens of states going into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 For example, 60s and 70s now return, Nashville, 71, 68 in St. Louis, temperatures in the 70s across the South here, Norah, and a trend that continues into Sunday and beyond.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 , thanks.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of teachers and school staffers went on strike today in Portland, Oregon, canceling school for about 49,000 students. The teachers, whose average salary is $82,000 a year, are demanding higher pay, more time to plan lessons, and a cap on class sizes. The union has advised parents to make plans for childcare, while 81 schools in the district are closed.\par \par \uc2 Now to the class action ruling that could change the way Americans buy and sell homes. A federal jury in Missouri on Tuesday found that the National Association of Realtors liable for artificially inflating commissions and awarded plaintiffs $1.8 billion in damages.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Carter Evans takes a look at what it means for the average consumer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEROD BREIT (Plaintiff): I think that this is going to drastically lower what commission rates are.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (voiceover): Jerod Breit`s long legal battle with the real estate industry began when he sold his St. Louis home back in 2017. He was frustrated that he had to pay a commission to the buyer`s broker.\par \par \uc2 JEROD BREIT: Certainly, I didn`t understand why I was paying for someone who I`d never met, will never meet, and wasn`t doing anything for me.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Here is how it works under the current system. For a $500,000 home, the seller agrees to pay a commission, typically 5 to 6 percent of the home`s selling price. That is then split between the seller`s agent and the buyer`s agent.\par \par \uc2 Sellers have to pay the commission in order to have their home included on the Multiple Listing Service, or MLS, a vast property database that feeds into popular sites like \uc2 Zillow\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KETCHMARK (Attorney For Jerod Breit): It doesn`t sound like that radical of a concept. You pay for the person who`s working for you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Breit`s attorney, Michael Ketchmark, brought the class action lawsuit against the National Association of Realtors and some of the largest real estate brokers in the country.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KETCHMARK: What we proved was, the National Association of Realtors has joined in a conspiracy with two of the largest corporate -- real estate brokers to use the system to fix prices.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: The court determined the current commission model makes it difficult for sellers and buyers to negotiate lower fees at a time when technology makes it easy for anyone to access real estate listings online.\par \par \uc2 The National Association of Realtors plans to appeal the verdict and says its rules serve the best interests of consumers.\par \par \uc2 PAMELA RICH (Realtor): It`s scary and it`s daunting. And so we will -- we will see. It`s a big shakeup.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: L.A. Realtor Pamela Rich says a shift in fees could put homes out of reach for some.\par \par \uc2 Who could this impact the most?\par \par \uc2 PAMELA RICH: I think the buyers. Although the seller may have some financial relief, the buyer is now the one that`s going to absorb the cost for the buyer`s fees. That`s a huge shift in the industry.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Now, it`s unclear if banks will allow buyers to fold those fees into their mortgage loans.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that comes at a time when interest rates are extremely high. Similar class action lawsuits are in the works across the country -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Such a big story.\par \par \uc2 Carter Evans, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now to some breaking news. Former Indiana Hoosiers basketball coach Bobby Knight has died at the age of 83. That`s according to an online statement by his family. Knight was one of the most successful coaches in the history of college basketball. He won three NCAA National Championships during his storied career at Army, Indiana, and \uc2 Texas Tech\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he was also known as much for his fiery temper on and off the court. Knight was fired as head coach at Indiana after getting into a confrontation with a student.\par \par \uc2 Millions more Americans should be tested for lung cancer. We have got the new guidelines for you next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: In tonight`s Health Watch: The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  has updated its guidelines for lung cancer screening.\par \par \uc2 The new recommendations expand the age range for testing to between 50 and 80 years old. And, for smokers who quit, the number of years since your last cigarette is no longer a factor for screening. It`s estimated that an additional five million Americans will now be eligible for regular screenings.\par \par \uc2 We have a consumer alert tonight about the cost of your Thanksgiving feast. That`s just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There is news tonight about the cost of hosting a Thanksgiving feast.\par \par \uc2 Turkey prices are down about 9 percent. That`s according to a new report from \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . The price of ham, however, is up more than 5 percent to $4.56 per pound. Fresh cranberries are 20 percent cheaper this -- than this time last year, but canned cranberries are up almost 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest names in music makes a rare public appearance. We will tell you who next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight, we leave you with some good news for fans of superstar Celine Dion.\par \par \uc2 The five-time Grammy Award winner made a rare public appearance on Monday to watch the NHL`s Las Vegas Golden Knights take on her hometown team, the \uc2 Montreal Canadiens\uc2 . It was her first public appearance in nearly four years after being diagnosed with a rare autoimmune and neurological disorder.\par \par \uc2 The "My Heart Will Go On" singer stepped into the locker room to meet the \uc2 Canadiens\uc2  players and was heard encouraging them, saying: "Just stay healthy, strong. Do what you do best."\par \par \uc2 Good to see her back.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231102ejb100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS For October 30, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Kevin Frazier, Jeff Pegues, Nancy Cordes, Ramy Inocencio, Debora  Patta, Charlie D`Agata, Norah O`Donnell \par \uc2 3,433 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: Israeli forces reach Gaza City, moving to cut off the main road there and ramping up strikes on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  targets. Plus, an Israeli female soldier taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is rescued, but there is sad news tonight about others held captive.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli Prime Minister): The Bible says that there was a time for peace and a time for war. This is a time for war.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Prime Minister Netanyahu stands firm in his mission to destroy \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , as the situation in Gaza grows desperate.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Despite the near-constant heavy bombardment of Gaza, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has shown itself capable of firing back. This just happened this morning. Residents here say a rocket came right through the window from the direction of Gaza and caused all this destruction.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Antisemitic incidents on the rise around the world. Hundreds storm a Russian airport looking for Israeli passengers. Back here at home, online threats against Jewish students at Cornell, the alarming spike.\par \par \uc2 The new details in the Maine mass shooting, why police were sent to the gunman`s house for a health and welfare check before the massacre that killed so many.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The makeshift memorials to the 18 victims are growing, as this community starts to heal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, tributes for Matthew Perry are pouring in. Plus, why it could take weeks to find out the cause of the sitcom star`s sudden death.\par \par \uc2 Warning for those who use over-the-counter eye drops. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  now says more than a dozen brands can cause infection and even vision loss. What you need to know.\par \par \uc2 End of the autoworkers strike? GM becomes the final company to reach a tentative deal after six weeks of picketing. What it means for wages.\par \par \uc2 And a Halloween treat for these sweet NICU babies and their families.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Having him in the NICU can feel really scary, so getting this little moment of joy is just a delight.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is escalating, with Israeli troops, tanks, and helicopters pushing deeper into Gaza. Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  tonight saying it is time for war and that there will be no cease-fire, because doing so would be like surrendering to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Israel`s military has reached Gaza City, and there are reports that they have cut off a key road that connects the territory. The military says a female soldier who has been held hostage since the October 7 attack was freed and is doing well.\par \par \uc2 The near-constant barrage of airstrikes continues, with Israel saying it hit hundreds of targets in the past 24 hours, killing dozens of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters. The \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -run Gaza Health Ministry says the number of people killed there now tops 8,000.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations is warning that the worsening humanitarian situation is resulting in the breakdown of civil order, with civilians ransacking relief centers.\par \par \uc2 We have team coverage night, starting with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Charlie D`Agata in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister`s address tonight was in English, clearly aimed at a global audience. He said the U.S. would not agree to a cease-fire after Pearl Harbor or 9/11; neither should Israel.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  released new video said to show its tanks and troops pushing deeper into Gaza, saying they struck 600 terror targets in the past 24 hours, and also announcing that an Israeli soldier held hostage in Gaza is back home.\par \par \uc2 Private Ori Megidish freed during an operation overnight, her emotional family reunion proof that, despite the risks, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said tonight, the ground incursion is working.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Israeli Prime Minister): The ground action actually creates the possibility, not the certainty, but the possibility of getting our hostages out, because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will not do it unless they`re under pressure.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But he earlier condemned as cruel psychological propaganda a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  video released today apparently showing three Israeli women being held captive, one seen fiercely criticizing the government, officials say likely under duress.\par \par \uc2 Today also brought tragic news about 23-year-old Shani Louk, the German- Israeli one of many young people kidnapped at a music concert, later seen paraded on the back of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  pickup truck. Investigators identified a piece of her skull matching her DNA.\par \par \uc2 The fate of the rest of more than 230 hostages remains in the balance as Israeli troops advance. Video on social media showed an Israeli tank in Gaza City blocking the territory`s main north-south highway and apparently opening fire on a vehicle.\par \par \uc2 And yet, even under intense bombardment, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  still managed to fire rockets into Israel today, with one hitting this apartment in Netivot. This doesn`t compare to the level of destruction in Gaza, but the Israeli government says, as long as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is capable of launching attacks like this, the fight in Gaza will go on.\par \par \uc2 With Netanyahu declaring tonight that international calls for a cease-fire are a call for Israel to surrender to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , the toll is growing for those trapped inside this bloody conflict.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  said today that the release of the American hostages remains a top priority and that negotiations are ongoing with Qatari mediators.\par \par \uc2 There are reports that a high-ranking Israeli intelligence chief is part of that; 10 Americans are still unaccounted for -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the White House is increasing pressure on Israel to allow more humanitarian aid into Gaza, as the situation worsens by the hour, the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . saying today conditions will soon make it impossible to operate in the besieged territory.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  Debora Patta reports, civilians are desperate for food and clean water.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice-over): This woman has been waiting for hours, and she is panicking.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "I just want some flour," she screams. "I need to feed my children."\par \par \uc2 Supplies of basic human necessities are running out. It`s become so desperate that residents broke into several United Nations warehouses. Underneath Gaza, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is dug into a maze of tunnels, where it`s believed they have been stockpiling enough food to last for months.\par \par \uc2 But for Palestinians, who`ve already endured 16 years of an Israeli blockade, daily life revolves around scrounging for anything they can find. And that`s if they survive another night.\par \par \uc2 CBS News producer Marwan Al Ghoul drove through what is left of the northern part of Gaza City.\par \par \uc2 MARWAN AL GHOUL: Suddenly, massive airstrike hit this house. I can see the bodies of a woman here. A little boy is here. And others are looking for more deaths and more victims.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Many are sheltering in hospitals, which continue to receive patients with catastrophic injuries, day in and day out, as doctors and nurses struggle to treat them. Grief is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: "They`re killing us," sobs this man.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military accuses \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of hiding beneath hospitals and has repeatedly ordered their evacuation. But the Red Crescent says hundreds will die if moved from intensive care. And for civilians seeking safety here, there is nowhere left to run to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Just 27 aid trucks entered Gaza today, but it is nowhere near enough, Norah.\par \par \uc2 And this comes just as the U.S. stresses Israel must prioritize the protection of civilians.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The White House was quick to condemn an intense display of antisemitism Sunday night, when a mob stormed an airport in the Russian-Muslim majority republic of Dagestan looking for Israeli passengers.\par \par \uc2 Today, National Security Council spokesman John Kirby called the incident a chilling demonstration of hate, bigotry and intimidation.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ramy Inocencio.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO (voice-over): An angry anti-Israel mob stormed the main airport in Dagestan Sunday evening, searching for Jewish passengers arriving on a flight from Tel Aviv, shouting "Allahu akbar," "God is great," waving Palestinian flags, some angry men breaking down doors in what appears to be secure areas.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Scared airport staff saying: "No, no, there are no Israelis here."\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: The crowd in the hundreds ran onto the tarmac, some surrounding one man and demanding his passport. He said he was from Uzbekistan.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 Red Wings\uc2  plane from Tel Aviv, passengers were told to get back on, and the doors were shut, this passenger filming police pinning people to the tarmac. The republic`s ministry officials say 60 people were detained for questioning and 20 people were injured. No Israeli citizens were harmed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Tel Aviv`s airport says it`s only operating at about 20 percent capacity, with most foreign carriers suspended.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday night, the office of Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  said Israel expects Russian law enforcement to protect all Israeli citizens -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Ramy Inocencio, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s also a troubling rise in antisemitism back here in the U.S. Now the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating disturbing and hate-filled threats against Jewish students at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes reports from the White House, where the Biden administration today announced new steps to combat the problem.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voice-over): A war being waged a half-a-world away is inflaming campuses here at home.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: If \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is worse than ISIS, then Israel is worse than Nazi Germany.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: At Cooper Union College in New York City, a group of Jewish students huddled inside a locked library during a pro-Palestinian protest a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CHELL (\uc2 NYPD\uc2  Chief of Patrol): For about roughly 10 minutes, approximately 10 minutes, they were banging on the doors of the library.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: At \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 , Jewish students were advised to avoid the kosher dining hall out of an abundance of caution due to online threats.\par \par \uc2 New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D-New York): No one should be afraid to walk from their dorm or their dining hall to a classroom.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: But Jewish students at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  said today they are afraid in a way they weren`t before.\par \par \uc2 ELI SHMIDMAN (Columbia Law School Student): We know now that there are students in our class that simply hate us because we`re Jewish.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Julia Jassey runs a nonprofit called Jewish on Campus.\par \par \uc2 In your view, what are some of the mistakes that some universities are making?\par \par \uc2 JULIA JASSEY, CEO (Jewish on Campus): I think it`s a really important time to understand that there`s a difference between political discourse and the harassment, the attacks on Jewish students on campus.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The sentiments aren`t confined to colleges. A Las Vegas man was just charged with one count of threatening a federal official after he left a series of antisemitic voice-mails, vowing to assault, kidnap or murder a U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell CBS News the target was Nevada Democrat \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2 . And, in Illinois, the man suspected of murdering a 6-year-old Palestinian-American boy, an alleged hate crime, made his first appearance in court today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Biden administration announced today it is detailing dozens of cybersecurity experts to help schools examine antisemitic and Islamophobic threats.\par \par \uc2 And the Departments of Justice and Homeland Security are now working with campus police departments to track hate-related rhetoric -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nancy Cordes at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the investigation into that mass shooting in Maine, CBS News just learned that the Army had directed the gunman`s commanders that he should not have a weapon, handle ammunition and not participate in live- fire activity, another sign that people knew how dangerous he was.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jeff Pegues reports on whether law enforcement could have done more to prevent the killing of 18 people.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES (voice-over): Tonight, the U.S. army confirmed to CBS News that, several months after Robert Card made threats and acted erratically in July, a health and welfare check request was initiated.\par \par \uc2 According to the Associated Press, the Sagadahoc County sheriff says, after the Army Reserve contacted his office, he sent a deputy to Card`s home for a welfare check, but he wasn`t there.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff`s office initiated a statewide alert, warning Maine law enforcement about Card in September. Maine`s Governor \uc2 Janet Mills\uc2  deflected questions about whether signs were missed.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JANET MILLS (D-Maine): The Maine State Police is undergoing a thorough investigation of every aspect of the case. Facts are important.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it did not receive any tips or information about Card from the military or Maine law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Chris Swecker is a former deputy director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SWECKER (Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director): It looks to me like the bigger failure was on the part of the Army. I think the sheriff`s department should have done a more proactive welfare check.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: We have an active shooter. We have multiple injuries.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: On Wednesday night, the gunman targeted this bar and grill and bowling alley. Ten-year-old Toni Asselin was inside at the time.\par \par \uc2 TONI ASSELIN (Witness): I saw someone get shot on the third shot.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Toni`s grandfather rushed to the bowling alley to reunite with his family. He waited anxiously for any signs of hope.\par \par \uc2 FERN ASSELIN (Grandfather): I heard four of the most beautiful words in the world I will never forget.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: What did you say to your grandpa?\par \par \uc2 TONI ASSELIN: "I`m not dead, Pepe."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Toni`s mother is concerned about the impact witnessing what happened here last Wednesday night is going to have on her 10-year-old daughter.\par \par \uc2 Norah, a gun shop owner in this area says that Card tried to purchase a silencer for one of his guns. Ultimately, he was found with numerous firearms in his possession.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Just chilling.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Pegues, thank you.\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  reached a tentative agreement, a contract agreement, with the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  today, finally ending the six-week strike. The union made similar deals with Ford and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The contract, which includes a 25 percent pay raise over more than four-and-a- half years, still has to be ratified by union members. It will be weeks before the factories are fully running again.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the shocking and tragic death of actor Matthew Perry. Toxicology results are still pending for the Friends star who died over the weekend at the age of 54. Tonight, we`re hearing for this first time from his fellow actors in the hit sitcom.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement to "People" magazine, they said: "We were more than just castmates. We are a family. We`re going to take a moment to grieve and process this unfathomable loss."\par \par \uc2 Here`s Entertainment Tonight co-host Kevin Frazier.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY (Actor): All right, somebody, kiss me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER (voice-over): You could say Matthew Perry became friends with millions of TV viewers as the quick-witted, often sarcastic Chandler Bing.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY: I got her machine.\par \par \uc2 MATT LEBLANC (Actor): Her answering machine?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY: No, interestingly enough, her leaf blower picked up.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And, tonight, remembrances continue to pour in for the friend they have lost, including in front of the fictional New York apartment where his character lived.\par \par \uc2 The 54-year-old Perry was found unresponsive in a hot tub at his Pacific Palisades home late Saturday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCH: Engine 23, Rescue 23, EMS 9, all on radio, respond to the drowning.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Investigators reportedly found no signs of foul play or illegal drugs in the home. Just a week ago, Perry posted an image of himself in his hot tub on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY: I`m not going to be embarrassed anymore.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: Friends premiered in 1994 and featured Perry for all 10 seasons, earning him one of several Emmy nominations.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY: I can`t do it, Toby. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: That praise includes his turn to drama as a Republican lawyer in The West Wing.\par \par \uc2 Perry`s decade-long struggle with drugs and alcohol were revealed in his memoir last year.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY: I want to drink all the time, because it`s the only time I feel good and normal. But then it turns on you. The drink turns on you and says, well, now I`m going to kill you.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: In the book, Perry described how he attended 6,000 A.A. meetings and had been in rehab 15 times. He claimed he spent around $9 million just to get sober.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW PERRY: If somebody comes up to me and says, I can`t stop drinking, can you help me, I can say yes and follow up and do it.\par \par \uc2 And when I die, I don`t want Friends to be the first thing that`s mentioned. I want that to be the first thing that`s mentioned. And I`m going to live the rest of my life proving that.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN FRAZIER: And Perry was working on expanding his foundation`s efforts to help as many as possible that were struggling with addiction. He was also working on his own well-being. Friends tell us he was playing pickleball twice a day.\par \par \uc2 Officials say it could take weeks to determine what caused his death -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Kevin Frazier, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the weather. Halloween deep freeze? The frigid forecast is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A major cold snap is set to grip much of the Central U.S., with 90 million Americans under freeze or frost alerts over the next two nights, that`s right, including Halloween.\par \par \uc2 Freeze warnings are posted in more than a dozen states from parts of California, Arizona, New Mexico all the way to the Northeast, making it a chilly day for trick-or-treaters. By Wednesday, we could see dozens of record lows.\par \par \uc2 A new warning tonight about some over-the-counter eye drops. What you need to know next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, an important consumer alert about widely used eye drops.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is warning people to stop using more than two dozen over-the- counter eye drops products because of a potential risk of eye infection that could lead to blindness. Federal health officials say CVS, \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2 , and Target are removing their products. For more information on the warning of products, go to CBSNews.com.\par \par \uc2 Trick-or-treat comes early for the newborns in this NICU. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight, we leave you with some of the sweetest treats.\par \par \uc2 Newborns at the Cedars-Sinai NICU continue the annual tradition of dressing up to celebrate the holiday. Volunteers have been making the outfits for more than 25 years to help the babies and their parents get into the Halloween spirit. This year`s costumes were inspired by children`s books and included a cat, sunflower, pumpkin, bat, and more.\par \par \uc2 That`s cute.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s CBS Evening News. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 We will leave you tonight with this video of President Biden and first lady Jill Biden handing out candy to a local public school students and military-connected children at the White House for Halloween.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020231031ejau00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for October 7, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Imtiaz Tyab, Ian Lee, Christina Ruffini, Elise  Preston, Michael George, Lynette Charles, Mark Strassmann, \par \uc2 3,565 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tonight, breaking news: Israel strikes back after a surprise attack. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants from Gaza stun Israel firing thousands of rockets and invading border towns and army bases. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  taking captives as people in Gaza celebrate.\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU (Through Translation): We are at war.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  says Israel is at war. Fighter jets take aim at high rises in Gaza. Hundreds are feared dead, thousands injured.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and other Western leaders condemning the attacks.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Let there be no mistake, the United States stands with the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We`ll have the latest.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, striking California health care workers return to work with no deal even as the job market booms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, migrant message. New York City`s mayor in Latin America warns his city has no more room as the crisis intensify.\par \par \uc2 And later, Washington`s National Cathedral has a new view. The story behind this stained glass and the stain on history it replaced.\par \par \uc2 KERRY JAMES MARSHALL (Artists and professor): Pretty brilliant. Pretty bright.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s glowing.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from Washington with Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Today, Israel battle to repel a surprise invasion of its territory after Palestinian militants launched highly coordinated attacks from Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of rockets were fired into Israel as gunmen infiltrated several border towns and bases, kidnapping civilians and soldiers. Israel`s Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  declared the country was, quote, "at war." He said their enemy would pay an unprecedented price.\par \par \uc2 Israeli warplanes took aim at Gaza, among the targets hit, this high rise which was reduced to rubble. Hundreds have been killed on both sides and it is a sharp escalation of violence for a region that bears the scars of centuries of conflict.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Imtiaz Tyab in Tel Aviv leads us off.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Adriana, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a fresh wave of rockets has hit Tel Aviv with the Israeli military saying it is fighting in 22 locations as the government confirms over 250 Israelis are now dead, at least 1,600 injured and an unknown number remain in captivity.\par \par \uc2 The question many Israelis are asking now is how? How was \uc2 Hamas\uc2  able to carry out such a massive attack? Remember, Israel arguably has the most powerful and best-funded intelligence agencies in the Middle East. It spends millions to secure its border fence with Gaza to prevent something like this from happening. And yet, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fighters were able to carry out such a catastrophic attack.\par \par \uc2 As for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , what it is calling Operation Al-Aqsa Flood will no doubt see the death toll in Gaza spiral as Israel continues to retaliate. So far, at least 198 Palestinians have been killed and over 1,600 wounded in a series of strikes, according to the territory`s Ministry of Health, as armed Palestinian groups in the occupied West Bank have vowed tonight to join \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in its ongoing assault.\par \par \uc2 And while the Israeli leadership is bound to inflict an, quote, "unprecedented price" on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , Adriana, this is unchartered territory for Israel, which now finds itself in a full-scale war.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Imtiaz Tyab in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tensions between \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Israel exploded on a major Jewish holiday catching the Israeli military off guard.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Ian Lee reports from London.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Gaza militants started their surprise attack this morning, launching thousands of rockets into Israel, following it up with an invasion by land, sea, and even air.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Social media showed them quickly sweeping into Israeli military bases and communities, killing hundreds and injuring hundreds more.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: This Israeli woman says she was able to escape an outdoor party attacked by gunmen.\par \par \uc2 Palestinian militants attacked on multiple fronts. Videos of the aftermath show bodies of Israeli civilians scattered across the streets.\par \par \uc2 During the assault, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  used drones to target machine guns, soldiers and to even hit a tank. While captured Israeli equipment was paraded through the streets of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Videos and photos also allegedly show Gaza militants abducting an unknown number of Israelis, including children and the elderly, and taking them back into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 With a country in shock, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  declared war.\par \par \uc2 (PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: He told his Security Cabinet, the first objective is to clear out enemy forces and restore security. The second is to exact an immense price from the enemy. And the third is to reinforce other fronts, so nobody mistakenly joins this war.\par \par \uc2 Already, Israel has responded with airstrikes targeting what they say are \uc2 Hamas\uc2  targets. Palestinian officials in Gaza say hundreds of people have been killed.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE shouting in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: A \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Commander called on Palestinians everywhere to rise up against Israel.\par \par \uc2 Israeli soldiers are now fighting to retake captured ground while tens of thousands of soldiers are being called up for duty.\par \par \uc2 (MEN shouting in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Tensions have been building for months between Palestinians and Israelis in the West Bank, and Jerusalem. Many in the international community have been quick to condemn today`s attack. But the scale, sophistication, and complexity of the attack have left many stunned, comparing it to the surprise attack by Egyptians during the Yom Kippur War 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Tonight, many are wondering how Israeli intelligence could fail to detect such an ambitious attack.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, I`ve been on both sides of the Israel-Gaza border. It is heavily monitored and fortified. But getting to the bottom of those questions might have to wait as the country prepares for what is shaping up to be a very long and bloody war.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ian Lee, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden condemned the attacks against Israel reaffirming the U.S.` commitment to the Jewish state security.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Christina Ruffini is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Christina, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good evening. Adriana.\par \par \uc2 The president was told about the attacks early this morning and senior staff worked through the night. The White House is also pushing back tonight against Republican claims that this attack by Iran-backed terror group \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has anything to do with assets that were unfrozen during the recent release of detained Americans from Tehran.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The United States stands with the people of Israel in the face of these terrorist assaults. Israel has the right to defend itself and its people.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Speaking by phone to Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , President Biden says America`s commitment to Israel`s security is unwavering.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : This is not a moment for any party hostile to Israel to exploit these attacks to seek advantage. The world is watching.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: But domestic political divisions could complicate that commitment.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Fox News)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): There is nothing the House can do until they elect a speaker.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Members of Congress, including ousted Speaker Kevin McCarthy are warning that the lack of a House speaker will hinder America`s ability to respond.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE SETH MOULTON (D-Massachusetts): So if we have to come together to deliver some emergency aid to Israel, we don`t even have a speaker in place to do that right now.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: It`s even complicating national security briefings for members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R, 2024 presidential candidate): Iran is funding \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: On the campaign trail, Republican candidates seized on a prisoner exchange the White House negotiated in September, which unfroze $6 billion in Iranian assets.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I would not be at all surprised if part of that tremendous wealth that they just accumulated went into all of a sudden watching this level of aggression.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: But the White House pushed back hard saying that not a penny of the money has been spent and that these funds have absolutely nothing to do with the horrific attacks today, and this is not a time to spread disinformation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: A senior White House official says it`s still too soon to say if Iran was directly involved in this \uc2 Hamas\uc2  assault, but that Iran has funded the group in the past -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Christina Ruffini, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today`s striking health care workers in five states and the District of Columbia returned to work after a three-day walkout. But other labor unrest goes on.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston is in Los Angeles tonight as economic forces pushed unions to the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Adriana, more than 450,000 people have walked off their jobs this year. Many who are still striking say they will keep fighting for higher wages.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Seventy-five thousand \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  health care workers are back at work today after a three-day strike failed to produce a deal.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are not done. We are ready to go next week.\par \par \uc2 (GROUP chanting)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Auto workers rallied in Chicago today as the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , after a concession from \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , decided not to expand its walkouts, while Ford announced more strike-related layoffs, now totaling 1,800 employees.\par \par \uc2 The workers are among the millions of Americans feeling the sting of inflation. The surprisingly strong U.S. jobs report with 336,000 added to payrolls could lead the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates again next month.\par \par \uc2 GREG MCBRIDE (Bankrate chief financial officer): Higher interest rates are designed to reduce demand in the economy so that inflation comes down and we can get prices back under control.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Borrowing money is more expensive, with mortgage rates nearing eight percent, and interest for credit cards soaring to 22 percent, a number not seen in 30 years.\par \par \uc2 Also straining household budgets, the price of gas and groceries\par \par \uc2 GREG MCBRIDE: Even though household costs aren`t going up as fast as they had been, they are still a lot higher than they were just two or three years ago, and the income hasn`t kept up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now the economy is proving strong despite some challenges. Experts advise, now is a good time to pay down credit card debt and ramp up your emergency savings -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good advice. Elise Preston, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, New York City Mayor Eric Adams ended a four-day trip to Latin America, urging countries in the region to find solutions to the migrant crisis being felt across the Americas.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Michael George is in New York with the latest -- Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Adriana, good evening.\par \par \uc2 We`re outside the Roosevelt Hotel. It`s a temporary home for many asylum seekers. Meanwhile, New York`s mayor is in Latin America trying to discourage migrants from coming to the city.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D-New York City): We need a real analysis.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: New York City Mayor Eric Adams is wrapping up a tour of Mexico, Ecuador, and Colombia, key points on the migrants` dangerous journey to America.\par \par \uc2 He says asylum seekers are being fed misinformation that New York has jobs and shelter, but the city has run out of both.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS: We want every single person who`s thinking about risking their lives and their children`s lives to come to New York City to know the truth about what we are dealing with.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Still, hundreds more migrants are bused from border states to northern cities every week. In Chicago, thousands including children are sleeping on the street, inside police stations, or the airport as that city hurries to build more shelters before winter.\par \par \uc2 LAURA MENDOZA (Resurrection Project): The concern is just how are they going to be able to withstand the cold.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: There were more than 200,000 apprehensions of migrants who crossed the border illegally in September, the highest level this year. Aid workers say the influx is draining resources.\par \par \uc2 J. ANTONIO FERNANDEZ (San Antonio Catholic Charities CEO): We are running out of money faster than we had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: With calls for more federal action growing louder, the Biden administration announced it will build a 20-mile section of border wall near the Rio Grande. Critics call it hypocrisy. The president says he has no choice.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Congress passes legislation to build something, whether it`s an aircraft carrier, a wall, or provide for a tax cut. I can`t say I don`t like it. I`m not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GEORGE: Adams will now return to New York City. He recently tightened shelter rules to limit adults to just 30 days in city-run facilities. He calls it a necessary step to reduce overcrowding -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Michael George, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, people across the country are facing a wide variety of weather risks including a blistering heat wave and sudden cold snap.\par \par \uc2 Lynette Charles has the latest from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LYNETTE CHARLES (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 It is going to be quite chilly out there, especially across the East Coast. We can see what`s going on with those temperatures in the 40s and the 50s. But as we go into your tomorrow morning and afternoon, across the West, yes, we`re looking at these temperatures a little bit warmer.\par \par \uc2 Look at Sacramento, temperatures in the 90s; LA coming in the 80s, and we can see Seattle down off towards the south. Plenty of the red there. So we`re looking at 71 million people above average as we move into your tomorrow; more of the same as we head into your Monday and then we also do have some rain to talk about as well.\par \par \uc2 Across the West here, plenty of rain and in the higher elevations, oh, yes, we`ll see some mountain snow out there as well.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, back to you.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Lynette Charles from \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Powerball jackpot is now standing at an astounding $1.4 billion. The big prize is drawing big lines for tickets. Powerball has not had a grand prize winner since July, a streak of 33 drawings. $1.4 billion would be the fifth largest payout in U.S. lottery history.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, a new take on space tourism that leaves the rockets on Earth.\par \par \uc2 And a disturbing discovery at a Colorado funeral home.\par \par \uc2 And the effort to right a wrong at one of our nation`s most important churches.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: A new space tourism venture is aiming high, promising to send people into space, but they won`t get there on rockets.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann in Tucson, Arizona has more on the next great leap.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RYAN HARTMAN\uc2  (\uc2 World View Enterprises\uc2  CEO): A stratellite is a set of technologies that includes a lift balloon, a ballast balloon and a -- and a craft.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 Ryan Hartman\uc2  is the CEO of \uc2 World View Enterprises\uc2 . His company uses high altitude helium balloons to collect data and images from the stratosphere. But Hartman has other aspirations -- to send up not just tools, but humans, too.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HARTMAN\uc2 : This is the balloon farm. So this is where we manufacture all of our stratospheric balloons. If it`s one of our future space tourism balloons, it`ll be the length of this table, two football fields long.\par \par \uc2 When we bring space tourism to the marketplace at scale, we`ll do 500 to 700 flights per year in space tourism.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Five hundred to 700 flights?\par \par \uc2 RYAN HARTMAN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HARTMAN\uc2 : This is a full-size mock-up of the Explorer space capsule. We often talk about what we`re selling is a view, right? And so the window has to be perfect.\par \par \uc2 You have to be able to design a window that is safe for operating in space, but also delivers the promise of an inspired perspective.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from World View)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: World View plans to operate seven launch destinations to send tourists high above seven unique wonders of the world. The price, $50,000.\par \par \uc2 RYAN HARTMAN\uc2 : Our approach is to deliver something that is more attainable from the point of view that it`s available to a larger age range of people, and it is something that that feels more, I`ll say, doable for people, that it`s a safer way to experience viewing our Earth from on high.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Mark Strassmann, CBS News, Tucson.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, what Colorado Police found when responding to a foul smell.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The hunt is on for a killer in Vermont. Honoree Fleming, a retired Vermont State University dean was shot dead on a trail in the town of Castleton. Police are looking for a dark-haired man carrying a gray backpack who was seen in the area shortly after gunfire was heard.\par \par \uc2 Fleming, who was 77, was the wife of best-selling author, Ron Powers.\par \par \uc2 Now to a grim discovery in rural Colorado, 115 decaying bodies were found at the Return to Nature Funeral Home in the town of Penrose. The company specialized in performing green burials that used biodegradable caskets and no embalming.\par \par \uc2 Officials described the scene as horrific.\par \par \uc2 Next on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, it is one of the world`s most prestigious races, the competition flying high in the sky.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tonight, what`s being called a Balloon Fiesta in Albuquerque, New Mexico. This video shows the mass launch on a beautiful fall day.\par \par \uc2 Among those gathered, crews from all over the world, who are racing in one of the most prestigious events in aviation. The team that travels the farthest without refueling wins.\par \par \uc2 The competition is expected to last three days and could stretch all the way into Canada.\par \par \uc2 When we return, correcting a national stain in stained glass.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We end tonight in Washington where the National Cathedral is shining a new light for the fight for racial justice by removing part of its own dark past.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: If the walls could talk at Washington`s National Cathedral, they`d recount some of our nation`s most profound moments.\par \par \uc2 But these walls have also upheld a stain in stained glass. Tributes to Confederate Generals Robert E. Lee, Thomas "Stonewall" Jackson, and their flag. They`ve now been replaced with protesters marching for equality.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN#1: I wanted to be here to witness it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN#2: I`ve never seen anything like. So I`m like, "Wow."\par \par \uc2 KERRY JAMES MARSHALL: It is pretty brilliant, pretty bright.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s glowing.\par \par \uc2 It`s the work of renowned Chicago artist, Kerry James Marshall, whose pieces can go for millions. His prize for this, $18.65, a nod to slavery`s end.\par \par \uc2 KERRY JAMES MARSHALL: I think these windows simply announce themselves to the passerby in a way that mostly the other windows into Cathedral don`t.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Also, unlike the other windows here, there`s a wheelchair, no faces and --\par \par \uc2 In a church, you`re so used to looking up at the windows and seeing White skin and seeing Black skin here, it is almost like you have to do a double take.\par \par \uc2 KERRY JAMES MARSHALL: I don`t think these windows exclude anybody because I think the activity of what they engage in is something that everybody can partake in.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Below are words by poet, Elizabeth Alexander:\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH ALEXANDER (Poet): Each day --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Who performed at President Obama`s first inauguration.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH ALEXANDER: The final line of the poem, "May this portal be where the light comes in," that can illuminate the beauty of the past and also sometimes the untruths of the past.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: What does it mean to you that your words are here represented in such a historical place?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH ALEXANDER: They will outlive me by a very, very, very, very long measure. And what that means is actually beyond words for me right now.\par \par \uc2 KERRY JAMES MARSHALL: I don`t think I could have asked for anything more meaningful to have done in my life as a kind of gift to the nation as a whole.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: An incredible gift.\par \par \uc2 That is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Saturday. First thing tomorrow, "Sunday Morning" with Jane Pauley followed by "Face the Nation." Among Margaret Brennan`s guests, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , and Israeli Ambassador Michael Herzog.\par \par \uc2 For now, I`m Adriana Diaz in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Goodnight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or bro